// Karteikarten: #22/#64/#65/#68 handgeschrieben, Rest automatisch aus den Stage-Zusammenfassungen (Lückentext/Tabellen)
window.STAGE_CARDS={"1":[["050.01.01.03:01","Structure of the atmosphere: fill in the gaps → Layers (by temperature): ____ (temperature falls with height, all weather, 0 → ____ ____), tropopause (boundary, temperature stops falling), stratosphere (isothermal then warming with height due to ozone absorbing UV, up to ~50 km), mesosphere, thermosphere. Up to FL 650 we deal with troposphere + lower stratosphere.","Layers (by temperature): troposphere (temperature falls with height, all weather, 0 → 8 km poles / 11 km mid-lat / 16–18 km equator), tropopause (boundary, temperature stops falling), stratosphere (isothermal then warming with height due to ozone absorbing UV, up to ~50 km), mesosphere, thermosphere. Up to FL 650 we deal with troposphere + lower stratosphere."],["050.01.01.02:03","Structure of the atmosphere: fill in the gaps → ____: ~75–80 % of the atmosphere's mass, almost all water vapour, clouds, convection. Gases (dry air, constant proportions): ____ + variable water vapour (0–4 %).","Troposphere: ~75–80 % of the atmosphere's mass, almost all water vapour, clouds, convection. Gases (dry air, constant proportions): N₂ 78 %, O₂ 21 %, Ar ~0.9 %, CO₂ 0.04 % + variable water vapour (0–4 %)."],["050.01.01.02:04","Structure of the atmosphere: fill in the gaps → ____: height ____ (FL 500–550, ~−75 °C) than over the poles (FL 250–300, ~−45 °C); ____, higher over high pressure/warm air, lower over lows/cold air; breaks at the polar front and subtropical front (jet streams located at the breaks).","Tropopause: height higher and colder over the equator (FL 500–550, ~−75 °C) than over the poles (FL 250–300, ~−45 °C); higher in summer, higher over high pressure/warm air, lower over lows/cold air; breaks at the polar front and subtropical front (jet streams located at the breaks)."],["050.01.01.03:02","Structure of the atmosphere: fill in the gaps → ____: stable, little weather, temperature roughly constant (ISA −56.5 °C to 20 km) then rising; ____ concentrations can occur at jet cruise altitudes (especially high latitudes, spring) — irritant to crew/passengers → ozone converters.","Stratosphere: stable, little weather, temperature roughly constant (ISA −56.5 °C to 20 km) then rising; ozone concentrations can occur at jet cruise altitudes (especially high latitudes, spring) — irritant to crew/passengers → ozone converters."],["050.01.02.01:01","Temperature: fill in the gaps → Air temperature = measure of molecular kinetic energy; units ____ ____ (°F = °C × 9/5 + 32; K = °C + 273).","Air temperature = measure of molecular kinetic energy; units °C, °F, K (°F = °C × 9/5 + 32; K = °C + 273)."],["050.01.02.02:01","Temperature: fill in the gaps → ____: decrease ~____ ____ in the troposphere, isothermal at tropopause/lower stratosphere. Cooling with height because the air is heated from below (surface absorbs solar radiation) and expanding (rising) air cools adiabatically.","Mean vertical distribution: decrease ~2 °C/1 000 ft (0.65 °C/100 m) in the troposphere, isothermal at tropopause/lower stratosphere. Cooling with height because the air is heated from below (surface absorbs solar radiation) and expanding (rising) air cools adiabatically."],["050.01.06.03:06","Temperature: fill in the gaps → ____: ISA temperature at a level = 15 − 2 × (altitude in 1 000 ft); e.g. FL 180: ISA −21 °C; actual −15 °C → ____.","ISA deviation: ISA temperature at a level = 15 − 2 × (altitude in 1 000 ft); e.g. FL 180: ISA −21 °C; actual −15 °C → ISA +6."],["050.01.02.03:04","Temperature: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ — sun emits ____ radiation, atmosphere absorbs little of it (filtering: ozone absorbs UV, clouds reflect, scattering), surface absorbs and re-emits long-wave (terrestrial, infrared) radiation which is absorbed by water vapour, CO₂ and clouds (greenhouse effect); clouds reflect sunlight by day and trap outgoing radiation at night.","Heat transfer: radiation — sun emits short-wave radiation, atmosphere absorbs little of it (filtering: ozone absorbs UV, clouds reflect, scattering), surface absorbs and re-emits long-wave (terrestrial, infrared) radiation which is absorbed by water vapour, CO₂ and clouds (greenhouse effect); clouds reflect sunlight by day and trap outgoing radiation at night."],["050.01.02.06:03","Temperature: fill in the gaps → ____ — heat exchange by contact (only affects a thin layer next to the surface; night cooling/day heating of the lowest air). ____ — vertical transport by rising warm air (strong surface heating, cold air over warm surface).","Conduction — heat exchange by contact (only affects a thin layer next to the surface; night cooling/day heating of the lowest air). Convection — vertical transport by rising warm air (strong surface heating, cold air over warm surface)."],["050.01.02.03:12","Temperature: fill in the gaps → ____ — horizontal transport of air (warm/cold air advection, sea breeze, fronts). ____ — mixing spreads heat through the friction layer. ____ — released on condensation/freezing, absorbed on evaporation/melting (major energy source for storms).","Advection — horizontal transport of air (warm/cold air advection, sea breeze, fronts). Turbulence — mixing spreads heat through the friction layer. Latent heat — released on condensation/freezing, absorbed on evaporation/melting (major energy source for storms)."],["050.01.02.04:01","Temperature: fill in the gaps → ____: ELR average ____; actual values vary (surface inversions, superadiabatic near hot ground). DALR 3 °C/1 000 ft, SALR ~1.8 °C/1 000 ft (see 050-02).","Lapse rates: ELR average 0.65 °C/100 m = ~2 °C/1 000 ft; actual values vary (surface inversions, superadiabatic near hot ground). DALR 3 °C/1 000 ft, SALR ~1.8 °C/1 000 ft (see 050-02)."],["050.01.02.01:01","Temperature: fill in the gaps → ____ (temperature increases with height) and ____ ____ are very stable, suppress vertical motion (trap haze, fog, pollution; cause wind shear at their boundary).","Inversions (temperature increases with height) and isothermal layers are very stable, suppress vertical motion (trap haze, fog, pollution; cause wind shear at their boundary)."],["050.01.02.05:03","Temperature: fill in the gaps → Types: ____ (clear calm night, surface cools), ____ (warm air over cold surface), ____ inversion (sinking air warms adiabatically in highs), frontal inversion (warm air above cold air at a front), inversion above the friction layer (turbulent mixing), valley inversion (cold air drains into valleys).","Types: ground/radiation inversion (clear calm night, surface cools), advection inversion (warm air over cold surface), subsidence inversion (sinking air warms adiabatically in highs), frontal inversion (warm air above cold air at a front), inversion above the friction layer (turbulent mixing), valley inversion (cold air drains into valleys)."],["050.01.02.06:04","Temperature: fill in the gaps → ____: daily cycle — maximum temperature ~____ ____, minimum around sunrise. Clouds reduce both daytime heating and night cooling (small diurnal range); wind mixes the air → less night cooling (no strong inversion) and less daytime heating; land heats/cools faster than sea.","Near the surface: daily cycle — maximum temperature ~2 h after noon, minimum around sunrise. Clouds reduce both daytime heating and night cooling (small diurnal range); wind mixes the air → less night cooling (no strong inversion) and less daytime heating; land heats/cools faster than sea."],["050.01.03.01:03","Pressure: fill in the gaps → ____ = weight of the air column above per unit area. Units ____, ____ (1013.25 hPa = 29.92 inHg). Mercury barometer (column height), aneroid barometer (evacuated capsule, used in altimeters).","Atmospheric pressure = weight of the air column above per unit area. Units hPa (= mbar), inHg (1013.25 hPa = 29.92 inHg). Mercury barometer (column height), aneroid barometer (evacuated capsule, used in altimeters)."],["050.01.03.01:05","Pressure: fill in the gaps → ____ = lines of equal pressure (QFF) on surface charts. ____ (anticyclone), ____ (depression), trough (elongated low, V-shaped isobars), ridge (elongated high), col (neutral area between two highs and two lows).","Isobars = lines of equal pressure (QFF) on surface charts. High (anticyclone), low (depression), trough (elongated low, V-shaped isobars), ridge (elongated high), col (neutral area between two highs and two lows)."],["050.01.03.02:03","Pressure: fill in the gaps → Pressure decreases with height, faster at low levels (dense air): ____ (use ____ for calculations), ~50 ft/hPa at 18 000 ft, ~70 ft at 30 000 ft; faster decrease in cold air. ____; density 50 % at ~22 000 ft, 25 % at ~40 000 ft.","Pressure decreases with height, faster at low levels (dense air): ~30 ft/hPa near MSL (use 30 ft/hPa for calculations), ~50 ft/hPa at 18 000 ft, ~70 ft at 30 000 ft; faster decrease in cold air. 50 % of MSL pressure at ~18 000 ft; density 50 % at ~22 000 ft, 25 % at ~40 000 ft."],["050.01.03.03:03","Pressure: fill in the gaps → ____: QFE reduced to MSL using the ____ (for weather charts); QNH uses ISA.","QFF: QFE reduced to MSL using the actual temperature (for weather charts); QNH uses ISA."],["050.01.03.04:01","Pressure: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ intensify with height; ____ at the surface become highs aloft and cold highs become lows aloft (pressure surfaces lower in cold air) — shown in vertical cross-sections of isobaric surfaces.","Surface vs upper pressure systems: cold lows / warm highs intensify with height; warm (thermal) lows at the surface become highs aloft and cold highs become lows aloft (pressure surfaces lower in cold air) — shown in vertical cross-sections of isobaric surfaces."],["050.01.04.01:02","Density & ISA: fill in the gaps → ____: density ↑ with pressure, ↓ with temperature (and ↓ with humidity). Density decreases with height (less rapidly than pressure because temperature also falls).","ρ = p/(R·T): density ↑ with pressure, ↓ with temperature (and ↓ with humidity). Density decreases with height (less rapidly than pressure because temperature also falls)."],["050.01.05.01:02","Density & ISA: fill in the gaps → ISA gives a reference for altimeter calibration, performance and instruments: ____, lapse rate ____ to the ____ km (36 090 ft), −56.5 °C, then constant −56.5 °C to 20 km (FL 650), then +1 °C/km.","ISA gives a reference for altimeter calibration, performance and instruments: MSL 1013.25 hPa, +15 °C, 1.225 kg/m³, lapse rate −1.98 °C/1 000 ft (6.5 °C/km) to the tropopause at 11 km (36 090 ft), −56.5 °C, then constant −56.5 °C to 20 km (FL 650), then +1 °C/km."],["050.01.06.01:01","Altimetry: fill in the gaps → ____ (vertical distance above a datum, e.g. aerodrome — QFE), ____ (above MSL — QNH), ____ (altitude in ISA corresponding to the pressure — 1013.25), flight level (pressure altitude in hundreds of ft), pressure level (level of constant pressure), true altitude (actual height above MSL), true height (actual height above ground), elevation (height of a point on the ground above MSL).","Height (vertical distance above a datum, e.g. aerodrome — QFE), altitude (above MSL — QNH), pressure altitude (altitude in ISA corresponding to the pressure — 1013.25), flight level (pressure altitude in hundreds of ft), pressure level (level of constant pressure), true altitude (actual height above MSL), true height (actual height above ground), elevation (height of a point on the ground above MSL)."],["050.01.06.03:07","Altimetry: fill in the gaps → ____; ____; ____ (lowest FL that gives the required terrain clearance, depends on QNH and temperature).","TA/TL/transition layer; terrain clearance; lowest usable FL (lowest FL that gives the required terrain clearance, depends on QNH and temperature)."],["050.01.06.03:01","Altimetry: fill in the gaps → ____: QNH below TA (and on the ground reads elevation), 1013.25 above TA (FL), QFE for height above the aerodrome/threshold.","Procedures: QNH below TA (and on the ground reads elevation), 1013.25 above TA (FL), QFE for height above the aerodrome/threshold."],["050.01.06.01:01","Altimetry: fill in the gaps → ____ (use 30 ft/hPa): aircraft at FL 50, QNH 1003 → altitude = 5 000 − (1013 − 1003) × 30 = ____. On the ground at elevation 1 200 ft, QNH 1023: QFE setting → 0 ft; QNH → 1 200 ft; 1013 → 1 200 + 10 × 30 = ____ (pressure altitude).","Calculations (use 30 ft/hPa): aircraft at FL 50, QNH 1003 → altitude = 5 000 − (1013 − 1003) × 30 = 4 700 ft. On the ground at elevation 1 200 ft, QNH 1023: QFE setting → 0 ft; QNH → 1 200 ft; 1013 → 1 200 + 10 × 30 = 1 500 ft (pressure altitude)."],["050.01.03.01:05","Altimetry: fill in the gaps → ____: in ____ air the pressure levels are closer together → ____ than indicated (\"from warm to cold or high to low, look out below\"); in warm air higher.","Temperature effect: in colder than ISA air the pressure levels are closer together → true altitude lower than indicated (\"from warm to cold or high to low, look out below\"); in warm air higher."],["050.01.06.03:06","Altimetry: fill in the gaps → ____: true altitude differs by ____ ISA deviation (≈ 4 ft per 1 °C per 1 000 ft), applied to the height above the pressure datum/station. Example: indicated 10 000 ft (QNH), ISA −20 °C → true ≈ 10 000 − 8 % = ____.","4 %-rule: true altitude differs by 4 % per 10 °C ISA deviation (≈ 4 ft per 1 °C per 1 000 ft), applied to the height above the pressure datum/station. Example: indicated 10 000 ft (QNH), ISA −20 °C → true ≈ 10 000 − 8 % = 9 200 ft."],["050.01.06.03:05","Altimetry: fill in the gaps → ____: flying from high to low pressure with the same setting → aircraft descends (true altitude lower).","Pressure areas: flying from high to low pressure with the same setting → aircraft descends (true altitude lower)."],["050.01.06.01:01","Altimetry: fill in the gaps → ____: e.g. minimum altitude 3 000 ft, QNH 993 (20 hPa below 1013 = 600 ft) → FL 30 corresponds to 2 400 ft → need ____ (3 400 ft) or higher; also correct for cold temperature.","Lowest usable FL: e.g. minimum altitude 3 000 ft, QNH 993 (20 hPa below 1013 = 600 ft) → FL 30 corresponds to 2 400 ft → need FL 40 (3 400 ft) or higher; also correct for cold temperature."],["050.01.06.04:01","Altimetry: fill in the gaps → ____: wind accelerated over ridges/through gaps → local static pressure ↓ → altimeter ____ ____ (aircraft lower than indicated) near mountains — together with mountain-wave downdrafts a hazard.","Topography/Bernoulli effect: wind accelerated over ridges/through gaps → local static pressure ↓ → altimeter over- reads (aircraft lower than indicated) near mountains — together with mountain-wave downdrafts a hazard."],["050.01.06.03:08","Exam trap (Altimetry): complete the fact → ____/hPa (ELC rule).","30 ft/hPa (ELC rule)."],["050.01.03.02:03","Exam trap (Altimetry): complete the fact → Pressure ____ at ____, density 50 % at 22 000 ft.","Pressure 50 % at 18 000 ft, density 50 % at 22 000 ft."],["050.01.01.02:02","Exam trap (Altimetry): complete the fact → ISA tropopause ____ / −____.","ISA tropopause 36 090 ft / −56.5 °C."],["050.01.01.01:01","EASA LO 050.01.01.01.01 → Describe the vertical division of the atmosphere up to flight level (FL) 650, based on the temperature variations with height.","Height (vertical distance above a datum, e.g. aerodrome — QFE), altitude (above MSL — QNH), pressure altitude (altitude in ISA corresponding to the pressure — 1013.25), flight level (pressure altitude in hundreds of ft), pressure level (level of constant pressure), true altitude (actual height above MSL), true height (actual height above ground), elevation (height of a point on the ground above MSL). / Layers (by temperature): troposphere (temperature falls with height, all weather, 0 → 8 km poles / 11 km mid-lat / 16–18 km equator), tropopause (boundary, temperature stops falling), stratosphere (isothermal then warming with height due to ozone absorbing UV, up to ~50 km), mesosphere, thermosphere. Up to FL 650 we deal with troposphere + lower stratosphere."],["050.01.01.01:02","EASA LO 050.01.01.01.02 → List the different layers and their main qualitative characteristics up to FL 650.","Layers (by temperature): troposphere (temperature falls with height, all weather, 0 → 8 km poles / 11 km mid-lat / 16–18 km equator), tropopause (boundary, temperature stops falling), stratosphere (isothermal then warming with height due to ozone absorbing UV, up to ~50 km), mesosphere, thermosphere. Up to FL 650 we deal with troposphere + lower stratosphere."],["050.01.01.02:01","EASA LO 050.01.01.02.01 → Describe the troposphere.","Mean vertical distribution: decrease ~2 °C/1 000 ft (0.65 °C/100 m) in the troposphere, isothermal at tropopause/lower stratosphere. Cooling with height because the air is heated from below (surface absorbs solar radiation) and expanding (rising) air cools adiabatically."],["050.01.01.02:05","EASA LO 050.01.01.02.05 → Describe the breaks in the tropopause along the boundaries of the main air masses.","Tropopause: height higher and colder over the equator (FL 500–550, ~−75 °C) than over the poles (FL 250–300, ~−45 °C); higher in summer, higher over high pressure/warm air, lower over lows/cold air; breaks at the polar front and subtropical front (jet streams located at the breaks)."],["050.01.02.02:02","EASA LO 050.01.02.02.02 → Mention the general causes of the cooling of the air in the troposphere with increasing altitude.","Mean vertical distribution: decrease ~2 °C/1 000 ft (0.65 °C/100 m) in the troposphere, isothermal at tropopause/lower stratosphere. Cooling with height because the air is heated from below (surface absorbs solar radiation) and expanding (rising) air cools adiabatically."],["050.01.02.03:02","EASA LO 050.01.02.03.02 → Describe radiation.","Heat transfer: radiation — sun emits short-wave radiation, atmosphere absorbs little of it (filtering: ozone absorbs UV, clouds reflect, scattering), surface absorbs and re-emits long-wave (terrestrial, infrared) radiation which is absorbed by water vapour, CO₂ and clouds (greenhouse effect); clouds reflect sunlight by day and trap outgoing radiation at night."],["050.01.02.03:03","EASA LO 050.01.02.03.03 → Describe solar radiation reaching the Earth.","Mean vertical distribution: decrease ~2 °C/1 000 ft (0.65 °C/100 m) in the troposphere, isothermal at tropopause/lower stratosphere. Cooling with height because the air is heated from below (surface absorbs solar radiation) and expanding (rising) air cools adiabatically."],["050.01.02.03:05","EASA LO 050.01.02.03.05 → Describe terrestrial radiation.","Heat transfer: radiation — sun emits short-wave radiation, atmosphere absorbs little of it (filtering: ozone absorbs UV, clouds reflect, scattering), surface absorbs and re-emits long-wave (terrestrial, infrared) radiation which is absorbed by water vapour, CO₂ and clouds (greenhouse effect); clouds reflect sunlight by day and trap outgoing radiation at night."],["050.01.02.03:06","EASA LO 050.01.02.03.06 → Explain how terrestrial radiation is absorbed by some components of the atmosphere.","Heat transfer: radiation — sun emits short-wave radiation, atmosphere absorbs little of it (filtering: ozone absorbs UV, clouds reflect, scattering), surface absorbs and re-emits long-wave (terrestrial, infrared) radiation which is absorbed by water vapour, CO₂ and clouds (greenhouse effect); clouds reflect sunlight by day and trap outgoing radiation at night."],["050.01.02.03:07","EASA LO 050.01.02.03.07 → Explain the effect of absorption and radiation in connection with clouds.","Heat transfer: radiation — sun emits short-wave radiation, atmosphere absorbs little of it (filtering: ozone absorbs UV, clouds reflect, scattering), surface absorbs and re-emits long-wave (terrestrial, infrared) radiation which is absorbed by water vapour, CO₂ and clouds (greenhouse effect); clouds reflect sunlight by day and trap outgoing radiation at night."],["050.01.02.03:14","EASA LO 050.01.02.03.14 → Describe the transfer of heat by turbulence.","Advection — horizontal transport of air (warm/cold air advection, sea breeze, fronts). Turbulence — mixing spreads heat through the friction layer. Latent heat — released on condensation/freezing, absorbed on evaporation/melting (major energy source for storms)."],["050.01.02.03:15","EASA LO 050.01.02.03.15 → Describe the transfer of latent heat.","Advection — horizontal transport of air (warm/cold air advection, sea breeze, fronts). Turbulence — mixing spreads heat through the friction layer. Latent heat — released on condensation/freezing, absorbed on evaporation/melting (major energy source for storms)."],["050.01.02.06:01","EASA LO 050.01.02.06.01 → Explain the cooling/warming of the surface of the Earth by radiation.","Heat transfer: radiation — sun emits short-wave radiation, atmosphere absorbs little of it (filtering: ozone absorbs UV, clouds reflect, scattering), surface absorbs and re-emits long-wave (terrestrial, infrared) radiation which is absorbed by water vapour, CO₂ and clouds (greenhouse effect); clouds reflect sunlight by day and trap outgoing radiation at night."],["050.01.02.06:02","EASA LO 050.01.02.06.02 → Explain the cooling/warming of the air by molecular or turbulent heat transfer to/from the earth or sea surfaces.","Near the surface: daily cycle — maximum temperature ~2 h after noon, minimum around sunrise. Clouds reduce both daytime heating and night cooling (small diurnal range); wind mixes the air → less night cooling (no strong inversion) and less daytime heating; land heats/cools faster than sea. / Air temperature = measure of molecular kinetic energy; units °C, °F, K (°F = °C × 9/5 + 32; K = °C + 273)."],["050.01.03.01:01","EASA LO 050.01.03.01.01 → Define ‘atmospheric pressure’.","Isobars = lines of equal pressure (QFF) on surface charts. High (anticyclone), low (depression), trough (elongated low, V-shaped isobars), ridge (elongated high), col (neutral area between two highs and two lows)."],["050.01.03.01:02","EASA LO 050.01.03.01.02 → List the units of measurement of the atmospheric pressure used in aviation (hPa, inches of mercury). (Refer to Subject 050 10 01 01)","Atmospheric pressure = weight of the air column above per unit area. Units hPa (= mbar), inHg (1013.25 hPa = 29.92 inHg). Mercury barometer (column height), aneroid barometer (evacuated capsule, used in altimeters)."],["050.01.03.01:04","EASA LO 050.01.03.01.04 → Define isobars and identify them on surface weather charts.","QFF: QFE reduced to MSL using the actual temperature (for weather charts); QNH uses ISA. / Isobars = lines of equal pressure (QFF) on surface charts. High (anticyclone), low (depression), trough (elongated low, V-shaped isobars), ridge (elongated high), col (neutral area between two highs and two lows)."],["050.01.03.02:02","EASA LO 050.01.03.02.02 → Describe quantitatively the variation of the barometric lapse rate. Remark: An approximation of the average value for the barometric lapse rate near mean sea level (MSL) is 30 ft (9 m) per 1 hPa.","ISA gives a reference for altimeter calibration, performance and instruments: MSL 1013.25 hPa, +15 °C, 1.225 kg/m³, lapse rate −1.98 °C/1 000 ft (6.5 °C/km) to the tropopause at 11 km (36 090 ft), −56.5 °C, then constant −56.5 °C to 20 km (FL 650), then +1 °C/km. / Pressure decreases with height, faster at low levels (dense air): ~30 ft/hPa near MSL (use 30 ft/hPa for calculations), ~50 ft/hPa at 18 000 ft, ~70 ft at 30 000 ft; faster decrease in cold air. 50 % of MSL pressure at ~18 000 ft; density 50 % at ~22 000 ft, 25 % at ~40 000 ft."],["050.01.03.03:01","EASA LO 050.01.03.03.01 → Define ‘QFF’.","QFF: QFE reduced to MSL using the actual temperature (for weather charts); QNH uses ISA. / Isobars = lines of equal pressure (QFF) on surface charts. High (anticyclone), low (depression), trough (elongated low, V-shaped isobars), ridge (elongated high), col (neutral area between two highs and two lows)."],["050.01.03.03:02","EASA LO 050.01.03.03.02 → Explain the reduction of measured pressure (QFE) to QFF (MSL).","QFF: QFE reduced to MSL using the actual temperature (for weather charts); QNH uses ISA. / Height (vertical distance above a datum, e.g. aerodrome — QFE), altitude (above MSL — QNH), pressure altitude (altitude in ISA corresponding to the pressure — 1013.25), flight level (pressure altitude in hundreds of ft), pressure level (level of constant pressure), true altitude (actual height above MSL), true height (actual height above ground), elevation (height of a point on the ground above MSL)."],["050.01.04.01:01","EASA LO 050.01.04.01.01 → Describe the relationship between pressure, temperature and density.","ρ = p/(R·T): density ↑ with pressure, ↓ with temperature (and ↓ with humidity). Density decreases with height (less rapidly than pressure because temperature also falls). / Pressure 50 % at 18 000 ft, density 50 % at 22 000 ft."],["050.01.06.01:02","EASA LO 050.01.06.01.02 → Describe the terms ‘transition altitude’, ‘transition level’, ‘transition layer’, ‘terrain clearance’, ‘lowest usable flight level’.","TA/TL/transition layer; terrain clearance; lowest usable FL (lowest FL that gives the required terrain clearance, depends on QNH and temperature). / Height (vertical distance above a datum, e.g. aerodrome — QFE), altitude (above MSL — QNH), pressure altitude (altitude in ISA corresponding to the pressure — 1013.25), flight level (pressure altitude in hundreds of ft), pressure level (level of constant pressure), true altitude (actual height above MSL), true height (actual height above ground), elevation (height of a point on the ground above MSL)."],["050.01.06.02:01","EASA LO 050.01.06.02.01 → Name the altimeter settings associated to height, altitude, pressure altitude and FL.","Height (vertical distance above a datum, e.g. aerodrome — QFE), altitude (above MSL — QNH), pressure altitude (altitude in ISA corresponding to the pressure — 1013.25), flight level (pressure altitude in hundreds of ft), pressure level (level of constant pressure), true altitude (actual height above MSL), true height (actual height above ground), elevation (height of a point on the ground above MSL)."],["050.01.06.03:02","EASA LO 050.01.06.03.02 → Illustrate with a numbered example the changes of altimeter setting and the associated changes in reading when the pilot climbs through the transition altitude or descends through the transition level.","Pressure areas: flying from high to low pressure with the same setting → aircraft descends (true altitude lower)."],["050.01.06.03:04","EASA LO 050.01.06.03.04 → Explain the influence of the air temperature on the distance between the ground and the level read on the altimeter and between two FLs.","Height (vertical distance above a datum, e.g. aerodrome — QFE), altitude (above MSL — QNH), pressure altitude (altitude in ISA corresponding to the pressure — 1013.25), flight level (pressure altitude in hundreds of ft), pressure level (level of constant pressure), true altitude (actual height above MSL), true height (actual height above ground), elevation (height of a point on the ground above MSL). / Isobars = lines of equal pressure (QFF) on surface charts. High (anticyclone), low (depression), trough (elongated low, V-shaped isobars), ridge (elongated high), col (neutral area between two highs and two lows)."]],"2":[["010.01.01.02:03","International law (overview): fill in the gaps → ____ → ICAO; ____ over airspace above territory (land + territorial waters 12 NM), high seas: Annex 2 applies. Non-scheduled flights with the ____ freedoms, scheduled services need permission, cabotage may be refused, customs airports, rules of the air, search of aircraft;","Chicago Convention 1944 → ICAO; sovereignty over airspace above territory (land + territorial waters 12 NM), high seas: Annex 2 applies. Non-scheduled flights with the 1st/2nd (technical) freedoms, scheduled services need permission, cabotage may be refused, customs airports, rules of the air, search of aircraft;"],["010.01.02.02:01","International law (overview): fill in the gaps → ____ (CofR, CofA, licences, journey log, radio licence, pax list, cargo manifest). ICAO ____ (all States, every 3 years), ____ (36, adopts SARPs), ANC (19); 19 Annexes. Freedoms 3–5 (Transport Agreement).","documents on board (CofR, CofA, licences, journey log, radio licence, pax list, cargo manifest). ICAO Assembly (all States, every 3 years), Council (36, adopts SARPs), ANC (19); 19 Annexes. Freedoms 3–5 (Transport Agreement)."],["010.01.02.05:01","International law (overview): fill in the gaps → ____ (offences on board, PIC powers), ticket = evidence of contract, ____ (€250/400/600), baggage limit ~1 288 SDR (Montreal 1999). IATA, EASA (Reg. 2018/1139; hard law vs CS/AMC/GM), EUROCONTROL/SES. → Full detail on the ____ page.","Tokyo 1963 (offences on board, PIC powers), ticket = evidence of contract, Reg. 261/2004 (€250/400/600), baggage limit ~1 288 SDR (Montreal 1999). IATA, EASA (Reg. 2018/1139; hard law vs CS/AMC/GM), EUROCONTROL/SES. → Full detail on the #93 page."],["010.07.01.02:01","ATS — general (Annex 11): fill in the gaps → ____: prevent collisions between aircraft (in the air and on the manoeuvring area) and with obstructions; ____ ____; provide advice and information; notify organisations for SAR and assist them.","Objectives of ATS: prevent collisions between aircraft (in the air and on the manoeuvring area) and with obstructions; expedite and maintain an orderly flow; provide advice and information; notify organisations for SAR and assist them."],["010.07.01.04:02","ATS — general (Annex 11): fill in the gaps → ____: ____, ____ information service (FIS), alerting service. Three ATC services: area control (ACC), approach control (APP), aerodrome control (TWR).","Three types of ATS: air traffic control service, flight information service (FIS), alerting service. Three ATC services: area control (ACC), approach control (APP), aerodrome control (TWR)."],["010.07.01.02:04","ATS — general (Annex 11): fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (ATC may also call on 243.0 MHz/other available frequencies).","Emergency frequency: 121.5 MHz (ATC may also call on 243.0 MHz/other available frequencies)."],["010.07.01.02:05","ATS — general (Annex 11): fill in the gaps → ____: at a ____ agreed between units (coordination); the aircraft is instructed to contact the next unit; the accepting unit takes control when the aircraft crosses the transfer point (or earlier by agreement).","Transfer of control: at a designated point, level or time agreed between units (coordination); the aircraft is instructed to contact the next unit; the accepting unit takes control when the aircraft crosses the transfer point (or earlier by agreement)."],["010.07.01.03:03","Airspace: fill in the gaps → ____: established so that FIS and alerting service can be provided throughout; all airspace over a State's territory (and oceanic areas assigned) is in an FIR; a UIR covers upper airspace above several FIRs (simplifies upper-airspace services and route structure).","FIR/UIR: established so that FIS and alerting service can be provided throughout; all airspace over a State's territory (and oceanic areas assigned) is in an FIR; a UIR covers upper airspace above several FIRs (simplifies upper-airspace services and route structure)."],["010.07.01.03:06","Airspace: fill in the gaps → FIS & alerting in ____; ATC in ____ ____; CTAs and CTRs within an FIR form part of that FIR.","FIS & alerting in FIRs; ATC in control areas (CTA), control zones (CTR), controlled aerodromes; CTAs and CTRs within an FIR form part of that FIR."],["010.07.01.03:12","Airspace: fill in the gaps → CTA lower limit: not less than ____ above ground or water; need not be uniform. CTR: lateral limits at least ____ ____ from the aerodrome centre in the directions of approaches; extends from the surface up to at least the lower limit of the overlying CTA.","CTA lower limit: not less than 200 m (700 ft) above ground or water; need not be uniform. CTR: lateral limits at least 5 NM (9.3 km) from the aerodrome centre in the directions of approaches; extends from the surface up to at least the lower limit of the overlying CTA."],["010.07.01.03:02","Airspace: fill in the gaps → ____ (ICAO/SERA):","Airspace classes (ICAO/SERA):"],["010.07.01.04:01","Airspace – A: what are the key facts?","Flights: IFR only · Separation: all · Service: ATC"],["010.07.01.04:01","Airspace – B: what are the key facts?","Flights: IFR & VFR · Separation: all from all · Service: ATC"],["010.07.02.07:01","Airspace – C: what are the key facts?","Flights: IFR & VFR · Separation: IFR from IFR and VFR; VFR from IFR · Service: ATC; VFR gets traffic info on VFR"],["010.07.02.07:01","Airspace – D: what are the key facts?","Flights: IFR & VFR · Separation: IFR from IFR · Service: ATC; traffic info IFR↔VFR, VFR↔VFR"],["010.07.02.07:01","Airspace – E: what are the key facts?","Flights: IFR & VFR · Separation: IFR from IFR · Service: ATC for IFR; VFR no clearance, traffic info as far as practicable"],["010.07.02.18:02","Airspace – F: what are the key facts?","Flights: IFR & VFR · Separation: IFR from IFR as far as practical · Service: air traffic advisory + FIS"],["010.07.02.07:01","Airspace – G: what are the key facts?","Flights: IFR & VFR · Separation: none · Service: FIS"],["010.07.02.07:01","Airspace: fill in the gaps → ____ applies in classes ____ (not A, B, not IFR in C). Radio communication required in A–E (VFR in E not), clearance required A–D (IFR in E).","250 kt IAS below FL 100 applies in classes C (VFR), D, E, F, G (not A, B, not IFR in C). Radio communication required in A–E (VFR in E not), clearance required A–D (IFR in E)."],["010.07.01.04:03","ATC service, FIS, alerting: fill in the gaps → ATC in classes ____ (E only for IFR). Units: ACC, APP, TWR; ____ ____ may be provided by the TWR or a separate apron unit/airport operator.","ATC in classes A–E (E only for IFR). Units: ACC, APP, TWR; apron management service may be provided by the TWR or a separate apron unit/airport operator."],["010.07.01.04:05","ATC service, FIS, alerting: fill in the gaps → ____ purpose: authorisation to proceed under specified conditions for ____. ____: aircraft identification, clearance limit, route, level(s), any necessary instructions/information (approach/departure manoeuvres, communications, clearance expiry time).","Clearance purpose: authorisation to proceed under specified conditions for preventing collisions and expediting traffic. Five parts: aircraft identification, clearance limit, route, level(s), any necessary instructions/information (approach/departure manoeuvres, communications, clearance expiry time)."],["010.07.01.04:06","ATC service, FIS, alerting: fill in the gaps → Movement of persons/vehicles/towed aircraft on the ____ is controlled by the TWR to avoid hazards to aircraft (runway incursions).","Movement of persons/vehicles/towed aircraft on the manoeuvring area is controlled by the TWR to avoid hazards to aircraft (runway incursions)."],["010.07.01.05:01","ATC service, FIS, alerting: fill in the gaps → ____ is provided to ____ which are provided with ATC or otherwise known to ATS.","FIS is provided to all aircraft likely to be affected which are provided with ATC or otherwise known to ATS."],["010.07.01.05:03","ATC service, FIS, alerting: fill in the gaps → Includes ____ ____, volcanic activity/ash, release of radioactive/toxic material, changes in NAVAID serviceability, aerodrome and facilities condition (incl. runway contamination), unmanned free balloons, plus ____, collision hazards (classes C–G), surface vessels for flights over water, and any other safety information.","Includes SIGMET and AIRMET, volcanic activity/ash, release of radioactive/toxic material, changes in NAVAID serviceability, aerodrome and facilities condition (incl. runway contamination), unmanned free balloons, plus weather at departure/destination/alternates, collision hazards (classes C–G), surface vessels for flights over water, and any other safety information."],["010.07.01.05:08","ATC service, FIS, alerting: fill in the gaps → ____ = ____: continuous recorded broadcast on a ____ (or VOR voice channel), for one aerodrome per broadcast (arrival and/or departure), identified by a letter (alphabetical sequence), updated immediately on significant change (weather, runway, TL, NAVAIDs) and routinely (e.g. every 30/60 min with METAR); pilots acknowledge receipt by quoting the letter;","ATIS = automatic terminal information service: continuous recorded broadcast on a discrete VHF frequency (or VOR voice channel), for one aerodrome per broadcast (arrival and/or departure), identified by a letter (alphabetical sequence), updated immediately on significant change (weather, runway, TL, NAVAIDs) and routinely (e.g. every 30/60 min with METAR); pilots acknowledge receipt by quoting the letter;"],["010.07.01.05:05","ATC service, FIS, alerting: what do you remember about this? → English (+ local …","English (+ local language)."],["010.07.01.05:07","ATC service, FIS, alerting: fill in the gaps → Content: aerodrome name, designator letter, time of observation, type of approach, runway(s) in use, runway surface conditions/braking, holding delay, ____, other operational info, ____, ____, present weather, cloud, temperature, dew point, altimeter setting(s), trend, specific instructions.","Content: aerodrome name, designator letter, time of observation, type of approach, runway(s) in use, runway surface conditions/braking, holding delay, transition level, other operational info, surface wind, visibility/RVR, present weather, cloud, temperature, dew point, altimeter setting(s), trend, specific instructions."],["010.07.01.06:04","ATC service, FIS, alerting: fill in the gaps → ____: provided by the ____ responsible (ACC/FIC collects info, initiates the phase) for ____ ATC, as far as practicable for all others that filed a flight plan or are known, and any aircraft known/believed subject to unlawful interference. The ATS unit immediately notifies the RCC.","Alerting service: provided by the ATS unit responsible (ACC/FIC collects info, initiates the phase) for all aircraft provided with ATC, as far as practicable for all others that filed a flight plan or are known, and any aircraft known/believed subject to unlawful interference. The ATS unit immediately notifies the RCC."],["010.07.01.06:06","ATC service, FIS, alerting: fill in the gaps → Phases: ____ (uncertainty — no contact within 30 min / not arrived 30 min after ETA), ____ (alert — failed further attempts, cleared to land but not landed within 5 min, impaired operating efficiency, unlawful interference), ____ (distress — fuel exhausted, forced landing imminent/occurred).","Phases: INCERFA (uncertainty — no contact within 30 min / not arrived 30 min after ETA), ALERFA (alert — failed further attempts, cleared to land but not landed within 5 min, impaired operating efficiency, unlawful interference), DETRESFA (distress — fuel exhausted, forced landing imminent/occurred)."],["010.07.01.06:07","ATC service, FIS, alerting: what do you remember about this? → Aircraft in the vicinity of an emergency/unlawful interference are …","Aircraft in the vicinity of an emergency/unlawful interference are informed of the nature of the emergency as far as practicable."],["010.07.01.07:02","RNP & ATS routes: fill in the gaps → ____ = ____: statement of the navigation performance necessary for operation within a defined airspace, based on ____ (95 % containment).","RNP = required navigation performance: statement of the navigation performance necessary for operation within a defined airspace, based on navigation accuracy (95 % containment)."],["010.07.01.07:03","RNP & ATS routes: fill in the gaps → Route designators and navigation specifications allow unambiguous reference (pilots, ATC, computers) and indicate required performance; the RNP type/navigation spec is ____ ____ (published separately).","Route designators and navigation specifications allow unambiguous reference (pilots, ATC, computers) and indicate required performance; the RNP type/navigation spec is not part of the route designator (published separately)."],["010.07.01.07:05","RNP & ATS routes: fill in the gaps → Designator: basic designator = ____: A, B, G, R (regional/ATS network), L, M, N, P (regional RNAV routes), H, J, V, W (non-regional), Q, T, Y, Z (non-regional RNAV); prefix U (upper), K (low-level helicopter), S (supersonic); suffix F (advisory service only), G (FIS only), Y/Z (RNP 1 with turns 22.5 NM/15 NM radius). E.g. ____, ____.","Designator: basic designator = one letter + number (1–999): A, B, G, R (regional/ATS network), L, M, N, P (regional RNAV routes), H, J, V, W (non-regional), Q, T, Y, Z (non-regional RNAV); prefix U (upper), K (low-level helicopter), S (supersonic); suffix F (advisory service only), G (FIS only), Y/Z (RNP 1 with turns 22.5 NM/15 NM radius). E.g. UN871, UL612."],["010.07.02.05:02","Doc 4444 — ATC procedures: fill in the gaps → Clearances from ____ ____ (pilot's responsibility) ____ vectored by radar.","Clearances from ATC do not include terrain/obstacle clearance (pilot's responsibility) except when an IFR flight is vectored by radar."],["010.07.02.03:01","Doc 4444 — ATC procedures: fill in the gaps → ____ implemented where traffic demand at times exceeds ATC capacity (slots, flow regulations — Network Manager in Europe).","ATFM implemented where traffic demand at times exceeds ATC capacity (slots, flow regulations — Network Manager in Europe)."],["010.07.02.04:01","Doc 4444 — ATC procedures: fill in the gaps → ____ within an FIR by the ____, in controlled airspace and at controlled aerodromes by the ____ concerned.","FIS & alerting within an FIR by the FIC/ACC, in controlled airspace and at controlled aerodromes by the ATC unit concerned."],["010.07.02.05:02","Doc 4444 — ATC procedures: fill in the gaps → ____ are based ____ (not on terrain); if not suitable, the ____ clearance; the PIC remains responsible for complying with rules. Purposes: expediting and separating traffic. Issued early enough to allow compliance.","Clearances are based solely on known traffic conditions (not on terrain); if not suitable, the PIC may request an amended clearance; the PIC remains responsible for complying with rules. Purposes: expediting and separating traffic. Issued early enough to allow compliance."],["010.07.02.05:07","Doc 4444 — ATC procedures: fill in the gaps → ____ = point to which the aircraft is granted a clearance. \"Cleared via flight planned route\" = follow the FPL route; \"via (designator) departure/arrival\" = follow that SID/STAR.","Clearance limit = point to which the aircraft is granted a clearance. \"Cleared via flight planned route\" = follow the FPL route; \"via (designator) departure/arrival\" = follow that SID/STAR."],["010.07.02.05:08","Doc 4444 — ATC procedures: fill in the gaps → ____ always: ____, clearances/instructions to ____ short of, cross, taxi and backtrack on any runway, runway in use, altimeter settings, SSR codes, level instructions, heading and speed instructions, transition levels.","Read-back always: ATC route clearances, clearances/instructions to enter, land on, take off from, hold short of, cross, taxi and backtrack on any runway, runway in use, altimeter settings, SSR codes, level instructions, heading and speed instructions, transition levels."],["010.07.02.06:01","Doc 4444 — ATC procedures: fill in the gaps → Speed control: to facilitate spacing/sequencing; adjustments in multiples of ____ (or 0.01 M), max changes about ____; ____ on final.","Speed control: to facilitate spacing/sequencing; adjustments in multiples of 10 kt IAS (or 0.01 M), max changes about ±20 kt; no speed control within 4 NM of the threshold on final."],["010.07.02.07:01","Doc 4444 — ATC procedures: fill in the gaps → ____: initiated by the pilot (\"____ ____\"); ATC acknowledges and passes known weather info (IMC ahead) but must not invite the change.","IFR → VFR: initiated by the pilot (\"CANCELLING MY IFR FLIGHT\"); ATC acknowledges and passes known weather info (IMC ahead) but must not invite the change."],["010.07.02.08:03","Doc 4444 — ATC procedures: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (≤ 7 000 kg), ____ (7 000–136 000 kg), H (≥ 136 000 kg), J (super, A380). Heavy aircraft add \"HEAVY\" after the call sign in the initial call. Separation minima: see #66 (radar 4/5/6 NM; timed 2/3 min).","Wake turbulence categories: L (≤ 7 000 kg), M (7 000–136 000 kg), H (≥ 136 000 kg), J (super, A380). Heavy aircraft add \"HEAVY\" after the call sign in the initial call. Separation minima: see #66 (radar 4/5/6 NM; timed 2/3 min)."],["010.07.02.09:05","Doc 4444 — ATC procedures: fill in the gaps → ____: TRL, transition layer, TA (see #90). ____ precision approach refers to threshold elevation if the threshold is ____ aerodrome elevation. Altimeter settings rounded down to the nearest whole hPa. Lowest usable FL: lowest FL that ensures the minimum terrain clearance;","Altimetry: TRL, transition layer, TA (see #90). QFE on NDB/non- precision approach refers to threshold elevation if the threshold is ≥ 2 m (7 ft) below aerodrome elevation. Altimeter settings rounded down to the nearest whole hPa. Lowest usable FL: lowest FL that ensures the minimum terrain clearance;"],["010.07.02.09:09","Doc 4444 — ATC procedures: fill in the gaps → ____ it → flight levels, ____ → altitudes. TRL established by the ____ (in consultation), passed in the approach clearance/ATIS; the pilot may request it.","at/above it → flight levels, below → altitudes. TRL established by the appropriate ATS unit (in consultation), passed in the approach clearance/ATIS; the pilot may request it."],["010.07.02.10:03","Doc 4444 — ATC procedures: fill in the gaps → ____ at compulsory (or requested) reporting points; ____: ____ level/altitude, next position and time over, ensuing significant point. Simplified report (omitting last three items) when in radar contact/as prescribed.","Position reports at compulsory (or requested) reporting points; six items: aircraft identification, position, time, flight level/altitude, next position and time over, ensuing significant point. Simplified report (omitting last three items) when in radar contact/as prescribed."],["010.07.02.10:04","Doc 4444 — ATC procedures: fill in the gaps → After changing frequency, the initial call includes the ____. Level may be omitted if ____ is verified. ____ included when required by the ATS unit (e.g. TAS change ≥ 5 %). ADS = automatic dependent surveillance. Levels relative to 1013.2 hPa are preceded by \"FLIGHT LEVEL\".","After changing frequency, the initial call includes the level (and cleared level). Level may be omitted if Mode C is verified. Airspeed included when required by the ATS unit (e.g. TAS change ≥ 5 %). ADS = automatic dependent surveillance. Levels relative to 1013.2 hPa are preceded by \"FLIGHT LEVEL\"."],["010.07.02.01:01","Doc 4444 — ATC procedures: fill in the gaps → ____: severe turbulence, severe icing, severe mountain wave, thunderstorms (obscured/embedded/widespread/squall line) with or without hail, heavy dust/sand storm, volcanic ash cloud, pre-eruption volcanic activity or eruption (and other phenomena affecting safety).","Special air-reports: severe turbulence, severe icing, severe mountain wave, thunderstorms (obscured/embedded/widespread/squall line) with or without hail, heavy dust/sand storm, volcanic ash cloud, pre-eruption volcanic activity or eruption (and other phenomena affecting safety)."],["010.07.02.12:04","Separation: fill in the gaps → Separation between controlled flights per airspace class (A/B all; C IFR–IFR, IFR–VFR; D/E IFR–IFR). In ____ the ____ remains responsible for ____ (see and avoid).","Separation between controlled flights per airspace class (A/B all; C IFR–IFR, IFR–VFR; D/E IFR–IFR). In VMC the pilot remains responsible for collision avoidance (see and avoid)."],["010.07.02.12:08","Separation: fill in the gaps → Vertical: assign different levels — ____ up to ____ (and to ____), 2 000 ft above. Aircraft to the same destination: levels assigned so the first to arrive is at the lowest level (approach sequence). Vertical separation during climb/descent can be maintained by assigning specified rates of climb/descent to both aircraft.","Vertical: assign different levels — 1 000 ft up to FL 290 (and to FL 410 in RVSM), 2 000 ft above. Aircraft to the same destination: levels assigned so the first to arrive is at the lowest level (approach sequence). Vertical separation during climb/descent can be maintained by assigning specified rates of climb/descent to both aircraft."],["010.07.02.12:15","Separation: fill in the gaps → Horizontal: lateral (different routes/geographical locations — geographical separation by fixes/visual; track separation from the same aid: ____, ____ ____, DR ≥ 45° and ≥ 15 NM) and longitudinal (time, distance (DME/GNSS), Mach number technique).","Horizontal: lateral (different routes/geographical locations — geographical separation by fixes/visual; track separation from the same aid: VOR ≥ 15° and ≥ 15 NM, NDB ≥ 30° and ≥ 15 NM, DR ≥ 45° and ≥ 15 NM) and longitudinal (time, distance (DME/GNSS), Mach number technique)."],["010.07.02.12:14","Separation: fill in the gaps → Standard ____ (3 NM in approach with suitable radar). ____: turbojets on the same track keep assigned Mach numbers → spacing maintained over long oceanic routes.","Standard radar separation 5 NM (3 NM in approach with suitable radar). Mach technique: turbojets on the same track keep assigned Mach numbers → spacing maintained over long oceanic routes."],["010.07.02.13:02","Separation: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ = any aircraft, vehicle or person on/near the manoeuvring area or traffic in the vicinity that constitutes a hazard → information passed. Take-off not into wind: PIC may decline and ____ for the into-wind runway (delay).","Aerodrome vicinity: essential local traffic = any aircraft, vehicle or person on/near the manoeuvring area or traffic in the vicinity that constitutes a hazard → information passed. Take-off not into wind: PIC may decline and wait for the into-wind runway (delay)."],["010.07.02.13:04","Separation: fill in the gaps → ____ (IFR) may be cleared when the pilot reports the ____ and ceiling is at/above the initial approach level; ATC still ____ from other arriving/departing traffic.","Visual approach (IFR) may be cleared when the pilot reports the aerodrome or preceding aircraft in sight and ceiling is at/above the initial approach level; ATC still separates it from other arriving/departing traffic."],["010.07.02.13:05","Separation: fill in the gaps → If the crew is not familiar with the procedure, the initial approach level, procedure-turn details and final approach track are given — ____ ____ if a ____ is made. First aircraft over a holding fix: lowest level.","If the crew is not familiar with the procedure, the initial approach level, procedure-turn details and final approach track are given — only the final approach track if a straight-in approach is made. First aircraft over a holding fix: lowest level."],["010.07.02.13:06","Separation: fill in the gaps → ____: aircraft in emergency first; landing aircraft priority over departures. A pilot wishing to ____ is cleared to hold, others may approach before.","Priority: aircraft in emergency first; landing aircraft priority over departures. A pilot wishing to hold for weather improvement is cleared to hold, others may approach before."],["010.07.02.13:09","Separation: fill in the gaps → ____ (expected approach time) = time at which ATC expects a delayed arrival to leave the holding fix to complete its approach; revised when it changes by ____ — used for COM failure procedures.","EAT (expected approach time) = time at which ATC expects a delayed arrival to leave the holding fix to complete its approach; revised when it changes by ≥ 5 min — used for COM failure procedures."],["010.07.02.13:02","Separation: what do you remember about this? → Runway other than into wind for noise, runway length, obstacles, …","Runway other than into wind for noise, runway length, obstacles, traffic flow; if unsuitable the PIC may refuse (possible delay)."],["010.07.02.05:07","Separation: fill in the gaps → ____: a departure may be cleared in a direction differing by ____ from the reciprocal of the approach direction until ____ before the arrival's estimated time over the instrument runway (or until it starts its procedure/base turn).","Departing/arriving: a departure may be cleared in a direction differing by ≥ 45° from the reciprocal of the approach direction until 3 min before the arrival's estimated time over the instrument runway (or until it starts its procedure/base turn)."],["010.07.02.14:03","Separation: fill in the gaps → Non-radar wake separation: arrivals ____, L ____… (L behind H 3 min); departures ____ (3 min from intermediate position). \"Maintain own separation\" in VMC (on pilot request, climb/descent, class D/E, below FL 100, daytime) → the pilot takes responsibility.","Non-radar wake separation: arrivals M/L behind H 2 min, L behind M 3 min… (L behind H 3 min); departures 2 min (3 min from intermediate position). \"Maintain own separation\" in VMC (on pilot request, climb/descent, class D/E, below FL 100, daytime) → the pilot takes responsibility."],["010.07.02.14:04","Separation: fill in the gaps → ____ = controlled traffic to which separation should apply but is not provided → essential traffic information. ____ where regionally agreed, with better navigation/surveillance and safety assessment.","Essential traffic = controlled traffic to which separation should apply but is not provided → essential traffic information. Reduced minima where regionally agreed, with better navigation/surveillance and safety assessment."],["010.07.02.15:07","Arriving & departing traffic, TWR: fill in the gaps → ____: type of approach, ____, ____ (wind, visibility/RVR, weather, cloud, temperature, altimeter), runway surface conditions, NAVAID status, wind shear reports. Commencement of final: significant changes in mean surface wind, latest RVR/visibility, wind shear. During final: sudden hazards, significant wind changes, RVR changes.","Early approach information: type of approach, runway in use, MET (wind, visibility/RVR, weather, cloud, temperature, altimeter), runway surface conditions, NAVAID status, wind shear reports. Commencement of final: significant changes in mean surface wind, latest RVR/visibility, wind shear. During final: sudden hazards, significant wind changes, RVR changes."],["010.07.02.15:06","Arriving & departing traffic, TWR: fill in the gaps → ____: landing (or final stage of approach) aircraft ____ departing aircraft. To ____ aircraft without delay: significant changes in surface wind (direction/speed), visibility/RVR, wind shear, TS/Cb, temperature changes affecting performance. To arriving aircraft: significant changes in MET and runway conditions.","Priority: landing (or final stage of approach) aircraft before departing aircraft. To departing aircraft without delay: significant changes in surface wind (direction/speed), visibility/RVR, wind shear, TS/Cb, temperature changes affecting performance. To arriving aircraft: significant changes in MET and runway conditions."],["010.07.02.16:02","Arriving & departing traffic, TWR: fill in the gaps → ____: reports immediately ____ of aerodrome equipment (lights, NAVAIDs); informs the ACC/FIC if an aircraft ____ of the expected time (alerting). VFR suspended: hold VFR departures, recall local VFR flights, notify ACC/APP.","TWR: reports immediately failures/irregularities of aerodrome equipment (lights, NAVAIDs); informs the ACC/FIC if an aircraft does not land within 5 min of the expected time (alerting). VFR suspended: hold VFR departures, recall local VFR flights, notify ACC/APP."],["010.07.02.16:06","Arriving & departing traffic, TWR: fill in the gaps → ____: most suitable, normally ____ ____; surface wind to pilots is magnetic. \"Runway vacated\" = the entire aircraft is beyond the relevant runway-holding position. Information before taxi: runway, surface wind, QNH, temperature, visibility/RVR, time; before take-off: significant changes, wind shear;","Runway in use: most suitable, normally into wind; surface wind to pilots is magnetic. \"Runway vacated\" = the entire aircraft is beyond the relevant runway-holding position. Information before taxi: runway, surface wind, QNH, temperature, visibility/RVR, time; before take-off: significant changes, wind shear;"],["010.07.02.16:05","Arriving & departing traffic, TWR: fill in the gaps → ____: runway, wind, QNH, traffic.","before entering the circuit: runway, wind, QNH, traffic."],["010.07.02.10:05","Radar, advisory, emergencies: fill in the gaps → ____: SSR (code, IDENT/SPI, Mode S aircraft ID), correlation of a position report with a radar return, turn of ≥ ____, departing aircraft observed within ____ of the runway end. PSR = primary surveillance radar.","Identification: SSR (code, IDENT/SPI, Mode S aircraft ID), correlation of a position report with a radar return, turn of ≥ 30°, departing aircraft observed within 1 NM of the runway end. PSR = primary surveillance radar."],["010.07.02.17:04","Radar, advisory, emergencies: fill in the gaps → Position information given: on identification, when pilot requests, when estimates differ significantly, ____; forms: ____, distance from touchdown, position relative to a route, over a well-known geographical position.","Position information given: on identification, when pilot requests, when estimates differ significantly, when resuming own navigation after vectoring; forms: bearing & distance from a fix, distance from touchdown, position relative to a route, over a well-known geographical position."],["010.07.02.17:08","Radar, advisory, emergencies: fill in the gaps → ____ = navigational guidance in the form of ____ ____. Aims: separation, noise abatement, weather avoidance, expediting traffic, positioning for approach (ILS intercept). ATC informs the pilot of the purpose and the limit; terminating: \"resume own navigation\" + position (and heading/track instructions if needed).","Radar vectoring = navigational guidance in the form of specific headings. Aims: separation, noise abatement, weather avoidance, expediting traffic, positioning for approach (ILS intercept). ATC informs the pilot of the purpose and the limit; terminating: \"resume own navigation\" + position (and heading/track instructions if needed)."],["010.07.02.17:01","Radar, advisory, emergencies: fill in the gaps → ____ (surveillance radar approach): controller gives ____, distance from touchdown and recommended altitudes (every NM), until ____ (or the MAPt), then pilot continues visually or goes around.","SRA (surveillance radar approach): controller gives track/heading guidance, distance from touchdown and recommended altitudes (every NM), until 2 NM (or the MAPt), then pilot continues visually or goes around."],["010.07.02.19:01","Radar, advisory, emergencies: fill in the gaps → Transponder in emergency: keep the assigned code unless instructed; otherwise ____ (emergency), ____ (unlawful interference), ____ (COM failure).","Transponder in emergency: keep the assigned code unless instructed; otherwise 7700 (emergency), 7500 (unlawful interference), 7600 (COM failure)."],["010.07.02.18:02","Radar, advisory, emergencies: fill in the gaps → ____ (class ____): aims to ensure separation between ____ as far as practicable; provided to IFR flights that choose to participate; issues advisory information/suggestions, not clearances (by ACC/advisory unit).","Air traffic advisory service (class F): aims to ensure separation between IFR flights on advisory routes/areas as far as practicable; provided to IFR flights that choose to participate; issues advisory information/suggestions, not clearances (by ACC/advisory unit)."],["010.07.02.19:03","Radar, advisory, emergencies: fill in the gaps → ____: aircraft in emergency gets ____, maximum assistance. After an ATS broadcast of an ____, other aircraft leave the area specified and follow instructions.","Emergency: aircraft in emergency gets priority, maximum assistance. After an ATS broadcast of an emergency descent, other aircraft leave the area specified and follow instructions."],["010.07.02.19:06","Radar, advisory, emergencies: fill in the gaps → ____: check the aircraft receives by asking it to ____ ____ or make a turn; transmit information on all available frequencies incl. NAVAID voice. Strayed aircraft: deviated significantly from its intended track or reports it is lost; unidentified aircraft: observed/reported but not identified.","COM failure: check the aircraft receives by asking it to squawk IDENT/change code or make a turn; transmit information on all available frequencies incl. NAVAID voice. Strayed aircraft: deviated significantly from its intended track or reports it is lost; unidentified aircraft: observed/reported but not identified."],["010.07.02.19:08","Radar, advisory, emergencies: fill in the gaps → ____ ____: to reduce landing mass in an emergency — ATC gives the area and level: at least ____ above ground and away from other traffic. Call-sign change requested to avoid confusion with similar call signs.","Fuel dumping: to reduce landing mass in an emergency — ATC gives the area and level: at least 6 000 ft (1 800 m) above ground and away from other traffic. Call-sign change requested to avoid confusion with similar call signs."],["010.07.02.20:02","Radar, advisory, emergencies: fill in the gaps → ____: situation where distance between aircraft and relative positions/speeds were such that ____ ____; air traffic incident report filed (by pilot) to allow investigation and prevention.","AIRPROX: situation where distance between aircraft and relative positions/speeds were such that safety may have been compromised; air traffic incident report filed (by pilot) to allow investigation and prevention."],["100.01.00.00:01","ICAO core competencies (KSA 100.01): fill in the gaps → ICAO Doc 9995 (EBT): ____, ____, ____ automation, Aircraft flight path management — manual control, Leadership & teamwork, Problem solving & decision making, Situation awareness, Workload management (+ Knowledge in newer editions).","ICAO Doc 9995 (EBT): Application of procedures, Communication, Aircraft flight path management — automation, Aircraft flight path management — manual control, Leadership & teamwork, Problem solving & decision making, Situation awareness, Workload management (+ Knowledge in newer editions)."],["010.07.01.02:03","Exam trap (ICAO core competencies (KSA 100.01)): complete the fact → ____ATS types vs ____ATC services.","3 ATS types vs 3 ATC services."],["010.07.01.03:07","Exam trap (ICAO core competencies (KSA 100.01)): complete the fact → CTA lower limit ≥ ____; CTR ≥ ____.","CTA lower limit ≥ 700 ft; CTR ≥ 5 NM."],["010.07.02.06:01","Exam trap (ICAO core competencies (KSA 100.01)): complete the fact → No speed control inside ____.","No speed control inside 4 NM."],["010.01.01.02:02","EASA LO 010.01.01.02.02 → General principles Describe the application of the following terms in civil aviation: — sovereignty; — territory and high seas according to the UN Convention on the High Seas.","Chicago Convention 1944 → ICAO; sovereignty over airspace above territory (land + territorial waters 12 NM), high seas: Annex 2 applies. Non-scheduled flights with the 1st/2nd (technical) freedoms, scheduled services need permission, cabotage may be refused, customs airports, rules of the air, search of aircraft;"],["010.01.01.02:04","EASA LO 010.01.01.02.04 → Explain the duties of Contracting States in relation to: — documents carried on board the aircraft:","documents on board (CofR, CofA, licences, journey log, radio licence, pax list, cargo manifest). ICAO Assembly (all States, every 3 years), Council (36, adopts SARPs), ANC (19); 19 Annexes. Freedoms 3–5 (Transport Agreement)."],["010.01.01.03:01","EASA LO 010.01.01.03.01 → Describe the objectives of ICAO.","Airspace classes (ICAO/SERA):"],["010.01.01.03:02","EASA LO 010.01.01.03.02 → Recognise the organisation and duties of the ICAO Assembly, Council and Air Navigation Commission (ANC).","documents on board (CofR, CofA, licences, journey log, radio licence, pax list, cargo manifest). ICAO Assembly (all States, every 3 years), Council (36, adopts SARPs), ANC (19); 19 Annexes. Freedoms 3–5 (Transport Agreement)."],["010.01.02.01:01","EASA LO 010.01.02.01.01 → Explain the two technical freedoms of the air.","Chicago Convention 1944 → ICAO; sovereignty over airspace above territory (land + territorial waters 12 NM), high seas: Annex 2 applies. Non-scheduled flights with the 1st/2nd (technical) freedoms, scheduled services need permission, cabotage may be refused, customs airports, rules of the air, search of aircraft; / documents on board (CofR, CofA, licences, journey log, radio licence, pax list, cargo manifest). ICAO Assembly (all States, every 3 years), Council (36, adopts SARPs), ANC (19); 19 Annexes. Freedoms 3–5 (Transport Agreement)."],["010.01.04.01:01","EASA LO 010.01.04.01.01 → Describe the objectives of EASA.","Tokyo 1963 (offences on board, PIC powers), ticket = evidence of contract, Reg. 261/2004 (€250/400/600), baggage limit ~1 288 SDR (Montreal 1999). IATA, EASA (Reg. 2018/1139; hard law vs CS/AMC/GM), EUROCONTROL/SES. → Full detail on the #93 page."],["010.01.04.01:03","EASA LO 010.01.04.01.03 → State that the structure of the regulatory material related to EASA involves: — hard law (regulations, delegated acts, implementing acts, and implementing rules);","Tokyo 1963 (offences on board, PIC powers), ticket = evidence of contract, Reg. 261/2004 (€250/400/600), baggage limit ~1 288 SDR (Montreal 1999). IATA, EASA (Reg. 2018/1139; hard law vs CS/AMC/GM), EUROCONTROL/SES. → Full detail on the #93 page."],["010.01.04.02:01","EASA LO 010.01.04.02.01 → Describe the Single European Sky (SES) regulations.","Tokyo 1963 (offences on board, PIC powers), ticket = evidence of contract, Reg. 261/2004 (€250/400/600), baggage limit ~1 288 SDR (Montreal 1999). IATA, EASA (Reg. 2018/1139; hard law vs CS/AMC/GM), EUROCONTROL/SES. → Full detail on the #93 page."],["010.07.01.02:02","EASA LO 010.07.01.02.02 → Describe the three basic types of ATS.","Three types of ATS: air traffic control service, flight information service (FIS), alerting service. Three ATC services: area control (ACC), approach control (APP), aerodrome control (TWR). / 3 ATS types vs 3 ATC services."],["010.07.01.03:01","EASA LO 010.07.01.03.01 → Describe the purpose for establishing flight information regions (FIRs) including upper flight information regions (UIRs).","FIR/UIR: established so that FIS and alerting service can be provided throughout; all airspace over a State's territory (and oceanic areas assigned) is in an FIR; a UIR covers upper airspace above several FIRs (simplifies upper-airspace services and route structure)."],["010.07.01.03:04","EASA LO 010.07.01.03.04 → State the designation for those portions of the airspace where flight information service (FIS) and alerting service shall be provided.","FIS is provided to all aircraft likely to be affected which are provided with ATC or otherwise known to ATS. / Three types of ATS: air traffic control service, flight information service (FIS), alerting service. Three ATC services: area control (ACC), approach control (APP), aerodrome control (TWR)."],["010.07.01.03:08","EASA LO 010.07.01.03.08 → State whether or not the lower limit of a CTA has to be established uniformly.","CTA lower limit ≥ 700 ft; CTR ≥ 5 NM. / CTA lower limit: not less than 200 m (700 ft) above ground or water; need not be uniform. CTR: lateral limits at least 5 NM (9.3 km) from the aerodrome centre in the directions of approaches; extends from the surface up to at least the lower limit of the overlying CTA."],["010.07.01.03:09","EASA LO 010.07.01.03.09 → Explain why a UIR or upper CTA should be delineated to include the upper airspace within the lateral limits of a number of lower FIRs or CTAs.","FIR/UIR: established so that FIS and alerting service can be provided throughout; all airspace over a State's territory (and oceanic areas assigned) is in an FIR; a UIR covers upper airspace above several FIRs (simplifies upper-airspace services and route structure). / FIS & alerting in FIRs; ATC in control areas (CTA), control zones (CTR), controlled aerodromes; CTAs and CTRs within an FIR form part of that FIR."],["010.07.01.03:10","EASA LO 010.07.01.03.10 → Describe in general the lateral limits of CTRs.","FIS & alerting in FIRs; ATC in control areas (CTA), control zones (CTR), controlled aerodromes; CTAs and CTRs within an FIR form part of that FIR. / CTA lower limit: not less than 200 m (700 ft) above ground or water; need not be uniform. CTR: lateral limits at least 5 NM (9.3 km) from the aerodrome centre in the directions of approaches; extends from the surface up to at least the lower limit of the overlying CTA."],["010.07.01.03:11","EASA LO 010.07.01.03.11 → State the minimum extension (in NM) of the lateral limits of a CTR.","CTA lower limit: not less than 200 m (700 ft) above ground or water; need not be uniform. CTR: lateral limits at least 5 NM (9.3 km) from the aerodrome centre in the directions of approaches; extends from the surface up to at least the lower limit of the overlying CTA. / CTA lower limit ≥ 700 ft; CTR ≥ 5 NM."],["010.07.01.04:04","EASA LO 010.07.01.04.04 → State the purpose of clearances issued by an ATC unit.","Three types of ATS: air traffic control service, flight information service (FIS), alerting service. Three ATC services: area control (ACC), approach control (APP), aerodrome control (TWR). / Clearances are based solely on known traffic conditions (not on terrain); if not suitable, the PIC may request an amended clearance; the PIC remains responsible for complying with rules. Purposes: expediting and separating traffic. Issued early enough to allow compliance."],["010.07.01.05:02","EASA LO 010.07.01.05.02 → State whether or not FIS shall include the provision of pertinent significant meteorological information (SIGMET) and air meteorological information report (AIRMET) information.","Three types of ATS: air traffic control service, flight information service (FIS), alerting service. Three ATC services: area control (ACC), approach control (APP), aerodrome control (TWR)."],["010.07.01.05:04","EASA LO 010.07.01.05.04 → Indicate which other information the FIS shall include in addition to the special information given in Annex 11.","Three types of ATS: air traffic control service, flight information service (FIS), alerting service. Three ATC services: area control (ACC), approach control (APP), aerodrome control (TWR). / Emergency frequency: 121.5 MHz (ATC may also call on 243.0 MHz/other available frequencies)."],["010.07.01.06:01","EASA LO 010.07.01.06.01 → State who provides the alerting service.","Three types of ATS: air traffic control service, flight information service (FIS), alerting service. Three ATC services: area control (ACC), approach control (APP), aerodrome control (TWR)."],["010.07.01.06:02","EASA LO 010.07.01.06.02 → State who is responsible for initiating the appropriate emergency phase.","Alerting service: provided by the ATS unit responsible (ACC/FIC collects info, initiates the phase) for all aircraft provided with ATC, as far as practicable for all others that filed a flight plan or are known, and any aircraft known/believed subject to unlawful interference. The ATS unit immediately notifies the RCC. / Aircraft in the vicinity of an emergency/unlawful interference are informed of the nature of the emergency as far as practicable."],["010.07.01.06:03","EASA LO 010.07.01.06.03 → State the aircraft to which alerting service shall be provided.","FIS is provided to all aircraft likely to be affected which are provided with ATC or otherwise known to ATS. / Three types of ATS: air traffic control service, flight information service (FIS), alerting service. Three ATC services: area control (ACC), approach control (APP), aerodrome control (TWR)."],["010.07.01.06:05","EASA LO 010.07.01.06.05 → Name the three stages of emergency and describe the basic conditions for each kind of emergency.","Aircraft in the vicinity of an emergency/unlawful interference are informed of the nature of the emergency as far as practicable. / Three types of ATS: air traffic control service, flight information service (FIS), alerting service. Three ATC services: area control (ACC), approach control (APP), aerodrome control (TWR)."],["010.07.01.07:01","EASA LO 010.07.01.07.01 → State the meaning of the acronym ‘RNP’.","Route designators and navigation specifications allow unambiguous reference (pilots, ATC, computers) and indicate required performance; the RNP type/navigation spec is not part of the route designator (published separately). / RNP = required navigation performance: statement of the navigation performance necessary for operation within a defined airspace, based on navigation accuracy (95 % containment)."],["010.07.01.07:04","EASA LO 010.07.01.07.04 → State whether or not a prescribed RNP type is considered an integral part of the ATS route designator.","Route designators and navigation specifications allow unambiguous reference (pilots, ATC, computers) and indicate required performance; the RNP type/navigation spec is not part of the route designator (published separately). / RNP = required navigation performance: statement of the navigation performance necessary for operation within a defined airspace, based on navigation accuracy (95 % containment)."],["010.07.02.01:01","EASA LO 010.07.02.01.01 → State which ATS units provide clearances that do, and do not, include the prevention of collision with terrain.","Clearances from ATC do not include terrain/obstacle clearance (pilot's responsibility) except when an IFR flight is vectored by radar."],["010.07.02.02:01","EASA LO 010.07.02.02.01 → Recall all definitions given in ICAO Doc 4444 except the following: — accepting unit/controller, AD taxi circuit, aeronautical fixed service (AFS), aeronautical fixed station, air-taxiing, allocation, approach funnel, assignment, data convention, data processing, discrete code, D-value, flight status, ground effect, receiving unit/controller, sending unit/controller, transfer of control point, transferring unit/controller, unmanned free balloon.","Transfer of control: at a designated point, level or time agreed between units (coordination); the aircraft is instructed to contact the next unit; the accepting unit takes control when the aircraft crosses the transfer point (or earlier by agreement)."],["010.07.02.05:01","EASA LO 010.07.02.05.01 → State which information the issue of an ATC clearance is based on.","Clearances are based solely on known traffic conditions (not on terrain); if not suitable, the PIC may request an amended clearance; the PIC remains responsible for complying with rules. Purposes: expediting and separating traffic. Issued early enough to allow compliance. / Clearances from ATC do not include terrain/obstacle clearance (pilot's responsibility) except when an IFR flight is vectored by radar."],["010.07.02.05:03","EASA LO 010.07.02.05.03 → State who bears the responsibility for adhering to the applicable rules and regulations whilst flying under the control of an ATC unit.","Clearances from ATC do not include terrain/obstacle clearance (pilot's responsibility) except when an IFR flight is vectored by radar."],["010.07.02.05:04","EASA LO 010.07.02.05.04 → State the two primary purposes of clearances issued by ATC units.","Clearances are based solely on known traffic conditions (not on terrain); if not suitable, the PIC may request an amended clearance; the PIC remains responsible for complying with rules. Purposes: expediting and separating traffic. Issued early enough to allow compliance. / Clearances from ATC do not include terrain/obstacle clearance (pilot's responsibility) except when an IFR flight is vectored by radar."],["010.07.02.05:05","EASA LO 010.07.02.05.05 → State why clearances must be issued ‘early enough’ to aircraft.","Clearances are based solely on known traffic conditions (not on terrain); if not suitable, the PIC may request an amended clearance; the PIC remains responsible for complying with rules. Purposes: expediting and separating traffic. Issued early enough to allow compliance. / Clearances from ATC do not include terrain/obstacle clearance (pilot's responsibility) except when an IFR flight is vectored by radar."],["010.07.02.05:06","EASA LO 010.07.02.05.06 → Explain what is meant by the expression ‘clearance limit’.","Clearances from ATC do not include terrain/obstacle clearance (pilot's responsibility) except when an IFR flight is vectored by radar. / Clearance purpose: authorisation to proceed under specified conditions for preventing collisions and expediting traffic. Five parts: aircraft identification, clearance limit, route, level(s), any necessary instructions/information (approach/departure manoeuvres, communications, clearance expiry time)."],["010.07.02.06:02","EASA LO 010.07.02.06.02 → Define the maximum speed changes that ATC may impose.","Speed control: to facilitate spacing/sequencing; adjustments in multiples of 10 kt IAS (or 0.01 M), max changes about ±20 kt; no speed control within 4 NM of the threshold on final. / No speed control inside 4 NM."],["010.07.02.06:03","EASA LO 010.07.02.06.03 → State within what distance from the THR the PIC should not expect any kind of speed control.","No speed control inside 4 NM. / Read-back always: ATC route clearances, clearances/instructions to enter, land on, take off from, hold short of, cross, taxi and backtrack on any runway, runway in use, altimeter settings, SSR codes, level instructions, heading and speed instructions, transition levels."],["010.07.02.07:02","EASA LO 010.07.02.07.02 → Describe the expected reaction of the appropriate ATC unit upon a request to change from IFR to VFR.","Flights: IFR & VFR · Separation: IFR from IFR · Service: ATC; traffic info IFR↔VFR, VFR↔VFR / Flights: IFR & VFR · Separation: IFR from IFR and VFR; VFR from IFR · Service: ATC; VFR gets traffic info on VFR"],["010.07.02.08:01","EASA LO 010.07.02.08.01 → State the wake-turbulence categories of aircraft.","Wake turbulence categories: L (≤ 7 000 kg), M (7 000–136 000 kg), H (≥ 136 000 kg), J (super, A380). Heavy aircraft add \"HEAVY\" after the call sign in the initial call. Separation minima: see #66 (radar 4/5/6 NM; timed 2/3 min)."],["010.07.02.08:02","EASA LO 010.07.02.08.02 → State the wake-turbulence separation minima.","Wake turbulence categories: L (≤ 7 000 kg), M (7 000–136 000 kg), H (≥ 136 000 kg), J (super, A380). Heavy aircraft add \"HEAVY\" after the call sign in the initial call. Separation minima: see #66 (radar 4/5/6 NM; timed 2/3 min). / Non-radar wake separation: arrivals M/L behind H 2 min, L behind M 3 min… (L behind H 3 min); departures 2 min (3 min from intermediate position). \"Maintain own separation\" in VMC (on pilot request, climb/descent, class D/E, below FL 100, daytime) → the pilot takes responsibility."],["010.07.02.09:01","EASA LO 010.07.02.09.01 → Define the following terms: — TRL; — transition layer; and — TA.","Altimetry: TRL, transition layer, TA (see #90). QFE on NDB/non- precision approach refers to threshold elevation if the threshold is ≥ 2 m (7 ft) below aerodrome elevation. Altimeter settings rounded down to the nearest whole hPa. Lowest usable FL: lowest FL that ensures the minimum terrain clearance;"],["010.07.02.09:02","EASA LO 010.07.02.09.02 → Describe how the vertical position of an aircraft in the vicinity of an AD shall be expressed at or below the TA, at or above the TRL, and while climbing or descending through the transition layer.","Altimetry: TRL, transition layer, TA (see #90). QFE on NDB/non- precision approach refers to threshold elevation if the threshold is ≥ 2 m (7 ft) below aerodrome elevation. Altimeter settings rounded down to the nearest whole hPa. Lowest usable FL: lowest FL that ensures the minimum terrain clearance;"],["010.07.02.09:03","EASA LO 010.07.02.09.03 → Describe when the HGT of an aircraft using QFE during an NDB approach is referred to the landing THR instead of the AD elevation.","Altimetry: TRL, transition layer, TA (see #90). QFE on NDB/non- precision approach refers to threshold elevation if the threshold is ≥ 2 m (7 ft) below aerodrome elevation. Altimeter settings rounded down to the nearest whole hPa. Lowest usable FL: lowest FL that ensures the minimum terrain clearance;"],["010.07.02.09:04","EASA LO 010.07.02.09.04 → State in which margin altimeter settings provided to aircraft shall be rounded up or down.","Altimetry: TRL, transition layer, TA (see #90). QFE on NDB/non- precision approach refers to threshold elevation if the threshold is ≥ 2 m (7 ft) below aerodrome elevation. Altimeter settings rounded down to the nearest whole hPa. Lowest usable FL: lowest FL that ensures the minimum terrain clearance; / Read-back always: ATC route clearances, clearances/instructions to enter, land on, take off from, hold short of, cross, taxi and backtrack on any runway, runway in use, altimeter settings, SSR codes, level instructions, heading and speed instructions, transition levels."],["010.07.02.09:06","EASA LO 010.07.02.09.06 → Determine how the vertical position of an aircraft on an en-route flight is expressed at or above the lowest usable FL and below the lowest usable FL.","Altimetry: TRL, transition layer, TA (see #90). QFE on NDB/non- precision approach refers to threshold elevation if the threshold is ≥ 2 m (7 ft) below aerodrome elevation. Altimeter settings rounded down to the nearest whole hPa. Lowest usable FL: lowest FL that ensures the minimum terrain clearance; / Vertical: assign different levels — 1 000 ft up to FL 290 (and to FL 410 in RVSM), 2 000 ft above. Aircraft to the same destination: levels assigned so the first to arrive is at the lowest level (approach sequence). Vertical separation during climb/descent can be maintained by assigning specified rates of climb/descent to both aircraft."],["010.07.02.09:08","EASA LO 010.07.02.09.08 → Decide how and when a flight crew member shall be informed about the TRL.","Aircraft in the vicinity of an emergency/unlawful interference are informed of the nature of the emergency as far as practicable."],["010.07.02.10:01","EASA LO 010.07.02.10.01 → Describe when position reports shall be made by an aircraft flying on routes defined by designated significant points.","Position reports at compulsory (or requested) reporting points; six items: aircraft identification, position, time, flight level/altitude, next position and time over, ensuing significant point. Simplified report (omitting last three items) when in radar contact/as prescribed. / RNP = required navigation performance: statement of the navigation performance necessary for operation within a defined airspace, based on navigation accuracy (95 % containment)."],["010.07.02.10:02","EASA LO 010.07.02.10.02 → List the six items that are normally included in a voice position report.","Position reports at compulsory (or requested) reporting points; six items: aircraft identification, position, time, flight level/altitude, next position and time over, ensuing significant point. Simplified report (omitting last three items) when in radar contact/as prescribed."],["010.07.02.10:06","EASA LO 010.07.02.10.06 → Explain in which circumstances the airspeed should be included in a position report.","Position reports at compulsory (or requested) reporting points; six items: aircraft identification, position, time, flight level/altitude, next position and time over, ensuing significant point. Simplified report (omitting last three items) when in radar contact/as prescribed."],["010.07.02.10:07","EASA LO 010.07.02.10.07 → Explain the meaning of the acronym ‘ADS’.","Position reports at compulsory (or requested) reporting points; six items: aircraft identification, position, time, flight level/altitude, next position and time over, ensuing significant point. Simplified report (omitting last three items) when in radar contact/as prescribed."],["010.07.02.10:08","EASA LO 010.07.02.10.08 → Describe which expression shall precede the level figures in a position report if the level is reported in relation to 1013.2 hPa (standard pressure).","After changing frequency, the initial call includes the level (and cleared level). Level may be omitted if Mode C is verified. Airspeed included when required by the ATS unit (e.g. TAS change ≥ 5 %). ADS = automatic dependent surveillance. Levels relative to 1013.2 hPa are preceded by \"FLIGHT LEVEL\". / Position reports at compulsory (or requested) reporting points; six items: aircraft identification, position, time, flight level/altitude, next position and time over, ensuing significant point. Simplified report (omitting last three items) when in radar contact/as prescribed."],["010.07.02.12:01","EASA LO 010.07.02.12.01 → Explain the general provisions for the separation of controlled air traffic.","FIS & alerting within an FIR by the FIC/ACC, in controlled airspace and at controlled aerodromes by the ATC unit concerned. / Essential traffic = controlled traffic to which separation should apply but is not provided → essential traffic information. Reduced minima where regionally agreed, with better navigation/surveillance and safety assessment."],["010.07.02.12:03","EASA LO 010.07.02.12.03 → State the difference between the type of separation provided within the various classes of airspace and the various types of flight.","Airspace classes (ICAO/SERA):"],["010.07.02.12:05","EASA LO 010.07.02.12.05 → Describe how vertical separation is obtained.","Vertical: assign different levels — 1 000 ft up to FL 290 (and to FL 410 in RVSM), 2 000 ft above. Aircraft to the same destination: levels assigned so the first to arrive is at the lowest level (approach sequence). Vertical separation during climb/descent can be maintained by assigning specified rates of climb/descent to both aircraft."],["010.07.02.12:06","EASA LO 010.07.02.12.06 → State the required vertical separation minimum.","250 kt IAS below FL 100 applies in classes C (VFR), D, E, F, G (not A, B, not IFR in C). Radio communication required in A–E (VFR in E not), clearance required A–D (IFR in E). / Vertical: assign different levels — 1 000 ft up to FL 290 (and to FL 410 in RVSM), 2 000 ft above. Aircraft to the same destination: levels assigned so the first to arrive is at the lowest level (approach sequence). Vertical separation during climb/descent can be maintained by assigning specified rates of climb/descent to both aircraft."],["010.07.02.12:07","EASA LO 010.07.02.12.07 → Describe how the cruising levels of aircraft flying to the same destination and in the expected approach sequence are correlated with each other.","Vertical: assign different levels — 1 000 ft up to FL 290 (and to FL 410 in RVSM), 2 000 ft above. Aircraft to the same destination: levels assigned so the first to arrive is at the lowest level (approach sequence). Vertical separation during climb/descent can be maintained by assigning specified rates of climb/descent to both aircraft."],["010.07.02.12:10","EASA LO 010.07.02.12.10 → Describe how lateral separation of aircraft at the same level may be obtained.","Horizontal: lateral (different routes/geographical locations — geographical separation by fixes/visual; track separation from the same aid: VOR ≥ 15° and ≥ 15 NM, NDB ≥ 30° and ≥ 15 NM, DR ≥ 45° and ≥ 15 NM) and longitudinal (time, distance (DME/GNSS), Mach number technique). / Visual approach (IFR) may be cleared when the pilot reports the aerodrome or preceding aircraft in sight and ceiling is at/above the initial approach level; ATC still separates it from other arriving/departing traffic."],["010.07.02.12:11","EASA LO 010.07.02.12.11 → Explain the term ‘geographical separation’.","Horizontal: lateral (different routes/geographical locations — geographical separation by fixes/visual; track separation from the same aid: VOR ≥ 15° and ≥ 15 NM, NDB ≥ 30° and ≥ 15 NM, DR ≥ 45° and ≥ 15 NM) and longitudinal (time, distance (DME/GNSS), Mach number technique)."],["010.07.02.12:12","EASA LO 010.07.02.12.12 → Describe track separation between aircraft using the same navigation aid or method.","Horizontal: lateral (different routes/geographical locations — geographical separation by fixes/visual; track separation from the same aid: VOR ≥ 15° and ≥ 15 NM, NDB ≥ 30° and ≥ 15 NM, DR ≥ 45° and ≥ 15 NM) and longitudinal (time, distance (DME/GNSS), Mach number technique). / Standard radar separation 5 NM (3 NM in approach with suitable radar). Mach technique: turbojets on the same track keep assigned Mach numbers → spacing maintained over long oceanic routes."],["010.07.02.13:01","EASA LO 010.07.02.13.01 → Describe the expression ‘essential local traffic’. X X X X X X Exam BK Remarks","English (+ local language). / Aerodrome vicinity: essential local traffic = any aircraft, vehicle or person on/near the manoeuvring area or traffic in the vicinity that constitutes a hazard → information passed. Take-off not into wind: PIC may decline and wait for the into-wind runway (delay)."],["010.07.02.13:03","EASA LO 010.07.02.13.03 → State the condition to enable ATC to initiate a visual approach for an IFR flight.","Visual approach (IFR) may be cleared when the pilot reports the aerodrome or preceding aircraft in sight and ceiling is at/above the initial approach level; ATC still separates it from other arriving/departing traffic. / Flights: IFR & VFR · Separation: IFR from IFR · Service: ATC; traffic info IFR↔VFR, VFR↔VFR"],["010.07.02.13:07","EASA LO 010.07.02.13.07 → State which kinds of priority can be applied to aircraft for a landing.","Priority: aircraft in emergency first; landing aircraft priority over departures. A pilot wishing to hold for weather improvement is cleared to hold, others may approach before."],["010.07.02.13:08","EASA LO 010.07.02.13.08 → Describe the situation when a pilot of an aircraft in an approach sequence indicates their intention to hold for weather improvements.","Priority: aircraft in emergency first; landing aircraft priority over departures. A pilot wishing to hold for weather improvement is cleared to hold, others may approach before."],["010.07.02.13:10","EASA LO 010.07.02.13.10 → State the reasons which could probably lead to the decision to use another take-off or landing direction than the one into the wind.","Aerodrome vicinity: essential local traffic = any aircraft, vehicle or person on/near the manoeuvring area or traffic in the vicinity that constitutes a hazard → information passed. Take-off not into wind: PIC may decline and wait for the into-wind runway (delay). / Runway other than into wind for noise, runway length, obstacles, traffic flow; if unsuitable the PIC may refuse (possible delay)."],["010.07.02.13:11","EASA LO 010.07.02.13.11 → State the possible consequences for a PIC if the ‘RWY-in-use’ is not considered suitable for the operation involved.","Clearances are based solely on known traffic conditions (not on terrain); if not suitable, the PIC may request an amended clearance; the PIC remains responsible for complying with rules. Purposes: expediting and separating traffic. Issued early enough to allow compliance."],["010.07.02.14:01","EASA LO 010.07.02.14.01 → State the minimum separation between departing and arriving aircraft.","Visual approach (IFR) may be cleared when the pilot reports the aerodrome or preceding aircraft in sight and ceiling is at/above the initial approach level; ATC still separates it from other arriving/departing traffic. / Priority: landing (or final stage of approach) aircraft before departing aircraft. To departing aircraft without delay: significant changes in surface wind (direction/speed), visibility/RVR, wind shear, TS/Cb, temperature changes affecting performance. To arriving aircraft: significant changes in MET and runway conditions."],["010.07.02.14:02","EASA LO 010.07.02.14.02 → State the non-radar wake-turbulence longitudinal separation minima.","Non-radar wake separation: arrivals M/L behind H 2 min, L behind M 3 min… (L behind H 3 min); departures 2 min (3 min from intermediate position). \"Maintain own separation\" in VMC (on pilot request, climb/descent, class D/E, below FL 100, daytime) → the pilot takes responsibility. / Wake turbulence categories: L (≤ 7 000 kg), M (7 000–136 000 kg), H (≥ 136 000 kg), J (super, A380). Heavy aircraft add \"HEAVY\" after the call sign in the initial call. Separation minima: see #66 (radar 4/5/6 NM; timed 2/3 min)."],["010.07.02.15:01","EASA LO 010.07.02.15.01 → List the elements of information which shall be transmitted to an aircraft as early as practicable if an approach for landing is intended.","Priority: landing (or final stage of approach) aircraft before departing aircraft. To departing aircraft without delay: significant changes in surface wind (direction/speed), visibility/RVR, wind shear, TS/Cb, temperature changes affecting performance. To arriving aircraft: significant changes in MET and runway conditions. / Visual approach (IFR) may be cleared when the pilot reports the aerodrome or preceding aircraft in sight and ceiling is at/above the initial approach level; ATC still separates it from other arriving/departing traffic."],["010.07.02.15:02","EASA LO 010.07.02.15.02 → List the elements of information to be transmitted to an aircraft at the commencement of final approach.","Early approach information: type of approach, runway in use, MET (wind, visibility/RVR, weather, cloud, temperature, altimeter), runway surface conditions, NAVAID status, wind shear reports. Commencement of final: significant changes in mean surface wind, latest RVR/visibility, wind shear. During final: sudden hazards, significant wind changes, RVR changes. / Priority: landing (or final stage of approach) aircraft before departing aircraft. To departing aircraft without delay: significant changes in surface wind (direction/speed), visibility/RVR, wind shear, TS/Cb, temperature changes affecting performance. To arriving aircraft: significant changes in MET and runway conditions."],["010.07.02.15:03","EASA LO 010.07.02.15.03 → List the elements of information to be transmitted to an aircraft during final approach.","Priority: landing (or final stage of approach) aircraft before departing aircraft. To departing aircraft without delay: significant changes in surface wind (direction/speed), visibility/RVR, wind shear, TS/Cb, temperature changes affecting performance. To arriving aircraft: significant changes in MET and runway conditions. / Early approach information: type of approach, runway in use, MET (wind, visibility/RVR, weather, cloud, temperature, altimeter), runway surface conditions, NAVAID status, wind shear reports. Commencement of final: significant changes in mean surface wind, latest RVR/visibility, wind shear. During final: sudden hazards, significant wind changes, RVR changes."],["010.07.02.15:05","EASA LO 010.07.02.15.05 → State the sequence of priority between aircraft landing (or in the final stage of an approach to land) and aircraft intending to depart.","Priority: landing (or final stage of approach) aircraft before departing aircraft. To departing aircraft without delay: significant changes in surface wind (direction/speed), visibility/RVR, wind shear, TS/Cb, temperature changes affecting performance. To arriving aircraft: significant changes in MET and runway conditions. / Priority: aircraft in emergency first; landing aircraft priority over departures. A pilot wishing to hold for weather improvement is cleared to hold, others may approach before."],["010.07.02.16:01","EASA LO 010.07.02.16.01 → Name the operational failure or irregularity of AD equipment which shall be reported by the TWR immediately.","TWR: reports immediately failures/irregularities of aerodrome equipment (lights, NAVAIDs); informs the ACC/FIC if an aircraft does not land within 5 min of the expected time (alerting). VFR suspended: hold VFR departures, recall local VFR flights, notify ACC/APP. / Three types of ATS: air traffic control service, flight information service (FIS), alerting service. Three ATC services: area control (ACC), approach control (APP), aerodrome control (TWR)."],["010.07.02.16:03","EASA LO 010.07.02.16.03 → Describe the procedures to be observed by the TWR whenever VFR operations are suspended.","TWR: reports immediately failures/irregularities of aerodrome equipment (lights, NAVAIDs); informs the ACC/FIC if an aircraft does not land within 5 min of the expected time (alerting). VFR suspended: hold VFR departures, recall local VFR flights, notify ACC/APP. / Three types of ATS: air traffic control service, flight information service (FIS), alerting service. Three ATC services: area control (ACC), approach control (APP), aerodrome control (TWR)."],["010.07.02.16:04","EASA LO 010.07.02.16.04 → Explain the term ‘RWY-in-use’ and its selection.","Three types of ATS: air traffic control service, flight information service (FIS), alerting service. Three ATC services: area control (ACC), approach control (APP), aerodrome control (TWR)."],["010.07.02.16:07","EASA LO 010.07.02.16.07 → Explain the exact meaning of the expression ‘RWY vacated’.","Three types of ATS: air traffic control service, flight information service (FIS), alerting service. Three ATC services: area control (ACC), approach control (APP), aerodrome control (TWR)."],["010.07.02.17:01","EASA LO 010.07.02.17.01 → State the basic identification procedures used with radar.","Identification: SSR (code, IDENT/SPI, Mode S aircraft ID), correlation of a position report with a radar return, turn of ≥ 30°, departing aircraft observed within 1 NM of the runway end. PSR = primary surveillance radar."],["010.07.02.17:02","EASA LO 010.07.02.17.02 → Define the term ‘PSR’.","Identification: SSR (code, IDENT/SPI, Mode S aircraft ID), correlation of a position report with a radar return, turn of ≥ 30°, departing aircraft observed within 1 NM of the runway end. PSR = primary surveillance radar."],["010.07.02.17:03","EASA LO 010.07.02.17.03 → Describe the circumstances under which an aircraft provided with radar service should be informed of its position.","Aircraft in the vicinity of an emergency/unlawful interference are informed of the nature of the emergency as far as practicable. / FIS is provided to all aircraft likely to be affected which are provided with ATC or otherwise known to ATS."],["010.07.02.17:06","EASA LO 010.07.02.17.06 → State the aims of radar vectoring as shown in ICAO Doc 4444.","Clearances from ATC do not include terrain/obstacle clearance (pilot's responsibility) except when an IFR flight is vectored by radar. / Radar vectoring = navigational guidance in the form of specific headings. Aims: separation, noise abatement, weather avoidance, expediting traffic, positioning for approach (ILS intercept). ATC informs the pilot of the purpose and the limit; terminating: \"resume own navigation\" + position (and heading/track instructions if needed)."],["010.07.02.17:10","EASA LO 010.07.02.17.10 → Describe what kind of action (concerning the transponder) the pilot is expected to perform in case of emergency if they have previously been directed by ATC to operate the transponder on a specific code.","Transponder in emergency: keep the assigned code unless instructed; otherwise 7700 (emergency), 7500 (unlawful interference), 7600 (COM failure)."],["010.07.02.18:01","EASA LO 010.07.02.18.01 → Describe the objective and basic principles of the air traffic advisory service.","Flights: IFR & VFR · Separation: IFR from IFR as far as practical · Service: air traffic advisory + FIS / Air traffic advisory service (class F): aims to ensure separation between IFR flights on advisory routes/areas as far as practicable; provided to IFR flights that choose to participate; issues advisory information/suggestions, not clearances (by ACC/advisory unit)."],["010.07.02.18:03","EASA LO 010.07.02.18.03 → Explain the difference between advisory information and clearances, stating which ATS units are responsible for their issue.","Air traffic advisory service (class F): aims to ensure separation between IFR flights on advisory routes/areas as far as practicable; provided to IFR flights that choose to participate; issues advisory information/suggestions, not clearances (by ACC/advisory unit). / Flights: IFR & VFR · Separation: IFR from IFR as far as practical · Service: air traffic advisory + FIS"],["010.07.02.19:02","EASA LO 010.07.02.19.02 → State the special rights an aircraft in a state of emergency can expect from ATC.","Transponder in emergency: keep the assigned code unless instructed; otherwise 7700 (emergency), 7500 (unlawful interference), 7600 (COM failure)."],["010.07.02.19:04","EASA LO 010.07.02.19.04 → State how it can be ascertained, in case of a failure of two-way COM, whether the aircraft is able to receive transmissions from the ATS unit.","EAT (expected approach time) = time at which ATC expects a delayed arrival to leave the holding fix to complete its approach; revised when it changes by ≥ 5 min — used for COM failure procedures. / Transponder in emergency: keep the assigned code unless instructed; otherwise 7700 (emergency), 7500 (unlawful interference), 7600 (COM failure)."],["010.07.02.19:05","EASA LO 010.07.02.19.05 → State on which frequencies appropriate information, for an aircraft encountering two-way COM failure, shall be sent by ATS.","Transponder in emergency: keep the assigned code unless instructed; otherwise 7700 (emergency), 7500 (unlawful interference), 7600 (COM failure). / EAT (expected approach time) = time at which ATC expects a delayed arrival to leave the holding fix to complete its approach; revised when it changes by ≥ 5 min — used for COM failure procedures."],["010.07.02.19:07","EASA LO 010.07.02.19.07 → Explain the reasons for fuel-dumping and state the minimum level.","Fuel dumping: to reduce landing mass in an emergency — ATC gives the area and level: at least 6 000 ft (1 800 m) above ground and away from other traffic. Call-sign change requested to avoid confusion with similar call signs."]],"3":[["062.01.01.01:02","Electromagnetic waves: fill in the gaps → Radio waves travel at the ____ (3 × 10⁸ m/s; ~162 000 NM/s). ____ = one complete series of values of a periodic process.","Radio waves travel at the speed of light ≈ 300 000 km/s (3 × 10⁸ m/s; ~162 000 NM/s). Cycle = one complete series of values of a periodic process."],["062.01.01.02:02","Electromagnetic waves: fill in the gaps → ____ = cycles per second (____). Wavelength (λ) = distance travelled during one cycle. Amplitude = maximum deflection of the wave. λ = c / f → e.g. ____: 300 MHz → 1 m; 110 MHz ILS → 2.7 m; 300 kHz → 1 000 m.","Frequency (f) = cycles per second (Hz). Wavelength (λ) = distance travelled during one cycle. Amplitude = maximum deflection of the wave. λ = c / f → e.g. λ (m) = 300 / f (MHz): 300 MHz → 1 m; 110 MHz ILS → 2.7 m; 300 kHz → 1 000 m."],["062.01.01.02:06","Electromagnetic waves: fill in the gaps → ____ = fraction of one wavelength in degrees (000– 360°); ____ = angular difference between corresponding points of two waves of equal wavelength (used in VOR bearing measurement).","Phase angle = fraction of one wavelength in degrees (000– 360°); phase difference = angular difference between corresponding points of two waves of equal wavelength (used in VOR bearing measurement)."],["062.01.01.03:01","Electromagnetic waves – VLF: what are the key facts?","Frequency: 3–30 kHz · Wavelength: 100–10 km · Examples: (Omega, historic)"],["062.01.01.03:01","Electromagnetic waves – LF: what are the key facts?","Frequency: 30–300 kHz · Wavelength: 10–1 km · Examples: NDB"],["062.01.01.03:01","Electromagnetic waves – MF: what are the key facts?","Frequency: 300–3 000 kHz · Wavelength: 1 000–100 m · Examples: NDB, broadcast"],["062.01.01.03:03","Electromagnetic waves – HF: what are the key facts?","Frequency: 3–30 MHz · Wavelength: 100–10 m · Examples: HF comms, HF-VOLMET"],["062.01.01.03:01","Electromagnetic waves – VHF: what are the key facts?","Frequency: 30–300 MHz · Wavelength: 10–1 m · Examples: VOR, ILS LOC, VHF COM, markers (75 MHz)"],["062.01.01.03:01","Electromagnetic waves – UHF: what are the key facts?","Frequency: 300–3 000 MHz · Wavelength: 1 m–10 cm · Examples: ILS GP, DME, SSR, GPS"],["062.01.01.03:01","Electromagnetic waves – SHF: what are the key facts?","Frequency: 3–30 GHz · Wavelength: 10–1 cm · Examples: weather radar, radio altimeter, MLS"],["062.01.01.03:01","Electromagnetic waves – EHF: what are the key facts?","Frequency: 30–300 GHz · Wavelength: 10–1 mm · Examples: —"],["062.01.01.03:03","Electromagnetic waves: fill in the gaps → A ____ radiates the ____ ____. HF VOLMET and HF two-way communication use single sideband (SSB) — carrier and one sideband suppressed → less power/bandwidth.","A modulated carrier radiates the carrier frequency plus upper and lower sidebands. HF VOLMET and HF two-way communication use single sideband (SSB) — carrier and one sideband suppressed → less power/bandwidth."],["062.01.01.03:04","Electromagnetic waves: fill in the gaps → ITU emission classes: ____ = unmodulated carrier (NDB, needs BFO for ident), ____ = keyed carrier Morse (NDB ident — BFO needed), ____ = amplitude-modulated Morse tone (NDB ident audible), (A3E = AM voice VHF COM, J3E = SSB voice HF).","ITU emission classes: N0N = unmodulated carrier (NDB, needs BFO for ident), A1A = keyed carrier Morse (NDB ident — BFO needed), A2A = amplitude-modulated Morse tone (NDB ident audible), (A3E = AM voice VHF COM, J3E = SSB voice HF)."],["062.01.01.04:01","Electromagnetic waves: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (duration), ____ (peak power during the pulse), continuous (mean) power = average power over time (much lower than peak).","Pulse: pulse length (duration), pulse power (peak power during the pulse), continuous (mean) power = average power over time (much lower than peak)."],["062.01.01.06:01","Electromagnetic waves: fill in the gaps → ____ = the radio wave that transports the information; ____ = impressing information onto it: ____ modulation (AM) — varies amplitude (VHF voice, NDB, VOR reference); frequency modulation (FM) — varies frequency (VOR reference sub-carrier, radio altimeter);","Carrier wave = the radio wave that transports the information; modulation = impressing information onto it: amplitude modulation (AM) — varies amplitude (VHF voice, NDB, VOR reference); frequency modulation (FM) — varies frequency (VOR reference sub-carrier, radio altimeter);"],["062.01.01.06:03","Electromagnetic waves: fill in the gaps → ____ — short pulses with long pauses (radar, DME, SSR); ____ ____ — phase reversals (GPS).","pulse modulation — short pulses with long pauses (radar, DME, SSR); phase modulation — phase reversals (GPS)."],["062.01.02.01:01","Antennas: fill in the gaps → ____ = device converting electrical power into radio waves and vice versa. Simplest: ____ = wire ____ long (also ¼ λ monopole with ground plane).","Antenna = device converting electrical power into radio waves and vice versa. Simplest: dipole = wire ½ λ long (also ¼ λ monopole with ground plane)."],["062.01.02.01:04","Antennas: fill in the gaps → An EM wave consists of an oscillating ____ and ____ ____ field, perpendicular to each other and to the direction of propagation, in phase, travelling at c.","An EM wave consists of an oscillating electric (E) and magnetic (H) field, perpendicular to each other and to the direction of propagation, in phase, travelling at c."],["062.01.02.02:01","Antennas: fill in the gaps → ____ = orientation of the plane of the ____: ____ (NDB, VHF COM) or horizontal (VOR, ILS); receiving antenna must match.","Polarisation = orientation of the plane of the E-field: vertical (NDB, VHF COM) or horizontal (VOR, ILS); receiving antenna must match."],["062.01.02.03:01","Antennas: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (old ADF — null when the loop plane is perpendicular to the signal), ____ (weather radar), slotted planar array (modern weather radar — flat plate with slots, narrower beam, less side lobes).","Directional antennas: loop (old ADF — null when the loop plane is perpendicular to the signal), parabolic dish (weather radar), slotted planar array (modern weather radar — flat plate with slots, narrower beam, less side lobes)."],["062.01.02.01:01","Antennas: fill in the gaps → ____: parts of the aircraft (fuselage, wings, gear, engine) block/reduce the signal path in certain attitudes/directions (e.g. VHF/GPS loss in steep turns). ____: top/bottom depending on the service (GPS on top, DME/transponder/marker and radio altimeter on the bottom), separated to avoid interference, away from obstructions; clear line of sight.","Antenna shadowing: parts of the aircraft (fuselage, wings, gear, engine) block/reduce the signal path in certain attitudes/directions (e.g. VHF/GPS loss in steep turns). Placement: top/bottom depending on the service (GPS on top, DME/transponder/marker and radio altimeter on the bottom), separated to avoid interference, away from obstructions; clear line of sight."],["062.01.01.03:01","Exam trap (Antennas): complete the fact → λ (m) = ____f (MHz).","λ (m) = 300/f (MHz)."],["062.01.02.01:01","Exam trap (Antennas): complete the fact → MF = ____ (the ELC correction!).","MF = 1 000–100 m (the ELC correction!)."],["062.01.01.01:01","EASA LO 062.01.01.01.01 → State that radio waves travel at the speed of light, being approximately 300 000 km/s.","Radio waves travel at the speed of light ≈ 300 000 km/s (3 × 10⁸ m/s; ~162 000 NM/s). Cycle = one complete series of values of a periodic process. / Frequency: 30–300 kHz · Wavelength: 10–1 km · Examples: NDB"],["062.01.01.02:01","EASA LO 062.01.01.02.01 → Define ‘frequency’: the number of cycles occurring in 1 second expressed in Hertz (Hz).","Frequency (f) = cycles per second (Hz). Wavelength (λ) = distance travelled during one cycle. Amplitude = maximum deflection of the wave. λ = c / f → e.g. λ (m) = 300 / f (MHz): 300 MHz → 1 m; 110 MHz ILS → 2.7 m; 300 kHz → 1 000 m."],["062.01.01.02:03","EASA LO 062.01.01.02.03 → Define ‘amplitude’: the maximum deflection in an oscillation or wave.","Frequency (f) = cycles per second (Hz). Wavelength (λ) = distance travelled during one cycle. Amplitude = maximum deflection of the wave. λ = c / f → e.g. λ (m) = 300 / f (MHz): 300 MHz → 1 m; 110 MHz ILS → 2.7 m; 300 kHz → 1 000 m."],["062.01.01.02:04","EASA LO 062.01.01.02.04 → State that the relationship between wavelength and frequency is: wavelength (λ) = speed of light (c) / frequency (f).","Phase angle = fraction of one wavelength in degrees (000– 360°); phase difference = angular difference between corresponding points of two waves of equal wavelength (used in VOR bearing measurement)."],["062.01.01.02:05","EASA LO 062.01.01.02.05 → Define ‘phase angle’: the fraction of one wavelength expressed in degrees from 000° to 360°.","Phase angle = fraction of one wavelength in degrees (000– 360°); phase difference = angular difference between corresponding points of two waves of equal wavelength (used in VOR bearing measurement). / Frequency: 300–3 000 kHz · Wavelength: 1 000–100 m · Examples: NDB, broadcast"],["062.01.01.03:02","EASA LO 062.01.01.03.02 → State that when a carrier wave is modulated, the resultant radiation consists of the carrier frequency plus additional upper and lower sidebands.","A modulated carrier radiates the carrier frequency plus upper and lower sidebands. HF VOLMET and HF two-way communication use single sideband (SSB) — carrier and one sideband suppressed → less power/bandwidth."],["062.01.01.05:01","EASA LO 062.01.01.05.01 → Define ‘carrier wave’: the radio wave acting as the carrier or transporter.","Carrier wave = the radio wave that transports the information; modulation = impressing information onto it: amplitude modulation (AM) — varies amplitude (VHF voice, NDB, VOR reference); frequency modulation (FM) — varies frequency (VOR reference sub-carrier, radio altimeter);"],["062.01.01.05:02","EASA LO 062.01.01.05.02 → Define ‘modulation’: the technical term for the process of impressing and transporting information by radio waves.","Carrier wave = the radio wave that transports the information; modulation = impressing information onto it: amplitude modulation (AM) — varies amplitude (VHF voice, NDB, VOR reference); frequency modulation (FM) — varies frequency (VOR reference sub-carrier, radio altimeter);"],["062.01.01.06:02","EASA LO 062.01.01.06.02 → Define ‘frequency modulation’: the information that is impressed onto the carrier wave by altering the frequency of the carrier.","Carrier wave = the radio wave that transports the information; modulation = impressing information onto it: amplitude modulation (AM) — varies amplitude (VHF voice, NDB, VOR reference); frequency modulation (FM) — varies frequency (VOR reference sub-carrier, radio altimeter);"],["062.01.01.06:04","EASA LO 062.01.01.06.04 → Describe ‘phase modulation’: a modulation form used in GPS where the phase of the carrier wave is reversed.","pulse modulation — short pulses with long pauses (radar, DME, SSR); phase modulation — phase reversals (GPS). / Carrier wave = the radio wave that transports the information; modulation = impressing information onto it: amplitude modulation (AM) — varies amplitude (VHF voice, NDB, VOR reference); frequency modulation (FM) — varies frequency (VOR reference sub-carrier, radio altimeter);"],["062.01.02.01:02","EASA LO 062.01.02.01.02 → State that the simplest type of antenna is a dipole, which is a wire of length equal to one half of the wavelength.","Antenna = device converting electrical power into radio waves and vice versa. Simplest: dipole = wire ½ λ long (also ¼ λ monopole with ground plane)."],["062.01.02.01:03","EASA LO 062.01.02.01.03 → State that an electromagnetic wave always consists of an oscillating electric (E) and an oscillating magnetic (H) field which propagates at the speed of light.","An EM wave consists of an oscillating electric (E) and magnetic (H) field, perpendicular to each other and to the direction of propagation, in phase, travelling at c."]],"4":[["021.09.01.01:01","Static electricity: fill in the gaps → Static charge builds up by ____ of air, precipitation and particles on the airframe — worst in ____ ____ and at high speed. Effects: radio interference (noise on ADF/HF), St Elmo's fire, discharge sparks, shocks.","Static charge builds up by friction of air, precipitation and particles on the airframe — worst in precipitation (rain, snow, ice crystals), dust/sand, clouds (Cb) and at high speed. Effects: radio interference (noise on ADF/HF), St Elmo's fire, discharge sparks, shocks."],["021.09.01.01:02","Static electricity: fill in the gaps → ____: thin rods/wicks at ____ ____ — discharge static charge to the air at low potential, reducing radio interference. Pilot checks during pre-flight that they are present and undamaged (MEL lists the minimum number).","Static dischargers (wicks): thin rods/wicks at trailing edges of wings, ailerons, elevators, rudder, wingtips — discharge static charge to the air at low potential, reducing radio interference. Pilot checks during pre-flight that they are present and undamaged (MEL lists the minimum number)."],["021.09.01.01:03","Static electricity: fill in the gaps → ____: aircraft and fuel truck are ____ to equalise potential → no spark that could ignite fuel vapour.","Grounding before refuelling/defuelling: aircraft and fuel truck are bonded/grounded to equalise potential → no spark that could ignite fuel vapour."],["021.09.01.01:04","Static electricity: fill in the gaps → ____: electrical connection of all metal parts of the airframe (bonding straps across hinges, control surfaces) → same potential: ____ (single-wire system), ____ (current flows safely through the skin), reduces radio interference, protects against static shocks.","Bonding: electrical connection of all metal parts of the airframe (bonding straps across hinges, control surfaces) → same potential: prevents sparks, provides a low-resistance return path (single-wire system), lightning protection (current flows safely through the skin), reduces radio interference, protects against static shocks."],["021.09.01.02:01","Direct current (DC): fill in the gaps → ____ = current flowing in ____; it can only flow in a ____ (source → load → back).","DC = current flowing in one direction; it can only flow in a closed circuit (source → load → back)."],["021.09.01.02:02","Direct current (DC): fill in the gaps → ____ (metals: copper, aluminium, silver — many free electrons), ____ (silicon, germanium — conductivity depends on doping/temperature), ____ (rubber, plastics, glass, ceramics).","Conductors (metals: copper, aluminium, silver — many free electrons), semiconductors (silicon, germanium — conductivity depends on doping/temperature), insulators (rubber, plastics, glass, ceramics)."],["021.09.01.02:01","Direct current (DC): fill in the gaps → ____: toggle, rocker, push-button, ____ (actuated mechanically by gear, doors, flaps), rotary; ____ normally closed, momentary vs latching, single/double-pole, single/double-throw — choice depends on function (position sensing, control).","Switches: toggle, rocker, push-button, micro-switch (actuated mechanically by gear, doors, flaps), rotary; normally open vs normally closed, momentary vs latching, single/double-pole, single/double-throw — choice depends on function (position sensing, control)."],["021.09.01.02:01","Direct current (DC): fill in the gaps → ____ = electrical potential difference (\"pressure\"); ____ = flow of charge. ____: U = I × R — current rises with voltage and falls with resistance (Ω).","Voltage (U, volt V) = electrical potential difference (\"pressure\"); current (I, ampere A) = flow of charge. Ohm's law: U = I × R — current rises with voltage and falls with resistance (Ω)."],["021.09.01.02:06","Direct current (DC): fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (same current; voltages add). Parallel: ____ (same voltage; total R smaller than the smallest resistor; currents add). Example: 6 Ω ∥ 3 Ω = 2 Ω.","Series: R = R₁ + R₂ + … (same current; voltages add). Parallel: 1/R = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂ + … (same voltage; total R smaller than the smallest resistor; currents add). Example: 6 Ω ∥ 3 Ω = 2 Ω."],["021.09.01.02:07","Direct current (DC): fill in the gaps → ____ (positive temperature coefficient: resistance ↑ with temperature — metals, self-limiting heaters, overcurrent protection) and ____ (negative: resistance ↓ with temperature — thermistors for ____, inrush current limiting).","PTC (positive temperature coefficient: resistance ↑ with temperature — metals, self-limiting heaters, overcurrent protection) and NTC (negative: resistance ↓ with temperature — thermistors for temperature sensing, inrush current limiting)."],["021.09.01.02:08","Direct current (DC): fill in the gaps → ____ (watt, W). Example: 28 V × 10 A = 280 W.","Power P = U × I = I² × R = U²/R (watt, W). Example: 28 V × 10 A = 280 W."],["021.09.01.06:01","Electromagnetism: fill in the gaps → A current produces a ____ around the conductor; field strength ∝ ____ (and number of turns in a coil; iron core increases it).","A current produces a magnetic field around the conductor; field strength ∝ current (and number of turns in a coil; iron core increases it)."],["021.09.01.06:01","Electromagnetism: fill in the gaps → ____: coil with a movable iron core (plunger) pulled in when energised → ____ (valves, locks, starter engagement; heavy-current switching).","Solenoid: coil with a movable iron core (plunger) pulled in when energised → mechanical actuation (valves, locks, starter engagement; heavy-current switching)."],["021.09.01.06:01","Electromagnetism: fill in the gaps → ____: electromagnetically operated ____ — a small control current in the coil closes/opens contacts carrying a ____ (remote switching, isolation); contactors for very high currents.","Relay: electromagnetically operated switch — a small control current in the coil closes/opens contacts carrying a large current (remote switching, isolation); contactors for very high currents."],["021.09.01.06:05","Electromagnetism: fill in the gaps → ____: a ____ (or conductor moving in a field) induces a voltage (generators, transformers). Circuits can affect each other by ____ interference (cables close together, EMI/HIRF) → screening, separation, twisted pairs.","Electromagnetic induction: a changing magnetic field (or conductor moving in a field) induces a voltage (generators, transformers). Circuits can affect each other by induced interference (cables close together, EMI/HIRF) → screening, separation, twisted pairs."],["021.09.01.07:02","Circuit protection: fill in the gaps → ____: thin wire/strip that ____ when current exceeds its rating → opens the circuit; rated in ____ (current it carries continuously; fuses must be protecting the wire). Replace only with the same rating.","Fuse: thin wire/strip that melts when current exceeds its rating → opens the circuit; rated in amperes (current it carries continuously; fuses must be protecting the wire). Replace only with the same rating."],["021.09.01.07:03","Circuit protection: fill in the gaps → ____: resettable. ____ — bimetal heats with ____ of current and trips (time-delayed on small overloads). Magnetic CB — responds to high currents instantly (reverse current CBs sense direction of current). Trip-free: cannot be held closed against a fault.","Circuit breaker (CB): resettable. Thermal CB — bimetal heats with magnitude of current and trips (time-delayed on small overloads). Magnetic CB — responds to high currents instantly (reverse current CBs sense direction of current). Trip-free: cannot be held closed against a fault."],["021.09.01.07:04","Circuit protection: fill in the gaps → CBs may be pulled and ____ to ____ only when a procedure (QRH/FCOM) says so.","CBs may be pulled and reset to restart a computer/system only when a procedure (QRH/FCOM) says so."],["021.09.01.07:05","Circuit protection: fill in the gaps → ____: resistance → ~0 → by Ohm's law ____ ____ (I = U/R), power (I²R) dissipated in wiring → extreme heat → fire; overcurrent (overload) also causes heating of wires/insulation. Overcurrents can be transient (motor start, inrush) — protection is designed to tolerate short transients.","Short circuit: resistance → ~0 → by Ohm's law current → very high (I = U/R), power (I²R) dissipated in wiring → extreme heat → fire; overcurrent (overload) also causes heating of wires/insulation. Overcurrents can be transient (motor start, inrush) — protection is designed to tolerate short transients."],["021.09.01.07:08","Circuit protection: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ of a tripped CB or a ____ fuse rating can feed a real fault → overheating, smoke, fire; normally one reset only after a cooling period (if allowed).","Hazards: multiple resets of a tripped CB or a wrong (higher) fuse rating can feed a real fault → overheating, smoke, fire; normally one reset only after a cooling period (if allowed)."],["021.09.02.01:01","Batteries: fill in the gaps → Function: ____, ____ for essential equipment, stabilising the DC bus, power on ground.","Function: engine/APU start, emergency/standby power for essential equipment, stabilising the DC bus, power on ground."],["021.09.02.01:03","Batteries: fill in the gaps → Types: ____ (2 V/cell; electrolyte sulphuric acid; voltage drops steadily under load; charge state by SG), ____ ____ (1.2 V/cell; flat voltage under load, high discharge currents, robust; thermal runaway risk on constant-voltage charging at high temperature), lithium-ion (3.6–3.7 V/cell; high energy density, light; thermal runaway risk).","Types: lead-acid (2 V/cell; electrolyte sulphuric acid; voltage drops steadily under load; charge state by SG), nickel-cadmium (NiCd) (1.2 V/cell; flat voltage under load, high discharge currents, robust; thermal runaway risk on constant-voltage charging at high temperature), lithium-ion (3.6–3.7 V/cell; high energy density, light; thermal runaway risk)."],["021.09.02.01:02","Batteries: fill in the gaps → Cells in ____ add voltage (e.g. 12 × 2 V = 24 V lead-acid; 20 × 1.2 V = 24 V NiCd); cells in ____ add capacity.","Cells in series add voltage (e.g. 12 × 2 V = 24 V lead-acid; 20 × 1.2 V = 24 V NiCd); cells in parallel add capacity."],["021.09.02.01:05","Batteries: fill in the gaps → ____ (e.g. 24 V) < ____ (e.g. ____ on the bus) — the charging voltage must be higher to push current into the battery.","Battery voltage (e.g. 24 V) < charging voltage (e.g. 28 V on the bus) — the charging voltage must be higher to push current into the battery."],["021.09.01.07:02","Batteries: fill in the gaps → ____ = charge it can deliver, ____; e.g. 40 Ah → 8 A for 5 h (at rated discharge). ____ reduces capacity and output (cold starts); high temperature increases self-discharge and runaway risk.","Capacity = charge it can deliver, ampere-hours (Ah); e.g. 40 Ah → 8 A for 5 h (at rated discharge). Low temperature reduces capacity and output (cold starts); high temperature increases self-discharge and runaway risk."],["021.09.02.01:08","Batteries: fill in the gaps → On ____, battery power is ____ (e.g. 30 min for essential loads) → load shedding, land ASAP.","On loss of all generators, battery power is time-limited (e.g. 30 min for essential loads) → load shedding, land ASAP."],["021.09.02.01:09","Batteries: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ on board (incl. passengers' PEDs, power banks), ____ (battery and environment), physical condition (damage, crushing), charging (over-charge) → thermal runaway: internal short → heat → neighbouring cells ignite in a chain reaction, toxic smoke, re-ignition.","Lithium batteries threat: number on board (incl. passengers' PEDs, power banks), temperature (battery and environment), physical condition (damage, crushing), charging (over-charge) → thermal runaway: internal short → heat → neighbouring cells ignite in a chain reaction, toxic smoke, re-ignition."],["021.09.02.01:01","Batteries: fill in the gaps → ____: installed batteries in fire-resistant boxes with venting; cabin fires with PEDs: extinguish flames (halon), then ____, immerse/contain, monitor for re-ignition; don't move a burning device; in flight: limited means → land ASAP.","Containment: installed batteries in fire-resistant boxes with venting; cabin fires with PEDs: extinguish flames (halon), then cool with water/non-alcoholic liquid, immerse/contain, monitor for re-ignition; don't move a burning device; in flight: limited means → land ASAP."],["021.09.01.02:03","EASA LO 021.09.01.02.03 → Describe the difference in use of the following mechanical switches and explain the difference in","Voltage (U, volt V) = electrical potential difference (\"pressure\"); current (I, ampere A) = flow of charge. Ohm's law: U = I × R — current rises with voltage and falls with resistance (Ω)."],["021.09.01.02:04","EASA LO 021.09.01.02.04 → Define voltage and current, and state their unit of measurement.","Voltage (U, volt V) = electrical potential difference (\"pressure\"); current (I, ampere A) = flow of charge. Ohm's law: U = I × R — current rises with voltage and falls with resistance (Ω). / Battery voltage (e.g. 24 V) < charging voltage (e.g. 28 V on the bus) — the charging voltage must be higher to push current into the battery."],["021.09.01.02:05","EASA LO 021.09.01.02.05 → Explain Ohm’s law in qualitative terms.","Voltage (U, volt V) = electrical potential difference (\"pressure\"); current (I, ampere A) = flow of charge. Ohm's law: U = I × R — current rises with voltage and falls with resistance (Ω)."],["021.09.01.06:02","EASA LO 021.09.01.06.02 → Describe how the strength of the magnetic field changes with the magnitude of the current.","A current produces a magnetic field around the conductor; field strength ∝ current (and number of turns in a coil; iron core increases it)."],["021.09.01.07:06","EASA LO 021.09.01.07.06 → Explain the risk of fire resulting from excessive heat in a circuit subjected to overcurrent.","Short circuit: resistance → ~0 → by Ohm's law current → very high (I = U/R), power (I²R) dissipated in wiring → extreme heat → fire; overcurrent (overload) also causes heating of wires/insulation. Overcurrents can be transient (motor start, inrush) — protection is designed to tolerate short transients."],["021.09.01.07:07","EASA LO 021.09.01.07.07 → Explain that overcurrent situations may be transient.","Short circuit: resistance → ~0 → by Ohm's law current → very high (I = U/R), power (I²R) dissipated in wiring → extreme heat → fire; overcurrent (overload) also causes heating of wires/insulation. Overcurrents can be transient (motor start, inrush) — protection is designed to tolerate short transients."],["021.09.02.01:04","EASA LO 021.09.02.01.04 → Explain the term ‘cell voltage’ and describe how a battery may consist of several cells that combined provide the desirable voltage and capacity.","Cells in series add voltage (e.g. 12 × 2 V = 24 V lead-acid; 20 × 1.2 V = 24 V NiCd); cells in parallel add capacity."],["021.09.02.01:10","EASA LO 021.09.02.01.10 → Describe how to contain a battery thermal runaway highlighting the following: — how one cell can affect the neighbouring cells; — challenges if it happens in an aircraft during flight.","Lithium batteries threat: number on board (incl. passengers' PEDs, power banks), temperature (battery and environment), physical condition (damage, crushing), charging (over-charge) → thermal runaway: internal short → heat → neighbouring cells ignite in a chain reaction, toxic smoke, re-ignition. / Types: lead-acid (2 V/cell; electrolyte sulphuric acid; voltage drops steadily under load; charge state by SG), nickel-cadmium (NiCd) (1.2 V/cell; flat voltage under load, high discharge currents, robust; thermal runaway risk on constant-voltage charging at high temperature), lithium-ion (3.6–3.7 V/cell; high energy density, light; thermal runaway risk)."]],"5":[["062.02.06.01:01","ILS principles: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ — lateral guidance; ____ (GP) — vertical guidance; marker beacons (or DME) — distance information.","Components: localiser (LOC) — lateral guidance; glide path (GP) — vertical guidance; marker beacons (or DME) — distance information."],["062.02.05.01:02","ILS principles: fill in the gaps → ____: LOC antenna on the extended ____ stop end of the runway; GP antenna ____ threshold, laterally displaced (~120–180 m) from the centre line (so the GP crosses the threshold at ~50 ft — ILS reference datum).","Siting: LOC antenna on the extended centre line beyond the stop end of the runway; GP antenna ~300 m beyond the threshold, laterally displaced (~120–180 m) from the centre line (so the GP crosses the threshold at ~50 ft — ILS reference datum)."],["062.02.05.01:03","ILS principles: fill in the gaps → ____: vertical fan-shaped radiation to indicate ____ from the threshold along the GP; can be replaced by a ____ with the LOC frequency (distance to threshold).","Marker beacons: vertical fan-shaped radiation to indicate predetermined distances from the threshold along the GP; can be replaced by a DME paired with the LOC frequency (distance to threshold)."],["062.02.05.01:05","ILS principles: fill in the gaps → ____: LOC ____ — only frequencies with an odd first decimal (108.10, 108.15, 108.30…) are ILS; even decimals are VORs. GP in the UHF band ____, automatically paired with the LOC frequency.","Frequencies: LOC 108.0–111.975 MHz (VHF) — only frequencies with an odd first decimal (108.10, 108.15, 108.30…) are ILS; even decimals are VORs. GP in the UHF band (329–335 MHz), automatically paired with the LOC frequency."],["062.02.05.01:07","ILS principles: fill in the gaps → Principle: two lobes modulated at ____ and ____. LOC: 90 Hz predominates on the left of the course (seen from the approaching aircraft), 150 Hz on the right; GP: ____, 150 Hz below the path. The receiver measures the difference in depth of modulation (DDM) → deflection ∝ angular deviation; on course DDM = 0.","Principle: two lobes modulated at 90 Hz and 150 Hz. LOC: 90 Hz predominates on the left of the course (seen from the approaching aircraft), 150 Hz on the right; GP: 90 Hz above, 150 Hz below the path. The receiver measures the difference in depth of modulation (DDM) → deflection ∝ angular deviation; on course DDM = 0."],["062.02.05.01:10","ILS principles: fill in the gaps → ____ → ____ ____ (GP false paths at ~6°, 9°… — reversed sensing possible); always intercept the GP from below and cross-check altitude/distance. Back beam of the LOC can be published as a non-precision approach (no GP).","Side lobes (false beams) → false centre lines/false glide paths (GP false paths at ~6°, 9°… — reversed sensing possible); always intercept the GP from below and cross-check altitude/distance. Back beam of the LOC can be published as a non-precision approach (no GP)."],["062.02.05.01:11","ILS principles: fill in the gaps → Recommended GP angle ____ (~300 ft per NM, 5.2 %).","Recommended GP angle 3° (~300 ft per NM, 5.2 %)."],["062.02.05.01:12","ILS principles: fill in the gaps → ____: all on ____, identification by audio tone and keying:","Markers: all on 75 MHz, identification by audio tone and keying:"],["062.02.05.01:12","ILS principles – Outer (OM): what are the key facts?","Audio: 400 Hz (low) · Keying: 2 dashes/s continuous · Light: blue · Typical distance: ~4–7 NM"],["062.02.05.01:12","ILS principles – Middle (MM): what are the key facts?","Audio: 1 300 Hz (medium) · Keying: alternate dots and dashes · Light: amber · Typical distance: ~3 500 ft (≈ CAT I DH)"],["062.02.05.04:04","ILS principles – Inner (IM): what are the key facts?","Audio: 3 000 Hz (high) · Keying: 6 dots/s · Light: white · Typical distance: ~1 000 ft (CAT II DH)"],["062.02.05.01:13","ILS principles: fill in the gaps → Final-approach ____ allows ____ ____ (check altitude at the fix).","Final-approach fix/facility (OM or DME) allows verification of the GP–altimeter relationship (check altitude at the fix)."],["062.02.05.02:03","Presentation & interpretation: fill in the gaps → ____: 2-or 3-letter Morse (often starting with ____, e.g. I-ZH) on 1 020 Hz on the LOC; some plain text. ____ monitoring checks shift of LOC/GP course line or reduction of signal strength/modulation → switches off or to standby transmitter.","Identification: 2-or 3-letter Morse (often starting with I, e.g. I-ZH) on 1 020 Hz on the LOC; some plain text. Automatic ground monitoring checks shift of LOC/GP course line or reduction of signal strength/modulation → switches off or to standby transmitter."],["062.02.05.02:04","Presentation & interpretation: fill in the gaps → ____ (LOC/GS) appear when received signal is below threshold: ____, ____ Hz absent), or one modulation missing, or receiver failure.","Warning flags (LOC/GS) appear when received signal is below threshold: no carrier, no modulation (simultaneous 90/150 Hz absent), or one modulation missing, or receiver failure."],["062.02.05.02:06","Presentation & interpretation: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ from the centre line (5 dots → 0.5°/dot); ____ (≈ 0.14°/dot).","CDI/HSI: full-scale LOC deflection ≈ 2.5° from the centre line (5 dots → 0.5°/dot); full-scale GP ≈ 0.7° (≈ 0.14°/dot)."],["062.02.05.02:08","Presentation & interpretation: fill in the gaps → ____: on a simple CDI/OBI the sensing is ____ (fly away from the needle); on an ____ set the course pointer to the front-course (inbound front beam) heading → correct sensing on the back beam too. For front beam set the published inbound course.","Back-beam approach: on a simple CDI/OBI the sensing is reversed (fly away from the needle); on an HSI set the course pointer to the front-course (inbound front beam) heading → correct sensing on the back beam too. For front beam set the published inbound course."],["062.02.05.03:01","Coverage: fill in the gaps → LOC: ____, ____ from the antenna (up to 2 000 ft above threshold/ 7° elevation). GP: ____ least 10 NM, elevation 0.45 θ to 1.75 θ (θ = GP angle, e.g. 1.35°–5.25° for 3°).","LOC: ±10° to 25 NM, ±35° to 17 NM from the antenna (up to 2 000 ft above threshold/ 7° elevation). GP: ±8° azimuth to at least 10 NM, elevation 0.45 θ to 1.75 θ (θ = GP angle, e.g. 1.35°–5.25° for 3°)."],["062.02.05.04:02","Errors & accuracy: fill in the gaps → ____ (ICAO Annex 10): CAT I (guidance to ____ above threshold), CAT II (to ____), CAT III (to and along the runway surface: IIIA no DH/DH < 100 ft, RVR ≥ 200 m (175 m); IIIB DH < 50 ft, RVR 50–200 m (75 m); IIIC no DH, no RVR limit).","Facility performance categories (ICAO Annex 10): CAT I (guidance to 200 ft above threshold), CAT II (to 50 ft), CAT III (to and along the runway surface: IIIA no DH/DH < 100 ft, RVR ≥ 200 m (175 m); IIIB DH < 50 ft, RVR 50–200 m (75 m); IIIC no DH, no RVR limit)."],["062.02.05.04:04","Errors & accuracy: fill in the gaps → ____ ____. Accuracy requirements increase CAT I → II → III because guidance must be reliable ____ with less time to correct errors.","CAT III guidance is provided from the coverage limit to and along the runway surface. Accuracy requirements increase CAT I → II → III because guidance must be reliable closer to the ground with less time to correct errors."],["062.02.05.04:06","Errors & accuracy: fill in the gaps → ____ on the LOC: within ____; must be within half-scale LOC before descending on the GP; stay within ____ on the GP. Deviation > half-scale LOC or > half-scale fly-up GP → immediate missed approach (obstacle clearance not guaranteed).","Established on the LOC: within half-scale deflection; must be within half-scale LOC before descending on the GP; stay within half-scale fly-up on the GP. Deviation > half-scale LOC or > half-scale fly-up GP → immediate missed approach (obstacle clearance not guaranteed)."],["062.02.05.04:08","Errors & accuracy: fill in the gaps → ____: deviations of the LOC/GP from the nominal path (terrain, buildings), found by ____. ____ interference: reflections from large objects (buildings, taxiing aircraft) within the coverage.","Beam bends: deviations of the LOC/GP from the nominal path (terrain, buildings), found by flight test. Multipath interference: reflections from large objects (buildings, taxiing aircraft) within the coverage."],["062.02.05.05:02","Errors & accuracy: fill in the gaps → ____: defined area around LOC/GP antennas where ____ during all ILS operations. ____: extends beyond the critical area; movement/parking controlled during ILS operations (LVP) to prevent interference. (Also: FM broadcast stations just below 108 MHz can interfere.)","ILS critical area: defined area around LOC/GP antennas where vehicles and aircraft are excluded during all ILS operations. ILS sensitive area: extends beyond the critical area; movement/parking controlled during ILS operations (LVP) to prevent interference. (Also: FM broadcast stations just below 108 MHz can interfere.)"],["062.02.06.01:01","MLS: fill in the gaps → ____: time-referenced scanning beams (____ and ____ scanning \"to-and-fro\"; the time between two passes gives the angle) provide horizontal course guidance, vertical guidance, back-azimuth for departure/missed approach, DME/P (precision DME) distance and data words (system/approach information).","Principle: time-referenced scanning beams (azimuth and elevation scanning \"to-and-fro\"; the time between two passes gives the angle) provide horizontal course guidance, vertical guidance, back-azimuth for departure/missed approach, DME/P (precision DME) distance and data words (system/approach information)."],["062.02.06.01:02","MLS: fill in the gaps → Operates in the ____. Can be sited where ____ (buildings/terrain: less multipath, wide coverage, no critical area problems).","Operates in the SHF band (5 031–5 091 MHz), 200 channels. Can be sited where ILS siting is difficult (buildings/terrain: less multipath, wide coverage, no critical area problems)."],["062.02.06.02:05","MLS: fill in the gaps → Airborne display shows position relative to a ____ ____ with distance. ____ approaches possible with computer-generated cross bars — only with DME/P (3D position). Without DME/P, MLS gives an ILS- lookalike straight-in approach. MMR (multimode receiver) receives ILS, MLS and GPS (GLS) in one unit (saves weight/space, flexibility).","Airborne display shows position relative to a pre-selected course and glide path with distance. Segmented/curved approaches possible with computer-generated cross bars — only with DME/P (3D position). Without DME/P, MLS gives an ILS- lookalike straight-in approach. MMR (multimode receiver) receives ILS, MLS and GPS (GLS) in one unit (saves weight/space, flexibility)."],["062.02.06.03:01","MLS: fill in the gaps → ____ (approach): ____; elevation 0.9°–15°, up to 20 000 ft.","Coverage (approach): ±40° of centre line to 20 NM; elevation 0.9°–15°, up to 20 000 ft."],["062.02.05.01:01","Charts (GSPRM introduction): fill in the gaps → ____: airways (designator, MEA, MOCA, distances, magnetic tracks), VOR/NDB/DME symbols & frequencies, reporting points (compulsory filled triangle, on-request open), FIR/airspace boundaries, restricted areas, MORA grid.","En-route chart legend: airways (designator, MEA, MOCA, distances, magnetic tracks), VOR/NDB/DME symbols & frequencies, reporting points (compulsory filled triangle, on-request open), FIR/airspace boundaries, restricted areas, MORA grid."],["062.02.05.01:01","Charts (GSPRM introduction): fill in the gaps → ____: plan view (procedure, MSA circle, facilities, frequencies), ____ (altitudes at fixes, GP angle, OM/FAF, DA/MDA), ____, missed approach text, aerodrome diagram, lighting.","Approach chart: plan view (procedure, MSA circle, facilities, frequencies), profile view (altitudes at fixes, GP angle, OM/FAF, DA/MDA), minima box, missed approach text, aerodrome diagram, lighting."],["062.02.05.01:07","Exam trap (Charts (GSPRM introduction)): complete the fact → ____Hz left/above, ____Hz right/below.","90 Hz left/above, 150 Hz right/below."],["062.02.05.02:06","Exam trap (Charts (GSPRM introduction)): complete the fact → LOC full scale ____°, GP ____°.","LOC full scale 2.5°, GP 0.7°."],["062.02.05.01:01","Exam trap (Charts (GSPRM introduction)): complete the fact → OM blue/____Hz/dashes, MM amber/____Hz/dot-dash, IM white/3 000 Hz/dots.","OM blue/400 Hz/dashes, MM amber/1 300 Hz/dot-dash, IM white/3 000 Hz/dots."],["062.02.05.03:01","Exam trap (Charts (GSPRM introduction)): complete the fact → LOC ±____° ____, ±35° 17 NM; GP ±8° 10 NM.","LOC ±10° 25 NM, ±35° 17 NM; GP ±8° 10 NM."],["062.02.05.01:01","EASA LO 062.02.05.01.01 → Name the three main components of an ILS: — the localiser (LOC); — the glide path (GP);","Components: localiser (LOC) — lateral guidance; glide path (GP) — vertical guidance; marker beacons (or DME) — distance information."],["062.02.05.01:04","EASA LO 062.02.05.01.04 → State that marker beacons are sometimes replaced by a DME paired with the LOC frequency.","Marker beacons: vertical fan-shaped radiation to indicate predetermined distances from the threshold along the GP; can be replaced by a DME paired with the LOC frequency (distance to threshold). / Components: localiser (LOC) — lateral guidance; glide path (GP) — vertical guidance; marker beacons (or DME) — distance information."],["062.02.05.01:06","EASA LO 062.02.05.01.06 → State that the GP operates in the UHF band.","Frequencies: LOC 108.0–111.975 MHz (VHF) — only frequencies with an odd first decimal (108.10, 108.15, 108.30…) are ILS; even decimals are VORs. GP in the UHF band (329–335 MHz), automatically paired with the LOC frequency. / Operates in the SHF band (5 031–5 091 MHz), 200 channels. Can be sited where ILS siting is difficult (buildings/terrain: less multipath, wide coverage, no critical area problems)."],["062.02.05.01:08","EASA LO 062.02.05.01.08 → State that the UHF GP frequency is selected automatically by being paired with the LOC frequency.","Frequencies: LOC 108.0–111.975 MHz (VHF) — only frequencies with an odd first decimal (108.10, 108.15, 108.30…) are ILS; even decimals are VORs. GP in the UHF band (329–335 MHz), automatically paired with the LOC frequency. / Marker beacons: vertical fan-shaped radiation to indicate predetermined distances from the threshold along the GP; can be replaced by a DME paired with the LOC frequency (distance to threshold)."],["062.02.05.01:09","EASA LO 062.02.05.01.09 → Explain that both the LOC and the GP antenna radiates side lobes (false beams) which can give rise to false centre-line and false GP indication.","Side lobes (false beams) → false centre lines/false glide paths (GP false paths at ~6°, 9°… — reversed sensing possible); always intercept the GP from below and cross-check altitude/distance. Back beam of the LOC can be published as a non-precision approach (no GP)."],["062.02.05.02:01","EASA LO 062.02.05.02.01 → Describe the ILS identification regarding frequency and Morse code or plain text.","Identification: 2-or 3-letter Morse (often starting with I, e.g. I-ZH) on 1 020 Hz on the LOC; some plain text. Automatic ground monitoring checks shift of LOC/GP course line or reduction of signal strength/modulation → switches off or to standby transmitter."],["062.02.05.02:02","EASA LO 062.02.05.02.02 → State that an ILS installation has an automatic ground monitoring system.","Identification: 2-or 3-letter Morse (often starting with I, e.g. I-ZH) on 1 020 Hz on the LOC; some plain text. Automatic ground monitoring checks shift of LOC/GP course line or reduction of signal strength/modulation → switches off or to standby transmitter."],["062.02.05.02:05","EASA LO 062.02.05.02.05 → Describe the circumstances in which warning flags will appear for both the LOC and the GP: — absence of the carrier frequency; — absence of the modulation simultaneously;","Warning flags (LOC/GS) appear when received signal is below threshold: no carrier, no modulation (simultaneous 90/150 Hz absent), or one modulation missing, or receiver failure."],["062.02.05.02:07","EASA LO 062.02.05.02.07 → Interpret the aircraft’s position in relation to the extended runway centre line on a back\u0002beam approach.","Siting: LOC antenna on the extended centre line beyond the stop end of the runway; GP antenna ~300 m beyond the threshold, laterally displaced (~120–180 m) from the centre line (so the GP crosses the threshold at ~50 ft — ILS reference datum). / Back-beam approach: on a simple CDI/OBI the sensing is reversed (fly away from the needle); on an HSI set the course pointer to the front-course (inbound front beam) heading → correct sensing on the back beam too. For front beam set the published inbound course."],["062.02.05.04:01","EASA LO 062.02.05.04.01 → Explain that ILS approaches are divided into facility performance categories defined in ICAO Annex 10.","Facility performance categories (ICAO Annex 10): CAT I (guidance to 200 ft above threshold), CAT II (to 50 ft), CAT III (to and along the runway surface: IIIA no DH/DH < 100 ft, RVR ≥ 200 m (175 m); IIIB DH < 50 ft, RVR 50–200 m (75 m); IIIC no DH, no RVR limit)."],["062.02.05.04:03","EASA LO 062.02.05.04.03 → Explain that all Category III ILS operations guidance information is provided from the coverage limits of the facility to, and along, the surface of the runway.","CAT III guidance is provided from the coverage limit to and along the runway surface. Accuracy requirements increase CAT I → II → III because guidance must be reliable closer to the ground with less time to correct errors. / Facility performance categories (ICAO Annex 10): CAT I (guidance to 200 ft above threshold), CAT II (to 50 ft), CAT III (to and along the runway surface: IIIA no DH/DH < 100 ft, RVR ≥ 200 m (175 m); IIIB DH < 50 ft, RVR 50–200 m (75 m); IIIC no DH, no RVR limit)."],["062.02.05.04:05","EASA LO 062.02.05.04.05 → Explain the following in accordance with ICAO Doc 8168: — the accuracy the pilot has to fly the ILS LOC to be considered established","Established on the LOC: within half-scale deflection; must be within half-scale LOC before descending on the GP; stay within half-scale fly-up on the GP. Deviation > half-scale LOC or > half-scale fly-up GP → immediate missed approach (obstacle clearance not guaranteed)."],["062.02.05.04:07","EASA LO 062.02.05.04.07 → Describe ILS beam bends as deviations from the nominal LOC and GP respectively which can be assessed by flight test.","Beam bends: deviations of the LOC/GP from the nominal path (terrain, buildings), found by flight test. Multipath interference: reflections from large objects (buildings, taxiing aircraft) within the coverage."],["062.02.05.05:01","EASA LO 062.02.05.05.01 → Define the ‘ILS-critical area’: an area of defined dimensions around the LOC and GP antennas where vehicles, including aircraft, are excluded during all ILS operations.","ILS critical area: defined area around LOC/GP antennas where vehicles and aircraft are excluded during all ILS operations. ILS sensitive area: extends beyond the critical area; movement/parking controlled during ILS operations (LVP) to prevent interference. (Also: FM broadcast stations just below 108 MHz can interfere.) / Operates in the SHF band (5 031–5 091 MHz), 200 channels. Can be sited where ILS siting is difficult (buildings/terrain: less multipath, wide coverage, no critical area problems)."],["062.02.06.01:03","EASA LO 062.02.06.01.03 → Explain the reason why MLS can be installed at aerodromes where, as a result of the effects of surrounding buildings or terrain, ILS siting is difficult.","Operates in the SHF band (5 031–5 091 MHz), 200 channels. Can be sited where ILS siting is difficult (buildings/terrain: less multipath, wide coverage, no critical area problems). / Beam bends: deviations of the LOC/GP from the nominal path (terrain, buildings), found by flight test. Multipath interference: reflections from large objects (buildings, taxiing aircraft) within the coverage."],["062.02.06.02:01","EASA LO 062.02.06.02.01 → Interpret the display of airborne equipment designed to continuously show the position of the aircraft in relation to a preselected course and glide path, along with distance information, during approach and departure.","Airborne display shows position relative to a pre-selected course and glide path with distance. Segmented/curved approaches possible with computer-generated cross bars — only with DME/P (3D position). Without DME/P, MLS gives an ILS- lookalike straight-in approach. MMR (multimode receiver) receives ILS, MLS and GPS (GLS) in one unit (saves weight/space, flexibility). / Components: localiser (LOC) — lateral guidance; glide path (GP) — vertical guidance; marker beacons (or DME) — distance information."],["062.02.06.02:02","EASA LO 062.02.06.02.02 → Explain that segmented approaches can be carried out with a presentation with two cross bars directed by a computer which has been programmed with the approach to be flown.","Airborne display shows position relative to a pre-selected course and glide path with distance. Segmented/curved approaches possible with computer-generated cross bars — only with DME/P (3D position). Without DME/P, MLS gives an ILS- lookalike straight-in approach. MMR (multimode receiver) receives ILS, MLS and GPS (GLS) in one unit (saves weight/space, flexibility)."],["062.02.06.02:03","EASA LO 062.02.06.02.03 → Illustrate that segmented and curved approaches can only be executed with DME/P installed.","Airborne display shows position relative to a pre-selected course and glide path with distance. Segmented/curved approaches possible with computer-generated cross bars — only with DME/P (3D position). Without DME/P, MLS gives an ILS- lookalike straight-in approach. MMR (multimode receiver) receives ILS, MLS and GPS (GLS) in one unit (saves weight/space, flexibility)."],["062.02.06.02:04","EASA LO 062.02.06.02.04 → Explain why aircraft are equipped with a multimode receiver (MMR) in order to be able to receive ILS, MLS and GPS.","Airborne display shows position relative to a pre-selected course and glide path with distance. Segmented/curved approaches possible with computer-generated cross bars — only with DME/P (3D position). Without DME/P, MLS gives an ILS- lookalike straight-in approach. MMR (multimode receiver) receives ILS, MLS and GPS (GLS) in one unit (saves weight/space, flexibility)."]],"6":[["062.01.03.01:01","Ionosphere: fill in the gaps → ____ = ionised part of the upper atmosphere, ~____ ____, formed by solar UV/X-ray radiation; three regions D, E, F (F splits into F1/F2 by day). Depth and ionisation vary with time (day/night, season, sunspot cycle).","Ionosphere = ionised part of the upper atmosphere, ~60 to 400 km, formed by solar UV/X-ray radiation; three regions D, E, F (F splits into F1/F2 by day). Depth and ionisation vary with time (day/night, season, sunspot cycle)."],["062.01.03.04:02","Ionosphere: fill in the gaps → ____ (~60–90 km): exists ____, mainly ____ LF/MF/HF energy (→ short daytime MF range, sky waves weaker by day). E layer (~90–130 km): refracts MF/lower HF, weaker at night. F layer (~150–400 km): main HF reflecting layer, persists at night (higher at night → longer skip).","D layer (~60–90 km): exists only by day, mainly absorbs LF/MF/HF energy (→ short daytime MF range, sky waves weaker by day). E layer (~90–130 km): refracts MF/lower HF, weaker at night. F layer (~150–400 km): main HF reflecting layer, persists at night (higher at night → longer skip)."],["062.01.03.01:03","Ionosphere: fill in the gaps → Waves refracted back to earth from the ____ ____. Higher frequencies penetrate further (less refraction); above the ____ (~30 MHz VHF) they pass through → no sky waves for VHF and above.","Waves refracted back to earth from the E and F layers = sky waves. Higher frequencies penetrate further (less refraction); above the critical frequency (~30 MHz VHF) they pass through → no sky waves for VHF and above."],["062.01.03.02:01","Wave types: fill in the gaps → ____: travel ____, following its curvature (diffraction); attenuated by the surface — ____ than over land, better at lower frequencies (LF/MF: hundreds of NM).","Ground (surface) waves: travel along the earth's surface, following its curvature (diffraction); attenuated by the surface — better over sea than over land, better at lower frequencies (LF/MF: hundreds of NM)."],["062.01.03.03:01","Wave types: fill in the gaps → ____: travel ____ (line of sight) from transmitter to receiver (plus ground-reflected wave).","Space (direct) waves: travel through the air directly (line of sight) from transmitter to receiver (plus ground-reflected wave)."],["062.01.03.04:02","Wave types: fill in the gaps → ____ (line of sight). ____ ____ (HF mainly sky wave, surface wave short).","VHF, UHF, SHF, EHF → space waves (line of sight). LF, MF, HF → surface waves and sky waves (HF mainly sky wave, surface wave short)."],["062.01.03.02:01","Wave types: fill in the gaps → ________ + √hrx) (h in ft). Example: aircraft at FL 100 → 1.23 × √10 000 = ____ (ground station at sea level).","Line-of-sight range (NM) ≈ 1.23 × (√htx + √hrx) (h in ft). Example: aircraft at FL 100 → 1.23 × √10 000 = 123 NM (ground station at sea level)."],["062.01.03.05:01","Doppler principle: fill in the gaps → Frequency received ____ when transmitter and receiver ____ each other and ____ when they move apart; shift ∝ relative (radial) speed. Used in Doppler VOR, weather radar turbulence/wind-shear detection, GPS velocity, Doppler navigation.","Frequency received increases when transmitter and receiver approach each other and decreases when they move apart; shift ∝ relative (radial) speed. Used in Doppler VOR, weather radar turbulence/wind-shear detection, GPS velocity, Doppler navigation."],["062.01.03.06:01","Factors affecting propagation: fill in the gaps → ____: distance from the transmitter to the point where the ____ returns to earth. ____: area between the end of the surface wave and the first sky-wave return — no reception. Skip distance larger at night (reflecting layer higher, D layer absent) and at higher frequencies → use lower HF frequency at night (\"half the frequency by night\").","Skip distance: distance from the transmitter to the point where the first sky wave returns to earth. Skip zone / dead space: area between the end of the surface wave and the first sky-wave return — no reception. Skip distance larger at night (reflecting layer higher, D layer absent) and at higher frequencies → use lower HF frequency at night (\"half the frequency by night\")."],["062.01.03.06:03","Factors affecting propagation: fill in the gaps → ____: receiver gets ____ (e.g. ground + sky wave, or two sky-wave paths) → interference → fluctuating strength/polarisation (NDB night effect).","Fading: receiver gets two signals of the same frequency (e.g. ground + sky wave, or two sky-wave paths) → interference → fluctuating strength/polarisation (NDB night effect)."],["062.01.03.06:04","Factors affecting propagation: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ — not reflected by the ionosphere and ____ → line of sight only.","VHF and above: limited range — not reflected by the ionosphere and no usable surface wave → line of sight only."],["062.01.03.06:05","Factors affecting propagation: fill in the gaps → ____ (wave bounces off a surface/layer), ____ (bending when passing between media of different density/ionisation — ionosphere, coastal refraction), ____ (bending around obstacles/earth curvature — surface waves, larger at longer wavelengths), absorption (energy loss to the medium — D layer, ground, rain), interference (superposition of waves → reinforcement or cancellation).","Reflection (wave bounces off a surface/layer), refraction (bending when passing between media of different density/ionisation — ionosphere, coastal refraction), diffraction (bending around obstacles/earth curvature — surface waves, larger at longer wavelengths), absorption (energy loss to the medium — D layer, ground, rain), interference (superposition of waves → reinforcement or cancellation)."],["062.01.03.06:06","Factors affecting propagation: fill in the gaps → ____: the signal reaches the receiver via ____ ____ (reflections from terrain, buildings, aircraft structure) → errors (ILS beam bends, GPS errors, VOR scalloping).","Multipath: the signal reaches the receiver via more than one path (reflections from terrain, buildings, aircraft structure) → errors (ILS beam bends, GPS errors, VOR scalloping)."],["062.01.03.06:01","Exam trap (Factors affecting propagation): complete the fact → Line of sight ____√h.","Line of sight 1.23 √h."],["062.01.03.01:02","EASA LO 062.01.03.01.02 → State that the layers of the ionosphere are named D, E and F layers, and their depth varies with time.","Ionosphere = ionised part of the upper atmosphere, ~60 to 400 km, formed by solar UV/X-ray radiation; three regions D, E, F (F splits into F1/F2 by day). Depth and ionisation vary with time (day/night, season, sunspot cycle)."],["062.01.03.04:01","EASA LO 062.01.03.04.01 → State that radio waves in VHF, UHF, SHF and EHF propagate as space waves.","VHF, UHF, SHF, EHF → space waves (line of sight). LF, MF, HF → surface waves and sky waves (HF mainly sky wave, surface wave short)."],["062.01.03.06:02","EASA LO 062.01.03.06.02 → State that skip zone/dead space is the distance between the limit of the surface wave and the sky wave.","Skip distance: distance from the transmitter to the point where the first sky wave returns to earth. Skip zone / dead space: area between the end of the surface wave and the first sky-wave return — no reception. Skip distance larger at night (reflecting layer higher, D layer absent) and at higher frequencies → use lower HF frequency at night (\"half the frequency by night\"). / VHF, UHF, SHF, EHF → space waves (line of sight). LF, MF, HF → surface waves and sky waves (HF mainly sky wave, surface wave short)."]],"7":[["081.01.01.01:01","Laws & definitions: fill in the gaps → ____: mass ____; acceleration ____; weight/force newton (N = kg·m/s²); velocity m/s; energy joule (J); density kg/m³; temperature kelvin (K); pressure pascal (Pa = N/m²); wing loading N/m²; power watt (W = J/s).","SI units: mass kg; acceleration m/s²; weight/force newton (N = kg·m/s²); velocity m/s; energy joule (J); density kg/m³; temperature kelvin (K); pressure pascal (Pa = N/m²); wing loading N/m²; power watt (W = J/s)."],["081.01.01.01:02","Laws & definitions: fill in the gaps → ____ = quantity of matter (kg, independent of location); ____ = cause of acceleration; ____ = rate of change of velocity; weight = force of gravity on a mass, W = m × g (g = 9.81 m/s²).","Mass = quantity of matter (kg, independent of location); force = cause of acceleration; acceleration = rate of change of velocity; weight = force of gravity on a mass, W = m × g (g = 9.81 m/s²)."],["081.01.01.01:03","Laws & definitions: fill in the gaps → Newton's laws: ____ — a body stays at rest or uniform straight motion unless acted on by a resultant force (inertia); ____ — F = m × a (force = rate of change of momentum); ____ — every action has an equal and opposite reaction (propeller/jet thrust, lift from downwash).","Newton's laws: 1 — a body stays at rest or uniform straight motion unless acted on by a resultant force (inertia); 2 — F = m × a (force = rate of change of momentum); 3 — every action has an equal and opposite reaction (propeller/jet thrust, lift from downwash)."],["081.01.01.01:15","Laws & definitions: fill in the gaps → ____: mass per unit volume; depends on ____ ____; ρ = p/(R·T) — relationship density, temperature, pressure. Higher altitude → lower density.","Air density ρ: mass per unit volume; depends on pressure (↑ρ), temperature (↓ρ), humidity (↓ρ); ρ = p/(R·T) — relationship density, temperature, pressure. Higher altitude → lower density."],["081.01.01.01:10","Laws & definitions: fill in the gaps → ____ p: pressure exerted by the air at rest/in all directions. ____: pressure due to motion = kinetic energy per unit volume; for a given IAS, q is the same at any altitude (IAS is a measure of q); at constant TAS, q falls with altitude.","Static pressure p: pressure exerted by the air at rest/in all directions. Dynamic pressure q = ½ρV²: pressure due to motion = kinetic energy per unit volume; for a given IAS, q is the same at any altitude (IAS is a measure of q); at constant TAS, q falls with altitude."],["081.01.01.01:12","Laws & definitions: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ ____ along a streamline (energy conservation). In a venturi, velocity ↑ where the tube narrows → static pressure ↓. Total pressure is constant within a system without energy addition but differs between systems (e.g. behind a propeller/fan it is higher).","Bernoulli (incompressible): p + ½ρV² = constant = total pressure along a streamline (energy conservation). In a venturi, velocity ↑ where the tube narrows → static pressure ↓. Total pressure is constant within a system without energy addition but differs between systems (e.g. behind a propeller/fan it is higher)."],["081.01.01.01:13","Laws & definitions: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (mass flow); incompressible: ____ → narrower stream tube = faster flow.","Continuity: ρ × A × V = constant (mass flow); incompressible: A × V = constant → narrower stream tube = faster flow."],["081.01.01.01:14","Laws & definitions: fill in the gaps → ____: pitot tube senses ____, static ports sense ____; ASI measures total − static = dynamic pressure → calibrated to IAS.","Pitot-static: pitot tube senses total pressure, static ports sense static pressure; ASI measures total − static = dynamic pressure → calibrated to IAS."],["081.01.01.01:17","Laws & definitions: fill in the gaps → ____ (indicated), ____ = IAS corrected for ____ position error, EAS = CAS corrected for compressibility, TAS = EAS corrected for density (TAS = EAS/√σ, σ = ρ/ρ₀). At altitude TAS > EAS (≈ +2 % per 1 000 ft).","IAS (indicated), CAS = IAS corrected for instrument and position error, EAS = CAS corrected for compressibility, TAS = EAS corrected for density (TAS = EAS/√σ, σ = ρ/ρ₀). At altitude TAS > EAS (≈ +2 % per 1 000 ft)."],["081.01.01.02:01","Airflow & forces: fill in the gaps → ____: velocity/pressure at each point constant with time; ____: changing. ____ = path of a particle in steady flow; stream tube = bundle of streamlines — no flow across its walls (continuity applies).","Steady flow: velocity/pressure at each point constant with time; unsteady: changing. Streamline = path of a particle in steady flow; stream tube = bundle of streamlines — no flow across its walls (continuity applies)."],["081.01.01.05:01","Airflow & forces: fill in the gaps → ____: flow around an infinite-span aerofoil section (no spanwise flow); ____: real finite wing with spanwise flow, tip vortices, induced drag.","2D flow: flow around an infinite-span aerofoil section (no spanwise flow); 3D: real finite wing with spanwise flow, tip vortices, induced drag."],["081.01.01.03:02","Airflow & forces: fill in the gaps → ____ from the pressure distribution acts at the ____; resolved into ____ (perpendicular to the relative airflow) and drag (parallel to it, opposite to motion).","Resultant aerodynamic force from the pressure distribution acts at the centre of pressure (CP); resolved into lift (perpendicular to the relative airflow) and drag (parallel to it, opposite to motion)."],["081.01.01.03:07","Airflow & forces: fill in the gaps → ____ about a reference point (e.g. AC) depends on camber, α, speed (q), chord; ____ ____: zero moment about the AC (CP stays at the AC ≈ 25 % chord); positively cambered: nose-down moment (constant about AC); negatively cambered: nose-up.","Aerodynamic (pitching) moment about a reference point (e.g. AC) depends on camber, α, speed (q), chord; symmetrical aerofoil: zero moment about the AC (CP stays at the AC ≈ 25 % chord); positively cambered: nose-down moment (constant about AC); negatively cambered: nose-up."],["081.01.01.03:08","Airflow & forces: fill in the gaps → ____ = angle between the ____ and the ____.","Angle of attack α = angle between the chord line and the relative airflow (free stream)."],["081.01.01.04:04","Aerofoil & wing geometry: fill in the gaps → ____, ____, ____ (straight line LE–TE), thickness/chord ratio (relative thickness, e.g. 12 %), location of max thickness (% chord), camber line (mean line halfway between upper and lower surface), camber (max distance camber line–chord), nose radius. Symmetrical aerofoil: camber line = chord line; asymmetrical (cambered): produces lift at α = 0.","Leading edge, trailing edge, chord line (straight line LE–TE), thickness/chord ratio (relative thickness, e.g. 12 %), location of max thickness (% chord), camber line (mean line halfway between upper and lower surface), camber (max distance camber line–chord), nose radius. Symmetrical aerofoil: camber line = chord line; asymmetrical (cambered): produces lift at α = 0."],["081.01.01.05:07","Aerofoil & wing geometry: fill in the gaps → ____ (tip to tip), ____, ____ = tip chord/root chord, wing area S, planform (rectangular, tapered, elliptical, swept, delta), mean geometric chord = S/b, MAC (mean aerodynamic chord — chord of an equivalent rectangular wing having the same aerodynamic characteristics; reference for CG in % MAC), aspect ratio AR = b²/S (= span/chord for rectangular), dihedral (upward angle of wings from horizontal), sweep angle (angle of the quarter-chord line to the lateral axis), wing twist — geometric (washout: incidence decreases towards the tip) and aerodynamic (different aerofoil sections along the span), angle of incidence = angle between the longitudinal axis and the wing-root chord (fixed, not α!).","Span (tip to tip), root and tip chord, taper ratio = tip chord/root chord, wing area S, planform (rectangular, tapered, elliptical, swept, delta), mean geometric chord = S/b, MAC (mean aerodynamic chord — chord of an equivalent rectangular wing having the same aerodynamic characteristics; reference for CG in % MAC), aspect ratio AR = b²/S (= span/chord for rectangular), dihedral (upward angle of wings from horizontal), sweep angle (angle of the quarter-chord line to the lateral axis), wing twist — geometric (washout: incidence decreases towards the tip) and aerodynamic (different aerofoil sections along the span), angle of incidence = angle between the longitudinal axis and the wing-root chord (fixed, not α!)."],["081.01.01.01:07","2D flow around an aerofoil: fill in the gaps → Streamlines ____ over the upper surface → speed ↑, static pressure ↓; ____ → speed ↓, pressure ↑. ____ ahead of the wing, downwash behind.","Streamlines converge over the upper surface → speed ↑, static pressure ↓; diverge → speed ↓, pressure ↑. Upwash ahead of the wing, downwash behind."],["081.01.02.02:02","2D flow around an aerofoil: fill in the gaps → ____: point on the LE where the flow comes to rest (V = 0, pressure = total pressure); moves ____ ____ (forward/up as α decreases).","Stagnation point: point on the LE where the flow comes to rest (V = 0, pressure = total pressure); moves down and aft on the lower surface as α increases (forward/up as α decreases)."],["081.01.02.03:01","2D flow around an aerofoil: fill in the gaps → ____: suction peak near the upper-surface LE; more camber/α → stronger suction, greater lift. Local speeds highest over the upper surface.","Pressure distribution: suction peak near the upper-surface LE; more camber/α → stronger suction, greater lift. Local speeds highest over the upper surface."],["081.01.02.04:01","2D flow around an aerofoil: fill in the gaps → ____ = point where the resultant acts — ____ increases (normal range) on a cambered aerofoil, then aft at the stall. AC = point about which the pitching moment is constant with α (~____ subsonic).","CP = point where the resultant acts — moves forward as α increases (normal range) on a cambered aerofoil, then aft at the stall. AC = point about which the pitching moment is constant with α (~25 % chord subsonic)."],["081.01.02.06:04","2D flow around an aerofoil: fill in the gaps → ____ from ____ (viscous shear in the boundary layer) and ____ (pressure difference due to separation/wake). Drag and wake = loss of momentum/energy transferred to the air.","Drag from skin friction (viscous shear in the boundary layer) and form/pressure drag (pressure difference due to separation/wake). Drag and wake = loss of momentum/energy transferred to the air."],["081.01.02.09:01","2D flow around an aerofoil: fill in the gaps → ____ (CL ∝ α) up to the critical angle.","α ↑ → lift ↑ (CL ∝ α) up to the critical angle."],["081.01.03.02:02","2D flow around an aerofoil: fill in the gaps → ____L–α graph: straight line through zero lift at α____ (negative for cambered, zero for symmetrical aerofoils), crossing the vertical axis at C____ (> 0 cambered), rising to CLmax at αcrit (~15–16°), then falling (stall).","CL–α graph: straight line through zero lift at α0L (negative for cambered, zero for symmetrical aerofoils), crossing the vertical axis at CL0 (> 0 cambered), rising to CLmax at αcrit (~15–16°), then falling (stall)."],["081.01.01.05:04","Lift coefficient: fill in the gaps → ________ × ½ρV² × S. Lift depends on CL (α, shape, camber, Reynolds/Mach), density, V², wing area. Example: double the speed at constant α → ____.","L = CL × ½ρV² × S. Lift depends on CL (α, shape, camber, Reynolds/Mach), density, V², wing area. Example: double the speed at constant α → 4× lift."],["081.01.03.02:02","Lift coefficient: fill in the gaps → ____ shifts the CL–α line ____ (positive CL at α = 0, higher CLmax); negative camber shifts it down. ____ (thick, cambered, large nose radius) → high CLmax, gentle stall; fast aerofoils (thin, little camber) → lower CLmax, sharp stall, higher Mcrit.","Camber shifts the CL–α line up/left (positive CL at α = 0, higher CLmax); negative camber shifts it down. Slow aerofoils (thick, cambered, large nose radius) → high CLmax, gentle stall; fast aerofoils (thin, little camber) → lower CLmax, sharp stall, higher Mcrit."],["081.01.03.02:04","Lift coefficient: fill in the gaps → ________ = maximum lift coefficient; ____crit = stalling α. Variables: α, camber, thickness, flaps/slats, surface condition (ice, contamination), Reynolds number, Mach number.","CLmax = maximum lift coefficient; αcrit = stalling α. Variables: α, camber, thickness, flaps/slats, surface condition (ice, contamination), Reynolds number, Mach number."],["081.01.04.01:02","3D flow: fill in the gaps → ____ (3D): angle between the aeroplane ____ (or wing reference) and the ____ — not the attitude (pitch attitude is relative to the horizon; attitude = α + flight path angle).","α (3D): angle between the aeroplane longitudinal axis (or wing reference) and the relative airflow — not the attitude (pitch attitude is relative to the horizon; attitude = α + flight path angle)."],["081.01.04.02:03","3D flow: fill in the gaps → ____: high pressure below flows ____ towards the tip, low pressure above flows ____; they meet at the TE → trailing vortices, strongest at the tips = wingtip vortices, which produce downwash behind the wing. Vortices are stronger at high α (high CL, low speed).","Spanwise flow: high pressure below flows outward towards the tip, low pressure above flows inward; they meet at the TE → trailing vortices, strongest at the tips = wingtip vortices, which produce downwash behind the wing. Vortices are stronger at high α (high CL, low speed)."],["081.01.04.02:05","3D flow: what do you remember about this? → Spanwise lift distribution: elliptical ideal; rectangular wing — lift …","Spanwise lift distribution: elliptical ideal; rectangular wing — lift concentrated at the root; tapered/swept — more lift outboard (tip stall tendency)."],["081.01.04.02:07","3D flow: fill in the gaps → ____: tip vortices trailing behind the aircraft, from rotation to touchdown, persist ~2–3 min, descend and drift with the wind; strongest for ____ aircraft; ____ deflection moves vortex generation inboard (flap-tip vortices), somewhat reducing strength.","Wake turbulence: tip vortices trailing behind the aircraft, from rotation to touchdown, persist ~2–3 min, descend and drift with the wind; strongest for heavy, clean, slow aircraft; flap deflection moves vortex generation inboard (flap-tip vortices), somewhat reducing strength."],["081.01.04.03:08","3D flow: fill in the gaps → ____: downwash tilts the local airflow → ____ geometric α − induced α; the lift vector (perpendicular to the effective flow) tilts rearwards → rearward component = induced drag. CDi ≈ CL²/(π·AR·e). Induced drag ____ (high at low speed), ↑ with mass (more CL at a given IAS), ↑ in turns (load factor), ↓ with higher aspect ratio, elliptical lift distribution.","Induced drag: downwash tilts the local airflow → effective α = geometric α − induced α; the lift vector (perpendicular to the effective flow) tilts rearwards → rearward component = induced drag. CDi ≈ CL²/(π·AR·e). Induced drag ∝ 1/V² (high at low speed), ↑ with mass (more CL at a given IAS), ↑ in turns (load factor), ↓ with higher aspect ratio, elliptical lift distribution."],["081.01.04.03:05","3D flow: fill in the gaps → Means to reduce: ____, ____, ____, wing twist (washout), camber change (for elliptical-like lift distribution).","Means to reduce: high aspect ratio, winglets, tip tanks, wing twist (washout), camber change (for elliptical-like lift distribution)."],["081.01.04.03:13","3D flow: fill in the gaps → ____: low-AR wing → ____L–α slope, higher αcrit; polar: more drag for the same CL. Polar CD = C____ + k·CL² (parabolic). Minimum drag (max L/D) = tangent from the origin to the polar. L/D (CL/CD) ratio measures aerodynamic efficiency (range, glide).","Effect on graphs: low-AR wing → flatter CL–α slope, higher αcrit; polar: more drag for the same CL. Polar CD = CD0 + k·CL² (parabolic). Minimum drag (max L/D) = tangent from the origin to the polar. L/D (CL/CD) ratio measures aerodynamic efficiency (range, glide)."],["081.01.02.06:02","Total drag: fill in the gaps → ____. ____: ____ (pressure) drag (shape, frontal area, separation), interference drag (at junctions wing–fuselage, fairings reduce it), skin- friction drag (surface area, roughness, laminar vs turbulent boundary layer). Parasite drag ∝ V² (∝ IAS²).","Total drag = parasite + induced. Parasite drag: form (pressure) drag (shape, frontal area, separation), interference drag (at junctions wing–fuselage, fairings reduce it), skin- friction drag (surface area, roughness, laminar vs turbulent boundary layer). Parasite drag ∝ V² (∝ IAS²)."],["081.01.05.07:02","Total drag: fill in the gaps → ____: induced falls, parasite rises → minimum at ________ where induced = parasite. Heavier aircraft → curve moves up and right (VMD higher). Altitude: drag–IAS curve unchanged; drag–TAS curve shifts right (same drag at higher TAS).","Drag–speed curve: induced falls, parasite rises → minimum at VMD where induced = parasite. Heavier aircraft → curve moves up and right (VMD higher). Altitude: drag–IAS curve unchanged; drag–TAS curve shifts right (same drag at higher TAS)."],["081.01.05.07:04","Total drag: fill in the gaps → ____: ________ stable (speed ↓ → drag ↓ → aircraft accelerates back); at VMD neutral; below VMD (back of the drag curve) unstable — if speed decreases, drag increases, speed decreases further (e.g. low-speed approach, turbulence) → needs active thrust management.","Speed stability: above VMD stable (speed ↓ → drag ↓ → aircraft accelerates back); at VMD neutral; below VMD (back of the drag curve) unstable — if speed decreases, drag increases, speed decreases further (e.g. low-speed approach, turbulence) → needs active thrust management."],["081.01.06.01:02","Ground effect: fill in the gaps → Within ~____ (strong at < ½ span) of the ground, the ground ____ → ____ CDi reduced → less induced drag, more lift at a given α (effective α ↑).","Within ~one wingspan (strong at < ½ span) of the ground, the ground restricts downwash and tip vortices → induced α and CDi reduced → less induced drag, more lift at a given α (effective α ↑)."],["081.01.06.04:01","Ground effect: fill in the gaps → ________ ____ (stall at a lower α); CL at a given α ↑. Entering ground effect: floating on landing (longer flare), nose-down pitch (less downwash on tail), static pressure errors (ASI/altimeter); leaving it after take-off: drag ↑, lift ↓ → aircraft may sink/settle if lifted off too early; nose-up tendency.","αcrit decreases slightly (stall at a lower α); CL at a given α ↑. Entering ground effect: floating on landing (longer flare), nose-down pitch (less downwash on tail), static pressure errors (ASI/altimeter); leaving it after take-off: drag ↑, lift ↓ → aircraft may sink/settle if lifted off too early; nose-up tendency."],["081.01.06.01:01","Ground effect: fill in the gaps → ____ aircraft are more affected (wing closer to the ground) — more float; high-wing less.","Low-wing aircraft are more affected (wing closer to the ground) — more float; high-wing less."],["081.01.04.03:03","C vs speed: fill in the gaps → Level flight: L = W → CL ____ → speed ↑ → CL (α) must ↓: C____·V₁² = C____·V₂². Example: speed doubled → CL ¼. Graph: CL required vs IAS is a hyperbola; minimum speed at CLmax (stall speed).","Level flight: L = W → CL = 2W/(ρV²S) → speed ↑ → CL (α) must ↓: CL1·V₁² = CL2·V₂². Example: speed doubled → CL ¼. Graph: CL required vs IAS is a hyperbola; minimum speed at CLmax (stall speed)."],["081.01.02.04:01","Exam trap (C vs speed): complete the fact → CP moves forward with α; AC fixed ~____.","CP moves forward with α; AC fixed ~25 %."],["081.01.04.03:04","Exam trap (C vs speed): complete the fact → Induced drag ∝ ____V² and ∝ mass².","Induced drag ∝ 1/V² and ∝ mass²."],["081.01.01.01:04","EASA LO 081.01.01.01.04 → Explain air density.","Air density ρ: mass per unit volume; depends on pressure (↑ρ), temperature (↓ρ), humidity (↓ρ); ρ = p/(R·T) — relationship density, temperature, pressure. Higher altitude → lower density. / Static pressure p: pressure exerted by the air at rest/in all directions. Dynamic pressure q = ½ρV²: pressure due to motion = kinetic energy per unit volume; for a given IAS, q is the same at any altitude (IAS is a measure of q); at constant TAS, q falls with altitude."],["081.01.01.01:05","EASA LO 081.01.01.01.05 → List the atmospheric properties that effect air density.","Air density ρ: mass per unit volume; depends on pressure (↑ρ), temperature (↓ρ), humidity (↓ρ); ρ = p/(R·T) — relationship density, temperature, pressure. Higher altitude → lower density."],["081.01.01.01:06","EASA LO 081.01.01.01.06 → Explain how temperature and pressure changes affect air density.","Air density ρ: mass per unit volume; depends on pressure (↑ρ), temperature (↓ρ), humidity (↓ρ); ρ = p/(R·T) — relationship density, temperature, pressure. Higher altitude → lower density. / SI units: mass kg; acceleration m/s²; weight/force newton (N = kg·m/s²); velocity m/s; energy joule (J); density kg/m³; temperature kelvin (K); pressure pascal (Pa = N/m²); wing loading N/m²; power watt (W = J/s)."],["081.01.01.01:08","EASA LO 081.01.01.01.08 → Define ‘dynamic pressure’.","Pitot-static: pitot tube senses total pressure, static ports sense static pressure; ASI measures total − static = dynamic pressure → calibrated to IAS. / Static pressure p: pressure exerted by the air at rest/in all directions. Dynamic pressure q = ½ρV²: pressure due to motion = kinetic energy per unit volume; for a given IAS, q is the same at any altitude (IAS is a measure of q); at constant TAS, q falls with altitude."],["081.01.01.01:09","EASA LO 081.01.01.01.09 → State the formula for ‘dynamic pressure’.","Pitot-static: pitot tube senses total pressure, static ports sense static pressure; ASI measures total − static = dynamic pressure → calibrated to IAS. / Static pressure p: pressure exerted by the air at rest/in all directions. Dynamic pressure q = ½ρV²: pressure due to motion = kinetic energy per unit volume; for a given IAS, q is the same at any altitude (IAS is a measure of q); at constant TAS, q falls with altitude."],["081.01.01.01:11","EASA LO 081.01.01.01.11 → State Bernoulli’s equation for incompressible flow.","Continuity: ρ × A × V = constant (mass flow); incompressible: A × V = constant → narrower stream tube = faster flow. / Bernoulli (incompressible): p + ½ρV² = constant = total pressure along a streamline (energy conservation). In a venturi, velocity ↑ where the tube narrows → static pressure ↓. Total pressure is constant within a system without energy addition but differs between systems (e.g. behind a propeller/fan it is higher)."],["081.01.01.01:16","EASA LO 081.01.01.01.16 → Explain the equation of continuity and its application to the flow through a stream tube.","Continuity: ρ × A × V = constant (mass flow); incompressible: A × V = constant → narrower stream tube = faster flow. / Steady flow: velocity/pressure at each point constant with time; unsteady: changing. Streamline = path of a particle in steady flow; stream tube = bundle of streamlines — no flow across its walls (continuity applies)."],["081.01.01.02:02","EASA LO 081.01.01.02.02 → Explain the concept of a streamline and a stream tube.","Continuity: ρ × A × V = constant (mass flow); incompressible: A × V = constant → narrower stream tube = faster flow. / Angle of attack α = angle between the chord line and the relative airflow (free stream)."],["081.01.01.02:03","EASA LO 081.01.01.02.03 → Describe and explain airflow through a stream tube.","Angle of attack α = angle between the chord line and the relative airflow (free stream). / Continuity: ρ × A × V = constant (mass flow); incompressible: A × V = constant → narrower stream tube = faster flow."],["081.01.01.02:04","EASA LO 081.01.01.02.04 → Explain the difference between two- and three-dimensional airflow.","Angle of attack α = angle between the chord line and the relative airflow (free stream)."],["081.01.01.03:01","EASA LO 081.01.01.03.01 → Describe the originating point and direction of the resultant force caused by the pressure distribution around an aerofoil.","Resultant aerodynamic force from the pressure distribution acts at the centre of pressure (CP); resolved into lift (perpendicular to the relative airflow) and drag (parallel to it, opposite to motion). / CP = point where the resultant acts — moves forward as α increases (normal range) on a cambered aerofoil, then aft at the stall. AC = point about which the pitching moment is constant with α (~25 % chord subsonic)."],["081.01.01.03:04","EASA LO 081.01.01.03.04 → Define the ‘aerodynamic moment’.","Aerodynamic (pitching) moment about a reference point (e.g. AC) depends on camber, α, speed (q), chord; symmetrical aerofoil: zero moment about the AC (CP stays at the AC ≈ 25 % chord); positively cambered: nose-down moment (constant about AC); negatively cambered: nose-up."],["081.01.01.03:05","EASA LO 081.01.01.03.05 → List the factors that affect the aerodynamic moment.","Aerodynamic (pitching) moment about a reference point (e.g. AC) depends on camber, α, speed (q), chord; symmetrical aerofoil: zero moment about the AC (CP stays at the AC ≈ 25 % chord); positively cambered: nose-down moment (constant about AC); negatively cambered: nose-up."],["081.01.01.03:06","EASA LO 081.01.01.03.06 → Describe the aerodynamic moment for a symmetrical aerofoil.","Aerodynamic (pitching) moment about a reference point (e.g. AC) depends on camber, α, speed (q), chord; symmetrical aerofoil: zero moment about the AC (CP stays at the AC ≈ 25 % chord); positively cambered: nose-down moment (constant about AC); negatively cambered: nose-up. / CL–α graph: straight line through zero lift at α0L (negative for cambered, zero for symmetrical aerofoils), crossing the vertical axis at CL0 (> 0 cambered), rising to CLmax at αcrit (~15–16°), then falling (stall)."],["081.01.01.04:01","EASA LO 081.01.01.04.01 → Describe the following parameter of an aerofoil section: leading edge.","Leading edge, trailing edge, chord line (straight line LE–TE), thickness/chord ratio (relative thickness, e.g. 12 %), location of max thickness (% chord), camber line (mean line halfway between upper and lower surface), camber (max distance camber line–chord), nose radius. Symmetrical aerofoil: camber line = chord line; asymmetrical (cambered): produces lift at α = 0. / 2D flow: flow around an infinite-span aerofoil section (no spanwise flow); 3D: real finite wing with spanwise flow, tip vortices, induced drag."],["081.01.01.04:02","EASA LO 081.01.01.04.02 → Describe the following parameter of an aerofoil section: trailing edge.","Leading edge, trailing edge, chord line (straight line LE–TE), thickness/chord ratio (relative thickness, e.g. 12 %), location of max thickness (% chord), camber line (mean line halfway between upper and lower surface), camber (max distance camber line–chord), nose radius. Symmetrical aerofoil: camber line = chord line; asymmetrical (cambered): produces lift at α = 0. / 2D flow: flow around an infinite-span aerofoil section (no spanwise flow); 3D: real finite wing with spanwise flow, tip vortices, induced drag."],["081.01.01.04:03","EASA LO 081.01.01.04.03 → Describe the following parameter of an aerofoil section: chord line.","Leading edge, trailing edge, chord line (straight line LE–TE), thickness/chord ratio (relative thickness, e.g. 12 %), location of max thickness (% chord), camber line (mean line halfway between upper and lower surface), camber (max distance camber line–chord), nose radius. Symmetrical aerofoil: camber line = chord line; asymmetrical (cambered): produces lift at α = 0. / 2D flow: flow around an infinite-span aerofoil section (no spanwise flow); 3D: real finite wing with spanwise flow, tip vortices, induced drag."],["081.01.01.04:05","EASA LO 081.01.01.04.05 → Describe the following parameter of an aerofoil section: location of maximum thickness.","Leading edge, trailing edge, chord line (straight line LE–TE), thickness/chord ratio (relative thickness, e.g. 12 %), location of max thickness (% chord), camber line (mean line halfway between upper and lower surface), camber (max distance camber line–chord), nose radius. Symmetrical aerofoil: camber line = chord line; asymmetrical (cambered): produces lift at α = 0. / CLmax = maximum lift coefficient; αcrit = stalling α. Variables: α, camber, thickness, flaps/slats, surface condition (ice, contamination), Reynolds number, Mach number."],["081.01.01.04:06","EASA LO 081.01.01.04.06 → Describe the following parameter of an aerofoil section: camber line.","Leading edge, trailing edge, chord line (straight line LE–TE), thickness/chord ratio (relative thickness, e.g. 12 %), location of max thickness (% chord), camber line (mean line halfway between upper and lower surface), camber (max distance camber line–chord), nose radius. Symmetrical aerofoil: camber line = chord line; asymmetrical (cambered): produces lift at α = 0. / 2D flow: flow around an infinite-span aerofoil section (no spanwise flow); 3D: real finite wing with spanwise flow, tip vortices, induced drag."],["081.01.01.04:07","EASA LO 081.01.01.04.07 → Describe the following parameter of an aerofoil section: camber.","2D flow: flow around an infinite-span aerofoil section (no spanwise flow); 3D: real finite wing with spanwise flow, tip vortices, induced drag. / Leading edge, trailing edge, chord line (straight line LE–TE), thickness/chord ratio (relative thickness, e.g. 12 %), location of max thickness (% chord), camber line (mean line halfway between upper and lower surface), camber (max distance camber line–chord), nose radius. Symmetrical aerofoil: camber line = chord line; asymmetrical (cambered): produces lift at α = 0."],["081.01.01.04:08","EASA LO 081.01.01.04.08 → Describe the following parameter of an aerofoil section: nose radius.","Leading edge, trailing edge, chord line (straight line LE–TE), thickness/chord ratio (relative thickness, e.g. 12 %), location of max thickness (% chord), camber line (mean line halfway between upper and lower surface), camber (max distance camber line–chord), nose radius. Symmetrical aerofoil: camber line = chord line; asymmetrical (cambered): produces lift at α = 0. / 2D flow: flow around an infinite-span aerofoil section (no spanwise flow); 3D: real finite wing with spanwise flow, tip vortices, induced drag."],["081.01.01.04:09","EASA LO 081.01.01.04.09 → Describe a symmetrical and an asymmetrical aerofoil section.","Leading edge, trailing edge, chord line (straight line LE–TE), thickness/chord ratio (relative thickness, e.g. 12 %), location of max thickness (% chord), camber line (mean line halfway between upper and lower surface), camber (max distance camber line–chord), nose radius. Symmetrical aerofoil: camber line = chord line; asymmetrical (cambered): produces lift at α = 0. / 2D flow: flow around an infinite-span aerofoil section (no spanwise flow); 3D: real finite wing with spanwise flow, tip vortices, induced drag."],["081.01.01.05:02","EASA LO 081.01.01.05.02 → Describe the following parameter of a wing: tip and root chord.","Span (tip to tip), root and tip chord, taper ratio = tip chord/root chord, wing area S, planform (rectangular, tapered, elliptical, swept, delta), mean geometric chord = S/b, MAC (mean aerodynamic chord — chord of an equivalent rectangular wing having the same aerodynamic characteristics; reference for CG in % MAC), aspect ratio AR = b²/S (= span/chord for rectangular), dihedral (upward angle of wings from horizontal), sweep angle (angle of the quarter-chord line to the lateral axis), wing twist — geometric (washout: incidence decreases towards the tip) and aerodynamic (different aerofoil sections along the span), angle of incidence = angle between the longitudinal axis and the wing-root chord (fixed, not α!). / Spanwise lift distribution: elliptical ideal; rectangular wing — lift concentrated at the root; tapered/swept — more lift outboard (tip stall tendency)."],["081.01.01.05:03","EASA LO 081.01.01.05.03 → Describe the following parameter of a wing: taper ratio.","Span (tip to tip), root and tip chord, taper ratio = tip chord/root chord, wing area S, planform (rectangular, tapered, elliptical, swept, delta), mean geometric chord = S/b, MAC (mean aerodynamic chord — chord of an equivalent rectangular wing having the same aerodynamic characteristics; reference for CG in % MAC), aspect ratio AR = b²/S (= span/chord for rectangular), dihedral (upward angle of wings from horizontal), sweep angle (angle of the quarter-chord line to the lateral axis), wing twist — geometric (washout: incidence decreases towards the tip) and aerodynamic (different aerofoil sections along the span), angle of incidence = angle between the longitudinal axis and the wing-root chord (fixed, not α!)."],["081.01.01.05:05","EASA LO 081.01.01.05.05 → Describe the following parameter of a wing: wing planform.","L = CL × ½ρV² × S. Lift depends on CL (α, shape, camber, Reynolds/Mach), density, V², wing area. Example: double the speed at constant α → 4× lift. / Span (tip to tip), root and tip chord, taper ratio = tip chord/root chord, wing area S, planform (rectangular, tapered, elliptical, swept, delta), mean geometric chord = S/b, MAC (mean aerodynamic chord — chord of an equivalent rectangular wing having the same aerodynamic characteristics; reference for CG in % MAC), aspect ratio AR = b²/S (= span/chord for rectangular), dihedral (upward angle of wings from horizontal), sweep angle (angle of the quarter-chord line to the lateral axis), wing twist — geometric (washout: incidence decreases towards the tip) and aerodynamic (different aerofoil sections along the span), angle of incidence = angle between the longitudinal axis and the wing-root chord (fixed, not α!)."],["081.01.01.05:06","EASA LO 081.01.01.05.06 → Describe the following parameter of a wing: mean geometric chord.","Span (tip to tip), root and tip chord, taper ratio = tip chord/root chord, wing area S, planform (rectangular, tapered, elliptical, swept, delta), mean geometric chord = S/b, MAC (mean aerodynamic chord — chord of an equivalent rectangular wing having the same aerodynamic characteristics; reference for CG in % MAC), aspect ratio AR = b²/S (= span/chord for rectangular), dihedral (upward angle of wings from horizontal), sweep angle (angle of the quarter-chord line to the lateral axis), wing twist — geometric (washout: incidence decreases towards the tip) and aerodynamic (different aerofoil sections along the span), angle of incidence = angle between the longitudinal axis and the wing-root chord (fixed, not α!). / Leading edge, trailing edge, chord line (straight line LE–TE), thickness/chord ratio (relative thickness, e.g. 12 %), location of max thickness (% chord), camber line (mean line "],["081.01.01.05:08","EASA LO 081.01.01.05.08 → Describe the following parameter of a wing: aspect ratio.","Means to reduce: high aspect ratio, winglets, tip tanks, wing twist (washout), camber change (for elliptical-like lift distribution). / Span (tip to tip), root and tip chord, taper ratio = tip chord/root chord, wing area S, planform (rectangular, tapered, elliptical, swept, delta), mean geometric chord = S/b, MAC (mean aerodynamic chord — chord of an equivalent rectangular wing having the same aerodynamic characteristics; reference for CG in % MAC), aspect ratio AR = b²/S (= span/chord for rectangular), dihedral (upward angle of wings from horizontal), sweep angle (angle of the quarter-chord line to the lateral axis), wing twist — geometric (washout: incidence decreases towards the tip) and aerodynamic (different aerofoil sections along the span), angle of incidence = angle between the longitudinal axis and the wing-root chord (fixed, not α!)."],["081.01.01.05:09","EASA LO 081.01.01.05.09 → Describe the following parameter of a wing: dihedral angle.","Span (tip to tip), root and tip chord, taper ratio = tip chord/root chord, wing area S, planform (rectangular, tapered, elliptical, swept, delta), mean geometric chord = S/b, MAC (mean aerodynamic chord — chord of an equivalent rectangular wing having the same aerodynamic characteristics; reference for CG in % MAC), aspect ratio AR = b²/S (= span/chord for rectangular), dihedral (upward angle of wings from horizontal), sweep angle (angle of the quarter-chord line to the lateral axis), wing twist — geometric (washout: incidence decreases towards the tip) and aerodynamic (different aerofoil sections along the span), angle of incidence = angle between the longitudinal axis and the wing-root chord (fixed, not α!). / Angle of attack α = angle between the chord line and the relative airflow (free stream)."],["081.01.01.05:10","EASA LO 081.01.01.05.10 → Describe the following parameter of a wing: sweep angle.","Span (tip to tip), root and tip chord, taper ratio = tip chord/root chord, wing area S, planform (rectangular, tapered, elliptical, swept, delta), mean geometric chord = S/b, MAC (mean aerodynamic chord — chord of an equivalent rectangular wing having the same aerodynamic characteristics; reference for CG in % MAC), aspect ratio AR = b²/S (= span/chord for rectangular), dihedral (upward angle of wings from horizontal), sweep angle (angle of the quarter-chord line to the lateral axis), wing twist — geometric (washout: incidence decreases towards the tip) and aerodynamic (different aerofoil sections along the span), angle of incidence = angle between the longitudinal axis and the wing-root chord (fixed, not α!). / Angle of attack α = angle between the chord line and the relative airflow (free stream)."],["081.01.01.05:11","EASA LO 081.01.01.05.11 → Describe the following parameter of a wing: wing twist, geometric and aerodynamic.","Span (tip to tip), root and tip chord, taper ratio = tip chord/root chord, wing area S, planform (rectangular, tapered, elliptical, swept, delta), mean geometric chord = S/b, MAC (mean aerodynamic chord — chord of an equivalent rectangular wing having the same aerodynamic characteristics; reference for CG in % MAC), aspect ratio AR = b²/S (= span/chord for rectangular), dihedral (upward angle of wings from horizontal), sweep angle (angle of the quarter-chord line to the lateral axis), wing twist — geometric (washout: incidence decreases towards the tip) and aerodynamic (different aerofoil sections along the span), angle of incidence = angle between the longitudinal axis and the wing-root chord (fixed, not α!). / Means to reduce: high aspect ratio, winglets, tip tanks, wing twist (washout), camber change (for elliptical-like lift distribution)."],["081.01.01.05:12","EASA LO 081.01.01.05.12 → Describe the following parameter of a wing: angle of incidence. Remark: In certain textbooks, angle of incidence is used as angle of attack (α). For Part-FCL theoretical knowledge examination purposes, this use is discontinued, and the angle of incidence is defined as the angle between the aeroplane longitudinal axis and the wing-root chord line.","Span (tip to tip), root and tip chord, taper ratio = tip chord/root chord, wing area S, planform (rectangular, tapered, elliptical, swept, delta), mean geometric chord = S/b, MAC (mean aerodynamic chord — chord of an equivalent rectangular wing having the same aerodynamic characteristics; reference for CG in % MAC), aspect ratio AR = b²/S (= span/chord for rectangular), dihedral (upward angle of wings from horizontal), sweep angle (angle of the quarter-chord line to the lateral axis), wing twist — geometric (washout: incidence decreases towards the tip) and aerodynamic (different aerofoil sections along the span), angle of incidence = angle between the longitudinal axis and the wing-root chord (fixed, not α!). / Angle of attack α = angle between the chord line and the relative airflow (free stream)."],["081.01.02.01:02","EASA LO 081.01.02.01.02 → Describe converging and diverging streamlines, and their effect on static pressure and velocity.","Streamlines converge over the upper surface → speed ↑, static pressure ↓; diverge → speed ↓, pressure ↑. Upwash ahead of the wing, downwash behind. / Steady flow: velocity/pressure at each point constant with time; unsteady: changing. Streamline = path of a particle in steady flow; stream tube = bundle of streamlines — no flow across its walls (continuity applies)."],["081.01.02.01:03","EASA LO 081.01.02.01.03 → Describe upwash and downwash.","Streamlines converge over the upper surface → speed ↑, static pressure ↓; diverge → speed ↓, pressure ↑. Upwash ahead of the wing, downwash behind."],["081.01.02.02:01","EASA LO 081.01.02.02.01 → Describe the stagnation point. X X Exam BK Remarks","Stagnation point: point on the LE where the flow comes to rest (V = 0, pressure = total pressure); moves down and aft on the lower surface as α increases (forward/up as α decreases)."],["081.01.02.06:03","EASA LO 081.01.02.06.03 → Describe form (pressure) drag.","Drag from skin friction (viscous shear in the boundary layer) and form/pressure drag (pressure difference due to separation/wake). Drag and wake = loss of momentum/energy transferred to the air. / Total drag = parasite + induced. Parasite drag: form (pressure) drag (shape, frontal area, separation), interference drag (at junctions wing–fuselage, fairings reduce it), skin- friction drag (surface area, roughness, laminar vs turbulent boundary layer). Parasite drag ∝ V² (∝ IAS²)."],["081.01.02.07:01","EASA LO 081.01.02.07.01 → Explain the influence of α on lift.","Angle of attack α = angle between the chord line and the relative airflow (free stream). / α ↑ → lift ↑ (CL ∝ α) up to the critical angle."],["081.01.02.09:02","EASA LO 081.01.02.09.02 → Explain the significant points: — point where the curve crosses the horizontal axis (zero lift); — point where the curve crosses the vertical axis (α = 0);","CL–α graph: straight line through zero lift at α0L (negative for cambered, zero for symmetrical aerofoils), crossing the vertical axis at CL0 (> 0 cambered), rising to CLmax at αcrit (~15–16°), then falling (stall). / Drag–speed curve: induced falls, parasite rises → minimum at VMD where induced = parasite. Heavier aircraft → curve moves up and right (VMD higher). Altitude: drag–IAS curve unchanged; drag–TAS curve shifts right (same drag at higher TAS)."],["081.01.04.01:01","EASA LO 081.01.04.01.01 → Define ‘angle of attack’ (α). Remark: For theoretical knowledge examination purposes, the angle-of-attack definition requires a reference line. This reference line for 3D has","Angle of attack α = angle between the chord line and the relative airflow (free stream). / α (3D): angle between the aeroplane longitudinal axis (or wing reference) and the relative airflow — not the attitude (pitch attitude is relative to the horizon; attitude = α + flight path angle)."],["081.01.04.02:02","EASA LO 081.01.04.02.02 → Explain and describe the causes of spanwise flow over top and bottom surfaces.","2D flow: flow around an infinite-span aerofoil section (no spanwise flow); 3D: real finite wing with spanwise flow, tip vortices, induced drag."],["081.01.04.02:04","EASA LO 081.01.04.02.04 → Explain why wing tip vortices vary with α.","2D flow: flow around an infinite-span aerofoil section (no spanwise flow); 3D: real finite wing with spanwise flow, tip vortices, induced drag. / Spanwise flow: high pressure below flows outward towards the tip, low pressure above flows inward; they meet at the TE → trailing vortices, strongest at the tips = wingtip vortices, which produce downwash behind the wing. Vortices are stronger at high α (high CL, low speed)."],["081.01.04.03:01","EASA LO 081.01.04.03.01 → Explain the factors that cause induced drag.","Induced drag ∝ 1/V² and ∝ mass²."],["081.01.04.03:06","EASA LO 081.01.04.03.06 → Describe the influence of lift distribution on induced drag.","Induced drag ∝ 1/V² and ∝ mass². / Induced drag: downwash tilts the local airflow → effective α = geometric α − induced α; the lift vector (perpendicular to the effective flow) tilts rearwards → rearward component = induced drag. CDi ≈ CL²/(π·AR·e). Induced drag ∝ 1/V² (high at low speed), ↑ with mass (more CL at a given IAS), ↑ in turns (load factor), ↓ with higher aspect ratio, elliptical lift distribution."],["081.01.04.03:07","EASA LO 081.01.04.03.07 → Describe the influence of downwash on the effective airflow.","Induced drag: downwash tilts the local airflow → effective α = geometric α − induced α; the lift vector (perpendicular to the effective flow) tilts rearwards → rearward component = induced drag. CDi ≈ CL²/(π·AR·e). Induced drag ∝ 1/V² (high at low speed), ↑ with mass (more CL at a given IAS), ↑ in turns (load factor), ↓ with higher aspect ratio, elliptical lift distribution. / Within ~one wingspan (strong at < ½ span) of the ground, the ground restricts downwash and tip vortices → induced α and CDi reduced → less induced drag, more lift at a given α (effective α ↑)."],["081.01.04.03:09","EASA LO 081.01.04.03.09 → Explain the influence of the induced α on the direction of the lift vector.","Induced drag: downwash tilts the local airflow → effective α = geometric α − induced α; the lift vector (perpendicular to the effective flow) tilts rearwards → rearward component = induced drag. CDi ≈ CL²/(π·AR·e). Induced drag ∝ 1/V² (high at low speed), ↑ with mass (more CL at a given IAS), ↑ in turns (load factor), ↓ with higher aspect ratio, elliptical lift distribution. / Induced drag ∝ 1/V² and ∝ mass²."],["081.01.04.03:10","EASA LO 081.01.04.03.10 → Explain the relationship between induced drag and: — speed; — aspect ratio; — wing planform; — bank angle in a horizontal coordinated turn.","Span (tip to tip), root and tip chord, taper ratio = tip chord/root chord, wing area S, planform (rectangular, tapered, elliptical, swept, delta), mean geometric chord = S/b, MAC (mean aerodynamic chord — chord of an equivalent rectangular wing having the same aerodynamic characteristics; reference for CG in % MAC), aspect ratio AR = b²/S (= span/chord for rectangular), dihedral (upward angle of wings from horizontal), sweep angle (angle of the quarter-chord line to the lateral axis), wing twist — geometric (washout: incidence decreases towards the tip) and aerodynamic (different aerofoil sections along the span), angle of incidence = angle between the longitudinal axis and the wing-root chord (fixed, not α!). / Angle of attack α = angle between the chord line and the relative airflow (free stream)."],["081.01.04.03:11","EASA LO 081.01.04.03.11 → Explain the induced drag coefficient and its relationship with the lift coefficient and aspect ratio.","Induced drag: downwash tilts the local airflow → effective α = geometric α − induced α; the lift vector (perpendicular to the effective flow) tilts rearwards → rearward component = induced drag. CDi ≈ CL²/(π·AR·e). Induced drag ∝ 1/V² (high at low speed), ↑ with mass (more CL at a given IAS), ↑ in turns (load factor), ↓ with higher aspect ratio, elliptical lift distribution. / Induced drag ∝ 1/V² and ∝ mass²."],["081.01.04.03:12","EASA LO 081.01.04.03.12 → Explain the influence of induced drag on: X X Exam BK Remarks — the CL–α graph, and show the effect on the graph when comparing high- and low-aspect ratio wings; — the CL–CD (aeroplane polar), and show the effect on the graph when comparing high\u0002and low-aspect ratio wings; — the parabolic aeroplane polar in a graph and as a formula [CD = CPD + kCL","Effect on graphs: low-AR wing → flatter CL–α slope, higher αcrit; polar: more drag for the same CL. Polar CD = CD0 + k·CL² (parabolic). Minimum drag (max L/D) = tangent from the origin to the polar. L/D (CL/CD) ratio measures aerodynamic efficiency (range, glide). / Induced drag: downwash tilts the local airflow → effective α = geometric α − induced α; the lift vector (perpendicular to the effective flow) tilts rearwards → rearward component = induced drag. CDi ≈ CL²/(π·AR·e). Induced drag ∝ 1/V² (high at low speed), ↑ with mass (more CL at a given IAS), ↑ in turns (load factor), ↓ with higher aspect ratio, elliptical lift distribution."],["081.01.04.03:14","EASA LO 081.01.04.03.14 → Indicate minimum drag on the graph.","Drag–speed curve: induced falls, parasite rises → minimum at VMD where induced = parasite. Heavier aircraft → curve moves up and right (VMD higher). Altitude: drag–IAS curve unchanged; drag–TAS curve shifts right (same drag at higher TAS). / Induced drag ∝ 1/V² and ∝ mass²."],["081.01.04.03:15","EASA LO 081.01.04.03.15 → Explain why the CL–CD ratio is important as a measure of performance.","Effect on graphs: low-AR wing → flatter CL–α slope, higher αcrit; polar: more drag for the same CL. Polar CD = CD0 + k·CL² (parabolic). Minimum drag (max L/D) = tangent from the origin to the polar. L/D (CL/CD) ratio measures aerodynamic efficiency (range, glide)."],["081.01.05.01:01","EASA LO 081.01.05.01.01 → State that total drag consists of parasite drag and induced drag.","Drag–speed curve: induced falls, parasite rises → minimum at VMD where induced = parasite. Heavier aircraft → curve moves up and right (VMD higher). Altitude: drag–IAS curve unchanged; drag–TAS curve shifts right (same drag at higher TAS). / Total drag = parasite + induced. Parasite drag: form (pressure) drag (shape, frontal area, separation), interference drag (at junctions wing–fuselage, fairings reduce it), skin- friction drag (surface area, roughness, laminar vs turbulent boundary layer). Parasite drag ∝ V² (∝ IAS²)."],["081.01.05.02:01","EASA LO 081.01.05.02.01 → Describe the types of drag that are included in parasite drag.","Total drag = parasite + induced. Parasite drag: form (pressure) drag (shape, frontal area, separation), interference drag (at junctions wing–fuselage, fairings reduce it), skin- friction drag (surface area, roughness, laminar vs turbulent boundary layer). Parasite drag ∝ V² (∝ IAS²). / Drag–speed curve: induced falls, parasite rises → minimum at VMD where induced = parasite. Heavier aircraft → curve moves up and right (VMD higher). Altitude: drag–IAS curve unchanged; drag–TAS curve shifts right (same drag at higher TAS)."],["081.01.05.02:02","EASA LO 081.01.05.02.02 → Describe form (pressure) drag and the factors which affect its magnitude.","Total drag = parasite + induced. Parasite drag: form (pressure) drag (shape, frontal area, separation), interference drag (at junctions wing–fuselage, fairings reduce it), skin- friction drag (surface area, roughness, laminar vs turbulent boundary layer). Parasite drag ∝ V² (∝ IAS²)."],["081.01.05.02:03","EASA LO 081.01.05.02.03 → Describe interference drag and the factors which affect its magnitude.","Total drag = parasite + induced. Parasite drag: form (pressure) drag (shape, frontal area, separation), interference drag (at junctions wing–fuselage, fairings reduce it), skin- friction drag (surface area, roughness, laminar vs turbulent boundary layer). Parasite drag ∝ V² (∝ IAS²)."],["081.01.05.02:04","EASA LO 081.01.05.02.04 → Describe friction drag and the factors which affect its magnitude.","Total drag = parasite + induced. Parasite drag: form (pressure) drag (shape, frontal area, separation), interference drag (at junctions wing–fuselage, fairings reduce it), skin- friction drag (surface area, roughness, laminar vs turbulent boundary layer). Parasite drag ∝ V² (∝ IAS²)."],["081.01.05.03:01","EASA LO 081.01.05.03.01 → Describe the relationship between parasite drag and speed.","Drag–speed curve: induced falls, parasite rises → minimum at VMD where induced = parasite. Heavier aircraft → curve moves up and right (VMD higher). Altitude: drag–IAS curve unchanged; drag–TAS curve shifts right (same drag at higher TAS). / Total drag = parasite + induced. Parasite drag: form (pressure) drag (shape, frontal area, separation), interference drag (at junctions wing–fuselage, fairings reduce it), skin- friction drag (surface area, roughness, laminar vs turbulent boundary layer). Parasite drag ∝ V² (∝ IAS²)."],["081.01.05.05:02","EASA LO 081.01.05.05.02 → Indicate the speed for minimum drag.","Level flight: L = W → CL = 2W/(ρV²S) → speed ↑ → CL (α) must ↓: CL1·V₁² = CL2·V₂². Example: speed doubled → CL ¼. Graph: CL required vs IAS is a hyperbola; minimum speed at CLmax (stall speed). / Drag–speed curve: induced falls, parasite rises → minimum at VMD where induced = parasite. Heavier aircraft → curve moves up and right (VMD higher). Altitude: drag–IAS curve unchanged; drag–TAS curve shifts right (same drag at higher TAS)."],["081.01.05.07:01","EASA LO 081.01.05.07.01 → Describe the effect of aeroplane gross mass on the graph.","Induced drag ∝ 1/V² and ∝ mass²."],["081.01.05.07:03","EASA LO 081.01.05.07.03 → Describe speed stability from the graph.","Speed stability: above VMD stable (speed ↓ → drag ↓ → aircraft accelerates back); at VMD neutral; below VMD (back of the drag curve) unstable — if speed decreases, drag increases, speed decreases further (e.g. low-speed approach, turbulence) → needs active thrust management. / Level flight: L = W → CL = 2W/(ρV²S) → speed ↑ → CL (α) must ↓: CL1·V₁² = CL2·V₂². Example: speed doubled → CL ¼. Graph: CL required vs IAS is a hyperbola; minimum speed at CLmax (stall speed)."],["081.01.05.07:05","EASA LO 081.01.05.07.05 → Explain what happens to the IAS and drag in the non-stable region if speed suddenly decreases and why this could occur.","Speed stability: above VMD stable (speed ↓ → drag ↓ → aircraft accelerates back); at VMD neutral; below VMD (back of the drag curve) unstable — if speed decreases, drag increases, speed decreases further (e.g. low-speed approach, turbulence) → needs active thrust management."],["081.01.06.01:03","EASA LO 081.01.06.01.03 → Explain the effects of entering and leaving ground effect.","αcrit decreases slightly (stall at a lower α); CL at a given α ↑. Entering ground effect: floating on landing (longer flare), nose-down pitch (less downwash on tail), static pressure errors (ASI/altimeter); leaving it after take-off: drag ↑, lift ↓ → aircraft may sink/settle if lifted off too early; nose-up tendency. / Within ~one wingspan (strong at < ½ span) of the ground, the ground restricts downwash and tip vortices → induced α and CDi reduced → less induced drag, more lift at a given α (effective α ↑)."],["081.01.06.02:01","EASA LO 081.01.06.02.01 → Describe the influence of ground effect on αCRIT.","CLmax = maximum lift coefficient; αcrit = stalling α. Variables: α, camber, thickness, flaps/slats, surface condition (ice, contamination), Reynolds number, Mach number. / Angle of attack α = angle between the chord line and the relative airflow (free stream)."],["081.01.06.03:01","EASA LO 081.01.06.03.01 → Describe the influence of ground effect on the effective  and CL.","Within ~one wingspan (strong at < ½ span) of the ground, the ground restricts downwash and tip vortices → induced α and CDi reduced → less induced drag, more lift at a given α (effective α ↑). / L = CL × ½ρV² × S. Lift depends on CL (α, shape, camber, Reynolds/Mach), density, V², wing area. Example: double the speed at constant α → 4× lift."],["081.01.06.04:02","EASA LO 081.01.06.04.02 → Describe the difference in take-off and landing characteristics of high- and low-wing aeroplanes.","αcrit decreases slightly (stall at a lower α); CL at a given α ↑. Entering ground effect: floating on landing (longer flare), nose-down pitch (less downwash on tail), static pressure errors (ASI/altimeter); leaving it after take-off: drag ↑, lift ↓ → aircraft may sink/settle if lifted off too early; nose-up tendency."],["081.01.07.01:01","EASA LO 081.01.07.01.01 → Explain the effect on CL during speed increase/decrease in steady, straight, and level flight, and perform simple calculations.","Level flight: L = W → CL = 2W/(ρV²S) → speed ↑ → CL (α) must ↓: CL1·V₁² = CL2·V₂². Example: speed doubled → CL ¼. Graph: CL required vs IAS is a hyperbola; minimum speed at CLmax (stall speed)."],["081.01.07.02:01","EASA LO 081.01.07.02.01 → Explain, by using a graph, the effect on speed of CL changes at a given weight.","Level flight: L = W → CL = 2W/(ρV²S) → speed ↑ → CL (α) must ↓: CL1·V₁² = CL2·V₂². Example: speed doubled → CL ¼. Graph: CL required vs IAS is a hyperbola; minimum speed at CLmax (stall speed)."],["081.01.03.02:01","EASA LO 081.01.03.02.01 → Explain the lift formula, the factors that affect lift, and perform simple calculations.","L = CL × ½ρV² × S. Lift depends on CL (α, shape, camber, Reynolds/Mach), density, V², wing area. Example: double the speed at constant α → 4× lift."],["081.01.03.02:03","EASA LO 081.01.03.02.03 → Describe the typical difference in the CL– graph for fast and slow aerofoil design.","Camber shifts the CL–α line up/left (positive CL at α = 0, higher CLmax); negative camber shifts it down. Slow aerofoils (thick, cambered, large nose radius) → high CLmax, gentle stall; fast aerofoils (thin, little camber) → lower CLmax, sharp stall, higher Mcrit. / α ↑ → lift ↑ (CL ∝ α) up to the critical angle."],["081.01.03.02:05","EASA LO 081.01.03.02.05 → Describe CL and explain the variables that affect it in low subsonic flight.","Level flight: L = W → CL = 2W/(ρV²S) → speed ↑ → CL (α) must ↓: CL1·V₁² = CL2·V₂². Example: speed doubled → CL ¼. Graph: CL required vs IAS is a hyperbola; minimum speed at CLmax (stall speed). / L = CL × ½ρV² × S. Lift depends on CL (α, shape, camber, Reynolds/Mach), density, V², wing area. Example: double the speed at constant α → 4× lift."]],"8":[["061.01.01.01:01","The Earth: fill in the gaps → ____ = irregular shape of the mean sea surface under gravity and centrifugal force only. For mapping, ____ approximate it; ____ is the required reference ellipsoid for aeronautical coordinates (GPS). Earth slightly flattened at the poles (oblate spheroid).","Geoid = irregular shape of the mean sea surface under gravity and centrifugal force only. For mapping, ellipsoids approximate it; WGS-84 is the required reference ellipsoid for aeronautical coordinates (GPS). Earth slightly flattened at the poles (oblate spheroid)."],["061.01.01.01:03","The Earth: fill in the gaps → ____ (360° × 60′).","Circumference ≈ 40 000 km ≈ 21 600 NM (360° × 60′)."],["061.01.01.02:01","The Earth: fill in the gaps → ____: once per day about its spin axis, ____ (sun appears to move east→west); orbit around the sun in the ____ the ecliptic once per year.","Rotation: once per day about its spin axis, west to east (sun appears to move east→west); orbit around the sun in the plane of the ecliptic once per year."],["061.01.01.02:01","The Earth: fill in the gaps → Spin axis tilted ____ to the perpendicular of the ecliptic (66.5° to the ecliptic plane) and points in a fixed direction in space → ____: changing sun elevation (____) and duration of daylight with latitude through the year (summer solstice NH ~21 Jun: sun over Tropic of Cancer 23.5°N; equinoxes ~21 Mar/23 Sep: sun over the equator, day = night everywhere).","Spin axis tilted ~23.5° to the perpendicular of the ecliptic (66.5° to the ecliptic plane) and points in a fixed direction in space → seasons: changing sun elevation (insolation) and duration of daylight with latitude through the year (summer solstice NH ~21 Jun: sun over Tropic of Cancer 23.5°N; equinoxes ~21 Mar/23 Sep: sun over the equator, day = night everywhere)."],["061.01.04.03:01","Position: fill in the gaps → Position on WGS-84 by ____. ____: angle north/south of the equator (0–90° N/S); ____ of latitude = small circles parallel to the equator. Longitude: angle east/west of the prime (Greenwich) meridian (0–180° E/W); meridians = half great circles from pole to pole.","Position on WGS-84 by geodetic latitude and longitude. Latitude: angle north/south of the equator (0–90° N/S); parallels of latitude = small circles parallel to the equator. Longitude: angle east/west of the prime (Greenwich) meridian (0–180° E/W); meridians = half great circles from pole to pole."],["100.04.00.00:04","Position: fill in the gaps → ____: same hemisphere → subtract; opposite → add. E.g. 50°20′N to 10°40′S → ____. Difference of longitude (d.long): same side → subtract; opposite sides → add (if > 180° subtract from 360°). E.g. 170°E to 170°W → ____.","Difference of latitude (d.lat): same hemisphere → subtract; opposite → add. E.g. 50°20′N to 10°40′S → 61°00′. Difference of longitude (d.long): same side → subtract; opposite sides → add (if > 180° subtract from 360°). E.g. 170°E to 170°W → 20°."],["061.01.03.01:01","Direction: fill in the gaps → ____: direction of the geographic North Pole along the meridian; true directions measured on charts from the meridian (protractor at mid-meridian). ____: direction a freely suspended magnet points (horizontal component of earth's field).","True north (TN): direction of the geographic North Pole along the meridian; true directions measured on charts from the meridian (protractor at mid-meridian). Magnetic north (MN): direction a freely suspended magnet points (horizontal component of earth's field)."],["061.01.03.01:05","Direction: fill in the gaps → ____ = angle between TN and MN (E or W) — ____ ____ (\"East is least, West is best\"). Variation changes with position (isogonals on charts) and time (annual change, magnetic pole wanders).","Variation = angle between TN and MN (E or W) — MH = TH − var E / + var W (\"East is least, West is best\"). Variation changes with position (isogonals on charts) and time (annual change, magnetic pole wanders)."],["061.01.03.01:06","Direction: fill in the gaps → ____: direction the aircraft's compass points; ____ = angle MN–CN due to aircraft magnetism: ____ − dev E / + dev W.","Compass north (CN): direction the aircraft's compass points; deviation = angle MN–CN due to aircraft magnetism: CH = MH − dev E / + dev W."],["061.01.03.02:03","Direction: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ = angle between heading and track (wind from left → drift right); ____ = correction applied to heading to make good the track (into the wind). Track = heading ± drift; track error (TKE) = difference between planned and actual track.","Track and heading: drift = angle between heading and track (wind from left → drift right); WCA = correction applied to heading to make good the track (into the wind). Track = heading ± drift; track error (TKE) = difference between planned and actual track."],["061.01.03.02:01","Direction: fill in the gaps → ____: trigonometry ____ ____; MDR: 30° → ½, 45° → 0.7, 60° → 0.9, ≥ 60° → ~full. Drift/WCA (°) ≈ XWC ÷ SF, SF = TAS/60 (speed factor).","Crosswind component (XWC): trigonometry XWC = WS × sin(angle); MDR: 30° → ½, 45° → 0.7, 60° → 0.9, ≥ 60° → ~full. Drift/WCA (°) ≈ XWC ÷ SF, SF = TAS/60 (speed factor)."],["061.01.03.02:05","Direction: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ flown; ____; heading change to reach the next waypoint = TKE + CA. Average drift from an off-course fix = planned drift + TKE. Example: 4 NM left after 40 NM, 80 NM to go → TKE 6°, CA 3° → alter 9° right.","1-in-60 rule: TKE (°) = off-track distance × 60 / distance flown; closing angle = off × 60 / distance to go; heading change to reach the next waypoint = TKE + CA. Average drift from an off-course fix = planned drift + TKE. Example: 4 NM left after 40 NM, 80 NM to go → TKE 6°, CA 3° → alter 9° right."],["061.01.04.01:01","Distance: fill in the gaps → ____ = average length of ____ on WGS-84 (EASA LO text says \"1 852 km\" — typo; it's metres). ____ longitude at the equator ≈ 1 NM; elsewhere 1′ long = cos(lat) NM.","1 NM = 1 852 m = average length of 1′ of latitude on WGS-84 (EASA LO text says \"1 852 km\" — typo; it's metres). 1′ of longitude at the equator ≈ 1 NM; elsewhere 1′ long = cos(lat) NM."],["061.01.04.02:01","Distance: fill in the gaps → Conversions: ____, ____ ____, 1 km = 0.54 NM, 1 m = 3.28 ft, 1 ft = 12 in = 0.3048 m, 1 in = 2.54 cm.","Conversions: 1 NM = 1.852 km = 6 076 ft, 1 SM = 1.609 km = 5 280 ft, 1 km = 0.54 NM, 1 m = 3.28 ft, 1 ft = 12 in = 0.3048 m, 1 in = 2.54 cm."],["061.01.04.03:01","Distance: fill in the gaps → ____: along a ____: d.lat in minutes = NM. Across the pole (____, d.long 180°): distance = (90 − lat1) + (90 − lat2) in degrees × 60. Along a parallel (departure): dep (NM) = d.long (′) × cos(lat). New position: d.lat = N/S distance ÷ 60; d.long = E/W distance ÷ (60 × cos lat). Example: 60°N, 100 NM east → d.long = 100/(60 × 0.5) = 3°20′.","Graticule distances: along a meridian: d.lat in minutes = NM. Across the pole (antipodal meridians, d.long 180°): distance = (90 − lat1) + (90 − lat2) in degrees × 60. Along a parallel (departure): dep (NM) = d.long (′) × cos(lat). New position: d.lat = N/S distance ÷ 60; d.long = E/W distance ÷ (60 × cos lat). Example: 60°N, 100 NM east → d.long = 100/(60 × 0.5) = 3°20′."],["061.01.04.04:02","Distance: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ — headwind → more air miles; altitude affects TAS (and wind). Example: 300 NGM, TAS 400, GS 350 → ____.","Air miles: NAM = NGM × TAS / GS — headwind → more air miles; altitude affects TAS (and wind). Example: 300 NGM, TAS 400, GS 350 → 343 NAM."],["100.04.00.00:02","E6-B/CRP computer basics: fill in the gaps → Outer scale (distance/fuel/TAS) and inner scale (time/minutes); ____ for time–speed–distance: set the 60 index opposite GS → read distance over time. Multiplication/division, unit conversions (NM/km/SM, kg/lb, l/USG/IG), fuel burn.","Outer scale (distance/fuel/TAS) and inner scale (time/minutes); speed index (60) for time–speed–distance: set the 60 index opposite GS → read distance over time. Multiplication/division, unit conversions (NM/km/SM, kg/lb, l/USG/IG), fuel burn."],["100.04.00.00:04","Mental maths & MDR (KSA 100.04): fill in the gaps → Fuel volume ↔ mass (l × 0.8 = kg JET A-1; USG × 3.785 = l); ____ = distance ÷ speed (NM per minute = GS/60); ____ on 3°; ____; TOD: distance (NM) ≈ (height to lose in ft ÷ 1 000) × 3; fuel burn = FF × time; time available = (fuel − reserve)/FF; percentages (wet LD +15 %);","Fuel volume ↔ mass (l × 0.8 = kg JET A-1; USG × 3.785 = l); time = distance ÷ speed (NM per minute = GS/60); ROD ≈ GS × 5 on 3°; height on a 3° slope ≈ 300 ft/NM; TOD: distance (NM) ≈ (height to lose in ft ÷ 1 000) × 3; fuel burn = FF × time; time available = (fuel − reserve)/FF; percentages (wet LD +15 %);"],["100.04.00.00:10","Mental maths & MDR (KSA 100.04): fill in the gaps → ____ ____ headings; head/crosswind components by sine/cosine rules of thumb.","1- in-60 headings; head/crosswind components by sine/cosine rules of thumb."],["061.01.04.01:02","Exam trap (Mental maths & MDR (KSA 100.04)): complete the fact → ____′ lat = ____ everywhere, 1′ long = 1 NM only at the equator.","1′ lat = 1 NM everywhere, 1′ long = 1 NM only at the equator."],["061.01.01.01:01","Exam trap (Mental maths & MDR (KSA 100.04)): complete the fact → Crossing ____°: d.long = ____um.","Crossing 180°: d.long = 360 − sum."],["061.01.01.01:02","EASA LO 061.01.01.01.02 → State that a number of different ellipsoids are used to describe the shape of the Earth for mapping but that WGS-84 is the reference ellipsoid required for geographical coordinates.","Geoid = irregular shape of the mean sea surface under gravity and centrifugal force only. For mapping, ellipsoids approximate it; WGS-84 is the required reference ellipsoid for aeronautical coordinates (GPS). Earth slightly flattened at the poles (oblate spheroid)."],["061.01.01.02:02","EASA LO 061.01.01.02.02 → Explain the effect that the inclination of the Earth’s spin axis has on insolation and duration of daylight.","Spin axis tilted ~23.5° to the perpendicular of the ecliptic (66.5° to the ecliptic plane) and points in a fixed direction in space → seasons: changing sun elevation (insolation) and duration of daylight with latitude through the year (summer solstice NH ~21 Jun: sun over Tropic of Cancer 23.5°N; equinoxes ~21 Mar/23 Sep: sun over the equator, day = night everywhere). / Rotation: once per day about its spin axis, west to east (sun appears to move east→west); orbit around the sun in the plane of the ecliptic once per year."],["061.01.02.01:01","EASA LO 061.01.02.01.01 → State that geodetic latitude and longitude is used to define a position on the WGS-84 ellipsoid.","Position on WGS-84 by geodetic latitude and longitude. Latitude: angle north/south of the equator (0–90° N/S); parallels of latitude = small circles parallel to the equator. Longitude: angle east/west of the prime (Greenwich) meridian (0–180° E/W); meridians = half great circles from pole to pole. / 1 NM = 1 852 m = average length of 1′ of latitude on WGS-84 (EASA LO text says \"1 852 km\" — typo; it's metres). 1′ of longitude at the equator ≈ 1 NM; elsewhere 1′ long = cos(lat) NM."],["061.01.02.01:02","EASA LO 061.01.02.01.02 → Define geographic (geodetic) latitude and parallels of latitude.","Position on WGS-84 by geodetic latitude and longitude. Latitude: angle north/south of the equator (0–90° N/S); parallels of latitude = small circles parallel to the equator. Longitude: angle east/west of the prime (Greenwich) meridian (0–180° E/W); meridians = half great circles from pole to pole."],["061.01.02.01:04","EASA LO 061.01.02.01.04 → Define geographic (geodetic) longitude and meridians.","Position on WGS-84 by geodetic latitude and longitude. Latitude: angle north/south of the equator (0–90° N/S); parallels of latitude = small circles parallel to the equator. Longitude: angle east/west of the prime (Greenwich) meridian (0–180° E/W); meridians = half great circles from pole to pole."],["061.01.03.01:03","EASA LO 061.01.03.01.03 → Define ‘magnetic north’ (MN).","Variation = angle between TN and MN (E or W) — MH = TH − var E / + var W (\"East is least, West is best\"). Variation changes with position (isogonals on charts) and time (annual change, magnetic pole wanders). / True north (TN): direction of the geographic North Pole along the meridian; true directions measured on charts from the meridian (protractor at mid-meridian). Magnetic north (MN): direction a freely suspended magnet points (horizontal component of earth's field)."],["061.01.03.01:04","EASA LO 061.01.03.01.04 → Define and apply variation.","Variation = angle between TN and MN (E or W) — MH = TH − var E / + var W (\"East is least, West is best\"). Variation changes with position (isogonals on charts) and time (annual change, magnetic pole wanders)."],["061.01.03.02:02","EASA LO 061.01.03.02.02 → Explain and apply the concepts of drift and WCA.","Track and heading: drift = angle between heading and track (wind from left → drift right); WCA = correction applied to heading to make good the track (into the wind). Track = heading ± drift; track error (TKE) = difference between planned and actual track."],["061.01.03.02:04","EASA LO 061.01.03.02.04 → Calculate TKE with appropriate data of WCA and drift.","Track and heading: drift = angle between heading and track (wind from left → drift right); WCA = correction applied to heading to make good the track (into the wind). Track = heading ± drift; track error (TKE) = difference between planned and actual track. / 1-in-60 rule: TKE (°) = off-track distance × 60 / distance flown; closing angle = off × 60 / distance to go; heading change to reach the next waypoint = TKE + CA. Average drift from an off-course fix = planned drift + TKE. Example: 4 NM left after 40 NM, 80 NM to go → TKE 6°, CA 3° → alter 9° right."],["061.01.03.02:06","EASA LO 061.01.03.02.06 → Calculate the average drift angle based upon an off-course fix observation.","1-in-60 rule: TKE (°) = off-track distance × 60 / distance flown; closing angle = off × 60 / distance to go; heading change to reach the next waypoint = TKE + CA. Average drift from an off-course fix = planned drift + TKE. Example: 4 NM left after 40 NM, 80 NM to go → TKE 6°, CA 3° → alter 9° right. / Track and heading: drift = angle between heading and track (wind from left → drift right); WCA = correction applied to heading to make good the track (into the wind). Track = heading ± drift; track error (TKE) = difference between planned and actual track."],["061.01.04.04:01","EASA LO 061.01.04.04.01 → Evaluate the effect of wind and altitude on air distance.","Air miles: NAM = NGM × TAS / GS — headwind → more air miles; altitude affects TAS (and wind). Example: 300 NGM, TAS 400, GS 350 → 343 NAM."],["100.04.00.00:05","EASA LO 100.04.00.00.05 → Calculate fuel burn given time and fuel flow.","Fuel volume ↔ mass (l × 0.8 = kg JET A-1; USG × 3.785 = l); time = distance ÷ speed (NM per minute = GS/60); ROD ≈ GS × 5 on 3°; height on a 3° slope ≈ 300 ft/NM; TOD: distance (NM) ≈ (height to lose in ft ÷ 1 000) × 3; fuel burn = FF × time; time available = (fuel − reserve)/FF; percentages (wet LD +15 %); / Outer scale (distance/fuel/TAS) and inner scale (time/minutes); speed index (60) for time–speed–distance: set the 60 index opposite GS → read distance over time. Multiplication/division, unit conversions (NM/km/SM, kg/lb, l/USG/IG), fuel burn."],["100.04.00.00:06","EASA LO 100.04.00.00.06 → Calculate the time available (for decision-making) given relevant fuel information.","Fuel volume ↔ mass (l × 0.8 = kg JET A-1; USG × 3.785 = l); time = distance ÷ speed (NM per minute = GS/60); ROD ≈ GS × 5 on 3°; height on a 3° slope ≈ 300 ft/NM; TOD: distance (NM) ≈ (height to lose in ft ÷ 1 000) × 3; fuel burn = FF × time; time available = (fuel − reserve)/FF; percentages (wet LD +15 %);"],["100.04.00.00:08","EASA LO 100.04.00.00.08 → Determine the values that vary by a percentage, e.g. dry-to-wet landing distance and fuel burn.","Fuel volume ↔ mass (l × 0.8 = kg JET A-1; USG × 3.785 = l); time = distance ÷ speed (NM per minute = GS/60); ROD ≈ GS × 5 on 3°; height on a 3° slope ≈ 300 ft/NM; TOD: distance (NM) ≈ (height to lose in ft ÷ 1 000) × 3; fuel burn = FF × time; time available = (fuel − reserve)/FF; percentages (wet LD +15 %);"],["100.04.00.00:11","EASA LO 100.04.00.00.11 → Estimate headwind and crosswind components given wind speed and direction and runway in use.","1- in-60 headings; head/crosswind components by sine/cosine rules of thumb."]],"9":[["031.01.01.01:02","Why mass & balance matters: fill in the gaps → ____: loads/stresses rise with mass (and load factor) → mass limits keep ____ (limit/ultimate loads, fatigue). Overload → structural damage, reduced safety margin in gusts/manoeuvres, heavy landings.","Structure: loads/stresses rise with mass (and load factor) → mass limits keep adequate strength margins (limit/ultimate loads, fatigue). Overload → structural damage, reduced safety margin in gusts/manoeuvres, heavy landings."],["031.01.02.02:02","Why mass & balance matters: fill in the gaps → ____: higher mass → longer take-off and landing distances, higher stall/take-off/landing speeds, lower climb rate & gradient, lower ceiling, higher fuel burn, reduced range/endurance → mass limited for adequate performance.","Performance: higher mass → longer take-off and landing distances, higher stall/take-off/landing speeds, lower climb rate & gradient, lower ceiling, higher fuel burn, reduced range/endurance → mass limited for adequate performance."],["031.01.02.01:02","Why mass & balance matters: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ → more stable, heavier control forces, ____ (rotation/flare difficult), higher stall speed, more trim drag/fuel. CG ahead of forward limit → insufficient elevator to rotate/flare, very heavy forces, nose-wheel overload.","CG & stability/control: forward CG → more stable, heavier control forces, more elevator needed (rotation/flare difficult), higher stall speed, more trim drag/fuel. CG ahead of forward limit → insufficient elevator to rotate/flare, very heavy forces, nose-wheel overload."],["031.01.02.01:03","Why mass & balance matters: fill in the gaps → ____ → less stable, light forces, easier overstress; ____ → unstable/uncontrollable in pitch, possible stall/spin recovery problems, tail tipping on the ground.","Aft CG → less stable, light forces, easier overstress; behind aft limit → unstable/uncontrollable in pitch, possible stall/spin recovery problems, tail tipping on the ground."],["031.01.02.02:02","Why mass & balance matters: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ → higher VS (tail download adds to effective weight) → higher speeds, longer distances, ____ drag → less range/endurance, lower ceiling; aft CG → less trim drag → better range (within limits).","CG & performance: forward CG → higher VS (tail download adds to effective weight) → higher speeds, longer distances, more drag → less range/endurance, lower ceiling; aft CG → less trim drag → better range (within limits)."],["031.02.01.01:01","Mass terms: fill in the gaps → ____: aircraft + fixed equipment + unusable fuel + full operating fluids (oil, hydraulic), per weighing.","Basic empty mass (BEM): aircraft + fixed equipment + unusable fuel + full operating fluids (oil, hydraulic), per weighing."],["031.02.01.01:01","Mass terms: fill in the gaps → ____: total mass ready for a specific operation ____ (includes crew & baggage, catering, removable pax service equipment, potable water, lavatory chemicals). ____ = DOM + take-off fuel.","Dry operating mass (DOM): total mass ready for a specific operation excluding usable fuel and traffic load (includes crew & baggage, catering, removable pax service equipment, potable water, lavatory chemicals). Operating mass (OM) = DOM + take-off fuel."],["031.02.01.01:01","Mass terms: fill in the gaps → ____ = mass at start of take-off run (including everything). ____ = TOM − trip fuel. ____ = TOM + taxi fuel. In-flight (gross) mass. Zero fuel mass (ZFM) = DOM + traffic load (no usable fuel).","Take-off mass (TOM) = mass at start of take-off run (including everything). Landing mass (LM) = TOM − trip fuel. Ramp/taxi mass = TOM + taxi fuel. In-flight (gross) mass. Zero fuel mass (ZFM) = DOM + traffic load (no usable fuel)."],["031.02.01.02:01","Mass terms: fill in the gaps → Load terms: ____ = passengers + baggage + cargo + mail (incl. non-revenue load). ____ = total fuel on board at start-up; ____; take-off fuel = block − taxi; trip fuel; reserve fuel = contingency + alternate + final reserve + additional; extra fuel (discretionary).","Load terms: traffic load (payload) = passengers + baggage + cargo + mail (incl. non-revenue load). Block (ramp) fuel = total fuel on board at start-up; taxi fuel; take-off fuel = block − taxi; trip fuel; reserve fuel = contingency + alternate + final reserve + additional; extra fuel (discretionary)."],["031.02.02.01:02","Mass terms: fill in the gaps → Relationships: ____; ____ ____; LM = TOM − trip fuel; ramp mass = TOM + taxi fuel; OM = DOM + TOF.","Relationships: ZFM = DOM + TL; TOM = ZFM + TOF = DOM + TL + TOF; LM = TOM − trip fuel; ramp mass = TOM + taxi fuel; OM = DOM + TOF."],["031.02.01.01:01","Mass terms: fill in the gaps → Fuel conversions: ____; ____; USG = 3.785 l; Imp gal = 4.546 l. Example: 5 000 USG × 3.785 = 18 925 l × 0.79 = ____.","Fuel conversions: kg = litres × SG; lb = kg × 2.205; USG = 3.785 l; Imp gal = 4.546 l. Example: 5 000 USG × 3.785 = 18 925 l × 0.79 = 14 951 kg."],["031.02.02.01:04","Limitations: fill in the gaps → Structural: ____ (max zero-fuel mass — limits wing-root bending; fuel in wings relieves it), ____ (ground manoeuvring, gear), ____, max in-flight mass with external load (helicopters), MLM (landing loads on gear/structure).","Structural: MZFM (max zero-fuel mass — limits wing-root bending; fuel in wings relieves it), max ramp/taxi mass (ground manoeuvring, gear), MTOM, max in-flight mass with external load (helicopters), MLM (landing loads on gear/structure)."],["031.02.03.01:02","Limitations: fill in the gaps → ____: take-off (field length, climb/WAT, obstacle, tyre speed, brake energy), en-route, landing (field length, approach/landing climb) → ____ ____ may be lower than structural.","Performance/regulated limits: take-off (field length, climb/WAT, obstacle, tyre speed, brake energy), en-route, landing (field length, approach/landing climb) → performance-limited TOM/LM may be lower than structural."],["031.02.02.03:02","Limitations: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (per unit ____, kg/m²) and maximum running load (per unit length of fuselage, kg/m); also compartment max mass, cumulative loads.","Cargo compartment limits: maximum floor load (per unit area, kg/m²) and maximum running load (per unit length of fuselage, kg/m); also compartment max mass, cumulative loads."],["031.02.02.02:01","Allowed masses & loads: fill in the gaps → ____ = lowest of structural MTOM and performance-limited TOM; ____ similarly.","Regulated (max) TOM = lowest of structural MTOM and performance-limited TOM; regulated LM similarly."],["031.02.02.02:01","Allowed masses & loads: fill in the gaps → ____ = ____ of: ____, regulated TOM, regulated LM + trip fuel.","Allowed take-off mass = lowest of: MZFM + TOF, regulated TOM, regulated LM + trip fuel."],["031.02.03.02:02","Allowed masses & loads: fill in the gaps → ____ = allowed TOM − ____ (DOM + TOF). ____ = allowed TL − actual TL (spare capacity); overload if negative → offload cargo/pax or reduce fuel (only extra fuel).","Max allowed traffic load = allowed TOM − operating mass (DOM + TOF). Underload = allowed TL − actual TL (spare capacity); overload if negative → offload cargo/pax or reduce fuel (only extra fuel)."],["031.02.03.01:01","Allowed masses & loads: fill in the gaps → Example: DOM 40 t, TOF 12 t, trip 8 t; MZFM 60 t, MTOM 75 t, MLM 64 t → MZFM + TOF = 72; MTOM 75; MLM + trip = 72 → ____ → allowed TL = 72 − 52 = ____.","Example: DOM 40 t, TOF 12 t, trip 8 t; MZFM 60 t, MTOM 75 t, MLM 64 t → MZFM + TOF = 72; MTOM 75; MLM + trip = 72 → allowed TOM 72 t → allowed TL = 72 − 52 = 20 t."],["031.02.03.03:02","Allowed masses & loads: fill in the gaps → Standard masses (EU Air Ops, e.g. ≥ 20 seats): ____ (male 88/female 70; incl. hand baggage), ____, ____ (< 2 y), checked bag ~15 kg (varies by flight type); crew: flight crew 85 kg, cabin crew 75 kg (use the values given in the question/OM). Traffic load = pax × standard mass + baggage + cargo + mail.","Standard masses (EU Air Ops, e.g. ≥ 20 seats): adults ~84 kg (male 88/female 70; incl. hand baggage), children 35 kg, infants 0 (< 2 y), checked bag ~15 kg (varies by flight type); crew: flight crew 85 kg, cabin crew 75 kg (use the values given in the question/OM). Traffic load = pax × standard mass + baggage + cargo + mail."],["031.06.01.01:01","Cargo handling: fill in the gaps → Types: ____ (ULD containers) and ____ cargo — fast loading/unloading, protection, efficient securing, weight and CG known per ULD; ____ cargo (loose, loaded by hand).","Types: containerised (ULD containers) and palletised cargo — fast loading/unloading, protection, efficient securing, weight and CG known per ULD; bulk cargo (loose, loaded by hand)."],["031.06.02.01:01","Cargo handling: fill in the gaps → ____: required ____. Example: 600 kg on max 400 kg/m² → ____ (use spreader/shoring). Max container mass = area × max floor load.","Floor load: required contact area = mass ÷ max floor load. Example: 600 kg on max 400 kg/m² → 1.5 m² (use spreader/shoring). Max container mass = area × max floor load."],["031.02.02.03:02","Cargo handling: fill in the gaps → ____: load per metre of length: ____ the fuselage ≤ max running load. Example: 1 200 kg container 1.5 m long → ____.","Running load: load per metre of length: mass ÷ length along the fuselage ≤ max running load. Example: 1 200 kg container 1.5 m long → 800 kg/m."],["031.06.03.01:02","Cargo handling: fill in the gaps → ____: prevent shifting in turbulence, accelerations, emergency landing (CG shift, structural damage, injury). Methods: ____ or tied to tie-down points with straps; ____ restrained by nets attached to attachment/tie-down points; dividers.","Securing: prevent shifting in turbulence, accelerations, emergency landing (CG shift, structural damage, injury). Methods: ULDs locked by latches on roller tracks or tied to tie-down points with straps; bulk cargo restrained by nets attached to attachment/tie-down points; dividers."],["031.01.01.02:02","EASA LO 031.01.01.02.02 → Describe why aircraft mass must be limited to ensure adequate aircraft performance.","Performance: higher mass → longer take-off and landing distances, higher stall/take-off/landing speeds, lower climb rate & gradient, lower ceiling, higher fuel burn, reduced range/endurance → mass limited for adequate performance. / Regulated (max) TOM = lowest of structural MTOM and performance-limited TOM; regulated LM similarly."],["031.02.01.02:03","EASA LO 031.02.01.02.03 → Calculate the mass of particular components from other given components.","Take-off mass (TOM) = mass at start of take-off run (including everything). Landing mass (LM) = TOM − trip fuel. Ramp/taxi mass = TOM + taxi fuel. In-flight (gross) mass. Zero fuel mass (ZFM) = DOM + traffic load (no usable fuel)."],["031.02.01.02:04","EASA LO 031.02.01.02.04 → Convert fuel mass, fuel volume and fuel density given in different units used in aviation.","Take-off mass (TOM) = mass at start of take-off run (including everything). Landing mass (LM) = TOM − trip fuel. Ramp/taxi mass = TOM + taxi fuel. In-flight (gross) mass. Zero fuel mass (ZFM) = DOM + traffic load (no usable fuel)."],["031.02.02.01:01","EASA LO 031.02.02.01.01 → Define the maximum zero fuel mass.","Take-off mass (TOM) = mass at start of take-off run (including everything). Landing mass (LM) = TOM − trip fuel. Ramp/taxi mass = TOM + taxi fuel. In-flight (gross) mass. Zero fuel mass (ZFM) = DOM + traffic load (no usable fuel). / Structural: MZFM (max zero-fuel mass — limits wing-root bending; fuel in wings relieves it), max ramp/taxi mass (ground manoeuvring, gear), MTOM, max in-flight mass with external load (helicopters), MLM (landing loads on gear/structure)."],["031.02.02.01:03","EASA LO 031.02.02.01.03 → Define the maximum take-off mass.","Take-off mass (TOM) = mass at start of take-off run (including everything). Landing mass (LM) = TOM − trip fuel. Ramp/taxi mass = TOM + taxi fuel. In-flight (gross) mass. Zero fuel mass (ZFM) = DOM + traffic load (no usable fuel). / Performance: higher mass → longer take-off and landing distances, higher stall/take-off/landing speeds, lower climb rate & gradient, lower ceiling, higher fuel burn, reduced range/endurance → mass limited for adequate performance."],["031.02.02.01:05","EASA LO 031.02.02.01.05 → Define the maximum landing mass.","Structural: MZFM (max zero-fuel mass — limits wing-root bending; fuel in wings relieves it), max ramp/taxi mass (ground manoeuvring, gear), MTOM, max in-flight mass with external load (helicopters), MLM (landing loads on gear/structure). / Performance/regulated limits: take-off (field length, climb/WAT, obstacle, tyre speed, brake energy), en-route, landing (field length, approach/landing climb) → performance-limited TOM/LM may be lower than structural."],["031.02.02.03:01","EASA LO 031.02.02.03.01 → Describe the maximum floor load (maximum load per unit of area).","Cargo compartment limits: maximum floor load (per unit area, kg/m²) and maximum running load (per unit length of fuselage, kg/m); also compartment max mass, cumulative loads. / Floor load: required contact area = mass ÷ max floor load. Example: 600 kg on max 400 kg/m² → 1.5 m² (use spreader/shoring). Max container mass = area × max floor load."],["031.02.03.01:01","EASA LO 031.02.03.01.01 → Calculate the maximum mass for take-off (regulated take-off mass) given mass-and-load components and structural/ performance limits.","Allowed take-off mass = lowest of: MZFM + TOF, regulated TOM, regulated LM + trip fuel. / Performance: higher mass → longer take-off and landing distances, higher stall/take-off/landing speeds, lower climb rate & gradient, lower ceiling, higher fuel burn, reduced range/endurance → mass limited for adequate performance."],["031.02.03.01:03","EASA LO 031.02.03.01.03 → Calculate the allowed mass for take-off.","Allowed take-off mass = lowest of: MZFM + TOF, regulated TOM, regulated LM + trip fuel. / Performance: higher mass → longer take-off and landing distances, higher stall/take-off/landing speeds, lower climb rate & gradient, lower ceiling, higher fuel burn, reduced range/endurance → mass limited for adequate performance."],["031.02.03.02:01","EASA LO 031.02.03.02.01 → Calculate the maximum allowed traffic load and fuel load in order not to exceed the given allowed take-off mass.","Allowed take-off mass = lowest of: MZFM + TOF, regulated TOM, regulated LM + trip fuel. / Take-off mass (TOM) = mass at start of take-off run (including everything). Landing mass (LM) = TOM − trip fuel. Ramp/taxi mass = TOM + taxi fuel. In-flight (gross) mass. Zero fuel mass (ZFM) = DOM + traffic load (no usable fuel)."],["031.02.03.03:01","EASA LO 031.02.03.03.01 → Extract the appropriate standard masses for passengers, baggage and crew from relevant documents or operator requirements.","Standard masses (EU Air Ops, e.g. ≥ 20 seats): adults ~84 kg (male 88/female 70; incl. hand baggage), children 35 kg, infants 0 (< 2 y), checked bag ~15 kg (varies by flight type); crew: flight crew 85 kg, cabin crew 75 kg (use the values given in the question/OM). Traffic load = pax × standard mass + baggage + cargo + mail."],["031.06.02.01:02","EASA LO 031.06.02.01.02 → Calculate the maximum mass of a container with given floor-contact area to avoid exceeding the maximum permissible floor load of a cargo compartment.","Floor load: required contact area = mass ÷ max floor load. Example: 600 kg on max 400 kg/m² → 1.5 m² (use spreader/shoring). Max container mass = area × max floor load. / Cargo compartment limits: maximum floor load (per unit area, kg/m²) and maximum running load (per unit length of fuselage, kg/m); also compartment max mass, cumulative loads."],["031.06.02.01:03","EASA LO 031.06.02.01.03 → Calculate the linear load distribution of a container to avoid exceeding the maximum permissible running load.","Cargo compartment limits: maximum floor load (per unit area, kg/m²) and maximum running load (per unit length of fuselage, kg/m); also compartment max mass, cumulative loads. / Floor load: required contact area = mass ÷ max floor load. Example: 600 kg on max 400 kg/m² → 1.5 m² (use spreader/shoring). Max container mass = area × max floor load."]],"10":[["061.01.05.01:01","Speeds: fill in the gaps → ____: computer (set pressure altitude against OAT in the airspeed window, read TAS on outer scale opposite CAS) or ____: CAS 150 kt at 10 000 ft → ≈ 150 × 1.2 = ____.","TAS from CAS: computer (set pressure altitude against OAT in the airspeed window, read TAS on outer scale opposite CAS) or rule of thumb +2 % per 1 000 ft: CAS 150 kt at 10 000 ft → ≈ 150 × 1.2 = 180 kt."],["061.01.05.02:01","Speeds: fill in the gaps → ____: M = TAS / LSS, ____ (≈ 661 kt at ISA MSL, ≈ 573 kt at −56.5 °C). Example: M 0.80 at −50 °C (223 K): LSS = 38.95 × 14.93 = 581.6 kt → ____.","Mach number: M = TAS / LSS, LSS (kt) = 38.95 √T(K) (≈ 661 kt at ISA MSL, ≈ 573 kt at −56.5 °C). Example: M 0.80 at −50 °C (223 K): LSS = 38.95 × 14.93 = 581.6 kt → TAS 465 kt."],["061.01.05.03:02","Speeds: fill in the gaps → ____: TAS ↑ and M ↑ (air thinner, colder). ____ (above crossover): TAS ↓ (colder → lower LSS) in the troposphere, ____; constant TAS above the tropopause (isothermal). Descent at constant M → CAS ↑ (until reaching the target CAS, then constant CAS → M ↓, TAS ↓). Cruise at constant CAS: higher altitude → higher TAS.","Climb at constant CAS: TAS ↑ and M ↑ (air thinner, colder). Climb at constant M (above crossover): TAS ↓ (colder → lower LSS) in the troposphere, CAS ↓; constant TAS above the tropopause (isothermal). Descent at constant M → CAS ↑ (until reaching the target CAS, then constant CAS → M ↓, TAS ↓). Cruise at constant CAS: higher altitude → higher TAS."],["061.01.05.04:01","Ground speed: fill in the gaps → ____ ____; MDR: 30° → 0.9, 45° → 0.7, 60° → 0.5 (cosine), 90° → 0. ____. With a large WCA, GS is further reduced (TAS component along track = TAS × cos WCA).","HWC/TWC = wind speed × cos(angle between wind and track); MDR: 30° → 0.9, 45° → 0.7, 60° → 0.5 (cosine), 90° → 0. GS ≈ TAS − HWC (+ TWC). With a large WCA, GS is further reduced (TAS component along track = TAS × cos WCA)."],["061.01.05.04:07","Ground speed: fill in the gaps → GS by computer (TOV) or MDR. ____; ____ ____ = remaining distance ÷ remaining time; average GS between two fixes = distance ÷ time.","GS by computer (TOV) or MDR. Time = distance/GS; revised GS to reach a waypoint on time = remaining distance ÷ remaining time; average GS between two fixes = distance ÷ time."],["061.01.05.05:01","Ground speed: fill in the gaps → ____: enter revised headings, GS, leg times, ETAs, fuel after each fix.","Flight log: enter revised headings, GS, leg times, ETAs, fuel after each fix."],["061.01.05.06:01","Gradients & rate of climb/descent: fill in the gaps → Rules of thumb (1 NM ≈ 6 000 ft): ____ ____; ____ distance (NM); exact: arctan (height/distance in ft). Example: 3 000 ft over 10 NM → 3° / 5 %.","Rules of thumb (1 NM ≈ 6 000 ft): gradient (°) ≈ height (ft)/100 ÷ distance (NM); gradient (%) ≈ height (ft)/60 ÷ distance (NM); exact: arctan (height/distance in ft). Example: 3 000 ft over 10 NM → 3° / 5 %."],["061.01.05.06:02","Gradients & rate of climb/descent: fill in the gaps → ____; ROD = GP % × GS (kt); ____. Example: 3°, GS 120 kt (2 NM/min) → ____; 3° ≈ 318 ft/NM → 120 × 318/60 ≈ 636 ft/min.","ROD (ft/min) = GP° × GS (NM/min) × 100; ROD = GP % × GS (kt); VS (ft/min) = GS (kt) × gradient (ft/NM) ÷ 60. Example: 3°, GS 120 kt (2 NM/min) → 600 ft/min; 3° ≈ 318 ft/NM → 120 × 318/60 ≈ 636 ft/min."],["061.01.05.06:04","Gradients & rate of climb/descent: fill in the gaps → Accurate ____ is essential for terrain clearance.","Accurate position before starting descent is essential for terrain clearance."],["061.01.06.01:01","Triangle of velocities: what do you remember about this? → Air vector (HDG/TAS, 1 arrow) + wind vector (W/V, 2 arrows) = ground …","Air vector (HDG/TAS, 1 arrow) + wind vector (W/V, 2 arrows) = ground vector (TR/GS, 3 arrows). Solve for heading & GS, W/V, or track & GS on the CRP (wind side) or by MDR (see #53)."],["061.01.07.01:01","Dead reckoning: fill in the gaps → ____ = last known position + track and distance flown (GS × time) with the forecast/assumed wind. A ____ (actual observed position) compared with DR shows the ____ → revise wind, heading, GS, ETA.","DR position = last known position + track and distance flown (GS × time) with the forecast/assumed wind. A fix (actual observed position) compared with DR shows the wind/navigation error → revise wind, heading, GS, ETA."],["061.01.07.01:04","Dead reckoning: fill in the gaps → ____ (NM per minute). ____ SF. Example: TAS 180 (SF 3), XWC 21 kt → WCA ____.","Speed factor SF = TAS ÷ 60 (NM per minute). WCA = XWC ÷ SF. Example: TAS 180 (SF 3), XWC 21 kt → WCA 7°."],["061.01.08.01:01","Climb & descent navigation: fill in the gaps → ____: at ____ altitude (climb from the ground); for descent: at ____ descent altitude. Average GS = average TAS ± average wind component along track.","Average TAS & wind for climb: at 2/3 of the cruising altitude (climb from the ground); for descent: at 1/2 of the descent altitude. Average GS = average TAS ± average wind component along track."],["061.01.08.03:01","Climb & descent navigation: fill in the gaps → Most AFMs/POHs provide ____ for time, fuel and distance to climb/descend.","Most AFMs/POHs provide graphs/tables for time, fuel and distance to climb/descend."],["061.01.05.06:02","Climb & descent navigation: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (= GS × 5); ROD = SF × ____. Example: GS 140 → 700 ft/min.","3° glide path: ROD = GS × 10 / 2 (= GS × 5); ROD = SF × GP° × 100. Example: GS 140 → 700 ft/min."],["061.01.05.06:02","Climb & descent navigation: fill in the gaps → ____: ROD = height to lose ÷ time available (time = distance ÷ GS). Example: descend 12 000 ft in 40 NM at GS 300 kt (8 min) → ____.","Required rate: ROD = height to lose ÷ time available (time = distance ÷ GS). Example: descend 12 000 ft in 40 NM at GS 300 kt (8 min) → 1 500 ft/min."],["061.01.05.06:02","Climb & descent navigation: fill in the gaps → ____: time = height ÷ ROD; distance = GS × time. Example: lose 9 000 ft at 1 500 ft/min = 6 min at GS 240 kt (4 NM/min) → ____.","Distance required: time = height ÷ ROD; distance = GS × time. Example: lose 9 000 ft at 1 500 ft/min = 6 min at GS 240 kt (4 NM/min) → 24 NM."],["061.01.08.03:02","Climb & descent navigation: fill in the gaps → ____: time to climb = height ÷ ROC; distance = average GS × time.","Climb: time to climb = height ÷ ROC; distance = average GS × time."],["061.01.05.06:02","Exam trap (Climb & descent navigation): complete the fact → ROD on ____° = GS × ____","ROD on 3° = GS × 5."],["061.01.05.03:01","EASA LO 061.01.05.03.01 → Deduce the CAS, TAS and M relationship in climb/descent/cruise (flying at constant CAS or M).","Climb at constant CAS: TAS ↑ and M ↑ (air thinner, colder). Climb at constant M (above crossover): TAS ↓ (colder → lower LSS) in the troposphere, CAS ↓; constant TAS above the tropopause (isothermal). Descent at constant M → CAS ↑ (until reaching the target CAS, then constant CAS → M ↓, TAS ↓). Cruise at constant CAS: higher altitude → higher TAS. / TAS from CAS: computer (set pressure altitude against OAT in the airspeed window, read TAS on outer scale opposite CAS) or rule of thumb +2 % per 1 000 ft: CAS 150 kt at 10 000 ft → ≈ 150 × 1.2 = 180 kt."],["061.01.05.04:02","EASA LO 061.01.05.04.02 → Apply HWC and TWC to determine GS from TAS and vice versa.","HWC/TWC = wind speed × cos(angle between wind and track); MDR: 30° → 0.9, 45° → 0.7, 60° → 0.5 (cosine), 90° → 0. GS ≈ TAS − HWC (+ TWC). With a large WCA, GS is further reduced (TAS component along track = TAS × cos WCA)."],["061.01.05.04:03","EASA LO 061.01.05.04.03 → Explain the relationship between GS and TAS with increasing WCA.","HWC/TWC = wind speed × cos(angle between wind and track); MDR: 30° → 0.9, 45° → 0.7, 60° → 0.5 (cosine), 90° → 0. GS ≈ TAS − HWC (+ TWC). With a large WCA, GS is further reduced (TAS component along track = TAS × cos WCA). / GS by computer (TOV) or MDR. Time = distance/GS; revised GS to reach a waypoint on time = remaining distance ÷ remaining time; average GS between two fixes = distance ÷ time."],["061.01.05.04:04","EASA LO 061.01.05.04.04 → Calculate GS with: − mechanical computer (TOV solution); and − MDR (given track, TAS and WV).","GS by computer (TOV) or MDR. Time = distance/GS; revised GS to reach a waypoint on time = remaining distance ÷ remaining time; average GS between two fixes = distance ÷ time. / Average TAS & wind for climb: at 2/3 of the cruising altitude (climb from the ground); for descent: at 1/2 of the descent altitude. Average GS = average TAS ± average wind component along track."],["061.01.05.04:05","EASA LO 061.01.05.04.05 → Perform GS, distance and time calculations.","Climb: time to climb = height ÷ ROC; distance = average GS × time. / GS by computer (TOV) or MDR. Time = distance/GS; revised GS to reach a waypoint on time = remaining distance ÷ remaining time; average GS between two fixes = distance ÷ time."],["061.01.05.04:06","EASA LO 061.01.05.04.06 → Calculate revised GS to reach a waypoint at a specific time.","GS by computer (TOV) or MDR. Time = distance/GS; revised GS to reach a waypoint on time = remaining distance ÷ remaining time; average GS between two fixes = distance ÷ time. / Climb: time to climb = height ÷ ROC; distance = average GS × time."],["061.01.05.06:03","EASA LO 061.01.05.06.03 → Calculate climb/descent gradient (ft/NM, % and degrees), GS or vertical speed according to the following formula: — Vertical speed (ft/min) = (GS (kt) × gradient (ft/NM)) / 60.","ROD (ft/min) = GP° × GS (NM/min) × 100; ROD = GP % × GS (kt); VS (ft/min) = GS (kt) × gradient (ft/NM) ÷ 60. Example: 3°, GS 120 kt (2 NM/min) → 600 ft/min; 3° ≈ 318 ft/NM → 120 × 318/60 ≈ 636 ft/min. / Rules of thumb (1 NM ≈ 6 000 ft): gradient (°) ≈ height (ft)/100 ÷ distance (NM); gradient (%) ≈ height (ft)/60 ÷ distance (NM); exact: arctan (height/distance in ft). Example: 3 000 ft over 10 NM → 3° / 5 %."],["061.01.06.02:01","EASA LO 061.01.06.02.01 → Resolve the TOV for: — heading and GS (with mechanical computer and MDR); — WV (with mechanical computer); and — track and GS (with mechanical computer and MDR.","GS by computer (TOV) or MDR. Time = distance/GS; revised GS to reach a waypoint on time = remaining distance ÷ remaining time; average GS between two fixes = distance ÷ time."],["061.01.07.01:02","EASA LO 061.01.07.01.02 → Evaluate the difference between a DR and a fix position.","DR position = last known position + track and distance flown (GS × time) with the forecast/assumed wind. A fix (actual observed position) compared with DR shows the wind/navigation error → revise wind, heading, GS, ETA."],["061.01.07.01:03","EASA LO 061.01.07.01.03 → Define ‘speed factor’ (SF). Speed divided by 60, used for mental flight-path calculations.","Speed factor SF = TAS ÷ 60 (NM per minute). WCA = XWC ÷ SF. Example: TAS 180 (SF 3), XWC 21 kt → WCA 7°."],["061.01.08.01:02","EASA LO 061.01.08.01.02 → Average TAS used for descent problems is calculated at the altitude 1/2 of the descent altitude.","Average TAS & wind for climb: at 2/3 of the cruising altitude (climb from the ground); for descent: at 1/2 of the descent altitude. Average GS = average TAS ± average wind component along track. / Climb: time to climb = height ÷ ROC; distance = average GS × time."],["061.01.08.02:01","EASA LO 061.01.08.02.01 → WV used for climb problems is the WV at the altitude 2/3 of the cruising altitude.","Average TAS & wind for climb: at 2/3 of the cruising altitude (climb from the ground); for descent: at 1/2 of the descent altitude. Average GS = average TAS ± average wind component along track."],["061.01.08.02:02","EASA LO 061.01.08.02.02 → WV used for descent problems is the WV at the altitude 1/2 of the descent altitude.","Average TAS & wind for climb: at 2/3 of the cruising altitude (climb from the ground); for descent: at 1/2 of the descent altitude. Average GS = average TAS ± average wind component along track."],["061.01.08.02:03","EASA LO 061.01.08.02.03 → Calculate the average climb/descent GS from given TAS at various altitudes, and WV at various altitudes and true track.","Average TAS & wind for climb: at 2/3 of the cruising altitude (climb from the ground); for descent: at 1/2 of the descent altitude. Average GS = average TAS ± average wind component along track."],["061.01.08.03:03","EASA LO 061.01.08.03.03 → Given distance, speed and present altitude, calculate the rate of climb/descent in order to reach a certain position at a given altitude.","Average TAS & wind for climb: at 2/3 of the cruising altitude (climb from the ground); for descent: at 1/2 of the descent altitude. Average GS = average TAS ± average wind component along track. / Climb: time to climb = height ÷ ROC; distance = average GS × time."],["061.01.08.03:04","EASA LO 061.01.08.03.04 → Given speed, rate of climb/descent and altitude, calculate the distance required in order to reach a certain position at a given altitude.","Average TAS & wind for climb: at 2/3 of the cruising altitude (climb from the ground); for descent: at 1/2 of the descent altitude. Average GS = average TAS ± average wind component along track. / Climb: time to climb = height ÷ ROC; distance = average GS × time."],["061.01.08.03:05","EASA LO 061.01.08.03.05 → Given speed, distance to go and altitude to climb/descent, calculate the rate of climb/descent.","Average TAS & wind for climb: at 2/3 of the cruising altitude (climb from the ground); for descent: at 1/2 of the descent altitude. Average GS = average TAS ± average wind component along track. / Climb: time to climb = height ÷ ROC; distance = average GS × time."]],"12":[["062.02.01.02:02","VHF direction finding (VDF): fill in the gaps → ____: the ground station measures the ____ ____ (pilot transmits on the frequency) and gives the pilot a bearing — QDM (magnetic bearing to the station) or QDR (magnetic bearing from the station), QTE (true from), QUJ (true to). Used for homing, lost aircraft, emergency (121.5). Needs only a VHF radio in the aircraft.","Ground DF: the ground station measures the direction of the aircraft's VHF transmission (pilot transmits on the frequency) and gives the pilot a bearing — QDM (magnetic bearing to the station) or QDR (magnetic bearing from the station), QTE (true from), QUJ (true to). Used for homing, lost aircraft, emergency (121.5). Needs only a VHF radio in the aircraft."],["062.02.01.03:01","VHF direction finding (VDF): fill in the gaps → Range limited by line of sight (VHF space waves): range (NM) ____tx ____rx (heights in ft). Example: station 100 ft, aircraft 10 000 ft → 12.3 + 123 = ____.","Range limited by line of sight (VHF space waves): range (NM) = 1.23 √htx + 1.23 √hrx (heights in ft). Example: station 100 ft, aircraft 10 000 ft → 12.3 + 123 = 135 NM."],["062.02.01.02:03","VHF direction finding (VDF): fill in the gaps → ____ (triangulation) → ____ passed to the pilot.","Several ground stations (triangulation) → position fix passed to the pilot."],["062.02.01.04:02","VHF direction finding (VDF): fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (two aircraft transmitting at the same time → wrong/averaged bearing), ____ (reflections from terrain/buildings → bearing errors), site errors, propagation.","Errors: synchronous transmissions (two aircraft transmitting at the same time → wrong/averaged bearing), multipath signals (reflections from terrain/buildings → bearing errors), site errors, propagation."],["062.02.01.04:03","VHF direction finding (VDF): fill in the gaps → VDF bearing classes (Annex 10): ____, ____, ____, D worse than class C.","VDF bearing classes (Annex 10): A ±2°, B ±5°, C ±10°, D worse than class C."],["062.02.02.01:01","NDB/ADF principles: fill in the gaps → ____ (non-directional radio beacon) = ____ part; ____ (automatic direction finder) = airborne receiver measuring the bearing of the NDB relative to the aircraft's nose (relative bearing).","NDB (non-directional radio beacon) = ground part; ADF (automatic direction finder) = airborne receiver measuring the bearing of the NDB relative to the aircraft's nose (relative bearing)."],["062.02.02.01:07","NDB/ADF principles: fill in the gaps → NDB operates in ____, aeronautical band ____. Locator beacon = low-power LF/MF NDB used for final approach (often co-located with OM/MM), range ____. Commercial broadcast stations in the band can also be used (not for approaches, no monitoring).","NDB operates in LF/MF, aeronautical band 190–1 750 kHz. Locator beacon = low-power LF/MF NDB used for final approach (often co-located with OM/MM), range 10–25 NM. Commercial broadcast stations in the band can also be used (not for approaches, no monitoring)."],["062.02.02.01:09","NDB/ADF principles: fill in the gaps → NDB has ____ ____ (Annex 10), but the aircraft gets ____ — the ADF needle just points somewhere (monitor ident!).","NDB has automatic ground monitoring (Annex 10), but the aircraft gets no failure flag — the ADF needle just points somewhere (monitor ident!)."],["062.02.02.01:10","NDB/ADF principles: what do you remember about this? → Navigation use: homing, tracking, holding, approaches, en-route …","Navigation use: homing, tracking, holding, approaches, en-route position lines."],["062.02.02.01:16","NDB/ADF principles: fill in the gaps → ____: 2–3 letter Morse; emission ____ (keyed carrier → needs BFO — beat frequency oscillator — which mixes a local frequency to make an audible tone; on modern ADFs activated automatically) or ____ (modulated tone, audible without BFO).","Identification: 2–3 letter Morse; emission N0N/A1A (keyed carrier → needs BFO — beat frequency oscillator — which mixes a local frequency to make an audible tone; on modern ADFs activated automatically) or N0N/A2A (modulated tone, audible without BFO)."],["062.02.02.01:12","NDB/ADF principles: fill in the gaps → ____: above the NDB the needle swings/becomes unreliable (vertical signal) — station passage = needle swings to the tail.","Cone of confusion: above the NDB the needle swings/becomes unreliable (vertical signal) — station passage = needle swings to the tail."],["062.02.02.02:02","Presentation & ADF procedures: fill in the gaps → Indicators: ____, ____ (card slaved to compass → needle head reads ____, tail QDR directly), fixed-card ADF (RBI) — shows relative bearing (0 at nose), moving-card ADF (card set to heading manually → needle reads QDM).","Indicators: electronic (ND), RMI (card slaved to compass → needle head reads QDM, tail QDR directly), fixed-card ADF (RBI) — shows relative bearing (0 at nose), moving-card ADF (card set to heading manually → needle reads QDM)."],["062.02.01.02:01","Presentation & ADF procedures: fill in the gaps → ____; ____; ____ station = QDM + var (at aircraft, E+); from compass: MH = CH + dev.","QDM = MH + RB; QDR = QDM ± 180; true bearing to station = QDM + var (at aircraft, E+); from compass: MH = CH + dev."],["062.02.02.02:01","Presentation & ADF procedures: what do you remember about this? → Example: CH 095°, dev 2°E → MH 097°; RB 250° → QDM = 347°; var 5°W → …","Example: CH 095°, dev 2°E → MH 097°; RB 250° → QDM = 347°; var 5°W → true bearing to NDB 342°, QTE 162°."],["062.02.02.02:06","Presentation & ADF procedures: fill in the gaps → ____: keep needle on the nose → curved path downwind (drift). ____: apply WCA into the wind so the needle is offset by the drift (needle points to the side ____ correction, e.g. inbound with drift right, heading left of QDM, needle right of nose).","Homing: keep needle on the nose → curved path downwind (drift). Tracking: apply WCA into the wind so the needle is offset by the drift (needle points to the side opposite to the wind correction, e.g. inbound with drift right, heading left of QDM, needle right of nose)."],["062.02.02.02:06","Presentation & ADF procedures: fill in the gaps → ____: inbound QDM — turn to a heading giving an intercept angle (e.g. 30–60°) towards the desired QDM; when RB = intercept angle (needle reaches the \"lead\"), turn onto QDM. Outbound QDR — similar with the tail. ____ QDM/QDR: turn to new intercept heading, watch the needle/RMI. ____ passage: needle swings from ahead to behind; abeam: RB = 090° or 270°.","Intercepting: inbound QDM — turn to a heading giving an intercept angle (e.g. 30–60°) towards the desired QDM; when RB = intercept angle (needle reaches the \"lead\"), turn onto QDM. Outbound QDR — similar with the tail. Changing QDM/QDR: turn to new intercept heading, watch the needle/RMI. Station passage: needle swings from ahead to behind; abeam: RB = 090° or 270°."],["062.02.02.03:03","Coverage & range: fill in the gaps → Range limited by ____: range ∝ √power (double range needs ____). Propagation: ____ by day (long range over sea); at night sky waves from the ionosphere add → fading and errors.","Range limited by transmitter power: range ∝ √power (double range needs 4× power). Propagation: ground (surface) waves by day (long range over sea); at night sky waves from the ionosphere add → fading and errors."],["062.02.02.03:05","Coverage & range: fill in the gaps → Established on an NDB track (Doc 8168) within ____.","Established on an NDB track (Doc 8168) within ±5°."],["062.02.02.03:06","Coverage & range: fill in the gaps → ____ for NDB failure → identify continuously.","No warning flag for NDB failure → identify continuously."],["062.02.02.04:01","Errors: fill in the gaps → ____: wave crossing the coastline at an oblique angle ____ and bends → bearing error; minimise by using NDBs near the coast and signals crossing the coast at ~90°, fly higher.","Coastal refraction: wave crossing the coastline at an oblique angle speeds up over water and bends → bearing error; minimise by using NDBs near the coast and signals crossing the coast at ~90°, fly higher."],["062.02.02.04:02","Errors: fill in the gaps → ____: sky waves and ground waves arrive with different ____ → fluctuating needle, bearing errors (strongest around dusk/dawn); also ____ other NDBs on the same frequency via sky waves at night.","Night (twilight) effect: sky waves and ground waves arrive with different phase and polarisation → fluctuating needle, bearing errors (strongest around dusk/dawn); also interference from other NDBs on the same frequency via sky waves at night."],["062.02.02.05:01","Errors: fill in the gaps → ____: reflection/____ of the waves in mountainous terrain → errors. ____: static from Cb attracts the needle towards the storm. Dip (bank) error: in a banked turn the loop antenna is tilted → bearing error (read bearings wings level). (Quadrantal error from airframe reflections.)","Mountain effect: reflection/diffraction of the waves in mountainous terrain → errors. Thunderstorm effect: static from Cb attracts the needle towards the storm. Dip (bank) error: in a banked turn the loop antenna is tilted → bearing error (read bearings wings level). (Quadrantal error from airframe reflections.)"],["062.02.02.01:06","Exam trap (Errors): complete the fact → NDB band ____kHz.","NDB band 190–1 750 kHz."],["062.02.02.03:05","Exam trap (Errors): complete the fact → Established within ±____°.","Established within ±5°."],["062.02.01.01:01","EASA LO 062.02.01.01.01 → Describe the use of a ground DF.","Navigation use: homing, tracking, holding, approaches, en-route position lines. / Ground DF: the ground station measures the direction of the aircraft's VHF transmission (pilot transmits on the frequency) and gives the pilot a bearing — QDM (magnetic bearing to the station) or QDR (magnetic bearing from the station), QTE (true from), QUJ (true to). Used for homing, lost aircraft, emergency (121.5). Needs only a VHF radio in the aircraft."],["062.02.01.04:01","EASA LO 062.02.01.04.01 → Explain why synchronous transmissions will cause errors.","Errors: synchronous transmissions (two aircraft transmitting at the same time → wrong/averaged bearing), multipath signals (reflections from terrain/buildings → bearing errors), site errors, propagation."],["062.02.02.01:02","EASA LO 062.02.02.01.02 → Define the acronym ‘ADF’: automatic direction-finding equipment.","NDB (non-directional radio beacon) = ground part; ADF (automatic direction finder) = airborne receiver measuring the bearing of the NDB relative to the aircraft's nose (relative bearing)."],["062.02.02.01:03","EASA LO 062.02.02.01.03 → State that the NDB is the ground part of the system.","NDB (non-directional radio beacon) = ground part; ADF (automatic direction finder) = airborne receiver measuring the bearing of the NDB relative to the aircraft's nose (relative bearing)."],["062.02.02.01:04","EASA LO 062.02.02.01.04 → State that the ADF is the airborne part of the system.","NDB (non-directional radio beacon) = ground part; ADF (automatic direction finder) = airborne receiver measuring the bearing of the NDB relative to the aircraft's nose (relative bearing)."],["062.02.02.01:05","EASA LO 062.02.02.01.05 → State that the NDB operates in the LF and MF frequency bands.","NDB operates in LF/MF, aeronautical band 190–1 750 kHz. Locator beacon = low-power LF/MF NDB used for final approach (often co-located with OM/MM), range 10–25 NM. Commercial broadcast stations in the band can also be used (not for approaches, no monitoring)."],["062.02.02.01:08","EASA LO 062.02.02.01.08 → State that certain commercial radio stations transmit within the frequency band of the NDB.","NDB operates in LF/MF, aeronautical band 190–1 750 kHz. Locator beacon = low-power LF/MF NDB used for final approach (often co-located with OM/MM), range 10–25 NM. Commercial broadcast stations in the band can also be used (not for approaches, no monitoring). / NDB band 190–1 750 kHz."],["062.02.02.01:13","EASA LO 062.02.02.01.13 → State that an NDB station emits a N0N/A1A or a N0N/A2A signal.","Identification: 2–3 letter Morse; emission N0N/A1A (keyed carrier → needs BFO — beat frequency oscillator — which mixes a local frequency to make an audible tone; on modern ADFs activated automatically) or N0N/A2A (modulated tone, audible without BFO). / Cone of confusion: above the NDB the needle swings/becomes unreliable (vertical signal) — station passage = needle swings to the tail."],["062.02.02.01:14","EASA LO 062.02.02.01.14 → State the function of the beat frequency oscillator (BFO).","Identification: 2–3 letter Morse; emission N0N/A1A (keyed carrier → needs BFO — beat frequency oscillator — which mixes a local frequency to make an audible tone; on modern ADFs activated automatically) or N0N/A2A (modulated tone, audible without BFO)."],["062.02.02.01:15","EASA LO 062.02.02.01.15 → State that in order to identify a N0N/A1A NDB, the BFO circuit of the receiver has to be activated.","Identification: 2–3 letter Morse; emission N0N/A1A (keyed carrier → needs BFO — beat frequency oscillator — which mixes a local frequency to make an audible tone; on modern ADFs activated automatically) or N0N/A2A (modulated tone, audible without BFO)."],["062.02.02.02:01","EASA LO 062.02.02.02.01 → Name the types of indicators commonly in use: − electronic display; − radio magnetic indicator (RMI); − fixed-card ADF (radio compass);","Indicators: electronic (ND), RMI (card slaved to compass → needle head reads QDM, tail QDR directly), fixed-card ADF (RBI) — shows relative bearing (0 at nose), moving-card ADF (card set to heading manually → needle reads QDM)."],["062.02.02.02:04","EASA LO 062.02.02.02.04 → Calculate the true bearing from the compass heading and relative bearing.","Indicators: electronic (ND), RMI (card slaved to compass → needle head reads QDM, tail QDR directly), fixed-card ADF (RBI) — shows relative bearing (0 at nose), moving-card ADF (card set to heading manually → needle reads QDM). / NDB (non-directional radio beacon) = ground part; ADF (automatic direction finder) = airborne receiver measuring the bearing of the NDB relative to the aircraft's nose (relative bearing)."],["062.02.02.02:05","EASA LO 062.02.02.02.05 → Convert the compass bearing into magnetic bearing and true bearing.","Ground DF: the ground station measures the direction of the aircraft's VHF transmission (pilot transmits on the frequency) and gives the pilot a bearing — QDM (magnetic bearing to the station) or QDR (magnetic bearing from the station), QTE (true from), QUJ (true to). Used for homing, lost aircraft, emergency (121.5). Needs only a VHF radio in the aircraft. / QDM = MH + RB; QDR = QDM ± 180; true bearing to station = QDM + var (at aircraft, E+); from compass: MH = CH + dev."],["062.02.02.03:01","EASA LO 062.02.02.03.01 → State that the power of the transmitter limits the range of an NDB.","Range limited by transmitter power: range ∝ √power (double range needs 4× power). Propagation: ground (surface) waves by day (long range over sea); at night sky waves from the ionosphere add → fading and errors. / No warning flag for NDB failure → identify continuously."],["062.02.02.03:02","EASA LO 062.02.02.03.02 → Explain the relationship between power and range.","Range limited by transmitter power: range ∝ √power (double range needs 4× power). Propagation: ground (surface) waves by day (long range over sea); at night sky waves from the ionosphere add → fading and errors."],["062.02.02.03:04","EASA LO 062.02.02.03.04 → Explain that the interference between sky waves and ground waves leads to ‘fading’.","Range limited by transmitter power: range ∝ √power (double range needs 4× power). Propagation: ground (surface) waves by day (long range over sea); at night sky waves from the ionosphere add → fading and errors. / Night (twilight) effect: sky waves and ground waves arrive with different phase and polarisation → fluctuating needle, bearing errors (strongest around dusk/dawn); also interference from other NDBs on the same frequency via sky waves at night."],["062.02.02.04:03","EASA LO 062.02.02.04.03 → State that interference from other NDB stations on the same frequency may occur at night due to sky-wave contamination.","Night (twilight) effect: sky waves and ground waves arrive with different phase and polarisation → fluctuating needle, bearing errors (strongest around dusk/dawn); also interference from other NDBs on the same frequency via sky waves at night."],["062.02.02.05:03","EASA LO 062.02.02.05.03 → Explain that the bank angle of the aircraft causes a dip error.","Mountain effect: reflection/diffraction of the waves in mountainous terrain → errors. Thunderstorm effect: static from Cb attracts the needle towards the storm. Dip (bank) error: in a banked turn the loop antenna is tilted → bearing error (read bearings wings level). (Quadrantal error from airframe reflections.)"]],"13":[["062.02.03.01:01","VOR principles: fill in the gaps → The VOR transmits a ____ signal (same phase in all directions) and a ____ signal whose phase ____ with azimuth. The receiver measures the phase difference = magnetic bearing from the station (radial); at magnetic north both are in phase (0°).","The VOR transmits a reference phase signal (same phase in all directions) and a variable phase signal whose phase changes with azimuth. The receiver measures the phase difference = magnetic bearing from the station (radial); at magnetic north both are in phase (0°)."],["062.02.03.01:02","VOR principles: fill in the gaps → ____: VHF ____; in ____ only even first decimals are VOR (odd = ILS LOC).","Band: VHF 108.0–117.975 MHz; in 108.0–111.975 only even first decimals are VOR (odd = ILS LOC)."],["062.02.03.01:04","VOR principles: fill in the gaps → Types: ____ (conventional: 30 Hz AM reference... rotating/variable pattern; susceptible to site errors) and ____ (Doppler VOR: circular antenna array with commutated sidebands — Doppler effect; much less affected by site reflections, used where terrain/buildings cause errors). (TVOR = terminal, low power; VOT = test signal.)","Types: CVOR (conventional: 30 Hz AM reference... rotating/variable pattern; susceptible to site errors) and DVOR (Doppler VOR: circular antenna array with commutated sidebands — Doppler effect; much less affected by site reflections, used where terrain/buildings cause errors). (TVOR = terminal, low power; VOT = test signal.)"],["062.02.03.01:05","VOR principles: fill in the gaps → ____ may be transmitted on VOR frequencies (voice channel).","ATIS may be transmitted on VOR frequencies (voice channel)."],["062.02.03.01:06","VOR principles: fill in the gaps → Airborne components: ____.","Airborne components: antenna, receiver, indicator."],["062.02.03.01:09","VOR principles: fill in the gaps → ____: 3-letter ____ (~every 10 s) and sometimes plain voice. ____: if the station exceeds limits (bearing shift, signal loss), the identification and navigation components are removed or radiation stops (so no ident = don't use).","Identification: 3-letter Morse (~every 10 s) and sometimes plain voice. Automatic ground monitoring: if the station exceeds limits (bearing shift, signal loss), the identification and navigation components are removed or radiation stops (so no ident = don't use)."],["062.02.03.02:01","Presentation & VOR procedures: fill in the gaps → ____: needle head shows ____ (bearing to), ____ radial (QDR) you are on.","RMI: needle head shows QDM (bearing to), tail shows the radial (QDR) you are on."],["062.02.03.02:02","Presentation & VOR procedures: fill in the gaps → ____: select a course with the OBS; the needle shows angular displacement from the selected radial/course — full scale typically ____ (5 dots → ____); TO/FROM flag indicates whether the selected course, if followed, leads to or from the station (independent of heading!).","OBI/CDI/HSI: select a course with the OBS; the needle shows angular displacement from the selected radial/course — full scale typically 10° (5 dots → 2°/dot); TO/FROM flag indicates whether the selected course, if followed, leads to or from the station (independent of heading!)."],["062.02.03.02:03","Presentation & VOR procedures: what do you remember about this? → Position: selected 090, FROM, needle right → aircraft is on a radial …","Position: selected 090, FROM, needle right → aircraft is on a radial north of 090 (e.g. 080)… (on an HSI, simply read the picture: aircraft symbol relative to the course line)."],["062.02.03.02:05","Presentation & VOR procedures: fill in the gaps → Procedures: ____ with wind correction (bracket until needle centred with drift applied); ____ a radial inbound/outbound (set course, turn to intercept heading 30–90°, turn onto course as needle comes alive); ____.","Procedures: tracking with wind correction (bracket until needle centred with drift applied); intercepting a radial inbound/outbound (set course, turn to intercept heading 30–90°, turn onto course as needle comes alive); changing radials."],["062.02.03.02:06","Presentation & VOR procedures: fill in the gaps → ____: use the ____ (radials are magnetic at the station).","Radial → true bearing: use the variation at the VOR station (radials are magnetic at the station)."],["062.02.03.04:01","VOR errors: fill in the gaps → Established on a VOR track in approach procedures (Doc 8168): within ____ of the required track. ____: reflections from terrain/buildings bend radials → wrong/fluctuating indications. (Accuracy ±5°, cone of confusion overhead, line-of-sight range 1.23 √h.)","Established on a VOR track in approach procedures (Doc 8168): within half-scale deflection of the required track. Scalloping: reflections from terrain/buildings bend radials → wrong/fluctuating indications. (Accuracy ±5°, cone of confusion overhead, line-of-sight range 1.23 √h.)"],["062.02.04.01:03","DME: fill in the gaps → ____ (962–1 213 MHz). Components: airborne interrogator and ground transponder. The interrogator transmits pulse pairs; the ground transponder replies on a ____ after a fixed ____; the aircraft measures the round-trip time → distance (12.36 µs per NM). Each aircraft recognises its own replies by its random pulse-repetition pattern (jitter).","UHF band (962–1 213 MHz). Components: airborne interrogator and ground transponder. The interrogator transmits pulse pairs; the ground transponder replies on a different frequency (±63 MHz) after a fixed 50 µs delay; the aircraft measures the round-trip time → distance (12.36 µs per NM). Each aircraft recognises its own replies by its random pulse-repetition pattern (jitter)."],["062.02.04.01:05","DME: fill in the gaps → Distance = ____ (line-of-sight distance aircraft–station). Position line = ____ centred on the station.","Distance = slant range (line-of-sight distance aircraft–station). Position line = circle centred on the station."],["062.02.04.02:01","DME: fill in the gaps → ____: VHF VOR/ILS and UHF DME channels are paired → selecting the VOR/ILS frequency automatically tunes the DME. Co-located VOR/DME or ILS/DME: same ident; DME ident is transmitted about ____ (____) — one DME ident for every three/four VOR idents. TACAN (military UHF) provides DME to civil aircraft (VORTAC).","Frequency pairing: VHF VOR/ILS and UHF DME channels are paired → selecting the VOR/ILS frequency automatically tunes the DME. Co-located VOR/DME or ILS/DME: same ident; DME ident is transmitted about every 40 s (higher-tone, 1 350 Hz) — one DME ident for every three/four VOR idents. TACAN (military UHF) provides DME to civil aircraft (VORTAC)."],["062.02.04.01:04","DME: fill in the gaps → ____ = √(slant range² − height²) (height in NM: ft/6 076). Example: DME 10 NM at 36 000 ft (5.9 NM) → ground ≈ ____ NM; overhead the station at FL 360 DME reads ~____. Error negligible when distance > ~ 1 NM per 1 000 ft of height.","Ground distance = √(slant range² − height²) (height in NM: ft/6 076). Example: DME 10 NM at 36 000 ft (5.9 NM) → ground ≈ 8.1 NM; overhead the station at FL 360 DME reads ~6 NM. Error negligible when distance > ~ 1 NM per 1 000 ft of height."],["062.02.04.02:03","DME: fill in the gaps → ____ (Doc 8168): fly at constant DME distance by turning 10– 20° every time the RMI needle moves 10° behind the wingtip (keep needle on wingtip ±10°); lead the turn onto the arc by ~0.5 % of GS.","DME arc (Doc 8168): fly at constant DME distance by turning 10– 20° every time the RMI needle moves 10° behind the wingtip (keep needle on wingtip ±10°); lead the turn onto the arc by ~0.5 % of GS."],["062.02.04.02:04","DME: fill in the gaps → Read-outs: ____ computed from rate of change of distance.","Read-outs: GS and time to station computed from rate of change of distance."],["062.02.04.03:02","DME: fill in the gaps → ____: a ground station can handle about ____ (reply rate limit ~2 700 pulse pairs/s). When overloaded, it replies to the ____ — aircraft with the weakest signals (usually the most distant) are denied first.","Capacity: a ground station can handle about 100 aircraft (reply rate limit ~2 700 pulse pairs/s). When overloaded, it replies to the strongest interrogations — aircraft with the weakest signals (usually the most distant) are denied first."],["062.02.04.05:01","DME: fill in the gaps → ____: it measures only the ____ (rate of change of slant range) → ____ when not flying directly to/from the station, zero on a DME arc, and near/over the station at altitude.","GS read-out errors: it measures only the radial speed (rate of change of slant range) → less than actual GS when not flying directly to/from the station, zero on a DME arc, and near/over the station at altitude."],["062.02.03.01:03","Exam trap (DME): complete the fact → VOR = even first decimal in ____","VOR = even first decimal in 108–112."],["062.02.04.01:04","Exam trap (DME): complete the fact → DME = slant range, UHF, ____µs delay, ~____aircraft (far ones dropped first).","DME = slant range, UHF, 50 µs delay, ~100 aircraft (far ones dropped first)."],["062.02.04.02:01","Exam trap (DME): complete the fact → DME ident every ____, higher tone.","DME ident every 40 s, higher tone."],["062.02.03.01:07","EASA LO 062.02.03.01.07 → Describe the identification of a VOR in terms of Morse-code letters and additional plain text.","Identification: 3-letter Morse (~every 10 s) and sometimes plain voice. Automatic ground monitoring: if the station exceeds limits (bearing shift, signal loss), the identification and navigation components are removed or radiation stops (so no ident = don't use)."],["062.02.03.01:08","EASA LO 062.02.03.01.08 → State that according to ICAO Annex 10, a VOR station has an automatic ground monitoring system.","Identification: 3-letter Morse (~every 10 s) and sometimes plain voice. Automatic ground monitoring: if the station exceeds limits (bearing shift, signal loss), the identification and navigation components are removed or radiation stops (so no ident = don't use)."],["062.02.03.04:02","EASA LO 062.02.03.04.02 → State that due to reflections from terrain, radials can be bent and lead to wrong or fluctuating indications, which is called ‘scalloping’.","Established on a VOR track in approach procedures (Doc 8168): within half-scale deflection of the required track. Scalloping: reflections from terrain/buildings bend radials → wrong/fluctuating indications. (Accuracy ±5°, cone of confusion overhead, line-of-sight range 1.23 √h.)"],["062.02.04.01:02","EASA LO 062.02.04.01.02 → State that the system comprises two basic components: — the aircraft component: the interrogator;","Airborne components: antenna, receiver, indicator."],["062.02.04.01:06","EASA LO 062.02.04.01.06 → State that the pairing of VHF and UHF frequencies (VOR/DME) enables the selection of two items of navigation information from one frequency setting.","Frequency pairing: VHF VOR/ILS and UHF DME channels are paired → selecting the VOR/ILS frequency automatically tunes the DME. Co-located VOR/DME or ILS/DME: same ident; DME ident is transmitted about every 40 s (higher-tone, 1 350 Hz) — one DME ident for every three/four VOR idents. TACAN (military UHF) provides DME to civil aircraft (VORTAC)."],["062.02.04.01:07","EASA LO 062.02.04.01.07 → Describe, in the case of co-location with VOR and ILS, the frequency pairing and identification procedure.","Frequency pairing: VHF VOR/ILS and UHF DME channels are paired → selecting the VOR/ILS frequency automatically tunes the DME. Co-located VOR/DME or ILS/DME: same ident; DME ident is transmitted about every 40 s (higher-tone, 1 350 Hz) — one DME ident for every three/four VOR idents. TACAN (military UHF) provides DME to civil aircraft (VORTAC)."],["062.02.04.01:08","EASA LO 062.02.04.01.08 → State that military UHF tactical air navigation aid (TACAN) stations may be used for DME information.","Frequency pairing: VHF VOR/ILS and UHF DME channels are paired → selecting the VOR/ILS frequency automatically tunes the DME. Co-located VOR/DME or ILS/DME: same ident; DME ident is transmitted about every 40 s (higher-tone, 1 350 Hz) — one DME ident for every three/four VOR idents. TACAN (military UHF) provides DME to civil aircraft (VORTAC)."],["062.02.04.02:02","EASA LO 062.02.04.02.02 → Calculate ground distance from given slant range and altitude.","GS read-out errors: it measures only the radial speed (rate of change of slant range) → less than actual GS when not flying directly to/from the station, zero on a DME arc, and near/over the station at altitude. / Distance = slant range (line-of-sight distance aircraft–station). Position line = circle centred on the station."]],"14":[["061.02.01.01:01","Ground features & visual identification: fill in the gaps → ____ for VFR navigation: ____ ____, shown on the chart and unlikely to be confused: coastlines, large lakes, rivers, motorways, railways, dams, bridges, towns with distinctive shape, airfields, mountain peaks, large masts, power lines (line features for track/position lines, point features for fixes).","Suitable features for VFR navigation: unique, prominent, easily identifiable from the air and from a distance, shown on the chart and unlikely to be confused: coastlines, large lakes, rivers, motorways, railways, dams, bridges, towns with distinctive shape, airfields, mountain peaks, large masts, power lines (line features for track/position lines, point features for fixes)."],["061.02.01.01:01","Ground features & visual identification: what do you remember about this? → Seasonal changes (snow, flooding, vegetation) can alter …","Seasonal changes (snow, flooding, vegetation) can alter appearance."],["061.02.01.02:01","Ground features & visual identification: fill in the gaps → ____: limited field of view and ____ ____, features pass quickly, perspective differs from the chart, obstacles (masts, wires), terrain shielding, workload, radio range; reduced visibility from haze, mist/fog, smoke, precipitation, low cloud, sun glare/low sun, windscreen contamination, dust.","Low-level problems: limited field of view and short sighting distance, features pass quickly, perspective differs from the chart, obstacles (masts, wires), terrain shielding, workload, radio range; reduced visibility from haze, mist/fog, smoke, precipitation, low cloud, sun glare/low sun, windscreen contamination, dust."],["061.02.01.02:02","Ground features & visual identification: fill in the gaps → ____: only ____ (towns, roads with traffic, airport beacons/lights) visible, water and dark areas hard to see, ____ (CFIT, inadvertent IMC), illusions (false horizon), night vision limitations, need for accurate DR and radio aids.","Night VFR: only lit features (towns, roads with traffic, airport beacons/lights) visible, water and dark areas hard to see, clouds/terrain invisible (CFIT, inadvertent IMC), illusions (false horizon), night vision limitations, need for accurate DR and radio aids."],["061.02.01.01:01","Map reading & VFR technique: fill in the gaps → ____: identifying position by comparing the chart with the ground. ____: prominent feature to confirm position (preferably a combination of line + point features).","Map reading: identifying position by comparing the chart with the ground. Visual checkpoint: prominent feature to confirm position (preferably a combination of line + point features)."],["061.02.02.01:01","Map reading & VFR technique: fill in the gaps → Compare ____ → correct wind, heading, GS, ETA.","Compare DR positions with fixes → correct wind, heading, GS, ETA."],["061.02.02.01:06","Map reading & VFR technique: fill in the gaps → ____ from two or more intersecting ____ (visual transits/line features). A ____ checks progress: ⊥ to track → timing/GS; parallel to track → track keeping.","Fix from two or more intersecting position lines (visual transits/line features). A single position line checks progress: ⊥ to track → timing/GS; parallel to track → track keeping."],["061.02.02.01:01","Map reading & VFR technique: fill in the gaps → ____: draw tracks, 10-NM/time marks, drift lines (±5°/10°), checkpoints, minimum altitudes, airspace, frequencies; fold for the route; in flight ____ to the direction of flight (track up).","Chart preparation: draw tracks, 10-NM/time marks, drift lines (±5°/10°), checkpoints, minimum altitudes, airspace, frequencies; fold for the route; in flight orient the chart to the direction of flight (track up)."],["061.02.02.01:01","Map reading & VFR technique: fill in the gaps → ____: hold accurate ____; use the DR position to ____ features (\"chart to ground\"), then identify by shape, size and surroundings; revise ETA (GS) and heading (1-in-60: TKE = off × 60/dist flown; CA = off × 60/dist to go).","Technique: hold accurate heading and time; use the DR position to anticipate features (\"chart to ground\"), then identify by shape, size and surroundings; revise ETA (GS) and heading (1-in-60: TKE = off × 60/dist flown; CA = off × 60/dist to go)."],["061.02.02.01:09","Map reading & VFR technique: fill in the gaps → ____: turn ____ onto the new heading, then confirm with the next features.","No checkpoint at a turning point: turn on ETA onto the new heading, then confirm with the next features."],["061.02.02.01:01","Map reading & VFR technique: fill in the gaps → ____: featureless terrain (desert, forest, sea, snow), too many similar features, mountains (changed perspective, obscured), ____.","Difficulties: featureless terrain (desert, forest, sea, snow), too many similar features, mountains (changed perspective, obscured), inaccurate/outdated charts."],["061.02.02.01:01","Map reading & VFR technique: fill in the gaps → ____ = lines joining points of equal elevation → shape and steepness of terrain (close spacing = steep). ____ = colour bands for elevation ranges (green low → brown high) for a quick impression of relief. From contours: hills (closed rings), valleys (V pointing uphill), ridges (V pointing downhill), saddles, cliffs.","Contour lines = lines joining points of equal elevation → shape and steepness of terrain (close spacing = steep). Layer tinting = colour bands for elevation ranges (green low → brown high) for a quick impression of relief. From contours: hills (closed rings), valleys (V pointing uphill), ridges (V pointing downhill), saddles, cliffs."],["061.02.02.02:01","Unplanned events: fill in the gaps → ____: decide early, pick a clear starting point, estimate new track/heading (mental DR, 1-in-60, clock code), distance, time, fuel, minimum altitude/terrain, airspace, weather; inform ATC; refine after settling.","Diversion: decide early, pick a clear starting point, estimate new track/heading (mental DR, 1-in-60, clock code), distance, time, fuel, minimum altitude/terrain, airspace, weather; inform ATC; refine after settling."],["061.02.02.02:01","Unplanned events: fill in the gaps → ____: keep flying the heading, ____ (time × GS along track), consider the circle of uncertainty, look for line features, use radio aids (VOR/DME, GNSS), ask ATC/FIS.","Unsure of position: keep flying the heading, check DR (time × GS along track), consider the circle of uncertainty, look for line features, use radio aids (VOR/DME, GNSS), ask ATC/FIS."],["061.02.02.02:01","Unplanned events: fill in the gaps → ____: the 5 C's — ____ (visibility, radio range, terrain), ____ (ATC, 121.5, D&D), Confess, Comply, Conserve fuel; fly to a prominent line feature and follow it; precautionary landing in good time if fuel or daylight run short.","Lost: the 5 C's — Climb (visibility, radio range, terrain), Communicate (ATC, 121.5, D&D), Confess, Comply, Conserve fuel; fly to a prominent line feature and follow it; precautionary landing in good time if fuel or daylight run short."]],"15":[["081.01.03.01:01","Why coefficients?: fill in the gaps → Aerodynamic forces depend on ____, ____ ____ and the shape/α of the body. Dimensionless coefficients (CL, CD, CM) isolate the effect of shape and α from speed, density and size → results from wind tunnels/models can be applied to full-size aircraft at any speed/altitude, and different designs can be compared.","Aerodynamic forces depend on dynamic pressure (½ρV²), size (S) and the shape/α of the body. Dimensionless coefficients (CL, CD, CM) isolate the effect of shape and α from speed, density and size → results from wind tunnels/models can be applied to full-size aircraft at any speed/altitude, and different designs can be compared."],["081.01.03.01:01","Why coefficients?: fill in the gaps → General form: ____.","General form: Force = Coefficient × ½ρV² × S."],["081.01.03.02:02","Lift coefficient: fill in the gaps → ________ ____ — lift increases with CL (α, camber, flaps), density, V² and wing area.","Lift formula: L = CL × ½ρV² × S — lift increases with CL (α, camber, flaps), density, V² and wing area."],["081.01.03.02:01","Lift coefficient: fill in the gaps → Calculations: at constant α and density, ____ (+10 % speed → +21 % lift; double speed → 4× lift). In level flight L = W → ____L ∝ ____: halving the speed requires 4× CL. At higher altitude (lower ρ) the same IAS keeps the same q → same CL/α.","Calculations: at constant α and density, L ∝ V² (+10 % speed → +21 % lift; double speed → 4× lift). In level flight L = W → CL ∝ 1/V²: halving the speed requires 4× CL. At higher altitude (lower ρ) the same IAS keeps the same q → same CL/α."],["081.01.03.02:01","Lift coefficient: fill in the gaps → Example: W = 50 000 N, S = 20 m², ρ = 1.225, V = 50 m/s → q = 1 531 Pa → CL = 50 000/(1 531 × 20) = ____.","Example: W = 50 000 N, S = 20 m², ρ = 1.225, V = 50 m/s → q = 1 531 Pa → CL = 50 000/(1 531 × 20) = 1.63."],["081.01.03.02:04","Lift coefficient: fill in the gaps → ____ on the CL–α graph: ____ aerofoil — line passes through the origin (CL = 0 at α = 0); ____ — curve shifted up/left: positive CL at α = 0, zero lift at a negative α, higher CLmax (slightly lower αcrit); negative camber — shifted down/right.","Camber on the CL–α graph: symmetrical aerofoil — line passes through the origin (CL = 0 at α = 0); positive camber — curve shifted up/left: positive CL at α = 0, zero lift at a negative α, higher CLmax (slightly lower αcrit); negative camber — shifted down/right."],["081.01.03.02:03","Lift coefficient: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (thick, highly cambered, large nose radius) → high CLmax, gentle stall, more drag; ____ (thin, low camber, small nose radius) → lower CLmax, lower drag, higher Mcrit, more abrupt stall.","Fast vs slow aerofoils: slow (thick, highly cambered, large nose radius) → high CLmax, gentle stall, more drag; fast (thin, low camber, small nose radius) → lower CLmax, lower drag, higher Mcrit, more abrupt stall."],["081.01.03.02:05","Lift coefficient: fill in the gaps → ________ = highest point of the curve; ____crit (stalling α) at CLmax. Variables affecting CL in low subsonic flight: α, aerofoil shape (camber, thickness, nose radius), high-lift devices, surface condition (ice, contamination, roughness), Reynolds number (boundary-layer behaviour), (aspect ratio for the 3D wing).","CLmax = highest point of the curve; αcrit (stalling α) at CLmax. Variables affecting CL in low subsonic flight: α, aerofoil shape (camber, thickness, nose radius), high-lift devices, surface condition (ice, contamination, roughness), Reynolds number (boundary-layer behaviour), (aspect ratio for the 3D wing)."],["081.01.03.03:02","Drag coefficient: fill in the gaps → ________ ____ (S = reference area — frontal or wing area).","2D drag formula: D = CD × ½ρV² × S (S = reference area — frontal or wing area)."],["081.01.03.03:02","Drag coefficient: fill in the gaps → ____: streamlined shapes have much lower CD than bluff bodies (flat plate ⊥ flow CD ≈ 1.2, sphere ≈ 0.5, streamlined body ≈ 0.05) because they avoid flow ____ and large wakes (form drag). ____ ↑ → drag ↑ (with the same CD referred to frontal area).","Body shape: streamlined shapes have much lower CD than bluff bodies (flat plate ⊥ flow CD ≈ 1.2, sphere ≈ 0.5, streamlined body ≈ 0.05) because they avoid flow separation and large wakes (form drag). Cross-sectional (frontal) area ↑ → drag ↑ (with the same CD referred to frontal area)."],["081.01.03.03:01","Drag coefficient: fill in the gaps → ____ ↑ → skin friction ↑ and earlier transition to turbulent boundary layer (but can delay separation on bluff bodies).","Surface roughness ↑ → skin friction ↑ and earlier transition to turbulent boundary layer (but can delay separation on bluff bodies)."],["081.01.03.01:01","Boundary layer (KB): fill in the gaps → Thin layer where air speed goes from zero at the surface (no-slip) to free-stream. ____ (smooth layers, low friction, separates easily) → ____ → ____ (mixing, more friction but more energy → resists separation). Separation occurs in an adverse pressure gradient (rising pressure towards the TE) → stall, form drag. Vortex generators/turbulators energise the BL.","Thin layer where air speed goes from zero at the surface (no-slip) to free-stream. Laminar (smooth layers, low friction, separates easily) → transition → turbulent (mixing, more friction but more energy → resists separation). Separation occurs in an adverse pressure gradient (rising pressure towards the TE) → stall, form drag. Vortex generators/turbulators energise the BL."],["081.01.03.02:02","Exam trap (Boundary layer (KB)): complete the fact → Cambered aerofoil: lift at α = ____zero-lift α negative.","Cambered aerofoil: lift at α = 0, zero-lift α negative."],["081.01.03.03:01","EASA LO 081.01.03.03.01 → Describe the two-dimensional drag formula.","2D drag formula: D = CD × ½ρV² × S (S = reference area — frontal or wing area)."]],"16":[["gen",": what do you remember about this? → Stage without its own EASA LO codes in the ELC — content: approach …","Stage without its own EASA LO codes in the ELC — content: approach chart types (GSPRM Introduction) and radio-navigation interceptions (QDB 62.1.7). Built on #5 (ILS), #12 (NDB/ADF), #13 (VOR/DME)."],["gen","Types of instrument approaches – Precision approach (PA): what are the key facts?","Guidance: lateral + vertical, ground-based precision aid · Minima: DA/H · Examples: ILS CAT I/II/III, MLS, GLS (GBAS), PAR"],["gen","Types of instrument approaches – Approach with vertical guidance (APV): what are the key facts?","Guidance: lateral + vertical (not meeting PA standards) · Minima: DA/H · Examples: RNP APCH to LPV (SBAS), LNAV/VNAV (baro- VNAV)"],["gen","Types of instrument approaches – Non- precision approach (NPA): what are the key facts?","Guidance: lateral only · Minima: MDA/H · Examples: VOR, VOR/DME, NDB, LOC-only, LOC/DME, RNP APCH to LNAV, SRA"],["gen","Types of instrument approaches – Circling: what are the key facts?","Guidance: visual manoeuvring after an instrument approach · Minima: MDA/H (by category) · Examples: —"],["gen","Types of instrument approaches – Visual approach: what are the key facts?","Guidance: visual reference · Minima: — · Examples: —"],["gen","Types of instrument approaches: fill in the gaps → Many NPAs are flown as ____ (continuous descent final approach) with a constant ~3° path to DA/(MDA + add-on) instead of \"dive and drive\".","Many NPAs are flown as CDFA (continuous descent final approach) with a constant ~3° path to DA/(MDA + add-on) instead of \"dive and drive\"."],["gen","Types of instrument approaches: fill in the gaps → Chart elements (Jeppesen/GSPRM): ____ (procedure name, frequencies, final approach course, FAF altitude, DA/MDA, airport elevation, TDZE, MSA), ____, ____ (IAF/IF/FAF, procedure turns, holding, MSA circle 25 NM), profile view (altitudes at fixes, glide slope angle, DME distance/altitude table, MAPt), minima box (straight-in vs circling by category A–D, RVR/visibility, lighting), missed approach text, conversion table (GS → ROD, FAF–MAPt timing).","Chart elements (Jeppesen/GSPRM): heading box (procedure name, frequencies, final approach course, FAF altitude, DA/MDA, airport elevation, TDZE, MSA), briefing strip, plan view (IAF/IF/FAF, procedure turns, holding, MSA circle 25 NM), profile view (altitudes at fixes, glide slope angle, DME distance/altitude table, MAPt), minima box (straight-in vs circling by category A–D, RVR/visibility, lighting), missed approach text, conversion table (GS → ROD, FAF–MAPt timing)."],["gen","Types of instrument approaches: fill in the gaps → ____ (see #90): arrival → ____ (IAF–IF) → ____ (IF–FAF) → final (FAF/FAP–MAPt) → missed approach. Reversal procedures: procedure turn (45°/180°), base turn (teardrop), racetrack (see #19).","Approach segments (see #90): arrival → initial (IAF–IF) → intermediate (IF–FAF) → final (FAF/FAP–MAPt) → missed approach. Reversal procedures: procedure turn (45°/180°), base turn (teardrop), racetrack (see #19)."],["gen","Interception problems (typical exam logic): fill in the gaps → ____: to intercept a track/radial/QDM, turn to a heading that makes an ____ with the desired track (typically ____; 45° standard), fly it until the needle comes \"alive\", and turn onto the track with a lead to avoid overshooting.","Principle: to intercept a track/radial/QDM, turn to a heading that makes an intercept angle (IA) with the desired track (typically 30°–90°; 45° standard), fly it until the needle comes \"alive\", and turn onto the track with a lead to avoid overshooting."],["gen","Interception problems (typical exam logic): fill in the gaps → ____: set the desired course; the needle shows on which side the course is (fly towards the needle with correct TO/FROM sense). Intercept heading = desired course ± IA (towards the needle).","VOR (HSI/CDI): set the desired course; the needle shows on which side the course is (fly towards the needle with correct TO/FROM sense). Intercept heading = desired course ± IA (towards the needle)."],["gen","Interception problems (typical exam logic): fill in the gaps → Example: to intercept R-090 outbound when on R-060 (north of it): set 090, needle right (course south of you) → heading e.g. ____ (IA 45°), turn onto 090 when the needle centres.","Example: to intercept R-090 outbound when on R-060 (north of it): set 090, needle right (course south of you) → heading e.g. 135° (IA 45°), turn onto 090 when the needle centres."],["gen","Interception problems (typical exam logic): fill in the gaps → ____: intercept ____ — rule: if the ____ is greater than the desired QDM, heading = desired QDM + IA; if smaller, heading = desired QDM − IA. You are on track when the needle head shows the desired QDM (i.e. RB = desired QDM − heading); then turn onto the QDM.","NDB/RMI: intercept inbound QDM — rule: if the present QDM is greater than the desired QDM, heading = desired QDM + IA; if smaller, heading = desired QDM − IA. You are on track when the needle head shows the desired QDM (i.e. RB = desired QDM − heading); then turn onto the QDM."],["gen","Interception problems (typical exam logic): fill in the gaps → ____: heading = desired QDR ± IA towards the desired radial; on track when the needle ____ shows the desired QDR.","Outbound QDR: heading = desired QDR ± IA towards the desired radial; on track when the needle tail shows the desired QDR."],["gen","Interception problems (typical exam logic): fill in the gaps → Example: MH 360, QDM 330 (needle 30° left), desired inbound QDM 300 → present QDM > desired → heading ____ (IA 45°); intercept when the needle is ____ → turn left onto 300.","Example: MH 360, QDM 330 (needle 30° left), desired inbound QDM 300 → present QDM > desired → heading 345 (IA 45°); intercept when the needle is 45° left (RB 315) → turn left onto 300."],["gen","Interception problems (typical exam logic): fill in the gaps → ____ For RMI: the ____ of the needle shows the radial/QDR the aircraft is on; the head shows QDM. For a fixed-card ADF: QDM = MH + RB.","Which side am I? For RMI: the tail of the needle shows the radial/QDR the aircraft is on; the head shows QDM. For a fixed-card ADF: QDM = MH + RB."],["gen","Interception problems (typical exam logic): what do you remember about this? → Wind: apply WCA after interception; tracking inbound with drift → …","Wind: apply WCA after interception; tracking inbound with drift → heading offset into the wind, needle offset by the drift angle on the other side."],["gen","Interception problems (typical exam logic): what do you remember about this? → DME arcs: keep the RMI needle on the wing-tip reference (±10°), turn …","DME arcs: keep the RMI needle on the wing-tip reference (±10°), turn 10–20° into the arc as the needle drifts behind."]],"17":[["031.04.01.01:03","Datum, arms, moments: fill in the gaps → ____ = reference point/plane (chosen by the manufacturer, e.g. nose, firewall or a point ahead of the nose) from which all ____ are measured. ____ = horizontal distance from the datum to the item's CG (aft = +, forward = −). Moment = mass × arm (kg·m, lb·in). Data found in the AFM/POH (mass & balance section), weight & balance report, loading manual.","Datum = reference point/plane (chosen by the manufacturer, e.g. nose, firewall or a point ahead of the nose) from which all arms are measured. Moment arm = horizontal distance from the datum to the item's CG (aft = +, forward = −). Moment = mass × arm (kg·m, lb·in). Data found in the AFM/POH (mass & balance section), weight & balance report, loading manual."],["031.04.01.02:02","CG position: fill in the gaps → ____ = point through which the total weight acts (where the aircraft would balance). CG at ____ is in the ____ report/schedule; CG limits in the AFM (limitations section/CG envelope).","CG = point through which the total weight acts (where the aircraft would balance). CG at BEM is in the mass (weight) report/schedule; CG limits in the AFM (limitations section/CG envelope)."],["031.04.01.03:03","CG position: fill in the gaps → Presentation: ____ (m, in), ____, or ____ MAC.","Presentation: distance from datum (m, in), index units, or % MAC."],["031.04.01.03:03","CG position: fill in the gaps → ____: CG position as a percentage of the mean aerodynamic chord from its leading edge (LEMAC): CG %MAC = (CG arm − ____. Example: LEMAC at 18.0 m, MAC 4.0 m, CG at 19.2 m → ____. Used on large aircraft because it relates the CG directly to aerodynamic characteristics (stability).","% MAC: CG position as a percentage of the mean aerodynamic chord from its leading edge (LEMAC): CG %MAC = (CG arm − LEMAC arm) / MAC × 100. Example: LEMAC at 18.0 m, MAC 4.0 m, CG at 19.2 m → 30 % MAC. Used on large aircraft because it relates the CG directly to aerodynamic characteristics (stability)."],["031.04.01.04:01","Limits & compartment data: fill in the gaps → ____ (forward/aft, often varying with mass — CG envelope graph) and ____ (helicopters, fuel imbalance, asymmetric loads) extracted from sample documents (CAP 696).","Longitudinal CG limits (forward/aft, often varying with mass — CG envelope graph) and lateral CG limits (helicopters, fuel imbalance, asymmetric loads) extracted from sample documents (CAP 696)."],["031.04.01.06:01","Limits & compartment data: fill in the gaps → ____: seating schemes (zones/rows with arms), compartment dimensions, max compartment mass, floor/running load limits, door sizes.","Passenger/cargo data: seating schemes (zones/rows with arms), compartment dimensions, max compartment mass, floor/running load limits, door sizes."],["031.04.01.07:02","Limits & compartment data: fill in the gaps → ____: tank capacities and ____ (fuel index tables). ____ as fuel is burnt: burning fuel from a tank aft of the CG → CG moves forward; from a tank forward of the CG → CG moves aft. Swept wings: outer wing tanks lie aft of inner tanks → using outer fuel moves CG forward;","Fuel system data: tank capacities and arms (fuel index tables). CG shift in flight as fuel is burnt: burning fuel from a tank aft of the CG → CG moves forward; from a tank forward of the CG → CG moves aft. Swept wings: outer wing tanks lie aft of inner tanks → using outer fuel moves CG forward;"],["031.04.01.07:02","Limits & compartment data: fill in the gaps → ____ usually near the CG. ____: fuel moved aft in cruise → ____ trim drag, better fuel burn; risk: CG too far aft if transfer fails/imbalance (must transfer forward before landing), system complexity, additional monitoring.","centre tank usually near the CG. Tail/fin/horizontal-stabiliser trim tanks: fuel moved aft in cruise → aft CG → less tail download → less trim drag, better fuel burn; risk: CG too far aft if transfer fails/imbalance (must transfer forward before landing), system complexity, additional monitoring."],["031.04.02.01:02","Weighing: fill in the gaps → Aircraft are ____ at manufacture (or new), after ____ modifications/repairs affecting mass, after repainting, when mass/CG records are in doubt, and periodically (EU: individual aeroplanes every ____; fleet weighing every 9 years); recalculated when equipment changes (with documentation). Result in the mass (weight) report: BEM, CG/moment, equipment list, date.","Aircraft are weighed at manufacture (or new), after major modifications/repairs affecting mass, after repainting, when mass/CG records are in doubt, and periodically (EU: individual aeroplanes every 4 years; fleet weighing every 9 years); recalculated when equipment changes (with documentation). Result in the mass (weight) report: BEM, CG/moment, equipment list, date."],["031.04.02.02:01","Weighing: fill in the gaps → ____: weigh at each jacking/wheel point; ____ mass = Σ reactions; CG = Σ(reaction × arm) / total mass. Example: nose 1 000 kg at 2 m, each main 3 000 kg at 8 m → total 7 000 kg, moments 2 000 + 48 000 = 50 000 → ____.","CG from weighing: weigh at each jacking/wheel point; total mass = Σ reactions; CG = Σ(reaction × arm) / total mass. Example: nose 1 000 kg at 2 m, each main 3 000 kg at 8 m → total 7 000 kg, moments 2 000 + 48 000 = 50 000 → CG 7.14 m."],["031.04.03.03:01","BEM/DOM data: fill in the gaps → Extract ____, their ____ from given documents (CAP 696 SEP/MEP/MRJT). ____ from standard configuration (extra crew members, optional equipment, extra fuel tanks, galley changes) → add/subtract mass and moment to get the actual DOM/DOI.","Extract BEM/DOM, their CG positions/moments or indices from given documents (CAP 696 SEP/MEP/MRJT). Deviations from standard configuration (extra crew members, optional equipment, extra fuel tanks, galley changes) → add/subtract mass and moment to get the actual DOM/DOI."],["031.04.01.01:01","EASA LO 031.04.01.01.01 → State where the datum and moment arms for aircraft can be found.","Datum = reference point/plane (chosen by the manufacturer, e.g. nose, firewall or a point ahead of the nose) from which all arms are measured. Moment arm = horizontal distance from the datum to the item's CG (aft = +, forward = −). Moment = mass × arm (kg·m, lb·in). Data found in the AFM/POH (mass & balance section), weight & balance report, loading manual."],["031.04.01.01:02","EASA LO 031.04.01.01.02 → Extract the appropriate data from given documents.","Datum = reference point/plane (chosen by the manufacturer, e.g. nose, firewall or a point ahead of the nose) from which all arms are measured. Moment arm = horizontal distance from the datum to the item's CG (aft = +, forward = −). Moment = mass × arm (kg·m, lb·in). Data found in the AFM/POH (mass & balance section), weight & balance report, loading manual. / Extract BEM/DOM, their CG positions/moments or indices from given documents (CAP 696 SEP/MEP/MRJT). Deviations from standard configuration (extra crew members, optional equipment, extra fuel tanks, galley changes) → add/subtract mass and moment to get the actual DOM/DOI."],["031.04.01.02:01","EASA LO 031.04.01.02.01 → State where the CG position for an aircraft at basic empty mass can be found.","Presentation: distance from datum (m, in), index units, or % MAC. / CG = point through which the total weight acts (where the aircraft would balance). CG at BEM is in the mass (weight) report/schedule; CG limits in the AFM (limitations section/CG envelope)."],["031.04.01.02:03","EASA LO 031.04.01.02.03 → Describe the different forms in presenting CG position as distance from datum or other references.","Presentation: distance from datum (m, in), index units, or % MAC."],["031.04.01.02:04","EASA LO 031.04.01.02.04 → Explain the meaning of centre of gravity (CG).","Presentation: distance from datum (m, in), index units, or % MAC."],["031.04.01.03:01","EASA LO 031.04.01.03.01 → Extract MAC information from aircraft documents.","% MAC: CG position as a percentage of the mean aerodynamic chord from its leading edge (LEMAC): CG %MAC = (CG arm − LEMAC arm) / MAC × 100. Example: LEMAC at 18.0 m, MAC 4.0 m, CG at 19.2 m → 30 % MAC. Used on large aircraft because it relates the CG directly to aerodynamic characteristics (stability). / Presentation: distance from datum (m, in), index units, or % MAC."],["031.04.01.03:02","EASA LO 031.04.01.03.02 → Explain the principle of using % MAC for the description of the CG position.","% MAC: CG position as a percentage of the mean aerodynamic chord from its leading edge (LEMAC): CG %MAC = (CG arm − LEMAC arm) / MAC × 100. Example: LEMAC at 18.0 m, MAC 4.0 m, CG at 19.2 m → 30 % MAC. Used on large aircraft because it relates the CG directly to aerodynamic characteristics (stability). / Presentation: distance from datum (m, in), index units, or % MAC."],["031.04.01.05:01","EASA LO 031.04.01.05.01 → Extract the appropriate data from given sample documents.","Longitudinal CG limits (forward/aft, often varying with mass — CG envelope graph) and lateral CG limits (helicopters, fuel imbalance, asymmetric loads) extracted from sample documents (CAP 696)."],["031.04.01.07:01","EASA LO 031.04.01.07.01 → Extract the appropriate data (e.g. fuel-tank capacities and fuel-tank positions) from given sample documents.","Fuel system data: tank capacities and arms (fuel index tables). CG shift in flight as fuel is burnt: burning fuel from a tank aft of the CG → CG moves forward; from a tank forward of the CG → CG moves aft. Swept wings: outer wing tanks lie aft of inner tanks → using outer fuel moves CG forward;"],["031.04.03.01:01","EASA LO 031.04.03.01.01 → Extract values for BEM or DOM from given documents.","Extract BEM/DOM, their CG positions/moments or indices from given documents (CAP 696 SEP/MEP/MRJT). Deviations from standard configuration (extra crew members, optional equipment, extra fuel tanks, galley changes) → add/subtract mass and moment to get the actual DOM/DOI."],["031.04.03.02:01","EASA LO 031.04.03.02.01 → Extract values for CG position and moment at BEM or DOM from given documents.","Extract BEM/DOM, their CG positions/moments or indices from given documents (CAP 696 SEP/MEP/MRJT). Deviations from standard configuration (extra crew members, optional equipment, extra fuel tanks, galley changes) → add/subtract mass and moment to get the actual DOM/DOI."]],"18":[["021.09.04.03:04","Alternating current: fill in the gaps → ____ = current that periodically ____ (sine wave). Advantages: easy voltage change with ____, lighter generators/motors (brushless), efficient distribution; but more complex (frequency control, phase, paralleling).","AC = current that periodically reverses direction (sine wave). Advantages: easy voltage change with transformers, lighter generators/motors (brushless), efficient distribution; but more complex (frequency control, phase, paralleling)."],["021.09.01.03:03","Alternating current: fill in the gaps → ____: one sine-wave output. ____ (one winding) vs ____ (three windings 120° apart → smoother power, more efficient; 115 V phase-to-neutral / 200 V phase-to-phase). Aircraft use single-phase (small, e.g. inverters) or three-phase (main generation).","Phase: one sine-wave output. Single-phase (one winding) vs three-phase (three windings 120° apart → smoother power, more efficient; 115 V phase-to-neutral / 200 V phase-to-phase). Aircraft use single-phase (small, e.g. inverters) or three-phase (main generation)."],["021.09.01.03:05","Alternating current: fill in the gaps → ____ = cycles per second, ____; aircraft ____ (lighter components than 50/60 Hz). Phase shift = time/angle difference between two waves of the same frequency (e.g. current lagging voltage in inductive loads).","Frequency = cycles per second, hertz (Hz); aircraft 400 Hz (lighter components than 50/60 Hz). Phase shift = time/angle difference between two waves of the same frequency (e.g. current lagging voltage in inductive loads)."],["021.09.03.02:02","DC generation: fill in the gaps → ____: conductors rotating in a magnetic field → AC induced, converted to DC by a ____ and brushes. ____ alternator (light aircraft): generates AC (rotating field), converted by diodes (rectifier) → lighter, more output at low rpm.","DC generator: conductors rotating in a magnetic field → AC induced, converted to DC by a commutator and brushes. DC alternator (light aircraft): generates AC (rotating field), converted by diodes (rectifier) → lighter, more output at low rpm."],["021.09.03.02:02","DC generation: fill in the gaps → ____ (voltage regulator varies ____): output voltage otherwise changes with ____ → constant ~28 V (or 14 V) protects equipment and charges the battery correctly; over-voltage protection trips the field.","Voltage control (voltage regulator varies field current): output voltage otherwise changes with rpm and load → constant ~28 V (or 14 V) protects equipment and charges the battery correctly; over-voltage protection trips the field."],["021.09.03.01:03","DC generation: fill in the gaps → ____ (reverse-current relay/cut-out or diodes): disconnects the generator when its voltage falls below the battery/bus voltage — prevents the ____ ____ (motoring it, draining the battery).","Reverse current protection (reverse-current relay/cut-out or diodes): disconnects the generator when its voltage falls below the battery/bus voltage — prevents the battery discharging into the generator (motoring it, draining the battery)."],["021.09.03.01:04","DC generation: fill in the gaps → ____ (turboprops/small turbines, APUs): one machine works as a ____ for engine start and then as a ____ → saves weight.","Starter-generator (turboprops/small turbines, APUs): one machine works as a starter motor for engine start and then as a DC generator → saves weight."],["021.09.03.02:02","AC generation: fill in the gaps → ____: a ____ ____ supplies the exciter field (via GCU); the exciter rotor output is rectified by rotating diodes and feeds the main rotating field; the stationary three-phase stator delivers output — no brushes/slip rings (reliable, high altitude). Voltage controlled by the generator field (excitation) current.","Brushless three-phase AC generator: a permanent magnet generator (PMG) supplies the exciter field (via GCU); the exciter rotor output is rectified by rotating diodes and feeds the main rotating field; the stationary three-phase stator delivers output — no brushes/slip rings (reliable, high altitude). Voltage controlled by the generator field (excitation) current."],["021.09.03.02:04","AC generation: fill in the gaps → ____ → proportional to rpm: 400 Hz needs constant speed. ____: frequency varies with engine speed (e.g. 360–800 Hz, B787/A380 or de-icing heaters) — used for loads insensitive to frequency.","Frequency f = (rpm × pole pairs) / 60 → proportional to rpm: 400 Hz needs constant speed. Frequency-wild generator: frequency varies with engine speed (e.g. 360–800 Hz, B787/A380 or de-icing heaters) — used for loads insensitive to frequency."],["021.09.03.02:05","AC generation: fill in the gaps → Drive sources (aeroplane): ____ (accessory gearbox), ____, ____ (ram air turbine, emergency), hydraulic (hydraulic-motor-driven generator, emergency). Helicopter: engine, APU, main gearbox.","Drive sources (aeroplane): engine (accessory gearbox), APU, RAT (ram air turbine, emergency), hydraulic (hydraulic-motor-driven generator, emergency). Helicopter: engine, APU, main gearbox."],["021.09.03.03:02","CSD & IDG: fill in the gaps → ____ (constant speed drive): hydro-mechanical transmission between engine gearbox and generator that converts ____ engine rpm into constant generator rpm → constant ____. Monitored: oil temperature (in/out, rise), oil pressure (low pressure warning), generator frequency; drive disconnect on overheat/low pressure.","CSD (constant speed drive): hydro-mechanical transmission between engine gearbox and generator that converts variable engine rpm into constant generator rpm → constant 400 Hz. Monitored: oil temperature (in/out, rise), oil pressure (low pressure warning), generator frequency; drive disconnect on overheat/low pressure."],["021.09.03.03:01","CSD & IDG: fill in the gaps → ____ (integrated drive generator) = ____ ____ (lighter, shared oil cooling).","IDG (integrated drive generator) = CSD + generator in one unit (lighter, shared oil cooling)."],["021.09.03.03:04","CSD & IDG: fill in the gaps → ____ (flight-deck switch — mechanical decoupling from the gearbox) on high temperature/low oil pressure: the CSD/IDG and its generator are lost for the rest of the flight — ____ (engine stopped). Don't disconnect with engine stopped/low rpm (damage).","Disconnect (flight-deck switch — mechanical decoupling from the gearbox) on high temperature/low oil pressure: the CSD/IDG and its generator are lost for the rest of the flight — reconnection only on the ground (engine stopped). Don't disconnect with engine stopped/low rpm (damage)."],["021.09.03.03:05","CSD & IDG: fill in the gaps → CSD/IDG has its ____ (cooled by fuel/oil or air/oil coolers); a leak in the IDG cooler into engine oil can look like ____ (or IDG oil loss shows as engine oil leak/rise) — diagnose with IDG temperature/pressure indications.","CSD/IDG has its own independent oil system (cooled by fuel/oil or air/oil coolers); a leak in the IDG cooler into engine oil can look like engine oil quantity changes (or IDG oil loss shows as engine oil leak/rise) — diagnose with IDG temperature/pressure indications."],["021.09.04.03:03","Distribution: fill in the gaps → ____ = conductor that distributes power from sources to consumers (via CBs), allows switching/isolation.","Busbar = conductor that distributes power from sources to consumers (via CBs), allows switching/isolation."],["021.09.04.01:02","Distribution: fill in the gaps → Buses: ____ (AC loads), ____ (via TRUs), ____ (vital loads — powered by battery/static inverter/RAT on total generator loss), essential AC/DC bus (essential flight loads, powered with priority), battery bus (connected to the battery), hot (battery) bus (permanently connected to the battery — clocks, fire extinguisher squibs, emergency lights), ground servicing / maintenance bus (power for cabin cleaning/refuelling from external power without energising the whole system).","Buses: AC bus (AC loads), DC bus (via TRUs), emergency AC/DC bus (vital loads — powered by battery/static inverter/RAT on total generator loss), essential AC/DC bus (essential flight loads, powered with priority), battery bus (connected to the battery), hot (battery) bus (permanently connected to the battery — clocks, fire extinguisher squibs, emergency lights), ground servicing / maintenance bus (power for cabin cleaning/refuelling from external power without energising the whole system)."],["021.09.04.01:03","Distribution: fill in the gaps → ____ (single-wire system — return current through the airframe) → requires good ____ of all parts (low resistance).","Structure as common earth (single-wire system — return current through the airframe) → requires good bonding of all parts (low resistance)."],["021.09.04.01:05","Distribution: fill in the gaps → ____ (GPU): 115 V/400 Hz AC or 28 V DC on the ground, conserves APU/battery; checked by monitoring (voltage, frequency, phase sequence) before connection.","External power (GPU): 115 V/400 Hz AC or 28 V DC on the ground, conserves APU/battery; checked by monitoring (voltage, frequency, phase sequence) before connection."],["021.09.04.01:05","Distribution: fill in the gaps → ____ of sources: typically ____ ____ (or external power has priority on the ground when connected); automatic transfer.","Priority sequence of sources: typically own engine generator > APU generator > external power (or external power has priority on the ground when connected); automatic transfer."],["021.09.04.01:06","Distribution: fill in the gaps → ____: generators operating in parallel share the load equally (real & reactive load control). ____: automatic (or manual) disconnection of non-essential loads when generating capacity is reduced — e.g. ____ entertainment, cabin lighting, some heaters.","Load sharing: generators operating in parallel share the load equally (real & reactive load control). Load shedding: automatic (or manual) disconnection of non-essential loads when generating capacity is reduced — e.g. galley power, in-flight entertainment, cabin lighting, some heaters."],["021.09.04.01:07","Distribution: fill in the gaps → ____ (e.g. one generator lost) → its buses are transferred to another source (bus tie) — little effect except load shedding; ____ (fault on a bus) → all consumers on that bus are lost even though generators work (bus isolated, cannot be re-powered).","Supply failure (e.g. one generator lost) → its buses are transferred to another source (bus tie) — little effect except load shedding; bus failure (fault on a bus) → all consumers on that bus are lost even though generators work (bus isolated, cannot be re-powered)."],["021.09.03.01:03","DC & AC distribution: fill in the gaps → ____: battery + alternator on a main bus via master switch, ammeter, CBs, avionics master; alternator failure → battery only (≈ 30 min).","Single-engine light aircraft: battery + alternator on a main bus via master switch, ammeter, CBs, avionics master; alternator failure → battery only (≈ 30 min)."],["021.09.04.02:02","DC & AC distribution: fill in the gaps → ____: two generators feeding split/paralleled buses with bus ties; one generator lost → other takes all (load shedding); bus failure → loss of connected consumers.","Multi-engine (CS-23/27): two generators feeding split/paralleled buses with bus ties; one generator lost → other takes all (load shedding); bus failure → loss of connected consumers."],["021.09.04.01:02","DC & AC distribution: fill in the gaps → ____: DC from ____ (AC → 28 V DC) and batteries, DC buses, essential/battery/hot buses; loss of DC supply → battery/emergency buses.","Transport (CS-25/29): DC from TRUs (AC → 28 V DC) and batteries, DC buses, essential/battery/hot buses; loss of DC supply → battery/emergency buses."],["021.09.04.02:04","DC & AC distribution: what do you remember about this? → DC consumers: engine start, emergency lighting, instruments, …","DC consumers: engine start, emergency lighting, instruments, avionics, relays, fire extinguishing squibs, standby systems. AC consumers: galleys, heaters, window heat, hydraulic pumps, fuel pumps, fans, TRUs."],["021.09.04.02:01","DC & AC distribution: fill in the gaps → ____: each generator feeds its own buses — ____ ____ (bus tie open; closes only when one generator fails) → no frequency synchronisation needed, a fault affects one side only.","Split system: each generator feeds its own buses — not paralleled (bus tie open; closes only when one generator fails) → no frequency synchronisation needed, a fault affects one side only."],["021.09.04.01:06","DC & AC distribution: fill in the gaps → ____: all generators connected to a common tie bus — share load, uninterrupted supply when one fails, but need ____ to parallel (and load-sharing control).","Parallel system: all generators connected to a common tie bus — share load, uninterrupted supply when one fails, but need equal voltage, frequency, phase sequence and phase angle to parallel (and load-sharing control)."],["021.09.04.03:05","DC & AC distribution: fill in the gaps → Unit of total (apparent) power in AC systems: ____ ____.","Unit of total (apparent) power in AC systems: volt-ampere (VA, kVA)."],["021.09.04.04:01","Load management & monitoring: fill in the gaps → ____: monitors generator output (voltage, frequency, current), controls excitation, protects the network. ____ ____: controls the generator field (switch off = de-excite). Generator contactor/breaker (GCB/GLC): connects the generator to its bus. Bus-tie contactor (BTC): connects buses together.","GCU: monitors generator output (voltage, frequency, current), controls excitation, protects the network. Exciter contactor/field relay: controls the generator field (switch off = de-excite). Generator contactor/breaker (GCB/GLC): connects the generator to its bus. Bus-tie contactor (BTC): connects buses together."],["021.09.04.04:01","Load management & monitoring: fill in the gaps → ____ (flight deck): manual control of the exciter (ON/OFF/reset). ____: mechanical disconnection (irreversible in flight, guarded). ____: AUTO/OFF only.","Generator switch (flight deck): manual control of the exciter (ON/OFF/reset). IDG/CSD disconnect switch: mechanical disconnection (irreversible in flight, guarded). Bus-tie switch: AUTO/OFF only."],["021.09.04.04:02","Load management & monitoring: fill in the gaps → Functions (normal/degraded): ____ (automatic source transfer, bus ties), ____, protection against ____ voltage, over/under-frequency, differential fault (current in/out of the feeder differ → short circuit to earth → generator tripped).","Functions (normal/degraded): distribution (automatic source transfer, bus ties), monitoring, protection against over/under- voltage, over/under-frequency, differential fault (current in/out of the feeder differ → short circuit to earth → generator tripped)."],["021.09.04.04:04","Load management & monitoring: fill in the gaps → ____: voltage, charge current, ____ — NiCd and lithium batteries can go into ____ → overheat warnings, charger disconnect.","Battery monitoring: voltage, charge current, temperature — NiCd and lithium batteries can go into thermal runaway → overheat warnings, charger disconnect."],["021.09.04.04:05","Load management & monitoring: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ after engine start (recharging), then decreasing to near zero; ____ (discharge) = battery supplying loads (generator failed/insufficient); zero = no charge/discharge; high continuous charge = battery fault/low charge.","Battery ammeter: positive (charge) after engine start (recharging), then decreasing to near zero; negative (discharge) = battery supplying loads (generator failed/insufficient); zero = no charge/discharge; high continuous charge = battery fault/low charge."],["021.09.04.04:01","Exam trap (Load management & monitoring): complete the fact → f = rpm × pole pairs/____","f = rpm × pole pairs/60."],["021.09.01.03:02","EASA LO 021.09.01.03.02 → Define the term ‘phase’, and explain the basic principle of single-phase and three-phase AC.","Phase: one sine-wave output. Single-phase (one winding) vs three-phase (three windings 120° apart → smoother power, more efficient; 115 V phase-to-neutral / 200 V phase-to-phase). Aircraft use single-phase (small, e.g. inverters) or three-phase (main generation). / Brushless three-phase AC generator: a permanent magnet generator (PMG) supplies the exciter field (via GCU); the exciter rotor output is rectified by rotating diodes and feeds the main rotating field; the stationary three-phase stator delivers output — no brushes/slip rings (reliable, high altitude). Voltage controlled by the generator field (excitation) current."],["021.09.01.03:04","EASA LO 021.09.01.03.04 → Define frequency and state the unit of measurement.","Unit of total (apparent) power in AC systems: volt-ampere (VA, kVA)."],["021.09.03.01:01","EASA LO 021.09.03.01.01 → Describe the basic working principle of a simple DC generator or DC alternator.","Single-engine light aircraft: battery + alternator on a main bus via master switch, ammeter, CBs, avionics master; alternator failure → battery only (≈ 30 min). / DC generator: conductors rotating in a magnetic field → AC induced, converted to DC by a commutator and brushes. DC alternator (light aircraft): generates AC (rotating field), converted by diodes (rectifier) → lighter, more output at low rpm."],["021.09.03.01:02","EASA LO 021.09.03.01.02 → Explain the principle of voltage control and why it is required.","Reverse current protection (reverse-current relay/cut-out or diodes): disconnects the generator when its voltage falls below the battery/bus voltage — prevents the battery discharging into the generator (motoring it, draining the battery)."],["021.09.03.02:01","EASA LO 021.09.03.02.01 → Describe the working principle of a brushless three-phase AC generator.","Brushless three-phase AC generator: a permanent magnet generator (PMG) supplies the exciter field (via GCU); the exciter rotor output is rectified by rotating diodes and feeds the main rotating field; the stationary three-phase stator delivers output — no brushes/slip rings (reliable, high altitude). Voltage controlled by the generator field (excitation) current. / Phase: one sine-wave output. Single-phase (one winding) vs three-phase (three windings 120° apart → smoother power, more efficient; 115 V phase-to-neutral / 200 V phase-to-phase). Aircraft use single-phase (small, e.g. inverters) or three-phase (main generation)."],["021.09.03.02:03","EASA LO 021.09.03.02.03 → State the relationship between output frequency and the rpm of a three-phase AC generator.","Phase: one sine-wave output. Single-phase (one winding) vs three-phase (three windings 120° apart → smoother power, more efficient; 115 V phase-to-neutral / 200 V phase-to-phase). Aircraft use single-phase (small, e.g. inverters) or three-phase (main generation). / Brushless three-phase AC generator: a permanent magnet generator (PMG) supplies the exciter field (via GCU); the exciter rotor output is rectified by rotating diodes and feeds the main rotating field; the stationary three-phase stator delivers output — no brushes/slip rings (reliable, high altitude). Voltage controlled by the generator field (excitation) current."],["021.09.03.02:06","EASA LO 021.09.03.02.06 → List the following different power sources that can be used for a helicopter to drive an AC generator: — engine; — APU; — gearbox.","Drive sources (aeroplane): engine (accessory gearbox), APU, RAT (ram air turbine, emergency), hydraulic (hydraulic-motor-driven generator, emergency). Helicopter: engine, APU, main gearbox. / Priority sequence of sources: typically own engine generator > APU generator > external power (or external power has priority on the ground when connected); automatic transfer."],["021.09.03.03:03","EASA LO 021.09.03.03.03 → Describe the function of an IDG.","IDG (integrated drive generator) = CSD + generator in one unit (lighter, shared oil cooling). / CSD (constant speed drive): hydro-mechanical transmission between engine gearbox and generator that converts variable engine rpm into constant generator rpm → constant 400 Hz. Monitored: oil temperature (in/out, rise), oil pressure (low pressure warning), generator frequency; drive disconnect on overheat/low pressure."],["021.09.04.01:04","EASA LO 021.09.04.01.04 → Explain the function of external power.","Priority sequence of sources: typically own engine generator > APU generator > external power (or external power has priority on the ground when connected); automatic transfer."],["021.09.04.02:03","EASA LO 021.09.04.02.03 → Describe the DC part of an electrical system of a transport aircraft (CS-25/CS-29) including the distribution consequences of loss of DC supply or bus failure.","Transport (CS-25/29): DC from TRUs (AC → 28 V DC) and batteries, DC buses, essential/battery/hot buses; loss of DC supply → battery/emergency buses. / Multi-engine (CS-23/27): two generators feeding split/paralleled buses with bus ties; one generator lost → other takes all (load shedding); bus failure → loss of connected consumers."],["021.09.04.03:01","EASA LO 021.09.04.03.01 → Explain the difference in the principle of operation for a split AC electrical system and a parallel AC electrical system.","Parallel system: all generators connected to a common tie bus — share load, uninterrupted supply when one fails, but need equal voltage, frequency, phase sequence and phase angle to parallel (and load-sharing control)."],["021.09.04.03:02","EASA LO 021.09.04.03.02 → Describe the following distribution consequences: — power transfer between different power supplies; — power transfer in the event of a supply failure;","Priority sequence of sources: typically own engine generator > APU generator > external power (or external power has priority on the ground when connected); automatic transfer."],["021.09.04.04:03","EASA LO 021.09.04.04.03 → Describe the requirement for monitoring the aircraft batteries.","Battery monitoring: voltage, charge current, temperature — NiCd and lithium batteries can go into thermal runaway → overheat warnings, charger disconnect."]],"19":[["062.02.05.01:01",": what do you remember about this? → Stage topics: holdings and reversal procedures (GSPRM Introduction) + …","Stage topics: holdings and reversal procedures (GSPRM Introduction) + review of the ILS (LOs 062.02.05 — full detail on #5)."],["062.02.05.01:01","Holding procedures (PANS-OPS, see also #90): fill in the gaps → Standard holding: right turns, inbound leg ____ at/below ____ ____, 1.5 min above; bank 25° or rate 3°/s (whichever less). Outbound timing starts over or abeam the fix, whichever is later; DME holdings: outbound leg ends at the specified DME distance.","Standard holding: right turns, inbound leg 1 min at/below 14 000 ft, 1.5 min above; bank 25° or rate 3°/s (whichever less). Outbound timing starts over or abeam the fix, whichever is later; DME holdings: outbound leg ends at the specified DME distance."],["062.02.05.01:10","Holding procedures (PANS-OPS, see also #90): fill in the gaps → ____ (sectors relative to the ____, ±5° flexibility): ____ (110°): fly outbound parallel to the inbound track on the non-holding side, then turn into the holding side back to the fix.","Entries (sectors relative to the inbound track, ±5° flexibility): Sector 1 — parallel (110°): fly outbound parallel to the inbound track on the non-holding side, then turn into the holding side back to the fix."],["062.02.05.01:01","Holding procedures (PANS-OPS, see also #90): fill in the gaps → ____ (70°): outbound on a track ____ offset into the holding side, then turn to intercept inbound. ____ (180°): fly to the fix and turn directly into the pattern. Outbound entry time 1 min/1.5 min (still air) or to the DME limit.","Sector 2 — offset/teardrop (70°): outbound on a track 30° offset into the holding side, then turn to intercept inbound. Sector 3 — direct (180°): fly to the fix and turn directly into the pattern. Outbound entry time 1 min/1.5 min (still air) or to the DME limit."],["062.02.05.01:01","Holding procedures (PANS-OPS, see also #90): fill in the gaps → Quick method: draw the inbound track through the fix; the line at ____ from the fix on the holding side separates sectors 1 and 2; the extension of the inbound course separates 3 from the others.","Quick method: draw the inbound track through the fix; the line at 70° from the fix on the holding side separates sectors 1 and 2; the extension of the inbound course separates 3 from the others."],["062.02.05.01:01","Holding procedures (PANS-OPS, see also #90): fill in the gaps → ____: outbound ____ (triple drift), ____ to obtain a 1-min inbound leg (headwind inbound → shorter outbound, tailwind inbound → longer).","Wind correction: outbound heading drift × 3 (triple drift), adjust outbound time to obtain a 1-min inbound leg (headwind inbound → shorter outbound, tailwind inbound → longer)."],["062.02.05.01:01","Holding procedures (PANS-OPS, see also #90): fill in the gaps → Speeds (ICAO): ≤ 14 000 ft ____ (Cat A/B 170 kt), 14 000–20 000 ft ____, 20 000–34 000 ft ____, above M 0.83 (turbulence: up to 280 kt/M 0.8 with ATC approval).","Speeds (ICAO): ≤ 14 000 ft 230 kt (Cat A/B 170 kt), 14 000–20 000 ft 240 kt, 20 000–34 000 ft 265 kt, above M 0.83 (turbulence: up to 280 kt/M 0.8 with ATC approval)."],["062.02.05.01:13","Reversal procedures: what do you remember about this? → Used when the aircraft arrives at the IAF on a heading opposite to …","Used when the aircraft arrives at the IAF on a heading opposite to the final approach:"],["062.02.05.01:01","Reversal procedures: fill in the gaps → ____: outbound on the specified track, turn ____ away (fly 1 min Cat A/B, 1 min 15 s Cat C/D/E from start of turn), then a ____ turn in the opposite direction to intercept the inbound track. 80°/260° variant: turn 80° then immediately 260° the other way.","Procedure turn 45°/180°: outbound on the specified track, turn 45° away (fly 1 min Cat A/B, 1 min 15 s Cat C/D/E from start of turn), then a 180° turn in the opposite direction to intercept the inbound track. 80°/260° variant: turn 80° then immediately 260° the other way."],["062.02.05.01:01","Reversal procedures: fill in the gaps → ____: outbound on a track diverging from the reciprocal of the inbound track (e.g. 30°), for a specified time/distance, then a turn onto the inbound track.","Base turn (teardrop): outbound on a track diverging from the reciprocal of the inbound track (e.g. 30°), for a specified time/distance, then a turn onto the inbound track."],["062.02.05.01:01","Reversal procedures: fill in the gaps → ____: holding-like pattern (1–3 min outbound) used when a reversal is needed but the aircraft can enter as a holding; entries as for holdings (offset limited).","Racetrack: holding-like pattern (1–3 min outbound) used when a reversal is needed but the aircraft can enter as a holding; entries as for holdings (offset limited)."],["062.02.05.04:02","Reversal procedures: fill in the gaps → Remain within the ____ by observing the published ____ (category-dependent).","Remain within the protected area by observing the published times, distances, altitudes and speed limits (category-dependent)."],["062.02.05.01:05","ILS review: fill in the gaps → ____ 108.10–111.95 MHz (odd first decimal), GP UHF paired; ____ Hz left/above, ____ right/below (DDM); side lobes → false beams (intercept GP from below); back beam → NPA.","LOC 108.10–111.95 MHz (odd first decimal), GP UHF paired; 90 Hz left/above, 150 Hz right/below (DDM); side lobes → false beams (intercept GP from below); back beam → NPA."],["062.02.05.01:02","ILS review: fill in the gaps → ____ (~300 ft/NM), GP antenna ~300 m past threshold, offset; LOC antenna beyond stop end.","GP 3° (~300 ft/NM), GP antenna ~300 m past threshold, offset; LOC antenna beyond stop end."],["062.02.05.01:01","ILS review: fill in the gaps → ____: OM 400 Hz dashes ____, MM 1 300 Hz dot-dash ____, IM 3 000 Hz dots white; OM/DME used to check GP– altimeter.","Markers 75 MHz: OM 400 Hz dashes blue, MM 1 300 Hz dot-dash amber, IM 3 000 Hz dots white; OM/DME used to check GP– altimeter."],["062.02.05.02:08","ILS review: fill in the gaps → Ident Morse (I-XX), ____ (course shift/signal loss → off), flags on loss of carrier/modulation. ____, GP ____. Back beam: CDI reversed, HSI set front course.","Ident Morse (I-XX), ground monitoring (course shift/signal loss → off), flags on loss of carrier/modulation. Full scale LOC 2.5°, GP 0.7°. Back beam: CDI reversed, HSI set front course."],["062.02.05.03:01","ILS review: fill in the gaps → ____: LOC ±10°/25 NM, ±35°/17 NM; GP ±8°/10 NM, 0.45θ– 1.75θ.","Coverage: LOC ±10°/25 NM, ±35°/17 NM; GP ±8°/10 NM, 0.45θ– 1.75θ."],["062.02.05.04:04","ILS review: fill in the gaps → ____: CAT I (200 ft), CAT II (50 ft), CAT III A/B/C (to/along the runway). Established within ____; > half-scale LOC or half-scale fly-up GP → go-around. ____, multipath; critical area (excluded), sensitive area (controlled).","Categories: CAT I (200 ft), CAT II (50 ft), CAT III A/B/C (to/along the runway). Established within half-scale; > half-scale LOC or half-scale fly-up GP → go-around. Beam bends, multipath; critical area (excluded), sensitive area (controlled)."],["062.02.05.01:01","Exam trap (ILS review): complete the fact → Parallel entry sector ____°, offset ____° (30° offset), direct 180°.","Parallel entry sector 110°, offset 70° (30° offset), direct 180°."],["062.02.05.01:01","Exam trap (ILS review): complete the fact → ____°/____° PT: 1 min (A/B), 1 min 15 s (C–E).","45°/180° PT: 1 min (A/B), 1 min 15 s (C–E)."],["062.02.05.01:08","EASA LO 062.02.05.01.08 → State that the UHF GP frequency is selected automatically by being paired with the LOC frequency.","LOC 108.10–111.95 MHz (odd first decimal), GP UHF paired; 90 Hz left/above, 150 Hz right/below (DDM); side lobes → false beams (intercept GP from below); back beam → NPA."],["062.02.05.01:09","EASA LO 062.02.05.01.09 → Explain that both the LOC and the GP antenna radiates side lobes (false beams) which can give rise to false centre-line and false GP indication.","LOC 108.10–111.95 MHz (odd first decimal), GP UHF paired; 90 Hz left/above, 150 Hz right/below (DDM); side lobes → false beams (intercept GP from below); back beam → NPA."],["062.02.05.02:03","EASA LO 062.02.05.02.03 → State that the LOC and GP monitoring system monitors any shift in the LOC and GP mean course line or reduction in signal strength.","Ident Morse (I-XX), ground monitoring (course shift/signal loss → off), flags on loss of carrier/modulation. Full scale LOC 2.5°, GP 0.7°. Back beam: CDI reversed, HSI set front course."],["062.02.05.02:05","EASA LO 062.02.05.02.05 → Describe the circumstances in which warning flags will appear for both the LOC and the GP: — absence of the carrier frequency; — absence of the modulation simultaneously;","Ident Morse (I-XX), ground monitoring (course shift/signal loss → off), flags on loss of carrier/modulation. Full scale LOC 2.5°, GP 0.7°. Back beam: CDI reversed, HSI set front course."],["062.02.05.02:06","EASA LO 062.02.05.02.06 → Interpret the indications on a CDI and an HSI: — full-scale deflection of the CDI needle corresponds to approximately 2.5° displacement from the ILS centre line; — full-scale deflection on the GP corresponds to approximately 0.7° from the ILS GP centre line.","Ident Morse (I-XX), ground monitoring (course shift/signal loss → off), flags on loss of carrier/modulation. Full scale LOC 2.5°, GP 0.7°. Back beam: CDI reversed, HSI set front course."],["062.02.05.04:06","EASA LO 062.02.05.04.06 → State that if a pilot deviates by more than half-course deflection on the LOC or by more than half-dot deflection on the GP, an immediate go-around should be executed because obstacle clearance may no longer be guaranteed.","Categories: CAT I (200 ft), CAT II (50 ft), CAT III A/B/C (to/along the runway). Established within half-scale; > half-scale LOC or half-scale fly-up GP → go-around. Beam bends, multipath; critical area (excluded), sensitive area (controlled)."],["062.02.05.04:08","EASA LO 062.02.05.04.08 → Explain that multipath interference is caused by reflections from objects within the ILS coverage area.","Categories: CAT I (200 ft), CAT II (50 ft), CAT III A/B/C (to/along the runway). Established within half-scale; > half-scale LOC or half-scale fly-up GP → go-around. Beam bends, multipath; critical area (excluded), sensitive area (controlled)."],["062.02.05.05:01","EASA LO 062.02.05.05.01 → Define the ‘ILS-critical area’: an area of defined dimensions around the LOC and GP antennas where vehicles, including aircraft, are excluded during all ILS operations.","Categories: CAT I (200 ft), CAT II (50 ft), CAT III A/B/C (to/along the runway). Established within half-scale; > half-scale LOC or half-scale fly-up GP → go-around. Beam bends, multipath; critical area (excluded), sensitive area (controlled)."],["062.02.05.05:02","EASA LO 062.02.05.05.02 → Define the ‘ILS-sensitive area’: an area extending beyond the ILS-critical area where the parking or movement of vehicles, including aircraft, is controlled to prevent the possibility of unacceptable interference to the ILS signal during ILS operations.","Categories: CAT I (200 ft), CAT II (50 ft), CAT III A/B/C (to/along the runway). Established within half-scale; > half-scale LOC or half-scale fly-up GP → go-around. Beam bends, multipath; critical area (excluded), sensitive area (controlled)."]],"20":[["081.03.01.01:06","Flow separation & the stall: fill in the gaps → ____: thin layer in which the velocity rises from ____ to the free-stream value (viscosity). ____: thin, smooth layers, low skin friction, but little energy → separates easily in an adverse pressure gradient.","Boundary layer (BL): thin layer in which the velocity rises from zero at the surface to the free-stream value (viscosity). Laminar BL: thin, smooth layers, low skin friction, but little energy → separates easily in an adverse pressure gradient."],["081.03.01.01:05","Flow separation & the stall: fill in the gaps → ____: thicker, mixing brings energy from outside → ____ ____ but resists separation better. Transition point: where the BL changes from laminar to turbulent.","Turbulent BL: thicker, mixing brings energy from outside → more friction but resists separation better. Transition point: where the BL changes from laminar to turbulent."],["081.03.01.01:07","Flow separation & the stall: fill in the gaps → As α increases, the pressure recovery over the aft upper surface becomes steeper (____) → airflow over the aft part ____ and the ____ forward from the TE.","As α increases, the pressure recovery over the aft upper surface becomes steeper (adverse pressure gradient) → airflow over the aft part slows down and the separation point moves forward from the TE."],["081.03.01.01:10","Flow separation & the stall: fill in the gaps → ________ = angle of attack at CLmax — beyond it lift decreases (stall). In straight and level flight increasing α → speed decreases, CL increases, then ____, reduced control effectiveness (ailerons may be ineffective or reversed near the stall), stall warning, nose drop/wing drop at the stall.","αcrit = angle of attack at CLmax — beyond it lift decreases (stall). In straight and level flight increasing α → speed decreases, CL increases, then buffet, reduced control effectiveness (ailerons may be ineffective or reversed near the stall), stall warning, nose drop/wing drop at the stall."],["081.03.01.01:11","Flow separation & the stall: fill in the gaps → ____: the separated, turbulent wake hits the tailplane/airframe → vibration. ____ decreases near the stall (ailerons at high α can cause tip stall — use rudder to keep wings level).","Natural (pre-stall) buffet: the separated, turbulent wake hits the tailplane/airframe → vibration. Control effectiveness decreases near the stall (ailerons at high α can cause tip stall — use rudder to keep wings level)."],["081.03.01.01:13","Flow separation & the stall: fill in the gaps → ____: lift loss → ____ (CP moves aft, less downwash on tail), possible wing drop; recover by reducing α.","Post-stall (straight wing): lift loss → nose drops (CP moves aft, less downwash on tail), possible wing drop; recover by reducing α."],["081.03.01.01:07","Flow separation & the stall: fill in the gaps → Using ailerons near the stall: the down-going aileron increases local α → that wing stalls first → ____.","Using ailerons near the stall: the down-going aileron increases local α → that wing stalls first → wing drop/spin entry."],["081.03.01.01:15","Flow separation & the stall: fill in the gaps → ____: buffet so strong that it deters the pilot from further α increase — accepted as the ____ (e.g. for swept-wing jets without a clear CLmax break).","Deterrent buffet: buffet so strong that it deters the pilot from further α increase — accepted as the stall limit (e.g. for swept-wing jets without a clear CLmax break)."],["081.03.01.02:01","Stall speed: fill in the gaps → V____ = stall speed in landing configuration; V____ = stall speed in a specified configuration; VSR = reference stall speed (CS-25, ≥ VS1g); V____ = stall speed at 1 g (CLmax at n = 1).","VS0 = stall speed in landing configuration; VS1 = stall speed in a specified configuration; VSR = reference stall speed (CS-25, ≥ VS1g); VS1g = stall speed at 1 g (CLmax at n = 1)."],["081.03.01.02:03","Stall speed: fill in the gaps → ________ ____Lmax)) (in EAS: independent of altitude except Mach effects). Factors: forward CG → higher VS (more tail download = more lift needed); thrust component (thrust tilted up at high α supports part of the weight → lower VS power-on); slipstream over the wing → lower VS (props); wing loading/mass ↑ → VS ↑;","VS = √(2W / (ρ·S·CLmax)) (in EAS: independent of altitude except Mach effects). Factors: forward CG → higher VS (more tail download = more lift needed); thrust component (thrust tilted up at high α supports part of the weight → lower VS power-on); slipstream over the wing → lower VS (props); wing loading/mass ↑ → VS ↑;"],["081.03.01.02:03","Stall speed: fill in the gaps → ____ (ice/frost) → lower CLmax and αcrit → VS ↑; ____ → lower CLmax → higher VS; ____: EAS stall speed constant at low Mach, but rises at high altitude due to compressibility (lower CLmax at high Mach).","contamination (ice/frost) → lower CLmax and αcrit → VS ↑; sweep → lower CLmax → higher VS; altitude: EAS stall speed constant at low Mach, but rises at high altitude due to compressibility (lower CLmax at high Mach)."],["081.03.01.02:06","Stall speed: fill in the gaps → ____. In a level turn ____; in a ____ n > 1, in a push-over n < 1.","Load factor n = L/W. In a level turn n = 1/cos Ø; in a pull-up n > 1, in a push-over n < 1."],["081.03.01.02:04","Stall speed: fill in the gaps → VS(n) = V____ × √n → accelerated stall = stall at a speed above VS1g because of load factor (not \"high-speed stall\"). Turn: ____ ____, 45° → × 1.19, 60° → × 1.41. Mass: VS2 = VS1 × √(m₂/m₁) (+21 % mass → +10 % VS). Example: VS 100 kt, 60° bank → 141 kt.","VS(n) = VS1g × √n → accelerated stall = stall at a speed above VS1g because of load factor (not \"high-speed stall\"). Turn: 30° → × 1.07, 45° → × 1.19, 60° → × 1.41. Mass: VS2 = VS1 × √(m₂/m₁) (+21 % mass → +10 % VS). Example: VS 100 kt, 60° bank → 141 kt."],["081.03.01.03:01","Spanwise stall progression: fill in the gaps → ____ wing: stalls ____ across the span (no warning, aileron loss). ____: stalls at the root first — good (aileron control kept, buffet on tail). Moderate taper: stall starts mid-span/outboard; high taper and sweepback/delta: tip stall first (spanwise flow thickens tip BL, higher local CL) → loss of aileron control, CP moves forward → pitch-up.","Elliptical wing: stalls evenly across the span (no warning, aileron loss). Rectangular: stalls at the root first — good (aileron control kept, buffet on tail). Moderate taper: stall starts mid-span/outboard; high taper and sweepback/delta: tip stall first (spanwise flow thickens tip BL, higher local CL) → loss of aileron control, CP moves forward → pitch-up."],["081.03.01.03:02","Spanwise stall progression: fill in the gaps → ____ (geometric: tip incidence lower; aerodynamic: tip section with higher αcrit) → root stalls first.","Washout (geometric: tip incidence lower; aerodynamic: tip section with higher αcrit) → root stalls first."],["081.03.01.03:04","Spanwise stall progression: fill in the gaps → ____ stop or energise ____ and create vortices; ____ energise the BL (delay separation); nacelle strakes create a vortex over the wing at high α to improve CLmax.","Fences/vortilons/saw teeth (dogtooth) stop or energise spanwise flow and create vortices; vortex generators energise the BL (delay separation); nacelle strakes create a vortex over the wing at high α to improve CLmax."],["081.03.01.01:11","Stall warning: fill in the gaps → Needed because the stall causes sudden lift loss, altitude loss, possible spin. ____ (natural buffet) when the design gives adequate natural buffet; ____ (horn, light, stick shaker) when natural warning is insufficient (swept wings, clean jets, flaps). Warning at least ____ above the stall (CS-23/CS-25).","Needed because the stall causes sudden lift loss, altitude loss, possible spin. Aerodynamic warning (natural buffet) when the design gives adequate natural buffet; artificial (horn, light, stick shaker) when natural warning is insufficient (swept wings, clean jets, flaps). Warning at least 5 kt or 5 % above the stall (CS-23/CS-25)."],["081.03.01.04:03","Stall warning: fill in the gaps → CS-23/25 require ____ for take-off and landing speeds (e.g. V2 ≥ 1.13 VSR, VREF ≥ 1.23 VSR0 / 1.3 VS0) to allow for gusts, manoeuvring, errors.","CS-23/25 require margins above the stall for take-off and landing speeds (e.g. V2 ≥ 1.13 VSR, VREF ≥ 1.23 VSR0 / 1.3 VS0) to allow for gusts, manoeuvring, errors."],["081.03.01.04:04","Stall warning: fill in the gaps → ____ (vibration of separated flow); ____ (small sharp wedge on the inboard leading edge that forces the root to stall first — warning + aileron control).","Buffet (vibration of separated flow); stall strip (small sharp wedge on the inboard leading edge that forces the root to stall first — warning + aileron control)."],["081.03.01.04:05","Stall warning: fill in the gaps → ____: after ____: reduce α (push), apply power, level wings. After ____: reduce α first (nose down/push until unstalled), wings level (rudder/ailerons), then thrust, recover without secondary stall. After stick-pusher actuation: do not fight it — let the nose go down, then recover.","Recovery: after stall warning: reduce α (push), apply power, level wings. After stall: reduce α first (nose down/push until unstalled), wings level (rudder/ailerons), then thrust, recover without secondary stall. After stick-pusher actuation: do not fight it — let the nose go down, then recover."],["081.03.01.05:02","Special phenomena: fill in the gaps → ____ (e.g. take-off/go-around): lower stall speed, more pronounced wing drop (torque, slipstream), higher nose attitude; ____: gentler; recovery: power-on → reduce α, power as needed; power-off → reduce α, add power.","Power-on stall (e.g. take-off/go-around): lower stall speed, more pronounced wing drop (torque, slipstream), higher nose attitude; power-off: gentler; recovery: power-on → reduce α, power as needed; power-off → reduce α, add power."],["081.03.01.05:03","Special phenomena: fill in the gaps → ____: in a ____ the ____ has the higher α (inner wing climbs on a steeper helix) → outer wing stalls first; in a descending turn the inner wing has the higher α → stalls first → roll into/out of the turn, spin tendency. Recover by unloading (reduce α), levelling wings, power.","Stall in turns: in a climbing turn the outer wing has the higher α (inner wing climbs on a steeper helix) → outer wing stalls first; in a descending turn the inner wing has the higher α → stalls first → roll into/out of the turn, spin tendency. Recover by unloading (reduce α), levelling wings, power."],["081.03.01.05:05","Special phenomena: fill in the gaps → ____: tip stall moves CP forward → nose-up (towards deep stall). ____: at high α the tail is blanked by the separated wake → ____ — stable stalled condition with elevator ineffective → stick pusher prevents reaching it (pushes the stick forward at a set α).","Swept wing pitch-up: tip stall moves CP forward → nose-up (towards deep stall). T-tail: at high α the tail is blanked by the separated wake → deep (super) stall — stable stalled condition with elevator ineffective → stick pusher prevents reaching it (pushes the stick forward at a set α)."],["081.03.01.05:08","Special phenomena: fill in the gaps → ____: ice contamination (stall at lower α than the warning), wrong configuration settings, sensor failure → very dangerous.","Absence of stall warning: ice contamination (stall at lower α than the warning), wrong configuration settings, sensor failure → very dangerous."],["081.03.01.05:08","Special phenomena: fill in the gaps → ____ due to ice: tail has a smaller LE radius → accretes ice faster; with ____ (more downwash → higher negative tail α) the tail may stall → sudden ____, stick lightening, buffet in the controls.","Tailplane (negative tail) stall due to ice: tail has a smaller LE radius → accretes ice faster; with flap extension (more downwash → higher negative tail α) the tail may stall → sudden nose-down pitch, stick lightening, buffet in the controls."],["081.03.01.05:09","Special phenomena: fill in the gaps → Recovery: ____ to the previous setting, apply ____ ____, don't increase speed/power abruptly (follow AFM). Expect in-flight icing in visible moisture at OAT ~+5 to −20 °C.","Recovery: retract flaps to the previous setting, apply nose-up elevator, don't increase speed/power abruptly (follow AFM). Expect in-flight icing in visible moisture at OAT ~+5 to −20 °C."],["081.03.01.05:12","Special phenomena: fill in the gaps → ____: frost/ice/snow → ____Lmax ____ αcrit ↓, VS ↑, drag ↑, stall warning may come too late.","Contaminated wing: frost/ice/snow → CLmax ↓ (up to ~30 %), αcrit ↓, VS ↑, drag ↑, stall warning may come too late."],["081.03.01.05:14","Special phenomena: fill in the gaps → ____ ____: contamination on parked aircraft (frost, snow, freezing rain); take-off with contamination → lift loss at rotation, roll-off, stall in ground effect → de-icing/anti-icing required; after ____ expires the fluid no longer protects → re-contamination → re-treat.","Winter ground operations: contamination on parked aircraft (frost, snow, freezing rain); take-off with contamination → lift loss at rotation, roll-off, stall in ground effect → de-icing/anti-icing required; after holdover time expires the fluid no longer protects → re-contamination → re-treat."],["081.03.01.05:15","Special phenomena: fill in the gaps → ____: water film roughens the surface → ________ ↓, drag ↑, higher stall speed, also mass of water → use higher speeds/thrust margins, avoid heavy rain on approach/take-off.","Heavy tropical rain: water film roughens the surface → CLmax ↓, drag ↑, higher stall speed, also mass of water → use higher speeds/thrust margins, avoid heavy rain on approach/take-off."],["081.03.01.06:01","Spin: fill in the gaps → ____ by not stalling in uncoordinated flight (yaw at the stall), keeping the ball centred, correct CG, not using aileron to pick up a dropping wing.","Avoid by not stalling in uncoordinated flight (yaw at the stall), keeping the ball centred, correct CG, not using aileron to pick up a dropping wing."],["081.03.01.06:01","Spin: fill in the gaps → ____: stall + ____ (rudder, asymmetric thrust, adverse yaw, slip/skid) → one wing more stalled → ____.","Causes: stall + yaw (rudder, asymmetric thrust, adverse yaw, slip/skid) → one wing more stalled → autorotation."],["081.03.01.06:03","Spin: fill in the gaps → ____: first ~2 turns, not yet stable — early recovery easier. ____: stable rotation, high rate of descent, α above critical on both wings. Recovery (standard PARE): ____ idle, Ailerons neutral, Rudder full opposite, Elevator forward until rotation stops, then neutralise rudder, recover from the dive.","Incipient spin: first ~2 turns, not yet stable — early recovery easier. Developed spin: stable rotation, high rate of descent, α above critical on both wings. Recovery (standard PARE): Power idle, Ailerons neutral, Rudder full opposite, Elevator forward until rotation stops, then neutralise rudder, recover from the dive."],["081.03.01.06:04","Spin: fill in the gaps → ____: ____; ____ → flatter spin, higher rotation, more difficult or impossible recovery.","CG: forward CG → steeper nose-down spin, easier recovery; aft CG → flatter spin, higher rotation, more difficult or impossible recovery."],["081.03.01.01:01","EASA LO 081.03.01.01.01 → Define the ‘boundary layer’.","Boundary layer (BL): thin layer in which the velocity rises from zero at the surface to the free-stream value (viscosity). Laminar BL: thin, smooth layers, low skin friction, but little energy → separates easily in an adverse pressure gradient."],["081.03.01.01:02","EASA LO 081.03.01.01.02 → Describe the thickness of a typical laminar and turbulent boundary layer.","Turbulent BL: thicker, mixing brings energy from outside → more friction but resists separation better. Transition point: where the BL changes from laminar to turbulent."],["081.03.01.01:08","EASA LO 081.03.01.01.08 → Define the ‘separation point’ and describe its location as a function of α.","Turbulent BL: thicker, mixing brings energy from outside → more friction but resists separation better. Transition point: where the BL changes from laminar to turbulent."],["081.03.01.01:09","EASA LO 081.03.01.01.09 → Define αCRIT.","Washout (geometric: tip incidence lower; aerodynamic: tip section with higher αcrit) → root stalls first. / αcrit = angle of attack at CLmax — beyond it lift decreases (stall). In straight and level flight increasing α → speed decreases, CL increases, then buffet, reduced control effectiveness (ailerons may be ineffective or reversed near the stall), stall warning, nose drop/wing drop at the stall."],["081.03.01.01:12","EASA LO 081.03.01.01.12 → Describe the effectiveness of the flight controls in a pre-stall condition.","αcrit = angle of attack at CLmax — beyond it lift decreases (stall). In straight and level flight increasing α → speed decreases, CL increases, then buffet, reduced control effectiveness (ailerons may be ineffective or reversed near the stall), stall warning, nose drop/wing drop at the stall. / Natural (pre-stall) buffet: the separated, turbulent wake hits the tailplane/airframe → vibration. Control effectiveness decreases near the stall (ailerons at high α can cause tip stall — use rudder to keep wings level)."],["081.03.01.01:16","EASA LO 081.03.01.01.16 → Explain the occurrence of the deterrent buffet and why this phenomenon is considered to be a stall limit.","Deterrent buffet: buffet so strong that it deters the pilot from further α increase — accepted as the stall limit (e.g. for swept-wing jets without a clear CLmax break)."],["081.03.01.02:02","EASA LO 081.03.01.02.02 → Solve VS1G from the lift formula given varying CL.","VS0 = stall speed in landing configuration; VS1 = stall speed in a specified configuration; VSR = reference stall speed (CS-25, ≥ VS1g); VS1g = stall speed at 1 g (CLmax at n = 1)."],["081.03.01.02:05","EASA LO 081.03.01.02.05 → Explain why the load factor increases in a turn.","Load factor n = L/W. In a level turn n = 1/cos Ø; in a pull-up n > 1, in a push-over n < 1. / VS(n) = VS1g × √n → accelerated stall = stall at a speed above VS1g because of load factor (not \"high-speed stall\"). Turn: 30° → × 1.07, 45° → × 1.19, 60° → × 1.41. Mass: VS2 = VS1 × √(m₂/m₁) (+21 % mass → +10 % VS). Example: VS 100 kt, 60° bank → 141 kt."],["081.03.01.02:07","EASA LO 081.03.01.02.07 → Describe and explain the influence of the ‘load factor n’ on stall speed.","Load factor n = L/W. In a level turn n = 1/cos Ø; in a pull-up n > 1, in a push-over n < 1. / VS(n) = VS1g × √n → accelerated stall = stall at a speed above VS1g because of load factor (not \"high-speed stall\"). Turn: 30° → × 1.07, 45° → × 1.19, 60° → × 1.41. Mass: VS2 = VS1 × √(m₂/m₁) (+21 % mass → +10 % VS). Example: VS 100 kt, 60° bank → 141 kt."],["081.03.01.02:08","EASA LO 081.03.01.02.08 → Explain the expression ‘accelerated stall’. Remark: Sometimes, accelerated stall is also erroneously referred to as high-speed stall. This latter expression will not be used for Subject 081.","VS(n) = VS1g × √n → accelerated stall = stall at a speed above VS1g because of load factor (not \"high-speed stall\"). Turn: 30° → × 1.07, 45° → × 1.19, 60° → × 1.41. Mass: VS2 = VS1 × √(m₂/m₁) (+21 % mass → +10 % VS). Example: VS 100 kt, 60° bank → 141 kt."],["081.03.01.02:09","EASA LO 081.03.01.02.09 → Calculate the change of stall speed as a function of the load factor.","Load factor n = L/W. In a level turn n = 1/cos Ø; in a pull-up n > 1, in a push-over n < 1. / VS(n) = VS1g × √n → accelerated stall = stall at a speed above VS1g because of load factor (not \"high-speed stall\"). Turn: 30° → × 1.07, 45° → × 1.19, 60° → × 1.41. Mass: VS2 = VS1 × √(m₂/m₁) (+21 % mass → +10 % VS). Example: VS 100 kt, 60° bank → 141 kt."],["081.03.01.02:10","EASA LO 081.03.01.02.10 → Calculate the increase of stall speed in a horizontal coordinated turn as a function of bank angle.","VS(n) = VS1g × √n → accelerated stall = stall at a speed above VS1g because of load factor (not \"high-speed stall\"). Turn: 30° → × 1.07, 45° → × 1.19, 60° → × 1.41. Mass: VS2 = VS1 × √(m₂/m₁) (+21 % mass → +10 % VS). Example: VS 100 kt, 60° bank → 141 kt. / Load factor n = L/W. In a level turn n = 1/cos Ø; in a pull-up n > 1, in a push-over n < 1."],["081.03.01.05:04","EASA LO 081.03.01.05.04 → Describe the pitch-up effect on a swept wing aeroplane and also an aeroplane with a T-tail.","Swept wing pitch-up: tip stall moves CP forward → nose-up (towards deep stall). T-tail: at high α the tail is blanked by the separated wake → deep (super) stall — stable stalled condition with elevator ineffective → stick pusher prevents reaching it (pushes the stick forward at a set α)."],["081.03.01.05:06","EASA LO 081.03.01.05.06 → Describe the philosophy behind the stick-pusher system.","Swept wing pitch-up: tip stall moves CP forward → nose-up (towards deep stall). T-tail: at high α the tail is blanked by the separated wake → deep (super) stall — stable stalled condition with elevator ineffective → stick pusher prevents reaching it (pushes the stick forward at a set α)."],["081.03.01.05:13","EASA LO 081.03.01.05.13 → Explain the hazards associated with airframe contamination when parked and during ground operations in winter conditions, and the aerodynamic effects when attempting a take-off.","Winter ground operations: contamination on parked aircraft (frost, snow, freezing rain); take-off with contamination → lift loss at rotation, roll-off, stall in ground effect → de-icing/anti-icing required; after holdover time expires the fluid no longer protects → re-contamination → re-treat."],["081.03.01.06:02","EASA LO 081.03.01.06.02 → List the factors that cause a spin to develop.","CG: forward CG → steeper nose-down spin, easier recovery; aft CG → flatter spin, higher rotation, more difficult or impossible recovery."],["100.04.00.00:03","EASA LO 100.04.00.00.03 → Estimate the rate of climb or rate of descent, distance and time.","Incipient spin: first ~2 turns, not yet stable — early recovery easier. Developed spin: stable rotation, high rate of descent, α above critical on both wings. Recovery (standard PARE): Power idle, Ailerons neutral, Rudder full opposite, Elevator forward until rotation stops, then neutralise rudder, recover from the dive."]],"21":[["061.03.01.01:04","Great circles: fill in the gaps → ____: circle on the earth's surface whose plane passes through the ____ → divides the earth into two halves; ____ between two points is the shorter arc of the GC through them. Small circle: plane does not pass through the centre (e.g. parallels of latitude other than the equator).","Great circle (GC): circle on the earth's surface whose plane passes through the earth's centre → divides the earth into two halves; shortest distance between two points is the shorter arc of the GC through them. Small circle: plane does not pass through the centre (e.g. parallels of latitude other than the equator)."],["061.03.01.02:01","Great circles: fill in the gaps → Examples of GCs: ____, ____ ____, any route circle through the centre. The vertex = the point of highest latitude reached by a GC (where its track is exactly 090°/270°).","Examples of GCs: equator, every meridian with its anti- meridian, any route circle through the centre. The vertex = the point of highest latitude reached by a GC (where its track is exactly 090°/270°)."],["061.03.01.02:01","Great circles: fill in the gaps → The track of a GC (other than meridians/equator) ____ ____ because meridians ____ towards the poles — the GC crosses each meridian at a different angle.","The track of a GC (other than meridians/equator) changes continuously because meridians converge towards the poles — the GC crosses each meridian at a different angle."],["061.03.01.02:02","Great circles: fill in the gaps → ____ (angle between two meridians at a given latitude) ≈ ____. Example: Δlong 20° at mean lat 50° → 20 × 0.766 = ____ = GC track change between the two points.","Earth convergency (angle between two meridians at a given latitude) ≈ Δlong × sin(mean latitude). Example: Δlong 20° at mean lat 50° → 20 × 0.766 = 15.3° = GC track change between the two points."],["061.03.02.01:02","Rhumb lines: fill in the gaps → ____: line crossing ____ ____ (constant true track); spiral towards the pole. Meridians and the equator are both GC and RL; parallels of latitude are RLs (not GCs).","Rhumb line (RL, loxodrome): line crossing all meridians at the same angle (constant true track); spiral towards the pole. Meridians and the equator are both GC and RL; parallels of latitude are RLs (not GCs)."],["061.03.02.01:02","Rhumb lines: fill in the gaps → A RL is ____ route (except meridians/equator); the difference between GC and RL distance ____ ____ (large on long E–W routes at high latitude; nil N– S).","A RL is not the shortest route (except meridians/equator); the difference between GC and RL distance increases with latitude and with Δlong (large on long E–W routes at high latitude; nil N– S)."],["061.03.02.01:01","Rhumb lines: fill in the gaps → ____ = angle between the GC and RL at either end. In the NH the GC lies polewards of the RL (RL on the equatorial side). RL track = GC initial track ± CA, GC final track = RL track ± CA. Example: A → B eastbound at mid-lat 50°N, Δlong 20°: convergency 15.3°, CA 7.7°; if RL track = 090° → GC initial ____, GC final ____.","Conversion angle (CA) = ½ × convergency = angle between the GC and RL at either end. In the NH the GC lies polewards of the RL (RL on the equatorial side). RL track = GC initial track ± CA, GC final track = RL track ± CA. Example: A → B eastbound at mid-lat 50°N, Δlong 20°: convergency 15.3°, CA 7.7°; if RL track = 090° → GC initial 082.3°, GC final 097.7°."],["061.04.01.01:01","Chart requirements (ICAO Annex 4): fill in the gaps → Aeronautical charts should be ____ (correct angles/shapes locally; scale at a point the same in all directions) and a ____ ____.","Aeronautical charts should be conformal (correct angles/shapes locally; scale at a point the same in all directions) and a straight line should approximate a great circle."],["061.04.01.02:01","Chart requirements (ICAO Annex 4): fill in the gaps → ____ (Lambert). ____. A straight line on the chart ≈ GC where ____ — i.e. near the parallel of origin (Lambert), everywhere on polar stereographic near the pole (n = 1 ≈ sin 90°), never on Mercator (n = 0).","Constant of the cone n = sin(parallel of origin) (Lambert). Chart convergence = Δlong × n. A straight line on the chart ≈ GC where chart convergence = earth convergence — i.e. near the parallel of origin (Lambert), everywhere on polar stereographic near the pole (n = 1 ≈ sin 90°), never on Mercator (n = 0)."],["061.04.01.01:01","Chart requirements (ICAO Annex 4): fill in the gaps → ____: representative fraction (1:500 000), statement (\"1 cm = 5 km\"), graduated scale line. Scale = chart length / earth length (same units). Examples: 1:1 000 000 → 1 cm = 10 km = ____; 1:500 000 → 1 cm = 5 km = 2.7 NM; a 108 NM route (200 km) on a 1:500 000 chart = ____.","Scale: representative fraction (1:500 000), statement (\"1 cm = 5 km\"), graduated scale line. Scale = chart length / earth length (same units). Examples: 1:1 000 000 → 1 cm = 10 km = 5.4 NM; 1:500 000 → 1 cm = 5 km = 2.7 NM; a 108 NM route (200 km) on a 1:500 000 chart = 40 cm."],["061.03.01.01:01","EASA LO 061.03.01.01.01 → Describe the geometric properties of a great circle (including the vertex) and a small circle.","Examples of GCs: equator, every meridian with its anti- meridian, any route circle through the centre. The vertex = the point of highest latitude reached by a GC (where its track is exactly 090°/270°)."],["061.03.01.01:03","EASA LO 061.03.01.01.03 → Explain why a great-circle route is the shortest distance between any two positions on the Earth.","Great circle (GC): circle on the earth's surface whose plane passes through the earth's centre → divides the earth into two halves; shortest distance between two points is the shorter arc of the GC through them. Small circle: plane does not pass through the centre (e.g. parallels of latitude other than the equator). / A RL is not the shortest route (except meridians/equator); the difference between GC and RL distance increases with latitude and with Δlong (large on long E–W routes at high latitude; nil N– S)."],["061.03.03.01:01","EASA LO 061.03.03.01.01 → Explain that the variation in distance of the great-circle route and rhumb-line route between any two positions increases with increasing latitude or change in longitude.","A RL is not the shortest route (except meridians/equator); the difference between GC and RL distance increases with latitude and with Δlong (large on long E–W routes at high latitude; nil N– S). / Earth convergency (angle between two meridians at a given latitude) ≈ Δlong × sin(mean latitude). Example: Δlong 20° at mean lat 50° → 20 × 0.766 = 15.3° = GC track change between the two points."],["061.04.01.02:02","EASA LO 061.04.01.02.02 → Explain the relationship between Earth and chart convergence with respect to the ICAO requirement for a straight line to approximate a great circle.","Constant of the cone n = sin(parallel of origin) (Lambert). Chart convergence = Δlong × n. A straight line on the chart ≈ GC where chart convergence = earth convergence — i.e. near the parallel of origin (Lambert), everywhere on polar stereographic near the pole (n = 1 ≈ sin 90°), never on Mercator (n = 0). / Aeronautical charts should be conformal (correct angles/shapes locally; scale at a point the same in all directions) and a straight line should approximate a great circle."],["061.04.01.03:01","EASA LO 061.04.01.03.01 → Recognise methods of representing scale on aeronautical charts.","Aeronautical charts should be conformal (correct angles/shapes locally; scale at a point the same in all directions) and a straight line should approximate a great circle."]],"22":[["033.02.01.03:01","What is the difference between MEA and MOCA?","MEA = minimum en-route altitude: obstacle clearance AND NAVAID reception (and comms) on the airway segment. MOCA = obstacle clearance only (1 000 / 2 000 ft); NAV reception only guaranteed within ~22 NM of the aid."],["033.02.01.03:01","MSA: what clearance and what radius?","Minimum sector altitude: 1 000 ft clearance within 25 NM of the approach aid/fix, given per sector."],["033.02.01.03:01","What are route MORA and Grid MORA?","Jeppesen minimum off-route altitudes. Route MORA: 1 000 ft (terrain ≤ 5 000 ft) or 2 000 ft (> 5 000 ft) within 10 NM either side of the route. Grid MORA: same clearance within a 1° × 1° lat/long grid square, shown as large figures (55 = 5 500 ft)."],["033.02.01.03:01","What are MCA, MAA and MHA?","MCA = minimum crossing altitude at a fix (in flight direction, climb before the next higher MEA). MAA = maximum authorised altitude on a segment. MHA = minimum holding altitude."],["033.02.01.03:04","Minimum obstacle clearance for IFR en route and who is responsible for terrain clearance?","1 000 ft above the highest obstacle within 8 km (5 NM) of the estimated position; 2 000 ft in mountainous areas. Pilot is responsible, in or outside controlled airspace, unless radar vectored (then ATC assigns altitudes ≥ minimum vectoring altitude)."],["033.02.01.03:07","Minimum FL calculation: obstacle 5 500 ft, clearance 2 000 ft, ISA −10 °C, QNH 1003, track 090°. Which FL?","Required true altitude 7 500 ft → cold correction +4 % per 10 °C below ISA ≈ 7 800 ft → pressure alt = 7 800 + (1013 − 1003) × 30 = 8 100 ft → track 090° = odd levels → FL 90."],["033.02.01.03:06","Cold temperature: who corrects minimum altitudes?","Published minimum altitudes assume ISA. In cold weather the pilot must correct them (approach, MSA, DA/MDA). When radar vectoring, ATC applies the correction to assigned altitudes."],["033.02.01.01:02","Name the types of ATS routes and the three CDR categories.","Conventional (ground NAVAIDs), RNAV (waypoints, e.g. B-RNAV), RNP (with on-board monitoring), conditional routes (CDRs), direct (DCT) / free-route. CDR Cat 1 = permanently plannable, Cat 2 = not plannable in advance, Cat 3 = ATC tactical use."],["033.02.01.04:06","SID/STAR: what is an RNAV overlay SID/STAR?","A conventional SID/STAR coded as waypoints in the FMS database; it can be flown either conventionally or with RNAV. A pure RNAV SID/STAR needs RNAV/RNP equipment. SID/STAR charts are pictorial, not to scale."],["033.02.01.05:04","Approach classification: Type A vs Type B, 2D vs 3D.","Type A: MDH/DH ≥ 250 ft. Type B: DH < 250 ft (CAT I–III). 2D = lateral guidance only; 3D = lateral + vertical."],["033.02.01.05:04","Which approaches are NPA, which APV, which precision?","NPA: VOR, NDB, LOC, RNP APCH to LNAV or LP. APV: LNAV/VNAV (Baro-VNAV) and LPV (SBAS). Precision: ILS, MLS, GLS (GBAS). RNP AR APCH needs authorisation (RNP ≤ 0.3, curved paths)."],["033.02.01.07:09","Typical weaknesses of a GNSS/FMC flight plan?","Pilot input errors (FL, wind, temperature, fuel, ZFW), actual wind differs from forecast, non-standard configuration (gear/flap down, engine out) not detected so predictions are optimistic, database errors or outdated cycle."],["033.02.01.03:02","EASA LO 033.02.01.03.02 → Extract the following altitudes from the chart(s): — MEA; — MOCA; — MSA; — MORA; — Grid MORA; — MAA; — MCA; — MHA.","MCA = minimum crossing altitude at a fix (in flight direction, climb before the next higher MEA). MAA = maximum authorised altitude on a segment. MHA = minimum holding altitude. / Jeppesen minimum off-route altitudes. Route MORA: 1 000 ft (terrain ≤ 5 000 ft) or 2 000 ft (> 5 000 ft) within 10 NM either side of the route. Grid MORA: same clearance within a 1° × 1° lat/long grid square, shown as large figures (55 = 5 500 ft)."],["033.02.01.03:03","EASA LO 033.02.01.03.03 → State who is responsible for terrain separation during IFR flight inside and outside controlled airspace.","1 000 ft above the highest obstacle within 8 km (5 NM) of the estimated position; 2 000 ft in mountainous areas. Pilot is responsible, in or outside controlled airspace, unless radar vectored (then ATC assigns altitudes ≥ minimum vectoring altitude)."],["033.02.01.03:05","EASA LO 033.02.01.03.05 → State when a temperature error correction must be applied by either the pilot or ATC.","Published minimum altitudes assume ISA. In cold weather the pilot must correct them (approach, MSA, DA/MDA). When radar vectoring, ATC applies the correction to assigned altitudes."],["033.02.01.04:01","EASA LO 033.02.01.04.01 → State the reasons for studying SID and STAR charts.","A conventional SID/STAR coded as waypoints in the FMS database; it can be flown either conventionally or with RNAV. A pure RNAV SID/STAR needs RNAV/RNP equipment. SID/STAR charts are pictorial, not to scale."],["033.02.01.04:02","EASA LO 033.02.01.04.02 → State that SID and STAR charts show procedures only in a pictorial presentation style which may not be true to scale.","A conventional SID/STAR coded as waypoints in the FMS database; it can be flown either conventionally or with RNAV. A pure RNAV SID/STAR needs RNAV/RNP equipment. SID/STAR charts are pictorial, not to scale."],["033.02.01.04:03","EASA LO 033.02.01.04.03 → Interpret all data and information represented on SID and STAR charts, particularly: — routings; — distances; — courses; — radials;","A conventional SID/STAR coded as waypoints in the FMS database; it can be flown either conventionally or with RNAV. A pure RNAV SID/STAR needs RNAV/RNP equipment. SID/STAR charts are pictorial, not to scale."],["033.02.01.04:04","EASA LO 033.02.01.04.04 → Identify SID and STAR charts which might be relevant for a planned flight.","A conventional SID/STAR coded as waypoints in the FMS database; it can be flown either conventionally or with RNAV. A pure RNAV SID/STAR needs RNAV/RNP equipment. SID/STAR charts are pictorial, not to scale."],["033.02.01.04:05","EASA LO 033.02.01.04.05 → Define SID and STAR for RNAV only.","A conventional SID/STAR coded as waypoints in the FMS database; it can be flown either conventionally or with RNAV. A pure RNAV SID/STAR needs RNAV/RNP equipment. SID/STAR charts are pictorial, not to scale."],["033.02.01.07:02","EASA LO 033.02.01.07.02 → Find the SID and STAR routes to be flown or to be expected.","A conventional SID/STAR coded as waypoints in the FMS database; it can be flown either conventionally or with RNAV. A pure RNAV SID/STAR needs RNAV/RNP equipment. SID/STAR charts are pictorial, not to scale."]],"23":[["062.02.06.01:01",": what do you remember about this? → Review stage: interceptions, holdings and reversals (see #16 and #19) …","Review stage: interceptions, holdings and reversals (see #16 and #19) + MLS."],["062.02.06.01:01","MLS: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ — an ____ fan beam sweeps \"to\" and \"fro\" across the coverage; the aircraft measures the time interval between the two passes → angle from the centre line.","Principle: time-referenced scanning beams — an azimuth fan beam sweeps \"to\" and \"fro\" across the coverage; the aircraft measures the time interval between the two passes → angle from the centre line."],["062.02.06.01:01","MLS: fill in the gaps → Same for the ____ beam (vertical guidance), a ____ station gives guidance for ____ and missed approach, DME/P (precision DME) gives accurate distance, and data words transmit system/approach information (runway, station status, weather).","Same for the elevation beam (vertical guidance), a back-azimuth station gives guidance for departure and missed approach, DME/P (precision DME) gives accurate distance, and data words transmit system/approach information (runway, station status, weather)."],["062.02.06.01:03","MLS: fill in the gaps → ____. Advantages over ILS: less sensitive to reflections/siting (buildings, terrain) → installable where ILS siting is difficult; wide coverage; many channels; no large critical areas; curved/segmented approaches.","SHF band (5 031–5 090.7 MHz), 200 channels. Advantages over ILS: less sensitive to reflections/siting (buildings, terrain) → installable where ILS siting is difficult; wide coverage; many channels; no large critical areas; curved/segmented approaches."],["062.02.06.02:02","MLS: fill in the gaps → ____ continuously shows position relative to a ____ ____, with distance, for approach and departure. With a computer programmed with the approach, segmented/curved approaches are flown using two command cross bars — possible only with DME/P (3D position needed).","Airborne display continuously shows position relative to a pre- selected course and glide path, with distance, for approach and departure. With a computer programmed with the approach, segmented/curved approaches are flown using two command cross bars — possible only with DME/P (3D position needed)."],["062.02.06.02:05","MLS: fill in the gaps → ____ MLS gives an ____ ____ approach (angle guidance only).","Without DME/P MLS gives an ILS-lookalike straight- in approach (angle guidance only)."],["062.02.06.02:04","MLS: fill in the gaps → ____: one receiver for ____ ____ → saves weight/space, flexibility at airports with different systems.","MMR (multimode receiver): one receiver for ILS, MLS and GNSS (GLS) → saves weight/space, flexibility at airports with different systems."],["062.02.06.03:01","MLS: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ from the threshold (elevation up to 15°, 20 000 ft).","Coverage (approach): ±40° of the centre line to 20 NM from the threshold (elevation up to 15°, 20 000 ft)."],["062.02.06.01:01","Review — interceptions: what do you remember about this? → Intercept angle 30–90° (45° standard); inbound NDB: if present QDM > …","Intercept angle 30–90° (45° standard); inbound NDB: if present QDM > desired → heading = desired QDM + IA; < → desired − IA; on track when needle shows desired QDM; outbound: needle tail. VOR: set course, fly towards the needle, turn in with lead as the needle comes alive; RMI tail = radial you're on."],["062.02.06.01:01","Review — holdings & reversals: fill in the gaps → Right turns; 1 min ≤ 14 000 ft (1.5 min above); 25° bank or 3°/s; timing over/abeam whichever later; ____: parallel (sector 1, 110°), offset/teardrop 30° (sector 2, 70°), direct (sector 3, 180°); wind: triple drift outbound, adjust outbound time. Holding speeds 230/240/265 kt/M 0.83.","Right turns; 1 min ≤ 14 000 ft (1.5 min above); 25° bank or 3°/s; timing over/abeam whichever later; entries: parallel (sector 1, 110°), offset/teardrop 30° (sector 2, 70°), direct (sector 3, 180°); wind: triple drift outbound, adjust outbound time. Holding speeds 230/240/265 kt/M 0.83."],["062.02.06.01:01","Review — holdings & reversals: fill in the gaps → ____ (1 min A/B, 1 min 15 s C–E), ____, ____, racetrack — stay within published times/altitudes/speeds (protected areas by aircraft category).","Procedure turn 45°/180° (1 min A/B, 1 min 15 s C–E), 80°/260°, base turn, racetrack — stay within published times/altitudes/speeds (protected areas by aircraft category)."],["062.02.06.02:05","Exam trap (Review — holdings & reversals): complete the fact → MLS = SHF, ____channels, ±____°/20 NM; curved approaches need DME/P; without DME/P → ILS-lookalike.","MLS = SHF, 200 channels, ±40°/20 NM; curved approaches need DME/P; without DME/P → ILS-lookalike."],["062.02.06.01:02","EASA LO 062.02.06.01.02 → State that MLS operates in the SHF band on any one of 200 channels, on assigned frequencies.","MLS = SHF, 200 channels, ±40°/20 NM; curved approaches need DME/P; without DME/P → ILS-lookalike. / SHF band (5 031–5 090.7 MHz), 200 channels. Advantages over ILS: less sensitive to reflections/siting (buildings, terrain) → installable where ILS siting is difficult; wide coverage; many channels; no large critical areas; curved/segmented approaches."],["062.02.06.02:01","EASA LO 062.02.06.02.01 → Interpret the display of airborne equipment designed to continuously show the position of the aircraft in relation to a preselected course and glide path, along with distance information, during approach and departure.","Airborne display continuously shows position relative to a pre- selected course and glide path, with distance, for approach and departure. With a computer programmed with the approach, segmented/curved approaches are flown using two command cross bars — possible only with DME/P (3D position needed)."],["062.02.06.02:03","EASA LO 062.02.06.02.03 → Illustrate that segmented and curved approaches can only be executed with DME/P installed.","Airborne display continuously shows position relative to a pre- selected course and glide path, with distance, for approach and departure. With a computer programmed with the approach, segmented/curved approaches are flown using two command cross bars — possible only with DME/P (3D position needed). / MLS = SHF, 200 channels, ±40°/20 NM; curved approaches need DME/P; without DME/P → ILS-lookalike."]],"24":[["050.02.01.01:02","Wind — definition & measurement: fill in the gaps → Wind = horizontal movement of air; surface wind = wind measured ____ above ground. Direction in degrees true in reports (METAR/TAF/charts), magnetic from the tower/ATIS (runway use); speed kt (or m/s, km/h). Average over ____ for local reports and ATS units (tower wind), ____ for METAR/SPECI; anemometer (+ wind vane).","Wind = horizontal movement of air; surface wind = wind measured 10 m above ground. Direction in degrees true in reports (METAR/TAF/charts), magnetic from the tower/ATIS (runway use); speed kt (or m/s, km/h). Average over 2 min for local reports and ATS units (tower wind), 10 min for METAR/SPECI; anemometer (+ wind vane)."],["050.02.02.01:02","Causes of wind: fill in the gaps → ____ = pressure change per unit distance (isobar spacing) → ____ acts ____, ⊥ to the isobars, ∝ gradient.","Horizontal pressure gradient = pressure change per unit distance (isobar spacing) → pressure gradient force (PGF) acts from high to low, ⊥ to the isobars, ∝ gradient."],["050.02.02.01:06","Causes of wind: fill in the gaps → ____: apparent force due to earth rotation, acting ____ ____ — to the right in the NH, left in the SH; ∝ wind speed × sin(latitude) (zero at the equator).","Coriolis force: apparent force due to earth rotation, acting ⊥ to the wind — to the right in the NH, left in the SH; ∝ wind speed × sin(latitude) (zero at the equator)."],["050.02.02.01:06","Causes of wind: fill in the gaps → ____: balance ____ → wind blows ____ — NH with low pressure on the left (Buys-Ballot: back to the wind, low on the left); SH low on the right. For the same gradient, geostrophic speed decreases with increasing latitude (Coriolis larger) → higher at low latitudes (not valid near the equator, < ~15°).","Geostrophic wind: balance PGF = Coriolis → wind blows parallel to straight isobars/contours — NH with low pressure on the left (Buys-Ballot: back to the wind, low on the left); SH low on the right. For the same gradient, geostrophic speed decreases with increasing latitude (Coriolis larger) → higher at low latitudes (not valid near the equator, < ~15°)."],["050.02.02.01:07","Causes of wind: fill in the gaps → ____: with ____ the centripetal force is added: around a ____ the wind is weaker than geostrophic (sub-geostrophic); around a high (anticyclonic) stronger (super-geostrophic) — for the same isobar spacing.","Gradient wind: with curved isobars the centripetal force is added: around a low (cyclonic) the wind is weaker than geostrophic (sub-geostrophic); around a high (anticyclonic) stronger (super-geostrophic) — for the same isobar spacing."],["050.02.02.02:01","Causes of wind: fill in the gaps → Friction layer (surface to ~____, typ. 2 000–3 000 ft): friction slows the wind → Coriolis weaker → wind turns towards low pressure, crossing isobars: NH surface wind backs and decreases compared with the gradient wind — over ____ ____, over sea ~10° / ~70 %. With height the wind veers and increases to the gradient wind at the top of the friction layer.","Friction layer (surface to ~1 000–3 000 ft, typ. 2 000–3 000 ft): friction slows the wind → Coriolis weaker → wind turns towards low pressure, crossing isobars: NH surface wind backs and decreases compared with the gradient wind — over land ~30° / ~50 % speed, over sea ~10° / ~70 %. With height the wind veers and increases to the gradient wind at the top of the friction layer."],["050.02.02.02:02","Causes of wind: fill in the gaps → ____: by ____ (unstable, turbulent mixing) the surface wind is ____ (closer to the gradient wind); at night (stable, inversion) it is weaker and backed (and a low-level jet may form just above the inversion). The depth of the friction layer increases with rough terrain, higher wind speed and instability.","Diurnal variation: by day (unstable, turbulent mixing) the surface wind is stronger and veered (closer to the gradient wind); at night (stable, inversion) it is weaker and backed (and a low-level jet may form just above the inversion). The depth of the friction layer increases with rough terrain, higher wind speed and instability."],["050.02.02.02:04","Causes of wind: what do you remember about this? → Wind direction/speed from isobars: parallel (above friction), speed ∝ …","Wind direction/speed from isobars: parallel (above friction), speed ∝ closeness of isobars."],["050.02.02.03:02","Causes of wind: fill in the gaps → ____ (air flowing together horizontally) → ____ → clouds/precipitation; ____ → sinking air → clear. Surface low = convergence at the surface + divergence aloft (maintains/deepens the low); surface high = divergence at the surface + convergence aloft. Upper divergence ahead of upper troughs develops surface lows.","Convergence (air flowing together horizontally) → rising air → clouds/precipitation; divergence → sinking air → clear. Surface low = convergence at the surface + divergence aloft (maintains/deepens the low); surface high = divergence at the surface + convergence aloft. Upper divergence ahead of upper troughs develops surface lows."],["050.02.03.01:02","General circulation: fill in the gaps → Three-cell model (Hadley, Ferrel, polar). Surface pressure belts: ____, ____, ____, polar highs; surface winds: NE/SE trades, westerlies (30–60°), polar easterlies. In January the belts shift south (continental highs over Siberia/N-America in winter), in July north (thermal lows over continents, Asian monsoon low).","Three-cell model (Hadley, Ferrel, polar). Surface pressure belts: equatorial trough/ITCZ (low), subtropical highs (~30°), subpolar lows (~60°), polar highs; surface winds: NE/SE trades, westerlies (30–60°), polar easterlies. In January the belts shift south (continental highs over Siberia/N-America in winter), in July north (thermal lows over continents, Asian monsoon low)."],["050.02.03.01:03","General circulation: fill in the gaps → High level: ____ dominate the troposphere at mid/high latitudes (stronger in winter); ____ in the tropics (tropical easterly jet in summer over India/Africa).","High level: westerlies dominate the troposphere at mid/high latitudes (stronger in winter); easterlies in the tropics (tropical easterly jet in summer over India/Africa)."],["050.02.04.01:02","Local winds: fill in the gaps → ____: by day, air heated on slopes ____ (weak). ____: at night, air cooled on slopes flows downhill (can be strong, cold, → valley fog/frost pockets; Bora/Mistral are large-scale katabatic/fall winds). Mountain and valley winds: by day up the valley (valley wind), at night down the valley (mountain wind).","Anabatic: by day, air heated on slopes rises upslope (weak). Katabatic: at night, air cooled on slopes flows downhill (can be strong, cold, → valley fog/frost pockets; Bora/Mistral are large-scale katabatic/fall winds). Mountain and valley winds: by day up the valley (valley wind), at night down the valley (mountain wind)."],["050.02.04.01:03","Local winds: fill in the gaps → ____: wind ____ through narrow valleys/gaps and over ridges (convergence) → stronger winds, turbulence, lower pressure (altimeter over-reads).","Venturi effect: wind accelerated through narrow valleys/gaps and over ridges (convergence) → stronger winds, turbulence, lower pressure (altimeter over-reads)."],["050.02.04.01:04","Local winds: fill in the gaps → ____: by day land heats faster → air rises over land → ____ (up to ~10–15 kt, ~10–20 NM inland), ____ front (convergence, Cu, wind shift); land breeze at night: offshore, weaker.","Sea breeze: by day land heats faster → air rises over land → onshore wind (up to ~10–15 kt, ~10–20 NM inland), sea-breeze front (convergence, Cu, wind shift); land breeze at night: offshore, weaker."],["050.02.04.01:05","Local winds: fill in the gaps → ____: in the evening/night above the nocturnal inversion, winds can accelerate strongly (wind shear on approach).","Low-level jets: in the evening/night above the nocturnal inversion, winds can accelerate strongly (wind shear on approach)."],["050.02.02.02:01","Mountain waves: fill in the gaps → ____: stable air flowing over a mountain ridge is displaced and oscillates downstream → standing lee waves. Conditions: wind ____, speed ____, increasing with height with little direction change, stable layer at/above ridge level (with less stable air above/below).","Origin: stable air flowing over a mountain ridge is displaced and oscillates downstream → standing lee waves. Conditions: wind ⊥ (within ~30°) to the ridge, speed ≥ 15–20 kt at ridge top, increasing with height with little direction change, stable layer at/above ridge level (with less stable air above/below)."],["050.02.05.01:05","Mountain waves: fill in the gaps → ____: wave crests and troughs downstream (wavelength 2– 25 km), ____ (> 2 000 ft/min, can exceed aircraft climb capability), ____ under the crests at low level (severe turbulence), can extend into the stratosphere.","Structure: wave crests and troughs downstream (wavelength 2– 25 km), strong up-and downdrafts (> 2 000 ft/min, can exceed aircraft climb capability), rotors under the crests at low level (severe turbulence), can extend into the stratosphere."],["050.02.05.01:01","Mountain waves: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ stationary over crests, ____ (Cu fractus) at low level, cap cloud (Foehn wall) on the ridge; may be invisible in dry air.","Identification: lenticular clouds (Ac lenticularis) stationary over crests, rotor clouds (Cu fractus) at low level, cap cloud (Foehn wall) on the ridge; may be invisible in dry air."],["050.02.05.01:06","Mountain waves: fill in the gaps → Effects can exceed ____ (downdrafts, severe turbulence, altimeter errors). Waves propagate from low to ____ (e.g. over Greenland, Rockies, Alps) → CAT at cruise levels.","Effects can exceed aircraft performance or structural limits (downdrafts, severe turbulence, altimeter errors). Waves propagate from low to high levels (e.g. over Greenland, Rockies, Alps) → CAT at cruise levels."],["050.02.06.01:02","Turbulence: fill in the gaps → ____ = irregular eddying motion; ____ = rapid changes of wind speed/direction. Types: ____ (thermals, Cu/Cb, afternoon over land), mechanical (friction over rough terrain/buildings, strong wind), orographic (mountains, rotors), frontal (wind shear at fronts), CAT.","Turbulence = irregular eddying motion; gustiness = rapid changes of wind speed/direction. Types: convective (thermals, Cu/Cb, afternoon over land), mechanical (friction over rough terrain/buildings, strong wind), orographic (mountains, rotors), frontal (wind shear at fronts), CAT."],["050.02.06.02:02","Turbulence: fill in the gaps → Found near rough ground and relief, ____ (shear), ____, jet streams, fronts, wake turbulence.","Found near rough ground and relief, inversion layers (shear), Cb, jet streams, fronts, wake turbulence."],["050.02.06.03:04","Turbulence: fill in the gaps → ____: turbulence in ____ at high levels, caused by strong ____ (Kelvin-Helmholtz waves) — near jet streams (especially on the cold/polar side, just below the core and above it), in upper troughs/ridges, sharply curved flow, near the tropopause. Cannot be detected by satellites or radar, forecasting limited → PIREPs are very valuable.","CAT: turbulence in clear air at high levels, caused by strong vertical/horizontal wind shear (Kelvin-Helmholtz waves) — near jet streams (especially on the cold/polar side, just below the core and above it), in upper troughs/ridges, sharply curved flow, near the tropopause. Cannot be detected by satellites or radar, forecasting limited → PIREPs are very valuable."],["050.02.07.01:03","Jet streams: fill in the gaps → ____ = narrow, strong wind band (≥ ____) near the tropopause; typical dimensions: ____ hundred km wide, a few km deep; core speeds 100–200 kt (up to 300 kt).","Jet stream = narrow, strong wind band (≥ 60 kt) near the tropopause; typical dimensions: thousands of km long, a few hundred km wide, a few km deep; core speeds 100–200 kt (up to 300 kt)."],["050.02.07.02:01","Jet streams: fill in the gaps → Types: ____ (~FL 250–300, near the arctic front, winter), Polar-front jet (~____, 40–60°, at the break between polar and tropical tropopause, stronger in winter, moves south in winter/north in summer, varies in position), Subtropical jet (~____, ~25–30°, fairly steady, westerly, stronger in winter), Tropical easterly (equatorial) jet (~FL 450–500, easterly, summer over India/Africa/southeast Asia).","Types: Arctic (front) jet (~FL 250–300, near the arctic front, winter), Polar-front jet (~FL 300–350, 40–60°, at the break between polar and tropical tropopause, stronger in winter, moves south in winter/north in summer, varies in position), Subtropical jet (~FL 400, ~25–30°, fairly steady, westerly, stronger in winter), Tropical easterly (equatorial) jet (~FL 450–500, easterly, summer over India/Africa/southeast Asia)."],["050.02.07.03:02","Jet streams: fill in the gaps → Location: polar-front jet lies in the ____ ____, ____; worst wind shear/CAT on the cold (polar) side below the core and above it near the tropopause.","Location: polar-front jet lies in the warm air above the surface front, just below the tropopause break; worst wind shear/CAT on the cold (polar) side below the core and above it near the tropopause."],["050.03.01.01:04","Thermodynamics: fill in the gaps → ____ (H₂O lighter than N₂/O₂). Water vapour: source of clouds/precipitation, latent heat transport, greenhouse effect; sources: evaporation from oceans, lakes, vegetation (transpiration). ____: air holds the maximum water vapour at that temperature (RH 100 %).","Moist air is less dense than dry air (H₂O lighter than N₂/O₂). Water vapour: source of clouds/precipitation, latent heat transport, greenhouse effect; sources: evaporation from oceans, lakes, vegetation (transpiration). Saturation: air holds the maximum water vapour at that temperature (RH 100 %)."],["050.03.01.03:03","Thermodynamics: fill in the gaps → ____ = temperature to which air must be cooled (at constant pressure/moisture) to become saturated. ____ ____ = actual vapour ÷ saturation vapour × 100 %; at constant moisture RH rises when temperature falls → diurnal cycle: RH max at dawn, min in the afternoon. Spread (T − Td) small → RH high (spread 0 = 100 %; ~1 °C ≈ 94 %; ~ 5 % per °C roughly near saturation).","Dew point = temperature to which air must be cooled (at constant pressure/moisture) to become saturated. Relative humidity = actual vapour ÷ saturation vapour × 100 %; at constant moisture RH rises when temperature falls → diurnal cycle: RH max at dawn, min in the afternoon. Spread (T − Td) small → RH high (spread 0 = 100 %; ~1 °C ≈ 94 %; ~ 5 % per °C roughly near saturation)."],["050.03.02.01:01","Thermodynamics: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (vapour → liquid, releases latent heat), ____ (liquid → vapour, absorbs heat), sublimation/deposition (vapour ↔ ice directly), freezing/melting.","Changes of state: condensation (vapour → liquid, releases latent heat), evaporation (liquid → vapour, absorbs heat), sublimation/deposition (vapour ↔ ice directly), freezing/melting."],["050.03.02.01:08","Thermodynamics: fill in the gaps → Condensation requires ____ ____ (dust, salt, smoke — hygroscopic); releases latent heat → fuels convection/storms. Freezing needs ____ nuclei; without them water stays liquid below 0 °C = supercooled water (down to −40 °C). Deposition needs sublimation nuclei (ice crystals).","Condensation requires saturation and condensation nuclei (dust, salt, smoke — hygroscopic); releases latent heat → fuels convection/storms. Freezing needs freezing nuclei; without them water stays liquid below 0 °C = supercooled water (down to −40 °C). Deposition needs sublimation nuclei (ice crystals)."],["050.03.02.01:14","Thermodynamics: what do you remember about this? → Examples: dew/frost (surface cooling), cloud formation …","Examples: dew/frost (surface cooling), cloud formation (condensation), hoar frost (deposition), evaporative cooling (carb icing), latent heat release in Cb."],["050.03.03.01:01","Thermodynamics: fill in the gaps → Adiabatic processes: rising air expands and cools without heat exchange; unsaturated air cools at the ____ ____ — its ____, so RH rises until saturation (cloud base ≈ (T − Td) × 400 ft); saturated air cools at the SALR ~1.5–2 °C/1 000 ft (~0.6 °C/100 m; smaller in warm air, larger in cold) because latent heat is released; descending air warms (DALR if unsaturated).","Adiabatic processes: rising air expands and cools without heat exchange; unsaturated air cools at the DALR 3 °C/1 000 ft (1 °C/100 m) — its dew point falls ~0.5 °C/1 000 ft, so RH rises until saturation (cloud base ≈ (T − Td) × 400 ft); saturated air cools at the SALR ~1.5–2 °C/1 000 ft (~0.6 °C/100 m; smaller in warm air, larger in cold) because latent heat is released; descending air warms (DALR if unsaturated)."],["050.03.03.01:07","Thermodynamics: fill in the gaps → ____ (compare ELR with DALR/SALR): ____ ELR < SALR (incl. inversions, isothermal layers); ____ ELR > DALR; conditionally unstable SALR < ELR < DALR (unstable only if saturated); indifferent (neutral) ELR = adiabatic rate.","Stability (compare ELR with DALR/SALR): absolutely stable ELR < SALR (incl. inversions, isothermal layers); absolutely unstable ELR > DALR; conditionally unstable SALR < ELR < DALR (unstable only if saturated); indifferent (neutral) ELR = adiabatic rate."],["050.03.01.01:01","Thermodynamics: fill in the gaps → ____: moist air forced up the windward side cools at DALR to cloud base, then at SALR (precipitation, moisture lost); descending on the lee side it warms at ____ all the way → ____ drier at the same level on the lee side.","Foehn: moist air forced up the windward side cools at DALR to cloud base, then at SALR (precipitation, moisture lost); descending on the lee side it warms at DALR all the way → warmer and drier at the same level on the lee side."],["050.03.03.01:09","Thermodynamics: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ ____; cold air advection aloft or warm at surface → less stable (e.g. cold air moving over a warm sea → showers).","Advection: warm air advection aloft / cold at surface → more stable; cold air advection aloft or warm at surface → less stable (e.g. cold air moving over a warm sea → showers)."],["050.02.01.01:01","Exam trap (Thermodynamics): complete the fact → Surface wind backs & slows (____°/____ land).","Surface wind backs & slows (30°/50 % land)."],["050.02.02.01:03","Exam trap (Thermodynamics): complete the fact → Coriolis ____at the equator.","Coriolis 0 at the equator."],["050.02.01.01:03","EASA LO 050.02.01.01.03 → Describe that the reported wind is an average wind derived from measurements with an anemometer at a height of 10 m over 2 min for local routine and special reports and ATS units, and over 10 min for aerodrome routine meteorological reports (METARs) and aerodrome special meteorological reports (SPECIs).","Wind = horizontal movement of air; surface wind = wind measured 10 m above ground. Direction in degrees true in reports (METAR/TAF/charts), magnetic from the tower/ATIS (runway use); speed kt (or m/s, km/h). Average over 2 min for local reports and ATS units (tower wind), 10 min for METAR/SPECI; anemometer (+ wind vane)."],["050.02.02.01:01","EASA LO 050.02.02.01.01 → Define the term ‘horizontal pressure gradient’.","Horizontal pressure gradient = pressure change per unit distance (isobar spacing) → pressure gradient force (PGF) acts from high to low, ⊥ to the isobars, ∝ gradient. / Friction layer (surface to ~1 000–3 000 ft, typ. 2 000–3 000 ft): friction slows the wind → Coriolis weaker → wind turns towards low pressure, crossing isobars: NH surface wind backs and decreases compared with the gradient wind — over land ~30° / ~50 % speed, over sea ~10° / ~70 %. With height the wind veers and increases to the gradient wind at the top of the friction layer."],["050.02.02.01:04","EASA LO 050.02.02.01.04 → Explain the development of the geostrophic wind.","Gradient wind: with curved isobars the centripetal force is added: around a low (cyclonic) the wind is weaker than geostrophic (sub-geostrophic); around a high (anticyclonic) stronger (super-geostrophic) — for the same isobar spacing. / Horizontal pressure gradient = pressure change per unit distance (isobar spacing) → pressure gradient force (PGF) acts from high to low, ⊥ to the isobars, ∝ gradient."],["050.02.02.01:05","EASA LO 050.02.02.01.05 → Indicate how the geostrophic wind flows in relation to the isobars/isohypses in the northern and in the southern hemisphere.","Horizontal pressure gradient = pressure change per unit distance (isobar spacing) → pressure gradient force (PGF) acts from high to low, ⊥ to the isobars, ∝ gradient. / Gradient wind: with curved isobars the centripetal force is added: around a low (cyclonic) the wind is weaker than geostrophic (sub-geostrophic); around a high (anticyclonic) stronger (super-geostrophic) — for the same isobar spacing."],["050.02.02.02:03","EASA LO 050.02.02.02.03 → Name terrain, wind speed and stability as the main factors that influence the vertical extent of the friction layer.","Diurnal variation: by day (unstable, turbulent mixing) the surface wind is stronger and veered (closer to the gradient wind); at night (stable, inversion) it is weaker and backed (and a low-level jet may form just above the inversion). The depth of the friction layer increases with rough terrain, higher wind speed and instability. / Wind direction/speed from isobars: parallel (above friction), speed ∝ closeness of isobars."],["050.02.02.03:01","EASA LO 050.02.02.03.01 → Describe atmospheric convergence and divergence.","Convergence (air flowing together horizontally) → rising air → clouds/precipitation; divergence → sinking air → clear. Surface low = convergence at the surface + divergence aloft (maintains/deepens the low); surface high = divergence at the surface + convergence aloft. Upper divergence ahead of upper troughs develops surface lows."],["050.02.04.01:01","EASA LO 050.02.04.01.01 → Describe and explain anabatic and katabatic winds.","Anabatic: by day, air heated on slopes rises upslope (weak). Katabatic: at night, air cooled on slopes flows downhill (can be strong, cold, → valley fog/frost pockets; Bora/Mistral are large-scale katabatic/fall winds). Mountain and valley winds: by day up the valley (valley wind), at night down the valley (mountain wind)."],["050.02.05.01:01","EASA LO 050.02.05.01.01 → Explain the origin and formation of mountain waves.","Origin: stable air flowing over a mountain ridge is displaced and oscillates downstream → standing lee waves. Conditions: wind ⊥ (within ~30°) to the ridge, speed ≥ 15–20 kt at ridge top, increasing with height with little direction change, stable layer at/above ridge level (with less stable air above/below)."],["050.02.05.01:02","EASA LO 050.02.05.01.02 → State the conditions necessary for the formation of mountain waves.","Origin: stable air flowing over a mountain ridge is displaced and oscillates downstream → standing lee waves. Conditions: wind ⊥ (within ~30°) to the ridge, speed ≥ 15–20 kt at ridge top, increasing with height with little direction change, stable layer at/above ridge level (with less stable air above/below)."],["050.02.06.01:01","EASA LO 050.02.06.01.01 → Describe turbulence and gustiness.","Turbulence = irregular eddying motion; gustiness = rapid changes of wind speed/direction. Types: convective (thermals, Cu/Cb, afternoon over land), mechanical (friction over rough terrain/buildings, strong wind), orographic (mountains, rotors), frontal (wind shear at fronts), CAT."],["050.02.06.02:01","EASA LO 050.02.06.02.01 → Explain the formation of convective turbulence, mechanical and orographic turbulence, and frontal turbulence.","Turbulence = irregular eddying motion; gustiness = rapid changes of wind speed/direction. Types: convective (thermals, Cu/Cb, afternoon over land), mechanical (friction over rough terrain/buildings, strong wind), orographic (mountains, rotors), frontal (wind shear at fronts), CAT."],["050.02.06.03:01","EASA LO 050.02.06.03.01 → Describe CAT.","Location: polar-front jet lies in the warm air above the surface front, just below the tropopause break; worst wind shear/CAT on the cold (polar) side below the core and above it near the tropopause."],["050.02.06.03:02","EASA LO 050.02.06.03.02 → Describe the formation of CAT.","Location: polar-front jet lies in the warm air above the surface front, just below the tropopause break; worst wind shear/CAT on the cold (polar) side below the core and above it near the tropopause."],["050.02.06.03:05","EASA LO 050.02.06.03.05 → State that pilot reports of turbulence are a very valuable source of information as remote measurements are not available.","CAT: turbulence in clear air at high levels, caused by strong vertical/horizontal wind shear (Kelvin-Helmholtz waves) — near jet streams (especially on the cold/polar side, just below the core and above it), in upper troughs/ridges, sharply curved flow, near the tropopause. Cannot be detected by satellites or radar, forecasting limited → PIREPs are very valuable."],["050.02.07.01:01","EASA LO 050.02.07.01.01 → Describe jet streams.","Found near rough ground and relief, inversion layers (shear), Cb, jet streams, fronts, wake turbulence. / Location: polar-front jet lies in the warm air above the surface front, just below the tropopause break; worst wind shear/CAT on the cold (polar) side below the core and above it near the tropopause."],["050.02.07.01:02","EASA LO 050.02.07.01.02 → State the defined minimum speed of a jet stream (60 kt).","Jet stream = narrow, strong wind band (≥ 60 kt) near the tropopause; typical dimensions: thousands of km long, a few hundred km wide, a few km deep; core speeds 100–200 kt (up to 300 kt)."],["050.02.07.03:01","EASA LO 050.02.07.03.01 → Sketch or describe where polar front and arctic jet streams are found in the troposphere in relation to the tropopause and to fronts.","Location: polar-front jet lies in the warm air above the surface front, just below the tropopause break; worst wind shear/CAT on the cold (polar) side below the core and above it near the tropopause. / Found near rough ground and relief, inversion layers (shear), Cb, jet streams, fronts, wake turbulence."],["050.03.01.01:01","EASA LO 050.03.01.01.01 → State that the density of moist air is less than the density of dry air.","Moist air is less dense than dry air (H₂O lighter than N₂/O₂). Water vapour: source of clouds/precipitation, latent heat transport, greenhouse effect; sources: evaporation from oceans, lakes, vegetation (transpiration). Saturation: air holds the maximum water vapour at that temperature (RH 100 %)."],["050.03.01.01:02","EASA LO 050.03.01.01.02 → Describe the significance for meteorology of water vapour in the atmosphere.","Moist air is less dense than dry air (H₂O lighter than N₂/O₂). Water vapour: source of clouds/precipitation, latent heat transport, greenhouse effect; sources: evaporation from oceans, lakes, vegetation (transpiration). Saturation: air holds the maximum water vapour at that temperature (RH 100 %). / Changes of state: condensation (vapour → liquid, releases latent heat), evaporation (liquid → vapour, absorbs heat), sublimation/deposition (vapour ↔ ice directly), freezing/melting."],["050.03.01.01:03","EASA LO 050.03.01.01.03 → Indicate the sources of atmospheric humidity.","Moist air is less dense than dry air (H₂O lighter than N₂/O₂). Water vapour: source of clouds/precipitation, latent heat transport, greenhouse effect; sources: evaporation from oceans, lakes, vegetation (transpiration). Saturation: air holds the maximum water vapour at that temperature (RH 100 %)."],["050.03.01.03:01","EASA LO 050.03.01.03.01 → Define ‘dew point’.","Dew point = temperature to which air must be cooled (at constant pressure/moisture) to become saturated. Relative humidity = actual vapour ÷ saturation vapour × 100 %; at constant moisture RH rises when temperature falls → diurnal cycle: RH max at dawn, min in the afternoon. Spread (T − Td) small → RH high (spread 0 = 100 %; ~1 °C ≈ 94 %; ~ 5 % per °C roughly near saturation)."],["050.03.01.03:02","EASA LO 050.03.01.03.02 → Define ‘relative humidity’.","Dew point = temperature to which air must be cooled (at constant pressure/moisture) to become saturated. Relative humidity = actual vapour ÷ saturation vapour × 100 %; at constant moisture RH rises when temperature falls → diurnal cycle: RH max at dawn, min in the afternoon. Spread (T − Td) small → RH high (spread 0 = 100 %; ~1 °C ≈ 94 %; ~ 5 % per °C roughly near saturation)."],["050.03.01.03:04","EASA LO 050.03.01.03.04 → Explain the diurnal variation of the relative humidity.","Dew point = temperature to which air must be cooled (at constant pressure/moisture) to become saturated. Relative humidity = actual vapour ÷ saturation vapour × 100 %; at constant moisture RH rises when temperature falls → diurnal cycle: RH max at dawn, min in the afternoon. Spread (T − Td) small → RH high (spread 0 = 100 %; ~1 °C ≈ 94 %; ~ 5 % per °C roughly near saturation)."],["050.03.01.03:05","EASA LO 050.03.01.03.05 → Describe the relationship between temperature and dew point.","Dew point = temperature to which air must be cooled (at constant pressure/moisture) to become saturated. Relative humidity = actual vapour ÷ saturation vapour × 100 %; at constant moisture RH rises when temperature falls → diurnal cycle: RH max at dawn, min in the afternoon. Spread (T − Td) small → RH high (spread 0 = 100 %; ~1 °C ≈ 94 %; ~ 5 % per °C roughly near saturation)."],["050.03.01.03:06","EASA LO 050.03.01.03.06 → Estimate the relative humidity of the air from the difference between dew point and temperature.","Dew point = temperature to which air must be cooled (at constant pressure/moisture) to become saturated. Relative humidity = actual vapour ÷ saturation vapour × 100 %; at constant moisture RH rises when temperature falls → diurnal cycle: RH max at dawn, min in the afternoon. Spread (T − Td) small → RH high (spread 0 = 100 %; ~1 °C ≈ 94 %; ~ 5 % per °C roughly near saturation)."],["050.03.02.01:02","EASA LO 050.03.02.01.02 → List the conditions for condensation/evaporation.","Changes of state: condensation (vapour → liquid, releases latent heat), evaporation (liquid → vapour, absorbs heat), sublimation/deposition (vapour ↔ ice directly), freezing/melting. / Condensation requires saturation and condensation nuclei (dust, salt, smoke — hygroscopic); releases latent heat → fuels convection/storms. Freezing needs freezing nuclei; without them water stays liquid below 0 °C = supercooled water (down to −40 °C). Deposition needs sublimation nuclei (ice crystals)."],["050.03.02.01:03","EASA LO 050.03.02.01.03 → Explain the condensation process.","Changes of state: condensation (vapour → liquid, releases latent heat), evaporation (liquid → vapour, absorbs heat), sublimation/deposition (vapour ↔ ice directly), freezing/melting. / Condensation requires saturation and condensation nuclei (dust, salt, smoke — hygroscopic); releases latent heat → fuels convection/storms. Freezing needs freezing nuclei; without them water stays liquid below 0 °C = supercooled water (down to −40 °C). Deposition needs sublimation nuclei (ice crystals)."],["050.03.02.01:04","EASA LO 050.03.02.01.04 → Explain the nature of and the need for condensation nuclei.","Changes of state: condensation (vapour → liquid, releases latent heat), evaporation (liquid → vapour, absorbs heat), sublimation/deposition (vapour ↔ ice directly), freezing/melting. / Condensation requires saturation and condensation nuclei (dust, salt, smoke — hygroscopic); releases latent heat → fuels convection/storms. Freezing needs freezing nuclei; without them water stays liquid below 0 °C = supercooled water (down to −40 °C). Deposition needs sublimation nuclei (ice crystals)."],["050.03.02.01:05","EASA LO 050.03.02.01.05 → Explain the effects of condensation on the weather.","Changes of state: condensation (vapour → liquid, releases latent heat), evaporation (liquid → vapour, absorbs heat), sublimation/deposition (vapour ↔ ice directly), freezing/melting. / Condensation requires saturation and condensation nuclei (dust, salt, smoke — hygroscopic); releases latent heat → fuels convection/storms. Freezing needs freezing nuclei; without them water stays liquid below 0 °C = supercooled water (down to −40 °C). Deposition needs sublimation nuclei (ice crystals)."],["050.03.02.01:06","EASA LO 050.03.02.01.06 → List the conditions for freezing/melting.","Changes of state: condensation (vapour → liquid, releases latent heat), evaporation (liquid → vapour, absorbs heat), sublimation/deposition (vapour ↔ ice directly), freezing/melting. / Condensation requires saturation and condensation nuclei (dust, salt, smoke — hygroscopic); releases latent heat → fuels convection/storms. Freezing needs freezing nuclei; without them water stays liquid below 0 °C = supercooled water (down to −40 °C). Deposition needs sublimation nuclei (ice crystals)."],["050.03.02.01:07","EASA LO 050.03.02.01.07 → Explain the process of freezing.","Changes of state: condensation (vapour → liquid, releases latent heat), evaporation (liquid → vapour, absorbs heat), sublimation/deposition (vapour ↔ ice directly), freezing/melting. / Condensation requires saturation and condensation nuclei (dust, salt, smoke — hygroscopic); releases latent heat → fuels convection/storms. Freezing needs freezing nuclei; without them water stays liquid below 0 °C = supercooled water (down to −40 °C). Deposition needs sublimation nuclei (ice crystals)."],["050.03.02.01:09","EASA LO 050.03.02.01.09 → Define ‘supercooled water’. (Refer to Subject 050 09 01 01)","Condensation requires saturation and condensation nuclei (dust, salt, smoke — hygroscopic); releases latent heat → fuels convection/storms. Freezing needs freezing nuclei; without them water stays liquid below 0 °C = supercooled water (down to −40 °C). Deposition needs sublimation nuclei (ice crystals). / Changes of state: condensation (vapour → liquid, releases latent heat), evaporation (liquid → vapour, absorbs heat), sublimation/deposition (vapour ↔ ice directly), freezing/melting."],["050.03.02.01:10","EASA LO 050.03.02.01.10 → List the conditions for sublimation.","Changes of state: condensation (vapour → liquid, releases latent heat), evaporation (liquid → vapour, absorbs heat), sublimation/deposition (vapour ↔ ice directly), freezing/melting. / Condensation requires saturation and condensation nuclei (dust, salt, smoke — hygroscopic); releases latent heat → fuels convection/storms. Freezing needs freezing nuclei; without them water stays liquid below 0 °C = supercooled water (down to −40 °C). Deposition needs sublimation nuclei (ice crystals)."],["050.03.02.01:11","EASA LO 050.03.02.01.11 → Explain the sublimation process.","Changes of state: condensation (vapour → liquid, releases latent heat), evaporation (liquid → vapour, absorbs heat), sublimation/deposition (vapour ↔ ice directly), freezing/melting. / Condensation requires saturation and condensation nuclei (dust, salt, smoke — hygroscopic); releases latent heat → fuels convection/storms. Freezing needs freezing nuclei; without them water stays liquid below 0 °C = supercooled water (down to −40 °C). Deposition needs sublimation nuclei (ice crystals)."],["050.03.02.01:12","EASA LO 050.03.02.01.12 → Explain the nature of and the need for sublimation nuclei.","Changes of state: condensation (vapour → liquid, releases latent heat), evaporation (liquid → vapour, absorbs heat), sublimation/deposition (vapour ↔ ice directly), freezing/melting. / Condensation requires saturation and condensation nuclei (dust, salt, smoke — hygroscopic); releases latent heat → fuels convection/storms. Freezing needs freezing nuclei; without them water stays liquid below 0 °C = supercooled water (down to −40 °C). Deposition needs sublimation nuclei (ice crystals)."],["050.03.02.01:13","EASA LO 050.03.02.01.13 → Describe the absorption or release of latent heat in each change of state of water.","Changes of state: condensation (vapour → liquid, releases latent heat), evaporation (liquid → vapour, absorbs heat), sublimation/deposition (vapour ↔ ice directly), freezing/melting. / Condensation requires saturation and condensation nuclei (dust, salt, smoke — hygroscopic); releases latent heat → fuels convection/storms. Freezing needs freezing nuclei; without them water stays liquid below 0 °C = supercooled water (down to −40 °C). Deposition needs sublimation nuclei (ice crystals)."],["050.03.03.01:02","EASA LO 050.03.03.01.02 → Explain the variation of temperature of an unsaturated rising or descending air particle.","Adiabatic processes: rising air expands and cools without heat exchange; unsaturated air cools at the DALR 3 °C/1 000 ft (1 °C/100 m) — its dew point falls ~0.5 °C/1 000 ft, so RH rises until saturation (cloud base ≈ (T − Td) × 400 ft); saturated air cools at the SALR ~1.5–2 °C/1 000 ft (~0.6 °C/100 m; smaller in warm air, larger in cold) because latent heat is released; descending air warms (DALR if unsaturated)."],["050.03.03.01:03","EASA LO 050.03.03.01.03 → Explain the variation of humidity of an unsaturated rising or descending air particle.","Adiabatic processes: rising air expands and cools without heat exchange; unsaturated air cools at the DALR 3 °C/1 000 ft (1 °C/100 m) — its dew point falls ~0.5 °C/1 000 ft, so RH rises until saturation (cloud base ≈ (T − Td) × 400 ft); saturated air cools at the SALR ~1.5–2 °C/1 000 ft (~0.6 °C/100 m; smaller in warm air, larger in cold) because latent heat is released; descending air warms (DALR if unsaturated)."],["050.03.03.01:04","EASA LO 050.03.03.01.04 → Describe the adiabatic process in a saturated rising or descending air particle.","Adiabatic processes: rising air expands and cools without heat exchange; unsaturated air cools at the DALR 3 °C/1 000 ft (1 °C/100 m) — its dew point falls ~0.5 °C/1 000 ft, so RH rises until saturation (cloud base ≈ (T − Td) × 400 ft); saturated air cools at the SALR ~1.5–2 °C/1 000 ft (~0.6 °C/100 m; smaller in warm air, larger in cold) because latent heat is released; descending air warms (DALR if unsaturated)."]],"25":[["033.01.01.01:01","VFR charts — route selection: fill in the gaps → Select the route considering ____ (clearances, VMC minima, radio/transponder requirements), restricted/prohibited/danger areas (and activation times), VFR semicircular levels (above 3 000 ft AGL: magnetic track 000–179° → ____ (3 500, 5 500…), 180–359° → ____), visually conspicuous points, radio- navigation aids for cross-checking.","Select the route considering airspace classification (clearances, VMC minima, radio/transponder requirements), restricted/prohibited/danger areas (and activation times), VFR semicircular levels (above 3 000 ft AGL: magnetic track 000–179° → odd thousands + 500 ft (3 500, 5 500…), 180–359° → even + 500 ft), visually conspicuous points, radio- navigation aids for cross-checking."],["033.01.01.01:03","VFR charts — route selection: fill in the gaps → From the chart: NAVAID frequencies/idents; ____ ____ (control units, ____, weather stations/VOLMET, ATIS).","From the chart: NAVAID frequencies/idents; communication frequencies and call signs (control units, FIS, weather stations/VOLMET, ATIS)."],["033.01.01.02:01","VFR charts — route selection: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ ____, visible from a distance (lakes, towns, motorway junctions, towers, dams).","Visual waypoints: large, unique, good contrast, vertical extent, visible from a distance (lakes, towns, motorway junctions, towers, dams)."],["033.01.01.02:03","VFR charts — route selection: fill in the gaps → Measure ____ and distances (scale); find the ____ either side of the course (e.g. 5 NM) → minimum altitude. Transfer waypoints, distances, true/magnetic courses (TT − E var/+ W var) to the navigation plan.","Measure true courses at the mid-meridian and distances (scale); find the highest obstacle within a given distance either side of the course (e.g. 5 NM) → minimum altitude. Transfer waypoints, distances, true/magnetic courses (TT − E var/+ W var) to the navigation plan."],["033.01.01.02:05","VFR charts — route selection: fill in the gaps → ____: obstacle/terrain + required clearance (e.g. 1 000 ft) = required true altitude → correct for temperature (cold → fly higher: ~4 % per 10 °C below ISA) → indicated (QNH) altitude → ____ ft (use ____). Or from a minimum grid-area altitude.","Minimum altitude: obstacle/terrain + required clearance (e.g. 1 000 ft) = required true altitude → correct for temperature (cold → fly higher: ~4 % per 10 °C below ISA) → indicated (QNH) altitude → pressure altitude = altitude + (1013 − QNH) × 30 ft (use 30 ft/hPa). Or from a minimum grid-area altitude."],["033.01.01.02:06","VFR charts — route selection: fill in the gaps → ____: time = height ÷ ROC; distance = GS × time (use tables where given).","Climb/descent: time = height ÷ ROC; distance = GS × time (use tables where given)."],["033.01.01.02:07","VFR charts — route selection: fill in the gaps → ____: a VFR point not in the database is entered as a ____ using ____ significant point (e.g. VOR radial/distance \"PBD\") or lat/long coordinates.","GNSS flight plan: a VFR point not in the database is entered as a user waypoint using bearing/distance from an existing significant point (e.g. VOR radial/distance \"PBD\") or lat/long coordinates."],["033.01.01.03:01","Aerodrome charts & directory: fill in the gaps → Study ____ (VFR reporting points, entry/exit routes, circuit altitudes, noise-sensitive areas, PPR) and ____ for departure, destination and alternates.","Study visual departure and arrival procedures (VFR reporting points, entry/exit routes, circuit altitudes, noise-sensitive areas, PPR) and available approaches for departure, destination and alternates."],["033.01.01.03:03","Aerodrome charts & directory: what do you remember about this? → Directory/AIP AD: elevation, runways (dimensions, surface, declared …","Directory/AIP AD: elevation, runways (dimensions, surface, declared distances), frequencies, opening hours, customs, fuel types, circuit direction, restrictions, lighting."],["033.01.01.05:03","Navigation plan: fill in the gaps → ____ from performance data (power setting, pressure altitude, OAT). ____ from forecast wind (CRP/MDR). Individual and accumulated ____ to destination and alternate; ETA; fuel per leg.","TAS from performance data (power setting, pressure altitude, OAT). WCA/drift and GS from forecast wind (CRP/MDR). Individual and accumulated leg times to destination and alternate; ETA; fuel per leg."],["033.03.01.01:01","Fuel planning — general: fill in the gaps → Volume/mass conversions: ____ (AVGAS 0.72, JET A-1 0.8); 1 USG = 3.785 l; 1 IG = 4.546 l; 1 kg = 2.205 lb.","Volume/mass conversions: kg = l × SG (AVGAS 0.72, JET A-1 0.8); 1 USG = 3.785 l; 1 IG = 4.546 l; 1 kg = 2.205 lb."],["033.03.01.01:02","Fuel planning — general: fill in the gaps → AFM data: fuel capacity (usable), fuel flow vs power/altitude/temperature. ____; ____; ____.","AFM data: fuel capacity (usable), fuel flow vs power/altitude/temperature. Endurance = usable fuel ÷ FF; range = endurance × GS; required fuel = time × FF."],["033.03.02.03:05","Fuel planning — general: fill in the gaps → ____ (NCO/CAT VFR aeroplanes): trip + reserve — ____ min at normal cruise; ____ (CAT VFR day local flights 10 min extra per rules). Final reserve on arrival must remain; Minimum fuel / MAYDAY FUEL declarations.","VFR fuel (NCO/CAT VFR aeroplanes): trip + reserve — day: 30 min at normal cruise; night: 45 min (CAT VFR day local flights 10 min extra per rules). Final reserve on arrival must remain; Minimum fuel / MAYDAY FUEL declarations."],["033.03.02.03:05","Fuel planning — general: fill in the gaps → ____: taxi + trip + contingency + alternate + final reserve + additional + extra — ____ (contingency 5 % or 5 min holding at 1 500 ft; final reserve 30 min turbine/45 min piston at 1 500 ft).","IFR fuel: taxi + trip + contingency + alternate + final reserve + additional + extra — details and rules on the #45 page (contingency 5 % or 5 min holding at 1 500 ft; final reserve 30 min turbine/45 min piston at 1 500 ft)."],["033.03.02.01:01","Fuel components & fuel plan (summary) – Taxi: what are the key facts?","AFM tables, local conditions"],["033.03.02.02:01","Fuel components & fuel plan (summary) – Trip: what are the key facts?","take-off → climb → cruise → descent → approach & landing at destination"],["033.03.02.02:01","Fuel components & fuel plan (summary) – Contingency: what are the key facts?","higher of 5 % trip (or remaining trip) / 5 min holding 1 500 ft"],["033.03.02.03:03","Fuel components & fuel plan (summary) – Alternate: what are the key facts?","missed approach → alternate → landing"],["033.03.02.03:05","Fuel components & fuel plan (summary) – Final reserve: what are the key facts?","30 min (turbine) / 45 min (piston) holding 1 500 ft, arrival mass"],["033.03.02.03:07","Fuel components & fuel plan (summary) – Additional: what are the key facts?","engine failure/depressurisation cases, isolated aerodrome"],["033.03.02.04:03","Fuel components & fuel plan (summary) – Extra: what are the key facts?","discretionary (weather, delays, tankering) — fuel penalty for extra mass"],["033.03.02.05:02","Fuel components & fuel plan (summary): fill in the gaps → ____ = sum; complete the fuel section of the nav plan (time and fuel per item).","Total fuel = sum; complete the fuel section of the nav plan (time and fuel per item)."],["033.03.02.01:01","Exam trap (Fuel components & fuel plan (summary)): complete the fact → VFR semicircular: magnetic track, odd/even thousands + ____.","VFR semicircular: magnetic track, odd/even thousands + 500 ft."],["033.01.01.02:05","Exam trap (Fuel components & fuel plan (summary)): complete the fact → Pressure altitude = altitude + (____QNH) × ____","Pressure altitude = altitude + (1013 − QNH) × 30."],["033.03.02.03:05","Exam trap (Fuel components & fuel plan (summary)): complete the fact → VFR reserve: ____ day, ____ night.","VFR reserve: 30 min day, 45 min night."],["033.01.01.01:02","EASA LO 033.01.01.01.02 → Find the frequencies or identifiers of radio-navigation aids from charts.","Select the route considering airspace classification (clearances, VMC minima, radio/transponder requirements), restricted/prohibited/danger areas (and activation times), VFR semicircular levels (above 3 000 ft AGL: magnetic track 000–179° → odd thousands + 500 ft (3 500, 5 500…), 180–359° → even + 500 ft), visually conspicuous points, radio- navigation aids for cross-checking. / From the chart: NAVAID frequencies/idents; communication frequencies and call signs (control units, FIS, weather stations/VOLMET, ATIS)."],["033.01.01.02:02","EASA LO 033.01.01.02.02 → Measure courses and distances from a VFR chart.","Measure true courses at the mid-meridian and distances (scale); find the highest obstacle within a given distance either side of the course (e.g. 5 NM) → minimum altitude. Transfer waypoints, distances, true/magnetic courses (TT − E var/+ W var) to the navigation plan."],["033.01.01.02:04","EASA LO 033.01.01.02.04 → Find the following data from a VFR chart and transfer them to a navigation plan: — waypoints or turning points; — distances; — true/magnetic courses.","Measure true courses at the mid-meridian and distances (scale); find the highest obstacle within a given distance either side of the course (e.g. 5 NM) → minimum altitude. Transfer waypoints, distances, true/magnetic courses (TT − E var/+ W var) to the navigation plan."],["033.01.01.03:02","EASA LO 033.01.01.03.02 → Find all visual procedures which can be expected at the departure, destination and alternate aerodromes.","Study visual departure and arrival procedures (VFR reporting points, entry/exit routes, circuit altitudes, noise-sensitive areas, PPR) and available approaches for departure, destination and alternates."],["033.01.01.05:01","EASA LO 033.01.01.05.01 → Calculate the true airspeed (TAS) from given aircraft performance data, altitude and OAT.","TAS from performance data (power setting, pressure altitude, OAT). WCA/drift and GS from forecast wind (CRP/MDR). Individual and accumulated leg times to destination and alternate; ETA; fuel per leg. / Measure true courses at the mid-meridian and distances (scale); find the highest obstacle within a given distance either side of the course (e.g. 5 NM) → minimum altitude. Transfer waypoints, distances, true/magnetic courses (TT − E var/+ W var) to the navigation plan."],["033.01.01.05:02","EASA LO 033.01.01.05.02 → Calculate wind correction angles (WCAs), drift and ground speeds (GS).","TAS from performance data (power setting, pressure altitude, OAT). WCA/drift and GS from forecast wind (CRP/MDR). Individual and accumulated leg times to destination and alternate; ETA; fuel per leg."],["033.03.01.01:03","EASA LO 033.03.01.01.03 → Calculate the attainable flight time/range from given average fuel flow/consumption and available amount of fuel.","AFM data: fuel capacity (usable), fuel flow vs power/altitude/temperature. Endurance = usable fuel ÷ FF; range = endurance × GS; required fuel = time × FF."],["033.03.01.01:04","EASA LO 033.03.01.01.04 → Calculate the required fuel from given average fuel flow/ consumption and required time/range to be flown.","AFM data: fuel capacity (usable), fuel flow vs power/altitude/temperature. Endurance = usable fuel ÷ FF; range = endurance × GS; required fuel = time × FF."],["033.03.01.01:05","EASA LO 033.03.01.01.05 → Calculate the required fuel for a VFR flight from given forecast meteorological conditions.","AFM tables, local conditions"],["033.03.01.01:06","EASA LO 033.03.01.01.06 → State the minimum amount of remaining fuel required on arrival at the destination and alternate aerodromes/ heliports.","higher of 5 % trip (or remaining trip) / 5 min holding 1 500 ft / missed approach → alternate → landing"],["033.03.01.01:08","EASA LO 033.03.01.01.08 → Calculate the required fuel for an IFR flight from given forecast meteorological conditions.","AFM tables, local conditions"],["033.03.02.02:02","EASA LO 033.03.02.02.02 → Determine the trip fuel for the flight by using data from the fuel tables or graphs from the flight manual.","higher of 5 % trip (or remaining trip) / 5 min holding 1 500 ft / AFM tables, local conditions"],["033.03.02.03:01","EASA LO 033.03.02.03.01 → Explain the reasons for having contingency fuel.","higher of 5 % trip (or remaining trip) / 5 min holding 1 500 ft / IFR fuel: taxi + trip + contingency + alternate + final reserve + additional + extra — details and rules on the #45 page (contingency 5 % or 5 min holding at 1 500 ft; final reserve 30 min turbine/45 min piston at 1 500 ft)."],["033.03.02.03:02","EASA LO 033.03.02.03.02 → Calculate the contingency fuel according to the applicable operational requirements.","higher of 5 % trip (or remaining trip) / 5 min holding 1 500 ft / IFR fuel: taxi + trip + contingency + alternate + final reserve + additional + extra — details and rules on the #45 page (contingency 5 % or 5 min holding at 1 500 ft; final reserve 30 min turbine/45 min piston at 1 500 ft)."],["033.03.02.03:04","EASA LO 033.03.02.03.04 → Calculate the alternate fuel in accordance with the applicable operational requirements and relevant data from the navigation plan and the flight manual.","missed approach → alternate → landing / IFR fuel: taxi + trip + contingency + alternate + final reserve + additional + extra — details and rules on the #45 page (contingency 5 % or 5 min holding at 1 500 ft; final reserve 30 min turbine/45 min piston at 1 500 ft)."],["033.03.02.03:06","EASA LO 033.03.02.03.06 → Calculate the final reserve fuel for an aircraft in accordance with the applicable operational requirements and by using relevant data from the flight manual.","IFR fuel: taxi + trip + contingency + alternate + final reserve + additional + extra — details and rules on the #45 page (contingency 5 % or 5 min holding at 1 500 ft; final reserve 30 min turbine/45 min piston at 1 500 ft). / 30 min (turbine) / 45 min (piston) holding 1 500 ft, arrival mass"],["033.03.02.03:08","EASA LO 033.03.02.03.08 → Calculate the additional fuel for a flight in accordance with the applicable operational requirements.","IFR fuel: taxi + trip + contingency + alternate + final reserve + additional + extra — details and rules on the #45 page (contingency 5 % or 5 min holding at 1 500 ft; final reserve 30 min turbine/45 min piston at 1 500 ft). / engine failure/depressurisation cases, isolated aerodrome"],["033.03.02.04:01","EASA LO 033.03.02.04.01 → Explain the reasons and regulations for having extra fuel in accordance with the applicable operational requirements.","discretionary (weather, delays, tankering) — fuel penalty for extra mass"],["033.03.02.04:02","EASA LO 033.03.02.04.02 → Calculate the possible extra fuel under given conditions.","discretionary (weather, delays, tankering) — fuel penalty for extra mass"],["033.03.02.05:01","EASA LO 033.03.02.05.01 → Calculate the total fuel required for a given flight.","Total fuel = sum; complete the fuel section of the nav plan (time and fuel per item)."]],"26":[["021.09.03.04:01","Transformers, TRUs, static inverters: fill in the gaps → ____: changes ____ (step-up/step-down) by electromagnetic induction between primary and secondary windings on an iron core; ____ (U₂/U₁ = N₂/N₁); power (almost) unchanged → current changes inversely; works only with AC (changing flux). Example: 115 V → 26 V for instruments. Autotransformer: single winding with tap.","Transformer: changes AC voltage (step-up/step-down) by electromagnetic induction between primary and secondary windings on an iron core; voltage ratio = turns ratio (U₂/U₁ = N₂/N₁); power (almost) unchanged → current changes inversely; works only with AC (changing flux). Example: 115 V → 26 V for instruments. Autotransformer: single winding with tap."],["021.09.03.04:01","Transformers, TRUs, static inverters: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ — transformer steps 115/200 V three-phase AC down, rectifier (diodes) converts to ____ for the DC buses (and battery charging) on AC-generation aircraft.","TRU (transformer-rectifier unit): AC → DC — transformer steps 115/200 V three-phase AC down, rectifier (diodes) converts to 28 V DC for the DC buses (and battery charging) on AC-generation aircraft."],["021.09.03.04:03","Transformers, TRUs, static inverters: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ — converts battery/DC bus power (28 V DC) into ____ for essential AC loads (standby instruments, avionics) when the main AC generation fails or on DC-generation aircraft. \"Static\" = solid-state, no moving parts (replaced rotary inverters).","Static inverter: DC → AC — converts battery/DC bus power (28 V DC) into 115 V (and 26 V) single-phase 400 Hz AC for essential AC loads (standby instruments, avionics) when the main AC generation fails or on DC-generation aircraft. \"Static\" = solid-state, no moving parts (replaced rotary inverters)."],["021.09.05.01:02","Electric motors: fill in the gaps → Purpose: ____ (the reverse of a generator). Because the design is similar, a generator and a motor can be combined into a ____ ____ (turboprops, APUs, some jets).","Purpose: convert electrical energy into mechanical energy (the reverse of a generator). Because the design is similar, a generator and a motor can be combined into a starter- generator (turboprops, APUs, some jets)."],["021.09.05.01:03","Electric motors: fill in the gaps → ____: larger engines need much more energy to start → small turbines/pistons use ____; large turbofans use ____ (electric starting only possible with very high power, e.g. B787 uses starter-generators on a high-voltage system).","Starting: larger engines need much more energy to start → small turbines/pistons use electric starters; large turbofans use pneumatic (air turbine) starters (electric starting only possible with very high power, e.g. B787 uses starter-generators on a high-voltage system)."],["021.09.05.02:01","Electric motors: fill in the gaps → ____ is produced by the interaction of the ____ of stator and rotor; it depends on the ____ voltage (field strength): DC series-wound motors give very high starting torque (starters, actuators); shunt-wound motors keep near-constant speed; AC induction motors (rotating field) — simple, robust (pumps, fans); synchronous motors (constant speed).","Torque is produced by the interaction of the magnetic fields of stator and rotor; it depends on the supplied current and voltage (field strength): DC series-wound motors give very high starting torque (starters, actuators); shunt-wound motors keep near-constant speed; AC induction motors (rotating field) — simple, robust (pumps, fans); synchronous motors (constant speed)."],["021.09.05.02:03","Electric motors: fill in the gaps → ____: rotor blocked → no back-EMF → ____ ____ → overheating, burnt windings, fire risk → protection trips (CB, thermal cut-out). Rotor runaway: loss of load or control → overspeed → mechanical damage/disintegration, system malfunction (e.g. trim runaway) → cut power (CB/switch).","Rotor seizure: rotor blocked → no back-EMF → very high current → overheating, burnt windings, fire risk → protection trips (CB, thermal cut-out). Rotor runaway: loss of load or control → overspeed → mechanical damage/disintegration, system malfunction (e.g. trim runaway) → cut power (CB/switch)."],["021.09.05.03:01","Electric motors: fill in the gaps → Components: ____ (rotating part — armature) and ____ (stationary part — field windings/magnets); brushes/commutator in DC motors.","Components: rotor (rotating part — armature) and stator (stationary part — field windings/magnets); brushes/commutator in DC motors."],["021.09.03.04:02","EASA LO 021.09.03.04.02 → State the function of a TRU and its purpose, including type of output.","TRU (transformer-rectifier unit): AC → DC — transformer steps 115/200 V three-phase AC down, rectifier (diodes) converts to 28 V DC for the DC buses (and battery charging) on AC-generation aircraft."],["021.09.05.01:01","EASA LO 021.09.05.01.01 → State that the purpose of an electrical motor is to convert electrical energy into mechanical energy.","Purpose: convert electrical energy into mechanical energy (the reverse of a generator). Because the design is similar, a generator and a motor can be combined into a starter- generator (turboprops, APUs, some jets)."],["021.09.05.02:02","EASA LO 021.09.05.02.02 → State that electrical motors can be either AC or DC.","Purpose: convert electrical energy into mechanical energy (the reverse of a generator). Because the design is similar, a generator and a motor can be combined into a starter- generator (turboprops, APUs, some jets)."]],"27":[["081.03.03.01:01","Pilot-induced buffet/stall situations: fill in the gaps → ____: forgotten/incorrect ____ → ____Lmax lower, αcrit lower → at normal VR/V2 the wing is close to or beyond the stall → buffet, wing drop, failure to climb (e.g. Spanair, Northwest 255).","Inappropriate take-off configuration: forgotten/incorrect leading-edge devices (slats/flaps) → CLmax lower, αcrit lower → at normal VR/V2 the wing is close to or beyond the stall → buffet, wing drop, failure to climb (e.g. Spanair, Northwest 255)."],["081.03.03.01:01","Pilot-induced buffet/stall situations: fill in the gaps → Mitigation: ____, cross-check of V-speeds/configuration, stall warning tied to actual configuration.","Mitigation: checklists, take-off configuration warning, cross-check of V-speeds/configuration, stall warning tied to actual configuration."],["081.03.03.01:01","Pilot-induced buffet/stall situations: fill in the gaps → ____: load factor ____ → stall speed × √n (60° → 1.41 VS) → stall/buffet at speeds that are normally safe. Mitigation: limit bank, maintain speed margin, adding power.","Steep turns: load factor n = 1/cos Ø → stall speed × √n (60° → 1.41 VS) → stall/buffet at speeds that are normally safe. Mitigation: limit bank, maintain speed margin, adding power."],["081.03.03.01:01","Pilot-induced buffet/stall situations: fill in the gaps → ____: thrust line below CG → strong ____ + slow speed + high trim → excessive pitch → speed decays → stall (unless pilot pushes and re-trims). Mitigation: anticipate the pitch-up, target pitch ~15°, push/trim, manage thrust.","Go-around with TOGA thrust (underslung engines): thrust line below CG → strong nose-up pitching moment + slow speed + high trim → excessive pitch → speed decays → stall (unless pilot pushes and re-trims). Mitigation: anticipate the pitch-up, target pitch ~15°, push/trim, manage thrust."],["081.03.03.01:02","Environmental — low altitude: fill in the gaps → ____: strong up/downdrafts and gusts suddenly increase α; hail/ice; heavy rain reduces CLmax. ____ ____: sudden loss of headwind/downdraft → IAS drops, α increases as the pilot pulls to hold the path → stall. Turbulence: vertical gusts change α (Δα ≈ gust/TAS) → momentary stall at low speed margin.","Thunderstorms: strong up/downdrafts and gusts suddenly increase α; hail/ice; heavy rain reduces CLmax. Wind shear/microburst: sudden loss of headwind/downdraft → IAS drops, α increases as the pilot pulls to hold the path → stall. Turbulence: vertical gusts change α (Δα ≈ gust/TAS) → momentary stall at low speed margin."],["081.03.03.01:02","Environmental — low altitude: fill in the gaps → ____: induced roll/sink, α changes near the ground. ____: reduced CLmax/αcrit, higher VS, possibly no stall warning, tailplane stall.","Wake turbulence: induced roll/sink, α changes near the ground. Icing: reduced CLmax/αcrit, higher VS, possibly no stall warning, tailplane stall."],["081.03.03.01:02","Environmental — low altitude: what do you remember about this? → Mitigation: avoid (weather radar, delay), extra speed margins (VREF …","Mitigation: avoid (weather radar, delay), extra speed margins (VREF additives), wind-shear recovery (TOGA, pitch ~15°, no config change), wake separations, de/anti-icing, icing speeds."],["081.03.03.01:02","Environmental — high altitude: fill in the gaps → ____: very high tops, severe turbulence/updrafts, ice crystals (probe icing → unreliable speed, e.g. AF447) at altitudes where the ____ (low-speed and high-speed buffet close together — coffin corner).","Thunderstorms in the ITCZ: very high tops, severe turbulence/updrafts, ice crystals (probe icing → unreliable speed, e.g. AF447) at altitudes where the buffet margin is small (low-speed and high-speed buffet close together — coffin corner)."],["081.03.03.01:02","Environmental — high altitude: fill in the gaps → ____ ____: CAT and strong wind shear → gusts/speed changes → buffet onset (low-or high-speed). Mitigation: avoid Cb (≥ 20 NM), use turbulence penetration speed, choose a lower level with a larger ____, reduce mass-related altitude (step climb timing).","Jet streams: CAT and strong wind shear → gusts/speed changes → buffet onset (low-or high-speed). Mitigation: avoid Cb (≥ 20 NM), use turbulence penetration speed, choose a lower level with a larger buffet margin (1.3 g), reduce mass-related altitude (step climb timing)."],["081.03.03.01:04","Automation/system situations: fill in the gaps → ____: e.g. ____ with a high rate selected and insufficient thrust → the AP holds V/S while ____ → stall (no speed protection in V/S). Mitigation: use speed-on-elevator modes (LVL CHG/OP CLB) for large climbs, monitor speed/FMA, autothrust.","Inappropriate autopilot climb mode: e.g. V/S mode with a high rate selected and insufficient thrust → the AP holds V/S while speed decays → stall (no speed protection in V/S). Mitigation: use speed-on-elevator modes (LVL CHG/OP CLB) for large climbs, monitor speed/FMA, autothrust."],["081.03.03.01:01","Automation/system situations: fill in the gaps → ____ (pitot icing, blockage, ADC failure): pilot/automation reacting to false speed → overspeed or stall. Mitigation: recognise (disagreeing ASIs, attitude/thrust mismatch), ____ from the QRH, use AoA/GPS/stall warning cues.","Loss of or unreliable airspeed (pitot icing, blockage, ADC failure): pilot/automation reacting to false speed → overspeed or stall. Mitigation: recognise (disagreeing ASIs, attitude/thrust mismatch), AP/AT off, fly pitch & thrust from the QRH, use AoA/GPS/stall warning cues."],["081.03.04.01:01","Recognising a stall: fill in the gaps → A stall can occur at ____ — it happens whenever ________ (e.g. high-g pull at high speed, nose-down attitude with a high descent rate).","A stall can occur at any airspeed, attitude or altitude — it happens whenever α exceeds αcrit (e.g. high-g pull at high speed, nose-down attitude with a high descent rate)."],["081.03.04.01:02","Recognising a stall: fill in the gaps → ____ is recognised by ____ ____ plus at least one of: buffet (can be heavy), lack of pitch authority, uncommanded pitch-down and/or roll, inability to arrest the descent rate.","Stalled condition is recognised by continuous stall-warning activation plus at least one of: buffet (can be heavy), lack of pitch authority, uncommanded pitch-down and/or roll, inability to arrest the descent rate."],["081.03.04.01:03","Recognising a stall: fill in the gaps → ____ = natural or synthetic indication when ____ the stall: ____, reduced roll stability and aileron effectiveness, visual or aural clues/warnings (horn, voice \"STALL\"), stick shaker, reduced elevator (pitch) authority, inability to maintain altitude or arrest the descent.","Stall warning = natural or synthetic indication when approaching the stall: aerodynamic buffeting, reduced roll stability and aileron effectiveness, visual or aural clues/warnings (horn, voice \"STALL\"), stick shaker, reduced elevator (pitch) authority, inability to maintain altitude or arrest the descent."],["081.03.04.01:01","Recognising a stall: fill in the gaps → Recovery (UPRT principle): ____ (nose down/push, reduce back pressure; trim if needed), ____, ____ appropriate (beware pitch-up with underslung engines), speedbrakes retracted, return to the desired path avoiding secondary stall.","Recovery (UPRT principle): reduce α first (nose down/push, reduce back pressure; trim if needed), wings level, thrust as appropriate (beware pitch-up with underslung engines), speedbrakes retracted, return to the desired path avoiding secondary stall."]],"28":[["021.09.01.08:01","Semiconductors: fill in the gaps → Semiconductor materials (silicon, germanium) with doping (N-type, P-type) → ____ (PN junction: current flows in one direction only — rectification, reverse-polarity protection), ____ (constant voltage reference), ____ (switching/amplifying), LEDs, thyristors, integrated circuits.","Semiconductor materials (silicon, germanium) with doping (N-type, P-type) → diodes (PN junction: current flows in one direction only — rectification, reverse-polarity protection), Zener diodes (constant voltage reference), transistors (switching/amplifying), LEDs, thyristors, integrated circuits."],["021.09.01.08:01","Semiconductors: fill in the gaps → ____: semiconductor conductivity ____ ____ (NTC behaviour; leakage current rises) → at high temperature: incorrect function, drift, thermal runaway, reduced longevity or destruction → cooling (heat sinks, avionics cooling fans/air); avionics cooling failure → overheat warnings/shutdown. Very low temperatures also affect performance.","Temperature: semiconductor conductivity increases with temperature (NTC behaviour; leakage current rises) → at high temperature: incorrect function, drift, thermal runaway, reduced longevity or destruction → cooling (heat sinks, avionics cooling fans/air); avionics cooling failure → overheat warnings/shutdown. Very low temperatures also affect performance."],["021.09.01.08:01","Logic functions – AND: what are the key facts?","Output = 1 when…: all inputs are 1 · Truth (A,B → Q): 11→1, otherwise 0"],["021.09.01.08:01","Logic functions – OR: what are the key facts?","Output = 1 when…: at least one input is 1 · Truth (A,B → Q): 00→0, otherwise 1"],["021.09.01.08:01","Logic functions – NOT (inverter): what are the key facts?","Output = 1 when…: input is 0 (inverts) · Truth (A,B → Q): 0→1, 1→0"],["021.09.01.08:02","Logic functions – NAND (NOT-AND): what are the key facts?","Output = 1 when…: not all inputs are 1 · Truth (A,B → Q): 11→0, otherwise 1"],["021.09.01.08:02","Logic functions – NOR (NOT-OR): what are the key facts?","Output = 1 when…: all inputs are 0 · Truth (A,B → Q): 00→1, otherwise 0"],["021.09.01.08:02","Logic functions: fill in the gaps → ____: AND = D-shaped body (flat input side); OR = curved (shield-shaped) input side with pointed output; NOT = triangle with a small ____ at the output; NAND/NOR = AND/OR symbol ____ on the output (a bubble on any input/output means inversion).","ANSI/MIL symbols: AND = D-shaped body (flat input side); OR = curved (shield-shaped) input side with pointed output; NOT = triangle with a small circle (bubble) at the output; NAND/NOR = AND/OR symbol with a bubble on the output (a bubble on any input/output means inversion)."],["021.09.01.08:01","Reading FCOM logic schematics: what do you remember about this? → Follow signals from the inputs (sensors, switches, \"air/ground\" squat …","Follow signals from the inputs (sensors, switches, \"air/ground\" squat switch, thrust-lever position, speeds) through the gates to the output (warning, valve command, relay)."],["021.09.01.08:01","Reading FCOM logic schematics: fill in the gaps → Example — ____: thrust levers advanced ____ aircraft on ground ____ (flaps not in take-off range OR speedbrakes not stowed OR stab trim outside green band OR parking brake set) → warning.","Example — take-off configuration warning: thrust levers advanced AND aircraft on ground AND (flaps not in take-off range OR speedbrakes not stowed OR stab trim outside green band OR parking brake set) → warning."],["021.09.01.08:01","Reading FCOM logic schematics: fill in the gaps → Example — ____: throttle retarded ____ gear ____ down-and-locked → horn. Example — a valve closes if (fire handle pulled) OR (overheat AND NOT override).","Example — gear warning: throttle retarded AND gear NOT down-and-locked → horn. Example — a valve closes if (fire handle pulled) OR (overheat AND NOT override)."],["021.09.01.08:01","Reading FCOM logic schematics: fill in the gaps → Check each condition's state (1/0), evaluate gate by gate; watch ____ (inversions) and ____ inputs (\"not on ground\" = airborne).","Check each condition's state (1/0), evaluate gate by gate; watch bubbles (inversions) and NOT inputs (\"not on ground\" = airborne)."],["021.09.01.08:01","Exam trap (Reading FCOM logic schematics): complete the fact → NAND gives ____only when all inputs are ____; NOR gives 1 only when all inputs are 0.","NAND gives 0 only when all inputs are 1; NOR gives 1 only when all inputs are 0."]],"29":[["040.01.01.01:01","Competence, TEM & SHELL: fill in the gaps → ____. The ____: application of procedures · communication · flight path management (automation) · flight path management (manual control) · leadership & teamwork · problem-solving & decision-making · situation awareness · workload management.","Competence = knowledge + skills + attitudes (KSA). The ICAO 8 core competencies: application of procedures · communication · flight path management (automation) · flight path management (manual control) · leadership & teamwork · problem-solving & decision-making · situation awareness · workload management."],["040.01.03.01:08","Competence, TEM & SHELL: fill in the gaps → ____ (ICAO Doc 9683) has 3 components: ____, ____ and undesired aircraft states (UAS).","TEM model (ICAO Doc 9683) has 3 components: threats, errors and undesired aircraft states (UAS)."],["040.01.03.01:07","Competence, TEM & SHELL: fill in the gaps → ____ are events or errors outside the crew's influence that increase complexity.","Threats are events or errors outside the crew's influence that increase complexity."],["040.01.03.01:04","Competence, TEM & SHELL: fill in the gaps → ____ threats are hidden in the system: equipment design, regulatory gaps, organisational culture, poor procedures.","Latent threats are hidden in the system: equipment design, regulatory gaps, organisational culture, poor procedures."],["040.01.03.01:03","Competence, TEM & SHELL: fill in the gaps → ____ threats: weather, terrain, ATC, airport, traffic.","Environmental threats: weather, terrain, ATC, airport, traffic."],["040.01.03.01:04","Competence, TEM & SHELL: fill in the gaps → ____ threats: commercial pressure, scheduling, maintenance, documentation.","Organisational threats: commercial pressure, scheduling, maintenance, documentation."],["040.01.03.01:04","Competence, TEM & SHELL: fill in the gaps → ____ = a crew action or inaction that leads to a deviation from crew or organisational intentions/expectations. Types:","Error = a crew action or inaction that leads to a deviation from crew or organisational intentions/expectations. Types:"],["040.01.03.01:07","Competence, TEM & SHELL: fill in the gaps → ____: wrong checklist or SOP deviation.","Procedural: wrong checklist or SOP deviation."],["040.01.01.01:01","Competence, TEM & SHELL: fill in the gaps → ____: misheard clearance, crew miscommunication.","Communication: misheard clearance, crew miscommunication."],["040.01.03.01:07","Competence, TEM & SHELL: fill in the gaps → ____: manual flying or automation errors.","Aircraft handling: manual flying or automation errors."],["040.02.02.06:05","Competence, TEM & SHELL: fill in the gaps → ____ = a crew-induced position/speed/attitude/configuration deviation that reduces safety margins (e.g. unstable approach, wrong configuration, runway incursion, altitude bust).","UAS = a crew-induced position/speed/attitude/configuration deviation that reduces safety margins (e.g. unstable approach, wrong configuration, runway incursion, altitude bust)."],["040.01.01.01:01","Competence, TEM & SHELL: fill in the gaps → ____: SOPs, checklists, briefings, monitoring & cross-checking, CRM, planning, automation management.","Countermeasures: SOPs, checklists, briefings, monitoring & cross-checking, CRM, planning, automation management."],["040.01.03.01:08","Competence, TEM & SHELL: fill in the gaps → ____: ____oftware (procedures, checklists), ____ardware (aircraft, controls), Environment, Liveware (the central human), Liveware (other people).","SHELL model: Software (procedures, checklists), Hardware (aircraft, controls), Environment, Liveware (the central human), Liveware (other people)."],["040.01.03.01:06","Competence, TEM & SHELL: fill in the gaps → Mismatches at the ____ create errors → used to design cockpits, procedures and teamwork.","Mismatches at the interfaces with the central L create errors → used to design cockpits, procedures and teamwork."],["040.01.04.01:01","Safety culture & SMS: fill in the gaps → ____: errors are reported and discussed, trust, learning. ____: blame, concealment.","Open culture: errors are reported and discussed, trust, learning. Closed culture: blame, concealment."],["040.01.04.01:02","Safety culture & SMS: what do you remember about this? → National culture (e.g. power distance, individualism) shapes how open …","National culture (e.g. power distance, individualism) shapes how open crews are to speaking up."],["040.01.04.01:03","Safety culture & SMS: fill in the gaps → \"____\" in a commercial company competes with production pressure → safety needs to be a real priority backed by resources.","\"Safety first\" in a commercial company competes with production pressure → safety needs to be a real priority backed by resources."],["040.01.04.01:04","Safety culture & SMS: fill in the gaps → ____: defences are layers with holes (____ + ____). An accident happens when the holes line up.","Reason's Swiss Cheese Model: defences are layers with holes (latent conditions + active failures). An accident happens when the holes line up."],["040.01.04.01:07","Safety culture & SMS: fill in the gaps → ____ of safety culture: ____ ____.","Reason's 5 components of safety culture: informed, reporting, learning, just, flexible."],["040.01.04.01:05","Safety culture & SMS: what do you remember about this? → Good safety culture needs management commitment, reporting systems, …","Good safety culture needs management commitment, reporting systems, feedback, training and a just culture."],["040.01.04.01:06","Safety culture & SMS: fill in the gaps → ____: honest errors aren't punished, but ____ ____. A non-punitive culture doesn't punish anything.","Just culture: honest errors aren't punished, but gross negligence, wilful violations and destructive acts are. A non-punitive culture doesn't punish anything."],["040.01.04.01:08","Safety culture & SMS: fill in the gaps → ____: safety policy, ____ (assess probability × severity, mitigate), safety assurance, promotion.","SMS: safety policy, hazard identification & risk management (assess probability × severity, mitigate), safety assurance, promotion."],["040.01.04.01:08","Safety culture & SMS: what do you remember about this? → It defines how the organisation manages risk, identifies workplace …","It defines how the organisation manages risk, identifies workplace risks and controls them, and ensures communication at all levels. Safety culture is its foundation."],["040.02.02.06:01","Senses & nervous system: fill in the gaps → ____: vision, hearing, equilibrium (vestibular), touch/pressure, proprioception (kinaesthetic), smell, taste, temperature, pain.","Senses: vision, hearing, equilibrium (vestibular), touch/pressure, proprioception (kinaesthetic), smell, taste, temperature, pain."],["040.02.02.02:01","Senses & nervous system: fill in the gaps → ____ = the minimum stimulus that can be perceived.","Sensory threshold = the minimum stimulus that can be perceived."],["040.02.02.02:03","Senses & nervous system: fill in the gaps → ____ = the ability to detect weak stimuli. For the eye it changes with light adaptation (rods are very sensitive).","Sensitivity = the ability to detect weak stimuli. For the eye it changes with light adaptation (rods are very sensitive)."],["040.02.02.02:03","Senses & nervous system: fill in the gaps → ____: the response falls under a constant stimulus (e.g. smell, pressure of the seat, dark adaptation).","Sensory adaptation: the response falls under a constant stimulus (e.g. smell, pressure of the seat, dark adaptation)."],["040.02.02.02:04","Senses & nervous system: fill in the gaps → ____ = reduced response to repeated stimuli → risk of ____ or ignoring alarms (complacency).","Habituation = reduced response to repeated stimuli → risk of missing warnings or ignoring alarms (complacency)."],["040.02.02.03:01","Vision: fill in the gaps → Cornea → aqueous humour → iris/pupil → lens → vitreous → ____ (rods/cones) → optic nerve → visual cortex (occipital).","Cornea → aqueous humour → iris/pupil → lens → vitreous → retina (rods/cones) → optic nerve → visual cortex (occipital)."],["040.02.02.03:01","Vision: fill in the gaps → ____: most of the refraction. ____: fine focus. ____: light control.","Cornea: most of the refraction. Lens: fine focus. Iris: light control."],["040.02.02.03:03","Vision: fill in the gaps → ____ = the lens changing shape to focus near/far.","Accommodation = the lens changing shape to focus near/far."],["040.02.02.03:01","Vision: fill in the gaps → ~7 million, concentrated in the ____.","~7 million, concentrated in the fovea."],["040.02.02.03:09","Vision: fill in the gaps → Colour, high ____, need bright light (____ vision).","Colour, high acuity, need bright light (photopic vision)."],["040.02.02.03:01","Vision: fill in the gaps → ~120 million, in the ____; none in the fovea.","~120 million, in the periphery; none in the fovea."],["040.02.02.02:02","Vision: fill in the gaps → Black/white, high sensitivity, motion detection, night vision (____ vision).","Black/white, high sensitivity, motion detection, night vision (scotopic vision)."],["040.02.02.03:06","Vision: fill in the gaps → ____ is highest in the fovea (central ~1–2°); the visual field is ~180°+ horizontally. ____ vision detects motion.","Visual acuity is highest in the fovea (central ~1–2°); the visual field is ~180°+ horizontally. Peripheral vision detects motion."],["040.02.02.03:01","Vision: fill in the gaps → Acuity is degraded by hypoxia, fatigue, alcohol, dirty windscreens, glare, ____, low contrast.","Acuity is degraded by hypoxia, fatigue, alcohol, dirty windscreens, glare, empty-field myopia, low contrast."],["040.02.02.03:01","Vision: fill in the gaps → There is a ____ at night (fovea cones inactive) → ____ (off-centre viewing).","There is a central blind area at night (fovea cones inactive) → look ~10–20° off-centre (off-centre viewing)."],["040.02.02.03:08","Vision: fill in the gaps → ____: by day, move in ____ with ____ stops. At night, scan slowly with off-centre viewing.","Scanning: by day, move in ~10° sectors with ~1 s stops. At night, scan slowly with off-centre viewing."],["040.02.02.03:09","Vision: fill in the gaps → ____: to dark takes ____ (rods; cones ~7 min); to bright light takes a few ____. Protect with red/dim light.","Adaptation: to dark takes ~30 min (rods; cones ~7 min); to bright light takes a few seconds to ~10 s. Protect with red/dim light."],["040.02.02.03:10","Vision: fill in the gaps → ____ measurably reduce night vision.","Hypoxia, smoking (CO) and altitude > 5 000 ft measurably reduce night vision."],["040.02.02.03:11","Vision: fill in the gaps → ____: usually hereditary red-green deficiency, far more common in males.","Colour blindness: usually hereditary red-green deficiency, far more common in males."],["040.02.02.03:14","Vision: fill in the gaps → ____ gives depth perception (stereopsis) only up to ~____; beyond that, ____ are used:","Binocular vision gives depth perception (stereopsis) only up to ~6 m; beyond that, monocular cues are used:"],["040.02.02.06:03","Vision: what do you remember about this? → relative size, overlap/interposition, texture gradient, linear …","relative size, overlap/interposition, texture gradient, linear perspective, aerial perspective (haze), motion parallax, light & shadow."],["040.02.02.03:01","Vision: what do you remember about this? → This matters for judging height/distance on …","This matters for judging height/distance on approach."],["040.02.02.03:16","Vision: fill in the gaps → ____: the image falls behind the retina → convex (+) lens.","Long-sightedness (hypermetropia): the image falls behind the retina → convex (+) lens."],["040.02.02.03:16","Vision: fill in the gaps → ____: the image falls in front → concave (−) lens.","Short-sightedness (myopia): the image falls in front → concave (−) lens."],["040.02.02.03:01","Vision: fill in the gaps → ____: irregular corneal curvature → cylindrical lens.","Astigmatism: irregular corneal curvature → cylindrical lens."],["040.02.02.03:03","Vision: fill in the gaps → ____: age-related loss of accommodation (lens elasticity) → reading glasses/multifocals.","Presbyopia: age-related loss of accommodation (lens elasticity) → reading glasses/multifocals."],["040.02.02.03:15","Vision: fill in the gaps → ____: lens clouding (age, UV) → surgery; wear UV protection.","Cataract: lens clouding (age, UV) → surgery; wear UV protection."],["040.02.02.03:06","Vision: fill in the gaps → ____: raised eye pressure → optic nerve damage, loss of the peripheral field → regular checks.","Glaucoma: raised eye pressure → optic nerve damage, loss of the peripheral field → regular checks."],["040.02.02.03:18","Vision: fill in the gaps → ____:","Sunglasses and eye protection:"],["040.02.02.03:15","Vision: what do you remember about this? → Sunglasses protect the retina from blue light and …","Sunglasses protect the retina from blue light and UV."],["040.02.02.03:01","Vision: fill in the gaps → ____ polarised lenses (hide windscreen stress patterns and LCD displays), photochromic lenses (slow reaction behind the windscreen), strongly tinted or coloured lenses (colour perception) and thin-framed lenses (they restrict the field).","Avoid polarised lenses (hide windscreen stress patterns and LCD displays), photochromic lenses (slow reaction behind the windscreen), strongly tinted or coloured lenses (colour perception) and thin-framed lenses (they restrict the field)."],["040.02.02.03:19","Vision: fill in the gaps → ____: turn up cockpit lights at night near Cb; look inside; use one eye; visors.","Flash blindness: turn up cockpit lights at night near Cb; look inside; use one eye; visors."],["040.02.02.03:20","Vision: fill in the gaps → ____: dry cockpit air → irritation; they can move or be lost; hypoxia of the cornea; no bifocal/monovision lenses.","Contact lenses: dry cockpit air → irritation; they can move or be lost; hypoxia of the cornea; no bifocal/monovision lenses."],["040.02.02.03:21","Vision: fill in the gaps → ____: if corrective lenses are required, the medical carries a limitation. Pilots must ____ ____.","Rules: if corrective lenses are required, the medical carries a limitation. Pilots must wear them and carry a spare pair of spectacles."],["040.02.02.03:22","Vision: fill in the gaps → ____ (optic disc, ~15° temporal): an object there is unseen with one eye. The other eye covers it unless it's blocked by the frame → move your head and scan.","Blind spot (optic disc, ~15° temporal): an object there is unseen with one eye. The other eye covers it unless it's blocked by the frame → move your head and scan."],["040.02.02.04:02","Hearing: fill in the gaps → ____ (pinna, canal) → ____ (eardrum, ossicles: hammer/anvil/stirrup, ____ for pressure equalisation) → inner ear (cochlea = hearing; vestibular apparatus = balance).","Outer ear (pinna, canal) → middle ear (eardrum, ossicles: hammer/anvil/stirrup, Eustachian tube for pressure equalisation) → inner ear (cochlea = hearing; vestibular apparatus = balance)."],["040.02.02.04:01","Hearing: fill in the gaps → ____: blockage in the outer/middle ear (wax, infection, perforated eardrum, ossicle damage).","Conductive deafness: blockage in the outer/middle ear (wax, infection, perforated eardrum, ossicle damage)."],["040.02.02.04:03","Hearing: fill in the gaps → ____: damage to the cochlear hair cells from noise.","NIHL: damage to the cochlear hair cells from noise."],["040.02.02.04:03","Hearing: fill in the gaps → ____: age-related loss of high frequencies.","Presbycusis: age-related loss of high frequencies."],["040.02.02.04:05","Hearing: fill in the gaps → ____: NIHL risk from ____ over ~8 h per day. Short very loud noises (> ~120–140 dB) cause immediate damage.","Noise exposure: NIHL risk from ~85–90 dB(A) over ~8 h per day. Short very loud noises (> ~120–140 dB) cause immediate damage."],["040.02.02.04:04","Hearing: what do you remember about this? → Environmental noise also causes fatigue, masking of communications …","Environmental noise also causes fatigue, masking of communications and annoyance."],["040.02.02.04:01","Hearing: fill in the gaps → ____: engines, propellers, airflow, headsets turned too loud, ramp noise, cockpit alerts.","Sources in flight: engines, propellers, airflow, headsets turned too loud, ramp noise, cockpit alerts."],["040.02.02.04:01","Hearing: fill in the gaps → ____: ANR headsets, low volume, ear protection on the ramp, audiometric checks.","Precautions: ANR headsets, low volume, ear protection on the ramp, audiometric checks."],["040.02.02.05:04","Equilibrium & air sickness: fill in the gaps → ____ (fluid-filled, at right angles, with ____ and hair cells) detect ____.","3 semicircular canals (fluid-filled, at right angles, with cupula and hair cells) detect angular acceleration."],["040.02.02.05:03","Equilibrium & air sickness: fill in the gaps → ____ (utricle, saccule: crystals on hair cells) detect ____ ____.","Otoliths (utricle, saccule: crystals on hair cells) detect linear acceleration and gravity."],["040.02.02.05:04","Equilibrium & air sickness: fill in the gaps → The canals are stimulated when fluid (endolymph) ____ during angular acceleration and bends the cupula.","The canals are stimulated when fluid (endolymph) lags during angular acceleration and bends the cupula."],["040.02.02.05:06","Equilibrium & air sickness: fill in the gaps → At ____ the fluid catches up → ____ (threshold ~2°/s²). When rotation stops, you feel a false opposite turn.","At constant rotation the fluid catches up → no sensation (threshold ~2°/s²). When rotation stops, you feel a false opposite turn."],["040.02.02.05:06","Equilibrium & air sickness: fill in the gaps → ____: a mismatch between visual and vestibular inputs.","Air sickness: a mismatch between visual and vestibular inputs."],["040.02.02.05:05","Equilibrium & air sickness: what do you remember about this? → Symptoms: pallor, sweating, nausea, vomiting, salivation, headache, …","Symptoms: pallor, sweating, nausea, vomiting, salivation, headache, apathy."],["040.02.02.05:06","Equilibrium & air sickness: what do you remember about this? → Causes: turbulence, head movements, anxiety, heat, reading, lack of …","Causes: turbulence, head movements, anxiety, heat, reading, lack of an outside view."],["040.02.02.05:07","Equilibrium & air sickness: fill in the gaps → Actions: look at the horizon, fresh cool air, minimise head movement, take control, loosen clothing. ____ ____.","Actions: look at the horizon, fresh cool air, minimise head movement, take control, loosen clothing. No medication without an AME's approval."],["040.02.02.06:01","Illusions & spatial disorientation: fill in the gaps → ____ comes from vision (~80 %), the vestibular system and proprioception.","Orientation comes from vision (~80 %), the vestibular system and proprioception."],["040.02.02.06:02","Illusions & spatial disorientation: fill in the gaps → ____ = a false perception of reality.","Illusion = a false perception of reality."],["040.02.02.06:03","Illusions & spatial disorientation: fill in the gaps → ____: a sloping runway seems a normal shape.","Shape constancy: a sloping runway seems a normal shape."],["040.02.02.06:01","Illusions & spatial disorientation: fill in the gaps → ____: a larger or smaller runway than usual.","Size constancy: a larger or smaller runway than usual."],["040.02.02.06:03","Illusions & spatial disorientation: fill in the gaps → ____: haze makes things look further away, clear air nearer.","Aerial/atmospheric perspective: haze makes things look further away, clear air nearer."],["040.02.02.06:03","Illusions & spatial disorientation: fill in the gaps → ____: black hole, empty field.","Absence of focal/ambient cues: black hole, empty field."],["040.02.02.06:01","Illusions & spatial disorientation: fill in the gaps → ____: a fixed light at night seems to move → keep eyes moving.","Autokinesis: a fixed light at night seems to move → keep eyes moving."],["040.02.02.06:01","Illusions & spatial disorientation: fill in the gaps → ____: a moving surrounding seems like self-motion.","Vection: a moving surrounding seems like self-motion."],["040.02.02.06:06","Illusions & spatial disorientation: fill in the gaps → ____: sloping cloud tops, lights, coastlines.","False horizons: sloping cloud tops, lights, coastlines."],["040.02.02.06:01","Illusions & spatial disorientation: fill in the gaps → ____: the eye focuses ~1–2 m in featureless sky.","Empty-field myopia: the eye focuses ~1–2 m in featureless sky."],["040.02.02.06:03","Illusions & spatial disorientation: fill in the gaps → ____: sloping terrain is taken as the horizon.","Surface planes: sloping terrain is taken as the horizon."],["040.02.02.06:01","Illusions & spatial disorientation: fill in the gaps → ____ — see the table below; stick to glidepath aids (PAPI/ILS) and cross-check altitude/distance.","Approach illusions — see the table below; stick to glidepath aids (PAPI/ILS) and cross-check altitude/distance."],["040.02.02.06:01","Illusions & spatial disorientation: fill in the gaps → ____ (strobes, propeller/rotor light at ____) → disorientation, nausea, even seizures → switch strobes off in cloud, change rpm.","Flicker vertigo (strobes, propeller/rotor light at ~4–20 Hz) → disorientation, nausea, even seizures → switch strobes off in cloud, change rpm."],["040.02.02.06:01","Illusions & spatial disorientation: fill in the gaps → ____: a slow roll goes undetected; the correction is felt as banking the opposite way.","Leans: a slow roll goes undetected; the correction is felt as banking the opposite way."],["040.02.02.06:01","Illusions & spatial disorientation: fill in the gaps → ____: head movement during a turn stimulates a second canal → violent tumbling.","Coriolis illusion: head movement during a turn stimulates a second canal → violent tumbling."],["040.02.02.06:01","Illusions & spatial disorientation: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ is felt as ____ → the pilot pushes down (dangerous at night after take-off/go-around); deceleration is felt as pitch-down.","Somatogravic illusion: acceleration is felt as pitch-up → the pilot pushes down (dangerous at night after take-off/go-around); deceleration is felt as pitch-down."],["040.02.02.06:10","Illusions & spatial disorientation: fill in the gaps → ____.","G-effect / inversion illusion."],["040.02.02.06:10","Illusions & spatial disorientation: fill in the gaps → ____ = the sensation of spinning. ____ = a specific vestibular cross-coupling. ____ = a false perception of attitude/position/motion.","Vertigo = the sensation of spinning. Coriolis = a specific vestibular cross-coupling. Spatial disorientation = a false perception of attitude/position/motion."],["040.02.02.06:09","Illusions & spatial disorientation: fill in the gaps → The ____ without a visual horizon or in IMC.","The \"seat-of-the-pants\" sense is completely unreliable without a visual horizon or in IMC."],["040.02.02.06:09","Illusions & spatial disorientation: fill in the gaps → ____: instrument training and ____, avoid head movements in turns, don't mix VMC/IMC flying, use the autopilot, and hand over control.","Prevention: instrument training and trust the instruments, avoid head movements in turns, don't mix VMC/IMC flying, use the autopilot, and hand over control."],["040.02.02.06:05","Illusions & spatial disorientation – Upsloping / narrow / long runway: what are the key facts?","Perception: too high · Result: flies too low, flares late"],["040.02.02.06:05","Illusions & spatial disorientation – Downsloping / wide runway: what are the key facts?","Perception: too low · Result: flies too high, flares early"],["040.02.02.06:05","Illusions & spatial disorientation – Black hole (dark terrain): what are the key facts?","Perception: too high · Result: flies too low, CFIT risk"],["040.02.02.06:05","Illusions & spatial disorientation – Rain on windscreen / haze: what are the key facts?","Perception: further/higher · Result: low approach"],["040.02.02.03:13","Application — KSA, TEM, UPRT: fill in the gaps → ____: weather, NOTAMs, performance, fuel, mass & balance, alternates. Apply ____ (brief threats and countermeasures).","Pre-flight planning: weather, NOTAMs, performance, fuel, mass & balance, alternates. Apply TEM phase by phase (brief threats and countermeasures)."],["040.01.03.01:03","Application — KSA, TEM, UPRT: what do you remember about this? → Environmental: turbulence, wake, icing, wind shear, …","Environmental: turbulence, wake, icing, wind shear, Cb."],["040.01.03.01:08","Application — KSA, TEM, UPRT: what do you remember about this? → Aircraft/system: unreliable speed, …","Aircraft/system: unreliable speed, automation."],["100.03.02.00:05","Application — KSA, TEM, UPRT: what do you remember about this? → Human: distraction, fatigue, low energy …","Human: distraction, fatigue, low energy awareness."],["100.03.02.00:02","Application — KSA, TEM, UPRT: what do you remember about this? → Upset errors: wrong mode, speed decay, poor monitoring. Managed with …","Upset errors: wrong mode, speed decay, poor monitoring. Managed with energy awareness, callouts and early correction."],["100.03.01.00:02","Application — KSA, TEM, UPRT: fill in the gaps → ____: environment, system failures, pilot-induced (inappropriate control, spatial disorientation, startle).","Causes of upsets: environment, system failures, pilot-induced (inappropriate control, spatial disorientation, startle)."],["100.03.02.00:05","Application — KSA, TEM, UPRT: fill in the gaps → ____: manage startle and surprise (\"push – roll – power – stabilise\"), keep situation awareness.","Resilience: manage startle and surprise (\"push – roll – power – stabilise\"), keep situation awareness."],["100.03.02.00:05","Application — KSA, TEM, UPRT: fill in the gaps → ____ narrow attention → recognise the signs and use CRM, breaks and rest.","Stress, fatigue and lifestyle narrow attention → recognise the signs and use CRM, breaks and rest."],["100.03.01.00:01","Exam trap (Application — KSA, TEM, UPRT): complete the fact → rods = periphery/night; cones = fovea/colour; dark adaptation ____.","rods = periphery/night; cones = fovea/colour; dark adaptation 30 min."],["100.03.01.00:01","Exam trap (Application — KSA, TEM, UPRT): complete the fact → NIHL from ~____dB.","NIHL from ~85–90 dB."],["040.01.03.01:01","EASA LO 040.01.03.01.01 → Explain the three components of the TEM model.","TEM model (ICAO Doc 9683) has 3 components: threats, errors and undesired aircraft states (UAS)."],["040.01.03.01:02","EASA LO 040.01.03.01.02 → Explain and give examples of latent threats. X X X X X X X 1 Exam BK Remarks","Latent threats are hidden in the system: equipment design, regulatory gaps, organisational culture, poor procedures."],["040.01.03.01:05","EASA LO 040.01.03.01.05 → Explain and give a definition of ‘error’ according to the TEM model of ICAO Doc 9683 (Part II, Chapter 2).","TEM model (ICAO Doc 9683) has 3 components: threats, errors and undesired aircraft states (UAS)."],["040.01.03.01:09","EASA LO 040.01.03.01.09 → State the components of the SHELL model.","TEM model (ICAO Doc 9683) has 3 components: threats, errors and undesired aircraft states (UAS). / SHELL model: Software (procedures, checklists), Hardware (aircraft, controls), Environment, Liveware (the central human), Liveware (other people)."],["040.02.02.01:01","EASA LO 040.02.02.01.01 → List the different senses.","Senses: vision, hearing, equilibrium (vestibular), touch/pressure, proprioception (kinaesthetic), smell, taste, temperature, pain."],["040.02.02.03:02","EASA LO 040.02.02.03.02 → State the basic functions of the parts of the eye.","Sunglasses and eye protection:"],["040.02.02.03:04","EASA LO 040.02.02.03.04 → Distinguish between the functions of the rod and cone cells.","NIHL: damage to the cochlear hair cells from noise."],["040.02.02.03:05","EASA LO 040.02.02.03.05 → Describe the distribution of rod and cone cells in the retina and explain their relevance to vision.","Sunglasses protect the retina from blue light and UV."],["040.02.02.03:07","EASA LO 040.02.02.03.07 → List the factors that may degrade visual acuity and the importance of ‘lookout’.","Visual acuity is highest in the fovea (central ~1–2°); the visual field is ~180°+ horizontally. Peripheral vision detects motion."],["040.02.02.03:12","EASA LO 040.02.02.03.12 → Distinguish between monocular and binocular vision.","Binocular vision gives depth perception (stereopsis) only up to ~6 m; beyond that, monocular cues are used:"],["040.02.02.03:17","EASA LO 040.02.02.03.17 → List the causes of and the precautions that may be taken to reduce the probability of vision loss due to: — presbyopia; — cataract; — glaucoma.","Hypoxia, smoking (CO) and altitude > 5 000 ft measurably reduce night vision."],["040.02.02.04:06","EASA LO 040.02.02.04.06 → Identify the potential occupational risks that may cause hearing loss.","Presbycusis: age-related loss of high frequencies."],["040.02.02.04:07","EASA LO 040.02.02.04.07 → List the main sources of hearing loss in the flying environment.","Aircraft handling: manual flying or automation errors. / Presbycusis: age-related loss of high frequencies."],["040.02.02.04:08","EASA LO 040.02.02.04.08 → List the precautions that may be taken to reduce the probability of onset of hearing loss.","Hypoxia, smoking (CO) and altitude > 5 000 ft measurably reduce night vision. / Presbycusis: age-related loss of high frequencies."],["040.02.02.05:01","EASA LO 040.02.02.05.01 → List the main elements of the vestibular apparatus.","Air sickness: a mismatch between visual and vestibular inputs."],["040.02.02.05:02","EASA LO 040.02.02.05.02 → State the functions of the vestibular apparatus on the ground and in flight.","Air sickness: a mismatch between visual and vestibular inputs. / Outer ear (pinna, canal) → middle ear (eardrum, ossicles: hammer/anvil/stirrup, Eustachian tube for pressure equalisation) → inner ear (cochlea = hearing; vestibular apparatus = balance)."],["040.02.02.06:07","EASA LO 040.02.02.06.07 → Describe vestibular illusions caused by the angular accelerations (the Leans, Coriolis) and linear accelerations (somatogravic, G-effect).","G-effect / inversion illusion."],["100.03.02.00:03","EASA LO 100.03.02.00.03 → Explain the causes of and contributing factors to upsets.","Causes of upsets: environment, system failures, pilot-induced (inappropriate control, spatial disorientation, startle)."]],"30":[["021.01.01.01:01","Design concepts & certification: fill in the gaps → ____ design: the part is replaced after a fixed life (cycles/hours) before a fatigue failure is expected. Example: landing gear.","Safe-life design: the part is replaced after a fixed life (cycles/hours) before a fatigue failure is expected. Example: landing gear."],["021.01.01.01:01","Design concepts & certification: fill in the gaps → ____ design: ____. If one member fails, the others carry the load until the failure is detected.","Fail-safe design: multiple load paths. If one member fails, the others carry the load until the failure is detected."],["021.01.01.01:01","Design concepts & certification: fill in the gaps → ____ design: the structure tolerates cracks for a known period. Crack growth is predictable and found by scheduled inspections, which saves weight.","Damage-tolerant design: the structure tolerates cracks for a known period. Crack growth is predictable and found by scheduled inspections, which saves weight."],["021.01.01.02:01","Design concepts & certification: fill in the gaps → ____ means duplicated or triplicated systems, so that a single failure doesn't cause loss of function. Critical systems are often triplicated.","Redundancy means duplicated or triplicated systems, so that a single failure doesn't cause loss of function. Critical systems are often triplicated."],["021.01.01.02:02","Design concepts & certification: fill in the gaps → Aircraft are certified to ____ set by the competent authority (design, materials, build quality).","Aircraft are certified to certification specifications set by the competent authority (design, materials, build quality)."],["021.01.01.02:03","Design concepts & certification: fill in the gaps → Aeroplanes: ____ (normal, utility, aerobatic, commuter) and ____ ____ (large aeroplanes).","Aeroplanes: CS-23 (normal, utility, aerobatic, commuter) and CS- 25 (large aeroplanes)."],["021.01.01.02:04","Design concepts & certification: fill in the gaps → Rotorcraft: ____ (small) and ____ (large).","Rotorcraft: CS-27 (small) and CS-29 (large)."],["021.01.02.01:01","Stress, strain & loads: fill in the gaps → ____ = internal force per unit area caused by external loads. ____ = the deformation it causes. Both are ____ (parked: weight and gear loads; in flight: lift, manoeuvre loads).","Stress = internal force per unit area caused by external loads. Strain = the deformation it causes. Both are always present (parked: weight and gear loads; in flight: lift, manoeuvre loads)."],["021.01.02.01:02","Stress, strain & loads: fill in the gaps → ____: constant, e.g. the weight on the ground.","Static: constant, e.g. the weight on the ground."],["021.01.02.01:01","Stress, strain & loads: fill in the gaps → ____: suddenly applied, e.g. gusts, hard landing, turbulence, abrupt manoeuvres.","Dynamic: suddenly applied, e.g. gusts, hard landing, turbulence, abrupt manoeuvres."],["021.02.04.01:03","Stress, strain & loads: fill in the gaps → ____: repeated, e.g. pressurisation cycles, gear cycles, wing bending.","Cyclic: repeated, e.g. pressurisation cycles, gear cycles, wing bending."],["021.01.02.01:01","Stress, strain & loads: what do you remember about this? → The pilot can limit them through smooth control inputs, respecting …","The pilot can limit them through smooth control inputs, respecting VA/VRA and n limits, gentle landings and correct loading."],["021.01.02.01:03","Stress, strain & loads: fill in the gaps → ____: check stress-prone areas — wing roots, gear attachments, engine mounts, door frames, control surface hinges, skin around rivets.","Pre-flight: check stress-prone areas — wing roots, gear attachments, engine mounts, door frames, control surface hinges, skin around rivets."],["021.01.02.01:03","Stress, strain & loads: fill in the gaps → Look for ____, popped/smoking rivets (dark streaks), cracks, and dents. Visual cues are ____.","Look for wrinkled skin, popped/smoking rivets (dark streaks), cracks, and dents. Visual cues are limited."],["021.01.03.01:01","Corrosion & fatigue: fill in the gaps → ____ is a chemical/electrochemical attack on metal that reduces strength. It shows as ____ on aluminium, ____ on steel, blistered paint, or pitting.","Corrosion is a chemical/electrochemical attack on metal that reduces strength. It shows as white/grey powder on aluminium, red rust on steel, blistered paint, or pitting."],["021.01.03.01:01","Corrosion & fatigue: fill in the gaps → Risk is higher in ____ air, with industrial pollution, standing water, galvanic contact between different metals, and ____.","Risk is higher in salty/humid/coastal air, with industrial pollution, standing water, galvanic contact between different metals, and toilet/galley areas."],["021.01.03.01:02","Corrosion & fatigue: what do you remember about this? → Minimise it by washing, drainage, protective coatings, and hangar …","Minimise it by washing, drainage, protective coatings, and hangar storage."],["021.01.03.01:03","Corrosion & fatigue: fill in the gaps → Aircraft carry ____ on board: battery electrolyte, hydraulic fluid (phosphate ester), toilet fluid, de-icing fluid.","Aircraft carry corrosive fluids on board: battery electrolyte, hydraulic fluid (phosphate ester), toilet fluid, de-icing fluid."],["021.02.06.01:02","Corrosion & fatigue: fill in the gaps → ____: repeated (cyclic) stress below the ultimate strength causes crack initiation and growth → failure. This ____ ____ of the airframe.","Fatigue: repeated (cyclic) stress below the ultimate strength causes crack initiation and growth → failure. This limits the useful life of the airframe."],["021.01.03.01:04","Corrosion & fatigue: fill in the gaps → Fatigue is accelerated by corrosion, ____, ____, ____ and poor maintenance.","Fatigue is accelerated by corrosion, number of cycles, manoeuvre severity, high stress levels and poor maintenance."],["021.01.05.01:01","Maintenance: fill in the gaps → ____: overhaul or replace at fixed intervals (hours, cycles, calendar).","Hard-time (fixed-time): overhaul or replace at fixed intervals (hours, cycles, calendar)."],["021.01.05.01:01","Maintenance: fill in the gaps → ____: periodic inspection or test against a standard; the part stays in service while it is serviceable.","On-condition: periodic inspection or test against a standard; the part stays in service while it is serviceable."],["021.01.05.01:01","Maintenance: fill in the gaps → ____: no fixed maintenance; data (trend monitoring, failure rates) reveal deterioration.","Condition monitoring: no fixed maintenance; data (trend monitoring, failure rates) reveal deterioration."],["021.02.01.01:01","Attachments & materials: fill in the gaps → ____: rivets, bolts, pins, ____, ____ bonding.","Attachment methods: rivets, bolts, pins, welding, adhesive bonding."],["021.02.01.01:01","Attachments & materials: fill in the gaps → ____ show as loose or smoking rivets (black streaks), cracks around fasteners, gaps, fretting marks, and paint cracks.","Faulty attachments show as loose or smoking rivets (black streaks), cracks around fasteners, gaps, fretting marks, and paint cracks."],["021.02.02.01:01","Attachments & materials: fill in the gaps → ____ are fibres (____) in a ____, plus filler/core (honeycomb).","Composites are fibres (carbon, glass, Kevlar aramid) in a resin matrix, plus filler/core (honeycomb)."],["021.02.02.01:02","Attachments & materials: fill in the gaps → ____: high ____ ratio, strength can be ____ to the load direction (fibre orientation), high stiffness, no corrosion, good fatigue resistance.","Advantages: high strength-to-weight ratio, strength can be tailored to the load direction (fibre orientation), high stiffness, no corrosion, good fatigue resistance."],["021.02.03.01:01","Attachments & materials: fill in the gaps → ____: damage is hard to detect (delamination under an intact surface), low impact resistance, lightning protection needed, costly repair, UV/moisture sensitivity.","Disadvantages: damage is hard to detect (delamination under an intact surface), low impact resistance, lightning protection needed, costly repair, UV/moisture sensitivity."],["021.02.02.01:03","Attachments & materials: what do you remember about this? → Several materials are used (Al alloys, titanium, steel, composites), …","Several materials are used (Al alloys, titanium, steel, composites), chosen per component by the properties needed."],["021.02.03.01:01","Wing & tail design: what do you remember about this? → Pros: good ground clearance for engines/props, lateral stability …","Pros: good ground clearance for engines/props, lateral stability (pendulum effect), easy loading, view down."],["021.02.03.01:01","Wing & tail design: what do you remember about this? → Cons: heavy carry-through box over the cabin, narrow gear track, …","Cons: heavy carry-through box over the cabin, narrow gear track, poorer upward view in turns, ditching (fuselage sinks)."],["021.02.03.01:01","Wing & tail design: what do you remember about this? → Pros: wide gear track, good ditching (wing floats), short gear stored …","Pros: wide gear track, good ditching (wing floats), short gear stored in the wing, carry-through under the floor."],["021.02.03.01:01","Wing & tail design: what do you remember about this? → Cons: needs dihedral, poor ground clearance, poor downward …","Cons: needs dihedral, poor ground clearance, poor downward view."],["021.02.03.01:01","Wing & tail design: what do you remember about this? → Pros: simple, light, effective, good stall …","Pros: simple, light, effective, good stall behaviour."],["021.02.03.01:01","Wing & tail design: what do you remember about this? → Cons: in the downwash and …","Cons: in the downwash and propwash."],["021.02.03.01:01","Wing & tail design: what do you remember about this? → Pros: out of the downwash/propwash (effective, suits rear engines), …","Pros: out of the downwash/propwash (effective, suits rear engines), end-plate effect on the fin."],["021.02.03.01:01","Wing & tail design: fill in the gaps → Cons: heavier fin structure, flutter risk, ____ risk (blanketed by the wing wake at high α).","Cons: heavier fin structure, flutter risk, deep stall risk (blanketed by the wing wake at high α)."],["021.02.03.02:01","Wing & tail design: fill in the gaps → ____ (web + caps/girders) carries bending and shear.","Spar (web + caps/girders) carries bending and shear."],["021.02.03.01:01","Wing & tail design: fill in the gaps → ____ hold the aerofoil shape and transfer loads to the spar.","Ribs hold the aerofoil shape and transfer loads to the spar."],["021.02.03.01:01","Wing & tail design: fill in the gaps → ____ stiffen the skin against buckling.","Stringers stiffen the skin against buckling."],["021.02.03.01:01","Wing & tail design: fill in the gaps → ____ carries torsion/shear and provides the aerodynamic surface (____).","Skin carries torsion/shear and provides the aerodynamic surface (stressed skin)."],["021.02.03.03:01","Loads & flutter: fill in the gaps → ____ the wing hangs down (weight of wing, fuel, engines) → the upper skin is in tension and the lower skin in compression.","On the ground the wing hangs down (weight of wing, fuel, engines) → the upper skin is in tension and the lower skin in compression."],["021.02.03.03:01","Loads & flutter: fill in the gaps → ____ lift bends the wing ____ → the upper skin is in ____ and the lower skin in tension. Drag and thrust give horizontal loads.","In flight lift bends the wing upward → the upper skin is in compression and the lower skin in tension. Drag and thrust give horizontal loads."],["021.02.03.03:02","Loads & flutter: fill in the gaps → ____ causes asymmetric thrust and yaw → high fin/rudder loads and wing torsion.","Engine failure on a multi-engine aircraft causes asymmetric thrust and yaw → high fin/rudder loads and wing torsion."],["021.02.03.03:01","Loads & flutter: what do you remember about this? → Over-controlling (full rudder reversals) can overstress the …","Over-controlling (full rudder reversals) can overstress the fin."],["021.02.03.03:03","Loads & flutter: fill in the gaps → ____: an aeroelastic oscillation — aerodynamic, elastic and inertial forces couple at the structure's natural frequency (____). It grows with speed → destruction.","Flutter: an aeroelastic oscillation — aerodynamic, elastic and inertial forces couple at the structure's natural frequency (resonance). It grows with speed → destruction."],["021.02.03.03:03","Loads & flutter: fill in the gaps → ____ of control surfaces (mass ahead of the hinge line).","Mass balancing of control surfaces (mass ahead of the hinge line)."],["021.02.03.03:04","Loads & flutter: what do you remember about this? → Chord-and spanwise positioning of masses (engines ahead of the wing, …","Chord-and spanwise positioning of masses (engines ahead of the wing, fuel distribution)."],["021.02.03.03:04","Loads & flutter: fill in the gaps → ____ and bending flexibility.","Torsional stiffness and bending flexibility."],["021.02.03.03:04","Loads & flutter: fill in the gaps → ____ (keep wing-tip fuel for bending relief).","Fuel balancing/sequencing (keep wing-tip fuel for bending relief)."],["021.02.04.01:01","Fuselage: fill in the gaps → ____: the skin carries all loads; no internal stiffeners.","Monocoque: the skin carries all loads; no internal stiffeners."],["021.02.04.01:01","Fuselage: fill in the gaps → ____: skin plus frames, stringers and longerons share the loads → standard; damage-tolerant.","Semi-monocoque: skin plus frames, stringers and longerons share the loads → standard; damage-tolerant."],["021.02.04.01:07","Fuselage: fill in the gaps → ____: pressure bulkheads (front/aft) close the pressurised hull.","Bulkheads: pressure bulkheads (front/aft) close the pressurised hull."],["021.02.04.01:01","Fuselage: fill in the gaps → ____: longitudinal stiffeners.","Stringers/longerons: longitudinal stiffeners."],["021.02.04.01:01","Fuselage: fill in the gaps → ____ (reinforcement around cut-outs).","Skin + doublers (reinforcement around cut-outs)."],["021.02.04.01:01","Fuselage: fill in the gaps → ____ (crossbeams) and ____.","Floor beams (crossbeams) and floor panels."],["021.02.03.03:02","Fuselage: fill in the gaps → ____: fireproof, between engine and structure.","Firewall: fireproof, between engine and structure."],["021.02.04.01:01","Fuselage: fill in the gaps → ____ puts the skin in ____ (hoop stress is ~2× longitudinal), cycle by cycle (fatigue).","Pressurisation puts the skin in hoop and longitudinal tension (hoop stress is ~2× longitudinal), cycle by cycle (fatigue)."],["021.02.04.01:04","Fuselage: fill in the gaps → ____ at touchdown are vertical plus horizontal (spin-up, drag). Bogies ____ in tight turns (torsion).","Main gear loads at touchdown are vertical plus horizontal (spin-up, drag). Bogies scrub in tight turns (torsion)."],["021.02.04.01:05","Fuselage: fill in the gaps → ____: overload of the nose strut and the forward fuselage, bouncing/wheelbarrowing.","Nose-wheel-first landing: overload of the nose strut and the forward fuselage, bouncing/wheelbarrowing."],["021.02.04.01:06","Fuselage: fill in the gaps → ____ damages the aft fuselage and the ____ ____ (pressurisation failure later, e.g. JAL 123).","Tail strike damages the aft fuselage and the aft pressure bulkhead (pressurisation failure later, e.g. JAL 123)."],["021.02.04.01:01","Fuselage: fill in the gaps → ____ doors open inward/outward and are sealed by cabin pressure (cannot open under pressure).","Plug-type doors open inward/outward and are sealed by cabin pressure (cannot open under pressure)."],["021.02.04.01:14","Fuselage: what do you remember about this? → Doors have locks and …","Doors have locks and indications."],["021.02.04.01:14","Fuselage: fill in the gaps → ____ run on rails (unpressurised, cargo).","Sliding doors run on rails (unpressurised, cargo)."],["021.02.04.01:01","Fuselage: fill in the gaps → ____: best for pressure (hoop stress even), lightest.","Circular: best for pressure (hoop stress even), lightest."],["021.02.04.01:08","Fuselage: fill in the gaps → ____: more cargo/cabin space.","Double bubble: more cargo/cabin space."],["021.02.03.03:01","Fuselage: fill in the gaps → ____: space, but bending stresses.","Oval: space, but bending stresses."],["021.02.04.01:07","Fuselage: fill in the gaps → ____: best volume, unpressurised only.","Rectangular: best volume, unpressurised only."],["021.02.04.01:09","Fuselage: fill in the gaps → Flight-deck windows have ____ for redundancy and bird strike.","Flight-deck windows have multiple layers for redundancy and bird strike."],["021.02.04.01:01","Fuselage: fill in the gaps → ____ keeps the interlayer ____ (impact resistance) and prevents fogging/icing.","Window heat keeps the interlayer flexible (impact resistance) and prevents fogging/icing."],["021.02.04.01:11","Fuselage: fill in the gaps → ____: an openable side window, so the pilot can see if the windscreen is obscured (CS 25.773).","Direct-vision window: an openable side window, so the pilot can see if the windscreen is obscured (CS 25.773)."],["021.02.04.01:12","Fuselage: fill in the gaps → ____: seat height set so the view outside and of the instruments is correct (approach visual segment).","Eye-reference position: seat height set so the view outside and of the instruments is correct (approach visual segment)."],["021.02.04.01:13","Fuselage: fill in the gaps → ____ equalise pressure between the cabin and the cargo hold after a sudden decompression → prevent floor collapse.","Floor venting / blow-out panels equalise pressure between the cabin and the cargo hold after a sudden decompression → prevent floor collapse."],["021.02.06.01:03","Maximum structural masses: fill in the gaps → ____: maximum mass for ground manoeuvring (MTOM + taxi fuel).","MRM (Maximum Ramp Mass): maximum mass for ground manoeuvring (MTOM + taxi fuel)."],["021.02.06.01:03","Maximum structural masses: fill in the gaps → ____: maximum mass at the start of the take-off run.","MTOM (Maximum Take-Off Mass): maximum mass at the start of the take-off run."],["021.02.06.01:03","Maximum structural masses: fill in the gaps → ____: maximum mass without usable fuel. It limits ____, since fuel in the wing relieves the bending moment.","MZFM (Maximum Zero Fuel Mass): maximum mass without usable fuel. It limits wing root bending, since fuel in the wing relieves the bending moment."],["021.02.06.01:03","Maximum structural masses: fill in the gaps → ____: limited by landing loads on the gear/structure (touchdown sink rate).","MLM (Maximum Landing Mass): limited by landing loads on the gear/structure (touchdown sink rate)."],["021.02.06.01:02","Maximum structural masses: fill in the gaps → ____ is limited by ____: alternating stress × number of ____.","Airframe life is limited by fatigue: alternating stress × number of load cycles."],["021.01.01.01:02","EASA LO 021.01.01.01.02 → Explain the purpose of redundancy in aircraft design.","Aircraft are certified to certification specifications set by the competent authority (design, materials, build quality). / Flight-deck windows have multiple layers for redundancy and bird strike."],["021.01.03.01:01","EASA LO 021.01.03.01.01 → Describe the effects of corrosion and how it can be visually identified by a pilot during the pre-flight inspection.","Fatigue is accelerated by corrosion, number of cycles, manoeuvre severity, high stress levels and poor maintenance. / Advantages: high strength-to-weight ratio, strength can be tailored to the load direction (fibre orientation), high stiffness, no corrosion, good fatigue resistance."],["021.02.01.01:02","EASA LO 021.02.01.01.02 → Explain how the development of a faulty attachment between aircraft parts or components can be detected by a pilot during the pre-flight inspection.","Attachment methods: rivets, bolts, pins, welding, adhesive bonding."],["021.02.06.01:01","EASA LO 021.02.06.01.01 → Define and explain the following maximum structural masses: — maximum ramp mass; — maximum take-off mass; — maximum zero fuel mass; — maximum landing mass. Remark: These limitations may also be found in the relevant part of Subjects 031 ‘Mass and balance’, 032 ‘Performance (aeroplane)’ and 034 ‘Performance (helicopter)’.","MTOM (Maximum Take-Off Mass): maximum mass at the start of the take-off run. / MRM (Maximum Ramp Mass): maximum mass for ground manoeuvring (MTOM + taxi fuel)."],["021.02.06.01:04","EASA LO 021.02.06.01.04 → Explain that airframe life is limited by fatigue, created by load cycles.","Airframe life is limited by fatigue: alternating stress × number of load cycles. / MLM (Maximum Landing Mass): limited by landing loads on the gear/structure (touchdown sink rate)."]],"31":[["021.03.01.01:01","Hydraulic principles & fluids: fill in the gaps → ____: pressure in a static fluid acts ____ ____. P = F/A → force multiplication (small piston → large piston), but with a proportional decrease in travel (work is conserved).","Pascal's law: pressure in a static fluid acts equally in all directions. P = F/A → force multiplication (small piston → large piston), but with a proportional decrease in travel (work is conserved)."],["021.03.02.01:01","Hydraulic principles & fluids: fill in the gaps → ____: ____, ____ corrosiveness, high flashpoint/low flammability, low volatility, suitable viscosity (stable with temperature), good lubrication, and incompressibility (at the pressures used).","Desirable fluid properties: thermal stability, low corrosiveness, high flashpoint/low flammability, low volatility, suitable viscosity (stable with temperature), good lubrication, and incompressibility (at the pressures used)."],["021.03.02.01:03","Hydraulic principles & fluids: fill in the gaps → ____ (red): lighter aircraft, flammable.","Mineral (red): lighter aircraft, flammable."],["021.03.02.01:02","Hydraulic principles & fluids: fill in the gaps → ____ phosphate-ester (Skydrol, purple/blue): transport aircraft, fire-resistant, ____, attacks paint.","Synthetic phosphate-ester (Skydrol, purple/blue): transport aircraft, fire-resistant, irritates skin and eyes, attacks paint."],["021.03.02.01:04","Hydraulic principles & fluids: fill in the gaps → ____ — this damages the seals.","Never mix the types — this damages the seals."],["021.03.02.02:01","System principles: what do you remember about this? → A pump draws fluid from the reservoir and delivers it under pressure …","A pump draws fluid from the reservoir and delivers it under pressure through the selector valve to the actuator; the fluid returns to the reservoir."],["021.05.04.01:03","System principles: fill in the gaps → ____ (active) system: pressure is always available.","Constant-pressure (active) system: pressure is always available."],["021.03.02.02:02","System principles: fill in the gaps → ____ system: pressure only when a service is selected (open-centre / power pack).","Demand-pressurised system: pressure only when a service is selected (open-centre / power pack)."],["021.03.02.02:03","System principles: fill in the gaps → ____: no pump — manual/master-cylinder, e.g. light-aircraft brakes. ____: pump-driven.","Passive system: no pump — manual/master-cylinder, e.g. light-aircraft brakes. Active system: pump-driven."],["021.03.02.02:04","System principles: fill in the gaps → ____:","Hydraulic vs. mechanical actuation:"],["021.03.02.02:01","System principles: fill in the gaps → Hydraulic gives ____ from small components, with a better ____ ratio for big loads and flexible routing.","Hydraulic gives large forces from small components, with a better power-to-weight ratio for big loads and flexible routing."],["021.05.02.01:09","System principles: what do you remember about this? → Mechanical is simpler and lighter for small …","Mechanical is simpler and lighter for small forces."],["021.05.02.01:02","System principles: fill in the gaps → ____: flight controls, gear retraction/extension, brakes, nose-wheel steering, flaps/slats, spoilers, thrust reversers, cargo doors.","Uses: flight controls, gear retraction/extension, brakes, nose-wheel steering, flaps/slats, spoilers, thrust reversers, cargo doors."],["021.03.02.02:06","System principles: fill in the gaps → ____ is up to ~____.","Low pressure is up to ~2 000 psi."],["021.03.02.02:07","System principles: fill in the gaps → High pressure is typically ____, and some aircraft use ____ ____ (A380, B787).","High pressure is typically 3 000 psi, and some aircraft use 4 000–5 000 psi (A380, B787)."],["021.03.02.02:07","System principles: what do you remember about this? → A high-pressure system has smaller, lighter components and lines, but …","A high-pressure system has smaller, lighter components and lines, but more leaks, stronger seals, and more heat."],["021.03.02.02:10","System principles: fill in the gaps → ____ pump: axial pistons with a ____ that changes its angle to keep the pressure constant.","Constant-pressure (variable-displacement) pump: axial pistons with a swash plate / cam plate that changes its angle to keep the pressure constant."],["021.03.02.02:10","System principles: fill in the gaps → ____ pump (____): output depends on ____ → needs a relief valve or unloading.","Constant-displacement pump (gear type): output depends on rpm → needs a relief valve or unloading."],["021.05.01.05:04","System principles: fill in the gaps → ____ hand pump — emergency, ground.","Manual hand pump — emergency, ground."],["021.03.02.02:12","System principles: fill in the gaps → ____-driven pump (EDP) via the gearbox — main source.","Engine-driven pump (EDP) via the gearbox — main source."],["021.03.02.02:14","System principles: fill in the gaps → ____ motor pump (ACMP) — backup or demand pump.","Electric motor pump (ACMP) — backup or demand pump."],["021.03.02.02:11","System principles: fill in the gaps → ____: air-driven pump (bleed air) or ____ (ram-air turbine, emergency, speed-dependent).","Air: air-driven pump (bleed air) or RAT (ram-air turbine, emergency, speed-dependent)."],["021.03.02.02:11","System principles: fill in the gaps → ____: transfers power between systems ____.","PTU (power transfer unit): transfers power between systems without fluid transfer."],["021.03.02.02:01","System principles: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (bleed air or bootstrap) to prevent pump ____ at altitude; unpressurised on small aircraft.","Reservoir: pressurised (bleed air or bootstrap) to prevent pump cavitation at altitude; unpressurised on small aircraft."],["021.03.02.02:01","System principles: fill in the gaps → ____: stores pressure (gas-precharged, N₂). It damps surges, supplies peak demand, and provides emergency pressure (e.g. parking brake).","Accumulator: stores pressure (gas-precharged, N₂). It damps surges, supplies peak demand, and provides emergency pressure (e.g. parking brake)."],["021.03.02.02:13","System principles: fill in the gaps → ____ lines: cool and lubricate the pump; the flow is monitored (pump wear). Fluid ____ in the return line.","Case drain lines: cool and lubricate the pump; the flow is monitored (pump wear). Fluid cooler in the return line."],["021.03.02.02:13","System principles: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (return by spring/gravity) and ____ acting (pressure both ways).","Actuators: single-acting (return by spring/gravity) and double- acting (pressure both ways)."],["021.05.02.01:04","System principles: fill in the gaps → ____: continuous rotation (flap drives, screw jacks).","Hydraulic motors: continuous rotation (flap drives, screw jacks)."],["021.03.02.02:13","System principles: fill in the gaps → ____: pressure and return lines, with bypass and ΔP indicator.","Filters: pressure and return lines, with bypass and ΔP indicator."],["021.03.02.02:13","System principles: fill in the gaps → ____: one-way flow.","Check (non-return) valve: one-way flow."],["021.03.02.02:01","System principles: fill in the gaps → ____: limits the maximum pressure.","Relief valve: limits the maximum pressure."],["021.05.02.01:07","System principles: fill in the gaps → ____: slows the flow (e.g. gear retraction or flap speed).","Restrictor: slows the flow (e.g. gear retraction or flap speed)."],["021.03.02.02:13","System principles: fill in the gaps → ____: 2/4-port, linear or rotary.","Selector valves: 2/4-port, linear or rotary."],["021.03.02.02:01","System principles: fill in the gaps → ____: switches automatically to the alternate/emergency supply.","Shuttle valve: switches automatically to the alternate/emergency supply."],["021.03.02.02:01","System principles: fill in the gaps → ____: isolates supply to the engine pump.","Fire shut-off valve: isolates supply to the engine pump."],["021.03.02.02:17","System principles: fill in the gaps → ____ (usually electric) supplements the EDP during high demand (take-off/landing, gear/flap operation).","Demand pump (usually electric) supplements the EDP during high demand (take-off/landing, gear/flap operation)."],["021.03.02.02:01","System principles: fill in the gaps → ____: 2–4 independent systems (e.g. A320 Green/Blue/Yellow; B737 A/B/standby). Multiple pumps per system, PTU, RAT, accumulators, and priority valves.","Redundancy: 2–4 independent systems (e.g. A320 Green/Blue/Yellow; B737 A/B/standby). Multiple pumps per system, PTU, RAT, accumulators, and priority valves."],["021.03.02.02:14","System principles: fill in the gaps → ____ (many services at once) causes pressure to drop and services to slow → priority valves protect the flight controls.","High system demand (many services at once) causes pressure to drop and services to slow → priority valves protect the flight controls."],["021.03.02.02:19","System principles: fill in the gaps → ____: quantity, pressure, temperature, pump low-pressure lights, filter ΔP, case-drain temperature.","Monitoring: quantity, pressure, temperature, pump low-pressure lights, filter ΔP, case-drain temperature."],["021.03.02.02:19","System principles: fill in the gaps → ____ → quantity decreasing, then pressure loss → isolate (fire shut-off valve) and plan for alternate gear/brakes.","Leak / low level → quantity decreasing, then pressure loss → isolate (fire shut-off valve) and plan for alternate gear/brakes."],["021.03.02.02:19","System principles: fill in the gaps → ____ → pump failure or leak → use the backup pump/PTU.","Low pressure → pump failure or leak → use the backup pump/PTU."],["021.03.02.02:01","System principles: fill in the gaps → ____ → pump failing (overheat, internal wear) → switch the pump off.","High temperature → pump failing (overheat, internal wear) → switch the pump off."],["021.05.01.01:02","Primary flight controls: fill in the gaps → ____ act about the main axes: ____ (pitch), ____ (roll), rudder (yaw).","Primary controls act about the main axes: elevator (pitch), ailerons / roll spoilers / flaperons (roll), rudder (yaw)."],["021.05.01.01:01","Primary flight controls: fill in the gaps → ____ controls: cables/rods move the surfaces directly. The pilot feels the aerodynamic loads; aerodynamic balancing and tabs are used.","Manual (reversible) controls: cables/rods move the surfaces directly. The pilot feels the aerodynamic loads; aerodynamic balancing and tabs are used."],["021.05.01.03:01","Primary flight controls: fill in the gaps → ____ controls: hydraulic actuators move the surfaces; ____ to the pilot.","Fully powered (irreversible) controls: hydraulic actuators move the surfaces; air loads are not fed back to the pilot."],["021.03.02.02:09","Primary flight controls: fill in the gaps → An ____ system (springs, q-feel) is needed to avoid over-controlling.","An artificial feel system (springs, q-feel) is needed to avoid over-controlling."],["021.05.01.03:05","Primary flight controls: fill in the gaps → ____: pitch via a ____. Rudder/aileron trim moves the ____ (not tabs).","Trim: pitch via a trimmable horizontal stabiliser (THS). Rudder/aileron trim moves the neutral position of the feel unit (not tabs)."],["021.05.01.04:02","Primary flight controls: fill in the gaps → ____ controls: a hydraulic booster assists, but part of the air load is fed back → ____ ____.","Partially powered (reversible) controls: a hydraulic booster assists, but part of the air load is fed back → no artificial feel system is needed."],["021.05.01.05:01","Primary flight controls: fill in the gaps → ____: actuators (servo), control valves, cables/rods, electrical wiring, surface ____ (indication, FBW feedback).","Components: actuators (servo), control valves, cables/rods, electrical wiring, surface position sensors (indication, FBW feedback)."],["021.05.04.01:10","Primary flight controls: fill in the gaps → ____ on large aircraft: several hydraulic systems per surface, split surfaces (several actuators), several surfaces per axis, mechanical backup.","Redundancy on large aircraft: several hydraulic systems per surface, split surfaces (several actuators), several surfaces per axis, mechanical backup."],["021.05.01.01:02","Primary flight controls: fill in the gaps → ____: jammed cables or a jammed surface → ____ (e.g. elevator/aileron disconnect, override with breakout force, spoilers for roll).","Control jamming: jammed cables or a jammed surface → disconnect/override mechanisms (e.g. elevator/aileron disconnect, override with breakout force, spoilers for roll)."],["021.05.01.05:04","Primary flight controls: fill in the gaps → ____: mechanical on light aircraft; on powered aircraft the hydraulic damping holds the surfaces. A ____ ____ triggers if the lock is engaged.","Gust/control locks: mechanical on light aircraft; on powered aircraft the hydraulic damping holds the surfaces. A take-off config warning triggers if the lock is engaged."],["021.05.01.05:05","Primary flight controls: fill in the gaps → ____: rudder travel is reduced as ____ to avoid overstressing the fin. Methods:","Rudder limiter: rudder travel is reduced as speed increases to avoid overstressing the fin. Methods:"],["021.05.01.05:05","Primary flight controls: fill in the gaps → ____: the pedal-to-rudder ratio falls with speed.","Rudder ratio changer: the pedal-to-rudder ratio falls with speed."],["021.05.01.05:05","Primary flight controls: fill in the gaps → ____: travel limited.","Variable stops: travel limited."],["021.05.01.05:05","Primary flight controls: fill in the gaps → ____: the actuator force is limited, so the air load reduces the deflection.","Blow-back: the actuator force is limited, so the air load reduces the deflection."],["021.05.02.01:02","Secondary flight controls: fill in the gaps → ____: ____, ____, flight and ground spoilers, trim devices (tabs, THS).","Secondary controls: flaps/slats, speed brakes, flight and ground spoilers, trim devices (tabs, THS)."],["021.05.02.01:04","Secondary flight controls: fill in the gaps → ____: hydraulic motors or electric motors via torque tubes and ____ (ball-screws) or actuators.","Actuation: hydraulic motors or electric motors via torque tubes and screw jacks (ball-screws) or actuators."],["021.05.02.01:04","Secondary flight controls: fill in the gaps → A ____ (no-back brake) holds the screw jack in place if hydraulic power is lost.","A mechanical lock (no-back brake) holds the screw jack in place if hydraulic power is lost."],["021.05.02.01:05","Secondary flight controls: fill in the gaps → ____: VFE (flaps), VLE/VLO (gear), speed-brake limits → to prevent structural overload.","Limiting speeds: VFE (flaps), VLE/VLO (gear), speed-brake limits → to prevent structural overload."],["021.05.02.01:06","Secondary flight controls: fill in the gaps → ____: flaps ____ when VFE is exceeded (flap load relief); ____; slats auto-extend at high α.","Load relief: flaps retract automatically when VFE is exceeded (flap load relief); auto-retraction; slats auto-extend at high α."],["021.05.02.01:07","Secondary flight controls: fill in the gaps → ____: sensors compare the left and right position. Asymmetry stops the drive (brakes lock the torque tube).","Asymmetry protection: sensors compare the left and right position. Asymmetry stops the drive (brakes lock the torque tube)."],["021.05.01.01:02","Secondary flight controls: what do you remember about this? → An asymmetry creates a rolling moment → roll control problems; a …","An asymmetry creates a rolling moment → roll control problems; a different speed/landing configuration is needed."],["021.05.02.01:08","Secondary flight controls: fill in the gaps → ____: slats extend automatically near the stall (high α) → higher αcrit.","Auto-slat: slats extend automatically near the stall (high α) → higher αcrit."],["021.05.02.01:09","Secondary flight controls: fill in the gaps → ____: aerodynamic forces overpower the actuators at high speed, so the surface is pushed back.","Blow-back: aerodynamic forces overpower the actuators at high speed, so the surface is pushed back."],["021.05.04.01:01","Fly-by-wire: fill in the gaps → ____: pilot input (sidestick/column/pedals) → electrical signals → ____ → actuators → surfaces, with ____ of the aircraft response (sensors) to the computers.","Composition: pilot input (sidestick/column/pedals) → electrical signals → flight control computers → actuators → surfaces, with feedback of the aircraft response (sensors) to the computers."],["021.05.04.01:02","Fly-by-wire: fill in the gaps → ____: weight saving, reduced pilot workload, ____ ____ (α, speed, attitude, g-load).","Advantages: weight saving, reduced pilot workload, flight- envelope protection (α, speed, attitude, g-load)."],["021.05.04.01:03","Fly-by-wire: fill in the gaps → FBW is ____: there is no mechanical link to transmit air loads; the computer drives the actuators.","FBW is always irreversible: there is no mechanical link to transmit air loads; the computer drives the actuators."],["021.05.04.01:04","Fly-by-wire: fill in the gaps → ____: full protections.","Normal law: full protections."],["021.05.04.01:04","Fly-by-wire: fill in the gaps → ____: reduced or no protections.","Alternate law / secondary mode: reduced or no protections."],["021.05.04.01:05","Fly-by-wire: fill in the gaps → ____: stick deflection gives a proportional surface deflection, with no protection. Workload rises; the pilot must respect the limits.","Direct law: stick deflection gives a proportional surface deflection, with no protection. Workload rises; the pilot must respect the limits."],["021.05.04.01:07","Fly-by-wire: fill in the gaps → With ____, the inputs are ____ → \"DUAL INPUT\" warning.","With dual input, the inputs are summed algebraically → \"DUAL INPUT\" warning."],["021.05.04.01:01","Fly-by-wire: fill in the gaps → ____ (priority): the last pressed takes priority.","Takeover pushbutton (priority): the last pressed takes priority."],["021.05.04.01:09","Fly-by-wire: fill in the gaps → ____ (different hardware/software/suppliers) prevent a ____.","Dissimilar computers (different hardware/software/suppliers) prevent a common-mode failure."],["021.05.04.01:10","Fly-by-wire: fill in the gaps → ____ (redundancy) and ____ (voting, detection of a faulty sensor) are used.","Several surfaces per axis (redundancy) and several sensors (voting, detection of a faulty sensor) are used."],["021.03.02.02:08","EASA LO 021.03.02.02.08 → Explain the working principle of a low-pressure (0–2000 psi) system.","Low pressure is up to ~2 000 psi. / High pressure is typically 3 000 psi, and some aircraft use 4 000–5 000 psi (A380, B787)."],["021.05.01.01:01","EASA LO 021.05.01.01.01 → Define a ‘primary flight control’.","Fully powered (irreversible) controls: hydraulic actuators move the surfaces; air loads are not fed back to the pilot. / Redundancy on large aircraft: several hydraulic systems per surface, split surfaces (several actuators), several surfaces per axis, mechanical backup."],["021.05.01.01:03","EASA LO 021.05.01.01.03 → List the various means of control surface actuation including: — manual; — fully powered (irreversible); — partially powered (reversible).","Fully powered (irreversible) controls: hydraulic actuators move the surfaces; air loads are not fed back to the pilot."],["021.05.01.02:01","EASA LO 021.05.01.02.01 → Explain the basic principle of a fully manual control system.","Passive system: no pump — manual/master-cylinder, e.g. light-aircraft brakes. Active system: pump-driven. / Manual hand pump — emergency, ground."],["021.05.01.03:02","EASA LO 021.05.01.03.02 → Explain the concept of irreversibility in a flight control system.","Fully powered (irreversible) controls: hydraulic actuators move the surfaces; air loads are not fed back to the pilot."],["021.05.01.03:03","EASA LO 021.05.01.03.03 → Explain the need for a ‘feel system’ in a fully powered control system.","Fully powered (irreversible) controls: hydraulic actuators move the surfaces; air loads are not fed back to the pilot. / An artificial feel system (springs, q-feel) is needed to avoid over-controlling."],["021.05.01.03:04","EASA LO 021.05.01.03.04 → Explain the operating principle of a stabiliser trim system in a fully powered control system.","Fully powered (irreversible) controls: hydraulic actuators move the surfaces; air loads are not fed back to the pilot. / Trim: pitch via a trimmable horizontal stabiliser (THS). Rudder/aileron trim moves the neutral position of the feel unit (not tabs)."],["021.05.01.04:01","EASA LO 021.05.01.04.01 → Explain the basic principle of a partially powered control system.","Partially powered (reversible) controls: a hydraulic booster assists, but part of the air load is fed back → no artificial feel system is needed."],["021.05.01.05:02","EASA LO 021.05.01.05.02 → Explain how redundancy is obtained in primary flight control systems of large transport aeroplanes.","Redundancy on large aircraft: several hydraulic systems per surface, split surfaces (several actuators), several surfaces per axis, mechanical backup."],["021.05.02.01:01","EASA LO 021.05.02.01.01 → Define a ‘secondary flight control’.","Secondary controls: flaps/slats, speed brakes, flight and ground spoilers, trim devices (tabs, THS)."],["021.05.02.01:03","EASA LO 021.05.02.01.03 → Describe secondary flight control actuation methods and sources of actuating power.","Hydraulic vs. mechanical actuation:"],["021.05.04.01:11","EASA LO 021.05.04.01.11 → Explain why several sensors are needed on critical parameters.","Several surfaces per axis (redundancy) and several sensors (voting, detection of a faulty sensor) are used."]],"32":[["081.01.09.01:01","Trailing-edge flaps: fill in the gaps → ____, in increasing effectiveness:","Types, in increasing effectiveness:"],["081.01.09.02:03","Trailing-edge flaps: fill in the gaps → ____ flap: the rear part of the wing hinges down.","Plain flap: the rear part of the wing hinges down."],["081.01.09.01:01","Trailing-edge flaps: fill in the gaps → ____ flap: only the lower surface hinges down (high drag).","Split flap: only the lower surface hinges down (high drag)."],["081.01.09.01:01","Trailing-edge flaps: fill in the gaps → ____ flap: a slot lets high-energy air from the lower surface re-energise the boundary layer over the flap.","Slotted flap: a slot lets high-energy air from the lower surface re-energise the boundary layer over the flap."],["081.01.09.01:01","Trailing-edge flaps: fill in the gaps → ____ flap: moves ____ (increases ____) and down, usually slotted — highest CLmax.","Fowler flap: moves aft (increases wing area) and down, usually slotted — highest CLmax."],["081.01.09.01:02","Trailing-edge flaps: fill in the gaps → Flaps increase the ____ (and area with Fowler flaps) → ________ and CD increase → lower speeds are possible.","Flaps increase the effective camber (and area with Fowler flaps) → CL and CD increase → lower speeds are possible."],["081.01.09.01:03","Trailing-edge flaps: fill in the gaps → ____: the CP moves ____ → ____ pitching moment (the tailplane downwash change can counter this).","CP and pitching moment: the CP moves aft → nose-down pitching moment (the tailplane downwash change can counter this)."],["081.01.09.02:03","Trailing-edge flaps: fill in the gaps → ____ goes ____ because CLmax increases.","Stall speed goes down because CLmax increases."],["081.01.09.01:04","Trailing-edge flaps: fill in the gaps → ____________: shifts up/left. CL is higher at any α, CLmax is higher, αcrit is lower, and a given CL is reached at a lower α. The zero-lift α becomes more negative.","CL–α curve: shifts up/left. CL is higher at any α, CLmax is higher, αcrit is lower, and a given CL is reached at a lower α. The zero-lift α becomes more negative."],["081.01.09.01:05","Trailing-edge flaps: fill in the gaps → ____________D curve: (CL/CD)max is lower than for the clean wing (drag rises proportionally more).","CL–CD curve: (CL/CD)max is lower than for the clean wing (drag rises proportionally more)."],["081.01.09.01:06","Trailing-edge flaps: fill in the gaps → ____ becomes ____ (lower L/D).","Glide angle becomes steeper (lower L/D)."],["081.01.09.01:01","Trailing-edge flaps: fill in the gaps → ____ (one side extends) → strong ____, which can exceed aileron authority.","Flap asymmetry (one side extends) → strong rolling moment, which can exceed aileron authority."],["081.01.09.01:04","Trailing-edge flaps: fill in the gaps → ____ for a given CL → better view, tail-strike margin, shorter gear.","Lower nose attitude for a given CL → better view, tail-strike margin, shorter gear."],["081.01.09.01:08","Trailing-edge flaps: fill in the gaps → ________ → lower VR/VREF → shorter distances.","Lower VS → lower VR/VREF → shorter distances."],["081.01.09.01:08","Trailing-edge flaps: fill in the gaps → ____ (too little flap) at take-off → VR too low for the configuration → stall/tail strike, or long distances.","Mis-selection (too little flap) at take-off → VR too low for the configuration → stall/tail strike, or long distances."],["081.01.09.01:09","Trailing-edge flaps: fill in the gaps → ____ → sink and stall (CLmax falls).","Premature retraction → sink and stall (CLmax falls)."],["081.01.09.01:09","Trailing-edge flaps: fill in the gaps → ____ → overspeed (VFE).","Late extension → overspeed (VFE)."],["081.01.09.01:09","Trailing-edge flaps: what do you remember about this? → Too much flap → poor climb …","Too much flap → poor climb gradient."],["081.01.09.01:09","Trailing-edge flaps: fill in the gaps → Wrong flaps reduce the ____.","Wrong flaps reduce the stall/buffet margins."],["081.01.09.02:03","Leading-edge devices: fill in the gaps → ____: hinges from the lower leading edge forward and down (inboard wings of large jets).","Krueger flap: hinges from the lower leading edge forward and down (inboard wings of large jets)."],["081.01.09.02:01","Leading-edge devices: fill in the gaps → ____: a flexible Krueger that shapes the camber.","Variable-camber flap: a flexible Krueger that shapes the camber."],["081.01.09.02:04","Leading-edge devices: fill in the gaps → ____: a movable leading-edge section that forms a ____ (fixed slots also exist).","Slat: a movable leading-edge section that forms a slot (fixed slots also exist)."],["081.01.09.02:02","Leading-edge devices: fill in the gaps → ____: high-pressure air from the lower surface flows through the slot and ____ ____ → separation is delayed to a higher α.","Function of the slot: high-pressure air from the lower surface flows through the slot and re-energises the upper-surface boundary layer → separation is delayed to a higher α."],["081.01.09.02:03","Leading-edge devices: fill in the gaps → A leading-edge flap increases ____ → smoother flow at high α.","A leading-edge flap increases camber at the nose → smoother flow at high α."],["081.01.09.02:05","Leading-edge devices: fill in the gaps → ____ goes ____. The main mechanism is a ____crit (not a higher CL at a given α, as with trailing-edge flaps).","Stall speed goes down. The main mechanism is a higher αcrit (not a higher CL at a given α, as with trailing-edge flaps)."],["081.01.09.02:05","Leading-edge devices: fill in the gaps → ____________: the curve is extended — about the same slope, CLmax higher, αcrit considerably higher (e.g. 15° → 25°). Trailing-edge flaps shift the curve up but lower αcrit.","CL–α curve: the curve is extended — about the same slope, CLmax higher, αcrit considerably higher (e.g. 15° → 25°). Trailing-edge flaps shift the curve up but lower αcrit."],["081.01.09.02:05","Leading-edge devices: fill in the gaps → ____________D curve: higher CLmax reached with somewhat more drag; slightly lower L/D at normal CL.","CL–CD curve: higher CLmax reached with somewhat more drag; slightly lower L/D at normal CL."],["081.01.09.02:07","Leading-edge devices: fill in the gaps → ____: one wing stalls earlier → ____ near the stall.","Slat asymmetry: one wing stalls earlier → roll/wing drop near the stall."],["081.01.09.02:08","Leading-edge devices: fill in the gaps → ____: lower VS → shorter distances. The ____ is the ____ needed to use the high α → risk of tail strike, poor view.","Why they are used: lower VS → shorter distances. The disadvantage is the high nose-up attitude needed to use the high α → risk of tail strike, poor view."],["081.01.09.01:08","Leading-edge devices: fill in the gaps → That is why they are ____, which lower the attitude.","That is why they are combined with trailing-edge flaps, which lower the attitude."],["081.01.09.03:01","Vortex generators: fill in the gaps → ____: delay flow ____ — improve control effectiveness, reduce the stall speed, delay shock-induced separation, improve buffet margins.","Purpose: delay flow separation — improve control effectiveness, reduce the stall speed, delay shock-induced separation, improve buffet margins."],["081.01.09.03:02","Vortex generators: fill in the gaps → ____: small vanes at an angle produce ____ that mix ____ from outside the boundary layer into it → the boundary layer stays attached longer.","Principle: small vanes at an angle produce tip vortices that mix high-energy air from outside the boundary layer into it → the boundary layer stays attached longer."],["081.01.09.03:03","Vortex generators: fill in the gaps → ____: simple, cheap, effective. ____: ____ parasite drag in cruise, and ice accretion on them.","Advantages: simple, cheap, effective. Disadvantages: extra parasite drag in cruise, and ice accretion on them."],["081.01.10.01:01","Spoilers: fill in the gaps → ____: deflect ____ (up on the down-going wing) to assist or replace the ailerons → no adverse yaw.","Roll spoilers: deflect asymmetrically (up on the down-going wing) to assist or replace the ailerons → no adverse yaw."],["081.01.10.01:01","Spoilers: fill in the gaps → ____: deflect symmetrically in flight → descend or decelerate.","Flight spoilers (speed brakes): deflect symmetrically in flight → descend or decelerate."],["081.01.10.01:01","Spoilers: fill in the gaps → ____: fully deployed on touchdown → ____ and put the weight on the wheels (brake effectiveness), plus drag.","Ground spoilers (lift dumpers): fully deployed on touchdown → destroy lift and put the weight on the wheels (brake effectiveness), plus drag."],["081.01.10.02:01","Spoilers: fill in the gaps → ____________: CL is lower at any α, CLmax is lower → stall speed increases. Keep the speed margin when using them!","CL–α curve: CL is lower at any α, CLmax is lower → stall speed increases. Keep the speed margin when using them!"],["081.01.10.01:03","Spoilers: fill in the gaps → ____________D curve: much more drag, less lift → L/D decreases significantly.","CL–CD curve: much more drag, less lift → L/D decreases significantly."],["081.01.10.02:03","Speed brakes: fill in the gaps → ____ are surfaces (fuselage panels or wing spoilers) used to increase drag. Uses:","Speed brakes are surfaces (fuselage panels or wing spoilers) used to increase drag. Uses:"],["081.01.10.02:05","Speed brakes: what do you remember about this? → rapid descent without …","rapid descent without overspeed,"],["081.01.10.02:03","Speed brakes: what do you remember about this? → reducing speed in level …","reducing speed in level flight,"],["081.01.10.01:01","Speed brakes: what do you remember about this? → landing roll (with …","landing roll (with spoilers)."],["081.01.10.02:03","Speed brakes: fill in the gaps → They ____ because the extended surface increases the frontal area and causes separation.","They increase parasite (form) drag because the extended surface increases the frontal area and causes separation."],["081.01.10.02:03","Speed brakes: fill in the gaps → They ____; the drag curve shifts ____; ____MD is lower (the parasite-drag share increases).","They reduce L/D; the drag curve shifts up; VMD is lower (the parasite-drag share increases)."],["081.01.10.02:05","Speed brakes: fill in the gaps → They give a ____ at the same speed. Spoiler-type speed brakes also raise the stall speed.","They give a higher rate and steeper angle of descent at the same speed. Spoiler-type speed brakes also raise the stall speed."],["081.01.09.02:06","EASA LO 081.01.09.02.06 → Compare their influence on the CL–CD graph.","CL–CD curve: higher CLmax reached with somewhat more drag; slightly lower L/D at normal CL. / CL–CD curve: (CL/CD)max is lower than for the clean wing (drag rises proportionally more)."],["081.01.10.01:02","EASA LO 081.01.10.01.02 → Describe the effect of spoilers on the CL– graph and stall speed.","Flight spoilers (speed brakes): deflect symmetrically in flight → descend or decelerate. / CL–α curve: CL is lower at any α, CLmax is lower → stall speed increases. Keep the speed margin when using them!"],["081.01.10.02:02","EASA LO 081.01.10.02.02 → State their influence on the CL–CD graph and lift–drag ratio.","CL–CD curve: much more drag, less lift → L/D decreases significantly. / CL–CD curve: (CL/CD)max is lower than for the clean wing (drag rises proportionally more)."],["081.01.10.02:04","EASA LO 081.01.10.02.04 → Describe how speed brakes affect the minimum drag speed.","reducing speed in level flight, / They give a higher rate and steeper angle of descent at the same speed. Spoiler-type speed brakes also raise the stall speed."]],"33":[["033.05.01.01:01","Why a fixed format: fill in the gaps → The ICAO ATS flight plan (____) has a ____ so that it can be ____ automatically by computers (e.g. IFPS/Eurocontrol NM).","The ICAO ATS flight plan (ICAO Doc 4444, Appendix 2) has a standardised, fixed format so that it can be processed automatically by computers (e.g. IFPS/Eurocontrol NM)."],["033.05.01.01:01","Why a fixed format: fill in the gaps → It is understood ____ by all ATS units without ambiguity, it transmits quickly (AFTN), and it gives ____ the data it needs.","It is understood internationally by all ATS units without ambiguity, it transmits quickly (AFTN), and it gives SAR the data it needs."],["033.05.01.01:01","Why a fixed format: fill in the gaps → Detailed filing rules and items 16–19 are covered on the ____ ____.","Detailed filing rules and items 16–19 are covered on the #65 page."],["033.05.01.01:02","Filling in / decoding the FPL – 7 Aircraft identification: what are the key facts?","Content: max 7 characters, no hyphens · Examples / rules: registration HBSGT (not HB- SGT) or ICAO operator code + flight number SWR123"],["033.05.01.01:02","Filling in / decoding the FPL – 8 Flight rules / type of flight: what are the key facts?","Content: I IFR, V VFR, Y IFR first → VFR, Z VFR first → IFR · type: S scheduled, N non-scheduled, G general aviation, M military, X other · Examples / rules: \"Y\" / \"Z\" → the change point goes in Item 15 (e.g. VFR or IFR after a point)"],["033.05.01.01:02","Filling in / decoding the FPL – 9 Number, type, wake category: what are the key facts?","Content: number (only if > 1), ICAO type designator (Doc 8643, e.g. C172, A320, else ZZZZ + TYP/ in Item 18) · WTC L ≤ 7 000 kg, M 7 000– 136 000 kg, H ≥ 136 000 kg, J super (A380) · Examples / rules: 2F16/M…"],["033.05.01.01:01","Filling in / decoding the FPL: fill in the gaps → ____ before \"/\" COM/NAV/approach: ____ \"SDFGRWY/S\" Equipment & none or ____ standard (VHF RTF, VOR, capabilities ILS), G GNSS, D DME, F ADF, O VOR, L ILS, R PBN approved (→ PBN/ in 18), W RVSM, Y 8.33 kHz, I inertial… · after \"/\" surveillance: N, A Mode A, C Mode A+C, S Mode S (with pressure alt. + ID), E/H/L","10 before \"/\" COM/NAV/approach: N \"SDFGRWY/S\" Equipment & none or S standard (VHF RTF, VOR, capabilities ILS), G GNSS, D DME, F ADF, O VOR, L ILS, R PBN approved (→ PBN/ in 18), W RVSM, Y 8.33 kHz, I inertial… · after \"/\" surveillance: N, A Mode A, C Mode A+C, S Mode S (with pressure alt. + ID), E/H/L"],["033.05.01.01:02","Filling in / decoding the FPL – 13 Departure aerodrome & time: what are the key facts?","Content: ICAO 4-letter code (unknown: ZZZZ + DEP/ in 18; AFIL for filing in the air) + EOBT (estimated off- block time) in UTC, 4 digits · Examples / rules: LSZH0930"],["033.05.01.01:01","Filling in / decoding the FPL – 15 Route: what are the key facts?","Content: cruising speed (N0450 kt, K0830 km/h, M082 Mach) + level (F350, A045 altitude in hundreds of ft, S1130 metres, VFR) + route: SID/ATS routes (DCT for direct), significant points, changes of speed/level (point/N0450F370), flight-rules change (…VFR/IFR), STAR · Examples / rules: N0450F350 DCT ZUE UN871 …"],["033.05.01.01:01","Filling in / decoding the FPL: fill in the gaps → ____: read each item in the order above.","Decoding practice: read each item in the order above."],["033.05.01.01:01","Filling in / decoding the FPL: fill in the gaps → Example: Item 8 \"____\" = IFR, general aviation.","Example: Item 8 \"IG\" = IFR, general aviation."],["033.05.01.01:01","Filling in / decoding the FPL: fill in the gaps → Speed/level groups in Item 15 appear ____ when they change.","Speed/level groups in Item 15 appear after a point when they change."],["033.05.01.01:01","Filling in / decoding the FPL: fill in the gaps → EOBT is in UTC; the FPL is normally filed ____ (see #65).","EOBT is in UTC; the FPL is normally filed ≥ 60 min before EOBT (see #65)."],["033.05.02.01:01","RPL: fill in the gaps → ____: submitted for ____ flight.","Individual FPL: submitted for one flight."],["033.05.02.01:01","RPL: fill in the gaps → ____: for ____ regularly — same days, same basic features (route, times, aircraft type), ____.","RPL (repetitive flight plan): for IFR flights operated regularly — same days, same basic features (route, times, aircraft type), at least 10 occasions."],["033.05.02.01:01","RPL: fill in the gaps → It is submitted ____, in list form, by the operator to the ATS units and ____ for automatic activation.","It is submitted once, in list form, by the operator to the ATS units and stored for automatic activation."],["033.05.02.01:01","RPL: what do you remember about this? → Not used for VFR, and not for irregular flights. Temporary changes …","Not used for VFR, and not for irregular flights. Temporary changes are handled with the normal FPL messages (CHG/CNL/DLA)."],["033.05.02.01:01","Exam trap (RPL): complete the fact → Item ____as no hyphens and max ____characters.","Item 7 has no hyphens and max 7 characters."],["033.05.02.01:01","Exam trap (RPL): complete the fact → WTC M/H boundary is ____; L ≤ ____.","WTC M/H boundary is 136 000 kg; L ≤ 7 000 kg."],["033.05.02.01:01","Exam trap (RPL): complete the fact → Item ____altitude \"A045\" = ____.","Item 15 altitude \"A045\" = 4 500 ft."],["033.05.02.01:01","Exam trap (RPL): complete the fact → RPL = IFR only, ≥ ____occasions.","RPL = IFR only, ≥ 10 occasions."]],"34":[["021.04.01.01:01","Gear types & components: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ gear or ____ (conventional) gear.","Aeroplanes: nose-wheel (tricycle) gear or tail-wheel (conventional) gear."],["021.04.01.01:01","Gear types & components: what do you remember about this? → Tricycle: stable on the ground, good view, easier …","Tricycle: stable on the ground, good view, easier braking."],["021.04.01.01:01","Gear types & components: what do you remember about this? → Tail-wheel: ground-loop …","Tail-wheel: ground-loop tendency."],["021.04.01.01:02","Gear types & components: fill in the gaps → ____: nose wheel, tail wheel, or ____.","Helicopters: nose wheel, tail wheel, or skids."],["021.04.01.02:02","Gear types & components: fill in the gaps → ____: absorbs landing energy — nitrogen/air as the spring, oil forced through an orifice as the damper.","Oleo (shock) strut: absorbs landing energy — nitrogen/air as the spring, oil forced through an orifice as the damper."],["021.04.01.02:01","Gear types & components: fill in the gaps → ____: multiple wheels on large jets spread the load.","Bogie & bogie beam: multiple wheels on large jets spread the load."],["021.04.01.02:02","Gear types & components: fill in the gaps → ____: take the fore/aft loads.","Drag struts: take the fore/aft loads."],["021.04.01.02:02","Gear types & components: fill in the gaps → ____: take the lateral loads; fold for retraction.","Side stays/struts: take the lateral loads; fold for retraction."],["021.04.01.01:01","Gear types & components: fill in the gaps → ____: keep the wheel aligned and stop the piston rotating in the cylinder.","Torsion links (scissors): keep the wheel aligned and stop the piston rotating in the cylinder."],["021.04.01.02:01","Gear types & components: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ links (down-lock) plus uplocks.","Locks: over-centre links (down-lock) plus uplocks."],["021.04.01.01:01","Gear types & components: fill in the gaps → ____: fairing, sequenced with the gear.","Gear doors: fairing, sequenced with the gear."],["021.04.01.02:08","Gear types & components: fill in the gaps → ____: selector UP → doors open → down-locks release → gear retracts → uplocks engage → doors close. Extension is the reverse.","Normal sequence (retraction): selector UP → doors open → down-locks release → gear retracts → uplocks engage → doors close. Extension is the reverse."],["021.04.01.01:01","Gear types & components: what do you remember about this? → Wheels are braked automatically before they enter the bay …","Wheels are braked automatically before they enter the bay (spin-down)."],["021.04.01.01:01","Gear types & components: fill in the gaps → ____ = down & locked; ____ = in transit/disagree; nothing = up & locked.","Three green = down & locked; red = in transit/disagree; nothing = up & locked."],["021.04.01.02:05","Gear types & components: fill in the gaps → A ____ sounds if the gear is not down with throttle retarded or flaps in the landing position.","A gear warning (horn) sounds if the gear is not down with throttle retarded or flaps in the landing position."],["021.04.01.02:06","Gear types & components: fill in the gaps → ____:","Protection against retraction on the ground:"],["021.04.01.02:06","Gear types & components: fill in the gaps → ____ (removed before flight — ____ ____ after take-off → higher drag, worse climb, and possibly damage).","Ground lock pins (removed before flight — with the pins in, the gear cannot retract after take-off → higher drag, worse climb, and possibly damage)."],["021.04.01.01:01","Gear types & components: fill in the gaps → A ____ blocks selector movement on the ground.","A squat (weight-on-wheels) switch blocks selector movement on the ground."],["021.04.01.01:01","Gear types & components: what do you remember about this? → A mechanical selector …","A mechanical selector latch/baulk."],["021.04.01.02:07","Gear types & components: fill in the gaps → ________ = max speed to ____ the gear (VLO retraction can be lower than extension, because the doors are open).","VLO = max speed to operate the gear (VLO retraction can be lower than extension, because the doors are open)."],["021.04.01.02:07","Gear types & components: fill in the gaps → ________ = max speed with the gear ____ (VLE ≥ VLO).","VLE = max speed with the gear extended (VLE ≥ VLO)."],["021.04.01.02:08","Gear types & components: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (release uplocks/doors) → ____ (gear falls) → down-lock (checked by indication).","Emergency extension: unlock (release uplocks/doors) → operate (gear falls) → down-lock (checked by indication)."],["021.04.01.02:09","Gear types & components: fill in the gaps → Methods: ____ (aided by aerodynamic loads/springs), hand pump, compressed nitrogen bottle, alternate hydraulic system.","Methods: gravity/free fall (aided by aerodynamic loads/springs), hand pump, compressed nitrogen bottle, alternate hydraulic system."],["021.04.01.01:01","Gear types & components: what do you remember about this? → Retraction is not possible afterwards on many …","Retraction is not possible afterwards on many types."],["021.04.02.01:03","Steering: fill in the gaps → ____: hydraulic actuators controlled via a ____ (large angles, taxi) or ____ (small angles, take-off/landing), with a follow-up mechanism.","Nose-wheel steering: hydraulic actuators controlled via a tiller (large angles, taxi) or rudder pedals (small angles, take-off/landing), with a follow-up mechanism."],["021.04.02.01:02","Steering: fill in the gaps → ____: steer by braking left/right (light aircraft, some helicopters).","Differential braking with a free-castoring nose wheel: steer by braking left/right (light aircraft, some helicopters)."],["021.04.02.01:04","Steering: fill in the gaps → A ____ (cam in the strut) ____ ____ when the strut extends after lift-off → safe retraction into the bay.","A centring mechanism (cam in the strut) centres the nose wheel when the strut extends after lift-off → safe retraction into the bay."],["021.04.02.01:05","Steering: fill in the gaps → ____ = rapid oscillation of the wheel about its steering axis → vibration, damage to the strut, tyre wear, loss of control → reduced by a ____ (hydraulic/friction). Main wheels can shimmy too (torsion links).","Shimmy = rapid oscillation of the wheel about its steering axis → vibration, damage to the strut, tyre wear, loss of control → reduced by a shimmy damper (hydraulic/friction). Main wheels can shimmy too (torsion links)."],["021.04.02.01:06","Steering: fill in the gaps → ____: the rear main gear steers during tight turns → reduces ____ and side loads.","Body-gear (main-wheel) steering: the rear main gear steers during tight turns → reduces tyre scrubbing and side loads."],["021.04.03.01:01","Brakes: fill in the gaps → ____: rotors (turning with the wheel) and stators (fixed) are clamped by hydraulic pistons → kinetic energy becomes ____.","Disc brakes: rotors (turning with the wheel) and stators (fixed) are clamped by hydraulic pistons → kinetic energy becomes heat."],["021.04.03.01:01","Brakes: fill in the gaps → ____: cheaper and heavier, better when cold, wear faster, lower max temperature.","Steel: cheaper and heavier, better when cold, wear faster, lower max temperature."],["021.04.03.01:01","Brakes: fill in the gaps → ____: ____, higher heat capacity and temperature limit, longer life, wear more per application ____ (taxi), more expensive.","Carbon: lighter, higher heat capacity and temperature limit, longer life, wear more per application when cold (taxi), more expensive."],["021.04.01.02:07","Brakes: fill in the gaps → ____: maximum energy the brakes can absorb → limits ________ (max brake energy speed) for take-off/RTO.","Brake energy limit: maximum energy the brakes can absorb → limits VMBE (max brake energy speed) for take-off/RTO."],["021.04.04.01:04","Brakes: what do you remember about this? → After heavy braking: cooling times; risk of fire or tyre deflation …","After heavy braking: cooling times; risk of fire or tyre deflation (fuse plugs)."],["021.04.03.02:04","Brakes: fill in the gaps → ____: hydraulic (normal/alternate/accumulator) or ____ (B787).","Actuation: hydraulic (normal/alternate/accumulator) or electric (B787)."],["021.04.03.01:01","Brakes: fill in the gaps → ____: stops the wheels spinning before retraction into the bay (heat and debris).","In-flight wheel braking: stops the wheels spinning before retraction into the bay (heat and debris)."],["021.04.03.02:04","Brakes: fill in the gaps → ____: stored pressure for ____ ____ when the hydraulic system is off.","Brake accumulator: stored pressure for emergency and parking brake when the hydraulic system is off."],["021.04.03.02:04","Brakes: fill in the gaps → ____: holds the pressure on the brakes (locked), usually from the accumulator.","Parking brake: holds the pressure on the brakes (locked), usually from the accumulator."],["021.04.03.02:06","Brakes: fill in the gaps → ____: pins that protrude from the brake housing; the pin length is checked on walk-around (flush = replace).","Brake-wear indicators: pins that protrude from the brake housing; the pin length is checked on walk-around (flush = replace)."],["021.04.03.02:07","Brakes: fill in the gaps → ____: monitor heat → before take-off (an RTO needs cool brakes), turnaround cooling, fire risk, fuse plugs.","Brake temperature indicator: monitor heat → before take-off (an RTO needs cool brakes), turnaround cooling, fire risk, fuse plugs."],["021.04.03.03:01","Anti-skid & autobrake: fill in the gaps → ____: automatically ____ when a wheel starts to skid.","Anti-skid: automatically releases brake pressure when a wheel starts to skid."],["021.04.03.03:02","Anti-skid & autobrake: fill in the gaps → It compares ____ with ____ → keeps the optimum ____ for max friction.","It compares wheel speed with aircraft reference speed → keeps the optimum slip ratio (~10–20 %) for max friction."],["021.04.03.03:02","Anti-skid & autobrake: fill in the gaps → It gives ____ (compare wheels), ____ (no braking before spin-up/WOW), and hydroplaning protection.","It gives locked-wheel protection (compare wheels), touchdown protection (no braking before spin-up/WOW), and hydroplaning protection."],["021.04.04.01:06","Anti-skid & autobrake: what do you remember about this? → Result: shorter stop distances, no flat spots or …","Result: shorter stop distances, no flat spots or bursts."],["021.04.03.03:03","Anti-skid & autobrake: fill in the gaps → ____ → manual careful braking, longer landing/ASDA distances (performance penalty), some dispatch limits.","Failure → manual careful braking, longer landing/ASDA distances (performance penalty), some dispatch limits."],["021.04.03.04:04","Anti-skid & autobrake: fill in the gaps → ____: applies brake pressure automatically for a selected ____ (LOW/MED/MAX, 1–4) or ____ mode (max braking if thrust is retarded above a speed).","Autobrake: applies brake pressure automatically for a selected deceleration (LOW/MED/MAX, 1–4) or RTO mode (max braking if thrust is retarded above a speed)."],["021.04.03.04:02","Anti-skid & autobrake: fill in the gaps → ____, because autobrakes would otherwise lock the wheels.","Anti-skid must be operative, because autobrakes would otherwise lock the wheels."],["021.04.03.04:03","Anti-skid & autobrake: fill in the gaps → ____: ____; ____ (ready — applies at touchdown/wheel spin-up or on an RTO); ACTIVATED (braking); deactivated by pedal braking, thrust advance or a switch.","Modes: OFF; ARMED (ready — applies at touchdown/wheel spin-up or on an RTO); ACTIVATED (braking); deactivated by pedal braking, thrust advance or a switch."],["021.04.03.04:04","Anti-skid & autobrake: fill in the gaps → A ____ mode brakes ____ when ____ contributes (constant total deceleration).","A deceleration-rate mode brakes less when reverse thrust contributes (constant total deceleration)."],["021.04.03.03:03","Anti-skid & autobrake: fill in the gaps → On a ____ the target may be unachievable (anti-skid limits it).","On a slippery runway the target may be unachievable (anti-skid limits it)."],["021.04.04.01:01","Tyres: fill in the gaps → ____ (bias): plies cross at an angle.","Diagonal / cross-ply (bias): plies cross at an angle."],["021.04.04.01:01","Tyres: fill in the gaps → ____: plies at 90° to the rim — lighter, cooler, longer tread life.","Radial: plies at 90° to the rim — lighter, cooler, longer tread life."],["021.04.04.01:01","Tyres: fill in the gaps → ____ = a strength index (not the actual number of plies).","Ply rating = a strength index (not the actual number of plies)."],["021.04.04.01:01","Tyres: fill in the gaps → ____ = the grooved rubber contact surface.","Tread = the grooved rubber contact surface."],["021.04.04.01:02","Tyres: fill in the gaps → ____ = tyre slipping around the rim (check the ____ — misalignment beyond limits = replace).","Creep = tyre slipping around the rim (check the creep marks — misalignment beyond limits = replace)."],["021.04.04.01:02","Tyres: fill in the gaps → ____ = new tread vulcanised onto a sound carcass (cover).","Retread = new tread vulcanised onto a sound carcass (cover)."],["021.04.04.01:03","Tyres: fill in the gaps → ____ in the wheels ____ at high temperature (heavy braking) → ____ in a controlled way and prevent an explosion.","Thermal/fusible plugs in the wheels melt at high temperature (heavy braking) → deflate the tyre in a controlled way and prevent an explosion."],["021.04.04.01:04","Tyres: fill in the gaps → ____: bulges, exposed cords, uneven wear → higher risk of ____.","Tread separation/wear: bulges, exposed cords, uneven wear → higher risk of tyre burst."],["021.04.04.01:01","Tyres: fill in the gaps → ____ (e.g. 225 mph): centrifugal forces and heat → structural failure.","Tyre groundspeed limit (e.g. 225 mph): centrifugal forces and heat → structural failure."],["021.04.04.01:01","Tyres: fill in the gaps → ____: FOD, runway debris.","Cuts/damage: FOD, runway debris."],["021.04.03.02:05","Tyres: fill in the gaps → ____: locked-wheel skid (braking without anti-skid, parking brake set on landing).","Flat spots: locked-wheel skid (braking without anti-skid, parking brake set on landing)."],["021.04.04.01:01","Tyres: fill in the gaps → Also check inflation (____ → overheat), creep marks, and wear.","Also check inflation (under-inflation → overheat), creep marks, and wear."],["021.04.01.02:03","EASA LO 021.04.01.02.03 → Name the different components of a landing gear, using a diagram.","Protection against retraction on the ground:"],["021.04.01.02:04","EASA LO 021.04.01.02.04 → Describe the sequence of events during normal operation of the landing gear.","Actuation: hydraulic (normal/alternate/accumulator) or electric (B787)."],["021.04.02.01:01","EASA LO 021.04.02.01.01 → Explain the operating principle of nose-wheel steering.","Helicopters: nose wheel, tail wheel, or skids."],["021.04.03.02:01","EASA LO 021.04.03.02.01 → Explain the limitation of brake energy and describe the operational consequences.","Brake energy limit: maximum energy the brakes can absorb → limits VMBE (max brake energy speed) for take-off/RTO."],["021.04.03.02:03","EASA LO 021.04.03.02.03 → Explain the purpose of an in-flight wheel brake system.","Brake accumulator: stored pressure for emergency and parking brake when the hydraulic system is off."]],"35":[["062.02.02.01:01",": what do you remember about this? → Review stage — condensed recap of NDB/ADF, VOR, DME (see #12/#13 for …","Review stage — condensed recap of NDB/ADF, VOR, DME (see #12/#13 for the full treatment)."],["062.02.02.01:01","NDB / ADF: fill in the gaps → ____ = non-directional radio beacon (the ____ part). ____ = automatic direction finder (the airborne part).","NDB = non-directional radio beacon (the ground part). ADF = automatic direction finder (the airborne part)."],["062.02.02.01:08","NDB / ADF: fill in the gaps → Bands: ____, ICAO band ____. Broadcast stations also transmit in this band and can be used, but aren't monitored.","Bands: LF/MF, ICAO band 190–1 750 kHz. Broadcast stations also transmit in this band and can be used, but aren't monitored."],["062.02.02.01:07","NDB / ADF: fill in the gaps → ____ = LF/MF NDB used for the final approach, range ____ ____.","Locator = LF/MF NDB used for the final approach, range 10–25 NM."],["062.02.02.03:06","NDB / ADF: fill in the gaps → NDBs have ____, but there is ____ ____ → monitor the ident continuously.","NDBs have automatic ground monitoring, but there is no failure flag on board → monitor the ident continuously."],["062.02.02.01:16","NDB / ADF: fill in the gaps → ____: unmodulated carrier + keyed ident → the ____ must be ON to hear the ident (automatic on modern sets).","N0N/A1A: unmodulated carrier + keyed ident → the BFO must be ON to hear the ident (automatic on modern sets)."],["062.02.02.01:13","NDB / ADF: fill in the gaps → ____: carrier + amplitude-modulated tone ident → no BFO needed.","N0N/A2A: carrier + amplitude-modulated tone ident → no BFO needed."],["062.02.02.01:10","NDB / ADF: fill in the gaps → ____ by the Morse ident before use.","Identify by the Morse ident before use."],["062.02.02.01:12","NDB / ADF: fill in the gaps → ____: over the station the needle is unstable; station passage = needle swings from ahead to behind.","Cone of confusion: over the station the needle is unstable; station passage = needle swings from ahead to behind."],["062.02.02.02:02","NDB / ADF: fill in the gaps → ____ shows the ____ only.","Fixed-card ADF shows the relative bearing (RB) only."],["062.02.02.02:02","NDB / ADF: fill in the gaps → ____ (heading-slaved or manually set) shows the QDM.","Moving-card (heading-slaved or manually set) shows the QDM."],["062.02.02.02:02","NDB / ADF: fill in the gaps → ____ (card slaved to heading): head of the needle = ____, tail = ____.","RMI (card slaved to heading): head of the needle = QDM, tail = QDR."],["062.02.02.02:04","NDB / ADF: fill in the gaps → ____; ____ ____; compass heading → (± dev) → MH → (± var) → TH.","QDM = MH + RB; QTE (true bearing from the station) = TH + RB ± 180; compass heading → (± dev) → MH → (± var) → TH."],["062.02.03.02:06","NDB / ADF: fill in the gaps → For ____, apply the ____ ____.","For true bearings from an NDB, apply the variation at the aircraft."],["062.02.02.03:03","NDB / ADF: fill in the gaps → ____: keep the needle on the nose → curved path downwind.","Homing: keep the needle on the nose → curved path downwind."],["062.02.03.02:05","NDB / ADF: fill in the gaps → ____: apply a wind correction angle; for inbound tracking with a WCA, the needle is offset ____.","Tracking: apply a wind correction angle; for inbound tracking with a WCA, the needle is offset opposite to the WCA."],["062.02.03.02:05","NDB / ADF: fill in the gaps → ____: typically 30–45° inbound, 30° outbound.","Interception: typically 30–45° inbound, 30° outbound."],["062.02.02.02:06","NDB / ADF: fill in the gaps → ____, and ____ at RB 090/270.","Changing QDM/QDR, and abeam at RB 090/270."],["062.02.02.03:01","NDB / ADF: fill in the gaps → ____: set by ____ (range ∝ √power — 4× power = 2× range). Mainly ____, which follows the curvature. Sky waves at night.","Range: set by transmitter power (range ∝ √power — 4× power = 2× range). Mainly ground (surface) wave, which follows the curvature. Sky waves at night."],["062.02.02.03:04","NDB / ADF: fill in the gaps → ____ from ground/sky-wave interference.","Fading from ground/sky-wave interference."],["062.02.02.03:05","NDB / ADF: fill in the gaps → Established on an NDB approach = within ____ of the required bearing.","Established on an NDB approach = within ±5° of the required bearing."],["062.02.02.04:01","NDB / ADF: fill in the gaps → ____: the wave speeds up and bends when crossing the coast from land to sea → minimise it by using stations near the coast, or crossings near 90°.","Coastal refraction: the wave speeds up and bends when crossing the coast from land to sea → minimise it by using stations near the coast, or crossings near 90°."],["062.02.02.04:02","NDB / ADF: fill in the gaps → ____: sky waves + ground wave with phase/polarisation differences → wandering needle; also co-channel interference at night.","Night (twilight) effect: sky waves + ground wave with phase/polarisation differences → wandering needle; also co-channel interference at night."],["062.02.02.05:01","NDB / ADF: fill in the gaps → ____: reflection/diffraction.","Mountain effect: reflection/diffraction."],["062.02.02.01:01","NDB / ADF: fill in the gaps → ____: the needle points to the ____.","Thunderstorm effect: the needle points to the Cb."],["062.02.02.05:03","NDB / ADF: fill in the gaps → ____ in turns.","Dip (bank) error in turns."],["062.02.02.05:03","NDB / ADF: what do you remember about this? → Quadrantal error …","Quadrantal error (airframe)."],["062.02.03.01:01","VOR: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (omnidirectional) and ____ phase (rotating). The phase difference = magnetic bearing FROM the station (radial).","Principle: reference phase (omnidirectional) and variable phase (rotating). The phase difference = magnetic bearing FROM the station (radial)."],["062.02.03.01:04","VOR: fill in the gaps → ____ reduces site errors. CVOR = conventional.","Doppler VOR (DVOR) reduces site errors. CVOR = conventional."],["062.02.03.01:03","VOR: fill in the gaps → ____. ____ ____.","VHF 108.0–117.975 MHz. 108.0–111.975 MHz with an odd first decimal = ILS localizer."],["062.02.03.01:05","VOR: fill in the gaps → ____ can be broadcast on the VOR frequency.","ATIS can be broadcast on the VOR frequency."],["062.02.03.01:06","VOR: fill in the gaps → ____: antenna, receiver, indicator.","Airborne equipment: antenna, receiver, indicator."],["062.02.03.01:07","VOR: fill in the gaps → ____: 3-letter Morse (± voice).","Ident: 3-letter Morse (± voice)."],["062.02.03.01:09","VOR: fill in the gaps → ____: if out of tolerance, the ____ ____ or radiation stops.","Automatic monitoring: if out of tolerance, the ident and/or navigation components are removed or radiation stops."],["062.02.03.02:01","VOR: fill in the gaps → ____: the tail shows the ____.","RMI: the tail shows the radial."],["062.02.03.02:04","VOR: fill in the gaps → ____: deviation from the selected course (usually ____ dot, ____ on a 5-dot scale), independent of heading.","HSI/CDI/OBI: deviation from the selected course (usually 2° per dot, 10° full scale on a 5-dot scale), independent of heading."],["062.02.03.01:01","VOR: fill in the gaps → ____: shows whether the selected course brings you towards the station or away — also ____.","TO/FROM: shows whether the selected course brings you towards the station or away — also independent of heading."],["062.02.03.02:06","VOR: fill in the gaps → Converting a radial to a true bearing: use the ____ ____.","Converting a radial to a true bearing: use the variation at the VOR station."],["062.02.03.02:05","VOR: fill in the gaps → ____: tracking with WCA, radial interception inbound/outbound, changing radials.","Procedures: tracking with WCA, radial interception inbound/outbound, changing radials."],["062.02.03.04:01","VOR: fill in the gaps → Established on a VOR approach = within ____ ____.","Established on a VOR approach = within half full-scale deflection."],["062.02.03.04:02","VOR: fill in the gaps → ____: radials bent by terrain/buildings → fluctuations.","Scalloping: radials bent by terrain/buildings → fluctuations."],["062.02.04.01:04","DME: fill in the gaps → ____. The aircraft ____ sends pulse pairs; the ground transponder replies on a frequency ±63 MHz after a ____. Distance comes from the time → slant range.","UHF (962–1 213 MHz). The aircraft interrogator sends pulse pairs; the ground transponder replies on a frequency ±63 MHz after a 50 µs delay. Distance comes from the time → slant range."],["062.02.04.01:05","DME: fill in the gaps → The position line is a ____ around the station.","The position line is a circle around the station."],["062.02.04.01:06","DME: fill in the gaps → DME is ____ with the VOR/ILS: selecting the VHF channel tunes the DME too. ____ can supply DME.","DME is frequency-paired with the VOR/ILS: selecting the VHF channel tunes the DME too. TACAN can supply DME."],["062.02.04.02:01","DME: fill in the gaps → Ident: co-located DME transmits its ident about ____ at a higher tone (____) — ____ idents are DME.","Ident: co-located DME transmits its ident about every 30–40 s at a higher tone (1 350 Hz) — 1 in 4 idents are DME."],["062.02.04.01:04","DME: fill in the gaps → ____ = √(slant² − height²), with height in NM = ft ÷ 6 076.","Ground distance = √(slant² − height²), with height in NM = ft ÷ 6 076."],["062.02.04.01:01","DME: what do you remember about this? → Example: FL 360 overhead → the DME reads ≈ 6 …","Example: FL 360 overhead → the DME reads ≈ 6 NM."],["062.02.04.01:04","DME: fill in the gaps → ____: keep the distance constant with turns of ~10° (bearing pointer at the wing-tip ±).","DME arc: keep the distance constant with turns of ~10° (bearing pointer at the wing-tip ±)."],["062.02.04.05:01","DME: fill in the gaps → ____ is a ____ → correct only when flying directly to/from the station at a large distance. It reads low when displaced, and ____.","GS read-out is a rate of change of slant range → correct only when flying directly to/from the station at a large distance. It reads low when displaced, and 0 on an arc."],["062.02.04.03:02","DME: fill in the gaps → ____. Beyond that, the ____ ____ (furthest aircraft) are dropped first.","Capacity ~100 aircraft. Beyond that, the weakest interrogations (furthest aircraft) are dropped first."],["062.02.04.05:01","Exam trap (DME): complete the fact → DME = slant range, ~____aircraft capacity, GS ____on an arc.","DME = slant range, ~100 aircraft capacity, GS 0 on an arc."],["062.02.02.01:02","EASA LO 062.02.02.01.02 → Define the acronym ‘ADF’: automatic direction-finding equipment.","NDB = non-directional radio beacon (the ground part). ADF = automatic direction finder (the airborne part)."],["062.02.02.01:03","EASA LO 062.02.02.01.03 → State that the NDB is the ground part of the system.","NDB = non-directional radio beacon (the ground part). ADF = automatic direction finder (the airborne part). / Fading from ground/sky-wave interference."],["062.02.02.01:04","EASA LO 062.02.02.01.04 → State that the ADF is the airborne part of the system.","NDB = non-directional radio beacon (the ground part). ADF = automatic direction finder (the airborne part). / Airborne equipment: antenna, receiver, indicator."],["062.02.02.01:05","EASA LO 062.02.02.01.05 → State that the NDB operates in the LF and MF frequency bands.","Locator = LF/MF NDB used for the final approach, range 10–25 NM. / Bands: LF/MF, ICAO band 190–1 750 kHz. Broadcast stations also transmit in this band and can be used, but aren't monitored."],["062.02.02.01:06","EASA LO 062.02.02.01.06 → State that the frequency band assigned to aeronautical NDBs according to ICAO Annex 10 is 190–1 750 kHz.","Bands: LF/MF, ICAO band 190–1 750 kHz. Broadcast stations also transmit in this band and can be used, but aren't monitored."],["062.02.02.01:09","EASA LO 062.02.02.01.09 → State that according to ICAO Annex 10, an NDB station has an automatic ground monitoring system.","NDBs have automatic ground monitoring, but there is no failure flag on board → monitor the ident continuously."],["062.02.02.01:15","EASA LO 062.02.02.01.15 → State that in order to identify a N0N/A1A NDB, the BFO circuit of the receiver has to be activated.","Airborne equipment: antenna, receiver, indicator. / N0N/A1A: unmodulated carrier + keyed ident → the BFO must be ON to hear the ident (automatic on modern sets)."],["062.02.02.02:01","EASA LO 062.02.02.02.01 → Name the types of indicators commonly in use: − electronic display; − radio magnetic indicator (RMI); − fixed-card ADF (radio compass);","Fixed-card ADF shows the relative bearing (RB) only."],["062.02.02.02:05","EASA LO 062.02.02.02.05 → Convert the compass bearing into magnetic bearing and true bearing.","Converting a radial to a true bearing: use the variation at the VOR station. / Principle: reference phase (omnidirectional) and variable phase (rotating). The phase difference = magnetic bearing FROM the station (radial)."],["062.02.02.03:02","EASA LO 062.02.02.03.02 → Explain the relationship between power and range.","Range: set by transmitter power (range ∝ √power — 4× power = 2× range). Mainly ground (surface) wave, which follows the curvature. Sky waves at night."],["062.02.02.04:03","EASA LO 062.02.02.04.03 → State that interference from other NDB stations on the same frequency may occur at night due to sky-wave contamination.","Fading from ground/sky-wave interference. / Night (twilight) effect: sky waves + ground wave with phase/polarisation differences → wandering needle; also co-channel interference at night."],["062.02.03.01:02","EASA LO 062.02.03.01.02 → State that the frequency band allocated to VOR according to ICAO Annex 10 is VHF, and the frequencies used are 108.0–117.975 MHz.","VHF 108.0–117.975 MHz. 108.0–111.975 MHz with an odd first decimal = ILS localizer. / Bands: LF/MF, ICAO band 190–1 750 kHz. Broadcast stations also transmit in this band and can be used, but aren't monitored."],["062.02.03.02:02","EASA LO 062.02.03.02.02 → Read off the angular displacement in relation to a preselected radial on a horizontal situation indicator (HSI) or omnibearing indicator (OBI).","Airborne equipment: antenna, receiver, indicator."],["062.02.03.02:03","EASA LO 062.02.03.02.03 → Explain the use of the TO/FROM indicator in order to determine aircraft position relative to the VOR considering also the heading of the aircraft.","The position line is a circle around the station. / Airborne equipment: antenna, receiver, indicator."],["062.02.04.01:01","EASA LO 062.02.04.01.01 → State that DME operates in the UHF band.","Bands: LF/MF, ICAO band 190–1 750 kHz. Broadcast stations also transmit in this band and can be used, but aren't monitored."],["062.02.04.01:03","EASA LO 062.02.04.01.03 → Describe the principle of distance measurement using DME in terms of a timed transmission from the interrogator and reply from the transponder on different frequencies.","UHF (962–1 213 MHz). The aircraft interrogator sends pulse pairs; the ground transponder replies on a frequency ±63 MHz after a 50 µs delay. Distance comes from the time → slant range."],["062.02.04.02:02","EASA LO 062.02.04.02.02 → Calculate ground distance from given slant range and altitude.","Ground distance = √(slant² − height²), with height in NM = ft ÷ 6 076. / UHF (962–1 213 MHz). The aircraft interrogator sends pulse pairs; the ground transponder replies on a frequency ±63 MHz after a 50 µs delay. Distance comes from the time → slant range."],["062.02.04.02:03","EASA LO 062.02.04.02.03 → Describe the use of DME to fly a DME arc in accordance with ICAO Doc 8168 Volume 1.","DME = slant range, ~100 aircraft capacity, GS 0 on an arc."],["062.02.04.02:04","EASA LO 062.02.04.02.04 → State that a DME system may have a ground speed (GS) and time to station read-out combined with the DME read-out.","GS read-out is a rate of change of slant range → correct only when flying directly to/from the station at a large distance. It reads low when displaced, and 0 on an arc. / DME = slant range, ~100 aircraft capacity, GS 0 on an arc."],["062.02.04.03:01","EASA LO 062.02.04.03.01 → Explain why a ground station can generally respond to a maximum of 100 aircraft.","DME = slant range, ~100 aircraft capacity, GS 0 on an arc."]],"36":[["061.04.02.01:01",": what do you remember about this? → Chart projections — full detail on the #77 page; this is the core …","Chart projections — full detail on the #77 page; this is the core summary."],["061.04.02.01:01","Methods of projection: fill in the gaps → ____: the projection plane touches the earth at one point (usually a ____) → ____ (light source at the opposite pole).","Azimuthal (plane): the projection plane touches the earth at one point (usually a pole) → polar stereographic (light source at the opposite pole)."],["061.04.02.01:01","Methods of projection: fill in the gaps → ____: a cylinder touches the ____ → ____.","Cylindrical: a cylinder touches the equator → direct Mercator."],["061.04.02.04:06","Methods of projection: fill in the gaps → ____: a cone touches a ____ (or cuts two ____) → Lambert conformal conic.","Conical: a cone touches a parallel of origin (or cuts two standard parallels) → Lambert conformal conic."],["061.04.02.02:02","Polar stereographic: fill in the gaps → ____ (orthomorphic). Meridians = ____ ____; parallels = concentric circles.","Conformal (orthomorphic). Meridians = straight lines radiating from the pole; parallels = concentric circles."],["061.04.02.02:01","Polar stereographic: fill in the gaps → ____ (n = ____).","Convergence = change of longitude (n = 1)."],["061.04.02.04:03","Polar stereographic: fill in the gaps → Scale is correct at the pole and ____ (≈ 2 % at 70°).","Scale is correct at the pole and expands away from it (≈ 2 % at 70°)."],["061.04.02.02:02","Polar stereographic: fill in the gaps → A ____ (near the pole); a rhumb line is curved towards the pole (it would spiral into the pole).","A great circle ≈ straight line (near the pole); a rhumb line is curved towards the pole (it would spiral into the pole)."],["061.04.02.02:02","Polar stereographic: fill in the gaps → ____ = ____ (sin 90° = 1).","Track change along a straight line = ch. long × 1 (sin 90° = 1)."],["061.04.02.02:02","Polar stereographic: fill in the gaps → Example: a straight line from 070°W to 010°E changes its track by ____.","Example: a straight line from 070°W to 010°E changes its track by 80°."],["061.04.02.03:01","Direct Mercator: fill in the gaps → ____. Meridians = ____ ____; parallels = straight lines further apart towards the poles.","Conformal. Meridians = parallel, equally spaced straight lines; parallels = straight lines further apart towards the poles."],["061.04.02.03:01","Direct Mercator: fill in the gaps → ____ on the chart.","Convergence = 0 on the chart."],["061.04.02.04:07","Direct Mercator: fill in the gaps → ____; a great circle is a curve ____ ____.","Rhumb line = straight line; a great circle is a curve concave to the equator."],["061.04.02.03:02","Direct Mercator: fill in the gaps → Scale is correct at the equator and ____.","Scale is correct at the equator and expands with sec lat."],["061.04.02.03:02","Direct Mercator: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ ____.","Scale formula: scale at lat₂ = scale at lat₁ × (cos lat₁ / cos lat₂)."],["061.04.02.03:02","Direct Mercator: fill in the gaps → Example: 1 : 1 000 000 at the equator → at 60° the scale is ____ ____ (cos 60 = 0.5).","Example: 1 : 1 000 000 at the equator → at 60° the scale is 1 : 500 000 (cos 60 = 0.5)."],["061.04.02.03:03","Direct Mercator: fill in the gaps → ____: a given chart length represents ____ (earth distance ∝ ____ lat).","Chart length ↔ earth distance: a given chart length represents less earth distance at high latitudes (earth distance ∝ cos lat)."],["061.04.02.03:01","Direct Mercator: what do you remember about this? → Example: 10 cm = 100 NM at 0° → the same 10 cm = 50 NM at …","Example: 10 cm = 100 NM at 0° → the same 10 cm = 50 NM at 60°."],["061.04.02.04:01","Lambert conformal conic: fill in the gaps → ____. Meridians = ____ at the apex (above the pole); parallels = arcs of concentric circles.","Conformal. Meridians = straight lines converging at the apex (above the pole); parallels = arcs of concentric circles."],["061.04.02.04:06","Lambert conformal conic: fill in the gaps → ____, where ____ ____.","Convergence = ch. long × n, where n = constant of cone = sin(parallel of origin)."],["061.04.02.04:07","Lambert conformal conic: fill in the gaps → ____ (concave to the parallel of origin); a rhumb line is curved concave to the pole.","Great circle ≈ straight line (concave to the parallel of origin); a rhumb line is curved concave to the pole."],["061.04.02.04:03","Lambert conformal conic: fill in the gaps → ____.","Correct at the two standard parallels."],["061.04.02.04:03","Lambert conformal conic: fill in the gaps → ____ (smaller) between them, and ____ outside them — scale increases away from the parallel of origin.","Contracted (smaller) between them, and expanded outside them — scale increases away from the parallel of origin."],["061.04.02.04:03","Lambert conformal conic: fill in the gaps → Within the standard parallels, the scale differs ____ from the stated scale.","Within the standard parallels, the scale differs by less than 1 % from the stated scale."],["061.04.02.04:05","Lambert conformal conic: fill in the gaps → ____ ≈ the mean of the two standard parallels.","Parallel of origin ≈ the mean of the two standard parallels."],["061.04.02.04:01","Lambert conformal conic: what do you remember about this? → Example: standard parallels 37° and 65° → PoO ≈ 51°, n = sin 51 ≈ …","Example: standard parallels 37° and 65° → PoO ≈ 51°, n = sin 51 ≈ 0.777."],["061.04.02.04:02","Lambert conformal conic: fill in the gaps → ____ = ch. long × n.","Straight-line track change = ch. long × n."],["061.04.02.04:02","Lambert conformal conic: fill in the gaps → Example: n = 0.8, ch. long 20° → track changes by ____.","Example: n = 0.8, ch. long 20° → track changes by 16°."],["061.04.02.04:04","Lambert conformal conic: fill in the gaps → ____:","Initial/final/rhumb-line tracks:"],["061.04.02.04:04","Lambert conformal conic: fill in the gaps → ____ (RL) = the mean of the initial and final GC tracks.","Rhumb line (RL) = the mean of the initial and final GC tracks."],["061.04.02.04:05","Lambert conformal conic: fill in the gaps → ____ = ½ × earth convergency = ½ × ch. long × sin(mean lat).","Conversion angle = ½ × earth convergency = ½ × ch. long × sin(mean lat)."],["061.04.02.04:01","Lambert conformal conic: fill in the gaps → The GC lies ____ of the RL.","The GC lies on the pole side of the RL."],["061.04.02.04:04","Lambert conformal conic: what do you remember about this? → Example: GC initial track 060°, ch. long 10° at mean lat 50° …","Example: GC initial track 060°, ch. long 10° at mean lat 50° (convergency 7.66°) → RL 063.8°, final GC 067.7° (NH, going east)."],["061.04.02.04:06","Lambert conformal conic: fill in the gaps → ____: n = chart convergence ÷ ch. long → ____.","Parallel of origin from data: n = chart convergence ÷ ch. long → PoO = arcsin n."],["061.04.02.04:07","Lambert conformal conic: fill in the gaps → ____ = (GC track 2 − GC track 1) ÷ n.","Difference of longitude = (GC track 2 − GC track 1) ÷ n."],["061.04.02.04:02","Lambert conformal conic: fill in the gaps → Example: track changes from 045° to 061°, n = 0.8 → ch. long = ____.","Example: track changes from 045° to 061°, n = 0.8 → ch. long = 20°."],["061.04.02.04:02","Exam trap (Lambert conformal conic): complete the fact → Mercator: rhumb line straight, scale × sec lat, convergence ____","Mercator: rhumb line straight, scale × sec lat, convergence 0."],["061.04.02.02:01","Exam trap (Lambert conformal conic): complete the fact → Polar stereographic: n = ____","Polar stereographic: n = 1."],["061.04.02.04:01","EASA LO 061.04.02.04.01 → State the properties of a Lambert projection.","Conical: a cone touches a parallel of origin (or cuts two standard parallels) → Lambert conformal conic."]],"37":[["gen",": what do you remember about this? → This stage has no separate EASA LOs — it is a practical exercise (CAP …","This stage has no separate EASA LOs — it is a practical exercise (CAP 697 / FPM ch. 4a). It applies the fuel and nav-log LOs from #22/#25/#45."],["gen","Company flight plan (CFP) London – Munich: fill in the gaps → ____: produce an operational flight plan for an MRJT (CAP 697 data) — route, levels, times, fuel.","Objective: produce an operational flight plan for an MRJT (CAP 697 data) — route, levels, times, fuel."],["gen","Company flight plan (CFP) London – Munich: fill in the gaps → ____: 1. ____: from the airway chart (E(HI)) — SID exit, airways, significant points, STAR entry. Measure ____ (NM) and magnetic tracks. 2. Level selection: IFR semicircular rule (magnetic track 000–179° → odd FL, 180–359° → even FL), route availability, optimum level for the mass. 3.","Steps: 1. Route: from the airway chart (E(HI)) — SID exit, airways, significant points, STAR entry. Measure distance per leg (NM) and magnetic tracks. 2. Level selection: IFR semicircular rule (magnetic track 000–179° → odd FL, 180–359° → even FL), route availability, optimum level for the mass. 3."],["gen","Company flight plan (CFP) London – Munich: fill in the gaps → ____ per leg from forecast charts → ____ from Mach and temperature; ____ from the wind component; leg times. 4. Climb/descent: from the MRJT tables — time, fuel, distance (NAM) from brake release/TOC and TOD. Correct the NAM to NGM for the wind. 5.","Wind/temperature per leg from forecast charts → TAS from Mach and temperature; GS from the wind component; leg times. 4. Climb/descent: from the MRJT tables — time, fuel, distance (NAM) from brake release/TOC and TOD. Correct the NAM to NGM for the wind. 5."],["gen","Company flight plan (CFP) London – Munich: fill in the gaps → ____: fuel = time × fuel flow (tables for mass, FL, ISA deviation) — or the ____ ____. 6. Fuel summary: taxi + trip + contingency (5 %) + alternate + final reserve (30 min @ 1 500 ft) + extra = block fuel; check against MTOM/MLM/tank capacity. 7.","Cruise: fuel = time × fuel flow (tables for mass, FL, ISA deviation) — or the simplified/integrated range tables. 6. Fuel summary: taxi + trip + contingency (5 %) + alternate + final reserve (30 min @ 1 500 ft) + extra = block fuel; check against MTOM/MLM/tank capacity. 7."],["gen","Company flight plan (CFP) London – Munich: fill in the gaps → ____: fill in ATA, fuel remaining, and compare with the plan (see #46 monitoring).","In-flight: fill in ATA, fuel remaining, and compare with the plan (see #46 monitoring)."],["gen","NAM ↔ NGM formula: fill in the gaps → ____ = the distance flown ____ ____. NGM (ground nautical miles) = the distance over the ground.","NAM (nautical air miles) = the distance flown through the air mass. NGM (ground nautical miles) = the distance over the ground."],["gen","NAM ↔ NGM formula: fill in the gaps → ____ and ____.","NAM = NGM × TAS / GS and NGM = NAM × GS / TAS."],["gen","NAM ↔ NGM formula: fill in the gaps → ____ (GS < TAS) → NAM > NGM → more fuel.","Headwind (GS < TAS) → NAM > NGM → more fuel."],["gen","NAM ↔ NGM formula: fill in the gaps → ____ give ____ (specific air range). ____ NGM ÷ GS = NAM ÷ TAS.","Cruise tables give fuel per NAM (specific air range). Time = NGM ÷ GS = NAM ÷ TAS."],["gen","NAM ↔ NGM formula: fill in the gaps → ____: leg 300 NGM, TAS 460 kt, headwind 60 kt → GS 400 → ____ → time = 345/460 = 0.75 h = ____.","Example: leg 300 NGM, TAS 460 kt, headwind 60 kt → GS 400 → NAM = 300 × 460/400 = 345 NAM → time = 345/460 = 0.75 h = 45 min."],["gen","NAM ↔ NGM formula: fill in the gaps → ____ = NAM/kg; ____ = NGM/kg = SR × GS/TAS.","Specific range (SR) = NAM/kg; specific ground range (SGR) = NGM/kg = SR × GS/TAS."],["gen","NAM ↔ NGM formula: fill in the gaps → ____ give time/fuel/NAM from brake release to the level. Convert the NAM to ground distance with the average climb wind (average TAS ≈ TAS at 2/3 of the climb altitude).","Climb tables give time/fuel/NAM from brake release to the level. Convert the NAM to ground distance with the average climb wind (average TAS ≈ TAS at 2/3 of the climb altitude)."]],"38":[["081.01.12.01:01","Ice & contamination: fill in the gaps → ____: leading edges (wings, tail, struts), ____ ____, propellers/spinners, pitot/static ports and AoA vanes, antennas, windscreen. Thin surfaces (tail) collect ice faster than the thick wing.","Where ice builds up: leading edges (wings, tail, struts), engine intakes, propellers/spinners, pitot/static ports and AoA vanes, antennas, windscreen. Thin surfaces (tail) collect ice faster than the thick wing."],["081.01.12.01:01","Ice & contamination: fill in the gaps → ________ ____ (even frost with sandpaper-like roughness can reduce it by ~30 %).","CLmax falls (even frost with sandpaper-like roughness can reduce it by ~30 %)."],["081.01.12.01:01","Ice & contamination: fill in the gaps → ____ (parasite and form drag) and weight increases.","Drag rises (parasite and form drag) and weight increases."],["081.03.01.01:10","Ice & contamination: fill in the gaps → ________ ____ → the stall may occur before the stall warning (it is calibrated for a clean wing).","αcrit falls → the stall may occur before the stall warning (it is calibrated for a clean wing)."],["081.01.12.01:01","Ice & contamination: fill in the gaps → ____ are reduced (tail stall, aileron snatch, control hinge moments).","Stability and control are reduced (tail stall, aileron snatch, control hinge moments)."],["081.03.01.05:12","Ice & contamination: fill in the gaps → ____: a contaminated wing may stall at normal VR and rotation attitude → poor climb, wing drop just after lift-off (ground effect masks it until lift-off). ____ (clean-wing concept).","At take-off: a contaminated wing may stall at normal VR and rotation attitude → poor climb, wing drop just after lift-off (ground effect masks it until lift-off). The wing must be clean (clean-wing concept)."],["081.01.12.02:01","Ice & contamination: fill in the gaps → ____: dents, deformation, repairs, poorly fitted panels, gaps, sealant and paint layers → ____ ____ (higher fuel flow) and possibly asymmetric stall.","Ageing aircraft: dents, deformation, repairs, poorly fitted panels, gaps, sealant and paint layers → higher drag, worse performance (higher fuel flow) and possibly asymmetric stall."],["081.03.01.01:01","Ice & contamination: fill in the gaps → Roughness makes the boundary layer ____ and ____ it (more skin friction, earlier separation).","Roughness makes the boundary layer turbulent earlier and thickens it (more skin friction, earlier separation)."],["081.03.01.01:01","Boundary layer & flow separation: fill in the gaps → ____: a thin layer near the surface where the speed increases from ____ to the free-stream value (viscosity).","Boundary layer: a thin layer near the surface where the speed increases from 0 at the surface to the free-stream value (viscosity)."],["081.03.01.01:06","Boundary layer & flow separation: fill in the gaps → ____: ____, smooth parallel layers, ____. It has little kinetic energy near the wall → it separates easily.","Laminar layer: thin, smooth parallel layers, low skin friction. It has little kinetic energy near the wall → it separates easily."],["081.03.01.01:01","Boundary layer & flow separation: fill in the gaps → ____: ____, mixing, ____, but more energy near the wall → it stays attached longer.","Turbulent layer: thicker, mixing, higher skin friction, but more energy near the wall → it stays attached longer."],["081.03.01.01:05","Boundary layer & flow separation: fill in the gaps → ____: where the laminar layer becomes turbulent (moves ____ with increasing speed/Re, roughness, α).","Transition point: where the laminar layer becomes turbulent (moves forward with increasing speed/Re, roughness, α)."],["081.03.01.01:01","Boundary layer & flow separation: fill in the gaps → As α increases, the ____ on the aft upper surface grows (the air must slow from low to higher pressure) → the air near the surface ____ → ____ moves forward with increasing α.","As α increases, the adverse pressure gradient on the aft upper surface grows (the air must slow from low to higher pressure) → the air near the surface decelerates, stops, reverses → separation point moves forward with increasing α."],["081.03.01.01:01","Boundary layer & flow separation: fill in the gaps → ________ = the angle of attack at ____Lmax; beyond it lift decreases (stall).","αcrit = the angle of attack at CLmax; beyond it lift decreases (stall)."],["081.03.01.01:09","Boundary layer & flow separation: fill in the gaps → ____: speed decreases, CP moves forward (up to αcrit), the separated area grows → ____, ____ become less effective (mushy), then the stall (nose drops, possibly a wing drop).","Straight & level, increasing α: speed decreases, CP moves forward (up to αcrit), the separated area grows → buffet, controls become less effective (mushy), then the stall (nose drops, possibly a wing drop)."],["081.03.01.01:11","Boundary layer & flow separation: fill in the gaps → ____ = separated turbulent wake hitting the tail/structure.","Natural (pre-stall) buffet = separated turbulent wake hitting the tail/structure."],["081.03.01.01:13","Boundary layer & flow separation: fill in the gaps → ____: the root stalls first → nose-down pitch (CP moves aft), usually benign with the ailerons still partly effective.","Post-stall (straight wing): the root stalls first → nose-down pitch (CP moves aft), usually benign with the ailerons still partly effective."],["081.03.01.01:14","Boundary layer & flow separation: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ down-deflection increases the local α → ____ → use the rudder to pick up the wing, and reduce α first.","Using the controls close to the stall: aileron down-deflection increases the local α → wing drop/incipient spin → use the rudder to pick up the wing, and reduce α first."],["081.03.01.01:15","Boundary layer & flow separation: fill in the gaps → ____: buffet so severe that it is a strong ____ ____ → accepted as the stall limit in certification when no clear nose drop occurs.","Deterrent buffet: buffet so severe that it is a strong deterrent to further α increase → accepted as the stall limit in certification when no clear nose drop occurs."],["081.03.01.02:01","Stall speed: fill in the gaps → V____: stall speed / min steady flight speed in the landing configuration. V____: in a specified configuration. VSR: reference stall speed (CS-25, ≈ VS1g). V____: the 1-g stall speed.","VS0: stall speed / min steady flight speed in the landing configuration. VS1: in a specified configuration. VSR: reference stall speed (CS-25, ≈ VS1g). VS1g: the 1-g stall speed."],["081.03.01.02:01","Stall speed: fill in the gaps → ________ ____Lmax)).","VS1g = √(2W / (ρ S CLmax))."],["081.03.01.02:01","Stall speed: what do you remember about this? → Example: CLmax 1.5 → 2.0 → VS × √(1.5/2) = × …","Example: CLmax 1.5 → 2.0 → VS × √(1.5/2) = × 0.87."],["081.03.01.06:04","Stall speed: fill in the gaps → ____ → higher VS (more tail download).","Forward CG → higher VS (more tail download)."],["081.03.01.01:07","Stall speed: fill in the gaps → ____ (high nose, thrust carries part of the weight) and ____ (prop wash over the wing) → lower VS (power-on).","Thrust component (high nose, thrust carries part of the weight) and slipstream (prop wash over the wing) → lower VS (power-on)."],["081.03.01.02:03","Stall speed: fill in the gaps → ____ → higher VS.","Higher wing loading/mass → higher VS."],["081.03.01.02:03","Stall speed: fill in the gaps → ____ → lower CLmax → higher VS.","Sweep → lower CLmax → higher VS."],["081.03.01.02:03","Stall speed: fill in the gaps → ____: IAS roughly constant; at high altitude/Mach, compressibility lowers CLmax → ____.","Altitude: IAS roughly constant; at high altitude/Mach, compressibility lowers CLmax → IAS stall rises."],["081.03.01.05:04","Stall speed: fill in the gaps → ____ lift must also provide the centripetal force → ____ ____.","In a turn lift must also provide the centripetal force → n = 1/cos Ø."],["081.03.01.02:06","Stall speed: fill in the gaps → ____ → n > 1; ____ → n < 1.","Pull-up → n > 1; push-over → n < 1."],["081.03.01.02:08","Stall speed: fill in the gaps → ____: a stall at n > 1, at speeds above VS1g (the term \"high-speed stall\" is not used).","Accelerated stall: a stall at n > 1, at speeds above VS1g (the term \"high-speed stall\" is not used)."],["081.03.01.02:10","Stall speed: what do you remember about this? → 60° bank → n = 2 → VS × …","60° bank → n = 2 → VS × 1.41."],["081.03.01.02:11","Stall speed: what do you remember about this? → Mass +21 % → VS × √1.21 = × …","Mass +21 % → VS × √1.21 = × 1.10."],["081.03.01.03:01","Spanwise stall: fill in the gaps → ____:","Stall pattern by planform:"],["081.03.01.04:01","Spanwise stall: fill in the gaps → ____: stalls ____ across the span (no warning).","Elliptical: stalls evenly across the span (no warning)."],["081.03.01.03:01","Spanwise stall: fill in the gaps → ____: stalls at the ____ (good — ailerons stay effective, buffet on the tail).","Rectangular: stalls at the root first (good — ailerons stay effective, buffet on the tail)."],["081.03.01.03:01","Spanwise stall: fill in the gaps → ____: stalls mid-span.","Moderate taper: stalls mid-span."],["081.03.01.05:04","Spanwise stall: fill in the gaps → ____: stalls at the ____ → wing drop, loss of aileron, ____ (swept wing).","High taper / sweepback / delta: stalls at the tip first → wing drop, loss of aileron, pitch-up (swept wing)."],["081.03.01.03:02","Spanwise stall: fill in the gaps → ____ delays the tip stall:","Washout delays the tip stall:"],["081.03.01.03:02","Spanwise stall: fill in the gaps → ____: lower angle of incidence at the tip.","Geometric twist: lower angle of incidence at the tip."],["081.03.01.03:02","Spanwise stall: fill in the gaps → ____: a different aerofoil at the tip.","Aerodynamic twist: a different aerofoil at the tip."],["081.03.01.03:04","Spanwise stall: fill in the gaps → ____, ____, ____ → vortices block the spanwise (outward) flow of the boundary layer.","Fences, vortilons, saw-tooth leading edges → vortices block the spanwise (outward) flow of the boundary layer."],["081.03.01.01:01","Spanwise stall: fill in the gaps → ____ energise the boundary layer.","Vortex generators energise the boundary layer."],["081.03.01.03:04","Spanwise stall: fill in the gaps → ____ create a vortex over the wing at high α.","Nacelle strakes create a vortex over the wing at high α."],["081.03.01.04:03","Stall warning: fill in the gaps → ____: the pilot must recognise the approach to the stall in time. Required ____: natural buffet or artificial warning ≥ ____ above the stall (CS-23/25).","Why it's needed: the pilot must recognise the approach to the stall in time. Required margin: natural buffet or artificial warning ≥ 5 kt or 5 % above the stall (CS-23/25)."],["081.03.01.04:02","Stall warning: fill in the gaps → ____ warning is used if the buffet is sufficient; ____ warning (horn, stick shaker) when the natural warning is insufficient (swept wings, high-lift devices).","Aerodynamic warning is used if the buffet is sufficient; artificial warning (horn, stick shaker) when the natural warning is insufficient (swept wings, high-lift devices)."],["081.03.01.04:03","Stall warning: fill in the gaps → ____ for take-off/landing: V2 ≥ 1.13 VSR, VREF ≥ 1.23 VSR (CS-25); 1.2/1.3 VS (CS-23). This protects against gusts, turns and errors.","Speed margins for take-off/landing: V2 ≥ 1.13 VSR, VREF ≥ 1.23 VSR (CS-25); 1.2/1.3 VS (CS-23). This protects against gusts, turns and errors."],["081.03.01.04:04","Stall warning: fill in the gaps → ____ = the natural warning. A ____ is a sharp wedge on the ____ → forces the root to stall first.","Buffet = the natural warning. A stall strip is a sharp wedge on the inboard leading edge → forces the root to stall first."],["081.03.01.04:01","Stall warning: what do you remember about this? → Warning devices: flapper switch (reed), AoA vane, pressure …","Warning devices: flapper switch (reed), AoA vane, pressure port."],["081.03.01.04:05","Stall warning: fill in the gaps → After the ____: reduce α (release back pressure/lower the nose), apply power, level the wings.","After the warning: reduce α (release back pressure/lower the nose), apply power, level the wings."],["081.03.01.04:05","Stall warning: fill in the gaps → After the ____: ____ (push), wings level, power, then recover without a secondary stall.","After the stall: reduce α (push), wings level, power, then recover without a secondary stall."],["081.03.01.04:05","Stall warning: fill in the gaps → After a ____ actuation: ____; reduce α, then recover.","After a stick-pusher actuation: do not fight it; reduce α, then recover."],["081.03.01.05:02","Special stall phenomena: fill in the gaps → ____:","Power-on vs power-off stall:"],["081.03.01.05:02","Special stall phenomena: fill in the gaps → Power-on: ________, higher nose attitude, more violent wing drop (____).","Power-on: lower VS, higher nose attitude, more violent wing drop (torque/slipstream)."],["081.03.01.05:03","Special stall phenomena: fill in the gaps → ____: the ____ tends to stall first (higher α).","Climbing turn: the outer wing tends to stall first (higher α)."],["081.03.01.05:03","Special stall phenomena: fill in the gaps → ____: the ____ stalls first.","Descending turn: the inner wing stalls first."],["081.03.01.02:10","Special stall phenomena: what do you remember about this? → Recover with α reduction and coordinated wings …","Recover with α reduction and coordinated wings level."],["081.03.01.05:04","Special stall phenomena: fill in the gaps → ____: the tip stalls first → the CP moves ____ → nose-up.","Swept wing: the tip stalls first → the CP moves forward → nose-up."],["081.03.01.05:04","Special stall phenomena: fill in the gaps → ____: the wing wake blanks the tail at high α.","T-tail: the wing wake blanks the tail at high α."],["081.03.01.05:05","Special stall phenomena: fill in the gaps → ____: at very high α the T-tail sits in the separated wake → ____ → the stall may be unrecoverable → ____ required.","Deep (super) stall: at very high α the T-tail sits in the separated wake → no pitch-down authority → the stall may be unrecoverable → stick pusher required."],["081.03.01.05:05","Special stall phenomena: fill in the gaps → ____: pushes the nose down at a set α ____ the dangerous region, so the pilot cannot enter a deep stall.","Stick-pusher philosophy: pushes the nose down at a set α before the dangerous region, so the pilot cannot enter a deep stall."],["081.03.01.04:01","Special stall phenomena: fill in the gaps → ____ may be caused by ice (αcrit lower than the warning setting), a failed sensor, or a wrong configuration → stall without warning.","No stall warning may be caused by ice (αcrit lower than the warning setting), a failed sensor, or a wrong configuration → stall without warning."],["081.03.01.05:08","Special stall phenomena: fill in the gaps → ____ from ice on the tail: happens when ____ (more downwash → higher negative α at the tail).","Tailplane (negative) stall from ice on the tail: happens when extending flaps (more downwash → higher negative α at the tail)."],["081.03.01.05:02","Special stall phenomena: fill in the gaps → Signs: control buffet, light/nervous elevator, sudden ____ ____.","Signs: control buffet, light/nervous elevator, sudden nose-down pitch."],["081.03.01.05:02","Special stall phenomena: fill in the gaps → Recovery: ____ to the previous setting, ____, reduce power (opposite to a wing stall).","Recovery: retract the flaps to the previous setting, pull, reduce power (opposite to a wing stall)."],["081.03.01.05:02","Special stall phenomena: fill in the gaps → In-flight icing is expected in visible moisture at ____ ____ (most severe ____); supercooled large drops (SLD) and freezing rain are the worst.","In-flight icing is expected in visible moisture at OAT +10 °C to −40 °C (most severe 0 to −15 °C); supercooled large drops (SLD) and freezing rain are the worst."],["081.03.01.05:04","Special stall phenomena: fill in the gaps → ____ them raises the wing CLmax but increases tail loading (tail stall).","Extending them raises the wing CLmax but increases tail loading (tail stall)."],["081.03.01.05:10","Special stall phenomena: fill in the gaps → ____ them raises the wing VS.","Retracting them raises the wing VS."],["081.03.01.05:12","Special stall phenomena: fill in the gaps → ____: higher VS, lower αcrit.","Contaminated wing: higher VS, lower αcrit."],["081.03.01.05:14","Special stall phenomena: fill in the gaps → Frost, snow or ice on a parked aircraft → ____ (de-icing).","Frost, snow or ice on a parked aircraft → must be removed (de-icing)."],["081.03.01.05:02","Special stall phenomena: what do you remember about this? → Contamination picked up during taxi (slush) affects the gear and …","Contamination picked up during taxi (slush) affects the gear and flaps."],["081.03.01.05:12","Special stall phenomena: what do you remember about this? → Take-off with a contaminated wing gives reduced lift, higher drag, an …","Take-off with a contaminated wing gives reduced lift, higher drag, an early stall, and a roll-off."],["081.03.01.05:14","Special stall phenomena: fill in the gaps → ____ = the time the anti-icing fluid protects the surfaces. After it expires, the fluid can fail and contamination forms → re-inspect or re-treat.","Holdover time (HOT) = the time the anti-icing fluid protects the surfaces. After it expires, the fluid can fail and contamination forms → re-inspect or re-treat."],["081.03.01.05:15","Special stall phenomena: fill in the gaps → ____: water film roughness and mass → ________ lower, drag higher, higher VS → mitigate by adding speed, avoiding the rain, and early go-around.","Heavy tropical rain: water film roughness and mass → CLmax lower, drag higher, higher VS → mitigate by adding speed, avoiding the rain, and early go-around."],["081.03.01.06:01","Spin: fill in the gaps → ____ it by not stalling with ____ (keep the ball centred) and by recovering early from a wing drop.","Avoid it by not stalling with yaw (keep the ball centred) and by recovering early from a wing drop."],["081.03.01.05:02","Spin: fill in the gaps → ____: stall + ____ (uncoordinated flight, aileron at high α, asymmetric power) → ____.","Causes: stall + yaw/sideslip (uncoordinated flight, aileron at high α, asymmetric power) → autorotation."],["081.03.01.06:03","Spin: fill in the gaps → ____ spin: first ~2 turns, unstable.","Incipient spin: first ~2 turns, unstable."],["081.03.01.06:03","Spin: fill in the gaps → ____ spin: steady rotation, steady rate of descent at high α.","Developed spin: steady rotation, steady rate of descent at high α."],["081.03.01.06:01","Spin: fill in the gaps → ____: throttle idle, ailerons neutral, ____ ____, then stick forward until the rotation stops, rudder neutral, then pull out gently.","Recovery (standard): throttle idle, ailerons neutral, full opposite rudder, then stick forward until the rotation stops, rudder neutral, then pull out gently."],["081.03.01.06:04","Spin: fill in the gaps → ____ → ____ spin, higher rotation rate, ____ impossible recovery.","Aft CG → flatter spin, higher rotation rate, harder or impossible recovery."],["081.03.01.06:04","Spin: what do you remember about this? → Forward CG → steeper, easier …","Forward CG → steeper, easier recovery."],["081.01.12.01:01","EASA LO 081.01.12.01.01 → Describe the locations on an aeroplane where ice build-up will occur during flight.","αcrit falls → the stall may occur before the stall warning (it is calibrated for a clean wing)."],["081.01.12.01:03","EASA LO 081.01.12.01.03 → Explain the aerodynamic effects of icing during take-off.","Take-off with a contaminated wing gives reduced lift, higher drag, an early stall, and a roll-off. / Frost, snow or ice on a parked aircraft → must be removed (de-icing)."],["081.01.12.02:02","EASA LO 081.01.12.02.02 → Explain the effect on boundary layer condition of an ageing aeroplane.","Ageing aircraft: dents, deformation, repairs, poorly fitted panels, gaps, sealant and paint layers → higher drag, worse performance (higher fuel flow) and possibly asymmetric stall."],["081.03.01.01:02","EASA LO 081.03.01.01.02 → Describe the thickness of a typical laminar and turbulent boundary layer.","Transition point: where the laminar layer becomes turbulent (moves forward with increasing speed/Re, roughness, α). / Natural (pre-stall) buffet = separated turbulent wake hitting the tail/structure."],["081.03.01.01:03","EASA LO 081.03.01.01.03 → Describe the properties, advantages and disadvantages of the laminar boundary layer.","Transition point: where the laminar layer becomes turbulent (moves forward with increasing speed/Re, roughness, α). / Vortex generators energise the boundary layer."],["081.03.01.01:04","EASA LO 081.03.01.01.04 → Describe the properties, advantages and disadvantages of the turbulent boundary layer.","Transition point: where the laminar layer becomes turbulent (moves forward with increasing speed/Re, roughness, α). / Natural (pre-stall) buffet = separated turbulent wake hitting the tail/structure."],["081.03.01.01:08","EASA LO 081.03.01.01.08 → Define the ‘separation point’ and describe its location as a function of α.","Transition point: where the laminar layer becomes turbulent (moves forward with increasing speed/Re, roughness, α). / As α increases, the adverse pressure gradient on the aft upper surface grows (the air must slow from low to higher pressure) → the air near the surface decelerates, stops, reverses → separation point moves forward with increasing α."],["081.03.01.01:12","EASA LO 081.03.01.01.12 → Describe the effectiveness of the flight controls in a pre-stall condition.","Natural (pre-stall) buffet = separated turbulent wake hitting the tail/structure."],["081.03.01.01:16","EASA LO 081.03.01.01.16 → Explain the occurrence of the deterrent buffet and why this phenomenon is considered to be a stall limit.","Deterrent buffet: buffet so severe that it is a strong deterrent to further α increase → accepted as the stall limit in certification when no clear nose drop occurs."],["081.03.01.02:02","EASA LO 081.03.01.02.02 → Solve VS1G from the lift formula given varying CL.","VS1g = √(2W / (ρ S CLmax))."],["081.03.01.02:05","EASA LO 081.03.01.02.05 → Explain why the load factor increases in a turn.","Descending turn: the inner wing stalls first."],["081.03.01.05:06","EASA LO 081.03.01.05.06 → Describe the philosophy behind the stick-pusher system.","Stick-pusher philosophy: pushes the nose down at a set α before the dangerous region, so the pilot cannot enter a deep stall. / After a stick-pusher actuation: do not fight it; reduce α, then recover."],["081.03.01.05:13","EASA LO 081.03.01.05.13 → Explain the hazards associated with airframe contamination when parked and during ground operations in winter conditions, and the aerodynamic effects when attempting a take-off.","At take-off: a contaminated wing may stall at normal VR and rotation attitude → poor climb, wing drop just after lift-off (ground effect masks it until lift-off). The wing must be clean (clean-wing concept). / Power-on vs power-off stall:"],["081.03.01.06:02","EASA LO 081.03.01.06.02 → List the factors that cause a spin to develop.","Incipient spin: first ~2 turns, unstable. / Aft CG → flatter spin, higher rotation rate, harder or impossible recovery."]],"39":[["062.02.05.04:01","ILS categories: fill in the gaps → ILS approaches use ____ (ICAO Annex 10). ____ categories (DH/RVR):","ILS approaches use facility performance categories (ICAO Annex 10). Operational categories (DH/RVR):"],["062.02.05.04:01","ILS categories – CAT I: what are the key facts?","DH: ≥ 200 ft · RVR: ≥ 550 m (or visibility 800 m)"],["062.02.05.04:04","ILS categories – CAT II: what are the key facts?","DH: < 200 ft to ≥ 100 ft · RVR: ≥ 300 m"],["062.02.05.04:01","ILS categories – CAT IIIA: what are the key facts?","DH: < 100 ft or no DH · RVR: ≥ 175 m (EU ops 200 m)"],["062.02.05.04:01","ILS categories – CAT IIIB: what are the key facts?","DH: < 50 ft or no DH · RVR: < 175 m to ≥ 50 m (EU 75 m)"],["062.02.05.04:01","ILS categories – CAT IIIC: what are the key facts?","DH: no DH · RVR: no RVR limit (not operated)"],["062.02.05.04:03","ILS categories: fill in the gaps → ____: guidance is provided from the coverage limit ____ ____ (for autoland and rollout).","CAT III facility: guidance is provided from the coverage limit to and along the runway surface (for autoland and rollout)."],["062.02.05.04:01","ILS categories: fill in the gaps → ____ from CAT I to III because there is less time and less visual reference to correct deviations at lower DH. Stricter monitoring, redundancy and integrity are needed (the signal must not fail near the ground).","Accuracy requirements increase from CAT I to III because there is less time and less visual reference to correct deviations at lower DH. Stricter monitoring, redundancy and integrity are needed (the signal must not fail near the ground)."],["062.02.05.04:05","ILS categories: fill in the gaps → ____ (ICAO Doc 8168): ____ ____.","Established on the ILS (ICAO Doc 8168): localizer within half full-scale deflection."],["062.02.05.04:06","ILS categories: fill in the gaps → ____: deviation ____ or ____ on the GP → immediate go-around (obstacle clearance no longer guaranteed).","Go-around: deviation > half-scale on the LOC or > half-dot on the GP → immediate go-around (obstacle clearance no longer guaranteed)."],["062.02.05.04:07","ILS categories: fill in the gaps → ____: deviations of the LOC/GP from the nominal path caused by reflections. They are measured by ____ and must stay within limits.","Beam bends: deviations of the LOC/GP from the nominal path caused by reflections. They are measured by flight test and must stay within limits."],["062.02.05.04:08","ILS categories: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ from objects (buildings, aircraft, vehicles, terrain) within the coverage area → distorted guidance.","Multipath interference: reflections from objects (buildings, aircraft, vehicles, terrain) within the coverage area → distorted guidance."],["062.02.05.05:01","Critical & sensitive areas: fill in the gaps → ____: a defined area around the ____ ____ where vehicles and aircraft are excluded during all ILS operations (their presence would distort the signal).","ILS-critical area: a defined area around the LOC and GP antennas where vehicles and aircraft are excluded during all ILS operations (their presence would distort the signal)."],["062.02.05.05:02","Critical & sensitive areas: fill in the gaps → ____: the area ____ where parking/movement is ____ during low-visibility (CAT II/III) operations to prevent interference.","ILS-sensitive area: the area beyond the critical area where parking/movement is controlled during low-visibility (CAT II/III) operations to prevent interference."],["062.02.05.05:01","Critical & sensitive areas: fill in the gaps → Hence the ____ further from the runway, and larger spacing between arrivals under LVP.","Hence the CAT II/III holding points further from the runway, and larger spacing between arrivals under LVP."],["062.02.05.05:01","Critical & sensitive areas: fill in the gaps → ____:","Other factors on range/accuracy:"],["062.02.05.04:08","Critical & sensitive areas: what do you remember about this? → Ground reflections, terrain, snow on the GP reflector …","Ground reflections, terrain, snow on the GP reflector area."],["062.02.05.05:01","Critical & sensitive areas: fill in the gaps → False glide path lobes (at ____+) → intercept the GP ____ ____.","False glide path lobes (at ~6° and 9°+) → intercept the GP from below."],["062.02.05.05:01","Critical & sensitive areas: what do you remember about this? → Outside the coverage the indications are unreliable: LOC ±10° to 25 …","Outside the coverage the indications are unreliable: LOC ±10° to 25 NM, ±35° to 17 NM; GP 8° either side to 10 NM."],["062.02.05.04:01","Approach lighting (GSPRM / Annex 14): fill in the gaps → ____ (non-instrument/non-precision): row of lights on the extended centreline, ≥ ____, with a crossbar at 300 m.","Simple approach lighting (non-instrument/non-precision): row of lights on the extended centreline, ≥ 420 m, with a crossbar at 300 m."],["062.02.05.04:01","Approach lighting (GSPRM / Annex 14): fill in the gaps → ____: centreline with barrettes/lights ____ long plus a ____ (Calvert type has several crossbars).","Precision CAT I system: centreline with barrettes/lights 900 m long plus a crossbar at 300 m (Calvert type has several crossbars)."],["062.02.05.04:01","Approach lighting (GSPRM / Annex 14): fill in the gaps → ____: the inner ____ has side-row barrettes (red) plus a crossbar at 150 m; runway touchdown zone lights (TDZ, first 900 m) and centreline lights (white; alternating red/white from 900 m before the end, ____).","CAT II/III systems: the inner 300 m has side-row barrettes (red) plus a crossbar at 150 m; runway touchdown zone lights (TDZ, first 900 m) and centreline lights (white; alternating red/white from 900 m before the end, red for the last 300 m)."],["062.02.05.04:01","Approach lighting (GSPRM / Annex 14): fill in the gaps → ____ are white (the last 600 m or 1/3 may be yellow = caution zone). ____ lights green; ____ lights red.","Runway edge lights are white (the last 600 m or 1/3 may be yellow = caution zone). Threshold lights green; runway end lights red."],["062.02.05.04:01","Approach lighting (GSPRM / Annex 14): fill in the gaps → ____ (4 units, left side, 3°):","PAPI (4 units, left side, 3°):"],["062.02.05.04:01","Approach lighting (GSPRM / Annex 14): fill in the gaps → 2 white + 2 red = ____.","2 white + 2 red = on slope."],["062.02.05.04:01","Approach lighting (GSPRM / Annex 14): what do you remember about this? → 3 white = slightly high; 4 white = too …","3 white = slightly high; 4 white = too high."],["062.02.05.04:01","Approach lighting (GSPRM / Annex 14): what do you remember about this? → 3 red = slightly low; 4 red = too …","3 red = slightly low; 4 red = too low."],["062.02.05.04:04","Exam trap (Approach lighting (GSPRM / Annex 14)): complete the fact → CAT I DH ____/RVR ____; CAT II 100 ft/300 m.","CAT I DH 200 ft/RVR 550 m; CAT II 100 ft/300 m."],["062.02.05.04:01","Exam trap (Approach lighting (GSPRM / Annex 14)): complete the fact → PAPI ____W/____R = on slope.","PAPI 2W/2R = on slope."]],"40":[["050.04.01.01:04","Cloud formation: fill in the gaps → ____ forms clouds:","Cooling to the dew point forms clouds:"],["050.04.01.01:01","Cloud formation: fill in the gaps → ____ by lifting — the main process.","Adiabatic cooling by lifting — the main process."],["050.04.01.03:02","Cloud formation: fill in the gaps → ____ — air cooled by contact with a cold surface → fog/stratus.","Conduction — air cooled by contact with a cold surface → fog/stratus."],["050.04.02.03:01","Cloud formation: fill in the gaps → ____ — warm moist air over a cold surface.","Advection — warm moist air over a cold surface."],["050.04.02.02:01","Cloud formation: fill in the gaps → ____ — night cooling → fog/St.","Radiation — night cooling → fog/St."],["050.04.01.01:03","Cloud formation: fill in the gaps → ____ (thermals, unstable) → Cu/Cb.","Convection (thermals, unstable) → Cu/Cb."],["050.04.01.01:03","Cloud formation: fill in the gaps → ____ lifting (forced up a slope) → St/Sc/Ns if stable, Cu/Cb if unstable; cap clouds.","Orographic lifting (forced up a slope) → St/Sc/Ns if stable, Cu/Cb if unstable; cap clouds."],["050.04.01.01:02","Cloud formation: fill in the gaps → ____ lifting (warm air over cold) → layered clouds (warm front), or convective clouds (cold front).","Frontal lifting (warm air over cold) → layered clouds (warm front), or convective clouds (cold front)."],["050.04.01.01:01","Cloud formation: fill in the gaps → ____ (lows, troughs, ITCZ) → large-scale rising.","Convergence (lows, troughs, ITCZ) → large-scale rising."],["050.04.01.03:03","Cloud formation: fill in the gaps → ____ in the friction layer → Sc/St below an inversion.","Turbulent mixing in the friction layer → Sc/St below an inversion."],["050.04.01.01:03","Cloud formation: fill in the gaps → ____ → ____ clouds (St, Sc, As, Ns, Cs), poor visibility, steady precipitation, smooth air.","Stable air → stratiform clouds (St, Sc, As, Ns, Cs), poor visibility, steady precipitation, smooth air."],["050.04.01.02:02","Cloud formation: fill in the gaps → ____ → ____ clouds (Cu, Cb, Ac castellanus), showers, good visibility outside showers, turbulence.","Unstable air → cumuliform clouds (Cu, Cb, Ac castellanus), showers, good visibility outside showers, turbulence."],["050.04.02.03:01","Cloud formation: fill in the gaps → ____: warming (subsidence, solar heating, Foehn descent), mixing with drier air, precipitation fall-out, advection over a warmer/drier surface.","Dissipation: warming (subsidence, solar heating, Foehn descent), mixing with drier air, precipitation fall-out, advection over a warmer/drier surface."],["050.04.01.02:02","Cloud types & classification: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (fibrous, ice), ____ (heaps, vertical development), stratiform (layers).","Three basic shapes: cirriform (fibrous, ice), cumuliform (heaps, vertical development), stratiform (layers)."],["050.04.01.02:01","Cloud types & classification: fill in the gaps → ____ (mid-latitudes):","The 10 genera and WMO étages (mid-latitudes):"],["050.04.01.02:01","Cloud types & classification – High: what are the key facts?","Height (mid- lat.): 5–13 km (≈ 16 500–45 000 ft) · Genera: Ci, Cc, Cs · Composition: ice"],["050.04.01.02:06","Cloud types & classification – Medium: what are the key facts?","Height (mid- lat.): 2–7 km (≈ 6 500– 23 000 ft) · Genera: Ac, As (Ns extends) · Composition: mixed (water, supercooled, ice)"],["050.04.01.02:01","Cloud types & classification – Low: what are the key facts?","Height (mid- lat.): surface–2 km (≈ 6 500 ft) · Genera: St, Sc (Ns base low) · Composition: water (supercooled if < 0 °C)"],["050.04.01.02:06","Cloud types & classification – Vertical development: what are the key facts?","Height (mid- lat.): base low, tops up to the tropopause · Genera: Cu, Cb (Ns) · Composition: water → mixed → ice (anvil)"],["050.04.01.02:01","Cloud types & classification: fill in the gaps → ____: turret-like tops on a layer (Ac cas) → ____ ____, forecasts afternoon Cb.","Castellanus: turret-like tops on a layer (Ac cas) → mid-level instability, forecasts afternoon Cb."],["050.04.01.02:01","Cloud types & classification: fill in the gaps → ____: lens-shaped → ____.","Lenticularis: lens-shaped → mountain waves."],["050.04.01.02:01","Cloud types & classification: fill in the gaps → ____: towering Cu (TCU), strong updrafts.","Congestus: towering Cu (TCU), strong updrafts."],["050.04.01.02:01","Cloud types & classification: fill in the gaps → ____: Cb top starting to become smooth/fibrous (no anvil yet).","Calvus: Cb top starting to become smooth/fibrous (no anvil yet)."],["050.04.01.02:02","Cloud types & classification: fill in the gaps → ____: Cb with a ____ — mature stage.","Capillatus: Cb with a cirriform anvil top — mature stage."],["050.04.01.02:01","Cloud types & classification: fill in the gaps → ____: precipitation trails that ____ ____ → downdrafts/microbursts possible.","Virga: precipitation trails that evaporate before reaching the ground → downdrafts/microbursts possible."],["050.04.01.02:06","Cloud types & classification: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (Ci, Cc, Cs, Cb anvil); ____ (As, Ns, Ac, Cb middle); water clouds (St, Sc, Cu, low Ac — can be supercooled).","Composition: ice clouds (Ci, Cc, Cs, Cb anvil); mixed clouds (As, Ns, Ac, Cb middle); water clouds (St, Sc, Cu, low Ac — can be supercooled)."],["050.04.01.03:04","Inversions & clouds: fill in the gaps → An inversion is a ____ that ____ (acts as a lid).","An inversion is a very stable layer that stops vertical motion (acts as a lid)."],["050.04.01.03:02","Inversions & clouds: fill in the gaps → ____ spread out ____ (turbulent mixing brings moisture up to the inversion base).","Stratus/Sc spread out under an inversion (turbulent mixing brings moisture up to the inversion base)."],["050.04.02.02:01","Inversions & clouds: fill in the gaps → A ____ traps moisture and pollution at night → ____.","A ground (radiation) inversion traps moisture and pollution at night → radiation fog."],["050.04.01.03:04","Inversions & clouds: fill in the gaps → The ____ limits the vertical growth of ____ → the anvil spreads out. Strong updrafts can ____ the tropopause (overshooting tops).","The tropopause inversion limits the vertical growth of Cb → the anvil spreads out. Strong updrafts can overshoot the tropopause (overshooting tops)."],["050.04.01.04:01","Flying conditions per cloud type – Ci, Cc, Cs: what are the key facts?","Icing: nil (ice crystals) · Turbulence: nil–light (Cc slight, near jets CAT)"],["050.04.01.04:01","Flying conditions per cloud type – Ac: what are the key facts?","Icing: light–moderate rime (supercooled) · Turbulence: light–moderate (Ac cas more)"],["050.04.01.04:01","Flying conditions per cloud type – As: what are the key facts?","Icing: light–moderate (mixed) · Turbulence: nil–light"],["050.04.01.04:01","Flying conditions per cloud type: fill in the gaps → Ns ____ (thick, light–moderate supercooled, freezing rain","Ns moderate–severe (thick, light–moderate supercooled, freezing rain"],["050.04.01.04:01","Flying conditions per cloud type – St: what are the key facts?","Icing: light rime (if < 0 °C) · Turbulence: nil–light; low ceilings/visibility"],["050.04.01.04:01","Flying conditions per cloud type – Sc: what are the key facts?","Icing: light–moderate · Turbulence: light–moderate"],["050.04.01.04:01","Flying conditions per cloud type – Cu: what are the key facts?","Icing: moderate clear ice (large drops) · Turbulence: moderate"],["050.04.01.04:01","Flying conditions per cloud type – Cb: what are the key facts?","Icing: severe (clear ice, hail) · Turbulence: severe–extreme"],["050.04.02.01:01","Fog, mist & haze: fill in the gaps → ____: visibility ____ from water droplets/ice crystals (RH ~100 %).","Fog: visibility < 1 000 m from water droplets/ice crystals (RH ~100 %)."],["050.04.02.01:01","Fog, mist & haze: fill in the gaps → ____: visibility ____, humidity high (≥ ~95 %).","Mist (BR): visibility 1 000–5 000 m, humidity high (≥ ~95 %)."],["050.04.02.04:01","Fog, mist & haze: fill in the gaps → ____: visibility reduced by ____ (dust, smoke, salt), RH low; visibility ≤ 5 000 m for reporting.","Haze (HZ): visibility reduced by dry particles (dust, smoke, salt), RH low; visibility ≤ 5 000 m for reporting."],["050.04.02.01:02","Fog, mist & haze: fill in the gaps → ____ = cooling to the dew point or adding moisture near the surface.","Fog/mist formation = cooling to the dew point or adding moisture near the surface."],["050.04.02.01:04","Fog, mist & haze: fill in the gaps → Contributing factors: ____, ____ (clear skies, long nights), ____, condensation nuclei (industry), and moist ground.","Contributing factors: high humidity, cooling (clear skies, long nights), light wind (2–8 kt), condensation nuclei (industry), and moist ground."],["050.04.02.01:02","Fog, mist & haze: fill in the gaps → ____ comes from pollution, dust, a stable atmosphere/inversion, and dry weather.","Haze comes from pollution, dust, a stable atmosphere/inversion, and dry weather."],["050.04.02.01:02","Fog, mist & haze: fill in the gaps → ____: supercooled droplets at T < 0 °C → ____ on surfaces and aircraft.","Freezing fog (FZFG): supercooled droplets at T < 0 °C → rime on surfaces and aircraft."],["050.04.02.03:02","Fog, mist & haze: fill in the gaps → ____: ice crystals at very low temperatures (< about −30 °C), arctic regions.","Ice fog: ice crystals at very low temperatures (< about −30 °C), arctic regions."],["050.04.02.02:01","Fog types: fill in the gaps → ____, ____, moist air, small spread → the ground cools by radiation → an inversion forms.","Clear night, light wind (2–8 kt), moist air, small spread → the ground cools by radiation → an inversion forms."],["050.04.02.02:01","Fog types: fill in the gaps → It forms over ____, in ____, in ____. Depth from a few metres to ~500 ft (thicker in winter).","It forms over land, in valleys and low ground, in autumn/winter. Depth from a few metres to ~500 ft (thicker in winter)."],["050.04.02.02:01","Fog types: fill in the gaps → Worst visibility often ____.","Worst visibility often just after sunrise."],["050.04.02.03:01","Fog types: fill in the gaps → It dissipates by ____ (burns off from the edges/bottom and lifts as St), ____ (mixes it → St), and drier air advection. It may persist all day in winter (high pressure).","It dissipates by solar heating (burns off from the edges/bottom and lifts as St), stronger wind (mixes it → St), and drier air advection. It may persist all day in winter (high pressure)."],["050.04.02.02:01","Fog types: fill in the gaps → ____ and cools from below.","Warm moist air moves over a colder surface and cools from below."],["050.04.02.04:01","Fog types: fill in the gaps → Over the ____ (warm air over a cold current, e.g. the Grand Banks, California, North Sea in spring/summer).","Over the sea (warm air over a cold current, e.g. the Grand Banks, California, North Sea in spring/summer)."],["050.04.02.03:02","Fog types: fill in the gaps → Over ____ in ____ (mild maritime air over cold/snow-covered ground).","Over land in winter (mild maritime air over cold/snow-covered ground)."],["050.04.02.04:01","Fog types: fill in the gaps → At ____ (sea fog drifts inland with a sea breeze).","At coasts (sea fog drifts inland with a sea breeze)."],["050.04.02.02:01","Fog types: fill in the gaps → It can occur with ____ and at any time of day; it is ____, and several hundred ft deep.","It can occur with stronger winds (up to ~15 kt) and at any time of day; it is extensive, persistent, and several hundred ft deep."],["050.04.02.02:01","Fog types: what do you remember about this? → Stronger wind (> ~15–20 kt) lifts it to …","Stronger wind (> ~15–20 kt) lifts it to St."],["050.04.02.02:01","Fog types: fill in the gaps → It dissipates with a ____, warming of the surface, or stronger wind.","It dissipates with a change of air mass/wind direction, warming of the surface, or stronger wind."],["050.04.01.01:03","Fog types: fill in the gaps → ____ → evaporation into cold air → steaming. Typical of arctic regions and of cold air over lakes or leads in the ice.","Very cold air flows over much warmer water → evaporation into cold air → steaming. Typical of arctic regions and of cold air over lakes or leads in the ice."],["050.04.01.04:01","Fog types: fill in the gaps → Needs a large temperature difference (≥ ~10 °C). It is usually shallow, and ____.","Needs a large temperature difference (≥ ~10 °C). It is usually shallow, and icing is possible."],["050.04.02.02:01","Fog types: what do you remember about this? → It dissipates when the wind changes or the temperature difference is …","It dissipates when the wind changes or the temperature difference is reduced."],["050.04.02.02:01","Fog types: fill in the gaps → Rain from warm air (warm front/occlusion) ____, evaporates and saturates it; plus lowering cloud bases.","Rain from warm air (warm front/occlusion) falls into cold air, evaporates and saturates it; plus lowering cloud bases."],["050.04.02.02:01","Fog types: fill in the gaps → It forms in a ____, can be extensive, and can be accompanied by ____.","It forms in a band ahead of the warm front, can be extensive, and can be accompanied by freezing rain."],["050.04.02.02:01","Fog types: fill in the gaps → It dissipates after the ____ (change of air mass).","It dissipates after the front passes (change of air mass)."],["050.04.01.01:03","Fog types: fill in the gaps → Moist stable air forced ____ cools adiabatically → cloud on the hills (low stratus). ____ → CFIT risk.","Moist stable air forced up a slope cools adiabatically → cloud on the hills (low stratus). Hill tops are in cloud → CFIT risk."],["050.04.02.02:01","Fog types: fill in the gaps → It persists as long as the upslope wind continues; it dissipates with a ____ or drier air.","It persists as long as the upslope wind continues; it dissipates with a wind change or drier air."],["050.05.01.01:02","Precipitation processes: fill in the gaps → ____:","Wegener–Bergeron–Findeisen (ice-crystal) process:"],["050.04.01.02:06","Precipitation processes: fill in the gaps → In ____ (supercooled droplets + ice crystals), the ____ than over water → ice crystals ____.","In mixed clouds (supercooled droplets + ice crystals), the saturation vapour pressure over ice is lower than over water → ice crystals grow at the expense of the droplets."],["050.05.01.01:04","Precipitation processes: fill in the gaps → They become heavy enough to fall as ____; they ____ below the freezing level. This is the main process in ____ latitudes.","They become heavy enough to fall as snow; they melt → rain below the freezing level. This is the main process in mid- latitudes."],["050.05.01.01:04","Precipitation processes: fill in the gaps → ____: in ____ (T > 0 °C, tropics, Cu), droplets of different sizes ____ → drizzle/rain.","Coalescence: in warm clouds (T > 0 °C, tropics, Cu), droplets of different sizes collide and merge → drizzle/rain."],["050.05.02.01:01","Precipitation processes: fill in the gaps → ____: ice crystals aggregating.","Snow: ice crystals aggregating."],["050.05.01.01:04","Precipitation processes: fill in the gaps → ____: melted snow or coalescence.","Rain: melted snow or coalescence."],["050.05.01.01:01","Precipitation processes: fill in the gaps → ____: small droplets from thin St/Sc (weak updrafts).","Drizzle: small droplets from thin St/Sc (weak updrafts)."],["050.05.01.01:01","Precipitation processes: fill in the gaps → ____: in Cb with ____ — ice embryos are carried up and down repeatedly through supercooled water, adding layers of clear/rime ice.","Hail: in Cb with strong updrafts — ice embryos are carried up and down repeatedly through supercooled water, adding layers of clear/rime ice."],["050.05.01.01:04","Types of precipitation: fill in the gaps → ____ drizzle; ____ rain; ____ snow; SG snow grains (from St, the \"drizzle of snow\").","DZ drizzle; RA rain; SN snow; SG snow grains (from St, the \"drizzle of snow\")."],["050.05.02.01:01","Types of precipitation: fill in the gaps → ____ ice pellets (rain frozen on the way down); ____ hail (≥ 5 mm); ____ small hail/snow pellets.","PL ice pellets (rain frozen on the way down); GR hail (≥ 5 mm); GS small hail/snow pellets."],["050.05.02.01:01","Types of precipitation: fill in the gaps → ____ ice crystals (diamond dust).","IC ice crystals (diamond dust)."],["050.05.02.01:01","Types of precipitation: fill in the gaps → ____ freezing drizzle/rain.","FZDZ/FZRA freezing drizzle/rain."],["050.05.02.01:06","Types of precipitation: fill in the gaps → Intensity ____; ____ = showers (convective); ____ = thunderstorm.","Intensity −/(none)/+; SH = showers (convective); TS = thunderstorm."],["050.04.01.02:03","Types of precipitation: fill in the gaps → Drop sizes: cloud droplets ~____ (~10–20 µm); ____; ____.","Drop sizes: cloud droplets ~0.002–0.05 mm (~10–20 µm); drizzle < 0.5 mm; rain ≥ 0.5 mm."],["050.05.02.01:03","Types of precipitation: fill in the gaps → ____ can cause ____ (windscreens, radome, leading edges, engines), even in clear air ____.","Hail can cause severe damage (windscreens, radome, leading edges, engines), even in clear air near/under the anvil."],["050.05.02.01:01","Types of precipitation: fill in the gaps → Rain falls from a ____ into a ____ ____. The droplets become supercooled and freeze on impact → clear ice.","Rain falls from a warm layer aloft (> 0 °C) into a sub-zero layer near the ground. The droplets become supercooled and freeze on impact → clear ice."],["050.04.01.01:03","Types of precipitation: fill in the gaps → Typical ____ (warm air overrunning cold air) → a temperature inversion.","Typical ahead of a warm front / warm occlusion in winter (warm air overrunning cold air) → a temperature inversion."],["050.05.02.01:01","Types of precipitation: what do you remember about this? → Freezing drizzle can also form without a warm layer (supercooled …","Freezing drizzle can also form without a warm layer (supercooled coalescence in St)."],["050.05.02.01:06","Types of precipitation: fill in the gaps → ____ (Cu, Cb) → ____: ____ — short, heavy, variable.","Convective clouds (Cu, Cb) → showers: SHRA, SHSN, GS, GR — short, heavy, variable."],["050.05.02.01:06","Types of precipitation: fill in the gaps → ____ → ____, light–moderate: As/Ns → RA/SN; St → DZ/SG.","Stratiform clouds → continuous/intermittent, light–moderate: As/Ns → RA/SN; St → DZ/SG."],["050.05.02.01:07","Types of precipitation: fill in the gaps → ____:","Typical cloud → precipitation:"],["050.05.02.01:01","Types of precipitation: what do you remember about this? → Ns: continuous moderate …","Ns: continuous moderate RA/SN."],["050.05.02.01:01","Types of precipitation: what do you remember about this? → As: light–moderate …","As: light–moderate continuous."],["050.05.02.01:01","Types of precipitation: what do you remember about this? → Cb: heavy SHRA, GR, GS, …","Cb: heavy SHRA, GR, GS, TS."],["050.05.02.01:01","Types of precipitation: what do you remember about this? → Ci/Cc/Cs: none reaching the …","Ci/Cc/Cs: none reaching the ground."],["050.05.02.01:08","Types of precipitation: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ reduces visibility far more than rain. ____ (near 0 °C, high water content) → visibility very poor; small dry flakes → also poor but more variable; heavy snow can reduce it to < 500 m.","Visibility in winter precipitation: snow reduces visibility far more than rain. Large wet flakes (near 0 °C, high water content) → visibility very poor; small dry flakes → also poor but more variable; heavy snow can reduce it to < 500 m."],["050.04.01.02:04","Thunderstorms: fill in the gaps → ____: ____, ____ castellanus/floccus (mid-level instability, morning), Cb calvus/capillatus.","Indicators of development: TCU (Cu congestus), Ac castellanus/floccus (mid-level instability, morning), Cb calvus/capillatus."],["050.04.02.06:03","Thunderstorms: fill in the gaps → ____: ____, ____ moisture, and a trigger (heating, orographic lifting, fronts, convergence).","Conditions: unstable air (conditional instability), high moisture, and a trigger (heating, orographic lifting, fronts, convergence)."],["050.04.02.03:02","Thunderstorms: fill in the gaps → ____ (heat) thunderstorm: over land on ____ ____ (heating) or over the sea at night (winter). Isolated, single cells.","Air-mass (heat) thunderstorm: over land on summer afternoons (heating) or over the sea at night (winter). Isolated, single cells."],["050.09.04.01:01","Thunderstorms: fill in the gaps → ____ thunderstorm: mainly ____ (can be embedded in stratiform cloud at warm fronts/occlusions); in lines, any time of day.","Frontal thunderstorm: mainly cold fronts (can be embedded in stratiform cloud at warm fronts/occlusions); in lines, any time of day."],["050.09.04.01:01","Thunderstorms: fill in the gaps → ____: a line of Cb ____ (up to ~300 km ahead), multicell, severe gusts, hail, microbursts, tornadoes.","Squall line: a line of Cb ahead of a cold front (up to ~300 km ahead), multicell, severe gusts, hail, microbursts, tornadoes."],["050.09.04.02:02","Thunderstorms: fill in the gaps → ____: a single, very large, long-lived ____ ____ storm with strong wind shear with height. Huge hail, tornadoes; it can last for hours.","Supercell: a single, very large, long-lived rotating (mesocyclone) storm with strong wind shear with height. Huge hail, tornadoes; it can last for hours."],["050.04.01.01:03","Thunderstorms: fill in the gaps → ____: forced lifting of unstable air over mountains (summer, windward side).","Orographic: forced lifting of unstable air over mountains (summer, windward side)."],["050.09.04.02:04","Thunderstorms: fill in the gaps → ____ (~____, whole cell about ____; multicells last longer): 1. Cumulus stage: updrafts only, growing Cu/TCU, no precipitation yet (~15–20 min). 2. Mature stage: updrafts and downdrafts, precipitation at the ground, lightning, gust front, hail; most severe (~15–30 min). 3.","Life cycle of a single cell (~20–30 min per stage, whole cell about 1–2 h; multicells last longer): 1. Cumulus stage: updrafts only, growing Cu/TCU, no precipitation yet (~15–20 min). 2. Mature stage: updrafts and downdrafts, precipitation at the ground, lightning, gust front, hail; most severe (~15–30 min). 3."],["050.09.04.02:02","Thunderstorms: fill in the gaps → ____: ____, light rain, anvil remains (~30 min).","Dissipating stage: downdrafts dominate, light rain, anvil remains (~30 min)."],["050.09.04.02:02","Thunderstorms: fill in the gaps → ____: initial (as a multicell) → ____ (rotating updraft, separate updraft/downdraft areas, hook echo) → ____ stage (wall cloud, funnel) → dissipating.","Supercell stages: initial (as a multicell) → supercell (rotating updraft, separate updraft/downdraft areas, hook echo) → tornado stage (wall cloud, funnel) → dissipating."],["050.09.04.03:02","Thunderstorms: fill in the gaps → ____, ____, ____, lightning.","Severe turbulence, severe icing, hail, lightning."],["050.09.04.01:01","Thunderstorms: fill in the gaps → ____ (up to ~15–20 NM ahead).","Wind shear / microbursts / gust front (up to ~15–20 NM ahead)."],["050.09.04.01:01","Thunderstorms: fill in the gaps → Heavy rain (engine flame-out, visibility), ____ (pressure changes).","Heavy rain (engine flame-out, visibility), altimeter errors (pressure changes)."],["050.09.04.01:01","Thunderstorms: what do you remember about this? → Tornadoes, static …","Tornadoes, static electricity."],["050.09.04.01:01","Thunderstorms: what do you remember about this? → Inside the cloud: the core, updraft/downdraft …","Inside the cloud: the core, updraft/downdraft boundaries."],["050.09.04.04:03","Thunderstorms: fill in the gaps → ____: downbursts, heavy rain.","Below the base: downbursts, heavy rain."],["050.09.04.01:01","Thunderstorms: fill in the gaps → ____: the ____ (roll cloud) → wind shear.","Ahead: the gust front (roll cloud) → wind shear."],["050.09.04.01:01","Thunderstorms: what do you remember about this? → Above the top: …","Above the top: turbulence."],["050.09.04.01:01","Thunderstorms: fill in the gaps → In a multicell, the new cells on the ____ are growing.","In a multicell, the new cells on the flank (usually SW) are growing."],["050.09.04.03:01","Lightning: fill in the gaps → ____: the ionosphere is positive, the earth negative (fair-weather field). In a Cb, charges separate (collisions of ice/graupel): the ____, the ____ (plus a small positive pocket at the base); the ground below becomes positively charged.","Electric field: the ionosphere is positive, the earth negative (fair-weather field). In a Cb, charges separate (collisions of ice/graupel): the top is positive, the lower part negative (plus a small positive pocket at the base); the ground below becomes positively charged."],["050.09.04.03:02","Lightning: fill in the gaps → ____ (cloud-to-ground).","Ground stroke (cloud-to-ground)."],["050.09.04.03:02","Lightning: fill in the gaps → ____ — the most common.","Intra-cloud — the most common."],["050.09.04.03:02","Lightning: fill in the gaps → ____ (from tall structures).","Upward lightning (from tall structures)."],["050.09.04.03:02","Lightning: fill in the gaps → ____: a ____ (negative) → an upward ____ (very bright) → repeated dart leaders.","Development: a stepped leader (negative) → an upward return stroke (very bright) → repeated dart leaders."],["050.09.04.03:01","Lightning: fill in the gaps → ____: a bluish ____ from pointed parts (windscreen, wing tips, pitot) in a strong electric field. It is ____ in itself but indicates the risk of a lightning strike, and causes radio static.","St. Elmo's fire: a bluish corona discharge from pointed parts (windscreen, wing tips, pitot) in a strong electric field. It is harmless in itself but indicates the risk of a lightning strike, and causes radio static."],["050.09.04.03:05","Lightning: fill in the gaps → ____:","Effects of a strike on the aircraft:"],["050.09.04.03:01","Lightning: what do you remember about this? → Structural (burn/pitting at entry/exit points: nose, tips, …","Structural (burn/pitting at entry/exit points: nose, tips, tail)."],["050.09.04.03:01","Lightning: fill in the gaps → ____ (flash).","Temporary blindness (flash)."],["050.09.04.03:04","Lightning: fill in the gaps → ____, radio interference.","Compass and electrical/avionics disturbances, radio interference."],["050.09.04.03:01","Lightning: what do you remember about this? → Possible fuel vapour ignition …","Possible fuel vapour ignition (rare)."],["050.04.01.02:03","Lightning: fill in the gaps → Most strikes occur ____, in or near cloud, even up to ~____ from the storm.","Most strikes occur near the freezing level (0 to −10 °C), in or near cloud, even up to ~15 NM from the storm."],["050.09.04.04:04","Downbursts: fill in the gaps → ____ = a strong localised ____ that spreads out on hitting the ground.","Downburst = a strong localised downdraft that spreads out on hitting the ground."],["050.09.04.04:02","Downbursts: fill in the gaps → ____: outflow ____ (2.2 NM) across; lasts ____; winds up to ~60 m/s.","Macroburst: outflow > 4 km (2.2 NM) across; lasts 5–20 min; winds up to ~60 m/s."],["050.09.04.04:01","Downbursts: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ across; lasts ____; downdrafts up to ~6 000 ft/min; very intense and short. It can be dry (virga — evaporation cools the air) or wet.","Microburst: ≤ 4 km across; lasts 1–5 min; downdrafts up to ~6 000 ft/min; very intense and short. It can be dry (virga — evaporation cools the air) or wet."],["050.09.04.04:03","Downbursts: fill in the gaps → ____: mature Cb, heavy showers, ____ ____ (evaporative cooling).","Situations: mature Cb, heavy showers, high-based Cb/virga in dry air (evaporative cooling)."],["050.09.04.04:01","Downbursts: fill in the gaps → ____: precipitation drag + ____ → negatively buoyant air accelerates downward → spreads horizontally at the ground (ring vortex).","Process: precipitation drag + evaporative cooling → negatively buoyant air accelerates downward → spreads horizontally at the ground (ring vortex)."],["050.09.04.04:01","Downbursts: fill in the gaps → ____: 1. On entry, ____ ____ → IAS rises, the aircraft goes above the glide path (performance increase). 2. Then a downdraft. 3. Then a tailwind → IAS falls sharply and the aircraft sinks below the path → risk of impact.","Effects on an approaching aircraft: 1. On entry, increasing headwind → IAS rises, the aircraft goes above the glide path (performance increase). 2. Then a downdraft. 3. Then a tailwind → IAS falls sharply and the aircraft sinks below the path → risk of impact."],["050.09.04.04:01","Downbursts: what do you remember about this? → Don't reduce power in the initial headwind …","Don't reduce power in the initial headwind phase!"],["050.09.04.05:01","Thunderstorm avoidance: fill in the gaps → ____: TAF/METAR TS/CB/TCU, ____ (EMBD/OBSC/FRQ/SQL TS), SIGWX charts, satellite/radar images, lightning maps.","Pre-flight briefing: TAF/METAR TS/CB/TCU, SIGMET (EMBD/OBSC/FRQ/SQL TS), SIGWX charts, satellite/radar images, lightning maps."],["050.09.04.05:01","Thunderstorm avoidance: fill in the gaps → ____: visual observation, VOLMET/ATIS, PIREPs, ____ ____ via ATC, airborne weather radar, stormscope (lightning detector).","In flight: visual observation, VOLMET/ATIS, PIREPs, ground weather radar via ATC, airborne weather radar, stormscope (lightning detector)."],["050.04.01.02:03","Thunderstorm avoidance: fill in the gaps → Circumnavigate by ____ (at high level ____) from severe echoes; pass ____; don't fly under the anvil.","Circumnavigate by ≥ 10 NM (at high level ≥ 20 NM) from severe echoes; pass upwind; don't fly under the anvil."],["050.09.04.05:01","Thunderstorm avoidance: fill in the gaps → ____ — and don't try to cross gaps between cells < 40 NM, or fly under a Cb.","Don't penetrate — and don't try to cross gaps between cells < 40 NM, or fly under a Cb."],["050.09.04.05:01","Thunderstorm avoidance: what do you remember about this? → Don't land or take off with a Cb approaching the …","Don't land or take off with a Cb approaching the aerodrome."],["050.09.04.05:01","Thunderstorm avoidance: fill in the gaps → ____:","If you inadvertently penetrate:"],["050.09.04.05:01","Thunderstorm avoidance: fill in the gaps → Fly at the ____, attitude flying (don't chase altitude).","Fly at the turbulence penetration speed, attitude flying (don't chase altitude)."],["050.09.04.05:01","Thunderstorm avoidance: what do you remember about this? → Autopilot altitude hold off (or keep a soft mode per the …","Autopilot altitude hold off (or keep a soft mode per the SOP)."],["050.09.04.05:01","Thunderstorm avoidance: fill in the gaps → ____ (flash blindness), anti-ice on, seatbelts, don't turn back if deep inside (continue straight), avoid the freezing level ±5 000 ft.","Cockpit lights on full (flash blindness), anti-ice on, seatbelts, don't turn back if deep inside (continue straight), avoid the freezing level ±5 000 ft."],["050.09.04.05:01","Thunderstorm avoidance: what do you remember about this? → Radar tilt down to scan …","Radar tilt down to scan below."],["050.04.02.01:02","Exam trap (Thunderstorm avoidance): complete the fact → Fog < ____; mist ____ (water); haze = dry particles.","Fog < 1 000 m; mist 1–5 km (water); haze = dry particles."],["050.09.04.04:02","Exam trap (Thunderstorm avoidance): complete the fact → Microburst ≤ ____, ____; initial headwind → IAS up, then tailwind → IAS down.","Microburst ≤ 4 km, 1–5 min; initial headwind → IAS up, then tailwind → IAS down."],["050.04.01.02:05","EASA LO 050.04.01.02.05 → Distinguish between low-, medium- and high-level clouds according to the World Meteorological Organization’s (WMO) ‘cloud etage’.","The 10 genera and WMO étages (mid-latitudes):"],["050.04.01.03:01","EASA LO 050.04.01.03.01 → Explain the influence of inversions on vertical movements in the atmosphere.","An inversion is a very stable layer that stops vertical motion (acts as a lid)."],["050.04.02.01:03","EASA LO 050.04.02.01.03 → Name the factors that generally contribute to the formation of fog and mist.","Contributing factors: high humidity, cooling (clear skies, long nights), light wind (2–8 kt), condensation nuclei (industry), and moist ground."],["050.04.02.01:05","EASA LO 050.04.02.01.05 → Describe freezing fog and ice fog.","FZDZ/FZRA freezing drizzle/rain. / Freezing fog (FZFG): supercooled droplets at T < 0 °C → rime on surfaces and aircraft."],["050.04.02.02:02","EASA LO 050.04.02.02.02 → Describe the significant characteristics of radiation fog, and its vertical extent.","Radiation — night cooling → fog/St. / A ground (radiation) inversion traps moisture and pollution at night → radiation fog."],["050.04.02.02:03","EASA LO 050.04.02.02.03 → Summarise the conditions for the dissipation of radiation fog.","Radiation — night cooling → fog/St. / A ground (radiation) inversion traps moisture and pollution at night → radiation fog."],["050.04.02.03:03","EASA LO 050.04.02.03.03 → Describe the significant characteristics of advection fog.","Advection — warm moist air over a cold surface."],["050.04.02.03:04","EASA LO 050.04.02.03.04 → Summarise the conditions for the dissipation of advection fog.","Dissipation: warming (subsidence, solar heating, Foehn descent), mixing with drier air, precipitation fall-out, advection over a warmer/drier surface. / Advection — warm moist air over a cold surface."],["050.04.02.04:02","EASA LO 050.04.02.04.02 → Explain the conditions for the development of sea smoke.","At coasts (sea fog drifts inland with a sea breeze). / Haze (HZ): visibility reduced by dry particles (dust, smoke, salt), RH low; visibility ≤ 5 000 m for reporting."],["050.04.02.04:03","EASA LO 050.04.02.04.03 → Summarise the conditions for the dissipation of sea smoke.","At coasts (sea fog drifts inland with a sea breeze). / Haze (HZ): visibility reduced by dry particles (dust, smoke, salt), RH low; visibility ≤ 5 000 m for reporting."],["050.04.02.05:01","EASA LO 050.04.02.05.01 → Explain the formation of frontal fog.","Frontal lifting (warm air over cold) → layered clouds (warm front), or convective clouds (cold front). / Radiation — night cooling → fog/St."],["050.04.02.06:01","EASA LO 050.04.02.06.01 → Summarise the features of orographic fog.","Conditions: unstable air (conditional instability), high moisture, and a trigger (heating, orographic lifting, fronts, convergence)."],["050.04.02.06:02","EASA LO 050.04.02.06.02 → Describe the significant characteristics of orographic fog.","Conditions: unstable air (conditional instability), high moisture, and a trigger (heating, orographic lifting, fronts, convergence)."],["050.05.01.01:01","EASA LO 050.05.01.01.01 → Describe the two basic processes of forming precipitation (the Wegener–Bergeron–Findeisen process, Coalescence).","Wegener–Bergeron–Findeisen (ice-crystal) process: / Rain: melted snow or coalescence."],["050.05.01.01:03","EASA LO 050.05.01.01.03 → Summarise the outlines of the coalescence process.","Rain: melted snow or coalescence. / Adiabatic cooling by lifting — the main process."],["050.05.02.01:02","EASA LO 050.05.02.01.02 → State the ICAO/WMO approximate diameters for cloud, drizzle and rain drops.","FZDZ/FZRA freezing drizzle/rain. / Typical cloud → precipitation:"],["050.05.02.01:04","EASA LO 050.05.02.01.04 → Explain the mechanism for the formation of freezing precipitation.","Typical cloud → precipitation: / FZDZ/FZRA freezing drizzle/rain."],["050.05.02.01:05","EASA LO 050.05.02.01.05 → Describe the weather conditions that give rise to freezing precipitation.","Typical cloud → precipitation: / FZDZ/FZRA freezing drizzle/rain."],["050.09.04.04:01","EASA LO 050.09.04.04.01 → Define the term ‘downburst’.","Effects of a strike on the aircraft: / Below the base: downbursts, heavy rain."],["050.09.04.04:05","EASA LO 050.09.04.04.05 → Give the typical duration of a downburst.","Typical cloud → precipitation: / Effects of a strike on the aircraft:"],["050.09.04.04:06","EASA LO 050.09.04.04.06 → Describe the effects of downbursts. X X X X X X X Exam Remarks","Effects of a strike on the aircraft: / Below the base: downbursts, heavy rain."]],"41":[["081.04.01.02:01","Basics: fill in the gaps → ____ is the ____ after a disturbance from equilibrium:","Static stability is the initial tendency after a disturbance from equilibrium:"],["081.04.05.03:01","Basics: fill in the gaps → ____ — tends to ____ (restoring moment).","Positive — tends to return (restoring moment)."],["081.04.03.01:05","Basics: fill in the gaps → ____ — stays in the new position.","Neutral — stays in the new position."],["081.04.01.01:04","Basics: fill in the gaps → ____ — ____.","Negative — diverges further."],["081.04.01.01:01","Basics: fill in the gaps → Aeroplanes are designed ____ (a tail behind the CG, a fin, dihedral/sweep) so they can be flown without constant correction.","Aeroplanes are designed statically stable (a tail behind the CG, a fin, dihedral/sweep) so they can be flown without constant correction."],["081.04.01.03:01","Basics: fill in the gaps → ____ = the ease of changing the flight path (control forces/deflections needed).","Manoeuvrability = the ease of changing the flight path (control forces/deflections needed)."],["081.04.01.01:01","Basics: fill in the gaps → ____ (higher control forces), and vice versa. It's a compromise: transport aircraft are more stable, fighters/aerobatic aircraft less.","More stability → less manoeuvrability (higher control forces), and vice versa. It's a compromise: transport aircraft are more stable, fighters/aerobatic aircraft less."],["081.04.01.01:04","Basics: fill in the gaps → ____ describes the ____ after a disturbance:","Dynamic stability describes the motion over time after a disturbance:"],["081.04.01.01:01","Basics: fill in the gaps → ____ — the oscillation is damped and dies out.","Positive — the oscillation is damped and dies out."],["081.04.01.01:01","Basics: fill in the gaps → ____ — constant amplitude.","Neutral — constant amplitude."],["081.04.01.01:01","Basics: fill in the gaps → ____ — the amplitude grows.","Negative — the amplitude grows."],["081.04.01.01:04","Basics: fill in the gaps → The motion can be ____ (oscillating) or ____ (no oscillation, a steady convergence or divergence).","The motion can be periodic (oscillating) or aperiodic (no oscillation, a steady convergence or divergence)."],["081.04.01.01:01","Basics: fill in the gaps → Dynamic stability ____ positive static stability. A statically stable aircraft can still be dynamically unstable.","Dynamic stability requires positive static stability. A statically stable aircraft can still be dynamically unstable."],["081.04.01.04:01","Basics: fill in the gaps → ____ (trim) is the precondition: ____ (lift, weight, thrust, drag, tail force) and ____ about all three axes: pitch (lateral axis), roll (longitudinal axis), yaw (normal axis).","Equilibrium (trim) is the precondition: sum of forces = 0 (lift, weight, thrust, drag, tail force) and sum of moments = 0 about all three axes: pitch (lateral axis), roll (longitudinal axis), yaw (normal axis)."],["081.04.01.04:01","Basics: what do you remember about this? → If the sum of moments ≠ 0, the aircraft rotates (angular …","If the sum of moments ≠ 0, the aircraft rotates (angular acceleration) about that axis."],["081.04.01.04:02","Balance (trim) in pitch: fill in the gaps → The ____ produces the force that makes the ____.","The stabiliser (tailplane) produces the force that makes the sum of pitching moments zero."],["081.04.03.01:02","Balance (trim) in pitch: fill in the gaps → The wing ____ (normal) gives a nose-down moment → the tail provides a ____. With the CP ahead of the CG, the tail provides an upward force.","The wing CP behind the CG (normal) gives a nose-down moment → the tail provides a downforce. With the CP ahead of the CG, the tail provides an upward force."],["081.04.03.01:02","Balance (trim) in pitch: fill in the gaps → ____: at ____ the CP moves aft (low α) → a larger nose-down moment → more tail ____. At low IAS the CP is further forward.","Influence of IAS: at high IAS the CP moves aft (low α) → a larger nose-down moment → more tail down-load. At low IAS the CP is further forward."],["081.04.03.01:04","Balance (trim) in pitch: fill in the gaps → The balancing force is produced by ____ or the ____.","The balancing force is produced by elevator deflection or the stabiliser incidence (THS trim)."],["081.04.03.01:05","Balance (trim) in pitch: fill in the gaps → ____ (underslung): more thrust → ____ → needs nose-down elevator/trim.","Engines below the CG (underslung): more thrust → nose-up → needs nose-down elevator/trim."],["081.04.03.01:05","Balance (trim) in pitch: fill in the gaps → ____ (rear/high-mounted): more thrust → ____ → needs nose-up elevator.","Engines above the CG (rear/high-mounted): more thrust → nose-down → needs nose-up elevator."],["081.04.01.03:01","Static longitudinal stability & neutral point: fill in the gaps → ____ → ____, ____, higher stick forces, more tail download (more drag, higher VS).","Forward CG → more stable, less manoeuvrable, higher stick forces, more tail download (more drag, higher VS)."],["081.04.01.03:01","Static longitudinal stability & neutral point: fill in the gaps → ____ → less stable, more manoeuvrable, lighter forces.","Aft CG → less stable, more manoeuvrable, lighter forces."],["081.04.03.04:01","Static longitudinal stability & neutral point: fill in the gaps → ____ = the CG position at which the aircraft has ____. It is the ____ centre of the whole aircraft (wing + tail).","Neutral point (NP) = the CG position at which the aircraft has neutral static longitudinal stability. It is the aerodynamic centre of the whole aircraft (wing + tail)."],["081.04.03.03:02","Static longitudinal stability & neutral point: fill in the gaps → It depends ____ (wing and tail geometry, arm, downwash), ____.","It depends only on the aerodynamic design (wing and tail geometry, arm, downwash), not on mass/loading."],["081.04.05.06:01","Static longitudinal stability & neutral point: fill in the gaps → It lies ____, aft of the wing AC (because of the tail's contribution).","It lies between the wing AC and the tail AC, aft of the wing AC (because of the tail's contribution)."],["081.04.03.05:03","Static longitudinal stability & neutral point: fill in the gaps → ____ = the ____ (as % MAC). Stable when the CG is ____ — the larger the margin, the more stable.","Static margin = the distance between the CG and the NP (as % MAC). Stable when the CG is ahead of the NP — the larger the margin, the more stable."],["081.04.03.05:02","Static longitudinal stability & neutral point: fill in the gaps → ____: maximum stick forces and ____ ____ (enough authority to flare/rotate at low speed with a forward CG).","Forward limit: maximum stick forces and elevator effectiveness (enough authority to flare/rotate at low speed with a forward CG)."],["081.04.03.12:02","Static longitudinal stability & neutral point: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (a minimum static margin, a minimum stick force per g), to avoid over-control.","Aft limit: minimum stability (a minimum static margin, a minimum stick force per g), to avoid over-control."],["081.04.03.06:01","Static longitudinal stability & neutral point: fill in the gaps → ____________: a negative slope = stable (an increase in α produces a nose-down Cm).","Cm–α graph: a negative slope = stable (an increase in α produces a nose-down Cm)."],["081.04.01.01:01","Static longitudinal stability & neutral point: fill in the gaps → ____ → more stable. ____ = neutral. ____ = unstable.","Steeper negative slope → more stable. Zero slope = neutral. Positive slope = unstable."],["081.04.03.06:01","Static longitudinal stability & neutral point: fill in the gaps → The ____ is where Cm = 0.","The trim point is where Cm = 0."],["081.04.04.04:02","Static longitudinal stability & neutral point: fill in the gaps → ____ → the slope becomes ____. ____ → the slope becomes flatter (at the NP: horizontal).","CG forward → the slope becomes steeper. CG aft → the slope becomes flatter (at the NP: horizontal)."],["081.04.03.02:01","Static longitudinal stability & neutral point: fill in the gaps → ____ → the line ____ (trim at a higher α, lower speed) without a change in slope. ____ → shifts downward.","Elevator up → the line shifts parallel upward (trim at a higher α, lower speed) without a change in slope. Elevator down → shifts downward."],["081.04.03.10:01","Stick force stability: fill in the gaps → ____: a stable aircraft needs a ____ ____ and a pull force to fly slower than the trim speed. It returns towards the trim speed when released.","Stick force vs IAS: a stable aircraft needs a push force to fly faster and a pull force to fly slower than the trim speed. It returns towards the trim speed when released."],["081.04.03.12:01","Stick force stability: fill in the gaps → ____ → a ____ (higher forces per knot). Aft CG → flatter. ____: the gradient is roughly IAS-based. Friction produces a small band without a force change.","Forward CG → a steeper gradient (higher forces per knot). Aft CG → flatter. Altitude: the gradient is roughly IAS-based. Friction produces a small band without a force change."],["081.04.03.12:01","Stick force stability: fill in the gaps → ____ = the pull force needed per unit increase of load factor. It ____ with n.","Stick force per g = the pull force needed per unit increase of load factor. It increases linearly with n."],["081.04.03.12:02","Stick force stability: fill in the gaps → It has a ____ (so the pilot cannot overstress the aircraft by mistake) and a ____ (so it isn't too heavy to manoeuvre).","It has a prescribed minimum (so the pilot cannot overstress the aircraft by mistake) and a maximum (so it isn't too heavy to manoeuvre)."],["081.04.03.12:02","Stick force stability: fill in the gaps → It ____ (less aerodynamic damping from the tail at high TAS/low density).","It decreases with increasing altitude (less aerodynamic damping from the tail at high TAS/low density)."],["081.04.03.12:01","Stick force stability: fill in the gaps → ____ → higher stick force per g; ____ → lower, and ____ at the manoeuvre point (behind the NP).","CG forward → higher stick force per g; CG aft → lower, and zero at the manoeuvre point (behind the NP)."],["081.04.03.12:01","Stick force stability: fill in the gaps → ____ doesn't change the stick force per g (it only moves the zero-force speed).","Trim doesn't change the stick force per g (it only moves the zero-force speed)."],["081.04.03.16:01","Dynamic longitudinal stability: fill in the gaps → A ____ (~20–100 s), ____.","A long period (~20–100 s), lightly damped."],["081.04.03.10:01","Dynamic longitudinal stability: fill in the gaps → Large changes in ____ at an almost ____ (a slow exchange of kinetic and potential energy).","Large changes in speed and altitude at an almost constant α (a slow exchange of kinetic and potential energy)."],["081.04.03.16:03","Dynamic longitudinal stability: what do you remember about this? → Easily controlled by the …","Easily controlled by the pilot."],["081.04.03.16:02","Dynamic longitudinal stability: fill in the gaps → ____:","Short-period oscillation:"],["081.04.03.16:02","Dynamic longitudinal stability: fill in the gaps → A ____ (~1–2 s), normally ____.","A short period (~1–2 s), normally heavily damped."],["081.04.03.10:01","Dynamic longitudinal stability: fill in the gaps → Changes in ____ at an almost ____ (and altitude).","Changes in α and pitch at an almost constant speed (and altitude)."],["081.04.03.16:03","Dynamic longitudinal stability: fill in the gaps → It is more ____: it is too fast for the pilot to damp, pilot reaction can amplify it → ____, and it gives high loads on the structure.","It is more hazardous: it is too fast for the pilot to damp, pilot reaction can amplify it → PIO, and it gives high loads on the structure."],["081.04.03.16:03","Dynamic longitudinal stability: fill in the gaps → ____: pilot inputs ____ with the aircraft response amplify the oscillation → best action: ____, and let the aircraft damp it out.","PIO (pilot-induced oscillation): pilot inputs out of phase with the aircraft response amplify the oscillation → best action: release the controls/freeze, and let the aircraft damp it out."],["081.04.03.16:03","Dynamic longitudinal stability: fill in the gaps → ____ reduces the ____ (low density) → oscillations are less damped (a yaw damper or pitch damper is needed).","High altitude reduces the aerodynamic damping (low density) → oscillations are less damped (a yaw damper or pitch damper is needed)."],["081.04.03.16:05","Dynamic longitudinal stability: fill in the gaps → ____ → less damping, a lower frequency, possible instability. ____ → more stable.","Aft CG → less damping, a lower frequency, possible instability. Forward CG → more stable."],["081.04.06.02:01","Directional stability: fill in the gaps → ____ = the tendency to yaw back into the relative wind after a ____ (weathercock stability).","Static directional stability = the tendency to yaw back into the relative wind after a sideslip (weathercock stability)."],["081.04.06.03:01","Directional stability: fill in the gaps → ____ → large sideslips, Dutch roll tendencies.","Too weak → large sideslips, Dutch roll tendencies."],["081.04.06.02:01","Directional stability: fill in the gaps → ____ → a ____ tendency, large yaw response to gusts, crosswind handling problems.","Too strong → a spiral dive tendency, large yaw response to gusts, crosswind handling problems."],["081.04.04.02:01","Directional stability: fill in the gaps → ____ = the angle between the relative airflow and the longitudinal axis (the plane of symmetry).","Sideslip angle β = the angle between the relative airflow and the longitudinal axis (the plane of symmetry)."],["081.04.04.03:01","Directional stability: fill in the gaps → ________ = the dimensionless yawing moment (N = Cn ½ρV² S b).","Yaw-moment coefficient Cn = the dimensionless yawing moment (N = Cn ½ρV² S b)."],["081.04.04.04:02","Directional stability: fill in the gaps → A stable aircraft produces a Cn that yaws the nose ____ ____ (towards the relative wind): ____n ∝ β.","A stable aircraft produces a Cn that yaws the nose into the sideslip (towards the relative wind): Cn ∝ β."],["081.04.04.04:01","Directional stability: fill in the gaps → Cn = 0 at the equilibrium β, which is ____ ____.","Cn = 0 at the equilibrium β, which is 0 with symmetric thrust/controls/loading."],["081.04.04.04:02","Directional stability: fill in the gaps → ____________: slope = the measure of directional stability (steeper = more stable). Altitude: the slope decreases with altitude/Mach (the fin lift slope falls at high Mach) → less directional stability.","Cn–β graph: slope = the measure of directional stability (steeper = more stable). Altitude: the slope decreases with altitude/Mach (the fin lift slope falls at high Mach) → less directional stability."],["081.04.04.01:01","Directional stability: fill in the gaps → ____ (the main contributor, a large arm).","Fin (the main contributor, a large arm)."],["081.04.04.05:01","Directional stability: fill in the gaps → ____ (improves stability at large β, delays the fin stall).","Dorsal fin (improves stability at large β, delays the fin stall)."],["081.04.04.05:01","Directional stability: fill in the gaps → ____ (stability at high α, when the fin is blanketed).","Ventral fin (stability at high α, when the fin is blanketed)."],["081.04.05.06:01","Directional stability: fill in the gaps → ____ (the advancing wing has more drag → yaw back).","Wing sweepback (the advancing wing has more drag → yaw back)."],["081.04.04.05:01","Directional stability: fill in the gaps → ____ (longer arm, higher stall β).","Fin sweep (longer arm, higher stall β)."],["081.04.04.05:01","Directional stability: fill in the gaps → The ____ ahead of the CG is ____ (worse at high α).","The fuselage ahead of the CG is destabilising (worse at high α)."],["081.04.04.05:01","Directional stability: fill in the gaps → ____ → the fin arm is shorter → ____.","CG aft → the fin arm is shorter → less directional stability."],["081.04.05.04:01","Lateral stability: fill in the gaps → ____ = the tendency to ____ after a disturbance in roll (via the ____ it creates).","Static lateral stability = the tendency to roll wings-level after a disturbance in roll (via the sideslip it creates)."],["081.04.06.02:01","Lateral stability: fill in the gaps → ____ → a spiral dive tendency. ____ → Dutch roll, heavy aileron forces, crosswind landing problems.","Too weak → a spiral dive tendency. Too strong → Dutch roll, heavy aileron forces, crosswind landing problems."],["081.04.05.03:01","Lateral stability: fill in the gaps → ____ = the angle between the lateral axis and the horizontal. ________ = the dimensionless rolling moment.","Bank angle Ø = the angle between the lateral axis and the horizontal. Roll-moment coefficient Cl = the dimensionless rolling moment."],["081.04.05.04:01","Lateral stability: fill in the gaps → Bank without coordination → the lift vector is tilted → the aircraft ____ (____) → a stable aircraft rolls away from the sideslip.","Bank without coordination → the lift vector is tilted → the aircraft slips towards the low wing (β created) → a stable aircraft rolls away from the sideslip."],["081.04.05.05:02","Lateral stability: fill in the gaps → ____________: a negative slope (Cl opposes β) = stable; the slope is the measure of lateral stability. Altitude: at high altitude the aircraft flies at a higher CL → the sweepback contribution (∝ CL) grows → the slope becomes steeper (lateral stability increases), while directional stability decreases → more Dutch roll tendency.","Cl–β graph: a negative slope (Cl opposes β) = stable; the slope is the measure of lateral stability. Altitude: at high altitude the aircraft flies at a higher CL → the sweepback contribution (∝ CL) grows → the slope becomes steeper (lateral stability increases), while directional stability decreases → more Dutch roll tendency."],["081.04.05.06:01","Lateral stability: fill in the gaps → ____ (the lower wing has a higher α in a sideslip) → stabilising. ____ → destabilising.","Dihedral (the lower wing has a higher α in a sideslip) → stabilising. Anhedral → destabilising."],["081.04.05.06:01","Lateral stability: fill in the gaps → ____ → stabilising (____ — sideslip flow around the fuselage). ____ → destabilising.","High wing → stabilising (keel/pendulum effect — sideslip flow around the fuselage). Low wing → destabilising."],["081.04.05.06:01","Lateral stability: fill in the gaps → ____ → stabilising (the lower wing has more effective speed). This is why swept high wings often use ____.","Sweepback → stabilising (the lower wing has more effective speed). This is why swept high wings often use anhedral."],["081.04.05.01:01","Lateral stability: what do you remember about this? → Also the fin (above the CG), and flaps (reduce …","Also the fin (above the CG), and flaps (reduce stability)."],["081.04.06.03:02","Dynamic lateral/directional stability: fill in the gaps → ____: yaw causes roll (the outer wing is faster), and roll causes yaw (sideslip) → lateral and directional stability always interact.","Coupling: yaw causes roll (the outer wing is faster), and roll causes yaw (sideslip) → lateral and directional stability always interact."],["081.04.05.01:02","Dynamic lateral/directional stability: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ ____.","Spiral divergence: strong directional + weak lateral stability."],["081.04.06.02:03","Dynamic lateral/directional stability: fill in the gaps → After a disturbance in roll, the aircraft yaws into the slip, the bank increases slowly, and the nose drops → an ____ ____ (a slow spiral).","After a disturbance in roll, the aircraft yaws into the slip, the bank increases slowly, and the nose drops → an increasing bank, nose-low attitude, rising speed, losing altitude (a slow spiral)."],["081.04.06.02:01","Dynamic lateral/directional stability: fill in the gaps → It is slow (aperiodic), so the pilot can correct it. The spiral is ____ if lateral stability is sufficient relative to directional.","It is slow (aperiodic), so the pilot can correct it. The spiral is neutral/stable if lateral stability is sufficient relative to directional."],["081.04.06.03:01","Dynamic lateral/directional stability: fill in the gaps → ____: a ____ (the wing tips trace a figure-8) when ____ (typical of swept wings).","Dutch roll: a combined yaw–roll oscillation (the wing tips trace a figure-8) when lateral stability > directional stability (typical of swept wings)."],["081.04.03.16:04","Dynamic lateral/directional stability: fill in the gaps → It is ____ if damped; ____/____ with weak directional stability, at high altitude, or at low speed.","It is stable if damped; neutral/unstable with weak directional stability, at high altitude, or at low speed."],["081.04.06.03:02","Dynamic lateral/directional stability: fill in the gaps → A ____ senses the yaw rate and applies automatic rudder inputs to damp it.","A yaw damper senses the yaw rate and applies automatic rudder inputs to damp it."],["081.04.03.12:01","Dynamic lateral/directional stability: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (per the AFM), and damp with ____ (small inputs). Avoid large rudder inputs (risk of PIO).","Yaw damper inoperative: reduce altitude and increase IAS (per the AFM), and damp with ailerons (small inputs). Avoid large rudder inputs (risk of PIO)."],["081.04.06.03:03","Dynamic lateral/directional stability: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ on the two wings (different local Mach number with sideslip) → asymmetric lift/drag → it can trigger Dutch roll.","High Mach: asymmetric shock waves on the two wings (different local Mach number with sideslip) → asymmetric lift/drag → it can trigger Dutch roll."],["081.04.06.04:01","Dynamic lateral/directional stability: fill in the gaps → ____ dynamic lateral/directional stability (less aerodynamic damping).","Increasing pressure altitude reduces dynamic lateral/directional stability (less aerodynamic damping)."],["081.04.01.01:02","EASA LO 081.04.01.01.02 → Explain manoeuvrability.","Manoeuvrability = the ease of changing the flight path (control forces/deflections needed)."],["081.04.03.01:01","EASA LO 081.04.03.01.01 → Explain the stabiliser as the means to satisfy the condition of nullifying the total sum of the moments about the lateral axis.","If the sum of moments ≠ 0, the aircraft rotates (angular acceleration) about that axis. / Equilibrium (trim) is the precondition: sum of forces = 0 (lift, weight, thrust, drag, tail force) and sum of moments = 0 about all three axes: pitch (lateral axis), roll (longitudinal axis), yaw (normal axis)."],["081.04.03.01:03","EASA LO 081.04.03.01.03 → Explain the influence of the indicated airspeed on the magnitude and direction of the balancing force on the stabiliser.","The balancing force is produced by elevator deflection or the stabiliser incidence (THS trim). / The stabiliser (tailplane) produces the force that makes the sum of pitching moments zero."],["081.04.03.03:01","EASA LO 081.04.03.03.01 → Define ‘neutral point’. X X Exam BK Remarks","Steeper negative slope → more stable. Zero slope = neutral. Positive slope = unstable. / The trim point is where Cm = 0."],["081.04.03.05:01","EASA LO 081.04.03.05.01 → Explain the influence of the CG location on the static longitudinal stability of the aeroplane.","Neutral point (NP) = the CG position at which the aircraft has neutral static longitudinal stability. It is the aerodynamic centre of the whole aircraft (wing + tail)."],["081.04.03.07:01","EASA LO 081.04.03.07.01 → Explain: — the effect on the Cm– graph of a shift of CG in the forward and aft direction; — the effect on the Cm– graph when the elevator is moved up or down;","Cm–α graph: a negative slope = stable (an increase in α produces a nose-down Cm). / The trim point is where Cm = 0."],["081.04.03.14:01","EASA LO 081.04.03.14.01 → Explain the influence on stick force per g of: — CG location; — trim setting.","Trim doesn't change the stick force per g (it only moves the zero-force speed). / CG forward → higher stick force per g; CG aft → lower, and zero at the manoeuvre point (behind the NP)."],["081.04.04.01:01","EASA LO 081.04.04.01.01 → Define ‘static directional stability’.","Spiral divergence: strong directional + weak lateral stability. / Increasing pressure altitude reduces dynamic lateral/directional stability (less aerodynamic damping)."],["081.04.04.01:02","EASA LO 081.04.04.01.02 → Explain the effects of static directional stability being too weak or too strong.","Spiral divergence: strong directional + weak lateral stability. / Too weak → a spiral dive tendency. Too strong → Dutch roll, heavy aileron forces, crosswind landing problems."],["081.04.04.02:02","EASA LO 081.04.04.02.02 → Identify β as the symbol used for the sideslip angle.","Sideslip angle β = the angle between the relative airflow and the longitudinal axis (the plane of symmetry). / Bank angle Ø = the angle between the lateral axis and the horizontal. Roll-moment coefficient Cl = the dimensionless rolling moment."],["081.04.04.03:02","EASA LO 081.04.04.03.02 → Define the relationship between Cn and β for an aeroplane with static directional stability.","Yaw-moment coefficient Cn = the dimensionless yawing moment (N = Cn ½ρV² S b). / Bank angle Ø = the angle between the lateral axis and the horizontal. Roll-moment coefficient Cl = the dimensionless rolling moment."],["081.04.04.04:03","EASA LO 081.04.04.04.03 → Identify how the slope of the Cn–β graph is affected by altitude.","Cn–β graph: slope = the measure of directional stability (steeper = more stable). Altitude: the slope decreases with altitude/Mach (the fin lift slope falls at high Mach) → less directional stability. / Cn = 0 at the equilibrium β, which is 0 with symmetric thrust/controls/loading."],["081.04.04.05:02","EASA LO 081.04.04.05.02 → Explain the reduction in static directional stability when the CG moves aft.","CG aft → the fin arm is shorter → less directional stability. / Influence of IAS: at high IAS the CP moves aft (low α) → a larger nose-down moment → more tail down-load. At low IAS the CP is further forward."],["081.04.05.01:01","EASA LO 081.04.05.01.01 → Define ‘static lateral stability’.","Spiral divergence: strong directional + weak lateral stability. / Increasing pressure altitude reduces dynamic lateral/directional stability (less aerodynamic damping)."],["081.04.05.02:01","EASA LO 081.04.05.02.01 → Define ‘bank angle Ø’.","Bank angle Ø = the angle between the lateral axis and the horizontal. Roll-moment coefficient Cl = the dimensionless rolling moment. / Sideslip angle β = the angle between the relative airflow and the longitudinal axis (the plane of symmetry)."],["081.04.05.05:01","EASA LO 081.04.05.05.01 → Describe the Cl– graph.","Cl–β graph: a negative slope (Cl opposes β) = stable; the slope is the measure of lateral stability. Altitude: at high altitude the aircraft flies at a higher CL → the sweepback contribution (∝ CL) grows → the slope becomes steeper (lateral stability increases), while directional stability decreases → more Dutch roll tendency. / Bank angle Ø = the angle between the lateral axis and the horizontal. Roll-moment coefficient Cl = the dimensionless rolling moment."],["081.04.05.05:03","EASA LO 081.04.05.05.03 → Identify how the slope of the Cl–β graph is affected by altitude.","Cl–β graph: a negative slope (Cl opposes β) = stable; the slope is the measure of lateral stability. Altitude: at high altitude the aircraft flies at a higher CL → the sweepback contribution (∝ CL) grows → the slope becomes steeper (lateral stability increases), while directional stability decreases → more Dutch roll tendency. / Cn–β graph: slope = the measure of directional stability (steeper = more stable). Altitude: the slope decreases with altitude/Mach (the fin lift slope falls at high Mach) → less directional stability."],["081.04.06.02:02","EASA LO 081.04.06.02.02 → Explain how high static directional stability and low static lateral stability may cause spiral divergence (unstable spiral dive), and under which conditions the spiral dive mode is neutral or stable.","Spiral divergence: strong directional + weak lateral stability. / Too weak → a spiral dive tendency. Too strong → Dutch roll, heavy aileron forces, crosswind landing problems."]],"42":[["050.06.01.01:04","Air masses: fill in the gaps → ____ = a large body of air (thousands of km) with ____ ____ horizontally, acquired over a source region.","Air mass = a large body of air (thousands of km) with uniform temperature and humidity horizontally, acquired over a source region."],["050.06.01.02:03","Air masses: fill in the gaps → ____ are large, uniform surfaces (oceans, continents, ice) with ____ (anticyclones).","Source regions are large, uniform surfaces (oceans, continents, ice) with stagnant/slow air (anticyclones)."],["050.06.01.02:02","Air masses: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ continental (dry) / ____ maritime (moist).","Humidity: c continental (dry) / m maritime (moist)."],["050.06.01.01:07","Air masses: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ arctic, ____ polar, T tropical, E equatorial.","Source: A arctic, P polar, T tropical, E equatorial."],["050.06.01.01:07","Air masses: fill in the gaps → ____ (third letter): ____ colder → unstable; ____ warmer → stable.","Temperature relative to the surface (third letter): c colder → unstable; w warmer → stable."],["050.06.01.02:02","Air masses: fill in the gaps → E.g. ____ = maritime polar, colder than the surface.","E.g. mPc = maritime polar, colder than the surface."],["050.06.01.01:05","Air masses – mA: what are the key facts?","Source: Arctic ocean (via Norwegian Sea) · Characteristic weather (Europe): very cold, unstable over the warm sea → snow showers, Cb, icing, good vis. between showers"],["050.06.01.01:05","Air masses – mP (most frequent): what are the key facts?","Source: N-Atlantic (Canada/Greenland) · Characteristic weather (Europe): cool, moist, heated from below → unstable: Cu/Cb, showers, good visibility, gusts"],["050.06.01.01:05","Air masses – mT: what are the key facts?","Source: Azores high (subtropical Atlantic) · Characteristic weather (Europe): warm, moist, cooled from below → stable: St, drizzle, advection fog, poor visibility (esp. in winter)"],["050.06.01.01:05","Air masses – cP: what are the key facts?","Source: Siberia/Scandinavia (winter) · Characteristic weather (Europe): very cold, dry, stable: clear skies, severe frost, haze; over the North Sea → Sc/snow showers"],["050.06.01.01:05","Air masses – cT: what are the key facts?","Source: N-Africa, Middle East · Characteristic weather (Europe): hot, dry, dusty: haze, dust, heat, poor visibility (summer), thermal Cb possible"],["050.06.01.01:06","Air masses: fill in the gaps → The ____ are ____ (plus mA, cT).","The 3 main air masses affecting Europe are mP, mT, cP (plus mA, cT)."],["050.06.01.01:07","Air masses: fill in the gaps → ____ them on surface charts by wind trajectory, temperature/dew point and weather (showers → cold/unstable; fog/drizzle → warm/stable).","Classify them on surface charts by wind trajectory, temperature/dew point and weather (showers → cold/unstable; fog/drizzle → warm/stable)."],["050.06.01.02:04","Air-mass modification: fill in the gaps → The final properties depend on the ____ (sea/land), the ____, the ____, and the time of travel. Orographic and thermal effects over land also change them.","The final properties depend on the trajectory (sea/land), the surface temperature, the season, and the time of travel. Orographic and thermal effects over land also change them."],["050.06.01.02:04","Air-mass modification: fill in the gaps → A ____ adds moisture (continental air becomes moist); a ____ makes the air drier (and hotter/colder by season).","A maritime track adds moisture (continental air becomes moist); a continental track makes the air drier (and hotter/colder by season)."],["050.06.01.02:01","Air-mass modification: fill in the gaps → ____ → heated from below → ____: Cu/Cb, showers, turbulence, good visibility.","Cold air over a warm surface → heated from below → unstable: Cu/Cb, showers, turbulence, good visibility."],["050.06.01.02:01","Air-mass modification: fill in the gaps → ____ → cooled from below → ____: inversion, St/fog, drizzle, poor visibility, smooth air.","Warm air over a cold surface → cooled from below → stable: inversion, St/fog, drizzle, poor visibility, smooth air."],["050.06.01.02:04","Air-mass modification: fill in the gaps → mP in ____ over land → afternoon Cb (heating).","mP in summer over land → afternoon Cb (heating)."],["050.06.01.02:04","Air-mass modification: what do you remember about this? → mT in winter → fog/St over cold land; in summer → hazy, possibly …","mT in winter → fog/St over cold land; in summer → hazy, possibly thunderstorms."],["050.07.03.01:02","Air-mass modification: what do you remember about this? → Mountains cause orographic cloud/rain on the windward side and Foehn …","Mountains cause orographic cloud/rain on the windward side and Foehn on the lee side."],["050.06.01.02:01","Air-mass modification: fill in the gaps → ____ air → showers, Cb, icing (clear), turbulence, wind shear.","Unstable air → showers, Cb, icing (clear), turbulence, wind shear."],["050.06.01.02:01","Air-mass modification: fill in the gaps → ____ air → low ceilings, fog, poor visibility, rime/freezing drizzle in winter.","Stable air → low ceilings, fog, poor visibility, rime/freezing drizzle in winter."],["050.07.02.01:01","Fronts — general: fill in the gaps → A ____ = the ____ of different temperature/density on the surface (a line on the map).","A front = the boundary between two air masses of different temperature/density on the surface (a line on the map)."],["050.06.02.01:02","Fronts — general: fill in the gaps → A ____ = the 3-D sloping transition layer between the air masses (in the vertical, ~1–2 km thick; horizontally 50–200 km wide).","A frontal zone = the 3-D sloping transition layer between the air masses (in the vertical, ~1–2 km thick; horizontally 50–200 km wide)."],["050.06.02.01:01","Fronts — general: fill in the gaps → ____: between polar and tropical air. Mean position ____ ____ — further south in winter (~30–40°N over the Atlantic/Mediterranean) and north in summer (~50–60°N).","Polar front: between polar and tropical air. Mean position ~40– 60°N — further south in winter (~30–40°N over the Atlantic/Mediterranean) and north in summer (~50–60°N)."],["050.06.02.01:03","Fronts — general: fill in the gaps → ____: between arctic and polar air, ____ (winter further south).","Arctic front: between arctic and polar air, ~60–70°N (winter further south)."],["050.06.02.03:01","Warm front: fill in the gaps → ____ and ____ (cold air retreats).","Warm air advances and rises gently over the cold air (cold air retreats)."],["050.06.02.02:04","Warm front: fill in the gaps → ____; cloud system ahead ____ (from Ci to Ns).","Slope ~1:100–1:200; cloud system ahead ~600–1 000 km (from Ci to Ns)."],["050.06.02.02:01","Warm front: fill in the gaps → ____ (as it approaches):","Cloud sequence (as it approaches):"],["050.06.02.04:01","Warm front: fill in the gaps → ____ (halo) → ____ → ____ (continuous rain/snow, low St fractus) → front passage → warm sector.","Ci → Cs (halo) → As → Ns (continuous rain/snow, low St fractus) → front passage → warm sector."],["050.06.02.02:01","Warm front: fill in the gaps → ____ ahead of the surface front.","Precipitation belt ~300–400 km ahead of the surface front."],["050.06.02.08:05","Warm front: fill in the gaps → If the warm air is ____ → ____ in the Ns (hidden TS!).","If the warm air is unstable → embedded Cb in the Ns (hidden TS!)."],["050.06.02.05:01","Warm front: fill in the gaps → ____: moderate → ____, ____ before the front, low ceilings.","Ground visibility: moderate → poor in rain, frontal fog before the front, low ceilings."],["050.06.02.02:01","Warm front: fill in the gaps → ____: widespread ____ in Ns/As; ____ ahead of the front in winter (warm air above cold) → severe clear ice; low ceilings; embedded Cb; wind shear across the front.","Hazards: widespread icing in Ns/As; freezing rain ahead of the front in winter (warm air above cold) → severe clear ice; low ceilings; embedded Cb; wind shear across the front."],["050.06.02.02:01","Warm front: fill in the gaps → ____: freezing rain/snow, fog, very low St.","Winter: freezing rain/snow, fog, very low St."],["050.06.02.02:01","Warm front: fill in the gaps → ____: higher bases, possibly embedded TS, less icing.","Summer: higher bases, possibly embedded TS, less icing."],["050.06.02.04:02","Warm front: fill in the gaps → ____: pressure falls steadily ahead, then ____; ____; the wind veers (NH, e.g. S → SW); rain stops or becomes drizzle; visibility is often poor in the warm sector.","Passage: pressure falls steadily ahead, then stops falling; temperature and dew point rise; the wind veers (NH, e.g. S → SW); rain stops or becomes drizzle; visibility is often poor in the warm sector."],["050.06.02.03:01","Cold front: fill in the gaps → ____ under the warm air and ____.","Cold air advances under the warm air and lifts it steeply."],["050.06.02.03:04","Cold front: fill in the gaps → ____ (steeper). Narrow weather band (~50–100 km).","Slope ~1:50–1:100 (steeper). Narrow weather band (~50–100 km)."],["050.06.02.02:01","Cold front: fill in the gaps → It moves ____ than a warm front.","It moves faster than a warm front."],["050.06.02.03:01","Cold front: fill in the gaps → ____ → ____ ____, gusts, hail, turbulence, wind shear, severe icing → a narrow band.","Unstable warm air ahead → Cb, TS, heavy showers, squall line, gusts, hail, turbulence, wind shear, severe icing → a narrow band."],["050.06.02.05:02","Cold front: fill in the gaps → ____ → ____, continuous rain, less severe (an \"ana-type\" front with rain behind the front).","Stable warm air ahead → Ns/As, continuous rain, less severe (an \"ana-type\" front with rain behind the front)."],["050.06.01.02:03","Cold front: fill in the gaps → ____: poor in heavy precipitation, ____ ____.","Visibility: poor in heavy precipitation, very good after passage."],["050.06.02.03:01","Cold front: fill in the gaps → ____: very active (heating) — TS, hail.","Summer: very active (heating) — TS, hail."],["050.06.02.05:02","Cold front: fill in the gaps → ____: less convection, but snow showers/icing behind the front.","Winter: less convection, but snow showers/icing behind the front."],["050.06.02.03:01","Cold front: fill in the gaps → ____: pressure ____; temperature and dew point ____; a sharp veer of the wind (e.g. SW → NW) with gusts; showery heavy rain → clearing.","Passage: pressure rises sharply; temperature and dew point fall; a sharp veer of the wind (e.g. SW → NW) with gusts; showery heavy rain → clearing."],["050.06.02.04:01","Warm sector & behind the cold front: fill in the gaps → ____ = the area of warm (mT) air between the warm and cold fronts of a depression.","Warm sector = the area of warm (mT) air between the warm and cold fronts of a depression."],["050.06.02.05:01","Warm sector & behind the cold front: fill in the gaps → ____, ____, ____, advection fog/low St (esp. winter over land and cold sea); low ceilings, hill fog.","St/Sc, drizzle, poor visibility, advection fog/low St (esp. winter over land and cold sea); low ceilings, hill fog."],["050.06.02.05:01","Warm sector & behind the cold front: fill in the gaps → In ____ over land: Sc/Cu, better visibility, haze; possibly TS if unstable.","In summer over land: Sc/Cu, better visibility, haze; possibly TS if unstable."],["050.06.02.05:02","Warm sector & behind the cold front: fill in the gaps → ____ (polar air, mP) is ____ over a warmer surface:","Behind the cold front (polar air, mP) is cold unstable air over a warmer surface:"],["050.06.02.05:01","Warm sector & behind the cold front: fill in the gaps → ____ (hail, snow showers in winter), ____ ____ outside showers, gusty winds, turbulence, icing in Cb.","Cu/Cb, showers (hail, snow showers in winter), very good visibility outside showers, gusty winds, turbulence, icing in Cb."],["050.06.01.02:04","Warm sector & behind the cold front: fill in the gaps → Showers are more frequent over the ____ and over ____ ____ afternoons (heating); nights over land are clearer.","Showers are more frequent over the sea in winter and over land in summer afternoons (heating); nights over land are clearer."],["050.06.02.06:01","Occlusion: fill in the gaps → An ____ forms when the ____ ____ → the warm sector air is lifted off the ground. The occluded front is the surface front where the two cold air masses meet.","An occlusion forms when the faster cold front catches up with the warm front → the warm sector air is lifted off the ground. The occluded front is the surface front where the two cold air masses meet."],["050.06.02.06:06","Occlusion: fill in the gaps → The ____ (triple point) moves ____ ____ along the warm front as the occlusion grows. The low then starts to fill.","The occlusion point (triple point) moves away from the low centre along the warm front as the occlusion grows. The low then starts to fill."],["050.06.02.03:01","Occlusion: fill in the gaps → ____: the air ____ than the air ahead of the warm front → the cold front ____ the warm-front air. The weather looks like a cold front (Cb, showers) following warm-front clouds. More common in summer.","Cold occlusion: the air behind the cold front is colder than the air ahead of the warm front → the cold front undercuts the warm-front air. The weather looks like a cold front (Cb, showers) following warm-front clouds. More common in summer."],["050.06.02.03:01","Occlusion: fill in the gaps → ____: the air ____ than the air ahead → the cold front ____ over the warm-front cold air. The weather looks like a warm front, with embedded Cb possible. Typical in winter over Europe (very cold continental air ahead).","Warm occlusion: the air behind the cold front is warmer than the air ahead → the cold front rides up over the warm-front cold air. The weather looks like a warm front, with embedded Cb possible. Typical in winter over Europe (very cold continental air ahead)."],["050.06.02.06:03","Occlusion: fill in the gaps → ____: a wide band of cloud and precipitation (combined warm-and cold-front weather); ____; icing; low ceilings; in winter, freezing rain (warm occlusion).","Hazards: a wide band of cloud and precipitation (combined warm-and cold-front weather); embedded Cb; icing; low ceilings; in winter, freezing rain (warm occlusion)."],["050.06.02.07:01","Stationary front: fill in the gaps → A ____ is a front that is barely moving (____ ____; the air masses don't displace each other).","A stationary front is a front that is barely moving (wind parallel to the front; the air masses don't displace each other)."],["050.06.02.07:02","Stationary front: fill in the gaps → The weather is similar to a warm front but persistent: ____, continuous precipitation, poor visibility, fog, and prolonged low ceilings over the same area → flooding, days of bad weather. Waves may develop along it.","The weather is similar to a warm front but persistent: As/Ns, continuous precipitation, poor visibility, fog, and prolonged low ceilings over the same area → flooding, days of bad weather. Waves may develop along it."],["050.06.02.08:02","Movement & life cycle: fill in the gaps → Fronts move with the component of the ____ ____ to them (the isobar direction in the warm sector).","Fronts move with the component of the geostrophic wind at right angles to them (the isobar direction in the warm sector)."],["050.06.02.08:03","Movement & life cycle: fill in the gaps → A ____ moves at ~____ (faster).","A cold front moves at ~the full geostrophic component (faster)."],["050.06.02.06:06","Movement & life cycle: fill in the gaps → A ____ moves at ~____ of it (slower) → occlusion.","A warm front moves at ~⅔ of it (slower) → occlusion."],["050.06.02.08:04","Movement & life cycle: fill in the gaps → ____ move in the direction of the ____ ____ (parallel to the warm-sector isobars / the 500-hPa flow), roughly with the upper-level steering winds (W → E in mid-latitudes).","Frontal depressions move in the direction of the isobars in the warm sector (parallel to the warm-sector isobars / the 500-hPa flow), roughly with the upper-level steering winds (W → E in mid-latitudes)."],["050.06.02.08:01","Movement & life cycle: what do you remember about this? → They slow and fill when …","They slow and fill when occluded."],["050.06.02.07:01","Movement & life cycle: fill in the gaps → ____: 1. A stationary front with ____ ____. 2. Wave formation (a small low forms). 3. A mature (open-wave) depression with warm and cold fronts and a warm sector. 4. The occlusion begins (cold front catches the warm front). 5. Fully occluded, filling, dissipating.","Life cycle (polar-front theory): 1. A stationary front with wind shear. 2. Wave formation (a small low forms). 3. A mature (open-wave) depression with warm and cold fronts and a warm sector. 4. The occlusion begins (cold front catches the warm front). 5. Fully occluded, filling, dissipating."],["050.07.03.01:02","Movement & life cycle: fill in the gaps → Families of depressions form along the polar front (secondary lows forming on the trailing cold front). Total life ~____.","Families of depressions form along the polar front (secondary lows forming on the trailing cold front). Total life ~5–7 days."],["050.06.02.08:05","Movement & life cycle: fill in the gaps → ____: a wide band (≈ 300–400 km) ahead of the warm front; a narrow band at the cold front; drizzle in the warm sector.","Cloud/rain belts: a wide band (≈ 300–400 km) ahead of the warm front; a narrow band at the cold front; drizzle in the warm sector."],["050.06.02.09:01","Movement & life cycle: fill in the gaps → ____:","Frontal wave — changes (NH):"],["050.06.02.05:02","Movement & life cycle: fill in the gaps → ____: falls ahead of the warm front → steady in the warm sector → rises behind the cold front.","Pressure: falls ahead of the warm front → steady in the warm sector → rises behind the cold front."],["050.06.02.03:01","Movement & life cycle: fill in the gaps → ____: rises at the warm front, drops at the cold front.","Temperature: rises at the warm front, drops at the cold front."],["050.06.01.01:05","Movement & life cycle: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ at each front (e.g. S → SW → NW).","Wind: veers at each front (e.g. S → SW → NW)."],["050.06.02.05:02","Movement & life cycle: fill in the gaps → ____: veers with height ahead of the warm front (____ ____) and backs with height behind the cold front (cold advection).","Upper wind: veers with height ahead of the warm front (warm advection) and backs with height behind the cold front (cold advection)."],["050.07.01.01:01","Principal pressure areas: fill in the gaps → Strong ____ and ____ (cold continents).","Strong Siberian high and North American high (cold continents)."],["050.07.01.01:03","Principal pressure areas: fill in the gaps → ____ and ____ deep.","Icelandic and Aleutian lows deep."],["050.07.01.01:03","Principal pressure areas: what do you remember about this? → The subtropical highs (Azores, Pacific) are displaced …","The subtropical highs (Azores, Pacific) are displaced south."],["050.07.01.01:03","Principal pressure areas: fill in the gaps → The ____ over the continents.","The ITCZ is south of the equator over the continents."],["050.07.01.01:01","Principal pressure areas: fill in the gaps → ____ over the continents (____, Sahara, SW USA).","Thermal lows over the continents (Asian/Indian monsoon low, Sahara, SW USA)."],["050.07.01.01:03","Principal pressure areas: fill in the gaps → The ____ and ____ are strong and shifted ____.","The Azores and Pacific highs are strong and shifted north."],["050.07.01.01:01","Principal pressure areas: what do you remember about this? → The Icelandic low is …","The Icelandic low is weak."],["050.07.01.01:03","Principal pressure areas: fill in the gaps → The ____ (up to ~20–25°N over India/Africa).","The ITCZ is north (up to ~20–25°N over India/Africa)."],["050.07.01.01:03","Principal pressure areas: fill in the gaps → ____: continental heating/cooling.","Thermal: continental heating/cooling."],["050.07.03.01:02","Principal pressure areas: fill in the gaps → ____: the subtropical highs form from the ____ ____; the subpolar lows from the polar-front depressions.","Dynamic: the subtropical highs form from the subsiding Hadley-cell air; the subpolar lows from the polar-front depressions."],["050.07.01.01:03","Principal pressure areas: fill in the gaps → The pressure belts move with the ____: ____ ____, south in NH winter — more so over the continents.","The pressure belts move with the sun (season): north in NH summer, south in NH winter — more so over the continents."],["050.07.02.01:04","Anticyclones & ridges: fill in the gaps → ____ highs — shallow; they weaken with height.","Cold (thermal) highs — shallow; they weaken with height."],["050.07.02.01:07","Anticyclones & ridges: fill in the gaps → ____ highs — deep, e.g. the subtropical highs.","Warm (dynamic) highs — deep, e.g. the subtropical highs."],["050.07.02.01:08","Anticyclones & ridges: fill in the gaps → ____; ____ highs; transitory highs between depressions.","Ridges; blocking highs; transitory highs between depressions."],["050.07.02.01:02","Anticyclones & ridges: fill in the gaps → ____ → the air ____ → surface pressure rises (a high).","Upper-level convergence → the air subsides → surface pressure rises (a high)."],["050.07.02.01:07","Anticyclones & ridges: fill in the gaps → ____: the sinking air warms adiabatically → a ____ ____ → stable air, dry.","Subsidence: the sinking air warms adiabatically → a subsidence inversion → stable air, dry."],["050.07.02.01:01","Anticyclones & ridges: fill in the gaps → Clear skies, or a layer of ____ (\"anticyclonic gloom\" in winter).","Clear skies, or a layer of Sc under the inversion (\"anticyclonic gloom\" in winter)."],["050.07.02.01:01","Anticyclones & ridges: fill in the gaps → ____ (pollution trapped), light winds.","Haze, fog, poor visibility (pollution trapped), light winds."],["050.07.02.01:08","Anticyclones & ridges: fill in the gaps → ____: warm air through a deep layer; they intensify with height (e.g. the ____, a blocking high). Settled weather, subsidence inversion, summer heat.","Warm anticyclones: warm air through a deep layer; they intensify with height (e.g. the Azores high, a blocking high). Settled weather, subsidence inversion, summer heat."],["050.07.02.01:04","Anticyclones & ridges: fill in the gaps → ____: cold dense air near the surface (e.g. ____ in winter); ____ — above ~3 km a low may exist. Very cold, clear, frost, fog/haze.","Cold anticyclones: cold dense air near the surface (e.g. Siberian high in winter); shallow — above ~3 km a low may exist. Very cold, clear, frost, fog/haze."],["050.07.02.01:02","Anticyclones & ridges: fill in the gaps → ____: an extension of a high between two lows (U-or V-shaped isobars pointing away from the high). ____, subsidence, often short-lived and ____ between depressions.","Ridges: an extension of a high between two lows (U-or V-shaped isobars pointing away from the high). Fair weather, subsidence, often short-lived and mobile between depressions."],["050.06.02.07:01","Anticyclones & ridges: fill in the gaps → ____: a quasi-stationary warm high in mid-latitudes (e.g. over Scandinavia) → ____; depressions are diverted north/south. Persistent weather for days/weeks (heat waves, cold spells).","Blocking anticyclone: a quasi-stationary warm high in mid-latitudes (e.g. over Scandinavia) → blocks the westerly flow; depressions are diverted north/south. Persistent weather for days/weeks (heat waves, cold spells)."],["050.07.03.01:01","Depressions & troughs: fill in the gaps → ____ (ahead of upper troughs, near jets) removes air → the ____ → a low forms/deepens.","Upper-level divergence (ahead of upper troughs, near jets) removes air → the surface pressure falls → a low forms/deepens."],["050.07.03.01:03","Depressions & troughs: fill in the gaps → ____: surface heating (continents in summer, e.g. Iberia, Sahara) → ____, weak; can trigger afternoon Cb.","Thermal low: surface heating (continents in summer, e.g. Iberia, Sahara) → shallow, weak; can trigger afternoon Cb."],["050.07.03.01:02","Depressions & troughs: fill in the gaps → ____: forms on the ____ ____ (e.g. the Genoa low south of the Alps with northerly flow; Po Valley) → bad weather, it can develop into a frontal depression.","Orographic (lee) low: forms on the lee side of mountain ranges (e.g. the Genoa low south of the Alps with northerly flow; Po Valley) → bad weather, it can develop into a frontal depression."],["050.07.03.01:03","Depressions & troughs: fill in the gaps → ____: a small, intense low in ____ ____ (winter) → heavy snow showers, strong winds, poor visibility.","Polar low: a small, intense low in cold arctic air over a warmer sea (winter) → heavy snow showers, strong winds, poor visibility."],["050.07.03.01:01","Depressions & troughs: fill in the gaps → ____: forms within the circulation of a primary low (often at the triple point or along the cold front) → it can deepen rapidly and become dominant.","Secondary depression: forms within the circulation of a primary low (often at the triple point or along the cold front) → it can deepen rapidly and become dominant."],["050.06.02.03:01","Depressions & troughs: fill in the gaps → ____: an elongated area of low pressure (V-shaped isobars pointing away from the low) → ____ → clouds, showers/TS, a wind shift along the trough line (behind a cold front: a showery trough).","Trough: an elongated area of low pressure (V-shaped isobars pointing away from the low) → convergence, rising air → clouds, showers/TS, a wind shift along the trough line (behind a cold front: a showery trough)."],["050.07.04.01:01","Tropical revolving storms (TRS): fill in the gaps → ____ (warm water to depth, huge latent heat).","Sea surface ≥ 26–27 °C (warm water to depth, huge latent heat)."],["050.07.04.01:01","Tropical revolving storms (TRS): fill in the gaps → ____ (enough Coriolis force — ____).","Latitude ≥ ~5° (enough Coriolis force — not at the equator)."],["050.07.04.01:03","Tropical revolving storms (TRS): what do you remember about this? → An initial disturbance (easterly …","An initial disturbance (easterly wave)."],["050.07.03.01:01","Tropical revolving storms (TRS): what do you remember about this? → Upper-level divergence …","Upper-level divergence (outflow)."],["050.07.04.01:06","Tropical revolving storms (TRS): fill in the gaps → ____: initially ____ (~10–15 kt), curving ____, then recurving eastward into the westerlies (NH: W → NW → N → NE). The track is erratic and rarely forecast exactly.","Movement: initially westward with the trade winds (~10–15 kt), curving poleward, then recurving eastward into the westerlies (NH: W → NW → N → NE). The track is erratic and rarely forecast exactly."],["050.07.04.01:03","Tropical revolving storms (TRS): fill in the gaps → ____ (sustained wind):","Development stages (sustained wind):"],["050.07.04.01:03","Tropical revolving storms (TRS): fill in the gaps → ____ (< 34 kt).","Tropical depression (< 34 kt)."],["050.07.04.01:03","Tropical revolving storms (TRS): fill in the gaps → ____ (34–47 kt).","Tropical storm (34–47 kt)."],["050.07.04.01:03","Tropical revolving storms (TRS): fill in the gaps → ____ (48–63 kt).","Severe tropical storm (48–63 kt)."],["050.07.04.01:03","Tropical revolving storms (TRS): fill in the gaps → ____ / hurricane / typhoon (____).","Tropical revolving storm / hurricane / typhoon (≥ 64 kt)."],["050.07.04.01:01","Tropical revolving storms (TRS): fill in the gaps → The ____ (~10–50 km): calm, clear, subsiding air, lowest pressure.","The eye (~10–50 km): calm, clear, subsiding air, lowest pressure."],["050.07.04.01:01","Tropical revolving storms (TRS): fill in the gaps → The ____: towering Cb, ____, extreme turbulence.","The eye wall: towering Cb, strongest winds and rain, extreme turbulence."],["050.07.04.01:03","Tropical revolving storms (TRS): fill in the gaps → ____ outward with Cb, torrential rain, storm surge.","Spiral rain bands outward with Cb, torrential rain, storm surge."],["050.07.04.01:01","Tropical revolving storms (TRS): what do you remember about this? → Very low pressure (up to 100 hPa below …","Very low pressure (up to 100 hPa below normal)."],["050.07.04.01:05","Tropical revolving storms (TRS): fill in the gaps → ____: diameter ~____ (typically ~500 km); cloud tops up to 15–18 km.","Dimensions: diameter ~200–1 000 km (typically ~500 km); cloud tops up to 15–18 km."],["050.07.04.01:01","Tropical revolving storms (TRS): fill in the gaps → ____: N-Atlantic/Caribbean/Gulf of Mexico, NE Pacific — ____ (peak Aug–Oct).","Hurricane: N-Atlantic/Caribbean/Gulf of Mexico, NE Pacific — June–November (peak Aug–Oct)."],["050.07.04.01:01","Tropical revolving storms (TRS): fill in the gaps → ____: NW Pacific (Philippines, China, Japan) — ____, peak ____, the most frequent (~25–30/yr).","Typhoon: NW Pacific (Philippines, China, Japan) — all year, peak July–October, the most frequent (~25–30/yr)."],["050.07.04.01:01","Tropical revolving storms (TRS): fill in the gaps → N Indian Ocean (Bay of Bengal, Arabian Sea): ____ ____.","N Indian Ocean (Bay of Bengal, Arabian Sea): April–June and October–December."],["050.07.04.01:01","Tropical revolving storms (TRS): fill in the gaps → S Indian Ocean (Madagascar, NW Australia) and SW Pacific: ____.","S Indian Ocean (Madagascar, NW Australia) and SW Pacific: December–April."],["050.07.04.01:01","Tropical revolving storms (TRS): fill in the gaps → ____ in the S-Atlantic or SE Pacific (cold water, shear).","Not in the S-Atlantic or SE Pacific (cold water, shear)."],["050.06.01.01:07","Exam trap (Tropical revolving storms (TRS)): complete the fact → ____rd letter c = colder than the surface → unstable.","3rd letter c = colder than the surface → unstable."],["050.06.02.03:04","Exam trap (Tropical revolving storms (TRS)): complete the fact → Warm front slope ____:____cold front 1:80.","Warm front slope 1:150, cold front 1:80."],["050.07.04.01:01","Exam trap (Tropical revolving storms (TRS)): complete the fact → TRS need ≥ ____ sea and ≥ ____° latitude; ≥ 64 kt = hurricane.","TRS need ≥ 26–27 °C sea and ≥ 5° latitude; ≥ 64 kt = hurricane."],["050.06.01.01:01","EASA LO 050.06.01.01.01 → Define the term ‘air mass’.","Source regions are large, uniform surfaces (oceans, continents, ice) with stagnant/slow air (anticyclones). / Air mass = a large body of air (thousands of km) with uniform temperature and humidity horizontally, acquired over a source region."],["050.06.01.01:02","EASA LO 050.06.01.01.02 → Describe the properties of the source regions.","Source regions are large, uniform surfaces (oceans, continents, ice) with stagnant/slow air (anticyclones). / Source: A arctic, P polar, T tropical, E equatorial."],["050.06.01.01:03","EASA LO 050.06.01.01.03 → Summarise the classification of air masses by source regions.","Source regions are large, uniform surfaces (oceans, continents, ice) with stagnant/slow air (anticyclones). / Source: A arctic, P polar, T tropical, E equatorial."],["050.06.01.02:01","EASA LO 050.06.01.02.01 → List the environmental factors that affect the final properties of an air mass.","The final properties depend on the trajectory (sea/land), the surface temperature, the season, and the time of travel. Orographic and thermal effects over land also change them."],["050.06.01.02:05","EASA LO 050.06.01.02.05 → Assess the tendencies of the stability of an air mass and describe the typical resulting air-mass weather including the hazards for aviation.","Warm occlusion: the air behind the cold front is warmer than the air ahead → the cold front rides up over the warm-front cold air. The weather looks like a warm front, with embedded Cb possible. Typical in winter over Europe (very cold continental air ahead)."],["050.06.02.01:04","EASA LO 050.06.02.01.04 → State the approximate seasonal latitudes and geographic positions of the polar front and the arctic front.","Arctic front: between arctic and polar air, ~60–70°N (winter further south). / Source: A arctic, P polar, T tropical, E equatorial."],["050.06.02.02:02","EASA LO 050.06.02.02.02 → Describe the cloud, weather, ground visibility and aviation hazards at a warm front depending on the stability of the warm air.","Cold occlusion: the air behind the cold front is colder than the air ahead of the warm front → the cold front undercuts the warm-front air. The weather looks like a cold front (Cb, showers) following warm-front clouds. More common in summer. / Hazards: a wide band of cloud and precipitation (combined warm-and cold-front weather); embedded Cb; icing; low ceilings; in winter, freezing rain (warm occlusion)."],["050.06.02.02:03","EASA LO 050.06.02.02.03 → Explain the seasonal differences in the weather at warm fronts.","Warm sector = the area of warm (mT) air between the warm and cold fronts of a depression. / Cold occlusion: the air behind the cold front is colder than the air ahead of the warm front → the cold front undercuts the warm-front air. The weather looks like a cold front (Cb, showers) following warm-front clouds. More common in summer."],["050.06.02.02:05","EASA LO 050.06.02.02.05 → Sketch a cross section of a warm front showing weather, cloud and aviation hazards.","Hazards: a wide band of cloud and precipitation (combined warm-and cold-front weather); embedded Cb; icing; low ceilings; in winter, freezing rain (warm occlusion). / Cold occlusion: the air behind the cold front is colder than the air ahead of the warm front → the cold front undercuts the warm-front air. The weather looks like a cold front (Cb, showers) following warm-front clouds. More common in summer."],["050.06.02.03:02","EASA LO 050.06.02.03.02 → Describe the cloud, weather, ground visibility and aviation hazards at a cold front depending on the stability of the warm air.","Cold occlusion: the air behind the cold front is colder than the air ahead of the warm front → the cold front undercuts the warm-front air. The weather looks like a cold front (Cb, showers) following warm-front clouds. More common in summer. / Hazards: a wide band of cloud and precipitation (combined warm-and cold-front weather); embedded Cb; icing; low ceilings; in winter, freezing rain (warm occlusion)."],["050.06.02.03:03","EASA LO 050.06.02.03.03 → Explain the seasonal differences in the weather at cold fronts.","Warm sector = the area of warm (mT) air between the warm and cold fronts of a depression. / Cold occlusion: the air behind the cold front is colder than the air ahead of the warm front → the cold front undercuts the warm-front air. The weather looks like a cold front (Cb, showers) following warm-front clouds. More common in summer."],["050.06.02.03:05","EASA LO 050.06.02.03.05 → Sketch a cross section of a cold front showing weather, cloud and aviation hazards.","Hazards: a wide band of cloud and precipitation (combined warm-and cold-front weather); embedded Cb; icing; low ceilings; in winter, freezing rain (warm occlusion). / Cold occlusion: the air behind the cold front is colder than the air ahead of the warm front → the cold front undercuts the warm-front air. The weather looks like a cold front (Cb, showers) following warm-front clouds. More common in summer."],["050.06.02.04:03","EASA LO 050.06.02.04.03 → Explain the seasonal differences in the weather in the warm sector.","Warm sector = the area of warm (mT) air between the warm and cold fronts of a depression. / Pressure: falls ahead of the warm front → steady in the warm sector → rises behind the cold front."],["050.06.02.04:04","EASA LO 050.06.02.04.04 → Sketch a cross section of a warm sector showing weather, cloud and aviation hazards.","Warm sector = the area of warm (mT) air between the warm and cold fronts of a depression. / Hazards: a wide band of cloud and precipitation (combined warm-and cold-front weather); embedded Cb; icing; low ceilings; in winter, freezing rain (warm occlusion)."],["050.06.02.06:02","EASA LO 050.06.02.06.02 → Describe the cloud, weather, ground visibility and aviation hazards in a cold occlusion.","Hazards: a wide band of cloud and precipitation (combined warm-and cold-front weather); embedded Cb; icing; low ceilings; in winter, freezing rain (warm occlusion). / Cold occlusion: the air behind the cold front is colder than the air ahead of the warm front → the cold front undercuts the warm-front air. The weather looks like a cold front (Cb, showers) following warm-front clouds. More common in summer."],["050.07.01.01:02","EASA LO 050.07.01.01.02 → Explain how these pressure areas are formed.","The ITCZ is south of the equator over the continents. / Icelandic and Aleutian lows deep."],["050.07.02.01:03","EASA LO 050.07.02.01.03 → Describe air-mass subsidence, its effect on the environmental lapse rate, and the associated weather.","Subsidence: the sinking air warms adiabatically → a subsidence inversion → stable air, dry. / Warm anticyclones: warm air through a deep layer; they intensify with height (e.g. the Azores high, a blocking high). Settled weather, subsidence inversion, summer heat."],["050.07.02.01:05","EASA LO 050.07.02.01.05 → Describe the formation of ridges. X X X X X X X 3 Exam Remarks","Subsidence: the sinking air warms adiabatically → a subsidence inversion → stable air, dry. / Ridges; blocking highs; transitory highs between depressions."],["050.07.02.01:06","EASA LO 050.07.02.01.06 → Describe the properties of and the weather associated with warm and cold anticyclones.","Warm anticyclones: warm air through a deep layer; they intensify with height (e.g. the Azores high, a blocking high). Settled weather, subsidence inversion, summer heat. / Subsidence: the sinking air warms adiabatically → a subsidence inversion → stable air, dry."],["050.07.04.01:01","EASA LO 050.07.04.01.01 → State the conditions necessary for the formation of tropical revolving storms.","Tropical revolving storm / hurricane / typhoon (≥ 64 kt). / Tropical depression (< 34 kt)."],["050.07.04.01:02","EASA LO 050.07.04.01.02 → State how a tropical revolving storm generally moves in its area of occurrence.","Tropical revolving storm / hurricane / typhoon (≥ 64 kt). / Tropical storm (34–47 kt)."],["050.07.04.01:04","EASA LO 050.07.04.01.04 → Describe the meteorological conditions in and near a tropical revolving storm.","Tropical revolving storm / hurricane / typhoon (≥ 64 kt). / Tropical storm (34–47 kt)."],["050.07.04.02:01","EASA LO 050.07.04.02.01 → List the areas of origin and occurrence of tropical revolving storms, and their specified names (hurricane, typhoon, tropical cyclone).","Tropical revolving storm / hurricane / typhoon (≥ 64 kt)."]],"43":[["040.02.03.02:01","Body rhythm & sleep: fill in the gaps → ____: sleep/wake, hormones (melatonin, cortisol), alertness. ____ is the ____ marker.","Body rhythms: sleep/wake, hormones (melatonin, cortisol), alertness. Body temperature is the most important marker."],["040.02.03.02:08","Body rhythm & sleep: fill in the gaps → ____ → lowest performance and alertness, easiest sleep.","Minimum ~04:00–06:00 → lowest performance and alertness, easiest sleep."],["040.02.03.02:01","Body rhythm & sleep: what do you remember about this? → Maximum in the late afternoon/evening → best performance, hardest to …","Maximum in the late afternoon/evening → best performance, hardest to fall asleep."],["040.02.03.02:06","Body rhythm & sleep: fill in the gaps → ____ = a ~24 h biological cycle.","Circadian rhythm = a ~24 h biological cycle."],["040.02.03.02:11","Body rhythm & sleep: fill in the gaps → Without time cues (____: daylight, meals, social contact) it ____ (24.5–25 h).","Without time cues (zeitgebers: daylight, meals, social contact) it free-runs at ~25 h (24.5–25 h)."],["040.02.03.02:01","Body rhythm & sleep: fill in the gaps → The ____ (suprachiasmatic nucleus, hypothalamus) is synchronised daily by the zeitgebers → it regulates temperature, melatonin and sleep drive.","The internal clock (suprachiasmatic nucleus, hypothalamus) is synchronised daily by the zeitgebers → it regulates temperature, melatonin and sleep drive."],["040.02.03.02:05","Body rhythm & sleep: fill in the gaps → ____; performance follows the temperature curve (the ____ is a small secondary dip).","Sleep is best when started as the temperature falls; performance follows the temperature curve (the post-lunch dip is a small secondary dip)."],["040.02.03.02:06","Body rhythm & sleep: fill in the gaps → ____ (cycle ~____, 4–6 cycles per night):","Sleep stages (cycle ~90 min, 4–6 cycles per night):"],["040.02.03.02:01","Body rhythm & sleep: fill in the gaps → ____ (drowsiness, light).","NREM stage 1 (drowsiness, light)."],["040.02.03.02:08","Body rhythm & sleep: fill in the gaps → ____ (light, ~50 % of sleep).","Stage 2 (light, ~50 % of sleep)."],["040.02.03.02:06","Body rhythm & sleep: fill in the gaps → ____ (deep, ____ — physical recovery; mostly in the first half of the night).","Stages 3–4 (deep, slow-wave sleep — physical recovery; mostly in the first half of the night)."],["040.02.03.02:07","Body rhythm & sleep: fill in the gaps → ____ (rapid eye movement: dreams, memory consolidation; brain active, muscles relaxed; periods get longer towards the morning).","REM (rapid eye movement: dreams, memory consolidation; brain active, muscles relaxed; periods get longer towards the morning)."],["040.02.03.02:08","Body rhythm & sleep: fill in the gaps → ____: physical and mental restoration.","Function of sleep: physical and mental restoration."],["040.02.03.02:08","Body rhythm & sleep: fill in the gaps → ____ → slower reactions, poor attention/vigilance, memory lapses, poor decisions, irritability, ____.","Insufficient sleep → slower reactions, poor attention/vigilance, memory lapses, poor decisions, irritability, micro-sleeps."],["040.02.03.04:09","Body rhythm & sleep: fill in the gaps → 17–19 h awake ≈ the impairment of ____ blood alcohol.","17–19 h awake ≈ the impairment of 0.5 ‰ blood alcohol."],["040.02.03.02:09","Body rhythm & sleep: fill in the gaps → ____ (8 h sleep ≈ 16 h awake).","1 h of sleep gives ~2 h of wakefulness credit (8 h sleep ≈ 16 h awake)."],["040.02.03.02:10","Body rhythm & sleep: fill in the gaps → Staying awake longer creates a ____. Without full recovery the debit is ____ over successive days (chronic fatigue) → recovery needs more than one night.","Staying awake longer creates a debit. Without full recovery the debit is cumulative over successive days (chronic fatigue) → recovery needs more than one night."],["040.02.03.02:11","Body rhythm & sleep: fill in the gaps → ____: about ____ ____ (≈ 1–1.5 days/h; faster westbound).","Time-zone adjustment: about 1 day per time zone (hour) crossed (≈ 1–1.5 days/h; faster westbound)."],["040.02.03.02:12","Body rhythm & sleep: fill in the gaps → ____: sleep disturbance, fatigue, reduced performance, digestive problems, mood changes. The body systems resynchronise at different rates (internal desynchronisation).","Jet lag (circadian dysrhythmia): sleep disturbance, fatigue, reduced performance, digestive problems, mood changes. The body systems resynchronise at different rates (internal desynchronisation)."],["040.02.03.02:13","Body rhythm & sleep: fill in the gaps → ____ (day lengthened, in the direction of the free-running rhythm) → ____ adaptation. ____ (day shortened, phase advance) → harder.","Westbound travel (day lengthened, in the direction of the free-running rhythm) → easier adaptation. Eastbound (day shortened, phase advance) → harder."],["040.02.03.02:14","Body rhythm & sleep: fill in the gaps → ____: vigilance decreases with ____ duty and at the circadian low (the \"window of circadian low\" ____) → the effects add up during night flights → the highest risk at the end of a night duty.","Circadian rhythm and duty: vigilance decreases with time on duty and at the circadian low (the \"window of circadian low\" 02:00–06:00) → the effects add up during night flights → the highest risk at the end of a night duty."],["040.02.03.02:01","Body rhythm & sleep: what do you remember about this? → For short stays, stay on home …","For short stays, stay on home time."],["040.02.03.02:11","Body rhythm & sleep: what do you remember about this? → Otherwise adopt local time quickly (daylight exposure, meals, …","Otherwise adopt local time quickly (daylight exposure, meals, activity)."],["040.02.03.02:01","Body rhythm & sleep: fill in the gaps → Controlled ____; good sleep hygiene (dark, quiet, cool room).","Controlled napping (< 30–40 min); good sleep hygiene (dark, quiet, cool room)."],["040.02.03.03:19","Body rhythm & sleep: what do you remember about this? → Avoid alcohol/caffeine near …","Avoid alcohol/caffeine near bedtime."],["040.02.03.02:01","Body rhythm & sleep: fill in the gaps → Melatonin/sleeping pills only with ____.","Melatonin/sleeping pills only with AME approval."],["040.02.03.03:01","Common ailments & barotrauma: fill in the gaps → The ____ connects the middle ear and the throat and ____.","The Eustachian tube connects the middle ear and the throat and equalises the pressure."],["040.02.03.03:05","Common ailments & barotrauma: fill in the gaps → The ____ (pressure changes, dry air, hypoxia).","The flight environment worsens minor symptoms (pressure changes, dry air, hypoxia)."],["040.02.03.03:01","Common ailments & barotrauma: fill in the gaps → ____ → a blocked Eustachian tube and sinuses → ____ ____, reduced performance, medication side effects → don't fly with a cold.","Cold/flu → a blocked Eustachian tube and sinuses → ear/sinus barotrauma, dental pain, reduced performance, medication side effects → don't fly with a cold."],["040.02.03.03:04","Common ailments & barotrauma: fill in the gaps → ____: after surgery or an injury, when regularly taking any medication, with a hospital admission, pregnancy, or new corrective lenses — ____ ____.","Seek AME/AeMC advice: after surgery or an injury, when regularly taking any medication, with a hospital admission, pregnancy, or new corrective lenses — and whenever unsure about fitness."],["040.02.03.02:01","Common ailments & barotrauma: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ ____ (pinch the nose and blow gently), slow descent rates, don't fly with a cold. If blocked: climb back up and descend slowly. Decongestants only if AME-approved.","Preventing/clearing pressure problems: swallow, yawn, Valsalva (pinch the nose and blow gently), slow descent rates, don't fly with a cold. If blocked: climb back up and descend slowly. Decongestants only if AME-approved."],["040.02.03.03:01","Common ailments & barotrauma: fill in the gaps → ____ = injury from a ____ between a gas-filled body cavity and the environment:","Barotrauma = injury from a pressure difference between a gas-filled body cavity and the environment:"],["040.02.03.03:01","Common ailments & barotrauma: fill in the gaps → ____: middle ear — pain, eardrum rupture.","Otic: middle ear — pain, eardrum rupture."],["040.02.03.02:01","Common ailments & barotrauma: fill in the gaps → ____: frontal/maxillary — sharp pain, bleeding.","Sinus: frontal/maxillary — sharp pain, bleeding."],["040.02.03.02:01","Common ailments & barotrauma: fill in the gaps → ____: gas expands at altitude → discomfort, pain → avoid gas-forming food and carbonated drinks.","Gastrointestinal: gas expands at altitude → discomfort, pain → avoid gas-forming food and carbonated drinks."],["040.02.03.03:07","Common ailments & barotrauma: fill in the gaps → ____: toothache from trapped air under fillings/in inflamed teeth → good dental care.","Aerodontalgia: toothache from trapped air under fillings/in inflamed teeth → good dental care."],["040.02.03.03:08","Common ailments & barotrauma: fill in the gaps → ____: the Eustachian tube acts like a ____. Air escapes easily in the climb (expanding), but during the descent the higher outside pressure ____ → active opening is needed.","Otic barotrauma is worse in the descent: the Eustachian tube acts like a one-way valve. Air escapes easily in the climb (expanding), but during the descent the higher outside pressure closes the tube → active opening is needed."],["040.02.03.03:11","Common ailments & barotrauma: fill in the gaps → ____: diarrhoea, vomiting, cramps → sudden ____, dehydration.","Gastrointestinal upsets: diarrhoea, vomiting, cramps → sudden incapacitation, dehydration."],["040.02.03.03:16","Common ailments & barotrauma: fill in the gaps → ____: contaminated food/water, poor hygiene (____ ____).","Sources: contaminated food/water, poor hygiene (salads, raw seafood, ice cubes, unpeeled fruit, undercooked meat)."],["040.02.03.02:01","Common ailments & barotrauma: fill in the gaps → ____: hygiene, \"boil it, cook it, peel it or forget it\", bottled water, and ____ at different times.","Precautions: hygiene, \"boil it, cook it, peel it or forget it\", bottled water, and pilots eat different meals at different times."],["040.02.03.02:01","Lifestyle health: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (overweight 25–29.9); BMI = mass (kg) / height² (m²).","Obesity: BMI ≥ 30 (overweight 25–29.9); BMI = mass (kg) / height² (m²)."],["040.02.03.03:13","Lifestyle health: fill in the gaps → Harmful effects: ____ ____, hypertension, sleep apnoea, reduced fitness, joint/back problems, reduced hypoxia tolerance.","Harmful effects: coronary heart disease, diabetes, reduced G-tolerance, hypertension, sleep apnoea, reduced fitness, joint/back problems, reduced hypoxia tolerance."],["040.02.03.03:12","Lifestyle health: fill in the gaps → ____: obesity, inactivity, poor diet, age, genetics.","Risk factors: obesity, inactivity, poor diet, age, genetics."],["040.02.03.04:09","Lifestyle health: fill in the gaps → ____ → high blood sugar.","Insulin resistance → high blood sugar."],["040.02.03.03:14","Lifestyle health: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (heart attack, stroke, kidneys, eyes) and ____ (neuropathy). Treatment with insulin or some oral drugs → hypoglycaemia risk → medical licence restrictions.","Complications: vascular (heart attack, stroke, kidneys, eyes) and neurological (neuropathy). Treatment with insulin or some oral drugs → hypoglycaemia risk → medical licence restrictions."],["040.02.03.03:14","Lifestyle health: fill in the gaps → ____ more than anyone else.","Pilots are not protected more than anyone else."],["040.02.03.03:15","Lifestyle health: fill in the gaps → ____ (unspecific back pain, ____) come from long sitting, vibration, and lifting bags.","Back problems (unspecific back pain, slipped disc) come from long sitting, vibration, and lifting bags."],["040.02.03.03:15","Lifestyle health: fill in the gaps → Prevention: ____, ____, fitness (core muscles), in-flight exercise, physiotherapy.","Prevention: good posture, lumbar support, fitness (core muscles), in-flight exercise, physiotherapy."],["040.02.03.03:16","Lifestyle health: fill in the gaps → ____: avoid contaminated food and liquids (tap water, ice, raw food, street food, dairy).","Food abroad: avoid contaminated food and liquids (tap water, ice, raw food, street food, dairy)."],["040.02.03.02:01","Lifestyle health: fill in the gaps → ____: bacteria (Salmonella, E. coli), viruses, parasites, toxins, poor hygiene/refrigeration.","Contamination sources: bacteria (Salmonella, E. coli), viruses, parasites, toxins, poor hygiene/refrigeration."],["040.02.03.03:18","Lifestyle health: fill in the gaps → ____: carbohydrates (complex), proteins, fats (limited), vitamins, minerals, fibre, water — balanced, regular meals.","Healthy diet: carbohydrates (complex), proteins, fats (limited), vitamins, minerals, fibre, water — balanced, regular meals."],["040.02.03.02:01","Lifestyle health: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (don't skip breakfast); complex carbohydrates rather than sugar.","Avoid hypoglycaemia: regular meals/snacks (don't skip breakfast); complex carbohydrates rather than sugar."],["040.02.03.02:14","Lifestyle health: fill in the gaps → ____: the dry cabin air (RH ~10–20 %) → dehydration → fatigue, headache, reduced performance, kidney stones. Drink ~2 l/day plus more on duty; limit coffee/alcohol.","Hydration: the dry cabin air (RH ~10–20 %) → dehydration → fatigue, headache, reduced performance, kidney stones. Drink ~2 l/day plus more on duty; limit coffee/alcohol."],["040.02.03.02:01","Tropical climates: fill in the gaps → ____: heat and humidity (heat stress, dehydration, sunburn, heat exhaustion/stroke), fatigue.","Problems: heat and humidity (heat stress, dehydration, sunburn, heat exhaustion/stroke), fatigue."],["040.02.03.05:02","Tropical climates: fill in the gaps → ____:","Causes of incapacitation:"],["040.02.03.02:01","Tropical climates: fill in the gaps → Poor ____; ____ (cholera, typhoid, hepatitis A).","Poor hygiene; contaminated water (cholera, typhoid, hepatitis A)."],["040.02.03.03:22","Tropical climates: fill in the gaps → ____ (____, dengue, yellow fever, Zika).","Insect-borne diseases (malaria, dengue, yellow fever, Zika)."],["040.02.03.03:22","Tropical climates: fill in the gaps → ____ (bilharzia — don't swim in fresh water).","Parasitic worms (bilharzia — don't swim in fresh water)."],["040.02.03.03:22","Tropical climates: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (yellow fever, hepatitis A/B, typhoid, rabies), ____ (AME-approved), insect repellent/nets/long clothing, bottled water, food hygiene, protected sex, avoid animals and fresh water.","Precautions: vaccinations (yellow fever, hepatitis A/B, typhoid, rabies), malaria prophylaxis (AME-approved), insect repellent/nets/long clothing, bottled water, food hygiene, protected sex, avoid animals and fresh water."],["040.02.03.02:01","Tropical climates: fill in the gaps → ____: ____, yellow fever, cholera, typhoid, hepatitis, HIV/AIDS, rabies, dengue.","Major killers: malaria, yellow fever, cholera, typhoid, hepatitis, HIV/AIDS, rabies, dengue."],["040.02.03.03:25","Tropical climates: fill in the gaps → ____ (spraying the cabin with insecticide) prevents disease-carrying insects from being transported between areas.","Disinsection (spraying the cabin with insecticide) prevents disease-carrying insects from being transported between areas."],["040.02.03.02:08","Intoxication: fill in the gaps → ____: bronchitis, cancer, reduced lung function.","Respiratory system: bronchitis, cancer, reduced lung function."],["040.02.03.04:01","Intoxication: fill in the gaps → ____: vasoconstriction, heart disease, arteriosclerosis.","Cardiovascular system: vasoconstriction, heart disease, arteriosclerosis."],["040.02.03.04:02","Intoxication: fill in the gaps → ____ binds to haemoglobin (~200× more than O₂) → ____ ____ (a smoker at sea level is already \"at ~5 000– 8 000 ft\").","CO binds to haemoglobin (~200× more than O₂) → lower hypoxia tolerance (a smoker at sea level is already \"at ~5 000– 8 000 ft\")."],["040.02.03.04:01","Intoxication: fill in the gaps → ____ and ____.","Reduced G-tolerance and night vision."],["040.02.03.04:02","Intoxication: fill in the gaps → ____: performance is degraded above about ____ (~3–4 cups of coffee) → nervousness, tremor, insomnia, dehydration. Also in ____, chocolate.","Caffeine: performance is degraded above about 300 mg/day (~3–4 cups of coffee) → nervousness, tremor, insomnia, dehydration. Also in tea, cola, energy drinks, chocolate."],["040.02.03.04:04","Intoxication: fill in the gaps → Limit for flight crew (EU Air Ops): ____ (0.09 mg/l breath) and ____ before duty (\"bottle to throttle\").","Limit for flight crew (EU Air Ops): 0.2 ‰ blood alcohol (0.09 mg/l breath) and no alcohol within 8 h before duty (\"bottle to throttle\")."],["040.02.03.04:05","Intoxication: fill in the gaps → Effects: ____ and judgement; ____ ____ (risk-taking); vision (blurred, nystagmus); balance disturbed, more vestibular illusions (alcohol in the endolymph, lasting up to ~48 h); sleep quality reduced (REM suppression); hypoxia effects intensified (alcohol acts like extra altitude).","Effects: impaired reasoning and judgement; reduced inhibitions/self-control (risk-taking); vision (blurred, nystagmus); balance disturbed, more vestibular illusions (alcohol in the endolymph, lasting up to ~48 h); sleep quality reduced (REM suppression); hypoxia effects intensified (alcohol acts like extra altitude)."],["040.02.03.04:01","Intoxication: fill in the gaps → ____ (sedatives, antihistamines, painkillers) → ____, unpredictable.","With other drugs (sedatives, antihistamines, painkillers) → potentiation, unpredictable."],["040.02.03.04:07","Intoxication: fill in the gaps → ____: drinking alone or early, smell on the breath, lateness/absence, mood swings, shaking hands, denial, memory blackouts, tolerance.","Alcoholism signs: drinking alone or early, smell on the breath, lateness/absence, mood swings, shaking hands, denial, memory blackouts, tolerance."],["040.02.03.04:01","Intoxication: fill in the gaps → ____: stress, loneliness (hotel life), boredom, family problems, genetics, availability, social pressure.","Risk factors: stress, loneliness (hotel life), boredom, family problems, genetics, availability, social pressure."],["040.02.03.04:09","Intoxication: fill in the gaps → ____ ≈ ____ (~½ pint of beer / 1 small glass of wine / 1 shot). ____ (≈ 0.1–0.15 ‰/h) — it can't be accelerated (coffee and a cold shower don't help).","Unit ≈ 8–10 g pure alcohol (~½ pint of beer / 1 small glass of wine / 1 shot). Elimination ~1 unit per hour (≈ 0.1–0.15 ‰/h) — it can't be accelerated (coffee and a cold shower don't help)."],["040.02.03.04:10","Intoxication: fill in the gaps → ____: max ____ (spread over ≥ 3 days, with alcohol-free days); not more than ~2–3 units/day.","Low-risk intake: max ~14 units/week (spread over ≥ 3 days, with alcohol-free days); not more than ~2–3 units/day."],["040.02.03.04:11","Intoxication: fill in the gaps → ____: don't cover up — speak to them, use ____, inform the chief pilot/AME. Treatment is possible and the licence can be regained.","Suspected alcoholic crew member: don't cover up — speak to them, use peer support programmes, inform the chief pilot/AME. Treatment is possible and the licence can be regained."],["040.02.03.04:12","Intoxication: fill in the gaps → ____: danger of ____ such as drowsiness, dizziness and blurred vision, masking of symptoms, and interactions.","Non-prescription drugs: danger of side effects such as drowsiness, dizziness and blurred vision, masking of symptoms, and interactions."],["040.02.03.04:13","Intoxication: fill in the gaps → ____ (hay fever, colds) → ____, slowed reactions. ____ → tachycardia, nervousness. Codeine, aspirin, etc.","Antihistamines (hay fever, colds) → sedation, slowed reactions. Decongestants → tachycardia, nervousness. Codeine, aspirin, etc."],["040.02.03.02:11","Intoxication: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ after taking any new drug until the reaction is known (____ or per the AME). Consult the AME.","New medication: don't fly after taking any new drug until the reaction is known (at least 24 h or per the AME). Consult the AME."],["040.02.03.04:15","Intoxication: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ ____.","General rule: if you need medication, you should consider yourself unfit to fly."],["040.02.03.04:16","Intoxication: fill in the gaps → ____ (if uncontained): mercury, ____, hydraulic fluid, de-icing fluid, oxygen, fuel, fire extinguishing agents, dangerous goods, radioactive materials.","Hazardous materials on board (if uncontained): mercury, batteries (lithium), hydraulic fluid, de-icing fluid, oxygen, fuel, fire extinguishing agents, dangerous goods, radioactive materials."],["040.02.03.04:01","Intoxication: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (PVC → HCl), insulation, wiring (HCN, CO), seats, carpets.","Toxic fumes when burning: cabin interior plastics (PVC → HCl), insulation, wiring (HCN, CO), seats, carpets."],["040.02.03.04:01","Intoxication: fill in the gaps → ____: contaminated bleed air (oil leak in the engine/APU → pyrolysed oil, TCP) → smell (\"dirty socks\"), smoke/haze → ____ incapacitation → ____, follow the smoke/fumes checklist.","Fume event: contaminated bleed air (oil leak in the engine/APU → pyrolysed oil, TCP) → smell (\"dirty socks\"), smoke/haze → headache, dizziness, nausea, confusion, irritation, incapacitation → oxygen masks 100 %, follow the smoke/fumes checklist."],["040.02.03.05:01","Incapacitation in flight: fill in the gaps → It is ____ (gradual, subtle — e.g. ____, hypoglycaemia, CO), because it isn't recognised.","It is most dangerous when insidious (gradual, subtle — e.g. hypoxia, hypoglycaemia, CO), because it isn't recognised."],["040.02.03.05:02","Incapacitation in flight: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ problems (most common), hypoxia, fainting, heart attack, stroke, epilepsy, hypoglycaemia, intoxication/fumes, severe pain (barotrauma), psychological problems.","Causes: gastrointestinal problems (most common), hypoxia, fainting, heart attack, stroke, epilepsy, hypoglycaemia, intoxication/fumes, severe pain (barotrauma), psychological problems."],["040.02.03.05:03","Incapacitation in flight: fill in the gaps → The crew must ____: monitoring, ____ ____ (no response to two communications, or a significant deviation → suspect incapacitation).","The crew must recognise and act promptly: monitoring, \"two- communication rule\" (no response to two communications, or a significant deviation → suspect incapacitation)."],["040.02.03.05:01","Incapacitation in flight: fill in the gaps → ____: 1. ____ of the aircraft and keep flying it. 2. ____ the incapacitated pilot (seat back, harness locked, oxygen) with cabin crew help. 3. Declare an emergency; land at the nearest suitable aerodrome. 4. Get assistance (cabin crew, a medical doctor on board).","Procedure: 1. Take control of the aircraft and keep flying it. 2. Secure the incapacitated pilot (seat back, harness locked, oxygen) with cabin crew help. 3. Declare an emergency; land at the nearest suitable aerodrome. 4. Get assistance (cabin crew, a medical doctor on board)."],["040.03.05.01:04","Personality, attitude & behaviour: fill in the gaps → ____ = a stable, lasting set of traits (largely ____ ____).","Personality = a stable, lasting set of traits (largely innate + formed in early childhood)."],["040.03.05.01:04","Personality, attitude & behaviour: fill in the gaps → ____ = a learned tendency to react (more changeable; formed by ____).","Attitude = a learned tendency to react (more changeable; formed by upbringing, education, experience)."],["040.03.05.01:04","Personality, attitude & behaviour: fill in the gaps → ____ = the observable action — a ____ ____ at significant moments (childhood, school, training). Good and bad habits are formed through behaviour.","Behaviour = the observable action — a product of personality, attitude and environment/situation at significant moments (childhood, school, training). Good and bad habits are formed through behaviour."],["040.03.05.01:06","Personality, attitude & behaviour: fill in the gaps → ____ can degrade crew performance (conflict, poor CRM).","Personality differences and selfish attitudes can degrade crew performance (conflict, poor CRM)."],["040.03.05.02:01","Personality, attitude & behaviour: fill in the gaps → ____: two axes — ____ and ____.","Eysenck's model: two axes — extraversion–introversion and stability–neuroticism (emotional instability)."],["040.03.05.02:01","Personality, attitude & behaviour: fill in the gaps → The ____ = a ____ (calm, sociable, confident, reliable). The Big-Five model (conscientiousness etc.) is also used.","The ideal pilot = a stable extravert (calm, sociable, confident, reliable). The Big-Five model (conscientiousness etc.) is also used."],["040.03.05.02:02","Personality, attitude & behaviour: fill in the gaps → ____ = how a person sees themselves. Change of personality/behaviour is possible only if it is consistent with or modifies the self-concept.","Self-concept = how a person sees themselves. Change of personality/behaviour is possible only if it is consistent with or modifies the self-concept."],["040.03.05.02:03","Personality, attitude & behaviour: fill in the gaps → ____ can be masked by ____ ____ (overcompensation).","Under-confidence can be masked by aggression and over- assertiveness (overcompensation)."],["040.03.05.02:04","Personality, attitude & behaviour: fill in the gaps → ____ = following rules/SOPs even when unobserved or inconvenient → the basis of ____ (no shortcuts, no violations).","Self-discipline = following rules/SOPs even when unobserved or inconvenient → the basis of flight safety (no shortcuts, no violations)."],["040.03.05.03:02","Personality, attitude & behaviour: fill in the gaps → ____:","Hazardous attitudes and antidotes:"],["040.03.05.03:01","Personality, attitude & behaviour – Anti-authority: what are the key facts?","Thought: \"Don't tell me\" · Antidote: follow the rules, they're usually right"],["040.03.05.01:02","Personality, attitude & behaviour – Impulsivity: what are the key facts?","Thought: \"Do something — quickly\" · Antidote: not so fast, think first"],["040.03.05.01:02","Personality, attitude & behaviour – Invulnerability: what are the key facts?","Thought: \"It won't happen to me\" · Antidote: it could happen to me"],["040.03.05.01:04","Personality, attitude & behaviour – Macho: what are the key facts?","Thought: \"I can do it\" · Antidote: taking chances is foolish"],["040.03.05.01:04","Personality, attitude & behaviour – Resignation: what are the key facts?","Thought: \"What's the use?\" · Antidote: I'm not helpless, I can make a difference"],["040.03.05.01:02","Personality, attitude & behaviour – Complacency: what are the key facts?","Thought: \"It's always fine\" · Antidote: stay vigilant, check"],["040.03.05.03:02","Personality, attitude & behaviour: fill in the gaps → ____: self-disciplined, stable, assertive but ____, open to criticism, good communication, situation awareness, a team player.","Ideal crew member: self-disciplined, stable, assertive but cooperative, open to criticism, good communication, situation awareness, a team player."],["040.03.05.01:01","Personality, attitude & behaviour: fill in the gaps → ____: it shapes decision-making, SOP adherence, communication and risk-taking.","Attitude affects the work in the cockpit: it shapes decision-making, SOP adherence, communication and risk-taking."],["040.02.03.02:03","Exam trap (Personality, attitude & behaviour): complete the fact → free-running rhythm ~____.","free-running rhythm ~25 h."],["040.02.03.02:09","Exam trap (Personality, attitude & behaviour): complete the fact → ____ sleep = ____ credit.","1 h sleep = 2 h credit."],["040.02.03.04:09","Exam trap (Personality, attitude & behaviour): complete the fact → Alcohol ____‰ / ____; elimination ~1 unit/h.","Alcohol 0.2 ‰ / 8 h; elimination ~1 unit/h."],["040.02.03.04:02","Exam trap (Personality, attitude & behaviour): complete the fact → Performance degraded above ~____g caffeine.","Performance degraded above ~300 mg caffeine."],["040.02.03.02:02","EASA LO 040.02.03.02.02 → Explain the term ‘circadian rhythm’.","Circadian rhythm = a ~24 h biological cycle. / Circadian rhythm and duty: vigilance decreases with time on duty and at the circadian low (the \"window of circadian low\" 02:00–06:00) → the effects add up during night flights → the highest risk at the end of a night duty."],["040.02.03.02:04","EASA LO 040.02.03.02.04 → Explain the significance of the ‘internal clock’ in regulating the normal circadian rhythm.","The internal clock (suprachiasmatic nucleus, hypothalamus) is synchronised daily by the zeitgebers → it regulates temperature, melatonin and sleep drive. / Jet lag (circadian dysrhythmia): sleep disturbance, fatigue, reduced performance, digestive problems, mood changes. The body systems resynchronise at different rates (internal desynchronisation)."],["040.02.03.02:15","EASA LO 040.02.03.02.15 → Describe the main effects of lack of sleep on an individual’s performance.","Minimum ~04:00–06:00 → lowest performance and alertness, easiest sleep."],["040.02.03.02:16","EASA LO 040.02.03.02.16 → List the possible strategies to cope with jet lag.","Jet lag (circadian dysrhythmia): sleep disturbance, fatigue, reduced performance, digestive problems, mood changes. The body systems resynchronise at different rates (internal desynchronisation)."],["040.02.03.03:02","EASA LO 040.02.03.03.02 → State that the in-flight environment may increase the severity of symptoms which may be minor while on the ground.","The flight environment worsens minor symptoms (pressure changes, dry air, hypoxia)."],["040.02.03.03:03","EASA LO 040.02.03.03.03 → List the negative effects of suffering from colds or flu on flight operations especially with regard to the middle ear, the sinuses, and the teeth.","Cold/flu → a blocked Eustachian tube and sinuses → ear/sinus barotrauma, dental pain, reduced performance, medication side effects → don't fly with a cold. / Otic: middle ear — pain, eardrum rupture."],["040.02.03.03:06","EASA LO 040.02.03.03.06 → Define ‘barotrauma’.","The flight environment worsens minor symptoms (pressure changes, dry air, hypoxia). / Barotrauma = injury from a pressure difference between a gas-filled body cavity and the environment:"],["040.02.03.03:09","EASA LO 040.02.03.03.09 → State the effects of gastrointestinal upsets that may occur during flight.","Gastrointestinal upsets: diarrhoea, vomiting, cramps → sudden incapacitation, dehydration."],["040.02.03.03:20","EASA LO 040.02.03.03.20 → State the importance of adequate hydration. X X X X X X Tropical climates","Causes of incapacitation:"],["040.02.03.03:21","EASA LO 040.02.03.03.21 → List the problems associated with operating in tropical climates.","Causes of incapacitation:"],["040.02.03.04:14","EASA LO 040.02.03.04.14 → Interpret the rules relevant to using (prescription or non-prescription) drugs that the pilot has not used before.","Non-prescription drugs: danger of side effects such as drowsiness, dizziness and blurred vision, masking of symptoms, and interactions."],["040.02.03.05:04","EASA LO 040.02.03.05.04 → Explain methods and procedures to cope with incapacitation in flight.","Causes of incapacitation:"],["040.03.05.01:01","EASA LO 040.03.05.01.01 → Describe the factors that determine an individual’s behaviour.","Hazardous attitudes and antidotes: / 1 h sleep = 2 h credit."],["040.03.05.01:03","EASA LO 040.03.05.01.03 → State the origin of personality and attitude.","Hazardous attitudes and antidotes: / 1 h sleep = 2 h credit."],["040.03.05.01:05","EASA LO 040.03.05.01.05 → Explain how behaviour is generally a product of personality, attitude and the environment to which one was exposed at significant moments (childhood, schooling and training).","Behaviour = the observable action — a product of personality, attitude and environment/situation at significant moments (childhood, school, training). Good and bad habits are formed through behaviour. / Hazardous attitudes and antidotes:"],["040.03.05.03:03","EASA LO 040.03.05.03.03 → Summarise how a person’s attitude influences their work in the flight crew compartment.","Hazardous attitudes and antidotes:"]],"44":[["081.05.01.01:01","Axes, planes & basics: fill in the gaps → ____ (nose–tail, through CG) → ____, controlled by ailerons/spoilers. ____ (wingtip–wingtip) → pitch, elevator/stabiliser. Normal axis (perpendicular to both) → yaw, rudder. All three pass through the CG.","Longitudinal axis (nose–tail, through CG) → roll, controlled by ailerons/spoilers. Lateral axis (wingtip–wingtip) → pitch, elevator/stabiliser. Normal axis (perpendicular to both) → yaw, rudder. All three pass through the CG."],["081.05.01.01:01","Axes, planes & basics: fill in the gaps → ____ θ: longitudinal axis vs. horizon. ____ Ø: lateral axis vs. horizon. ____: longitudinal axis vs. a reference direction.","Pitch angle θ: longitudinal axis vs. horizon. Bank angle Ø: lateral axis vs. horizon. Yaw angle: longitudinal axis vs. a reference direction."],["081.05.01.02:01","Axes, planes & basics: fill in the gaps → A control-surface deflection ____ of the whole aerofoil (wing/tail) → changes CL at the same α. Trailing edge ____ = more camber = more lift on that surface (and more drag).","A control-surface deflection changes the camber of the whole aerofoil (wing/tail) → changes CL at the same α. Trailing edge down = more camber = more lift on that surface (and more drag)."],["081.05.01.03:01","Axes, planes & basics: fill in the gaps → Deflecting a control surface also changes the ____ of that surface: TE down → higher local α → more lift, closer to the local stall α.","Deflecting a control surface also changes the local effective α of that surface: TE down → higher local α → more lift, closer to the local stall α."],["081.05.02.01:01","Pitch control: fill in the gaps → ____: TE up → tailplane produces more ____-load → nose-up pitching moment. ____: the whole surface rotates; more powerful, used at high speed/transonic (no elevator shock problems); often has an anti-balance tab for feel.","Elevator: TE up → tailplane produces more down-load → nose-up pitching moment. All-flying tail (stabilator): the whole surface rotates; more powerful, used at high speed/transonic (no elevator shock problems); often has an anti-balance tab for feel."],["081.05.02.02:01","Pitch control: fill in the gaps → ____ from the wing reduces the tailplane α: αtail = αwing + incidence difference − downwash angle ε. When α increases, ε also increases, so the tail sees a ____ ____. Flaps down → more downwash → nose-up tendency (depending on design); ground effect reduces downwash → nose-down tendency near the ground.","Downwash from the wing reduces the tailplane α: αtail = αwing + incidence difference − downwash angle ε. When α increases, ε also increases, so the tail sees a smaller change of α than the wing. Flaps down → more downwash → nose-up tendency (depending on design); ground effect reduces downwash → nose-down tendency near the ground."],["081.05.02.04:02","Pitch control: fill in the gaps → ____: more forward CG → longer arm between CG and centre of pressure → more ____ → more elevator up-deflection and stick force per g needed.","CG location: more forward CG → longer arm between CG and centre of pressure → more nose-down moment → more elevator up-deflection and stick force per g needed."],["081.05.02.04:02","Pitch control: fill in the gaps → The ____ is set by elevator authority: enough nose-up control must remain to rotate at VR and flare at landing (low speed, flaps, ground effect = worst case). Aft CG → less deflection, lighter forces, less stable.","The forward CG limit is set by elevator authority: enough nose-up control must remain to rotate at VR and flare at landing (low speed, flaps, ground effect = worst case). Aft CG → less deflection, lighter forces, less stable."],["081.05.02.05:01","Pitch control: fill in the gaps → ____: thrust line ____ the CG → nose-____ moment when power increases (e.g. under-wing engines); thrust line above the CG → nose-down moment with power (e.g. high-mounted/T-tail rear engines).","Engine thrust: thrust line below the CG → nose-up moment when power increases (e.g. under-wing engines); thrust line above the CG → nose-down moment with power (e.g. high-mounted/T-tail rear engines)."],["081.05.03.01:01","Yaw control: fill in the gaps → ____ deflection changes the fin's camber → side force on the fin → moment about the normal axis. Moment ∝ rudder deflection (and dynamic pressure). A ____ produces a fin side force that yaws the nose ____ (weathercock / directional stability) and so opposes the rudder.","Rudder deflection changes the fin's camber → side force on the fin → moment about the normal axis. Moment ∝ rudder deflection (and dynamic pressure). A sideslip produces a fin side force that yaws the nose back into the relative airflow (weathercock / directional stability) and so opposes the rudder."],["081.05.03.02:01","Yaw control: fill in the gaps → ____: at high IAS a full deflection would overstress the fin/aft fuselage. CAT aeroplanes limit deflection (or ratio changer / variable-stop) as ____; full travel remains available at low speed (engine failure → VMC).","Rudder limiting: at high IAS a full deflection would overstress the fin/aft fuselage. CAT aeroplanes limit deflection (or ratio changer / variable-stop) as IAS increases; full travel remains available at low speed (engine failure → VMC)."],["081.05.04.04:01","Roll control: fill in the gaps → ____: one goes down (more lift), the other up (less lift) → rolling moment. Adverse effects: ____ (more induced drag on the up-going wing), ____ at high speed (wing twist) and higher local α → tip stall risk at low speed.","Ailerons: one goes down (more lift), the other up (less lift) → rolling moment. Adverse effects: adverse yaw (more induced drag on the up-going wing), aileron reversal at high speed (wing twist) and higher local α → tip stall risk at low speed."],["081.05.04.01:04","Roll control: fill in the gaps → ____: outboard ones are powerful at low speed; above a given speed they are ____ to prevent over-control, exceeding structural limits and aeroelastic phenomena (____). Inboard (high-speed) ailerons act close to the stiff wing root.","Inboard + outboard ailerons: outboard ones are powerful at low speed; above a given speed they are locked to prevent over-control, exceeding structural limits and aeroelastic phenomena (flutter, divergence, aileron reversal). Inboard (high-speed) ailerons act close to the stiff wing root."],["081.05.04.01:05","Roll control: fill in the gaps → Use in: normal turns (roll in, then neutral), ____ ____ (wing down into wind), ____ (bank max 5° towards the live engine).","Use in: normal turns (roll in, then neutral), sideslip / crosswind landing (wing down into wind), engine failure (bank max 5° towards the live engine)."],["081.05.04.01:06","Roll control: fill in the gaps → ____ = rate of change of bank angle (°/s). Increases with aileron deflection, surface size & arm, IAS; decreases with wing span (roll damping), wing flexibility/aileron reversal and inertia.","Roll rate = rate of change of bank angle (°/s). Increases with aileron deflection, surface size & arm, IAS; decreases with wing span (roll damping), wing flexibility/aileron reversal and inertia."],["081.05.04.01:08","Roll control: fill in the gaps → ____: ailerons that also droop symmetrically as flaps. ____: both ailerons lowered with flap for more lift.","Flaperons: ailerons that also droop symmetrically as flaps. Aileron droop: both ailerons lowered with flap for more lift."],["081.05.04.03:01","Roll control: fill in the gaps → ____ as roll control: spoiler raised on the down-going wing → lift loss + drag on that side → roll ____ favourable (pro-turn) yaw; can be used with or instead of ailerons (no reversal problem at high speed).","Spoilers as roll control: spoiler raised on the down-going wing → lift loss + drag on that side → roll and favourable (pro-turn) yaw; can be used with or instead of ailerons (no reversal problem at high speed)."],["081.05.04.01:01","Roll control: fill in the gaps → ____: the up-going wing has more lift → more ____ ____ → yaws the nose away from the turn.","Adverse yaw: the up-going wing has more lift → more induced drag → yaws the nose away from the turn."],["081.05.04.05:01","Roll control: fill in the gaps → Reducing adverse yaw: ____ (nose of the up-going aileron protrudes into airflow → extra drag on the down-going wing), ____ (up-going aileron deflects more than the down-going), ____, roll spoilers.","Reducing adverse yaw: Frise ailerons (nose of the up-going aileron protrudes into airflow → extra drag on the down-going wing), differential ailerons (up-going aileron deflects more than the down-going), rudder–aileron interconnect, roll spoilers."],["081.05.05.01:02","Roll/yaw interaction: fill in the gaps → ____: bank → sideslip towards the lower wing → fin/dihedral effect → yaw towards the lower wing.","Secondary effect of roll: bank → sideslip towards the lower wing → fin/dihedral effect → yaw towards the lower wing."],["081.05.05.01:02","Roll/yaw interaction: fill in the gaps → ____: yaw → outer wing moves faster (more lift) + sideslip with dihedral effect → ____ ____.","Secondary effect of yaw: yaw → outer wing moves faster (more lift) + sideslip with dihedral effect → roll in the direction of yaw."],["081.05.06.01:03","Reducing control forces: fill in the gaps → ____ reduces hinge moment/stick force: ____ ____ (part of surface ahead of hinge at the tip), internal balance (seal/plate ahead of hinge in a vented chamber), set-back hinge.","Aerodynamic balance reduces hinge moment/stick force: horn balance (part of surface ahead of hinge at the tip), internal balance (seal/plate ahead of hinge in a vented chamber), set-back hinge."],["081.05.06.01:04","Reducing control forces: fill in the gaps → ____: moves ____ to the surface → reduces force. ____: moves in the same direction → increases force (feel, e.g. stabilator). Servo tab: pilot moves only the tab, which flies the surface. Spring tab: servo action only at high speed (spring deflection).","Balance tab: moves opposite to the surface → reduces force. Anti-balance tab: moves in the same direction → increases force (feel, e.g. stabilator). Servo tab: pilot moves only the tab, which flies the surface. Spring tab: servo action only at high speed (spring deflection)."],["081.05.06.02:01","Reducing control forces: fill in the gaps → ____ controls: pilot provides part of the force, feel is natural. ____ (irreversible): hydraulics carry all the load, no aerodynamic feedback → ____ (springs, q-feel) needed so forces rise with speed and the pilot cannot overstress the aircraft.","Power-assisted controls: pilot provides part of the force, feel is natural. Fully powered (irreversible): hydraulics carry all the load, no aerodynamic feedback → artificial feel (springs, q-feel) needed so forces rise with speed and the pilot cannot overstress the aircraft."],["081.05.07.01:02","Fly-by-wire control laws: fill in the gaps → Normal pitch law in level flight typically commands ____ ____ or ____ (C*), with automatic trim. Using the CG position in the FBW computer allows flight with a more aft CG (less trim drag, better fuel burn) while artificial stability is maintained.","Normal pitch law in level flight typically commands load factor (g) or pitch rate (C*), with automatic trim. Using the CG position in the FBW computer allows flight with a more aft CG (less trim drag, better fuel burn) while artificial stability is maintained."],["081.05.07.01:03","Fly-by-wire control laws: fill in the gaps → In case of failure: ____ (alternate law with fewer/no protections, direct law = stick directly to surface, mechanical back-up).","In case of failure: degraded laws (alternate law with fewer/no protections, direct law = stick directly to surface, mechanical back-up)."],["081.05.07.01:04","Fly-by-wire control laws: fill in the gaps → ____: limit cannot be exceeded (e.g. max α, bank, load factor). ____: limit can be exceeded with extra pilot force/awareness.","Hard protection: limit cannot be exceeded (e.g. max α, bank, load factor). Soft protection: limit can be exceeded with extra pilot force/awareness."],["081.05.06.01:01","Trimming: fill in the gaps → Purpose: reduce the ____ in steady flight after changes of speed, power, CG, configuration. A ____ changes the zero-force position; balance tabs only reduce force during deflection.","Purpose: reduce the stick force to zero in steady flight after changes of speed, power, CG, configuration. A trim tab changes the zero-force position; balance tabs only reduce force during deflection."],["081.05.08.03:03","Trimming: fill in the gaps → ____ moves opposite to the desired elevator deflection (nose-up trim = tab down → elevator up); indication = trim wheel / position indicator.","Trim tab moves opposite to the desired elevator deflection (nose-up trim = tab down → elevator up); indication = trim wheel / position indicator."],["081.05.08.03:02","Trimming: fill in the gaps → ____ (trimmable horizontal stabiliser, THS): whole stabiliser incidence changes (jackscrew). Advantages: large trim range, elevator stays streamlined (full elevator authority, less drag); disadvantages: complex, heavy, runaway/jam is critical. Flight deck: trim wheel + ____ for take-off.","Stabiliser trim (trimmable horizontal stabiliser, THS): whole stabiliser incidence changes (jackscrew). Advantages: large trim range, elevator stays streamlined (full elevator authority, less drag); disadvantages: complex, heavy, runaway/jam is critical. Flight deck: trim wheel + green band for take-off."],["081.05.08.03:04","Trimming: fill in the gaps → ____ depends on CG (and flap/thrust): forward CG → more nose-up trim. Wrong setting: too nose-____ → heavy rotation, delayed lift-off; too nose-____ → early/over-rotation, tail strike risk. Take-off config warning if outside green band.","Take-off setting depends on CG (and flap/thrust): forward CG → more nose-up trim. Wrong setting: too nose-down → heavy rotation, delayed lift-off; too nose-up → early/over-rotation, tail strike risk. Take-off config warning if outside green band."],["081.05.08.03:06","Trimming: fill in the gaps → ____ → counteract with elevator, trim cut-out switches. ____: limited elevator authority; take-off/landing/go-around may need higher speeds, altered flap setting; a nose-down jam on go-around is critical (large nose-up force from thrust).","Runaway → counteract with elevator, trim cut-out switches. Jammed stabiliser: limited elevator authority; take-off/landing/go-around may need higher speeds, altered flap setting; a nose-down jam on go-around is critical (large nose-up force from thrust)."],["081.05.01.01:02","EASA LO 081.05.01.01.02 → — Define: — pitch angle; — bank angle (Ø); — yaw angle.","Pitch angle θ: longitudinal axis vs. horizon. Bank angle Ø: lateral axis vs. horizon. Yaw angle: longitudinal axis vs. a reference direction. / Longitudinal axis (nose–tail, through CG) → roll, controlled by ailerons/spoilers. Lateral axis (wingtip–wingtip) → pitch, elevator/stabiliser. Normal axis (perpendicular to both) → yaw, rudder. All three pass through the CG."],["081.05.01.01:03","EASA LO 081.05.01.01.03 → Describe the motion about the three axes.","Longitudinal axis (nose–tail, through CG) → roll, controlled by ailerons/spoilers. Lateral axis (wingtip–wingtip) → pitch, elevator/stabiliser. Normal axis (perpendicular to both) → yaw, rudder. All three pass through the CG."],["081.05.01.01:04","EASA LO 081.05.01.01.04 → Name and describe the devices that control these motions.","Longitudinal axis (nose–tail, through CG) → roll, controlled by ailerons/spoilers. Lateral axis (wingtip–wingtip) → pitch, elevator/stabiliser. Normal axis (perpendicular to both) → yaw, rudder. All three pass through the CG."],["081.05.02.04:01","EASA LO 081.05.02.04.01 → Explain the relationship between elevator deflection and CG location to produce a given aeroplane response.","CG location: more forward CG → longer arm between CG and centre of pressure → more nose-down moment → more elevator up-deflection and stick force per g needed."],["081.05.04.01:03","EASA LO 081.05.04.01.03 → Explain why some aeroplanes have inboard and outboard ailerons.","Inboard + outboard ailerons: outboard ones are powerful at low speed; above a given speed they are locked to prevent over-control, exceeding structural limits and aeroelastic phenomena (flutter, divergence, aileron reversal). Inboard (high-speed) ailerons act close to the stiff wing root."],["081.05.04.01:07","EASA LO 081.05.04.01.07 → List the factors that affect roll rate.","Roll rate = rate of change of bank angle (°/s). Increases with aileron deflection, surface size & arm, IAS; decreases with wing span (roll damping), wing flexibility/aileron reversal and inertia."],["081.05.05.01:01","EASA LO 081.05.05.01.01 → Explain the secondary effect of roll.","Secondary effect of yaw: yaw → outer wing moves faster (more lift) + sideslip with dihedral effect → roll in the direction of yaw. / Secondary effect of roll: bank → sideslip towards the lower wing → fin/dihedral effect → yaw towards the lower wing."],["081.05.06.01:02","EASA LO 081.05.06.01.02 → Describe the working principle of the horn balance.","Aerodynamic balance reduces hinge moment/stick force: horn balance (part of surface ahead of hinge at the tip), internal balance (seal/plate ahead of hinge in a vented chamber), set-back hinge."],["081.05.06.02:02","EASA LO 081.05.06.02.02 → Describe power-assisted controls.","Power-assisted controls: pilot provides part of the force, feel is natural. Fully powered (irreversible): hydraulics carry all the load, no aerodynamic feedback → artificial feel (springs, q-feel) needed so forces rise with speed and the pilot cannot overstress the aircraft."],["081.05.06.02:03","EASA LO 081.05.06.02.03 → Describe the advantages of artificial feel in fully powered control.","Power-assisted controls: pilot provides part of the force, feel is natural. Fully powered (irreversible): hydraulics carry all the load, no aerodynamic feedback → artificial feel (springs, q-feel) needed so forces rise with speed and the pilot cannot overstress the aircraft."],["081.05.07.01:01","EASA LO 081.05.07.01.01 → Explain which parameters may be controlled in level flight with the pitch control law.","Normal pitch law in level flight typically commands load factor (g) or pitch rate (C*), with automatic trim. Using the CG position in the FBW computer allows flight with a more aft CG (less trim drag, better fuel burn) while artificial stability is maintained. / In case of failure: degraded laws (alternate law with fewer/no protections, direct law = stick directly to surface, mechanical back-up)."],["081.05.08.01:02","EASA LO 081.05.08.01.02 → Explain the difference between a trim tab and the various balance tabs.","Purpose: reduce the stick force to zero in steady flight after changes of speed, power, CG, configuration. A trim tab changes the zero-force position; balance tabs only reduce force during deflection. / Trim tab moves opposite to the desired elevator deflection (nose-up trim = tab down → elevator up); indication = trim wheel / position indicator."],["081.05.08.02:01","EASA LO 081.05.08.02.01 → Describe the working principle of a trim tab including cockpit indications.","Trim tab moves opposite to the desired elevator deflection (nose-up trim = tab down → elevator up); indication = trim wheel / position indicator."],["081.05.08.03:01","EASA LO 081.05.08.03.01 → Describe the working principle of a stabiliser trim including the flight deck indications.","Stabiliser trim (trimmable horizontal stabiliser, THS): whole stabiliser incidence changes (jackscrew). Advantages: large trim range, elevator stays streamlined (full elevator authority, less drag); disadvantages: complex, heavy, runaway/jam is critical. Flight deck: trim wheel + green band for take-off."],["081.05.08.03:05","EASA LO 081.05.08.03.05 → Discuss the effects of jammed and runaway stabiliser.","Stabiliser trim (trimmable horizontal stabiliser, THS): whole stabiliser incidence changes (jackscrew). Advantages: large trim range, elevator stays streamlined (full elevator authority, less drag); disadvantages: complex, heavy, runaway/jam is critical. Flight deck: trim wheel + green band for take-off. / Runaway → counteract with elevator, trim cut-out switches. Jammed stabiliser: limited elevator authority; take-off/landing/go-around may need higher speeds, altered flap setting; a nose-down jam on go-around is critical (large nose-up force from thrust)."]],"45":[["033.03.01.01:01","Fuel planning — general: fill in the gaps → ____ mass = volume × density (specific gravity, SG). 1 US gal = 3.785 l, 1 Imp gal = 4.546 l, 1 kg = 2.205 lb. Typical SG: ____, ____ (kg/l). Example: 2 000 l JET A-1 × 0.8 = 1 600 kg.","Conversions: mass = volume × density (specific gravity, SG). 1 US gal = 3.785 l, 1 Imp gal = 4.546 l, 1 kg = 2.205 lb. Typical SG: AVGAS 0.72, JET A-1 0.79–0.80 (kg/l). Example: 2 000 l JET A-1 × 0.8 = 1 600 kg."],["033.03.01.01:02","Fuel planning — general: fill in the gaps → ____: fuel flow depends on power/thrust setting, altitude (jet: less fuel per NM at high level), temperature and mass. Read tables/graphs for cruise (CAP 697 SEP/MEP/MRJT).","Flight manual data: fuel flow depends on power/thrust setting, altitude (jet: less fuel per NM at high level), temperature and mass. Read tables/graphs for cruise (CAP 697 SEP/MEP/MRJT)."],["033.03.01.01:04","Fuel planning — general: fill in the gaps → ____ = usable fuel ÷ fuel flow. ____ = endurance × ground speed. ____ = time × fuel flow (+ reserves).","Endurance = usable fuel ÷ fuel flow. Range = endurance × ground speed. Required fuel = time × fuel flow (+ reserves)."],["033.03.01.01:07","Fuel planning — general: fill in the gaps → ____ (air vs ground distance): ____. Headwind → NAM > NGM → more fuel. Many MRJT tables use NAM, so always convert first.","NAM ↔ NGM (air vs ground distance): NAM = NGM × TAS ÷ GS. Headwind → NAM > NGM → more fuel. Many MRJT tables use NAM, so always convert first."],["033.03.01.01:05","Fuel planning — general: fill in the gaps → ____ fuel: compute trip time from forecast wind (GS), then add taxi, contingency, alternate, final reserve etc.","VFR/IFR flight fuel: compute trip time from forecast wind (GS), then add taxi, contingency, alternate, final reserve etc."],["033.03.01.01:06","Fuel planning — general: fill in the gaps → ____: the ____ must still be in the tanks on landing; a pilot must declare ____ when any change would make landing with less than final reserve likely, and \"MAYDAY FUEL\" when the calculated usable fuel at landing is less than final reserve.","Minimum remaining fuel on arrival: the final reserve fuel must still be in the tanks on landing; a pilot must declare \"MINIMUM FUEL\" when any change would make landing with less than final reserve likely, and \"MAYDAY FUEL\" when the calculated usable fuel at landing is less than final reserve."],["033.03.02.01:01","Components of the fuel plan (CAT, basic fuel scheme) – Taxi fuel: what are the key facts?","Start-up, taxi, APU use before take-off; from AFM tables (local conditions, expected taxi time)."],["033.03.02.01:01","Components of the fuel plan (CAT, basic fuel scheme) – Trip fuel: what are the key facts?","Take-off & climb to cruise, cruise, descent, approach and landing at destination (incl. arrival procedure & SID). From AFM/CAP 697 tables."],["033.03.02.02:01","Components of the fuel plan (CAT, basic fuel scheme) – Contingency fuel: what are the key facts?","Unforeseen factors (wind/temperature deviations, ATC routing, level changes, mass). Typically the higher of 5 % of planned trip fuel (or of the remaining trip fuel from the point of re-planning) or 5 min at holding speed at 1 500 ft above destination in ISA."],["033.03.02.03:03","Components of the fuel plan (CAT, basic fuel scheme) – Destination alternate fuel: what are the key facts?","Missed approach at destination, climb, cruise, descent, approach and landing at the alternate. Two alternates → fuel for the more distant one."],["033.03.02.03:05","Components of the fuel plan (CAT, basic fuel scheme) – Final reserve fuel: what are the key facts?","Turbine aeroplanes: 30 min at holding speed at 1 500 ft above aerodrome elevation (ISA). Piston aeroplanes: 45 min. Use mass at arrival at alternate/destination."],["033.03.02.01:01","Components of the fuel plan (CAT, basic fuel scheme) – Additional fuel: what are the key facts?","Only if the above is not enough, e.g. to allow an engine failure/depressurisation at the most critical point, or to hold 15 min at 1 500 ft over destination when no destination alternate is required (isolated aerodrome see 033-08)."],["033.03.02.01:01","Components of the fuel plan (CAT, basic fuel scheme) – Extra fuel: what are the key facts?","At the commander's discretion (expected delays, weather, tankering)."],["033.03.02.03:05","Components of the fuel plan (CAT, basic fuel scheme): fill in the gaps → ____ = contingency + alternate + final reserve + additional fuel.","Reserve fuel = contingency + alternate + final reserve + additional fuel."],["033.03.02.04:03","Components of the fuel plan (CAT, basic fuel scheme): fill in the gaps → ____: carrying fuel increases mass → higher fuel flow for the whole trip. Rule of thumb for jets: roughly ____ ____ is burnt just to carry it. The planning tables show the burn-off penalty per 1 000 kg.","Extra fuel penalty: carrying fuel increases mass → higher fuel flow for the whole trip. Rule of thumb for jets: roughly 3–4 % of the extra fuel per hour of flight is burnt just to carry it. The planning tables show the burn-off penalty per 1 000 kg."],["033.03.02.03:05","Components of the fuel plan (CAT, basic fuel scheme): fill in the gaps → ____ = taxi + trip + contingency + alternate + final reserve + additional + extra. ____ = block fuel − taxi fuel. Check that TOM and landing mass stay within limits (tank capacity, MTOM, MLM) — limiting item may reduce extra fuel.","Total (block) fuel = taxi + trip + contingency + alternate + final reserve + additional + extra. Take-off fuel = block fuel − taxi fuel. Check that TOM and landing mass stay within limits (tank capacity, MTOM, MLM) — limiting item may reduce extra fuel."],["033.03.02.02:01","Completing the fuel plan: what do you remember about this? → Trip fuel from nav plan legs (leg time × FF, or CAP 697 simplified …","Trip fuel from nav plan legs (leg time × FF, or CAP 697 simplified tables with NAM, mass, level)."],["033.03.02.02:01","Completing the fuel plan: what do you remember about this? → Contingency: 5 % of trip (check vs 5 min …","Contingency: 5 % of trip (check vs 5 min holding)."],["033.03.02.03:03","Completing the fuel plan: what do you remember about this? → Alternate fuel from alternate planning chart (distance, wind, …","Alternate fuel from alternate planning chart (distance, wind, mass)."],["033.03.02.03:05","Completing the fuel plan: what do you remember about this? → Final reserve from holding table (landing …","Final reserve from holding table (landing mass)."],["033.03.02.05:02","Completing the fuel plan: fill in the gaps → Add additional/extra, then taxi → ____; enter in the fuel section of the navigation plan with times.","Add additional/extra, then taxi → block fuel; enter in the fuel section of the navigation plan with times."],["033.03.01.01:03","EASA LO 033.03.01.01.03 → Calculate the attainable flight time/range from given average fuel flow/consumption and available amount of fuel.","Endurance = usable fuel ÷ fuel flow. Range = endurance × ground speed. Required fuel = time × fuel flow (+ reserves)."],["033.03.01.01:08","EASA LO 033.03.01.01.08 → Calculate the required fuel for an IFR flight from given forecast meteorological conditions.","VFR/IFR flight fuel: compute trip time from forecast wind (GS), then add taxi, contingency, alternate, final reserve etc."],["033.03.02.02:02","EASA LO 033.03.02.02.02 → Determine the trip fuel for the flight by using data from the fuel tables or graphs from the flight manual.","Flight manual data: fuel flow depends on power/thrust setting, altitude (jet: less fuel per NM at high level), temperature and mass. Read tables/graphs for cruise (CAP 697 SEP/MEP/MRJT)."],["033.03.02.03:01","EASA LO 033.03.02.03.01 → Explain the reasons for having contingency fuel.","Reserve fuel = contingency + alternate + final reserve + additional fuel. / VFR/IFR flight fuel: compute trip time from forecast wind (GS), then add taxi, contingency, alternate, final reserve etc."],["033.03.02.03:02","EASA LO 033.03.02.03.02 → Calculate the contingency fuel according to the applicable operational requirements.","Reserve fuel = contingency + alternate + final reserve + additional fuel."],["033.03.02.03:04","EASA LO 033.03.02.03.04 → Calculate the alternate fuel in accordance with the applicable operational requirements and relevant data from the navigation plan and the flight manual.","Reserve fuel = contingency + alternate + final reserve + additional fuel. / Add additional/extra, then taxi → block fuel; enter in the fuel section of the navigation plan with times."],["033.03.02.03:06","EASA LO 033.03.02.03.06 → Calculate the final reserve fuel for an aircraft in accordance with the applicable operational requirements and by using relevant data from the flight manual.","Reserve fuel = contingency + alternate + final reserve + additional fuel."],["033.03.02.03:07","EASA LO 033.03.02.03.07 → Explain the reasons and regulations for having additional fuel.","Reserve fuel = contingency + alternate + final reserve + additional fuel. / Total (block) fuel = taxi + trip + contingency + alternate + final reserve + additional + extra. Take-off fuel = block fuel − taxi fuel. Check that TOM and landing mass stay within limits (tank capacity, MTOM, MLM) — limiting item may reduce extra fuel."],["033.03.02.03:08","EASA LO 033.03.02.03.08 → Calculate the additional fuel for a flight in accordance with the applicable operational requirements.","Reserve fuel = contingency + alternate + final reserve + additional fuel."],["033.03.02.05:01","EASA LO 033.03.02.05.01 → Calculate the total fuel required for a given flight.","Add additional/extra, then taxi → block fuel; enter in the fuel section of the navigation plan with times."]],"46":[["021.06.01.01:01","Pneumatic supply on piston aircraft: fill in the gaps → Air for pneumatic systems comes from an ____ ____ or a vacuum pump (suction, typically ____, shown on a suction gauge).","Air for pneumatic systems comes from an engine-driven compressor (pressure pump) or a vacuum pump (suction, typically 4.5–5.5 inHg, shown on a suction gauge)."],["021.06.01.01:02","Pneumatic supply on piston aircraft: fill in the gaps → Needed for ____ (air-driven gyros: attitude indicator, DI), ____ (cabin heat via exhaust heat exchanger/ram air) and ____ (inflatable pneumatic boots use pressure to inflate, vacuum to hold them flat).","Needed for instrumentation (air-driven gyros: attitude indicator, DI), heating (cabin heat via exhaust heat exchanger/ram air) and de-icing (inflatable pneumatic boots use pressure to inflate, vacuum to hold them flat)."],["021.06.01.01:01","Pneumatic supply on piston aircraft: what do you remember about this? → Failure → gyro instruments slowly become unreliable (turn coordinator …","Failure → gyro instruments slowly become unreliable (turn coordinator is usually electric as back-up)."],["021.06.03.01:01","Pressurisation & air conditioning: fill in the gaps → The system controls ____ (fresh-air flow), ____ and ____ (cabin altitude). Humidity: very dry air at altitude; water separator downstream of the air-cycle machine removes water to prevent fog/ice; some aircraft add humidifiers.","The system controls ventilation (fresh-air flow), temperature and pressure (cabin altitude). Humidity: very dry air at altitude; water separator downstream of the air-cycle machine removes water to prevent fog/ice; some aircraft add humidifiers."],["021.06.03.01:03","Pressurisation & air conditioning: fill in the gaps → ____: pneumatic (bleed-air) system as source → ____ + ____ (controls how much air leaves) → pressure controller (auto/manual) → positive (excessive) differential pressure-relief valve (protects structure against over-pressure) → negative differential pressure-relief valve (lets outside air in when outside pressure > cabin pressure, e.g. fast descent) + dump valve/ditching function.","Pressurisation components: pneumatic (bleed-air) system as source → outflow valve + actuator (controls how much air leaves) → pressure controller (auto/manual) → positive (excessive) differential pressure-relief valve (protects structure against over-pressure) → negative differential pressure-relief valve (lets outside air in when outside pressure > cabin pressure, e.g. fast descent) + dump valve/ditching function."],["021.06.03.01:04","Pressurisation & air conditioning: fill in the gaps → ____: hot bleed air → primary heat exchanger (ram air) → ____ (raises pressure & temperature) → secondary heat exchanger → ____ (expansion → strong cooling, drives the compressor) → water separator → mix manifold. Pack-valve and ram-air doors regulate.","Air-conditioning pack (air-cycle machine, bootstrap): hot bleed air → primary heat exchanger (ram air) → compressor (raises pressure & temperature) → secondary heat exchanger → turbine (expansion → strong cooling, drives the compressor) → water separator → mix manifold. Pack-valve and ram-air doors regulate."],["021.06.03.01:05","Pressurisation & air conditioning: fill in the gaps → ____: hot bleed air added downstream of the packs to fine-tune the temperature of each cabin zone (packs deliver air for the coldest zone).","Hot trim air: hot bleed air added downstream of the packs to fine-tune the temperature of each cabin zone (packs deliver air for the coldest zone)."],["021.06.03.01:06","Pressurisation & air conditioning: fill in the gaps → ____ = pressure altitude equivalent to cabin pressure; cabin vertical speed = rate of change of cabin altitude (typ. max ____, ____); differential pressure (ΔP) = cabin − outside pressure; ground pressurisation = slight pressurisation on ground before take-off to avoid pressure bumps at rotation.","Definitions: cabin altitude = pressure altitude equivalent to cabin pressure; cabin vertical speed = rate of change of cabin altitude (typ. max ~500 ft/min up, ~300 ft/min down); differential pressure (ΔP) = cabin − outside pressure; ground pressurisation = slight pressurisation on ground before take-off to avoid pressure bumps at rotation."],["021.06.03.01:09","Pressurisation & air conditioning: fill in the gaps → ____: constant inflow from packs; the controller modulates the ____ → closing it raises cabin pressure (lower cabin altitude), opening it lowers pressure.","Principle: constant inflow from packs; the controller modulates the outflow valve → closing it raises cabin pressure (lower cabin altitude), opening it lowers pressure."],["021.06.03.01:08","Pressurisation & air conditioning: fill in the gaps → ____: pilot positions the outflow valve directly (open/close switch) while watching cabin altitude, rate and ΔP.","Emergency/manual mode: pilot positions the outflow valve directly (open/close switch) while watching cabin altitude, rate and ΔP."],["021.06.03.01:01","Pressurisation & air conditioning: fill in the gaps → ____: uses selected cruise level, landing field elevation and aircraft altitude to schedule a cabin-altitude profile; normally two channels (auto 1/2) + manual.","Electronic cabin-pressure controller: uses selected cruise level, landing field elevation and aircraft altitude to schedule a cabin-altitude profile; normally two channels (auto 1/2) + manual."],["021.06.03.01:10","Pressurisation & air conditioning: fill in the gaps → ____ is determined by the ____ certified ΔP (structure) together with the max allowed cabin altitude (typ. ____ for passenger comfort/hypoxia).","Maximum operating altitude is determined by the max certified ΔP (structure) together with the max allowed cabin altitude (typ. 8 000 ft for passenger comfort/hypoxia)."],["021.06.03.01:11","Pressurisation & air conditioning: fill in the gaps → Typical ____ of large transport aeroplanes: ____ (e.g. 8.6 psi). Cabin altitude, max ΔP and max aeroplane altitude are linked: at max altitude, outside pressure + max ΔP = cabin pressure of ≈ 8 000 ft.","Typical max ΔP of large transport aeroplanes: ≈ 8–9 psi (e.g. 8.6 psi). Cabin altitude, max ΔP and max aeroplane altitude are linked: at max altitude, outside pressure + max ΔP = cabin pressure of ≈ 8 000 ft."],["021.06.03.01:12","Pressurisation & air conditioning: fill in the gaps → ____: when ____ → aural (intermittent horn) and visual \"CABIN ALTITUDE\" warning → oxygen masks on, emergency descent. Passenger masks drop automatically at ____ cabin altitude.","Warning: when cabin altitude exceeds 10 000 ft → aural (intermittent horn) and visual \"CABIN ALTITUDE\" warning → oxygen masks on, emergency descent. Passenger masks drop automatically at ~14 000 ft cabin altitude."],["021.06.03.01:08","Pressurisation & air conditioning: fill in the gaps → ____: cabin altitude, cabin rate of climb/descent, ΔP, outflow-valve position, landing elevation, mode (auto/manual), warnings/cautions.","Indications: cabin altitude, cabin rate of climb/descent, ΔP, outflow-valve position, landing elevation, mode (auto/manual), warnings/cautions."],["021.06.03.01:14","Pressurisation & air conditioning: fill in the gaps → ____ (e.g. B787, no bleed system): electrically driven cabin compressors take in ram air; more efficient (no bleed penalty on engines), independent of engine power setting, but large electrical demand; bleed systems depend on engine rpm and reduce engine thrust/efficiency.","Bleed-air vs electric air conditioning (e.g. B787, no bleed system): electrically driven cabin compressors take in ram air; more efficient (no bleed penalty on engines), independent of engine power setting, but large electrical demand; bleed systems depend on engine rpm and reduce engine thrust/efficiency."],["021.06.03.01:12","Exam trap (Pressurisation & air conditioning): complete the fact → Cabin-altitude warning at ____, masks drop at ~____.","Cabin-altitude warning at 10 000 ft, masks drop at ~14 000 ft."]],"47":[["050.08.01.01:01","Climatic zones: fill in the gaps → ____: three cells per hemisphere — ____ (0– 30°: rising air at the equator, sinking at the subtropical highs), ____ (30–60°, westerlies) and polar cell. Surface: trade winds (NE/SE), westerlies, polar easterlies. Upper level: subtropical jet (~30°, ~FL 400) and polar-front jet (~40–60°, ~FL 300). Tropopause ≈ 16–18 km at the equator, 8–10 km at the poles.","General circulation: three cells per hemisphere — Hadley (0– 30°: rising air at the equator, sinking at the subtropical highs), Ferrel (30–60°, westerlies) and polar cell. Surface: trade winds (NE/SE), westerlies, polar easterlies. Upper level: subtropical jet (~30°, ~FL 400) and polar-front jet (~40–60°, ~FL 300). Tropopause ≈ 16–18 km at the equator, 8–10 km at the poles."],["050.08.01.02:01","Climatic zones: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ tropical rain climate (hot, wet, ITCZ), ____ dry climate (subtropical highs, deserts ~20–30°), C warm temperate/mid-latitude (westerlies, fronts), D subarctic/cold snow-forest, E polar/snow climate. The seasonal movement of the sun shifts the belts → transitional zones (e.g.","Köppen zones: A tropical rain climate (hot, wet, ITCZ), B dry climate (subtropical highs, deserts ~20–30°), C warm temperate/mid-latitude (westerlies, fronts), D subarctic/cold snow-forest, E polar/snow climate. The seasonal movement of the sun shifts the belts → transitional zones (e.g."],["050.08.03.02:01","Climatic zones: what do you remember about this? → Mediterranean: dry summer under the high, wet winter with westerlies; …","Mediterranean: dry summer under the high, wet winter with westerlies; savanna: wet season with ITCZ, dry season with trades)."],["050.08.02.01:01","Tropical climatology: fill in the gaps → ____ need high humidity, high temperature (instability) and a high tropopause → very tall Cb (tops up to ____ ____). They organise in cloud clusters / mesoscale convective complexes. Tropical squall lines: lines of Cb moving westward (e.g. West Africa), gust front, violent squalls, heavy rain.","Tropical showers/TS need high humidity, high temperature (instability) and a high tropopause → very tall Cb (tops up to FL 500+). They organise in cloud clusters / mesoscale convective complexes. Tropical squall lines: lines of Cb moving westward (e.g. West Africa), gust front, violent squalls, heavy rain."],["050.08.02.01:03","Tropical climatology: fill in the gaps → ____: convergence of NE and SE trades → lifting → Cb lines, showers, TS. Typical tropics: surface ____, very high humidity, ____ (FL 150–160).","ITCZ: convergence of NE and SE trades → lifting → Cb lines, showers, TS. Typical tropics: surface T 25–30 °C, very high humidity, 0 °C isotherm ≈ 15 000–16 000 ft (FL 150–160)."],["050.08.02.02:03","Tropical climatology: fill in the gaps → ____: steady NE (NH) / SE (SH) winds on the equatorward side of the subtropical highs; fair weather, small cumulus under a ____. ____: calm, variable winds near the equator with showers/TS. Horse latitudes (subtropical highs, ~30°): subsidence, calm winds, dry, clear.","Trade winds: steady NE (NH) / SE (SH) winds on the equatorward side of the subtropical highs; fair weather, small cumulus under a trade-wind inversion. Doldrums: calm, variable winds near the equator with showers/TS. Horse latitudes (subtropical highs, ~30°): subsidence, calm winds, dry, clear."],["050.08.02.04:05","Tropical climatology: fill in the gaps → ____: follows the sun with a lag and is displaced towards the heated continents — ____: far north over Asia/West Africa (~20°N over India/Sahara south edge);","ITCZ position: follows the sun with a lag and is displaced towards the heated continents — July: far north over Asia/West Africa (~20°N over India/Sahara south edge);"],["050.08.02.04:07","Tropical climatology: fill in the gaps → ____: south of the equator over southern Africa, South America and northern Australia; over the oceans it moves little (mostly just north of the equator). Hazards: Cb, severe turbulence, icing, heavy rain, lightning, hail.","January: south of the equator over southern Africa, South America and northern Australia; over the oceans it moves little (mostly just north of the equator). Hazards: Cb, severe turbulence, icing, heavy rain, lightning, hail."],["050.08.02.04:06","Tropical climatology: fill in the gaps → ____ = seasonal wind reversal (≥ 120°) due to land/sea heating. Trade winds that cross the equator are deflected by Coriolis (turn from SE to ____ in the NH) and pick up moisture over the ocean → ____.","Monsoon = seasonal wind reversal (≥ 120°) due to land/sea heating. Trade winds that cross the equator are deflected by Coriolis (turn from SE to SW in the NH) and pick up moisture over the ocean → wet SW monsoon."],["050.08.02.04:05","Tropical climatology: fill in the gaps → ____: SW monsoon in summer (rain, TS, squall lines); NE Harmattan in winter (dry, dust).","West Africa: SW monsoon in summer (rain, TS, squall lines); NE Harmattan in winter (dry, dust)."],["050.08.02.04:06","Tropical climatology: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (heavy rain, embedded Cb, low cloud, flooding); ____ Dec–Feb dry (except SE India/Sri Lanka).","India: SW monsoon Jun–Sep (heavy rain, embedded Cb, low cloud, flooding); NE monsoon Dec–Feb dry (except SE India/Sri Lanka)."],["050.08.02.04:07","Tropical climatology: fill in the gaps → ____: NE winter monsoon over China (cold, dry; rain on coasts), SE/SW summer monsoon (wet); northern Australia wet NW monsoon in Dec–Feb.","Far East / N-Australia: NE winter monsoon over China (cold, dry; rain on coasts), SE/SW summer monsoon (wet); northern Australia wet NW monsoon in Dec–Feb."],["050.09.09.02:02","Tropical climatology: fill in the gaps → ____: strong winds over dry, loose ground and unstable air lift sand/dust up to several thousand ft → visibility below 1 000 m, engine damage.","Sandstorms: strong winds over dry, loose ground and unstable air lift sand/dust up to several thousand ft → visibility below 1 000 m, engine damage."],["050.08.02.04:10","Tropical climatology: fill in the gaps → ____: polar air pushing into subtropics — ____ (Argentina, squalls), ____ (North America, strong wind + snow), also Norther in the Gulf of Mexico.","Cold-air outbreaks: polar air pushing into subtropics — Pampero (Argentina, squalls), Blizzard (North America, strong wind + snow), also Norther in the Gulf of Mexico."],["050.08.02.05:01","Tropical climatology: fill in the gaps → ____: troughs in the trade-wind flow moving west; convergence ____ → Cb, showers; may develop into tropical storms.","Easterly waves: troughs in the trade-wind flow moving west; convergence behind (east of) the trough axis → Cb, showers; may develop into tropical storms."],["050.08.03.01:01","Mid-latitude situations: fill in the gaps → ____: a series of polar-front depressions and ridges moving W→E, fronts every 1–2 days.","Westerly situation: a series of polar-front depressions and ridges moving W→E, fronts every 1–2 days."],["050.08.03.01:01","Mid-latitude situations: fill in the gaps → ____: subsidence → stable, dry; in winter fog/stratus under an inversion, poor visibility (haze); in summer fair weather, afternoon Cu. Identify: closed isobars with H.","Highs: subsidence → stable, dry; in winter fog/stratus under an inversion, poor visibility (haze); in summer fair weather, afternoon Cu. Identify: closed isobars with H."],["050.09.04.01:02","Mid-latitude situations: fill in the gaps → ____: weak gradient, light winds; summer → air-mass thunderstorms; winter → fog.","Flat pressure pattern: weak gradient, light winds; summer → air-mass thunderstorms; winter → fog."],["050.08.03.04:03","Mid-latitude situations: fill in the gaps → ____: an isolated low of very cold upper air cut off from the polar flow, often invisible on the surface chart (identify on ____). Very unstable → persistent showers, TS, icing, turbulence, not well forecast; slow and erratic movement.","Cold-air drop (cold pool): an isolated low of very cold upper air cut off from the polar flow, often invisible on the surface chart (identify on 500 hPa charts). Very unstable → persistent showers, TS, icing, turbulence, not well forecast; slow and erratic movement."],["050.08.04.01:01","Local winds: fill in the gaps → ____: stable air forced over mountains; upwind: lifting, cloud & precipitation (____ cooling); downwind: descent at ____ → warm, dry, gusty wind, clear sky (Foehn wall, lenticular clouds, rotor turbulence). Chinook = Rocky Mountains.","Foehn / Chinook: stable air forced over mountains; upwind: lifting, cloud & precipitation (SALR cooling); downwind: descent at DALR → warm, dry, gusty wind, clear sky (Foehn wall, lenticular clouds, rotor turbulence). Chinook = Rocky Mountains."],["050.08.04.01:03","Local winds: fill in the gaps → ____ (Rhône valley to Gulf of Lion) and ____ (Adriatic, Croatian coast): cold, dry, strong ____ when cold air lies to the north and low pressure over the Mediterranean; severe turbulence, clear skies, Bora gusts > 100 kt possible. (Scirocco, Ghibli, Khamsin = hot desert winds from the Sahara, dust.)","Mistral (Rhône valley to Gulf of Lion) and Bora (Adriatic, Croatian coast): cold, dry, strong katabatic/fall winds when cold air lies to the north and low pressure over the Mediterranean; severe turbulence, clear skies, Bora gusts > 100 kt possible. (Scirocco, Ghibli, Khamsin = hot desert winds from the Sahara, dust.)"],["050.08.02.04:05","Local winds: fill in the gaps → ____: dry NE wind from the Sahara over West Africa in winter carrying fine dust → ____, dust haze up to ~10 000 ft.","Harmattan: dry NE wind from the Sahara over West Africa in winter carrying fine dust → visibility often < 2 000 m, dust haze up to ~10 000 ft."],["050.09.01.01:01","Icing: fill in the gaps → ____: supercooled water droplets (liquid below 0 °C) + airframe ____. Most likely between ____; below about −20/−40 °C clouds consist mainly of ice crystals. Sublimation (hoar frost) in clear air if the surface is below 0 °C and air is moist.","Conditions: supercooled water droplets (liquid below 0 °C) + airframe surface ≤ 0 °C. Most likely between 0 °C and −15 °C; below about −20/−40 °C clouds consist mainly of ice crystals. Sublimation (hoar frost) in clear air if the surface is below 0 °C and air is moist."],["050.09.01.01:02","Icing: fill in the gaps → ____: venturi cooling + fuel evaporation can drop the temperature by up to ~30 °C → icing possible at OAT ____ ____ with high humidity; worst at low power (descent).","Carburettor icing: venturi cooling + fuel evaporation can drop the temperature by up to ~30 °C → icing possible at OAT up to ~+25/+30 °C with high humidity; worst at low power (descent)."],["050.09.01.01:10","Icing: fill in the gaps → Cloud type: ____: large droplets, high liquid-water content → heavy ____. ____: small droplets → rime, but long exposure. Warm air → more supercooled water; cold air → less.","Cloud type: Cu/Cb: large droplets, high liquid-water content → heavy clear ice. St/Ns: small droplets → rime, but long exposure. Warm air → more supercooled water; cold air → less."],["050.09.01.01:01","Icing: fill in the gaps → ____ (rain falling from a warm layer into sub-zero air, e.g. ahead of a warm front) → worst clear ice, even outside cloud. ____ similar.","Freezing rain (rain falling from a warm layer into sub-zero air, e.g. ahead of a warm front) → worst clear ice, even outside cloud. Freezing drizzle similar."],["050.09.01.01:09","Icing: fill in the gaps → ____: frost, freezing fog, snow, freezing precipitation; ____ ____ in wing tanks (after a long high flight) can freeze moisture even at +10 °C OAT; radiative cooling at night.","On ground: frost, freezing fog, snow, freezing precipitation; cold- soaked fuel in wing tanks (after a long high flight) can freeze moisture even at +10 °C OAT; radiative cooling at night."],["050.09.01.01:12","Icing: fill in the gaps → ____ increases with liquid-water content, droplet size, speed (collection efficiency), thin surfaces (collect more), time in cloud. ____: forced ascent → higher water content → much more icing on windward slopes, also in stratiform orographic cloud.","Intensity increases with liquid-water content, droplet size, speed (collection efficiency), thin surfaces (collect more), time in cloud. Orography: forced ascent → higher water content → much more icing on windward slopes, also in stratiform orographic cloud."],["050.09.01.02:03","Icing: fill in the gaps → ____: large droplets, 0 to −10 °C; droplets flow back before freezing (latent heat slows freezing) → ____ ____, changes profile. Rime: small droplets freeze instantly, air trapped → white, opaque, light, brittle, −10 to −20 °C, on leading edges.","Clear ice: large droplets, 0 to −10 °C; droplets flow back before freezing (latent heat slows freezing) → transparent, heavy, hard, strongly adhering, changes profile. Rime: small droplets freeze instantly, air trapped → white, opaque, light, brittle, −10 to −20 °C, on leading edges."],["050.09.01.02:08","Icing: fill in the gaps → ____: both, in mixed cloud/snow. ____ can stick to wet surfaces. ____: sublimation on a sub-zero surface in clear moist air (e.g. descending from cold into warm moist air) → white feathery crystals, thin, easy to remove but spoils lift and visibility.","Mixed ice: both, in mixed cloud/snow. Snow can stick to wet surfaces. Hoar frost: sublimation on a sub-zero surface in clear moist air (e.g. descending from cold into warm moist air) → white feathery crystals, thin, easy to remove but spoils lift and visibility."],["050.09.01.03:01","Icing: fill in the gaps → ____: ____. Hazards: lift ↓, drag ↑, stall speed ↑, mass ↑, control jamming, instrument errors (pitot), propeller imbalance, windshield obscured, engine intake icing.","ICAO intensity: light, moderate, severe. Hazards: lift ↓, drag ↑, stall speed ↑, mass ↑, control jamming, instrument errors (pitot), propeller imbalance, windshield obscured, engine intake icing."],["050.09.01.02:03","Icing: fill in the gaps → ____: warm front (freezing rain ahead of it), cold front and Cb (heavy clear ice), St/Sc tops near −5 °C. ____: briefing (freezing level, SIGMET), choose level with T < −15 °C or > 0 °C, avoid Cb, leave freezing rain by climbing into the warm layer or turning back.","Dangerous zones: warm front (freezing rain ahead of it), cold front and Cb (heavy clear ice), St/Sc tops near −5 °C. Avoidance: briefing (freezing level, SIGMET), choose level with T < −15 °C or > 0 °C, avoid Cb, leave freezing rain by climbing into the warm layer or turning back."],["050.09.01.04:04","Icing: fill in the gaps → ____: high concentrations of small ice crystals (____ ____) near and above strong convection (anvils of tropical MCS) → crystals melt and re-freeze inside hot engine cores → power loss/flame-out, probe blockage; weather radar shows little (small crystals).","Ice-crystal icing: high concentrations of small ice crystals (high ice water content, IWC) near and above strong convection (anvils of tropical MCS) → crystals melt and re-freeze inside hot engine cores → power loss/flame-out, probe blockage; weather radar shows little (small crystals)."],["050.09.01.01:01","Icing: what do you remember about this? → Signs: \"rain\" on the windscreen at low temperature, TAT anomaly. …","Signs: \"rain\" on the windscreen at low temperature, TAT anomaly. Avoid areas downwind of Cb tops, deviate generously."],["050.09.02.01:01","Turbulence & CAT: fill in the gaps → ____: light, moderate, severe (and extreme). Effects: altitude/attitude changes, loads, loss of control, injuries. Turbulence = irregular eddies; ____ = rapid wind fluctuations; ____ = change of wind over distance.","ICAO terms: light, moderate, severe (and extreme). Effects: altitude/attitude changes, loads, loss of control, injuries. Turbulence = irregular eddies; gusts = rapid wind fluctuations; wind shear = change of wind over distance."],["050.09.02.01:05","Turbulence & CAT: fill in the gaps → Avoid: briefing (SIGWX, SIGMET), choose track/level, fly at ____ (VRA/VB). Forecasts are unreliable → ____ are valuable.","Avoid: briefing (SIGWX, SIGMET), choose track/level, fly at turbulence penetration speed (VRA/VB). Forecasts are unreliable → PIREPs are valuable."],["050.09.03.01:01","Turbulence & CAT: fill in the gaps → ____: in clear air near ____ (mostly on the ____ side, just below the core), in strong vertical and horizontal wind shear, near troughs/ridges. Avoid: change level by ~ 2 000–4 000 ft or move towards the warm side.","CAT: in clear air near jet streams (mostly on the cold/polar side, just below the core), in strong vertical and horizontal wind shear, near troughs/ridges. Avoid: change level by ~ 2 000–4 000 ft or move towards the warm side."],["050.09.03.01:01","Wind shear: fill in the gaps → ____ = change of wind speed/direction with height (vertical) or distance (horizontal). ____: below ____ along the final approach/take-off path.","Wind shear = change of wind speed/direction with height (vertical) or distance (horizontal). Low-level wind shear: below 1 600 ft AGL along the final approach/take-off path."],["050.09.03.02:01","Wind shear: fill in the gaps → Causes: thunderstorms/downbursts, squall lines, fronts, ____ (low-level jet at night), land/sea breeze, friction layer, relief/buildings.","Causes: thunderstorms/downbursts, squall lines, fronts, inversions (low-level jet at night), land/sea breeze, friction layer, relief/buildings."],["050.09.06.01:01","Wind shear: what do you remember about this? → Effect: sudden IAS change → loss of lift/overshoot; increasing …","Effect: sudden IAS change → loss of lift/overshoot; increasing headwind → IAS up (performance-increasing), decreasing headwind/increasing tailwind → IAS down, sink (dangerous). Avoid: delay take-off/approach near Cb, use reports (LLWAS, PIREP), extra speed, go-around with max thrust."],["050.09.04.01:02","Thunderstorms: fill in the gaps → Indicators: ____, Cb. Types: ____ (summer afternoon heating), ____ (cold front, embedded in warm front), squall line (ahead of cold front), supercell (rotating updraft, severe, long-lived), orographic.","Indicators: TCU, Ac castellanus, Cb. Types: air-mass (summer afternoon heating), frontal (cold front, embedded in warm front), squall line (ahead of cold front), supercell (rotating updraft, severe, long-lived), orographic."],["050.09.04.02:02","Thunderstorms: fill in the gaps → Life cycle single cell: ____ (updrafts only, ~15 min), ____ (up-and downdrafts, precipitation, lightning, gust front; ~15–30 min), dissipating (downdrafts, anvil). Total ~ ____. Supercell: initial → supercell (rotating mesocyclone) → tornado → dissipating; lasts several hours.","Life cycle single cell: cumulus stage (updrafts only, ~15 min), mature (up-and downdrafts, precipitation, lightning, gust front; ~15–30 min), dissipating (downdrafts, anvil). Total ~ 1 h. Supercell: initial → supercell (rotating mesocyclone) → tornado → dissipating; lasts several hours."],["050.09.01.03:02","Thunderstorms: what do you remember about this? → Hazards: severe turbulence, icing, hail (also below the anvil), …","Hazards: severe turbulence, icing, hail (also below the anvil), lightning, downbursts, wind shear, gust front, heavy rain, altimeter errors. Most dangerous: inside the mature cell, below the cloud base (downdraft/microburst) and under the anvil; for multicell storms the new cells on the leading side."],["050.09.04.03:02","Thunderstorms: fill in the gaps → ____: fair-weather field positive aloft; Cb separates charge (positive top, negative base). Lightning: ____, intra-cloud, cloud-to-cloud, upward (from tall objects). ____: corona discharge on aircraft extremities in strong electric fields — harmless but indicates lightning risk.","Electricity: fair-weather field positive aloft; Cb separates charge (positive top, negative base). Lightning: cloud-to-ground, intra-cloud, cloud-to-cloud, upward (from tall objects). St. Elmo's fire: corona discharge on aircraft extremities in strong electric fields — harmless but indicates lightning risk."],["050.09.04.03:05","Thunderstorms: what do you remember about this? → Lightning strike: temporary blindness, compass errors, avionics …","Lightning strike: temporary blindness, compass errors, avionics damage, burn holes; rarely fuel ignition."],["050.09.04.04:02","Thunderstorms: fill in the gaps → ____: strong downdraft spreading out near the ground. Microburst: diameter ____, ____ (max winds), downdraft up to 6 000 ft/min; macroburst > 4 km, lasts 5–30 min. Forms in Cb and high-based convection (virga, evaporative cooling). On approach: headwind increase → then strong downdraft → tailwind.","Downburst: strong downdraft spreading out near the ground. Microburst: diameter < 4 km, 1–5 min (max winds), downdraft up to 6 000 ft/min; macroburst > 4 km, lasts 5–30 min. Forms in Cb and high-based convection (virga, evaporative cooling). On approach: headwind increase → then strong downdraft → tailwind."],["050.09.04.05:01","Thunderstorms: fill in the gaps → ____: briefing, SIGMET, observation, ground and ____ weather radar, stormscope; keep ____ severe) from Cb, never fly under the anvil or between close cells, avoid by flying upwind side.","Avoidance: briefing, SIGMET, observation, ground and airborne weather radar, stormscope; keep ≥ 10 NM (20 NM for severe) from Cb, never fly under the anvil or between close cells, avoid by flying upwind side."],["050.09.05.01:01","Tornadoes, inversions, stratosphere: fill in the gaps → ____: violent rotating column below a Cb (supercell), diameter ~100 m, winds up to ____, moves 20–40 kt, spring/summer afternoon; very common in the USA (\"Tornado Alley\"), rare & weaker in Europe. ____: small, fair-weather, from surface heating, short-lived.","Tornado: violent rotating column below a Cb (supercell), diameter ~100 m, winds up to 300 kt, moves 20–40 kt, spring/summer afternoon; very common in the USA (\"Tornado Alley\"), rare & weaker in Europe. Dust devils: small, fair-weather, from surface heating, short-lived."],["050.09.06.01:01","Tornadoes, inversions, stratosphere: fill in the gaps → ____ at take-off/approach: sudden wind shear at the inversion, warm air above reduces performance, poor visibility (haze/fog trapped).","Strong inversion at take-off/approach: sudden wind shear at the inversion, warm air above reduces performance, poor visibility (haze/fog trapped)."],["050.09.07.01:02","Tornadoes, inversions, stratosphere: fill in the gaps → ____: advantages — above most weather, smooth, low temperature → better jet efficiency, TAS. Hazards: CAT near tropopause/jet, ozone, radiation, low density (coffin corner).","Stratosphere: advantages — above most weather, smooth, low temperature → better jet efficiency, TAS. Hazards: CAT near tropopause/jet, ozone, radiation, low density (coffin corner)."],["050.09.08.02:02","Mountains & valleys: fill in the gaps → Mountains: forced ascent → more cloud/precipitation on windward side, fronts slowed/distorted. ____ (stable air, wind ⊥ ridge ≥ 20–25 kt increasing with height): lenticular clouds, strong up/downdrafts, ____ below wave crests downwind (severe turbulence). Increased icing on windward slope.","Mountains: forced ascent → more cloud/precipitation on windward side, fronts slowed/distorted. Mountain waves (stable air, wind ⊥ ridge ≥ 20–25 kt increasing with height): lenticular clouds, strong up/downdrafts, rotor zones below wave crests downwind (severe turbulence). Increased icing on windward slope."],["050.09.08.03:02","Mountains & valleys: fill in the gaps → ____: cold katabatic air drains into valleys at night (fog/stratus), or warm air aloft (Foehn) caps cold valley air → wind shear and turbulence at the inversion, poor visibility.","Valley inversion: cold katabatic air drains into valleys at night (fog/stratus), or warm air aloft (Foehn) caps cold valley air → wind shear and turbulence at the inversion, poor visibility."],["050.09.09.01:02","Visibility: fill in the gaps → Precipitation: drizzle (poor), rain (moderate reduction), ____ (strongest). Obscurations: ____, mist ≥ 1 000 m (humid), haze (dry particles), smoke, volcanic ash, sand/dust.","Precipitation: drizzle (poor), rain (moderate reduction), snow (strongest). Obscurations: fog < 1 000 m, mist ≥ 1 000 m (humid), haze (dry particles), smoke, volcanic ash, sand/dust."],["050.09.09.01:04","Visibility: fill in the gaps → Above a haze/fog layer the ____ visibility can be good while ____ visibility on approach is poor; ground vs flight visibility can differ greatly.","Above a haze/fog layer the vertical visibility can be good while slant visibility on approach is poor; ground vs flight visibility can differ greatly."],["050.09.09.02:02","Visibility: fill in the gaps → Other: blowing/drifting snow, dust or sand, ____ ____ (vis < 1 000 m), windshield icing, sun low in front, glare from haze or cloud tops.","Other: blowing/drifting snow, dust or sand, dust storm/sandstorm (vis < 1 000 m), windshield icing, sun low in front, glare from haze or cloud tops."],["050.09.01.02:05","Exam trap (Visibility): complete the fact → clear ice = large droplets & ____to −____; rime = small droplets & colder.","clear ice = large droplets & 0 to −10 °C; rime = small droplets & colder."],["050.09.01.02:11","Exam trap (Visibility): complete the fact → Carb icing possible well above ____ OAT.","Carb icing possible well above 0 °C OAT."],["050.09.04.04:02","Exam trap (Visibility): complete the fact → Microburst < ____, strongest winds for only a few minutes.","Microburst < 4 km, strongest winds for only a few minutes."],["050.08.01.02:02","EASA LO 050.08.01.02.02 → Explain how the seasonal movement of the sun generates the transitional climate zones.","Köppen zones: A tropical rain climate (hot, wet, ITCZ), B dry climate (subtropical highs, deserts ~20–30°), C warm temperate/mid-latitude (westerlies, fronts), D subarctic/cold snow-forest, E polar/snow climate. The seasonal movement of the sun shifts the belts → transitional zones (e.g."],["050.08.02.01:02","EASA LO 050.08.02.01.02 → Describe the characteristics of tropical squall lines.","Tropical showers/TS need high humidity, high temperature (instability) and a high tropopause → very tall Cb (tops up to FL 500+). They organise in cloud clusters / mesoscale convective complexes. Tropical squall lines: lines of Cb moving westward (e.g. West Africa), gust front, violent squalls, heavy rain. / West Africa: SW monsoon in summer (rain, TS, squall lines); NE Harmattan in winter (dry, dust)."],["050.08.02.01:04","EASA LO 050.08.02.01.04 → State the typical figures for tropical surface air temperatures and humidities, and for heights of the zero-degree isotherm.","ITCZ: convergence of NE and SE trades → lifting → Cb lines, showers, TS. Typical tropics: surface T 25–30 °C, very high humidity, 0 °C isotherm ≈ 15 000–16 000 ft (FL 150–160)."],["050.08.02.03:02","EASA LO 050.08.02.03.02 → Explain the seasonal movement of the ITCZ.","Köppen zones: A tropical rain climate (hot, wet, ITCZ), B dry climate (subtropical highs, deserts ~20–30°), C warm temperate/mid-latitude (westerlies, fronts), D subarctic/cold snow-forest, E polar/snow climate. The seasonal movement of the sun shifts the belts → transitional zones (e.g."],["050.08.02.03:03","EASA LO 050.08.02.03.03 → Describe the weather and winds at the ITCZ. X X Exam Remarks","Köppen zones: A tropical rain climate (hot, wet, ITCZ), B dry climate (subtropical highs, deserts ~20–30°), C warm temperate/mid-latitude (westerlies, fronts), D subarctic/cold snow-forest, E polar/snow climate. The seasonal movement of the sun shifts the belts → transitional zones (e.g."],["050.08.02.03:04","EASA LO 050.08.02.03.04 → Explain the flight hazards associated with the ITCZ.","Köppen zones: A tropical rain climate (hot, wet, ITCZ), B dry climate (subtropical highs, deserts ~20–30°), C warm temperate/mid-latitude (westerlies, fronts), D subarctic/cold snow-forest, E polar/snow climate. The seasonal movement of the sun shifts the belts → transitional zones (e.g."],["050.08.02.04:03","EASA LO 050.08.02.04.03 → Explain how trade winds change character after a long track and become monsoon winds.","On ground: frost, freezing fog, snow, freezing precipitation; cold- soaked fuel in wing tanks (after a long high flight) can freeze moisture even at +10 °C OAT; radiative cooling at night."],["050.08.02.04:04","EASA LO 050.08.02.04.04 → Explain the weather and the flight hazards associated with a monsoon.","Far East / N-Australia: NE winter monsoon over China (cold, dry; rain on coasts), SE/SW summer monsoon (wet); northern Australia wet NW monsoon in Dec–Feb."],["050.08.03.04:01","EASA LO 050.08.03.04.01 → Define ‘cold-air drop’.","Cold-air drop (cold pool): an isolated low of very cold upper air cut off from the polar flow, often invisible on the surface chart (identify on 500 hPa charts). Very unstable → persistent showers, TS, icing, turbulence, not well forecast; slow and erratic movement. / Carburettor icing: venturi cooling + fuel evaporation can drop the temperature by up to ~30 °C → icing possible at OAT up to ~+25/+30 °C with high humidity; worst at low power (descent)."],["050.08.03.04:02","EASA LO 050.08.03.04.02 → Describe the formation of a cold-air drop.","Cold-air drop (cold pool): an isolated low of very cold upper air cut off from the polar flow, often invisible on the surface chart (identify on 500 hPa charts). Very unstable → persistent showers, TS, icing, turbulence, not well forecast; slow and erratic movement. / Carburettor icing: venturi cooling + fuel evaporation can drop the temperature by up to ~30 °C → icing possible at OAT up to ~+25/+30 °C with high humidity; worst at low power (descent)."],["050.08.04.01:02","EASA LO 050.08.04.01.02 → Describe the weather associated with Foehn winds.","Foehn / Chinook: stable air forced over mountains; upwind: lifting, cloud & precipitation (SALR cooling); downwind: descent at DALR → warm, dry, gusty wind, clear sky (Foehn wall, lenticular clouds, rotor turbulence). Chinook = Rocky Mountains. / Mistral (Rhône valley to Gulf of Lion) and Bora (Adriatic, Croatian coast): cold, dry, strong katabatic/fall winds when cold air lies to the north and low pressure over the Mediterranean; severe turbulence, clear skies, Bora gusts > 100 kt possible. (Scirocco, Ghibli, Khamsin = hot desert winds from the Sahara, dust.)"],["050.08.04.02:01","EASA LO 050.08.04.02.01 → Describe the Harmattan wind and the associated visibility problems as an example of local winds affecting visibility.","Harmattan: dry NE wind from the Sahara over West Africa in winter carrying fine dust → visibility often < 2 000 m, dust haze up to ~10 000 ft. / West Africa: SW monsoon in summer (rain, TS, squall lines); NE Harmattan in winter (dry, dust)."],["050.09.01.01:03","EASA LO 050.09.01.01.03 → Explain the general weather conditions under which ice accretion occurs on airframe.","Conditions: supercooled water droplets (liquid below 0 °C) + airframe surface ≤ 0 °C. Most likely between 0 °C and −15 °C; below about −20/−40 °C clouds consist mainly of ice crystals. Sublimation (hoar frost) in clear air if the surface is below 0 °C and air is moist."],["050.09.01.01:04","EASA LO 050.09.01.01.04 → Explain the formation of supercooled water in clouds, rain and drizzle. (Refer to Subject 050 03 02 01)","Conditions: supercooled water droplets (liquid below 0 °C) + airframe surface ≤ 0 °C. Most likely between 0 °C and −15 °C; below about −20/−40 °C clouds consist mainly of ice crystals. Sublimation (hoar frost) in clear air if the surface is below 0 °C and air is moist."],["050.09.01.01:05","EASA LO 050.09.01.01.05 → Explain qualitatively the relationship between the air temperature and the amount of supercooled water.","Conditions: supercooled water droplets (liquid below 0 °C) + airframe surface ≤ 0 °C. Most likely between 0 °C and −15 °C; below about −20/−40 °C clouds consist mainly of ice crystals. Sublimation (hoar frost) in clear air if the surface is below 0 °C and air is moist. / Cloud type: Cu/Cb: large droplets, high liquid-water content → heavy clear ice. St/Ns: small droplets → rime, but long exposure. Warm air → more supercooled water; cold air → less."],["050.09.01.01:06","EASA LO 050.09.01.01.06 → Explain qualitatively the relationship between the type of cloud and the size and number of the droplets in cumuliform and stratiform clouds.","Cloud type: Cu/Cb: large droplets, high liquid-water content → heavy clear ice. St/Ns: small droplets → rime, but long exposure. Warm air → more supercooled water; cold air → less. / clear ice = large droplets & 0 to −10 °C; rime = small droplets & colder."],["050.09.01.01:07","EASA LO 050.09.01.01.07 → Indicate in which circumstances ice can form on an aircraft on the ground: air temperature, humidity, precipitation.","Carburettor icing: venturi cooling + fuel evaporation can drop the temperature by up to ~30 °C → icing possible at OAT up to ~+25/+30 °C with high humidity; worst at low power (descent)."],["050.09.01.02:01","EASA LO 050.09.01.02.01 → Define ‘clear ice’.","clear ice = large droplets & 0 to −10 °C; rime = small droplets & colder. / Freezing rain (rain falling from a warm layer into sub-zero air, e.g. ahead of a warm front) → worst clear ice, even outside cloud. Freezing drizzle similar."],["050.09.01.02:02","EASA LO 050.09.01.02.02 → Describe the conditions for the formation of clear ice.","clear ice = large droplets & 0 to −10 °C; rime = small droplets & colder."],["050.09.01.02:06","EASA LO 050.09.01.02.06 → Describe the conditions for the formation of rime ice.","clear ice = large droplets & 0 to −10 °C; rime = small droplets & colder. / Cloud type: Cu/Cb: large droplets, high liquid-water content → heavy clear ice. St/Ns: small droplets → rime, but long exposure. Warm air → more supercooled water; cold air → less."],["050.09.01.02:07","EASA LO 050.09.01.02.07 → Describe the aspects of rime ice: appearance, weight, solidity.","clear ice = large droplets & 0 to −10 °C; rime = small droplets & colder."],["050.09.01.02:09","EASA LO 050.09.01.02.09 → Describe the conditions for the formation of mixed ice.","Mixed ice: both, in mixed cloud/snow. Snow can stick to wet surfaces. Hoar frost: sublimation on a sub-zero surface in clear moist air (e.g. descending from cold into warm moist air) → white feathery crystals, thin, easy to remove but spoils lift and visibility."],["050.09.01.02:12","EASA LO 050.09.01.02.12 → Define ‘hoar frost’. X X X X X X X 3 Exam Remarks","Conditions: supercooled water droplets (liquid below 0 °C) + airframe surface ≤ 0 °C. Most likely between 0 °C and −15 °C; below about −20/−40 °C clouds consist mainly of ice crystals. Sublimation (hoar frost) in clear air if the surface is below 0 °C and air is moist."],["050.09.01.02:13","EASA LO 050.09.01.02.13 → Describe the conditions for the formation of hoar frost.","Conditions: supercooled water droplets (liquid below 0 °C) + airframe surface ≤ 0 °C. Most likely between 0 °C and −15 °C; below about −20/−40 °C clouds consist mainly of ice crystals. Sublimation (hoar frost) in clear air if the surface is below 0 °C and air is moist."],["050.09.01.02:14","EASA LO 050.09.01.02.14 → Describe the aspects of hoar frost: appearance, solidity.","Conditions: supercooled water droplets (liquid below 0 °C) + airframe surface ≤ 0 °C. Most likely between 0 °C and −15 °C; below about −20/−40 °C clouds consist mainly of ice crystals. Sublimation (hoar frost) in clear air if the surface is below 0 °C and air is moist."],["050.09.01.04:01","EASA LO 050.09.01.04.01 → Describe ice crystal icing.","Ice-crystal icing: high concentrations of small ice crystals (high ice water content, IWC) near and above strong convection (anvils of tropical MCS) → crystals melt and re-freeze inside hot engine cores → power loss/flame-out, probe blockage; weather radar shows little (small crystals)."],["050.09.01.04:02","EASA LO 050.09.01.04.02 → Describe the atmospheric processes leading to high ice crystal concentration. Define the variable ice water content (IWC).","Ice-crystal icing: high concentrations of small ice crystals (high ice water content, IWC) near and above strong convection (anvils of tropical MCS) → crystals melt and re-freeze inside hot engine cores → power loss/flame-out, probe blockage; weather radar shows little (small crystals). / Cloud type: Cu/Cb: large droplets, high liquid-water content → heavy clear ice. St/Ns: small droplets → rime, but long exposure. Warm air → more supercooled water; cold air → less."],["050.09.01.04:03","EASA LO 050.09.01.04.03 → Identify weather situations and their relevant areas where high concentrations of ice crystals are likely to occur.","Ice-crystal icing: high concentrations of small ice crystals (high ice water content, IWC) near and above strong convection (anvils of tropical MCS) → crystals melt and re-freeze inside hot engine cores → power loss/flame-out, probe blockage; weather radar shows little (small crystals). / Conditions: supercooled water droplets (liquid below 0 °C) + airframe surface ≤ 0 °C. Most likely between 0 °C and −15 °C; below about −20/−40 °C clouds consist mainly of ice crystals. Sublimation (hoar frost) in clear air if the surface is below 0 °C and air is moist."],["050.09.01.04:05","EASA LO 050.09.01.04.05 → Explain how a pilot may possibly avoid areas with a high concentration of ice crystals.","Ice-crystal icing: high concentrations of small ice crystals (high ice water content, IWC) near and above strong convection (anvils of tropical MCS) → crystals melt and re-freeze inside hot engine cores → power loss/flame-out, probe blockage; weather radar shows little (small crystals)."],["050.09.02.01:03","EASA LO 050.09.02.01.03 → Indicate the possibilities of avoiding turbulence: — in the flight planning: weather briefing, selection of track and altitude; — during flight: selection of appropriate track and altitude.","Avoid: briefing (SIGWX, SIGMET), choose track/level, fly at turbulence penetration speed (VRA/VB). Forecasts are unreliable → PIREPs are valuable."],["050.09.02.02:02","EASA LO 050.09.02.02.02 → Indicate the possibilities of avoiding CAT in flight: — in the flight planning: weather briefing, selection of track and altitude;","Avoid: briefing (SIGWX, SIGMET), choose track/level, fly at turbulence penetration speed (VRA/VB). Forecasts are unreliable → PIREPs are valuable. / ICAO terms: light, moderate, severe (and extreme). Effects: altitude/attitude changes, loads, loss of control, injuries. Turbulence = irregular eddies; gusts = rapid wind fluctuations; wind shear = change of wind over distance."],["050.09.03.01:02","EASA LO 050.09.03.01.02 → Define ‘low-level wind shear’.","Wind shear = change of wind speed/direction with height (vertical) or distance (horizontal). Low-level wind shear: below 1 600 ft AGL along the final approach/take-off path. / Causes: thunderstorms/downbursts, squall lines, fronts, inversions (low-level jet at night), land/sea breeze, friction layer, relief/buildings."],["050.09.03.03:01","EASA LO 050.09.03.03.01 → Describe the effects of wind shear on flight.","ICAO terms: light, moderate, severe (and extreme). Effects: altitude/attitude changes, loads, loss of control, injuries. Turbulence = irregular eddies; gusts = rapid wind fluctuations; wind shear = change of wind over distance."],["050.09.04.02:04","EASA LO 050.09.04.02.04 → Indicate on a sketch the most dangerous zones in and around a single-cell and a multi-cell thunderstorm.","Life cycle single cell: cumulus stage (updrafts only, ~15 min), mature (up-and downdrafts, precipitation, lightning, gust front; ~15–30 min), dissipating (downdrafts, anvil). Total ~ 1 h. Supercell: initial → supercell (rotating mesocyclone) → tornado → dissipating; lasts several hours. / Hazards: severe turbulence, icing, hail (also below the anvil), lightning, downbursts, wind shear, gust front, heavy rain, altimeter errors. Most dangerous: inside the mature cell, below the cloud base (downdraft/microburst) and under the anvil; for multicell storms the new cells on the leading side."],["050.09.04.03:01","EASA LO 050.09.04.03.01 → Describe the basic outline of the electric field in the atmosphere.","Electricity: fair-weather field positive aloft; Cb separates charge (positive top, negative base). Lightning: cloud-to-ground, intra-cloud, cloud-to-cloud, upward (from tall objects). St. Elmo's fire: corona discharge on aircraft extremities in strong electric fields — harmless but indicates lightning risk."],["050.09.04.04:03","EASA LO 050.09.04.04.03 → State the weather situations leading to the formation of downbursts.","Hazards: severe turbulence, icing, hail (also below the anvil), lightning, downbursts, wind shear, gust front, heavy rain, altimeter errors. Most dangerous: inside the mature cell, below the cloud base (downdraft/microburst) and under the anvil; for multicell storms the new cells on the leading side."],["050.09.08.02:01","EASA LO 050.09.08.02.01 → Describe the vertical movements, wind shear and turbulence that are typical of mountain areas.","Wind shear = change of wind speed/direction with height (vertical) or distance (horizontal). Low-level wind shear: below 1 600 ft AGL along the final approach/take-off path. / CAT: in clear air near jet streams (mostly on the cold/polar side, just below the core), in strong vertical and horizontal wind shear, near troughs/ridges. Avoid: change level by ~ 2 000–4 000 ft or move towards the warm side."],["050.09.08.02:03","EASA LO 050.09.08.02.03 → Explain the influence of relief on ice accretion.","Causes: thunderstorms/downbursts, squall lines, fronts, inversions (low-level jet at night), land/sea breeze, friction layer, relief/buildings."],["050.09.08.03:01","EASA LO 050.09.08.03.01 → Describe the formation of a valley inversion due to katabatic winds.","Valley inversion: cold katabatic air drains into valleys at night (fog/stratus), or warm air aloft (Foehn) caps cold valley air → wind shear and turbulence at the inversion, poor visibility. / Mistral (Rhône valley to Gulf of Lion) and Bora (Adriatic, Croatian coast): cold, dry, strong katabatic/fall winds when cold air lies to the north and low pressure over the Mediterranean; severe turbulence, clear skies, Bora gusts > 100 kt possible. (Scirocco, Ghibli, Khamsin = hot desert winds from the Sahara, dust.)"],["050.09.08.03:03","EASA LO 050.09.08.03.03 → Describe the effects of a valley inversion for an aircraft in flight.","Valley inversion: cold katabatic air drains into valleys at night (fog/stratus), or warm air aloft (Foehn) caps cold valley air → wind shear and turbulence at the inversion, poor visibility."],["050.09.09.01:01","EASA LO 050.09.09.01.01 → Describe the reduction of visibility caused by precipitation: drizzle, rain, snow.","Precipitation: drizzle (poor), rain (moderate reduction), snow (strongest). Obscurations: fog < 1 000 m, mist ≥ 1 000 m (humid), haze (dry particles), smoke, volcanic ash, sand/dust."],["050.09.09.01:03","EASA LO 050.09.09.01.03 → Describe the reduction of visibility caused by obscurations: — sand (SA), dust (DU).","Precipitation: drizzle (poor), rain (moderate reduction), snow (strongest). Obscurations: fog < 1 000 m, mist ≥ 1 000 m (humid), haze (dry particles), smoke, volcanic ash, sand/dust. / Other: blowing/drifting snow, dust or sand, dust storm/sandstorm (vis < 1 000 m), windshield icing, sun low in front, glare from haze or cloud tops."],["050.09.09.02:01","EASA LO 050.09.09.02.01 → Describe the reduction of visibility caused by low drifting and blowing snow.","Other: blowing/drifting snow, dust or sand, dust storm/sandstorm (vis < 1 000 m), windshield icing, sun low in front, glare from haze or cloud tops."],["050.09.09.02:03","EASA LO 050.09.09.02.03 → Describe the reduction of visibility caused by dust storm (DS) and sandstorm (SS).","Other: blowing/drifting snow, dust or sand, dust storm/sandstorm (vis < 1 000 m), windshield icing, sun low in front, glare from haze or cloud tops."],["050.09.09.02:04","EASA LO 050.09.09.02.04 → Describe the reduction of visibility caused by icing (windshield).","Other: blowing/drifting snow, dust or sand, dust storm/sandstorm (vis < 1 000 m), windshield icing, sun low in front, glare from haze or cloud tops."],["050.09.09.02:06","EASA LO 050.09.09.02.06 → Describe the reduction of visibility caused by the reflection of the sun’s rays from the top of the layers of haze, fog and clouds.","Other: blowing/drifting snow, dust or sand, dust storm/sandstorm (vis < 1 000 m), windshield icing, sun low in front, glare from haze or cloud tops. / Above a haze/fog layer the vertical visibility can be good while slant visibility on approach is poor; ground vs flight visibility can differ greatly."]],"48":[["032.01.01.03:02","Legislation & safety: fill in the gaps → ____: normal, utility, aerobatic, commuter aeroplanes (≤ 19 pax, MTOM ≤ 8 618 kg); ____: large aeroplanes (turbine, larger). CS-25 requirements are much more stringent (e.g. engine failure at any point of take-off must be covered) → allows a wider range of operating conditions (IMC, night, obstacles).","CS-23: normal, utility, aerobatic, commuter aeroplanes (≤ 19 pax, MTOM ≤ 8 618 kg); CS-25: large aeroplanes (turbine, larger). CS-25 requirements are much more stringent (e.g. engine failure at any point of take-off must be covered) → allows a wider range of operating conditions (IMC, night, obstacles)."],["032.02.01.01:05","Legislation & safety: fill in the gaps → Operational requirements differ with performance → ____ (CAT): ____ = multi-engine turbo-prop > 9 pax or MTOM > 5 700 kg and all multi-engine turbojets; ____ B = propeller aeroplanes ≤ 9 pax and MTOM ≤ 5 700 kg; Class C = piston aeroplanes > 9 pax or > 5 700 kg (not Class A).","Operational requirements differ with performance → performance classes (CAT): Class A = multi-engine turbo-prop > 9 pax or MTOM > 5 700 kg and all multi-engine turbojets; Class B = propeller aeroplanes ≤ 9 pax and MTOM ≤ 5 700 kg; Class C = piston aeroplanes > 9 pax or > 5 700 kg (not Class A)."],["032.02.04.01:01","Legislation & safety: fill in the gaps → Performance limits may ____ (runway, climb, obstacle, en-route, landing). Safety = airworthiness standards ____ operational limitations.","Performance limits may reduce the dispatch mass (runway, climb, obstacle, en-route, landing). Safety = airworthiness standards + operational limitations."],["032.01.01.04:02","Legislation & safety: fill in the gaps → ____ performance = obtained in flight test with a test pilot/new aircraft. ____ = average performance of the fleet (measured, corrected to standard). ____ = gross degraded by safety factors (margins for pilot, wear, weather).","Measured performance = obtained in flight test with a test pilot/new aircraft. Gross = average performance of the fleet (measured, corrected to standard). Net = gross degraded by safety factors (margins for pilot, wear, weather)."],["032.01.01.04:04","Legislation & safety: what do you remember about this? → The size of a factor depends on the likelihood of the event and the …","The size of a factor depends on the likelihood of the event and the scatter of the data (rarer event → smaller margin). Net distances = gross × factor (larger); net climb gradient = gross − margin (smaller); net descent gradient steeper."],["032.01.02.02:02","Definitions: fill in the gaps → ____ γ (degrees, relative to the horizon, in still air); ____ ____ = height gain ÷ horizontal distance × 100 % (≈ tan γ). Flight-path angle/gradient = relative to the ground (includes wind); climb angle is relative to the air mass. Descent analogously.","Climb angle γ (degrees, relative to the horizon, in still air); climb gradient = height gain ÷ horizontal distance × 100 % (≈ tan γ). Flight-path angle/gradient = relative to the ground (includes wind); climb angle is relative to the air mass. Descent analogously."],["032.01.02.02:05","Definitions: fill in the gaps → ____: altitude where max rate of climb = 0 (service ceiling: ROC 100 ft/min for props / 500 ft/min for jets).","Absolute ceiling: altitude where max rate of climb = 0 (service ceiling: ROC 100 ft/min for props / 500 ft/min for jets)."],["032.02.03.01:13","Definitions: fill in the gaps → ____: area beyond the runway end under the control of the aerodrome, obstacle-free (≤ 1.25 % plane), usable for the airborne part of take-off. ____: area beyond the runway, able to support the aeroplane during an aborted take-off.","Clearway: area beyond the runway end under the control of the aerodrome, obstacle-free (≤ 1.25 % plane), usable for the airborne part of take-off. Stopway: area beyond the runway, able to support the aeroplane during an aborted take-off."],["032.01.02.02:07","Definitions: fill in the gaps → ____ = runway length available for the ground run. ____ = TORA + clearway. ____ = TORA + stopway. Read from AIP/aerodrome charts.","TORA = runway length available for the ground run. TODA = TORA + clearway. ASDA = TORA + stopway. Read from AIP/aerodrome charts."],["032.02.03.01:12","Definitions: fill in the gaps → ____: height at the end of TOD/start of LD — Class B ____; Class A take-off: ____ dry (15 ft wet), Class A landing 50 ft.","Screen height: height at the end of TOD/start of LD — Class B take-off & landing: 50 ft; Class A take-off: 35 ft dry (15 ft wet), Class A landing 50 ft."],["032.01.02.02:10","Definitions: fill in the gaps → ____ = distance flown with the fuel; ____ = time. ____ = NAM per kg fuel = TAS ÷ fuel flow; SRG = NGM per kg = GS ÷ fuel flow.","Range = distance flown with the fuel; endurance = time. SR = NAM per kg fuel = TAS ÷ fuel flow; SRG = NGM per kg = GS ÷ fuel flow."],["032.01.05.01:02","Definitions: fill in the gaps → ____ = drag × TAS; ____ = thrust × TAS.","Power required = drag × TAS; power available = thrust × TAS."],["032.01.02.03:01","Variables influencing performance: what do you remember about this? → Factors: pressure altitude & temperature (density), wind, weight, …","Factors: pressure altitude & temperature (density), wind, weight, configuration, anti-skid status, CG, runway surface, runway slope."],["032.01.02.03:03","Variables influencing performance: fill in the gaps → ____: thrust available ____ with speed; power available roughly constant (rises to max then falls). ____: thrust available roughly constant with speed; power available rises linearly with TAS. Both fall with increasing density altitude.","Propeller: thrust available decreases with speed; power available roughly constant (rises to max then falls). Turbojet: thrust available roughly constant with speed; power available rises linearly with TAS. Both fall with increasing density altitude."],["032.01.02.03:05","Variables influencing performance: fill in the gaps → ____ is independent of altitude (same curve); ____ it shifts right with altitude. ____ curve shifts up and right with altitude (P = D × TAS). Higher weight or flaps/gear → drag and power-required curves shift up (and right).","Drag vs IAS is independent of altitude (same curve); vs TAS it shifts right with altitude. Power required curve shifts up and right with altitude (P = D × TAS). Higher weight or flaps/gear → drag and power-required curves shift up (and right)."],["032.01.03.01:02","Level flight, range, endurance: fill in the gaps → Drag curve: induced drag falls with speed, parasite rises → ________ (best L/D). Power required minimum at ____MP < VMD. Excess thrust/power → acceleration. Max level speed where thrust available = thrust required. Max TAS increases with altitude to a point, max IAS decreases.","Drag curve: induced drag falls with speed, parasite rises → minimum drag at VMD (best L/D). Power required minimum at VMP < VMD. Excess thrust/power → acceleration. Max level speed where thrust available = thrust required. Max TAS increases with altitude to a point, max IAS decreases."],["032.01.02.02:10","Level flight, range, endurance: fill in the gaps → ____ = fuel flow per unit thrust (kg/N/h); ____ = fuel flow per unit power. SR = TAS/(SFC × thrust).","SFC jet = fuel flow per unit thrust (kg/N/h); SFC prop = fuel flow per unit power. SR = TAS/(SFC × thrust)."],["032.01.03.02:03","Level flight, range, endurance: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ ____MD (in practice LRC, slightly faster); prop → VMD (best L/D, tangent from origin to the power curve).","Max range: jet → speed where TAS/drag is maximum = tangent from the origin to the drag–TAS curve ≈ 1.32 VMD (in practice LRC, slightly faster); prop → VMD (best L/D, tangent from origin to the power curve)."],["032.01.03.03:04","Level flight, range, endurance: fill in the gaps → ____: ________ (minimum drag = min fuel flow); prop → VMP (minimum power) — low speed, high AoA, speed- unstable (back of the drag curve), so in practice fly slightly faster.","Max endurance: jet → VMD (minimum drag = min fuel flow); prop → VMP (minimum power) — low speed, high AoA, speed- unstable (back of the drag curve), so in practice fly slightly faster."],["032.01.03.02:05","Level flight, range, endurance: fill in the gaps → ____: heavier → more drag → less range, higher optimum speed; forward CG → more trim drag → less range. ____ improves at ____ and higher rpm/thrust (near design point).","Weight: heavier → more drag → less range, higher optimum speed; forward CG → more trim drag → less range. Jet SFC improves at lower temperature and higher rpm/thrust (near design point)."],["032.01.03.02:10","Level flight, range, endurance: fill in the gaps → ____ up to an ____ (which ____ — step climbs); prop SRG roughly independent of altitude (best at moderate altitudes; supercharged engines better higher up).","Jet SR increases with altitude up to an optimum altitude (which rises as weight decreases — step climbs); prop SRG roughly independent of altitude (best at moderate altitudes; supercharged engines better higher up)."],["032.01.03.03:05","Level flight, range, endurance: fill in the gaps → ____: headwind → fly ____ than the still-air optimum; tailwind → slightly slower. Endurance is not affected by wind. Jet endurance improves with altitude (lower SFC); prop endurance best low.","Wind: headwind → fly faster than the still-air optimum; tailwind → slightly slower. Endurance is not affected by wind. Jet endurance improves with altitude (lower SFC); prop endurance best low."],["032.01.03.03:07","Level flight, range, endurance: fill in the gaps → ____ (slow down early when a delay is known) saves fuel compared with holding (ATC-commanded or pilot-initiated).","Speed management en route (slow down early when a delay is known) saves fuel compared with holding (ATC-commanded or pilot-initiated)."],["032.01.04.01:01","Climb: fill in the gaps → Forces: ____ → climb gradient ≈ (T − D)/W. ____ ____ = TAS × sin γ = (PA − PR)/W.","Forces: sin γ = (T − D)/W → climb gradient ≈ (T − D)/W. Rate of climb = TAS × sin γ = (PA − PR)/W."],["032.01.04.01:02","Climb: fill in the gaps → ________ (best angle) = max excess ____; VY (best rate) = max excess power; VX < VY. Jet: VX ≈ VMD. At the absolute ceiling VX = VY.","VX (best angle) = max excess thrust; VY (best rate) = max excess power; VX < VY. Jet: VX ≈ VMD. At the absolute ceiling VX = VY."],["032.01.04.01:11","Climb: fill in the gaps → ____: the one whose failure most adversely affects performance/handling (on props with clockwise rotation seen from behind: the ____ engine, P-factor).","Critical engine: the one whose failure most adversely affects performance/handling (on props with clockwise rotation seen from behind: the left engine, P-factor)."],["032.01.04.01:09","Climb: fill in the gaps → Heavier: angle & rate ↓, VX/VY ↑. Higher altitude/temperature: angle & rate ↓ (VX IAS roughly constant/increases slightly, VY decreases → they converge). ____: climbing into warmer air reduces performance. Flaps/gear: more drag → angle & rate ↓, speeds ↓.","Heavier: angle & rate ↓, VX/VY ↑. Higher altitude/temperature: angle & rate ↓ (VX IAS roughly constant/increases slightly, VY decreases → they converge). Inversion: climbing into warmer air reduces performance. Flaps/gear: more drag → angle & rate ↓, speeds ↓."],["032.01.04.01:10","Climb: fill in the gaps → ____ on a twin: loses 50 % thrust but ~80 % of the ____ power → climb capability drops dramatically; VX, VY slightly lower (VYSE, blue line).","Engine failure on a twin: loses 50 % thrust but ~80 % of the excess power → climb capability drops dramatically; VX, VY slightly lower (VYSE, blue line)."],["032.01.04.01:01","Climb: fill in the gaps → ____: gradient = (T − D)/W. Example: 2 engines × 60 kN, D = 50 kN, W = 1 000 kN → AEO (120−50)/1000 = ____; OEI (60−50)/1000 = ____.","Gradient calculation: gradient = (T − D)/W. Example: 2 engines × 60 kN, D = 50 kN, W = 1 000 kN → AEO (120−50)/1000 = 7 %; OEI (60−50)/1000 = 1 %."],["032.01.05.01:05","Descent & glide: fill in the gaps → Glide: ____ → gradient = 1/(L/D). Best glide range at ________ (max L/D); minimum sink at VMP.","Glide: sin γ = D/W → gradient = 1/(L/D). Best glide range at VMD (max L/D); minimum sink at VMP."],["032.01.05.01:05","Descent & glide: fill in the gaps → ____ does not change the glide angle (only the speed: heavier → faster for the same angle, arrives sooner). ____ → shorter glide distance (fly faster); tailwind → longer. Flaps/gear → steeper. Altitude does not change the angle (TAS higher, same distance).","Mass does not change the glide angle (only the speed: heavier → faster for the same angle, arrives sooner). Headwind → shorter glide distance (fly faster); tailwind → longer. Flaps/gear → steeper. Altitude does not change the angle (TAS higher, same distance)."],["032.01.05.01:06","Descent & glide: what do you remember about this? → Powered descent: gradient = (D − T)/W; idle thrust + drag excess …","Powered descent: gradient = (D − T)/W; idle thrust + drag excess determines angle; higher speed/weight/altitude affect rate."],["032.02.01.01:01","Class B airworthiness (CS-23): fill in the gaps → V____ = stall speed landing configuration; V____ = stall speed in a specified configuration; VR rotation speed; V____ speed at 50 ft; VREF reference landing speed at 50 ft.","VS0 = stall speed landing configuration; VS1 = stall speed in a specified configuration; VR rotation speed; V50 speed at 50 ft; VREF reference landing speed at 50 ft."],["032.02.01.01:01","Class B airworthiness (CS-23): fill in the gaps → ____: V50 ≥ 1.2 VS1; VREF ≥ 1.3 VS0. ____: VR ≥ 1.05 VMC and ≥ 1.1 VS1; V50 ≥ 1.1 VMC and ≥ 1.2 VS1; VREF ≥ 1.3 VS0 and ≥ VMC. (Example: VS0 = 60 kt → VREF ≥ 78 kt.)","SEP: V50 ≥ 1.2 VS1; VREF ≥ 1.3 VS0. MEP: VR ≥ 1.05 VMC and ≥ 1.1 VS1; V50 ≥ 1.1 VMC and ≥ 1.2 VS1; VREF ≥ 1.3 VS0 and ≥ VMC. (Example: VS0 = 60 kt → VREF ≥ 78 kt.)"],["032.02.01.01:06","Class B airworthiness (CS-23): fill in the gaps → Engine failure in a twin: power required ↑ (asymmetric drag, sideslip, windmilling/feathered prop), climb reduced strongly; ________ ____ (less thrust from the live engine).","Engine failure in a twin: power required ↑ (asymmetric drag, sideslip, windmilling/feathered prop), climb reduced strongly; VMC decreases with altitude/temperature (less thrust from the live engine)."],["032.02.03.01:01","Take-off & landing: fill in the gaps → ____ = distance to 50 ft; ____ = from 50 ft to full stop; ground roll part. ____ = lowest of structural and performance limits.","TOD = distance to 50 ft; LD = from 50 ft to full stop; ground roll part. Max allowed TOM/LM = lowest of structural and performance limits."],["032.02.03.01:02","Take-off & landing: fill in the gaps → ____: take-off flap shortens ground roll but reduces climb; landing flap shortens LD.","Flaps: take-off flap shortens ground roll but reduces climb; landing flap shortens LD."],["032.02.03.01:03","Take-off & landing: fill in the gaps → ____ → longer take-off (+ ~____ slope for Class B), shorter landing; ____ → longer landing (+5 % per 1 %). Wet/grass/contaminated → longer (dry grass ×1.2, wet grass ×1.3 on take-off; wet runway landing ×1.15). Elevation/temperature ↑ → density ↓ → all distances ↑, climb ↓.","Upslope → longer take-off (+ ~5 % per 1 % slope for Class B), shorter landing; downslope → longer landing (+5 % per 1 %). Wet/grass/contaminated → longer (dry grass ×1.2, wet grass ×1.3 on take-off; wet runway landing ×1.15). Elevation/temperature ↑ → density ↓ → all distances ↑, climb ↓."],["032.02.03.01:05","Take-off & landing: fill in the gaps → ____ prop: thrust low at the start, efficiency increases with speed; ____: full power available from brake release. ____ → longer TOD and ASD.","Fixed-pitch prop: thrust low at the start, efficiency increases with speed; constant-speed: full power available from brake release. Brake release before full power → longer TOD and ASD."],["032.02.03.01:07","Take-off & landing: fill in the gaps → ____: use ____, ____ tailwind. Crosswind = wind × sin(angle); rule of thumb: 30° → ½, 45° → ¾ (0.7), 60° → 0.9, ≥ 70° → full. Max demonstrated crosswind in AFM (typ. 0.2 VS0).","Wind accountability: use ≤ 50 % of headwind, ≥ 150 % of tailwind. Crosswind = wind × sin(angle); rule of thumb: 30° → ½, 45° → ¾ (0.7), 60° → 0.9, ≥ 70° → full. Max demonstrated crosswind in AFM (typ. 0.2 VS0)."],["032.02.03.01:11","Take-off & landing: fill in the gaps → Landing: airborne distance (from 50 ft to touchdown) + ground roll. ____ at the screen (e.g. 100 ft) or ____ (+10 % speed → ~+20 % distance) increase LD significantly.","Landing: airborne distance (from 50 ft to touchdown) + ground roll. Too high at the screen (e.g. 100 ft) or too fast (+10 % speed → ~+20 % distance) increase LD significantly."],["032.02.03.01:13","Take-off & landing: fill in the gaps → MEP take-off flight path from 50 ft to 1 500 ft; domain (obstacle accountability area): half-width ____ (D = distance from end of TODA), max ____ (track change ≤ 15°, VMC/visual guidance) or ____; obstacle clearance 50 ft vertical.","MEP take-off flight path from 50 ft to 1 500 ft; domain (obstacle accountability area): half-width 90 m + 0.125 × D (D = distance from end of TODA), max 300 m (track change ≤ 15°, VMC/visual guidance) or 600 m; obstacle clearance 50 ft vertical."],["032.02.04.01:01","Climb, cruise, descent requirements: what do you remember about this? → En route: SEP must be able to reach a place for a safe forced landing …","En route: SEP must be able to reach a place for a safe forced landing if the engine fails; MEP with one engine inoperative must reach an aerodrome while clearing obstacles (net performance). IMC/night single-engine CAT needs special approval (SET-IMC)."],["032.02.04.01:02","Climb, cruise, descent requirements: fill in the gaps → ____: height gained at the obstacle = gradient × distance from reference zero; compare with obstacle + margin. ____. Example: L/D 10 → gross 10 % → net 10.5 %; from 4 000 ft above terrain, still air: 4 000/0.105 ≈ 38 000 ft ≈ 6.3 NM; headwind reduces the distance (× GS/TAS).","SEP obstacle clearance: height gained at the obstacle = gradient × distance from reference zero; compare with obstacle + margin. Net glide gradient = gross + 0.5 %. Example: L/D 10 → gross 10 % → net 10.5 %; from 4 000 ft above terrain, still air: 4 000/0.105 ≈ 38 000 ft ≈ 6.3 NM; headwind reduces the distance (× GS/TAS)."],["032.01.02.02:09","Exam trap (Climb, cruise, descent requirements): complete the fact → jet max range ≈ ____VMD, prop max range = VMD; jet max endurance = VMD, prop = VMP.","jet max range ≈ 1.32 VMD, prop max range = VMD; jet max endurance = VMD, prop = VMP."],["032.01.01.04:03","Exam trap (Climb, cruise, descent requirements): complete the fact → Factors: ____ headwind / ____ tailwind.","Factors: 50 % headwind / 150 % tailwind."],["032.01.01.01:01","EASA LO 032.01.01.01.01 → Describe the application of certification specification (CSs) with regard to the different kinds of aeroplanes.","CS-23: normal, utility, aerobatic, commuter aeroplanes (≤ 19 pax, MTOM ≤ 8 618 kg); CS-25: large aeroplanes (turbine, larger). CS-25 requirements are much more stringent (e.g. engine failure at any point of take-off must be covered) → allows a wider range of operating conditions (IMC, night, obstacles)."],["032.01.01.01:02","EASA LO 032.01.01.01.02 → Describe the general differences between aeroplanes certified according to CS-23 and CS-25.","CS-23: normal, utility, aerobatic, commuter aeroplanes (≤ 19 pax, MTOM ≤ 8 618 kg); CS-25: large aeroplanes (turbine, larger). CS-25 requirements are much more stringent (e.g. engine failure at any point of take-off must be covered) → allows a wider range of operating conditions (IMC, night, obstacles)."],["032.01.01.02:01","EASA LO 032.01.01.02.01 → Describe the basic concept that the applicable operational requirements differ depending on aeroplane performance.","Operational requirements differ with performance → performance classes (CAT): Class A = multi-engine turbo-prop > 9 pax or MTOM > 5 700 kg and all multi-engine turbojets; Class B = propeller aeroplanes ≤ 9 pax and MTOM ≤ 5 700 kg; Class C = piston aeroplanes > 9 pax or > 5 700 kg (not Class A). / Performance limits may reduce the dispatch mass (runway, climb, obstacle, en-route, landing). Safety = airworthiness standards + operational limitations."],["032.01.01.02:02","EASA LO 032.01.01.02.02 → Describe the performance classes for commercial air transport according to the applicable operational requirements.","Performance limits may reduce the dispatch mass (runway, climb, obstacle, en-route, landing). Safety = airworthiness standards + operational limitations. / Operational requirements differ with performance → performance classes (CAT): Class A = multi-engine turbo-prop > 9 pax or MTOM > 5 700 kg and all multi-engine turbojets; Class B = propeller aeroplanes ≤ 9 pax and MTOM ≤ 5 700 kg; Class C = piston aeroplanes > 9 pax or > 5 700 kg (not Class A)."],["032.01.01.03:01","EASA LO 032.01.01.03.01 → State that aeroplane performance required for commercial air transport may limit the weight of a dispatched aeroplane in order to achieve a sufficient level of safety.","Performance limits may reduce the dispatch mass (runway, climb, obstacle, en-route, landing). Safety = airworthiness standards + operational limitations."],["032.01.01.04:01","EASA LO 032.01.01.04.01 → Describe measured performance and explain how it is determined.","Measured performance = obtained in flight test with a test pilot/new aircraft. Gross = average performance of the fleet (measured, corrected to standard). Net = gross degraded by safety factors (margins for pilot, wear, weather)."],["032.01.01.04:05","EASA LO 032.01.01.04.05 → Describe the relationship between net and gross take-off and landing distances, and net and gross climb and descent gradients.","The size of a factor depends on the likelihood of the event and the scatter of the data (rarer event → smaller margin). Net distances = gross × factor (larger); net climb gradient = gross − margin (smaller); net descent gradient steeper. / Measured performance = obtained in flight test with a test pilot/new aircraft. Gross = average performance of the fleet (measured, corrected to standard). Net = gross degraded by safety factors (margins for pilot, wear, weather)."],["032.01.02.02:03","EASA LO 032.01.02.02.03 → Define the terms ‘descent angle’ and ‘descent gradient’.","Powered descent: gradient = (D − T)/W; idle thrust + drag excess determines angle; higher speed/weight/altitude affect rate. / Climb angle γ (degrees, relative to the horizon, in still air); climb gradient = height gain ÷ horizontal distance × 100 % (≈ tan γ). Flight-path angle/gradient = relative to the ground (includes wind); climb angle is relative to the air mass. Descent analogously."],["032.01.02.02:04","EASA LO 032.01.02.02.04 → Explain the difference between climb/descent angle and flight-path angle.","Climb angle γ (degrees, relative to the horizon, in still air); climb gradient = height gain ÷ horizontal distance × 100 % (≈ tan γ). Flight-path angle/gradient = relative to the ground (includes wind); climb angle is relative to the air mass. Descent analogously."],["032.01.02.02:06","EASA LO 032.01.02.02.06 → Describe ‘clearway’ and ‘stopway’ according to CS-Definitions.","TORA = runway length available for the ground run. TODA = TORA + clearway. ASDA = TORA + stopway. Read from AIP/aerodrome charts."],["032.01.02.02:08","EASA LO 032.01.02.02.08 → Describe ‘screen height’ including its various values.","Screen height: height at the end of TOD/start of LD — Class B take-off & landing: 50 ft; Class A take-off: 35 ft dry (15 ft wet), Class A landing 50 ft."],["032.01.02.02:11","EASA LO 032.01.02.02.11 → Define the power available and power required.","Power required = drag × TAS; power available = thrust × TAS. / Drag curve: induced drag falls with speed, parasite rises → minimum drag at VMD (best L/D). Power required minimum at VMP < VMD. Excess thrust/power → acceleration. Max level speed where thrust available = thrust required. Max TAS increases with altitude to a point, max IAS decreases."],["032.01.02.03:02","EASA LO 032.01.02.03.02 → Describe how, for different density altitudes, the thrust and power available vary with speed for a propeller-driven aeroplane.","Propeller: thrust available decreases with speed; power available roughly constant (rises to max then falls). Turbojet: thrust available roughly constant with speed; power available rises linearly with TAS. Both fall with increasing density altitude. / Factors: pressure altitude & temperature (density), wind, weight, configuration, anti-skid status, CG, runway surface, runway slope."],["032.01.02.03:04","EASA LO 032.01.02.03.04 → Describe how, for different density altitudes, the drag and power required vary with indicated airspeeds (IAS) and true airspeeds (TAS).","Power required = drag × TAS; power available = thrust × TAS. / Drag vs IAS is independent of altitude (same curve); vs TAS it shifts right with altitude. Power required curve shifts up and right with altitude (P = D × TAS). Higher weight or flaps/gear → drag and power-required curves shift up (and right)."],["032.01.03.01:01","EASA LO 032.01.03.01.01 → Explain how drag (thrust) and power required vary with speed in straight and level flight.","Power required = drag × TAS; power available = thrust × TAS. / Drag curve: induced drag falls with speed, parasite rises → minimum drag at VMD (best L/D). Power required minimum at VMP < VMD. Excess thrust/power → acceleration. Max level speed where thrust available = thrust required. Max TAS increases with altitude to a point, max IAS decreases."],["032.01.03.01:03","EASA LO 032.01.03.01.03 → Interpret the ‘thrust/power required’ and ‘thrust/power available’ curves in straight and level flight.","Power required = drag × TAS; power available = thrust × TAS. / Drag curve: induced drag falls with speed, parasite rises → minimum drag at VMD (best L/D). Power required minimum at VMP < VMD. Excess thrust/power → acceleration. Max level speed where thrust available = thrust required. Max TAS increases with altitude to a point, max IAS decreases."],["032.01.03.01:04","EASA LO 032.01.03.01.04 → Describe how the maximum achievable straight and level flight IAS and TAS vary with altitude.","Drag curve: induced drag falls with speed, parasite rises → minimum drag at VMD (best L/D). Power required minimum at VMP < VMD. Excess thrust/power → acceleration. Max level speed where thrust available = thrust required. Max TAS increases with altitude to a point, max IAS decreases. / Power required = drag × TAS; power available = thrust × TAS."],["032.01.03.01:05","EASA LO 032.01.03.01.05 → Describe situations in which a pilot may elect to fly for ‘maximum endurance’ or ‘maximum range’.","Wind: headwind → fly faster than the still-air optimum; tailwind → slightly slower. Endurance is not affected by wind. Jet endurance improves with altitude (lower SFC); prop endurance best low."],["032.01.03.02:01","EASA LO 032.01.03.02.01 → Define a turbojet aeroplane’s specific fuel consumption (SFC) and describe how it affects fuel flow and specific range.","SFC jet = fuel flow per unit thrust (kg/N/h); SFC prop = fuel flow per unit power. SR = TAS/(SFC × thrust). / Range = distance flown with the fuel; endurance = time. SR = NAM per kg fuel = TAS ÷ fuel flow; SRG = NGM per kg = GS ÷ fuel flow."],["032.01.03.02:02","EASA LO 032.01.03.02.02 → Define a propeller-driven aeroplane’s SFC and describe how it affects fuel flow and specific range.","SFC jet = fuel flow per unit thrust (kg/N/h); SFC prop = fuel flow per unit power. SR = TAS/(SFC × thrust). / Range = distance flown with the fuel; endurance = time. SR = NAM per kg fuel = TAS ÷ fuel flow; SRG = NGM per kg = GS ÷ fuel flow."],["032.01.03.02:04","EASA LO 032.01.03.02.04 → Explain the optimum speed to achieve maximum SR for a propeller-driven aeroplane in relation to the power required and drag graphs.","Power required = drag × TAS; power available = thrust × TAS."],["032.01.03.02:06","EASA LO 032.01.03.02.06 → State how a turbojet engine’s SFC varies with temperature and thrust setting.","SFC jet = fuel flow per unit thrust (kg/N/h); SFC prop = fuel flow per unit power. SR = TAS/(SFC × thrust)."],["032.01.03.02:09","EASA LO 032.01.03.02.09 → Explain the effect of weight on the optimum altitude for maximum range.","Weight: heavier → more drag → less range, higher optimum speed; forward CG → more trim drag → less range. Jet SFC improves at lower temperature and higher rpm/thrust (near design point). / Jet SR increases with altitude up to an optimum altitude (which rises as weight decreases — step climbs); prop SRG roughly independent of altitude (best at moderate altitudes; supercharged engines better higher up)."],["032.01.03.03:01","EASA LO 032.01.03.03.01 → Explain fuel flow in relation to TAS and thrust for a turbojet aeroplane.","SFC jet = fuel flow per unit thrust (kg/N/h); SFC prop = fuel flow per unit power. SR = TAS/(SFC × thrust). / Range = distance flown with the fuel; endurance = time. SR = NAM per kg fuel = TAS ÷ fuel flow; SRG = NGM per kg = GS ÷ fuel flow."],["032.01.03.03:03","EASA LO 032.01.03.03.03 → Explain fuel flow in relation to TAS and thrust for a propeller-driven aeroplane.","SFC jet = fuel flow per unit thrust (kg/N/h); SFC prop = fuel flow per unit power. SR = TAS/(SFC × thrust). / Range = distance flown with the fuel; endurance = time. SR = NAM per kg fuel = TAS ÷ fuel flow; SRG = NGM per kg = GS ÷ fuel flow."],["032.01.03.03:06","EASA LO 032.01.03.03.06 → Explain the effect of wind and altitude on endurance, and the maximum endurance speed for a propeller-driven aeroplane.","Wind: headwind → fly faster than the still-air optimum; tailwind → slightly slower. Endurance is not affected by wind. Jet endurance improves with altitude (lower SFC); prop endurance best low."],["032.01.04.01:03","EASA LO 032.01.04.01.03 → Explain climb performance in relation to the thrust available and thrust required (angle of climb), and power available and power required (rate of climb).","Power required = drag × TAS; power available = thrust × TAS. / Drag curve: induced drag falls with speed, parasite rises → minimum drag at VMD (best L/D). Power required minimum at VMP < VMD. Excess thrust/power → acceleration. Max level speed where thrust available = thrust required. Max TAS increases with altitude to a point, max IAS decreases."],["032.01.04.01:04","EASA LO 032.01.04.01.04 → Explain the meaning and effect of ‘excess thrust’ and ‘excess power’ in a steady climb.","VX (best angle) = max excess thrust; VY (best rate) = max excess power; VX < VY. Jet: VX ≈ VMD. At the absolute ceiling VX = VY. / Powered descent: gradient = (D − T)/W; idle thrust + drag excess determines angle; higher speed/weight/altitude affect rate."],["032.01.04.01:05","EASA LO 032.01.04.01.05 → Interpret the ‘thrust/power required’ and ‘thrust/power available’ curves in a steady climb.","Power required = drag × TAS; power available = thrust × TAS. / Drag curve: induced drag falls with speed, parasite rises → minimum drag at VMD (best L/D). Power required minimum at VMP < VMD. Excess thrust/power → acceleration. Max level speed where thrust available = thrust required. Max TAS increases with altitude to a point, max IAS decreases."],["032.01.04.01:06","EASA LO 032.01.04.01.06 → State the difference between climb angle and gradient.","Forces: sin γ = (T − D)/W → climb gradient ≈ (T − D)/W. Rate of climb = TAS × sin γ = (PA − PR)/W. / Climb angle γ (degrees, relative to the horizon, in still air); climb gradient = height gain ÷ horizontal distance × 100 % (≈ tan γ). Flight-path angle/gradient = relative to the ground (includes wind); climb angle is relative to the air mass. Descent analogously."],["032.01.04.01:07","EASA LO 032.01.04.01.07 → Explain the effect of weight on the climb angle and rate of climb, and the speed for best angle and best rate of climb.","VX (best angle) = max excess thrust; VY (best rate) = max excess power; VX < VY. Jet: VX ≈ VMD. At the absolute ceiling VX = VY. / Heavier: angle & rate ↓, VX/VY ↑. Higher altitude/temperature: angle & rate ↓ (VX IAS roughly constant/increases slightly, VY decreases → they converge). Inversion: climbing into warmer air reduces performance. Flaps/gear: more drag → angle & rate ↓, speeds ↓."],["032.01.04.01:08","EASA LO 032.01.04.01.08 → Explain the effects of pressure altitude and temperature, including an inversion on climb performance (angle and rate of climb).","Heavier: angle & rate ↓, VX/VY ↑. Higher altitude/temperature: angle & rate ↓ (VX IAS roughly constant/increases slightly, VY decreases → they converge). Inversion: climbing into warmer air reduces performance. Flaps/gear: more drag → angle & rate ↓, speeds ↓. / Factors: pressure altitude & temperature (density), wind, weight, configuration, anti-skid status, CG, runway surface, runway slope."],["032.01.05.01:01","EASA LO 032.01.05.01.01 → Resolve the forces during steady descent and in the glide.","Glide: sin γ = D/W → gradient = 1/(L/D). Best glide range at VMD (max L/D); minimum sink at VMP. / Forces: sin γ = (T − D)/W → climb gradient ≈ (T − D)/W. Rate of climb = TAS × sin γ = (PA − PR)/W."],["032.01.05.01:03","EASA LO 032.01.05.01.03 → Explain the meaning of ‘excess thrust required’ (excess drag) and ‘excess power required’ in a steady descent.","Powered descent: gradient = (D − T)/W; idle thrust + drag excess determines angle; higher speed/weight/altitude affect rate. / Power required = drag × TAS; power available = thrust × TAS."],["032.01.05.01:04","EASA LO 032.01.05.01.04 → Interpret the ‘thrust/power required’ and ‘thrust/power available’ curves in a steady descent.","Power required = drag × TAS; power available = thrust × TAS. / Drag curve: induced drag falls with speed, parasite rises → minimum drag at VMD (best L/D). Power required minimum at VMP < VMD. Excess thrust/power → acceleration. Max level speed where thrust available = thrust required. Max TAS increases with altitude to a point, max IAS decreases."],["032.02.01.01:02","EASA LO 032.02.01.01.02 → Describe the limitations on VR, on the speed at 50 ft above the take-off surface and on VREF, and given the appropriate stall speed, estimate the values based on these limitations for a single-engine, class B aeroplane.","VS0 = stall speed landing configuration; VS1 = stall speed in a specified configuration; VR rotation speed; V50 speed at 50 ft; VREF reference landing speed at 50 ft. / Screen height: height at the end of TOD/start of LD — Class B take-off & landing: 50 ft; Class A take-off: 35 ft dry (15 ft wet), Class A landing 50 ft."],["032.02.01.01:03","EASA LO 032.02.01.01.03 → Describe the limitations on VR, on the speed at 50 ft above the take-off surface and on VREF, and given the appropriate stall speed, estimate the values based on these limitations for a multi-engine, class B aeroplane.","VS0 = stall speed landing configuration; VS1 = stall speed in a specified configuration; VR rotation speed; V50 speed at 50 ft; VREF reference landing speed at 50 ft. / Screen height: height at the end of TOD/start of LD — Class B take-off & landing: 50 ft; Class A take-off: 35 ft dry (15 ft wet), Class A landing 50 ft."],["032.02.01.01:04","EASA LO 032.02.01.01.04 → Describe the European Union airworthiness requirements according to CS-23 relating to aeroplane performance (stall, take-off, climb, landing).","VS0 = stall speed landing configuration; VS1 = stall speed in a specified configuration; VR rotation speed; V50 speed at 50 ft; VREF reference landing speed at 50 ft."],["032.02.01.01:07","EASA LO 032.02.01.01.07 → Explain the effect of engine failure on the minimum control speed of a multi-engine aeroplane under given conditions (temperature and pressure altitude).","Factors: pressure altitude & temperature (density), wind, weight, configuration, anti-skid status, CG, runway surface, runway slope. / Engine failure in a twin: power required ↑ (asymmetric drag, sideslip, windmilling/feathered prop), climb reduced strongly; VMC decreases with altitude/temperature (less thrust from the live engine)."],["032.02.03.01:04","EASA LO 032.02.03.01.04 → For both fixed-pitch and constant-speed propeller aeroplanes, explain the effect of airspeed on thrust during the take-off run.","Propeller: thrust available decreases with speed; power available roughly constant (rises to max then falls). Turbojet: thrust available roughly constant with speed; power available rises linearly with TAS. Both fall with increasing density altitude. / Fixed-pitch prop: thrust low at the start, efficiency increases with speed; constant-speed: full power available from brake release. Brake release before full power → longer TOD and ASD."],["032.02.03.01:06","EASA LO 032.02.03.01.06 → Explain the effect of wind on take-off and landing distances, and determine the actual","Upslope → longer take-off (+ ~5 % per 1 % slope for Class B), shorter landing; downslope → longer landing (+5 % per 1 %). Wet/grass/contaminated → longer (dry grass ×1.2, wet grass ×1.3 on take-off; wet runway landing ×1.15). Elevation/temperature ↑ → density ↓ → all distances ↑, climb ↓. / Flaps: take-off flap shortens ground roll but reduces climb; landing flap shortens LD."],["032.02.03.01:08","EASA LO 032.02.03.01.08 → Explain the percentage of accountability for headwind and tailwind components during take-off and landing calculations.","Factors: 50 % headwind / 150 % tailwind. / Wind accountability: use ≤ 50 % of headwind, ≥ 150 % of tailwind. Crosswind = wind × sin(angle); rule of thumb: 30° → ½, 45° → ¾ (0.7), 60° → 0.9, ≥ 70° → full. Max demonstrated crosswind in AFM (typ. 0.2 VS0)."],["032.02.03.01:09","EASA LO 032.02.03.01.09 → Explain the effect of runway conditions on the landing distance.","Upslope → longer take-off (+ ~5 % per 1 % slope for Class B), shorter landing; downslope → longer landing (+5 % per 1 %). Wet/grass/contaminated → longer (dry grass ×1.2, wet grass ×1.3 on take-off; wet runway landing ×1.15). Elevation/temperature ↑ → density ↓ → all distances ↑, climb ↓. / Landing: airborne distance (from 50 ft to touchdown) + ground roll. Too high at the screen (e.g. 100 ft) or too fast (+10 % speed → ~+20 % distance) increase LD significantly."],["032.02.03.01:10","EASA LO 032.02.03.01.10 → Explain the effects of pressure altitude and temperature on the take-off distance, take-off climb, landing distance and approach climb.","Upslope → longer take-off (+ ~5 % per 1 % slope for Class B), shorter landing; downslope → longer landing (+5 % per 1 %). Wet/grass/contaminated → longer (dry grass ×1.2, wet grass ×1.3 on take-off; wet runway landing ×1.15). Elevation/temperature ↑ → density ↓ → all distances ↑, climb ↓. / Flaps: take-off flap shortens ground roll but reduces climb; landing flap shortens LD."],["032.02.04.01:03","EASA LO 032.02.04.01.03 → For a single-engine aeroplane, calculate the net glide gradient and net glide distance, given aeroplane altitude, terrain elevation, gross gradient or lift/drag ratio (L/D ratio), and headwind or tailwind component.","SEP obstacle clearance: height gained at the obstacle = gradient × distance from reference zero; compare with obstacle + margin. Net glide gradient = gross + 0.5 %. Example: L/D 10 → gross 10 % → net 10.5 %; from 4 000 ft above terrain, still air: 4 000/0.105 ≈ 38 000 ft ≈ 6.3 NM; headwind reduces the distance (× GS/TAS). / Factors: 50 % headwind / 150 % tailwind."]],"49":[["032.03.03.01:01","How to work the CAP 698 MEP1 charts: fill in the gaps → Charts are entered in order: ____ ____. Follow the guide lines parallel between reference lines; wind lines already contain the ____ factors in CAP 698 graphs (check the chart notes).","Charts are entered in order: OAT → pressure altitude (reference line) → mass → wind → (slope/obstacle) → result. Follow the guide lines parallel between reference lines; wind lines already contain the 50 % headwind / 150 % tailwind factors in CAP 698 graphs (check the chart notes)."],["032.03.03.01:01","How to work the CAP 698 MEP1 charts: what do you remember about this? → Take-off speeds (VR, V50) and landing speeds (VREF) are read from the …","Take-off speeds (VR, V50) and landing speeds (VREF) are read from the tables next to the graph as a function of mass."],["032.03.03.01:01","Take-off (MEP, Class B): fill in the gaps → ____: work the chart ____ — from the available (defactored) distance back to the mass line.","Field-length-limited TOM: work the chart backwards — from the available (defactored) distance back to the mass line."],["032.03.03.01:02","Take-off (MEP, Class B): fill in the gaps → ____ data: distance to accelerate to decision speed, lose an engine and either continue to 50 ft or stop; MEP Class B has ____ after engine failure before 50 ft (only data provided).","Accelerate-go / accelerate-stop data: distance to accelerate to decision speed, lose an engine and either continue to 50 ft or stop; MEP Class B has no requirement to be able to continue after engine failure before 50 ft (only data provided)."],["032.03.03.01:03","Take-off (MEP, Class B): fill in the gaps → ____ from the graphs; ____ ____ data (rate/gradient at the specified climb speed, gear up, flap up).","Ground roll and TOD from the graphs; AEO and OEI take-off climb data (rate/gradient at the specified climb speed, gear up, flap up)."],["032.03.03.01:06","Take-off (MEP, Class B): fill in the gaps → ____ for a given mass: enter from mass and required distance, read the wind needed.","Minimum headwind / maximum tailwind for a given mass: enter from mass and required distance, read the wind needed."],["032.03.03.01:01","Take-off (MEP, Class B): fill in the gaps → ____:","Factoring for CAT (Class B, CAT.POL.A.305):"],["032.03.03.01:08","Take-off (MEP, Class B) – No stopway/clearway: what are the key facts?","TOD × 1.25 ≤ TORA"],["032.03.03.01:07","Take-off (MEP, Class B) – With stopway and/or clearway: what are the key facts?","TOD ≤ TORA; TOD × 1.15 ≤ TODA; TOD × 1.3 ≤ ASDA"],["032.03.03.01:08","Take-off (MEP, Class B) – Surface: what are the key facts?","dry grass × 1.2, wet grass × 1.3 (firm soil)"],["032.03.03.01:01","Take-off (MEP, Class B) – Slope: what are the key facts?","+ 5 % per 1 % upslope (no credit for downslope)"],["032.03.03.01:05","Take-off (MEP, Class B) – Wet paved RWY: what are the key facts?","factor as given in AFM (typ. × 1.0–1.15)"],["032.03.03.01:01","Take-off (MEP, Class B): fill in the gaps → ____ (the distance you may enter into the graph) = available distance ÷ all applicable factors. Example: TORA 1 000 m, no stopway, dry grass, 1 % upslope: 1 000 ÷ (1.25 × 1.2 × 1.05) = ____.","Defactored distance (the distance you may enter into the graph) = available distance ÷ all applicable factors. Example: TORA 1 000 m, no stopway, dry grass, 1 % upslope: 1 000 ÷ (1.25 × 1.2 × 1.05) = 635 m."],["032.03.03.01:08","Take-off (MEP, Class B): fill in the gaps → ____ = graph distance × factors (inverse of the above).","Minimum TORA/TODA required = graph distance × factors (inverse of the above)."],["032.03.03.02:01","Climb: fill in the gaps → ____ from the climb chart (OAT, pressure altitude, mass → ROC ft/min), AEO and OEI.","Rate of climb from the climb chart (OAT, pressure altitude, mass → ROC ft/min), AEO and OEI."],["032.03.03.01:05","Climb: fill in the gaps → ____ = ROC ÷ TAS: ____ ____ (≈ ROC/TAS in %). Flight-path gradient with wind: use GS instead of TAS (headwind → steeper flight path).","Gradient = ROC ÷ TAS: gradient (%) ≈ ROC (ft/min) ÷ TAS (kt) × 0.98 (≈ ROC/TAS in %). Flight-path gradient with wind: use GS instead of TAS (headwind → steeper flight path)."],["032.03.03.02:02","Climb: fill in the gaps → ____: height at obstacle = 50 ft + gradient × horizontal distance from the end of TOD; compare with obstacle height + 50 ft margin (Class B).","Obstacle clearance: height at obstacle = 50 ft + gradient × horizontal distance from the end of TOD; compare with obstacle height + 50 ft margin (Class B)."],["032.03.03.04:01","Landing: fill in the gaps → ____: work backwards from the defactored LDA.","Field-length-limited landing mass: work backwards from the defactored LDA."],["032.03.03.04:02","Landing: fill in the gaps → ____ data: rate/gradient with all engines, full landing flap, gear down.","Balked-landing climb data: rate/gradient with all engines, full landing flap, gear down."],["032.03.03.04:03","Landing: fill in the gaps → ____ from the graph (flap position, mass, pressure altitude, OAT, wind, slope).","Landing distance & ground roll from the graph (flap position, mass, pressure altitude, OAT, wind, slope)."],["032.03.03.04:01","Landing: fill in the gaps → ____:","Factoring for CAT (Class B, CAT.POL.A.325–335):"],["032.03.03.04:05","Landing – Basic dry, paved: what are the key facts?","LD ≤ 70 % of LDA → LD × 1.43 (= 1/0.7)"],["032.03.03.04:05","Landing – Wet runway: what are the key facts?","additional × 1.15"],["032.03.03.04:01","Landing – Dry grass: what are the key facts?","additional × 1.15 (wet grass × 1.3 in total, see AFM)"],["032.03.03.04:01","Landing – Downslope: what are the key facts?","+ 5 % per 1 % downslope"],["032.03.03.04:01","Landing: fill in the gaps → Example: LDA 1 200 m, dry paved, level → defactored = 1 200 × 0.7 = ____; wet → 840 ÷ 1.15 = ____.","Example: LDA 1 200 m, dry paved, level → defactored = 1 200 × 0.7 = 840 m; wet → 840 ÷ 1.15 = 730 m."],["032.03.03.04:05","Landing: what do you remember about this? → Minimum LDA required = graph LD ÷ 0.7 × other …","Minimum LDA required = graph LD ÷ 0.7 × other factors."],["032.03.03.01:04","EASA LO 032.03.03.01.04 → Determine the all-engine-out and critical-engine-out take-off climb data.","Accelerate-go / accelerate-stop data: distance to accelerate to decision speed, lose an engine and either continue to 50 ft or stop; MEP Class B has no requirement to be able to continue after engine failure before 50 ft (only data provided)."],["032.03.03.02:03","EASA LO 032.03.03.02.03 → Determine the still-air and flight-path gradients for given IAS, altitude, temperature, aeroplane weight and, if relevant, wind component.","Gradient = ROC ÷ TAS: gradient (%) ≈ ROC (ft/min) ÷ TAS (kt) × 0.98 (≈ ROC/TAS in %). Flight-path gradient with wind: use GS instead of TAS (headwind → steeper flight path)."],["032.03.03.04:04","EASA LO 032.03.03.04.04 → Calculate, given the landing distance available (LDA), slope and surface type and condition, the defactored distance to be used for commercial air transport using the appropriate landing graphs.","Field-length-limited landing mass: work backwards from the defactored LDA."]],"50":[["010.13.01.01:01","ICAO Annex 13 definitions: fill in the gaps → ____: an occurrence associated with the operation of an aircraft (from boarding with intention of flight until disembarkation; for UAS from ready-to-move until shut-down) in which: ____ a person is ____ (in the aircraft, by direct contact with any part of it incl. detached parts, or by jet blast) — except natural causes, self-inflicted, by other persons, stowaways; or (b) the aircraft sustains damage or structural failure that adversely affects strength/performance/flight characteristics and would normally require major repair or replacement (not: engine failure limited to the engine, damage to props, wingtips, antennas, tyres, brakes, fairings, small dents/holes); or (c) the aircraft is missing (official search terminated, wreckage not located) or completely inaccessible.","Accident: an occurrence associated with the operation of an aircraft (from boarding with intention of flight until disembarkation; for UAS from ready-to-move until shut-down) in which: (a) a person is fatally or seriously injured (in the aircraft, by direct contact with any part of it incl. detached parts, or by jet blast) — except natural causes, self-inflicted, by other persons, stowaways; or (b) the aircraft sustains damage or structural failure that adversely affects strength/performance/flight characteristics and would normally require major repair or replacement (not: engine failure limited to the engine, damage to props, wingtips, antennas, tyres, brakes, fairings, small dents/holes); or (c) the aircraft is missing (official search terminated, wreckage not located) or completely inaccessible."],["010.13.01.01:02","ICAO Annex 13 definitions: fill in the gaps → ____: an incident involving circumstances indicating that there was a ____ (the difference to an accident lies only in the result).","Serious incident: an incident involving circumstances indicating that there was a high probability of an accident (the difference to an accident lies only in the result)."],["010.13.01.01:01","ICAO Annex 13 definitions: what do you remember about this? → Examples: near-collision requiring avoiding manoeuvre, CFIT only …","Examples: near-collision requiring avoiding manoeuvre, CFIT only marginally avoided, aborted take-off on a closed/occupied runway, fire/smoke in cabin, fuel state requiring emergency, multiple system failures, incapacitation of a crew member."],["010.13.01.01:01","ICAO Annex 13 definitions: fill in the gaps → ____: an occurrence, other than an accident, associated with the operation of an aircraft which affects or could affect the safety of operation.","Incident: an occurrence, other than an accident, associated with the operation of an aircraft which affects or could affect the safety of operation."],["010.13.01.01:01","ICAO Annex 13 definitions: fill in the gaps → ____: hospitalisation ____ commencing within 7 days; most bone fractures (except simple fractures of fingers, toes, nose); lacerations causing severe haemorrhage, nerve/muscle/tendon damage; injury to internal organs; 2nd/3rd-degree burns or any burns > 5 % of body surface; verified exposure to infectious substances or injurious radiation.","Serious injury: hospitalisation > 48 h commencing within 7 days; most bone fractures (except simple fractures of fingers, toes, nose); lacerations causing severe haemorrhage, nerve/muscle/tendon damage; injury to internal organs; 2nd/3rd-degree burns or any burns > 5 % of body surface; verified exposure to infectious substances or injurious radiation."],["010.13.02.01:01","ICAO Annex 13 definitions: fill in the gaps → ____ ____: death within ____ of the accident.","Fatal injury: death within 30 days of the accident."],["010.13.01.01:01","ICAO Annex 13 definitions: fill in the gaps → ____: any recorder installed to complement accident/incident investigation. ____: maximum certificated take-off mass.","Flight recorder: any recorder installed to complement accident/incident investigation. Maximum mass: maximum certificated take-off mass."],["010.13.01.01:01","ICAO Annex 13 definitions: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (territory where it happened — normally conducts the investigation), ____, State of the Operator, State of Design (type design), State of Manufacture (final assembly).","States: State of Occurrence (territory where it happened — normally conducts the investigation), State of Registry, State of the Operator, State of Design (type design), State of Manufacture (final assembly)."],["010.13.01.01:01","ICAO Annex 13 definitions: fill in the gaps → ____: process to prevent accidents incl. gathering and analysis of information, conclusions, causes/contributing factors and safety recommendations. ____: person/organisation engaged in aircraft operation.","Investigation: process to prevent accidents incl. gathering and analysis of information, conclusions, causes/contributing factors and safety recommendations. Operator: person/organisation engaged in aircraft operation."],["010.13.03.01:04","Objective and procedure: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ of future accidents and incidents — ____. Investigations must be independent of judicial proceedings.","Sole objective: prevention of future accidents and incidents — not to apportion blame or liability. Investigations must be independent of judicial proceedings."],["010.13.01.01:01","Objective and procedure: fill in the gaps → Procedure: ____ without delay by the State of Occurrence to State of Registry, Operator, Design, Manufacture and ICAO (aircraft > 2 250 kg); protection of evidence and custody of the aircraft;","Procedure: notification without delay by the State of Occurrence to State of Registry, Operator, Design, Manufacture and ICAO (aircraft > 2 250 kg); protection of evidence and custody of the aircraft;"],["010.13.02.01:01","Objective and procedure: fill in the gaps → State of Occurrence ____ (may delegate); other States appoint ____ and advisers; flight recorders read out; ____ sent to involved States for comments (60 days);","State of Occurrence institutes the investigation (may delegate); other States appoint accredited representatives and advisers; flight recorders read out; draft final report sent to involved States for comments (60 days);"],["010.13.02.01:01","Objective and procedure: fill in the gaps → ____ published as soon as possible, ideally within ____ (otherwise an interim statement on each anniversary); ____ recommendations at any stage. ADREP reports to ICAO.","Final Report published as soon as possible, ideally within 12 months (otherwise an interim statement on each anniversary); safety recommendations at any stage. ADREP reports to ICAO."],["010.13.03.01:01","EU framework: fill in the gaps → ____: investigation and prevention of accidents and serious incidents in the EU; each Member State has an ____; investigation is ____ for accidents and serious incidents of aircraft covered by the Basic Regulation; uses the same definitions as Annex 13 (list of serious-incident examples in the annex).","Regulation (EU) 996/2010: investigation and prevention of accidents and serious incidents in the EU; each Member State has an independent safety investigation authority; investigation is mandatory for accidents and serious incidents of aircraft covered by the Basic Regulation; uses the same definitions as Annex 13 (list of serious-incident examples in the annex)."],["010.13.03.01:02","EU framework: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ ____ — mandatory and voluntary reporting systems, protection of the reporter (just culture), confidentiality, European Central Repository. 996/2010 covers investigation; 376/2014 covers collection of occurrence reports that feed safety analysis (both aim at prevention).","Regulation (EU) 376/2014: reporting, analysis and follow- up of occurrences — mandatory and voluntary reporting systems, protection of the reporter (just culture), confidentiality, European Central Repository. 996/2010 covers investigation; 376/2014 covers collection of occurrence reports that feed safety analysis (both aim at prevention)."],["010.13.03.01:03","EU framework: fill in the gaps → ____: occurrences which endanger or could endanger aircraft, occupants or third parties.","Scope of 376/2014: occurrences which endanger or could endanger aircraft, occupants or third parties."],["010.13.03.01:01","EU framework: fill in the gaps → ____ (Implementing Reg. 2015/1018 list): e.g. operational events (TCAS RA, level bust, runway incursion, unstabilised approach), technical failures, ATM events, bird strikes with damage, fuel emergencies, incapacitation. Report within ____ ____.","Mandatory reporting (Implementing Reg. 2015/1018 list): e.g. operational events (TCAS RA, level bust, runway incursion, unstabilised approach), technical failures, ATM events, bird strikes with damage, fuel emergencies, incapacitation. Report within 72 h."],["010.13.03.01:01","EU framework: fill in the gaps → ____: any other safety-related information the reporter considers relevant (hazards, near misses not on the mandatory list).","Voluntary reporting: any other safety-related information the reporter considers relevant (hazards, near misses not on the mandatory list)."],["010.13.03.01:01","EU framework: what do you remember about this? → Organisations collect reports in databases, classify (ECCAIRS …","Organisations collect reports in databases, classify (ECCAIRS taxonomy), analyse, determine follow-up actions and transfer them to the competent authority → European Central Repository; data protected, not used for blame."],["010.13.03.01:01","Exam trap (EU framework): complete the fact → hospitalised > ____ (within ____days) = serious injury; death within 30 days = fatal.","hospitalised > 48 h (within 7 days) = serious injury; death within 30 days = fatal."],["010.13.01.01:03","EASA LO 010.13.01.01.03 → Determine whether a certain occurrence has to be defined as a serious incident or as an accident.","Regulation (EU) 996/2010: investigation and prevention of accidents and serious incidents in the EU; each Member State has an independent safety investigation authority; investigation is mandatory for accidents and serious incidents of aircraft covered by the Basic Regulation; uses the same definitions as Annex 13 (list of serious-incident examples in the annex)."]],"51":[["050.10.01.01:02","Surface observations: fill in the gaps → Gust (METAR): maximum wind speed during the last ____ exceeds the mean by ____ → reported as G . METAR wind = ____, true direction; tower wind for take-off/landing = 2-min mean, magnetic direction, measured at the runway.","Gust (METAR): maximum wind speed during the last 10 min exceeds the mean by ≥ 10 kt → reported as G . METAR wind = 10-min mean, true direction; tower wind for take-off/landing = 2-min mean, magnetic direction, measured at the runway."],["050.10.01.01:10","Surface observations: fill in the gaps → ____: greatest distance at which a black object of suitable size near the ground can be seen and recognised against a bright background (by day), or lights of ~1 000 cd identified (night). Measured by observer or instruments (forward-scatter meters, transmissometers).","Visibility: greatest distance at which a black object of suitable size near the ground can be seen and recognised against a bright background (by day), or lights of ~1 000 cd identified (night). Measured by observer or instruments (forward-scatter meters, transmissometers)."],["050.10.01.01:05","Surface observations: fill in the gaps → ____: the value reached or exceeded within at least ____ of the horizon circle or half of the aerodrome surface (not necessarily contiguous). ____ also reported if < 1 500 m or < 50 % of prevailing (with direction).","Prevailing visibility: the value reached or exceeded within at least half of the horizon circle or half of the aerodrome surface (not necessarily contiguous). Minimum visibility also reported if < 1 500 m or < 50 % of prevailing (with direction)."],["050.10.01.01:07","Surface observations: fill in the gaps → ____ ____: visibility at an aerodrome as reported by an accredited observer or automatic system. Units: m, km (≥ 5 km), statute miles (US).","Ground visibility: visibility at an aerodrome as reported by an accredited observer or automatic system. Units: m, km (≥ 5 km), statute miles (US)."],["050.10.01.01:08","Surface observations: fill in the gaps → ____: the range over which the pilot on the runway centre line can see the runway surface markings or the lights. Measured by ____ beside the runway at ____, mid-point and stop end (~ 120 m from centre line, ~ 300 m from threshold).","RVR: the range over which the pilot on the runway centre line can see the runway surface markings or the lights. Measured by transmissometers/forward-scatter meters beside the runway at touchdown zone, mid-point and stop end (~ 120 m from centre line, ~ 300 m from threshold)."],["050.10.01.01:23","Surface observations: fill in the gaps → Reported when visibility or RVR ____; units m (ft in USA); limits 50–2 000 m. Transmitted in METAR ( R24/0550U ), ATIS, by ATC.","Reported when visibility or RVR < 1 500 m; units m (ft in USA); limits 50–2 000 m. Transmitted in METAR ( R24/0550U ), ATIS, by ATC."],["050.10.01.01:05","Surface observations: fill in the gaps → Comparison: ____ is runway-specific and uses lights (usually higher than meteorological visibility at night); prevailing visibility = observer, whole aerodrome; ground visibility = aerodrome visibility.","Comparison: RVR is runway-specific and uses lights (usually higher than meteorological visibility at night); prevailing visibility = observer, whole aerodrome; ground visibility = aerodrome visibility."],["050.10.01.01:14","Surface observations: fill in the gaps → ____: observer, present-weather sensors (precipitation type, intensity).","Present weather: observer, present-weather sensors (precipitation type, intensity)."],["050.10.01.01:18","Surface observations: fill in the gaps → ____: amount in ____ (eighths of sky): FEW 1–2, SCT 3–4, BKN 5–7, OVC 8; type (only ____ and TCU named in METAR/TAF; SIGMET: CB); base by ceilometer (laser/light beam), in ft above aerodrome elevation; tops from pilot reports/satellite. Cloud base = lowest height of the cloud layer.","Clouds: amount in oktas (eighths of sky): FEW 1–2, SCT 3–4, BKN 5–7, OVC 8; type (only CB and TCU named in METAR/TAF; SIGMET: CB); base by ceilometer (laser/light beam), in ft above aerodrome elevation; tops from pilot reports/satellite. Cloud base = lowest height of the cloud layer."],["050.10.01.01:18","Surface observations: fill in the gaps → ____ = height of the base of the lowest layer covering ____ the sky (BKN/OVC) below ____.","Ceiling = height of the base of the lowest layer covering more than half the sky (BKN/OVC) below 20 000 ft."],["050.10.01.01:22","Surface observations: fill in the gaps → ____ ( VV ): reported instead of cloud when the sky is ____ (fog, snow); vertical visual range into the obscuring medium, measured by ceilometer; in ____ (hundreds, VV002 = 200 ft).","Vertical visibility ( VV ): reported instead of cloud when the sky is obscured (fog, snow); vertical visual range into the obscuring medium, measured by ceilometer; in ft (hundreds, VV002 = 200 ft)."],["050.10.01.01:25","Surface observations: fill in the gaps → ____: shielded thermometer (Stevenson screen) at ~ 1.25–2 m; ____ in %, ____ in °C (or °F).","Temperature: shielded thermometer (Stevenson screen) at ~ 1.25–2 m; relative humidity in %, dew point in °C (or °F)."],["050.10.01.02:02","Upper-air, satellite, radar, aircraft: fill in the gaps → ____: balloon-borne sensors measure pressure, temperature, humidity (winds by GPS tracking) up to ~30 km, twice daily (00/12 UTC). On a T–P diagram: compare environmental lapse rate with DALR/SALR → stability, inversions, tropopause, freezing level, cloud layers (where T ≈ dew point).","Radiosonde: balloon-borne sensors measure pressure, temperature, humidity (winds by GPS tracking) up to ~30 km, twice daily (00/12 UTC). On a T–P diagram: compare environmental lapse rate with DALR/SALR → stability, inversions, tropopause, freezing level, cloud layers (where T ≈ dew point)."],["050.10.01.02:01","Upper-air, satellite, radar, aircraft: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (~36 000 km, fixed over the equator, continuous coverage, poor at high latitudes) and ____ (~800 km, high resolution, whole earth twice daily).","Satellites: geostationary (~36 000 km, fixed over the equator, continuous coverage, poor at high latitudes) and polar-orbiting (~800 km, high resolution, whole earth twice daily)."],["050.10.01.02:01","Upper-air, satellite, radar, aircraft: fill in the gaps → Imagery: ____ (reflected sunlight, daytime only; thick cloud bright), ____ (temperature — cold high tops white, works at night), ____ (moisture in mid/upper troposphere, jet streams). Interpretation: cumuliform = bright, lumpy, sharp shadows, cells;","Imagery: visible (reflected sunlight, daytime only; thick cloud bright), infrared (temperature — cold high tops white, works at night), water vapour (moisture in mid/upper troposphere, jet streams). Interpretation: cumuliform = bright, lumpy, sharp shadows, cells;"],["050.10.01.03:06","Upper-air, satellite, radar, aircraft: fill in the gaps → ____ = smooth, uniform; fronts = long cloud bands (comma shape at lows); ____ from cloud/water-vapour motion locate jet streams.","stratiform = smooth, uniform; fronts = long cloud bands (comma shape at lows); atmospheric motion vectors from cloud/water-vapour motion locate jet streams."],["050.10.01.04:02","Upper-air, satellite, radar, aircraft: fill in the gaps → ____: precipitation intensity (colour-coded), movement of cells, composites.","Ground weather radar: precipitation intensity (colour-coded), movement of cells, composites."],["050.10.01.04:04","Upper-air, satellite, radar, aircraft: fill in the gaps → ____ (~ 3 cm / 9 GHz X-band): detects ____, not cloud, dry hail or CAT; limits: ____ (heavy rain behind cells is hidden → radar shadow), beam width/tilt errors, ground clutter; colours green/yellow/red/magenta = increasing intensity; steep gradients = turbulence.","Airborne weather radar (~ 3 cm / 9 GHz X-band): detects precipitation (wet hail, rain), not cloud, dry hail or CAT; limits: attenuation (heavy rain behind cells is hidden → radar shadow), beam width/tilt errors, ground clutter; colours green/yellow/red/magenta = increasing intensity; steep gradients = turbulence."],["050.10.01.05:01","Upper-air, satellite, radar, aircraft: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ air-reports (position, time, level, observations) only when requested/designated;","Air-reports (AIREP): routine air-reports (position, time, level, observations) only when requested/designated;"],["050.10.04.01:05","Upper-air, satellite, radar, aircraft: fill in the gaps → ____ ____ — mandatory for the pilot when encountering: ____ thunderstorms (obscured, embedded, widespread, squall line) with/without hail, heavy dust/sand storm, volcanic ash cloud, pre-eruption volcanic activity; also moderate turbulence/icing in some cases (for AIRMET).","special air- report (ARS) — mandatory for the pilot when encountering: severe turbulence, severe icing, severe mountain wave, thunderstorms (obscured, embedded, widespread, squall line) with/without hail, heavy dust/sand storm, volcanic ash cloud, pre-eruption volcanic activity; also moderate turbulence/icing in some cases (for AIRMET)."],["050.10.02.01:01","Charts: fill in the gaps → ____ (significant weather charts): ____ (SFC–FL 100/150, SIGWX for GA), ____ (FL 100–450, regional) and high level (FL 250–630, WAFC).","SWC (significant weather charts): low level (SFC–FL 100/150, SIGWX for GA), medium level (FL 100–450, regional) and high level (FL 250–630, WAFC)."],["050.10.02.01:01","Charts: what do you remember about this? → Show: jet streams (arrows with speed in kt, FL, depth), tropopause …","Show: jet streams (arrows with speed in kt, FL, depth), tropopause heights (boxes), CB areas (ISOL/OCNL/FRQ/EMBD CB with base/top, XXX = above/below chart limit), moderate/severe turbulence (CAT areas), icing, fronts, tropical cyclones, volcanoes, freezing level (low/medium charts)."],["050.10.02.01:02","Charts: what do you remember about this? → Determine conditions along a route at a given …","Determine conditions along a route at a given FL."],["050.10.02.02:01","Charts: fill in the gaps → ____: isobars (every 4 hPa in Europe), highs/lows, ____ (elongated high), ____ (elongated low), cols (between two highs and two lows), fronts; frontal lows: frontal side (warm front ahead), warm sector, rear side (showers behind the cold front).","Surface charts: isobars (every 4 hPa in Europe), highs/lows, ridges (elongated high), troughs (elongated low), cols (between two highs and two lows), fronts; frontal lows: frontal side (warm front ahead), warm sector, rear side (showers behind the cold front)."],["050.10.02.02:02","Charts: what do you remember about this? → Wind: parallel to isobars (Buys-Ballot: NH low on the left with the …","Wind: parallel to isobars (Buys-Ballot: NH low on the left with the wind behind you), backed ~30° over land at the surface, speed ∝ isobar spacing."],["050.10.02.03:01","Charts: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ = height of a pressure surface (e.g. 500 hPa ≈ FL 180, 300 hPa ≈ FL 300, 250 hPa ≈ FL 340, 200 hPa ≈ FL 390). ____ = line of equal height of the pressure surface; isotherm = equal temperature; isotach = equal wind speed.","Upper-air charts: constant-pressure chart = height of a pressure surface (e.g. 500 hPa ≈ FL 180, 300 hPa ≈ FL 300, 250 hPa ≈ FL 340, 200 hPa ≈ FL 390). Isohypse/contour = line of equal height of the pressure surface; isotherm = equal temperature; isotach = equal wind speed."],["050.10.02.03:06","Charts: fill in the gaps → ____ ____ (wind arrows: pennant 50 kt, barb 10 kt, half-barb 5 kt; temperature negative unless marked PS). Interpolate between charts/grid points; ISA deviation = forecast T − ISA T at that level (ISA at FL 300 = −44.4 °C).","Forecast wind/temperature charts (wind arrows: pennant 50 kt, barb 10 kt, half-barb 5 kt; temperature negative unless marked PS). Interpolate between charts/grid points; ISA deviation = forecast T − ISA T at that level (ISA at FL 300 = −44.4 °C)."],["050.10.02.04:05","Charts: fill in the gaps → ____: numerical weather prediction on a 3D grid (lat/long + height/pressure levels). ____ (London, Washington) provide global gridded upper wind, temperature, humidity, tropopause FL & temperature, max wind, ____ turbulence data → used in flight-planning systems, can be merged with aircraft/pilot reports for better situational awareness.","Gridded forecasts: numerical weather prediction on a 3D grid (lat/long + height/pressure levels). WAFCs (London, Washington) provide global gridded upper wind, temperature, humidity, tropopause FL & temperature, max wind, CB, icing and turbulence data → used in flight-planning systems, can be merged with aircraft/pilot reports for better situational awareness."],["050.10.01.05:01","Messages: fill in the gaps → ____: routine report (every 30/60 min). ____: special report when conditions change significantly. TREND ( BECMG , TEMPO , NOSIG ): 2-h landing forecast appended to METAR. TAF: aerodrome forecast, ____ validity; change groups FM , BECMG , TEMPO , PROB30/40 .","METAR: routine report (every 30/60 min). SPECI: special report when conditions change significantly. TREND ( BECMG , TEMPO , NOSIG ): 2-h landing forecast appended to METAR. TAF: aerodrome forecast, 9–30 h validity; change groups FM , BECMG , TEMPO , PROB30/40 ."],["050.10.03.01:01","Messages: fill in the gaps → ____: en-route phenomena dangerous for all aircraft — ____ severe turbulence, severe icing (incl. FZRA), severe mountain waves, heavy DS/SS, volcanic ash, tropical cyclone, radioactive cloud; validity ≤ ____ (VA/TC up to 6 h).","SIGMET: en-route phenomena dangerous for all aircraft — TS (OBSC/EMBD/FRQ/SQL) with/without hail, severe turbulence, severe icing (incl. FZRA), severe mountain waves, heavy DS/SS, volcanic ash, tropical cyclone, radioactive cloud; validity ≤ 4 h (VA/TC up to 6 h)."],["050.10.04.01:06","Messages: fill in the gaps → ____: phenomena hazardous for ____ flights (below FL 100/150) not already in GAMET, e.g. moderate icing/turbulence, isolated TS, widespread low cloud/visibility. ____: area forecast for low-level flights. Tropical cyclone advisory: position, movement, max wind, forecast positions (text/graphic by TCAC).","AIRMET: phenomena hazardous for low-level flights (below FL 100/150) not already in GAMET, e.g. moderate icing/turbulence, isolated TS, widespread low cloud/visibility. GAMET: area forecast for low-level flights. Tropical cyclone advisory: position, movement, max wind, forecast positions (text/graphic by TCAC)."],["050.10.01.05:01","Messages: fill in the gaps → ____: local routine/special reports for the aerodrome (for departing/arriving aircraft, ATIS) in plain language, runway-specific wind.","MET REPORT / SPECIAL: local routine/special reports for the aerodrome (for departing/arriving aircraft, ATIS) in plain language, runway-specific wind."],["050.10.03.01:01","Messages: fill in the gaps → ____ in METAR: R24/451293 → runway, ____ (e.g. 5 = wet snow), ____ (1 = ≤ 10 %, 2 = 11–25 %, 5 = 26–50 %, 9 = 51–100 %), depth (mm), braking/friction (e.g. 93 = medium-poor, 95 = good); CLRD// = cleared; R/SNOCLO = aerodrome closed by snow. (EU now uses the Global Reporting Format/RCR.)","Runway state in METAR: R24/451293 → runway, deposit (e.g. 5 = wet snow), extent (1 = ≤ 10 %, 2 = 11–25 %, 5 = 26–50 %, 9 = 51–100 %), depth (mm), braking/friction (e.g. 93 = medium-poor, 95 = good); CLRD// = cleared; R/SNOCLO = aerodrome closed by snow. (EU now uses the Global Reporting Format/RCR.)"],["050.10.03.02:01","Messages: fill in the gaps → ____: continuous broadcast (VHF or ____) of METAR/TAF/SIGMET for a group of aerodromes; ____ for long-range/oceanic. ATIS: recorded terminal information (runway, wind, visibility, RVR, weather, cloud, temperature, dew point, QNH, trend, special info), updated with a new letter.","VOLMET: continuous broadcast (VHF or HF) of METAR/TAF/SIGMET for a group of aerodromes; HF-VOLMET for long-range/oceanic. ATIS: recorded terminal information (runway, wind, visibility, RVR, weather, cloud, temperature, dew point, QNH, trend, special info), updated with a new letter."],["050.10.03.04:01","Messages: fill in the gaps → ____: pre-flight documentation from MET offices/self-briefing — SWC, wind/temperature charts, METAR/TAF/SIGMET, AIRMET/GAMET, volcanic ash/tropical cyclone advisories. ____ from FIS: SIGMET/AIRMET, METAR/TAF on request, VOLMET/ATIS, special air-reports, wind-shear warnings → decide on continuation, diversion.","Briefing: pre-flight documentation from MET offices/self-briefing — SWC, wind/temperature charts, METAR/TAF/SIGMET, AIRMET/GAMET, volcanic ash/tropical cyclone advisories. In flight from FIS: SIGMET/AIRMET, METAR/TAF on request, VOLMET/ATIS, special air-reports, wind-shear warnings → decide on continuation, diversion."],["050.10.03.04:01","Messages: fill in the gaps → ____ (e.g. TS, hail, snow, freezing precipitation, strong surface wind, squall, frost, sand/dust storm) for aircraft on ground/operators; ____ (LLWAS, TDWR, pilot reports) for approach/climb-out paths, broadcast via ATIS/ATC.","Aerodrome warnings (e.g. TS, hail, snow, freezing precipitation, strong surface wind, squall, frost, sand/dust storm) for aircraft on ground/operators; wind-shear warnings/alerts (LLWAS, TDWR, pilot reports) for approach/climb-out paths, broadcast via ATIS/ATC."],["050.10.04.01:01","Organisations & providers: fill in the gaps → ____ (London, Washington): upper-air gridded forecasts (winds, temperatures, humidity, max wind, tropopause, CB, icing, turbulence, geopotential altitude of FL) and SIGWX. ____: aerodrome forecasts (TAF), briefing documents. ____ (meteorological watch offices): SIGMET and AIRMET.","WAFCs (London, Washington): upper-air gridded forecasts (winds, temperatures, humidity, max wind, tropopause, CB, icing, turbulence, geopotential altitude of FL) and SIGWX. MET offices: aerodrome forecasts (TAF), briefing documents. MWOs (meteorological watch offices): SIGMET and AIRMET."],["050.10.04.01:05","Organisations & providers: fill in the gaps → ____: METAR/SPECI and MET REPORT. ____: volcanic ash advisories (9 centres, e.g. London, Toulouse). ____: tropical cyclone advisories.","Aeronautical meteorological stations: METAR/SPECI and MET REPORT. VAACs: volcanic ash advisories (9 centres, e.g. London, Toulouse). TCACs: tropical cyclone advisories."],["050.10.04.02:01","Organisations & providers: fill in the gaps → ____ sets the standards (Annex 3 'Meteorological Service for International Air Navigation'); ____ (UN agency, Geneva) sets technical meteorological standards, codes, observation networks — works jointly with ICAO.","ICAO sets the standards (Annex 3 'Meteorological Service for International Air Navigation'); WMO (UN agency, Geneva) sets technical meteorological standards, codes, observation networks — works jointly with ICAO."],["050.10.04.01:01","Exam trap (Organisations & providers): complete the fact → METAR wind = true/____; ATC wind = magnetic/____.","METAR wind = true/10 min; ATC wind = magnetic/2 min."],["050.10.01.01:19","Exam trap (Organisations & providers): complete the fact → Ceiling needs BKN/OVC below ____.","Ceiling needs BKN/OVC below 20 000 ft."],["050.10.01.01:03","EASA LO 050.10.01.01.03 → Define ‘visibility’.","Comparison: RVR is runway-specific and uses lights (usually higher than meteorological visibility at night); prevailing visibility = observer, whole aerodrome; ground visibility = aerodrome visibility. / Ground visibility: visibility at an aerodrome as reported by an accredited observer or automatic system. Units: m, km (≥ 5 km), statute miles (US)."],["050.10.01.01:04","EASA LO 050.10.01.01.04 → Describe the meteorological measurement of visibility.","Comparison: RVR is runway-specific and uses lights (usually higher than meteorological visibility at night); prevailing visibility = observer, whole aerodrome; ground visibility = aerodrome visibility."],["050.10.01.01:06","EASA LO 050.10.01.01.06 → Define ‘ground visibility’.","Comparison: RVR is runway-specific and uses lights (usually higher than meteorological visibility at night); prevailing visibility = observer, whole aerodrome; ground visibility = aerodrome visibility. / Ground visibility: visibility at an aerodrome as reported by an accredited observer or automatic system. Units: m, km (≥ 5 km), statute miles (US)."],["050.10.01.01:09","EASA LO 050.10.01.01.09 → Describe the meteorological measurement of runway visual range.","Vertical visibility ( VV ): reported instead of cloud when the sky is obscured (fog, snow); vertical visual range into the obscuring medium, measured by ceilometer; in ft (hundreds, VV002 = 200 ft). / RVR: the range over which the pilot on the runway centre line can see the runway surface markings or the lights. Measured by transmissometers/forward-scatter meters beside the runway at touchdown zone, mid-point and stop end (~ 120 m from centre line, ~ 300 m from threshold)."],["050.10.01.01:11","EASA LO 050.10.01.01.11 → List the units used for runway visual range (m, ft).","Vertical visibility ( VV ): reported instead of cloud when the sky is obscured (fog, snow); vertical visual range into the obscuring medium, measured by ceilometer; in ft (hundreds, VV002 = 200 ft). / Reported when visibility or RVR < 1 500 m; units m (ft in USA); limits 50–2 000 m. Transmitted in METAR ( R24/0550U ), ATIS, by ATC."],["050.10.01.01:13","EASA LO 050.10.01.01.13 → Compare ground visibility, prevailing visibility, and runway visual range.","Comparison: RVR is runway-specific and uses lights (usually higher than meteorological visibility at night); prevailing visibility = observer, whole aerodrome; ground visibility = aerodrome visibility. / Prevailing visibility: the value reached or exceeded within at least half of the horizon circle or half of the aerodrome surface (not necessarily contiguous). Minimum visibility also reported if < 1 500 m or < 50 % of prevailing (with direction)."],["050.10.01.01:15","EASA LO 050.10.01.01.15 → Indicate the means of observing clouds for the purpose of recording: type, amount, height of base (ceilometers), and top.","Clouds: amount in oktas (eighths of sky): FEW 1–2, SCT 3–4, BKN 5–7, OVC 8; type (only CB and TCU named in METAR/TAF; SIGMET: CB); base by ceilometer (laser/light beam), in ft above aerodrome elevation; tops from pilot reports/satellite. Cloud base = lowest height of the cloud layer."],["050.10.01.01:16","EASA LO 050.10.01.01.16 → State the clouds which are indicated in METAR, TAF and SIGMET.","Briefing: pre-flight documentation from MET offices/self-briefing — SWC, wind/temperature charts, METAR/TAF/SIGMET, AIRMET/GAMET, volcanic ash/tropical cyclone advisories. In flight from FIS: SIGMET/AIRMET, METAR/TAF on request, VOLMET/ATIS, special air-reports, wind-shear warnings → decide on continuation, diversion. / Clouds: amount in oktas (eighths of sky): FEW 1–2, SCT 3–4, BKN 5–7, OVC 8; type (only CB and TCU named in METAR/TAF; SIGMET: CB); base by ceilometer (laser/light beam), in ft above aerodrome elevation; tops from pilot reports/satellite. Cloud base = lowest height of the cloud layer."],["050.10.01.01:20","EASA LO 050.10.01.01.20 → Name the unit and the reference level used for information about cloud base (ft).","Clouds: amount in oktas (eighths of sky): FEW 1–2, SCT 3–4, BKN 5–7, OVC 8; type (only CB and TCU named in METAR/TAF; SIGMET: CB); base by ceilometer (laser/light beam), in ft above aerodrome elevation; tops from pilot reports/satellite. Cloud base = lowest height of the cloud layer."],["050.10.01.01:21","EASA LO 050.10.01.01.21 → Define ‘vertical visibility’.","Vertical visibility ( VV ): reported instead of cloud when the sky is obscured (fog, snow); vertical visual range into the obscuring medium, measured by ceilometer; in ft (hundreds, VV002 = 200 ft). / Comparison: RVR is runway-specific and uses lights (usually higher than meteorological visibility at night); prevailing visibility = observer, whole aerodrome; ground visibility = aerodrome visibility."],["050.10.01.01:24","EASA LO 050.10.01.01.24 → Indicate the means of observation of air temperature (thermometer).","Temperature: shielded thermometer (Stevenson screen) at ~ 1.25–2 m; relative humidity in %, dew point in °C (or °F)."],["050.10.01.04:01","EASA LO 050.10.01.04.01 → Describe the basic principle and the type of information given by a ground weather radar.","Present weather: observer, present-weather sensors (precipitation type, intensity)."],["050.10.01.04:03","EASA LO 050.10.01.04.03 → Describe the basic principle and the type of information given by airborne weather radar.","Present weather: observer, present-weather sensors (precipitation type, intensity)."],["050.10.01.05:02","EASA LO 050.10.01.05.02 → State the obligation of a pilot to prepare air\u0002reports.X","Air-reports (AIREP): routine air-reports (position, time, level, observations) only when requested/designated;"],["050.10.02.03:02","EASA LO 050.10.02.03.02 → Define ‘isohypse (contour line)’. (Refer to Subject 050 01 03 02)","Upper-air charts: constant-pressure chart = height of a pressure surface (e.g. 500 hPa ≈ FL 180, 300 hPa ≈ FL 300, 250 hPa ≈ FL 340, 200 hPa ≈ FL 390). Isohypse/contour = line of equal height of the pressure surface; isotherm = equal temperature; isotach = equal wind speed."],["050.10.02.03:03","EASA LO 050.10.02.03.03 → Define ‘isotherm’.","Upper-air charts: constant-pressure chart = height of a pressure surface (e.g. 500 hPa ≈ FL 180, 300 hPa ≈ FL 300, 250 hPa ≈ FL 340, 200 hPa ≈ FL 390). Isohypse/contour = line of equal height of the pressure surface; isotherm = equal temperature; isotach = equal wind speed."],["050.10.02.03:04","EASA LO 050.10.02.03.04 → Define ‘isotach’.","Upper-air charts: constant-pressure chart = height of a pressure surface (e.g. 500 hPa ≈ FL 180, 300 hPa ≈ FL 300, 250 hPa ≈ FL 340, 200 hPa ≈ FL 390). Isohypse/contour = line of equal height of the pressure surface; isotherm = equal temperature; isotach = equal wind speed."],["050.10.02.03:05","EASA LO 050.10.02.03.05 → Describe forecast upper-wind and temperature charts.","Forecast wind/temperature charts (wind arrows: pennant 50 kt, barb 10 kt, half-barb 5 kt; temperature negative unless marked PS). Interpolate between charts/grid points; ISA deviation = forecast T − ISA T at that level (ISA at FL 300 = −44.4 °C). / Gridded forecasts: numerical weather prediction on a 3D grid (lat/long + height/pressure levels). WAFCs (London, Washington) provide global gridded upper wind, temperature, humidity, tropopause FL & temperature, max wind, CB, icing and turbulence data → used in flight-planning systems, can be merged with aircraft/pilot reports for better situational awareness."],["050.10.02.04:01","EASA LO 050.10.02.04.01 → State that numerical weather prediction uses a 3D grid of weather data, consisting of horizontal data (latitude-longitude) and vertical data (height or pressure).","Gridded forecasts: numerical weather prediction on a 3D grid (lat/long + height/pressure levels). WAFCs (London, Washington) provide global gridded upper wind, temperature, humidity, tropopause FL & temperature, max wind, CB, icing and turbulence data → used in flight-planning systems, can be merged with aircraft/pilot reports for better situational awareness."],["050.10.02.04:02","EASA LO 050.10.02.04.02 → Explain that world area forecast centres prepare global sets of gridded forecasts for flight planning purposes (upper wind, temperature, humidity).","Gridded forecasts: numerical weather prediction on a 3D grid (lat/long + height/pressure levels). WAFCs (London, Washington) provide global gridded upper wind, temperature, humidity, tropopause FL & temperature, max wind, CB, icing and turbulence data → used in flight-planning systems, can be merged with aircraft/pilot reports for better situational awareness. / WAFCs (London, Washington): upper-air gridded forecasts (winds, temperatures, humidity, max wind, tropopause, CB, icing, turbulence, geopotential altitude of FL) and SIGWX. MET offices: aerodrome forecasts (TAF), briefing documents. MWOs (meteorological watch offices): SIGMET and AIRMET."],["050.10.02.04:03","EASA LO 050.10.02.04.03 → State that the WAFCs also produce gridded datasets for Flight Level and temperature of the tropopause, direction and speed of maximum wind, cumulonimbus clouds, icing and turbulence.","Gridded forecasts: numerical weather prediction on a 3D grid (lat/long + height/pressure levels). WAFCs (London, Washington) provide global gridded upper wind, temperature, humidity, tropopause FL & temperature, max wind, CB, icing and turbulence data → used in flight-planning systems, can be merged with aircraft/pilot reports for better situational awareness. / WAFCs (London, Washington): upper-air gridded forecasts (winds, temperatures, humidity, max wind, tropopause, CB, icing, turbulence, geopotential altitude of FL) and SIGWX. MET offices: aerodrome forecasts (TAF), briefing documents. MWOs (meteorological watch offices): SIGMET and AIRMET."],["050.10.02.04:04","EASA LO 050.10.02.04.04 → Explain that the data on CB and turbulence can be used in the visualization of flight hazards.","Gridded forecasts: numerical weather prediction on a 3D grid (lat/long + height/pressure levels). WAFCs (London, Washington) provide global gridded upper wind, temperature, humidity, tropopause FL & temperature, max wind, CB, icing and turbulence data → used in flight-planning systems, can be merged with aircraft/pilot reports for better situational awareness."],["050.10.03.01:02","EASA LO 050.10.03.01.02 → Describe, decode and interpret the tropical cyclone advisory information in written and graphical form.","AIRMET: phenomena hazardous for low-level flights (below FL 100/150) not already in GAMET, e.g. moderate icing/turbulence, isolated TS, widespread low cloud/visibility. GAMET: area forecast for low-level flights. Tropical cyclone advisory: position, movement, max wind, forecast positions (text/graphic by TCAC)."],["050.10.03.01:03","EASA LO 050.10.03.01.03 → Describe the general meaning of MET REPORT and SPECIAL REPORT.","MET REPORT / SPECIAL: local routine/special reports for the aerodrome (for departing/arriving aircraft, ATIS) in plain language, runway-specific wind. / Aeronautical meteorological stations: METAR/SPECI and MET REPORT. VAACs: volcanic ash advisories (9 centres, e.g. London, Toulouse). TCACs: tropical cyclone advisories."],["050.10.03.03:01","EASA LO 050.10.03.03.01 → Describe meteorological briefing and advice.","WAFCs (London, Washington): upper-air gridded forecasts (winds, temperatures, humidity, max wind, tropopause, CB, icing, turbulence, geopotential altitude of FL) and SIGWX. MET offices: aerodrome forecasts (TAF), briefing documents. MWOs (meteorological watch offices): SIGMET and AIRMET."],["050.10.04.01:02","EASA LO 050.10.04.01.02 → Name the meteorological (MET) offices as the provider for aerodrome forecasts and briefing documents.","WAFCs (London, Washington): upper-air gridded forecasts (winds, temperatures, humidity, max wind, tropopause, CB, icing, turbulence, geopotential altitude of FL) and SIGWX. MET offices: aerodrome forecasts (TAF), briefing documents. MWOs (meteorological watch offices): SIGMET and AIRMET. / Briefing: pre-flight documentation from MET offices/self-briefing — SWC, wind/temperature charts, METAR/TAF/SIGMET, AIRMET/GAMET, volcanic ash/tropical cyclone advisories. In flight from FIS: SIGMET/AIRMET, METAR/TAF on request, VOLMET/ATIS, special air-reports, wind-shear warnings → decide on continuation, diversion."],["050.10.04.01:03","EASA LO 050.10.04.01.03 → Name the meteorological watch offices (MWOs) as the provider for SIGMET and AIRMET information.","WAFCs (London, Washington): upper-air gridded forecasts (winds, temperatures, humidity, max wind, tropopause, CB, icing, turbulence, geopotential altitude of FL) and SIGWX. MET offices: aerodrome forecasts (TAF), briefing documents. MWOs (meteorological watch offices): SIGMET and AIRMET. / Briefing: pre-flight documentation from MET offices/self-briefing — SWC, wind/temperature charts, METAR/TAF/SIGMET, AIRMET/GAMET, volcanic ash/tropical cyclone advisories. In flight from FIS: SIGMET/AIRMET, METAR/TAF on request, VOLMET/ATIS, special air-reports, wind-shear warnings → decide on continuation, diversion."],["050.10.04.01:04","EASA LO 050.10.04.01.04 → Name the aeronautical meteorological stations as the provider for METAR and MET reports.","Aeronautical meteorological stations: METAR/SPECI and MET REPORT. VAACs: volcanic ash advisories (9 centres, e.g. London, Toulouse). TCACs: tropical cyclone advisories. / Briefing: pre-flight documentation from MET offices/self-briefing — SWC, wind/temperature charts, METAR/TAF/SIGMET, AIRMET/GAMET, volcanic ash/tropical cyclone advisories. In flight from FIS: SIGMET/AIRMET, METAR/TAF on request, VOLMET/ATIS, special air-reports, wind-shear warnings → decide on continuation, diversion."]],"52":[["033.04.02.02:06","Stage focus: planning minima for IFR flights (FPM ch. 14, GSPRM): fill in the gaps → When the latest weather arrives, the crew must check that each aerodrome in the plan is ____ and update the nav plan and fuel.","When the latest weather arrives, the crew must check that each aerodrome in the plan is usable and update the nav plan and fuel."],["033.04.02.02:01","Stage focus: planning minima for IFR flights (FPM ch. 14, GSPRM): fill in the gaps → Selection of aerodromes (CAT, EU Air Ops) - Take-off alternate required when weather at departure is below landing minima or other reasons prevent return. Must be within: twin-engine — ____ flight time at OEI cruise speed (still air, actual TOM); ____ — ____ at OEI cruise speed;","Selection of aerodromes (CAT, EU Air Ops) - Take-off alternate required when weather at departure is below landing minima or other reasons prevent return. Must be within: twin-engine — 1 h flight time at OEI cruise speed (still air, actual TOM); 3+ engines — 2 h at OEI cruise speed;"],["033.04.02.02:01","Stage focus: planning minima for IFR flights (FPM ch. 14, GSPRM): fill in the gaps → ETOPS aircraft — within approved diversion time (max 2 h). - ____: at least one required for IFR, unless the flight time is ≤ 6 h and two separate runways are usable with good weather (approx. ceiling ≥ 2 000 ft or circling height + 500 ft, visibility ≥ 5 km) from 1 h before to 1 h after ETA, ____ the destination is ____ (no adequate alternate).","ETOPS aircraft — within approved diversion time (max 2 h). - Destination alternate: at least one required for IFR, unless the flight time is ≤ 6 h and two separate runways are usable with good weather (approx. ceiling ≥ 2 000 ft or circling height + 500 ft, visibility ≥ 5 km) from 1 h before to 1 h after ETA, or the destination is isolated (no adequate alternate)."],["033.04.02.02:05","Stage focus: planning minima for IFR flights (FPM ch. 14, GSPRM): fill in the gaps → ____ ____ when destination weather is below planning minima or no forecast is available. - ____ for ETOPS/long-range as required.","Two destination alternates when destination weather is below planning minima or no forecast is available. - En-route alternates for ETOPS/long-range as required."],["033.04.02.02:01","Stage focus: planning minima for IFR flights (FPM ch. 14, GSPRM): fill in the gaps → ____","Planning minima (table, AMC to CAT.OP.MPA.182)"],["033.04.02.02:01","Stage focus: planning minima for IFR flights (FPM ch. 14, GSPRM) – CAT II/III: what are the key facts?","CAT I RVR"],["033.04.02.02:01","Stage focus: planning minima for IFR flights (FPM ch. 14, GSPRM) – CAT I: what are the key facts?","NPA RVR/VIS; ceiling ≥ MDH"],["033.04.02.02:01","Stage focus: planning minima for IFR flights (FPM ch. 14, GSPRM) – NPA: what are the key facts?","NPA RVR/VIS + 1 000 m; ceiling ≥ MDH + 200 ft"],["033.04.02.02:01","Stage focus: planning minima for IFR flights (FPM ch. 14, GSPRM) – Circling: what are the key facts?","Circling minima"],["033.04.02.02:06","Stage focus: planning minima for IFR flights (FPM ch. 14, GSPRM): fill in the gaps → Applies to the ____ ____; the ____ itself only needs forecast ≥ applicable aerodrome operating minima.","Applies to the take-off alternate, destination alternate(s), isolated destination, fuel-ERA/en-route alternates; the destination itself only needs forecast ≥ applicable aerodrome operating minima."],["033.04.02.02:01","Stage focus: planning minima for IFR flights (FPM ch. 14, GSPRM): fill in the gaps → ____: the forecast must be at or above planning minima from ____ the estimated time of arrival (ETA ____).","Time window: the forecast must be at or above planning minima from 1 h before to 1 h after the estimated time of arrival (ETA ± 1 h)."],["033.04.02.02:01","Stage focus: planning minima for IFR flights (FPM ch. 14, GSPRM): what do you remember about this? → Minima come from the GSPRM approach charts (DA/MDA, RVR); the table …","Minima come from the GSPRM approach charts (DA/MDA, RVR); the table raises them to have a margin at the alternate."],["033.04.02.02:01","Update of navigation plan with latest MET: fill in the gaps → ____: compare wind components and temperatures per level (from the wind/temperature forecast); choose the level with the best ____ (for jets higher is usually better unless strong headwind; wind-altitude trade tables).","Most fuel-efficient level: compare wind components and temperatures per level (from the wind/temperature forecast); choose the level with the best ground speed / fuel flow (for jets higher is usually better unless strong headwind; wind-altitude trade tables)."],["033.04.02.02:02","Update of navigation plan with latest MET: fill in the gaps → ____ for minimum clearance: true altitude ≈ indicated altitude corrected for temperature: ____ ____ (colder than ISA → aircraft lower than indicated) and for pressure (QNH vs 1013). Ensure MSA/grid MORA plus legal clearance (e.g. 1 000 ft/2 000 ft in mountains) is still met.","True altitude check for minimum clearance: true altitude ≈ indicated altitude corrected for temperature: 4 ft per 1 °C deviation from ISA per 1 000 ft (colder than ISA → aircraft lower than indicated) and for pressure (QNH vs 1013). Ensure MSA/grid MORA plus legal clearance (e.g. 1 000 ft/2 000 ft in mountains) is still met."],["033.04.02.02:01","Update of navigation plan with latest MET: fill in the gaps → ____: new forecast wind → recompute WCA and GS per leg (CRP/E6-B), apply variation.","Magnetic heading & GS: new forecast wind → recompute WCA and GS per leg (CRP/E6-B), apply variation."],["033.04.02.02:06","Update of navigation plan with latest MET: fill in the gaps → ____ = distance ÷ GS; ____; ____ destination; time destination → alternate (with forecast alternate winds).","Leg times = distance ÷ GS; total time en route; time to destination; time destination → alternate (with forecast alternate winds)."],["033.04.02.02:05","Update of the fuel plan: what do you remember about this? → Recompute trip fuel with new times/levels, then contingency (5 % of …","Recompute trip fuel with new times/levels, then contingency (5 % of new trip), alternate fuel (new alternate or wind), final reserve; compare with fuel on board → extra fuel available or need to add fuel/re-plan (lower mass, closer alternate, tech stop)."],["033.04.02.05:01","Exam trap (Update of the fuel plan): complete the fact → NPA planning = +____ and +____.","NPA planning = +1 000 m and +200 ft."],["033.04.02.05:01","Exam trap (Update of the fuel plan): complete the fact → Window = ETA ± ____.","Window = ETA ± 1 h."],["033.04.02.02:04","EASA LO 033.04.02.02.04 → Confirm the individual leg times and the total time en route.","Leg times = distance ÷ GS; total time en route; time to destination; time destination → alternate (with forecast alternate winds)."]],"53":[["061.01.06.01:01","Triangle of velocities (TOV): fill in the gaps → Three vectors: ____ (true heading + TAS, one arrow), ____ ____ (W/V, direction the wind blows from, speed; two arrows, drawn towards where it blows), ground vector (track + GS, three arrows). Air vector + wind vector = ground vector.","Three vectors: air vector (true heading + TAS, one arrow), wind vector (W/V, direction the wind blows from, speed; two arrows, drawn towards where it blows), ground vector (track + GS, three arrows). Air vector + wind vector = ground vector."],["061.01.06.01:01","Triangle of velocities (TOV): fill in the gaps → ____ = angle between heading and track (wind from the left → drift right → track to the right of heading). ____ = heading correction to hold a track (opposite to drift).","Drift = angle between heading and track (wind from the left → drift right → track to the right of heading). WCA = heading correction to hold a track (opposite to drift)."],["061.01.06.01:01","Triangle of velocities (TOV): what do you remember about this? → Label the diagram: HDG/TAS side, W/V side from the nose of the air …","Label the diagram: HDG/TAS side, W/V side from the nose of the air vector, closing side TR/GS."],["061.01.06.02:01","Triangle of velocities (TOV): fill in the gaps → ____ ____ 1. Heading &","Solutions on the mechanical computer (CRP-5/E6-B, \"wind side\") 1. Heading &"],["061.01.06.01:01","Triangle of velocities (TOV): fill in the gaps → GS from track, TAS, W/V: set W/V (rotate to wind direction, mark wind dot above centre at wind speed), set TAS under the centre dot, rotate ____ under the index, read drift under the wind dot, apply → heading, re-set (iterate) and read GS under the centre. 2.","GS from track, TAS, W/V: set W/V (rotate to wind direction, mark wind dot above centre at wind speed), set TAS under the centre dot, rotate track under the index, read drift under the wind dot, apply → heading, re-set (iterate) and read GS under the centre. 2."],["061.01.06.01:01","Triangle of velocities (TOV): fill in the gaps → ____: set heading at index and TAS under centre, mark the point at drift angle and GS; rotate so the mark is vertically above centre → read wind direction at the index and speed as distance. 3. Track &","W/V from heading, TAS, track, GS: set heading at index and TAS under centre, mark the point at drift angle and GS; rotate so the mark is vertically above centre → read wind direction at the index and speed as distance. 3. Track &"],["061.01.06.02:01","Triangle of velocities (TOV): fill in the gaps → GS from heading, ____: set heading, plot wind as before, read drift → track = heading ± drift, GS under the wind mark. - ____ (MDR): crosswind = wind speed × sin(wind angle), head/tailwind = wind speed × cos(angle); drift (°) ≈ ____; GS ≈ TAS − headwind component.","GS from heading, TAS, W/V: set heading, plot wind as before, read drift → track = heading ± drift, GS under the wind mark. - Mental dead reckoning (MDR): crosswind = wind speed × sin(wind angle), head/tailwind = wind speed × cos(angle); drift (°) ≈ crosswind × 60 ÷ TAS; GS ≈ TAS − headwind component."],["061.01.06.01:01","Triangle of velocities (TOV): what do you remember about this? → Sine rule of thumb: 30° → 0.5, 45° → 0.7, 60° → 0.9, ≥ 60°–90° → ~1. …","Sine rule of thumb: 30° → 0.5, 45° → 0.7, 60° → 0.9, ≥ 60°–90° → ~1. -"],["061.01.06.01:01","Triangle of velocities (TOV): fill in the gaps → Example: track 090°, TAS 120 kt, W/V 360/30 → wind from the left at 90°: XW 30 kt → drift ≈ 30 × 60/120 = ____ right → heading ____; headwind component ≈ 0 → GS ≈ ____ (TAS × cos 15°).","Example: track 090°, TAS 120 kt, W/V 360/30 → wind from the left at 90°: XW 30 kt → drift ≈ 30 × 60/120 = 15° right → heading 075°; headwind component ≈ 0 → GS ≈ 116 kt (TAS × cos 15°)."],["061.05.01.01:01","Time: mean solar day & LMT: fill in the gaps → The ____ (sun transit to transit) varies in length (Earth's elliptical orbit, axial tilt). The ____ is based on a fictitious mean sun moving uniformly → ____; the difference is the equation of time (up to ±16 min).","The apparent solar day (sun transit to transit) varies in length (Earth's elliptical orbit, axial tilt). The mean solar day is based on a fictitious mean sun moving uniformly → 24 h; the difference is the equation of time (up to ±16 min)."],["061.05.01.02:01","Time: mean solar day & LMT: fill in the gaps → ____ (Local Mean Time): time based on the mean sun at the observer's meridian — ____ when the mean sun crosses the local meridian. Every meridian has its own LMT.","LMT (Local Mean Time): time based on the mean sun at the observer's meridian — 12:00 LMT when the mean sun crosses the local meridian. Every meridian has its own LMT."],["061.05.01.01:01","Time: mean solar day & LMT: fill in the gaps → ____ ≈ LMT at the Greenwich meridian (0°).","UTC ≈ LMT at the Greenwich meridian (0°)."],["061.05.01.01:01","Time: mean solar day & LMT: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ ____.","Arc to time: 360° = 24 h → 15° = 1 h, 1° = 4 min, 15′ = 1 min, 1′ = 4 s."],["061.05.01.01:01","Time: mean solar day & LMT: fill in the gaps → ____; ____ ____ (\"longitude east, Greenwich least; longitude west, Greenwich best\").","LMT = UTC + longitude East (in time); LMT = UTC − longitude West (\"longitude east, Greenwich least; longitude west, Greenwich best\")."],["061.05.01.02:01","Time: mean solar day & LMT: fill in the gaps → Example: UTC 10:00, position 045°W → 45 × 4 min = 3 h → LMT = ____. Position 120°30′E, LMT 18:00 → arc 8 h 02 min → UTC = ____.","Example: UTC 10:00, position 045°W → 45 × 4 min = 3 h → LMT = 07:00. Position 120°30′E, LMT 18:00 → arc 8 h 02 min → UTC = 09:58."],["061.05.01.01:01","Time: mean solar day & LMT: what do you remember about this? → Crossing the date line or calculating across midnight: add/subtract …","Crossing the date line or calculating across midnight: add/subtract days carefully."]],"54":[["033.04.02.02:01","Stage focus: applying aerodrome forecasts to planning (FPM ch. 14, GSPRM): fill in the gaps → ____ ____","Which part of a TAF counts at the planning stage (AMC1 CAT.OP.MPA.182 table)"],["033.04.02.02:01","Stage focus: applying aerodrome forecasts to planning (FPM ch. 14, GSPRM) – Main body (BASE): what are the key facts?","Destination & alternates — deterioration: applies · Improvement: applies"],["033.04.02.02:01","Stage focus: applying aerodrome forecasts to planning (FPM ch. 14, GSPRM) – FM / BECMG (deterioration): what are the key facts?","Destination & alternates — deterioration: applies from the start of the change period · Improvement: applies from the end of the period (BECMG) / from time (FM)"],["033.04.02.02:01","Stage focus: applying aerodrome forecasts to planning (FPM ch. 14, GSPRM) – TEMPO / PROB TEMPO (deterioration): what are the key facts?","Destination & alternates — deterioration: Transient/showery (e.g. SHRA, TSRA, gusts): may be disregarded; persistent (e.g. HZ, BR, FG, DZ, RA, SN): must be considered (lowest value) · Improvement: disregard improvements"],["033.04.02.02:01","Stage focus: applying aerodrome forecasts to planning (FPM ch. 14, GSPRM) – PROB30/40 (deterioration): what are the key facts?","Destination & alternates — deterioration: may be disregarded (PROB30/40 alone), PROB TEMPO: treat like TEMPO · Improvement: disregard"],["033.04.02.02:01","Stage focus: applying aerodrome forecasts to planning (FPM ch. 14, GSPRM): fill in the gaps → Mean wind ____; gusts beyond crosswind limits in TEMPO may be disregarded.","Mean wind must be within limits; gusts beyond crosswind limits in TEMPO may be disregarded."],["033.04.02.02:01","Stage focus: applying aerodrome forecasts to planning (FPM ch. 14, GSPRM): fill in the gaps → A destination or alternate is usable only if the applicable forecast is at/above the minima during the ____ window (planning minima for alternates, operating minima for the destination).","A destination or alternate is usable only if the applicable forecast is at/above the minima during the ETA ± 1 h window (planning minima for alternates, operating minima for the destination)."],["033.04.02.02:01","Stage focus: applying aerodrome forecasts to planning (FPM ch. 14, GSPRM): fill in the gaps → If the weather at the alternate is below planning minima → choose another alternate or carry two; if destination below minima → ____ ____ required.","If the weather at the alternate is below planning minima → choose another alternate or carry two; if destination below minima → two alternates required."],["033.04.02.02:01","Stage focus: applying aerodrome forecasts to planning (FPM ch. 14, GSPRM): fill in the gaps → ____ BECMG 1214 = change between 12 and 14 UTC (worst case: from 12 for deterioration); TEMPO = fluctuations lasting < 1 h each, in total < half the period; PROB30 = 30 % probability; FM1230 = from 12:30 a complete new situation; CAVOK = vis ≥ 10 km, no cloud below 5 000 ft or MSA (whichever higher), no CB/TCU, no significant weather.","Decoding reminders: BECMG 1214 = change between 12 and 14 UTC (worst case: from 12 for deterioration); TEMPO = fluctuations lasting < 1 h each, in total < half the period; PROB30 = 30 % probability; FM1230 = from 12:30 a complete new situation; CAVOK = vis ≥ 10 km, no cloud below 5 000 ft or MSA (whichever higher), no CB/TCU, no significant weather."],["033.04.02.02:01","Update of navigation plan with latest MET: fill in the gaps → ____: compare levels using the latest winds and temperatures (GS per level, fuel flow) → choose best level.","Fuel-efficient altitude: compare levels using the latest winds and temperatures (GS per level, fuel flow) → choose best level."],["033.04.02.02:02","Update of navigation plan with latest MET: fill in the gaps → ____: correct indicated altitude for temperature (____; colder = lower) and QNH to confirm the statutory minimum obstacle clearance.","True altitude check: correct indicated altitude for temperature (4 ft/°C/1 000 ft; colder = lower) and QNH to confirm the statutory minimum obstacle clearance."],["033.04.02.02:03","Update of navigation plan with latest MET: fill in the gaps → ____ recomputed with the new wind (CRP/E6-B), apply variation.","Magnetic headings and GS recomputed with the new wind (CRP/E6-B), apply variation."],["033.04.02.02:04","Update of navigation plan with latest MET: fill in the gaps → ____, ____, ____, time destination → alternate recalculated.","Leg times, total time en route, time to destination, time destination → alternate recalculated."],["033.04.02.05:01","Update of the fuel plan: what do you remember about this? → Revise trip fuel, contingency, alternate fuel with the new …","Revise trip fuel, contingency, alternate fuel with the new times/levels; compare with fuel on board; adjust extra fuel or re-plan."],["033.04.02.02:05","EASA LO 033.04.02.02.05 → Confirm the total time en route for the trip to the destination.","Leg times, total time en route, time to destination, time destination → alternate recalculated. / Revise trip fuel, contingency, alternate fuel with the new times/levels; compare with fuel on board; adjust extra fuel or re-plan."],["033.04.02.02:06","EASA LO 033.04.02.02.06 → Confirm the total time from destination to the alternate aerodrome.","Leg times, total time en route, time to destination, time destination → alternate recalculated."]],"55":[["021.07.01.01:04","Ice protection: fill in the gaps → ____ = ____ ice from forming (system switched on ____ entering icing: thermal, fluid). De-icing = remove ice after it has formed (cyclic: boots, some electrical systems).","Anti-icing = prevent ice from forming (system switched on before entering icing: thermal, fluid). De-icing = remove ice after it has formed (cyclic: boots, some electrical systems)."],["021.07.01.01:02","Ice protection: fill in the gaps → Protected components: ____ (and slats), ____, ____, propellers/spinners, windshields, pitot tubes, static ports, AoA vanes, TAT probes, drain masts, water/waste lines, sometimes radome. On many modern jets the tail has no ice protection (sized so that the ice accretion is acceptable).","Protected components: wing leading edges (and slats), tailplane/fin leading edges, engine air intakes/nacelle lips, propellers/spinners, windshields, pitot tubes, static ports, AoA vanes, TAT probes, drain masts, water/waste lines, sometimes radome. On many modern jets the tail has no ice protection (sized so that the ice accretion is acceptable)."],["021.07.01.01:01","Ice protection: fill in the gaps → ____: engine bleed air ducted inside wing/slat leading edges (piccolo tubes) and engine intakes — ____, most jets.","Hot air (bleed air): engine bleed air ducted inside wing/slat leading edges (piccolo tubes) and engine intakes — anti-icing, most jets."],["021.07.01.01:01","Ice protection: fill in the gaps → ____: heating elements/mats — windshields, probes, propellers (often cyclic de-icing to save power), some wings (B787).","Electrical: heating elements/mats — windshields, probes, propellers (often cyclic de-icing to save power), some wings (B787)."],["021.07.01.01:01","Ice protection: fill in the gaps → ____ (TKS/\"weeping wing\"): glycol-based fluid pumped through porous leading edges — anti-and de-icing; limited by fluid quantity.","Fluid (TKS/\"weeping wing\"): glycol-based fluid pumped through porous leading edges — anti-and de-icing; limited by fluid quantity."],["021.07.01.01:01","Ice protection: fill in the gaps → ____ (see below) — de-icing.","Pneumatic boots (see below) — de-icing."],["021.07.01.01:01","Ice protection: fill in the gaps → ____: rubber boots on leading edges; air from the pneumatic system inflates tubes ____ (e.g. 6 s), cracking the ice which is blown away; vacuum holds them flat otherwise. Operate once ~ ____ of ice has accumulated (per AFM; modern practice: at first sign of ice).","Inflatable boots: rubber boots on leading edges; air from the pneumatic system inflates tubes cyclically (e.g. 6 s), cracking the ice which is blown away; vacuum holds them flat otherwise. Operate once ~ ¼–½ inch of ice has accumulated (per AFM; modern practice: at first sign of ice)."],["021.07.01.01:01","Ice protection: what do you remember about this? → Limitations: cannot prevent ice, residual ice remains, damage by …","Limitations: cannot prevent ice, residual ice remains, damage by stones/wear, not for use at very low temperature (brittle), speed limits."],["021.07.01.01:03","Ice protection: what do you remember about this? → Indications/warnings: ice detector light, \"ICE\" caution, system …","Indications/warnings: ice detector light, \"ICE\" caution, system on/off, duct overheat/leak warnings, low pressure, probe heat fail."],["021.07.02.01:01","Ice detection: fill in the gaps → ____: small probe with holes facing the airflow; ice blocks the holes → pressure difference triggers the warning (and the probe is heated to clear).","Mechanical (air pressure): small probe with holes facing the airflow; ice blocks the holes → pressure difference triggers the warning (and the probe is heated to clear)."],["021.07.02.01:01","Ice detection: fill in the gaps → ____: a probe vibrates at its ____; accreted ice adds mass → ____ → signal \"ICE\"; the probe is then heated and the cycle repeats.","Electromechanical (vibrating/resonance): a probe vibrates at its natural frequency (~40 kHz); accreted ice adds mass → frequency decreases → signal \"ICE\"; the probe is then heated and the cycle repeats."],["021.07.02.01:01","Ice detection: fill in the gaps → ____: alert the crew (caution light, EICAS/ECAM message) and in some aircraft automatically switch on anti-ice; visual cues (ice light on the wing, windshield wiper arm) complement them.","Ice warning systems: alert the crew (caution light, EICAS/ECAM message) and in some aircraft automatically switch on anti-ice; visual cues (ice light on the wing, windshield wiper arm) complement them."],["021.12.03.01:01","Rain protection: fill in the gaps → ____: electrically/hydraulically driven, two speeds; not used on a dry windshield (scratching); speed limit.","Wipers: electrically/hydraulically driven, two speeds; not used on a dry windshield (scratching); speed limit."],["021.12.03.01:01","Rain protection: fill in the gaps → ____ liquid: sprayed on the windshield to form a water-repellent film so raindrops bead and are blown off; only in moderate/heavy rain (on a dry screen it smears); many types phased out.","Rain repellent liquid: sprayed on the windshield to form a water-repellent film so raindrops bead and are blown off; only in moderate/heavy rain (on a dry screen it smears); many types phased out."],["021.12.03.01:01","Rain protection: fill in the gaps → ____: permanent water-repellent coating on the windshield — no crew action, degrades over time.","Hydrophobic coating: permanent water-repellent coating on the windshield — no crew action, degrades over time."],["021.12.03.01:01","Rain protection: what do you remember about this? → (Air curtains/blowers use bleed air to deflect rain on some …","(Air curtains/blowers use bleed air to deflect rain on some aircraft.)"],["021.12.03.01:02","Rain protection: fill in the gaps → ____: similar principle, larger single blades; low rotor downwash speed means wipers are more important at low speed.","Helicopter wipers: similar principle, larger single blades; low rotor downwash speed means wipers are more important at low speed."],["021.07.01.01:05","EASA LO 021.07.01.01.05 → Describe the operating principle of the inflatable boot de-icing system.","Indications/warnings: ice detector light, \"ICE\" caution, system on/off, duct overheat/leak warnings, low pressure, probe heat fail."]],"56":[["081.07.01.01:01","Propeller basics: fill in the gaps → Blade element = small wing: the resultant aerodynamic force splits into ____ (relative to local airflow) or into ____ (along the axis) and ____ (in the plane of rotation). At constant rpm and blade angle, thrust and aerodynamic torque decrease as IAS increases (blade α decreases).","Blade element = small wing: the resultant aerodynamic force splits into lift/drag (relative to local airflow) or into thrust (along the axis) and torque force (in the plane of rotation). At constant rpm and blade angle, thrust and aerodynamic torque decrease as IAS increases (blade α decreases)."],["081.07.01.02:01","Propeller basics: fill in the gaps → Geometry: ____; ____ (2πrn); ____ vector; the resultant = helix (advance) angle φ; blade (pitch) angle θ = chord vs plane of rotation; blade α = θ − φ.","Geometry: chord line; rotational velocity (2πrn); TAS vector; the resultant = helix (advance) angle φ; blade (pitch) angle θ = chord vs plane of rotation; blade α = θ − φ."],["081.07.01.02:01","Propeller basics: fill in the gaps → ____ = theoretical distance advanced in one revolution at zero blade α. ____ = actual distance advanced per revolution. ____ = geometric − effective pitch.","Geometric pitch = theoretical distance advanced in one revolution at zero blade α. Effective pitch = actual distance advanced per revolution. Slip = geometric − effective pitch."],["081.07.01.02:02","Propeller basics: fill in the gaps → ____ = small blade angle (take-off, high rpm); ____ ____ = large blade angle (cruise).","Fine pitch = small blade angle (take-off, high rpm); coarse pitch = large blade angle (cruise)."],["081.07.01.03:01","Propeller basics: fill in the gaps → ____: blade angle decreases from root to tip because rotational speed increases with radius → keeps a roughly ____ (efficient, even thrust).","Blade twist: blade angle decreases from root to tip because rotational speed increases with radius → keeps a roughly constant α along the blade (efficient, even thrust)."],["081.07.01.04:01","Types & operation: fill in the gaps → ____, ____, ____. Fixed pitch: simple, light, cheap but efficient only at one speed (climb prop = fine, cruise prop = coarse). Constant speed: efficient over a wide speed range, rpm held constant by governor; complex, heavier.","Fixed pitch, adjustable/variable pitch non-governing, constant speed (governing). Fixed pitch: simple, light, cheap but efficient only at one speed (climb prop = fine, cruise prop = coarse). Constant speed: efficient over a wide speed range, rpm held constant by governor; complex, heavier."],["081.07.01.04:04","Types & operation: fill in the gaps → Fixed pitch: as TAS ↑ the helix angle ↑ → blade α ↓ (thrust ↓, rpm ↑). Constant speed: governor ____ as speed increases to keep α (and rpm) constant.","Fixed pitch: as TAS ↑ the helix angle ↑ → blade α ↓ (thrust ↓, rpm ↑). Constant speed: governor increases blade angle as speed increases to keep α (and rpm) constant."],["081.07.01.04:05","Types & operation: fill in the gaps → Forces: ____: thrust forward, torque against rotation. ____ (blade α negative): airflow drives the prop → ____ and it drives the engine. Feathered (blade ~90°): minimum drag, prop stops. Reverse (negative blade angle): thrust rearward for braking.","Forces: normal: thrust forward, torque against rotation. Windmilling (blade α negative): airflow drives the prop → negative thrust (drag) and it drives the engine. Feathered (blade ~90°): minimum drag, prop stops. Reverse (negative blade angle): thrust rearward for braking."],["081.07.01.04:06","Types & operation: what do you remember about this? → Changing pitch at constant IAS: coarser → blade α ↑ → more torque …","Changing pitch at constant IAS: coarser → blade α ↑ → more torque needed → rpm drops unless power increased."],["081.07.01.04:04","Types & operation: fill in the gaps → ____ = thrust power ÷ shaft power = T × TAS ÷ P. Zero at zero TAS, max (~80–88 %) at the design speed, falls at high speed (tip Mach, low α). Constant-speed props keep η high over a wide TAS range; fixed pitch only near design point. More blade angle → more thrust (within limits) at the same rpm, if power is available.","Propeller efficiency η = thrust power ÷ shaft power = T × TAS ÷ P. Zero at zero TAS, max (~80–88 %) at the design speed, falls at high speed (tip Mach, low α). Constant-speed props keep η high over a wide TAS range; fixed pitch only near design point. More blade angle → more thrust (within limits) at the same rpm, if power is available."],["081.07.01.06:01","Types & operation: fill in the gaps → ____: changes blade profile → thrust ↓, efficiency ↓, ____, ice shedding hitting the fuselage.","Ice: changes blade profile → thrust ↓, efficiency ↓, vibration/imbalance, ice shedding hitting the fuselage."],["081.07.02.01:01","Engine failure: fill in the gaps → ____ prop produces large ____ → big yaw towards the dead engine, performance loss. ____ reduces drag to a minimum → less yaw moment, better climb, lower VMC effect, easier control.","Windmilling prop produces large drag → big yaw towards the dead engine, performance loss. Feathering reduces drag to a minimum → less yaw moment, better climb, lower VMC effect, easier control."],["081.07.03.01:01","Design for power absorption: fill in the gaps → More power absorbed by: ____ ____.","More power absorbed by: larger diameter, more blades, wider chord (higher solidity), larger blade angle, higher rpm."],["081.07.03.04:01","Design for power absorption: fill in the gaps → Diameter is limited by ____ (Mach ~0.85–0.9 → shock waves, noise, efficiency loss) and ____.","Diameter is limited by tip speed (Mach ~0.85–0.9 → shock waves, noise, efficiency loss) and ground/fuselage clearance."],["081.07.03.03:01","Design for power absorption: fill in the gaps → ____ = total blade area ÷ disc area. More blades: absorb more power with smaller diameter, smoother, but more weight, cost and lower efficiency per blade (interference).","Solidity = total blade area ÷ disc area. More blades: absorb more power with smaller diameter, smoother, but more weight, cost and lower efficiency per blade (interference)."],["081.07.03.03:01","Design for power absorption: fill in the gaps → ____ reduced by lower tip speed (rpm/diameter), more blades, swept/scimitar blades, synchrophasing.","Noise reduced by lower tip speed (rpm/diameter), more blades, swept/scimitar blades, synchrophasing."],["081.07.04.01:02","Secondary effects: fill in the gaps → ____: prop turning clockwise (seen from behind) rolls the aircraft ____ → more load on the left wheel → yaw left on the ground. Counter-measures: rigging, trim; ____ (engines turn in opposite directions on a twin → no critical engine) and contra-rotating (two props on the same shaft in opposite directions → torque cancels).","Torque reaction: prop turning clockwise (seen from behind) rolls the aircraft left → more load on the left wheel → yaw left on the ground. Counter-measures: rigging, trim; counter-rotating (engines turn in opposite directions on a twin → no critical engine) and contra-rotating (two props on the same shaft in opposite directions → torque cancels)."],["081.07.04.02:01","Secondary effects: fill in the gaps → ____: a force on the spinning disc acts ____ ____ in the direction of rotation. Clockwise prop: pitch-up → yaw right, pitch-down → yaw left (tail-wheel lift-off → yaw left); yaw right → pitch down, yaw left → pitch up.","Gyroscopic precession: a force on the spinning disc acts 90° later in the direction of rotation. Clockwise prop: pitch-up → yaw right, pitch-down → yaw left (tail-wheel lift-off → yaw left); yaw right → pitch down, yaw left → pitch up."],["081.08.02.03:02","Secondary effects: fill in the gaps → ____: spiralling air hits the fin on one side (clockwise prop → strikes left side of fin → yaw ____); also increases tail effectiveness, changes wing lift behind the prop.","Slipstream: spiralling air hits the fin on one side (clockwise prop → strikes left side of fin → yaw left); also increases tail effectiveness, changes wing lift behind the prop."],["081.07.04.04:02","Secondary effects: fill in the gaps → ____: at high α the ____ ____ has a larger α/speed → more thrust on the right side (clockwise prop) → yaw left. On twins with both props clockwise the left engine is critical (thrust line of the right engine further from the centre line). Counter-rotating (left clockwise, right anticlockwise, \"inward\") → no critical engine.","Asymmetric blade effect (P-factor): at high α the down- going blade has a larger α/speed → more thrust on the right side (clockwise prop) → yaw left. On twins with both props clockwise the left engine is critical (thrust line of the right engine further from the centre line). Counter-rotating (left clockwise, right anticlockwise, \"inward\") → no critical engine."],["081.07.04.05:03","Secondary effects: fill in the gaps → ____ (clockwise prop): torque, slipstream, P-factor → ____ ____, gyroscopic effect at tail lift-off (tailwheel) → left. Go-around: sudden full power at low speed with high flap → strong yaw/roll; with loss of the critical engine, crosswind from the dead-engine side, high flap and high engine performance (low density altitude) → VMC higher and control harder.","Take-off (clockwise prop): torque, slipstream, P-factor → yaw left, gyroscopic effect at tail lift-off (tailwheel) → left. Go-around: sudden full power at low speed with high flap → strong yaw/roll; with loss of the critical engine, crosswind from the dead-engine side, high flap and high engine performance (low density altitude) → VMC higher and control harder."],["081.08.01.02:06","Flight mechanics: straight flight: fill in the gaps → ____: lift = weight, thrust = drag. Lift acts at the centre of pressure, weight at the CG, thrust along the thrust line, drag through the drag line; the ____ provides the balancing force (usually a down-load) to cancel pitching moments.","Level flight: lift = weight, thrust = drag. Lift acts at the centre of pressure, weight at the CG, thrust along the thrust line, drag through the drag line; the tailplane provides the balancing force (usually a down-load) to cancel pitching moments."],["081.08.01.02:01","Flight mechanics: straight flight: fill in the gaps → ____: flight-path angle γ = angle of the flight path to the horizon; ____ (no wind, no bank). Parallel forces: T = D + W sin γ → thrust > drag. Perpendicular: L = W cos γ → lift < weight. Small angles: sin γ ≈ γ (rad) ≈ T/W − ____. Climb at constant vertical speed & thrust: as altitude increases, TAS must stay (IAS decreases), α increases.","Climb: flight-path angle γ = angle of the flight path to the horizon; pitch attitude = γ + α (no wind, no bank). Parallel forces: T = D + W sin γ → thrust > drag. Perpendicular: L = W cos γ → lift < weight. Small angles: sin γ ≈ γ (rad) ≈ T/W − 1/(L/D). Climb at constant vertical speed & thrust: as altitude increases, TAS must stay (IAS decreases), α increases."],["081.08.01.03:03","Flight mechanics: straight flight: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ → thrust < drag; ____ → lift < weight.","Descent: T = D − W sin γ → thrust < drag; L = W cos γ → lift < weight."],["081.08.01.04:03","Flight mechanics: straight flight: fill in the gaps → ____: T = 0 → D = W sin γ, L = W cos γ → tan γ = D/L = ____. Glide range = height × L/D (still air). Example: 6 000 ft, L/D 12 → 72 000 ft ≈ ____; with 20 kt headwind at 100 kt: × 80/100 → 9.5 NM.","Glide: T = 0 → D = W sin γ, L = W cos γ → tan γ = D/L = 1/(L/D). Glide range = height × L/D (still air). Example: 6 000 ft, L/D 12 → 72 000 ft ≈ 11.8 NM; with 20 kt headwind at 100 kt: × 80/100 → 9.5 NM."],["081.08.01.04:09","Flight mechanics: straight flight: fill in the gaps → ________ = speed for minimum drag = at the α for ____ (≈ 4° AoA) → flattest glide (min angle). VMP (min power, ≈ 0.76 VMD) → minimum rate of descent (max endurance glide).","VMD = speed for minimum drag = at the α for max L/D (≈ 4° AoA) → flattest glide (min angle). VMP (min power, ≈ 0.76 VMD) → minimum rate of descent (max endurance glide)."],["081.08.01.04:06","Flight mechanics: straight flight: fill in the gaps → ____: headwind → steeper flight-path angle, shorter distance, same time; fly ____ than VMD. Tailwind → longer distance; fly slightly slower. ____: at VMD the glide angle/distance is the same, but VMD is higher → shorter duration. At constant speed (not VMD) mass changes α and the angle. Flaps/gear → steeper, shorter duration.","Wind: headwind → steeper flight-path angle, shorter distance, same time; fly faster than VMD. Tailwind → longer distance; fly slightly slower. Mass: at VMD the glide angle/distance is the same, but VMD is higher → shorter duration. At constant speed (not VMD) mass changes α and the angle. Flaps/gear → steeper, shorter duration."],["081.08.01.04:08","Flight mechanics: straight flight: fill in the gaps → ____ (≈ TAS × gradient). Fly ____ ____MP for speed stability; min-sink is chosen to maximise time (e.g. to restart engine), min-angle to maximise distance.","Rate of descent = TAS × sin γ (≈ TAS × gradient). Fly faster than VMP for speed stability; min-sink is chosen to maximise time (e.g. to restart engine), min-angle to maximise distance."],["081.08.01.05:04","Turns: fill in the gaps → In a coordinated turn: ____ (vertical), ____ ____ (centripetal) → tan Ø = V²/(gR) → R = V²/(g tan Ø). Bank angle for a given radius depends only on TAS (not mass).","In a coordinated turn: L cos Ø = W (vertical), L sin Ø = W V²/(gR) (centripetal) → tan Ø = V²/(gR) → R = V²/(g tan Ø). Bank angle for a given radius depends only on TAS (not mass)."],["081.08.01.05:01","Turns: fill in the gaps → ____ (kt, NM): ____ in NM with V in kt (R[m] = V[m/s]²/(9.81 tan Ø)). Example: 180 kt, 30° → 32 400/(68 600 × 0.577) ≈ ____.","Turn radius (kt, NM): R ≈ V²/(68 600 × tan Ø) in NM with V in kt (R[m] = V[m/s]²/(9.81 tan Ø)). Example: 180 kt, 30° → 32 400/(68 600 × 0.577) ≈ 0.82 NM."],["081.08.01.05:11","Turns: fill in the gaps → ____: 30° → 1.15, 45° → 1.41, ____. ____S × √n: 60° bank → VS × 1.41.","Load factor n = 1/cos Ø: 30° → 1.15, 45° → 1.41, 60° → 2.0. Stall speed in turn = VS × √n: 60° bank → VS × 1.41."],["081.08.01.05:10","Turns: fill in the gaps → Angular velocity/rate of turn ω = V/R (°/s). ____ ____ (360° in 2 min). Bank for rate 1 ≈ ____ (°). At a given bank angle, higher TAS → larger radius, lower rate of turn.","Angular velocity/rate of turn ω = V/R (°/s). Rate-1 turn = 3°/s (360° in 2 min). Bank for rate 1 ≈ TAS/10 + 7 (°). At a given bank angle, higher TAS → larger radius, lower rate of turn."],["081.08.02.01:03","Turns: fill in the gaps → ____: slip (ball towards the inside, too much bank/too little rudder) or skid (ball outside) → \"step on the ball\".","Uncoordinated: slip (ball towards the inside, too much bank/too little rudder) or skid (ball outside) → \"step on the ball\"."],["081.08.01.05:12","Turns: fill in the gaps → Turn radius matters for ____, approach speed categories (A–E) for circling/procedure protected areas — exceeding the speed takes you outside the protected area (terrain!).","Turn radius matters for speed limits on SIDs/STARs, approach speed categories (A–E) for circling/procedure protected areas — exceeding the speed takes you outside the protected area (terrain!)."],["081.08.01.05:13","Turns: fill in the gaps → ____ in a swept-wing aircraft to tighten a turn → sideslip → strong ____ (dihedral effect of sweep), possible structural overload of the fin with cyclic rudder inputs (AA587).","Excessive rudder in a swept-wing aircraft to tighten a turn → sideslip → strong roll (dihedral effect of sweep), possible structural overload of the fin with cyclic rudder inputs (AA587)."],["081.07.04.04:02","Asymmetric thrust: fill in the gaps → Engine failure → ____ (thrust of live engine × arm + drag of dead engine) towards the dead engine and roll (loss of slipstream lift). Jets: engines close to the fuselage → smaller moment; props: direction of rotation → critical engine (P-factor).","Engine failure → yawing moment (thrust of live engine × arm + drag of dead engine) towards the dead engine and roll (loss of slipstream lift). Jets: engines close to the fuselage → smaller moment; props: direction of rotation → critical engine (P-factor)."],["081.08.02.01:03","Asymmetric thrust: fill in the gaps → ____: flying wings level with rudder → ball ____ is not zero sideslip; balance with ____ engine (\"raise the dead\") and ball ~½ out towards the live engine → zero sideslip, best performance. Crosswind from the dead-engine side increases the rudder needed.","Slip indicator: flying wings level with rudder → ball centered is not zero sideslip; balance with ~5° bank towards the live engine (\"raise the dead\") and ball ~½ out towards the live engine → zero sideslip, best performance. Crosswind from the dead-engine side increases the rudder needed."],["081.08.02.02:03","Asymmetric thrust: fill in the gaps → ____: thrust × arm vs rudder side force × fin arm. Higher density → more thrust → larger moment. Engines further from the CG → larger moment. Balance with ____ (and bank), reduce thrust on the live engine if needed.","Yaw moments about the CG: thrust × arm vs rudder side force × fin arm. Higher density → more thrust → larger moment. Engines further from the CG → larger moment. Balance with rudder (and bank), reduce thrust on the live engine if needed."],["081.08.02.03:01","Asymmetric thrust: fill in the gaps → ____: rudder force balanced by a ____ ____ (drag ↑). Wing-down method: bank tilts the lift vector to provide the side force → less sideslip, less rudder.","Wings-level method: rudder force balanced by a fuselage side force due to sideslip (drag ↑). Wing-down method: bank tilts the lift vector to provide the side force → less sideslip, less rudder."],["081.08.02.03:02","Asymmetric thrust: fill in the gaps → Hazards at ________: high α (close to stall), large sideslip, high ____ loads, fin α near its stall (sideslip adds to fin α).","Hazards at VMC: high α (close to stall), large sideslip, high fin loads, fin α near its stall (sideslip adds to fin α)."],["081.08.02.04:01","Asymmetric thrust: fill in the gaps → ____ → lower stall speeds → you can fly slower, closer to/below VMC → controllability becomes the limit.","Low mass → lower stall speeds → you can fly slower, closer to/below VMC → controllability becomes the limit."],["081.08.02.08:01","Asymmetric thrust: fill in the gaps → ________: minimum speed at which directional control can be maintained after sudden failure of the critical engine with max ____ bank towards the live engine, rudder force ≤ 150 lbf, heading change ≤ 20°.","VMC(A): minimum speed at which directional control can be maintained after sudden failure of the critical engine with max 5° bank towards the live engine, rudder force ≤ 150 lbf, heading change ≤ 20°."],["081.08.02.08:02","Asymmetric thrust: fill in the gaps → Determined with: take-off power on the live engine, ____, dead engine windmilling (or feathered if automatic), flaps take-off, gear up, max sea-level density. ____ → shorter rudder arm → ____MC.","Determined with: take-off power on the live engine, most adverse (aft) CG, dead engine windmilling (or feathered if automatic), flaps take-off, gear up, max sea-level density. Aft CG → shorter rudder arm → higher VMC."],["081.08.02.10:01","Asymmetric thrust: fill in the gaps → ________: same in approach/landing configuration (go-around thrust). VMCG: on the ground, using aerodynamic controls only (no nose-wheel steering), lateral deviation ≤ ____ from the centre line. CG aft → higher (VMCG additionally affected by nose-wheel load).","VMCL: same in approach/landing configuration (go-around thrust). VMCG: on the ground, using aerodynamic controls only (no nose-wheel steering), lateral deviation ≤ 30 ft from the centre line. CG aft → higher (VMCG additionally affected by nose-wheel load)."],["081.08.02.11:02","Asymmetric thrust: fill in the gaps → ____ (high altitude/temperature) → less thrust → ________, VMCL, VMCG decrease.","Lower density (high altitude/temperature) → less thrust → VMC, VMCL, VMCG decrease."],["081.08.01.04:04","Polar curve: fill in the gaps → CL vs CD: ____ = ____ (VMD, best glide/prop range); ____ = stall; point for min power (C 3/2/C L max L D max); CD min (zero lift); jet max range where √CL/CD is max (faster than VMD).","CL vs CD: tangent from the origin = max L/D (VMD, best glide/prop range); C = stall; point for min power (C 3/2/C L max L D max); CD min (zero lift); jet max range where √CL/CD is max (faster than VMD)."],["100.04.00.00:05","Mental maths (KSA 100.04): fill in the gaps → ____: litres × SG = kg; USG × 3.785 = l; kg × 2.2 = lb. ____: min = NM × 60/GS. ____ = FF × time; time available = (fuel − reserve)/FF.","Fuel: litres × SG = kg; USG × 3.785 = l; kg × 2.2 = lb. Time/distance/speed: min = NM × 60/GS. Fuel burn = FF × time; time available = (fuel − reserve)/FF."],["100.04.00.00:02","Mental maths (KSA 100.04): fill in the gaps → ____ (ft/min); ____ ____ (318). TOD: distance (NM) ≈ height to lose (ft)/1 000 × 3.","ROD for 3° glide ≈ GS × 5 (ft/min); height on 3° slope ≈ 300 ft per NM (318). TOD: distance (NM) ≈ height to lose (ft)/1 000 × 3."],["100.04.00.00:10","Mental maths (KSA 100.04): fill in the gaps → ____: 1 NM off track after 60 NM = 1°; track error = offset × 60/distance gone; closing angle = offset × 60/distance to go.","1-in-60 rule: 1 NM off track after 60 NM = 1°; track error = offset × 60/distance gone; closing angle = offset × 60/distance to go."],["100.04.00.00:08","Mental maths (KSA 100.04): fill in the gaps → ____: XW = V × sin, HW = V × cos (30° ½, 45° 0.7, 60° 0.9). ____: wet LD = dry × 1.15; +10 % of 1 200 = 1 320.","Wind components: XW = V × sin, HW = V × cos (30° ½, 45° 0.7, 60° 0.9). Percentages: wet LD = dry × 1.15; +10 % of 1 200 = 1 320."],["081.08.01.05:10","Exam trap (Mental maths (KSA 100.04)): complete the fact → Rate-____bank ≈ TAS/____+ 7.","Rate-1 bank ≈ TAS/10 + 7."],["081.07.01.01:02","EASA LO 081.07.01.01.02 → Describe how propeller thrust and aerodynamic torque vary with IAS.","Blade element = small wing: the resultant aerodynamic force splits into lift/drag (relative to local airflow) or into thrust (along the axis) and torque force (in the plane of rotation). At constant rpm and blade angle, thrust and aerodynamic torque decrease as IAS increases (blade α decreases). / Changing pitch at constant IAS: coarser → blade α ↑ → more torque needed → rpm drops unless power increased."],["081.07.01.03:02","EASA LO 081.07.01.03.02 → Explain why blade twist is necessary.","Blade twist: blade angle decreases from root to tip because rotational speed increases with radius → keeps a roughly constant α along the blade (efficient, even thrust)."],["081.07.01.04:02","EASA LO 081.07.01.04.02 → Discuss the advantages and disadvantages of fixed-pitch and constant-speed propellers.","Fixed pitch: as TAS ↑ the helix angle ↑ → blade α ↓ (thrust ↓, rpm ↑). Constant speed: governor increases blade angle as speed increases to keep α (and rpm) constant. / Fixed pitch, adjustable/variable pitch non-governing, constant speed (governing). Fixed pitch: simple, light, cheap but efficient only at one speed (climb prop = fine, cruise prop = coarse). Constant speed: efficient over a wide speed range, rpm held constant by governor; complex, heavier."],["081.07.01.04:03","EASA LO 081.07.01.04.03 → Discuss climb and cruise propellers.","Fixed pitch, adjustable/variable pitch non-governing, constant speed (governing). Fixed pitch: simple, light, cheap but efficient only at one speed (climb prop = fine, cruise prop = coarse). Constant speed: efficient over a wide speed range, rpm held constant by governor; complex, heavier. / Fixed pitch: as TAS ↑ the helix angle ↑ → blade α ↓ (thrust ↓, rpm ↑). Constant speed: governor increases blade angle as speed increases to keep α (and rpm) constant."],["081.07.01.05:01","EASA LO 081.07.01.05.01 → Define ‘propeller efficiency’.","Diameter is limited by tip speed (Mach ~0.85–0.9 → shock waves, noise, efficiency loss) and ground/fuselage clearance. / Ice: changes blade profile → thrust ↓, efficiency ↓, vibration/imbalance, ice shedding hitting the fuselage."],["081.07.01.05:02","EASA LO 081.07.01.05.02 → Explain and describe the relationship between propeller efficiency and speed (TAS) for different types of propellers.","Ice: changes blade profile → thrust ↓, efficiency ↓, vibration/imbalance, ice shedding hitting the fuselage. / Propeller efficiency η = thrust power ÷ shaft power = T × TAS ÷ P. Zero at zero TAS, max (~80–88 %) at the design speed, falls at high speed (tip Mach, low α). Constant-speed props keep η high over a wide TAS range; fixed pitch only near design point. More blade angle → more thrust (within limits) at the same rpm, if power is available."],["081.07.01.05:03","EASA LO 081.07.01.05.03 → Explain the relationship between blade angle and thrust.","Ice: changes blade profile → thrust ↓, efficiency ↓, vibration/imbalance, ice shedding hitting the fuselage. / Fixed pitch: as TAS ↑ the helix angle ↑ → blade α ↓ (thrust ↓, rpm ↑). Constant speed: governor increases blade angle as speed increases to keep α (and rpm) constant."],["081.07.02.02:01","EASA LO 081.07.02.02.01 → Explain the reasons for feathering a propeller, including the effect on the yaw moment, performance and controllability.","Windmilling prop produces large drag → big yaw towards the dead engine, performance loss. Feathering reduces drag to a minimum → less yaw moment, better climb, lower VMC effect, easier control."],["081.07.03.02:01","EASA LO 081.07.03.02.01 → Explain the reasons for restricting propeller diameter.","Noise reduced by lower tip speed (rpm/diameter), more blades, swept/scimitar blades, synchrophasing. / Diameter is limited by tip speed (Mach ~0.85–0.9 → shock waves, noise, efficiency loss) and ground/fuselage clearance."],["081.07.03.03:02","EASA LO 081.07.03.03.02 → Describe the advantages and disadvantages of increasing the number of blades.","Noise reduced by lower tip speed (rpm/diameter), more blades, swept/scimitar blades, synchrophasing."],["081.07.04.01:01","EASA LO 081.07.04.01.01 → Describe the effects of engine/propeller torque.","Torque reaction: prop turning clockwise (seen from behind) rolls the aircraft left → more load on the left wheel → yaw left on the ground. Counter-measures: rigging, trim; counter-rotating (engines turn in opposite directions on a twin → no critical engine) and contra-rotating (two props on the same shaft in opposite directions → torque cancels). / Changing pitch at constant IAS: coarser → blade α ↑ → more torque needed → rpm drops unless power increased."],["081.07.04.02:02","EASA LO 081.07.04.02.02 → Describe the effect on the aeroplane due to the gyroscopic effect.","Take-off (clockwise prop): torque, slipstream, P-factor → yaw left, gyroscopic effect at tail lift-off (tailwheel) → left. Go-around: sudden full power at low speed with high flap → strong yaw/roll; with loss of the critical engine, crosswind from the dead-engine side, high flap and high engine performance (low density altitude) → VMC higher and control harder."],["081.07.04.03:01","EASA LO 081.07.04.03.01 → Describe the possible effects of the rotating propeller slipstream.","Take-off (clockwise prop): torque, slipstream, P-factor → yaw left, gyroscopic effect at tail lift-off (tailwheel) → left. Go-around: sudden full power at low speed with high flap → strong yaw/roll; with loss of the critical engine, crosswind from the dead-engine side, high flap and high engine performance (low density altitude) → VMC higher and control harder. / Excessive rudder in a swept-wing aircraft to tighten a turn → sideslip → strong roll (dihedral effect of sweep), possible structural overload of the fin with cyclic rudder inputs (AA587)."],["081.07.04.05:01","EASA LO 081.07.04.05.01 → Describe, given direction of propeller rotation, the propeller effects during take-off run, rotation and initial climb, and their consequence on controllability.","Fine pitch = small blade angle (take-off, high rpm); coarse pitch = large blade angle (cruise). / Gyroscopic precession: a force on the spinning disc acts 90° later in the direction of rotation. Clockwise prop: pitch-up → yaw right, pitch-down → yaw left (tail-wheel lift-off → yaw left); yaw right → pitch down, yaw left → pitch up."],["081.08.01.01:01","EASA LO 081.08.01.01.01 → Describe the forces that act on an aeroplane in straight, horizontal, and steady flight.","Descent: T = D − W sin γ → thrust < drag; L = W cos γ → lift < weight."],["081.08.01.02:02","EASA LO 081.08.01.02.02 → Describe the relationship between pitch attitude,  and α for zero-wind and zero-bank conditions.","Climb: flight-path angle γ = angle of the flight path to the horizon; pitch attitude = γ + α (no wind, no bank). Parallel forces: T = D + W sin γ → thrust > drag. Perpendicular: L = W cos γ → lift < weight. Small angles: sin γ ≈ γ (rad) ≈ T/W − 1/(L/D). Climb at constant vertical speed & thrust: as altitude increases, TAS must stay (IAS decreases), α increases. / Geometric pitch = theoretical distance advanced in one revolution at zero blade α. Effective pitch = actual distance advanced per revolution. Slip = geometric − effective pitch."],["081.08.01.02:03","EASA LO 081.08.01.02.03 → Describe the forces that act on an aeroplane in a straight, steady climb.","Climb: flight-path angle γ = angle of the flight path to the horizon; pitch attitude = γ + α (no wind, no bank). Parallel forces: T = D + W sin γ → thrust > drag. Perpendicular: L = W cos γ → lift < weight. Small angles: sin γ ≈ γ (rad) ≈ T/W − 1/(L/D). Climb at constant vertical speed & thrust: as altitude increases, TAS must stay (IAS decreases), α increases."],["081.08.01.02:04","EASA LO 081.08.01.02.04 → Name the forces parallel and perpendicular to the direction of flight. — Apply the formula relating to the parallel forces (T = D + W sin ).","Climb: flight-path angle γ = angle of the flight path to the horizon; pitch attitude = γ + α (no wind, no bank). Parallel forces: T = D + W sin γ → thrust > drag. Perpendicular: L = W cos γ → lift < weight. Small angles: sin γ ≈ γ (rad) ≈ T/W − 1/(L/D). Climb at constant vertical speed & thrust: as altitude increases, TAS must stay (IAS decreases), α increases. / Descent: T = D − W sin γ → thrust < drag; L = W cos γ → lift < weight."],["081.08.01.02:05","EASA LO 081.08.01.02.05 → Explain why thrust is greater than drag.","Descent: T = D − W sin γ → thrust < drag; L = W cos γ → lift < weight."],["081.08.01.02:07","EASA LO 081.08.01.02.07 → Explain the formula (for small angles) that gives the relationship between , thrust, weight, and lift–drag ratio, and use this formula for simple calculations.","Descent: T = D − W sin γ → thrust < drag; L = W cos γ → lift < weight. / Climb: flight-path angle γ = angle of the flight path to the horizon; pitch attitude = γ + α (no wind, no bank). Parallel forces: T = D + W sin γ → thrust > drag. Perpendicular: L = W cos γ → lift < weight. Small angles: sin γ ≈ γ (rad) ≈ T/W − 1/(L/D). Climb at constant vertical speed & thrust: as altitude increases, TAS must stay (IAS decreases), α increases."],["081.08.01.02:08","EASA LO 081.08.01.02.08 → Explain how IAS, α, and  change in a climb performed with constant vertical speed and constant thrust setting.","Climb: flight-path angle γ = angle of the flight path to the horizon; pitch attitude = γ + α (no wind, no bank). Parallel forces: T = D + W sin γ → thrust > drag. Perpendicular: L = W cos γ → lift < weight. Small angles: sin γ ≈ γ (rad) ≈ T/W − 1/(L/D). Climb at constant vertical speed & thrust: as altitude increases, TAS must stay (IAS decreases), α increases. / Fixed pitch: as TAS ↑ the helix angle ↑ → blade α ↓ (thrust ↓, rpm ↑). Constant speed: governor increases blade angle as speed increases to keep α (and rpm) constant."],["081.08.01.03:01","EASA LO 081.08.01.03.01 → Describe the forces that act on an aeroplane in a straight, steady descent.","Descent: T = D − W sin γ → thrust < drag; L = W cos γ → lift < weight."],["081.08.01.03:02","EASA LO 081.08.01.03.02 → Name the forces parallel and perpendicular to the direction of flight. — Apply the formula for forces parallel to the direction of flight (T = D – W sin ). — Apply the formula relating to the perpendicular forces (L = W cos ).","Climb: flight-path angle γ = angle of the flight path to the horizon; pitch attitude = γ + α (no wind, no bank). Parallel forces: T = D + W sin γ → thrust > drag. Perpendicular: L = W cos γ → lift < weight. Small angles: sin γ ≈ γ (rad) ≈ T/W − 1/(L/D). Climb at constant vertical speed & thrust: as altitude increases, TAS must stay (IAS decreases), α increases. / Descent: T = D − W sin γ → thrust < drag; L = W cos γ → lift < weight."],["081.08.01.03:04","EASA LO 081.08.01.03.04 → Explain why thrust is less than drag.","Descent: T = D − W sin γ → thrust < drag; L = W cos γ → lift < weight. / Wings-level method: rudder force balanced by a fuselage side force due to sideslip (drag ↑). Wing-down method: bank tilts the lift vector to provide the side force → less sideslip, less rudder."],["081.08.01.04:01","EASA LO 081.08.01.04.01 → Describe the forces that act on an aeroplane in a straight, steady glide.","VMD = speed for minimum drag = at the α for max L/D (≈ 4° AoA) → flattest glide (min angle). VMP (min power, ≈ 0.76 VMD) → minimum rate of descent (max endurance glide)."],["081.08.01.04:02","EASA LO 081.08.01.04.02 → Name the forces parallel and perpendicular to the direction of flight.","Climb: flight-path angle γ = angle of the flight path to the horizon; pitch attitude = γ + α (no wind, no bank). Parallel forces: T = D + W sin γ → thrust > drag. Perpendicular: L = W cos γ → lift < weight. Small angles: sin γ ≈ γ (rad) ≈ T/W − 1/(L/D). Climb at constant vertical speed & thrust: as altitude increases, TAS must stay (IAS decreases), α increases. / Descent: T = D − W sin γ → thrust < drag; L = W cos γ → lift < weight."],["081.08.01.04:05","EASA LO 081.08.01.04.05 → Explain the effect of wind component on glide angle, duration, and distance.","Wind: headwind → steeper flight-path angle, shorter distance, same time; fly faster than VMD. Tailwind → longer distance; fly slightly slower. Mass: at VMD the glide angle/distance is the same, but VMD is higher → shorter duration. At constant speed (not VMD) mass changes α and the angle. Flaps/gear → steeper, shorter duration. / VMD = speed for minimum drag = at the α for max L/D (≈ 4° AoA) → flattest glide (min angle). VMP (min power, ≈ 0.76 VMD) → minimum rate of descent (max endurance glide)."],["081.08.01.04:07","EASA LO 081.08.01.04.07 → Explain the effect of configuration change on glide angle and duration.","Wind: headwind → steeper flight-path angle, shorter distance, same time; fly faster than VMD. Tailwind → longer distance; fly slightly slower. Mass: at VMD the glide angle/distance is the same, but VMD is higher → shorter duration. At constant speed (not VMD) mass changes α and the angle. Flaps/gear → steeper, shorter duration. / VMD = speed for minimum drag = at the α for max L/D (≈ 4° AoA) → flattest glide (min angle). VMP (min power, ≈ 0.76 VMD) → minimum rate of descent (max endurance glide)."],["081.08.01.04:10","EASA LO 081.08.01.04.10 → Discuss when a pilot could elect to fly for minimum glide rate of descent or minimum glide angle, and why speed stability or headwinds/tailwinds may favour a speed that is faster or slower than the optimum airspeed in still air.","Rate of descent = TAS × sin γ (≈ TAS × gradient). Fly faster than VMP for speed stability; min-sink is chosen to maximise time (e.g. to restart engine), min-angle to maximise distance. / VMD = speed for minimum drag = at the α for max L/D (≈ 4° AoA) → flattest glide (min angle). VMP (min power, ≈ 0.76 VMD) → minimum rate of descent (max endurance glide)."],["081.08.01.05:01","EASA LO 081.08.01.05.01 → Describe the forces that act on an aeroplane in a steady, coordinated turn.","In a coordinated turn: L cos Ø = W (vertical), L sin Ø = W V²/(gR) (centripetal) → tan Ø = V²/(gR) → R = V²/(g tan Ø). Bank angle for a given radius depends only on TAS (not mass)."],["081.08.01.05:02","EASA LO 081.08.01.05.02 → Resolve the forces that act horizontally and vertically during a coordinated turn (tan gR V","In a coordinated turn: L cos Ø = W (vertical), L sin Ø = W V²/(gR) (centripetal) → tan Ø = V²/(gR) → R = V²/(g tan Ø). Bank angle for a given radius depends only on TAS (not mass)."],["081.08.01.05:05","EASA LO 081.08.01.05.05 → Resolve the forces to show that for a given angle of bank the radius of turn is determined solely by airspeed (tan gR V","In a coordinated turn: L cos Ø = W (vertical), L sin Ø = W V²/(gR) (centripetal) → tan Ø = V²/(gR) → R = V²/(g tan Ø). Bank angle for a given radius depends only on TAS (not mass). / Angular velocity/rate of turn ω = V/R (°/s). Rate-1 turn = 3°/s (360° in 2 min). Bank for rate 1 ≈ TAS/10 + 7 (°). At a given bank angle, higher TAS → larger radius, lower rate of turn."],["081.08.01.05:06","EASA LO 081.08.01.05.06 → Calculate the turn radius of a steady turn given TAS and angle of bank.","Angular velocity/rate of turn ω = V/R (°/s). Rate-1 turn = 3°/s (360° in 2 min). Bank for rate 1 ≈ TAS/10 + 7 (°). At a given bank angle, higher TAS → larger radius, lower rate of turn. / In a coordinated turn: L cos Ø = W (vertical), L sin Ø = W V²/(gR) (centripetal) → tan Ø = V²/(gR) → R = V²/(g tan Ø). Bank angle for a given radius depends only on TAS (not mass)."],["081.08.01.05:07","EASA LO 081.08.01.05.07 → Explain the effects of bank angle on: − load factor (LF = 1/cos  );","Load factor n = 1/cos Ø: 30° → 1.15, 45° → 1.41, 60° → 2.0. Stall speed in turn = VS × √n: 60° bank → VS × 1.41. / In a coordinated turn: L cos Ø = W (vertical), L sin Ø = W V²/(gR) (centripetal) → tan Ø = V²/(gR) → R = V²/(g tan Ø). Bank angle for a given radius depends only on TAS (not mass)."],["081.08.01.05:08","EASA LO 081.08.01.05.08 → Define ‘angular velocity’.","Angular velocity/rate of turn ω = V/R (°/s). Rate-1 turn = 3°/s (360° in 2 min). Bank for rate 1 ≈ TAS/10 + 7 (°). At a given bank angle, higher TAS → larger radius, lower rate of turn."],["081.08.01.05:09","EASA LO 081.08.01.05.09 → Define ‘rate of turn’ and ‘rate-1 turn’.","Angular velocity/rate of turn ω = V/R (°/s). Rate-1 turn = 3°/s (360° in 2 min). Bank for rate 1 ≈ TAS/10 + 7 (°). At a given bank angle, higher TAS → larger radius, lower rate of turn. / Rate-1 bank ≈ TAS/10 + 7."],["081.08.02.01:02","EASA LO 081.08.02.01.02 → Explain critical engine, and explain, for a propeller-driven aeroplane, the effect of the direction of propeller rotation.","Engine failure → yawing moment (thrust of live engine × arm + drag of dead engine) towards the dead engine and roll (loss of slipstream lift). Jets: engines close to the fuselage → smaller moment; props: direction of rotation → critical engine (P-factor)."],["081.08.02.02:01","EASA LO 081.08.02.02.01 → Explain the yaw moments about the CG.","Yaw moments about the CG: thrust × arm vs rudder side force × fin arm. Higher density → more thrust → larger moment. Engines further from the CG → larger moment. Balance with rudder (and bank), reduce thrust on the live engine if needed. / Level flight: lift = weight, thrust = drag. Lift acts at the centre of pressure, weight at the CG, thrust along the thrust line, drag through the drag line; the tailplane provides the balancing force (usually a down-load) to cancel pitching moments."],["081.08.02.02:02","EASA LO 081.08.02.02.02 → Explain the change to the yaw moment caused by the effect of air density on thrust.","Yaw moments about the CG: thrust × arm vs rudder side force × fin arm. Higher density → more thrust → larger moment. Engines further from the CG → larger moment. Balance with rudder (and bank), reduce thrust on the live engine if needed."],["081.08.02.02:04","EASA LO 081.08.02.02.04 → Describe the methods to achieve directional balance following engine loss.","Yaw moments about the CG: thrust × arm vs rudder side force × fin arm. Higher density → more thrust → larger moment. Engines further from the CG → larger moment. Balance with rudder (and bank), reduce thrust on the live engine if needed."],["081.08.02.03:03","EASA LO 081.08.02.03.03 → Explain the effect on fin α due to sideslip.","Hazards at VMC: high α (close to stall), large sideslip, high fin loads, fin α near its stall (sideslip adds to fin α). / Excessive rudder in a swept-wing aircraft to tighten a turn → sideslip → strong roll (dihedral effect of sweep), possible structural overload of the fin with cyclic rudder inputs (AA587)."],["081.08.02.08:03","EASA LO 081.08.02.08.03 → Explain the influence of the CG location.","Windmilling prop produces large drag → big yaw towards the dead engine, performance loss. Feathering reduces drag to a minimum → less yaw moment, better climb, lower VMC effect, easier control. / VMC(A): minimum speed at which directional control can be maintained after sudden failure of the critical engine with max 5° bank towards the live engine, rudder force ≤ 150 lbf, heading change ≤ 20°."],["081.08.02.09:01","EASA LO 081.08.02.09.01 → Define ‘VMCL’.","Lower density (high altitude/temperature) → less thrust → VMC, VMCL, VMCG decrease. / VMCL: same in approach/landing configuration (go-around thrust). VMCG: on the ground, using aerodynamic controls only (no nose-wheel steering), lateral deviation ≤ 30 ft from the centre line. CG aft → higher (VMCG additionally affected by nose-wheel load)."],["081.08.02.09:02","EASA LO 081.08.02.09.02 → Describe how VMCL is determined.","Lower density (high altitude/temperature) → less thrust → VMC, VMCL, VMCG decrease. / VMCL: same in approach/landing configuration (go-around thrust). VMCG: on the ground, using aerodynamic controls only (no nose-wheel steering), lateral deviation ≤ 30 ft from the centre line. CG aft → higher (VMCG additionally affected by nose-wheel load)."],["081.08.02.09:03","EASA LO 081.08.02.09.03 → Explain the influence of the CG location.","VMCL: same in approach/landing configuration (go-around thrust). VMCG: on the ground, using aerodynamic controls only (no nose-wheel steering), lateral deviation ≤ 30 ft from the centre line. CG aft → higher (VMCG additionally affected by nose-wheel load)."],["081.08.02.10:02","EASA LO 081.08.02.10.02 → Describe how VMCG is determined.","VMCL: same in approach/landing configuration (go-around thrust). VMCG: on the ground, using aerodynamic controls only (no nose-wheel steering), lateral deviation ≤ 30 ft from the centre line. CG aft → higher (VMCG additionally affected by nose-wheel load). / Lower density (high altitude/temperature) → less thrust → VMC, VMCL, VMCG decrease."],["081.08.02.10:03","EASA LO 081.08.02.10.03 → Explain the influence of the CG location.","VMCL: same in approach/landing configuration (go-around thrust). VMCG: on the ground, using aerodynamic controls only (no nose-wheel steering), lateral deviation ≤ 30 ft from the centre line. CG aft → higher (VMCG additionally affected by nose-wheel load)."],["081.08.02.11:01","EASA LO 081.08.02.11.01 → Describe the influence of density on thrust during asymmetric flight.","Lower density (high altitude/temperature) → less thrust → VMC, VMCL, VMCG decrease. / Yaw moments about the CG: thrust × arm vs rudder side force × fin arm. Higher density → more thrust → larger moment. Engines further from the CG → larger moment. Balance with rudder (and bank), reduce thrust on the live engine if needed."],["100.04.00.00:03","EASA LO 100.04.00.00.03 → Estimate the rate of climb or rate of descent, distance and time.","Rate-1 bank ≈ TAS/10 + 7. / Rate of descent = TAS × sin γ (≈ TAS × gradient). Fly faster than VMP for speed stability; min-sink is chosen to maximise time (e.g. to restart engine), min-angle to maximise distance."],["100.04.00.00:04","EASA LO 100.04.00.00.04 → Add or subtract time, distance, and fuel mass.","Fuel: litres × SG = kg; USG × 3.785 = l; kg × 2.2 = lb. Time/distance/speed: min = NM × 60/GS. Fuel burn = FF × time; time available = (fuel − reserve)/FF. / Wind: headwind → steeper flight-path angle, shorter distance, same time; fly faster than VMD. Tailwind → longer distance; fly slightly slower. Mass: at VMD the glide angle/distance is the same, but VMD is higher → shorter duration. At constant speed (not VMD) mass changes α and the angle. Flaps/gear → steeper, shorter duration."],["100.04.00.00:06","EASA LO 100.04.00.00.06 → Calculate the time available (for decision-making) given relevant fuel information.","Fuel: litres × SG = kg; USG × 3.785 = l; kg × 2.2 = lb. Time/distance/speed: min = NM × 60/GS. Fuel burn = FF × time; time available = (fuel − reserve)/FF."]],"57":[["033.03.03.04:01","Why special procedures?: fill in the gaps → The basic scheme (033-05) sometimes carries a lot of contingency fuel on long flights or makes a flight impossible (no alternate). The operational requirements (CAT.OP.MPA.181 + AMC) therefore allow special procedures that reduce fuel ____ ____, subject to the operator's fuel scheme approval.","The basic scheme (033-05) sometimes carries a lot of contingency fuel on long flights or makes a flight impossible (no alternate). The operational requirements (CAT.OP.MPA.181 + AMC) therefore allow special procedures that reduce fuel while keeping the same safety level, subject to the operator's fuel scheme approval."],["033.03.03.01:02","Reduced contingency fuel (RCF, \"decision point\") procedure: fill in the gaps → ____: contingency fuel is only needed for the part of the flight still to be flown. Plan to a ____ en route and to a ____ (a closer en-route alternate / \"commercial\" destination) so that contingency is calculated on a shorter trip.","Idea: contingency fuel is only needed for the part of the flight still to be flown. Plan to a decision point (DP) en route and to a destination 2 (a closer en-route alternate / \"commercial\" destination) so that contingency is calculated on a shorter trip."],["033.03.03.01:01","Reduced contingency fuel (RCF, \"decision point\") procedure: fill in the gaps → At the decision point the crew checks fuel: if enough to continue to the ____ with its reserves → continue; otherwise ____ ____ (en-route alternate).","At the decision point the crew checks fuel: if enough to continue to the destination 1 with its reserves → continue; otherwise land at destination 2 (en-route alternate)."],["033.03.03.01:02","Reduced contingency fuel (RCF, \"decision point\") procedure: fill in the gaps → ____ is the greater of: 1. taxi + trip fuel to destination 1 via the DP + ____ ____ + alternate fuel for destination 1 + final reserve + additional fuel;","Fuel required is the greater of: 1. taxi + trip fuel to destination 1 via the DP + contingency = 5 % of the trip fuel from DP to destination 1 + alternate fuel for destination 1 + final reserve + additional fuel;"],["033.03.03.01:02","Reduced contingency fuel (RCF, \"decision point\") procedure: fill in the gaps → or 2. taxi + trip fuel to the decision-point ____ via DP + contingency ____ ____ + alternate fuel for destination 2 (if required) + final reserve + additional.","or 2. taxi + trip fuel to the decision-point alternate (destination 2) via DP + contingency 5 % of trip fuel from departure to destination 2 + alternate fuel for destination 2 (if required) + final reserve + additional."],["033.03.03.01:02","Reduced contingency fuel (RCF, \"decision point\") procedure: what do you remember about this? → Calculation: compute both lines, take the larger → block fuel. …","Calculation: compute both lines, take the larger → block fuel. Contingency is only applied to the leg DP → destination 1, which saves fuel compared with 5 % of the whole trip."],["033.03.03.02:02","Isolated aerodrome procedure: fill in the gaps → Destination with ____ → no alternate fuel; instead ____: aeroplanes (turbine) must carry fuel for ____ incl. final reserve (piston: 45 min + 15 % of flight time or 2 h, whichever less). A point of no return (PNR) is defined — beyond it the aircraft is committed to the isolated destination.","Destination with no adequate alternate → no alternate fuel; instead additional fuel: aeroplanes (turbine) must carry fuel for 2 h at normal cruise consumption above the destination incl. final reserve (piston: 45 min + 15 % of flight time or 2 h, whichever less). A point of no return (PNR) is defined — beyond it the aircraft is committed to the isolated destination."],["033.03.03.03:01","Predetermined-point (PDP) procedure: fill in the gaps → ____: used when the destination has only one runway / no destination alternate but there is an en-route alternate. A ____ is chosen from which the aircraft can still reach an ____.","Idea: used when the destination has only one runway / no destination alternate but there is an en-route alternate. A predetermined point is chosen from which the aircraft can still reach an en-route alternate."],["033.03.03.03:01","Predetermined-point (PDP) procedure: fill in the gaps → Fuel at departure is the ____ of: 1. fuel to fly to the ____ + contingency + ____ (piston: 45 min + 15 % of flight time from PDP or 2 h; turbine: 2 h at normal cruise above destination incl. final reserve), or 2. fuel to fly to the en-route alternate via the PDP + contingency + final reserve.","Fuel at departure is the greater of: 1. fuel to fly to the destination via the PDP + contingency + additional fuel (piston: 45 min + 15 % of flight time from PDP or 2 h; turbine: 2 h at normal cruise above destination incl. final reserve), or 2. fuel to fly to the en-route alternate via the PDP + contingency + final reserve."],["033.03.03.03:01","Predetermined-point (PDP) procedure: what do you remember about this? → At the PDP the crew decides to continue (weather at destination still …","At the PDP the crew decides to continue (weather at destination still acceptable) or divert to the en-route alternate."],["033.03.03.04:01","Fuel tankering: fill in the gaps → ____: carry ____ from a station with cheap fuel for the next sector(s) instead of refuelling at an expensive station (or where fuel is unavailable/slow, or to shorten turnaround).","Idea: carry extra fuel from a station with cheap fuel for the next sector(s) instead of refuelling at an expensive station (or where fuel is unavailable/slow, or to shorten turnaround)."],["033.03.03.04:01","Fuel tankering: fill in the gaps → Carrying extra mass costs fuel: part of the tankered fuel is burnt to carry it (____ ____, from the tankering graph). Tankering pays when ____. Example: penalty 12 % → tanker only if fuel is > 12 % more expensive at destination.","Carrying extra mass costs fuel: part of the tankered fuel is burnt to carry it (burn-off penalty ≈ 3–4 % of the tankered fuel per flight hour, from the tankering graph). Tankering pays when price at destination > price at departure × (1 + penalty). Example: penalty 12 % → tanker only if fuel is > 12 % more expensive at destination."],["033.03.03.04:01","Fuel tankering: fill in the gaps → Limits: ____, tank capacity, performance (longer runway, lower climb), environmental/CO₂ cost.","Limits: MLM/MTOM, tank capacity, performance (longer runway, lower climb), environmental/CO₂ cost."],["033.03.03.04:01","Fuel tankering: fill in the gaps → ____ (CAP 697 / tankering chart): enter with trip distance or time, cruise level and landing mass → read the percentage of fuel to be burnt/extra fuel needed per 1 000 kg carried or the break-even price ratio. ____ = (MLM − planned landing mass) ÷ (1 − penalty) as a rough limit, or = lowest of (MTOM − TOM), (MLM − LM) considering burn-off, tank capacity.","Graphs/tables (CAP 697 / tankering chart): enter with trip distance or time, cruise level and landing mass → read the percentage of fuel to be burnt/extra fuel needed per 1 000 kg carried or the break-even price ratio. Max tankering fuel = (MLM − planned landing mass) ÷ (1 − penalty) as a rough limit, or = lowest of (MTOM − TOM), (MLM − LM) considering burn-off, tank capacity."],["033.03.02.05:02","Total fuel & fuel plan: what do you remember about this? → Total = taxi + trip + contingency (as per procedure) + alternate + …","Total = taxi + trip + contingency (as per procedure) + alternate + final reserve + additional + extra (+ tankered fuel). Fill the fuel section of the navigation plan line by line (time and mass per item), then check mass limits."],["033.03.02.05:02","Exam trap (Total fuel & fuel plan): complete the fact → in RCF the contingency is computed on the leg DP → destination ____(line ____) and the whole trip to destination 2 (line 2); take the larger result.","in RCF the contingency is computed on the leg DP → destination 1 (line 1) and the whole trip to destination 2 (line 2); take the larger result."],["033.03.02.05:02","Exam trap (Total fuel & fuel plan): complete the fact → Isolated destination = no alternate fuel but ____ additional (turbine).","Isolated destination = no alternate fuel but 2 h additional (turbine)."],["033.03.03.01:01","EASA LO 033.03.03.01.01 → Explain the reasons and regulations for reduced contingency fuel as stated in the applicable operational requirements.","Fuel required is the greater of: 1. taxi + trip fuel to destination 1 via the DP + contingency = 5 % of the trip fuel from DP to destination 1 + alternate fuel for destination 1 + final reserve + additional fuel; / or 2. taxi + trip fuel to the decision-point alternate (destination 2) via DP + contingency 5 % of trip fuel from departure to destination 2 + alternate fuel for destination 2 (if required) + final reserve + additional."],["033.03.02.05:01","EASA LO 033.03.02.05.01 → Calculate the total fuel required for a given flight.","Total = taxi + trip + contingency (as per procedure) + alternate + final reserve + additional + extra (+ tankered fuel). Fill the fuel section of the navigation plan line by line (time and mass per item), then check mass limits. / Isolated destination = no alternate fuel but 2 h additional (turbine)."]],"58":[["021.06.01.02:01","Bleed-air sources: fill in the gaps → ____ (compressor bleed from low-pressure/intermediate ____ stage in normal cruise, ____ stage at low engine power), APU (on ground and in flight up to its limit), ground supply (ground air cart via a connector for engine start/air conditioning).","Engine (compressor bleed from low-pressure/intermediate LP/IP stage in normal cruise, HP stage at low engine power), APU (on ground and in flight up to its limit), ground supply (ground air cart via a connector for engine start/air conditioning)."],["021.06.01.02:03","Users of bleed air: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (wing, engine intakes), ____ conditioning and pressurisation (packs), engine starting (air starter), pressurisation of hydraulic reservoirs, potable water tank pressurisation, some air-driven hydraulic pumps (ADP), cargo heating, thrust-reverser actuation (older types).","Aeroplane: ice protection (wing, engine intakes), air conditioning and pressurisation (packs), engine starting (air starter), pressurisation of hydraulic reservoirs, potable water tank pressurisation, some air-driven hydraulic pumps (ADP), cargo heating, thrust-reverser actuation (older types)."],["021.06.01.02:03","Users of bleed air: fill in the gaps → ____: ____, ____, pressurisation of the hydraulic reservoir (+ cabin heating).","Helicopter: anti-icing, engine air starter, pressurisation of the hydraulic reservoir (+ cabin heating)."],["021.06.01.02:01","Components: fill in the gaps → ____ (insulated, with leak-detection loops along them).","Pneumatic ducts (insulated, with leak-detection loops along them)."],["021.06.01.02:04","Components: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ and ____ — the HP valve opens automatically at low power when IP pressure is insufficient and closes at high power.","Engine bleed valves: IP (check) valve and HP valve — the HP valve opens automatically at low power when IP pressure is insufficient and closes at high power."],["021.06.01.02:01","Components: fill in the gaps → ____: limits duct pressure (e.g. ~ 40–45 psi) and closes on overheat/overpressure/engine fire handle.","Pressure-regulating (and shut-off) valve (PRSOV): limits duct pressure (e.g. ~ 40–45 psi) and closes on overheat/overpressure/engine fire handle."],["021.06.01.02:04","Components: fill in the gaps → ____ (heat exchanger): cools the hot compressor air (up to ~ 400–500 °C) with fan air, controlled by a fan-air valve, to ~ 200 °C.","Fan-air pre-cooler (heat exchanger): cools the hot compressor air (up to ~ 400–500 °C) with fan air, controlled by a fan-air valve, to ~ 200 °C."],["021.06.01.02:01","Components: fill in the gaps → ____: separates left and right systems or connects them (engine start from the other side or APU, single-pack operation).","Isolation / cross-bleed valve: separates left and right systems or connects them (engine start from the other side or APU, single-pack operation)."],["021.06.01.02:04","Components: fill in the gaps → ____, overheat and leak detectors, APU bleed valve, ground connection check valve.","Temperature and pressure sensors, overheat and leak detectors, APU bleed valve, ground connection check valve."],["021.06.01.02:04","Schematic reading (FCOM level): what do you remember about this? → Engine → IP check valve / HP valve → PRSOV → pre-cooler → duct → …","Engine → IP check valve / HP valve → PRSOV → pre-cooler → duct → (isolation valve) → packs, wing anti-ice, engine start; APU bleed and ground cart join at the cross-bleed manifold through check valves. Check-valve arrows show the only possible flow direction."],["021.06.01.02:06","Indications, control & monitoring: fill in the gaps → Indications: ____ (per side), bleed valve positions (open/closed/disagree), APU bleed, cross-bleed valve, ____ ____ lights, pack and wing anti-ice status.","Indications: duct pressure (per side), bleed valve positions (open/closed/disagree), APU bleed, cross-bleed valve, BLEED FAULT/OVHT/LEAK lights, pack and wing anti-ice status."],["021.06.01.02:04","Indications, control & monitoring: fill in the gaps → Control: bleed switches per engine and APU, cross-bleed/isolation selector (auto/open/closed); the ____ ____ regulates pressure and temperature, selects IP/HP, closes valves automatically on faults, and priorities (APU bleed closes engine bleed etc.).","Control: bleed switches per engine and APU, cross-bleed/isolation selector (auto/open/closed); the bleed monitoring computer (BMC) regulates pressure and temperature, selects IP/HP, closes valves automatically on faults, and priorities (APU bleed closes engine bleed etc.)."],["021.06.01.02:08","Malfunctions: fill in the gaps → ____ (pre-cooler failure) → valve closes, \"BLEED OVHT\".","Over-temperature (pre-cooler failure) → valve closes, \"BLEED OVHT\"."],["021.06.01.02:08","Malfunctions: fill in the gaps → ____ → PRSOV/HP valve closes.","Over-pressure → PRSOV/HP valve closes."],["021.06.01.02:01","Malfunctions: fill in the gaps → ____ (hot air around structure, fuel lines, wiring) → leak detection loops (eutectic salt or thermal switches) trigger \"BLEED LEAK\" → the affected section is isolated.","Duct leak (hot air around structure, fuel lines, wiring) → leak detection loops (eutectic salt or thermal switches) trigger \"BLEED LEAK\" → the affected section is isolated."],["021.06.01.02:01","Malfunctions: fill in the gaps → ____, loss of bleed on one side → cross-bleed or APU bleed to keep packs/anti-ice running; ____ if all bleed is lost.","Valve failure / disagree, loss of bleed on one side → cross-bleed or APU bleed to keep packs/anti-ice running; pressurisation loss if all bleed is lost."],["021.06.01.02:02","EASA LO 021.06.01.02.02 → State that for an aeroplane a bleed-air supply can be used for the following systems or components: — ice protection;","Aeroplane: ice protection (wing, engine intakes), air conditioning and pressurisation (packs), engine starting (air starter), pressurisation of hydraulic reservoirs, potable water tank pressurisation, some air-driven hydraulic pumps (ADP), cargo heating, thrust-reverser actuation (older types)."],["021.06.01.02:05","EASA LO 021.06.01.02.05 → Interpret a basic pneumatic system schematic to the level of detail as found in an FCOM.","Engine → IP check valve / HP valve → PRSOV → pre-cooler → duct → (isolation valve) → packs, wing anti-ice, engine start; APU bleed and ground cart join at the cross-bleed manifold through check valves. Check-valve arrows show the only possible flow direction."],["021.06.01.02:07","EASA LO 021.06.01.02.07 → Explain how the bleed-air supply system is controlled and monitored.","Fan-air pre-cooler (heat exchanger): cools the hot compressor air (up to ~ 400–500 °C) with fan air, controlled by a fan-air valve, to ~ 200 °C."]],"59":[["081.05.04.01:02",": what do you remember about this? → Review stage of 081.05 Control (same LOs as #44). This page is …","Review stage of 081.05 Control (same LOs as #44). This page is written as a rapid-fire exam review — use #44 for the full explanations."],["081.05.01.01:03","Axes & basic principle – Longitudinal: what are the key facts?","Motion: Roll · Control: Ailerons, roll spoilers · Stability about it: Lateral"],["081.05.01.01:03","Axes & basic principle – Lateral: what are the key facts?","Motion: Pitch · Control: Elevator / stabiliser · Stability about it: Longitudinal"],["081.05.03.01:01","Axes & basic principle – Normal: what are the key facts?","Motion: Yaw · Control: Rudder · Stability about it: Directional"],["081.05.01.02:01","Axes & basic principle: fill in the gaps → Control surface down → ____ and ____ → CL ↑ of that surface (at the same aircraft α).","Control surface down → camber ↑ and local α ↑ → CL ↑ of that surface (at the same aircraft α)."],["081.05.06.01:04","Pitch: what do you remember about this? → Elevator up → tail down-force ↑ → nose up. Stabilator: powerful, used …","Elevator up → tail down-force ↑ → nose up. Stabilator: powerful, used on fast aircraft; anti-balance tab for feel."],["081.05.02.02:01","Pitch: fill in the gaps → ____ reduces tail α; the tail sees a ____ than the wing → contributes to stability; ground effect reduces downwash → nose-down near the ground.","Downwash reduces tail α; the tail sees a smaller α change than the wing → contributes to stability; ground effect reduces downwash → nose-down near the ground."],["081.05.02.04:02","Pitch: fill in the gaps → ____ → more elevator needed; forward limit = enough elevator for ____ (low speed, full flap, ground effect). Aft CG → lighter, less stable.","Forward CG → more elevator needed; forward limit = enough elevator for rotation/flare (low speed, full flap, ground effect). Aft CG → lighter, less stable."],["081.05.02.04:02","Pitch: fill in the gaps → Thrust line ____ → power up = ____; above CG → nose down.","Thrust line below CG → power up = nose up; above CG → nose down."],["081.05.03.01:01","Yaw: fill in the gaps → Rudder deflection ∝ yawing moment; ____ creates a restoring fin force (weathercock). ____ reduces max deflection with increasing IAS (structural protection of the fin); full travel at low speed for engine-out.","Rudder deflection ∝ yawing moment; sideslip creates a restoring fin force (weathercock). Rudder limiter reduces max deflection with increasing IAS (structural protection of the fin); full travel at low speed for engine-out."],["081.05.04.01:04","Roll: fill in the gaps → Ailerons: roll + ____ (induced drag of the up-going wing). High speed: ____ (wing torsion), flutter, divergence → outboard ailerons ____ at high speed, inboard ailerons used.","Ailerons: roll + adverse yaw (induced drag of the up-going wing). High speed: aileron reversal (wing torsion), flutter, divergence → outboard ailerons locked out at high speed, inboard ailerons used."],["081.05.04.01:06","Roll: fill in the gaps → ____ ↑ with deflection, IAS, aileron size/arm; ↓ with span (damping), flexibility, inertia.","Roll rate ↑ with deflection, IAS, aileron size/arm; ↓ with span (damping), flexibility, inertia."],["081.05.04.05:01","Roll: fill in the gaps → Anti-adverse-yaw: ____, ____, ____ interconnect, roll spoilers (spoiler on the down-going wing gives pro-turn yaw).","Anti-adverse-yaw: Frise, differential, aileron–rudder interconnect, roll spoilers (spoiler on the down-going wing gives pro-turn yaw)."],["081.05.04.01:08","Roll: fill in the gaps → ____ = aileron + flap function; ____ = both ailerons lowered with flaps.","Flaperons = aileron + flap function; aileron droop = both ailerons lowered with flaps."],["081.05.05.01:01","Roll: fill in the gaps → Secondary effects: ____ into the lower wing (sideslip + fin); ____ in the same direction (outer wing faster + dihedral).","Secondary effects: roll → yaw into the lower wing (sideslip + fin); yaw → roll in the same direction (outer wing faster + dihedral)."],["081.05.06.01:04","Control forces – Balance tab: what are the key facts?","Moves: opposite to surface · Effect: reduces force"],["081.05.06.01:04","Control forces – Anti-balance tab: what are the key facts?","Moves: same direction · Effect: increases force/feel"],["081.05.06.01:04","Control forces – Servo tab: what are the key facts?","Moves: pilot moves tab only · Effect: tab flies the surface"],["081.05.06.01:04","Control forces – Spring tab: what are the key facts?","Moves: servo action above a set force · Effect: light forces at high speed"],["081.05.06.01:02","Control forces – Horn / internal balance: what are the key facts?","Moves: area ahead of hinge · Effect: reduces hinge moment"],["081.05.06.02:03","Control forces: fill in the gaps → Power-assisted: pilot + hydraulics share the load (natural feel). ____: irreversible → ____ (spring, q-feel) mandatory.","Power-assisted: pilot + hydraulics share the load (natural feel). Fully powered: irreversible → artificial feel (spring, q-feel) mandatory."],["081.05.07.01:04","Fly-by-wire: fill in the gaps → Normal pitch law commands ____ (C*), auto-trim, neutral speed stability in normal law. CG input → can fly with ____ (less trim drag). Degraded: ____ law (reduced protections) → direct law (stick = surface) → mechanical back-up. Hard protections cannot be overridden (α-max, load factor), soft ones can with extra force.","Normal pitch law commands load factor / pitch rate (C*), auto-trim, neutral speed stability in normal law. CG input → can fly with aft CG (less trim drag). Degraded: alternate law (reduced protections) → direct law (stick = surface) → mechanical back-up. Hard protections cannot be overridden (α-max, load factor), soft ones can with extra force."],["081.05.08.03:05","Trim: fill in the gaps → Trim → zero stick force in steady flight. ____ moves ____ to the elevator it trims. ____: large authority, elevator stays faired; runaway/jam is critical.","Trim → zero stick force in steady flight. Trim tab moves opposite to the elevator it trims. Stabiliser trim: large authority, elevator stays faired; runaway/jam is critical."],["081.05.08.03:06","Trim: fill in the gaps → Take-off trim from CG (green band). ____ → heavy pull, late rotation, longer TOD; ____ → early rotation, tail strike risk. ____ → higher approach speeds, reduced flap, careful go-around (thrust pitch-up).","Take-off trim from CG (green band). Too nose-down trim → heavy pull, late rotation, longer TOD; too nose-up → early rotation, tail strike risk. Jammed stabiliser → higher approach speeds, reduced flap, careful go-around (thrust pitch-up)."],["081.05.01.01:01","EASA LO 081.05.01.01.01 → Define: — lateral axis; — longitudinal axis; — normal axis.","Motion: Pitch · Control: Elevator / stabiliser · Stability about it: Longitudinal / Motion: Roll · Control: Ailerons, roll spoilers · Stability about it: Lateral"],["081.05.01.03:01","EASA LO 081.05.01.03.01 → Explain the influence of local α change by movement of a control surface.","Control surface down → camber ↑ and local α ↑ → CL ↑ of that surface (at the same aircraft α)."],["081.05.04.01:03","EASA LO 081.05.04.01.03 → Explain why some aeroplanes have inboard and outboard ailerons.","Ailerons: roll + adverse yaw (induced drag of the up-going wing). High speed: aileron reversal (wing torsion), flutter, divergence → outboard ailerons locked out at high speed, inboard ailerons used."],["081.05.04.01:07","EASA LO 081.05.04.01.07 → List the factors that affect roll rate.","Motion: Roll · Control: Ailerons, roll spoilers · Stability about it: Lateral"],["081.05.04.03:01","EASA LO 081.05.04.03.01 → Explain how spoilers can be used to control the rolling movement in combination with or instead of the ailerons.","Motion: Roll · Control: Ailerons, roll spoilers · Stability about it: Lateral"],["081.05.04.04:01","EASA LO 081.05.04.04.01 → Explain why the use of ailerons induces adverse yaw.","Anti-adverse-yaw: Frise, differential, aileron–rudder interconnect, roll spoilers (spoiler on the down-going wing gives pro-turn yaw). / Ailerons: roll + adverse yaw (induced drag of the up-going wing). High speed: aileron reversal (wing torsion), flutter, divergence → outboard ailerons locked out at high speed, inboard ailerons used."],["081.05.05.01:02","EASA LO 081.05.05.01.02 → Explain the secondary effect of yaw.","Secondary effects: roll → yaw into the lower wing (sideslip + fin); yaw → roll in the same direction (outer wing faster + dihedral). / Anti-adverse-yaw: Frise, differential, aileron–rudder interconnect, roll spoilers (spoiler on the down-going wing gives pro-turn yaw)."],["081.05.06.01:03","EASA LO 081.05.06.01.03 → Describe the working principle of the internal balance.","Moves: area ahead of hinge · Effect: reduces hinge moment / Moves: opposite to surface · Effect: reduces force"],["081.05.06.02:01","EASA LO 081.05.06.02.01 → State the differences between fully powered controls and power-assisted controls.","Power-assisted: pilot + hydraulics share the load (natural feel). Fully powered: irreversible → artificial feel (spring, q-feel) mandatory."],["081.05.06.02:02","EASA LO 081.05.06.02.02 → Describe power-assisted controls.","Power-assisted: pilot + hydraulics share the load (natural feel). Fully powered: irreversible → artificial feel (spring, q-feel) mandatory."],["081.05.08.03:01","EASA LO 081.05.08.03.01 → Describe the working principle of a stabiliser trim including the flight deck indications.","Trim → zero stick force in steady flight. Trim tab moves opposite to the elevator it trims. Stabiliser trim: large authority, elevator stays faired; runaway/jam is critical. / Motion: Pitch · Control: Elevator / stabiliser · Stability about it: Longitudinal"],["081.05.08.03:02","EASA LO 081.05.08.03.02 → Explain the advantages and disadvantages of a stabiliser trim compared to a trim tab.","Trim → zero stick force in steady flight. Trim tab moves opposite to the elevator it trims. Stabiliser trim: large authority, elevator stays faired; runaway/jam is critical."],["081.05.08.03:03","EASA LO 081.05.08.03.03 → Explain the relationship between CG position, take-off trim setting, and stabiliser trim position.","Take-off trim from CG (green band). Too nose-down trim → heavy pull, late rotation, longer TOD; too nose-up → early rotation, tail strike risk. Jammed stabiliser → higher approach speeds, reduced flap, careful go-around (thrust pitch-up)."],["081.05.08.03:04","EASA LO 081.05.08.03.04 → Explain the effect of errors in the take-off stabiliser trim setting on the rotation characteristics and stick force during take-off rotation.","Take-off trim from CG (green band). Too nose-down trim → heavy pull, late rotation, longer TOD; too nose-up → early rotation, tail strike risk. Jammed stabiliser → higher approach speeds, reduced flap, careful go-around (thrust pitch-up). / Trim → zero stick force in steady flight. Trim tab moves opposite to the elevator it trims. Stabiliser trim: large authority, elevator stays faired; runaway/jam is critical."]],"60":[["010.05.01.01:01","Annex 2, SERA & applicability: fill in the gaps → ____ (Rules of the Air): international standards; over ____. SERA (Standardised European Rules of the Air, ____) transposes Annex 2 (+ parts of Annexes 3, 10, 11) into EU law — common rules of the air, ATS procedures, VMC minima, airspace classes, signals.","ICAO Annex 2 (Rules of the Air): international standards; over the high seas Annex 2 applies without exception. SERA (Standardised European Rules of the Air, Reg. (EU) 923/2012) transposes Annex 2 (+ parts of Annexes 3, 10, 11) into EU law — common rules of the air, ATS procedures, VMC minima, airspace classes, signals."],["010.05.02.01:01","Annex 2, SERA & applicability: fill in the gaps → ____: over a State's territory, that State's rules apply (in EU airspace: SERA + national supplements); aircraft must comply wherever they are.","Territorial principle: over a State's territory, that State's rules apply (in EU airspace: SERA + national supplements); aircraft must comply wherever they are."],["010.05.02.01:06","Annex 2, SERA & applicability: fill in the gaps → ____ is responsible for the operation in accordance with the rules and has ____ as to the disposition of the aircraft. The PIC may ____ when absolutely necessary in the interest of safety.","PIC is responsible for the operation in accordance with the rules and has final authority as to the disposition of the aircraft. The PIC may depart from the rules when absolutely necessary in the interest of safety."],["010.05.03.01:17","Annex 2, SERA & applicability: fill in the gaps → ____: become familiar with all available information — current weather reports and forecasts, fuel requirements, alternative course of action if the flight cannot be completed, ATC delays.","Pre-flight (IFR): become familiar with all available information — current weather reports and forecasts, fuel requirements, alternative course of action if the flight cannot be completed, ATC delays."],["010.05.02.01:07","Annex 2, SERA & applicability: fill in the gaps → ____ (alcohol, opioids, cannabinoids, sedatives, cocaine, stimulants, hallucinogens, volatile solvents): no person shall act as crew when under their influence rendering them unable to perform safely; problematic use is prohibited.","Psychoactive substances (alcohol, opioids, cannabinoids, sedatives, cocaine, stimulants, hallucinogens, volatile solvents): no person shall act as crew when under their influence rendering them unable to perform safely; problematic use is prohibited."],["010.05.03.01:07","General rules & collision avoidance: fill in the gaps → ____: aircraft that has the right of way maintains heading and speed. ____ → both alter heading to the ____.","Right of way: aircraft that has the right of way maintains heading and speed. Head-on → both alter heading to the right."],["010.05.03.01:07","General rules & collision avoidance: fill in the gaps → ____ at the same level → the aircraft that has the other on its ____ gives way, except: ____ gives way to airships, gliders, balloons; airships give way to gliders & balloons; gliders to balloons; power-driven give way to aircraft towing.","Converging at the same level → the aircraft that has the other on its right gives way, except: power-driven heavier-than-air gives way to airships, gliders, balloons; airships give way to gliders & balloons; gliders to balloons; power-driven give way to aircraft towing."],["010.05.02.01:06","General rules & collision avoidance: fill in the gaps → ____: the overtaken aircraft has right of way; overtake by turning to the ____. ____: aircraft landing/on final have priority over aircraft in flight/on ground; lower aircraft has priority, but must not cut in; emergency aircraft have priority.","Overtaking: the overtaken aircraft has right of way; overtake by turning to the right. Landing: aircraft landing/on final have priority over aircraft in flight/on ground; lower aircraft has priority, but must not cut in; emergency aircraft have priority."],["010.05.03.01:01","General rules & collision avoidance: fill in the gaps → ____: anti-collision light (red/white flashing); navigation lights: red left and green right, each ____ from dead ahead; white tail light ____ (70° each side). Nav lights on from sunset to sunrise; anti-collision lights also when engines running.","Lights: anti-collision light (red/white flashing); navigation lights: red left and green right, each 110° from dead ahead; white tail light 140° (70° each side). Nav lights on from sunset to sunrise; anti-collision lights also when engines running."],["010.05.03.01:01","General rules & collision avoidance: fill in the gaps → ____ (examples): arms raised above head, palms inward = ____; arms moving up/back = ____; crossed arms above head = stop; right arm down, left moving = turn left (aircraft's left); slash across throat = cut engines; arms extended, palms down, moving up and down = slow down; chocks inserted = arms down, palms inward, moving in.","Marshalling signals (examples): arms raised above head, palms inward = this bay; arms moving up/back = move ahead; crossed arms above head = stop; right arm down, left moving = turn left (aircraft's left); slash across throat = cut engines; arms extended, palms down, moving up and down = slow down; chocks inserted = arms down, palms inward, moving in."],["010.05.03.01:04","General rules & collision avoidance: fill in the gaps → Minimum heights (VFR): over congested areas / open-air assemblies: ____ m; elsewhere ____ (SERA). IFR: at least ____ above the highest obstacle within 8 km of the estimated position.","Minimum heights (VFR): over congested areas / open-air assemblies: 1 000 ft above the highest obstacle within 600 m; elsewhere 500 ft above ground or water (SERA). IFR: at least 1 000 ft (2 000 ft in mountainous areas) above the highest obstacle within 8 km of the estimated position."],["010.05.03.01:06","General rules & collision avoidance: fill in the gaps → ____: expressed as flight levels at or above the transition level/altitude (or above the lowest usable FL); as altitudes below the transition altitude. Semicircular rule (SERA, magnetic track): ____ (IFR FL 70, 90… / VFR FL 75, 95…), ____ (IFR FL 80… / VFR FL 85…); RVSM FL 290–410 in 2 000 ft steps (odd: 290, 310…; even 300, 320…).","Cruising levels: expressed as flight levels at or above the transition level/altitude (or above the lowest usable FL); as altitudes below the transition altitude. Semicircular rule (SERA, magnetic track): 000–179° → odd (IFR FL 70, 90… / VFR FL 75, 95…), 180–359° → even (IFR FL 80… / VFR FL 85…); RVSM FL 290–410 in 2 000 ft steps (odd: 290, 310…; even 300, 320…)."],["010.05.03.01:07","General rules & collision avoidance: fill in the gaps → ____: do not fly so close to others to create collision hazard; formation only by pre-arrangement. ____ ____: stop and hold at lighted stop bars; proceed only when lights are switched off.","Proximity: do not fly so close to others to create collision hazard; formation only by pre-arrangement. Runway holding positions/stop bars: stop and hold at lighted stop bars; proceed only when lights are switched off."],["010.05.03.01:01","General rules & collision avoidance: fill in the gaps → ____ (tower → aircraft): ____: in flight cleared to land / on ground cleared for take-off; ____: give way and continue circling / stop; flashing green: return for landing / cleared to taxi; flashing red: aerodrome unsafe, do not land / taxi clear of landing area;","Light signals (tower → aircraft): steady green: in flight cleared to land / on ground cleared for take-off; steady red: give way and continue circling / stop; flashing green: return for landing / cleared to taxi; flashing red: aerodrome unsafe, do not land / taxi clear of landing area;"],["010.05.03.01:02","General rules & collision avoidance: fill in the gaps → ____: land at this aerodrome and proceed to apron / return to starting point; ____: do not land for the time being. Aircraft acknowledgements: rocking wings (by day), flashing landing lights/nav lights (night).","flashing white: land at this aerodrome and proceed to apron / return to starting point; red pyrotechnic: do not land for the time being. Aircraft acknowledgements: rocking wings (by day), flashing landing lights/nav lights (night)."],["010.05.03.01:09","General rules & collision avoidance: fill in the gaps → ____: fully functioning dual controls + a ____ in a control seat as observer.","Simulated instrument flight: fully functioning dual controls + a safety pilot in a control seat as observer."],["010.05.03.01:01","General rules & collision avoidance: fill in the gaps → ____: observe other traffic, conform with or avoid the pattern, ____ turns unless otherwise instructed, land and take off into wind.","Aerodrome traffic: observe other traffic, conform with or avoid the pattern, left-hand turns unless otherwise instructed, land and take off into wind."],["010.05.03.01:15","General rules & collision avoidance: fill in the gaps → ____ required for: flights provided with ATC service, flights across international borders, flights within advisory airspace or designated areas, flights at night leaving the vicinity of the aerodrome (as required).","Flight plan (FPL) required for: flights provided with ATC service, flights across international borders, flights within advisory airspace or designated areas, flights at night leaving the vicinity of the aerodrome (as required)."],["010.05.03.01:01","General rules & collision avoidance: fill in the gaps → Submit at least ____ departure (or 3 h in the NAT/IFPS for some); in flight: in time for ATS unit to receive ≥ 10 min before entering controlled airspace. Delay: amend/cancel if > ____ (controlled flights) or > ____ (uncontrolled).","Submit at least 60 min before departure (or 3 h in the NAT/IFPS for some); in flight: in time for ATS unit to receive ≥ 10 min before entering controlled airspace. Delay: amend/cancel if > 30 min (controlled flights) or > 60 min (uncontrolled)."],["010.05.03.01:13","General rules & collision avoidance: fill in the gaps → Inadvertent changes: off track → regain track asap; TAS varies by ____ → inform ATS; time estimate at the next reporting point in error by more than ____ (ICAO; SERA uses ____ — check the CAE book) → revised estimate.","Inadvertent changes: off track → regain track asap; TAS varies by ≥ 5 % → inform ATS; time estimate at the next reporting point in error by more than 2 min (ICAO; SERA uses 3 min — check the CAE book) → revised estimate."],["010.05.03.01:14","General rules & collision avoidance: fill in the gaps → ____: arrival report to ATS at the destination (normally automatic when landing at a controlled aerodrome); otherwise within 30 min of landing.","Closing the flight plan: arrival report to ATS at the destination (normally automatic when landing at a controlled aerodrome); otherwise within 30 min of landing."],["010.05.03.01:17","General rules & collision avoidance: fill in the gaps → ____ required for: flights in controlled airspace (class A–E for IFR, A–D for VFR), controlled aerodrome traffic. Request by submitting an FPL or on the frequency; if the clearance is not satisfactory, the PIC may ____ (and will be issued one if practicable).","ATC clearance required for: flights in controlled airspace (class A–E for IFR, A–D for VFR), controlled aerodrome traffic. Request by submitting an FPL or on the frequency; if the clearance is not satisfactory, the PIC may request an amended clearance (and will be issued one if practicable)."],["010.05.03.01:16","General rules & collision avoidance: fill in the gaps → ____ when VMC can no longer be maintained: request an amended clearance to continue in VMC to destination/alternative aerodrome or leave the controlled airspace, or request ____ (control zone), or (if qualified) request to operate IFR.","Controlled VFR when VMC can no longer be maintained: request an amended clearance to continue in VMC to destination/alternative aerodrome or leave the controlled airspace, or request special VFR (control zone), or (if qualified) request to operate IFR."],["010.05.03.01:19","General rules & collision avoidance: fill in the gaps → ____ at compulsory reporting points: ____ position, time, flight level/altitude, next position and time, ____.","Position reports at compulsory reporting points: identification, position, time, flight level/altitude, next position and time, ensuing significant point."],["010.05.03.01:01","General rules & collision avoidance: fill in the gaps → COM failure (IFR, SERA): squawk ____; in VMC continue in VMC and land at the nearest suitable aerodrome, report arrival; in IMC: maintain last assigned speed and level (or minimum flight altitude if higher) for ____ after failing to report/at the compulsory point (in radar environment), then adjust to FPL level and speed, proceed to the destination navigation aid, begin descent at or close to the EAT/ETA and land within ____ after.","COM failure (IFR, SERA): squawk 7600; in VMC continue in VMC and land at the nearest suitable aerodrome, report arrival; in IMC: maintain last assigned speed and level (or minimum flight altitude if higher) for 7 min after failing to report/at the compulsory point (in radar environment), then adjust to FPL level and speed, proceed to the destination navigation aid, begin descent at or close to the EAT/ETA and land within 30 min after."],["010.05.03.01:21","General rules & collision avoidance: fill in the gaps → ____: squawk ____, notify ATS of the circumstances, deviations, and intentions.","Unlawful interference: squawk 7500, notify ATS of the circumstances, deviations, and intentions."],["010.05.04.01:01","VFR & IFR: fill in the gaps → VMC minima (SERA) in classes B–G above FL 100: ____ vis, ____ ____ from cloud; below FL 100 (and above 3 000 ft AMSL or 1 000 ft above terrain): 5 km, 1 500 m / 1 000 ft; at or below 3 000 ft AMSL/1 000 ft AGL in class F/G: 5 km, clear of cloud and in sight of the surface (reduced to 1 500 m for slow flights).","VMC minima (SERA) in classes B–G above FL 100: 8 km vis, 1 500 m horizontally / 1 000 ft vertically from cloud; below FL 100 (and above 3 000 ft AMSL or 1 000 ft above terrain): 5 km, 1 500 m / 1 000 ft; at or below 3 000 ft AMSL/1 000 ft AGL in class F/G: 5 km, clear of cloud and in sight of the surface (reduced to 1 500 m for slow flights)."],["010.05.04.01:01","VFR & IFR: fill in the gaps → Class A: no VFR. VFR not above ____ (normally) and not at transonic/supersonic speed. VFR take-off/landing at a control zone aerodrome needs ____ and ____.","Class A: no VFR. VFR not above FL 195 (normally) and not at transonic/supersonic speed. VFR take-off/landing at a control zone aerodrome needs ceiling ≥ 1 500 ft and ground visibility ≥ 5 km."],["010.05.04.01:01","VFR & IFR: fill in the gaps → ____: in CTR only, visibility ≥ 1 500 m (helicopters 800 m), ceiling ≥ 600 ft, speed ≤ 140 kt, clear of cloud, in sight of the surface.","Special VFR: in CTR only, visibility ≥ 1 500 m (helicopters 800 m), ceiling ≥ 600 ft, speed ≤ 140 kt, clear of cloud, in sight of the surface."],["010.05.03.01:05","VFR & IFR: fill in the gaps → ____: minimum levels (1 000 / 2 000 ft rule within 8 km), change from IFR to VFR only by pilot's statement \"cancelling my IFR flight\"; IFR in controlled airspace needs clearance; outside controlled airspace follow cruising level rules and position reports.","IFR: minimum levels (1 000 / 2 000 ft rule within 8 km), change from IFR to VFR only by pilot's statement \"cancelling my IFR flight\"; IFR in controlled airspace needs clearance; outside controlled airspace follow cruising level rules and position reports."],["010.05.06.01:04","Interception: fill in the gaps → Occurs when an aircraft is identified as unknown/suspected, lost, not complying, entering restricted areas, or for security. Intercepted aircraft ____ of the interceptor (visual signals), notify ATS, try to establish contact on ____ (or 243.0), squawk ____ (with Mode C) unless otherwise instructed.","Occurs when an aircraft is identified as unknown/suspected, lost, not complying, entering restricted areas, or for security. Intercepted aircraft immediately follows the instructions of the interceptor (visual signals), notify ATS, try to establish contact on 121.5 MHz (or 243.0), squawk Mode A 7700 (with Mode C) unless otherwise instructed."],["010.05.06.01:01","Interception: fill in the gaps → Signals: interceptor ____ (in front, slightly above, to the left) → \"____\" — intercepted aircraft rocks wings and follows. Interceptor makes an abrupt break-away climbing turn → \"____\". Interceptor lowers gear and overflies the runway → \"land at this aerodrome\".","Signals: interceptor rocks wings (in front, slightly above, to the left) → \"you have been intercepted, follow me\" — intercepted aircraft rocks wings and follows. Interceptor makes an abrupt break-away climbing turn → \"you may proceed\". Interceptor lowers gear and overflies the runway → \"land at this aerodrome\"."],["010.05.06.01:05","Interception: what do you remember about this? → Phrases: CALL SIGN, FOLLOW, DESCEND, YOU LAND, PROCEED; intercepted: …","Phrases: CALL SIGN, FOLLOW, DESCEND, YOU LAND, PROCEED; intercepted: WILCO, CAN NOT, REPEAT, AM LOST, MAYDAY, HIJACK."],["010.05.06.01:01","Exam trap (Interception): complete the fact → nav lights ____° each side, tail ____°.","nav lights 110° each side, tail 140°."],["010.05.03.01:04","Exam trap (Interception): complete the fact → Congested areas: ____ within ____.","Congested areas: 1 000 ft within 600 m."],["010.05.03.01:20","Exam trap (Interception): complete the fact → COM failure IMC: ____ then FPL.","COM failure IMC: 7 min then FPL."],["010.05.06.01:02","Exam trap (Interception): complete the fact → Intercepted: ____MHz and ____","Intercepted: 121.5 MHz and 7700."],["010.05.02.01:02","EASA LO 010.05.02.01.02 → Explain the necessity to comply with the Rules of the Air.","Territorial principle: over a State's territory, that State's rules apply (in EU airspace: SERA + national supplements); aircraft must comply wherever they are."],["010.05.02.01:03","EASA LO 010.05.02.01.03 → State the responsibilities of the PIC.","PIC is responsible for the operation in accordance with the rules and has final authority as to the disposition of the aircraft. The PIC may depart from the rules when absolutely necessary in the interest of safety."],["010.05.03.01:10","EASA LO 010.05.03.01.10 → Explain the basic rules for an aircraft operating on and in the vicinity of an aerodrome (AD).","Flight plan (FPL) required for: flights provided with ATC service, flights across international borders, flights within advisory airspace or designated areas, flights at night leaving the vicinity of the aerodrome (as required)."],["010.05.03.01:11","EASA LO 010.05.03.01.11 → Explain the requirements for the submission of an air traffic service (ATS) flight plan.","Flight plan (FPL) required for: flights provided with ATC service, flights across international borders, flights within advisory airspace or designated areas, flights at night leaving the vicinity of the aerodrome (as required)."],["010.05.03.01:18","EASA LO 010.05.03.01.18 → Describe the required actions to be carried out if the continuation of a controlled visual flight rule (VFR) flight in visual meteorological conditions (VMC) is not practicable any more.","ATC clearance required for: flights in controlled airspace (class A–E for IFR, A–D for VFR), controlled aerodrome traffic. Request by submitting an FPL or on the frequency; if the clearance is not satisfactory, the PIC may request an amended clearance (and will be issued one if practicable)."]],"61":[["032.01.00.00",": what do you remember about this? → FINAL review of 032 Performance — cheat sheet of the most examined …","FINAL review of 032 Performance — cheat sheet of the most examined facts."],["032.01.00.00","General: fill in the gaps → Classes: ____ (all jets, turboprops > 9 pax or > 5 700 kg), ____ (props ≤ 9 pax and ≤ 5 700 kg), ____ (large pistons). Gross → net by safety factors.","Classes: A (all jets, turboprops > 9 pax or > 5 700 kg), B (props ≤ 9 pax and ≤ 5 700 kg), C (large pistons). Gross → net by safety factors."],["032.01.00.00","General: fill in the gaps → ____: jet ≈ ________, prop VMD. Max endurance: jet VMD, prop VMP. Headwind → fly faster for range. Jet optimum altitude rises as mass falls.","Max range: jet ≈ 1.32 VMD, prop VMD. Max endurance: jet VMD, prop VMP. Headwind → fly faster for range. Jet optimum altitude rises as mass falls."],["032.01.00.00","General: fill in the gaps → ________ = max excess thrust (angle), ____Y = max excess power (rate). Gradient = (T − D)/W; ROC = TAS × gradient. Weight ↑, altitude/temperature ↑ → climb ↓.","VX = max excess thrust (angle), VY = max excess power (rate). Gradient = (T − D)/W; ROC = TAS × gradient. Weight ↑, altitude/temperature ↑ → climb ↓."],["032.01.00.00","General: what do you remember about this? → Glide: gradient = 1/(L/D); mass doesn't change the distance at …","Glide: gradient = 1/(L/D); mass doesn't change the distance at VMD."],["032.02.00.00","Class B: fill in the gaps → Screen height ____ (TO & landing). Wind: ____ ____.","Screen height 50 ft (TO & landing). Wind: 50 % headwind / 150 % tailwind."],["032.02.00.00","Class B: fill in the gaps → Take-off: no stopway/clearway: TOD × ____ ≤ TORA; with: TOD ≤ TORA, × ____ ≤ TODA, × ____ ≤ ASDA. Dry grass × 1.2, wet grass × 1.3; upslope +5 %/1 %.","Take-off: no stopway/clearway: TOD × 1.25 ≤ TORA; with: TOD ≤ TORA, × 1.15 ≤ TODA, × 1.3 ≤ ASDA. Dry grass × 1.2, wet grass × 1.3; upslope +5 %/1 %."],["032.02.00.00","Class B: fill in the gaps → Landing: LD ≤ ____ LDA (× 1.43), wet × ____, grass × 1.15, downslope ____.","Landing: LD ≤ 70 % LDA (× 1.43), wet × 1.15, grass × 1.15, downslope +5 %/1 %."],["032.02.00.00","Class B: fill in the gaps → MEP take-off domain half-width ____, max 300/600 m; obstacle margin ____. SEP net glide gradient = gross ____.","MEP take-off domain half-width 90 m + 0.125 D, max 300/600 m; obstacle margin 50 ft. SEP net glide gradient = gross + 0.5 %."],["032.02.00.00","Class B: fill in the gaps → VREF ≥ ________; MEP VR ≥ 1.05 VMC/1.1 VS1, V50 ≥ 1.1 VMC/1.2 VS1.","VREF ≥ 1.3 VS0; MEP VR ≥ 1.05 VMC/1.1 VS1, V50 ≥ 1.1 VMC/1.2 VS1."],["032.04.00.00","Class A theory: fill in the gaps → Speeds: VEF (engine failure assumed) ≥ VMCG; V____ (decision/action speed; VMCG ≤ V1 ≤ VR and ≤ VMBE); VR ≥ 1.05 VMCA, ≥ V1; VLOF; V____ ≥ ____SR (1.08 for 4-engine/FBW), ≥ 1.1 VMCA; VREF ≥ 1.23 VSR0; VMU (min unstick) → VLOF ≥ 1.1 VMU (AEO) / 1.05 (OEI).","Speeds: VEF (engine failure assumed) ≥ VMCG; V1 (decision/action speed; VMCG ≤ V1 ≤ VR and ≤ VMBE); VR ≥ 1.05 VMCA, ≥ V1; VLOF; V2 ≥ 1.13 VSR (1.08 for 4-engine/FBW), ≥ 1.1 VMCA; VREF ≥ 1.23 VSR0; VMU (min unstick) → VLOF ≥ 1.1 VMU (AEO) / 1.05 (OEI)."],["032.04.00.00","Class A theory: fill in the gaps → Take-off distances (dry): TOD = greater of OEI distance to ____ and ____ distance to 35 ft; TOR similar (to midpoint between VLOF and 35 ft; clearway ≤ ½ of the airborne part); ASD = greater of AEO and OEI accelerate-stop (+ 2 s at V1). Wet: screen height ____, reverse thrust credit on ASD.","Take-off distances (dry): TOD = greater of OEI distance to 35 ft and 1.15 × AEO distance to 35 ft; TOR similar (to midpoint between VLOF and 35 ft; clearway ≤ ½ of the airborne part); ASD = greater of AEO and OEI accelerate-stop (+ 2 s at V1). Wet: screen height 15 ft, reverse thrust credit on ASD."],["032.04.00.00","Class A theory: fill in the gaps → ____: TOD = ASD → balanced V____. More stopway → higher V1; more clearway → lower V1. Wet/contaminated → lower V1. V____ must not exceed VMBE (brake energy) and must be ≥ VMCG.","Balanced field: TOD = ASD → balanced V1. More stopway → higher V1; more clearway → lower V1. Wet/contaminated → lower V1. V1 must not exceed VMBE (brake energy) and must be ≥ VMCG."],["032.04.00.00","Class A theory: fill in the gaps → Climb segments (OEI, gross gradients, twin/tri/quad): ____ (gear down, VLOF→gear up): > ____ / 0.3 % / 0.5 %; ____ (gear up, V2, T/O flap, to ≥ 400 ft): 2.4 % / 2.7 % / 3.0 % — usually most limiting; 3rd acceleration & flap retraction; final (clean, ≥ 1.18 VSR, to 1 500 ft): 1.2 % / 1.5 % / 1.7 %.","Climb segments (OEI, gross gradients, twin/tri/quad): 1st (gear down, VLOF→gear up): > 0 % / 0.3 % / 0.5 %; 2nd (gear up, V2, T/O flap, to ≥ 400 ft): 2.4 % / 2.7 % / 3.0 % — usually most limiting; 3rd acceleration & flap retraction; final (clean, ≥ 1.18 VSR, to 1 500 ft): 1.2 % / 1.5 % / 1.7 %."],["032.04.00.00","Class A theory: fill in the gaps → Net take-off flight path = gross − ____ (twin), 0.9 % (3), 1.0 % (4); obstacle clearance ____; domain half-width ____ D (or 60 m + ½ wingspan + 0.125 D if span < 60 m), max 300/600 m (≤ 15° track change) or 600/900 m. Bank ≤ 15° below 400 ft (unless approved).","Net take-off flight path = gross − 0.8 % (twin), 0.9 % (3), 1.0 % (4); obstacle clearance 35 ft; domain half-width 90 m + 0.125 D (or 60 m + ½ wingspan + 0.125 D if span < 60 m), max 300/600 m (≤ 15° track change) or 600/900 m. Bank ≤ 15° below 400 ft (unless approved)."],["032.04.00.00","Class A theory: fill in the gaps → En route OEI: net flight path clears obstacles by ____ (level) / ____ (drift-down) within ____ (EU: 9.3 km/5 NM) either side; net = gross − 1.1 % (twin), 1.4 % (3), 1.6 % (4). 90 min rule for 3/4-engine aeroplanes, 2 engines inoperative. ETOPS/60-min rule for twins (OEI cruise speed, still air).","En route OEI: net flight path clears obstacles by 1 000 ft (level) / 2 000 ft (drift-down) within 5 NM (EU: 9.3 km/5 NM) either side; net = gross − 1.1 % (twin), 1.4 % (3), 1.6 % (4). 90 min rule for 3/4-engine aeroplanes, 2 engines inoperative. ETOPS/60-min rule for twins (OEI cruise speed, still air)."],["032.04.00.00","Class A theory: fill in the gaps → Landing: dry LD ≤ ____ of LDA (jets, × 1.67), turboprops ____; wet × ____; contaminated × 1.15 or contaminated distance, whichever greater. Approach climb (OEI, approach flap, gear up): 2.1 % (twin); landing climb (AEO, landing config): 3.2 %.","Landing: dry LD ≤ 60 % of LDA (jets, × 1.67), turboprops 70 %; wet × 1.15; contaminated × 1.15 or contaminated distance, whichever greater. Approach climb (OEI, approach flap, gear up): 2.1 % (twin); landing climb (AEO, landing config): 3.2 %."],["032.04.00.00","Class A theory: fill in the gaps → ____ ____ masses → ____ = performance-limited TOM. Improved climb: higher V2 on a long runway increases the climb-limited mass. Reduced (assumed temperature/flex) thrust: not on contaminated runways, max 25 % reduction; derate = a lower certified rating (VMC lower).","Field-length-limited, climb-limited (WAT), obstacle-limited, tyre-speed, brake-energy, en-route, landing-limited masses → lowest = performance-limited TOM. Improved climb: higher V2 on a long runway increases the climb-limited mass. Reduced (assumed temperature/flex) thrust: not on contaminated runways, max 25 % reduction; derate = a lower certified rating (VMC lower)."],["032.04.00.00","Class A theory: fill in the gaps → ____: use AFM contaminated data; dynamic hydroplaning: spin-down (rotating tyre) VP ≈ ____, spin-up (non-rotating tyre at touchdown) ≈ ____ (p in psi, kt) — the spin-up value is the relevant one for touchdown.","Contaminated runway: use AFM contaminated data; dynamic hydroplaning: spin-down (rotating tyre) VP ≈ 9 √p, spin-up (non-rotating tyre at touchdown) ≈ 7.7 √p (p in psi, kt) — the spin-up value is the relevant one for touchdown."],["032.05.00.00","Class A use of data (CAP 698 MRJT): fill in the gaps → ____, climb-limited TOM (WAT), obstacle-limited (net gradient needed = (obstacle height − 35 ft)/distance + margin), V____/VR/V____ tables with corrections for slope, wind, V1/VR ratio; quick turnaround / brake-energy limits (cooling time chart); landing distance and landing climb- limited mass; en-route drift-down net profiles.","Field-length-limited TOM chart, climb-limited TOM (WAT), obstacle-limited (net gradient needed = (obstacle height − 35 ft)/distance + margin), V1/VR/V2 tables with corrections for slope, wind, V1/VR ratio; quick turnaround / brake-energy limits (cooling time chart); landing distance and landing climb- limited mass; en-route drift-down net profiles."],["032.05.00.00","Exam trap (Class A use of data (CAP 698 MRJT)): complete the fact → Class A dry TOD uses ____ and ____× AEO; Class B 50 ft and × 1.25.","Class A dry TOD uses 35 ft and 1.15 × AEO; Class B 50 ft and × 1.25."],["032.05.00.00","Exam trap (Class A use of data (CAP 698 MRJT)): complete the fact → ____nd segment is usually the limiting one.","2nd segment is usually the limiting one."],["032.05.00.00","Exam trap (Class A use of data (CAP 698 MRJT)): complete the fact → Jet landing: ____ of LDA.","Jet landing: 60 % of LDA."]],"62":[["061.05.02.01:01","Standard time & daylight saving time: fill in the gaps → ____: the legal time adopted by a country/zone, usually a whole-hour offset from UTC (some half-hours, e.g. India UTC+5:30). Theoretical zones are 15° wide, centred on multiples of 15° longitude, but real boundaries follow borders.","Standard time (ST): the legal time adopted by a country/zone, usually a whole-hour offset from UTC (some half-hours, e.g. India UTC+5:30). Theoretical zones are 15° wide, centred on multiples of 15° longitude, but real boundaries follow borders."],["061.05.02.01:01","Standard time & daylight saving time: fill in the gaps → ____: ST advanced (usually +1 h) in summer. Central Europe: CET = UTC+1, CEST = UTC+2.","Daylight saving time (DST, summer time): ST advanced (usually +1 h) in summer. Central Europe: CET = UTC+1, CEST = UTC+2."],["061.05.03.01:01","Standard time & daylight saving time: fill in the gaps → Source: ____ \"Standard times\" tables list the correction to UTC for each country (sign convention: ____ ____ for East, − for West).","Source: Air Almanac / AIP GEN 2.1 \"Standard times\" tables list the correction to UTC for each country (sign convention: ST = UTC + correction for East, − for West)."],["061.05.02.01:01","Standard time & daylight saving time: fill in the gaps → Conversions always go ____: ST(A) → UTC → ST(B).","Conversions always go via UTC: ST(A) → UTC → ST(B)."],["061.05.02.01:01","Standard time & daylight saving time: fill in the gaps → Example: depart Zurich 10:00 CEST (UTC+2) = ____; flight time 9 h → arrival ____; New York EDT (UTC−4) → ____ local.","Example: depart Zurich 10:00 CEST (UTC+2) = 08:00 UTC; flight time 9 h → arrival 17:00 UTC; New York EDT (UTC−4) → 13:00 local."],["061.05.02.01:01","Standard time & daylight saving time: what do you remember about this? → Remember: standard time differs from LMT by the difference between …","Remember: standard time differs from LMT by the difference between the zone meridian and the actual longitude (1° = 4 min)."],["061.05.02.02:01","International Date Line: fill in the gaps → Roughly along ____ longitude (deviates around island groups). Crossing ____ (from America towards Asia) → ____ day (date advances); crossing eastbound → subtract one day. The time of day (zone time) is continuous; only the date changes.","Roughly along 180° longitude (deviates around island groups). Crossing westbound (from America towards Asia) → add one day (date advances); crossing eastbound → subtract one day. The time of day (zone time) is continuous; only the date changes."],["061.05.02.02:01","International Date Line: what do you remember about this? → LMT at 180°E and 180°W is the same time but a different date: 180°E …","LMT at 180°E and 180°W is the same time but a different date: 180°E is 12 h ahead of UTC, 180°W 12 h behind → 24 h date difference."],["061.05.03.01:01","Sunrise, sunset, civil twilight: fill in the gaps → ____: time when the ____ (upper limb) of the sun is on the observer's visible horizon (sun's centre ≈ ____ below the horizon, due to refraction + semi-diameter).","Sunrise/sunset: time when the upper edge (upper limb) of the sun is on the observer's visible horizon (sun's centre ≈ 0°50′ below the horizon, due to refraction + semi-diameter)."],["061.05.03.01:01","Sunrise, sunset, civil twilight: fill in the gaps → ____: period between sunset and when the sun's centre is ____ (evening), or from 6° below until sunrise (morning). Nautical twilight 12°, astronomical 18°. Aviation \"night\" in EU: from ____ morning civil twilight.","Civil twilight: period between sunset and when the sun's centre is 6° below the horizon (evening), or from 6° below until sunrise (morning). Nautical twilight 12°, astronomical 18°. Aviation \"night\" in EU: from end of evening civil twilight to beginning of morning civil twilight."],["061.05.03.01:02","Sunrise, sunset, civil twilight: fill in the gaps → ____ give ____ of sunrise/sunset/twilight for a given ____ (at sea level). Procedure: interpolate for latitude (and date) → LMT → convert to UTC with longitude (arc to time) → to standard time if required.","Almanac tables give LMT of sunrise/sunset/twilight for a given latitude and date (at sea level). Procedure: interpolate for latitude (and date) → LMT → convert to UTC with longitude (arc to time) → to standard time if required."],["061.05.03.01:01","Sunrise, sunset, civil twilight: fill in the gaps → Example: sunset at 47°N on a date = 19:20 LMT; position 008°E → 32 min → ____ → 20:48 CEST.","Example: sunset at 47°N on a date = 19:20 LMT; position 008°E → 32 min → 18:48 UTC → 20:48 CEST."],["061.05.03.01:01","Sunrise, sunset, civil twilight: fill in the gaps → ____: in the NH summer, days longer (sunrise earlier, sunset later); at the ____ (~21 Mar, ~23 Sep) day ≈ night ≈ 12 h everywhere.","Date: in the NH summer, days longer (sunrise earlier, sunset later); at the equinoxes (~21 Mar, ~23 Sep) day ≈ night ≈ 12 h everywhere."],["061.05.03.01:03","Sunrise, sunset, civil twilight: fill in the gaps → ____: in summer, higher latitude → longer day (____ ____ above the Arctic circle); in winter, shorter (polar night). At the equator day length ≈ 12 h all year, twilight is shortest (sun rises/sets vertically); twilight duration increases with latitude (sun rises at a shallow angle).","Latitude: in summer, higher latitude → longer day (midnight sun above the Arctic circle); in winter, shorter (polar night). At the equator day length ≈ 12 h all year, twilight is shortest (sun rises/sets vertically); twilight duration increases with latitude (sun rises at a shallow angle)."],["061.05.03.01:02","Sunrise, sunset, civil twilight: fill in the gaps → ____: higher observer sees a lower (dip) horizon → sunrise ____, sunset ____, twilight at altitude shorter/sun visible longer; on the ground in a valley the opposite (terrain).","Altitude: higher observer sees a lower (dip) horizon → sunrise earlier, sunset later, twilight at altitude shorter/sun visible longer; on the ground in a valley the opposite (terrain)."],["061.05.03.01:01","Sunrise, sunset, civil twilight: fill in the gaps → ____ is ____ ____ (March/September) and smallest near the solstices (June/December).","Rate of change of day length is greatest around the equinoxes (March/September) and smallest near the solstices (June/December)."],["061.05.02.02:01","Exam trap (Sunrise, sunset, civil twilight): complete the fact → Westbound across the date line → +____day.","Westbound across the date line → +1 day."]],"63":[["033.01.00.00",": what do you remember about this? → Review stage (FPM ch. 3, 4, 5, 11, GSPRM, CAP 697) — flight & fuel …","Review stage (FPM ch. 3, 4, 5, 11, GSPRM, CAP 697) — flight & fuel planning cheat sheet."],["033.01.00.00","VFR planning: fill in the gaps → Charts: ____ VFR charts / GSPRM VFR charts — tracks (true, measured at the mid-meridian), distances, MEF/obstacles, airspace, frequencies. ____: MT = TT − East variation / + West (\"East is least, West is best\"). Heading from TOV (wind), GS, leg times.","Charts: ICAO 1:500 000 VFR charts / GSPRM VFR charts — tracks (true, measured at the mid-meridian), distances, MEF/obstacles, airspace, frequencies. True → magnetic: MT = TT − East variation / + West (\"East is least, West is best\"). Heading from TOV (wind), GS, leg times."],["033.01.00.00","VFR planning: what do you remember about this? → Minimum altitudes: 500 ft AGL / 1 000 ft within 600 m over congested …","Minimum altitudes: 500 ft AGL / 1 000 ft within 600 m over congested areas. Semicircular VFR levels (odd + 500 eastbound 000–179° magnetic track)."],["033.01.00.00","VFR planning: fill in the gaps → Fuel VFR (NCO, aeroplanes): day ____, night ____ reserve at normal cruise after landing.","Fuel VFR (NCO, aeroplanes): day 30 min, night 45 min reserve at normal cruise after landing."],["033.02.00.00","IFR planning: fill in the gaps → Routes/airways from ____: MEA, MOCA/grid MORA, MRA; tracks and distances, SIDs/STARs, airway designators (upper/lower), CDR/conditional routes, FL availability. Choose levels (semicircular IFR: odd 000–179°, even 180–359°), ____ check (ISA dev: 4 ft/°C/1 000 ft).","Routes/airways from en-route charts (GSPRM IFR): MEA, MOCA/grid MORA, MRA; tracks and distances, SIDs/STARs, airway designators (upper/lower), CDR/conditional routes, FL availability. Choose levels (semicircular IFR: odd 000–179°, even 180–359°), true altitude check (ISA dev: 4 ft/°C/1 000 ft)."],["033.02.00.00","IFR planning: fill in the gaps → ____: for each leg — track (true/magnetic), distance, level, TAS, wind, drift, heading, GS, time, fuel; totals; ETA; alternate leg.","Navigation plan: for each leg — track (true/magnetic), distance, level, TAS, wind, drift, heading, GS, time, fuel; totals; ETA; alternate leg."],["033.03.00.00","Fuel planning: fill in the gaps → Components: ____ 1 500 ft), alternate, final reserve (30 min jet/45 min piston ____. Special procedures: ____ (contingency only on DP→dest; take larger of two lines), isolated aerodrome (2 h at cruise incl. final reserve, PNR), predetermined point, tankering (burn-off penalty ~3–4 %/h).","Components: taxi, trip, contingency (5 % or 5 min holding at 1 500 ft), alternate, final reserve (30 min jet/45 min piston at 1 500 ft), additional, extra. Special procedures: RCF/decision point (contingency only on DP→dest; take larger of two lines), isolated aerodrome (2 h at cruise incl. final reserve, PNR), predetermined point, tankering (burn-off penalty ~3–4 %/h)."],["033.03.00.00","Fuel planning: fill in the gaps → ____. Mass ↔ volume: kg = l × SG.","NAM = NGM × TAS/GS. Mass ↔ volume: kg = l × SG."],["033.03.00.00","Fuel planning: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ chart (trip fuel and time vs ground distance, wind, level, landing mass), ____ planning chart, holding table (final reserve), integrated range tables (NAM from mass difference), step climb. SEP/MEP: climb/cruise/descent tables (fuel, time, distance).","CAP 697 MRJT: simplified flight planning chart (trip fuel and time vs ground distance, wind, level, landing mass), alternate planning chart, holding table (final reserve), integrated range tables (NAM from mass difference), step climb. SEP/MEP: climb/cruise/descent tables (fuel, time, distance)."],["033.04.00.00","Pre-flight preparation: fill in the gaps → ____: facilities/services, departure/destination/alternate aerodromes, airway routings & airspace (restricted/danger areas).","NOTAM briefing: facilities/services, departure/destination/alternate aerodromes, airway routings & airspace (restricted/danger areas)."],["033.04.00.00","Pre-flight preparation: fill in the gaps → ____ → update nav plan and fuel (033-06/07): planning minima for alternates (NPA +1 000 m/+200 ft, CAT I → NPA, CAT II/III → CAT I), ETA ± 1 h window, TAF change-group rules (TEMPO persistent phenomena count).","MET briefing → update nav plan and fuel (033-06/07): planning minima for alternates (NPA +1 000 m/+200 ft, CAT I → NPA, CAT II/III → CAT I), ETA ± 1 h window, TAF change-group rules (TEMPO persistent phenomena count)."],["033.04.00.00","Pre-flight preparation: fill in the gaps → ____: point from which it takes the same time to continue or return. ____ where D = total distance, O = GS out (continuing), H = GS home (returning) — with the relevant speeds (e.g. OEI TAS). A headwind on the outbound leg moves the PET ____ — i.e. the PET always moves into the wind.","PET (point of equal time): point from which it takes the same time to continue or return. Distance to PET = D × H/(O + H) where D = total distance, O = GS out (continuing), H = GS home (returning) — with the relevant speeds (e.g. OEI TAS). A headwind on the outbound leg moves the PET towards the destination — i.e. the PET always moves into the wind."],["033.04.00.00","Pre-flight preparation: fill in the gaps → ____: furthest point from which the aircraft can return with the available (safe endurance) fuel. ____ ____ (E = safe endurance in hours); distance = time × O. Wind always reduces the PSR distance (any wind).","PSR (point of safe return): furthest point from which the aircraft can return with the available (safe endurance) fuel. Time to PSR = E × H/(O + H) (E = safe endurance in hours); distance = time × O. Wind always reduces the PSR distance (any wind)."],["033.02.00.00","ATS flight plan: what do you remember about this? → Item 7 ident, 8 rules/type (I, V, Y = IFR then VFR, Z = VFR then IFR; …","Item 7 ident, 8 rules/type (I, V, Y = IFR then VFR, Z = VFR then IFR; S/N/G/M/X), 9 type & wake category (L/M/H/J), 10 equipment (S = standard VHF/VOR/ILS; G GNSS;"],["033.05.00.00","ATS flight plan: what do you remember about this? → R PBN approved + PBN/ in item 18; transponder codes C, S, E…), 13 …","R PBN approved + PBN/ in item 18; transponder codes C, S, E…), 13 departure + EOBT, 15 speed (N0450), level (F350), route (DCT, airways), 16 destination + total EET + alternates, 18 other info (PBN/, DOF/, REG/, RMK/…), 19 supplementary (endurance, POB, emergency equipment — not transmitted)."],["033.05.00.00","ATS flight plan: fill in the gaps → File ≥ ____ before EOBT (IFPS: 3 h for NAT), delay > 15 min (IFPS)/30 min → DLA/CHG message.","File ≥ 60 min before EOBT (IFPS: 3 h for NAT), delay > 15 min (IFPS)/30 min → DLA/CHG message."],["033.06.00.00","Flight monitoring & re-planning: fill in the gaps → In-flight ____ at each waypoint: actual vs planned fuel/time; revise ETA and fuel on arrival; if the fuel at destination drops below required reserve → re-plan (lower speed/level, closer alternate, divert). ____ / ____ declarations. Point of no return for isolated destinations.","In-flight fuel checks at each waypoint: actual vs planned fuel/time; revise ETA and fuel on arrival; if the fuel at destination drops below required reserve → re-plan (lower speed/level, closer alternate, divert). Minimum fuel / MAYDAY FUEL declarations. Point of no return for isolated destinations."],["033.06.00.00","Flight monitoring & re-planning: fill in the gaps → ____ (e.g. RCF decision point, re-clearance): recompute trip fuel to new destination + reserves from current position.","In-flight re-planning (e.g. RCF decision point, re-clearance): recompute trip fuel to new destination + reserves from current position."],["033.06.00.00","Exam trap (Flight monitoring & re-planning): complete the fact → Item ____is not transmitted.","Item 19 is not transmitted."]],"64":[["021.08.01.01:01","AVGAS 100LL vs AVGAS 100: colours, flash point, density.","100LL = blue, 100 = green. Both very volatile (flash point ≈ −40 °C), freezing point ≈ −58 °C, density ≈ 0.72 kg/l, spark-ignition piston engines."],["021.08.01.01:01","Diesel and JET A-1 in piston engines: key values.","Diesel: compression-ignition piston engines, flash point > +55 °C, waxing (cloud point −10…−20 °C), density ≈ 0.84. JET A-1: for diesel/high-compression piston engines certified for it, flash point ≥ +38 °C, freezing ≤ −47 °C, density ≈ 0.80."],["021.08.01.01:01","What must never be mixed in piston engines?","Never AVGAS into diesel engines or JET fuel into AVGAS engines: detonation, possible engine destruction. Use approved grades only (AFM). Diesel waxes at low temperature."],["021.08.01.02:01","Tasks of a piston-aircraft fuel system.","Store fuel and deliver it clean, in the required quantity and pressure, at all attitudes and flight conditions; allow refuelling, venting, draining, indication; balance CG/tanks."],["021.08.01.02:03","Gravity feed vs pressure feed.","Gravity feed: high-wing, tanks above the engine, fuel flows by gravity (simple). Pressure feed: low-wing, needs pumps (engine-driven + electric boost pump)."],["021.08.01.02:02","Name the main components of a piston fuel system and what the boost pump is for.","Lines, boost (electric) pump, pressure valves, filter/strainer (gascolator at lowest point), tanks, vent system, sump, drain valves, quantity sensor (float or capacitance), temperature sensor. Boost pump: start, take-off/landing, engine-driven pump failure, vapour lock."],["021.08.01.02:04","Fuel tank types: advantages and disadvantages.","Drum/rigid tank: simple but heavy, wasted space. Bladder/flexible tank: fits shapes, but can wrinkle, trap water, deteriorate. Integral tank (sealed wing): lightest, maximum volume, but leaks are hard to repair and sealing ages."],["021.08.01.02:02","What is the purpose of the vent system, the sump and the drain valves?","Vent: keeps the tank at ambient pressure (no collapse or over-pressure). Sump: lowest point, collects water and sediment. Drain valves: check for water before flight."],["021.08.01.02:01","What is unusable fuel?","Fuel that cannot be safely used in flight (remaining in tanks/lines in the most adverse condition). Included in empty mass, not in usable fuel."],["021.08.01.01:02","JET A, JET A-1, JET B: freezing points and flash points.","JET A: ≥ +38 °C flash, ≤ −40 °C freeze (USA). JET A-1: ≥ +38 °C flash, ≤ −47 °C freeze (world standard). JET B: wide-cut, lowest flash point (≈ −20 °C), freeze ≈ −50/−60 °C. Density ≈ 0.80 (JET B ≈ 0.76)."],["021.08.02.01:03","Turbine fuel additives and ice protection.","FSII (fuel system icing inhibitor, prevents water freezing), anti-static, anti-oxidant, biocides. Fuel heaters (oil/fuel heat exchanger) prevent ice crystals blocking the filter."],["021.08.02.02:01","Why centrifugal boost pumps in the tanks, and which tank is used first?","Boost pumps (typ. 2 per tank, AC-driven) prevent cavitation/vapour lock at altitude. Centre-tank pumps have higher output pressure so the centre tank is used first and the wing tanks last (wing bending relief)."],["021.08.01.02:05","What happens with loss of boost-pump pressure?","The engine draws fuel by suction feed from its own tank via the engine-driven pump: only possible at lower altitudes (vapour formation), so an altitude limit applies (e.g. descend below FL 250–300). No cross-feed from a tank with failed pumps; centre-tank fuel may become unusable."],["021.08.02.02:06","Fuel dump/jettison: when is it fitted and what is required?","When MTOM significantly exceeds MLM. CS-25: jettison from MTOM to MLM in ≤ 15 min (or to meet climb requirements). Nozzles at wingtips/trailing edges; stops automatically at a preset quantity (e.g. 30–45 min of fuel remains). Not needed if the climb requirements are met at MTOM for landing."],["021.08.02.02:05","Purpose of the cross-feed, trim tank, baffles and the dip sticks.","Cross-feed: any engine from any tank (engine failure, balancing). Trim (tail) tank: shifts CG in cruise, less trim drag. Baffles: limit sloshing, check valves keep fuel near pumps. Drip/magnetic dip sticks: manual measurement under the wing when the indication is unserviceable (convert with tables)."],["021.08.02.01:01","EASA LO 021.08.02.01.01 → State the types of fuel used by a gas turbine engine: — JET-A; — JET-A1; — JET-B.","JET A: ≥ +38 °C flash, ≤ −40 °C freeze (USA). JET A-1: ≥ +38 °C flash, ≤ −47 °C freeze (world standard). JET B: wide-cut, lowest flash point (≈ −20 °C), freeze ≈ −50/−60 °C. Density ≈ 0.80 (JET B ≈ 0.76)."],["021.08.02.01:02","EASA LO 021.08.02.01.02 → State the main characteristics of these fuels and give typical values regarding their flash points, freezing points and density.","JET A: ≥ +38 °C flash, ≤ −40 °C freeze (USA). JET A-1: ≥ +38 °C flash, ≤ −47 °C freeze (world standard). JET B: wide-cut, lowest flash point (≈ −20 °C), freeze ≈ −50/−60 °C. Density ≈ 0.80 (JET B ≈ 0.76). / Diesel: compression-ignition piston engines, flash point > +55 °C, waxing (cloud point −10…−20 °C), density ≈ 0.84. JET A-1: for diesel/high-compression piston engines certified for it, flash point ≥ +38 °C, freezing ≤ −47 °C, density ≈ 0.80."],["021.08.02.02:01","EASA LO 021.08.02.02.01 → Explain the function of the fuel system: — lines; — centrifugal boost pump; — pressure valves; — fuel shut-off valve; — filter, strainer;","Lines, boost (electric) pump, pressure valves, filter/strainer (gascolator at lowest point), tanks, vent system, sump, drain valves, quantity sensor (float or capacitance), temperature sensor. Boost pump: start, take-off/landing, engine-driven pump failure, vapour lock."],["021.08.02.02:04","EASA LO 021.08.02.02.04 → Explain the limitations in the event of loss of booster pump fuel pressure.","The engine draws fuel by suction feed from its own tank via the engine-driven pump: only possible at lower altitudes (vapour formation), so an altitude limit applies (e.g. descend below FL 250–300). No cross-feed from a tank with failed pumps; centre-tank fuel may become unusable."]],"65":[["033.05.01.01:01","Why does the ICAO FPL have a fixed format?","Automatic processing by ATS computers (IFPS in Europe), unambiguous interpretation across borders, exchange between units, and fast alerting/SAR."],["033.05.01.01:02","FPL Item 7: maximum length and content?","Aircraft identification, max. 7 characters: registration (HBABC) or ICAO operator designator + flight number (SWR123). No hyphens."],["033.05.01.01:02","FPL Item 8: letters for flight rules and type of flight.","Rules: I IFR, V VFR, Y IFR first then VFR, Z VFR first then IFR. Type: S scheduled, N non-scheduled, G general aviation, M military, X other."],["033.05.01.01:02","FPL Item 9: wake turbulence category limits.","L ≤ 7 000 kg, M 7 000–136 000 kg, H ≥ 136 000 kg, J super (A380). Type designator from Doc 8643; ZZZZ + TYP/ in Item 18 if no designator exists."],["033.05.01.01:01","FPL Item 10: what does the letter R mean and what must be added?","R = PBN approved. The PBN capabilities must be given in Item 18 as PBN/. Before the slash = COM/NAV/approach aids, after the slash = surveillance equipment."],["033.05.01.01:02","FPL Item 13: what time is entered?","EOBT (estimated off-block time) in UTC, 4 digits, plus the ICAO 4-letter departure aerodrome. Not the take-off time."],["033.05.01.01:01","FPL Item 16 and Item 15: what do they contain?","Item 15: cruising speed/level (e.g. N0450F350), route (airways, significant points, DCT), speed/level changes. Item 16: destination ICAO code + total EET (HHMM) + up to 2 alternates."],["033.05.01.01:01","FPL Item 18: what is DOF/ and what does 0 mean?","DOF/ = date of flight (YYMMDD). Item 18 = other information with indicators (PBN/, NAV/, COM/, DEP/, DEST/, REG/, EET/, RMK/ …); 0 if there is nothing to add."],["033.05.01.01:01","FPL Item 19: what is it and is it transmitted?","Supplementary information: E/ endurance (HHMM), P/ persons on board, R/ emergency radio, S/ survival equipment, J/ life jackets, D/ dinghies, A/ aircraft colour, N/ remarks, C/ pilot in command. It is NOT transmitted to other units; it is kept by ATS for SAR."],["033.05.01.01:01","Filing and changes of an FPL: key times and messages.","File at least 60 min before EOBT (FPL accepted max. 120 h before). Changes with a CHG message, delay with DLA, cancel with CNL. Arrival report closes the plan."],["033.05.02.01:01","What is a repetitive flight plan (RPL)?","A flight plan for IFR flights operated regularly on the same days of consecutive weeks (≥ 10 occasions or every day for ≥ 10 consecutive days) with identical basic features (route, times, levels). Submitted in advance as a list and activated automatically; no individual FPL each time. Not for VFR."]],"66":[["071.01.01.01:01","ICAO Annex 6: fill in the gaps → ____: aerodrome to which an aircraft may proceed when it becomes impossible or inadvisable to proceed to/land at the intended aerodrome — ____ (if needed shortly after take-off, departure unusable), ____ alternate (landing after abnormal/emergency en route, incl.","Alternate aerodrome: aerodrome to which an aircraft may proceed when it becomes impossible or inadvisable to proceed to/land at the intended aerodrome — take-off alternate (if needed shortly after take-off, departure unusable), en-route alternate (landing after abnormal/emergency en route, incl."],["071.01.01.01:01","ICAO Annex 6: fill in the gaps → ETOPS), ____ (if landing at destination becomes impossible/inadvisable). ____: from the moment the aircraft ____ taking off until it finally comes to rest at the end of the flight (\"block to block\"). (Helicopters: rotors turning to rotors stopped.)","ETOPS), destination alternate (if landing at destination becomes impossible/inadvisable). Flight time (aeroplanes): from the moment the aircraft first moves for the purpose of taking off until it finally comes to rest at the end of the flight (\"block to block\"). (Helicopters: rotors turning to rotors stopped.)"],["071.01.01.02:02","ICAO Annex 6: fill in the gaps → ____ applies to aeroplanes operated by operators authorised for ____; Part II international GA aeroplanes; ____ all helicopters in international CAT or GA (except aerial work).","Part I applies to aeroplanes operated by operators authorised for international CAT; Part II international GA aeroplanes; Part III all helicopters in international CAT or GA (except aerial work)."],["071.01.01.03:03","ICAO Annex 6: fill in the gaps → General: comply with laws, regulations and procedures of the States where operating; operator of aeroplanes ____ ____ shall establish a ____ (part of the safety management system, non-punitive); flight safety documents system = organised set of documents (OM etc.) for crews;","General: comply with laws, regulations and procedures of the States where operating; operator of aeroplanes > 27 000 kg MCTOM shall establish a flight data analysis programme (part of the safety management system, non-punitive); flight safety documents system = organised set of documents (OM etc.) for crews;"],["071.01.01.03:05","ICAO Annex 6: fill in the gaps → ____ = certificate that maintenance was completed satisfactorily (CRS); aircraft lights: nav lights, anti-collision lights (see 010-03).","maintenance release = certificate that maintenance was completed satisfactorily (CRS); aircraft lights: nav lights, anti-collision lights (see 010-03)."],["071.01.02.01:01","EU operational requirements (Reg. 965/2012): fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (commercial air transport), Part-SPO (specialised operations, e.g. aerial work), Part-NCC (non-commercial complex), Part-NCO (non-commercial other), Part-ORO (organisation requirements), Part-SPA (specific approvals: PBN, MNPS, RVSM, LVO, DG, ETOPS, HEMS…).","Applicability: Part-CAT (commercial air transport), Part-SPO (specialised operations, e.g. aerial work), Part-NCC (non-commercial complex), Part-NCO (non-commercial other), Part-ORO (organisation requirements), Part-SPA (specific approvals: PBN, MNPS, RVSM, LVO, DG, ETOPS, HEMS…)."],["071.02.12.03:13","EU operational requirements (Reg. 965/2012): what do you remember about this? → CAT = carriage of passengers, cargo or mail for remuneration; …","CAT = carriage of passengers, cargo or mail for remuneration; exceptions e.g. A-to-A flights, cost-shared flights under conditions, introductory flights."],["071.01.02.02:03","EU operational requirements (Reg. 965/2012): fill in the gaps → ____: CAT flights must meet the rules to guarantee an equivalent safety level. ____ (helicopters): height-velocity combinations from which a safe landing after engine failure is not assured. ____: helicopter emergency medical service.","General: CAT flights must meet the rules to guarantee an equivalent safety level. HV envelope (helicopters): height-velocity combinations from which a safe landing after engine failure is not assured. HEMS: helicopter emergency medical service."],["071.01.02.02:04","EU operational requirements (Reg. 965/2012): fill in the gaps → ____: where a safe forced landing is not possible or occupants cannot be protected/rescued — conflict zones information from EASA CZIBs, State warnings, operator risk assessment. ____: approval for short VFR flights in a defined area.","Hostile environment: where a safe forced landing is not possible or occupants cannot be protected/rescued — conflict zones information from EASA CZIBs, State warnings, operator risk assessment. Local area operations: approval for short VFR flights in a defined area."],["071.01.02.02:06","EU operational requirements (Reg. 965/2012): fill in the gaps → ____: common language for crew communication; OM in a language understood by all personnel.","Language: common language for crew communication; OM in a language understood by all personnel."],["071.01.02.02:08","EU operational requirements (Reg. 965/2012): fill in the gaps → ____ (ORO.GEN.200): accountable manager, safety policy, ____, compliance monitoring, documentation, training. ____ prevention & flight safety programme incl. occurrence reporting, FDM (aeroplanes > 27 000 kg).","Management system (ORO.GEN.200): accountable manager, safety policy, hazard identification & risk management, compliance monitoring, documentation, training. Accident prevention & flight safety programme incl. occurrence reporting, FDM (aeroplanes > 27 000 kg)."],["071.01.02.02:11","EU operational requirements (Reg. 965/2012): fill in the gaps → ____: no person may be in a part of the aircraft not designed for persons unless temporarily permitted by the commander; ____: operator policy, commander may prohibit use; ____: persons under the influence shall not be admitted when safety is endangered; nobody shall recklessly endanger the aircraft or persons.","Carriage of persons: no person may be in a part of the aircraft not designed for persons unless temporarily permitted by the commander; PEDs: operator policy, commander may prohibit use; drugs/alcohol: persons under the influence shall not be admitted when safety is endangered; nobody shall recklessly endanger the aircraft or persons."],["071.01.02.01:01","EU operational requirements (Reg. 965/2012): fill in the gaps → ____ (CAT): ____, original ____, certificate of airworthiness, noise certificate, certified true copy of the AOC, operations specifications, radio licence, third-party liability insurance, journey log/technical log, crew licences with ratings and medicals, OM parts relevant to crew duties, operational flight plan, mass & balance documentation, NOTAM/AIS briefing, MET information, passenger/cargo manifests, notification of dangerous goods, search & rescue information, maps & charts, interception procedures, MEL/CDL, ARC.","Documents to be carried (CAT): AFM/equivalent, original certificate of registration, certificate of airworthiness, noise certificate, certified true copy of the AOC, operations specifications, radio licence, third-party liability insurance, journey log/technical log, crew licences with ratings and medicals, OM parts relevant to crew duties, operational flight plan, mass & balance documentation, NOTAM/AIS briefing, MET information, passenger/cargo manifests, notification of dangerous goods, search & rescue information, maps & charts, interception procedures, MEL/CDL, ARC."],["071.01.02.01:01","EU operational requirements (Reg. 965/2012): fill in the gaps → ____ (copy for the duration of the flight): operational flight plan, M&B documentation, NOTAMs, parts of the tech log, special loads notification.","Kept on the ground (copy for the duration of the flight): operational flight plan, M&B documentation, NOTAMs, parts of the tech log, special loads notification."],["071.01.02.02:17","EU operational requirements (Reg. 965/2012): fill in the gaps → ____: operator/commander provide documents to the authority within a reasonable time on request; keep flight records (e.g. OFP, M&B for ____).","Records: operator/commander provide documents to the authority within a reasonable time on request; keep flight records (e.g. OFP, M&B for 3 months)."],["071.01.02.02:07","EU operational requirements (Reg. 965/2012): fill in the gaps → ____: issued by the competent authority (state of principal place of business) when the operator has a management system, adequate facilities, qualified personnel (accountable manager + nominated persons: flight ops, crew training, ground ops, continuing airworthiness), aircraft registered, CAMO arrangement, and complies with Part-ORO/CAT.","AOC: issued by the competent authority (state of principal place of business) when the operator has a management system, adequate facilities, qualified personnel (accountable manager + nominated persons: flight ops, crew training, ground ops, continuing airworthiness), aircraft registered, CAMO arrangement, and complies with Part-ORO/CAT."],["071.01.02.03:04","EU operational requirements (Reg. 965/2012): what do you remember about this? → Contents: operator name, AOC number, validity, ops specifications …","Contents: operator name, AOC number, validity, ops specifications (aircraft types/registrations, areas, special authorisations). Unlimited validity, subject to continued compliance/oversight."],["071.01.02.09:04","Flight crew: fill in the gaps → ____: not less than the minimum in the AFM; two pilots for IFR/night CAT in most cases (single-pilot IFR/night only with conditions); ____ by suitably qualified pilots (cruise relief co-pilot above FL 200).","Composition: not less than the minimum in the AFM; two pilots for IFR/night CAT in most cases (single-pilot IFR/night only with conditions); in-flight relief by suitably qualified pilots (cruise relief co-pilot above FL 200)."],["071.01.02.09:02","Flight crew: fill in the gaps → ____ before flying a new type/operator (operator conversion course incl. CRM, line flying under supervision, ____); ____ (new variant/equipment requiring additional knowledge), familiarisation (knowledge only).","Conversion training & checking before flying a new type/operator (operator conversion course incl. CRM, line flying under supervision, line check); differences training (new variant/equipment requiring additional knowledge), familiarisation (knowledge only)."],["071.01.02.09:04","Flight crew: fill in the gaps → ____: command course (incl. CRM, simulator, line flying under supervision, ____ as commander), ____ set by the operator (e.g. 500 h/ATPL for multi-pilot CAT; CAT commander of MPA requires ATPL(A)); route & aerodrome competence.","Upgrade to commander: command course (incl. CRM, simulator, line flying under supervision, line check as commander), minimum experience set by the operator (e.g. 500 h/ATPL for multi-pilot CAT; CAT commander of MPA requires ATPL(A)); route & aerodrome competence."],["071.01.02.09:06","Flight crew: fill in the gaps → Recurrent training & checking: operator proficiency check (OPC) every ____ (valid until end of the 6th month; two OPCs per 12 months), line check every ____, emergency & safety equipment training/check every ____, CRM recurrent, ground training.","Recurrent training & checking: operator proficiency check (OPC) every 6 months (valid until end of the 6th month; two OPCs per 12 months), line check every 12 months, emergency & safety equipment training/check every 12 months, CRM recurrent, ground training."],["071.01.02.09:07","Flight crew: fill in the gaps → ____: commanders who may operate from the right seat (and co-pilots in the left) need specific training and checking.","Either pilot's seat: commanders who may operate from the right seat (and co-pilots in the left) need specific training and checking."],["071.01.02.09:08","Flight crew: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ as PF in the preceding ____ in the type or an FFS.","Recent experience: 3 take-offs and 3 landings as PF in the preceding 90 days in the type or an FFS."],["071.01.02.09:09","Flight crew: fill in the gaps → ____: the commander must have adequate knowledge of the route and aerodromes (terrain, MSA, weather, communication, ATS procedures, special aerodrome categories A/B/C — C requires a visit/simulator).","Route/aerodrome competence: the commander must have adequate knowledge of the route and aerodromes (terrain, MSA, weather, communication, ATS procedures, special aerodrome categories A/B/C — C requires a visit/simulator)."],["071.01.02.09:11","Flight crew: fill in the gaps → ____: limited and approved (OM), crossed recency; flight crew operating both helicopters and aeroplanes: ____.","More than one type/variant: limited and approved (OM), crossed recency; flight crew operating both helicopters and aeroplanes: one type of each."],["071.01.02.09:12","Flight crew: fill in the gaps → ____ kept for all training, checking and qualifications (at least 3 years).","Training records kept for all training, checking and qualifications (at least 3 years)."],["071.01.02.09:14","Flight crew: fill in the gaps → ____: perform duties, report incidents, not fly when unfit (fatigue, alcohol ____ or within ____ of drinking, medication), commander's authority over crew and passengers, may disembark persons who endanger safety. Persons on board: commander ensures briefing, seatbelts, safety of all persons.","Crew responsibilities: perform duties, report incidents, not fly when unfit (fatigue, alcohol ≥ 0.2 ‰ or within 8 h of drinking, medication), commander's authority over crew and passengers, may disembark persons who endanger safety. Persons on board: commander ensures briefing, seatbelts, safety of all persons."],["071.01.02.09:15","Flight crew: fill in the gaps → ____ training before first line flying (human error, situation awareness, communication, workload, automation, operator culture).","Initial operator's CRM training before first line flying (human error, situation awareness, communication, workload, automation, operator culture)."],["071.01.02.10:01","Cabin crew: fill in the gaps → ____ = crew member (not flight crew) performing safety duties in the cabin, assigned by the operator.","Cabin crew member = crew member (not flight crew) performing safety duties in the cabin, assigned by the operator."],["071.01.02.10:01","Cabin crew: fill in the gaps → ____: required for aircraft with ____ seats (or carrying ≥ 1 passenger with > 19 seats): ____ seats (or fraction) on the same deck, or the number from certification demonstration if higher.","Minimum number: required for aircraft with > 19 passenger seats (or carrying ≥ 1 passenger with > 19 seats): 1 per 50 seats (or fraction) on the same deck, or the number from certification demonstration if higher."],["071.01.02.10:05","Cabin crew: fill in the gaps → ____: ≥ 18 years, medical fitness, ____, completed initial/type/operator training and checks. Senior cabin crew member required when > 1 cabin crew; responsible to the commander for cabin safety. More than one type: max ____ (4 with approval).","Assignment: ≥ 18 years, medical fitness, cabin crew attestation, completed initial/type/operator training and checks. Senior cabin crew member required when > 1 cabin crew; responsible to the commander for cabin safety. More than one type: max 3 types (4 with approval)."],["071.01.02.10:06","Cabin crew: what do you remember about this? → Additional crew members (not trained) must be distinguishable to …","Additional crew members (not trained) must be distinguishable to passengers."],["071.01.02.12:03","Flight & duty time limitations (ORO.FTL): fill in the gaps → Definitions: ____ (flight duty period) from report until ____ ____ at the end of the last sector; duty period; rest period; acclimatised; standby; split duty; WOCL (window of circadian low 02:00–05:59).","Definitions: FDP (flight duty period) from report until engines off/on-blocks at the end of the last sector; duty period; rest period; acclimatised; standby; split duty; WOCL (window of circadian low 02:00–05:59)."],["071.01.02.12:03","Flight & duty time limitations (ORO.FTL): fill in the gaps → Max basic daily FDP (acclimatised): ____, reduced by 30 min per sector from the 3rd sector (max reduction 2 h) and when starting in the WOCL; minimum ____. Extensions by up to ____ (max 2× per 7 days) or with in-flight rest (augmented crew, rest facilities class 1–3 → up to 16–17 h).","Max basic daily FDP (acclimatised): 13 h, reduced by 30 min per sector from the 3rd sector (max reduction 2 h) and when starting in the WOCL; minimum 9 h. Extensions by up to 1 h (max 2× per 7 days) or with in-flight rest (augmented crew, rest facilities class 1–3 → up to 16–17 h)."],["071.01.02.12:02","Flight & duty time limitations (ORO.FTL): fill in the gaps → Cumulative limits: duty ____, ____, ____ / 28 days; flight time 100 h / 28 days, 900 h / calendar year, 1 000 h / 12 months.","Cumulative limits: duty 60 h / 7 days, 110 h / 14 days, 190 h / 28 days; flight time 100 h / 28 days, 900 h / calendar year, 1 000 h / 12 months."],["071.01.02.12:02","Flight & duty time limitations (ORO.FTL): fill in the gaps → Rest: at home base ≥ ____ or as long as the preceding duty (whichever greater); away ≥ ____ (incl. 8 h sleep opportunity) or as long as the preceding duty; recurrent extended rest ≥ ____ incl. 2 local nights, max 168 h between.","Rest: at home base ≥ 12 h or as long as the preceding duty (whichever greater); away ≥ 10 h (incl. 8 h sleep opportunity) or as long as the preceding duty; recurrent extended rest ≥ 36 h incl. 2 local nights, max 168 h between."],["071.01.02.12:06","Flight & duty time limitations (ORO.FTL): fill in the gaps → ____: unforeseen circumstances after reporting → FDP may be extended (e.g. up to ____, 3 h with augmented crew) after consultation with crew; report to operator.","Commander's discretion: unforeseen circumstances after reporting → FDP may be extended (e.g. up to 2 h, 3 h with augmented crew) after consultation with crew; report to operator."],["071.01.02.12:07","Flight & duty time limitations (ORO.FTL): fill in the gaps → ____ (airport/home): counts partly as duty; airport standby counts fully; max 16 h standby + FDP awake time.","Standby (airport/home): counts partly as duty; airport standby counts fully; max 16 h standby + FDP awake time."],["071.02.06.01:01","Decompression: fill in the gaps → ____ (door seal leak, pressurisation controller/pack failure): cabin altitude slowly rises — detected by ____, ear discomfort. Action: ____ if warning, try manual pressurisation control, descend if cabin cannot be controlled.","Slow decompression (door seal leak, pressurisation controller/pack failure): cabin altitude slowly rises — detected by cabin-altitude/rate indications, 10 000 ft warning, ear discomfort. Action: oxygen masks on (crew) if warning, try manual pressurisation control, descend if cabin cannot be controlled."],["071.02.06.02:01","Decompression: fill in the gaps → ____ (window/door failure, structural damage): loud bang, fog (condensation), dust, temperature drop, rush of air, passenger masks drop at ~14 000 ft cabin, cabin-altitude warning.","Rapid/explosive decompression (window/door failure, structural damage): loud bang, fog (condensation), dust, temperature drop, rush of air, passenger masks drop at ~14 000 ft cabin, cabin-altitude warning."],["071.02.07.02:01","Decompression: fill in the gaps → Actions: crew oxygen masks ____, establish communication, emergency descent (throttles idle, speedbrakes, max appropriate speed, turn off airway, squawk ____) to ____ or MSA, cabin crew take nearest seat/oxygen, check passengers.","Actions: crew oxygen masks on (100 %), establish communication, emergency descent (throttles idle, speedbrakes, max appropriate speed, turn off airway, squawk 7700) to 10 000 ft or MSA, cabin crew take nearest seat/oxygen, check passengers."],["071.02.06.03:02","Decompression: fill in the gaps → Effects: ____ (TUC: FL 250 ≈ 3–5 min, FL 300 ≈ 1–2 min, FL 350 ≈ 30–60 s, FL 400 ≈ 15–20 s; halved after rapid decompression), ____ (abdomen, teeth), ____ (N₂ bubbles), cold, mist, flying debris.","Effects: hypoxia (TUC: FL 250 ≈ 3–5 min, FL 300 ≈ 1–2 min, FL 350 ≈ 30–60 s, FL 400 ≈ 15–20 s; halved after rapid decompression), ear/sinus pain, gas expansion (abdomen, teeth), decompression sickness (N₂ bubbles), cold, mist, flying debris."],["071.02.07.01:01","Wind shear & microburst: fill in the gaps → Identify: significant/rapid ____ (> 15 kt), unusual ____ (> 500 ft/min deviation), glideslope deviation, pitch/thrust changes to hold the path, reactive/predictive ____ shear warning (\"WINDSHEAR\"), LLWAS/ATC reports, Cb/virga/dust rings.","Identify: significant/rapid IAS changes (> 15 kt), unusual vertical speed (> 500 ft/min deviation), glideslope deviation, pitch/thrust changes to hold the path, reactive/predictive wind- shear warning (\"WINDSHEAR\"), LLWAS/ATC reports, Cb/virga/dust rings."],["071.02.07.02:02","Wind shear & microburst: fill in the gaps → ____: delay departure/approach, use the longest runway, max take-off thrust (no reduced thrust), increased VR/approach speed (within limits), early config, avoid Cb within ~10 NM.","Take-off/approach precautions: delay departure/approach, use the longest runway, max take-off thrust (no reduced thrust), increased VR/approach speed (within limits), early config, avoid Cb within ~10 NM."],["071.02.07.02:03","Wind shear & microburst: fill in the gaps → ____ (strong downdraft/shear): ____, pitch up to ____ (or follow flight-director/PWS guidance, up to stick-shaker), do not change configuration (gear/flap) until clear, speedbrakes retracted, monitor vertical speed/radio altitude, report to ATC.","Recovery (strong downdraft/shear): TO/GA thrust, pitch up to ~15° (or follow flight-director/PWS guidance, up to stick-shaker), do not change configuration (gear/flap) until clear, speedbrakes retracted, monitor vertical speed/radio altitude, report to ATC."],["071.02.07.02:03","Wind shear & microburst: fill in the gaps → ____: < 4 km, 1–5 min strongest, downdraft up to 6 000 ft/min; on approach: first ____ (IAS ↑, above path) → ____ → tailwind (IAS ↓, sink) — don't reduce power on the initial IAS increase.","Microburst: < 4 km, 1–5 min strongest, downdraft up to 6 000 ft/min; on approach: first increasing headwind (IAS ↑, above path) → downdraft → tailwind (IAS ↓, sink) — don't reduce power on the initial IAS increase."],["071.02.08.01:02","Wake turbulence: fill in the gaps → ____ from the pressure difference between lower and upper wing surfaces; rotate ____ the tips (seen from behind: left vortex clockwise, right anticlockwise). ____ (nose-wheel lift-off) and end at touchdown of the nose wheel.","Tip vortices from the pressure difference between lower and upper wing surfaces; rotate outward, upward and around the tips (seen from behind: left vortex clockwise, right anticlockwise). Begin at rotation (nose-wheel lift-off) and end at touchdown of the nose wheel."],["071.02.08.01:04","Wake turbulence: fill in the gaps → They ____ (~300–500 ft/min, level off ~500– 900 ft below the flight path) and on the ground move outward ____; a ____ holds the upwind vortex over the runway; a light tailwind moves vortices into the touchdown zone.","They descend (~300–500 ft/min, level off ~500– 900 ft below the flight path) and on the ground move outward at ~5 kt; a light crosswind (~3–5 kt) holds the upwind vortex over the runway; a light tailwind moves vortices into the touchdown zone."],["071.02.08.02:01","Wake turbulence: fill in the gaps → Strongest when ____.","Strongest when heavy, clean, slow."],["071.02.08.01:01","Wake turbulence: fill in the gaps → ____: stay ____ the flight path of the preceding aircraft, land ____ its touchdown point, rotate before its rotation point, observe separations.","Avoidance: stay above the flight path of the preceding aircraft, land beyond its touchdown point, rotate before its rotation point, observe separations."],["071.02.08.03:01","Wake turbulence: fill in the gaps → ICAO radar separations (approach): ____ ____; behind J ____: H 6, M 7, L 8 NM. Timed (departures): L/M behind H 2 min (3 min from an intermediate take-off position); arrivals non-radar: M behind H 2 min, L behind H/M 3 min. (EU RECAT-EU uses 6 categories.)","ICAO radar separations (approach): H behind H 4 NM; M behind H 5 NM; L behind H 6 NM; L behind M 5 NM; behind J (A380): H 6, M 7, L 8 NM. Timed (departures): L/M behind H 2 min (3 min from an intermediate take-off position); arrivals non-radar: M behind H 2 min, L behind H/M 3 min. (EU RECAT-EU uses 6 categories.)"],["071.02.09.01:01","Security: fill in the gaps → ____: aircraft security check (inspection of the interior accessible to passengers and holds for prohibited articles), screening (technical/other means to identify/detect weapons, explosives), security (combination of measures and resources to safeguard civil aviation against unlawful interference), security-restricted area (airside area of priority risk with access control), unidentified baggage (baggage not claimed/identified with a passenger).","Definitions: aircraft security check (inspection of the interior accessible to passengers and holds for prohibited articles), screening (technical/other means to identify/detect weapons, explosives), security (combination of measures and resources to safeguard civil aviation against unlawful interference), security-restricted area (airside area of priority risk with access control), unidentified baggage (baggage not claimed/identified with a passenger)."],["071.02.09.03:03","Security: fill in the gaps → Objective: ____ ____.","Objective: safeguard civil aviation against acts of unlawful interference."],["071.02.09.01:01","Security: fill in the gaps → ____: notify ATS (squawk ____, radio if possible) of unlawful interference; if forced to depart track/level without contact: operate on 121.5, broadcast, apply contingency procedures; if ATS asks to confirm 7500 — reply confirms or (if not true) squawk correctly; if no reply, ATS assumes genuine hijack.","Commander: notify ATS (squawk 7500, radio if possible) of unlawful interference; if forced to depart track/level without contact: operate on 121.5, broadcast, apply contingency procedures; if ATS asks to confirm 7500 — reply confirms or (if not true) squawk correctly; if no reply, ATS assumes genuine hijack."],["071.02.09.03:04","Security: fill in the gaps → ____ implements and goes beyond ____ in the EU (common rules, stricter). Operators have ____ training programmes for crew; acts of unlawful interference must be reported to the authority/ATS without delay; aircraft search procedures (checklist of where to search for a bomb, least-risk bomb location).","Reg. (EC) 300/2008 implements and goes beyond Annex 17 in the EU (common rules, stricter). Operators have security training programmes for crew; acts of unlawful interference must be reported to the authority/ATS without delay; aircraft search procedures (checklist of where to search for a bomb, least-risk bomb location)."],["071.02.10.01:01","Emergency landing & ditching: fill in the gaps → ____: forced landing on water. ____: planned landing off-airport or at an aerodrome when continuation is inadvisable (fuel, weather, fire) while power is still available. ____: forced landing because the flight cannot continue (e.g. engine failure, fire).","Ditching: forced landing on water. Precautionary landing: planned landing off-airport or at an aerodrome when continuation is inadvisable (fuel, weather, fire) while power is still available. Emergency landing: forced landing because the flight cannot continue (e.g. engine failure, fire)."],["071.02.10.01:01","Emergency landing & ditching: what do you remember about this? → Causes: engine failure, fire/smoke, fuel exhaustion, structural …","Causes: engine failure, fire/smoke, fuel exhaustion, structural damage, lost/weather, medical emergency."],["071.02.07.02:03","Emergency landing & ditching: fill in the gaps → ____: into wind or parallel to the swell (land along the swell crest, into wind if light swell), gear ____, flaps as required, minimum speed & rate of descent, nose slightly up, ditching button (closes valves), life vests inflated only ____ the aircraft.","Ditching: into wind or parallel to the swell (land along the swell crest, into wind if light swell), gear up, flaps as required, minimum speed & rate of descent, nose slightly up, ditching button (closes valves), life vests inflated only after leaving the aircraft."],["071.02.10.01:01","Emergency landing & ditching: fill in the gaps → ____: select field, overhead inspection, standard circuit, short-field technique. ____: best glide, field selection, checklist, mayday, secure (fuel/electrics off) before touchdown.","Precautionary landing: select field, overhead inspection, standard circuit, short-field technique. Emergency landing: best glide, field selection, checklist, mayday, secure (fuel/electrics off) before touchdown."],["071.02.10.04:01","Emergency landing & ditching: fill in the gaps → ____: brace position, seat belts, life vests, exits, what to leave behind, signal for evacuation; after landing: crew commands evacuation, pax guided away, headcount, first aid, preserve evidence.","Passenger briefing: brace position, seat belts, life vests, exits, what to leave behind, signal for evacuation; after landing: crew commands evacuation, pax guided away, headcount, first aid, preserve evidence."],["071.02.10.05:01","Emergency landing & ditching: fill in the gaps → ____: aircraft must be stopped and engines shut down first (jet blast/props, fire, slides). ____: full evacuation of max passengers through half of the exits in ____.","Evacuation: aircraft must be stopped and engines shut down first (jet blast/props, fire, slides). CS-25: full evacuation of max passengers through half of the exits in ≤ 90 s."],["071.02.12.02:03","Dangerous goods: fill in the gaps → ____: dangerous goods — articles/substances capable of posing a risk to health, safety, property or the environment and listed in the Technical Instructions; DG accident — results in fatal/serious injury or major property damage;","Definitions: dangerous goods — articles/substances capable of posing a risk to health, safety, property or the environment and listed in the Technical Instructions; DG accident — results in fatal/serious injury or major property damage;"],["071.02.12.01:01","Dangerous goods: what do you remember about this? → DG incident — injury, damage, fire, spillage or leakage, other than …","DG incident — injury, damage, fire, spillage or leakage, other than an accident (or which seriously jeopardises the aircraft); exemption — authorisation by the State relieving from the TIs; incompatible — DG that could react dangerously if mixed; packaging — receptacles meeting TI specs;"],["071.02.12.01:01","Dangerous goods: what do you remember about this? → UN number — four-digit number identifying the …","UN number — four-digit number identifying the substance."],["071.02.12.02:03","Dangerous goods: fill in the gaps → Detailed rules in ____; in an in-flight emergency the ____ of DG on board.","Detailed rules in ICAO Doc 9284 (Technical Instructions); in an in-flight emergency the PIC informs ATC of DG on board."],["071.02.12.02:02","Dangerous goods: fill in the gaps → ____: incompatible classes (e.g. flammable liquids and oxidisers) are stowed apart; ____ ____ kept at minimum distance from persons/animals/film (transport index); dangerous goods list gives UN number, class, packing group, quantities pax/cargo aircraft, special provisions.","Compatibility & segregation: incompatible classes (e.g. flammable liquids and oxidisers) are stowed apart; radioactive materials kept at minimum distance from persons/animals/film (transport index); dangerous goods list gives UN number, class, packing group, quantities pax/cargo aircraft, special provisions."],["071.02.12.01:01","Dangerous goods: fill in the gaps → ____ ____: 1 explosives (orange), 2 gases (red flammable/green non-flammable/white toxic), 3 flammable liquids (red), 4 flammable solids (red/white stripes), 5 oxidisers & organic peroxides (yellow), 6 toxic & infectious (white, skull), 7 radioactive (yellow/white, trefoil), 8 corrosives (black/white), 9 miscellaneous (black/white stripes).","9 classes/labels: 1 explosives (orange), 2 gases (red flammable/green non-flammable/white toxic), 3 flammable liquids (red), 4 flammable solids (red/white stripes), 5 oxidisers & organic peroxides (yellow), 6 toxic & infectious (white, skull), 7 radioactive (yellow/white, trefoil), 8 corrosives (black/white), 9 miscellaneous (black/white stripes)."],["071.02.12.02:04","Dangerous goods: fill in the gaps → ____ label (orange) and \"magnetized material\" handling labels.","Cargo aircraft only label (orange) and \"magnetized material\" handling labels."],["071.02.12.03:06","Dangerous goods: fill in the gaps → ____: DG transport requires ____ (SPA.DG), training programmes, acceptance checklist, inspection for damage/leakage before loading, written information to the commander (NOTOC), reporting of DG incidents/accidents and undeclared/misdeclared DG (within ____).","Part-CAT/Part-SPA: DG transport requires operator approval (SPA.DG), training programmes, acceptance checklist, inspection for damage/leakage before loading, written information to the commander (NOTOC), reporting of DG incidents/accidents and undeclared/misdeclared DG (within 72 h)."],["071.02.12.03:09","Dangerous goods: fill in the gaps → ____: aircraft equipment, catering/cabin items, medical supplies for aeromedical flights, some passenger items. Forbidden items: substances that are explosive/unstable under normal conditions (never transported). ____ DG: larger quantities or higher hazard, crew access possible.","Exemptions: aircraft equipment, catering/cabin items, medical supplies for aeromedical flights, some passenger items. Forbidden items: substances that are explosive/unstable under normal conditions (never transported). Cargo-only DG: larger quantities or higher hazard, crew access possible."],["071.02.12.01:01","Exam trap (Dangerous goods): complete the fact → FDP basic max ____.","FDP basic max 13 h."],["071.01.02.09:08","Exam trap (Dangerous goods): complete the fact → Recent experience ____TO + ____LDG in 90 days.","Recent experience 3 TO + 3 LDG in 90 days."],["071.02.10.05:02","Exam trap (Dangerous goods): complete the fact → Evacuation in ____ with half the exits.","Evacuation in 90 s with half the exits."],["071.01.01.02:01","EASA LO 071.01.01.02.01 → State that Part I shall be applicable to the operation of aeroplanes by operators authorised to conduct international commercial air transport (CAT) operations.","Part I applies to aeroplanes operated by operators authorised for international CAT; Part II international GA aeroplanes; Part III all helicopters in international CAT or GA (except aerial work). / Applicability: Part-CAT (commercial air transport), Part-SPO (specialised operations, e.g. aerial work), Part-NCC (non-commercial complex), Part-NCO (non-commercial other), Part-ORO (organisation requirements), Part-SPA (specific approvals: PBN, MNPS, RVSM, LVO, DG, ETOPS, HEMS…)."],["071.01.01.03:01","EASA LO 071.01.01.03.01 → Explain the compliance with laws, regulations and procedures.","General: comply with laws, regulations and procedures of the States where operating; operator of aeroplanes > 27 000 kg MCTOM shall establish a flight data analysis programme (part of the safety management system, non-punitive); flight safety documents system = organised set of documents (OM etc.) for crews;"],["071.01.01.03:02","EASA LO 071.01.01.03.02 → State the condition(s) required for the establishment of a flight data analysis programme, and state what this programme is part of.","General: comply with laws, regulations and procedures of the States where operating; operator of aeroplanes > 27 000 kg MCTOM shall establish a flight data analysis programme (part of the safety management system, non-punitive); flight safety documents system = organised set of documents (OM etc.) for crews;"],["071.01.02.01:02","EASA LO 071.01.02.01.02 → State the nature of CAT operations and exceptions.","CAT = carriage of passengers, cargo or mail for remuneration; exceptions e.g. A-to-A flights, cost-shared flights under conditions, introductory flights."],["071.01.02.02:01","EASA LO 071.01.02.02.01 → Explain why CAT flights must meet the applicable operational requirements.","General: CAT flights must meet the rules to guarantee an equivalent safety level. HV envelope (helicopters): height-velocity combinations from which a safe landing after engine failure is not assured. HEMS: helicopter emergency medical service. / CAT = carriage of passengers, cargo or mail for remuneration; exceptions e.g. A-to-A flights, cost-shared flights under conditions, introductory flights."],["071.01.02.02:02","EASA LO 071.01.02.02.02 → Define ‘flight manual limitations — flight through the height velocity (HV) envelope’.","General: CAT flights must meet the rules to guarantee an equivalent safety level. HV envelope (helicopters): height-velocity combinations from which a safe landing after engine failure is not assured. HEMS: helicopter emergency medical service."],["071.01.02.02:05","EASA LO 071.01.02.02.05 → Define ‘local area operations — approval’.","Hostile environment: where a safe forced landing is not possible or occupants cannot be protected/rescued — conflict zones information from EASA CZIBs, State warnings, operator risk assessment. Local area operations: approval for short VFR flights in a defined area."],["071.01.02.02:09","EASA LO 071.01.02.02.09 → Explain which are the regulations concerning the carriage of persons on an aircraft.","Carriage of persons: no person may be in a part of the aircraft not designed for persons unless temporarily permitted by the commander; PEDs: operator policy, commander may prohibit use; drugs/alcohol: persons under the influence shall not be admitted when safety is endangered; nobody shall recklessly endanger the aircraft or persons. / CAT = carriage of passengers, cargo or mail for remuneration; exceptions e.g. A-to-A flights, cost-shared flights under conditions, introductory flights."],["071.01.02.02:12","EASA LO 071.01.02.02.12 → Explain the regulations concerning the endangerment of safety.","General: comply with laws, regulations and procedures of the States where operating; operator of aeroplanes > 27 000 kg MCTOM shall establish a flight data analysis programme (part of the safety management system, non-punitive); flight safety documents system = organised set of documents (OM etc.) for crews;"],["071.01.02.02:13","EASA LO 071.01.02.02.13 → List the documents to be carried on each flight.","General: comply with laws, regulations and procedures of the States where operating; operator of aeroplanes > 27 000 kg MCTOM shall establish a flight data analysis programme (part of the safety management system, non-punitive); flight safety documents system = organised set of documents (OM etc.) for crews;"],["071.01.02.02:14","EASA LO 071.01.02.02.14 → Explain the operator’s responsibility regarding manuals to be carried on board an aircraft.","Detailed rules in ICAO Doc 9284 (Technical Instructions); in an in-flight emergency the PIC informs ATC of DG on board."],["071.01.02.02:15","EASA LO 071.01.02.02.15 → List the additional information and forms to be carried on board an aircraft.","Detailed rules in ICAO Doc 9284 (Technical Instructions); in an in-flight emergency the PIC informs ATC of DG on board."],["071.01.02.02:16","EASA LO 071.01.02.02.16 → List the copies of items of information to be retained on the ground by the operator.","Exemptions: aircraft equipment, catering/cabin items, medical supplies for aeromedical flights, some passenger items. Forbidden items: substances that are explosive/unstable under normal conditions (never transported). Cargo-only DG: larger quantities or higher hazard, crew access possible."],["071.01.02.03:02","EASA LO 071.01.02.03.02 → Explain what the rules applicable to air operator certification are.","Detailed rules in ICAO Doc 9284 (Technical Instructions); in an in-flight emergency the PIC informs ATC of DG on board."],["071.01.02.09:01","EASA LO 071.01.02.09.01 → Explain the requirement regarding flight crew composition and in-flight relief.","Composition: not less than the minimum in the AFM; two pilots for IFR/night CAT in most cases (single-pilot IFR/night only with conditions); in-flight relief by suitably qualified pilots (cruise relief co-pilot above FL 200)."],["071.01.02.09:03","EASA LO 071.01.02.09.03 → Explain the requirement for differences training and familiarisation training.","Conversion training & checking before flying a new type/operator (operator conversion course incl. CRM, line flying under supervision, line check); differences training (new variant/equipment requiring additional knowledge), familiarisation (knowledge only). / Training records kept for all training, checking and qualifications (at least 3 years)."],["071.01.02.09:05","EASA LO 071.01.02.09.05 → Explain the minimum qualification requirements to operate as a commander.","Either pilot's seat: commanders who may operate from the right seat (and co-pilots in the left) need specific training and checking."],["071.01.02.09:10","EASA LO 071.01.02.09.10 → Explain the requirement to operate on more than one aircraft type or variant.","More than one type/variant: limited and approved (OM), crossed recency; flight crew operating both helicopters and aeroplanes: one type of each. / Assignment: ≥ 18 years, medical fitness, cabin crew attestation, completed initial/type/operator training and checks. Senior cabin crew member required when > 1 cabin crew; responsible to the commander for cabin safety. More than one type: max 3 types (4 with approval)."],["071.01.02.09:13","EASA LO 071.01.02.09.13 → Explain the crew members’ responsibilities in the execution of their duties, and define the commander’s authority.","Crew responsibilities: perform duties, report incidents, not fly when unfit (fatigue, alcohol ≥ 0.2 ‰ or within 8 h of drinking, medication), commander's authority over crew and passengers, may disembark persons who endanger safety. Persons on board: commander ensures briefing, seatbelts, safety of all persons. / Cabin crew member = crew member (not flight crew) performing safety duties in the cabin, assigned by the operator."],["071.01.02.10:02","EASA LO 071.01.02.10.02 → Detail the requirements regarding the number and composition of cabin crew.","Cabin crew member = crew member (not flight crew) performing safety duties in the cabin, assigned by the operator. / Additional crew members (not trained) must be distinguishable to passengers."],["071.01.02.10:03","EASA LO 071.01.02.10.03 → Explain the conditions and the additional conditions for assignment to duties.","Additional crew members (not trained) must be distinguishable to passengers. / Cabin crew member = crew member (not flight crew) performing safety duties in the cabin, assigned by the operator."],["071.01.02.10:04","EASA LO 071.01.02.10.04 → Explain the requirements regarding senior cabin crew members.","Assignment: ≥ 18 years, medical fitness, cabin crew attestation, completed initial/type/operator training and checks. Senior cabin crew member required when > 1 cabin crew; responsible to the commander for cabin safety. More than one type: max 3 types (4 with approval). / Additional crew members (not trained) must be distinguishable to passengers."],["071.01.02.12:01","EASA LO 071.01.02.12.01 → Explain the definitions used for the regulation of flight time limitations.","Definitions: FDP (flight duty period) from report until engines off/on-blocks at the end of the last sector; duty period; rest period; acclimatised; standby; split duty; WOCL (window of circadian low 02:00–05:59). / Cumulative limits: duty 60 h / 7 days, 110 h / 14 days, 190 h / 28 days; flight time 100 h / 28 days, 900 h / calendar year, 1 000 h / 12 months."],["071.01.02.12:04","EASA LO 071.01.02.12.04 → Explain the requirements regarding rest periods.","Rest: at home base ≥ 12 h or as long as the preceding duty (whichever greater); away ≥ 10 h (incl. 8 h sleep opportunity) or as long as the preceding duty; recurrent extended rest ≥ 36 h incl. 2 local nights, max 168 h between. / Definitions: FDP (flight duty period) from report until engines off/on-blocks at the end of the last sector; duty period; rest period; acclimatised; standby; split duty; WOCL (window of circadian low 02:00–05:59)."],["071.01.02.12:05","EASA LO 071.01.02.12.05 → Explain the possible extension of flight duty period due to in-flight rest.","Definitions: FDP (flight duty period) from report until engines off/on-blocks at the end of the last sector; duty period; rest period; acclimatised; standby; split duty; WOCL (window of circadian low 02:00–05:59). / Rest: at home base ≥ 12 h or as long as the preceding duty (whichever greater); away ≥ 10 h (incl. 8 h sleep opportunity) or as long as the preceding duty; recurrent extended rest ≥ 36 h incl. 2 local nights, max 168 h between."],["071.02.06.01:02","EASA LO 071.02.06.01.02 → Describe the actions required following a slow decompression.","Strongest when heavy, clean, slow."],["071.02.07.01:01","EASA LO 071.02.07.01.01 → Explain how to identify low-level wind shear.","Take-off/approach precautions: delay departure/approach, use the longest runway, max take-off thrust (no reduced thrust), increased VR/approach speed (within limits), early config, avoid Cb within ~10 NM."],["071.02.07.02:04","EASA LO 071.02.07.02.04 → Describe a microburst and its effects.","Take-off/approach precautions: delay departure/approach, use the longest runway, max take-off thrust (no reduced thrust), increased VR/approach speed (within limits), early config, avoid Cb within ~10 NM."],["071.02.08.01:01","EASA LO 071.02.08.01.01 → Describe the term ‘wake turbulence’.","Strongest when heavy, clean, slow."],["071.02.08.02:02","EASA LO 071.02.08.02.02 → Describe the wind conditions which are worst for wake turbulence near the ground.","Strongest when heavy, clean, slow."],["071.02.09.01:02","EASA LO 071.02.09.01.02 → State the objectives of security.","Reg. (EC) 300/2008 implements and goes beyond Annex 17 in the EU (common rules, stricter). Operators have security training programmes for crew; acts of unlawful interference must be reported to the authority/ATS without delay; aircraft search procedures (checklist of where to search for a bomb, least-risk bomb location)."],["071.02.09.03:01","EASA LO 071.02.09.03.01 → Describe the relationship between Regulation (EC) No 300/2008 and ICAO Annex 17.","Reg. (EC) 300/2008 implements and goes beyond Annex 17 in the EU (common rules, stricter). Operators have security training programmes for crew; acts of unlawful interference must be reported to the authority/ATS without delay; aircraft search procedures (checklist of where to search for a bomb, least-risk bomb location)."],["071.02.09.03:02","EASA LO 071.02.09.03.02 → Explain the requirements regarding training programmes.","Reg. (EC) 300/2008 implements and goes beyond Annex 17 in the EU (common rules, stricter). Operators have security training programmes for crew; acts of unlawful interference must be reported to the authority/ATS without delay; aircraft search procedures (checklist of where to search for a bomb, least-risk bomb location)."],["071.02.10.02:01","EASA LO 071.02.10.02.01 → List some circumstances that may require a ditching, a precautionary landing or an emergency landing.","Commander's discretion: unforeseen circumstances after reporting → FDP may be extended (e.g. up to 2 h, 3 h with augmented crew) after consultation with crew; report to operator. / Ditching: forced landing on water. Precautionary landing: planned landing off-airport or at an aerodrome when continuation is inadvisable (fuel, weather, fire) while power is still available. Emergency landing: forced landing because the flight cannot continue (e.g. engine failure, fire)."],["071.02.10.03:01","EASA LO 071.02.10.03.01 → Describe the briefing to be given to passengers before conducting a precautionary/emergency landing or ditching (including evacuation).","Passenger briefing: brace position, seat belts, life vests, exits, what to leave behind, signal for evacuation; after landing: crew commands evacuation, pax guided away, headcount, first aid, preserve evidence. / Evacuation: aircraft must be stopped and engines shut down first (jet blast/props, fire, slides). CS-25: full evacuation of max passengers through half of the exits in ≤ 90 s."],["071.02.12.01:02","EASA LO 071.02.12.01.02 → Explain that detailed provisions for the transport of dangerous goods by air are contained in the Technical Instructions for the Safe Transport of Dangerous Goods by Air.","Detailed rules in ICAO Doc 9284 (Technical Instructions); in an in-flight emergency the PIC informs ATC of DG on board. / Compatibility & segregation: incompatible classes (e.g. flammable liquids and oxidisers) are stowed apart; radioactive materials kept at minimum distance from persons/animals/film (transport index); dangerous goods list gives UN number, class, packing group, quantities pax/cargo aircraft, special provisions."],["071.02.12.01:03","EASA LO 071.02.12.01.03 → State that in the event of an in-flight emergency, the pilot-in-command must inform the ATC of the transport of dangerous goods by air.","Detailed rules in ICAO Doc 9284 (Technical Instructions); in an in-flight emergency the PIC informs ATC of DG on board."],["071.02.12.02:01","EASA LO 071.02.12.02.01 → Explain the principle of dangerous goods compatibility and segregation.","Detailed rules in ICAO Doc 9284 (Technical Instructions); in an in-flight emergency the PIC informs ATC of DG on board. / Compatibility & segregation: incompatible classes (e.g. flammable liquids and oxidisers) are stowed apart; radioactive materials kept at minimum distance from persons/animals/film (transport index); dangerous goods list gives UN number, class, packing group, quantities pax/cargo aircraft, special provisions."],["071.02.12.03:02","EASA LO 071.02.12.03.02 → Explain the scope of that Regulation.","Applicability: Part-CAT (commercial air transport), Part-SPO (specialised operations, e.g. aerial work), Part-NCC (non-commercial complex), Part-NCO (non-commercial other), Part-ORO (organisation requirements), Part-SPA (specific approvals: PBN, MNPS, RVSM, LVO, DG, ETOPS, HEMS…)."],["071.02.12.03:03","EASA LO 071.02.12.03.03 → Explain why the transport of dangerous goods by air is subject to operator approval.","Applicability: Part-CAT (commercial air transport), Part-SPO (specialised operations, e.g. aerial work), Part-NCC (non-commercial complex), Part-NCO (non-commercial other), Part-ORO (organisation requirements), Part-SPA (specific approvals: PBN, MNPS, RVSM, LVO, DG, ETOPS, HEMS…). / Part-CAT/Part-SPA: DG transport requires operator approval (SPA.DG), training programmes, acceptance checklist, inspection for damage/leakage before loading, written information to the commander (NOTOC), reporting of DG incidents/accidents and undeclared/misdeclared DG (within 72 h)."],["071.02.12.03:04","EASA LO 071.02.12.03.04 → Explain the limitations on the transport of dangerous goods by air.","Applicability: Part-CAT (commercial air transport), Part-SPO (specialised operations, e.g. aerial work), Part-NCC (non-commercial complex), Part-NCO (non-commercial other), Part-ORO (organisation requirements), Part-SPA (specific approvals: PBN, MNPS, RVSM, LVO, DG, ETOPS, HEMS…). / Part-CAT/Part-SPA: DG transport requires operator approval (SPA.DG), training programmes, acceptance checklist, inspection for damage/leakage before loading, written information to the commander (NOTOC), reporting of DG incidents/accidents and undeclared/misdeclared DG (within 72 h)."],["071.02.12.03:05","EASA LO 071.02.12.03.05 → Explain the requirements for the acceptance of dangerous goods.","Part-CAT/Part-SPA: DG transport requires operator approval (SPA.DG), training programmes, acceptance checklist, inspection for damage/leakage before loading, written information to the commander (NOTOC), reporting of DG incidents/accidents and undeclared/misdeclared DG (within 72 h). / Applicability: Part-CAT (commercial air transport), Part-SPO (specialised operations, e.g. aerial work), Part-NCC (non-commercial complex), Part-NCO (non-commercial other), Part-ORO (organisation requirements), Part-SPA (specific approvals: PBN, MNPS, RVSM, LVO, DG, ETOPS, HEMS…)."],["071.02.12.03:07","EASA LO 071.02.12.03.07 → Explain the requirement for the provision of information to flight crew.","Part-CAT/Part-SPA: DG transport requires operator approval (SPA.DG), training programmes, acceptance checklist, inspection for damage/leakage before loading, written information to the commander (NOTOC), reporting of DG incidents/accidents and undeclared/misdeclared DG (within 72 h)."],["071.02.12.03:08","EASA LO 071.02.12.03.08 → Explain the requirements for dangerous goods incident and accident reports.","DG incident — injury, damage, fire, spillage or leakage, other than an accident (or which seriously jeopardises the aircraft); exemption — authorisation by the State relieving from the TIs; incompatible — DG that could react dangerously if mixed; packaging — receptacles meeting TI specs; / Applicability: Part-CAT (commercial air transport), Part-SPO (specialised operations, e.g. aerial work), Part-NCC (non-commercial complex), Part-NCO (non-commercial other), Part-ORO (organisation requirements), Part-SPA (specific approvals: PBN, MNPS, RVSM, LVO, DG, ETOPS, HEMS…)."],["071.02.12.03:10","EASA LO 071.02.12.03.10 → Explain why some articles and substances may be forbidden for transport by air.","Exemptions: aircraft equipment, catering/cabin items, medical supplies for aeromedical flights, some passenger items. Forbidden items: substances that are explosive/unstable under normal conditions (never transported). Cargo-only DG: larger quantities or higher hazard, crew access possible. / Applicability: Part-CAT (commercial air transport), Part-SPO (specialised operations, e.g. aerial work), Part-NCC (non-commercial complex), Part-NCO (non-commercial other), Part-ORO (organisation requirements), Part-SPA (specific approvals: PBN, MNPS, RVSM, LVO, DG, ETOPS, HEMS…)."],["071.02.12.03:12","EASA LO 071.02.12.03.12 → Explain the need for an inspection prior to loading dangerous goods on an aircraft.","Part-CAT/Part-SPA: DG transport requires operator approval (SPA.DG), training programmes, acceptance checklist, inspection for damage/leakage before loading, written information to the commander (NOTOC), reporting of DG incidents/accidents and undeclared/misdeclared DG (within 72 h)."],["071.02.12.03:14","EASA LO 071.02.12.03.14 → Explain how misdeclared or undeclared dangerous goods found in baggage are to be reported.","Part-CAT/Part-SPA: DG transport requires operator approval (SPA.DG), training programmes, acceptance checklist, inspection for damage/leakage before loading, written information to the commander (NOTOC), reporting of DG incidents/accidents and undeclared/misdeclared DG (within 72 h)."]],"67":[["062.03.01.01:01","Pulse technique: fill in the gaps → Applications: ____ (primary PSR, secondary SSR, precision approach radar, surface movement radar), ____ ____ (ground weather radar), airborne weather radar (AWR).","Applications: ATC surveillance (primary PSR, secondary SSR, precision approach radar, surface movement radar), weather observation (ground weather radar), airborne weather radar (AWR)."],["062.03.01.01:02","Pulse technique: fill in the gaps → ____: a short high-power pulse is transmitted, reflected by the target and received; range = c × t / ____ (≈ ____ round trip). Direction from antenna pointing.","Primary radar = echo principle: a short high-power pulse is transmitted, reflected by the target and received; range = c × t / 2 (≈ 12.36 µs per NM round trip). Direction from antenna pointing."],["062.03.01.01:03","Pulse technique: fill in the gaps → Range depends on ____ (max unambiguous range = c/(2 × PRF) — lower PRF → longer range), ____ (longer → more energy/range but worse min range and resolution), pulse power, aircraft and antenna height (line of sight: ____), frequency (higher → more attenuation, better resolution) and antenna gain/beam width.","Range depends on PRF (max unambiguous range = c/(2 × PRF) — lower PRF → longer range), pulse length (longer → more energy/range but worse min range and resolution), pulse power, aircraft and antenna height (line of sight: 1.23 √h), frequency (higher → more attenuation, better resolution) and antenna gain/beam width."],["062.03.02.01:01","Ground radar: fill in the gaps → ____ gives ____ of any reflecting target, ____; needed to see aircraft without a transponder (or with a failed one) and for weather/clutter.","Primary radar gives bearing and distance of any reflecting target, no identity or altitude; needed to see aircraft without a transponder (or with a failed one) and for weather/clutter."],["062.03.02.02:01","Ground radar: fill in the gaps → Modern ATC displays use ____ — inputs from several PSR/SSR/Mode S radars, ____, multilateration → fused tracks with labels (callsign, FL, GS).","Modern ATC displays use multi-sensor tracking — inputs from several PSR/SSR/Mode S radars, ADS-B, multilateration → fused tracks with labels (callsign, FL, GS)."],["062.03.03.06:03","Airborne weather radar: fill in the gaps → Two tasks: ____ (precipitation, turbulence areas) and ____. Wavelength ≈ ____ (X-band ~9.3 GHz) → best reflection from wet hail and large raindrops; poor from dry hail, snow, ice crystals; clouds/CAT not detected.","Two tasks: weather detection (precipitation, turbulence areas) and navigation (ground mapping). Wavelength ≈ 3 cm (X-band ~9.3 GHz) → best reflection from wet hail and large raindrops; poor from dry hail, snow, ice crystals; clouds/CAT not detected."],["062.03.03.01:04","Airborne weather radar: fill in the gaps → Antenna is ____ in pitch and roll using the aircraft ____ reference (IRS/AHRS). Pencil beam (conical) ____ wide for weather; fan-shaped (cosecant²) beam for mapping (older sets).","Antenna is stabilised in pitch and roll using the aircraft attitude reference (IRS/AHRS). Pencil beam (conical) ~3–5° wide for weather; fan-shaped (cosecant²) beam for mapping (older sets)."],["062.03.03.02:01","Airborne weather radar: fill in the gaps → ____: OFF/ON (standby for warm-up), ____ (weather), ____ (weather + turbulence/Doppler, limited range ~40 NM), MAP (ground mapping), TEST; gain (AUTO calibrated for weather, MANUAL to analyse cells or for mapping); tilt (beam elevation, AUTOTILT adjusts with altitude/range).","Controls: OFF/ON (standby for warm-up), modes WX (weather), WX+T (weather + turbulence/Doppler, limited range ~40 NM), MAP (ground mapping), TEST; gain (AUTO calibrated for weather, MANUAL to analyse cells or for mapping); tilt (beam elevation, AUTOTILT adjusts with altitude/range)."],["062.03.03.02:03","Airborne weather radar: fill in the gaps → Colours: ____ (light) → ____ (moderate) → ____ (heavy) → magenta (very heavy/turbulence). Azimuth markers and range rings give relative bearing and distance of cells.","Colours: green (light) → yellow (moderate) → red (heavy) → magenta (very heavy/turbulence). Azimuth markers and range rings give relative bearing and distance of cells."],["062.03.03.01:01","Airborne weather radar: fill in the gaps → ____: tilt down, MAP mode — coastlines, lakes, cities (strong returns) vs water (dark) → fix position.","Mapping: tilt down, MAP mode — coastlines, lakes, cities (strong returns) vs water (dark) → fix position."],["062.03.03.01:01","Airborne weather radar: fill in the gaps → ____: high-power microwave radiation → danger to personnel (and fuel vapours); do not operate when people are within a few metres in front, during refuelling, or in hangars.","On the ground: high-power microwave radiation → danger to personnel (and fuel vapours); do not operate when people are within a few metres in front, during refuelling, or in hangars."],["062.03.03.05:01","Airborne weather radar: fill in the gaps → ____: behind heavy precipitation the beam is ____ → no echo → looks clear but may hide severe weather (\"radar shadow\"); avoid black holes behind red cells.","Shadow area: behind heavy precipitation the beam is attenuated → no echo → looks clear but may hide severe weather (\"radar shadow\"); avoid black holes behind red cells."],["062.03.03.05:03","Airborne weather radar: fill in the gaps → ____: too high → beam overshoots the ____ of the storm (tops with ice crystals reflect poorly) → Cb not detected; typical practice: tilt down until ground returns appear at the edge, then raise slightly; at high altitude tilt down, at low altitude tilt up.","Tilt: too high → beam overshoots the wet lower part of the storm (tops with ice crystals reflect poorly) → Cb not detected; typical practice: tilt down until ground returns appear at the edge, then raise slightly; at high altitude tilt down, at low altitude tilt up."],["062.03.03.01:01","Airborne weather radar: fill in the gaps → ____: deviate ____ of cells, ____ from strong returns at high level (10 NM low level), don't fly between close cells, avoid areas with steep colour gradients, hooks, fingers, scalloped edges.","Avoidance: deviate upwind of cells, ≥ 20 NM from strong returns at high level (10 NM low level), don't fly between close cells, avoid areas with steep colour gradients, hooks, fingers, scalloped edges."],["062.03.03.06:04","Airborne weather radar: fill in the gaps → ____ (not CAT): Doppler frequency-shift spread of precipitation particles (____ mode, magenta) within ~40 NM. ____ (predictive, PWS): Doppler detection of divergent wind in microbursts ahead of the aircraft below ~1 200–1 500 ft AGL, 3–5 NM ahead → \"WINDSHEAR AHEAD\".","Turbulence (not CAT): Doppler frequency-shift spread of precipitation particles (WX+T mode, magenta) within ~40 NM. Wind shear (predictive, PWS): Doppler detection of divergent wind in microbursts ahead of the aircraft below ~1 200–1 500 ft AGL, 3–5 NM ahead → \"WINDSHEAR AHEAD\"."],["062.03.04.01:01","SSR & transponder: fill in the gaps → ATC system based on ____ from airborne transponders to interrogations from ground ____ and from ____. SSR provides information PSR cannot: identity (code), pressure altitude, Mode S data. Advantages: much longer range with less power (one-way-ish losses), no clutter, active participation.","ATC system based on replies from airborne transponders to interrogations from ground SSR and from TCAS. SSR provides information PSR cannot: identity (code), pressure altitude, Mode S data. Advantages: much longer range with less power (one-way-ish losses), no clutter, active participation."],["062.03.04.02:04","SSR & transponder: fill in the gaps → ____: series of pulse pairs (P1–P3) on ____; reply on ____ (different frequencies). Spacing P1–P3 defines the mode: Mode A (8 µs) → identity code; Mode C (21 µs) → pressure altitude; Mode S → selective address, data link.","Interrogation: series of pulse pairs (P1–P3) on 1 030 MHz; reply on 1 090 MHz (different frequencies). Spacing P1–P3 defines the mode: Mode A (8 µs) → identity code; Mode C (21 µs) → pressure altitude; Mode S → selective address, data link."],["062.03.04.01:01","SSR & transponder: fill in the gaps → Mode A: 4 octal digits (0–7) → ____. Special codes: ____ unlawful interference, ____ radio failure, 7700 emergency, 2000 entering an SSR area without assignment (IFR), 7000 VFR conspicuity (Europe), 1200 VFR (USA).","Mode A: 4 octal digits (0–7) → 4 096 codes. Special codes: 7500 unlawful interference, 7600 radio failure, 7700 emergency, 2000 entering an SSR area without assignment (IFR), 7000 VFR conspicuity (Europe), 1200 VFR (USA)."],["062.03.04.02:06","SSR & transponder: fill in the gaps → ____ reports pressure altitude (reference ____) in ____ increments; ATC converts to altitude using QNH.","Mode C reports pressure altitude (reference 1013.25 hPa) in 100 ft increments; ATC converts to altitude using QNH."],["062.03.04.02:07","SSR & transponder: fill in the gaps → ____ (special position identification) pulse only when the pilot presses ____ (on ATC request) — highlights the target for ~18 s.","SPI (special position identification) pulse only when the pilot presses IDENT (on ATC request) — highlights the target for ~18 s."],["062.03.04.02:10","SSR & transponder: fill in the gaps → ____: compatible with Mode A/C (answers A/C interrogations); receives interrogations from SSR and TCAS; interrogation contains the aircraft's ____ (hard-coded, unique), a selective call or an all-call; ____ increments; downlink of aircraft parameters (ELS: address, callsign, altitude;","Mode S: compatible with Mode A/C (answers A/C interrogations); receives interrogations from SSR and TCAS; interrogation contains the aircraft's 24-bit ICAO address (hard-coded, unique), a selective call or an all-call; altitude in 25-ft increments; downlink of aircraft parameters (ELS: address, callsign, altitude;"],["062.03.04.01:01","SSR & transponder: fill in the gaps → ____: selected altitude, heading, speed, roll angle, etc.); the extended squitter supports ____.","EHS: selected altitude, heading, speed, roll angle, etc.); the extended squitter supports ADS-B."],["062.03.04.03:01","SSR & transponder: fill in the gaps → ____: unique code ident; label with ____ ____, GS, selected level, etc.","Display: unique code ident; label with callsign/code, pressure altitude/FL, GS, selected level, etc."],["062.03.04.03:03","SSR & transponder: fill in the gaps → ____: ____; ____ (warm, no replies — select on ground/when changing codes in the past); ON (Mode A only, no altitude); ALT (Mode A + C + S incl. altitude — normal); TEST (self-test); reply lamp flashes when replying/interrogated.","Selector: OFF; STBY (warm, no replies — select on ground/when changing codes in the past); ON (Mode A only, no altitude); ALT (Mode A + C + S incl. altitude — normal); TEST (self-test); reply lamp flashes when replying/interrogated."],["062.03.04.02:03","Exam trap (SSR & transponder): complete the fact → interrogation ____MHz, reply ____MHz.","interrogation 1 030 MHz, reply 1 090 MHz."],["062.03.04.02:06","Exam trap (SSR & transponder): complete the fact → Mode C = pressure altitude (____), ____ steps; Mode S 25 ft.","Mode C = pressure altitude (1013.25), 100 ft steps; Mode S 25 ft."],["062.03.02.01:02","EASA LO 062.03.02.01.02 → Explain that primary ground radar is used to detect aircraft that are not equipped with a secondary radar transponder.","Applications: ATC surveillance (primary PSR, secondary SSR, precision approach radar, surface movement radar), weather observation (ground weather radar), airborne weather radar (AWR)."],["062.03.03.01:01","EASA LO 062.03.03.01.01 → List the two main tasks of the weather radar in respect of weather and navigation.","Two tasks: weather detection (precipitation, turbulence areas) and navigation (ground mapping). Wavelength ≈ 3 cm (X-band ~9.3 GHz) → best reflection from wet hail and large raindrops; poor from dry hail, snow, ice crystals; clouds/CAT not detected."],["062.03.03.01:02","EASA LO 062.03.03.01.02 → State that modern weather radars employ frequencies that give wavelengths of about 3 cm that reflect best on wet hailstones.","Two tasks: weather detection (precipitation, turbulence areas) and navigation (ground mapping). Wavelength ≈ 3 cm (X-band ~9.3 GHz) → best reflection from wet hail and large raindrops; poor from dry hail, snow, ice crystals; clouds/CAT not detected."],["062.03.03.01:03","EASA LO 062.03.03.01.03 → State that the antenna is stabilised in the horizontal plane with signals from the aircraft’s attitude reference system.","Antenna is stabilised in pitch and roll using the aircraft attitude reference (IRS/AHRS). Pencil beam (conical) ~3–5° wide for weather; fan-shaped (cosecant²) beam for mapping (older sets)."],["062.03.03.02:02","EASA LO 062.03.03.02.02 → Name, for areas of differing reflection intensity, the colour gradations (green, yellow, red and magenta) indicating the increasing intensity of precipitation.","Colours: green (light) → yellow (moderate) → red (heavy) → magenta (very heavy/turbulence). Azimuth markers and range rings give relative bearing and distance of cells."],["062.03.03.03:01","EASA LO 062.03.03.03.01 → Explain how the radar is used for weather detection and for mapping (range, tilt and gain, if available).","Controls: OFF/ON (standby for warm-up), modes WX (weather), WX+T (weather + turbulence/Doppler, limited range ~40 NM), MAP (ground mapping), TEST; gain (AUTO calibrated for weather, MANUAL to analyse cells or for mapping); tilt (beam elevation, AUTOTILT adjusts with altitude/range)."],["062.03.04.01:02","EASA LO 062.03.04.01.02 → State that the ground ATC secondary radar uses techniques which provide the ATC with information that cannot be acquired by the primary radar.","Applications: ATC surveillance (primary PSR, secondary SSR, precision approach radar, surface movement radar), weather observation (ground weather radar), airborne weather radar (AWR). / ATC system based on replies from airborne transponders to interrogations from ground SSR and from TCAS. SSR provides information PSR cannot: identity (code), pressure altitude, Mode S data. Advantages: much longer range with less power (one-way-ish losses), no clutter, active participation."],["062.03.04.01:04","EASA LO 062.03.04.01.04 → State the advantages of secondary surveillance radar (SSR) over a primary radar regarding range and collected information due to transponder principal information and active participation of the aircraft.","ATC system based on replies from airborne transponders to interrogations from ground SSR and from TCAS. SSR provides information PSR cannot: identity (code), pressure altitude, Mode S data. Advantages: much longer range with less power (one-way-ish losses), no clutter, active participation. / Applications: ATC surveillance (primary PSR, secondary SSR, precision approach radar, surface movement radar), weather observation (ground weather radar), airborne weather radar (AWR)."],["062.03.04.02:01","EASA LO 062.03.04.02.01 → State that the interrogator transmits its interrogations in the form of a series of pulse pairs.","Interrogation: series of pulse pairs (P1–P3) on 1 030 MHz; reply on 1 090 MHz (different frequencies). Spacing P1–P3 defines the mode: Mode A (8 µs) → identity code; Mode C (21 µs) → pressure altitude; Mode S → selective address, data link."],["062.03.04.02:02","EASA LO 062.03.04.02.02 → Name the interrogation modes: — Mode A; — Mode C; — Mode S.","Mode C = pressure altitude (1013.25), 100 ft steps; Mode S 25 ft."],["062.03.04.02:09","EASA LO 062.03.04.02.09 → Explain that Mode S transponders receive interrogations from TCAS and SSR ground stations.","ATC system based on replies from airborne transponders to interrogations from ground SSR and from TCAS. SSR provides information PSR cannot: identity (code), pressure altitude, Mode S data. Advantages: much longer range with less power (one-way-ish losses), no clutter, active participation. / Mode S: compatible with Mode A/C (answers A/C interrogations); receives interrogations from SSR and TCAS; interrogation contains the aircraft's 24-bit ICAO address (hard-coded, unique), a selective call or an all-call; altitude in 25-ft increments; downlink of aircraft parameters (ELS: address, callsign, altitude;"],["062.03.04.02:11","EASA LO 062.03.04.02.11 → State that every aircraft is allocated an ICAO aircraft address, which is hard-coded into the Mode S transponder (Mode S address).","Mode S: compatible with Mode A/C (answers A/C interrogations); receives interrogations from SSR and TCAS; interrogation contains the aircraft's 24-bit ICAO address (hard-coded, unique), a selective call or an all-call; altitude in 25-ft increments; downlink of aircraft parameters (ELS: address, callsign, altitude;"],["062.03.04.02:12","EASA LO 062.03.04.02.12 → Explain that a 24-bit address is used in all Mode S transmissions, so that every interrogation can be directed to a specific aircraft.","Mode S: compatible with Mode A/C (answers A/C interrogations); receives interrogations from SSR and TCAS; interrogation contains the aircraft's 24-bit ICAO address (hard-coded, unique), a selective call or an all-call; altitude in 25-ft increments; downlink of aircraft parameters (ELS: address, callsign, altitude;"],["062.03.04.02:13","EASA LO 062.03.04.02.13 → State that Mode S can provide enhanced vertical tracking, using a 25-ft altitude increment.","Mode C = pressure altitude (1013.25), 100 ft steps; Mode S 25 ft. / Mode C reports pressure altitude (reference 1013.25 hPa) in 100 ft increments; ATC converts to altitude using QNH."],["062.03.04.03:02","EASA LO 062.03.04.03.02 → State which information can be presented on the ATC display system: — pressure altitude; — flight level;","Display: unique code ident; label with callsign/code, pressure altitude/FL, GS, selected level, etc. / ATC system based on replies from airborne transponders to interrogations from ground SSR and from TCAS. SSR provides information PSR cannot: identity (code), pressure altitude, Mode S data. Advantages: much longer range with less power (one-way-ish losses), no clutter, active participation."]],"68":[["040.02.01.01:01","How does the atmosphere's composition change with altitude, and what falls?","The percentages stay constant (O₂ 21 %, N₂ 78 %, 1 % other). Total pressure falls, so the partial pressure of O₂ falls (Dalton). Pressure halves at ~18 000 ft (500 hPa), quarter at ~34 000 ft."],["040.02.01.02:01","What is the main stimulus for breathing?","CO₂ (not O₂): chemoreceptors sense CO₂ / pH."],["040.02.01.02:26","Hypoxia types and the key altitude thresholds.","Hypoxic (low O₂ partial pressure) and anaemic (reduced carrying capacity: CO, anaemia, smoking, blood loss); also stagnant and histotoxic. Thresholds: reaction 7 000 ft, disturbance 10 000–12 000 ft, critical 22 000 ft. Night vision loss from 5 000 ft."],["040.02.01.02:29","TUC / EPT: values at rest for 20 000, 25 000, 30 000, 35 000, 40 000 ft.","20 000 ft ~30 min; 25 000 ft 3–5 min; 30 000 ft 1–2 min; 35 000 ft 30–60 s; 40 000 ft 15–20 s. Halved after rapid decompression or activity."],["040.02.01.02:24","Hyperventilation: cause, symptoms, remedies, and overlap with hypoxia.","Over-breathing washes out CO₂ so the blood turns alkaline and cerebral vessels constrict. Causes: anxiety, stress, pain, heat, vibration, hypoxia. Symptoms: dizziness, tingling, spasms, visual disturbance, unconsciousness. Remedies: breathe slowly, talk loudly, paper bag. Symptoms overlap with hypoxia, so in doubt give oxygen."],["040.02.01.02:24","Carbon monoxide: how does it act and what do you do?","Binds to haemoglobin ~200–250× stronger than O₂ (anaemic hypoxia). Symptoms: headache, dizziness, nausea, drowsiness, cherry-red lips. Immediately: heater off, fresh air, 100 % oxygen, land; no flying until medically cleared."],["040.02.01.02:42","Decompression sickness: what are the four forms, and waiting times after diving?","Dissolved nitrogen forms bubbles: bends (joints), creeps (skin), chokes (lungs), staggers (nervous system). Treatment: 100 % O₂, descend, recompression chamber. Wait 12 h after dives without deco stops, 24 h after dives with deco stops or multiple dives (cabin > 8 000 ft); after loss of pressurisation no flight for 24 h."],["040.02.01.02:47","G-forces +Gz: which effects at which G?","~3 G greyout, ~4 G blackout (vision lost, conscious), ~5 G G-LOC (unconscious). Depends on magnitude, duration, onset rate. −Gz gives redout. Counter: anti-G straining, G-suit, tensing muscles."],["040.02.01.03:04","Ozone, radiation, cabin humidity: key facts.","Ozone rises above ~40 000 ft (enters with bleed air; catalytic converters; irritates eyes/airways). Cosmic/solar radiation is higher at altitude and latitude (polar routes), risk: cancer, foetal damage. Cabin RH 5–20 %: drink water, no coffee/alcohol, eye drops."],["040.03.01.03:01","Working memory: capacity and duration, and how to improve it.","Short-term/working memory ~5 ± 2 items (Miller classic 7 ± 2), 10–20 s without rehearsal, disrupted by interruptions. Improve by chunking, rehearsal, write-down, mnemonics. Sensory memory < 1 s (iconic) / 2–8 s (echoic)."],["040.03.01.04:09","Rasmussen levels and Anderson skill phases.","Rasmussen: skill-based (automatic, risk slips), rule-based (if-then / SOPs, risk wrong rule), knowledge-based (novel, slow, error-prone under stress). Anderson: cognitive → associative → autonomous."],["040.03.02.02:02","Definition of situation awareness and how to regain it.","Endsley: perception of elements, comprehension of their meaning, projection of future status. Regain: fly the aircraft, increase monitoring, gather information, communicate, reduce workload/automation level."],["040.03.02.03:05","Error types: slip, lapse, mistake, violation; active vs latent.","Slip = right intention, wrong execution. Lapse = forgotten step. Mistake = wrong plan/knowledge. Violation = deliberate deviation. Active errors: front-line, immediate effect. Latent: design/management/training decisions, dormant. Reason's Swiss cheese: breaking any link prevents the accident."],["040.03.03.01:09","Decision-making models and group effects.","FOR-DEC: Facts, Options, Risks & benefits, Decide, Execute, Check. Also DECIDE, GRADE. Group effects: + cross-checking; − risky shift, groupthink, conformity, authority gradient. Biases: confirmation, anchoring, plan continuation (press-on-itis)."],["040.03.06.02:18","Yerkes–Dodson and General Adaptation Syndrome.","Performance follows an inverted U with arousal (best at moderate; too low = underload, too high = overload). Selye GAS: alarm → resistance → exhaustion (chronic stress)."],["040.03.06.05:01","Stress and fatigue: stressor types and fatigue forms.","Stressors: physical/environmental, physiological, psychological/cognitive, domestic/social. Stress = perceived demand exceeding perceived ability. Fatigue: acute (long duty, lack of sleep, recovered by rest) vs chronic (accumulated, needs longer recovery). FRMS = data-driven fatigue risk management."],["100.02.03.00:09","Five core competencies (KSA 100.02).","Communication; leadership & teamwork; problem solving & decision-making; situation awareness; workload management."],["040.02.01.02:23","EASA LO 040.02.01.02.23 → Define the two major forms of hypoxia (hypoxic and anaemic), and the common causes of both.","Hypoxic (low O₂ partial pressure) and anaemic (reduced carrying capacity: CO, anaemia, smoking, blood loss); also stagnant and histotoxic. Thresholds: reaction 7 000 ft, disturbance 10 000–12 000 ft, critical 22 000 ft. Night vision loss from 5 000 ft."],["040.02.01.02:25","EASA LO 040.02.01.02.25 → State that healthy people are able to compensate for altitudes up to approximately 10 000–12 000 ft.","Hypoxic (low O₂ partial pressure) and anaemic (reduced carrying capacity: CO, anaemia, smoking, blood loss); also stagnant and histotoxic. Thresholds: reaction 7 000 ft, disturbance 10 000–12 000 ft, critical 22 000 ft. Night vision loss from 5 000 ft. / 20 000 ft ~30 min; 25 000 ft 3–5 min; 30 000 ft 1–2 min; 35 000 ft 30–60 s; 40 000 ft 15–20 s. Halved after rapid decompression or activity."],["040.02.01.02:31","EASA LO 040.02.01.02.31 → State the equivalent altitudes when breathing ambient air and 100 % oxygen at mean sea level (MSL) and at approximately 10 000, 30 000 and 40 000 ft.","20 000 ft ~30 min; 25 000 ft 3–5 min; 30 000 ft 1–2 min; 35 000 ft 30–60 s; 40 000 ft 15–20 s. Halved after rapid decompression or activity. / Hypoxic (low O₂ partial pressure) and anaemic (reduced carrying capacity: CO, anaemia, smoking, blood loss); also stagnant and histotoxic. Thresholds: reaction 7 000 ft, disturbance 10 000–12 000 ft, critical 22 000 ft. Night vision loss from 5 000 ft."],["040.02.01.02:34","EASA LO 040.02.01.02.34 → List the factors that cause hyperventilation.","Over-breathing washes out CO₂ so the blood turns alkaline and cerebral vessels constrict. Causes: anxiety, stress, pain, heat, vibration, hypoxia. Symptoms: dizziness, tingling, spasms, visual disturbance, unconsciousness. Remedies: breathe slowly, talk loudly, paper bag. Symptoms overlap with hypoxia, so in doubt give oxygen."],["040.02.01.02:36","EASA LO 040.02.01.02.36 → List the signs and symptoms of hyperventilation.","Over-breathing washes out CO₂ so the blood turns alkaline and cerebral vessels constrict. Causes: anxiety, stress, pain, heat, vibration, hypoxia. Symptoms: dizziness, tingling, spasms, visual disturbance, unconsciousness. Remedies: breathe slowly, talk loudly, paper bag. Symptoms overlap with hypoxia, so in doubt give oxygen."],["040.02.01.02:37","EASA LO 040.02.01.02.37 → List the measures which may be taken to counteract hyperventilation: breath slowly, close one opening of the nose, speak loudly, place a paper bag over nose and mouth.","Over-breathing washes out CO₂ so the blood turns alkaline and cerebral vessels constrict. Causes: anxiety, stress, pain, heat, vibration, hypoxia. Symptoms: dizziness, tingling, spasms, visual disturbance, unconsciousness. Remedies: breathe slowly, talk loudly, paper bag. Symptoms overlap with hypoxia, so in doubt give oxygen."],["040.02.01.02:51","EASA LO 040.02.01.02.51 → List the signs and symptoms of carbon-monoxide poisoning.","Binds to haemoglobin ~200–250× stronger than O₂ (anaemic hypoxia). Symptoms: headache, dizziness, nausea, drowsiness, cherry-red lips. Immediately: heater off, fresh air, 100 % oxygen, land; no flying until medically cleared."],["040.02.01.03:01","EASA LO 040.02.01.03.01 → State how an increase in altitude may change the proportion of ozone in the atmosphere and that aircraft can be equipped with special ozone removers.","Ozone rises above ~40 000 ft (enters with bleed air; catalytic converters; irritates eyes/airways). Cosmic/solar radiation is higher at altitude and latitude (polar routes), risk: cancer, foetal damage. Cabin RH 5–20 %: drink water, no coffee/alcohol, eye drops."],["040.02.01.03:02","EASA LO 040.02.01.03.02 → State the sources of radiation at high altitude.","Ozone rises above ~40 000 ft (enters with bleed air; catalytic converters; irritates eyes/airways). Cosmic/solar radiation is higher at altitude and latitude (polar routes), risk: cancer, foetal damage. Cabin RH 5–20 %: drink water, no coffee/alcohol, eye drops."],["040.02.01.03:03","EASA LO 040.02.01.03.03 → List the effects of excessive exposure to radiation. X Humidity","Ozone rises above ~40 000 ft (enters with bleed air; catalytic converters; irritates eyes/airways). Cosmic/solar radiation is higher at altitude and latitude (polar routes), risk: cancer, foetal damage. Cabin RH 5–20 %: drink water, no coffee/alcohol, eye drops."],["040.02.01.03:05","EASA LO 040.02.01.03.05 → List the effects of low humidity on human body to be spurious thirst, dry eyes, skin and mucous membranes, and indicate measures that can be taken: drinking water, using eye drops and aqueous creams.","Ozone rises above ~40 000 ft (enters with bleed air; catalytic converters; irritates eyes/airways). Cosmic/solar radiation is higher at altitude and latitude (polar routes), risk: cancer, foetal damage. Cabin RH 5–20 %: drink water, no coffee/alcohol, eye drops."],["040.03.01.03:02","EASA LO 040.03.01.03.02 → Describe the differences between the types of memory in terms of capacity and retention time.","Short-term/working memory ~5 ± 2 items (Miller classic 7 ± 2), 10–20 s without rehearsal, disrupted by interruptions. Improve by chunking, rehearsal, write-down, mnemonics. Sensory memory < 1 s (iconic) / 2–8 s (echoic)."],["040.03.01.03:04","EASA LO 040.03.01.03.04 → State the average maximum number of separate items that may be held in working memory (5 ± 2).","Short-term/working memory ~5 ± 2 items (Miller classic 7 ± 2), 10–20 s without rehearsal, disrupted by interruptions. Improve by chunking, rehearsal, write-down, mnemonics. Sensory memory < 1 s (iconic) / 2–8 s (echoic)."],["040.03.01.03:05","EASA LO 040.03.01.03.05 → Stress how interruption can affect short-term/working memory.","Short-term/working memory ~5 ± 2 items (Miller classic 7 ± 2), 10–20 s without rehearsal, disrupted by interruptions. Improve by chunking, rehearsal, write-down, mnemonics. Sensory memory < 1 s (iconic) / 2–8 s (echoic)."],["040.03.01.03:06","EASA LO 040.03.01.03.06 → Give examples of items that are important for pilots to hold in working memory during flight.","Short-term/working memory ~5 ± 2 items (Miller classic 7 ± 2), 10–20 s without rehearsal, disrupted by interruptions. Improve by chunking, rehearsal, write-down, mnemonics. Sensory memory < 1 s (iconic) / 2–8 s (echoic)."],["040.03.01.03:07","EASA LO 040.03.01.03.07 → Describe how the capacity of the working-memory store may be increased.","Short-term/working memory ~5 ± 2 items (Miller classic 7 ± 2), 10–20 s without rehearsal, disrupted by interruptions. Improve by chunking, rehearsal, write-down, mnemonics. Sensory memory < 1 s (iconic) / 2–8 s (echoic)."],["040.03.01.03:10","EASA LO 040.03.01.03.10 → Describe amnesia and how it affects memory.","Short-term/working memory ~5 ± 2 items (Miller classic 7 ± 2), 10–20 s without rehearsal, disrupted by interruptions. Improve by chunking, rehearsal, write-down, mnemonics. Sensory memory < 1 s (iconic) / 2–8 s (echoic)."],["040.03.01.04:05","EASA LO 040.03.01.04.05 → Describe the advantage of planning and anticipation of future actions: — define the term ‘skills’; — state the three phases of learning a skill (Anderson: cognitive, associative and autonomous phase).","Rasmussen: skill-based (automatic, risk slips), rule-based (if-then / SOPs, risk wrong rule), knowledge-based (novel, slow, error-prone under stress). Anderson: cognitive → associative → autonomous."],["040.03.02.02:01","EASA LO 040.03.02.02.01 → Define the term ‘situation awareness’.","Communication; leadership & teamwork; problem solving & decision-making; situation awareness; workload management. / Endsley: perception of elements, comprehension of their meaning, projection of future status. Regain: fly the aircraft, increase monitoring, gather information, communicate, reduce workload/automation level."],["040.03.02.02:03","EASA LO 040.03.02.02.03 → List the factors that influence one’s situation awareness both positively and negatively, and stress the importance of situation awareness in the context of flight safety.","Communication; leadership & teamwork; problem solving & decision-making; situation awareness; workload management."],["040.03.03.01:01","EASA LO 040.03.03.01.01 → Define the terms ‘deciding’ and ‘decision-making’.","Communication; leadership & teamwork; problem solving & decision-making; situation awareness; workload management."],["040.03.03.01:06","EASA LO 040.03.03.01.06 → State the factors upon which an individual’s risk assessment is based.","Rasmussen: skill-based (automatic, risk slips), rule-based (if-then / SOPs, risk wrong rule), knowledge-based (novel, slow, error-prone under stress). Anderson: cognitive → associative → autonomous."],["040.03.06.01:01","EASA LO 040.03.06.01.01 → Explain the term ‘arousal’.","Performance follows an inverted U with arousal (best at moderate; too low = underload, too high = overload). Selye GAS: alarm → resistance → exhaustion (chronic stress)."],["040.03.06.01:02","EASA LO 040.03.06.01.02 → Describe the relationship between arousal and performance.","Performance follows an inverted U with arousal (best at moderate; too low = underload, too high = overload). Selye GAS: alarm → resistance → exhaustion (chronic stress)."],["040.03.06.02:09","EASA LO 040.03.06.02.09 → Name the principal causes of domestic stress.","Stressors: physical/environmental, physiological, psychological/cognitive, domestic/social. Stress = perceived demand exceeding perceived ability. Fatigue: acute (long duty, lack of sleep, recovered by rest) vs chronic (accumulated, needs longer recovery). FRMS = data-driven fatigue risk management."],["040.03.06.02:13","EASA LO 040.03.06.02.13 → State that stress is a result of perceived demands and perceived ability.","Stressors: physical/environmental, physiological, psychological/cognitive, domestic/social. Stress = perceived demand exceeding perceived ability. Fatigue: acute (long duty, lack of sleep, recovered by rest) vs chronic (accumulated, needs longer recovery). FRMS = data-driven fatigue risk management."],["040.03.06.02:24","EASA LO 040.03.06.02.24 → Describe the effect of human underload/overload on effectiveness in the flight crew compartment.","Performance follows an inverted U with arousal (best at moderate; too low = underload, too high = overload). Selye GAS: alarm → resistance → exhaustion (chronic stress)."],["040.03.06.05:02","EASA LO 040.03.06.05.02 → Name the causes of short-term and chronic fatigue.","Stressors: physical/environmental, physiological, psychological/cognitive, domestic/social. Stress = perceived demand exceeding perceived ability. Fatigue: acute (long duty, lack of sleep, recovered by rest) vs chronic (accumulated, needs longer recovery). FRMS = data-driven fatigue risk management."],["040.03.06.05:03","EASA LO 040.03.06.05.03 → Identify the symptoms and describe the effects of fatigue.","Stressors: physical/environmental, physiological, psychological/cognitive, domestic/social. Stress = perceived demand exceeding perceived ability. Fatigue: acute (long duty, lack of sleep, recovered by rest) vs chronic (accumulated, needs longer recovery). FRMS = data-driven fatigue risk management."],["040.03.06.05:04","EASA LO 040.03.06.05.04 → List the strategies that prevent or delay the onset of fatigue and hypovigilance.","Stressors: physical/environmental, physiological, psychological/cognitive, domestic/social. Stress = perceived demand exceeding perceived ability. Fatigue: acute (long duty, lack of sleep, recovered by rest) vs chronic (accumulated, needs longer recovery). FRMS = data-driven fatigue risk management."],["040.03.06.05:05","EASA LO 040.03.06.05.05 → List and describe strategies for coping with stress factors and stress reactions.","Stressors: physical/environmental, physiological, psychological/cognitive, domestic/social. Stress = perceived demand exceeding perceived ability. Fatigue: acute (long duty, lack of sleep, recovered by rest) vs chronic (accumulated, needs longer recovery). FRMS = data-driven fatigue risk management."],["040.03.06.05:06","EASA LO 040.03.06.05.06 → Distinguish between short-term and long-term methods of stress management.","Stressors: physical/environmental, physiological, psychological/cognitive, domestic/social. Stress = perceived demand exceeding perceived ability. Fatigue: acute (long duty, lack of sleep, recovered by rest) vs chronic (accumulated, needs longer recovery). FRMS = data-driven fatigue risk management."],["040.03.06.05:07","EASA LO 040.03.06.05.07 → Give examples of short-term methods of stress management.","Stressors: physical/environmental, physiological, psychological/cognitive, domestic/social. Stress = perceived demand exceeding perceived ability. Fatigue: acute (long duty, lack of sleep, recovered by rest) vs chronic (accumulated, needs longer recovery). FRMS = data-driven fatigue risk management."],["040.03.06.05:08","EASA LO 040.03.06.05.08 → Give examples of long-term methods of coping with stress.","Stressors: physical/environmental, physiological, psychological/cognitive, domestic/social. Stress = perceived demand exceeding perceived ability. Fatigue: acute (long duty, lack of sleep, recovered by rest) vs chronic (accumulated, needs longer recovery). FRMS = data-driven fatigue risk management."],["040.03.06.05:09","EASA LO 040.03.06.05.09 → Describe the fatigue risk management system (FRMS) as follows: a data-driven means of continuously monitoring and managing fatigue-related safety risks, based upon scientific principles and knowledge as well as operational experience that aims to ensure relevant personnel are performing at adequate levels of alertness.","Stressors: physical/environmental, physiological, psychological/cognitive, domestic/social. Stress = perceived demand exceeding perceived ability. Fatigue: acute (long duty, lack of sleep, recovered by rest) vs chronic (accumulated, needs longer recovery). FRMS = data-driven fatigue risk management."],["100.02.03.00:01","EASA LO 100.02.03.00.01 → Show the ability to seek accurate and adequate information from appropriate sources.","Communication; leadership & teamwork; problem solving & decision-making; situation awareness; workload management."],["100.02.03.00:02","EASA LO 100.02.03.00.02 → Show the ability to identify and verify what and why things have gone wrong.","Communication; leadership & teamwork; problem solving & decision-making; situation awareness; workload management."],["100.02.03.00:03","EASA LO 100.02.03.00.03 → Show the ability to employ proper problem-solving strategies.","Communication; leadership & teamwork; problem solving & decision-making; situation awareness; workload management."],["100.02.03.00:04","EASA LO 100.02.03.00.04 → Show the ability to persevere in working through problems.","Communication; leadership & teamwork; problem solving & decision-making; situation awareness; workload management."],["100.02.03.00:05","EASA LO 100.02.03.00.05 → Show the ability to use appropriate and timely decision-making processes.","Communication; leadership & teamwork; problem solving & decision-making; situation awareness; workload management."],["100.02.03.00:06","EASA LO 100.02.03.00.06 → Show the ability to set priorities appropriately.","Communication; leadership & teamwork; problem solving & decision-making; situation awareness; workload management."],["100.02.03.00:07","EASA LO 100.02.03.00.07 → Show the ability to identify and consider options effectively.","Communication; leadership & teamwork; problem solving & decision-making; situation awareness; workload management."],["100.02.03.00:08","EASA LO 100.02.03.00.08 → Show the ability to monitor, review and adapt decisions as required.","Communication; leadership & teamwork; problem solving & decision-making; situation awareness; workload management."]],"69":[["081.06.01.01:02","Operating limitations: fill in the gaps → ____: self-excited, rapidly increasing oscillation of a surface (wing, control surface) caused by interaction of aerodynamic, elastic and inertia forces. Likelihood increases with ____ (dynamic pressure) and when the ____ elastic/hinge axis.","Flutter: self-excited, rapidly increasing oscillation of a surface (wing, control surface) caused by interaction of aerodynamic, elastic and inertia forces. Likelihood increases with IAS (dynamic pressure) and when the CG of the surface lies aft of its elastic/hinge axis."],["081.06.01.01:02","Operating limitations: fill in the gaps → Prevention: ____ — control-surface balance weights ahead of the hinge line; ____ ____ move the wing section CG forward (also bending relief); stiff structure.","Prevention: mass balance — control-surface balance weights ahead of the hinge line; wing-mounted engines on pylons ahead of the wing move the wing section CG forward (also bending relief); stiff structure."],["081.06.01.01:03","Operating limitations: fill in the gaps → The certified envelope (up to VD/MD + margin) is ____; damaged mass balance/free play can cause flutter below.","The certified envelope (up to VD/MD + margin) is free of flutter; damaged mass balance/free play can cause flutter below."],["081.06.01.01:01","Operating limitations: fill in the gaps → ____: at high IAS the aileron deflection twists the wing so much that the effective α change opposes the aileron → roll reversal → inboard ailerons/spoilers at high speed.","Aileron reversal: at high IAS the aileron deflection twists the wing so much that the effective α change opposes the aileron → roll reversal → inboard ailerons/spoilers at high speed."],["081.06.01.03:01","Operating limitations: fill in the gaps → ____ protect doors, actuators and structure against air loads: ________ = max speed to operate (extend/retract) the gear; VLE = max speed with gear extended (locked). VLO < VLE on types where the doors are open during the cycle (weak), or VLO(retract) < VLO(extend).","Gear/flap limits protect doors, actuators and structure against air loads: VLO = max speed to operate (extend/retract) the gear; VLE = max speed with gear extended (locked). VLO < VLE on types where the doors are open during the cycle (weak), or VLO(retract) < VLO(extend)."],["081.06.01.03:03","Operating limitations: fill in the gaps → ________ = max speed with flaps extended (a value for each flap setting — decreases with more flap). Protections: ____ (flaps retract automatically when VFE exceeded), overspeed warnings, placards, flap gates, blowback.","VFE = max speed with flaps extended (a value for each flap setting — decreases with more flap). Protections: flap load relief (flaps retract automatically when VFE exceeded), overspeed warnings, placards, flap gates, blowback."],["081.06.01.04:03","Operating limitations: fill in the gaps → ________ (never exceed, CS-23 pistons: red line) ≤ 0.9 VD; ____NO (max structural cruising speed, top of green arc; above only in smooth air — yellow arc) ≈ VC; VMO (max operating speed, jets/turboprops; barber pole) ≈ VC, may not be deliberately exceeded in any phase. Hazards above VNE/VMO: structural overload, flutter, control reversal, gust loads beyond design, loss of control.","VNE (never exceed, CS-23 pistons: red line) ≤ 0.9 VD; VNO (max structural cruising speed, top of green arc; above only in smooth air — yellow arc) ≈ VC; VMO (max operating speed, jets/turboprops; barber pole) ≈ VC, may not be deliberately exceeded in any phase. Hazards above VNE/VMO: structural overload, flutter, control reversal, gust loads beyond design, loss of control."],["081.06.01.05:01","Operating limitations: fill in the gaps → ________ = max operating Mach number; limited by ____ effects — shock waves, high-speed buffet, Mach tuck, loss of control effectiveness, (and VD/MD margin). At high altitude MMO limits, at low altitude VMO (crossover altitude).","MMO = max operating Mach number; limited by compressibility effects — shock waves, high-speed buffet, Mach tuck, loss of control effectiveness, (and VD/MD margin). At high altitude MMO limits, at low altitude VMO (crossover altitude)."],["081.06.02.02:01","Manoeuvring envelope: fill in the gaps → ____: load factor n vs ____; bounded by the stall boundary (curve n = (V/VS)², positive and negative), limit load factors (horizontal lines) and VD (vertical line). ____ VS is where the stall curve meets n = 1.","V-n diagram: load factor n vs EAS; bounded by the stall boundary (curve n = (V/VS)², positive and negative), limit load factors (horizontal lines) and VD (vertical line). 1 g stall speed VS is where the stall curve meets n = 1."],["081.06.02.01:02","Manoeuvring envelope: fill in the gaps → ____ = max load expected in service — no permanent deformation. ____ — structure must withstand it for 3 s without failure; above limit → permanent deformation, above ultimate → structural failure.","Limit load factor = max load expected in service — no permanent deformation. Ultimate load factor = 1.5 × limit — structure must withstand it for 3 s without failure; above limit → permanent deformation, above ultimate → structural failure."],["081.06.02.01:06","Manoeuvring envelope: fill in the gaps → Limits: ____: ____ (up to +3.8 for small ones by formula) / ____ clean; flaps extended +2.0 / 0. CS-23: normal +3.8 / −1.52, utility +4.4 / −1.76, aerobatic +6.0 / −3.0; flaps +2.0.","Limits: CS-25: +2.5 (up to +3.8 for small ones by formula) / −1.0 clean; flaps extended +2.0 / 0. CS-23: normal +3.8 / −1.52, utility +4.4 / −1.76, aerobatic +6.0 / −3.0; flaps +2.0."],["081.06.02.01:08","Manoeuvring envelope: fill in the gaps → ________ = design manoeuvring speed: ____A = VS × √nlimit — at or below VA, a full abrupt elevator deflection stalls the wing before exceeding the limit load. Example: VS 100 kt, n = 2.5 → VA = 158 kt. Above VA an abrupt full nose-up input can overstress the structure (no rudder/elevator full reversals either).","VA = design manoeuvring speed: VA = VS × √nlimit — at or below VA, a full abrupt elevator deflection stalls the wing before exceeding the limit load. Example: VS 100 kt, n = 2.5 → VA = 158 kt. Above VA an abrupt full nose-up input can overstress the structure (no rudder/elevator full reversals either)."],["081.06.02.01:05","Manoeuvring envelope: fill in the gaps → ________ = design speed for maximum gust intensity; ____C = design cruising speed; VD = design diving speed (VMO/VNE kept below VC/VD with margins: VMO ≤ VC, VNE ≤ 0.9 VD). MC/MD = the Mach equivalents.","VB = design speed for maximum gust intensity; VC = design cruising speed; VD = design diving speed (VMO/VNE kept below VC/VD with margins: VMO ≤ VC, VNE ≤ 0.9 VD). MC/MD = the Mach equivalents."],["081.06.02.02:01","Manoeuvring envelope: fill in the gaps → Mass: limit load factors are defined at max mass; lighter aircraft has lower stall speed → VA decreases with mass: V____ = V____ × √(m____/m1) (e.g. 20 % lighter → × 0.894). The accelerated stall boundary moves left.","Mass: limit load factors are defined at max mass; lighter aircraft has lower stall speed → VA decreases with mass: VA2 = VA1 × √(m2/m1) (e.g. 20 % lighter → × 0.894). The accelerated stall boundary moves left."],["081.06.02.02:03","Manoeuvring envelope: fill in the gaps → At ____ VA loses significance because Mach effects (buffet boundary, MMO) limit the usable envelope and the stall speed in EAS rises with Mach.","At high altitude VA loses significance because Mach effects (buffet boundary, MMO) limit the usable envelope and the stall speed in EAS rises with Mach."],["081.06.03.01:01","Gust envelope: fill in the gaps → Gust-load diagram: load factor vs EAS with lines for design gusts (CS-25, EAS): at VB____, at VC: ____, at VD____ (values up to 20 000 ft, reduce above).","Gust-load diagram: load factor vs EAS with lines for design gusts (CS-25, EAS): at VB: 66 ft/s (≈ 20 m/s ≈ 39 kt), at VC: 50 ft/s (≈ 15 m/s ≈ 30 kt), at VD: 25 ft/s (≈ 7.6 m/s ≈ 15 kt) (values up to 20 000 ft, reduce above)."],["081.06.03.01:01","Gust envelope: fill in the gaps → ________ (rough-air/turbulence penetration speed): high enough to give adequate ____ in gusts, low enough to protect against structural overload (≤ VB-ish) and not too close to VMO/MMO; at high altitude the buffet margin between low-speed and high-speed buffet narrows.","VRA (rough-air/turbulence penetration speed): high enough to give adequate stall/buffet margin in gusts, low enough to protect against structural overload (≤ VB-ish) and not too close to VMO/MMO; at high altitude the buffet margin between low-speed and high-speed buffet narrows."],["081.06.03.01:03","Gust envelope: what do you remember about this? → Effects of turbulence: large load-factor changes, possible stall or …","Effects of turbulence: large load-factor changes, possible stall or overspeed, structural fatigue, loss of control, injuries, instrument reading difficulty."],["081.06.03.02:01","Gust envelope: fill in the gaps → ____ Δn ≈ (ρ₀ × EAS × U × a) / (2 × W/S): increases with ____ (higher ____ ↑, sweep ↓ reduces it), EAS, gust speed U; decreases with wing loading W/S (heavier/smaller wing → less response); higher altitude at constant EAS → about the same, at constant TAS → smaller; heavier aircraft → smaller Δn.","Gust load factor Δn ≈ (ρ₀ × EAS × U × a) / (2 × W/S): increases with lift-curve slope a (higher aspect ratio ↑, sweep ↓ reduces it), EAS, gust speed U; decreases with wing loading W/S (heavier/smaller wing → less response); higher altitude at constant EAS → about the same, at constant TAS → smaller; heavier aircraft → smaller Δn."],["081.07.01.04:04","Propellers (review): fill in the gaps → Blade α = blade angle − helix angle; twist gives constant α along the blade; fine pitch = small blade angle; constant-speed props increase blade angle with speed; ____, ____ minimises drag and yaw; efficiency η = T·V/P, max at design speed.","Blade α = blade angle − helix angle; twist gives constant α along the blade; fine pitch = small blade angle; constant-speed props increase blade angle with speed; windmilling = drag and yaw, feathering minimises drag and yaw; efficiency η = T·V/P, max at design speed."],["081.07.03.03:01","Propellers (review): fill in the gaps → Power absorption ↑: diameter, number of blades, chord/solidity, blade angle, rpm; diameter limited by ____ and ground clearance. Noise ↓: lower tip speed, more blades, blade shape.","Power absorption ↑: diameter, number of blades, chord/solidity, blade angle, rpm; diameter limited by tip Mach and ground clearance. Noise ↓: lower tip speed, more blades, blade shape."],["081.07.04.05:03","Propellers (review): fill in the gaps → Secondary effects (clockwise from behind): ____ → roll left; ____ → yaw left; ____ (down-going blade) → yaw left, left engine critical on co-rotating twins; gyro: pitch up → yaw right. Go-around with high power, low speed, high flap, crosswind from dead-engine side and critical engine lost → worst controllability.","Secondary effects (clockwise from behind): torque → roll left; slipstream → yaw left; P-factor (down-going blade) → yaw left, left engine critical on co-rotating twins; gyro: pitch up → yaw right. Go-around with high power, low speed, high flap, crosswind from dead-engine side and critical engine lost → worst controllability."],["081.06.02.01:02","Exam trap (Propellers (review)): complete the fact → Ultimate = ____× limit.","Ultimate = 1.5 × limit."],["081.06.02.01:03","Exam trap (Propellers (review)): complete the fact → Gust design speeds ____/s at VB/VC/VD.","Gust design speeds 66/50/25 ft/s at VB/VC/VD."],["081.06.01.01:01","EASA LO 081.06.01.01.01 → Describe the phenomenon of flutter and how IAS and mass distribution affects the likelihood of flutter occurrence.","The certified envelope (up to VD/MD + margin) is free of flutter; damaged mass balance/free play can cause flutter below."],["081.06.01.03:02","EASA LO 081.06.01.03.02 → Explain why there is a difference between VLO and VLE in the case of some aeroplane types.","Gear/flap limits protect doors, actuators and structure against air loads: VLO = max speed to operate (extend/retract) the gear; VLE = max speed with gear extended (locked). VLO < VLE on types where the doors are open during the cycle (weak), or VLO(retract) < VLO(extend)."],["081.06.01.03:04","EASA LO 081.06.01.03.04 → Describe flap design features, procedures and warnings to prevent overload.","VFE = max speed with flaps extended (a value for each flap setting — decreases with more flap). Protections: flap load relief (flaps retract automatically when VFE exceeded), overspeed warnings, placards, flap gates, blowback."],["081.06.01.04:01","EASA LO 081.06.01.04.01 → Define ‘VMO’, ‘VNO’, and ‘VNE’.","VB = design speed for maximum gust intensity; VC = design cruising speed; VD = design diving speed (VMO/VNE kept below VC/VD with margins: VMO ≤ VC, VNE ≤ 0.9 VD). MC/MD = the Mach equivalents. / VNE (never exceed, CS-23 pistons: red line) ≤ 0.9 VD; VNO (max structural cruising speed, top of green arc; above only in smooth air — yellow arc) ≈ VC; VMO (max operating speed, jets/turboprops; barber pole) ≈ VC, may not be deliberately exceeded in any phase. Hazards above VNE/VMO: structural overload, flutter, control reversal, gust loads beyond design, loss of control."],["081.06.01.04:02","EASA LO 081.06.01.04.02 → Explain the significance of VMO, VNO and VNE, and the differences between these airspeeds.","VB = design speed for maximum gust intensity; VC = design cruising speed; VD = design diving speed (VMO/VNE kept below VC/VD with margins: VMO ≤ VC, VNE ≤ 0.9 VD). MC/MD = the Mach equivalents."],["081.06.02.01:01","EASA LO 081.06.02.01.01 → Describe the manoeuvring–load diagram.","V-n diagram: load factor n vs EAS; bounded by the stall boundary (curve n = (V/VS)², positive and negative), limit load factors (horizontal lines) and VD (vertical line). 1 g stall speed VS is where the stall curve meets n = 1."],["081.06.02.01:04","EASA LO 081.06.02.01.04 → Identify and explain the varying features on the VN diagram: — load factor ‘n’; — speed scale, equivalent airspeed; — equivalent airspeed envelope; — 1g stall speed; — stall boundary (refer to 081 03 01 02).","V-n diagram: load factor n vs EAS; bounded by the stall boundary (curve n = (V/VS)², positive and negative), limit load factors (horizontal lines) and VD (vertical line). 1 g stall speed VS is where the stall curve meets n = 1."],["081.06.02.01:07","EASA LO 081.06.02.01.07 → Explain the relationship between VA and VS in a formula, and calculate the values.","Gust-load diagram: load factor vs EAS with lines for design gusts (CS-25, EAS): at VB: 66 ft/s (≈ 20 m/s ≈ 39 kt), at VC: 50 ft/s (≈ 15 m/s ≈ 30 kt), at VD: 25 ft/s (≈ 7.6 m/s ≈ 15 kt) (values up to 20 000 ft, reduce above). / V-n diagram: load factor n vs EAS; bounded by the stall boundary (curve n = (V/VS)², positive and negative), limit load factors (horizontal lines) and VD (vertical line). 1 g stall speed VS is where the stall curve meets n = 1."],["081.06.02.02:02","EASA LO 081.06.02.02.02 → Calculate the change of VA with changing mass.","Mass: limit load factors are defined at max mass; lighter aircraft has lower stall speed → VA decreases with mass: VA2 = VA1 × √(m2/m1) (e.g. 20 % lighter → × 0.894). The accelerated stall boundary moves left."],["081.06.02.02:04","EASA LO 081.06.02.02.04 → Define ‘MC’ and ‘MD’.","VB = design speed for maximum gust intensity; VC = design cruising speed; VD = design diving speed (VMO/VNE kept below VC/VD with margins: VMO ≤ VC, VNE ≤ 0.9 VD). MC/MD = the Mach equivalents."],["081.07.01.02:01","EASA LO 081.07.01.02.01 → Describe the geometry of a typical propeller blade element at the reference section: — blade chord line; — propeller rotational velocity vector; — true airspeed vector; — blade angle of attack; — pitch or blade angle; — advance or helix angle. Define ‘geometric pitch’, ‘effective pitch’, and ‘propeller slip’. Remark: For theoretical knowledge examination purposes, the following definition is used for geometric pitch: the theoretical distance a propeller would advance in one revolution at zero blade angle of attack.","Blade α = blade angle − helix angle; twist gives constant α along the blade; fine pitch = small blade angle; constant-speed props increase blade angle with speed; windmilling = drag and yaw, feathering minimises drag and yaw; efficiency η = T·V/P, max at design speed."],["081.07.01.02:02","EASA LO 081.07.01.02.02 → Describe how the terms ‘fine pitch’ and ‘coarse pitch’ can be used to express blade angle.","Blade α = blade angle − helix angle; twist gives constant α along the blade; fine pitch = small blade angle; constant-speed props increase blade angle with speed; windmilling = drag and yaw, feathering minimises drag and yaw; efficiency η = T·V/P, max at design speed."],["081.07.01.03:01","EASA LO 081.07.01.03.01 → Define ‘blade twist’.","Blade α = blade angle − helix angle; twist gives constant α along the blade; fine pitch = small blade angle; constant-speed props increase blade angle with speed; windmilling = drag and yaw, feathering minimises drag and yaw; efficiency η = T·V/P, max at design speed."],["081.07.01.03:02","EASA LO 081.07.01.03.02 → Explain why blade twist is necessary.","Blade α = blade angle − helix angle; twist gives constant α along the blade; fine pitch = small blade angle; constant-speed props increase blade angle with speed; windmilling = drag and yaw, feathering minimises drag and yaw; efficiency η = T·V/P, max at design speed."],["081.07.01.04:01","EASA LO 081.07.01.04.01 → List the different types of propellers: — fixed pitch; — adjustable pitch or variable pitch (non\u0002governing); — variable pitch (governing)/constant speed.","Blade α = blade angle − helix angle; twist gives constant α along the blade; fine pitch = small blade angle; constant-speed props increase blade angle with speed; windmilling = drag and yaw, feathering minimises drag and yaw; efficiency η = T·V/P, max at design speed."],["081.07.01.04:02","EASA LO 081.07.01.04.02 → Discuss the advantages and disadvantages of fixed-pitch and constant-speed propellers.","Blade α = blade angle − helix angle; twist gives constant α along the blade; fine pitch = small blade angle; constant-speed props increase blade angle with speed; windmilling = drag and yaw, feathering minimises drag and yaw; efficiency η = T·V/P, max at design speed."],["081.07.01.04:03","EASA LO 081.07.01.04.03 → Discuss climb and cruise propellers.","Blade α = blade angle − helix angle; twist gives constant α along the blade; fine pitch = small blade angle; constant-speed props increase blade angle with speed; windmilling = drag and yaw, feathering minimises drag and yaw; efficiency η = T·V/P, max at design speed."],["081.07.01.04:05","EASA LO 081.07.01.04.05 → Describe and explain the forces that act on a rotating blade element in normal, feathered, windmilling, and reverse operation.","Blade α = blade angle − helix angle; twist gives constant α along the blade; fine pitch = small blade angle; constant-speed props increase blade angle with speed; windmilling = drag and yaw, feathering minimises drag and yaw; efficiency η = T·V/P, max at design speed."],["081.07.01.04:06","EASA LO 081.07.01.04.06 → Explain the effects of changing propeller pitch at constant IAS.","Blade α = blade angle − helix angle; twist gives constant α along the blade; fine pitch = small blade angle; constant-speed props increase blade angle with speed; windmilling = drag and yaw, feathering minimises drag and yaw; efficiency η = T·V/P, max at design speed."],["081.07.01.05:01","EASA LO 081.07.01.05.01 → Define ‘propeller efficiency’.","Blade α = blade angle − helix angle; twist gives constant α along the blade; fine pitch = small blade angle; constant-speed props increase blade angle with speed; windmilling = drag and yaw, feathering minimises drag and yaw; efficiency η = T·V/P, max at design speed."],["081.07.01.05:02","EASA LO 081.07.01.05.02 → Explain and describe the relationship between propeller efficiency and speed (TAS) for different types of propellers.","Blade α = blade angle − helix angle; twist gives constant α along the blade; fine pitch = small blade angle; constant-speed props increase blade angle with speed; windmilling = drag and yaw, feathering minimises drag and yaw; efficiency η = T·V/P, max at design speed."],["081.07.01.05:03","EASA LO 081.07.01.05.03 → Explain the relationship between blade angle and thrust.","Blade α = blade angle − helix angle; twist gives constant α along the blade; fine pitch = small blade angle; constant-speed props increase blade angle with speed; windmilling = drag and yaw, feathering minimises drag and yaw; efficiency η = T·V/P, max at design speed."],["081.07.02.01:01","EASA LO 081.07.02.01.01 → Describe the effects of an inoperative engine on the performance and controllability of an aeroplane: — thrust loss/drag increase; — influence on yaw moment during asymmetric power.","Blade α = blade angle − helix angle; twist gives constant α along the blade; fine pitch = small blade angle; constant-speed props increase blade angle with speed; windmilling = drag and yaw, feathering minimises drag and yaw; efficiency η = T·V/P, max at design speed. / Secondary effects (clockwise from behind): torque → roll left; slipstream → yaw left; P-factor (down-going blade) → yaw left, left engine critical on co-rotating twins; gyro: pitch up → yaw right. Go-around with high power, low speed, high flap, crosswind from dead-engine side and critical engine lost → worst controllability."],["081.07.02.02:01","EASA LO 081.07.02.02.01 → Explain the reasons for feathering a propeller, including the effect on the yaw moment, performance and controllability.","Blade α = blade angle − helix angle; twist gives constant α along the blade; fine pitch = small blade angle; constant-speed props increase blade angle with speed; windmilling = drag and yaw, feathering minimises drag and yaw; efficiency η = T·V/P, max at design speed."],["081.07.03.01:01","EASA LO 081.07.03.01.01 → Name the propeller design characteristics that increase power absorption.","Power absorption ↑: diameter, number of blades, chord/solidity, blade angle, rpm; diameter limited by tip Mach and ground clearance. Noise ↓: lower tip speed, more blades, blade shape. / Blade α = blade angle − helix angle; twist gives constant α along the blade; fine pitch = small blade angle; constant-speed props increase blade angle with speed; windmilling = drag and yaw, feathering minimises drag and yaw; efficiency η = T·V/P, max at design speed."],["081.07.03.02:01","EASA LO 081.07.03.02.01 → Explain the reasons for restricting propeller diameter.","Power absorption ↑: diameter, number of blades, chord/solidity, blade angle, rpm; diameter limited by tip Mach and ground clearance. Noise ↓: lower tip speed, more blades, blade shape."],["081.07.03.03:02","EASA LO 081.07.03.03.02 → Describe the advantages and disadvantages of increasing the number of blades.","Power absorption ↑: diameter, number of blades, chord/solidity, blade angle, rpm; diameter limited by tip Mach and ground clearance. Noise ↓: lower tip speed, more blades, blade shape."],["081.07.03.04:01","EASA LO 081.07.03.04.01 → Describe how propeller noise can be minimised.","Power absorption ↑: diameter, number of blades, chord/solidity, blade angle, rpm; diameter limited by tip Mach and ground clearance. Noise ↓: lower tip speed, more blades, blade shape."],["081.07.04.01:01","EASA LO 081.07.04.01.01 → Describe the effects of engine/propeller torque.","Secondary effects (clockwise from behind): torque → roll left; slipstream → yaw left; P-factor (down-going blade) → yaw left, left engine critical on co-rotating twins; gyro: pitch up → yaw right. Go-around with high power, low speed, high flap, crosswind from dead-engine side and critical engine lost → worst controllability."],["081.07.04.01:02","EASA LO 081.07.04.01.02 → Describe the following methods for counteracting engine/propeller torque: — counter-rotating propellers; — contra-rotating propellers.","Secondary effects (clockwise from behind): torque → roll left; slipstream → yaw left; P-factor (down-going blade) → yaw left, left engine critical on co-rotating twins; gyro: pitch up → yaw right. Go-around with high power, low speed, high flap, crosswind from dead-engine side and critical engine lost → worst controllability."],["081.07.04.03:01","EASA LO 081.07.04.03.01 → Describe the possible effects of the rotating propeller slipstream.","Effects of turbulence: large load-factor changes, possible stall or overspeed, structural fatigue, loss of control, injuries, instrument reading difficulty. / Secondary effects (clockwise from behind): torque → roll left; slipstream → yaw left; P-factor (down-going blade) → yaw left, left engine critical on co-rotating twins; gyro: pitch up → yaw right. Go-around with high power, low speed, high flap, crosswind from dead-engine side and critical engine lost → worst controllability."],["081.07.04.04:02","EASA LO 081.07.04.04.02 → Explain the influence of direction of rotation on the critical engine on twin-engine aeroplanes.","Secondary effects (clockwise from behind): torque → roll left; slipstream → yaw left; P-factor (down-going blade) → yaw left, left engine critical on co-rotating twins; gyro: pitch up → yaw right. Go-around with high power, low speed, high flap, crosswind from dead-engine side and critical engine lost → worst controllability."],["081.07.04.05:02","EASA LO 081.07.04.05.02 → Describe, given the direction of propeller rotation, the propeller effects during a go-around and their consequence on controllability.","Secondary effects (clockwise from behind): torque → roll left; slipstream → yaw left; P-factor (down-going blade) → yaw left, left engine critical on co-rotating twins; gyro: pitch up → yaw right. Go-around with high power, low speed, high flap, crosswind from dead-engine side and critical engine lost → worst controllability."]],"70":[["010.04.01.01:01","ICAO Annex 1 vs Aircrew Regulation: fill in the gaps → ____ = ICAO Standards and Recommended Practices (minimum international standards). The ____ (Part-FCL, Part-MED, Part-ORA, Part-ARA…) transposes them into ____ and adds European requirements (e.g.","Annex 1 (Personnel Licensing) = ICAO Standards and Recommended Practices (minimum international standards). The Aircrew Regulation (EU) 1178/2011 (Part-FCL, Part-MED, Part-ORA, Part-ARA…) transposes them into directly binding EU law and adds European requirements (e.g."],["010.04.01.01:01","ICAO Annex 1 vs Aircrew Regulation: what do you remember about this? → LAPL, BIR, specific ratings, detailed training/checking). Where the …","LAPL, BIR, specific ratings, detailed training/checking). Where the EU deviates from Annex 1, differences are notified to ICAO. ICAO licences are mutually recognised under Art. 33 of the Chicago Convention."],["010.04.02.01:02","Definitions: fill in the gaps → ____ of aircraft: aeroplane, helicopter, powered-lift, airship, sailplane, balloon. ____: aeroplanes of similar characteristics not requiring a type rating (SEP land/sea, MEP land/sea, TMG, SET…). ____: aircraft of the same basic design requiring a type rating.","Category of aircraft: aeroplane, helicopter, powered-lift, airship, sailplane, balloon. Class: aeroplanes of similar characteristics not requiring a type rating (SEP land/sea, MEP land/sea, TMG, SET…). Type: aircraft of the same basic design requiring a type rating."],["010.04.02.01:01","Definitions: fill in the gaps → ____: flight between a departure and an arrival point following a pre-planned route using standard navigation procedures. ____: flight time during which a person receives instruction from an authorised instructor.","Cross-country: flight between a departure and an arrival point following a pre-planned route using standard navigation procedures. Dual instruction time: flight time during which a person receives instruction from an authorised instructor."],["010.04.02.01:01","Definitions: fill in the gaps → ____ ____: from first movement for taking off until finally coming to rest. ____: student acting as PIC under supervision of an instructor who only observes and does not influence the flight. Instrument time = instrument flight time (sole reference to instruments) + instrument ground time (in an FSTD).","Flight time (aeroplanes): from first movement for taking off until finally coming to rest. SPIC: student acting as PIC under supervision of an instructor who only observes and does not influence the flight. Instrument time = instrument flight time (sole reference to instruments) + instrument ground time (in an FSTD)."],["010.04.02.02:02","Definitions: fill in the gaps → ____: between the end of evening civil twilight and the beginning of morning civil twilight. ____: licence holder not allowed to fly for remuneration (non-commercial).","Night: between the end of evening civil twilight and the beginning of morning civil twilight. Private pilot: licence holder not allowed to fly for remuneration (non-commercial)."],["010.04.02.01:02","Definitions: fill in the gaps → ____: functioning of the flight crew as a team of cooperating members led by the PIC. ____: certified for operation with a minimum crew of at least two pilots. ____: entry in a licence stating special conditions, privileges or limitations.","MCC: functioning of the flight crew as a team of cooperating members led by the PIC. Multi-pilot aircraft: certified for operation with a minimum crew of at least two pilots. Rating: entry in a licence stating special conditions, privileges or limitations."],["010.04.02.05:02","Structure & general principles: fill in the gaps → ____: A general, ____, ____, D CPL, E MPL, F ATPL, G IR, H class & type ratings, I additional ratings, J instructors, K examiners.","Part-FCL subparts: A general, B LAPL, C PPL/SPL/BPL, D CPL, E MPL, F ATPL, G IR, H class & type ratings, I additional ratings, J instructors, K examiners."],["010.04.02.02:02","Structure & general principles: fill in the gaps → To act as flight crew of an aircraft registered in a Member State you need a ____ plus a ____ ____ — the two factors for exercising privileges: valid ratings and valid medical. Licence system: LAPL → PPL → CPL → ATPL (or MPL for multi-crew).","To act as flight crew of an aircraft registered in a Member State you need a valid licence and rating(s) plus a valid medical certificate — the two factors for exercising privileges: valid ratings and valid medical. Licence system: LAPL → PPL → CPL → ATPL (or MPL for multi-crew)."],["010.04.02.02:04","Structure & general principles: fill in the gaps → Language proficiency endorsement (English and/or language used) needed for radiotelephony: level ____ valid ____, level ____ valid 6 years, level 6 (expert) no revalidation.","Language proficiency endorsement (English and/or language used) needed for radiotelephony: level 4 valid 4 years, level 5 valid 6 years, level 6 (expert) no revalidation."],["010.04.02.02:02","Structure & general principles: fill in the gaps → ____: ____: may act as pilot in CAT only as a member of a multi-pilot crew (other pilot < 60); ____: no CAT at all.","Age 60+: 60–64: may act as pilot in CAT only as a member of a multi-pilot crew (other pilot < 60); 65+: no CAT at all."],["010.04.02.02:06","Structure & general principles: fill in the gaps → ____: authority designated by the Member State to which the person applies for the licence (or EASA where applicable).","Competent authority: authority designated by the Member State to which the person applies for the licence (or EASA where applicable)."],["010.04.02.02:07","Structure & general principles: fill in the gaps → Must ____ a valid licence, medical certificate and personal ID with photo, and present them on request of an authorised person.","Must carry a valid licence, medical certificate and personal ID with photo, and present them on request of an authorised person."],["010.04.02.03:01","CPL(A): fill in the gaps → Requirements: minimum age ____; hold PPL (modular) or integrated course; theoretical exams (CPL/ATPL subjects) — all passed within ____, max ____, max 4 sittings; credit valid 36 months; flight experience (modular): 200 h total (integrated: 150 h), incl.","Requirements: minimum age 18; hold PPL (modular) or integrated course; theoretical exams (CPL/ATPL subjects) — all passed within 18 months, max 6 attempts per subject, max 4 sittings; credit valid 36 months; flight experience (modular): 200 h total (integrated: 150 h), incl."],["010.04.02.01:01","CPL(A): fill in the gaps → ____ (integrated 70 h), ____ incl. a ____ cross-country with full-stop landings at two other aerodromes, 5 h night (incl. 5 take-offs & 5 landings as PIC), 10 h instrument dual (≤ 5 h FSTD), 5 h in a complex/variable-pitch aeroplane; skill test; Class 1 medical.","100 h PIC (integrated 70 h), 20 h VFR cross-country PIC incl. a 300 NM cross-country with full-stop landings at two other aerodromes, 5 h night (incl. 5 take-offs & 5 landings as PIC), 10 h instrument dual (≤ 5 h FSTD), 5 h in a complex/variable-pitch aeroplane; skill test; Class 1 medical."],["010.04.02.05:04","CPL(A): fill in the gaps → ____: all PPL privileges; act as ____ in operations other than CAT; ____ (subject to ratings); co-pilot in CAT.","Privileges: all PPL privileges; act as PIC or co-pilot in operations other than CAT; PIC in CAT of any single-pilot aeroplane (subject to ratings); co-pilot in CAT."],["010.04.02.01:01","ATPL(A) & MPL: fill in the gaps → ATPL requirements: age ____; hold CPL/MPL, multi-engine IR, MCC; ATPL theory credits; experience ____ in aeroplanes incl. ____; 500 h PIC under supervision or 250 h as PIC, or 70 h PIC + rest PICUS; 200 h cross-country (100 h as PIC/PICUS); 75 h instrument time (≤ 30 h ground);","ATPL requirements: age 21; hold CPL/MPL, multi-engine IR, MCC; ATPL theory credits; experience 1 500 h in aeroplanes incl. 500 h in multi-pilot operations; 500 h PIC under supervision or 250 h as PIC, or 70 h PIC + rest PICUS; 200 h cross-country (100 h as PIC/PICUS); 75 h instrument time (≤ 30 h ground);"],["010.04.02.04:04","ATPL(A) & MPL: fill in the gaps → ____ as PIC or co-pilot; skill test on a multi-pilot type; Class 1 medical. ____: all CPL/IR privileges + ____ in CAT on multi-pilot aeroplanes.","100 h night as PIC or co-pilot; skill test on a multi-pilot type; Class 1 medical. Privileges: all CPL/IR privileges + PIC in CAT on multi-pilot aeroplanes."],["010.04.02.05:03","ATPL(A) & MPL: fill in the gaps → ____: age ____; approved integrated competency-based course (≥ ____ incl. ≥ 70 h in actual aircraft, the rest in FSTDs), ATPL theory, 4 phases (core, basic, intermediate, advanced);","MPL requirements: age 18; approved integrated competency-based course (≥ 240 h incl. ≥ 70 h in actual aircraft, the rest in FSTDs), ATPL theory, 4 phases (core, basic, intermediate, advanced);"],["010.04.02.04:02","ATPL(A) & MPL: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ in CAT (IR limited to multi-pilot aeroplanes); can be converted to ATPL after meeting ATPL experience; single-pilot privileges only after meeting PPL/CPL requirements.","privileges: co-pilot in multi-pilot aeroplanes in CAT (IR limited to multi-pilot aeroplanes); can be converted to ATPL after meeting ATPL experience; single-pilot privileges only after meeting PPL/CPL requirements."],["010.04.02.05:01","Ratings: fill in the gaps → ____: SEP valid ____ (revalidation by proficiency check or 12 h flight time in the last 12 months incl. 6 h PIC, 12 TO & LDG, 1 h with an instructor); ____ (proficiency check within the last 3 months).","Class ratings: SEP valid 24 months (revalidation by proficiency check or 12 h flight time in the last 12 months incl. 6 h PIC, 12 TO & LDG, 1 h with an instructor); MEP 12 months (proficiency check within the last 3 months)."],["010.04.02.05:02","Ratings: fill in the gaps → ____: valid ____, revalidation by ____ within 3 months before expiry + 10 route sectors (or 1 with examiner). Privileges: fly the class/type.","Type ratings: valid 12 months, revalidation by proficiency check within 3 months before expiry + 10 route sectors (or 1 with examiner). Privileges: fly the class/type."],["010.04.02.05:03","Ratings: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ valid ____ (proficiency check); CB-IR (competency-based route, 40 h instrument time); BIR (basic instrument rating for PPL/LAPL, modules, not for CAT, limited privileges above 200 ft DH/1 500 m visibility…). IR privileges: fly IFR down to DH 200 ft (CAT I) — lower with LVO approval.","Instrument ratings: IR valid 12 months (proficiency check); CB-IR (competency-based route, 40 h instrument time); BIR (basic instrument rating for PPL/LAPL, modules, not for CAT, limited privileges above 200 ft DH/1 500 m visibility…). IR privileges: fly IFR down to DH 200 ft (CAT I) — lower with LVO approval."],["010.04.02.05:01","Ratings: fill in the gaps → ____ (Subpart I): ____, mountain, flight test, sailplane/banner ____, aerobatic, extension of privileges to TMG, etc.","Other ratings (Subpart I): night, mountain, flight test, sailplane/banner towing, aerobatic, extension of privileges to TMG, etc."],["010.04.02.05:01","Part-MED: fill in the gaps → Content: medical classes (____ professional, ____ private, LAPL medical, cabin crew), aero-medical examiners (AME) and centres (AeMC), requirements, validity.","Content: medical classes (Class 1 professional, Class 2 private, LAPL medical, cabin crew), aero-medical examiners (AME) and centres (AeMC), requirements, validity."],["010.04.03.01:02","Part-MED: fill in the gaps → ____: medical examination by an ____; initial Class 1 at an AeMC; certificate issued if fit; limitations may be endorsed (VDL corrective lenses, OML operational multi-pilot limitation…).","Issue: medical examination by an AeMC/AME; initial Class 1 at an AeMC; certificate issued if fit; limitations may be endorsed (VDL corrective lenses, OML operational multi-pilot limitation…)."],["010.04.02.02:05","Part-MED: fill in the gaps → ____ medical. Class 1 validity ____; ____ for single-pilot CAT with passengers ≥ 40 years and all ≥ 60 years. (Class 2: 60 months < 40, 24 months 40–50, 12 months > 50.)","CPL, MPL, ATPL → Class 1 medical. Class 1 validity 12 months; 6 months for single-pilot CAT with passengers ≥ 40 years and all ≥ 60 years. (Class 2: 60 months < 40, 24 months 40–50, 12 months > 50.)"],["010.04.03.01:04","Part-MED: fill in the gaps → ____: do not exercise the privileges when aware of any decrease; ____ without delay in case of: surgery/invasive procedure, regular use of medication, significant injury, ____, pregnancy, hospital admission, first need for correcting lenses.","Decrease in medical fitness: do not exercise the privileges when aware of any decrease; seek aero-medical advice without delay in case of: surgery/invasive procedure, regular use of medication, significant injury, illness > 21 days, pregnancy, hospital admission, first need for correcting lenses."],["010.04.02.03:02","Exam trap (Part-MED): complete the fact → CPL age ____ATPL ____; ATPL 1 500 h incl.","CPL age 18, ATPL 21; ATPL 1 500 h incl."],["010.04.02.04:02","Exam trap (Part-MED): complete the fact → ____ multi-pilot.","500 h multi-pilot."],["010.04.02.02:05","Exam trap (Part-MED): complete the fact → Age ____→ multi-pilot only; ____→ no CAT.","Age 60–64 → multi-pilot only; 65 → no CAT."],["010.04.03.01:01","Exam trap (Part-MED): complete the fact → Class ____= ____onths (6 months single-pilot CAT pax ≥ 40).","Class 1 = 12 months (6 months single-pilot CAT pax ≥ 40)."],["010.04.02.02:05","Exam trap (Part-MED): complete the fact → Language level ____= ____years, level 5 = 6 years.","Language level 4 = 4 years, level 5 = 6 years."],["010.04.02.02:01","EASA LO 010.04.02.02.01 → Explain the structure of Part-FCL.","Part-FCL subparts: A general, B LAPL, C PPL/SPL/BPL, D CPL, E MPL, F ATPL, G IR, H class & type ratings, I additional ratings, J instructors, K examiners."],["010.04.02.02:03","EASA LO 010.04.02.02.03 → List the two factors that are relevant to the exercise of the privileges of a licence.","To act as flight crew of an aircraft registered in a Member State you need a valid licence and rating(s) plus a valid medical certificate — the two factors for exercising privileges: valid ratings and valid medical. Licence system: LAPL → PPL → CPL → ATPL (or MPL for multi-crew)."],["010.04.02.03:01","EASA LO 010.04.02.03.01 → State the requirements for the issue of a CPL.","100 h night as PIC or co-pilot; skill test on a multi-pilot type; Class 1 medical. Privileges: all CPL/IR privileges + PIC in CAT on multi-pilot aeroplanes. / Night: between the end of evening civil twilight and the beginning of morning civil twilight. Private pilot: licence holder not allowed to fly for remuneration (non-commercial)."],["010.04.02.04:01","EASA LO 010.04.02.04.01 → State the requirements for the issue of an ATPL.","To act as flight crew of an aircraft registered in a Member State you need a valid licence and rating(s) plus a valid medical certificate — the two factors for exercising privileges: valid ratings and valid medical. Licence system: LAPL → PPL → CPL → ATPL (or MPL for multi-crew). / privileges: co-pilot in multi-pilot aeroplanes in CAT (IR limited to multi-pilot aeroplanes); can be converted to ATPL after meeting ATPL experience; single-pilot privileges only after meeting PPL/CPL requirements."],["010.04.02.04:03","EASA LO 010.04.02.04.03 → State the requirements for the issue of an MPL.","To act as flight crew of an aircraft registered in a Member State you need a valid licence and rating(s) plus a valid medical certificate — the two factors for exercising privileges: valid ratings and valid medical. Licence system: LAPL → PPL → CPL → ATPL (or MPL for multi-crew). / 500 h multi-pilot."],["010.04.03.01:01","EASA LO 010.04.03.01.01 → Describe the relevant content of Part-MED — Medical requirements (administrative parts and requirements related to licensing only).","Annex 1 (Personnel Licensing) = ICAO Standards and Recommended Practices (minimum international standards). The Aircrew Regulation (EU) 1178/2011 (Part-FCL, Part-MED, Part-ORA, Part-ARA…) transposes them into directly binding EU law and adds European requirements (e.g. / Age 60–64 → multi-pilot only; 65 → no CAT."],["010.04.03.01:03","EASA LO 010.04.03.01.03 → Name the class of medical certificate required when exercising the privileges of a CPL, MPL or ATPL.","To act as flight crew of an aircraft registered in a Member State you need a valid licence and rating(s) plus a valid medical certificate — the two factors for exercising privileges: valid ratings and valid medical. Licence system: LAPL → PPL → CPL → ATPL (or MPL for multi-crew). / 100 h night as PIC or co-pilot; skill test on a multi-pilot type; Class 1 medical. Privileges: all CPL/IR privileges + PIC in CAT on multi-pilot aeroplanes."]],"71":[["022.01.01.01:01","Pressure: fill in the gaps → ____ = force per unit area; ____ = relative to vacuum; ____ = difference between two pressures (e.g. cabin ΔP, pitot − static); gauge pressure = relative to ambient.","Pressure = force per unit area; absolute = relative to vacuum; differential = difference between two pressures (e.g. cabin ΔP, pitot − static); gauge pressure = relative to ambient."],["022.01.01.01:02","Pressure: fill in the gaps → Units: ____; ____ ____; 1 013.25 hPa = 29.92 inHg = 14.7 psi; 1 inHg ≈ 33.86 hPa.","Units: 1 hPa = 100 Pa = 1 mbar; 1 bar = 1 000 hPa ≈ 14.5 psi ≈ 29.53 inHg; 1 013.25 hPa = 29.92 inHg = 14.7 psi; 1 inHg ≈ 33.86 hPa."],["022.01.01.01:04","Pressure: fill in the gaps → Sensors: ____ (sealed, evacuated → absolute low pressures: altimeter, MAP), ____ (stack of capsules, larger movement, low/medium pressure, differential), ____ (thin flexible membrane, small pressures, ASI capsule), Bourdon tube (curved flattened tube straightens under pressure → high pressures: hydraulic, oil, fuel).","Sensors: aneroid capsule (sealed, evacuated → absolute low pressures: altimeter, MAP), bellows (stack of capsules, larger movement, low/medium pressure, differential), diaphragm (thin flexible membrane, small pressures, ASI capsule), Bourdon tube (curved flattened tube straightens under pressure → high pressures: hydraulic, oil, fuel)."],["022.01.01.01:05","Pressure: fill in the gaps → Aircraft pressure measurements: ____ (fuel, oil, hydraulic), ____ (bleed air, air conditioning, cabin), ____: manifold pressure (MAP, inHg) for piston engines with constant-speed prop, EPR (turbine exhaust/compressor inlet total pressure ratio).","Aircraft pressure measurements: liquids (fuel, oil, hydraulic), air (bleed air, air conditioning, cabin), engine: manifold pressure (MAP, inHg) for piston engines with constant-speed prop, EPR (turbine exhaust/compressor inlet total pressure ratio)."],["022.01.01.01:07","Pressure: fill in the gaps → Errors: ____ (blocked/broken line) → reads zero or frozen; ____ (sensor/transmitter failure) → cross-check with other parameters (e.g. oil pressure vs temperature, EPR vs N1/FF).","Errors: loss of sensing (blocked/broken line) → reads zero or frozen; incorrect indication (sensor/transmitter failure) → cross-check with other parameters (e.g. oil pressure vs temperature, EPR vs N1/FF)."],["022.01.02.01:01","Temperature: fill in the gaps → Temperature = measure of the kinetic energy of molecules. ____ ____; ____; °C = (°F − 32) × 5/9. (−40 °C = −40 °F.)","Temperature = measure of the kinetic energy of molecules. K = °C + 273.15; °F = °C × 9/5 + 32; °C = (°F − 32) × 5/9. (−40 °C = −40 °F.)"],["022.01.02.01:04","Temperature: fill in the gaps → Aircraft measurements: OAT/____, oil, fuel, cylinder-head (CHT), exhaust gas (EGT/ITT/TGT), cabin, hydraulic fluid, brakes.","Aircraft measurements: OAT/SAT & TAT, oil, fuel, cylinder-head (CHT), exhaust gas (EGT/ITT/TGT), cabin, hydraulic fluid, brakes."],["022.01.02.01:01","Temperature: fill in the gaps → Sensors: ____ (ratiometer/Wheatstone bridge; low temperatures: air, oil, fuel), ____ (two dissimilar metals — chromel/alumel — voltage ∝ temperature difference, ____, high temperatures: EGT, CHT), bimetallic strips (simple OAT gauges).","Sensors: resistance thermometers (ratiometer/Wheatstone bridge; low temperatures: air, oil, fuel), thermocouples (two dissimilar metals — chromel/alumel — voltage ∝ temperature difference, no external power, high temperatures: EGT, CHT), bimetallic strips (simple OAT gauges)."],["022.01.03.01:01","Fuel quantity & flow: fill in the gaps → Fuel measured by ____ (l, US gal = 3.785 l, Imp gal = 4.546 l) or ____ (kg, lb; 1 kg = 2.205 lb). Mass is what matters for performance and energy.","Fuel measured by volume (l, US gal = 3.785 l, Imp gal = 4.546 l) or mass (kg, lb; 1 kg = 2.205 lb). Mass is what matters for performance and energy."],["022.01.03.01:04","Fuel quantity & flow: fill in the gaps → Measurement affected by ____ (density changes: warm fuel has more volume, less density), ____ (fuel surface tilts — compensated by several probes), density/fuel type.","Measurement affected by temperature (density changes: warm fuel has more volume, less density), accelerations and attitude (fuel surface tilts — compensated by several probes), density/fuel type."],["022.01.03.01:05","Fuel quantity & flow: fill in the gaps → ____: float linked to a potentiometer → volume; simple, attitude-sensitive. ____: probes = capacitors; fuel (dielectric constant ~2.1) vs air (1) → capacitance ∝ fuel height; with a ____ gives mass, attitude errors averaged with multiple probes. (Ultrasonic: later.)","Float gauge: float linked to a potentiometer → volume; simple, attitude-sensitive. Capacitance gauge: probes = capacitors; fuel (dielectric constant ~2.1) vs air (1) → capacitance ∝ fuel height; with a densitometer/compensator gives mass, attitude errors averaged with multiple probes. (Ultrasonic: later.)"],["022.01.03.01:06","Fuel quantity & flow: fill in the gaps → ____: record ____ (litres on the bowser receipt) → convert to mass with the actual ____ → add to fuel before refuelling → compare with gauge; a discrepancy beyond tolerance (e.g. > 2–3 %) → investigate (gauge error, wrong density, leak, wrong tank) before flight, use dripsticks.","Post-refuelling check: record uplift volume (litres on the bowser receipt) → convert to mass with the actual density → add to fuel before refuelling → compare with gauge; a discrepancy beyond tolerance (e.g. > 2–3 %) → investigate (gauge error, wrong density, leak, wrong tank) before flight, use dripsticks."],["022.01.04.01:02","Fuel quantity & flow: fill in the gaps → ____ = quantity consumed per unit time, measured in the ____ (downstream of the fuel control, before the nozzles): mass flow ____ (turbines, compensated) or volume l/h, US gal/h, Imp gal/h (pistons). Total fuel used = integration of fuel flow over time (fuel-used counter); fuel remaining = initial − used.","Fuel flow = quantity consumed per unit time, measured in the line to the engine (downstream of the fuel control, before the nozzles): mass flow kg/h, lb/h (turbines, compensated) or volume l/h, US gal/h, Imp gal/h (pistons). Total fuel used = integration of fuel flow over time (fuel-used counter); fuel remaining = initial − used."],["022.01.05.01:01","Engine speed, thrust, torque: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (flexible drive shaft rotates a magnet → eddy-current drag cup; no power needed), ____ phase tacho-generator (generator on engine drives a synchronous motor in the indicator; no aircraft power needed), electronic (speed probe counts teeth of a phonic wheel → pulses → digital; needs electrical power).","Tachometers: mechanical (flexible drive shaft rotates a magnet → eddy-current drag cup; no power needed), electrical three- phase tacho-generator (generator on engine drives a synchronous motor in the indicator; no aircraft power needed), electronic (speed probe counts teeth of a phonic wheel → pulses → digital; needs electrical power)."],["022.01.07.01:02","Engine speed, thrust, torque: fill in the gaps → Units: ____ (pistons), ____ ____ (turbines, 100 % = reference rpm).","Units: rpm (pistons), % N1/N2 (turbines, 100 % = reference rpm)."],["022.01.06.01:02","Engine speed, thrust, torque: fill in the gaps → Thrust parameters: ____ (fan speed — directly related to thrust on high-bypass fans) and EPR (engine pressure ratio = Pt exhaust/Pt inlet). EPR engines are set by EPR; if EPR is incorrect/missing (e.g. iced inlet probe P____ → EPR over-reads → thrust set too low!) → revert to ____ (FADEC/crew).","Thrust parameters: N1 (fan speed — directly related to thrust on high-bypass fans) and EPR (engine pressure ratio = Pt exhaust/Pt inlet). EPR engines are set by EPR; if EPR is incorrect/missing (e.g. iced inlet probe Pt2 → EPR over-reads → thrust set too low!) → revert to N1 mode (FADEC/crew)."],["022.01.07.01:02","Engine speed, thrust, torque: fill in the gaps → ____ = force × arm (Nm, ft·lb, in·lb, or %). ____ ____ (P = 2π·n·T). Turboprops/helicopters use torque. Mechanical torquemeter: oil pressure from helical gears in the reduction gearbox (axial thrust ∝ torque) — heavier; electronic: phase shift between two phonic wheels on a torsion shaft — lighter, accurate.","Torque = force × arm (Nm, ft·lb, in·lb, or %). Power = torque × rpm × const. (P = 2π·n·T). Turboprops/helicopters use torque. Mechanical torquemeter: oil pressure from helical gears in the reduction gearbox (axial thrust ∝ torque) — heavier; electronic: phase shift between two phonic wheels on a torsion shaft — lighter, accurate."],["022.01.05.01:01","Engine speed, thrust, torque: fill in the gaps → ____: shows ____ (rotating disc/pointer — rotates towards the faster engine, stops when synchronised) → reduces noise/vibration (beat).","Synchroscope: shows rpm difference between engines (rotating disc/pointer — rotates towards the faster engine, stops when synchronised) → reduces noise/vibration (beat)."],["022.01.09.01:03","Engine speed, thrust, torque: fill in the gaps → ____ (jets): detects imbalance (blade damage, bearing failure) early. Sensors: ____ (accelerometer produces voltage under vibration) or ____ (velocity pickup). No physical unit — displayed as a number/level with threshold/advisory values (e.g. > 4 = amber).","Vibration monitoring (jets): detects imbalance (blade damage, bearing failure) early. Sensors: piezoelectric crystal (accelerometer produces voltage under vibration) or magnet/coil (velocity pickup). No physical unit — displayed as a number/level with threshold/advisory values (e.g. > 4 = amber)."],["022.01.10.01:01","Engine speed, thrust, torque: fill in the gaps → ____: time reference (UTC) for ACARS, FMS, flight recorders, engine/system maintenance records.","On-board clock: time reference (UTC) for ACARS, FMS, flight recorders, engine/system maintenance records."],["022.02.04.01:03","Altimeter: fill in the gaps → Units: ____ and ____ (1 m = 3.28 ft). ____ = vertical distance above a specified datum (e.g. aerodrome, QFE); altitude = above MSL (QNH).","Units: ft and m (1 m = 3.28 ft). Height = vertical distance above a specified datum (e.g. aerodrome, QFE); altitude = above MSL (QNH)."],["022.02.04.01:03","Altimeter: fill in the gaps → ____ = pressure reduced to MSL in ISA → altimeter reads ____ (aerodrome elevation on the ground). QFE = pressure at aerodrome level → reads height (0 on the ground). ____ (standard) → pressure altitude/flight level.","QNH = pressure reduced to MSL in ISA → altimeter reads altitude (aerodrome elevation on the ground). QFE = pressure at aerodrome level → reads height (0 on the ground). 1013.25 hPa (standard) → pressure altitude/flight level."],["022.02.04.01:01","Altimeter: fill in the gaps → ____: aneroid capsule (evacuated) expands as static pressure decreases; linkage converts to height scale calibrated in ISA (~____ near sea level).","Principle: aneroid capsule (evacuated) expands as static pressure decreases; linkage converts to height scale calibrated in ISA (~27–30 ft per hPa near sea level)."],["022.02.04.01:06","Altimeter: fill in the gaps → Types: ____ (one capsule, ~pointer, inaccurate), ____ (____, sub-scale setting, 3 pointers/drum — accurate), servo-assisted (capsule movement sensed by an E/I- bar transducer, servo drives display, very accurate at high altitude, less friction/lag — needed in RVSM). Displays: 3-pointer (misreading!), drum-pointer, counter, vertical tape (EFIS).","Types: simple (one capsule, ~pointer, inaccurate), sensitive (multiple capsules, sub-scale setting, 3 pointers/drum — accurate), servo-assisted (capsule movement sensed by an E/I- bar transducer, servo drives display, very accurate at high altitude, less friction/lag — needed in RVSM). Displays: 3-pointer (misreading!), drum-pointer, counter, vertical tape (EFIS)."],["022.02.04.01:07","Altimeter: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (static port location, AoA, config — correction table), instrument error (friction, manufacture), barometric error (wrong pressure setting or flying towards low pressure \"high to low, look out below\"), temperature error (air colder than ISA → aircraft lower than indicated; ≈ ____ per 10 °C, ~4 ft/°C/1 000 ft), lag (under-reads in climb, over-reads in descent).","Errors: static/position error (static port location, AoA, config — correction table), instrument error (friction, manufacture), barometric error (wrong pressure setting or flying towards low pressure \"high to low, look out below\"), temperature error (air colder than ISA → aircraft lower than indicated; ≈ 4 % per 10 °C, ~4 ft/°C/1 000 ft), lag (under-reads in climb, over-reads in descent)."],["022.02.04.01:08","Altimeter: what do you remember about this? → Correction tables: add temperature correction to DA/MDA/minimum …","Correction tables: add temperature correction to DA/MDA/minimum altitudes in cold weather; apply position-error correction."],["022.02.04.01:09","Altimeter: fill in the gaps → ____: altimeter ____ at the blockage altitude. ____ → reads cabin altitude (lower); unpressurised cabin (alternate static) → slightly higher reading (cabin pressure lower due to venturi).","Static blockage: altimeter freezes at the blockage altitude. Static leak inside a pressurised cabin → reads cabin altitude (lower); unpressurised cabin (alternate static) → slightly higher reading (cabin pressure lower due to venturi)."],["022.02.04.01:07","Altimeter: fill in the gaps → ____ (geometric, WGS-84 ellipsoid/geoid) can cross-check a suspected barometric error, but it differs from pressure altitude (temperature, pressure), has lower vertical accuracy and must not be used for vertical separation/approach unless certified.","GPS altitude (geometric, WGS-84 ellipsoid/geoid) can cross-check a suspected barometric error, but it differs from pressure altitude (temperature, pressure), has lower vertical accuracy and must not be used for vertical separation/approach unless certified."],["022.02.05.01:04","Vertical speed indicator: fill in the gaps → Units: ____ and ____ (1 m/s ≈ 197 ft/min ≈ 200). VSI: capsule fed with static pressure directly; case fed through a calibrated leak (metering unit) → pressure difference ∝ rate of change → lag ____.","Units: ft/min and m/s (1 m/s ≈ 197 ft/min ≈ 200). VSI: capsule fed with static pressure directly; case fed through a calibrated leak (metering unit) → pressure difference ∝ rate of change → lag ~6–9 s."],["022.02.05.01:03","Vertical speed indicator: fill in the gaps → ____: adds a ____ (accelerometer pistons) → immediate initial indication; in ____ shows fluctuations and in steep turns may show a false climb/descent. Inertial VSI from IRS data (blended with baro).","IVSI: adds a dashpot/inertia pump (accelerometer pistons) → immediate initial indication; in turbulence shows fluctuations and in steep turns may show a false climb/descent. Inertial VSI from IRS data (blended with baro)."],["022.02.05.01:04","Vertical speed indicator: fill in the gaps → Errors: static system errors, instrument error, ____. ____ → VSI reads ____.","Errors: static system errors, instrument error, lag. Static blocked → VSI reads zero."],["022.02.05.01:02","Vertical speed indicator: what do you remember about this? → In turbulence: VSI smooths, lags; IVSI jumps — use attitude + …","In turbulence: VSI smooths, lags; IVSI jumps — use attitude + average, don't chase needles."],["022.02.06.01:01","Airspeed indicator: fill in the gaps → Units: ____ (NM/h), ____, ____ (1 kt = 1.852 km/h = 1.15 mph).","Units: kt (NM/h), mph, km/h (1 kt = 1.852 km/h = 1.15 mph)."],["022.02.06.01:02","Airspeed indicator: fill in the gaps → ____: capsule fed with ____, case with ____ → measures dynamic pressure q = ½ρV² → calibrated in ISA sea-level density → IAS.","Principle: capsule fed with pitot (total) pressure, case with static → measures dynamic pressure q = ½ρV² → calibrated in ISA sea-level density → IAS."],["022.02.06.01:02","Airspeed indicator: fill in the gaps → Errors: ____, ____ (pitot/static location → ____ via correction table), compressibility (at high speed/altitude air compresses in the pitot → over-reads → CAS → EAS), density (non-standard density → EAS → TAS; TAS > EAS at altitude, ≈ +2 % per 1 000 ft).","Errors: instrument, position (pitot/static location → IAS → CAS via correction table), compressibility (at high speed/altitude air compresses in the pitot → over-reads → CAS → EAS), density (non-standard density → EAS → TAS; TAS > EAS at altitude, ≈ +2 % per 1 000 ft)."],["022.02.06.01:07","Airspeed indicator: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ = flap operating range (VS0 to VFE), ____ = normal operating range (VS1 to VNO), yellow arc = caution (VNO to VNE, smooth air only), red line = VNE; blue line = max autorotation speed (helicopters) / VYSE (twins, blue radial); red radial = VMC.","Colour codes: white arc = flap operating range (VS0 to VFE), green arc = normal operating range (VS1 to VNO), yellow arc = caution (VNO to VNE, smooth air only), red line = VNE; blue line = max autorotation speed (helicopters) / VYSE (twins, blue radial); red radial = VMC."],["022.02.06.01:08","Airspeed indicator: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ → IAS falls to zero; ____ → ASI acts as an altimeter (over-reads in climb, under-reads in descent). Static blocked → ASI under-reads in climb, over-reads in descent. Leak in the static line in a pressurised cabin → under-reads.","Blockages: pitot blocked (drain open) → IAS falls to zero; pitot blocked with drain blocked → ASI acts as an altimeter (over-reads in climb, under-reads in descent). Static blocked → ASI under-reads in climb, over-reads in descent. Leak in the static line in a pressurised cabin → under-reads."],["022.02.06.01:09","Airspeed indicator: fill in the gaps → ____: recognised by ____ ____, unexpected aircraft behaviour, speed not matching attitude/thrust, buffet, overspeed/stall warnings together, altitude disagreement.","Unreliable airspeed: recognised by disagreement between ASIs, unexpected aircraft behaviour, speed not matching attitude/thrust, buffet, overspeed/stall warnings together, altitude disagreement."],["022.03.03.01:02","Airspeed indicator: fill in the gaps → ____: memory items — ____, fly ____ from the QRH table (e.g. 10° pitch / CLB thrust at low level, level off, ~5°/check), flight directors off, then troubleshoot (compare, use GPS GS, AoA, back-up speed scale), don't rely on the speed.","Procedure: memory items — autopilot/autothrust OFF, fly pitch & thrust from the QRH table (e.g. 10° pitch / CLB thrust at low level, level off, ~5°/check), flight directors off, then troubleshoot (compare, use GPS GS, AoA, back-up speed scale), don't rely on the speed."],["022.03.01.01:01","Magnetism & compass: fill in the gaps → Earth's field approximates a ____; magnetic poles ≠ geographic poles; field lines horizontal at the magnetic equator, vertical at the magnetic poles. A ____ has N and S poles, like poles repel, aligns with field lines.","Earth's field approximates a dipole; magnetic poles ≠ geographic poles; field lines horizontal at the magnetic equator, vertical at the magnetic poles. A magnet has N and S poles, like poles repel, aligns with field lines."],["022.03.01.01:03","Magnetism & compass: fill in the gaps → ____ = angle between true and magnetic north (E or W); ____ (inclination) = angle between field and horizontal (0° at magnetic equator, 90° at poles). The compass aligns with the horizontal component; near the poles (H very weak) it becomes useless (roughly beyond ____).","Variation = angle between true and magnetic north (E or W); dip (inclination) = angle between field and horizontal (0° at magnetic equator, 90° at poles). The compass aligns with the horizontal component; near the poles (H very weak) it becomes useless (roughly beyond dip 70°+)."],["022.03.01.01:05","Magnetism & compass: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (\"East is least, West is best\"; with signs: E = +, W = −: MH = TH − var).","True ↔ magnetic: MH = TH − East variation / + West (\"East is least, West is best\"; with signs: E = +, W = −: MH = TH − var)."],["022.03.02.01:01","Magnetism & compass: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ — always present, magnitude fixed (steel parts); ____ — present when systems are powered/induced by earth field, varies with current and heading. Cause deviation.","Aircraft magnetism: permanent (hard iron) — always present, magnitude fixed (steel parts); electromagnetism/induced (soft iron) — present when systems are powered/induced by earth field, varies with current and heading. Cause deviation."],["022.03.02.01:02","Magnetism & compass: fill in the gaps → ____ (determine deviations on various headings on a compass base), ____ (adjust corrector magnets to minimise them), ____ (determine the residual deviations → deviation card). Swing required after installation, major modifications, electrical changes, lightning strike, periodically.","Compass swing (determine deviations on various headings on a compass base), compensation (adjust corrector magnets to minimise them), calibration (determine the residual deviations → deviation card). Swing required after installation, major modifications, electrical changes, lightning strike, periodically."],["022.03.02.01:05","Magnetism & compass: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (\"deviation East, compass least\"). Example: CH 090°, dev 3°W → MH 087°.","Deviation: CH → MH: MH = CH + East deviation / − West (\"deviation East, compass least\"). Example: CH 090°, dev 3°W → MH 087°."],["022.03.03.01:02","Magnetism & compass: fill in the gaps → ____ correct only in ____ ____. Acceleration errors (NH): on E/W headings, accelerate → apparent turn north (ANDS), decelerate → south.","Direct-reading compass correct only in straight, level, unaccelerated flight. Acceleration errors (NH): on E/W headings, accelerate → apparent turn north (ANDS), decelerate → south."],["022.03.01.01:01","Magnetism & compass: fill in the gaps → ____ (NH): on northerly headings the compass ____ → roll out ____ it shows the target heading; on southerly headings it leads → roll out after (UNOS: undershoot north, overshoot south). Pitch changes give similar errors.","Turning errors (NH): on northerly headings the compass lags → roll out before it shows the target heading; on southerly headings it leads → roll out after (UNOS: undershoot north, overshoot south). Pitch changes give similar errors."],["022.03.03.01:03","Magnetism & compass: fill in the gaps → ____ avoid compass errors: ____ → time (s) = heading change ÷ 3 (e.g. 90° → ____, 360° → 2 min).","Timed turns avoid compass errors: rate-1 = 3°/s → time (s) = heading change ÷ 3 (e.g. 90° → 30 s, 360° → 2 min)."],["022.03.02.01:04","Magnetism & compass: fill in the gaps → ____: liquid full (no bubbles), card free, not discoloured, reading plausible vs runway/DI, deviation card present and current.","Pre-flight check: liquid full (no bubbles), card free, not discoloured, reading plausible vs runway/DI, deviation card present and current."],["022.04.01.01:03","Gyroscope basics: fill in the gaps → ____ = spinning mass with high rotational speed. ____ (gyroscopic inertia): the spin axis keeps its direction in space — increases with mass, radius (moment of inertia) and rpm. Precession: a force applied to the rim acts ____ direction of rotation; rate ∝ force/rigidity.","Gyro = spinning mass with high rotational speed. Rigidity (gyroscopic inertia): the spin axis keeps its direction in space — increases with mass, radius (moment of inertia) and rpm. Precession: a force applied to the rim acts 90° later in the direction of rotation; rate ∝ force/rigidity."],["022.04.01.01:03","Gyroscope basics: fill in the gaps → ____ (convention: not counting the spin axis): ____ (rate gyro — turn indicator), ____ (displacement/space gyro — AI, DI).","Degrees of freedom (convention: not counting the spin axis): 1 (rate gyro — turn indicator), 2 (displacement/space gyro — AI, DI)."],["022.04.01.01:05","Gyroscope basics: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (bearing friction, imbalance), apparent wander (Earth rotation, ____ for a horizontal gyro), transport wander (movement over the Earth's surface — change of meridian convergence).","Wander/drift/topple: real wander (bearing friction, imbalance), apparent wander (Earth rotation, 15°/h × sin latitude for a horizontal gyro), transport wander (movement over the Earth's surface — change of meridian convergence)."],["022.04.01.01:01","Gyroscope basics: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (suction ~4.5–5.5 inHg, suction gauge; simple, but dirt/moisture, poor at altitude), ____ (higher rpm, rigidity, reliable at altitude; power-failure flag). Both have warning indications (vacuum gauge, OFF flag).","Drive: air/vacuum (suction ~4.5–5.5 inHg, suction gauge; simple, but dirt/moisture, poor at altitude), electric (higher rpm, rigidity, reliable at altitude; power-failure flag). Both have warning indications (vacuum gauge, OFF flag)."],["022.01.01.01:06","EASA LO 022.01.01.01.06 → Identify and read pressure measurement indications both for engine indications and other systems.","Errors: loss of sensing (blocked/broken line) → reads zero or frozen; incorrect indication (sensor/transmitter failure) → cross-check with other parameters (e.g. oil pressure vs temperature, EPR vs N1/FF)."],["022.01.03.01:02","EASA LO 022.01.03.01.02 → List the following units used for fuel quantity: — kilogramme; — pound; — litres; — gallons (US and imperial).","Fuel flow = quantity consumed per unit time, measured in the line to the engine (downstream of the fuel control, before the nozzles): mass flow kg/h, lb/h (turbines, compensated) or volume l/h, US gal/h, Imp gal/h (pistons). Total fuel used = integration of fuel flow over time (fuel-used counter); fuel remaining = initial − used."],["022.01.04.01:01","EASA LO 022.01.04.01.01 → Define ‘fuel flow’ and where it is measured.","Fuel flow = quantity consumed per unit time, measured in the line to the engine (downstream of the fuel control, before the nozzles): mass flow kg/h, lb/h (turbines, compensated) or volume l/h, US gal/h, Imp gal/h (pistons). Total fuel used = integration of fuel flow over time (fuel-used counter); fuel remaining = initial − used. / Fuel measured by volume (l, US gal = 3.785 l, Imp gal = 4.546 l) or mass (kg, lb; 1 kg = 2.205 lb). Mass is what matters for performance and energy."],["022.01.04.01:03","EASA LO 022.01.04.01.03 → List the following units used for fuel flow when measured by mass per hour: — kilogrammes/hour; — pounds/hour.","Fuel flow = quantity consumed per unit time, measured in the line to the engine (downstream of the fuel control, before the nozzles): mass flow kg/h, lb/h (turbines, compensated) or volume l/h, US gal/h, Imp gal/h (pistons). Total fuel used = integration of fuel flow over time (fuel-used counter); fuel remaining = initial − used. / Fuel measured by volume (l, US gal = 3.785 l, Imp gal = 4.546 l) or mass (kg, lb; 1 kg = 2.205 lb). Mass is what matters for performance and energy."],["022.01.04.01:04","EASA LO 022.01.04.01.04 → List the following units used for fuel flow when measured by volume per hour: — litres/hour; — imperial gallons/hour; — US gallons/hour.","Fuel flow = quantity consumed per unit time, measured in the line to the engine (downstream of the fuel control, before the nozzles): mass flow kg/h, lb/h (turbines, compensated) or volume l/h, US gal/h, Imp gal/h (pistons). Total fuel used = integration of fuel flow over time (fuel-used counter); fuel remaining = initial − used. / Fuel measured by volume (l, US gal = 3.785 l, Imp gal = 4.546 l) or mass (kg, lb; 1 kg = 2.205 lb). Mass is what matters for performance and energy."],["022.01.04.01:05","EASA LO 022.01.04.01.05 → Explain how total fuel consumption is obtained.","Fuel flow = quantity consumed per unit time, measured in the line to the engine (downstream of the fuel control, before the nozzles): mass flow kg/h, lb/h (turbines, compensated) or volume l/h, US gal/h, Imp gal/h (pistons). Total fuel used = integration of fuel flow over time (fuel-used counter); fuel remaining = initial − used."],["022.01.05.01:02","EASA LO 022.01.05.01.02 → Explain the typical units for engine speed: — rpm for piston-engine aircraft; — percentage for turbine-engine aircraft.","Aircraft pressure measurements: liquids (fuel, oil, hydraulic), air (bleed air, air conditioning, cabin), engine: manifold pressure (MAP, inHg) for piston engines with constant-speed prop, EPR (turbine exhaust/compressor inlet total pressure ratio). / Units: rpm (pistons), % N1/N2 (turbines, 100 % = reference rpm)."],["022.01.05.01:03","EASA LO 022.01.05.01.03 → Explain that some types of rpm indicators require electrical power to provide an indication.","Tachometers: mechanical (flexible drive shaft rotates a magnet → eddy-current drag cup; no power needed), electrical three- phase tacho-generator (generator on engine drives a synchronous motor in the indicator; no aircraft power needed), electronic (speed probe counts teeth of a phonic wheel → pulses → digital; needs electrical power). / Units: rpm (pistons), % N1/N2 (turbines, 100 % = reference rpm)."],["022.01.06.01:01","EASA LO 022.01.06.01.01 → List and describe the following two parameters used to represent thrust:","Thrust parameters: N1 (fan speed — directly related to thrust on high-bypass fans) and EPR (engine pressure ratio = Pt exhaust/Pt inlet). EPR engines are set by EPR; if EPR is incorrect/missing (e.g. iced inlet probe Pt2 → EPR over-reads → thrust set too low!) → revert to N1 mode (FADEC/crew)."],["022.01.06.01:03","EASA LO 022.01.06.01.03 → Give examples of display for N1 and EPR.","Errors: loss of sensing (blocked/broken line) → reads zero or frozen; incorrect indication (sensor/transmitter failure) → cross-check with other parameters (e.g. oil pressure vs temperature, EPR vs N1/FF). / Thrust parameters: N1 (fan speed — directly related to thrust on high-bypass fans) and EPR (engine pressure ratio = Pt exhaust/Pt inlet). EPR engines are set by EPR; if EPR is incorrect/missing (e.g. iced inlet probe Pt2 → EPR over-reads → thrust set too low!) → revert to N1 mode (FADEC/crew)."],["022.01.07.01:01","EASA LO 022.01.07.01.01 → Define ‘torque’.","Torque = force × arm (Nm, ft·lb, in·lb, or %). Power = torque × rpm × const. (P = 2π·n·T). Turboprops/helicopters use torque. Mechanical torquemeter: oil pressure from helical gears in the reduction gearbox (axial thrust ∝ torque) — heavier; electronic: phase shift between two phonic wheels on a torsion shaft — lighter, accurate."],["022.01.07.01:04","EASA LO 022.01.07.01.04 → State that engine torque can be displayed as a percentage.","Vibration monitoring (jets): detects imbalance (blade damage, bearing failure) early. Sensors: piezoelectric crystal (accelerometer produces voltage under vibration) or magnet/coil (velocity pickup). No physical unit — displayed as a number/level with threshold/advisory values (e.g. > 4 = amber)."],["022.01.07.01:05","EASA LO 022.01.07.01.05 → List and describe the following different types of torquemeters, and explain their operating principles: — mechanical; — electronic.","Torque = force × arm (Nm, ft·lb, in·lb, or %). Power = torque × rpm × const. (P = 2π·n·T). Turboprops/helicopters use torque. Mechanical torquemeter: oil pressure from helical gears in the reduction gearbox (axial thrust ∝ torque) — heavier; electronic: phase shift between two phonic wheels on a torsion shaft — lighter, accurate."],["022.01.09.01:02","EASA LO 022.01.09.01.02 → Describe the operating principle of a vibration-monitoring system using the following two types of sensors: — piezoelectric crystal; — magnet.","Vibration monitoring (jets): detects imbalance (blade damage, bearing failure) early. Sensors: piezoelectric crystal (accelerometer produces voltage under vibration) or magnet/coil (velocity pickup). No physical unit — displayed as a number/level with threshold/advisory values (e.g. > 4 = amber)."],["022.02.04.01:02","EASA LO 022.02.04.01.02 → Define the following terms: — height, altitude;","QNH = pressure reduced to MSL in ISA → altimeter reads altitude (aerodrome elevation on the ground). QFE = pressure at aerodrome level → reads height (0 on the ground). 1013.25 hPa (standard) → pressure altitude/flight level."],["022.02.04.01:05","EASA LO 022.02.04.01.05 → Describe and compare the following three types of altimeters and reason(s) why particular designs may be required in certain airspace: — simple altimeter (single capsule); — sensitive altimeter (multi-capsule); — servo-assisted altimeter.","Types: simple (one capsule, ~pointer, inaccurate), sensitive (multiple capsules, sub-scale setting, 3 pointers/drum — accurate), servo-assisted (capsule movement sensed by an E/I- bar transducer, servo drives display, very accurate at high altitude, less friction/lag — needed in RVSM). Displays: 3-pointer (misreading!), drum-pointer, counter, vertical tape (EFIS)."],["022.02.05.01:05","EASA LO 022.02.05.01.05 → Describe the effects on a VSI of a blockage or a leakage on the static pressure line.","Errors: static system errors, instrument error, lag. Static blocked → VSI reads zero. / In turbulence: VSI smooths, lags; IVSI jumps — use attitude + average, don't chase needles."],["022.02.05.01:06","EASA LO 022.02.05.01.06 → Give examples of a VSI display. X X X X X X Exam BK Remarks","Errors: static system errors, instrument error, lag. Static blocked → VSI reads zero. / In turbulence: VSI smooths, lags; IVSI jumps — use attitude + average, don't chase needles."],["022.02.05.01:07","EASA LO 022.02.05.01.07 → Compare the indications of a VSI and an IVSI during flight in turbulence and appropriate pilot technique during manoeuvring using either type.","In turbulence: VSI smooths, lags; IVSI jumps — use attitude + average, don't chase needles. / Errors: static system errors, instrument error, lag. Static blocked → VSI reads zero."],["022.02.06.01:04","EASA LO 022.02.06.01.04 → Give examples of an ASI display: pointer, vertical straight scale, and digital (HUD display).","Types: simple (one capsule, ~pointer, inaccurate), sensitive (multiple capsules, sub-scale setting, 3 pointers/drum — accurate), servo-assisted (capsule movement sensed by an E/I- bar transducer, servo drives display, very accurate at high altitude, less friction/lag — needed in RVSM). Displays: 3-pointer (misreading!), drum-pointer, counter, vertical tape (EFIS)."],["022.02.06.01:05","EASA LO 022.02.06.01.05 → Demonstrate the use of an ASI correction table for position error.","Correction tables: add temperature correction to DA/MDA/minimum altitudes in cold weather; apply position-error correction. / Errors: static system errors, instrument error, lag. Static blocked → VSI reads zero."],["022.02.06.01:06","EASA LO 022.02.06.01.06 → Define and explain the following colour codes that can be used on an ASI: — white arc (flap operating speed range);","Colour codes: white arc = flap operating range (VS0 to VFE), green arc = normal operating range (VS1 to VNO), yellow arc = caution (VNO to VNE, smooth air only), red line = VNE; blue line = max autorotation speed (helicopters) / VYSE (twins, blue radial); red radial = VMC."],["022.03.01.01:02","EASA LO 022.03.01.01.02 → Explain the properties of a magnet.","Earth's field approximates a dipole; magnetic poles ≠ geographic poles; field lines horizontal at the magnetic equator, vertical at the magnetic poles. A magnet has N and S poles, like poles repel, aligns with field lines. / Variation = angle between true and magnetic north (E or W); dip (inclination) = angle between field and horizontal (0° at magnetic equator, 90° at poles). The compass aligns with the horizontal component; near the poles (H very weak) it becomes useless (roughly beyond dip 70°+)."],["022.03.01.01:04","EASA LO 022.03.01.01.04 → Describe that a magnetic compass will align itself to both the horizontal (azimuth) and vertical (dip) components of the Earth’s magnetic field, thus will not function in the vicinity of the magnetic poles.","Earth's field approximates a dipole; magnetic poles ≠ geographic poles; field lines horizontal at the magnetic equator, vertical at the magnetic poles. A magnet has N and S poles, like poles repel, aligns with field lines. / Variation = angle between true and magnetic north (E or W); dip (inclination) = angle between field and horizontal (0° at magnetic equator, 90° at poles). The compass aligns with the horizontal component; near the poles (H very weak) it becomes useless (roughly beyond dip 70°+)."],["022.03.02.01:03","EASA LO 022.03.02.01.03 → Explain how permanent magnetism within the aircraft structure and electromagnetism from the aircraft systems affect the accuracy of a compass.","Aircraft magnetism: permanent (hard iron) — always present, magnitude fixed (steel parts); electromagnetism/induced (soft iron) — present when systems are powered/induced by earth field, varies with current and heading. Cause deviation."],["022.03.03.01:01","EASA LO 022.03.03.01.01 → Explain the purpose of a direct-reading magnetic compass.","Timed turns avoid compass errors: rate-1 = 3°/s → time (s) = heading change ÷ 3 (e.g. 90° → 30 s, 360° → 2 min). / Direct-reading compass correct only in straight, level, unaccelerated flight. Acceleration errors (NH): on E/W headings, accelerate → apparent turn north (ANDS), decelerate → south."],["022.03.03.01:04","EASA LO 022.03.03.01.04 → Describe the serviceability check for a direct-reading magnetic compass prior to flight, such as:","Timed turns avoid compass errors: rate-1 = 3°/s → time (s) = heading change ÷ 3 (e.g. 90° → 30 s, 360° → 2 min)."],["022.04.01.01:01","EASA LO 022.04.01.01.01 → Define a ‘gyro’.","Degrees of freedom (convention: not counting the spin axis): 1 (rate gyro — turn indicator), 2 (displacement/space gyro — AI, DI). / Wander/drift/topple: real wander (bearing friction, imbalance), apparent wander (Earth rotation, 15°/h × sin latitude for a horizontal gyro), transport wander (movement over the Earth's surface — change of meridian convergence)."],["022.04.01.01:04","EASA LO 022.04.01.01.04 → Explain the following terms: — rigidity; — precession; — wander (drift/topple).","Wander/drift/topple: real wander (bearing friction, imbalance), apparent wander (Earth rotation, 15°/h × sin latitude for a horizontal gyro), transport wander (movement over the Earth's surface — change of meridian convergence). / Gyro = spinning mass with high rotational speed. Rigidity (gyroscopic inertia): the spin axis keeps its direction in space — increases with mass, radius (moment of inertia) and rpm. Precession: a force applied to the rim acts 90° later in the direction of rotation; rate ∝ force/rigidity."]],"72":[["040.03.01.03:03",": what do you remember about this? → Review of 040.03 Basic Aviation Psychology. Information processing, …","Review of 040.03 Basic Aviation Psychology. Information processing, error, decision-making and stress are explained in full on the #68 page — here the recap plus the topics new in this stage: multi-crew cooperation, communication and personality."],["040.03.01.02:02","Recap (see #68): fill in the gaps → ____ selective/divided, ____ = sustained; hypovigilance with monotony/fatigue. Perception bottom-up + top-down → subjective, expectation bias. Memory: sensory → working (____ items, 10–20 s) → long-term (semantic, episodic, procedural). Learning: conditioning, insight, imitation; skill phases cognitive–associative–autonomous; Rasmussen skill/rule/knowledge-based.","Attention selective/divided, vigilance = sustained; hypovigilance with monotony/fatigue. Perception bottom-up + top-down → subjective, expectation bias. Memory: sensory → working (5 ± 2 items, 10–20 s) → long-term (semantic, episodic, procedural). Learning: conditioning, insight, imitation; skill phases cognitive–associative–autonomous; Rasmussen skill/rule/knowledge-based."],["040.03.02.04:03","Recap (see #68): fill in the gaps → ____ (perceive–comprehend–project); ____: slip, lapse/omission, mistake, violation; active vs latent; ____ cheese/error chain; action slip & environmental capture. Decision-making: biases, risky shift, FOR-DEC. Yerkes–Dodson, stress = perceived demand vs ability, GAS (alarm–resistance– exhaustion), fatigue acute/chronic, FRMS.","SA (perceive–comprehend–project); error types: slip, lapse/omission, mistake, violation; active vs latent; Swiss cheese/error chain; action slip & environmental capture. Decision-making: biases, risky shift, FOR-DEC. Yerkes–Dodson, stress = perceived demand vs ability, GAS (alarm–resistance– exhaustion), fatigue acute/chronic, FRMS."],["040.03.04.01:01","Safety awareness & coordination: fill in the gaps → Be aware of ____ performance (fatigue, stress, errors) — the other crew member is a safety barrier; unrecognised incapacitation or overload is a risk.","Be aware of own and others' performance (fatigue, stress, errors) — the other crew member is a safety barrier; unrecognised incapacitation or overload is a risk."],["040.03.04.02:03","Safety awareness & coordination: fill in the gaps → ____ objectives: redundancy, error detection/cross-checking, workload sharing, enhanced SA. Elements: ____, ____, checklists, briefings, standard callouts/communication, monitoring and cross-checking, CRM.","Multi-crew concept objectives: redundancy, error detection/cross-checking, workload sharing, enhanced SA. Elements: defined roles (PF/PM), SOPs, checklists, briefings, standard callouts/communication, monitoring and cross-checking, CRM."],["040.03.04.02:08","Safety awareness & coordination: fill in the gaps → ____: standardised, predictable actions → any crew can work together; reduce workload, catch errors (deviations become visible), manage threats (TEM). ____ (departure, approach): share the plan, expectations, threats, contingencies — interactive, relevant, not ritual.","SOPs: standardised, predictable actions → any crew can work together; reduce workload, catch errors (deviations become visible), manage threats (TEM). Briefings (departure, approach): share the plan, expectations, threats, contingencies — interactive, relevant, not ritual."],["040.03.01.03:05","Safety awareness & coordination: fill in the gaps → ____: read-do or challenge-response/do-verify; ensure no item forgotten, especially under stress/interruption.","Checklists: read-do or challenge-response/do-verify; ensure no item forgotten, especially under stress/interruption."],["040.03.04.02:09","Safety awareness & coordination: fill in the gaps → ____: after conversion to a new type/company old ____/habits resurface, differing company culture, complacency with routine, hazardous attitudes.","Threats of SOPs: after conversion to a new type/company old motor programmes/habits resurface, differing company culture, complacency with routine, hazardous attitudes."],["040.03.04.03:03","Cooperation: fill in the gaps → ____ = working together towards a common goal with interaction/interdependence; ____ = working in parallel on the same goal without interaction.","Cooperation = working together towards a common goal with interaction/interdependence; coaction = working in parallel on the same goal without interaction."],["040.03.04.03:05","Cooperation: fill in the gaps → ____ = two or more people interacting with common goals, roles and norms; ____ makes each member's performance affect others. Teamwork: + more resources, error detection, synergy, motivation; − groupthink, social loafing, conflict, slower decisions.","Group = two or more people interacting with common goals, roles and norms; interdependence makes each member's performance affect others. Teamwork: + more resources, error detection, synergy, motivation; − groupthink, social loafing, conflict, slower decisions."],["040.03.04.03:06","Cooperation: fill in the gaps → ____: team performance greater than the sum of individuals. ____: degree to which members feel attracted to and stay in the group. ____: striving for unanimity overrides realistic appraisal of alternatives (too high cohesion, strong leader, isolation). Good teamwork needs common goals, clear roles, open communication, mutual respect, feedback, shared mental model.","Synergy: team performance greater than the sum of individuals. Cohesion: degree to which members feel attracted to and stay in the group. Groupthink: striving for unanimity overrides realistic appraisal of alternatives (too high cohesion, strong leader, isolation). Good teamwork needs common goals, clear roles, open communication, mutual respect, feedback, shared mental model."],["040.03.04.03:10","Cooperation: fill in the gaps → ____ (expected behaviour for a position) and ____ (unwritten group rules) regulate behaviour; role patterns: leader, follower, expert, opponent, mediator, \"silent\" member.","Roles (expected behaviour for a position) and norms (unwritten group rules) regulate behaviour; role patterns: leader, follower, expert, opponent, mediator, \"silent\" member."],["040.03.04.03:11","Cooperation: fill in the gaps → Influences: ____ (convincing by argument), ____ (adapting to group norms/majority — Asch), ____ (public agreement without private acceptance), obedience (following orders of authority — Milgram).","Influences: persuasion (convincing by argument), conformity (adapting to group norms/majority — Asch), compliance (public agreement without private acceptance), obedience (following orders of authority — Milgram)."],["040.03.04.03:09","Cooperation: fill in the gaps → ____ = rank/prestige (seniority, age, experience); ____ = function (PF/PM). A mix (e.g. senior FO flying with young captain, or captain as PM) can confuse authority → speak-up problems.","Status = rank/prestige (seniority, age, experience); role = function (PF/PM). A mix (e.g. senior FO flying with young captain, or captain as PM) can confuse authority → speak-up problems."],["040.03.04.03:15","Cooperation: fill in the gaps → ____ = influencing others towards goals; ____ = actively supporting the leader, speaking up, monitoring. ____ cockpit authority gradient: too steep → autocratic captain, FO afraid to speak; too flat → laissez-faire, no clear decisions; ideal = synergistic/democratic (captain leads but invites input).","Leadership = influencing others towards goals; followership = actively supporting the leader, speaking up, monitoring. Trans- cockpit authority gradient: too steep → autocratic captain, FO afraid to speak; too flat → laissez-faire, no clear decisions; ideal = synergistic/democratic (captain leads but invites input)."],["040.03.04.03:16","Cooperation: what do you remember about this? → Positive leadership: clear, competent, open to input, sets example, …","Positive leadership: clear, competent, open to input, sets example, balances task & relationship, manages workload, handles conflict."],["040.03.04.04:03","Communication: fill in the gaps → ____ = transfer of information/meaning between sender and receiver. Components: ____ ____, noise/filters. Two-way (with feedback/read-back) is more accurate than one-way (faster but error-prone).","Communication = transfer of information/meaning between sender and receiver. Components: sender, message (encoded), channel, receiver (decodes), feedback, noise/filters. Two-way (with feedback/read-back) is more accurate than one-way (faster but error-prone)."],["040.03.04.04:01","Communication: fill in the gaps → ____ (tone, facial expression, gestures, posture, eye contact) carries a large part of the message, especially emotions; limited in the flight deck (side-by-side seating, masks, headsets, darkness).","Non-verbal (tone, facial expression, gestures, posture, eye contact) carries a large part of the message, especially emotions; limited in the flight deck (side-by-side seating, masks, headsets, darkness)."],["040.03.04.04:07","Communication: fill in the gaps → ____ communication = clearly stated (unambiguous, standard callouts; can be slower); ____ = assumed/unspoken (efficient in well-known crews, risky with unfamiliar crews/abnormal situations).","Explicit communication = clearly stated (unambiguous, standard callouts; can be slower); implicit = assumed/unspoken (efficient in well-known crews, risky with unfamiliar crews/abnormal situations)."],["040.03.04.04:08","Communication: fill in the gaps → ____ builds relationships at low workload; in high workload use concise ____ language (sterile cockpit below 10 000 ft).","Social language builds relationships at low workload; in high workload use concise professional language (sterile cockpit below 10 000 ft)."],["040.03.04.04:09","Communication: what do you remember about this? → Obstacles: noise, workload, jargon, language/culture, assumptions, …","Obstacles: noise, workload, jargon, language/culture, assumptions, expectation, authority gradient, emotions, poor listening, ambiguity."],["040.03.04.04:11","Communication: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (within oneself: conflicting goals) vs ____ (between persons). Escalation: differing views → debate → loss of objectivity → personal attacks → breakdown of cooperation. Consequences: poor communication, reduced SA, errors, unsafe decisions.","Conflict: intrapersonal (within oneself: conflicting goals) vs interpersonal (between persons). Escalation: differing views → debate → loss of objectivity → personal attacks → breakdown of cooperation. Consequences: poor communication, reduced SA, errors, unsafe decisions."],["040.03.04.04:13","Communication: fill in the gaps → Tools: ____ (ask questions), ____ ____ (summarise, confirm), advocacy (state your position and concerns assertively, e.g. PACE/\"I'm concerned… I'm uncomfortable… this is unsafe\"), conflict resolution focusing on \"what's right, not who's right\".","Tools: inquiry (ask questions), active listening (summarise, confirm), advocacy (state your position and concerns assertively, e.g. PACE/\"I'm concerned… I'm uncomfortable… this is unsafe\"), conflict resolution focusing on \"what's right, not who's right\"."],["040.03.04.04:14","Communication: what do you remember about this? → High workload: hearing and processing capacity limited, …","High workload: hearing and processing capacity limited, non-verbal/visual cues missed → keep messages short, prioritise, confirm."],["040.03.05.01:05","Human behaviour: fill in the gaps → ____ is determined by ____, ____ and environment/situation (upbringing, schooling, training). Personality = relatively stable characteristic pattern of thinking, feeling and acting (genetics + early experience);","Behaviour is determined by personality, attitude and environment/situation (upbringing, schooling, training). Personality = relatively stable characteristic pattern of thinking, feeling and acting (genetics + early experience);"],["040.03.05.01:04","Human behaviour: fill in the gaps → ____ = learned tendency to evaluate something positively/negatively (can be changed by training/experience); ____ = observable action. Good and bad ____ form through repetition. Personality differences and selfish attitudes affect crew performance.","attitude = learned tendency to evaluate something positively/negatively (can be changed by training/experience); behaviour = observable action. Good and bad habits form through repetition. Personality differences and selfish attitudes affect crew performance."],["040.03.05.02:01","Human behaviour: fill in the gaps → ____: two dimensions — ____ and ____; ideal pilot = stable extravert (emotionally stable, sociable, calm, communicative, not impulsive).","Eysenck model: two dimensions — introversion–extraversion and emotional stability–instability (neuroticism); ideal pilot = stable extravert (emotionally stable, sociable, calm, communicative, not impulsive)."],["040.03.05.02:03","Human behaviour: fill in the gaps → ____ = how a person sees themselves; change of personality/attitude requires a change of self-concept. ____ ____ can be masked by aggression and self- assertiveness (over-compensation). Self-discipline: adhering to SOPs, limits and rules even when unobserved — essential for safety.","Self-concept = how a person sees themselves; change of personality/attitude requires a change of self-concept. Under- confidence can be masked by aggression and self- assertiveness (over-compensation). Self-discipline: adhering to SOPs, limits and rules even when unobserved — essential for safety."],["040.03.05.03:01","Human behaviour: fill in the gaps → ____ & antidotes: ____ (\"don't tell me\") → follow the rules, they're usually right; ____ (\"I can do it\") → taking chances is foolish; impulsivity (\"do something quickly\") → not so fast, think first; invulnerability (\"it won't happen to me\") → it could happen to me;","Hazardous attitudes & antidotes: anti-authority (\"don't tell me\") → follow the rules, they're usually right; macho (\"I can do it\") → taking chances is foolish; impulsivity (\"do something quickly\") → not so fast, think first; invulnerability (\"it won't happen to me\") → it could happen to me;"],["040.03.07.02:04","Human behaviour: fill in the gaps → ____ (\"what's the use\") → I'm not helpless, I can make a difference; ____ (over-confidence in routine/automation) → stay vigilant.","resignation (\"what's the use\") → I'm not helpless, I can make a difference; complacency (over-confidence in routine/automation) → stay vigilant."],["040.03.05.03:02","Human behaviour: fill in the gaps → ____: stable, self-disciplined, assertive but cooperative, open to criticism, good situational and self-awareness, responsible. Attitudes shape how SOPs are applied, how conflicts are handled and how risks are accepted.","Ideal crew member: stable, self-disciplined, assertive but cooperative, open to criticism, good situational and self-awareness, responsible. Attitudes shape how SOPs are applied, how conflicts are handled and how risks are accepted."],["040.03.01.01:02","EASA LO 040.03.01.01.02 → Differentiate between ‘selective’ and ‘divided’ attention.","Attention selective/divided, vigilance = sustained; hypovigilance with monotony/fatigue. Perception bottom-up + top-down → subjective, expectation bias. Memory: sensory → working (5 ± 2 items, 10–20 s) → long-term (semantic, episodic, procedural). Learning: conditioning, insight, imitation; skill phases cognitive–associative–autonomous; Rasmussen skill/rule/knowledge-based."],["040.03.01.01:03","EASA LO 040.03.01.01.03 → Define ‘hypovigilance’.","Attention selective/divided, vigilance = sustained; hypovigilance with monotony/fatigue. Perception bottom-up + top-down → subjective, expectation bias. Memory: sensory → working (5 ± 2 items, 10–20 s) → long-term (semantic, episodic, procedural). Learning: conditioning, insight, imitation; skill phases cognitive–associative–autonomous; Rasmussen skill/rule/knowledge-based."],["040.03.01.03:01","EASA LO 040.03.01.03.01 → Explain the link between the types of memory (to include sensory, working/short-term and long-term memory).","Attention selective/divided, vigilance = sustained; hypovigilance with monotony/fatigue. Perception bottom-up + top-down → subjective, expectation bias. Memory: sensory → working (5 ± 2 items, 10–20 s) → long-term (semantic, episodic, procedural). Learning: conditioning, insight, imitation; skill phases cognitive–associative–autonomous; Rasmussen skill/rule/knowledge-based."],["040.03.01.04:01","EASA LO 040.03.01.04.01 → Explain and distinguish between the following basic forms of learning: — classic and operant conditioning (behaviouristic approach); — learning by insight (cognitive approach); — learning by imitating (modelling).","Attention selective/divided, vigilance = sustained; hypovigilance with monotony/fatigue. Perception bottom-up + top-down → subjective, expectation bias. Memory: sensory → working (5 ± 2 items, 10–20 s) → long-term (semantic, episodic, procedural). Learning: conditioning, insight, imitation; skill phases cognitive–associative–autonomous; Rasmussen skill/rule/knowledge-based."],["040.03.01.04:05","EASA LO 040.03.01.04.05 → Describe the advantage of planning and anticipation of future actions: — define the term ‘skills’; — state the three phases of learning a skill (Anderson: cognitive, associative and autonomous phase).","Attention selective/divided, vigilance = sustained; hypovigilance with monotony/fatigue. Perception bottom-up + top-down → subjective, expectation bias. Memory: sensory → working (5 ± 2 items, 10–20 s) → long-term (semantic, episodic, procedural). Learning: conditioning, insight, imitation; skill phases cognitive–associative–autonomous; Rasmussen skill/rule/knowledge-based."],["040.03.01.04:09","EASA LO 040.03.01.04.09 → State the possible problems or risks associated with skill-, rule- and knowledge-based behaviour.","Attention selective/divided, vigilance = sustained; hypovigilance with monotony/fatigue. Perception bottom-up + top-down → subjective, expectation bias. Memory: sensory → working (5 ± 2 items, 10–20 s) → long-term (semantic, episodic, procedural). Learning: conditioning, insight, imitation; skill phases cognitive–associative–autonomous; Rasmussen skill/rule/knowledge-based."],["040.03.02.03:05","EASA LO 040.03.02.03.05 → Distinguish between an active and a latent error, and give examples.","SA (perceive–comprehend–project); error types: slip, lapse/omission, mistake, violation; active vs latent; Swiss cheese/error chain; action slip & environmental capture. Decision-making: biases, risky shift, FOR-DEC. Yerkes–Dodson, stress = perceived demand vs ability, GAS (alarm–resistance– exhaustion), fatigue acute/chronic, FRMS."],["040.03.03.01:09","EASA LO 040.03.03.01.09 → Describe the positive and negative influences exerted by other group members on an individual’s decision-making process (risky shift).","SA (perceive–comprehend–project); error types: slip, lapse/omission, mistake, violation; active vs latent; Swiss cheese/error chain; action slip & environmental capture. Decision-making: biases, risky shift, FOR-DEC. Yerkes–Dodson, stress = perceived demand vs ability, GAS (alarm–resistance– exhaustion), fatigue acute/chronic, FRMS."],["040.03.03.01:10","EASA LO 040.03.03.01.10 → Explain the general idea behind the creation of a model for decision-making based upon: — definition of the aim; — collection of information; — risk assessment; — development of options; — evaluation of options; — decision; — implementation; — consequences; — review and feedback.","Review of 040.03 Basic Aviation Psychology. Information processing, error, decision-making and stress are explained in full on the #68 page — here the recap plus the topics new in this stage: multi-crew cooperation, communication and personality."],["040.03.04.02:01","EASA LO 040.03.04.02.01 → Name the objectives of the multi-crew concept.","Multi-crew concept objectives: redundancy, error detection/cross-checking, workload sharing, enhanced SA. Elements: defined roles (PF/PM), SOPs, checklists, briefings, standard callouts/communication, monitoring and cross-checking, CRM."],["040.03.04.02:02","EASA LO 040.03.04.02.02 → State and explain the elements of multi-crew concepts.","Multi-crew concept objectives: redundancy, error detection/cross-checking, workload sharing, enhanced SA. Elements: defined roles (PF/PM), SOPs, checklists, briefings, standard callouts/communication, monitoring and cross-checking, CRM."],["040.03.04.02:04","EASA LO 040.03.04.02.04 → Describe the purpose of and procedure for crew briefings.","Multi-crew concept objectives: redundancy, error detection/cross-checking, workload sharing, enhanced SA. Elements: defined roles (PF/PM), SOPs, checklists, briefings, standard callouts/communication, monitoring and cross-checking, CRM."],["040.03.04.02:05","EASA LO 040.03.04.02.05 → Describe the purpose of and procedure for checklists.","Multi-crew concept objectives: redundancy, error detection/cross-checking, workload sharing, enhanced SA. Elements: defined roles (PF/PM), SOPs, checklists, briefings, standard callouts/communication, monitoring and cross-checking, CRM."],["040.03.04.03:01","EASA LO 040.03.04.03.01 → Distinguish between cooperation and coaction.","Cooperation = working together towards a common goal with interaction/interdependence; coaction = working in parallel on the same goal without interaction."],["040.03.04.03:02","EASA LO 040.03.04.03.02 → Define the term ‘group’.","Roles (expected behaviour for a position) and norms (unwritten group rules) regulate behaviour; role patterns: leader, follower, expert, opponent, mediator, \"silent\" member. / Influences: persuasion (convincing by argument), conformity (adapting to group norms/majority — Asch), compliance (public agreement without private acceptance), obedience (following orders of authority — Milgram)."],["040.03.04.03:04","EASA LO 040.03.04.03.04 → List the advantages and disadvantages of teamwork.","Group = two or more people interacting with common goals, roles and norms; interdependence makes each member's performance affect others. Teamwork: + more resources, error detection, synergy, motivation; − groupthink, social loafing, conflict, slower decisions."],["040.03.04.03:07","EASA LO 040.03.04.03.07 → Define the term ‘groupthink’.","Group = two or more people interacting with common goals, roles and norms; interdependence makes each member's performance affect others. Teamwork: + more resources, error detection, synergy, motivation; − groupthink, social loafing, conflict, slower decisions."],["040.03.04.03:14","EASA LO 040.03.04.03.14 → Explain the terms ‘leadership’ and ‘followership’.","Positive leadership: clear, competent, open to input, sets example, balances task & relationship, manages workload, handles conflict."],["040.03.04.04:02","EASA LO 040.03.04.04.02 → List the most basic components of interpersonal communication.","Communication = transfer of information/meaning between sender and receiver. Components: sender, message (encoded), channel, receiver (decodes), feedback, noise/filters. Two-way (with feedback/read-back) is more accurate than one-way (faster but error-prone)."],["040.03.04.04:05","EASA LO 040.03.04.04.05 → Name the importance of non-verbal communication.","High workload: hearing and processing capacity limited, non-verbal/visual cues missed → keep messages short, prioritise, confirm."],["040.03.04.04:06","EASA LO 040.03.04.04.06 → Describe the general aspects of non-verbal communication.","High workload: hearing and processing capacity limited, non-verbal/visual cues missed → keep messages short, prioritise, confirm."],["040.03.04.04:10","EASA LO 040.03.04.04.10 → Explain the difference between intrapersonal and interpersonal conflict.","Conflict: intrapersonal (within oneself: conflicting goals) vs interpersonal (between persons). Escalation: differing views → debate → loss of objectivity → personal attacks → breakdown of cooperation. Consequences: poor communication, reduced SA, errors, unsafe decisions. / resignation (\"what's the use\") → I'm not helpless, I can make a difference; complacency (over-confidence in routine/automation) → stay vigilant."],["040.03.05.01:01","EASA LO 040.03.05.01.01 → Describe the factors that determine an individual’s behaviour.","Behaviour is determined by personality, attitude and environment/situation (upbringing, schooling, training). Personality = relatively stable characteristic pattern of thinking, feeling and acting (genetics + early experience);"],["040.03.05.01:02","EASA LO 040.03.05.01.02 → Define and distinguish between ‘personality’, ‘attitude’ and ‘behaviour’.","Behaviour is determined by personality, attitude and environment/situation (upbringing, schooling, training). Personality = relatively stable characteristic pattern of thinking, feeling and acting (genetics + early experience); / attitude = learned tendency to evaluate something positively/negatively (can be changed by training/experience); behaviour = observable action. Good and bad habits form through repetition. Personality differences and selfish attitudes affect crew performance."],["040.03.05.01:03","EASA LO 040.03.05.01.03 → State the origin of personality and attitude.","Behaviour is determined by personality, attitude and environment/situation (upbringing, schooling, training). Personality = relatively stable characteristic pattern of thinking, feeling and acting (genetics + early experience); / Self-concept = how a person sees themselves; change of personality/attitude requires a change of self-concept. Under- confidence can be masked by aggression and self- assertiveness (over-compensation). Self-discipline: adhering to SOPs, limits and rules even when unobserved — essential for safety."],["040.03.05.01:06","EASA LO 040.03.05.01.06 → State that personality differences and selfish attitude may have effects on flight crew performance.","attitude = learned tendency to evaluate something positively/negatively (can be changed by training/experience); behaviour = observable action. Good and bad habits form through repetition. Personality differences and selfish attitudes affect crew performance."],["040.03.05.02:02","EASA LO 040.03.05.02.02 → Define the term ‘self-concept’ and the role it plays in any change of personality.","Self-concept = how a person sees themselves; change of personality/attitude requires a change of self-concept. Under- confidence can be masked by aggression and self- assertiveness (over-compensation). Self-discipline: adhering to SOPs, limits and rules even when unobserved — essential for safety."],["040.03.05.02:04","EASA LO 040.03.05.02.04 → Define ‘self-discipline’ and justify its importance for flight safety.","Self-concept = how a person sees themselves; change of personality/attitude requires a change of self-concept. Under- confidence can be masked by aggression and self- assertiveness (over-compensation). Self-discipline: adhering to SOPs, limits and rules even when unobserved — essential for safety."],["040.03.06.02:05","EASA LO 040.03.06.02.05 → State the relationship between stress and performance.","Be aware of own and others' performance (fatigue, stress, errors) — the other crew member is a safety barrier; unrecognised incapacitation or overload is a risk."],["040.03.06.02:08","EASA LO 040.03.06.02.08 → Discuss the concept of ‘break point’ with regard to stress, overload and performance.","Be aware of own and others' performance (fatigue, stress, errors) — the other crew member is a safety barrier; unrecognised incapacitation or overload is a risk."],["040.03.06.02:13","EASA LO 040.03.06.02.13 → State that stress is a result of perceived demands and perceived ability.","SA (perceive–comprehend–project); error types: slip, lapse/omission, mistake, violation; active vs latent; Swiss cheese/error chain; action slip & environmental capture. Decision-making: biases, risky shift, FOR-DEC. Yerkes–Dodson, stress = perceived demand vs ability, GAS (alarm–resistance– exhaustion), fatigue acute/chronic, FRMS."],["040.03.06.02:18","EASA LO 040.03.06.02.18 → State the general effect of chronic stress and the biological reaction by means of the three stages of the general adaptation syndrome (Selye): alarm, resistance, and exhaustion.","SA (perceive–comprehend–project); error types: slip, lapse/omission, mistake, violation; active vs latent; Swiss cheese/error chain; action slip & environmental capture. Decision-making: biases, risky shift, FOR-DEC. Yerkes–Dodson, stress = perceived demand vs ability, GAS (alarm–resistance– exhaustion), fatigue acute/chronic, FRMS."],["040.03.06.02:24","EASA LO 040.03.06.02.24 → Describe the effect of human underload/overload on effectiveness in the flight crew compartment.","Be aware of own and others' performance (fatigue, stress, errors) — the other crew member is a safety barrier; unrecognised incapacitation or overload is a risk."],["040.03.06.05:01","EASA LO 040.03.06.05.01 → Explain the term ‘fatigue’ and differentiate between the two types of fatigue (short-term and chronic fatigue).","SA (perceive–comprehend–project); error types: slip, lapse/omission, mistake, violation; active vs latent; Swiss cheese/error chain; action slip & environmental capture. Decision-making: biases, risky shift, FOR-DEC. Yerkes–Dodson, stress = perceived demand vs ability, GAS (alarm–resistance– exhaustion), fatigue acute/chronic, FRMS."],["040.03.06.05:03","EASA LO 040.03.06.05.03 → Identify the symptoms and describe the effects of fatigue.","Be aware of own and others' performance (fatigue, stress, errors) — the other crew member is a safety barrier; unrecognised incapacitation or overload is a risk."]],"73":[["081.08.00.00",": what do you remember about this? → General review of 081 Principles of Flight — the most frequently …","General review of 081 Principles of Flight — the most frequently examined facts per chapter."],["081.01.00.00","Subsonic aerodynamics: fill in the gaps → ________, Drag D = ½ρV²·S·CD. Bernoulli: p + ½ρV² = const; continuity. Lift from pressure difference; CL rises linearly with α until CL max ____ — the stall occurs at a constant α, not a constant speed.","Lift L = ½ρV²·S·CL, Drag D = ½ρV²·S·CD. Bernoulli: p + ½ρV² = const; continuity. Lift from pressure difference; CL rises linearly with α until CL max at the critical α (~15–16°) — the stall occurs at a constant α, not a constant speed."],["081.01.00.00","Subsonic aerodynamics: fill in the gaps → ____ VS ∝ √(W/(S·CLmax)) → ∝ √mass (+10 % mass → +5 % VS), × √n in turns; ________ (more tail download). Flaps ↑ CL max (lower VS) but the critical α decreases; slats/LE devices raise the critical α.","Stall speed VS ∝ √(W/(S·CLmax)) → ∝ √mass (+10 % mass → +5 % VS), × √n in turns; forward CG → higher VS (more tail download). Flaps ↑ CL max (lower VS) but the critical α decreases; slats/LE devices raise the critical α."],["081.01.00.00","Subsonic aerodynamics: fill in the gaps → ____: parasite (form, skin friction, interference) ∝ V²; ____ ∝ 1/V² (∝ CL²/(π·AR·e)). ____ where both are equal → VMD = best L/D. Induced drag ↓ with higher aspect ratio, winglets, elliptical lift distribution; ↑ with mass, low speed. Ground effect: induced drag ↓, lift ↑ (within ~½ span), nose-down tendency, floating.","Drag: parasite (form, skin friction, interference) ∝ V²; induced ∝ 1/V² (∝ CL²/(π·AR·e)). Minimum drag where both are equal → VMD = best L/D. Induced drag ↓ with higher aspect ratio, winglets, elliptical lift distribution; ↑ with mass, low speed. Ground effect: induced drag ↓, lift ↑ (within ~½ span), nose-down tendency, floating."],["081.01.00.00","Subsonic aerodynamics: what do you remember about this? → Boundary layer: laminar → transition → turbulent; turbulent BL has …","Boundary layer: laminar → transition → turbulent; turbulent BL has more friction but resists separation. Vortex generators energise BL."],["081.01.00.00","Subsonic aerodynamics: fill in the gaps → Wing ____: rectangular stalls at the ____ (benign); tapered/swept stall at the ____ (aileron loss, pitch-up) → washout, stall strips, slots, fences.","Wing planform: rectangular stalls at the root (benign); tapered/swept stall at the tip (aileron loss, pitch-up) → washout, stall strips, slots, fences."],["081.01.00.00","Subsonic aerodynamics: fill in the gaps → Stall warning ≥ ____ above stall (CS-23/25). Deep stall (T-tail) → stick pusher. Spin: autorotation → PARE (power idle, ailerons neutral, rudder opposite, elevator forward).","Stall warning ≥ 5 kt or 5 % above stall (CS-23/25). Deep stall (T-tail) → stick pusher. Spin: autorotation → PARE (power idle, ailerons neutral, rudder opposite, elevator forward)."],["081.02.00.00","High-speed aerodynamics: fill in the gaps → ____ M = TAS/a; ____ kt; at constant IAS/CAS, M ↑ with altitude; in the troposphere at constant Mach, TAS ↓ with altitude (T falls).","Mach number M = TAS/a; a = 38.95 √T(K) kt; at constant IAS/CAS, M ↑ with altitude; in the troposphere at constant Mach, TAS ↓ with altitude (T falls)."],["081.02.00.00","High-speed aerodynamics: fill in the gaps → ________: free-stream Mach at which local sonic flow first appears on the wing. Above: ____ → wave drag, shock stall/buffet, Mach tuck (CP moves aft + reduced downwash on tail → nose down), control effectiveness loss, aileron buzz. Sweep raises Mcrit (only the component ⊥ to the leading edge counts), also thin/supercritical aerofoils.","Mcrit: free-stream Mach at which local sonic flow first appears on the wing. Above: shock waves → wave drag, shock stall/buffet, Mach tuck (CP moves aft + reduced downwash on tail → nose down), control effectiveness loss, aileron buzz. Sweep raises Mcrit (only the component ⊥ to the leading edge counts), also thin/supercritical aerofoils."],["081.02.00.00","High-speed aerodynamics: fill in the gaps → ____: low-speed and high-speed buffet converge with altitude → ____; 1.3 g buffet margin for cruise altitude. Mach trimmer compensates Mach tuck.","Buffet onset boundary: low-speed and high-speed buffet converge with altitude → coffin corner; 1.3 g buffet margin for cruise altitude. Mach trimmer compensates Mach tuck."],["081.01.00.00","High-speed aerodynamics: fill in the gaps → Expansion waves / normal & oblique shocks: across a ____ ____ flow becomes subsonic, pressure/density/temperature ↑, total pressure ↓.","Expansion waves / normal & oblique shocks: across a normal shock flow becomes subsonic, pressure/density/temperature ↑, total pressure ↓."],["081.03.00.00","Stall, Mach tuck, UPRT: fill in the gaps → Stall recovery: ____ (push), level wings, then thrust, avoid secondary stall. Swept wing: tip stall → pitch-up. Upset: nose-high → push/bank to lower the nose, nose-low → roll wings level first, then pull.","Stall recovery: reduce α first (push), level wings, then thrust, avoid secondary stall. Swept wing: tip stall → pitch-up. Upset: nose-high → push/bank to lower the nose, nose-low → roll wings level first, then pull."],["081.04.00.00","Stability: fill in the gaps → ____ = initial tendency to return; ____ = what happens over time (damped oscillation = positive). ____: CG ahead of the neutral point; static margin = distance CG– NP; aft CG → less stable, lighter stick forces; stick force per g decreases with aft CG.","Static stability = initial tendency to return; dynamic = what happens over time (damped oscillation = positive). Longitudinal: CG ahead of the neutral point; static margin = distance CG– NP; aft CG → less stable, lighter stick forces; stick force per g decreases with aft CG."],["081.04.00.00","Stability: fill in the gaps → ____: fin, sweep, keel area aft of CG. ____: dihedral, sweepback (effective dihedral), high wing, fin. ____; too much directional vs lateral → spiral instability. Phugoid (long period, speed/height exchange), short-period (α, well damped).","Directional: fin, sweep, keel area aft of CG. Lateral: dihedral, sweepback (effective dihedral), high wing, fin. Too much lateral vs directional stability → Dutch roll; too much directional vs lateral → spiral instability. Phugoid (long period, speed/height exchange), short-period (α, well damped)."],["081.05.00.00","Control & limitations: what do you remember about this? → See #44/#59 and #69: balance/anti-balance/servo tabs, adverse yaw …","See #44/#59 and #69: balance/anti-balance/servo tabs, adverse yaw remedies, stabiliser trim; VA = VS√n, CS-25 +2.5/−1, ultimate = 1.5 × limit; VLO/VLE/VFE; gusts 66/50/25 ft/s; flutter & mass balance."],["081.07.00.00","Propellers & flight mechanics: fill in the gaps → See #56: blade α = blade angle − helix angle; windmilling vs feathered; secondary effects (clockwise: yaw left, gyro pitch-up → yaw right); climb ____, glide ____; turn R = V²/(g tan Ø), ____, rate-1 3°/s; VMC (5° bank, aft CG worst, lower density → lower VMC).","See #56: blade α = blade angle − helix angle; windmilling vs feathered; secondary effects (clockwise: yaw left, gyro pitch-up → yaw right); climb T = D + W sin γ, glide tan γ = 1/(L/D); turn R = V²/(g tan Ø), n = 1/cos Ø, rate-1 3°/s; VMC (5° bank, aft CG worst, lower density → lower VMC)."]],"74":[["022.06.01.01:02","AFCS basics & control loops: fill in the gaps → AFCS purposes: reduce workload, improve precision, fly the aircraft in all phases incl. autoland, passenger comfort, stability augmentation. Two functions: ____ (inner loop — stabilise the aircraft around its ____, attitude/rates) and ____ (outer loop — follow a flight path: heading, altitude, NAV, G/S).","AFCS purposes: reduce workload, improve precision, fly the aircraft in all phases incl. autoland, passenger comfort, stability augmentation. Two functions: control (inner loop — stabilise the aircraft around its CG, attitude/rates) and guidance (outer loop — follow a flight path: heading, altitude, NAV, G/S)."],["022.06.01.01:03","AFCS basics & control loops: fill in the gaps → ____: output fed back and compared with the desired value (error → correction) — autopilot, governors. ____: no feedback (command executed regardless of result).","Closed loop: output fed back and compared with the desired value (error → correction) — autopilot, governors. Open loop: no feedback (command executed regardless of result)."],["022.06.01.01:04","AFCS basics & control loops: fill in the gaps → Closed-loop elements: ____ (target), ____ (compares input and feedback), ____ (applies control laws, gain), control element/actuator (servo moves surface), feedback signal.","Closed-loop elements: input signal (target), error detector (compares input and feedback), signal processor/computer (applies control laws, gain), control element/actuator (servo moves surface), feedback signal."],["022.06.01.01:05","AFCS basics & control loops: fill in the gaps → ____ occurs if the loop gain is too high, feedback is delayed/phase-shifted or data is erroneous → the system over-corrects repeatedly (pilot-/autopilot-induced oscillation).","Self-induced oscillation occurs if the loop gain is too high, feedback is delayed/phase-shifted or data is erroneous → the system over-corrects repeatedly (pilot-/autopilot-induced oscillation)."],["022.06.01.01:06","AFCS basics & control loops: fill in the gaps → Detected by oscillating attitude/surfaces, FMA/trim movement; effects: reduced controllability, structural loads, passenger injury → ____, fly manually with small, smooth inputs, reduce gain (avoid chasing).","Detected by oscillating attitude/surfaces, FMA/trim movement; effects: reduced controllability, structural loads, passenger injury → disconnect the autopilot promptly, fly manually with small, smooth inputs, reduce gain (avoid chasing)."],["022.06.02.01:02","Autopilot design & operation: fill in the gaps → Channels: pitch (elevator/stabiliser), roll (ailerons/spoilers), yaw (rudder). Types: ____ (roll only — wing leveller), ____ (pitch + roll), ____ (pitch, roll, yaw).","Channels: pitch (elevator/stabiliser), roll (ailerons/spoilers), yaw (rudder). Types: 1-axis (roll only — wing leveller), 2-axis (pitch + roll), 3-axis (pitch, roll, yaw)."],["022.06.02.01:12","Autopilot design & operation: fill in the gaps → Components: ____ (pilot selects modes & targets), ____ (shows engaged/armed modes — read it after every change), ____ (FCC), actuators/servos (on control cables or surface actuators).","Components: FCU/MCP (pilot selects modes & targets), FMA (shows engaged/armed modes — read it after every change), autopilot computer (FCC), actuators/servos (on control cables or surface actuators)."],["022.06.02.01:04","Autopilot design & operation: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (hold/turn to selected heading/track), ____ (capture and track radial/localiser), LNAV/NAV (follow the FMS route).","Lateral modes: HDG/TRK (hold/turn to selected heading/track), VOR/LOC (capture and track radial/localiser), LNAV/NAV (follow the FMS route)."],["022.06.02.01:06","Autopilot design & operation: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (hold vertical speed — no speed protection!), ____ (flight path angle), LVL CHG / OP CLB / OP DES (speed on elevator, thrust fixed climb/idle), SRS (speed reference system: V2+10 after take-off/go-around), ALT hold (and ALT ACQ/ALT* capture), VNAV / managed CLB/DES (FMS vertical profile), G/S (glideslope).","Vertical modes: V/S (hold vertical speed — no speed protection!), FPA (flight path angle), LVL CHG / OP CLB / OP DES (speed on elevator, thrust fixed climb/idle), SRS (speed reference system: V2+10 after take-off/go-around), ALT hold (and ALT ACQ/ALT* capture), VNAV / managed CLB/DES (FMS vertical profile), G/S (glideslope)."],["022.06.02.01:08","Autopilot design & operation: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (SRS + runway/NAV), ____ (GA thrust, SRS/pitch, GA track), APP (LOC + G/S).","Mixed: take-off (SRS + runway/NAV), go-around (GA thrust, SRS/pitch, GA track), APP (LOC + G/S)."],["022.06.02.01:05","Autopilot design & operation: fill in the gaps → ____ define how the computer converts errors into commanded attitudes/rates (limits on bank, pitch, rates, e.g. max 25–30° bank in HDG).","Control laws for pitch/roll modes define how the computer converts errors into commanded attitudes/rates (limits on bank, pitch, rates, e.g. max 25–30° bank in HDG)."],["022.06.02.01:07","Autopilot design & operation: fill in the gaps → ____: the autopilot adjusts the ____ ____ according to airspeed, configuration and flight phase (smaller deflections at high speed) — wrong data can cause sluggish or oscillatory responses.","Gain scheduling: the autopilot adjusts the magnitude of control inputs according to airspeed, configuration and flight phase (smaller deflections at high speed) — wrong data can cause sluggish or oscillatory responses."],["022.06.02.01:09","Autopilot design & operation: fill in the gaps → Configurations: ____ (column/wheel move with the surfaces — pilot feels/sees what the AP does, e.g. Boeing) vs ____ (sidesticks stay neutral — Airbus FBW; must monitor via displays).","Configurations: back-driven controls (column/wheel move with the surfaces — pilot feels/sees what the AP does, e.g. Boeing) vs static controls (sidesticks stay neutral — Airbus FBW; must monitor via displays)."],["022.06.02.01:10","Autopilot design & operation: fill in the gaps → Inputs: ____ (IRS/AHRS), ____ (FMS, NAV receivers, ILS), ____, airspeed (ADC), configuration (flaps, gear), FCU/MCP selections; outputs: servo commands, FMA annunciations, flight-director commands.","Inputs: attitude (IRS/AHRS), flight path/trajectory (FMS, NAV receivers, ILS), surface position, airspeed (ADC), configuration (flaps, gear), FCU/MCP selections; outputs: servo commands, FMA annunciations, flight-director commands."],["022.06.02.01:01","Autopilot design & operation: fill in the gaps → ____: before engagement the AP aligns its references to the current attitude/values → ____ on engagement; engage ____ so the servos don't have to hold large forces (and no sudden out-of-trim on disconnect).","Synchronisation: before engagement the AP aligns its references to the current attitude/values → no jerk on engagement; engage in trim so the servos don't have to hold large forces (and no sudden out-of-trim on disconnect)."],["022.06.02.01:14","Autopilot design & operation: fill in the gaps → ____: AP mode where the pilot manoeuvres through the AP computer/servos using the control column; AP holds the new attitude when released. ____: AP remains engaged but servos ____ while the button is held — manual control; on release servos re-engage in the previous modes.","CWS: AP mode where the pilot manoeuvres through the AP computer/servos using the control column; AP holds the new attitude when released. TCS (touch control steering): AP remains engaged but servos disconnected while the button is held — manual control; on release servos re-engage in the previous modes."],["022.06.02.01:15","Autopilot design & operation: fill in the gaps → Only ____ engaged at a time, except with ____ → second AP engages for ____.","Only one autopilot engaged at a time, except with APP armed → second AP engages for fail-operational autoland."],["022.06.02.01:17","Autopilot design & operation: fill in the gaps → ____ (waiting for capture criteria, shown in blue/white on FMA) vs ____ (controlling, green). Mode phases: ____ (attitude change towards new trajectory, rate based on the difference) → capture (predefined rate of change to reach target without overshoot, e.g. ALT*) → tracking/hold.","Armed (waiting for capture criteria, shown in blue/white on FMA) vs engaged/active (controlling, green). Mode phases: initial (attitude change towards new trajectory, rate based on the difference) → capture (predefined rate of change to reach target without overshoot, e.g. ALT*) → tracking/hold."],["022.06.02.01:18","Autopilot design & operation: fill in the gaps → ____ (automatic change): no valid data (e.g. ____ ____ in LNAV → HDG), target changed during capture (ALT ACQ → V/S), envelope protection (speed/α protection overrides selected mode). Always check the FMA.","Mode reversion (automatic change): no valid data (e.g. flight- plan discontinuity in LNAV → HDG), target changed during capture (ALT ACQ → V/S), envelope protection (speed/α protection overrides selected mode). Always check the FMA."],["022.06.02.01:19","Autopilot design & operation: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ → excessive V/S → speed decays to stall or overspeeds; arming ____ outside the protected area (false/side lobes, wrong capture).","Mismanagement: V/S without speed protection → excessive V/S → speed decays to stall or overspeeds; arming VOR/LOC/APP outside the protected area (false/side lobes, wrong capture)."],["022.06.05.01:02","Autopilot design & operation: fill in the gaps → Failures of other systems (ADC, IRS, radio altimeter, hydraulics) can make the AP unavailable or cause ____ ____ (e.g. erroneous radio altitude → retard/flare) → monitor raw data, intervene early.","Failures of other systems (ADC, IRS, radio altimeter, hydraulics) can make the AP unavailable or cause undesired states without warnings (e.g. erroneous radio altitude → retard/flare) → monitor raw data, intervene early."],["022.06.02.01:21","Autopilot design & operation: fill in the gaps → ____: use the ____ (instinctive disconnect button on the column/stick) → ____ aural + visual warning; unintended/abnormal disconnect (other method, failure) → continuous warning until cancelled. Both aural and visual to ensure awareness.","Disengagement: use the design method (instinctive disconnect button on the column/stick) → temporary aural + visual warning; unintended/abnormal disconnect (other method, failure) → continuous warning until cancelled. Both aural and visual to ensure awareness."],["022.06.02.01:22","Autopilot design & operation: fill in the gaps → ____: AP doesn't trim → ____; disconnecting with large trim forces causes an abrupt pitch change — hold the controls, trim, then disconnect.","AP with manual trim: AP doesn't trim → out-of-trim warning; disconnecting with large trim forces causes an abrupt pitch change — hold the controls, trim, then disconnect."],["022.06.05.01:02","Autoland: fill in the gaps → Purpose: automatic approach, ____, touchdown and ____ in very low visibility (CAT II/III). Needs ____, autothrust, radio altimeters (usually 2–3), ILS receivers, redundancy.","Purpose: automatic approach, flare, touchdown and roll-out in very low visibility (CAT II/III). Needs autopilot(s), autothrust, radio altimeters (usually 2–3), ILS receivers, redundancy."],["022.06.05.01:01","Autoland: fill in the gaps → ____: after a failure the aircraft is not significantly displaced, but the landing cannot be completed automatically (pilot takes over/goes around) — e.g. ____.","Fail-passive: after a failure the aircraft is not significantly displaced, but the landing cannot be completed automatically (pilot takes over/goes around) — e.g. CAT IIIA with DH."],["022.06.05.01:09","Autoland: fill in the gaps → ____ ____: after a failure the approach, flare and landing can still be completed automatically by the remaining system — ____ IIIB, no DH / alert height. Fail-operational hybrid: fail-passive autoland + independent guidance (HUD) to complete manually. Alert height (typ.","Fail- operational: after a failure the approach, flare and landing can still be completed automatically by the remaining system — CAT IIIB, no DH / alert height. Fail-operational hybrid: fail-passive autoland + independent guidance (HUD) to complete manually. Alert height (typ."],["022.06.05.01:06","Autoland: fill in the gaps → ____, often 100 ft): above it, a failure of a required redundant part → ____ (or revert to higher minima); below it, the approach ____ unless an alert (warning) occurs.","100–200 ft RA, often 100 ft): above it, a failure of a required redundant part → go-around (or revert to higher minima); below it, the approach continues unless an alert (warning) occurs."],["022.06.05.01:04","Autoland: fill in the gaps → Sequence (FMA): LAND 3/LAND 2 (fail-op/fail-passive capability) → ____ (decrab) → ____ engages at ~____ (if it doesn't → go-around or manual landing if visual) → RETARD/IDLE → ROLL-OUT on touchdown (LOC guidance on the ground) → disconnect AP before vacating the runway. Go-around: follow FMA + raw data.","Sequence (FMA): LAND 3/LAND 2 (fail-op/fail-passive capability) → ALIGN (decrab) → FLARE engages at ~40–50 ft RA (if it doesn't → go-around or manual landing if visual) → RETARD/IDLE → ROLL-OUT on touchdown (LOC guidance on the ground) → disconnect AP before vacating the runway. Go-around: follow FMA + raw data."],["022.06.05.01:01","Autoland: fill in the gaps → ____: crew qualification, aircraft certification, airport LVP in force, ILS category, ____ limits, runway conditions — autoland may be technically possible but not legal.","Operational limitations: crew qualification, aircraft certification, airport LVP in force, ILS category, crosswind/headwind/tailwind limits, runway conditions — autoland may be technically possible but not legal."],["022.06.05.01:07","Autoland: fill in the gaps → ____ triggering a warning: ____ ____, loss of ILS signal, excessive ILS deviation, radio-altimeter failure. Failures of hydraulics, electrical sources, autothrust, anti-skid, ADC/IRS can downgrade capability (LAND 3 → LAND 2 → CAT I) and raise minima.","Failures below alert height triggering a warning: all autopilots disengage, loss of ILS signal, excessive ILS deviation, radio-altimeter failure. Failures of hydraulics, electrical sources, autothrust, anti-skid, ADC/IRS can downgrade capability (LAND 3 → LAND 2 → CAT I) and raise minima."],["022.08.02.01:01","Yaw damper: fill in the gaps → Purpose: damp ____ (coupled yaw-roll oscillation of swept-wing jets) and improve turn coordination/comfort. Yaw-rate gyro/IRS signal → computer → rudder actuator (series actuator — ____).","Purpose: damp Dutch roll (coupled yaw-roll oscillation of swept-wing jets) and improve turn coordination/comfort. Yaw-rate gyro/IRS signal → computer → rudder actuator (series actuator — no rudder pedal movement)."],["022.08.02.01:02","Yaw damper: fill in the gaps → ____ (washout/band-pass): only the oscillatory yaw rate at the Dutch-roll frequency is opposed, ____ ____ so the damper doesn't oppose normal turns.","Dutch-roll filter (washout/band-pass): only the oscillatory yaw rate at the Dutch-roll frequency is opposed, steady turn rates are filtered out so the damper doesn't oppose normal turns."],["022.08.02.01:03","Yaw damper: fill in the gaps → Difference: yaw damper operates ____ (independent of AP, in manual flight too) and acts only on yaw oscillations; a ____ ____ provides guidance/coordination (e.g. engine-out, align/decrab in autoland) when engaged.","Difference: yaw damper operates continuously (independent of AP, in manual flight too) and acts only on yaw oscillations; a 3- axis autopilot rudder channel provides guidance/coordination (e.g. engine-out, align/decrab in autoland) when engaged."],["022.06.01.01:01","EASA LO 022.06.01.01.01 → Describe the following purposes of an automatic flight control system (AFCS):","AFCS purposes: reduce workload, improve precision, fly the aircraft in all phases incl. autoland, passenger comfort, stability augmentation. Two functions: control (inner loop — stabilise the aircraft around its CG, attitude/rates) and guidance (outer loop — follow a flight path: heading, altitude, NAV, G/S)."],["022.06.02.01:01","EASA LO 022.06.02.01.01 → Define the three basic control channels.","Channels: pitch (elevator/stabiliser), roll (ailerons/spoilers), yaw (rudder). Types: 1-axis (roll only — wing leveller), 2-axis (pitch + roll), 3-axis (pitch, roll, yaw)."],["022.06.02.01:16","EASA LO 022.06.02.01.16 → Explain the difference between an armed and an engaged mode: — not all modes have an armed state available; — a mode will only become armed if certain criteria are met;","Only one autopilot engaged at a time, except with APP armed → second AP engages for fail-operational autoland. / Armed (waiting for capture criteria, shown in blue/white on FMA) vs engaged/active (controlling, green). Mode phases: initial (attitude change towards new trajectory, rate based on the difference) → capture (predefined rate of change to reach target without overshoot, e.g. ALT*) → tracking/hold."],["022.06.02.01:20","EASA LO 022.06.02.01.20 → Describe how failure of other systems may influence the availability of the autopilot and how incorrect data from other systems may result in an undesirable aircraft state, potentially without any failure indications. Explain the importance of prompt and appropriate pilot intervention during such events.","Failures of other systems (ADC, IRS, radio altimeter, hydraulics) can make the AP unavailable or cause undesired states without warnings (e.g. erroneous radio altitude → retard/flare) → monitor raw data, intervene early."],["022.06.05.01:03","EASA LO 022.06.05.01.03 → Explain the following terms (reference to CS-AWO ‘All Weather Operations’): — fail-passive automatic landing system; — fail-operational automatic landing system; — fail-operational hybrid landing system; — alert height.","Fail- operational: after a failure the approach, flare and landing can still be completed automatically by the remaining system — CAT IIIB, no DH / alert height. Fail-operational hybrid: fail-passive autoland + independent guidance (HUD) to complete manually. Alert height (typ."]],"75":[["010.09.01.01:01","Aerodrome data: fill in the gaps → Aerodrome reference code relates aerodrome characteristics to the aeroplanes intended to operate there. ____ ____: aeroplane reference field length (< 800 m / 800–1 200 / 1 200–1 800 / ≥ 1 800 m). ____: wingspan (A < 15 m, B < 24, C < 36, D < 52, E < 65, F < 80 m). E.g. A320 = 4C, B747 = 4E, A380 = 4F.","Aerodrome reference code relates aerodrome characteristics to the aeroplanes intended to operate there. Element 1 (number 1–4): aeroplane reference field length (< 800 m / 800–1 200 / 1 200–1 800 / ≥ 1 800 m). Element 2 (letter A–F): wingspan (A < 15 m, B < 24, C < 36, D < 52, E < 65, F < 80 m). E.g. A320 = 4C, B747 = 4E, A380 = 4F."],["010.09.02.01:01","Aerodrome data: fill in the gaps → ____: located near the initial or planned ____ of the aerodrome; normally ____ where first established; published in WGS-84 coordinates.","Aerodrome reference point (ARP): located near the initial or planned geometric centre of the aerodrome; normally remains where first established; published in WGS-84 coordinates."],["010.09.02.02:01","Aerodrome data: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (aircraft classification number) = effect of an aircraft on a pavement; ____ (pavement classification number) = bearing strength of the pavement. Unrestricted operation if ACN ≤ PCN. Report format e.g.","Pavement strength: ACN (aircraft classification number) = effect of an aircraft on a pavement; PCN (pavement classification number) = bearing strength of the pavement. Unrestricted operation if ACN ≤ PCN. Report format e.g."],["010.09.02.02:02","Aerodrome data: fill in the gaps → PCN 80/R/B/W/T (pavement ____igid/____lexible, subgrade A–D, tyre pressure W–Z, evaluation Technical/Using aircraft). (Being replaced by ACR/PCR.) For aprons for aircraft ____: report maximum allowable aircraft mass and maximum tyre pressure (e.g. 4 000 kg/0.50 MPa).","PCN 80/R/B/W/T (pavement Rigid/Flexible, subgrade A–D, tyre pressure W–Z, evaluation Technical/Using aircraft). (Being replaced by ACR/PCR.) For aprons for aircraft ≤ 5 700 kg: report maximum allowable aircraft mass and maximum tyre pressure (e.g. 4 000 kg/0.50 MPa)."],["010.09.02.03:01","Aerodrome data: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (runway length available for the ground run), ____ (TORA + clearway), ASDA (TORA + stopway), LDA (runway length available for the landing ground run). Guidance in Attachment A to Annex 14.","Declared distances: TORA (runway length available for the ground run), TODA (TORA + clearway), ASDA (TORA + stopway), LDA (runway length available for the landing ground run). Guidance in Attachment A to Annex 14."],["010.09.02.04:02","Aerodrome data: fill in the gaps → ____: reported to ____ so pilots receive information affecting safety/performance: construction/maintenance work, rough/broken surfaces, ____ snow, slush, ice, frost, chemical de-icers, sand, rubber deposits, runway condition (RWYCC), snow banks, failure of lighting, obstacles, aircraft parked, closed areas.","Condition of the movement area: reported to AIS and ATS so pilots receive information affecting safety/performance: construction/maintenance work, rough/broken surfaces, water, snow, slush, ice, frost, chemical de-icers, sand, rubber deposits, runway condition (RWYCC), snow banks, failure of lighting, obstacles, aircraft parked, closed areas."],["010.09.02.04:04","Aerodrome data: fill in the gaps → ____: standing water, slush, wet/dry snow, compacted snow, ice, wet ice, frost, sand, oil, rubber. ____: ____ (thin, crystalline), ice (poor braking), wet ice (worst, \"less than poor\"), compacted snow (depends on OAT), slush (water-saturated snow; also causes impingement drag/hydroplaning).","Contaminants: standing water, slush, wet/dry snow, compacted snow, ice, wet ice, frost, sand, oil, rubber. Frozen water types: frost (thin, crystalline), ice (poor braking), wet ice (worst, \"less than poor\"), compacted snow (depends on OAT), slush (water-saturated snow; also causes impingement drag/hydroplaning)."],["010.09.02.04:05","Aerodrome data: fill in the gaps → Runway condition codes (GRF, RCAM): ____ dry — ____ (wet, frost, ≤ 3 mm water/slush/snow) — ____ (compacted snow at OAT ≤ −15 °C) — 3 medium (slippery wet, dry/wet snow > 3 mm, compacted snow at OAT > −15 °C) — 2 medium to poor (standing water/slush > 3 mm) — 1 poor (ice) — 0 less than poor (wet ice, water on compacted snow, snow on ice). Reported per runway third.","Runway condition codes (GRF, RCAM): 6 dry — 5 good (wet, frost, ≤ 3 mm water/slush/snow) — 4 good to medium (compacted snow at OAT ≤ −15 °C) — 3 medium (slippery wet, dry/wet snow > 3 mm, compacted snow at OAT > −15 °C) — 2 medium to poor (standing water/slush > 3 mm) — 1 poor (ice) — 0 less than poor (wet ice, water on compacted snow, snow on ice). Reported per runway third."],["010.09.02.01:01","Physical characteristics: fill in the gaps → ____: normally at the ____ of the runway; ____ when obstacles/unserviceable pavement require it (reduces LDA).","Threshold (THR): normally at the extremity of the runway; displaced when obstacles/unserviceable pavement require it (reduces LDA)."],["010.09.02.03:01","Physical characteristics: fill in the gaps → A runway with ____ allows declared distances beyond TORA; physical characteristics (slopes, strength) must meet requirements.","A runway with stopway/clearway allows declared distances beyond TORA; physical characteristics (slopes, strength) must meet requirements."],["010.09.03.03:01","Physical characteristics: fill in the gaps → ____: area including the runway (and stopway) intended to ____ ____ and to protect aircraft flying over it during take-off/landing (graded, obstacle-free; extends ≥ 60 m beyond the runway end).","Runway strip: area including the runway (and stopway) intended to reduce the risk of damage to aircraft running off the runway and to protect aircraft flying over it during take-off/landing (graded, obstacle-free; extends ≥ 60 m beyond the runway end)."],["010.09.03.03:01","Physical characteristics: fill in the gaps → ____: symmetrical about the extended centre line, adjacent to the end of the strip, to reduce damage to aeroplanes ____ (≥ 90 m, recommended 240 m for code 3/4).","RESA (runway-end safety area): symmetrical about the extended centre line, adjacent to the end of the strip, to reduce damage to aeroplanes undershooting or overrunning (≥ 90 m, recommended 240 m for code 3/4)."],["010.09.03.04:01","Physical characteristics: fill in the gaps → ____: defined rectangular area on the ground or water under control of the authority, suitable for an aeroplane to make part of its ____ (≤ ½ TORA long, ≥ 75 m either side of centre line, upward slope ≤ 1.25 %).","Clearway (CWY): defined rectangular area on the ground or water under control of the authority, suitable for an aeroplane to make part of its initial climb to a specified height (≤ ½ TORA long, ≥ 75 m either side of centre line, upward slope ≤ 1.25 %)."],["010.09.03.03:01","Physical characteristics: fill in the gaps → ____: defined rectangular area on the ground at the end of TORA, prepared as a suitable area in which an aircraft ____ ____ (same width as the runway).","Stopway (SWY): defined rectangular area on the ground at the end of TORA, prepared as a suitable area in which an aircraft can be stopped in the case of an abandoned take-off (same width as the runway)."],["010.09.03.07:02","Physical characteristics: fill in the gaps → ____: rapid-exit taxiways reduce runway occupancy time (angle 25–45°, preferably ____; designed for ~____ code 3/4, 65 km/h code 1/2). Widening in curves so that the main gear keeps the required clearance while the cockpit follows the centre line.","Taxiways: rapid-exit taxiways reduce runway occupancy time (angle 25–45°, preferably 30°; designed for ~93 km/h (50 kt) code 3/4, 65 km/h code 1/2). Widening in curves so that the main gear keeps the required clearance while the cockpit follows the centre line."],["010.09.04.04:08","Physical characteristics: fill in the gaps → ____ where traffic density is medium/heavy (allow sequencing). ____ at every taxiway/runway intersection and where a taxiing aircraft would infringe an obstacle surface/ILS critical area. ____ position where a road meets a runway.","Holding bays where traffic density is medium/heavy (allow sequencing). Runway holding positions at every taxiway/runway intersection and where a taxiing aircraft would infringe an obstacle surface/ILS critical area. Road holding position where a road meets a runway."],["010.09.03.07:06","Physical characteristics: fill in the gaps → ____ ____ on taxiways where a specific holding limit is needed (e.g. taxiway intersections).","Intermediate holding positions on taxiways where a specific holding limit is needed (e.g. taxiway intersections)."],["010.09.04.01:02","Visual aids — indicators, markings: fill in the gaps → ____: truncated cone of fabric (white/orange or red/white), at least one per aerodrome, lit at night. ____: ____ — land parallel to the shaft towards the cross-arm. Signalling lamp: red, green, white signals aimed at a target (see 010-03 light signals). Signal area: square, visible from the air, at aerodromes without radio.","Wind direction indicator: truncated cone of fabric (white/orange or red/white), at least one per aerodrome, lit at night. Landing direction indicator: \"T\" — land parallel to the shaft towards the cross-arm. Signalling lamp: red, green, white signals aimed at a target (see 010-03 light signals). Signal area: square, visible from the air, at aerodromes without radio."],["010.09.04.04:01","Visual aids — indicators, markings: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ = landings prohibited; red square with ____ = special precautions (poor manoeuvring area); white dumb-bell = use paved runways/taxiways only; dumb-bell with black bars = take-off/landing on paved runways only, other manoeuvres also on grass;","Signal area symbols: red square with yellow diagonals = landings prohibited; red square with one yellow diagonal = special precautions (poor manoeuvring area); white dumb-bell = use paved runways/taxiways only; dumb-bell with black bars = take-off/landing on paved runways only, other manoeuvres also on grass;"],["010.09.04.01:02","Visual aids — indicators, markings: fill in the gaps → ____ = area unfit; white ____ = landing direction; white ____ = runway in use; right-hand arrow = right-hand circuit; yellow C on black = ATS reporting office; double white cross = gliders operating.","white/red cross = area unfit; white T = landing direction; white numerals = runway in use; right-hand arrow = right-hand circuit; yellow C on black = ATS reporting office; double white cross = gliders operating."],["010.09.04.02:01","Visual aids — indicators, markings: fill in the gaps → ____: ____, ____ stand markings yellow, apron safety lines conspicuous colour contrasting with stand markings.","Colours: runway markings white, taxiway and aircraft stand markings yellow, apron safety lines conspicuous colour contrasting with stand markings."],["010.09.04.01:01","Visual aids — indicators, markings: fill in the gaps → ____ at thresholds (paved runways): two-digit number = magnetic heading/10 (e.g. 27), with ____ for parallel runways. ____: white dashed stripes; threshold marking: longitudinal stripes (number depends on width, e.g. 8 stripes for 30 m, 12 for 45 m);","Runway designation marking at thresholds (paved runways): two-digit number = magnetic heading/10 (e.g. 27), with L/C/R for parallel runways. Centre-line: white dashed stripes; threshold marking: longitudinal stripes (number depends on width, e.g. 8 stripes for 30 m, 12 for 45 m);"],["010.09.04.02:03","Visual aids — indicators, markings: fill in the gaps → ____ marking (two large bars ~300–400 m from THR); ____ markings: pairs of stripes decreasing towards mid-runway; ____ at edges (white).","aiming-point marking (two large bars ~300–400 m from THR); touchdown-zone markings: pairs of stripes decreasing towards mid-runway; side stripes at edges (white)."],["010.09.04.03:01","Lights: fill in the gaps → ____: elevated lights must be ____, low and light so as not to damage aircraft; approach lights in the first 300 m near THR as low as possible.","Mechanical safety: elevated lights must be frangible, low and light so as not to damage aircraft; approach lights in the first 300 m near THR as low as possible."],["010.09.04.03:02","Lights: fill in the gaps → ____: at aerodromes used at night where needed for visual navigation (poor visibility of the aerodrome, surrounded by lights); ____ or ____ white/green (land aerodromes); identification beacon flashing green (land).","Aerodrome beacon (ABN): at aerodromes used at night where needed for visual navigation (poor visibility of the aerodrome, surrounded by lights); flashing white or alternating white/green (land aerodromes); identification beacon flashing green (land)."],["010.09.04.04:05","Lights: fill in the gaps → Simple approach lighting (non-instrument / non-precision runways): ____ with a ____ ____ from THR; single lights or barrettes, fixed white.","Simple approach lighting (non-instrument / non-precision runways): centre line ≥ 420 m with a crossbar 18–30 m wide at 300 m from THR; single lights or barrettes, fixed white."],["010.09.04.03:01","Lights: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (single sources with distance coding 1-2-3 per 150 m section, or barrettes) + ____ (30 m wide) (Calvert version: crossbars every 150 m). Fixed white (sequenced flashers optional).","Precision CAT I: centre line 900 m (single sources with distance coding 1-2-3 per 150 m section, or barrettes) + crossbar at 300 m (30 m wide) (Calvert version: crossbars every 150 m). Fixed white (sequenced flashers optional)."],["010.09.08.03:04","Lights: fill in the gaps → CAT II/III: 900 m centre line; ____: centre-line barrettes (white) + side-row barrettes (red) + crossbars at ____ and ____ — gives roll guidance near the ground.","CAT II/III: 900 m centre line; inner 300 m: centre-line barrettes (white) + side-row barrettes (red) + crossbars at 150 m and 300 m — gives roll guidance near the ground."],["010.09.04.03:07","Lights: fill in the gaps → PAPI: ____ in a wing bar (normally left side); each shows white above / red below its angle. ____ ____; 3 white/1 red = slightly high; 4 white = too high; 1 white/3 red = slightly low; 4 red = too low. APAPI: 2 units (1 white/1 red on slope). HAPI (helicopters): flashing green = above, green = on slope, red = slightly below, flashing red = well below.","PAPI: 4 units in a wing bar (normally left side); each shows white above / red below its angle. 2 white + 2 red = on slope (3°); 3 white/1 red = slightly high; 4 white = too high; 1 white/3 red = slightly low; 4 red = too low. APAPI: 2 units (1 white/1 red on slope). HAPI (helicopters): flashing green = above, green = on slope, red = slightly below, flashing red = well below."],["010.09.04.03:09","Lights: fill in the gaps → ____ (colour, fixed unless noted):","Runway and taxiway lights (colour, fixed unless noted):"],["010.09.04.03:09","Lights – RWY edge: what are the key facts?","white fixed; last 600 m (or ⅓) yellow"],["010.09.04.03:07","Lights – THR & wing bars: what are the key facts?","green, unidirectional towards approach"],["010.09.03.03:01","Lights – RWY end: what are the key facts?","red, towards the runway"],["010.09.04.03:09","Lights – RWY centre line: what are the key facts?","white; red/white alternating 900–300 m from end; red last 300 m"],["010.09.04.03:09","Lights – TDZ: what are the key facts?","white barrettes, first 900 m"],["010.09.08.03:01","Lights – RWY lead-in: what are the key facts?","white flashing groups along the approach path"],["010.09.03.05:01","Lights – SWY: what are the key facts?","red (unidirectional)"],["010.09.04.03:09","Lights – TWY centre line: what are the key facts?","green; alternate green/yellow in exits/ILS sensitive areas"],["010.09.04.05:02","Lights – TWY edge: what are the key facts?","blue"],["010.09.04.03:09","Lights – Stop bar: what are the key facts?","red unidirectional"],["010.09.03.07:06","Lights – Intermediate holding position: what are the key facts?","yellow"],["010.09.04.03:09","Lights – RWY guard lights: what are the key facts?","flashing yellow pairs (wig-wag)"],["010.09.03.07:05","Lights – Road holding position: what are the key facts?","traffic light red/green"],["010.09.04.03:10","Lights: fill in the gaps → Ground lights for an arriving aircraft must be ____ ____ (per ATS procedures; typically ≥ 10–15 min — check the CAE book).","Ground lights for an arriving aircraft must be switched on in sufficient time before the ETA (per ATS procedures; typically ≥ 10–15 min — check the CAE book)."],["010.09.04.04:07","Signs & markers: fill in the gaps → ____ is used ____ (red background, ____ inscription). Types: runway designation, CAT I/II/III holding position, runway-holding position, road- holding position, NO ENTRY, STOP. Illuminated where RVR < 800 m or at night.","Red is used only on mandatory instruction signs (red background, white inscription). Types: runway designation, CAT I/II/III holding position, runway-holding position, road- holding position, NO ENTRY, STOP. Illuminated where RVR < 800 m or at night."],["010.09.04.04:05","Signs & markers: fill in the gaps → ____ holding position (non-instrument/NPA/take-off runway) supplemented by a ____; ____ (precision runway — ILS critical area) by a CAT I/II/III holding position sign. Runway designation signs at taxiway/runway intersections on both sides (left of taxiway when facing the runway);","Pattern A holding position (non-instrument/NPA/take-off runway) supplemented by a runway designation sign; Pattern B (precision runway — ILS critical area) by a CAT I/II/III holding position sign. Runway designation signs at taxiway/runway intersections on both sides (left of taxiway when facing the runway);"],["010.09.04.04:07","Signs & markers: what do you remember about this? → NO ENTRY at the entrance of the prohibited area on both sides; …","NO ENTRY at the entrance of the prohibited area on both sides; holding-position signs on both sides of the holding position marking."],["010.09.04.04:07","Signs & markers: fill in the gaps → ____ ____: runway holding position sign showing the taxiway designation + number (e.g. ____) — mandatory, red.","Sign indicating infringement of an obstacle surface or ILS critical/sensitive area: runway holding position sign showing the taxiway designation + number (e.g. B2) — mandatory, red."],["010.09.04.04:12","Signs & markers: fill in the gaps → ____: direction/destination = ____ ____; location = black background, yellow inscription + yellow border. Inscriptions: arrows, taxiway letters, \"RWY exit\", runway vacated, intersection take-off (TORA remaining). Aircraft stand identification sign (stand number, yellow/black, visible from cockpit). Road-holding position sign (road side).","Information signs: direction/destination = yellow background, black inscription; location = black background, yellow inscription + yellow border. Inscriptions: arrows, taxiway letters, \"RWY exit\", runway vacated, intersection take-off (TORA remaining). Aircraft stand identification sign (stand number, yellow/black, visible from cockpit). Road-holding position sign (road side)."],["010.09.04.05:02","Signs & markers: fill in the gaps → ____: ____, low height so as to clear propellers and engine pods. Unpaved RWY edge markers (white/orange), TWY edge markers (____), TWY centre-line markers (green), unpaved TWY edge (blue/orange), boundary markers (orange/white), stopway edge markers (distinct from runway edge markers).","Markers: frangible, low height so as to clear propellers and engine pods. Unpaved RWY edge markers (white/orange), TWY edge markers (blue), TWY centre-line markers (green), unpaved TWY edge (blue/orange), boundary markers (orange/white), stopway edge markers (distinct from runway edge markers)."],["010.09.05.01:03","Obstacles & restricted areas: fill in the gaps → ____: colours — ____ (large surfaces, orange/red + white), ____ (tall/thin objects), single colour (small); if colouring impracticable → markers (e.g. spheres on overhead wires), flags (orange/white or red, on mobile objects/temporary). Vehicles: yellow or red/white.","Marking of objects: colours — chequered pattern (large surfaces, orange/red + white), alternating bands (tall/thin objects), single colour (small); if colouring impracticable → markers (e.g. spheres on overhead wires), flags (orange/white or red, on mobile objects/temporary). Vehicles: yellow or red/white."],["010.09.05.02:03","Obstacles & restricted areas: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (fixed ____; flashing yellow on service vehicles, flashing blue emergency vehicles), medium- intensity (flashing red or flashing white), high-intensity (flashing white, very tall objects > 150 m, daytime too). Located at the top of the object (and intermediate levels on tall objects). Aircraft lights → Annex 2/SERA.","Obstacle lights: low-intensity (fixed red; flashing yellow on service vehicles, flashing blue emergency vehicles), medium- intensity (flashing red or flashing white), high-intensity (flashing white, very tall objects > 150 m, daytime too). Located at the top of the object (and intermediate levels on tall objects). Aircraft lights → Annex 2/SERA."],["010.09.06.01:02","Obstacles & restricted areas: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (runway) / ____ (taxiway). Non-load-bearing shoulders: taxi side-stripe marking (yellow) and transverse stripes. Pre-threshold area unsuitable for aircraft: yellow chevrons (arrowheads pointing to the threshold).","Closed runway/taxiway: white cross (runway) / yellow cross (taxiway). Non-load-bearing shoulders: taxi side-stripe marking (yellow) and transverse stripes. Pre-threshold area unsuitable for aircraft: yellow chevrons (arrowheads pointing to the threshold)."],["010.09.07.01:01","Operational services: fill in the gaps → RFF principal objective: save lives. ____ based on the overall length and fuselage width of the largest aeroplanes normally using the aerodrome. Response time: ≤ ____ min (objective), ____, to any point of each operational runway in good visibility/surface conditions.","RFF principal objective: save lives. RFF category (1–10) based on the overall length and fuselage width of the largest aeroplanes normally using the aerodrome. Response time: ≤ 2 min (objective), not exceeding 3 min, to any point of each operational runway in good visibility/surface conditions."],["010.09.07.02:01","Operational services: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ over vehicles (vehicles give way to taxiing aircraft; emergency vehicles have priority over other vehicles).","Apron: aircraft have right of way over vehicles (vehicles give way to taxiing aircraft; emergency vehicles have priority over other vehicles)."],["010.09.07.03:01","Operational services: fill in the gaps → ____: fire extinguishers available for immediate use (and ready access for RFF), evacuation route clear (doors/stairs), no ignition sources, ground power and bonding, stop fuelling if fuel is spilled, persons on board informed.","Ground servicing / fuelling: fire extinguishers available for immediate use (and ready access for RFF), evacuation route clear (doors/stairs), no ignition sources, ground power and bonding, stop fuelling if fuel is spilled, persons on board informed."],["010.09.02.03:01","Declared distances & approach lights (Attachment A): fill in the gaps → ____ when there is ____ and the ____. Clearway → TODA ↑; stopway → ASDA ↑; displaced THR → LDA ↓ (TORA/TODA/ASDA often unchanged).","All four equal when there is no stopway, no clearway and the threshold at the runway end. Clearway → TODA ↑; stopway → ASDA ↑; displaced THR → LDA ↓ (TORA/TODA/ASDA often unchanged)."],["010.09.08.03:04","Declared distances & approach lights (Attachment A): fill in the gaps → Approach lighting: ____ and ____ systems. Simple: two versions — ____ or barrettes on the centre line + crossbar. CAT I: distance-coded centre line (single sources) with one crossbar or barrette centre line with crossbars (Calvert). Inner 300 m CAT II/III: see above.","Approach lighting: simple and precision systems. Simple: two versions — single light sources or barrettes on the centre line + crossbar. CAT I: distance-coded centre line (single sources) with one crossbar or barrette centre line with crossbars (Calvert). Inner 300 m CAT II/III: see above."],["010.09.08.01:03","Declared distances & approach lights (Attachment A): fill in the gaps → The system's position is fixed relative to the THR — a ____ requires displacing/adapting the approach lights (or inset lights in the pavement).","The system's position is fixed relative to the THR — a displaced threshold requires displacing/adapting the approach lights (or inset lights in the pavement)."],["010.09.04.05:02","Exam trap (Declared distances & approach lights (Attachment A)): complete the fact → Runway edge lights yellow in the last ____; centre line red in the last ____.","Runway edge lights yellow in the last 600 m; centre line red in the last 300 m."],["010.09.04.03:07","Exam trap (Declared distances & approach lights (Attachment A)): complete the fact → PAPI ____white/____red = on slope.","PAPI 2 white/2 red = on slope."],["010.09.07.01:03","Exam trap (Declared distances & approach lights (Attachment A)): complete the fact → RFF response ≤ ____.","RFF response ≤ 3 min."],["010.09.02.03:02","EASA LO 010.09.02.03.02 → Recall the definitions for the four main declared distances.","RFF response ≤ 3 min. / A runway with stopway/clearway allows declared distances beyond TORA; physical characteristics (slopes, strength) must meet requirements."],["010.09.03.01:01","EASA LO 010.09.03.01.01 → Describe where a THR should normally be located.","Aerodrome reference point (ARP): located near the initial or planned geometric centre of the aerodrome; normally remains where first established; published in WGS-84 coordinates. / Simple approach lighting (non-instrument / non-precision runways): centre line ≥ 420 m with a crossbar 18–30 m wide at 300 m from THR; single lights or barrettes, fixed white."],["010.09.03.02:01","EASA LO 010.09.03.02.01 → Explain the term ‘runway strip’.","red, towards the runway / RESA (runway-end safety area): symmetrical about the extended centre line, adjacent to the end of the strip, to reduce damage to aeroplanes undershooting or overrunning (≥ 90 m, recommended 240 m for code 3/4)."],["010.09.03.07:01","EASA LO 010.09.03.07.01 → Describe the reasons and the requirements for rapid-exit TWYs.","Taxiways: rapid-exit taxiways reduce runway occupancy time (angle 25–45°, preferably 30°; designed for ~93 km/h (50 kt) code 3/4, 65 km/h code 1/2). Widening in curves so that the main gear keeps the required clearance while the cockpit follows the centre line."],["010.09.03.07:03","EASA LO 010.09.03.07.03 → Explain when and where holding bays should be provided.","Intermediate holding positions on taxiways where a specific holding limit is needed (e.g. taxiway intersections). / Holding bays where traffic density is medium/heavy (allow sequencing). Runway holding positions at every taxiway/runway intersection and where a taxiing aircraft would infringe an obstacle surface/ILS critical area. Road holding position where a road meets a runway."],["010.09.03.07:04","EASA LO 010.09.03.07.04 → Describe where RWY holding positions shall be established.","Intermediate holding positions on taxiways where a specific holding limit is needed (e.g. taxiway intersections). / Aerodrome reference point (ARP): located near the initial or planned geometric centre of the aerodrome; normally remains where first established; published in WGS-84 coordinates."],["010.09.04.01:03","EASA LO 010.09.04.01.03 → Explain the capabilities of a signalling lamp.","Wind direction indicator: truncated cone of fabric (white/orange or red/white), at least one per aerodrome, lit at night. Landing direction indicator: \"T\" — land parallel to the shaft towards the cross-arm. Signalling lamp: red, green, white signals aimed at a target (see 010-03 light signals). Signal area: square, visible from the air, at aerodromes without radio."],["010.09.04.01:04","EASA LO 010.09.04.01.04 → State which characteristics a signal area should have.","Apron: aircraft have right of way over vehicles (vehicles give way to taxiing aircraft; emergency vehicles have priority over other vehicles). / Wind direction indicator: truncated cone of fabric (white/orange or red/white), at least one per aerodrome, lit at night. Landing direction indicator: \"T\" — land parallel to the shaft towards the cross-arm. Signalling lamp: red, green, white signals aimed at a target (see 010-03 light signals). Signal area: square, visible from the air, at aerodromes without radio."],["010.09.04.01:05","EASA LO 010.09.04.01.05 → Interpret all indications and signals that may be used in a signal area.","Wind direction indicator: truncated cone of fabric (white/orange or red/white), at least one per aerodrome, lit at night. Landing direction indicator: \"T\" — land parallel to the shaft towards the cross-arm. Signalling lamp: red, green, white signals aimed at a target (see 010-03 light signals). Signal area: square, visible from the air, at aerodromes without radio."],["010.09.04.03:03","EASA LO 010.09.04.03.03 → Describe the different kinds of operations for which a simple approach lighting system shall be used.","Simple approach lighting (non-instrument / non-precision runways): centre line ≥ 420 m with a crossbar 18–30 m wide at 300 m from THR; single lights or barrettes, fixed white."],["010.09.04.03:04","EASA LO 010.09.04.03.04 → Describe the basic installations of a simple approach lighting system including the dimensions and distances normally used.","Simple approach lighting (non-instrument / non-precision runways): centre line ≥ 420 m with a crossbar 18–30 m wide at 300 m from THR; single lights or barrettes, fixed white. / The system's position is fixed relative to the THR — a displaced threshold requires displacing/adapting the approach lights (or inset lights in the pavement)."],["010.09.04.03:05","EASA LO 010.09.04.03.05 → Describe the principle of a precision approach category I lighting system including information such as location and characteristics.","Simple approach lighting (non-instrument / non-precision runways): centre line ≥ 420 m with a crossbar 18–30 m wide at 300 m from THR; single lights or barrettes, fixed white."],["010.09.04.03:06","EASA LO 010.09.04.03.06 → Describe the principle of a precision approach category II and III lighting system including information such as location and characteristics, especially the inner 300 m of the system.","Approach lighting: simple and precision systems. Simple: two versions — single light sources or barrettes on the centre line + crossbar. CAT I: distance-coded centre line (single sources) with one crossbar or barrette centre line with crossbars (Calvert). Inner 300 m CAT II/III: see above. / CAT II/III: 900 m centre line; inner 300 m: centre-line barrettes (white) + side-row barrettes (red) + crossbars at 150 m and 300 m — gives roll guidance near the ground."],["010.09.04.03:08","EASA LO 010.09.04.03.08 → Interpret what the pilot will see during an approach using a helicopter approach path indicator (HAPI).","white flashing groups along the approach path"],["010.09.04.04:03","EASA LO 010.09.04.04.03 → Name the kinds of signs which shall be included in mandatory instruction signs.","Sign indicating infringement of an obstacle surface or ILS critical/sensitive area: runway holding position sign showing the taxiway designation + number (e.g. B2) — mandatory, red. / Red is used only on mandatory instruction signs (red background, white inscription). Types: runway designation, CAT I/II/III holding position, runway-holding position, road- holding position, NO ENTRY, STOP. Illuminated where RVR < 800 m or at night."],["010.09.04.04:04","EASA LO 010.09.04.04.04 → Name the colours used for mandatory instruction signs.","Red is used only on mandatory instruction signs (red background, white inscription). Types: runway designation, CAT I/II/III holding position, runway-holding position, road- holding position, NO ENTRY, STOP. Illuminated where RVR < 800 m or at night. / Sign indicating infringement of an obstacle surface or ILS critical/sensitive area: runway holding position sign showing the taxiway designation + number (e.g. B2) — mandatory, red."],["010.09.04.04:06","EASA LO 010.09.04.04.06 → Describe by which sign a pattern ‘B’ RWY holding position (i.e. at an intersection of a TWY and a precision approach RWY) marking shall be supplemented.","Pattern A holding position (non-instrument/NPA/take-off runway) supplemented by a runway designation sign; Pattern B (precision runway — ILS critical area) by a CAT I/II/III holding position sign. Runway designation signs at taxiway/runway intersections on both sides (left of taxiway when facing the runway); / Information signs: direction/destination = yellow background, black inscription; location = black background, yellow inscription + yellow border. Inscriptions: arrows, taxiway letters, \"RWY exit\", runway vacated, intersection take-off (TORA remaining). Aircraft stand identification sign (stand number, yellow/black, visible from cockpit). Road-holding position sign (road side)."],["010.09.04.04:09","EASA LO 010.09.04.04.09 → Describe the various possible inscriptions on RWY designation signs and on holding position signs.","Red is used only on mandatory instruction signs (red background, white inscription). Types: runway designation, CAT I/II/III holding position, runway-holding position, road- holding position, NO ENTRY, STOP. Illuminated where RVR < 800 m or at night. / Sign indicating infringement of an obstacle surface or ILS critical/sensitive area: runway holding position sign showing the taxiway designation + number (e.g. B2) — mandatory, red."],["010.09.04.04:11","EASA LO 010.09.04.04.11 → Describe the possible inscriptions on information signs.","Information signs: direction/destination = yellow background, black inscription; location = black background, yellow inscription + yellow border. Inscriptions: arrows, taxiway letters, \"RWY exit\", runway vacated, intersection take-off (TORA remaining). Aircraft stand identification sign (stand number, yellow/black, visible from cockpit). Road-holding position sign (road side). / Mechanical safety: elevated lights must be frangible, low and light so as not to damage aircraft; approach lights in the first 300 m near THR as low as possible."],["010.09.04.04:13","EASA LO 010.09.04.04.13 → Explain the application, location and characteristics of road holding position signs.","traffic light red/green / Red is used only on mandatory instruction signs (red background, white inscription). Types: runway designation, CAT I/II/III holding position, runway-holding position, road- holding position, NO ENTRY, STOP. Illuminated where RVR < 800 m or at night."],["010.09.04.05:01","EASA LO 010.09.04.05.01 → Explain why markers located near a RWY or TWY shall be HGT limited.","Markers: frangible, low height so as to clear propellers and engine pods. Unpaved RWY edge markers (white/orange), TWY edge markers (blue), TWY centre-line markers (green), unpaved TWY edge (blue/orange), boundary markers (orange/white), stopway edge markers (distinct from runway edge markers). / Aerodrome reference point (ARP): located near the initial or planned geometric centre of the aerodrome; normally remains where first established; published in WGS-84 coordinates."],["010.09.05.01:01","EASA LO 010.09.05.01.01 → State how fixed or mobile objects shall be marked if colouring is not practicable.","Marking of objects: colours — chequered pattern (large surfaces, orange/red + white), alternating bands (tall/thin objects), single colour (small); if colouring impracticable → markers (e.g. spheres on overhead wires), flags (orange/white or red, on mobile objects/temporary). Vehicles: yellow or red/white."],["010.09.05.01:02","EASA LO 010.09.05.01.02 → Describe marking by colours (fixed or mobile objects).","Marking of objects: colours — chequered pattern (large surfaces, orange/red + white), alternating bands (tall/thin objects), single colour (small); if colouring impracticable → markers (e.g. spheres on overhead wires), flags (orange/white or red, on mobile objects/temporary). Vehicles: yellow or red/white. / Obstacle lights: low-intensity (fixed red; flashing yellow on service vehicles, flashing blue emergency vehicles), medium- intensity (flashing red or flashing white), high-intensity (flashing white, very tall objects > 150 m, daytime too). Located at the top of the object (and intermediate levels on tall objects). Aircraft lights → Annex 2/SERA."],["010.09.05.01:04","EASA LO 010.09.05.01.04 → Explain the use of flags for the marking of objects.","Marking of objects: colours — chequered pattern (large surfaces, orange/red + white), alternating bands (tall/thin objects), single colour (small); if colouring impracticable → markers (e.g. spheres on overhead wires), flags (orange/white or red, on mobile objects/temporary). Vehicles: yellow or red/white."],["010.09.05.02:04","EASA LO 010.09.05.02.04 → State that information about lights to be displayed by aircraft is provided in both ICAO Annex 2 (Rules of the Air) and SERA.","Obstacle lights: low-intensity (fixed red; flashing yellow on service vehicles, flashing blue emergency vehicles), medium- intensity (flashing red or flashing white), high-intensity (flashing white, very tall objects > 150 m, daytime too). Located at the top of the object (and intermediate levels on tall objects). Aircraft lights → Annex 2/SERA. / NO ENTRY at the entrance of the prohibited area on both sides; holding-position signs on both sides of the holding position marking."],["010.09.06.01:01","EASA LO 010.09.06.01.01 → Describe the colours and meaning of ‘closed markings’ on RWYs and TWYs.","Colours: runway markings white, taxiway and aircraft stand markings yellow, apron safety lines conspicuous colour contrasting with stand markings."],["010.09.06.01:03","EASA LO 010.09.06.01.03 → Describe the pre-THR marking (including colours) when the surface before the THR is not suitable for normal use by aircraft.","Closed runway/taxiway: white cross (runway) / yellow cross (taxiway). Non-load-bearing shoulders: taxi side-stripe marking (yellow) and transverse stripes. Pre-threshold area unsuitable for aircraft: yellow chevrons (arrowheads pointing to the threshold)."],["010.09.07.01:02","EASA LO 010.09.07.01.02 → Explain the basic information the AD category (for RFF) depends upon.","RFF response ≤ 3 min."],["010.09.08.01:01","EASA LO 010.09.08.01.01 → List the four types of ‘declared distances’ on a RWY and also the appropriate abbreviations.","RFF response ≤ 3 min. / A runway with stopway/clearway allows declared distances beyond TORA; physical characteristics (slopes, strength) must meet requirements."],["010.09.08.01:02","EASA LO 010.09.08.01.02 → Explain the circumstances which lead to the situation that the four declared distances on a RWY are equal to the length of the RWY.","white flashing groups along the approach path / Declared distances: TORA (runway length available for the ground run), TODA (TORA + clearway), ASDA (TORA + stopway), LDA (runway length available for the landing ground run). Guidance in Attachment A to Annex 14."],["010.09.08.03:02","EASA LO 010.09.08.03.02 → Describe the two different versions of a simple approach lighting system.","Approach lighting: simple and precision systems. Simple: two versions — single light sources or barrettes on the centre line + crossbar. CAT I: distance-coded centre line (single sources) with one crossbar or barrette centre line with crossbars (Calvert). Inner 300 m CAT II/III: see above. / Simple approach lighting (non-instrument / non-precision runways): centre line ≥ 420 m with a crossbar 18–30 m wide at 300 m from THR; single lights or barrettes, fixed white."],["010.09.08.03:03","EASA LO 010.09.08.03.03 → Describe the two different basic versions of precision approach lighting systems for CAT I.","Approach lighting: simple and precision systems. Simple: two versions — single light sources or barrettes on the centre line + crossbar. CAT I: distance-coded centre line (single sources) with one crossbar or barrette centre line with crossbars (Calvert). Inner 300 m CAT II/III: see above. / Simple approach lighting (non-instrument / non-precision runways): centre line ≥ 420 m with a crossbar 18–30 m wide at 300 m from THR; single lights or barrettes, fixed white."],["010.09.08.03:05","EASA LO 010.09.08.03.05 → Describe how the arrangement of an approach lighting system and the location of the appropriate THR are interrelated.","Simple approach lighting (non-instrument / non-precision runways): centre line ≥ 420 m with a crossbar 18–30 m wide at 300 m from THR; single lights or barrettes, fixed white. / The system's position is fixed relative to the THR — a displaced threshold requires displacing/adapting the approach lights (or inset lights in the pavement)."]],"76":[["021.10.01.01:01","Basics & design: fill in the gaps → ____ = revolutions per minute of the crankshaft; ____ = turning moment (force × arm, Nm); ____ (P = 2π·n·T); compression ratio = cylinder volume at BDC ÷ volume at TDC; displacement/swept volume.","rpm = revolutions per minute of the crankshaft; torque = turning moment (force × arm, Nm); power = torque × angular speed (P = 2π·n·T); compression ratio = cylinder volume at BDC ÷ volume at TDC; displacement/swept volume."],["021.10.01.02:01","Basics & design: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (inlet valve open, piston down, mixture in) → compression (valves closed, piston up) → power (spark ~20–30° before TDC, combustion pushes piston down) → exhaust (exhaust valve open, piston up). Crankshaft makes ____ revolutions per cycle; camshaft turns at half crankshaft speed; valve overlap at the end of exhaust/start of intake.","Four-stroke cycle (Otto): intake (inlet valve open, piston down, mixture in) → compression (valves closed, piston up) → power (spark ~20–30° before TDC, combustion pushes piston down) → exhaust (exhaust valve open, piston up). Crankshaft makes 2 revolutions per cycle; camshaft turns at half crankshaft speed; valve overlap at the end of exhaust/start of intake."],["021.10.01.02:01","Basics & design: fill in the gaps → Components: ____ (houses crankshaft, main bearings, oil), ____ (converts reciprocating to rotary motion), ____, piston + piston pin (gudgeon pin) + piston rings (compression rings seal, oil-control ring scrapes oil), cylinder, cylinder head (valves, spark plugs), valves (inlet/exhaust, valve springs close them), camshaft + push rods + rocker arms (open valves), camshaft gear, bearings.","Components: crankcase (houses crankshaft, main bearings, oil), crankshaft (converts reciprocating to rotary motion), connecting rod, piston + piston pin (gudgeon pin) + piston rings (compression rings seal, oil-control ring scrapes oil), cylinder, cylinder head (valves, spark plugs), valves (inlet/exhaust, valve springs close them), camshaft + push rods + rocker arms (open valves), camshaft gear, bearings."],["021.10.01.02:02","Basics & design: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (low frontal area, good cooling, smooth — most light aircraft), ____ (small frontal area, rear cylinders cool poorly), radial (short, good cooling, high drag, oil pooling → hydraulic lock).","Arrangements: horizontally opposed (low frontal area, good cooling, smooth — most light aircraft), in-line (small frontal area, rear cylinders cool poorly), radial (short, good cooling, high drag, oil pooling → hydraulic lock)."],["021.10.01.02:03","Basics & design: fill in the gaps → Petrol vs diesel: ignition by spark vs compression heat (self-ignition); compression ratio ____ (detonation limit) vs ____ ____; power controlled by throttle (mixture quantity) vs fuel quantity only (unthrottled air); exhaust: petrol CO, HC, lead (100LL) vs diesel NOₓ, soot/particles, less CO₂ per power.","Petrol vs diesel: ignition by spark vs compression heat (self-ignition); compression ratio ~6–10:1 (detonation limit) vs ~14– 22:1; power controlled by throttle (mixture quantity) vs fuel quantity only (unthrottled air); exhaust: petrol CO, HC, lead (100LL) vs diesel NOₓ, soot/particles, less CO₂ per power."],["021.10.08.01:06","Cooling: fill in the gaps → Reasons: keep ____ within limits → prevent detonation, pre-ignition, loss of oil viscosity, material damage.","Reasons: keep cylinder-head and oil temperatures within limits → prevent detonation, pre-ignition, loss of oil viscosity, material damage."],["021.10.05.01:03","Cooling: fill in the gaps → Air cooling (aeroplanes): ____ on cylinders, ____ directing ram air over them, pressure cowling, ____. Helicopters: engine-driven fan/impeller + scroll assembly and baffles (no ram air in hover).","Air cooling (aeroplanes): cooling fins on cylinders, baffles directing ram air over them, pressure cowling, cowl flaps. Helicopters: engine-driven fan/impeller + scroll assembly and baffles (no ram air in hover)."],["021.10.05.01:04","Cooling: fill in the gaps → ____: more uniform temperatures, allows closer cowlings, but heavier, complex, leaks; air cooling: simpler, lighter, but temperature differences, shock cooling.","Liquid cooling: more uniform temperatures, allows closer cowlings, but heavier, complex, leaks; air cooling: simpler, lighter, but temperature differences, shock cooling."],["021.10.05.01:05","Cooling: fill in the gaps → ____ indication (thermocouple on the hottest cylinder) monitors cooling. ____: ____ on ground, take-off and climb (high power, low speed); closed in descent/cruise as required (avoid shock cooling).","CHT indication (thermocouple on the hottest cylinder) monitors cooling. Cowl flaps: open on ground, take-off and climb (high power, low speed); closed in descent/cruise as required (avoid shock cooling)."],["021.10.06.01:02","Lubrication: fill in the gaps → ____ = resistance to flow; decreases with ____. Aviation grade numbers (e.g. ____, 65/80/100/120; multigrade 15W-50): higher number = more viscous.","Viscosity = resistance to flow; decreases with temperature. Aviation grade numbers (e.g. SAE 50 ≈ aviation 100, 65/80/100/120; multigrade 15W-50): higher number = more viscous."],["021.10.06.02:01","Lubrication: fill in the gaps → Functions: ____ (reduce friction/wear), ____, ____ (piston rings), clean (carry debris to filter), protect against corrosion, (hydraulic medium for prop governor).","Functions: lubricate (reduce friction/wear), cool, seal (piston rings), clean (carry debris to filter), protect against corrosion, (hydraulic medium for prop governor)."],["021.10.06.02:06","Lubrication: fill in the gaps → ____: oil kept in a ____; ____ feeds engine, scavenge pump(s) (higher capacity) return oil to the tank; components: tank, pressure pump, pressure relief valve, filter, oil cooler with thermostatic bypass valve, scavenge pump, pressure & temperature gauges.","Dry sump: oil kept in a separate tank; pressure pump feeds engine, scavenge pump(s) (higher capacity) return oil to the tank; components: tank, pressure pump, pressure relief valve, filter, oil cooler with thermostatic bypass valve, scavenge pump, pressure & temperature gauges."],["021.10.06.02:03","Lubrication: fill in the gaps → ____: oil stored in the crankcase sump, one pressure pump. Dry sump: works in aerobatics/inverted, better cooling, larger capacity, lower engine height; but heavier/complex. Wet sump: simple, light.","Wet sump: oil stored in the crankcase sump, one pressure pump. Dry sump: works in aerobatics/inverted, better cooling, larger capacity, lower engine height; but heavier/complex. Wet sump: simple, light."],["021.10.06.02:05","Lubrication: fill in the gaps → Oil consumption rises with thin/wrong grade, ____ ____, worn ____, high temperature.","Oil consumption rises with thin/wrong grade, cylinder/piston wear, worn piston rings, high temperature."],["021.10.06.02:06","Lubrication: fill in the gaps → Interaction: low oil quantity → temperature ↑, pressure ↓ (then fluctuating); ____ ____; high pressure + low temperature = cold oil (warm-up). Check pressure within ____ of start.","Interaction: low oil quantity → temperature ↑, pressure ↓ (then fluctuating); low pressure + high temperature → imminent failure; high pressure + low temperature = cold oil (warm-up). Check pressure within 30 s of start."],["021.10.07.01:01","Ignition: fill in the gaps → ____ = self-contained generator (rotating magnet → current in ____), ____ interrupt the primary current (opened by a cam) → collapse of the field induces high voltage in secondary coil (~20 kV); capacitor (condenser) prevents arcing at the points and speeds field collapse;","Magneto = self-contained generator (rotating magnet → current in primary coil), contact-breaker points interrupt the primary current (opened by a cam) → collapse of the field induces high voltage in secondary coil (~20 kV); capacitor (condenser) prevents arcing at the points and speeds field collapse;"],["021.10.07.01:01","Ignition: fill in the gaps → ____ routes HT to each cylinder's ____ via ____ cables; ignition switch earths (grounds) the primary circuit — OFF = grounded. A broken ground wire (\"P-lead\") = live magneto even when switched off → propeller danger!","distributor routes HT to each cylinder's spark plug via HT cables; ignition switch earths (grounds) the primary circuit — OFF = grounded. A broken ground wire (\"P-lead\") = live magneto even when switched off → propeller danger!"],["021.10.07.01:02","Ignition: fill in the gaps → ____ (two magnetos, two plugs per cylinder): ____ and ____ (two flame fronts → more power, less detonation).","Two independent systems (two magnetos, two plugs per cylinder): redundancy and better combustion (two flame fronts → more power, less detonation)."],["021.10.07.01:03","Ignition: fill in the gaps → ____ (low rpm → weak spark, late timing needed): ____ (spring winds up and snaps the magneto rotor quickly past the firing position, ____ the spark to TDC); starter vibrator/booster coil (provides a shower of sparks from the battery to the retard breaker); often only one magneto live during start.","Spark augmentation at start (low rpm → weak spark, late timing needed): impulse coupling (spring winds up and snaps the magneto rotor quickly past the firing position, retards the spark to TDC); starter vibrator/booster coil (provides a shower of sparks from the battery to the retard breaker); often only one magneto live during start."],["021.10.07.01:05","Ignition: fill in the gaps → ____ (run-up, e.g. 1 700–2 000 rpm): switch L and R — ____ = normal; ____ = magneto not grounding (live) or timing issue; large drop/rough = fouled plug/magneto fault.","Magneto check (run-up, e.g. 1 700–2 000 rpm): switch L and R — small rpm drop = normal; no drop = magneto not grounding (live) or timing issue; large drop/rough = fouled plug/magneto fault."],["021.10.07.01:01","Ignition: fill in the gaps → ____: air compressed to high temperature, fuel injected at high pressure → ____; glow plugs assist cold starts; FADEC controls injection.","Diesel: air compressed to high temperature, fuel injected at high pressure → self-ignition; glow plugs assist cold starts; FADEC controls injection."],["021.10.08.01:01","Mixture: fill in the gaps → Mixture = fuel/air ratio by mass. Stoichiometric (chemically correct) ≈ ____ — all fuel and oxygen burn; best power ≈ ____ — slightly rich, max power; best economy ≈ ____ — slightly lean. Range of combustion ~1:8 (rich limit) to ~1:20 (lean limit).","Mixture = fuel/air ratio by mass. Stoichiometric (chemically correct) ≈ 1:15 (0.067) — all fuel and oxygen burn; best power ≈ 1:12.5 (0.08) — slightly rich, max power; best economy ≈ 1:16 (0.0625) — slightly lean. Range of combustion ~1:8 (rich limit) to ~1:20 (lean limit)."],["021.10.08.01:01","Mixture: fill in the gaps → ____: cooler (excess fuel cools), less detonation, but higher consumption, fouled plugs, power loss if too rich. ____: economical, but higher CHT (near peak), detonation risk, rough running, power loss if too lean.","Rich: cooler (excess fuel cools), less detonation, but higher consumption, fouled plugs, power loss if too rich. Lean: economical, but higher CHT (near peak), detonation risk, rough running, power loss if too lean."],["021.10.08.01:01","Mixture: fill in the gaps → ____: minimum SFC slightly lean of stoichiometric; best power richer.","SFC vs mixture: minimum SFC slightly lean of stoichiometric; best power richer."],["021.10.08.01:01","Mixture: fill in the gaps → ____ peaks near stoichiometric → lean until peak EGT, then enrich e.g. ____ (or lean of peak with suitable engines).","EGT peaks near stoichiometric → lean until peak EGT, then enrich e.g. 50–100 °F rich of peak (or lean of peak with suitable engines)."],["021.10.05.01:06","Mixture: fill in the gaps → Lean mixture/high power → ____ (explosive combustion of the end gas after ignition) and ____ (ignition before the spark by hot spots). Enrich, reduce power, open cowl flaps.","Lean mixture/high power → CHT ↑ → detonation (explosive combustion of the end gas after ignition) and pre-ignition (ignition before the spark by hot spots). Enrich, reduce power, open cowl flaps."],["021.10.08.01:01","Mixture: fill in the gaps → ____ has ____ — always excess air; power set by fuel quantity (lever/FADEC).","Diesel has no mixture control — always excess air; power set by fuel quantity (lever/FADEC)."],["021.10.09.02:01","Constant-speed propeller: fill in the gaps → ____: pilot selects ____ with the prop lever; ____ (flyweights vs speeder spring) meters oil to/from the hub: overspeed → flyweights out → oil moves blades to coarser pitch → rpm ↓; underspeed → finer pitch. rpm constant, power set by throttle (MAP).","Principle: pilot selects rpm with the prop lever; governor (flyweights vs speeder spring) meters oil to/from the hub: overspeed → flyweights out → oil moves blades to coarser pitch → rpm ↓; underspeed → finer pitch. rpm constant, power set by throttle (MAP)."],["021.10.09.02:02","Constant-speed propeller: fill in the gaps → ____ (inHg) needed because with constant rpm the tachometer no longer shows power; power ≈ MAP × rpm. ____ (turboprops) shows the actual shaft torque/power.","MAP gauge (inHg) needed because with constant rpm the tachometer no longer shows power; power ≈ MAP × rpm. Torquemeter (turboprops) shows the actual shaft torque/power."],["021.10.09.02:04","Constant-speed propeller: fill in the gaps → ____: prevents blades going to very fine/reverse pitch in flight (overspeed, drag); centrifugal latches hold feathering-capable props out of feather at low rpm on shutdown so they don't feather on the ground (start possible).","Low-pitch stop / centrifugal latch: prevents blades going to very fine/reverse pitch in flight (overspeed, drag); centrifugal latches hold feathering-capable props out of feather at low rpm on shutdown so they don't feather on the ground (start possible)."],["021.10.09.02:05","Constant-speed propeller: fill in the gaps → ____: oil pressure moves blades one way (typ. ____ on singles), counterweights/springs/aerodynamic twisting moment the other way; singles: oil loss → ____ (max rpm, overspeed risk); twins: oil pressure → fine, spring + counterweights → feather (oil loss → feather). Double-acting: oil pressure in both directions (large props).","Single-acting: oil pressure moves blades one way (typ. fine on singles), counterweights/springs/aerodynamic twisting moment the other way; singles: oil loss → fine pitch (max rpm, overspeed risk); twins: oil pressure → fine, spring + counterweights → feather (oil loss → feather). Double-acting: oil pressure in both directions (large props)."],["021.10.09.02:01","Constant-speed propeller: fill in the gaps → ____ (equal rpm on all engines) and ____ (constant blade-phase relationship) reduce noise and vibration.","Synchronising (equal rpm on all engines) and synchrophasing (constant blade-phase relationship) reduce noise and vibration."],["021.10.09.02:07","Constant-speed propeller: fill in the gaps → ____: automatically feathers the prop of a failed engine at take-off (armed, torque drop sensed) → less drag/yaw. ____: electric unfeathering pump or accumulator provides oil pressure to move blades out of feather for restart.","Auto-feather: automatically feathers the prop of a failed engine at take-off (armed, torque drop sensed) → less drag/yaw. Unfeathering: electric unfeathering pump or accumulator provides oil pressure to move blades out of feather for restart."],["021.10.09.03:01","Constant-speed propeller: fill in the gaps → ____: allows the engine to run at efficient high rpm while the propeller turns slower (tip speed below sonic, better efficiency).","Reduction gearing: allows the engine to run at efficient high rpm while the propeller turns slower (tip speed below sonic, better efficiency)."],["021.10.07.01:05","Constant-speed propeller: fill in the gaps → ____: cycle the prop lever 2–3 times (rpm drop, oil pressure/MAP change) to circulate warm oil in the hub; feather check on twins.","Run-up check: cycle the prop lever 2–3 times (rpm drop, oil pressure/MAP change) to circulate warm oil in the hub; feather check on twins."],["021.10.09.02:01","Constant-speed propeller: fill in the gaps → ____: at constant rpm, ____; lever back → lower rpm/coarser pitch. ____ (governor failure/oil loss) → reduce power and airspeed, pull prop lever, climb/slow down. Feathering: prop lever to feather (fuel cut-off) → blades ~90°; unfeather with unfeather pump/starter.","In flight: at constant rpm, higher TAS → coarser pitch; lever back → lower rpm/coarser pitch. Overspeed (governor failure/oil loss) → reduce power and airspeed, pull prop lever, climb/slow down. Feathering: prop lever to feather (fuel cut-off) → blades ~90°; unfeather with unfeather pump/starter."],["021.10.09.02:01","Constant-speed propeller: fill in the gaps → ____: power levers aft through the gate (beta range) → blade angle negative for braking (turboprops).","Reverse: power levers aft through the gate (beta range) → blade angle negative for braking (turboprops)."],["021.10.09.02:01","Constant-speed propeller: fill in the gaps → ____: take-off full forward (max rpm, fine); climb/cruise reduce MAP first, then rpm (____ ____) to avoid high MAP at low rpm (overboost); approach/landing prop full forward (go-around).","Levers: take-off full forward (max rpm, fine); climb/cruise reduce MAP first, then rpm (power reduction: throttle then prop; increase: prop then throttle) to avoid high MAP at low rpm (overboost); approach/landing prop full forward (go-around)."],["021.10.05.01:01","EASA LO 021.10.05.01.01 → Specify the reasons for cooling a piston engine.","Reasons: keep cylinder-head and oil temperatures within limits → prevent detonation, pre-ignition, loss of oil viscosity, material damage."],["021.10.05.01:02","EASA LO 021.10.05.01.02 → Describe the design features to enhance cylinder air cooling for aeroplanes.","Air cooling (aeroplanes): cooling fins on cylinders, baffles directing ram air over them, pressure cowling, cowl flaps. Helicopters: engine-driven fan/impeller + scroll assembly and baffles (no ram air in hover)."],["021.10.06.02:02","EASA LO 021.10.06.02.02 → Describe the working principle of a dry-sump lubrication system and describe the functions of the following components:","Wet sump: oil stored in the crankcase sump, one pressure pump. Dry sump: works in aerobatics/inverted, better cooling, larger capacity, lower engine height; but heavier/complex. Wet sump: simple, light."],["021.10.06.02:04","EASA LO 021.10.06.02.04 → State the differences between a wet- and a dry-sump lubrication system and their advantages and disadvantages.","Wet sump: oil stored in the crankcase sump, one pressure pump. Dry sump: works in aerobatics/inverted, better cooling, larger capacity, lower engine height; but heavier/complex. Wet sump: simple, light."],["021.10.07.01:04","EASA LO 021.10.07.01.04 → State the function and operating principle of the following methods of spark augmentation: — starter vibrator (booster coil); — both magnetos live.","Spark augmentation at start (low rpm → weak spark, late timing needed): impulse coupling (spring winds up and snaps the magneto rotor quickly past the firing position, retards the spark to TDC); starter vibrator/booster coil (provides a shower of sparks from the battery to the retard breaker); often only one magneto live during start."]],"77":[["061.04.01.01:01","ICAO Annex 4 requirements, convergence, scale: fill in the gaps → Aeronautical charts should be ____ (angles and shapes correct locally, scale same in all directions at a point — ____) and a ____ great circle (so the pencil line is the shortest route).","Aeronautical charts should be conformal (angles and shapes correct locally, scale same in all directions at a point — orthomorphic) and a straight line should approximate a great circle (so the pencil line is the shortest route)."],["061.04.02.04:06","ICAO Annex 4 requirements, convergence, scale: fill in the gaps → ____ (angle between two meridians at a given latitude) = ____. ____ = angle between the meridians on the chart. Constant of the cone n = sin(parallel of origin) (Lambert); chart convergence = Δlong × n. A straight line approximates a great circle where chart convergence ≈ earth convergence, i.e. near the parallel of origin.","Earth convergency (angle between two meridians at a given latitude) = Δlong × sin(mean lat). Chart convergence = angle between the meridians on the chart. Constant of the cone n = sin(parallel of origin) (Lambert); chart convergence = Δlong × n. A straight line approximates a great circle where chart convergence ≈ earth convergence, i.e. near the parallel of origin."],["061.04.01.01:01","ICAO Annex 4 requirements, convergence, scale: fill in the gaps → ____ representation: representative fraction (1:500 000), statement (\"1 cm = 5 km\"), graduated scale line. Scale calculation: chart distance × scale denominator = earth distance. Example: 1:1 000 000, 10 cm → 10 km = ____; 54 NM on a 1:500 000 chart = 100 km/500 000 = ____.","Scale representation: representative fraction (1:500 000), statement (\"1 cm = 5 km\"), graduated scale line. Scale calculation: chart distance × scale denominator = earth distance. Example: 1:1 000 000, 10 cm → 10 km = 5.4 NM; 54 NM on a 1:500 000 chart = 100 km/500 000 = 20 cm."],["061.04.02.01:01","Projections: fill in the gaps → Families: ____ (polar stereographic — plane tangent at the pole), ____ (Mercator — cylinder tangent at the equator), ____ (Lambert — cone tangent/secant at parallels).","Families: azimuthal/plane (polar stereographic — plane tangent at the pole), cylindrical (Mercator — cylinder tangent at the equator), conical (Lambert — cone tangent/secant at parallels)."],["061.04.02.02:02","Projections: fill in the gaps → ____: conformal; meridians = ____ radiating from the pole, parallels = concentric circles; scale increases away from the pole (correct at the pole); ____ (chart convergence = Δlong); great circles ≈ straight lines near the pole; rhumb lines are curves (spirals) concave to the pole.","Polar stereographic: conformal; meridians = straight lines radiating from the pole, parallels = concentric circles; scale increases away from the pole (correct at the pole); n = 1 (chart convergence = Δlong); great circles ≈ straight lines near the pole; rhumb lines are curves (spirals) concave to the pole."],["061.04.02.04:02","Projections: fill in the gaps → ____ (e.g. flying a straight line across 30° of longitude → track changes by 30°).","Straight-line track change = Δlong (e.g. flying a straight line across 30° of longitude → track changes by 30°)."],["061.04.02.01:01","Projections: fill in the gaps → ____: conformal; meridians parallel, equally spaced, vertical; parallels straight, spacing increases towards the poles; ____; great circles are curves concave to the equator (convex to the nearer pole); ____;","Direct Mercator: conformal; meridians parallel, equally spaced, vertical; parallels straight, spacing increases towards the poles; rhumb lines = straight lines; great circles are curves concave to the equator (convex to the nearer pole); n = 0;"],["061.04.02.03:03","Projections: fill in the gaps → ____ ____: scale at lat 2 = scale at lat 1 × cos(lat 1)/cos(lat 2) — i.e. ____. Example: scale 1:1 000 000 at the equator → at 60° the scale is 1:500 000 (larger); a chart length representing 100 NM at the equator represents only 50 NM at 60°N.","scale ∝ sec(latitude): scale at lat 2 = scale at lat 1 × cos(lat 1)/cos(lat 2) — i.e. chart length of a given earth distance ∝ 1/cos lat. Example: scale 1:1 000 000 at the equator → at 60° the scale is 1:500 000 (larger); a chart length representing 100 NM at the equator represents only 50 NM at 60°N."],["061.04.02.04:06","Projections: fill in the gaps → ____: cone intersecting the earth at two ____; ____ ≈ midway (where the cone is parallel to the earth's surface, sin(PO) = n); meridians = straight lines converging to a point (apex), parallels = arcs of concentric circles;","Lambert conformal conic: cone intersecting the earth at two standard parallels; parallel of origin ≈ midway (where the cone is parallel to the earth's surface, sin(PO) = n); meridians = straight lines converging to a point (apex), parallels = arcs of concentric circles;"],["061.04.02.04:03","Projections: fill in the gaps → ____ (slightly ____ ____); rhumb lines = curves concave to the pole. Scale correct on the standard parallels, slightly smaller between them (error < 1 %), increasing outside them (away from the parallel of origin). Straight-line track change = Δlong × n.","great circle ≈ straight line (slightly concave to the parallel of origin); rhumb lines = curves concave to the pole. Scale correct on the standard parallels, slightly smaller between them (error < 1 %), increasing outside them (away from the parallel of origin). Straight-line track change = Δlong × n."],["061.04.02.04:04","Projections: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ between two points ≈ earth convergency = Δlong × sin(mean lat). ____ track = great-circle initial track ± conversion angle where conversion angle = ½ × convergency. Rhumb line lies on the equatorial side of the great circle.","Track calculations: great-circle track change between two points ≈ earth convergency = Δlong × sin(mean lat). Rhumb-line track = great-circle initial track ± conversion angle where conversion angle = ½ × convergency. Rhumb line lies on the equatorial side of the great circle."],["061.04.02.04:04","Projections: fill in the gaps → Example: A (50°N 010°W) → B (50°N 010°E): RL track = ____; convergency = 20 × sin 50° = 15.3° → conversion angle ____ → GC initial track ____, GC final track 097.7°. In the NH the great circle lies polewards of the rhumb line.","Example: A (50°N 010°W) → B (50°N 010°E): RL track = 090°; convergency = 20 × sin 50° = 15.3° → conversion angle 7.7° → GC initial track 082.3°, GC final track 097.7°. In the NH the great circle lies polewards of the rhumb line."],["061.04.02.04:02","Projections: fill in the gaps → ____: n = chart convergence/Δlong → PO = arcsin(n). E.g. straight-line track changes 30° over 50° Δlong → n = 0.6 → PO ≈ ____.","Parallel of origin from data: n = chart convergence/Δlong → PO = arcsin(n). E.g. straight-line track changes 30° over 50° Δlong → n = 0.6 → PO ≈ 36.9°."],["061.04.02.04:02","Projections: fill in the gaps → ____: Δlong = (GC track at B − GC track at A) ÷ n.","Δlong from tracks: Δlong = (GC track at B − GC track at A) ÷ n."],["061.04.03.01:01","Practical use: fill in the gaps → Recognise ____: aerodromes (civil/military, hard runway), VOR (hexagon), DME (square), VORTAC, NDB (dotted circle), reporting points (compulsory filled triangle, on-request open triangle), obstacles (with height AMSL/AGL), airspace boundaries, danger/restricted/prohibited areas (D/R/P), MEF/grid MORA, isogonals.","Recognise ICAO Annex 4 symbols: aerodromes (civil/military, hard runway), VOR (hexagon), DME (square), VORTAC, NDB (dotted circle), reporting points (compulsory filled triangle, on-request open triangle), obstacles (with height AMSL/AGL), airspace boundaries, danger/restricted/prohibited areas (D/R/P), MEF/grid MORA, isogonals."],["061.04.03.02:02","Practical use: fill in the gaps → ____: measure tracks with a protractor/plotter ____ meridian on Lambert (straight line ≈ GC), distances with the chart scale or ____. VOR/DME fix: plot the radial from the VOR (magnetic, apply the variation at the VOR station) and the DME arc (slant range) → intersection.","Plotting: measure tracks with a protractor/plotter at the mid- meridian on Lambert (straight line ≈ GC), distances with the chart scale or latitude scale (1′ lat = 1 NM). VOR/DME fix: plot the radial from the VOR (magnetic, apply the variation at the VOR station) and the DME arc (slant range) → intersection."],["061.04.03.02:03","Practical use: fill in the gaps → ____ ____: QDR/QDM from ____: QDM = MH + RB (bearing to the NDB); true bearing to the station = QDM + variation at the aircraft (E +, W −);","NDB bearing: QDR/QDM from relative bearing + heading: QDM = MH + RB (bearing to the NDB); true bearing to the station = QDM + variation at the aircraft (E +, W −);"],["061.04.03.01:01","Practical use: fill in the gaps → ____ (true bearing from the station) = that ± 180° — plotted from the station, corrected by the ____ between the aircraft's and the station's meridians on large Δlong.","QTE (true bearing from the station) = that ± 180° — plotted from the station, corrected by the convergency/conversion angle between the aircraft's and the station's meridians on large Δlong."],["061.04.02.02:01","Exam trap (Practical use): complete the fact → Polar stereographic n = ____","Polar stereographic n = 1."],["061.04.01.02:01","EASA LO 061.04.01.02.01 → Explain and calculate the constant of the cone (sine of parallel of origin).","Earth convergency (angle between two meridians at a given latitude) = Δlong × sin(mean lat). Chart convergence = angle between the meridians on the chart. Constant of the cone n = sin(parallel of origin) (Lambert); chart convergence = Δlong × n. A straight line approximates a great circle where chart convergence ≈ earth convergence, i.e. near the parallel of origin. / Lambert conformal conic: cone intersecting the earth at two standard parallels; parallel of origin ≈ midway (where the cone is parallel to the earth's surface, sin(PO) = n); meridians = straight lines converging to a point (apex), parallels = arcs of concentric circles;"],["061.04.01.02:02","EASA LO 061.04.01.02.02 → Explain the relationship between Earth and chart convergence with respect to the ICAO requirement for a straight line to approximate a great circle.","Earth convergency (angle between two meridians at a given latitude) = Δlong × sin(mean lat). Chart convergence = angle between the meridians on the chart. Constant of the cone n = sin(parallel of origin) (Lambert); chart convergence = Δlong × n. A straight line approximates a great circle where chart convergence ≈ earth convergence, i.e. near the parallel of origin. / Track calculations: great-circle track change between two points ≈ earth convergency = Δlong × sin(mean lat). Rhumb-line track = great-circle initial track ± conversion angle where conversion angle = ½ × convergency. Rhumb line lies on the equatorial side of the great circle."],["061.04.01.03:01","EASA LO 061.04.01.03.01 → Recognise methods of representing scale on aeronautical charts.","Aeronautical charts should be conformal (angles and shapes correct locally, scale same in all directions at a point — orthomorphic) and a straight line should approximate a great circle (so the pencil line is the shortest route). / scale ∝ sec(latitude): scale at lat 2 = scale at lat 1 × cos(lat 1)/cos(lat 2) — i.e. chart length of a given earth distance ∝ 1/cos lat. Example: scale 1:1 000 000 at the equator → at 60° the scale is 1:500 000 (larger); a chart length representing 100 NM at the equator represents only 50 NM at 60°N."],["061.04.02.04:05","EASA LO 061.04.02.04.05 → Given two positions (lat./long.) and information to determine convergency between the two positions, calculate the parallel of origin.","Earth convergency (angle between two meridians at a given latitude) = Δlong × sin(mean lat). Chart convergence = angle between the meridians on the chart. Constant of the cone n = sin(parallel of origin) (Lambert); chart convergence = Δlong × n. A straight line approximates a great circle where chart convergence ≈ earth convergence, i.e. near the parallel of origin. / Track calculations: great-circle track change between two points ≈ earth convergency = Δlong × sin(mean lat). Rhumb-line track = great-circle initial track ± conversion angle where conversion angle = ½ × convergency. Rhumb line lies on the equatorial side of the great circle."],["061.04.02.04:07","EASA LO 061.04.02.04.07 → Given constant of cone or parallel of origin, great-circle track at one position and great-circle track at another position, calculate the difference of longitude between the two positions.","Earth convergency (angle between two meridians at a given latitude) = Δlong × sin(mean lat). Chart convergence = angle between the meridians on the chart. Constant of the cone n = sin(parallel of origin) (Lambert); chart convergence = Δlong × n. A straight line approximates a great circle where chart convergence ≈ earth convergence, i.e. near the parallel of origin. / Track calculations: great-circle track change between two points ≈ earth convergency = Δlong × sin(mean lat). Rhumb-line track = great-circle initial track ± conversion angle where conversion angle = ½ × convergency. Rhumb line lies on the equatorial side of the great circle."],["061.04.03.02:01","EASA LO 061.04.03.02.01 → Measure tracks and distances on VFR and IFR en-route charts.","Plotting: measure tracks with a protractor/plotter at the mid- meridian on Lambert (straight line ≈ GC), distances with the chart scale or latitude scale (1′ lat = 1 NM). VOR/DME fix: plot the radial from the VOR (magnetic, apply the variation at the VOR station) and the DME arc (slant range) → intersection."]],"78":[["031.05.01.01:01","Determining the CG: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (moment = mass × arm from the datum; arms aft of datum +, forward −). Build a table: item, mass, arm, moment → total → CG. ____: CG in % MAC = (CG − LEMAC) ÷ MAC × 100.","Arithmetic method: CG = Σ moments ÷ Σ mass (moment = mass × arm from the datum; arms aft of datum +, forward −). Build a table: item, mass, arm, moment → total → CG. %MAC: CG in % MAC = (CG − LEMAC) ÷ MAC × 100."],["031.05.01.01:01","Determining the CG: fill in the gaps → ____: loading graph — start at the empty/basic moment, add each item's moment line (slope per compartment), read the resulting CG/moment on the envelope graph (CAP 696 SEP/MEP).","Graphic method: loading graph — start at the empty/basic moment, add each item's moment line (slope per compartment), read the resulting CG/moment on the envelope graph (CAP 696 SEP/MEP)."],["031.05.01.03:02","Determining the CG: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ ____ (e.g. (arm − 25 m) × mass/1 000) → small, manageable numbers, avoids large moments and negative values; DOI (dry operating index) = index of the aircraft at dry operating mass. Advantages: quick, simple addition/subtraction, easy graphical trim sheets, fewer arithmetic errors.","Index method: index = (arm − reference arm) × mass ÷ constant (e.g. (arm − 25 m) × mass/1 000) → small, manageable numbers, avoids large moments and negative values; DOI (dry operating index) = index of the aircraft at dry operating mass. Advantages: quick, simple addition/subtraction, easy graphical trim sheets, fewer arithmetic errors."],["031.05.02.04:04","Load & trim sheets: fill in the gaps → ____: documents the ____ (DOM, traffic load, fuel, ZFM, TOM, LM) and checks them against limits; signed by the commander before departure. ____: determines the CG/index at ZFM, TOM (and LM) and checks it against the CG envelope; gives take-off stab trim.","Load sheet: documents the masses (DOM, traffic load, fuel, ZFM, TOM, LM) and checks them against limits; signed by the commander before departure. Trim sheet: determines the CG/index at ZFM, TOM (and LM) and checks it against the CG envelope; gives take-off stab trim."],["031.05.02.03:01","Load & trim sheets: fill in the gaps → ____ = the ____ of: ____ fuel, MTOM (performance-/structurally limited), MLM + trip fuel. Allowed traffic load = allowed TOM − (DOM + take-off fuel). Underload = allowed traffic load − actual traffic load (available for last-minute changes). The limiting value is marked;","Allowed mass for take-off = the lowest of: MZFM + take-off fuel, MTOM (performance-/structurally limited), MLM + trip fuel. Allowed traffic load = allowed TOM − (DOM + take-off fuel). Underload = allowed traffic load − actual traffic load (available for last-minute changes). The limiting value is marked;"],["031.05.02.03:03","Load & trim sheets: fill in the gaps → ____ are there to ____ actual masses against limits.","boxed maximum figures are there to cross-check actual masses against limits."],["031.05.02.03:03","Load & trim sheets: what do you remember about this? → Sections: DOM/DOI, traffic load by compartment (pax by zone, cargo, …","Sections: DOM/DOI, traffic load by compartment (pax by zone, cargo, baggage, mail), ZFM, fuel, TOM, trip fuel → LM, last-minute changes, signatures. Complete, then cross-check totals and limits (MZFM, MTOM, MLM)."],["031.05.02.04:03","Load & trim sheets: fill in the gaps → ____: start at ____ → add index changes for cargo per compartment and pax per zone (graphical lines or tables) → ____ index → check in the ZFM envelope → add fuel index (from the fuel index correction table, non-linear due to tank geometry) → TOM index → %MAC → check the TOM envelope → read stabiliser trim units for take-off from %MAC (and flap/thrust).","Trim sheet: start at DOI → add index changes for cargo per compartment and pax per zone (graphical lines or tables) → ZFM index → check in the ZFM envelope → add fuel index (from the fuel index correction table, non-linear due to tank geometry) → TOM index → %MAC → check the TOM envelope → read stabiliser trim units for take-off from %MAC (and flap/thrust)."],["031.05.02.04:06","Load & trim sheets: fill in the gaps → ____ (AFM, structural/aerodynamic) vs ____ (narrower, set by the operator to account for in-flight movements of pax/crew/trolleys, fuel burn sequence, seating inaccuracies, gear/flap retraction).","Certified CG limits (AFM, structural/aerodynamic) vs operational CG limits (narrower, set by the operator to account for in-flight movements of pax/crew/trolleys, fuel burn sequence, seating inaccuracies, gear/flap retraction)."],["031.05.02.01:01","Load & trim sheets: fill in the gaps → ____: forward CG → more elevator/nose-up trim needed, higher stall speed, more fuel burn; aft CG → less stability, light forces, risk of insufficient nose-down authority/tail tipping on ground; ____ to rotate normally.","Pitch control & CG: forward CG → more elevator/nose-up trim needed, higher stall speed, more fuel burn; aft CG → less stability, light forces, risk of insufficient nose-down authority/tail tipping on ground; stab trim must match the CG to rotate normally."],["031.05.02.06:01","Load & trim sheets: fill in the gaps → ____: load and trim data via ____ (final load sheet uplinked), ____ mass & balance applications, LPC (less paper in the cockpit) software → electronic calculation, last-minute changes, same checks by the crew.","Other methods: load and trim data via ACARS (final load sheet uplinked), EFB mass & balance applications, LPC (less paper in the cockpit) software → electronic calculation, last-minute changes, same checks by the crew."],["031.05.03.01:01","Moving the CG: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ ____ — m = M × ΔCG / d. Equivalent: distance = M × ΔCG / m.","Shifting load: mass moved = total mass × CG shift ÷ distance moved — m = M × ΔCG / d. Equivalent: distance = M × ΔCG / m."],["031.05.03.01:02","Moving the CG: fill in the gaps → Example: M = 60 000 kg, CG must move 0.25 m aft, move cargo from front hold (arm 12 m) to rear hold (arm 32 m, d = 20 m) → m = 60 000 × 0.25/20 = ____.","Example: M = 60 000 kg, CG must move 0.25 m aft, move cargo from front hold (arm 12 m) to rear hold (arm 32 m, d = 20 m) → m = 60 000 × 0.25/20 = 750 kg."],["031.05.02.01:01","Moving the CG: fill in the gaps → ____: ________ × ΔCG / (arm of new load − new CG), or use the general formula new CG = (old moment ± m × arm)/(old mass ± m) and solve for m (or arm).","Adding/removing load or ballast: m = Mold × ΔCG / (arm of new load − new CG), or use the general formula new CG = (old moment ± m × arm)/(old mass ± m) and solve for m (or arm)."],["031.05.03.01:01","Moving the CG: fill in the gaps → Example: M = 2 000 kg, CG 2.40 m, required 2.45 m, ballast at 4.40 m → m = 2 000 × 0.05/(4.40 − 2.45) = ____.","Example: M = 2 000 kg, CG 2.40 m, required 2.45 m, ballast at 4.40 m → m = 2 000 × 0.05/(4.40 − 2.45) = 51.3 kg."],["031.05.03.01:03","Moving the CG: fill in the gaps → ____: compare the actual loading with the ____ ____ (compartment, ULD numbers, positions); visual inspection of ULDs; reverse loading (ULDs swapped fore/aft) shifts the CG — recompute: if the actual CG is outside limits → reload before departure.","Checking loading: compare the actual loading with the loading manifest/LIR (compartment, ULD numbers, positions); visual inspection of ULDs; reverse loading (ULDs swapped fore/aft) shifts the CG — recompute: if the actual CG is outside limits → reload before departure."],["031.05.02.04:02","EASA LO 031.05.02.04.02 → Check if the zero fuel mass CG or index is within the limits.","Trim sheet: start at DOI → add index changes for cargo per compartment and pax per zone (graphical lines or tables) → ZFM index → check in the ZFM envelope → add fuel index (from the fuel index correction table, non-linear due to tank geometry) → TOM index → %MAC → check the TOM envelope → read stabiliser trim units for take-off from %MAC (and flap/thrust)."],["031.05.02.04:05","EASA LO 031.05.02.04.05 → Determine ‘stabiliser trim units’ for take-off.","Trim sheet: start at DOI → add index changes for cargo per compartment and pax per zone (graphical lines or tables) → ZFM index → check in the ZFM envelope → add fuel index (from the fuel index correction table, non-linear due to tank geometry) → TOM index → %MAC → check the TOM envelope → read stabiliser trim units for take-off from %MAC (and flap/thrust). / Load sheet: documents the masses (DOM, traffic load, fuel, ZFM, TOM, LM) and checks them against limits; signed by the commander before departure. Trim sheet: determines the CG/index at ZFM, TOM (and LM) and checks it against the CG envelope; gives take-off stab trim."]],"79":[["040.03.04.01:01","Avoiding & managing errors — cockpit management: fill in the gaps → ____: monitor your ____ performance (fatigue, stress, overload, incapacitation, errors) before and during flight — the crew is a mutual safety barrier; unrecognised degradation leads to undetected errors.","Safety awareness: monitor your own and your colleague's performance (fatigue, stress, overload, incapacitation, errors) before and during flight — the crew is a mutual safety barrier; unrecognised degradation leads to undetected errors."],["040.03.04.02:09","Avoiding & managing errors — cockpit management: fill in the gaps → ____: objectives = redundancy, cross-checking, workload sharing, shared SA. Elements: ____, ____, checklists, briefings, standard callouts, monitoring, CRM. SOPs give predictability and make deviations visible (TEM); threats: old habits/motor programmes after conversion, company culture, complacency.","Multi-crew concept: objectives = redundancy, cross-checking, workload sharing, shared SA. Elements: PF/PM roles, SOPs, checklists, briefings, standard callouts, monitoring, CRM. SOPs give predictability and make deviations visible (TEM); threats: old habits/motor programmes after conversion, company culture, complacency."],["040.03.04.01:01","Avoiding & managing errors — cockpit management: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (external: weather, ATC, terrain, aircraft malfunction, time pressure) → ____ (crew actions/inactions) → undesired aircraft states (UAS: speed/altitude deviations, wrong configuration). Countermeasures: planning (briefings), execution (monitoring, cross-check, workload management), review (evaluate, modify plans).","Threat and error management (TEM): threats (external: weather, ATC, terrain, aircraft malfunction, time pressure) → errors (crew actions/inactions) → undesired aircraft states (UAS: speed/altitude deviations, wrong configuration). Countermeasures: planning (briefings), execution (monitoring, cross-check, workload management), review (evaluate, modify plans)."],["040.03.04.03:15","Avoiding & managing errors — cockpit management: fill in the gaps → ____: group vs coaction; synergy, cohesion, ____; roles & norms; persuasion, conformity, compliance, obedience; status vs role; leadership/followership; ____ (steep → autocratic, flat → laissez-faire, ideal synergistic).","Cooperation: group vs coaction; synergy, cohesion, groupthink; roles & norms; persuasion, conformity, compliance, obedience; status vs role; leadership/followership; authority gradient (steep → autocratic, flat → laissez-faire, ideal synergistic)."],["040.03.04.04:03","Avoiding & managing errors — cockpit management: fill in the gaps → ____: sender–message–channel–receiver–feedback; two-way > one-way; non-verbal; ____; professional language/sterile cockpit; obstacles; intra-vs interpersonal conflict, escalation, ____ advocacy; limits at high workload.","Communication: sender–message–channel–receiver–feedback; two-way > one-way; non-verbal; explicit vs implicit; professional language/sterile cockpit; obstacles; intra-vs interpersonal conflict, escalation, inquiry – active listening – advocacy; limits at high workload."],["040.03.07.01:01","Advanced cockpit automation: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ — the ____ authority (can override automation, soft limits, back-driven controls); Airbus — automation protects the envelope and can correct erroneous pilot action (hard protections in normal law).","Two philosophies: Boeing — the pilot remains the final authority (can override automation, soft limits, back-driven controls); Airbus — automation protects the envelope and can correct erroneous pilot action (hard protections in normal law)."],["040.03.07.01:02","Advanced cockpit automation: fill in the gaps → ____: no ____ in unknown/unanticipated situations, no ____ regarding safety (does exactly what it is told, even when wrong).","Fundamental restrictions of autoflight: no creativity in unknown/unanticipated situations, no personal motivation regarding safety (does exactly what it is told, even when wrong)."],["040.03.07.01:03","Advanced cockpit automation: fill in the gaps → ____: pilot — creativity, flexibility, judgement, handles novelty, but fatigue, variability, limited precision/endurance; autopilot — precision, endurance, consistency, fast, but rigid, dependent on correct inputs/sensors, no understanding.","Pilot vs autopilot: pilot — creativity, flexibility, judgement, handles novelty, but fatigue, variability, limited precision/endurance; autopilot — precision, endurance, consistency, fast, but rigid, dependent on correct inputs/sensors, no understanding."],["040.03.07.01:04","Advanced cockpit automation: fill in the gaps → ____ (Bainbridge): designers' errors/wrong interpretation of data; the tasks left to the pilot are those too complex to automate (the hardest ones!); ____ ____ through disuse. Countermeasure: regular manual flying/training flights, simulator training.","Ironies of automation (Bainbridge): designers' errors/wrong interpretation of data; the tasks left to the pilot are those too complex to automate (the hardest ones!); loss of manual and cognitive skills through disuse. Countermeasure: regular manual flying/training flights, simulator training."],["040.03.07.01:05","Advanced cockpit automation: fill in the gaps → ____: loss of manual flying skills (→ hand-fly when appropriate), ____ (→ program early, in low-workload phases, one pilot flies), ____ during programming (→ cross-check inputs, \"verify-execute\"), hypovigilance in cruise (→ active monitoring, callouts, planning).","Drawbacks & remedies: loss of manual flying skills (→ hand-fly when appropriate), extra workload through programming (→ program early, in low-workload phases, one pilot flies), slips during programming (→ cross-check inputs, \"verify-execute\"), hypovigilance in cruise (→ active monitoring, callouts, planning)."],["040.03.07.02:01","Advanced cockpit automation: fill in the gaps → ____ = ____ ____, assuming everything is fine. Monitoring weaknesses: hypovigilance, loss of flying skills.","Automation complacency = overconfidence in / reduced vigilance towards automated systems, assuming everything is fine. Monitoring weaknesses: hypovigilance, loss of flying skills."],["040.03.07.02:02","Advanced cockpit automation: fill in the gaps → Error terms: ____ (looking without processing), ____ (fixation on one display/task), ____ (\"what's it doing now?\"), mode awareness loss (wrong mode active). Call-outs (FMA changes, altitude captures, deviations) force active monitoring and keep both pilots in the loop.","Error terms: passive monitoring (looking without processing), blinkered concentration (fixation on one display/task), confusion (\"what's it doing now?\"), mode awareness loss (wrong mode active). Call-outs (FMA changes, altitude captures, deviations) force active monitoring and keep both pilots in the loop."],["040.03.07.03:01","Advanced cockpit automation: fill in the gaps → ____: reduced awareness of ____ ____ aircraft status and position — each pilot may \"talk to the computer\" instead of each other.","Effects on crew communication: reduced awareness of input errors, flight modes, failure detection and comprehension, aircraft status and position — each pilot may \"talk to the computer\" instead of each other."],["040.03.07.03:02","Advanced cockpit automation: fill in the gaps → ____: when comprehension is lost or workload is high, ____: ____ CHG) → manual flying (AP off, FD off/AT off) → regain the picture. Normal ops: automation reduces workload, but maintain manual skills;","Use the right level of automation: when comprehension is lost or workload is high, degrade: LNAV/VNAV → HDG/ALT (V/S, LVL CHG) → manual flying (AP off, FD off/AT off) → regain the picture. Normal ops: automation reduces workload, but maintain manual skills;"],["040.03.07.03:03","Advanced cockpit automation: fill in the gaps → ____ (ATC vectors, late runway change): selected modes/manual may be quicker than reprogramming; ____: use automation to free capacity for problem solving, but revert to manual if automation behaves unexpectedly.","frequent profile changes (ATC vectors, late runway change): selected modes/manual may be quicker than reprogramming; abnormal situations: use automation to free capacity for problem solving, but revert to manual if automation behaves unexpectedly."],["040.03.04.01:01","General review — 040 Human Performance in one glance – Human factors: what are the key facts?","SHELL model (Software, Hardware, Environment, Liveware), Reason's Swiss cheese, safety culture (just, reporting, learning, flexible, informed)"],["040.03.04.01:01","General review — 040 Human Performance in one glance – Atmosphere & hypoxia: what are the key facts?","O₂ always 21 %; thresholds 7 000 / 10–12 000 / 22 000 ft; TUC FL 300 1–2 min, FL 350 30–60 s; 100 % O₂ at 40 000 ft ≈ air at 10 000 ft"],["040.03.04.01:01","General review — 040 Human Performance in one glance – Hyperventilation: what are the key facts?","CO₂ ↓, tingling, dizziness; slow breathing; doubt → O₂"],["040.03.04.01:01","General review — 040 Human Performance in one glance – DCS & diving: what are the key facts?","bends/creeps/chokes/staggers; 12 h / 24 h after diving"],["040.03.04.01:01","General review — 040 Human Performance in one glance – Vision: what are the key facts?","cones (colour, fovea, day) vs rods (night, periphery); blind spot; night adaptation ~30 min; empty-field myopia; scan technique; illusions (black hole, runway slope/width, rain on windscreen)"],["040.03.04.01:01","General review — 040 Human Performance in one glance – Hearing: what are the key facts?","outer/middle/inner ear; NIHL > 85–90 dB; ear block in descent (Valsalva), presbycusis"],["040.03.04.01:01","General review — 040 Human Performance in one glance – Equilibrium: what are the key facts?","semicircular canals (angular), otoliths (linear/gravity); somatogravic (acceleration → pitch-up illusion), somatogyral (graveyard spiral), leans, Coriolis (head movement in a turn) — trust instruments"],["040.03.04.01:01","General review — 040 Human Performance in one glance – Health: what are the key facts?","sleep (REM/non-REM, circadian ~25 h, WOCL 02– 06), jet lag east harder; alcohol 0.2 ‰ / 8 h; medication (antihistamines!), BMI, nutrition, blood donation 24 h"],["040.03.04.01:01","General review — 040 Human Performance in one glance – Psychology: what are the key facts?","attention, perception, memory (5 ± 2), learning, Rasmussen, SA, error types, decision models (FOR- DEC), stress & Yerkes–Dodson, GAS"],["040.03.04.02:09","General review — 040 Human Performance in one glance – Behaviour: what are the key facts?","Eysenck stable extravert; hazardous attitudes + antidotes"],["040.03.07.02:04","General review — 040 Human Performance in one glance – CRM & automation: what are the key facts?","TEM, SOPs, authority gradient, communication, complacency, mode awareness, levels of automation"],["040.03.04.02:01","EASA LO 040.03.04.02.01 → Name the objectives of the multi-crew concept.","Multi-crew concept: objectives = redundancy, cross-checking, workload sharing, shared SA. Elements: PF/PM roles, SOPs, checklists, briefings, standard callouts, monitoring, CRM. SOPs give predictability and make deviations visible (TEM); threats: old habits/motor programmes after conversion, company culture, complacency."],["040.03.04.02:02","EASA LO 040.03.04.02.02 → State and explain the elements of multi-crew concepts.","Multi-crew concept: objectives = redundancy, cross-checking, workload sharing, shared SA. Elements: PF/PM roles, SOPs, checklists, briefings, standard callouts, monitoring, CRM. SOPs give predictability and make deviations visible (TEM); threats: old habits/motor programmes after conversion, company culture, complacency."],["040.03.04.02:03","EASA LO 040.03.04.02.03 → Describe the concepts of ‘standard operating procedures’ (SOPs), checklists and crew briefings.","Multi-crew concept: objectives = redundancy, cross-checking, workload sharing, shared SA. Elements: PF/PM roles, SOPs, checklists, briefings, standard callouts, monitoring, CRM. SOPs give predictability and make deviations visible (TEM); threats: old habits/motor programmes after conversion, company culture, complacency."],["040.03.04.02:08","EASA LO 040.03.04.02.08 → Explain how SOPs contribute to avoiding, reducing and managing threats and errors.","Multi-crew concept: objectives = redundancy, cross-checking, workload sharing, shared SA. Elements: PF/PM roles, SOPs, checklists, briefings, standard callouts, monitoring, CRM. SOPs give predictability and make deviations visible (TEM); threats: old habits/motor programmes after conversion, company culture, complacency."],["040.03.04.03:01","EASA LO 040.03.04.03.01 → Distinguish between cooperation and coaction.","Cooperation: group vs coaction; synergy, cohesion, groupthink; roles & norms; persuasion, conformity, compliance, obedience; status vs role; leadership/followership; authority gradient (steep → autocratic, flat → laissez-faire, ideal synergistic)."],["040.03.04.03:05","EASA LO 040.03.04.03.05 → Explain the term ‘synergy’.","Cooperation: group vs coaction; synergy, cohesion, groupthink; roles & norms; persuasion, conformity, compliance, obedience; status vs role; leadership/followership; authority gradient (steep → autocratic, flat → laissez-faire, ideal synergistic)."],["040.03.04.03:06","EASA LO 040.03.04.03.06 → Define the term ‘cohesion’.","Cooperation: group vs coaction; synergy, cohesion, groupthink; roles & norms; persuasion, conformity, compliance, obedience; status vs role; leadership/followership; authority gradient (steep → autocratic, flat → laissez-faire, ideal synergistic)."],["040.03.04.03:11","EASA LO 040.03.04.03.11 → Explain how behaviour can be affected by the following factors: — persuasion; — conformity; — compliance; — obedience.","Cooperation: group vs coaction; synergy, cohesion, groupthink; roles & norms; persuasion, conformity, compliance, obedience; status vs role; leadership/followership; authority gradient (steep → autocratic, flat → laissez-faire, ideal synergistic)."],["040.03.04.03:12","EASA LO 040.03.04.03.12 → Distinguish between status and role.","Cooperation: group vs coaction; synergy, cohesion, groupthink; roles & norms; persuasion, conformity, compliance, obedience; status vs role; leadership/followership; authority gradient (steep → autocratic, flat → laissez-faire, ideal synergistic)."],["040.03.04.03:14","EASA LO 040.03.04.03.14 → Explain the terms ‘leadership’ and ‘followership’.","Cooperation: group vs coaction; synergy, cohesion, groupthink; roles & norms; persuasion, conformity, compliance, obedience; status vs role; leadership/followership; authority gradient (steep → autocratic, flat → laissez-faire, ideal synergistic)."],["040.03.04.04:05","EASA LO 040.03.04.04.05 → Name the importance of non-verbal communication.","Communication: sender–message–channel–receiver–feedback; two-way > one-way; non-verbal; explicit vs implicit; professional language/sterile cockpit; obstacles; intra-vs interpersonal conflict, escalation, inquiry – active listening – advocacy; limits at high workload."],["040.03.04.04:06","EASA LO 040.03.04.04.06 → Describe the general aspects of non-verbal communication.","Communication: sender–message–channel–receiver–feedback; two-way > one-way; non-verbal; explicit vs implicit; professional language/sterile cockpit; obstacles; intra-vs interpersonal conflict, escalation, inquiry – active listening – advocacy; limits at high workload."],["040.03.04.04:07","EASA LO 040.03.04.04.07 → Describe the advantages/disadvantages of implicit and explicit communication.","Communication: sender–message–channel–receiver–feedback; two-way > one-way; non-verbal; explicit vs implicit; professional language/sterile cockpit; obstacles; intra-vs interpersonal conflict, escalation, inquiry – active listening – advocacy; limits at high workload."],["040.03.04.04:08","EASA LO 040.03.04.04.08 → Describe the advantages and possible problems of using ‘social’ and ‘professional’ language in high-and low-workload situations.","Communication: sender–message–channel–receiver–feedback; two-way > one-way; non-verbal; explicit vs implicit; professional language/sterile cockpit; obstacles; intra-vs interpersonal conflict, escalation, inquiry – active listening – advocacy; limits at high workload."],["040.03.04.04:10","EASA LO 040.03.04.04.10 → Explain the difference between intrapersonal and interpersonal conflict.","Communication: sender–message–channel–receiver–feedback; two-way > one-way; non-verbal; explicit vs implicit; professional language/sterile cockpit; obstacles; intra-vs interpersonal conflict, escalation, inquiry – active listening – advocacy; limits at high workload."],["040.03.04.04:11","EASA LO 040.03.04.04.11 → Describe the escalation process in human conflict.","Communication: sender–message–channel–receiver–feedback; two-way > one-way; non-verbal; explicit vs implicit; professional language/sterile cockpit; obstacles; intra-vs interpersonal conflict, escalation, inquiry – active listening – advocacy; limits at high workload."],["040.03.04.04:13","EASA LO 040.03.04.04.13 → Explain the following terms as part of the communication practice with regard to preventing or resolving conflicts: — inquiry; — active listening; — advocacy;","Communication: sender–message–channel–receiver–feedback; two-way > one-way; non-verbal; explicit vs implicit; professional language/sterile cockpit; obstacles; intra-vs interpersonal conflict, escalation, inquiry – active listening – advocacy; limits at high workload."],["040.03.04.04:14","EASA LO 040.03.04.04.14 → Describe the limitations of communication in situations of high workload in the flight crew compartment in view of listening, verbal, non-verbal and visual effects.","Communication: sender–message–channel–receiver–feedback; two-way > one-way; non-verbal; explicit vs implicit; professional language/sterile cockpit; obstacles; intra-vs interpersonal conflict, escalation, inquiry – active listening – advocacy; limits at high workload."],["040.03.07.02:03","EASA LO 040.03.07.02.03 → Explain how the method of call-outs counteracts ineffective monitoring of automatic systems.","Error terms: passive monitoring (looking without processing), blinkered concentration (fixation on one display/task), confusion (\"what's it doing now?\"), mode awareness loss (wrong mode active). Call-outs (FMA changes, altitude captures, deviations) force active monitoring and keep both pilots in the loop."]],"80":[["061.02.02.01:01","Map reading & visual navigation: fill in the gaps → ____ = identifying ground features by comparing the chart with the terrain to determine and confirm position. ____ ____ = a prominent, unmistakable ground feature used to fix position.","Map reading = identifying ground features by comparing the chart with the terrain to determine and confirm position. Visual checkpoint = a prominent, unmistakable ground feature used to fix position."],["061.02.02.01:01","Map reading & visual navigation: fill in the gaps → Good checkpoints: ____ from the air, visible from a distance, not easily confused — e.g. ____ (coastline, motorway, railway, river, large power line) combined with ____ (town with distinctive shape, lake, bridge/junction, dam, mountain peak, airfield, tall mast). Avoid small villages/roads in areas with many similar ones.","Good checkpoints: unique and easily identifiable from the air, visible from a distance, not easily confused — e.g. line features (coastline, motorway, railway, river, large power line) combined with point features (town with distinctive shape, lake, bridge/junction, dam, mountain peak, airfield, tall mast). Avoid small villages/roads in areas with many similar ones."],["061.02.02.01:04","Map reading & visual navigation: fill in the gaps → Comparing ____ reveals errors in wind/heading/GS → correct heading and ETA → better accuracy for the rest of the flight.","Comparing DR positions with actual fixes reveals errors in wind/heading/GS → correct heading and ETA → better accuracy for the rest of the flight."],["061.02.02.01:06","Map reading & visual navigation: fill in the gaps → ____ = intersection of two or more ____ (e.g. a railway line and a river, bearings from two landmarks). A ____ line (e.g. crossing a motorway ⊥ to track) checks progress/GS (timing); a line parallel to track checks track keeping.","Fix = intersection of two or more position lines (e.g. a railway line and a river, bearings from two landmarks). A single position line (e.g. crossing a motorway ⊥ to track) checks progress/GS (timing); a line parallel to track checks track keeping."],["061.02.02.01:07","Map reading & visual navigation: fill in the gaps → ____: draw track lines, 10-NM marks/time marks, headings, checkpoints, drift lines (±10°), MSA/terrain, airspace, frequencies; fold the chart for the route; in flight ____ ____ so the track points up (align with the direction of flight/heading) — \"chart to ground\".","Preparation: draw track lines, 10-NM marks/time marks, headings, checkpoints, drift lines (±10°), MSA/terrain, airspace, frequencies; fold the chart for the route; in flight orient the chart so the track points up (align with the direction of flight/heading) — \"chart to ground\"."],["061.02.02.01:01","Map reading & visual navigation: fill in the gaps → ____: fly accurate ____ (DR); from the DR position predict what should appear (\"chart to ground\" for anticipation, \"ground to chart\" to confirm); identify features by shape, size, relationships; at each check revise ETA (GS from time between checks) and heading (track error with the ____).","Technique: fly accurate heading and time (DR); from the DR position predict what should appear (\"chart to ground\" for anticipation, \"ground to chart\" to confirm); identify features by shape, size, relationships; at each check revise ETA (GS from time between checks) and heading (track error with the 1-in-60 rule)."],["061.02.02.01:01","Map reading & visual navigation: fill in the gaps → ____; ____; ____ correction = TE + CA (to reach the next point) or double the TE (to regain track in the same distance flown).","Track error (°) = distance off track × 60 / distance flown; closing angle = off × 60 / distance to go; heading correction = TE + CA (to reach the next point) or double the TE (to regain track in the same distance flown)."],["061.02.02.01:01","Map reading & visual navigation: fill in the gaps → Example: 3 NM right of track after 30 NM, 45 NM to go → TE 6°, CA 4° → turn ____ to reach the destination.","Example: 3 NM right of track after 30 NM, 45 NM to go → TE 6°, CA 4° → turn 10° left to reach the destination."],["061.02.02.01:09","Map reading & visual navigation: fill in the gaps → ____: turn at the ____ time on the planned heading, then confirm position with the next features.","No checkpoint at a turning point: turn at the DR (ETA) time on the planned heading, then confirm position with the next features."],["061.02.02.01:01","Map reading & visual navigation: fill in the gaps → ____: featureless terrain (deserts, forests, snow, sea), areas with many similar features (dense villages/lakes), mountains (obscured, changed perspective), ____ ____, poor visibility, night.","Difficult areas: featureless terrain (deserts, forests, snow, sea), areas with many similar features (dense villages/lakes), mountains (obscured, changed perspective), inaccurate or outdated charts, poor visibility, night."],["061.02.02.01:12","Map reading & visual navigation: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ join points of equal elevation (spacing shows slope: close = steep); ____ (colour gradient from green/low to brown/high) gives quick visual impression of relief; also spot heights, hachuring/relief shading. From contours you can predict the shape of a hill, valley, ridge, saddle.","Topography: contour lines join points of equal elevation (spacing shows slope: close = steep); layer tinting (colour gradient from green/low to brown/high) gives quick visual impression of relief; also spot heights, hachuring/relief shading. From contours you can predict the shape of a hill, valley, ridge, saddle."],["061.02.02.02:01","Unplanned events: fill in the gaps → ____: decide early; mark the new track (from a known position/landmark), estimate heading (drift from current WCA), distance and time/fuel with mental DR (1-in-60, clock code), check terrain/airspace/weather, inform ATC, then refine.","Diversion: decide early; mark the new track (from a known position/landmark), estimate heading (drift from current WCA), distance and time/fuel with mental DR (1-in-60, clock code), check terrain/airspace/weather, inform ATC, then refine."],["061.02.02.01:06","Unplanned events: fill in the gaps → ____: continue on heading (don't wander), check DR position (time × GS along the track line), identify the \"circle of uncertainty\", look for line features, use radio aids.","Unsure of position: continue on heading (don't wander), check DR position (time × GS along the track line), identify the \"circle of uncertainty\", look for line features, use radio aids."],["061.02.02.02:01","Unplanned events: fill in the gaps → ____: ____limb (better view/radio range, terrain clearance), ____ommunicate (ATC, 121.5, D&D), Confess, Comply, Conserve fuel; use radio aids/GPS, fly to a line feature (coast, motorway) and follow it; if fuel/daylight run short → precautionary landing before it's too late.","Lost: Climb (better view/radio range, terrain clearance), Communicate (ATC, 121.5, D&D), Confess, Comply, Conserve fuel; use radio aids/GPS, fly to a line feature (coast, motorway) and follow it; if fuel/daylight run short → precautionary landing before it's too late."],["061.02.02.02:01","Exam trap (Unplanned events): complete the fact → ____in-____— track error uses distance flown, closing angle uses distance to go.","1-in-60 — track error uses distance flown, closing angle uses distance to go."]],"81":[["022.04.03.01:05","Attitude indicator: fill in the gaps → Purpose: shows ____ attitude relative to the (artificial) horizon — primary instrument for instrument flying.","Purpose: shows pitch and bank attitude relative to the (artificial) horizon — primary instrument for instrument flying."],["022.04.03.01:02","Attitude indicator: fill in the gaps → Types: ____ (artificial horizon) and ____ ____ = AI + flight director command bars (and often ILS deviations, rate of turn, slip).","Types: attitude indicator (artificial horizon) and ADI (attitude director indicator) = AI + flight director command bars (and often ILS deviations, rate of turn, slip)."],["022.04.03.01:03","Attitude indicator: fill in the gaps → Gyro: ____ with a ____, ____ degrees of freedom (tied to the vertical by an erection system: pendulous vanes (air) or mercury/torque motors (electric)).","Gyro: earth (vertical) gyro with a vertical spin axis, 2 degrees of freedom (tied to the vertical by an erection system: pendulous vanes (air) or mercury/torque motors (electric))."],["022.04.03.01:01","Attitude indicator: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (air-driven: the pendulous vanes are affected → on acceleration the AI indicates ____ right (turn-right illusion); deceleration: pitch down, bank left);","Errors: acceleration error (air-driven: the pendulous vanes are affected → on acceleration the AI indicates pitch up and bank right (turn-right illusion); deceleration: pitch down, bank left);"],["022.04.03.01:01","Attitude indicator: fill in the gaps → ____ (erection system senses the resultant of gravity and centrifugal force → after a 360° turn small pitch/bank errors; max after 180°); electric gyros have ____ in turns/accelerations to reduce them. Also topple limits (older: ±60° bank / ±85° pitch).","turning errors (erection system senses the resultant of gravity and centrifugal force → after a 360° turn small pitch/bank errors; max after 180°); electric gyros have erection cut-out in turns/accelerations to reduce them. Also topple limits (older: ±60° bank / ±85° pitch)."],["022.04.03.01:01","Attitude indicator: fill in the gaps → ____: sky (blue) / ground (brown), ____, ____ symbol, bank scale (marks 10°, 20°, 30°, 45°, 60°) with sky pointer/index, pitch ladder (2.5°/5°/10°), slip indicator (EFIS).","Display: sky (blue) / ground (brown), horizon bar, aircraft symbol, bank scale (marks 10°, 20°, 30°, 45°, 60°) with sky pointer/index, pitch ladder (2.5°/5°/10°), slip indicator (EFIS)."],["022.04.04.01:01","Directional gyro (DG/DI): fill in the gaps → Purpose: stable ____ without the compass's turning/acceleration errors; must be ____ ____.","Purpose: stable heading reference without the compass's turning/acceleration errors; must be aligned with the magnetic compass."],["022.04.04.01:02","Directional gyro (DG/DI): fill in the gaps → Types: ____ and ____ (heading + course deviation + glideslope display).","Types: direction indicator (DI) and HSI (heading + course deviation + glideslope display)."],["022.04.03.01:03","Directional gyro (DG/DI): fill in the gaps → Gyro: ____, 2 degrees of freedom (tied to the horizontal plane).","Gyro: horizontal spin axis, 2 degrees of freedom (tied to the horizontal plane)."],["022.04.04.01:03","Directional gyro (DG/DI): fill in the gaps → ____ due to: ____ (apparent wander = ____; at the pole 15°/h, zero at the equator; compensated by a latitude nut for one latitude), aircraft manoeuvring (gimbal errors in bank/pitch, friction — real wander), movement over the Earth (transport wander — heading reference changes as meridians converge).","Drift due to: Earth rotation (apparent wander = 15°/h × sin latitude; at the pole 15°/h, zero at the equator; compensated by a latitude nut for one latitude), aircraft manoeuvring (gimbal errors in bank/pitch, friction — real wander), movement over the Earth (transport wander — heading reference changes as meridians converge)."],["022.04.04.01:04","Directional gyro (DG/DI): fill in the gaps → Alignment procedure: in ____ (compass settled), read the magnetic compass (apply deviation card), ____ and set the DI heading; check/reset about ____.","Alignment procedure: in straight, level, unaccelerated flight (compass settled), read the magnetic compass (apply deviation card), cage/push the setting knob and set the DI heading; check/reset about every 15 minutes."],["022.04.05.01:01","Remote-reading compass: fill in the gaps → Principle: a ____, mounted in the wingtip/tail away from aircraft magnetic fields, senses the ____; this signal continuously ____ a directional gyro → display combines gyro stability with magnetic north-seeking.","Principle: a flux valve (flux detector), mounted in the wingtip/tail away from aircraft magnetic fields, senses the direction of the Earth's horizontal field; this signal continuously slaves (corrects) a directional gyro → display combines gyro stability with magnetic north-seeking."],["022.04.05.01:02","Remote-reading compass: fill in the gaps → Components: ____ (flux valve: three-spoked soft-iron core with excitation coil (AC 400 Hz) and pick-off coils → output ∝ earth field direction), ____ (DG), ____, precession (slaving) amplifier (drives a torque motor to precess the gyro towards magnetic heading at a slow rate, e.g. 2–5°/min), annunciator (shows slaving is taking place — dot/cross oscillating slightly), display unit (compass card/RMI/HSI, synchronising/set-heading knob for fast alignment, DG/COMPASS (SLAVE/FREE) switch → DG mode in high latitudes or faulty flux valve).","Components: flux detection unit (flux valve: three-spoked soft-iron core with excitation coil (AC 400 Hz) and pick-off coils → output ∝ earth field direction), gyro unit (DG), transducers/synchros, precession (slaving) amplifier (drives a torque motor to precess the gyro towards magnetic heading at a slow rate, e.g. 2–5°/min), annunciator (shows slaving is taking place — dot/cross oscillating slightly), display unit (compass card/RMI/HSI, synchronising/set-heading knob for fast alignment, DG/COMPASS (SLAVE/FREE) switch → DG mode in high latitudes or faulty flux valve)."],["022.04.05.01:03","Remote-reading compass: fill in the gaps → ____ vs direct-reading compass: much reduced ____ ____, less deviation (flux valve in a magnetically quiet location), stable indication, can feed many users (RMI, HSI, autopilot, radar, FMS), gyro-stabilised.","Advantages vs direct-reading compass: much reduced turning & acceleration errors, less deviation (flux valve in a magnetically quiet location), stable indication, can feed many users (RMI, HSI, autopilot, radar, FMS), gyro-stabilised."],["022.04.05.01:01","Remote-reading compass: fill in the gaps → ____: complex, heavy, expensive, needs ____ ____ (fails with power loss), still subject to deviation/variation errors, slaving errors during prolonged turns/accelerations (slaving cut-out).","Disadvantages: complex, heavy, expensive, needs electrical power (fails with power loss), still subject to deviation/variation errors, slaving errors during prolonged turns/accelerations (slaving cut-out)."],["022.04.05.01:01","Exam trap (Remote-reading compass): complete the fact → DG apparent wander = ____° × sin lat per hour.","DG apparent wander = 15° × sin lat per hour."],["022.04.03.01:01","EASA LO 022.04.03.01.01 → Explain the purpose of the attitude indicator.","Purpose: shows pitch and bank attitude relative to the (artificial) horizon — primary instrument for instrument flying. / Types: direction indicator (DI) and HSI (heading + course deviation + glideslope display)."],["022.04.03.01:04","EASA LO 022.04.03.01.04 → Describe the effects of the aircraft’s acceleration and turns on instrument indications.","Purpose: shows pitch and bank attitude relative to the (artificial) horizon — primary instrument for instrument flying."]],"82":[["061.04.03.01:01",": what do you remember about this? → Review stage — practical chart use. Theory of projections: see …","Review stage — practical chart use. Theory of projections: see #77."],["061.04.03.01:01","Annex 4 symbology – Hexagon / hexagon in square / hexagon with lobes: what are the key facts?","VOR / VOR-DME / VORTAC"],["061.04.03.01:01","Annex 4 symbology – Square: what are the key facts?","DME"],["061.04.03.01:01","Annex 4 symbology – Dotted circle (with centre): what are the key facts?","NDB"],["061.04.03.01:01","Annex 4 symbology – Filled triangle / open triangle: what are the key facts?","compulsory / on-request reporting point"],["061.04.03.01:01","Annex 4 symbology – Circle/runway pattern, filled vs open: what are the key facts?","aerodromes (civil, military, joint; hard runway shown)"],["061.04.03.01:01","Annex 4 symbology – Inverted V (with dot): what are the key facts?","obstacle; group of obstacles; lit obstacle (rays) — elevation AMSL, height AGL in brackets"],["061.04.03.01:01","Annex 4 symbology – Hatched/blue/magenta boundaries: what are the key facts?","airspace (CTR, TMA, class A–G), P/R/D areas (prohibited, restricted, danger) with identifier and vertical limits"],["061.04.03.01:01","Annex 4 symbology – Dashed magenta lines: what are the key facts?","isogonals (lines of equal variation)"],["061.04.03.01:01","Annex 4 symbology – Large numbers in squares: what are the key facts?","MEF / grid MORA (e.g. 4 5 = 4 500 ft)"],["061.04.03.01:01","Annex 4 symbology – Airway box: what are the key facts?","designator, MEA/MOCA, magnetic track, distance, FL limits"],["061.04.03.02:01","Plotting — tracks & distances: fill in the gaps → ____: draw the line, measure the true track with the protractor at the ____ (Lambert: GC ≈ straight → the angle changes along the line), convert to ____ with the variation (isogonal) at mid-track: MT = TT − E / + W var. On IFR charts airway magnetic tracks are printed.","Track: draw the line, measure the true track with the protractor at the mid-meridian (Lambert: GC ≈ straight → the angle changes along the line), convert to magnetic with the variation (isogonal) at mid-track: MT = TT − E / + W var. On IFR charts airway magnetic tracks are printed."],["061.04.03.02:01","Plotting — tracks & distances: fill in the gaps → ____: use the chart ____ or the latitude scale on a meridian (____) close to the route (Lambert scale almost constant between standard parallels); dividers step off longer distances.","Distance: use the chart scale line or the latitude scale on a meridian (1′ of latitude = 1 NM) close to the route (Lambert scale almost constant between standard parallels); dividers step off longer distances."],["061.04.03.02:01","Plotting — tracks & distances: fill in the gaps → ____: from 47°30′N to 48°10′N along a meridian = 40′ = ____.","Worked check: from 47°30′N to 48°10′N along a meridian = 40′ = 40 NM."],["061.04.03.01:01","Position fixing with VOR/DME: fill in the gaps → ____ = ____ (QDR). Plot: convert to true with the ____ (TT = radial + E var / − W var) — many charts have compass roses aligned to magnetic north at the station, so plot directly with the rose.","VOR radial = magnetic bearing FROM the station (QDR). Plot: convert to true with the variation at the VOR (TT = radial + E var / − W var) — many charts have compass roses aligned to magnetic north at the station, so plot directly with the rose."],["061.04.03.02:02","Position fixing with VOR/DME: fill in the gaps → ____ gives ____ → at normal ranges ≈ ground distance (large error only when close/high: e.g. overhead at FL 360 the DME reads ~6 NM). Fix = radial line ∩ DME arc (or two VOR radials, or two DME arcs — ambiguous without a third).","DME gives slant range → at normal ranges ≈ ground distance (large error only when close/high: e.g. overhead at FL 360 the DME reads ~6 NM). Fix = radial line ∩ DME arc (or two VOR radials, or two DME arcs — ambiguous without a third)."],["061.04.03.02:02","Position fixing with VOR/DME: fill in the gaps → Example: ____, var 2° E at LUL → true bearing 247° from the station, measure 28 NM along that line → fix.","Example: R-245 LUL VOR / 28 DME, var 2° E at LUL → true bearing 247° from the station, measure 28 NM along that line → fix."],["061.04.03.01:01","Plotting NDB bearings: fill in the gaps → ADF gives ____ from the aircraft's nose. ____ (magnetic bearing TO the NDB) = MH + RB (subtract 360 if > 360). ____.","ADF gives relative bearing (RB) from the aircraft's nose. QDM (magnetic bearing TO the NDB) = MH + RB (subtract 360 if > 360). QDR/QDE (magnetic FROM) = QDM ± 180°."],["061.04.03.02:03","Plotting NDB bearings: fill in the gaps → For plotting: convert to ____ using the ____ (the bearing is measured at the aircraft): ____ station = QDM + var E / − var W; the reciprocal gives the QTE (true bearing from the station) — on a Lambert chart, plot from the NDB after correcting for chart convergence between the aircraft's and the station's meridian (≈ Δlong × n) if they are far apart; at short ranges this is negligible.","For plotting: convert to true using the variation at the aircraft (the bearing is measured at the aircraft): true bearing to station = QDM + var E / − var W; the reciprocal gives the QTE (true bearing from the station) — on a Lambert chart, plot from the NDB after correcting for chart convergence between the aircraft's and the station's meridian (≈ Δlong × n) if they are far apart; at short ranges this is negligible."],["061.04.03.02:03","Plotting NDB bearings: fill in the gaps → Example: MH 070°, RB 330° → QDM = 400 − 360 = ____; var 3° W → true 037° to the station → QTE = ____ → plot line 217° from the NDB.","Example: MH 070°, RB 330° → QDM = 400 − 360 = 040°; var 3° W → true 037° to the station → QTE = 217° → plot line 217° from the NDB."],["061.04.03.01:01","Plotting NDB bearings: fill in the gaps → ____ shows QDM directly (needle head) and QDR (tail).","Radio-magnetic indicator (RMI) shows QDM directly (needle head) and QDR (tail)."]],"83":[["062.06.01.01:02","GNSS overview: fill in the gaps → Four main GNSSs: ____ (USA), ____ (Russia), Galileo (EU), BeiDou (China). All use a constellation of satellites whose signals allow a suitably equipped receiver to determine ____.","Four main GNSSs: NAVSTAR GPS (USA), GLONASS (Russia), Galileo (EU), BeiDou (China). All use a constellation of satellites whose signals allow a suitably equipped receiver to determine 3D position, velocity and precise time."],["062.06.01.02:01","Operation (GPS): fill in the gaps → Services: ____ (standard positioning service, civil, on ____, originally deliberately less accurate — Selective Availability, switched off in 2000) and PPS (precise positioning service, authorised/military, ____).","Services: SPS (standard positioning service, civil, on L1, originally deliberately less accurate — Selective Availability, switched off in 2000) and PPS (precise positioning service, authorised/military, L1 + L2)."],["062.06.01.02:12","Operation (GPS): fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (≈ 24+ satellites (31 active), 6 orbital planes, 55° inclination, ~____ altitude, period ~12 h → ≥ 4 visible anywhere), control (master control station (Colorado), ground antennas (uplink), monitoring stations) — monitors constellation status, computes and uploads orbit (ephemeris) and clock corrections, manoeuvres satellites; user (receivers).","Segments: space (≈ 24+ satellites (31 active), 6 orbital planes, 55° inclination, ~20 200 km altitude, period ~12 h → ≥ 4 visible anywhere), control (master control station (Colorado), ground antennas (uplink), monitoring stations) — monitors constellation status, computes and uploads orbit (ephemeris) and clock corrections, manoeuvres satellites; user (receivers)."],["062.06.01.02:10","Operation (GPS): fill in the gaps → Signals: two UHF frequencies ____ and ____ ____ (L5 new). On L1: C/A code (coarse/acquisition — civil) and P code (precision, encrypted P(Y) — not used by SPS).","Signals: two UHF frequencies L1 (1 575.42 MHz) and L2 (1 227.6 MHz) (L5 new). On L1: C/A code (coarse/acquisition — civil) and P code (precision, encrypted P(Y) — not used by SPS)."],["062.06.01.02:09","Operation (GPS): fill in the gaps → Coded signal (PRN) used for ____ ____, plus the ____: satellite clock corrections, ephemeris, almanac, UTC parameters, ionospheric model (to estimate signal delay through the ionosphere), satellite health.","Coded signal (PRN) used for ranging and satellite identification, plus the navigation message: satellite clock corrections, ephemeris, almanac, UTC parameters, ionospheric model (to estimate signal delay through the ionosphere), satellite health."],["062.06.01.02:11","Operation (GPS): fill in the gaps → ____ (caesium/rubidium) in each satellite → very accurate time reference.","Atomic clocks (caesium/rubidium) in each satellite → very accurate time reference."],["062.06.01.02:17","Operation (GPS): fill in the gaps → ____: receiver measures ____ ____ × speed of light = range. Because the cheap receiver clock is not synchronised, the first result is a pseudo-range. Each range defines a sphere around the satellite.","Ranging: receiver measures time difference between transmission and reception × speed of light = range. Because the cheap receiver clock is not synchronised, the first result is a pseudo-range. Each range defines a sphere around the satellite."],["062.06.01.02:18","Operation (GPS): fill in the gaps → ____ → ____ needed for a 3D fix (3 + altitude aiding for 2D). With 4 satellites the receiver synchronises to GPS time.","Four unknowns (x, y, z and receiver clock error Δt) → 4 satellites needed for a 3D fix (3 + altitude aiding for 2D). With 4 satellites the receiver synchronises to GPS time."],["062.06.01.02:04","Operation (GPS): fill in the gaps → ____ from the ____ of the satellite signals (and position change).","Ground speed/velocity from the Doppler shift of the satellite signals (and position change)."],["062.06.01.02:21","Operation (GPS): fill in the gaps → ____ (receiver autonomous integrity monitoring): the receiver checks the consistency of redundant range measurements → ____ needed to detect a faulty satellite; ____ to exclude it (FDE, fault detection and exclusion). Pre-flight RAIM prediction required for GNSS approaches.","RAIM (receiver autonomous integrity monitoring): the receiver checks the consistency of redundant range measurements → 5 satellites needed to detect a faulty satellite; 6 to exclude it (FDE, fault detection and exclusion). Pre-flight RAIM prediction required for GNSS approaches."],["062.06.01.02:25","Operation (GPS): fill in the gaps → GPS and GLONASS have ____; systems use different ____ (GPS: ____, GLONASS: PZ-90), time scales, orbits and services — multi-constellation receivers convert.","GPS and GLONASS have interoperability agreements; systems use different reference frames (GPS: WGS-84, GLONASS: PZ-90), time scales, orbits and services — multi-constellation receivers convert."],["062.06.01.03:01","Errors: fill in the gaps → Factors: ____, tropospheric delay, ____ and ____ errors, multipath (reflections), receiver noise, dilution of precision.","Factors: ionospheric propagation delay (largest error), tropospheric delay, satellite clock and ephemeris (orbit) errors, multipath (reflections), receiver noise, dilution of precision."],["062.06.01.03:02","Errors: fill in the gaps → ____ (user equivalent range error) = combined range error from all these factors. ____.","UERE (user equivalent range error) = combined range error from all these factors. Position accuracy ≈ UERE × GDOP."],["062.06.01.03:03","Errors: fill in the gaps → ____ reduced by the ____ in the nav message (single frequency) or ____ frequencies (dual-frequency receivers compare delays on L1/L2).","Ionospheric delay reduced by the ionospheric model in the nav message (single frequency) or almost eliminated with two frequencies (dual-frequency receivers compare delays on L1/L2)."],["062.06.01.03:05","Errors: fill in the gaps → ____ from satellite ____: satellites widely spread = low DOP (good); clustered = high DOP (poor). (PDOP, HDOP, VDOP, TDOP components; vertical accuracy usually worse than horizontal.)","GDOP (geometric dilution of precision) from satellite geometry and number: satellites widely spread = low DOP (good); clustered = high DOP (poor). (PDOP, HDOP, VDOP, TDOP components; vertical accuracy usually worse than horizontal.)"],["062.06.01.03:07","Errors: fill in the gaps → ____ due to ____ and the ____ (corrected by control segment uploads). (Augmentation — ABAS/RAIM, SBAS e.g. EGNOS/WAAS, GBAS — improves accuracy and integrity.)","Orbit errors due to solar wind/radiation pressure and the gravitation of the Sun and Moon (corrected by control segment uploads). (Augmentation — ABAS/RAIM, SBAS e.g. EGNOS/WAAS, GBAS — improves accuracy and integrity.)"],["062.07.01.01:02","PBN principles: fill in the gaps → RNAV/RNP performance is defined by ____ ____ (and ____, functionality). PBN is needed to optimise airspace use (closer routes, curved/efficient paths, fewer ground aids). Crews and ATC must know the on-board system capability to check it meets the airspace navigation specification.","RNAV/RNP performance is defined by accuracy, integrity, continuity (and availability, functionality). PBN is needed to optimise airspace use (closer routes, curved/efficient paths, fewer ground aids). Crews and ATC must know the on-board system capability to check it meets the airspace navigation specification."],["062.07.01.01:06","PBN principles: fill in the gaps → ____ = conformance of the ____ ____ (95 % of flight time). Integrity = trust that can be placed in the correctness of the information, incl. the ability to provide timely and valid alerts.","Accuracy = conformance of the true position with the required position (95 % of flight time). Integrity = trust that can be placed in the correctness of the information, incl. the ability to provide timely and valid alerts."],["062.07.01.01:05","PBN principles: fill in the gaps → ____ = capability to perform the function ____ ____ during the intended operation. Availability = percentage of time (annually) during which the system is available for use.","Continuity = capability to perform the function without unscheduled interruptions during the intended operation. Availability = percentage of time (annually) during which the system is available for use."],["062.07.01.01:07","PBN principles: fill in the gaps → ____: PBN defines performance requirements; the aircraft may use GNSS, DME/DME, IRS, VOR/DME as long as the performance is met (unlike conventional navigation tied to specific beacons).","Not sensor-specific: PBN defines performance requirements; the aircraft may use GNSS, DME/DME, IRS, VOR/DME as long as the performance is met (unlike conventional navigation tied to specific beacons)."],["062.07.01.01:08","PBN principles: fill in the gaps → ____ = direct sensor information (VOR radial, DME distance, ILS deviation); ____ = processed by the RNAV/FMS computer (position, track, cross-track error) — errors in database/inputs are not visible in raw form → cross-check.","Raw data = direct sensor information (VOR radial, DME distance, ILS deviation); computed data = processed by the RNAV/FMS computer (position, track, cross-track error) — errors in database/inputs are not visible in raw form → cross-check."],["062.07.01.01:01","PBN principles: fill in the gaps → RNAV vs RNP: RNP specification requires on-board performance monitoring and alerting (OBPMA); RNAV does not. Designation shows lateral accuracy in NM (95 %): e.g. RNAV ____; ____ ____.","RNAV vs RNP: RNP specification requires on-board performance monitoring and alerting (OBPMA); RNAV does not. Designation shows lateral accuracy in NM (95 %): e.g. RNAV 10, RNAV 5 (B-RNAV), RNAV 1 (P-RNAV); RNP 4, RNP 2, RNP 1, Advanced RNP, RNP APCH (0.3), RNP AR APCH (≤ 0.3)."],["062.07.01.02:01","PBN components & scope: fill in the gaps → ____ ____ (routes/procedures in a given airspace).","PBN = navigation aid infrastructure + navigation specification + navigation application (routes/procedures in a given airspace)."],["062.07.01.03:01","PBN components & scope: fill in the gaps → ____: PBN is limited to ____ (linear lateral guidance along the path); vertical by barometric altitude.","Oceanic/remote, en-route and terminal: PBN is limited to lateral performance requirements (linear lateral guidance along the path); vertical by barometric altitude."],["062.07.01.03:02","PBN components & scope: fill in the gaps → ____: PBN accommodates ____ lateral guidance (____ APCH to LNAV, LNAV/VNAV minima: fixed lateral tolerance, e.g. ±0.3 NM) and angular lateral guidance (LPV with SBAS — sensitivity increases towards the runway like an ILS localiser). Vertical guidance by baro-VNAV (LNAV/VNAV) or SBAS (LPV).","Approach: PBN accommodates linear lateral guidance (RNP APCH to LNAV, LNAV/VNAV minima: fixed lateral tolerance, e.g. ±0.3 NM) and angular lateral guidance (LPV with SBAS — sensitivity increases towards the runway like an ILS localiser). Vertical guidance by baro-VNAV (LNAV/VNAV) or SBAS (LPV)."],["062.07.01.02:01","Exam trap (PBN components & scope): complete the fact → ____atellites for a ____D fix; 5 for RAIM detection, 6 for exclusion.","4 satellites for a 3D fix; 5 for RAIM detection, 6 for exclusion."],["062.06.01.01:01","EASA LO 062.06.01.01.01 → State that there are four main GNSSs. These are: — USA NAVigation System with Timing And Ranging Global Positioning System (NAVSTAR GPS); — Russian GLObal NAvigation Satellite System (GLONASS); — European Galileo (under construction); — Chinese BeiDou (under construction).","Four main GNSSs: NAVSTAR GPS (USA), GLONASS (Russia), Galileo (EU), BeiDou (China). All use a constellation of satellites whose signals allow a suitably equipped receiver to determine 3D position, velocity and precise time."],["062.06.01.02:02","EASA LO 062.06.01.02.02 → SPS was originally designed to provide civilian users with a less accurate positioning capability than PPS.","Services: SPS (standard positioning service, civil, on L1, originally deliberately less accurate — Selective Availability, switched off in 2000) and PPS (precise positioning service, authorised/military, L1 + L2)."],["062.06.01.02:03","EASA LO 062.06.01.02.03 → Name the three GNSS segments as follows: — space segment; — control segment; — user segment.","Orbit errors due to solar wind/radiation pressure and the gravitation of the Sun and Moon (corrected by control segment uploads). (Augmentation — ABAS/RAIM, SBAS e.g. EGNOS/WAAS, GBAS — improves accuracy and integrity.)"],["062.06.01.02:05","EASA LO 062.06.01.02.05 → State that SPS is a positioning and timing service provided on frequency L1.","Services: SPS (standard positioning service, civil, on L1, originally deliberately less accurate — Selective Availability, switched off in 2000) and PPS (precise positioning service, authorised/military, L1 + L2). / Ionospheric delay reduced by the ionospheric model in the nav message (single frequency) or almost eliminated with two frequencies (dual-frequency receivers compare delays on L1/L2)."],["062.06.01.02:06","EASA LO 062.06.01.02.06 → State that PPS uses both frequencies L1 and L2.","Ionospheric delay reduced by the ionospheric model in the nav message (single frequency) or almost eliminated with two frequencies (dual-frequency receivers compare delays on L1/L2). / Signals: two UHF frequencies L1 (1 575.42 MHz) and L2 (1 227.6 MHz) (L5 new). On L1: C/A code (coarse/acquisition — civil) and P code (precision, encrypted P(Y) — not used by SPS)."],["062.06.01.02:07","EASA LO 062.06.01.02.07 → State that the satellites transmit a coded signal used for ranging, identification","Coded signal (PRN) used for ranging and satellite identification, plus the navigation message: satellite clock corrections, ephemeris, almanac, UTC parameters, ionospheric model (to estimate signal delay through the ionosphere), satellite health."],["062.06.01.02:08","EASA LO 062.06.01.02.08 → State that the navigation message contains: — satellite clock correction parameters; — Universal Time Coordinated (UTC) parameters; — an ionospheric model; — satellite health data.","Coded signal (PRN) used for ranging and satellite identification, plus the navigation message: satellite clock corrections, ephemeris, almanac, UTC parameters, ionospheric model (to estimate signal delay through the ionosphere), satellite health."],["062.06.01.02:13","EASA LO 062.06.01.02.13 → State that the control segment provides: — monitoring of the constellation status;","Segments: space (≈ 24+ satellites (31 active), 6 orbital planes, 55° inclination, ~20 200 km altitude, period ~12 h → ≥ 4 visible anywhere), control (master control station (Colorado), ground antennas (uplink), monitoring stations) — monitors constellation status, computes and uploads orbit (ephemeris) and clock corrections, manoeuvres satellites; user (receivers)."],["062.06.01.02:14","EASA LO 062.06.01.02.14 → State that GNSS supplies three-dimensional position fixes and speed data, plus a precise time reference.","Ground speed/velocity from the Doppler shift of the satellite signals (and position change)."],["062.06.01.02:15","EASA LO 062.06.01.02.15 → State that a GNSS receiver is able to determine the distance to a satellite by determining the difference between the time of transmission by the satellite and the time of reception.","Ranging: receiver measures time difference between transmission and reception × speed of light = range. Because the cheap receiver clock is not synchronised, the first result is a pseudo-range. Each range defines a sphere around the satellite."],["062.06.01.02:16","EASA LO 062.06.01.02.16 → State that the initial distance calculated to the satellites is called pseudo-range because the difference between the GNSS receiver and the satellite time references initially creates an erroneous range.","Ranging: receiver measures time difference between transmission and reception × speed of light = range. Because the cheap receiver clock is not synchronised, the first result is a pseudo-range. Each range defines a sphere around the satellite."],["062.06.01.02:19","EASA LO 062.06.01.02.19 → State that the GNSS receiver is able to synchronise to the correct time reference when receiving four satellites.","Four unknowns (x, y, z and receiver clock error Δt) → 4 satellites needed for a 3D fix (3 + altitude aiding for 2D). With 4 satellites the receiver synchronises to GPS time."],["062.06.01.02:20","EASA LO 062.06.01.02.20 → State that the receiver is able to calculate aircraft ground speed using the space vehicle","Ground speed/velocity from the Doppler shift of the satellite signals (and position change)."],["062.06.01.02:22","EASA LO 062.06.01.02.22 → State that RAIM is achieved by consistency checks among range measurements.","RAIM (receiver autonomous integrity monitoring): the receiver checks the consistency of redundant range measurements → 5 satellites needed to detect a faulty satellite; 6 to exclude it (FDE, fault detection and exclusion). Pre-flight RAIM prediction required for GNSS approaches."],["062.06.01.02:24","EASA LO 062.06.01.02.24 → State that agreements have been concluded between the appropriate agencies for the compatibility and interoperability by any approved user of NAVSTAR and GLONASS systems.","GPS and GLONASS have interoperability agreements; systems use different reference frames (GPS: WGS-84, GLONASS: PZ-90), time scales, orbits and services — multi-constellation receivers convert."],["062.06.01.03:04","EASA LO 062.06.01.03.04 → State that ionospheric delay is the most significant error.","Factors: ionospheric propagation delay (largest error), tropospheric delay, satellite clock and ephemeris (orbit) errors, multipath (reflections), receiver noise, dilution of precision. / UERE (user equivalent range error) = combined range error from all these factors. Position accuracy ≈ UERE × GDOP."],["062.06.01.03:06","EASA LO 062.06.01.03.06 → State that the UERE in combination with the geometric dilution of precision (GDOP) allows for an estimation of position accuracy.","UERE (user equivalent range error) = combined range error from all these factors. Position accuracy ≈ UERE × GDOP. / Factors: ionospheric propagation delay (largest error), tropospheric delay, satellite clock and ephemeris (orbit) errors, multipath (reflections), receiver noise, dilution of precision."],["062.07.01.01:03","EASA LO 062.07.01.01.03 → State that it is necessary for flight crew and air traffic controllers to be aware of the on\u0002board RNAV or RNP system capabilities in order to determine whether the performance of the RNAV or RNP system is X X X 2, 3 Exa m appropriate for the specific airspace requirements.","RNAV vs RNP: RNP specification requires on-board performance monitoring and alerting (OBPMA); RNAV does not. Designation shows lateral accuracy in NM (95 %): e.g. RNAV 10, RNAV 5 (B-RNAV), RNAV 1 (P-RNAV); RNP 4, RNP 2, RNP 1, Advanced RNP, RNP APCH (0.3), RNP AR APCH (≤ 0.3). / RNAV/RNP performance is defined by accuracy, integrity, continuity (and availability, functionality). PBN is needed to optimise airspace use (closer routes, curved/efficient paths, fewer ground aids). Crews and ATC must know the on-board system capability to check it meets the airspace navigation specification."],["062.07.01.01:04","EASA LO 062.07.01.01.04 → Define accuracy as the conformance of the true position and the required position. X X X 2,","Accuracy = conformance of the true position with the required position (95 % of flight time). Integrity = trust that can be placed in the correctness of the information, incl. the ability to provide timely and valid alerts. / Ground speed/velocity from the Doppler shift of the satellite signals (and position change)."],["062.07.01.01:09","EASA LO 062.07.01.01.09 → Define availability as the percentage of time (annually) during which the system is available for use.","Continuity = capability to perform the function without unscheduled interruptions during the intended operation. Availability = percentage of time (annually) during which the system is available for use."]],"84":[["081.01.00.00",": what do you remember about this? → FINAL stage: wind shear (aerodynamic view) + general review …","FINAL stage: wind shear (aerodynamic view) + general review of"],["081.01.00.00",": what do you remember about this? → For the chapter cheat sheet see also …","For the chapter cheat sheet see also #73."],["081.01.00.00","Wind shear — the aerodynamics: fill in the gaps → The aircraft's ____ keeps its ground speed for a moment, so a sudden wind change is felt as an ____: ____ headwind / decreasing tailwind → IAS ↑ → lift ↑ → aircraft rises above the path (performance-increasing shear);","The aircraft's inertia keeps its ground speed for a moment, so a sudden wind change is felt as an airspeed change: increasing headwind / decreasing tailwind → IAS ↑ → lift ↑ → aircraft rises above the path (performance-increasing shear);"],["081.08.00.00","Wind shear — the aerodynamics: fill in the gaps → ____ ____ → IAS ↓ → lift ↓ → sink below the path (____ — dangerous near the ground). A downdraft additionally reduces α/flight path.","decreasing headwind / increasing tailwind → IAS ↓ → lift ↓ → sink below the path (performance-decreasing shear — dangerous near the ground). A downdraft additionally reduces α/flight path."],["081.02.00.00","Wind shear — the aerodynamics: fill in the gaps → ____: headwind first (IAS ↑, tempting to reduce thrust) → downdraft core → ____ (IAS ↓ sharply, high sink). Recovery: max (TO/GA) thrust, ____ / follow wind-shear guidance up to stick-shaker (use α margin, not speed), wings level, no configuration change, speedbrakes in; trade speed for height, don't chase airspeed.","Microburst on approach: headwind first (IAS ↑, tempting to reduce thrust) → downdraft core → tailwind (IAS ↓ sharply, high sink). Recovery: max (TO/GA) thrust, pitch towards ~15° / follow wind-shear guidance up to stick-shaker (use α margin, not speed), wings level, no configuration change, speedbrakes in; trade speed for height, don't chase airspeed."],["081.01.00.00","Wind shear — the aerodynamics: what do you remember about this? → Energy view: total energy = kinetic + potential; wind shear causes a …","Energy view: total energy = kinetic + potential; wind shear causes a rapid loss of airspeed energy → only thrust and giving up excess speed can maintain height."],["081.01.00.00","Rapid general review of 081 – Lift & drag: what are the key facts?","L = ½ρV²SCL; stall at critical α (~15–16°); VS ∝ √W, × √n in turns; forward CG → higher VS; induced drag ∝ 1/V², parasite ∝ V²; min drag at VMD (best L/D); high AR/winglets ↓ induced drag; ground effect ↓ induced drag"],["081.01.00.00","Rapid general review of 081 – High-lift: what are the key facts?","TE flaps: CLmax ↑, critical α ↓, drag ↑, nose-down pitch; LE slats: critical α ↑; spoilers reduce lift/brake"],["081.01.00.00","Rapid general review of 081 – Boundary layer & stall: what are the key facts?","turbulent BL resists separation; swept/tapered wings tip-stall → pitch-up; T-tail deep stall → stick pusher; stall warning ≥ 5 kt/5 % above VS"],["081.03.00.00","Rapid general review of 081: fill in the gaps → ____ M = TAS/a; a ∝ √T; Mcrit raised by sweep/thin aerofoil; above Mcrit: shock, wave drag, buffet, Mach tuck","High speed M = TAS/a; a ∝ √T; Mcrit raised by sweep/thin aerofoil; above Mcrit: shock, wave drag, buffet, Mach tuck"],["081.04.00.00","Rapid general review of 081 – Stability: what are the key facts?","static = initial tendency, dynamic = over time; CG ahead of neutral point; aft CG → less stable, lighter forces; dihedral/sweep/high wing → lateral stability; fin → directional; lateral ≫ directional → Dutch roll (yaw damper), directional ≫ lateral → spiral instability"],["081.05.00.00","Rapid general review of 081 – Control: what are the key facts?","adverse yaw (Frise, differential, spoilers); aileron reversal at high IAS; balance tab opposite, anti- balance same, servo/spring tabs; stab trim; FBW hard/soft protections"],["081.06.00.00","Rapid general review of 081 – Limitations: what are the key facts?","VA = VS√n (↓ with mass); CS-25 +2.5/−1; ultimate 1.5 × limit; gusts 66/50/25 ft/s at VB/VC/VD; VLO ≤ VLE; flutter → mass balance"],["081.07.00.00","Rapid general review of 081 – Propellers: what are the key facts?","blade α = blade angle − helix angle; constant speed coarsens with TAS; windmilling drag → feather; clockwise prop: torque/slipstream/P-factor → yaw left, left engine critical"],["081.08.00.00","Rapid general review of 081 – Flight mechanics: what are the key facts?","climb T = D + W sin γ, L = W cos γ; glide tan γ = 1/(L/D); turn tan Ø = V²/(gR), n = 1/cos Ø, rate-1 = 3°/s; VMC: 5° bank, aft CG ↑, altitude ↓"],["081.01.00.00","Typical numbers to know: fill in the gaps → Load factor at ____, ____, ____; stall speed in a 60° turn = 1.41 VS.","Load factor at 30° → 1.15, 45° → 1.41, 60° → 2.0; stall speed in a 60° turn = 1.41 VS."],["081.02.00.00","Typical numbers to know: fill in the gaps → ________; halving the speed → ____ (∝ V²).","+10 % mass → +5 % VS; halving the speed → 4× less lift/drag (∝ V²)."],["081.01.00.00","Typical numbers to know: fill in the gaps → Glide: L/D 15 from 10 000 ft ≈ ____ (still air).","Glide: L/D 15 from 10 000 ft ≈ 25 NM (still air)."],["081.01.00.00","Typical numbers to know: fill in the gaps → Rate-1 bank ≈ ____; radius at 180 kt, 30° ≈ ____.","Rate-1 bank ≈ TAS/10 + 7; radius at 180 kt, 30° ≈ 0.8 NM."]],"85":[["071.01.02.06:20","Instruments & equipment (Part-CAT, CAT.IDE.A): fill in the gaps → ____ for: spare fuses, independent portable lights, accurate time-piece, chart holder, first-aid kits, emergency medical kit, megaphones, survival and signalling equipment, sea anchors, child restraint devices (non-installed items).","No equipment approval needed for: spare fuses, independent portable lights, accurate time-piece, chart holder, first-aid kits, emergency medical kit, megaphones, survival and signalling equipment, sea anchors, child restraint devices (non-installed items)."],["071.01.02.06:02","Instruments & equipment (Part-CAT, CAT.IDE.A): fill in the gaps → ____: at least ____, whichever is greater (for fuses replaceable in flight).","Spare fuses: at least 10 % of each rating or 3 of each rating, whichever is greater (for fuses replaceable in flight)."],["071.01.02.06:03","Instruments & equipment (Part-CAT, CAT.IDE.A): fill in the gaps → ____ (or equivalent) at each pilot station: aeroplanes ____.","Windshield wipers (or equivalent) at each pilot station: aeroplanes MCTOM > 5 700 kg."],["071.01.02.06:04","Instruments & equipment (Part-CAT, CAT.IDE.A): fill in the gaps → ____: ____, ____ (h, min, s), sensitive pressure altimeter, airspeed indicator, slip indicator; + for night / VFR over water or out of sight of land / when not able to maintain attitude visually: attitude indicator, turn & slip / turn coordinator, heading indicator (DG), VSI, OAT; pitot heating when required.","VFR day: magnetic compass, accurate time (h, min, s), sensitive pressure altimeter, airspeed indicator, slip indicator; + for night / VFR over water or out of sight of land / when not able to maintain attitude visually: attitude indicator, turn & slip / turn coordinator, heading indicator (DG), VSI, OAT; pitot heating when required."],["071.01.02.06:06","Instruments & equipment (Part-CAT, CAT.IDE.A): fill in the gaps → ____: all of the above + ____ (failure warning on large aeroplanes), ____, two independent altitude/airspeed systems for two-pilot operations, standby attitude indicator (MCTOM > 5 700 kg or MOPSC > 9; own power ≥ 30 min, illuminated), chart holder, flight-director capability as required.","IFR (or night): all of the above + airspeed with pitot heat (failure warning on large aeroplanes), alternate static source, two independent altitude/airspeed systems for two-pilot operations, standby attitude indicator (MCTOM > 5 700 kg or MOPSC > 9; own power ≥ 30 min, illuminated), chart holder, flight-director capability as required."],["071.01.02.06:25","Instruments & equipment (Part-CAT, CAT.IDE.A): fill in the gaps → ____: ____ ____, headset with boom microphone (or equivalent), means of displaying charts readable in all light conditions.","Single-pilot IFR: autopilot with at least altitude hold and heading mode, headset with boom microphone (or equivalent), means of displaying charts readable in all light conditions."],["071.01.02.06:07","Instruments & equipment (Part-CAT, CAT.IDE.A): fill in the gaps → ____: turbine-powered aeroplanes with ____.","Altitude alerting system: turbine-powered aeroplanes with MCTOM > 5 700 kg or MOPSC > 9."],["071.01.02.06:06","Instruments & equipment (Part-CAT, CAT.IDE.A): fill in the gaps → ____: required for low-visibility operations (CAT II/III) and as part of TAWS/audio voice alerting; e.g. aeroplanes over water/IFR as specified.","Radio altimeter: required for low-visibility operations (CAT II/III) and as part of TAWS/audio voice alerting; e.g. aeroplanes over water/IFR as specified."],["071.01.02.06:10","Instruments & equipment (Part-CAT, CAT.IDE.A): fill in the gaps → ____: Class A (with forward-looking terrain avoidance) for turbine aeroplanes ____; Class B for turbine aeroplanes ____.","TAWS: Class A (with forward-looking terrain avoidance) for turbine aeroplanes MCTOM > 5 700 kg or MOPSC > 9; Class B for turbine aeroplanes MCTOM ≤ 5 700 kg with MOPSC 6–9."],["071.01.02.06:01","Instruments & equipment (Part-CAT, CAT.IDE.A): fill in the gaps → ____ (TCAS II v7.1): turbine aeroplanes ____ ____.","ACAS II (TCAS II v7.1): turbine aeroplanes MCTOM > 5 700 kg or MOPSC > 19."],["071.01.02.06:11","Instruments & equipment (Part-CAT, CAT.IDE.A): fill in the gaps → ____: pressurised aeroplanes; ____; unpressurised ____ when operating at night or in IMC in areas where thunderstorms or other potentially hazardous weather are expected.","Airborne weather radar: pressurised aeroplanes; unpressurised aeroplanes MCTOM > 5 700 kg; unpressurised MOPSC > 9 when operating at night or in IMC in areas where thunderstorms or other potentially hazardous weather are expected."],["071.01.02.06:12","Instruments & equipment (Part-CAT, CAT.IDE.A): fill in the gaps → ____ (aeroplanes first CofA after 1 April 1998): multi-engine turbine with ____ or ____ (details by date). Records flight-deck audio, radio, PA, interphone;","CVR (aeroplanes first CofA after 1 April 1998): multi-engine turbine with MOPSC > 9 or MCTOM > 5 700 kg (details by date). Records flight-deck audio, radio, PA, interphone;"],["071.01.02.06:01","Instruments & equipment (Part-CAT, CAT.IDE.A): fill in the gaps → ____ (____ ____ for large aeroplanes with CofA from 2021); starts automatically before the aeroplane moves under its own power, stops after it can no longer move; bright orange/yellow, crash-/fire-resistant, underwater locator beacon (≥ 90 days), located in the tail; erase function only on ground; not switched off in flight.","≥ 2 h (25 h for large aeroplanes with CofA from 2021); starts automatically before the aeroplane moves under its own power, stops after it can no longer move; bright orange/yellow, crash-/fire-resistant, underwater locator beacon (≥ 90 days), located in the tail; erase function only on ground; not switched off in flight."],["071.01.02.06:15","Instruments & equipment (Part-CAT, CAT.IDE.A): fill in the gaps → FDR (CofA after 1998): ____ or multi-engine turbine ____; records parameters (time, altitude, speed, heading, acceleration, attitude, controls, thrust…) for the ____ h; same construction/location rules (orange, ULB, automatic start/stop).","FDR (CofA after 1998): MCTOM > 5 700 kg or multi-engine turbine MOPSC > 9; records parameters (time, altitude, speed, heading, acceleration, attitude, controls, thrust…) for the last 25 h; same construction/location rules (orange, ULB, automatic start/stop)."],["071.01.02.06:16","Instruments & equipment (Part-CAT, CAT.IDE.A): fill in the gaps → ____: a seat or berth with belt for each person aged ____; infants < 2 on an adult's lap with a ____ or in an approved child restraint device; flight crew seats with safety harness (shoulder straps) automatically restraining; cabin crew seats near floor-level exits with harness, facing forward/aft.","Seats, belts, harnesses: a seat or berth with belt for each person aged ≥ 2 years; infants < 2 on an adult's lap with a supplementary loop belt or in an approved child restraint device; flight crew seats with safety harness (shoulder straps) automatically restraining; cabin crew seats near floor-level exits with harness, facing forward/aft."],["071.01.02.06:17","Instruments & equipment (Part-CAT, CAT.IDE.A): fill in the gaps → ____ visible from every passenger seat (when not all seats are visible from the flight deck).","\"Fasten seat belt\" and \"No smoking\" signs visible from every passenger seat (when not all seats are visible from the flight deck)."],["071.01.02.06:18","Instruments & equipment (Part-CAT, CAT.IDE.A): fill in the gaps → ____: door between flight deck and cabin with ____ placard and lock; ____ deck door (MOPSC > 19, security); curtains/doors to emergency exits must be securable in the open position for take-off and landing.","Internal doors & curtains: door between flight deck and cabin with \"crew only\" placard and lock; reinforced, lockable flight- deck door (MOPSC > 19, security); curtains/doors to emergency exits must be securable in the open position for take-off and landing."],["071.01.02.06:19","Instruments & equipment (Part-CAT, CAT.IDE.A): fill in the gaps → First-aid kits by seats: ____, ____, ____, 301–400: 4, 401–500: 5, > 500: 6 (readily accessible, inspected).","First-aid kits by seats: 0–100: 1, 101–200: 2, 201–300: 3, 301–400: 4, 401–500: 5, > 500: 6 (readily accessible, inspected)."],["071.01.02.06:20","Instruments & equipment (Part-CAT, CAT.IDE.A): fill in the gaps → Emergency medical kit: ____ and the aeroplane may be ____ flight time from an aerodrome with qualified medical assistance. First-aid oxygen: pressurised aeroplanes operating ____ with cabin crew required: for 2 % of passengers (at least 1 person), for the whole flight above 8 000 ft cabin altitude after depressurisation.","Emergency medical kit: MOPSC > 30 and the aeroplane may be > 60 min flight time from an aerodrome with qualified medical assistance. First-aid oxygen: pressurised aeroplanes operating above 25 000 ft with cabin crew required: for 2 % of passengers (at least 1 person), for the whole flight above 8 000 ft cabin altitude after depressurisation."],["071.01.02.06:21","Instruments & equipment (Part-CAT, CAT.IDE.A): fill in the gaps → ____: pressurised aeroplanes (and unpressurised > 5 700 kg or > 19 seats): for each flight-crew member on duty (at station) and ____ for cabin crew near fire extinguishers; protects eyes, nose, mouth; ≥ ____ of breathing gas.","Crew protective breathing equipment (PBE): pressurised aeroplanes (and unpressurised > 5 700 kg or > 19 seats): for each flight-crew member on duty (at station) and portable PBE for cabin crew near fire extinguishers; protects eyes, nose, mouth; ≥ 15 min of breathing gas."],["071.01.02.06:22","Instruments & equipment (Part-CAT, CAT.IDE.A): fill in the gaps → Handheld fire extinguishers: at least one on the flight deck, one in each galley not on the main deck, in accessible cargo compartments; cabin: ____, ____, ____, 201– 300: 4, 301–400: 5, 401–500: 6, 501–600: 7, > 600: 8; at least one halon (or equivalent) in the passenger compartment; evenly distributed.","Handheld fire extinguishers: at least one on the flight deck, one in each galley not on the main deck, in accessible cargo compartments; cabin: 7–30 seats: 1, 31–60: 2, 61–200: 3, 201– 300: 4, 301–400: 5, 401–500: 6, 501–600: 7, > 600: 8; at least one halon (or equivalent) in the passenger compartment; evenly distributed."],["071.01.02.06:01","Instruments & equipment (Part-CAT, CAT.IDE.A): fill in the gaps → ____: MCTOM > 5 700 kg or MOPSC > 9: ____ ____; MOPSC > 200: another near the rearmost galley, not visible to passengers.","Crash axe / crowbar: MCTOM > 5 700 kg or MOPSC > 9: one on the flight deck; MOPSC > 200: another near the rearmost galley, not visible to passengers."],["071.01.02.06:24","Instruments & equipment (Part-CAT, CAT.IDE.A): fill in the gaps → ____: marked by ____ (outlined in white if needed) ____ (bars 9 cm × 3 cm); if the corners are > 2 m apart, intermediate lines 9 × 3 cm.","Break-in points: marked by red or yellow (outlined in white if needed) right-angled corner markings (bars 9 cm × 3 cm); if the corners are > 2 m apart, intermediate lines 9 × 3 cm."],["071.01.02.06:25","Instruments & equipment (Part-CAT, CAT.IDE.A): fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (or equivalent) at passenger exits with ____ above ground (gear extended).","Emergency evacuation: slides (or equivalent) at passenger exits with sill height > 1.83 m (6 ft) above ground (gear extended)."],["071.01.02.06:26","Instruments & equipment (Part-CAT, CAT.IDE.A): fill in the gaps → ____: ____; ____ (portable, battery-powered, near cabin crew seats).","Megaphones: MOPSC 61–99: 1; ≥ 100: 2 (portable, battery-powered, near cabin crew seats)."],["071.01.02.06:27","Instruments & equipment (Part-CAT, CAT.IDE.A): fill in the gaps → ____: MOPSC > 9 — independent emergency lighting (general cabin, exit signs, ____ ____, exterior lighting at exits), battery for ≥ 10 min.","Emergency lighting & marking: MOPSC > 9 — independent emergency lighting (general cabin, exit signs, floor-proximity escape path marking, exterior lighting at exits), battery for ≥ 10 min."],["071.01.02.06:29","Instruments & equipment (Part-CAT, CAT.IDE.A): fill in the gaps → ____: ____: at least one automatic ELT or two ELTs (one automatic); others: at least one ELT (automatic for new CofA); transmits on ____. Survival ELT(S) in life rafts.","ELT: MOPSC > 19: at least one automatic ELT or two ELTs (one automatic); others: at least one ELT (automatic for new CofA); transmits on 121.5 and 406 MHz. Survival ELT(S) in life rafts."],["071.01.02.06:33","Instruments & equipment (Part-CAT, CAT.IDE.A): fill in the gaps → ____ (with survivor locator light) for each person when flying over water ____, or > 50 NM for land-planes/take-off/landing paths over water; infant flotation devices.","Life jackets (with survivor locator light) for each person when flying over water beyond gliding distance from shore, or > 50 NM for land-planes/take-off/landing paths over water; infant flotation devices."],["071.01.02.06:29","Instruments & equipment (Part-CAT, CAT.IDE.A): fill in the gaps → Life rafts: over water further than ____ ____ (whichever less) for aeroplanes able to continue OEI to an aerodrome; ____ for others; capacity for all persons (also if the largest raft is lost); with survival kit, pyrotechnic signals, survival ELT.","Life rafts: over water further than 120 min at cruising speed or 400 NM (whichever less) for aeroplanes able to continue OEI to an aerodrome; 30 min or 100 NM for others; capacity for all persons (also if the largest raft is lost); with survival kit, pyrotechnic signals, survival ELT."],["071.01.02.06:32","Instruments & equipment (Part-CAT, CAT.IDE.A): fill in the gaps → ____: helicopters over hostile sea when sea temperature/SAR time require (and aeroplanes as specified by the operator's risk assessment).","Crew survival suits: helicopters over hostile sea when sea temperature/SAR time require (and aeroplanes as specified by the operator's risk assessment)."],["071.01.02.06:31","Instruments & equipment (Part-CAT, CAT.IDE.A): fill in the gaps → ____: over areas where ____ ____ — signalling equipment, ELT, additional survival kit (water, food, first aid) appropriate to the route.","Survival equipment: over areas where SAR would be especially difficult — signalling equipment, ELT, additional survival kit (water, food, first aid) appropriate to the route."],["071.01.02.06:29","Instruments & equipment (Part-CAT, CAT.IDE.A): fill in the gaps → ____: survival suits for all, life jackets, life rafts, emergency flotation, rebreathers; ____ equipment for helicopters overwater beyond set distances.","Helicopters to/from helidecks in hostile sea areas: survival suits for all, life jackets, life rafts, emergency flotation, rebreathers; emergency flotation equipment for helicopters overwater beyond set distances."],["071.01.02.07:01","Communication & navigation equipment: what do you remember about this? → General: approved, installed and serviceable; able to communicate …","General: approved, installed and serviceable; able to communicate with ATS as required; not dependent on a single failure for both COM and NAV where redundancy required."],["071.01.02.07:02","Communication & navigation equipment: fill in the gaps → ____ (international emergency frequency) must be available — distress/urgency, interception, emergency contact when other frequencies fail.","121.5 MHz (international emergency frequency) must be available — distress/urgency, interception, emergency contact when other frequencies fail."],["071.01.02.07:03","Communication & navigation equipment: fill in the gaps → ____ for each required flight-crew member.","Audio selector panel for each required flight-crew member."],["071.01.02.07:04","Communication & navigation equipment: fill in the gaps → ____: radio equipment to communicate with appropriate ground stations and ATS (and transponder as required).","VFR by reference to visual landmarks: radio equipment to communicate with appropriate ground stations and ATS (and transponder as required)."],["071.01.02.07:04","Communication & navigation equipment: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ ____; navigation equipment for the route (e.g. VOR, ADF, DME as required, or RNAV/GNSS for PBN), ILS/GLS or equivalent for approaches, marker beacon receiver where needed; failure of one system must leave enough to navigate safely.","IFR / VFR not by visual landmarks: two independent radio communication systems; navigation equipment for the route (e.g. VOR, ADF, DME as required, or RNAV/GNSS for PBN), ILS/GLS or equivalent for approaches, marker beacon receiver where needed; failure of one system must leave enough to navigate safely."],["071.01.02.06:07","Communication & navigation equipment: fill in the gaps → ____: ____, ____, automatic altitude control (autopilot altitude hold), SSR transponder with altitude reporting connected to the altimetry used for height keeping.","RVSM: two independent altitude-measuring systems, altitude alerting system, automatic altitude control (autopilot altitude hold), SSR transponder with altitude reporting connected to the altimetry used for height keeping."],["071.01.02.07:07","Communication & navigation equipment: fill in the gaps → ____: aeroplanes with ____; public address: ____.","Crew interphone: aeroplanes with > 1 flight-crew member; public address: MOPSC > 19."],["071.01.02.07:10","Communication & navigation equipment: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (EU: Mode S; Mode S ELS, and EHS for aeroplanes > 5 700 kg or TAS > 250 kt).","Transponder: pressure-altitude-reporting SSR transponder (EU: Mode S; Mode S ELS, and EHS for aeroplanes > 5 700 kg or TAS > 250 kt)."],["071.01.02.07:11","Communication & navigation equipment: fill in the gaps → ____: the operator must use data from ____, keep them ____ cycle), check integrity, and inform crews of deficiencies/NOTAMs.","Aeronautical databases: the operator must use data from approved (EASA DAT) providers, keep them current (AIRAC cycle), check integrity, and inform crews of deficiencies/NOTAMs."],["071.02.13.02:02","Contaminated runways: fill in the gaps → ____ = ____ (6 = dry). ____ required when the braking action encountered is worse than reported (or poor/less than poor) — pilot reports \"BRAKING ACTION POOR\" etc.","Good braking action = RWYCC 5 (6 = dry). Special air-report required when the braking action encountered is worse than reported (or poor/less than poor) — pilot reports \"BRAKING ACTION POOR\" etc."],["071.02.13.02:02","Contaminated runways: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (standing water lifts the tyre; high speed), ____ (thin water film on smooth/rubber-contaminated surface, low speeds too), reverted-rubber (locked wheel, heat produces steam, rubber reverts — long skids).","Hydroplaning types: dynamic (standing water lifts the tyre; high speed), viscous (thin water film on smooth/rubber-contaminated surface, low speeds too), reverted-rubber (locked wheel, heat produces steam, rubber reverts — long skids)."],["071.02.13.03:02","Contaminated runways: fill in the gaps → Dynamic hydroplaning speeds: spin-down (rotating tyre) V ____; ____ (p = tyre pressure in psi, V in kt). The spin-up speed is the relevant one at touchdown on a flooded runway. Example: p = 200 psi → 9 × 14.1 = ____; 7.7 × 14.1 = 109 kt.","Dynamic hydroplaning speeds: spin-down (rotating tyre) V = 9 √p; spin-up (non-rotating tyre, touchdown) V = 7.7 √p (p = tyre pressure in psi, V in kt). The spin-up speed is the relevant one at touchdown on a flooded runway. Example: p = 200 psi → 9 × 14.1 = 127 kt; 7.7 × 14.1 = 109 kt."],["071.02.13.02:02","Contaminated runways: fill in the gaps → ____ (GRF format): location, date/time, ____ ____, RWYCC for each third (e.g. 5/5/3), % coverage per third, depth of loose contaminant (mm), condition description per third (DRY, WET, STANDING WATER, SLUSH, DRY/WET SNOW, COMPACTED SNOW, FROST, ICE, WET ICE…), width to which RWYCC applies;","SNOWTAM (GRF format): location, date/time, runway designator, RWYCC for each third (e.g. 5/5/3), % coverage per third, depth of loose contaminant (mm), condition description per third (DRY, WET, STANDING WATER, SLUSH, DRY/WET SNOW, COMPACTED SNOW, FROST, ICE, WET ICE…), width to which RWYCC applies;"],["071.02.13.02:02","Contaminated runways: fill in the gaps → ____: reduced runway length, drifting snow, loose sand, chemical treatment, snow banks, taxiway/apron conditions. Valid max ____.","situational awareness section: reduced runway length, drifting snow, loose sand, chemical treatment, snow banks, taxiway/apron conditions. Valid max 8 h."],["071.02.13.05:02","Contaminated runways: what do you remember about this? → Hazards from SNOWTAM/METAR: reduced friction → longer ASD/LD, …","Hazards from SNOWTAM/METAR: reduced friction → longer ASD/LD, directional control problems in crosswind, slush impingement drag on take-off, hydroplaning, frozen gear/brakes."],["071.02.13.02:02","Contaminated runways: fill in the gaps → ____: use contaminated performance data (reduced mass), no reduced thrust, max reverse/autobrake, lower crosswind limits, avoid take-off with deep contaminant, choose another runway/aerodrome, delay until treated.","Mitigation: use contaminated performance data (reduced mass), no reduced thrust, max reverse/autobrake, lower crosswind limits, avoid take-off with deep contaminant, choose another runway/aerodrome, delay until treated."],["071.02.13.03:03","Exam trap (Contaminated runways): complete the fact → spin-up ____√p is the touchdown value.","spin-up 7.7 √p is the touchdown value."],["071.02.13.02:02","Exam trap (Contaminated runways): complete the fact → Slides above ____ sill height.","Slides above 1.83 m sill height."],["071.01.02.06:10","Exam trap (Contaminated runways): complete the fact → ACAS II > ____ or > ____pax; TAWS A > 5 700 kg or > 9 pax.","ACAS II > 5 700 kg or > 19 pax; TAWS A > 5 700 kg or > 9 pax."],["071.01.02.06:19","Exam trap (Contaminated runways): complete the fact → First-aid kits: ____per ____eats.","First-aid kits: 1 per 100 seats."],["071.01.02.06:26","Exam trap (Contaminated runways): complete the fact → Megaphones: ____→ ____≥ 100 → 2.","Megaphones: 61–99 → 1, ≥ 100 → 2."],["071.01.02.06:01","EASA LO 071.01.02.06.01 → Explain which items do not require an equipment approval.","No equipment approval needed for: spare fuses, independent portable lights, accurate time-piece, chart holder, first-aid kits, emergency medical kit, megaphones, survival and signalling equipment, sea anchors, child restraint devices (non-installed items)."],["071.01.02.06:05","EASA LO 071.01.02.06.05 → List the minimum equipment required for IFR flights.","Radio altimeter: required for low-visibility operations (CAT II/III) and as part of TAWS/audio voice alerting; e.g. aeroplanes over water/IFR as specified."],["071.01.02.06:09","EASA LO 071.01.02.06.09 → State the requirements for ground proximity warning system (GPWS)/terrain awareness and warning system (TAWS).","TAWS: Class A (with forward-looking terrain avoidance) for turbine aeroplanes MCTOM > 5 700 kg or MOPSC > 9; Class B for turbine aeroplanes MCTOM ≤ 5 700 kg with MOPSC 6–9."],["071.01.02.06:13","EASA LO 071.01.02.06.13 → State the rules regarding the location, construction, installation, and operation of cockpit voice recorders (CVRs) (after 1998).","FDR (CofA after 1998): MCTOM > 5 700 kg or multi-engine turbine MOPSC > 9; records parameters (time, altitude, speed, heading, acceleration, attitude, controls, thrust…) for the last 25 h; same construction/location rules (orange, ULB, automatic start/stop)."],["071.01.02.06:14","EASA LO 071.01.02.06.14 → State the circumstances under which a flight data recorder (FDR) is compulsory (after 1998).","FDR (CofA after 1998): MCTOM > 5 700 kg or multi-engine turbine MOPSC > 9; records parameters (time, altitude, speed, heading, acceleration, attitude, controls, thrust…) for the last 25 h; same construction/location rules (orange, ULB, automatic start/stop). / CVR (aeroplanes first CofA after 1 April 1998): multi-engine turbine with MOPSC > 9 or MCTOM > 5 700 kg (details by date). Records flight-deck audio, radio, PA, interphone;"],["071.01.02.06:28","EASA LO 071.01.02.06.28 → Explain the requirements for an emergency locator transmitter (ELT).","ELT: MOPSC > 19: at least one automatic ELT or two ELTs (one automatic); others: at least one ELT (automatic for new CofA); transmits on 121.5 and 406 MHz. Survival ELT(S) in life rafts."],["071.01.02.06:30","EASA LO 071.01.02.06.30 → Explain the requirements for crew survival suit.","Audio selector panel for each required flight-crew member."],["071.01.02.07:05","EASA LO 071.01.02.07.05 → List the requirements for communication and navigation equipment when operating under IFR or under VFR over routes not navigated by reference to visual landmarks.","VFR by reference to visual landmarks: radio equipment to communicate with appropriate ground stations and ATS (and transponder as required). / IFR / VFR not by visual landmarks: two independent radio communication systems; navigation equipment for the route (e.g. VOR, ADF, DME as required, or RNAV/GNSS for PBN), ILS/GLS or equivalent for approaches, marker beacon receiver where needed; failure of one system must leave enough to navigate safely."],["071.01.02.07:08","EASA LO 071.01.02.07.08 → List the equipment for operations requiring a radio communication.","VFR by reference to visual landmarks: radio equipment to communicate with appropriate ground stations and ATS (and transponder as required). / Radio altimeter: required for low-visibility operations (CAT II/III) and as part of TAWS/audio voice alerting; e.g. aeroplanes over water/IFR as specified."],["071.01.02.07:09","EASA LO 071.01.02.07.09 → List the equipment for operations that require a radio navigation system.","VFR by reference to visual landmarks: radio equipment to communicate with appropriate ground stations and ATS (and transponder as required). / IFR / VFR not by visual landmarks: two independent radio communication systems; navigation equipment for the route (e.g. VOR, ADF, DME as required, or RNAV/GNSS for PBN), ILS/GLS or equivalent for approaches, marker beacon receiver where needed; failure of one system must leave enough to navigate safely."],["071.02.13.05:01","EASA LO 071.02.13.05.01 → Interpret from a SNOWTAM the contamination and braking action on a runway, taxiways and apron.","Good braking action = RWYCC 5 (6 = dry). Special air-report required when the braking action encountered is worse than reported (or poor/less than poor) — pilot reports \"BRAKING ACTION POOR\" etc."]],"86":[["010.08.01.01:01","Objective & definitions: fill in the gaps → ____: ensure the flow of ____ ____ of international air navigation.","Objective of AIS: ensure the flow of aeronautical information and data necessary for the safety, regularity and efficiency of international air navigation."],["010.08.02.01:01","Objective & definitions: fill in the gaps → ____ (aeronautical information circular): notice containing information that does ____ for NOTAM or AIP but relates to flight safety, air navigation, technical, administrative or legislative matters.","AIC (aeronautical information circular): notice containing information that does not qualify for NOTAM or AIP but relates to flight safety, air navigation, technical, administrative or legislative matters."],["010.08.04.01:04","Objective & definitions: fill in the gaps → ____ (aeronautical information publication): publication issued by or with the authority of a State containing ____ ____ essential to air navigation. AIP amendment: permanent changes to the AIP.","AIP (aeronautical information publication): publication issued by or with the authority of a State containing aeronautical information of a lasting character essential to air navigation. AIP amendment: permanent changes to the AIP."],["010.08.04.02:01","Objective & definitions: fill in the gaps → ____ ____: ____ changes (long duration or extensive text/graphics) on coloured (yellow) pages.","AIP supplement: temporary changes (long duration or extensive text/graphics) on coloured (yellow) pages."],["010.08.04.03:01","Objective & definitions: fill in the gaps → ____: notice distributed by telecommunication containing information on the establishment, condition or change of any facility, service, procedure or hazard, the ____ ____ to personnel concerned with flight operations.","NOTAM: notice distributed by telecommunication containing information on the establishment, condition or change of any facility, service, procedure or hazard, the timely knowledge of which is essential to personnel concerned with flight operations."],["010.08.04.02:04","Objective & definitions: fill in the gaps → ____: system for advance notification, based on ____ ____, of circumstances that necessitate significant changes in operating practices. ASHTAM: special series NOTAM on volcanic activity/ash. SNOWTAM: runway surface condition (snow, ice, water).","AIRAC: system for advance notification, based on common effective dates, of circumstances that necessitate significant changes in operating practices. ASHTAM: special series NOTAM on volcanic activity/ash. SNOWTAM: runway surface condition (snow, ice, water)."],["010.08.04.01:05","Objective & definitions: fill in the gaps → ____: AIP (incl. amendments & supplements), NOTAM, PIB, AIC, checklists and lists of valid NOTAM.","Integrated Aeronautical Information Package: AIP (incl. amendments & supplements), NOTAM, PIB, AIC, checklists and lists of valid NOTAM."],["010.08.03.01:01","AIS responsibilities: fill in the gaps → Where a 24-h service is not provided, AIS must be available during the whole period an aircraft is in flight in its area of ____ that period (and at other times on request).","Where a 24-h service is not provided, AIS must be available during the whole period an aircraft is in flight in its area of responsibility, plus 2 h before and after that period (and at other times on request)."],["010.08.03.01:02","AIS responsibilities: fill in the gaps → Makes available to flight crews: information on ____ ____ ____ — in suitable form (AIP, NOTAM, PIB, charts, digital data) for pre-flight planning and in-flight use (via ATS).","Makes available to flight crews: information on aerodromes, air navigation facilities and services, airspace, procedures, hazards — in suitable form (AIP, NOTAM, PIB, charts, digital data) for pre-flight planning and in-flight use (via ATS)."],["010.08.03.01:03","AIS responsibilities: fill in the gaps → Duties for the State's territory: ____ ____ aeronautical data/information concerning the entire territory (and areas outside where the State is responsible for ATS), ensure ____ (accuracy, resolution, integrity), obtain information from other States' AIS.","Duties for the State's territory: receive, collate/assemble, edit, format, publish/store and distribute aeronautical data/information concerning the entire territory (and areas outside where the State is responsible for ATS), ensure quality (accuracy, resolution, integrity), obtain information from other States' AIS."],["010.08.04.01:01","AIP: fill in the gaps → Primary purpose: to contain ____ ____; basic source for NOTAM changes.","Primary purpose: to contain information of a lasting character essential to air navigation; basic source for NOTAM changes."],["010.08.04.01:01","AIP: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (general — regulations, ____ ICAO SARPs, national regulations, units, abbreviations, charges, services, time system, standard time tables), ENR (en-route — rules and procedures, airspace, ATS routes, navigation aids, prohibited/restricted/danger areas, holding, obstacles, charts), AD (aerodromes/heliports — data, facilities, charts, RFF category, declared distances).","Three parts: GEN (general — regulations, differences from ICAO SARPs, national regulations, units, abbreviations, charges, services, time system, standard time tables), ENR (en-route — rules and procedures, airspace, ATS routes, navigation aids, prohibited/restricted/danger areas, holding, obstacles, charts), AD (aerodromes/heliports — data, facilities, charts, RFF category, declared distances)."],["010.08.04.01:03","AIP: fill in the gaps → Differences from ICAO SARPs → ____; ____ GEN 3; ____ ENR; aerodrome data AD 2.","Differences from ICAO SARPs → GEN 1.7; pre-flight/services GEN 3; airspace structure/rules ENR; aerodrome data AD 2."],["010.08.04.01:04","AIP: fill in the gaps → ____ → ____ (AIRAC or non-AIRAC); each amendment has a number, check list of pages. AIP Supplements for ____ months) or short duration with extensive text/graphics (e.g. major works, exercises); published on yellow paper and listed in the monthly checklist.","Permanent changes → AIP Amendment (AIRAC or non-AIRAC); each amendment has a number, check list of pages. AIP Supplements for temporary changes of long duration (≥ 3 months) or short duration with extensive text/graphics (e.g. major works, exercises); published on yellow paper and listed in the monthly checklist."],["010.08.04.02:01","NOTAM: fill in the gaps → Information that would belong in a NOTAM but includes ____ ____ → published as an ____ (and a NOTAM referring to it, trigger NOTAM).","Information that would belong in a NOTAM but includes extensive text or graphics → published as an AIP Supplement (and a NOTAM referring to it, trigger NOTAM)."],["010.08.04.01:04","NOTAM: fill in the gaps → Issued ____ when information is ____ ____, or permanent changes of operational significance made at short notice, e.g.: establishment/closure/changes of aerodromes, runways, NAVAIDs, lighting, ATS services, airspace (restricted/danger area activation), obstacles (erection/removal), hazards (volcanic ash, radioactive release, military exercises), changes in RFF category, runway condition, unserviceability of facilities.","Issued promptly when information is temporary and of short duration, or permanent changes of operational significance made at short notice, e.g.: establishment/closure/changes of aerodromes, runways, NAVAIDs, lighting, ATS services, airspace (restricted/danger area activation), obstacles (erection/removal), hazards (volcanic ash, radioactive release, military exercises), changes in RFF category, runway condition, unserviceability of facilities."],["010.08.04.02:01","NOTAM: fill in the gaps → ____: by ____ directly to addressees whenever practicable; otherwise by other telecommunication means; international exchange via ____ offices (and the European AIS Database EAD).","Distribution: by AFTN/AMHS (aeronautical fixed service) directly to addressees whenever practicable; otherwise by other telecommunication means; international exchange via NOTAM offices (and the European AIS Database EAD)."],["010.08.04.02:01","NOTAM: what do you remember about this? → NOTAM format: Q-line (FIR, code, traffic, purpose, scope, levels, …","NOTAM format: Q-line (FIR, code, traffic, purpose, scope, levels, coordinates/radius), A) location, B) from, C) to (EST/PERM), D) schedule, E) text, F/G) lower/upper limits."],["010.08.04.02:04","NOTAM: fill in the gaps → ____ on aerodrome pavements → reported by ____ (GRF runway condition report; max 8 h validity), otherwise NOTAM for other conditions.","Snow, slush, ice, standing water on aerodrome pavements → reported by SNOWTAM (GRF runway condition report; max 8 h validity), otherwise NOTAM for other conditions."],["010.08.04.02:01","NOTAM: fill in the gaps → ____: special NOTAM series on ____ — location, name/number of the volcano, ____ (green/yellow/orange/red alert level), ash cloud extent, levels, direction of movement, affected routes/airspace, sources.","ASHTAM: special NOTAM series on volcanic activity — location, name/number of the volcano, aviation colour code (green/yellow/orange/red alert level), ash cloud extent, levels, direction of movement, affected routes/airspace, sources."],["010.08.04.03:01","AIRAC: fill in the gaps → Information concerning ____ in operating practice shall be distributed under AIRAC (____ cycle, published at least ____ in advance): e.g. limits of FIR/CTA/CTR, ATS routes, prohibited/restricted/danger areas, positions/frequencies of radio NAVAIDs, holding & approach procedures, meteorological facilities, transition levels/altitudes, runways and stopways, aerodrome procedures;","Information concerning significant changes in operating practice shall be distributed under AIRAC (28-day cycle, published at least 28 days (56 days for major changes) in advance): e.g. limits of FIR/CTA/CTR, ATS routes, prohibited/restricted/danger areas, positions/frequencies of radio NAVAIDs, holding & approach procedures, meteorological facilities, transition levels/altitudes, runways and stopways, aerodrome procedures;"],["010.08.04.03:01","AIRAC: fill in the gaps → ____ for other items (e.g. new obstacles, charges, hours of services).","should for other items (e.g. new obstacles, charges, hours of services)."],["010.08.04.04:01","AIC: fill in the gaps → Content: long-term forecasts of major changes in legislation, regulations, procedures or facilities; purely explanatory/advisory information (e.g. safety recommendations, winter operations, hazards to navigation, ____, medical advice, new equipment requirements).","Content: long-term forecasts of major changes in legislation, regulations, procedures or facilities; purely explanatory/advisory information (e.g. safety recommendations, winter operations, hazards to navigation, birds, medical advice, new equipment requirements)."],["010.08.04.04:02","AIC: fill in the gaps → ____: numbered ____, colour-coded by subject (in some States), a ____ of AICs in force issued at least once a year; printed or electronic.","Organisation: numbered serially by calendar year, colour-coded by subject (in some States), a checklist of AICs in force issued at least once a year; printed or electronic."],["010.08.04.05:01","Pre- and post-flight information: fill in the gaps → ____ at aerodromes: AIP elements, maps/charts, plus ____ of the departure aerodrome: construction/maintenance work, rough surfaces, snow/ice/water, parked aircraft, lighting failures, NAVAID status, ____, RFF changes, ATS/MET services.","Pre-flight at aerodromes: AIP elements, maps/charts, plus additional current information of the departure aerodrome: construction/maintenance work, rough surfaces, snow/ice/water, parked aircraft, lighting failures, NAVAID status, bird hazards, RFF changes, ATS/MET services."],["010.08.04.05:02","Pre- and post-flight information: fill in the gaps → ____: current NOTAMs and other urgent information are provided as a ____, plain language, for the route/aerodromes.","Recapitulation: current NOTAMs and other urgent information are provided as a Pre-flight Information Bulletin (PIB), plain language, for the route/aerodromes."],["010.08.04.05:03","Pre- and post-flight information: fill in the gaps → ____: crews report to AIS (via ATS) ____ ____ noted in flight, presence of birds, observations relevant to safety (e.g. unserviceable lights, runway conditions).","Post-flight: crews report to AIS (via ATS) inadequacies or state/operation of air navigation facilities noted in flight, presence of birds, observations relevant to safety (e.g. unserviceable lights, runway conditions)."],["010.08.05.01:01","ATM & ANS provision: fill in the gaps → ____ lays down: ____ ____ (organisation, management system, certification), specific requirements for ATS, MET, AIS and CNS services (and ATFM/ASM, flight-procedure design, data services), and oversight by competent authorities.","Implementing Regulation (EU) 2017/373 lays down: general requirements for the provision of air navigation services (organisation, management system, certification), specific requirements for ATS, MET, AIS and CNS services (and ATFM/ASM, flight-procedure design, data services), and oversight by competent authorities."],["010.08.05.01:01","Exam trap (ATM & ANS provision): complete the fact → AIRAC cycle ____days, ____days lead time.","AIRAC cycle 28 days, 28/56 days lead time."],["010.08.04.01:02","EASA LO 010.08.04.01.02 → Name the different parts of the AIP.","AIP (aeronautical information publication): publication issued by or with the authority of a State containing aeronautical information of a lasting character essential to air navigation. AIP amendment: permanent changes to the AIP."],["010.08.04.02:03","EASA LO 010.08.04.02.03 → State how NOTAMs shall be distributed.","Recapitulation: current NOTAMs and other urgent information are provided as a Pre-flight Information Bulletin (PIB), plain language, for the route/aerodromes."],["010.08.04.02:06","EASA LO 010.08.04.02.06 → Define and state which information an ASHTAM may contain.","Primary purpose: to contain information of a lasting character essential to air navigation; basic source for NOTAM changes."]],"87":[["050.01.00.00",": what do you remember about this? → FINAL review of 050 Meteorology — one-page cheat sheet (details: #47 …","FINAL review of 050 Meteorology — one-page cheat sheet (details: #47 climatology/hazards, #51 information)."],["050.01.00.00","Atmosphere: fill in the gaps → ISA: MSL ____; lapse rate ____ ____ up to 11 km (36 090 ft), then −56.5 °C constant to 20 km. Troposphere height ~16–18 km equator, ~8 km poles (higher in summer). Composition N₂ 78 %, O₂ 21 %.","ISA: MSL 15 °C, 1013.25 hPa, 1.225 kg/m³; lapse rate 1.98 °C/1 000 ft (6.5 °C/km) up to 11 km (36 090 ft), then −56.5 °C constant to 20 km. Troposphere height ~16–18 km equator, ~8 km poles (higher in summer). Composition N₂ 78 %, O₂ 21 %."],["050.01.00.00","Atmosphere: fill in the gaps → ____: ~____ at MSL, ~____ at 18 000 ft; 500 hPa ≈ 18 000 ft, 300 hPa ≈ 30 000 ft, 200 hPa ≈ 39 000 ft. QNH, QFE, QFF, standard; cold air → pressure levels lower (\"from high to low / warm to cold, look out below\"). Density altitude: hot/high/humid → performance ↓.","Pressure: ~27 ft/hPa at MSL, ~50 ft/hPa at 18 000 ft; 500 hPa ≈ 18 000 ft, 300 hPa ≈ 30 000 ft, 200 hPa ≈ 39 000 ft. QNH, QFE, QFF, standard; cold air → pressure levels lower (\"from high to low / warm to cold, look out below\"). Density altitude: hot/high/humid → performance ↓."],["050.01.00.00","Atmosphere: fill in the gaps → ____: true altitude ≈ indicated ± ____ ____.","Temperature correction: true altitude ≈ indicated ± 4 ft × ISA dev × 1 000 ft."],["050.02.00.00","Wind: fill in the gaps → ____ wind parallel to isobars (PGF = Coriolis), ____ wind with curvature (stronger around highs than lows for the same gradient); surface friction → wind backs and weakens (NH: ~____, ~10° over sea, speed ~50 %/70 %). Buys- Ballot: NH, back to the wind → low on the left.","Geostrophic wind parallel to isobars (PGF = Coriolis), gradient wind with curvature (stronger around highs than lows for the same gradient); surface friction → wind backs and weakens (NH: ~30° over land, ~10° over sea, speed ~50 %/70 %). Buys- Ballot: NH, back to the wind → low on the left."],["050.02.00.00","Wind: fill in the gaps → ____ ∝ temperature gradient; ____ (> 60 kt): polar-front jet ~FL 300, subtropical ~FL 400; CAT on the cold side. Local winds: sea/land breeze, anabatic/katabatic, valley winds, Foehn, Mistral, Bora.","Thermal wind ∝ temperature gradient; jet streams (> 60 kt): polar-front jet ~FL 300, subtropical ~FL 400; CAT on the cold side. Local winds: sea/land breeze, anabatic/katabatic, valley winds, Foehn, Mistral, Bora."],["050.03.00.00","Thermodynamics & stability: fill in the gaps → ____, ____ (varies, larger when cold), ELR 2 °C/1 000 ft (ISA). Stable: ELR < SALR; unstable: ELR > DALR; conditionally unstable: in between. ____ (convective). Latent heat released by condensation/freezing. Inversion = temperature increasing with height (very stable).","DALR 3 °C/1 000 ft, SALR ~1.8 °C/1 000 ft (varies, larger when cold), ELR 2 °C/1 000 ft (ISA). Stable: ELR < SALR; unstable: ELR > DALR; conditionally unstable: in between. Cloud base (ft) ≈ 400 × (T − Td) (convective). Latent heat released by condensation/freezing. Inversion = temperature increasing with height (very stable)."],["050.04.00.00","Clouds, fog, precipitation: fill in the gaps → Families: high (Ci, Cc, Cs), middle (Ac, As; Ns), low (St, Sc, Cu, Cb). ____: vis < 1 000 m;","Families: high (Ci, Cc, Cs), middle (Ac, As; Ns), low (St, Sc, Cu, Cb). Fog: vis < 1 000 m;"],["050.04.00.00","Clouds, fog, precipitation: fill in the gaps → ____ (clear night, light wind 2–8 kt, moist air, over land; dissipates after sunrise), ____ (warm moist air over cold surface, any time, wind up to ~15 kt), ____ (cold air over warm water), frontal fog, orographic/hill fog. Mist ≥ 1 000 m (humid), haze (dry particles).","radiation fog (clear night, light wind 2–8 kt, moist air, over land; dissipates after sunrise), advection fog (warm moist air over cold surface, any time, wind up to ~15 kt), steam fog (cold air over warm water), frontal fog, orographic/hill fog. Mist ≥ 1 000 m (humid), haze (dry particles)."],["050.04.00.00","Clouds, fog, precipitation: fill in the gaps → Precipitation: drizzle from St, rain/snow from Ns/As (continuous), ____, ____, ____ (warm layer above cold) → severe clear ice. Bergeron process (ice crystals grow at the expense of droplets) in mixed clouds; coalescence in warm clouds.","Precipitation: drizzle from St, rain/snow from Ns/As (continuous), showers from Cu/Cb, hail from Cb, freezing rain (warm layer above cold) → severe clear ice. Bergeron process (ice crystals grow at the expense of droplets) in mixed clouds; coalescence in warm clouds."],["050.06.00.00","Air masses, fronts, pressure systems: fill in the gaps → Air masses: ____, ____; e.g. mP → showers, unstable; cT → hot, dry, dust.","Air masses: maritime/continental, polar/arctic/tropical; e.g. mP → showers, unstable; cT → hot, dry, dust."],["050.07.00.00","Air masses, fronts, pressure systems: fill in the gaps → ____ (slope ~1:150): sequence Ci → Cs → As → Ns, long continuous precipitation ahead, freezing rain risk in winter, low cloud/poor vis; passage: wind ____, T ↑, pressure fall stops. ____ (slope ~1:50–80): Cb, showers, TS, gusty squalls, veer, T/Td drop, pressure rises, good visibility behind.","Warm front (slope ~1:150): sequence Ci → Cs → As → Ns, long continuous precipitation ahead, freezing rain risk in winter, low cloud/poor vis; passage: wind veers, T ↑, pressure fall stops. Cold front (slope ~1:50–80): Cb, showers, TS, gusty squalls, veer, T/Td drop, pressure rises, good visibility behind."],["050.06.00.00","Air masses, fronts, pressure systems: fill in the gaps → ____ (warm/cold type). ____. ____ ahead of cold fronts.","Occlusion (warm/cold type). Stationary front. Squall lines ahead of cold fronts."],["050.06.00.00","Air masses, fronts, pressure systems: fill in the gaps → ____ (wave → mature → occluded), move with the warm-sector wind; ____ (subsidence, stable, fog/haze in winter), ____ (summer, over land), troughs (convergence, weather), ridges, cols; tropical revolving storms (≥ 64 kt = hurricane/typhoon, over warm sea > 26–27 °C, 5–20° latitude, not at the equator).","Polar-front depressions (wave → mature → occluded), move with the warm-sector wind; anticyclones (subsidence, stable, fog/haze in winter), thermal lows (summer, over land), troughs (convergence, weather), ridges, cols; tropical revolving storms (≥ 64 kt = hurricane/typhoon, over warm sea > 26–27 °C, 5–20° latitude, not at the equator)."],["050.08.00.00","Climatology & hazards: fill in the gaps → ITCZ follows the sun (July far north over continents), trades, monsoons (India SW Jun–Sep), local winds; ____ (0 to −15 °C, clear ice large drops, rime small drops, freezing rain worst, carb icing up to +30 °C); ____; ____ (< 1 600 ft low-level);","ITCZ follows the sun (July far north over continents), trades, monsoons (India SW Jun–Sep), local winds; icing (0 to −15 °C, clear ice large drops, rime small drops, freezing rain worst, carb icing up to +30 °C); turbulence/CAT; wind shear (< 1 600 ft low-level);"],["050.08.00.00","Climatology & hazards: fill in the gaps → ____ (cumulus/mature/dissipating, microburst < 4 km, 1–5 min); ____; ____ (fog < 1 000 m, sand/dust).","thunderstorms (cumulus/mature/dissipating, microburst < 4 km, 1–5 min); mountain waves & rotors; visibility (fog < 1 000 m, sand/dust)."],["050.10.00.00","Meteorological information: fill in the gaps → ____ (+TREND 2 h), ____ 9–30 h (BECMG/TEMPO/PROB/FM), ____ (MWO, ≤ 4 h, severe phenomena, TS, VA, TC), AIRMET/GAMET (low level), SWC (low/medium/high), wind/temperature charts, VOLMET, ATIS, AIREP/special air-reports, satellite & radar imagery.","METAR/SPECI (+TREND 2 h), TAF 9–30 h (BECMG/TEMPO/PROB/FM), SIGMET (MWO, ≤ 4 h, severe phenomena, TS, VA, TC), AIRMET/GAMET (low level), SWC (low/medium/high), wind/temperature charts, VOLMET, ATIS, AIREP/special air-reports, satellite & radar imagery."],["050.10.00.00","Meteorological information: fill in the gaps → Decoding essentials: wind true/10-min mean, ____ gusts ≥ 10 kt above mean; ____ (vis ≥ 10 km, no cloud < 5 000 ft or MSA, no CB/TCU, no sig WX); RVR ( R27/0600U ); cloud FEW/SCT/BKN/OVC, ____; VV vertical visibility; NSC , NCD , NSW ; recent weather RE , wind shear WS R27 .","Decoding essentials: wind true/10-min mean, G gusts ≥ 10 kt above mean; CAVOK (vis ≥ 10 km, no cloud < 5 000 ft or MSA, no CB/TCU, no sig WX); RVR ( R27/0600U ); cloud FEW/SCT/BKN/OVC, ceiling = BKN/OVC < 20 000 ft; VV vertical visibility; NSC , NCD , NSW ; recent weather RE , wind shear WS R27 ."],["050.05.00.00","Exam trap (Meteorological information): complete the fact → Top traps: SALR < DALR; fog < ____; warm front → freezing rain ahead; cold front → Cb/showers; wind backs at the surface (NH); CAT on the polar side of the jet; weather radar sees precipitation only.","Top traps: SALR < DALR; fog < 1 000 m; warm front → freezing rain ahead; cold front → Cb/showers; wind backs at the surface (NH); CAT on the polar side of the jet; weather radar sees precipitation only."]],"88":[["061.01.00.00",": what do you remember about this? → FINAL review of 061 General Navigation — calculation cheat …","FINAL review of 061 General Navigation — calculation cheat sheet."],["061.01.00.00","Basics of navigation: fill in the gaps → ____: oblate spheroid (WGS-84), polar diameter ~43 km less than equatorial. Solar system: Earth's orbit elliptical (perihelion ____, aphelion ~4 Jul), axis tilted ____ → seasons; equinoxes ~21 Mar/23 Sep, solstices ~21 Jun/21 Dec.","Earth: oblate spheroid (WGS-84), polar diameter ~43 km less than equatorial. Solar system: Earth's orbit elliptical (perihelion ~3 Jan, aphelion ~4 Jul), axis tilted 23.5° → seasons; equinoxes ~21 Mar/23 Sep, solstices ~21 Jun/21 Dec."],["061.01.00.00","Basics of navigation: fill in the gaps → ____ (plane through the centre, shortest distance) vs ____; rhumb line (constant track, crosses meridians at the same angle). Latitude (0–90° N/S), longitude (0–180° E/W); ____; departure (E–W distance) = Δlong (′) × cos lat.","Great circle (plane through the centre, shortest distance) vs small circle; rhumb line (constant track, crosses meridians at the same angle). Latitude (0–90° N/S), longitude (0–180° E/W); 1′ of latitude = 1 NM; departure (E–W distance) = Δlong (′) × cos lat."],["061.01.00.00","Basics of navigation: fill in the gaps → ____ = Δlong × sin(mean lat); ____ ____; GC track changes, RL track constant; NH: GC polewards of RL.","Convergency = Δlong × sin(mean lat); conversion angle = ½ convergency; GC track changes, RL track constant; NH: GC polewards of RL."],["061.01.00.00","Basics of navigation: fill in the gaps → ____: true, magnetic, compass, grid. ____ (E/W) — \"East is least, West is best\" (TT → MT); ____ (CH → MH); isogonals, agonic line; grid north on polar charts (grivation = grid variation).","Directions: true, magnetic, compass, grid. Variation (E/W) — \"East is least, West is best\" (TT → MT); deviation (CH → MH); isogonals, agonic line; grid north on polar charts (grivation = grid variation)."],["061.01.00.00","Basics of navigation: fill in the gaps → ____: ____, 1 SM = 1 609 m, ____; 1 m = 3.28 ft.","Distance units: 1 NM = 1 852 m = 6 076 ft, 1 SM = 1 609 m, 1 km = 0.54 NM; 1 m = 3.28 ft."],["061.05.00.00","Time & DR: fill in the gaps → ____: 15° = 1 h, 1° = 4 min. ____ W −); standard time via tables; date line westbound ____. Sunrise/sunset/civil twilight (−6°) from tables in LMT, day length changes fastest at equinoxes.","Arc–time: 15° = 1 h, 1° = 4 min. LMT = UTC ± longitude (E +, W −); standard time via tables; date line westbound +1 day. Sunrise/sunset/civil twilight (−6°) from tables in LMT, day length changes fastest at equinoxes."],["061.01.00.00","Time & DR: fill in the gaps → ____: HDG/TAS + W/V = TR/GS; drift ≈ XW × 60/TAS; ____ ____: TE = off × 60/dist flown, CA = off × 60/dist to go.","TOV: HDG/TAS + W/V = TR/GS; drift ≈ XW × 60/TAS; 1-in-60 rule: TE = off × 60/dist flown, CA = off × 60/dist to go."],["061.05.00.00","Time & DR: fill in the gaps → ____: TAS from CAS (pressure alt + OAT), density altitude, time–speed–distance, fuel, unit conversions, ____ ____.","CRP/E6-B: TAS from CAS (pressure alt + OAT), density altitude, time–speed–distance, fuel, unit conversions, Mach no. = TAS/(38.95 √T K)."],["061.01.00.00","Time & DR: fill in the gaps → ____: ROD (ft/min) = GS × gradient × 101 (≈ GS × 5 for 3°); ____ = height/(gradient); average wind/TAS on climb at ~⅔ of the altitude band.","Climb/descent: ROD (ft/min) = GS × gradient × 101 (≈ GS × 5 for 3°); distance to descend = height/(gradient); average wind/TAS on climb at ~⅔ of the altitude band."],["061.02.00.00","VFR navigation: what do you remember about this? → Map reading (chart-to-ground, line + point features), DR between …","Map reading (chart-to-ground, line + point features), DR between checkpoints, ETA revision, 1-in-60 corrections, lost procedure (5 C's), diversion technique."],["061.03.00.00","Great circles & rhumb lines: fill in the gaps → GC initial track ≠ final track (changes by convergency); along a meridian or the equator GC = RL. Departure formula for E–W distances; ____ = rhumb line.","GC initial track ≠ final track (changes by convergency); along a meridian or the equator GC = RL. Departure formula for E–W distances; flying east along a parallel = rhumb line."],["061.03.00.00","Great circles & rhumb lines: fill in the gaps → Example: 60°N, from 010°W to 020°E = 30° Δlong = 1 800′ × cos 60 = ____.","Example: 60°N, from 010°W to 020°E = 30° Δlong = 1 800′ × cos 60 = 900 NM."],["061.04.00.00","Charts – Conformal: what are the key facts?","Mercator: yes · Lambert: yes · Polar stereographic: yes"],["061.04.00.00","Charts – Meridians: what are the key facts?","Mercator: parallel straight · Lambert: straight, converging · Polar stereographic: straight, radiating"],["061.04.00.00","Charts – Great circle: what are the key facts?","Mercator: curve (concave to equator) · Lambert: ≈ straight · Polar stereographic: ≈ straight"],["061.04.00.00","Charts – Rhumb line: what are the key facts?","Mercator: straight · Lambert: curve · Polar stereographic: curve"],["061.04.00.00","Charts – Scale: what are the key facts?","Mercator: ∝ sec lat (↑ with lat) · Lambert: correct on std parallels, < 1 % error between · Polar stereographic: ↑ away from pole"],["061.04.00.00","Charts – Chart convergence: what are the key facts?","Mercator: 0 · Lambert: Δlong × sin PO (n) · Polar stereographic: Δlong (n = 1)"],["061.04.00.00","Charts: what do you remember about this? → Scale: chart length × denominator = earth distance; 1:500 000 → 1 cm …","Scale: chart length × denominator = earth distance; 1:500 000 → 1 cm = 5 km = 2.7 NM."],["061.04.00.00","Charts: fill in the gaps → Plotting: VOR radial with ____, NDB bearing (QDM = MH + RB) with ____; DME slant range; measure tracks at mid-meridian.","Plotting: VOR radial with variation at the station, NDB bearing (QDM = MH + RB) with variation at the aircraft; DME slant range; measure tracks at mid-meridian."],["061.01.00.00","Standard calculation checklist: fill in the gaps → Convert everything to the same units (NM, kt, ft); watch ____ ____ and ____; check E/W signs; check reasonableness (GS vs TAS with wind, drift direction).","Convert everything to the same units (NM, kt, ft); watch true vs magnetic and UTC vs local; check E/W signs; check reasonableness (GS vs TAS with wind, drift direction)."]],"89":[["033.06.00.00",": what do you remember about this? → FINAL review of 033 Flight Planning & Monitoring (all FPM chapters, …","FINAL review of 033 Flight Planning & Monitoring (all FPM chapters, GSPRM incl. ATC tab, CAP 697). See #63 for the fuel/PET/PSR cheat sheet."],["033.01.00.00","VFR flight planning: fill in the gaps → Chart work on ____ charts: true track (mid-meridian), distance, ____, heading/GS with forecast wind, leg times, ____ (terrain + margin), airspace (controlled/restricted), frequencies, VFR reporting points, semicircular VFR levels (magnetic track 000–179° → odd + 500 ft: 3 500, 5 500…; 180–359° → even + 500 ft).","Chart work on GSPRM VFR charts: true track (mid-meridian), distance, MT = TT ∓ var, heading/GS with forecast wind, leg times, minimum altitudes (terrain + margin), airspace (controlled/restricted), frequencies, VFR reporting points, semicircular VFR levels (magnetic track 000–179° → odd + 500 ft: 3 500, 5 500…; 180–359° → even + 500 ft)."],["033.01.00.00","VFR flight planning: fill in the gaps → Fuel (SEP/MEP tables CAP 697): climb (time/fuel/distance from the table ____ between altitudes), cruise (power setting → TAS, FF), descent; reserves (30/45 min day/night NCO).","Fuel (SEP/MEP tables CAP 697): climb (time/fuel/distance from the table difference between altitudes), cruise (power setting → TAS, FF), descent; reserves (30/45 min day/night NCO)."],["033.02.00.00","IFR flight planning: fill in the gaps → ____: airways (designator, MEA, MOCA, MRA, MAA, magnetic track, distance, direction of flight), ____, FIR/UIR boundaries, conditional routes; choose levels (IFR semicircular: odd 000–179°, even 180– 359°; RVSM FL 290–410), check ____ in cold weather.","GSPRM IFR en-route/SID/STAR/approach charts: airways (designator, MEA, MOCA, MRA, MAA, magnetic track, distance, direction of flight), grid MORA, FIR/UIR boundaries, conditional routes; choose levels (IFR semicircular: odd 000–179°, even 180– 359°; RVSM FL 290–410), check true altitude in cold weather."],["033.02.00.00","IFR flight planning: fill in the gaps → SID/STAR distances, speed constraints; approach minima (DA/MDA, RVR) and ____ for alternates.","SID/STAR distances, speed constraints; approach minima (DA/MDA, RVR) and planning minima for alternates."],["033.02.00.00","IFR flight planning: fill in the gaps → ____ per leg: track, distance, level, TAS, wind, drift, heading, GS, time, fuel, cumulative values; alternate leg; totals.","Nav log per leg: track, distance, level, TAS, wind, drift, heading, GS, time, fuel, cumulative values; alternate leg; totals."],["033.03.00.00","Fuel planning: fill in the gaps → Taxi, trip, ____, alternate, ____ ____, additional, extra; RCF/decision point, isolated aerodrome (2 h), PDP, tankering.","Taxi, trip, contingency (5 % / 5 min holding), alternate, final reserve (30 min turbine/45 min piston at 1 500 ft), additional, extra; RCF/decision point, isolated aerodrome (2 h), PDP, tankering."],["033.03.00.00","Fuel planning: fill in the gaps → CAP 697 MRJT: ____ (NAM, level, landing mass → trip fuel/time), ____, ____ (final reserve), integrated range tables (NAM between masses), non-standard temperature corrections.","CAP 697 MRJT: simplified flight planning (NAM, level, landing mass → trip fuel/time), alternate planning, holding (final reserve), integrated range tables (NAM between masses), non-standard temperature corrections."],["033.04.00.00","Pre-flight preparation: fill in the gaps → ____ (aerodromes, NAVAIDs, airspace), ____ (TAF/METAR, SWC, wind charts, SIGMET) → planning minima (____ ft, CAT I → NPA values, CAT II/III → CAT I, ETA ± 1 h), TAF change-group rules (TEMPO persistent phenomena count, showers/TS may be disregarded), update nav plan & fuel log.","NOTAM (aerodromes, NAVAIDs, airspace), MET (TAF/METAR, SWC, wind charts, SIGMET) → planning minima (NPA +1 000 m/+200 ft, CAT I → NPA values, CAT II/III → CAT I, ETA ± 1 h), TAF change-group rules (TEMPO persistent phenomena count, showers/TS may be disregarded), update nav plan & fuel log."],["033.04.00.00","Pre-flight preparation: fill in the gaps → ____ = D × H/(O + H) (moves ____); ____ time = E × H/(O + H) (reduced by any wind).","PET = D × H/(O + H) (moves into wind); PSR time = E × H/(O + H) (reduced by any wind)."],["033.02.00.00","ATS flight plan: fill in the gaps → Items 7, 8 (____, S/N/G/M/X), 9 (type/____), 10 (equipment/surveillance), 13 (dep + EOBT), 15 (speed N/M/K, level F/A, route), 16 (dest + EET + altn), 18 (PBN/, DOF/, REG/, RMK/…), 19 (not transmitted: endurance, POB, survival equipment). File ≥ ____ before EOBT; delays → DLA/CHG; RPL for regular IFR flights.","Items 7, 8 (I/V/Y/Z, S/N/G/M/X), 9 (type/WTC L/M/H/J), 10 (equipment/surveillance), 13 (dep + EOBT), 15 (speed N/M/K, level F/A, route), 16 (dest + EET + altn), 18 (PBN/, DOF/, REG/, RMK/…), 19 (not transmitted: endurance, POB, survival equipment). File ≥ 60 min before EOBT; delays → DLA/CHG; RPL for regular IFR flights."],["033.06.00.00","Flight monitoring & in-flight replanning: fill in the gaps → Monitor at each waypoint: ____ → revised ETAs, ____ vs required (alternate + final reserve). Trends: increased burn → slower/lower/alternate change/diversion. Fuel calculations in flight: ____; endurance = fuel on board ÷ FF.","Monitor at each waypoint: actual vs planned time and fuel → revised ETAs, fuel remaining at destination vs required (alternate + final reserve). Trends: increased burn → slower/lower/alternate change/diversion. Fuel calculations in flight: fuel required = remaining trip time × FF + reserves; endurance = fuel on board ÷ FF."],["033.06.00.00","Flight monitoring & in-flight replanning: fill in the gaps → ____: decision point procedure en route, re-clearance to closer destination, diversion — recompute trip + contingency + alternate + final reserve from the present position. Declare ____ per rules.","Replanning: decision point procedure en route, re-clearance to closer destination, diversion — recompute trip + contingency + alternate + final reserve from the present position. Declare MINIMUM FUEL / MAYDAY FUEL per rules."],["033.01.00.00","GSPRM \"Air Traffic Control\" tab essentials: fill in the gaps → Flight plan filing, IFPS addresses, ____ for some flows / 60 min minimum, ____ requirements, slot allocation (CTOT −5/+10 min tolerance), transponder codes (7500/7600/7700, 7000 VFR, 2000), COM failure procedures, speed limit ____ (airspace C–G), semicircular levels, VMC minima table.","Flight plan filing, IFPS addresses, FPL submission ≥ 3 h for some flows / 60 min minimum, RVSM requirements, slot allocation (CTOT −5/+10 min tolerance), transponder codes (7500/7600/7700, 7000 VFR, 2000), COM failure procedures, speed limit 250 kt below FL 100 (airspace C–G), semicircular levels, VMC minima table."],["033.01.00.00","Exam trap (GSPRM \"Air Traffic Control\" tab essentials): complete the fact → Top traps: use the difference in CAP ____climb tables; NGM → NAM before MRJT tables; final reserve at arrival mass; planning minima apply to alternates; slot window −____+10 min.","Top traps: use the difference in CAP 697 climb tables; NGM → NAM before MRJT tables; final reserve at arrival mass; planning minima apply to alternates; slot window −5/+10 min."]],"90":[["010.02.02.01:03","Airworthiness — CofA: fill in the gaps → ____ (EU: competent authority of the Member State of registry, per ____, Reg. (EU) 748/2012). Every aircraft engaged in international air navigation must hold a ____ (Chicago Conv. Art. 31) — carried on board.","Issued by the State of Registry (EU: competent authority of the Member State of registry, per Part-21, Reg. (EU) 748/2012). Every aircraft engaged in international air navigation must hold a valid CofA (Chicago Conv. Art. 31) — carried on board."],["010.02.02.01:04","Airworthiness — CofA: fill in the gaps → Prerequisites: aircraft conforms to an approved ____ ____, is in a ____ (statement of conformity, airworthiness review/inspection), registered, with an Airworthiness Review Certificate (ARC).","Prerequisites: aircraft conforms to an approved type design (type certificate), is in a condition for safe operation (statement of conformity, airworthiness review/inspection), registered, with an Airworthiness Review Certificate (ARC)."],["010.02.02.01:04","Airworthiness — CofA: fill in the gaps → ____ is determined by the ____ ____ (owner/operator/CAMO responsible for maintenance per Part-M/Part-CAMO).","Continuing airworthiness is determined by the State of Registry (owner/operator/CAMO responsible for maintenance per Part-M/Part-CAMO)."],["010.02.02.01:05","Airworthiness — CofA: fill in the gaps → CofA has ____ provided the aircraft is maintained in accordance with the regulations and has a ____ (renewed annually, extendable by a CAMO twice → 3 years); invalid if suspended/revoked or the aircraft is modified without approval.","CofA has unlimited duration provided the aircraft is maintained in accordance with the regulations and has a valid ARC (renewed annually, extendable by a CAMO twice → 3 years); invalid if suspended/revoked or the aircraft is modified without approval."],["010.06.04.01:08","PANS-OPS definitions (Doc 8168 Vol I): fill in the gaps → ____ (decision altitude/height, precision & APV): altitude/height at which a missed approach must be initiated if the required visual reference is not established. ____ (NPA/circling): below which descent must not be made without the required visual reference.","DA/H (decision altitude/height, precision & APV): altitude/height at which a missed approach must be initiated if the required visual reference is not established. MDA/H (NPA/circling): below which descent must not be made without the required visual reference."],["010.06.04.01:11","PANS-OPS definitions (Doc 8168 Vol I): fill in the gaps → ____: lowest altitude/height above the relevant threshold/aerodrome elevation used to establish compliance with obstacle-clearance criteria. ____: minimum obstacle clearance margin. ____ referenced to MSL, DH/MDH to threshold (or aerodrome) elevation.","OCA/H: lowest altitude/height above the relevant threshold/aerodrome elevation used to establish compliance with obstacle-clearance criteria. MOC: minimum obstacle clearance margin. DA/MDA referenced to MSL, DH/MDH to threshold (or aerodrome) elevation."],["010.06.04.01:11","PANS-OPS definitions (Doc 8168 Vol I): fill in the gaps → Relationship: ____, set by the operator taking into account operational factors; OCA/H = obstacle + MOC.","Relationship: DA/H or MDA/H ≥ OCA/H, set by the operator taking into account operational factors; OCA/H = obstacle + MOC."],["010.06.04.02:03","PANS-OPS definitions (Doc 8168 Vol I): what do you remember about this? → Others: IAF, IF, FAF, FAP, MAPt, SOC (start of climb), TP (turning …","Others: IAF, IF, FAF, FAP, MAPt, SOC (start of climb), TP (turning point), MSA, PDG (procedure design gradient), DER (departure end of runway), EAT, holding fix, RNAV terms."],["010.06.06.01:09","Departures: fill in the gaps → Design factors: ____, aircraft performance (____ = 2.5 % obstacle identification surface + 0.8 % MOC), ATC requirements, noise abatement, navigation aids. Turns not before ____ above DER elevation (unless specified).","Design factors: terrain and obstacles, aircraft performance (PDG 3.3 % = 2.5 % obstacle identification surface + 0.8 % MOC), ATC requirements, noise abatement, navigation aids. Turns not before 120 m (394 ft) above DER elevation (unless specified)."],["010.06.03.02:01","Departures: fill in the gaps → ____: initial departure track within ____ of the runway alignment. ____: > 15°.","Straight departure: initial departure track within 15° of the runway alignment. Turning departure: > 15°."],["010.06.04.01:05","Departures: fill in the gaps → ____: where no specific track guidance/SID is provided — climb straight ahead to ≥ 120 m then turn in ____ to join the en-route structure, possibly with sector restrictions (higher PDG or minimum turn altitudes in some sectors).","Omnidirectional departure: where no specific track guidance/SID is provided — climb straight ahead to ≥ 120 m then turn in any direction to join the en-route structure, possibly with sector restrictions (higher PDG or minimum turn altitudes in some sectors)."],["010.06.04.03:05","Approach — general criteria: fill in the gaps → ____: ____, ____, intermediate, final, missed approach.","Five segments: arrival, initial, intermediate, final, missed approach."],["010.06.04.01:01","Approach — general criteria: fill in the gaps → Areas: primary area (full MOC across its width) and secondary areas either side (MOC reduces linearly to zero at the outer edge). Fix tolerance from facility accuracy, intersection angles. Facility accuracy: ____, ____, ____.","Areas: primary area (full MOC across its width) and secondary areas either side (MOC reduces linearly to zero at the outer edge). Fix tolerance from facility accuracy, intersection angles. Facility accuracy: ILS LOC ±1.4°, VOR ±5.2°, NDB ±6.2°."],["010.06.04.01:07","Approach — general criteria: fill in the gaps → Aircraft categories (VAT = 1.3 VS0 or 1.23 VSR0): ____, B ____, ____, D 141–165, E 166–210 kt — because speed determines the turn radius and airspace/visibility needed → protected areas, circling areas and minima differ. The most significant factor influencing the conduct of instrument approaches = aircraft speed (performance).","Aircraft categories (VAT = 1.3 VS0 or 1.23 VSR0): A < 91 kt, B 91–120, C 121–140, D 141–165, E 166–210 kt — because speed determines the turn radius and airspace/visibility needed → protected areas, circling areas and minima differ. The most significant factor influencing the conduct of instrument approaches = aircraft speed (performance)."],["010.06.04.01:04","Approach — general criteria: fill in the gaps → ____ approach: final approach track within ____ of the runway centre line (NPA); otherwise circling.","Straight-in approach: final approach track within 30° of the runway centre line (NPA); otherwise circling."],["010.06.04.01:01","Approach — general criteria: fill in the gaps → ____: provides ____ within ____ of the reference facility/fix (sectors).","MSA: provides 300 m (1 000 ft) MOC within 25 NM of the reference facility/fix (sectors)."],["010.06.04.01:06","Approach — general criteria: fill in the gaps → Pilots must apply ____ (drift, timing) to stay within the protected area.","Pilots must apply wind corrections (drift, timing) to stay within the protected area."],["010.06.04.01:08","Approach — general criteria: fill in the gaps → Don't descend below ____: it is the minimum that guarantees obstacle clearance — for precision (glide path + OAS surfaces), NPA (MDA/H over the final segment), circling (per category), APV.","Don't descend below OCA/H: it is the minimum that guarantees obstacle clearance — for precision (glide path + OAS surfaces), NPA (MDA/H over the final segment), circling (per category), APV."],["010.06.04.05:03","Approach — general criteria: fill in the gaps → ____: OCA/H, aircraft category & equipment, crew qualification/experience, runway & approach/visual aids, ATC, meteorological conditions (RVR/visibility, ceiling), go-around performance, obstacles in missed approach.","Operational minima factors: OCA/H, aircraft category & equipment, crew qualification/experience, runway & approach/visual aids, ATC, meteorological conditions (RVR/visibility, ceiling), go-around performance, obstacles in missed approach."],["010.06.04.02:05","Approach — general criteria: fill in the gaps → ____ (final, precision): ____ (max 6.5 % NPA / 3.5° standard).","Optimum descent gradient (final, precision): 5.2 % = 3.0° (max 6.5 % NPA / 3.5° standard)."],["010.06.04.03:04","Segments: fill in the gaps → Arrival: from en-route to the IAF. Initial: IAF → IF — manoeuvre to intercept the intermediate track (MOC ____). Max interception angle initial→intermediate at the IF: ____, ____.","Arrival: from en-route to the IAF. Initial: IAF → IF — manoeuvre to intercept the intermediate track (MOC 300 m/984 ft). Max interception angle initial→intermediate at the IF: 90° precision, 120° non-precision."],["010.06.04.02:03","Segments: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ — adjust speed, configuration and position for final (MOC ____, then reducing). Final: FAF/FAP → MAPt — alignment and descent for landing; aims: straight-in landing or circling. FAP (ILS): point where the intermediate altitude intercepts the nominal glide path.","Intermediate: IF → FAF/FAP — adjust speed, configuration and position for final (MOC 150 m/492 ft, then reducing). Final: FAF/FAP → MAPt — alignment and descent for landing; aims: straight-in landing or circling. FAP (ILS): point where the intermediate altitude intercepts the nominal glide path."],["010.06.04.03:09","Segments: fill in the gaps → ____ → becomes a ____ ____ approach with higher OCA/H/MDA/H.","GP inoperative during ILS approach → becomes a LOC-only (non-precision) approach with higher OCA/H/MDA/H."],["010.06.05.01:06","Missed approach: fill in the gaps → Main task: provide protection from obstacles during the go-around. Three phases: initial (MAPt → SOC, no turns, climb begins), intermediate (from SOC, climb straight ahead until ____ ____ MOC is obtained and can be maintained; nominal gradient ____), final (to a point where a new approach, holding or en-route flight begins; turns allowed).","Main task: provide protection from obstacles during the go-around. Three phases: initial (MAPt → SOC, no turns, climb begins), intermediate (from SOC, climb straight ahead until 50 m (164 ft) MOC is obtained and can be maintained; nominal gradient 2.5 %), final (to a point where a new approach, holding or en-route flight begins; turns allowed)."],["010.06.04.04:03","Missed approach: fill in the gaps → ____: point at which the missed approach must be initiated if visual reference isn't established. Precision/APV: intersection of glide path with ____; NPA: a ____ or a distance (timing) from the FAF.","MAPt: point at which the missed approach must be initiated if visual reference isn't established. Precision/APV: intersection of glide path with DA/H; NPA: a fix/facility or a distance (timing) from the FAF."],["010.06.04.04:04","Missed approach: fill in the gaps → At MAPt without visual reference → ____ ____. If initiated ____ → fly to the MAPt (may climb) and then follow the published procedure (no early turns). Crossing the MAPt higher than required is permitted.","At MAPt without visual reference → execute the missed approach. If initiated before the MAPt → fly to the MAPt (may climb) and then follow the published procedure (no early turns). Crossing the MAPt higher than required is permitted."],["010.06.04.05:02","Circling (visual manoeuvring): fill in the gaps → Visual phase after an instrument approach to bring the aircraft into position for landing on a runway ____ ____. A ____ in the circling area outside the final/missed approach areas may be excluded by prohibiting circling in that sector.","Visual phase after an instrument approach to bring the aircraft into position for landing on a runway not suitably located for a straight-in. A prominent obstacle in the circling area outside the final/missed approach areas may be excluded by prohibiting circling in that sector."],["010.06.04.01:11","Circling (visual manoeuvring): fill in the gaps → OCA/H by category (MOC): ____, ____ ft), ____; lowest OCH A 120 m, B 150 m, C 180 m, D 210 m, E 240 m; MDA/H ≥ OCA/H (and not below the straight-in NPA minima).","OCA/H by category (MOC): A/B 90 m (295 ft), C/D 120 m (394 ft), E 150 m (492 ft); lowest OCH A 120 m, B 150 m, C 180 m, D 210 m, E 240 m; MDA/H ≥ OCA/H (and not below the straight-in NPA minima)."],["010.06.04.05:06","Circling (visual manoeuvring): fill in the gaps → Descend below MDA/H only when: ____ ____, ____, obstacle clearance can be maintained, in a position to land. No single procedure fits every wind/runway/obstacle situation → pilot judgement within the circling area.","Descend below MDA/H only when: visual reference established and maintained, runway environment/threshold in sight, obstacle clearance can be maintained, in a position to land. No single procedure fits every wind/runway/obstacle situation → pilot judgement within the circling area."],["010.06.04.05:07","Circling (visual manoeuvring): fill in the gaps → After visual contact: keep the runway environment in sight, stay within the circling area at MDA until descent. ____ ____: initiate a ____ to overhead, then follow the missed approach procedure of the instrument approach.","After visual contact: keep the runway environment in sight, stay within the circling area at MDA until descent. Visual reference lost: initiate a climbing turn towards the landing runway to overhead, then follow the missed approach procedure of the instrument approach."],["010.06.05.01:01","Holding: what do you remember about this? → Deviations are dangerous (protected area based on standard procedure) …","Deviations are dangerous (protected area based on standard procedure) → inform ATC early if unable to comply."],["010.06.05.01:07","Holding: fill in the gaps → Pattern: ____ (standard), holding fix, inbound/outbound legs, turns. ____, whichever requires less bank. Maintain tracks by applying wind corrections (double/triple drift on the outbound leg, adjust outbound time to get ____ inbound).","Pattern: right-hand (standard), holding fix, inbound/outbound legs, turns. Bank 25° or rate 3°/s, whichever requires less bank. Maintain tracks by applying wind corrections (double/triple drift on the outbound leg, adjust outbound time to get 1 min inbound)."],["010.06.05.01:07","Holding: fill in the gaps → Outbound timing starts over or abeam the fix, whichever occurs later (or at completion of the outbound turn if abeam can't be determined). Outbound leg ____ at/below ____, ____ above. With DME: outbound ends at the specified DME distance.","Outbound timing starts over or abeam the fix, whichever occurs later (or at completion of the outbound turn if abeam can't be determined). Outbound leg 1 min at/below 14 000 ft, 1.5 min above. With DME: outbound ends at the specified DME distance."],["010.06.05.01:09","Holding: fill in the gaps → Entry sectors (relative to the inbound track, ±5° flexibility): ____ (110° sector on the non-holding side), ____ (70° sector, 30° offset), ____ direct (180°). Outbound entry time 1 min / 1.5 min (still air) or to the DME limit.","Entry sectors (relative to the inbound track, ±5° flexibility): Sector 1 — parallel (110° sector on the non-holding side), Sector 2 — offset/teardrop (70° sector, 30° offset), Sector 3 — direct (180°). Outbound entry time 1 min / 1.5 min (still air) or to the DME limit."],["010.06.05.01:10","Holding: fill in the gaps → Given a ____, adjust the pattern to leave the fix at that time.","Given a time to leave the holding, adjust the pattern to leave the fix at that time."],["010.06.05.01:01","Holding: fill in the gaps → Speeds (ICAO): ≤ 14 000 ft ____ (170 kt Cat A/B), 14 000–20 000 ft ____, 20 000–34 000 ft ____, > 34 000 ft M 0.83.","Speeds (ICAO): ≤ 14 000 ft 230 kt (170 kt Cat A/B), 14 000–20 000 ft 240 kt, 20 000–34 000 ft 265 kt, > 34 000 ft M 0.83."],["010.06.05.02:02","Holding: fill in the gaps → Obstacle clearance: holding area + ____; minimum holding level gives ____ MOC above obstacles in the holding area, reduced in the buffer; over high terrain/mountainous areas up to ____.","Obstacle clearance: holding area + buffer area (5 NM); minimum holding level gives 300 m (984 ft) MOC above obstacles in the holding area, reduced in the buffer; over high terrain/mountainous areas up to 600 m (1 969 ft)."],["010.06.06.01:01","Altimeter settings: fill in the gaps → Objectives: ____ and ____. ____: altimeter reads altitude (elevation on ground); QFE: reads height above aerodrome/threshold.","Objectives: terrain clearance and vertical separation. QNH: altimeter reads altitude (elevation on ground); QFE: reads height above aerodrome/threshold."],["010.06.06.01:14","Altimeter settings: fill in the gaps → FL: surface of constant pressure related to ____, separated by ____ intervals; ____ at the 1013.2 hPa level. Transition altitude (TA): at or below which vertical position is expressed as altitude; specified for each aerodrome (not below 3 000 ft), rounded up to full 1 000 ft, published in the AIP and charts.","FL: surface of constant pressure related to 1013.2 hPa, separated by 500 ft intervals; FL 0 at the 1013.2 hPa level. Transition altitude (TA): at or below which vertical position is expressed as altitude; specified for each aerodrome (not below 3 000 ft), rounded up to full 1 000 ft, published in the AIP and charts."],["010.06.06.01:08","Altimeter settings: fill in the gaps → ____: lowest FL available above TA; passed in the ____. ____: between TA and TRL. Climbing through the layer → report FL; descending → report altitude.","Transition level (TRL): lowest FL available above TA; passed in the approach clearance/ATIS. Transition layer: between TA and TRL. Climbing through the layer → report FL; descending → report altitude."],["010.06.06.01:20","Altimeter settings: fill in the gaps → ____ in the ____ (or before take-off). En-route at/below TA → altitudes; above → flight levels. ____ so terrain clearance can be assured. Approaching aircraft receive QNH in the approach clearance/ATIS. Above the TRL, altitude may be used when cleared to an altitude on approach (after QNH is set, e.g. vectors).","QNH to departing aircraft in the taxi clearance (or before take-off). En-route at/below TA → altitudes; above → flight levels. QNH reports from sufficient locations so terrain clearance can be assured. Approaching aircraft receive QNH in the approach clearance/ATIS. Above the TRL, altitude may be used when cleared to an altitude on approach (after QNH is set, e.g. vectors)."],["010.06.06.02:01","Altimeter settings: fill in the gaps → ____: at least one altimeter set to ____ before take-off; change to ____ when passing the TA in the climb; obtain TRL before descent (ATIS/approach clearance) and the actual QNH before descending below the TRL; change to QNH when passing the TRL in descent.","Pilots: at least one altimeter set to QNH before take-off; change to 1013.2 when passing the TA in the climb; obtain TRL before descent (ATIS/approach clearance) and the actual QNH before descending below the TRL; change to QNH when passing the TRL in descent."],["010.06.07.01:01","Parallel runways: fill in the gaps → ____ parallel approaches (mode 1): no radar separation required between aircraft on adjacent ILS — requires ____ monitoring (runways ≥ 1 035 m apart); dependent (mode 2): minimum ____ between aircraft on adjacent localisers (≥ 915 m).","Independent parallel approaches (mode 1): no radar separation required between aircraft on adjacent ILS — requires NTZ monitoring (runways ≥ 1 035 m apart); dependent (mode 2): minimum diagonal radar separation (2 NM) between aircraft on adjacent localisers (≥ 915 m)."],["010.06.07.01:02","Parallel runways: fill in the gaps → ____ (mode 3), ____ operations (mode 4: one runway approaches, other departures), ____ (one runway mixed), mixed (both approaches and departures on both).","Independent departures (mode 3), segregated operations (mode 4: one runway approaches, other departures), semi-mixed (one runway mixed), mixed (both approaches and departures on both)."],["010.06.07.01:03","Parallel runways: fill in the gaps → ____ (normal operating zone): airspace either side of each localiser within which deviations are acceptable. ____ (no transgression zone): corridor ≥ ____ wide between the NOZs — entering it triggers controller intervention.","NOZ (normal operating zone): airspace either side of each localiser within which deviations are acceptable. NTZ (no transgression zone): corridor ≥ 610 m wide between the NOZs — entering it triggers controller intervention."],["010.06.07.01:07","Parallel runways: fill in the gaps → Avionics: ILS/MLS/GLS (appropriate for the approach) — standard. Guidance: ____. Radar: separate monitoring controllers with radar update ≤ 5 s (high-precision) where spacing requires; possible in all weather within limits (IMC). Localiser intercept angle ≤ ____, with ≥ 1 NM straight before interception. Missed approach/departure tracks ____.","Avionics: ILS/MLS/GLS (appropriate for the approach) — standard. Guidance: ICAO Doc 9643 (SOIR manual). Radar: separate monitoring controllers with radar update ≤ 5 s (high-precision) where spacing requires; possible in all weather within limits (IMC). Localiser intercept angle ≤ 30°, with ≥ 1 NM straight before interception. Missed approach/departure tracks diverge by ≥ 30°."],["010.06.08.01:05","Transponder & ACAS: fill in the gaps → Operate the transponder at all times in flight (and on the ground where SMR/MLAT) regardless of airspace; Mode C continuously unless directed; code ____ (IFR, no assignment; Europe VFR ____); IDENT only on ATC request. ____ emergency, 7600 COM failure, 7500 unlawful interference. Mode S operated whenever a transponder is required.","Operate the transponder at all times in flight (and on the ground where SMR/MLAT) regardless of airspace; Mode C continuously unless directed; code 2000 (IFR, no assignment; Europe VFR 7000); IDENT only on ATC request. 7700 emergency, 7600 COM failure, 7500 unlawful interference. Mode S operated whenever a transponder is required."],["010.06.08.01:07","Transponder & ACAS: fill in the gaps → Transponder ____: ATC tries to allow continuation to destination per plan; ____ unserviceable and no repair possible → inform ATS, get permission to fly to the nearest aerodrome where it can be repaired (may be excluded from some airspace).","Transponder failure in flight: ATC tries to allow continuation to destination per plan; before departure unserviceable and no repair possible → inform ATS, get permission to fly to the nearest aerodrome where it can be repaired (may be excluded from some airspace)."],["010.06.08.02:05","Transponder & ACAS: fill in the gaps → ____ purpose: reduce the risk of mid-air collision (last resort, independent of ATC). Following RAs is ____ (unless unsafe). ____ — TA gives no vertical guidance, bearing is inaccurate; a TA alerts crew to look out and prepare for a possible RA.","ACAS purpose: reduce the risk of mid-air collision (last resort, independent of ATC). Following RAs is mandatory (unless unsafe). Don't manoeuvre on TAs only — TA gives no vertical guidance, bearing is inaccurate; a TA alerts crew to look out and prepare for a possible RA."],["010.06.08.02:06","Transponder & ACAS: fill in the gaps → Follow RAs ____ (within ~5 s, 0.25 g) even if contrary to ATC — coordinated with the other aircraft's RA. May disregard only when: intruder ____ and no threat, or a ____ (stall, wind shear, GPWS/TAWS) takes precedence.","Follow RAs immediately (within ~5 s, 0.25 g) even if contrary to ATC — coordinated with the other aircraft's RA. May disregard only when: intruder positively identified visually and no threat, or a higher-priority warning (stall, wind shear, GPWS/TAWS) takes precedence."],["010.06.08.01:01","Transponder & ACAS: fill in the gaps → Inform ATC ____ as soon as possible; after ____ return promptly to the ATC clearance and report \"clear of conflict, returning to…\".","Inform ATC \"TCAS RA\" as soon as possible; after \"Clear of conflict\" return promptly to the ATC clearance and report \"clear of conflict, returning to…\"."],["010.06.09.03:01","Reg. (EU) 965/2012: fill in the gaps → Lays down rules for ____ — covers ____ ____ operations. Annexes: I definitions, II Part-ARO, III Part-ORO, IV Part-CAT, V Part-SPA, VI Part- NCC, VII Part-NCO, VIII Part-SPO.","Lays down rules for air operations — covers all commercial and non-commercial operations. Annexes: I definitions, II Part-ARO, III Part-ORO, IV Part-CAT, V Part-SPA, VI Part- NCC, VII Part-NCO, VIII Part-SPO."],["010.06.09.01:02","Reg. (EU) 965/2012: fill in the gaps → ____: operations requiring ____ (PBN, MNPS/NAT HLA, RVSM, ____, ETOPS, dangerous goods, NVIS, HHO, HEMS, EFB…). Part-NCC: non-commercial operations with complex motor-powered aircraft; Part-NCO: non-commercial operations with other-than-complex aircraft.","Part-SPA: operations requiring specific approval (PBN, MNPS/NAT HLA, RVSM, LVO, ETOPS, dangerous goods, NVIS, HHO, HEMS, EFB…). Part-NCC: non-commercial operations with complex motor-powered aircraft; Part-NCO: non-commercial operations with other-than-complex aircraft."],["010.11.01.01:01","Search & rescue (ICAO Annex 12): fill in the gaps → ____ = generic term for ____, alert or distress phase. INCERFA (uncertainty): no communication within ____ after a communication should have been received / aircraft fails to arrive within 30 min of ETA.","Emergency phase = generic term for uncertainty, alert or distress phase. INCERFA (uncertainty): no communication within 30 min after a communication should have been received / aircraft fails to arrive within 30 min of ETA."],["010.11.01.01:01","Search & rescue (ICAO Annex 12): fill in the gaps → ____ (alert): after uncertainty, further attempts fail; aircraft ____ ____ of estimated time and no contact; information that operating efficiency is impaired; unlawful interference. DETRESFA (distress): further unsuccessful attempts/widespread enquiries;","ALERFA (alert): after uncertainty, further attempts fail; aircraft cleared to land fails to land within 5 min of estimated time and no contact; information that operating efficiency is impaired; unlawful interference. DETRESFA (distress): further unsuccessful attempts/widespread enquiries;"],["010.11.02.01:05","Search & rescue (ICAO Annex 12): fill in the gaps → ____; forced landing is imminent or has occurred. ____ (rescue coordination centre): responsible for organising SAR services and coordinating SAR operations within a ____ (SRR).","fuel considered exhausted; forced landing is imminent or has occurred. RCC (rescue coordination centre): responsible for organising SAR services and coordinating SAR operations within a search and rescue region (SRR)."],["010.11.02.01:03","Search & rescue (ICAO Annex 12): fill in the gaps → States provide SAR services ____ over their territory and over ____ as agreed by ____; RCC for each SRR.","States provide SAR services 24 h/day over their territory and over high seas/areas of undetermined sovereignty as agreed by regional air navigation agreements; RCC for each SRR."],["010.11.04.01:01","Search & rescue (ICAO Annex 12): fill in the gaps → ____: keep the craft/survivors in sight, determine position, report to RCC/ATS (description, number of survivors, condition), act as instructed, drop survival equipment, ____ unless fuel requires. ____ a distress call: record position, take a bearing, inform the RCC/ATS, proceed to the position if appropriate, keep listening.","PIC first at the scene: keep the craft/survivors in sight, determine position, report to RCC/ATS (description, number of survivors, condition), act as instructed, drop survival equipment, don't leave before relieved unless fuel requires. Intercepting a distress call: record position, take a bearing, inform the RCC/ATS, proceed to the position if appropriate, keep listening."],["010.11.04.01:02","Search & rescue (ICAO Annex 12): fill in the gaps → ____: ____ = require assistance, ____ = require medical assistance, N = no/negative, Y = yes/affirmative, ↑ arrow = proceeding in this direction. Air-to- ground: message understood — rocking wings (day), flashing landing/navigation lights twice (night);","Ground-air visual signals (survivors): V = require assistance, X = require medical assistance, N = no/negative, Y = yes/affirmative, ↑ arrow = proceeding in this direction. Air-to- ground: message understood — rocking wings (day), flashing landing/navigation lights twice (night);"],["010.11.01.01:01","Search & rescue (ICAO Annex 12): fill in the gaps → ____ — straight and level without rocking wings / making a complete ____ (or flashing red/green).","not understood — straight and level without rocking wings / making a complete right-hand circle (or flashing red/green)."],["010.11.01.01:01","Exam trap (Search & rescue (ICAO Annex 12)): complete the fact → PDG ____, turns ≥ ____.","PDG 3.3 %, turns ≥ 120 m."],["010.11.01.01:01","Exam trap (Search & rescue (ICAO Annex 12)): complete the fact → MSA MOC ____ within ____.","MSA MOC 300 m within 25 NM."],["010.06.04.03:04","Exam trap (Search & rescue (ICAO Annex 12)): complete the fact → Initial MOC ____, intermediate ____, missed approach 50 m.","Initial MOC 300 m, intermediate 150 m, missed approach 50 m."],["010.06.05.01:06","Exam trap (Search & rescue (ICAO Annex 12)): complete the fact → Holding: timing starts over/abeam whichever later, ____ ≤ ____.","Holding: timing starts over/abeam whichever later, 1 min ≤ 14 000 ft."],["010.06.06.02:03","Exam trap (Search & rescue (ICAO Annex 12)): complete the fact → ALERFA: fails to land within ____.","ALERFA: fails to land within 5 min."],["010.02.02.01:01","EASA LO 010.02.02.01.01 → State the issuing authority of a CofA.","Prerequisites: aircraft conforms to an approved type design (type certificate), is in a condition for safe operation (statement of conformity, airworthiness review/inspection), registered, with an Airworthiness Review Certificate (ARC). / Issued by the State of Registry (EU: competent authority of the Member State of registry, per Part-21, Reg. (EU) 748/2012). Every aircraft engaged in international air navigation must hold a valid CofA (Chicago Conv. Art. 31) — carried on board."],["010.02.02.01:02","EASA LO 010.02.02.01.02 → State the necessity to hold a CofA.","Prerequisites: aircraft conforms to an approved type design (type certificate), is in a condition for safe operation (statement of conformity, airworthiness review/inspection), registered, with an Airworthiness Review Certificate (ARC). / Issued by the State of Registry (EU: competent authority of the Member State of registry, per Part-21, Reg. (EU) 748/2012). Every aircraft engaged in international air navigation must hold a valid CofA (Chicago Conv. Art. 31) — carried on board."],["010.06.03.03:01","EASA LO 010.06.03.03.01 → Explain what is the meaning of an ‘omnidirectional departure’.","Straight departure: initial departure track within 15° of the runway alignment. Turning departure: > 15°. / Omnidirectional departure: where no specific track guidance/SID is provided — climb straight ahead to ≥ 120 m then turn in any direction to join the en-route structure, possibly with sector restrictions (higher PDG or minimum turn altitudes in some sectors)."],["010.06.04.01:02","EASA LO 010.06.04.01.02 → Name the five possible segments of an instrument approach procedure.","Five segments: arrival, initial, intermediate, final, missed approach."],["010.06.04.01:03","EASA LO 010.06.04.01.03 → State the reasons for establishing aircraft categories for the approach.","Five segments: arrival, initial, intermediate, final, missed approach. / Aircraft categories (VAT = 1.3 VS0 or 1.23 VSR0): A < 91 kt, B 91–120, C 121–140, D 141–165, E 166–210 kt — because speed determines the turn radius and airspace/visibility needed → protected areas, circling areas and minima differ. The most significant factor influencing the conduct of instrument approaches = aircraft speed (performance)."],["010.06.04.01:09","EASA LO 010.06.04.01.09 → Describe in general terms the relevant factors for the calculation of operational minima.","Relationship: DA/H or MDA/H ≥ OCA/H, set by the operator taking into account operational factors; OCA/H = obstacle + MOC. / Operational minima factors: OCA/H, aircraft category & equipment, crew qualification/experience, runway & approach/visual aids, ATC, meteorological conditions (RVR/visibility, ceiling), go-around performance, obstacles in missed approach."],["010.06.04.01:10","EASA LO 010.06.04.01.10 → State the following acronyms in plain language: DA, DH, OCA, OCH, MDA, MDH, MOC, DA/H, OCA/H, MDA/H.","OCA/H: lowest altitude/height above the relevant threshold/aerodrome elevation used to establish compliance with obstacle-clearance criteria. MOC: minimum obstacle clearance margin. DA/MDA referenced to MSL, DH/MDH to threshold (or aerodrome) elevation. / OCA/H by category (MOC): A/B 90 m (295 ft), C/D 120 m (394 ft), E 150 m (492 ft); lowest OCH A 120 m, B 150 m, C 180 m, D 210 m, E 240 m; MDA/H ≥ OCA/H (and not below the straight-in NPA minima)."],["010.06.04.02:01","EASA LO 010.06.04.02.01 → Describe how the vertical cross section for each of the five approach segments is broken down into the various areas.","Five segments: arrival, initial, intermediate, final, missed approach."],["010.06.04.02:02","EASA LO 010.06.04.02.02 → State within which area of the cross section the minimum obstacle clearance (MOC) is provided for the whole width of the area.","Obstacle clearance: holding area + buffer area (5 NM); minimum holding level gives 300 m (984 ft) MOC above obstacles in the holding area, reduced in the buffer; over high terrain/mountainous areas up to 600 m (1 969 ft). / Descend below MDA/H only when: visual reference established and maintained, runway environment/threshold in sight, obstacle clearance can be maintained, in a position to land. No single procedure fits every wind/runway/obstacle situation → pilot judgement within the circling area."],["010.06.04.02:04","EASA LO 010.06.04.02.04 → State the accuracy of facilities providing track (VHF omnidirectional radio range (VOR), instrument landing system (ILS), non-directional beacon (NDB)).","Areas: primary area (full MOC across its width) and secondary areas either side (MOC reduces linearly to zero at the outer edge). Fix tolerance from facility accuracy, intersection angles. Facility accuracy: ILS LOC ±1.4°, VOR ±5.2°, NDB ±6.2°."],["010.06.04.03:01","EASA LO 010.06.04.03.01 → Name the five standard segments of an instrument approach procedure, and state the beginning and end for each of them.","Five segments: arrival, initial, intermediate, final, missed approach."],["010.06.04.03:02","EASA LO 010.06.04.03.02 → Describe where an arrival route normally ends.","Five segments: arrival, initial, intermediate, final, missed approach. / Arrival: from en-route to the IAF. Initial: IAF → IF — manoeuvre to intercept the intermediate track (MOC 300 m/984 ft). Max interception angle initial→intermediate at the IF: 90° precision, 120° non-precision."],["010.06.04.03:03","EASA LO 010.06.04.03.03 → State the main task of the initial approach segment.","Five segments: arrival, initial, intermediate, final, missed approach. / Arrival: from en-route to the IAF. Initial: IAF → IF — manoeuvre to intercept the intermediate track (MOC 300 m/984 ft). Max interception angle initial→intermediate at the IF: 90° precision, 120° non-precision."],["010.06.04.03:06","EASA LO 010.06.04.03.06 → State the main task of the final approach segment.","Five segments: arrival, initial, intermediate, final, missed approach. / Main task: provide protection from obstacles during the go-around. Three phases: initial (MAPt → SOC, no turns, climb begins), intermediate (from SOC, climb straight ahead until 50 m (164 ft) MOC is obtained and can be maintained; nominal gradient 2.5 %), final (to a point where a new approach, holding or en-route flight begins; turns allowed)."],["010.06.04.03:07","EASA LO 010.06.04.03.07 → Name the two possible aims of a final approach.","Five segments: arrival, initial, intermediate, final, missed approach. / Intermediate: IF → FAF/FAP — adjust speed, configuration and position for final (MOC 150 m/492 ft, then reducing). Final: FAF/FAP → MAPt — alignment and descent for landing; aims: straight-in landing or circling. FAP (ILS): point where the intermediate altitude intercepts the nominal glide path."],["010.06.04.03:08","EASA LO 010.06.04.03.08 → Explain the term ‘final approach point’ in case of an ILS approach.","Five segments: arrival, initial, intermediate, final, missed approach. / Intermediate: IF → FAF/FAP — adjust speed, configuration and position for final (MOC 150 m/492 ft, then reducing). Final: FAF/FAP → MAPt — alignment and descent for landing; aims: straight-in landing or circling. FAP (ILS): point where the intermediate altitude intercepts the nominal glide path."],["010.06.04.04:01","EASA LO 010.06.04.04.01 → Name the three phases of a missed approach procedure and describe their geometric limits.","Five segments: arrival, initial, intermediate, final, missed approach. / Main task: provide protection from obstacles during the go-around. Three phases: initial (MAPt → SOC, no turns, climb begins), intermediate (from SOC, climb straight ahead until 50 m (164 ft) MOC is obtained and can be maintained; nominal gradient 2.5 %), final (to a point where a new approach, holding or en-route flight begins; turns allowed)."],["010.06.04.04:02","EASA LO 010.06.04.04.02 → State the main task of a missed approach procedure.","Five segments: arrival, initial, intermediate, final, missed approach. / Initial MOC 300 m, intermediate 150 m, missed approach 50 m."],["010.06.04.04:05","EASA LO 010.06.04.04.05 → State the pilot’s action if, upon reaching the MAPt, the required visual reference is not established.","DA/H (decision altitude/height, precision & APV): altitude/height at which a missed approach must be initiated if the required visual reference is not established. MDA/H (NPA/circling): below which descent must not be made without the required visual reference. / At MAPt without visual reference → execute the missed approach. If initiated before the MAPt → fly to the MAPt (may climb) and then follow the published procedure (no early turns). Crossing the MAPt higher than required is permitted."],["010.06.04.04:06","EASA LO 010.06.04.04.06 → Describe what a pilot is expected to do in the event a missed approach is initiated prior to arriving at the MAPt.","At MAPt without visual reference → execute the missed approach. If initiated before the MAPt → fly to the MAPt (may climb) and then follow the published procedure (no early turns). Crossing the MAPt higher than required is permitted. / MAPt: point at which the missed approach must be initiated if visual reference isn't established. Precision/APV: intersection of glide path with DA/H; NPA: a fix/facility or a distance (timing) from the FAF."],["010.06.04.04:07","EASA LO 010.06.04.04.07 → State whether the pilot is obliged to cross the MAPt at the height (HGT)/altitude (ALT) required by the procedure or whether they are allowed to cross the MAPt at a HGT/ALT greater than that required by the procedure.","At MAPt without visual reference → execute the missed approach. If initiated before the MAPt → fly to the MAPt (may climb) and then follow the published procedure (no early turns). Crossing the MAPt higher than required is permitted. / DA/H (decision altitude/height, precision & APV): altitude/height at which a missed approach must be initiated if the required visual reference is not established. MDA/H (NPA/circling): below which descent must not be made without the required visual reference."],["010.06.04.05:01","EASA LO 010.06.04.05.01 → Describe what is meant by ‘visual manoeuvring (circling)’.","Visual phase after an instrument approach to bring the aircraft into position for landing on a runway not suitably located for a straight-in. A prominent obstacle in the circling area outside the final/missed approach areas may be excluded by prohibiting circling in that sector. / After visual contact: keep the runway environment in sight, stay within the circling area at MDA until descent. Visual reference lost: initiate a climbing turn towards the landing runway to overhead, then follow the missed approach procedure of the instrument approach."],["010.06.04.05:04","EASA LO 010.06.04.05.04 → Describe how the minimum descent altitude/height (MDA/H) is specified for visual manoeuvring (circling) if the OCA/H is known.","DA/H (decision altitude/height, precision & APV): altitude/height at which a missed approach must be initiated if the required visual reference is not established. MDA/H (NPA/circling): below which descent must not be made without the required visual reference. / After visual contact: keep the runway environment in sight, stay within the circling area at MDA until descent. Visual reference lost: initiate a climbing turn towards the landing runway to overhead, then follow the missed approach procedure of the instrument approach."],["010.06.04.05:05","EASA LO 010.06.04.05.05 → State the conditions to be fulfilled before descending below MDA/H in a visual manoeuvring (circling) approach.","Descend below MDA/H only when: visual reference established and maintained, runway environment/threshold in sight, obstacle clearance can be maintained, in a position to land. No single procedure fits every wind/runway/obstacle situation → pilot judgement within the circling area. / After visual contact: keep the runway environment in sight, stay within the circling area at MDA until descent. Visual reference lost: initiate a climbing turn towards the landing runway to overhead, then follow the missed approach procedure of the instrument approach."],["010.06.04.05:08","EASA LO 010.06.04.05.08 → Describe what the pilot is expected to do if visual reference is lost while circling to land from an instrument approach.","After visual contact: keep the runway environment in sight, stay within the circling area at MDA until descent. Visual reference lost: initiate a climbing turn towards the landing runway to overhead, then follow the missed approach procedure of the instrument approach. / Descend below MDA/H only when: visual reference established and maintained, runway environment/threshold in sight, obstacle clearance can be maintained, in a position to land. No single procedure fits every wind/runway/obstacle situation → pilot judgement within the circling area."],["010.06.05.01:02","EASA LO 010.06.05.01.02 → State that if for any reason a pilot is unable to conform to the procedures for normal conditions laid down for any particular holding pattern, this pilot should advise ATC as early as possible.","Deviations are dangerous (protected area based on standard procedure) → inform ATC early if unable to comply."],["010.06.05.01:03","EASA LO 010.06.05.01.03 → Describe the shape and terminology associated with the holding pattern.","Given a time to leave the holding, adjust the pattern to leave the fix at that time."],["010.06.05.01:04","EASA LO 010.06.05.01.04 → State the bank angle and rate of turn to be used whilst flying in a holding pattern.","Pattern: right-hand (standard), holding fix, inbound/outbound legs, turns. Bank 25° or rate 3°/s, whichever requires less bank. Maintain tracks by applying wind corrections (double/triple drift on the outbound leg, adjust outbound time to get 1 min inbound). / Given a time to leave the holding, adjust the pattern to leave the fix at that time."],["010.06.05.01:05","EASA LO 010.06.05.01.05 → Explain why a pilot in a holding pattern should attempt to maintain tracks and how this can be achieved.","Pattern: right-hand (standard), holding fix, inbound/outbound legs, turns. Bank 25° or rate 3°/s, whichever requires less bank. Maintain tracks by applying wind corrections (double/triple drift on the outbound leg, adjust outbound time to get 1 min inbound). / Given a time to leave the holding, adjust the pattern to leave the fix at that time."],["010.06.05.01:08","EASA LO 010.06.05.01.08 → Describe the three heading entry sectors for entries into a holding pattern.","Entry sectors (relative to the inbound track, ±5° flexibility): Sector 1 — parallel (110° sector on the non-holding side), Sector 2 — offset/teardrop (70° sector, 30° offset), Sector 3 — direct (180°). Outbound entry time 1 min / 1.5 min (still air) or to the DME limit. / Given a time to leave the holding, adjust the pattern to leave the fix at that time."],["010.06.05.01:11","EASA LO 010.06.05.01.11 → State the still-air time for flying the outbound entry heading with or without DME.","Entry sectors (relative to the inbound track, ±5° flexibility): Sector 1 — parallel (110° sector on the non-holding side), Sector 2 — offset/teardrop (70° sector, 30° offset), Sector 3 — direct (180°). Outbound entry time 1 min / 1.5 min (still air) or to the DME limit. / Outbound timing starts over or abeam the fix, whichever occurs later (or at completion of the outbound turn if abeam can't be determined). Outbound leg 1 min at/below 14 000 ft, 1.5 min above. With DME: outbound ends at the specified DME distance."],["010.06.05.01:12","EASA LO 010.06.05.01.12 → Describe what the pilot is expected to do when clearance is received specifying the time of departure from the holding point.","Given a time to leave the holding, adjust the pattern to leave the fix at that time."],["010.06.05.02:01","EASA LO 010.06.05.02.01 → Describe the layout of the basic holding area, entry area and buffer area of a holding pattern.","Obstacle clearance: holding area + buffer area (5 NM); minimum holding level gives 300 m (984 ft) MOC above obstacles in the holding area, reduced in the buffer; over high terrain/mountainous areas up to 600 m (1 969 ft). / Descend below MDA/H only when: visual reference established and maintained, runway environment/threshold in sight, obstacle clearance can be maintained, in a position to land. No single procedure fits every wind/runway/obstacle situation → pilot judgement within the circling area."],["010.06.06.01:02","EASA LO 010.06.06.01.02 → Define the terms ‘QNH’ and ‘QFE’.","Objectives: terrain clearance and vertical separation. QNH: altimeter reads altitude (elevation on ground); QFE: reads height above aerodrome/threshold."],["010.06.06.01:03","EASA LO 010.06.06.01.03 → Describe the different terms for ALT or flight levels (FLs) respectively, which are the references during climb or descent to change the altimeter settings from QNH to 1013.2 hPa and vice versa.","Pilots: at least one altimeter set to QNH before take-off; change to 1013.2 when passing the TA in the climb; obtain TRL before descent (ATIS/approach clearance) and the actual QNH before descending below the TRL; change to QNH when passing the TRL in descent. / QNH to departing aircraft in the taxi clearance (or before take-off). En-route at/below TA → altitudes; above → flight levels. QNH reports from sufficient locations so terrain clearance can be assured. Approaching aircraft receive QNH in the approach clearance/ATIS. Above the TRL, altitude may be used when cleared to an altitude on approach (after QNH is set, e.g. vectors)."],["010.06.06.01:04","EASA LO 010.06.06.01.04 → Define the term ‘flight level (FL)’.","Transition level (TRL): lowest FL available above TA; passed in the approach clearance/ATIS. Transition layer: between TA and TRL. Climbing through the layer → report FL; descending → report altitude. / FL: surface of constant pressure related to 1013.2 hPa, separated by 500 ft intervals; FL 0 at the 1013.2 hPa level. Transition altitude (TA): at or below which vertical position is expressed as altitude; specified for each aerodrome (not below 3 000 ft), rounded up to full 1 000 ft, published in the AIP and charts."],["010.06.06.01:05","EASA LO 010.06.06.01.05 → State where FL zero shall be located.","Transition level (TRL): lowest FL available above TA; passed in the approach clearance/ATIS. Transition layer: between TA and TRL. Climbing through the layer → report FL; descending → report altitude."],["010.06.06.01:12","EASA LO 010.06.06.01.12 → Define the term ‘transition level (TRL)’.","Transition level (TRL): lowest FL available above TA; passed in the approach clearance/ATIS. Transition layer: between TA and TRL. Climbing through the layer → report FL; descending → report altitude."],["010.06.06.01:13","EASA LO 010.06.06.01.13 → State when the TRL is normally passed on to the aircraft.","Transition level (TRL): lowest FL available above TA; passed in the approach clearance/ATIS. Transition layer: between TA and TRL. Climbing through the layer → report FL; descending → report altitude."],["010.06.06.01:15","EASA LO 010.06.06.01.15 → Define the term ‘transition layer’. X X X X X X X 2 3 Exam BK Remarks","Transition level (TRL): lowest FL available above TA; passed in the approach clearance/ATIS. Transition layer: between TA and TRL. Climbing through the layer → report FL; descending → report altitude."],["010.06.06.01:16","EASA LO 010.06.06.01.16 → Describe when the vertical position of an aircraft passing through the transition layer shall be expressed in terms of FLs and when in terms of ALT.","Transition level (TRL): lowest FL available above TA; passed in the approach clearance/ATIS. Transition layer: between TA and TRL. Climbing through the layer → report FL; descending → report altitude. / FL: surface of constant pressure related to 1013.2 hPa, separated by 500 ft intervals; FL 0 at the 1013.2 hPa level. Transition altitude (TA): at or below which vertical position is expressed as altitude; specified for each aerodrome (not below 3 000 ft), rounded up to full 1 000 ft, published in the AIP and charts."],["010.06.06.01:17","EASA LO 010.06.06.01.17 → State when the QNH altimeter setting shall be made available to departing aircraft.","QNH to departing aircraft in the taxi clearance (or before take-off). En-route at/below TA → altitudes; above → flight levels. QNH reports from sufficient locations so terrain clearance can be assured. Approaching aircraft receive QNH in the approach clearance/ATIS. Above the TRL, altitude may be used when cleared to an altitude on approach (after QNH is set, e.g. vectors)."],["010.06.06.01:18","EASA LO 010.06.06.01.18 → Explain when the vertical separation of an aircraft during en-route flight shall be assessed in terms of ALT and when in terms of FLs.","Main task: provide protection from obstacles during the go-around. Three phases: initial (MAPt → SOC, no turns, climb begins), intermediate (from SOC, climb straight ahead until 50 m (164 ft) MOC is obtained and can be maintained; nominal gradient 2.5 %), final (to a point where a new approach, holding or en-route flight begins; turns allowed)."],["010.06.06.01:21","EASA LO 010.06.06.01.21 → State how a QNH altimeter setting shall be made available to aircraft approaching a controlled aerodrome (AD) for landing.","QNH to departing aircraft in the taxi clearance (or before take-off). En-route at/below TA → altitudes; above → flight levels. QNH reports from sufficient locations so terrain clearance can be assured. Approaching aircraft receive QNH in the approach clearance/ATIS. Above the TRL, altitude may be used when cleared to an altitude on approach (after QNH is set, e.g. vectors)."],["010.06.06.01:22","EASA LO 010.06.06.01.22 → State under which circumstances the vertical position of an aircraft above the TRL may be referenced in ALT.","QNH to departing aircraft in the taxi clearance (or before take-off). En-route at/below TA → altitudes; above → flight levels. QNH reports from sufficient locations so terrain clearance can be assured. Approaching aircraft receive QNH in the approach clearance/ATIS. Above the TRL, altitude may be used when cleared to an altitude on approach (after QNH is set, e.g. vectors)."],["010.06.06.02:02","EASA LO 010.06.06.02.02 → State where during the climb the altimeter setting shall be changed from QNH to 1013.2 hPa.","Pilots: at least one altimeter set to QNH before take-off; change to 1013.2 when passing the TA in the climb; obtain TRL before descent (ATIS/approach clearance) and the actual QNH before descending below the TRL; change to QNH when passing the TRL in descent. / FL: surface of constant pressure related to 1013.2 hPa, separated by 500 ft intervals; FL 0 at the 1013.2 hPa level. Transition altitude (TA): at or below which vertical position is expressed as altitude; specified for each aerodrome (not below 3 000 ft), rounded up to full 1 000 ft, published in the AIP and charts."],["010.06.06.02:04","EASA LO 010.06.06.02.04 → Describe when a pilot of an aircraft intending to land at an AD shall obtain the actual QNH altimeter setting.","Pilots: at least one altimeter set to QNH before take-off; change to 1013.2 when passing the TA in the climb; obtain TRL before descent (ATIS/approach clearance) and the actual QNH before descending below the TRL; change to QNH when passing the TRL in descent."],["010.06.06.02:05","EASA LO 010.06.06.02.05 → State where the altimeter settings shall be changed from 1013.2 hPa to QNH during descent for landing.","Pilots: at least one altimeter set to QNH before take-off; change to 1013.2 when passing the TA in the climb; obtain TRL before descent (ATIS/approach clearance) and the actual QNH before descending below the TRL; change to QNH when passing the TRL in descent. / FL: surface of constant pressure related to 1013.2 hPa, separated by 500 ft intervals; FL 0 at the 1013.2 hPa level. Transition altitude (TA): at or below which vertical position is expressed as altitude; specified for each aerodrome (not below 3 000 ft), rounded up to full 1 000 ft, published in the AIP and charts."],["010.06.07.01:04","EASA LO 010.06.07.01.04 → State the aircraft avionics requirements for conducting parallel instrument approaches.","Independent parallel approaches (mode 1): no radar separation required between aircraft on adjacent ILS — requires NTZ monitoring (runways ≥ 1 035 m apart); dependent (mode 2): minimum diagonal radar separation (2 NM) between aircraft on adjacent localisers (≥ 915 m)."],["010.06.07.01:06","EASA LO 010.06.07.01.06 → State the radar requirements for simultaneous, independent, and parallel instrument approaches, and how weather conditions effect these.","Independent parallel approaches (mode 1): no radar separation required between aircraft on adjacent ILS — requires NTZ monitoring (runways ≥ 1 035 m apart); dependent (mode 2): minimum diagonal radar separation (2 NM) between aircraft on adjacent localisers (≥ 915 m). / Avionics: ILS/MLS/GLS (appropriate for the approach) — standard. Guidance: ICAO Doc 9643 (SOIR manual). Radar: separate monitoring controllers with radar update ≤ 5 s (high-precision) where spacing requires; possible in all weather within limits (IMC). Localiser intercept angle ≤ 30°, with ≥ 1 NM straight before interception. Missed approach/departure tracks diverge by ≥ 30°."],["010.06.07.01:08","EASA LO 010.06.07.01.08 → Describe the special conditions for tracks on missed approach procedures and departures in case of simultaneous or parallel operations.","Independent departures (mode 3), segregated operations (mode 4: one runway approaches, other departures), semi-mixed (one runway mixed), mixed (both approaches and departures on both)."],["010.06.08.01:01","EASA LO 010.06.08.01.01 → State when and where the pilot shall operate the transponder.","Operate the transponder at all times in flight (and on the ground where SMR/MLAT) regardless of airspace; Mode C continuously unless directed; code 2000 (IFR, no assignment; Europe VFR 7000); IDENT only on ATC request. 7700 emergency, 7600 COM failure, 7500 unlawful interference. Mode S operated whenever a transponder is required."],["010.06.08.01:02","EASA LO 010.06.08.01.02 → State the modes and codes that the pilot shall operate in the absence of any ATC directions or regional air navigation agreements.","States provide SAR services 24 h/day over their territory and over high seas/areas of undetermined sovereignty as agreed by regional air navigation agreements; RCC for each SRR."],["010.06.08.01:03","EASA LO 010.06.08.01.03 → State when the pilot shall operate Mode C.","Operate the transponder at all times in flight (and on the ground where SMR/MLAT) regardless of airspace; Mode C continuously unless directed; code 2000 (IFR, no assignment; Europe VFR 7000); IDENT only on ATC request. 7700 emergency, 7600 COM failure, 7500 unlawful interference. Mode S operated whenever a transponder is required. / Independent departures (mode 3), segregated operations (mode 4: one runway approaches, other departures), semi-mixed (one runway mixed), mixed (both approaches and departures on both)."],["010.06.08.01:06","EASA LO 010.06.08.01.06 → Describe the consequences of a transponder failure in flight.","Operate the transponder at all times in flight (and on the ground where SMR/MLAT) regardless of airspace; Mode C continuously unless directed; code 2000 (IFR, no assignment; Europe VFR 7000); IDENT only on ATC request. 7700 emergency, 7600 COM failure, 7500 unlawful interference. Mode S operated whenever a transponder is required. / Transponder failure in flight: ATC tries to allow continuation to destination per plan; before departure unserviceable and no repair possible → inform ATS, get permission to fly to the nearest aerodrome where it can be repaired (may be excluded from some airspace)."],["010.06.08.01:08","EASA LO 010.06.08.01.08 → State when the pilot shall operate Mode S.","Operate the transponder at all times in flight (and on the ground where SMR/MLAT) regardless of airspace; Mode C continuously unless directed; code 2000 (IFR, no assignment; Europe VFR 7000); IDENT only on ATC request. 7700 emergency, 7600 COM failure, 7500 unlawful interference. Mode S operated whenever a transponder is required. / Independent departures (mode 3), segregated operations (mode 4: one runway approaches, other departures), semi-mixed (one runway mixed), mixed (both approaches and departures on both)."],["010.06.08.02:07","EASA LO 010.06.08.02.07 → List the reasons which may force a pilot to disregard an RA.","Follow RAs immediately (within ~5 s, 0.25 g) even if contrary to ATC — coordinated with the other aircraft's RA. May disregard only when: intruder positively identified visually and no threat, or a higher-priority warning (stall, wind shear, GPWS/TAWS) takes precedence."],["010.06.09.03:02","EASA LO 010.06.09.03.02 → Explain the main content of these Parts, except the operational procedures.","Lays down rules for air operations — covers all commercial and non-commercial operations. Annexes: I definitions, II Part-ARO, III Part-ORO, IV Part-CAT, V Part-SPA, VI Part- NCC, VII Part-NCO, VIII Part-SPO. / Part-SPA: operations requiring specific approval (PBN, MNPS/NAT HLA, RVSM, LVO, ETOPS, dangerous goods, NVIS, HHO, HEMS, EFB…). Part-NCC: non-commercial operations with complex motor-powered aircraft; Part-NCO: non-commercial operations with other-than-complex aircraft."],["010.11.02.01:01","EASA LO 010.11.02.01.01 → Describe how ICAO Contracting States shall arrange for the establishment and prompt provision of SAR services.","States provide SAR services 24 h/day over their territory and over high seas/areas of undetermined sovereignty as agreed by regional air navigation agreements; RCC for each SRR. / fuel considered exhausted; forced landing is imminent or has occurred. RCC (rescue coordination centre): responsible for organising SAR services and coordinating SAR operations within a search and rescue region (SRR)."],["010.11.02.01:02","EASA LO 010.11.02.01.02 → Explain the establishment of SAR by Contracting States.","States provide SAR services 24 h/day over their territory and over high seas/areas of undetermined sovereignty as agreed by regional air navigation agreements; RCC for each SRR."],["010.11.02.01:04","EASA LO 010.11.02.01.04 → State the period of time per day within which SAR services shall be available.","States provide SAR services 24 h/day over their territory and over high seas/areas of undetermined sovereignty as agreed by regional air navigation agreements; RCC for each SRR. / fuel considered exhausted; forced landing is imminent or has occurred. RCC (rescue coordination centre): responsible for organising SAR services and coordinating SAR operations within a search and rescue region (SRR)."]],"91":[["071.02.01.01:06","Operations manual & records: fill in the gaps → ____ must contain all instructions, information and procedures for operating personnel; kept up to date, amendments distributed, in a language understood; approved/accepted by the authority.","OM must contain all instructions, information and procedures for operating personnel; kept up to date, amendments distributed, in a language understood; approved/accepted by the authority."],["071.02.01.01:06","Operations manual & records: fill in the gaps → Structure (Part-ORO AMC): ____ (all non-type-related policies & procedures), ____ ____ (limitations, normal/abnormal/emergency procedures, performance, M&B, MEL, icing limitations), Part C — Route/role/area and aerodrome instructions, Part D — Training.","Structure (Part-ORO AMC): Part A — General/basic (all non-type-related policies & procedures), Part B — Aeroplane operating matters (type-related) (limitations, normal/abnormal/emergency procedures, performance, M&B, MEL, icing limitations), Part C — Route/role/area and aerodrome instructions, Part D — Training."],["071.02.01.01:07","Operations manual & records: fill in the gaps → ____: ground ____, adverse & hazardous atmospheric conditions, ____, crew incapacitation, use of MEL/CDL, security, handling of accidents and occurrences, altitude alerting, GPWS/TAWS procedures, TCAS/ACAS policy (and rotor downwash for helicopters), FTL, fuel policy, operating minima, DG policy.","Part A includes: ground de-icing/anti-icing, adverse & hazardous atmospheric conditions, wake turbulence, crew incapacitation, use of MEL/CDL, security, handling of accidents and occurrences, altitude alerting, GPWS/TAWS procedures, TCAS/ACAS policy (and rotor downwash for helicopters), FTL, fuel policy, operating minima, DG policy."],["071.02.01.01:03","Operations manual & records: fill in the gaps → ____ (or equivalent — tech log/OFP): aircraft registration, date, crew names/duties, departure and arrival places & times, block/flight time, nature of flight, incidents/observations, commander's signature.","Journey log (or equivalent — tech log/OFP): aircraft registration, date, crew names/duties, departure and arrival places & times, block/flight time, nature of flight, incidents/observations, commander's signature."],["071.02.01.01:04","Operations manual & records: fill in the gaps → ____: route, levels, times, fuel, alternates, weather, planned vs actual; signed by commander; copy kept on the ground.","Operational flight plan (OFP): route, levels, times, fuel, alternates, weather, planned vs actual; signed by commander; copy kept on the ground."],["071.02.01.01:05","Operations manual & records: fill in the gaps → Storage periods (typical): OFP, journey log, M&B documentation, NOTAM/AIS & MET briefing, ____; flight/duty/rest records — ____; crew training/checking records — ____; FDR/CVR per accident/incident rules.","Storage periods (typical): OFP, journey log, M&B documentation, NOTAM/AIS & MET briefing, passenger/cargo manifests, DG notification — 3 months; flight/duty/rest records — 24 months; crew training/checking records — 3 years; FDR/CVR per accident/incident rules."],["071.02.01.03:01","MEL / MMEL: fill in the gaps → ____: the point when the aircraft ____ ____ to prepare for take-off. Inoperative: an item that does not accomplish its intended purpose or is not consistently functioning within its design limits.","Commencement of flight: the point when the aircraft begins to move under its own power to prepare for take-off. Inoperative: an item that does not accomplish its intended purpose or is not consistently functioning within its design limits."],["071.02.01.03:04","MEL / MMEL: fill in the gaps → ____: master list established by the ____, approved by EASA (OSD), for a type. ____: operator's list based on the MMEL (no less restrictive), tailored to its aircraft/operations, approved by the competent authority.","MMEL: master list established by the type-certificate holder, approved by EASA (OSD), for a type. MEL: operator's list based on the MMEL (no less restrictive), tailored to its aircraft/operations, approved by the competent authority."],["071.02.01.03:01","MEL / MMEL: fill in the gaps → Rectification interval: maximum time an item may remain inoperative — Cat A (as stated in the MEL remarks), ____, ____, ____ days (calendar days, excluding the day of discovery).","Rectification interval: maximum time an item may remain inoperative — Cat A (as stated in the MEL remarks), B 3 days, C 10 days, D 120 days (calendar days, excluding the day of discovery)."],["071.02.01.03:02","MEL / MMEL: fill in the gaps → ____: MEL covers only items listed in the MMEL (equipment whose loss is acceptable); items not listed must be operative; with (O) operational and (M) maintenance procedures.","Extent: MEL covers only items listed in the MMEL (equipment whose loss is acceptable); items not listed must be operative; with (O) operational and (M) maintenance procedures."],["071.02.01.03:01","MEL / MMEL: fill in the gaps → ____: establish MEL, obtain approval, procedures/rectification control, rectification interval extensions (RIE) under conditions. ____: approve MEL/MMEL. ____ crew: know the MEL and apply (O) procedures.","Operator: establish MEL, obtain approval, procedures/rectification control, rectification interval extensions (RIE) under conditions. Authority: approve MEL/MMEL. Flight crew: know the MEL and apply (O) procedures."],["071.02.01.03:01","MEL / MMEL: fill in the gaps → ____: decides whether to ____ an aircraft with inoperative items (may refuse even if the MEL allows); MEL does not apply after commencement of flight (then AFM/QRH).","Commander: decides whether to accept an aircraft with inoperative items (may refuse even if the MEL allows); MEL does not apply after commencement of flight (then AFM/QRH)."],["071.02.02.01:01","Icing — ground de/anti-icing: fill in the gaps → ____: removal of frost, ice, snow, slush to provide clean surfaces. ____: prevents formation/accumulation for a limited time. One-step: heated anti-icing fluid used to de-ice and anti-ice at once. Two-step: first de-icing (heated water/Type I), then anti-icing fluid ____ (before the first freezes).","De-icing: removal of frost, ice, snow, slush to provide clean surfaces. Anti-icing: prevents formation/accumulation for a limited time. One-step: heated anti-icing fluid used to de-ice and anti-ice at once. Two-step: first de-icing (heated water/Type I), then anti-icing fluid within 3 min (before the first freezes)."],["071.02.02.01:05","Icing — ground de/anti-icing: fill in the gaps → ____: estimated time the anti-icing fluid prevents accumulation on protected surfaces — starts at the ____.","Holdover time (HOT): estimated time the anti-icing fluid prevents accumulation on protected surfaces — starts at the beginning of the final (anti-icing) application."],["071.02.02.01:02","Icing — ground de/anti-icing: fill in the gaps → ____ (ICAO Doc 9640): ____ with ice, snow, slush or frost ____ (wings, control surfaces, stabilisers, rotors, propellers, engine inlets), except as permitted by the AFM (e.g. thin hoarfrost on fuselage, underwing frost in cold-soaked fuel area within limits).","Clean aircraft concept (ICAO Doc 9640): no take-off with ice, snow, slush or frost adhering to critical surfaces (wings, control surfaces, stabilisers, rotors, propellers, engine inlets), except as permitted by the AFM (e.g. thin hoarfrost on fuselage, underwing frost in cold-soaked fuel area within limits)."],["071.02.02.01:01","Icing — ground de/anti-icing: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (unthickened, low viscosity, heated, orange, short HOT — mainly de-icing); Type II (thickened, pseudoplastic, pale yellow/colourless, longer HOT, for aircraft with VR ____ — shears off during take-off roll); Type III (thickened, bright yellow, for slower aircraft VR > 60 kt); Type IV (thickened, green, longest HOT).","Fluids: Type I (unthickened, low viscosity, heated, orange, short HOT — mainly de-icing); Type II (thickened, pseudoplastic, pale yellow/colourless, longer HOT, for aircraft with VR > 100 kt — shears off during take-off roll); Type III (thickened, bright yellow, for slower aircraft VR > 60 kt); Type IV (thickened, green, longest HOT)."],["071.02.02.01:06","Icing — ground de/anti-icing: fill in the gaps → ____ → ____ (de-ice/anti-ice again), unless a ____ take-off contamination check (outside, within 5 min of take-off) shows surfaces clean where permitted.","HOT exceeded → re-treat (de-ice/anti-ice again), unless a pre- take-off contamination check (outside, within 5 min of take-off) shows surfaces clean where permitted."],["071.02.02.01:05","Icing — ground de/anti-icing: fill in the gaps → HOT tables are guidelines; reduced by ____ ____ ____ sunlight/radiational cooling effects, fluid thinning.","HOT tables are guidelines; reduced by heavy precipitation, high wind/jet blast, low OAT, cold-soaked wing (fuel colder than OAT), fluid dilution, aircraft skin temperature below OAT, direct sunlight/radiational cooling effects, fluid thinning."],["071.02.02.01:06","Icing — ground de/anti-icing: fill in the gaps → ____ by the commander from inside (wings visible) as close to take-off as possible; ____ so that both wings have the same aerodynamic characteristics (asymmetric lift/roll at rotation otherwise).","Pre-take-off check by the commander from inside (wings visible) as close to take-off as possible; symmetrical treatment so that both wings have the same aerodynamic characteristics (asymmetric lift/roll at rotation otherwise)."],["071.02.02.01:08","Icing — ground de/anti-icing: fill in the gaps → Operators must establish procedures (responsibilities, communication, inspections, HOT use); commander shall not take off unless surfaces are clear. ____: aircraft certified/equipped (anti-ice, ice detectors, lights to see ice), limitations in OM B.","Operators must establish procedures (responsibilities, communication, inspections, HOT use); commander shall not take off unless surfaces are clear. Operations in icing conditions: aircraft certified/equipped (anti-ice, ice detectors, lights to see ice), limitations in OM B."],["071.02.02.01:11","Icing — ground de/anti-icing: fill in the gaps → ____ — de-icing costs time and money, but icing accidents cost lives (e.g. Air Florida 90, Dryden).","Safety before commercial pressure — de-icing costs time and money, but icing accidents cost lives (e.g. Air Florida 90, Dryden)."],["071.02.02.02:02","Icing — performance deterioration: fill in the gaps → Effects are wide-ranging, unpredictable and depend on design. At a given speed/α, ____ and ____ ____ → higher stall speed (stall at lower α, stall warning may not activate in time), reduced controllability, altered handling (tailplane stall with flaps extended).","Effects are wide-ranging, unpredictable and depend on design. At a given speed/α, lift can be reduced by up to 30 % and drag increased by up to 40 % → higher stall speed (stall at lower α, stall warning may not activate in time), reduced controllability, altered handling (tailplane stall with flaps extended)."],["071.02.02.02:04","Icing — performance deterioration: fill in the gaps → Ice can ____ on take-off/in flight and be ingested by rear-mounted engines or hit control surfaces. Ice on ____ ____ → false altitude, airspeed, AoA and engine-power (EPR) data.","Ice can break away on take-off/in flight and be ingested by rear-mounted engines or hit control surfaces. Ice on pitot/static/AoA vanes → false altitude, airspeed, AoA and engine-power (EPR) data."],["071.02.02.02:05","Icing — performance deterioration: what do you remember about this? → Ground-formed contamination (frost roughness over the whole wing) can …","Ground-formed contamination (frost roughness over the whole wing) can affect flight characteristics very differently from in-flight leading-edge ice — even thin frost reduces CL max significantly."],["071.02.02.02:06","Icing — performance deterioration: fill in the gaps → Limitations for flight in ____ in ____; procedures and performance corrections (e.g. increased VREF, landing distance) in AFM/FCOM/QRH.","Limitations for flight in known icing in OM Part B/AFM; procedures and performance corrections (e.g. increased VREF, landing distance) in AFM/FCOM/QRH."],["071.02.03.01:03","Bird strikes: fill in the gaps → ____ are part of ____ (NOTAM, ATIS, AIP). Crews report observed birds to ATS → AIS distributes. AIP ____: bird migration and areas with sensitive fauna.","Bird hazards are part of pre-flight information (NOTAM, ATIS, AIP). Crews report observed birds to ATS → AIS distributes. AIP ENR 5.6: bird migration and areas with sensitive fauna."],["071.02.03.01:04","Bird strikes: fill in the gaps → ____ ____: bird control & reduction; most strikes occur ____ during take-off/landing (majority below ~500 ft), highest in migration seasons, dawn/dusk.","ICAO Doc 9137 (Airport Services Manual Part 3): bird control & reduction; most strikes occur on or near aerodromes during take-off/landing (majority below ~500 ft), highest in migration seasons, dawn/dusk."],["071.02.03.01:05","Bird strikes: fill in the gaps → Hazards: damage to ____ ____, loss of vision.","Hazards: damage to probes/sensors, engines (ingestion, flame-out), windscreens, radomes, airframe, landing gear, loss of vision."],["071.02.03.01:06","Bird strikes: fill in the gaps → ____: report bird hazards observed and ____ ____ after a strike (to operator/authority); strikes are occurrences under Reg. 376/2014.","Commander: report bird hazards observed and file a bird-strike report after a strike (to operator/authority); strikes are occurrences under Reg. 376/2014."],["071.02.03.01:07","Bird strikes: fill in the gaps → Birds flock where food is plentiful: ____ ____, natural habitats (wetlands, coasts, lakes).","Birds flock where food is plentiful: rubbish dumps, open sewage works, freshly ploughed land, natural habitats (wetlands, coasts, lakes)."],["071.02.04.01:02","Noise abatement: fill in the gaps → Operator establishes noise-abatement departure procedures (NADPs) ____; ____ NADPs per aeroplane type (one close-in, one distant). Commander may decide ____ an NADP if safety requires.","Operator establishes noise-abatement departure procedures (NADPs) not compromising safety; max two NADPs per aeroplane type (one close-in, one distant). Commander may decide not to fly an NADP if safety requires."],["071.02.04.01:02","Noise abatement: fill in the gaps → ____ (close-in noise relief): from ____ reduce to climb power/thrust, climb at V____ + 10–20 kt with take-off flaps until 3 000 ft AAL, then accelerate, retract flaps, normal climb.","NADP 1 (close-in noise relief): from 800 ft AAL reduce to climb power/thrust, climb at V2 + 10–20 kt with take-off flaps until 3 000 ft AAL, then accelerate, retract flaps, normal climb."],["071.02.04.01:01","Noise abatement: fill in the gaps → ____ (distant): at or above ____ start acceleration and flap/slat retraction while maintaining a positive climb, reduce power during or after retraction, climb at VZF ____ to 3 000 ft AAL, then normal en-route climb.","NADP 2 (distant): at or above 800 ft AAL start acceleration and flap/slat retraction while maintaining a positive climb, reduce power during or after retraction, climb at VZF + 10–20 kt to 3 000 ft AAL, then normal en-route climb."],["071.02.04.02:02","Noise abatement: fill in the gaps → ____ can provide visual guidance along a specific (noise-abatement) approach path. Detailed procedures in ____ (and AD 2.21 noise abatement).","Runway lead-in lighting can provide visual guidance along a specific (noise-abatement) approach path. Detailed procedures in AIP AD 2 and AD 3 (and AD 2.21 noise abatement)."],["071.02.04.02:03","Noise abatement: what do you remember about this? → Pilot influence: power settings, low-drag/low-power approach (CDA, …","Pilot influence: power settings, low-drag/low-power approach (CDA, delayed flap/gear), reverse thrust idle where permitted."],["071.02.05.01:01","Fire & smoke: fill in the gaps → ____: actions are ____ (memory items) — typically: fuel off, throttle closed/cut-off, mixture idle cut-off, fire extinguisher, land ASAP.","Carburettor fire / engine fire: actions are type-specific (memory items) — typically: fuel off, throttle closed/cut-off, mixture idle cut-off, fire extinguisher, land ASAP."],["071.02.05.01:01","Fire & smoke: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (class A — paper, wood, textiles; not on electrical or liquid fires), ____ (A, B, C incl. electrical — cabin and flight deck, lavatories fixed bottles), CO₂ (B and electrical; asphyxiation risk in confined spaces), dry powder (A/B/C;","Extinguishants: water (class A — paper, wood, textiles; not on electrical or liquid fires), halon/halon replacement (A, B, C incl. electrical — cabin and flight deck, lavatories fixed bottles), CO₂ (B and electrical; asphyxiation risk in confined spaces), dry powder (A/B/C;"],["071.02.05.01:01","Fire & smoke: fill in the gaps → ____ — visibility, corrosion, breathing), foam (B). Precautions: use PBE, avoid toxic fumes, don't spray water on electrical/oil fires, ventilate afterwards, watch for re-ignition.","not in the flight deck — visibility, corrosion, breathing), foam (B). Precautions: use PBE, avoid toxic fumes, don't spray water on electrical/oil fires, ventilate afterwards, watch for re-ignition."],["071.02.05.03:03","Fire & smoke: fill in the gaps → ____: halon (or equivalent) extinguisher; ____: halon + water; ____: automatic built-in halon extinguisher in waste bins + smoke detectors; cargo: fixed fire suppression (class C) or accessible with handheld extinguishers (class B).","Flight deck: halon (or equivalent) extinguisher; cabin: halon + water; lavatories: automatic built-in halon extinguisher in waste bins + smoke detectors; cargo: fixed fire suppression (class C) or accessible with handheld extinguishers (class B)."],["071.02.05.04:01","Fire & smoke: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ ____ and smoke goggles, establish communication, identify & isolate the source (electrical load shedding, air conditioning), smoke removal procedure, land as soon as possible; actions are type-specific (packs, outflow valve, recirculation fans).","Smoke in flight deck/cabin: oxygen masks (100 %/emergency) and smoke goggles, establish communication, identify & isolate the source (electrical load shedding, air conditioning), smoke removal procedure, land as soon as possible; actions are type-specific (packs, outflow valve, recirculation fans)."],["071.02.05.05:01","Fire & smoke: fill in the gaps → ____ after a heavy landing or ____: very high temperatures (hundreds of °C) → ____ melt to deflate tyres before they burst; risk of tyre explosion, wheel fire, hydraulic fluid fire; approach wheels from fore/aft, never from the side; don't set the parking brake if possible (brakes weld/heat), call fire services, cooling time charts before next take-off (brake-energy/quick-turnaround limit).","Overheated brakes after a heavy landing or RTO: very high temperatures (hundreds of °C) → fuse plugs melt to deflate tyres before they burst; risk of tyre explosion, wheel fire, hydraulic fluid fire; approach wheels from fore/aft, never from the side; don't set the parking brake if possible (brakes weld/heat), call fire services, cooling time charts before next take-off (brake-energy/quick-turnaround limit)."],["071.02.05.05:02","Fire & smoke: fill in the gaps → ____: carbon ____, absorbs more energy, keeps friction at high temperature, ____ of applications (so use fewer, firmer applications), but more expensive, oxidation; steel heavier, fades at high temperature, wear ∝ energy. Carbon brakes take longer to reach peak temperature → fuse-plug melting may occur long after stopping.","Steel vs carbon brakes: carbon lighter, absorbs more energy, keeps friction at high temperature, wear depends on number of applications (so use fewer, firmer applications), but more expensive, oxidation; steel heavier, fades at high temperature, wear ∝ energy. Carbon brakes take longer to reach peak temperature → fuse-plug melting may occur long after stopping."],["071.04.01.01:01","Specialised operations: fill in the gaps → CAT and commercial SPO operators: ____ ____ — ____ (every 6 months, two per 12 months), line check (12 months), emergency and safety equipment training and checking (12 months), CRM, ground and refresher training.","CAT and commercial SPO operators: recurrent training and checking — OPC (every 6 months, two per 12 months), line check (12 months), emergency and safety equipment training and checking (12 months), CRM, ground and refresher training."],["071.04.01.02:01","Specialised operations: fill in the gaps → ____ (Part-SPO): person assigned by the operator/third party performing tasks on the ground or in flight associated with the specialised activity; must follow the ____ and operator procedures, use safety equipment, report hazards/occurrences.","Task specialist (Part-SPO): person assigned by the operator/third party performing tasks on the ground or in flight associated with the specialised activity; must follow the PIC's instructions and operator procedures, use safety equipment, report hazards/occurrences."],["071.02.01.01:01","General review — 070 in one glance: what do you remember about this? → Annex 6 & EU Air-Ops structure; documents on board; FTL (FDP 13 h, …","Annex 6 & EU Air-Ops structure; documents on board; FTL (FDP 13 h, rest 12/10 h, 36 h weekly); decompression & emergency descent; wind shear & microburst recovery; wake turbulence separations (4/5/6 NM; 2 min); security (7500); ditching/evacuation (90 s, half exits);"],["071.02.01.01:01","General review — 070 in one glance: what do you remember about this? → DG (9 classes, NOTOC); equipment (ACAS/TAWS thresholds, first aid, …","DG (9 classes, NOTOC); equipment (ACAS/TAWS thresholds, first aid, megaphones, ELTs, life rafts 120 min/400 NM); contaminated runways (RWYCC, 9 √p / 7.7 √p); icing fluids (I orange … IV green); NADP 1/2; fire classes."],["071.02.04.01:02","Exam trap (General review — 070 in one glance): complete the fact → NADP ____: take-off flaps to ____; NADP 2: accelerate from 800 ft.","NADP 1: take-off flaps to 3 000 ft; NADP 2: accelerate from 800 ft."],["071.02.01.01:01","Exam trap (General review — 070 in one glance): complete the fact → MEL Cat C = ____days.","MEL Cat C = 10 days."],["071.02.01.01:01","EASA LO 071.02.01.01.01 → Explain the general rules for the operations manual.","Storage periods (typical): OFP, journey log, M&B documentation, NOTAM/AIS & MET briefing, passenger/cargo manifests, DG notification — 3 months; flight/duty/rest records — 24 months; crew training/checking records — 3 years; FDR/CVR per accident/incident rules. / Structure (Part-ORO AMC): Part A — General/basic (all non-type-related policies & procedures), Part B — Aeroplane operating matters (type-related) (limitations, normal/abnormal/emergency procedures, performance, M&B, MEL, icing limitations), Part C — Route/role/area and aerodrome instructions, Part D — Training."],["071.02.01.01:02","EASA LO 071.02.01.01.02 → Explain the structure and subject headings of the operations manual.","Structure (Part-ORO AMC): Part A — General/basic (all non-type-related policies & procedures), Part B — Aeroplane operating matters (type-related) (limitations, normal/abnormal/emergency procedures, performance, M&B, MEL, icing limitations), Part C — Route/role/area and aerodrome instructions, Part D — Training."],["071.02.01.01:08","EASA LO 071.02.01.01.08 → State that the following items are included into Part A: — altitude alerting system procedures; — ground proximity warning system procedures; — policy and procedures for the use of traffic alert and collision avoidance system (TCAS)/airborne collision avoidance system (ACAS).","Part A includes: ground de-icing/anti-icing, adverse & hazardous atmospheric conditions, wake turbulence, crew incapacitation, use of MEL/CDL, security, handling of accidents and occurrences, altitude alerting, GPWS/TAWS procedures, TCAS/ACAS policy (and rotor downwash for helicopters), FTL, fuel policy, operating minima, DG policy."],["071.02.01.03:05","EASA LO 071.02.01.03.05 → Explain the responsibilities of the flight crew members with regard to MEL.","Operator: establish MEL, obtain approval, procedures/rectification control, rectification interval extensions (RIE) under conditions. Authority: approve MEL/MMEL. Flight crew: know the MEL and apply (O) procedures. / Extent: MEL covers only items listed in the MMEL (equipment whose loss is acceptable); items not listed must be operative; with (O) operational and (M) maintenance procedures."],["071.02.01.03:06","EASA LO 071.02.01.03.06 → Explain the responsibilities of the commander with regard to MEL.","Extent: MEL covers only items listed in the MMEL (equipment whose loss is acceptable); items not listed must be operative; with (O) operational and (M) maintenance procedures. / MMEL: master list established by the type-certificate holder, approved by EASA (OSD), for a type. MEL: operator's list based on the MMEL (no less restrictive), tailored to its aircraft/operations, approved by the competent authority."],["071.02.02.01:03","EASA LO 071.02.02.01.03 → List the types of de-icing/anti-icing fluids available.","Holdover time (HOT): estimated time the anti-icing fluid prevents accumulation on protected surfaces — starts at the beginning of the final (anti-icing) application. / De-icing: removal of frost, ice, snow, slush to provide clean surfaces. Anti-icing: prevents formation/accumulation for a limited time. One-step: heated anti-icing fluid used to de-ice and anti-ice at once. Two-step: first de-icing (heated water/Type I), then anti-icing fluid within 3 min (before the first freezes)."],["071.02.02.01:04","EASA LO 071.02.02.01.04 → Explain the procedure to be followed when an aeroplane has exceeded the holdover time.","Holdover time (HOT): estimated time the anti-icing fluid prevents accumulation on protected surfaces — starts at the beginning of the final (anti-icing) application. / HOT exceeded → re-treat (de-ice/anti-ice again), unless a pre- take-off contamination check (outside, within 5 min of take-off) shows surfaces clean where permitted."],["071.02.02.01:07","EASA LO 071.02.02.01.07 → Explain why an aircraft has to be treated symmetrically.","Holdover time (HOT): estimated time the anti-icing fluid prevents accumulation on protected surfaces — starts at the beginning of the final (anti-icing) application. / De-icing: removal of frost, ice, snow, slush to provide clean surfaces. Anti-icing: prevents formation/accumulation for a limited time. One-step: heated anti-icing fluid used to de-ice and anti-ice at once. Two-step: first de-icing (heated water/Type I), then anti-icing fluid within 3 min (before the first freezes)."],["071.02.02.01:09","EASA LO 071.02.02.01.09 → Explain why a commander shall not commence take-off unless the external surfaces are clear of any deposit which might adversely affect the performance or controllability of the aircraft except as permitted in the flight manual.","Operators must establish procedures (responsibilities, communication, inspections, HOT use); commander shall not take off unless surfaces are clear. Operations in icing conditions: aircraft certified/equipped (anti-ice, ice detectors, lights to see ice), limitations in OM B. / HOT exceeded → re-treat (de-ice/anti-ice again), unless a pre- take-off contamination check (outside, within 5 min of take-off) shows surfaces clean where permitted."],["071.02.02.01:10","EASA LO 071.02.02.01.10 → Explain the requirements for operations in icing conditions.","Holdover time (HOT): estimated time the anti-icing fluid prevents accumulation on protected surfaces — starts at the beginning of the final (anti-icing) application. / De-icing: removal of frost, ice, snow, slush to provide clean surfaces. Anti-icing: prevents formation/accumulation for a limited time. One-step: heated anti-icing fluid used to de-ice and anti-ice at once. Two-step: first de-icing (heated water/Type I), then anti-icing fluid within 3 min (before the first freezes)."],["071.02.02.02:01","EASA LO 071.02.02.02.01 → Explain that the effects of icing are wide-ranging, unpredictable and dependent upon individual aircraft design. The magnitude of these effects is dependent upon many variables, but the effects can be both significant and dangerous.","Effects are wide-ranging, unpredictable and depend on design. At a given speed/α, lift can be reduced by up to 30 % and drag increased by up to 40 % → higher stall speed (stall at lower α, stall warning may not activate in time), reduced controllability, altered handling (tailplane stall with flaps extended)."],["071.02.02.02:03","EASA LO 071.02.02.02.03 → Explain that ice on critical surfaces and on the airframe may also break away during take-off and","Ice can break away on take-off/in flight and be ingested by rear-mounted engines or hit control surfaces. Ice on pitot/static/AoA vanes → false altitude, airspeed, AoA and engine-power (EPR) data. / HOT exceeded → re-treat (de-ice/anti-ice again), unless a pre- take-off contamination check (outside, within 5 min of take-off) shows surfaces clean where permitted."],["071.02.03.01:01","EASA LO 071.02.03.01.01 → Explain that the presence of birds that constitute a potential hazard to aircraft operations is part of the pre-flight information.","Bird hazards are part of pre-flight information (NOTAM, ATIS, AIP). Crews report observed birds to ATS → AIS distributes. AIP ENR 5.6: bird migration and areas with sensitive fauna."],["071.02.03.01:02","EASA LO 071.02.03.01.02 → Explain how information concerning the presence of birds observed by aircrews is made available to the aeronautical information service (AIS) for distribution as the circumstances dictate.","Bird hazards are part of pre-flight information (NOTAM, ATIS, AIP). Crews report observed birds to ATS → AIS distributes. AIP ENR 5.6: bird migration and areas with sensitive fauna."],["071.02.04.01:01","EASA LO 071.02.04.01.01 → Define the operator’s responsibilities regarding the establishment of noise-abatement procedures.","Runway lead-in lighting can provide visual guidance along a specific (noise-abatement) approach path. Detailed procedures in AIP AD 2 and AD 3 (and AD 2.21 noise abatement). / Operator establishes noise-abatement departure procedures (NADPs) not compromising safety; max two NADPs per aeroplane type (one close-in, one distant). Commander may decide not to fly an NADP if safety requires."],["071.02.04.01:03","EASA LO 071.02.04.01.03 → State that the PIC/commander has the authority to decide not to execute an NADP if conditions preclude the safe execution of the procedure.","Operator establishes noise-abatement departure procedures (NADPs) not compromising safety; max two NADPs per aeroplane type (one close-in, one distant). Commander may decide not to fly an NADP if safety requires."],["071.02.05.02:01","EASA LO 071.02.05.02.01 → Explain that the actions to be taken in the event of an engine fire may be type-specific and should be known by the pilot.","Carburettor fire / engine fire: actions are type-specific (memory items) — typically: fuel off, throttle closed/cut-off, mixture idle cut-off, fire extinguisher, land ASAP."],["071.02.05.03:01","EASA LO 071.02.05.03.01 → Identify the different types of extinguishants used in handheld fire extinguishers and the type of fire for which each one may be used.","Flight deck: halon (or equivalent) extinguisher; cabin: halon + water; lavatories: automatic built-in halon extinguisher in waste bins + smoke detectors; cargo: fixed fire suppression (class C) or accessible with handheld extinguishers (class B)."]],"92":[["022.04.02.01:08","Turn & slip, turn coordinator: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (1 degree of freedom, spin axis horizontal-lateral, gimbal restrained by springs) — precession in a turn deflects the gimbal ∝ rate of turn. ____ (360° in 2 min); marks for rate 1 (sometimes rate 2).","Rate-of-turn indicator: rate gyro (1 degree of freedom, spin axis horizontal-lateral, gimbal restrained by springs) — precession in a turn deflects the gimbal ∝ rate of turn. Rate-1 turn = 3°/s (360° in 2 min); marks for rate 1 (sometimes rate 2)."],["022.04.02.01:04","Turn & slip, turn coordinator: fill in the gaps → Bank required for a given rate ↑ with ____ (tan Ø = ω·V/g) → at high speed a rate-1 turn needs too much bank → ____ ____.","Bank required for a given rate ↑ with TAS (tan Ø = ω·V/g) → at high speed a rate-1 turn needs too much bank → bank angle (e.g. 25–30°) becomes the limiting factor."],["022.04.02.01:06","Turn & slip, turn coordinator: fill in the gaps → ____: ball in a curved liquid-filled tube — shows the direction of the ____ ____; ball centred = balanced (coordinated). Slip (too much bank/too little rudder): ball inside the turn (low side); skid (too much rudder): ball outside. \"Step on the ball\".","Balance (slip) indicator: ball in a curved liquid-filled tube — shows the direction of the resultant of gravity and centripetal force; ball centred = balanced (coordinated). Slip (too much bank/too little rudder): ball inside the turn (low side); skid (too much rudder): ball outside. \"Step on the ball\"."],["022.04.02.01:01","Turn & slip, turn coordinator: fill in the gaps → ____: gyro gimbal ____ → senses ____ and yaw rate → responds already when rolling into a turn; shows a small aircraft symbol (not a bank indicator!). Turn & slip shows yaw rate only (needle).","Turn coordinator: gyro gimbal tilted ~30° → senses roll rate and yaw rate → responds already when rolling into a turn; shows a small aircraft symbol (not a bank indicator!). Turn & slip shows yaw rate only (needle)."],["022.06.03.01:02","Flight director: fill in the gaps → Purpose: ____ ____ (direction and magnitude) needed to follow a selected trajectory — the pilot (or AP) flies the command bars. Displays: ____ (separate pitch & roll bars — fly to centre) or V-bar / command wedge (fly the aircraft symbol into the V).","Purpose: computes and displays the pitch/roll attitude commands (direction and magnitude) needed to follow a selected trajectory — the pilot (or AP) flies the command bars. Displays: crossbars (separate pitch & roll bars — fly to centre) or V-bar / command wedge (fly the aircraft symbol into the V)."],["022.06.03.01:07","Flight director: fill in the gaps → FD vs AP: the FD only ____, the AP ____; both use the ____ computer/modes and the same FCU/MCP → with AP engaged, the FD bars show what the AP commands → cross-check.","FD vs AP: the FD only indicates, the AP acts; both use the same computer/modes and the same FCU/MCP → with AP engaged, the FD bars show what the AP commands → cross-check."],["022.06.03.01:04","Flight director: fill in the gaps → Use: ____ (manual flying following bars), ____ (FD off), ____ + FD (monitoring), PF selects modes (or PM on PF's request when hand-flying). If FD is shown, follow it or switch it off — don't fly against the bars (confusing, wrong guidance). Check FMA/FMC data; if FD guidance is incorrect → don't follow, turn it off.","Use: FD only (manual flying following bars), AP only (FD off), AP + FD (monitoring), PF selects modes (or PM on PF's request when hand-flying). If FD is shown, follow it or switch it off — don't fly against the bars (confusing, wrong guidance). Check FMA/FMC data; if FD guidance is incorrect → don't follow, turn it off."],["022.10.01.01:02","Communication systems & data link: fill in the gaps → ____ transmits digital messages (text/data) instead of voice: fewer misunderstandings, less frequency congestion, printable/storable, but slower for urgent matters, no party-line information.","Data link transmits digital messages (text/data) instead of voice: fewer misunderstandings, less frequency congestion, printable/storable, but slower for urgent matters, no party-line information."],["022.10.01.01:04","Communication systems & data link: fill in the gaps → Links: ____ (long range via ionospheric reflection, poor quality/interference, slow data rate), ____ (line of sight, good quality, limited range ~200 NM at altitude, fast), ____ (global except polar regions (geostationary), good quality, costly, some latency).","Links: HF (long range via ionospheric reflection, poor quality/interference, slow data rate), VHF (line of sight, good quality, limited range ~200 NM at altitude, fast), SATCOM (global except polar regions (geostationary), good quality, costly, some latency)."],["022.10.01.01:05","Communication systems & data link: fill in the gaps → ____ = ground → aircraft; ____ = aircraft → ground.","Uplink = ground → aircraft; downlink = aircraft → ground."],["022.10.01.02:03","Communication systems & data link: fill in the gaps → ____ network (VHF/HF/SATCOM data): ____ (airline operational control) and ____ applications via the ATSU (air traffic service unit). On-board: ATSU communications computer, CDU/MCDU (AOC pages), DCDU (data communication display unit for ATC messages), ATC message visual annunciator (attention getter), printer.","ACARS network (VHF/HF/SATCOM data): AOC (airline operational control) and ATC applications via the ATSU (air traffic service unit). On-board: ATSU communications computer, CDU/MCDU (AOC pages), DCDU (data communication display unit for ATC messages), ATC message visual annunciator (attention getter), printer."],["022.10.01.02:04","Communication systems & data link: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (Out of gate, Off ground, On ground, Into gate), load sheet, passenger info/connecting flights, weather, maintenance messages, fuel figures. ____: departure clearance (DCL/PDC), oceanic clearance, D-ATIS, CPDLC.","AOC examples: OOOI (Out of gate, Off ground, On ground, Into gate), load sheet, passenger info/connecting flights, weather, maintenance messages, fuel figures. ATC examples: departure clearance (DCL/PDC), oceanic clearance, D-ATIS, CPDLC."],["022.10.02.01:03","Communication systems & data link: fill in the gaps → ____: part of ICAO CNS/ATM concept. ____ (Boeing/Airbus) uses the ACARS network; ____ uses ACARS and ATN (aeronautical telecommunication network, Link 2000+ in Europe). FANS 1/A applications: AFN (ATS facility notification — log-on), ADS-C (automatic dependent surveillance — contract), CPDLC.","FANS: part of ICAO CNS/ATM concept. FANS A/1 (Boeing/Airbus) uses the ACARS network; FANS B/2 uses ACARS and ATN (aeronautical telecommunication network, Link 2000+ in Europe). FANS 1/A applications: AFN (ATS facility notification — log-on), ADS-C (automatic dependent surveillance — contract), CPDLC."],["022.10.02.01:04","Communication systems & data link: fill in the gaps → ADS replaces ____ surveillance where no radar (oceanic); CPDLC replaces ____. An ATSU may use ADS only, CPDLC only, or both.","ADS replaces SSR surveillance where no radar (oceanic); CPDLC replaces VHF voice. An ATSU may use ADS only, CPDLC only, or both."],["022.10.02.01:05","Communication systems & data link: fill in the gaps → ____: pilot enters the ATSU's 4-letter code → message with flight ID, registration/24-bit address, position, application ____ → ATSU connects.","AFN log-on: pilot enters the ATSU's 4-letter code → message with flight ID, registration/24-bit address, position, application versions → ATSU connects."],["022.10.01.01:01","Communication systems & data link: fill in the gaps → ____: standard pre-formatted messages (clearances, requests \"REQUEST CLIMB TO FL360\", reports); pilot practice: ____ ____; don't accept conditional clearances without understanding; voice remains primary for urgent situations.","CPDLC: standard pre-formatted messages (clearances, requests \"REQUEST CLIMB TO FL360\", reports); pilot practice: read, both pilots agree, then WILCO/UNABLE/STANDBY; don't accept conditional clearances without understanding; voice remains primary for urgent situations."],["022.10.02.01:08","Communication systems & data link: fill in the gaps → ____ (set by the ATSU, not the pilot): ____ (at intervals), ____, on event (waypoint change, vertical/lateral deviation, level change), emergency mode — triggered by the pilot (or squawk 7700) → high reporting rate, overrides other contracts.","ADS contracts (set by the ATSU, not the pilot): periodic (at intervals), on demand, on event (waypoint change, vertical/lateral deviation, level change), emergency mode — triggered by the pilot (or squawk 7700) → high reporting rate, overrides other contracts."],["022.11.03.01:01","FMS / FMGS: fill in the gaps → Purpose: ____ ____ (to AP/FD/AT), fuel/time predictions, radio tuning.","Purpose: navigation, lateral & vertical flight planning, performance optimisation, guidance (to AP/FD/AT), fuel/time predictions, radio tuning."],["022.11.01.01:03","FMS / FMGS: fill in the gaps → ____: two ____, two ____, cross-talk bus (synchronises data). Failure of one FMC → use the other (single FMS mode, both CDUs driven by the remaining FMC, reduced redundancy/RNP capability); one CDU → use the other CDU; cross-talk bus → FMCs operate independently (inputs must be made on both, possible disagreement).","Dual architecture: two FMCs, two CDUs/MCDUs, cross-talk bus (synchronises data). Failure of one FMC → use the other (single FMS mode, both CDUs driven by the remaining FMC, reduced redundancy/RNP capability); one CDU → use the other CDU; cross-talk bus → FMCs operate independently (inputs must be made on both, possible disagreement)."],["022.11.03.01:07","FMS / FMGS: fill in the gaps → Integration: inputs from ____ ____; outputs to ____.","Integration: inputs from ADC, IRS, GPS, radio receivers (DME/VOR/LOC), fuel quantity, engine data, clock; outputs to AP/FD, autothrust, EFIS ND/PFD, radio tuning."],["022.11.02.01:04","FMS / FMGS: fill in the gaps → ____: waypoints, NAVAIDs, airports, runways, airways, SIDs/STARs/approaches, company routes.","Navigation database: waypoints, NAVAIDs, airports, runways, airways, SIDs/STARs/approaches, company routes."],["022.11.02.01:02","FMS / FMGS: fill in the gaps → ____ — ____ (active + standby) to switch at the effective date; pilot must check the ____ and activate the correct one (only on the ground); pilot cannot modify database data; changing database erases user-defined data/route (risky in flight, wrong procedures if expired DB).","28-day AIRAC — two databases (active + standby) to switch at the effective date; pilot must check the effective dates and activate the correct one (only on the ground); pilot cannot modify database data; changing database erases user-defined data/route (risky in flight, wrong procedures if expired DB)."],["022.11.02.01:03","FMS / FMGS: fill in the gaps → ____: ____, ____ (place/bearing/distance), PBX (place/bearing–place/bearing intersection), PD (along-track place/distance).","User-defined waypoints: lat/long, PBD (place/bearing/distance), PBX (place/bearing–place/bearing intersection), PD (along-track place/distance)."],["022.11.02.02:05","FMS / FMGS: fill in the gaps → ____: drag/engine models (fuel flow, speeds, limits); must match the ____; not modifiable by pilot. Performance factor corrects for individual aircraft degradation (fuel burn). Cost index (CI) = time cost ÷ fuel cost → ____, high CI = faster (min time); influences ECON speeds, optimum level, TOD.","Performance database: drag/engine models (fuel flow, speeds, limits); must match the engine type & variant; not modifiable by pilot. Performance factor corrects for individual aircraft degradation (fuel burn). Cost index (CI) = time cost ÷ fuel cost → CI 0 = max range (minimum fuel), high CI = faster (min time); influences ECON speeds, optimum level, TOD."],["022.11.03.01:01","FMS / FMGS: fill in the gaps → ____: LNAV/VNAV guidance, present position, ____ ____, altitude predictions; computed from database + entered data + measured data (wind, temperature, fuel). Wrong inputs (ZFW, fuel, CG, cruise level, winds, runway, V-speeds, temperature) → wrong predictions/speeds (e.g.","FMS outputs: LNAV/VNAV guidance, present position, time and fuel predictions, altitude predictions; computed from database + entered data + measured data (wind, temperature, fuel). Wrong inputs (ZFW, fuel, CG, cruise level, winds, runway, V-speeds, temperature) → wrong predictions/speeds (e.g."],["022.11.03.01:05","FMS / FMGS: fill in the gaps → ____ ____, tail strikes). Fuel predictions assume normal configuration — ____ down, flaps/spoilers extended not detected → predictions optimistic.","ZFW entered as TOW → speeds too high/low, tail strikes). Fuel predictions assume normal configuration — engine out, gear down, flaps/spoilers extended not detected → predictions optimistic."],["022.11.03.01:07","FMS / FMGS: fill in the gaps → ____ (DME/DME, VOR/DME) for position updating. ____: ____ threshold (take-off update). FMS computes its own FMS position from the best single source or a blended multi-sensor solution (e.g.","Auto-tuned radio receivers (DME/DME, VOR/DME) for position updating. Position inputs: GPS, IRS, DME, VOR, LOC, runway threshold (take-off update). FMS computes its own FMS position from the best single source or a blended multi-sensor solution (e.g."],["022.11.03.01:09","FMS / FMGS: fill in the gaps → GPS/IRS mix); loss of ____ → reduced accuracy (ANP ↑) → may lose ____. ____ (VOR/ILS needles) is independent of the FMS → cross-check computed guidance with raw data.","GPS/IRS mix); loss of GPS → reduced accuracy (ANP ↑) → may lose RNP/PBN approach capability. Raw data (VOR/ILS needles) is independent of the FMS → cross-check computed guidance with raw data."],["022.11.04.01:02","FMS / FMGS: fill in the gaps → ____: screen with pages, ____, ____, alphanumeric keys; scratchpad (bottom line) for entering/checking data before placing it on a line. Boxes (□□□) = compulsory data, dashes (---) = optional.","CDU/MCDU: screen with pages, line select keys, menu/function keys, alphanumeric keys; scratchpad (bottom line) for entering/checking data before placing it on a line. Boxes (□□□) = compulsory data, dashes (---) = optional."],["022.11.01.01:01","FMS / FMGS: fill in the gaps → ____ ____: identification/status (DB validity), position initialisation (IRS alignment), ____ (origin, destination, company route, SID/STAR, airways, runway), performance (ZFW/weights, fuel, reserves, CI, cruise FL, temperature), take-off data (flaps, thrust, V-speeds, trans alt), check with the other pilot and against the OFP.","Pre- flight set-up: identification/status (DB validity), position initialisation (IRS alignment), route (origin, destination, company route, SID/STAR, airways, runway), performance (ZFW/weights, fuel, reserves, CI, cruise FL, temperature), take-off data (flaps, thrust, V-speeds, trans alt), check with the other pilot and against the OFP."],["022.12.02.01:03","Alerting & proximity systems: fill in the gaps → ____ alert categories: ____ (immediate crew awareness and ____ — red, continuous aural), caution (immediate awareness, subsequent action — amber/yellow, attention-getter aural), advisory (awareness, may require subsequent action — other colour (not red/amber/yellow/green), no aural), status/memo.","CS-25/AMC 25.1322 alert categories: warning (immediate crew awareness and immediate action — red, continuous aural), caution (immediate awareness, subsequent action — amber/yellow, attention-getter aural), advisory (awareness, may require subsequent action — other colour (not red/amber/yellow/green), no aural), status/memo."],["022.12.02.01:04","Alerting & proximity systems: fill in the gaps → ____ (red, continuous aural): e.g. engine fire, cabin altitude, take-off config, autopilot disconnect, overspeed. ____ ____ (amber, single chime): e.g. generator failure, pump low pressure, anti-ice fault. Procedure: acknowledge (press master light), silence aural, identify & confirm the failure, run the procedure/checklist.","Master warning (red, continuous aural): e.g. engine fire, cabin altitude, take-off config, autopilot disconnect, overspeed. Master caution (amber, single chime): e.g. generator failure, pump low pressure, anti-ice fault. Procedure: acknowledge (press master light), silence aural, identify & confirm the failure, run the procedure/checklist."],["022.12.03.01:04","Alerting & proximity systems: fill in the gaps → ____: warns ____ with a unique warning (stick shaker, horn — must be unmistakable). Types: flapper switch/vane on the leading edge (light aircraft), AoA vane/probe with computer (inputs: AoA, flap/slat position, Mach) → stick shaker. Warning speed ≥ ____ stall (CS-25: sufficient margin); actual stall speed is lower.","Stall warning system (SWS): warns before the stall with a unique warning (stick shaker, horn — must be unmistakable). Types: flapper switch/vane on the leading edge (light aircraft), AoA vane/probe with computer (inputs: AoA, flap/slat position, Mach) → stick shaker. Warning speed ≥ 5 kt or 5 % above the stall (CS-25: sufficient margin); actual stall speed is lower."],["022.12.04.01:02","Alerting & proximity systems: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ the stall — ____ (mechanical controls, T-tail/deep-stall aircraft) or FBW α- protection (α-floor with autothrust TOGA, hard limit at α-max). SWS warns, protection acts.","Stall protection: prevents the stall — stick pusher (mechanical controls, T-tail/deep-stall aircraft) or FBW α- protection (α-floor with autothrust TOGA, hard limit at α-max). SWS warns, protection acts."],["022.12.05.01:03","Alerting & proximity systems: fill in the gaps → ____: aural (clacker) + ________/MMO pointer (\"barber pole\" needle on ASIs, barber-pole vertical band on the PFD speed tape); for large aeroplanes with electronic displays an aural warning is required.","Overspeed warning: aural (clacker) + VMO/MMO pointer (\"barber pole\" needle on ASIs, barber-pole vertical band on the PFD speed tape); for large aeroplanes with electronic displays an aural warning is required."],["022.12.06.01:01","Alerting & proximity systems: fill in the gaps → ____ (config warning, AMC 25.703): at take-off thrust with ____, ____, stabiliser trim out of green band, parking brake set, doors not closed, rudder trim not neutral.","Take-off warning (config warning, AMC 25.703): at take-off thrust with flaps/slats not in T/O range, speedbrakes/spoilers not retracted, stabiliser trim out of green band, parking brake set, doors not closed, rudder trim not neutral."],["022.12.10.01:12","Alerting & proximity systems: fill in the gaps → ____: approaching the selected altitude (e.g. ____ ____: chime/light) and deviation from the selected altitude (e.g. > 200–300 ft: warning).","Altitude alert: approaching the selected altitude (e.g. within ~900–1 000 ft: chime/light) and deviation from the selected altitude (e.g. > 200–300 ft: warning)."],["022.12.08.01:01","Alerting & proximity systems: fill in the gaps → ____ (low-range): measures ____ ground by the time/frequency shift of a signal reflected from the ground (FMCW, 4.2–4.4 GHz); range typically ____ ft (5 000 ft on some). Displays: analogue dial, digital readout on PFD, DH alert.","Radio altimeter (low-range): measures height above the ground by the time/frequency shift of a signal reflected from the ground (FMCW, 4.2–4.4 GHz); range typically 0–2 500 ft (5 000 ft on some). Displays: analogue dial, digital readout on PFD, DH alert."],["022.12.08.01:06","Alerting & proximity systems: fill in the gaps → Inputs to ____, autoland (____, align), autothrust, TCAS inhibits, configuration warnings, FD modes. ____ (e.g. reading −8 ft at altitude) → autothrust RETARD/idle on approach (Turkish 1951), wrong GPWS alerts, autoland problems.","Inputs to GPWS/TAWS, autoland (flare, retard, align), autothrust, TCAS inhibits, configuration warnings, FD modes. Faulty RA (e.g. reading −8 ft at altitude) → autothrust RETARD/idle on approach (Turkish 1951), wrong GPWS alerts, autoland problems."],["022.12.09.02:06","Alerting & proximity systems: fill in the gaps → ____: warns of dangerous proximity to terrain based on ____ ____ configuration (gear/flaps), airspeed.","GPWS: warns of dangerous proximity to terrain based on radio altitude, barometric vertical speed, ILS glideslope, configuration (gear/flaps), airspeed."],["022.04.02.01:02","Alerting & proximity systems: fill in the gaps → Modes: ____ excessive descent rate (\"SINK RATE\", \"PULL UP\"), ____ excessive terrain closure rate (\"TERRAIN TERRAIN\", \"PULL UP\"), ____ altitude loss after take-off/go-around (\"DON'T SINK\"), 4 unsafe terrain clearance not in landing config (\"TOO LOW GEAR/FLAPS/TERRAIN\"), 5 excessive glideslope deviation (\"GLIDESLOPE\"), 6 callouts/bank angle (\"MINIMUMS\", \"BANK ANGLE\"), 7 wind shear (\"WINDSHEAR\").","Modes: 1 excessive descent rate (\"SINK RATE\", \"PULL UP\"), 2 excessive terrain closure rate (\"TERRAIN TERRAIN\", \"PULL UP\"), 3 altitude loss after take-off/go-around (\"DON'T SINK\"), 4 unsafe terrain clearance not in landing config (\"TOO LOW GEAR/FLAPS/TERRAIN\"), 5 excessive glideslope deviation (\"GLIDESLOPE\"), 6 callouts/bank angle (\"MINIMUMS\", \"BANK ANGLE\"), 7 wind shear (\"WINDSHEAR\")."],["022.12.09.02:05","Alerting & proximity systems: fill in the gaps → ____ (HTAWS for helicopters): adds a ____ ____ comparison using precise position (GPS/FMS) → look-ahead alerts (\"CAUTION TERRAIN\" ~40–60 s, \"TERRAIN AHEAD, PULL UP\" ~20–30 s before impact) and terrain display on the ND (colours: green/yellow/red by relative height; magenta unknown), terrain-clearance floor.","TAWS/EGPWS (HTAWS for helicopters): adds a forward-looking terrain database comparison using precise position (GPS/FMS) → look-ahead alerts (\"CAUTION TERRAIN\" ~40–60 s, \"TERRAIN AHEAD, PULL UP\" ~20–30 s before impact) and terrain display on the ND (colours: green/yellow/red by relative height; magenta unknown), terrain-clearance floor."],["022.12.09.02:06","Alerting & proximity systems: fill in the gaps → Needs precise position — position error → false/missed alerts. ____ when flying close to terrain with ____ ____ (e.g. visual approaches in mountains).","Needs precise position — position error → false/missed alerts. Spurious warnings when flying close to terrain with high descent rate and/or high speed (e.g. visual approaches in mountains)."],["022.12.10.01:02","Alerting & proximity systems: fill in the gaps → ____: ICAO standard ____ system — a last-resort safety net that ____. Outputs: other traffic (open white diamond), proximate (<","ACAS II/TCAS II: ICAO standard anti-collision system — a last-resort safety net that does not guarantee any separation. Outputs: other traffic (open white diamond), proximate (<"],["022.12.10.01:16","Alerting & proximity systems: fill in the gaps → ____ and ____, filled white diamond), TA (amber filled circle, \"TRAFFIC TRAFFIC\", ~____ before CPA), RA (red filled square, \"CLIMB\", \"DESCEND\", \"MONITOR VERTICAL SPEED\", \"LEVEL OFF\" ~15–35 s).","6 NM and ± 1 200 ft, filled white diamond), TA (amber filled circle, \"TRAFFIC TRAFFIC\", ~20–48 s before CPA), RA (red filled square, \"CLIMB\", \"DESCEND\", \"MONITOR VERTICAL SPEED\", \"LEVEL OFF\" ~15–35 s)."],["022.12.10.01:07","Alerting & proximity systems: fill in the gaps → RAs in the ____, flown ____ (AP/FD off, smoothly, ~0.25 g within 5 s); a ____ requires no input — reacting instinctively may cause a real conflict. Two TCAS II aircraft → coordinated RAs (via Mode S). Can handle multiple threats.","RAs in the vertical plane only, flown manually (AP/FD off, smoothly, ~0.25 g within 5 s); a preventive RA requires no input — reacting instinctively may cause a real conflict. Two TCAS II aircraft → coordinated RAs (via Mode S). Can handle multiple threats."],["022.12.10.01:09","Alerting & proximity systems: fill in the gaps → Intruder ____ ____ (Mode A only) → ____ (no RA) — many GA targets in busy areas → distractions; intruder with no transponder is invisible.","Intruder without altitude reporting (Mode A only) → TA only (no RA) — many GA targets in busy areas → distractions; intruder with no transponder is invisible."],["022.12.10.01:12","Alerting & proximity systems: fill in the gaps → TCAS interacts with the ____ (coordination, own altitude), ____ (inhibits RAs near the ground: descend RAs < ~1 100 ft, all RAs < ~1 000 ft, aural < 500 ft), ____ (own altitude/performance limits); top and bottom antennas (directional top).","TCAS interacts with the Mode S transponder (coordination, own altitude), radio altimeter (inhibits RAs near the ground: descend RAs < ~1 100 ft, all RAs < ~1 000 ft, aural < 500 ft), ADC (own altitude/performance limits); top and bottom antennas (directional top)."],["022.12.10.01:12","Alerting & proximity systems: fill in the gaps → Interrogations on ____, replies 1 090 MHz; range ____ horizontally, ____ (normal), extended to ~10 000 ft ABV or BLW when selected; reduced surveillance/range in dense traffic (limit on number of tracked intruders).","Interrogations on 1 030 MHz, replies 1 090 MHz; range ~35–40 NM horizontally, ±2 000 ft (normal), extended to ~10 000 ft ABV or BLW when selected; reduced surveillance/range in dense traffic (limit on number of tracked intruders)."],["022.12.10.01:18","Alerting & proximity systems: fill in the gaps → Alarm criteria: ____ (time to closest point of approach = range/closure rate) and ____. RA display: ____ area on the VSI (\"avoid the red\"), some with green target band (\"fly the green\"). Never manoeuvre horizontally based on the TCAS display — bearing is inaccurate.","Alarm criteria: TAU (time to closest point of approach = range/closure rate) and altitude difference. RA display: red area on the VSI (\"avoid the red\"), some with green target band (\"fly the green\"). Never manoeuvre horizontally based on the TCAS display — bearing is inaccurate."],["022.13.01.01:01","Displays: fill in the gaps → Technologies: ____ (heavy, heat, poor in bright light) and ____ (lighter, less heat, but viewing angle/cold temperature response/glare); limitations: cockpit temperature, glare, resolution. ____ (attitude + FD bars, speed/rate of turn/slip, glideslope/localiser, DH) and HSI (heading card, course pointer, course deviation bar, TO/FROM, glideslope, DME, heading bug).","Technologies: CRT (heavy, heat, poor in bright light) and LCD (lighter, less heat, but viewing angle/cold temperature response/glare); limitations: cockpit temperature, glare, resolution. ADI (attitude + FD bars, speed/rate of turn/slip, glideslope/localiser, DH) and HSI (heading card, course pointer, course deviation bar, TO/FROM, glideslope, DME, heading bug)."],["022.13.03.01:02","Displays: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (baro setting, ND mode/range, data selectors, VOR/ADF needles, DA/DH/MINIMUMS), ____ (PFD, ND), symbol generators (DMCs) — if a display unit fails, transfer the image to another DU; if a symbol generator fails, switch to the other SG (both sides then show the same source → reduced independence).","EFIS: control panel (baro setting, ND mode/range, data selectors, VOR/ADF needles, DA/DH/MINIMUMS), display units (PFD, ND), symbol generators (DMCs) — if a display unit fails, transfer the image to another DU; if a symbol generator fails, switch to the other SG (both sides then show the same source → reduced independence)."],["022.13.03.01:01","Displays: fill in the gaps → ____ (ISIS: attitude, speed, altitude) needed if all displays fail — small, off to the side → difficult to scan. ____ adjusts brightness.","Standby instruments (ISIS: attitude, speed, altitude) needed if all displays fail — small, off to the side → difficult to scan. Remote light sensor adjusts brightness."],["022.13.03.02:03","Displays: fill in the gaps → ____ (\"basic T\"): ____ (horizon, aircraft symbol, FD bars) in the centre, ____ left (current speed, limit speeds per configuration (VMO, VFE, stall/α-protection band), speed trend vector (speed in 10 s), selected-speed bug;","PFD (\"basic T\"): attitude (horizon, aircraft symbol, FD bars) in the centre, speed tape left (current speed, limit speeds per configuration (VMO, VFE, stall/α-protection band), speed trend vector (speed in 10 s), selected-speed bug;"],["022.13.03.02:04","Displays: fill in the gaps → Mach readout; a selected Mach is converted into an IAS bug), ____ right (baro setting, selected altitude bug, altitude alert highlighting), ____ scale bottom (selected heading/track bug), ____ top, VSI right of the altitude (with TCAS RA red/green bands), radio altitude, ILS/RNP deviation scales (LOC, G/S, LDEV/VDEV for LPV/GLS), DA/H marker.","Mach readout; a selected Mach is converted into an IAS bug), altitude tape right (baro setting, selected altitude bug, altitude alert highlighting), heading scale bottom (selected heading/track bug), FMAs top, VSI right of the altitude (with TCAS RA red/green bands), radio altitude, ILS/RNP deviation scales (LOC, G/S, LDEV/VDEV for LPV/GLS), DA/H marker."],["022.13.03.03:04","Displays: fill in the gaps → ____ modes: ____ (heading/track up, route from FMS, aircraft symbol, range arcs, TAS/GS, ____ arrow, selected heading/track, RMI needles for VOR/ADF with ident, waypoints/NAVAIDs/airports, weather radar/terrain/TCAS overlays), VOR (ROSE VOR) and APP (ROSE LS) (full compass, HSI-like course pointer, deviation, TO/FROM, glideslope, frequency/ident), PLAN (north-up, centred on a selected waypoint — to check the route;","ND modes: MAP/ARC (heading/track up, route from FMS, aircraft symbol, range arcs, TAS/GS, wind arrow, selected heading/track, RMI needles for VOR/ADF with ident, waypoints/NAVAIDs/airports, weather radar/terrain/TCAS overlays), VOR (ROSE VOR) and APP (ROSE LS) (full compass, HSI-like course pointer, deviation, TO/FROM, glideslope, frequency/ident), PLAN (north-up, centred on a selected waypoint — to check the route;"],["022.13.03.03:05","Displays: fill in the gaps → ____, aircraft symbol oriented by heading). ____ shows a 90° sector ahead with longer range; ____ 360° (range = radius). Choose mode and range per flight phase (e.g. short range on approach) for best SA.","no compass/heading information, aircraft symbol oriented by heading). Expanded/ARC shows a 90° sector ahead with longer range; ROSE/full 360° (range = radius). Choose mode and range per flight phase (e.g. short range on approach) for best SA."],["022.13.04.01:01","Displays: fill in the gaps → ____: centralise engine instruments, crew alerts, system synoptics, ____.","Engine & systems displays: centralise engine instruments, crew alerts, system synoptics, electronic checklists."],["022.13.04.01:04","Displays: fill in the gaps → Names: ____ (Boeing), ____ (Airbus: engine/warning display + system display), ____ (vehicle and engine monitoring display — helicopters), IIDS (integrated instrument display system, helicopters); mission display (special ops).","Names: MFDU, EICAS (Boeing), EWD + S/D = ECAM (Airbus: engine/warning display + system display), VEMD (vehicle and engine monitoring display — helicopters), IIDS (integrated instrument display system, helicopters); mission display (special ops)."],["022.13.03.01:01","Displays: what do you remember about this? → Similar architecture to EFIS (data acquisition, symbol generators, …","Similar architecture to EFIS (data acquisition, symbol generators, display units, control panel)."],["022.13.04.01:08","Displays: fill in the gaps → ____: the computer only detects ____ ____ and triggers programmed actions — it cannot assess the overall situation (e.g. fuel leak vs imbalance).","Electronic checklists: the computer only detects threshold exceedances and triggers programmed actions — it cannot assess the overall situation (e.g. fuel leak vs imbalance)."],["022.13.04.01:09","Displays: fill in the gaps → Procedure: ____, ____ ____, work slowly and methodically, think about consequences (e.g. don't open cross-feed with a fuel leak even if the ECL asks).","Procedure: confirm the failure with the other pilot, confirm before moving guarded switches/thrust levers/fire handles, work slowly and methodically, think about consequences (e.g. don't open cross-feed with a fuel leak even if the ECL asks)."],["022.13.05.01:02","Displays: fill in the gaps → ____ (helicopters): combines several engine limits (N1, TOT, torque) in one indication — ____ ____ is shown; in limited composite mode (display failure) essential engine parameters only.","Engine first limit indicator (helicopters): combines several engine limits (N1, TOT, torque) in one indication — the parameter closest to its limit is shown; in limited composite mode (display failure) essential engine parameters only."],["022.13.06.01:02","Displays: fill in the gaps → ____: laptop, tablet, integrated avionics. ____: ____ (PED, not part of certified configuration, removable without tools) vs installed (aircraft part, airworthiness approval → MEL item).","EFB: laptop, tablet, integrated avionics. Hardware class: portable (PED, not part of certified configuration, removable without tools) vs installed (aircraft part, airworthiness approval → MEL item)."],["022.13.06.01:03","Displays: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (misuse/malfunction has ____ adverse safety effect — e.g. manuals) vs B (hazard evaluation required — e.g. performance, M&B, charts). Malfunctions in a paperless flight deck → loss of charts/performance data → back-up EFB or paper, procedures.","Software type: A (misuse/malfunction has no adverse safety effect — e.g. manuals) vs B (hazard evaluation required — e.g. performance, M&B, charts). Malfunctions in a paperless flight deck → loss of charts/performance data → back-up EFB or paper, procedures."],["022.14.01.01:02","Recorders & monitoring: fill in the gaps → ____: records ____ (area mic, radios, interphone, PA) for accident investigation; in the ____; tampering/erasing is prohibited (evidence!). Components: crash-protected memory + ULB, cockpit area microphone (CAM), control unit (auto/on, test, erase, headset jack); erase only on ground, parking brake set.","CVR: records flight-deck audio (area mic, radios, interphone, PA) for accident investigation; in the tail; tampering/erasing is prohibited (evidence!). Components: crash-protected memory + ULB, cockpit area microphone (CAM), control unit (auto/on, test, erase, headset jack); erase only on ground, parking brake set."],["022.14.02.01:02","Recorders & monitoring: fill in the gaps → ____: records flight parameters; in the tail; components: crash-protected recorder + ____, ____, recording system (DFDR), control units (start sequence, event mark button).","FDR: records flight parameters; in the tail; components: crash-protected recorder + ULB, flight data acquisition unit, recording system (DFDR), control units (start sequence, event mark button)."],["022.14.02.01:02","Recorders & monitoring: fill in the gaps → Minimum parameters: ____ ____ ____, plus control positions, engine thrust, etc. as capacity/requirements permit.","Minimum parameters: time, attitude (pitch & roll), airspeed, pressure altitude, heading, normal acceleration, plus control positions, engine thrust, etc. as capacity/requirements permit."],["022.14.03.03:02","Recorders & monitoring: fill in the gaps → ____ (aeroplane condition monitoring): inputs from aircraft systems (air conditioning, autoflight, flight controls, fuel, gear, navigation, pneumatics, APU, engines) and MCDU → ____ ____ → recorder (QAR/DAR) → outputs to printer/ACARS/ATSU; maintenance messages may be sent without crew notification.","ACMS (aeroplane condition monitoring): inputs from aircraft systems (air conditioning, autoflight, flight controls, fuel, gear, navigation, pneumatics, APU, engines) and MCDU → data management unit → recorder (QAR/DAR) → outputs to printer/ACARS/ATSU; maintenance messages may be sent without crew notification."],["022.14.03.03:06","Recorders & monitoring: fill in the gaps → Data used in ____ (flight data monitoring): de-identified/____, identifies ____ safety trends, tailors training; grave exceedances may be investigated (just culture).","Data used in FDM (flight data monitoring): de-identified/anonymous, identifies adverse safety trends, tailors training; grave exceedances may be investigated (just culture)."],["022.15.01.01:01","Digital circuits & computers: fill in the gaps → ____ = machine that manipulates data according to a list of instructions. ____ = wiring/optical fibre transferring data between parts of a computer or between systems (e.g. ARINC 429, AFDX). ____ = physical components; software = programs/instructions.","Computer = machine that manipulates data according to a list of instructions. Bus = wiring/optical fibre transferring data between parts of a computer or between systems (e.g. ARINC 429, AFDX). Hardware = physical components; software = programs/instructions."],["022.15.01.01:04","Digital circuits & computers: fill in the gaps → Examples: ____ ← pitot/static probes, TAT → indicators, AP, FMS, transponder; ____ ← GPS, IRS, CDU/MCDU → ND, AP; ____ ← radio altimeter, ADC, ILS → aural alerts, ND terrain.","Examples: ADC ← pitot/static probes, TAT → indicators, AP, FMS, transponder; FMS ← GPS, IRS, CDU/MCDU → ND, AP; GPWS ← radio altimeter, ADC, ILS → aural alerts, ND terrain."],["022.11.02.02:05","Exam trap (Digital circuits & computers): complete the fact → CI ____= max range.","CI 0 = max range."],["022.12.10.01:04","Exam trap (Digital circuits & computers): complete the fact → Proximate = ____/±____; RA vertical only; no RA against Mode-A-only traffic.","Proximate = 6 NM/±1 200 ft; RA vertical only; no RA against Mode-A-only traffic."],["022.04.02.01:03","EASA LO 022.04.02.01.03 → Describe the indications given by a rate-of-turn indicator.","Bank required for a given rate ↑ with TAS (tan Ø = ω·V/g) → at high speed a rate-1 turn needs too much bank → bank angle (e.g. 25–30°) becomes the limiting factor. / Turn coordinator: gyro gimbal tilted ~30° → senses roll rate and yaw rate → responds already when rolling into a turn; shows a small aircraft symbol (not a bank indicator!). Turn & slip shows yaw rate only (needle)."],["022.04.02.01:05","EASA LO 022.04.02.01.05 → Explain the purpose of a balance (slip) indicator and its principle of operation.","Bank required for a given rate ↑ with TAS (tan Ø = ω·V/g) → at high speed a rate-1 turn needs too much bank → bank angle (e.g. 25–30°) becomes the limiting factor. / Turn coordinator: gyro gimbal tilted ~30° → senses roll rate and yaw rate → responds already when rolling into a turn; shows a small aircraft symbol (not a bank indicator!). Turn & slip shows yaw rate only (needle)."],["022.04.02.01:07","EASA LO 022.04.02.01.07 → Describe the indications given by a turn coordinator (or turn-and-bank indicator).","Bank required for a given rate ↑ with TAS (tan Ø = ω·V/g) → at high speed a rate-1 turn needs too much bank → bank angle (e.g. 25–30°) becomes the limiting factor. / Turn coordinator: gyro gimbal tilted ~30° → senses roll rate and yaw rate → responds already when rolling into a turn; shows a small aircraft symbol (not a bank indicator!). Turn & slip shows yaw rate only (needle)."],["022.06.03.01:01","EASA LO 022.06.03.01.01 → Explain the purpose of a flight director system.","FD vs AP: the FD only indicates, the AP acts; both use the same computer/modes and the same FCU/MCP → with AP engaged, the FD bars show what the AP commands → cross-check. / Use: FD only (manual flying following bars), AP only (FD off), AP + FD (monitoring), PF selects modes (or PM on PF's request when hand-flying). If FD is shown, follow it or switch it off — don't fly against the bars (confusing, wrong guidance). Check FMA/FMC data; if FD guidance is incorrect → don't follow, turn it off."],["022.06.03.01:03","EASA LO 022.06.03.01.03 → Explain the differences between a flight director and an autopilot and how the flight director provides a means of cross-checking the control/guidance commands sent to the autopilot.","FD vs AP: the FD only indicates, the AP acts; both use the same computer/modes and the same FCU/MCP → with AP engaged, the FD bars show what the AP commands → cross-check."],["022.06.03.01:06","EASA LO 022.06.03.01.06 → Explain that the flight director computes and indicates the direction and magnitude of control inputs required in order to achieve an attitude to follow a trajectory.","Purpose: computes and displays the pitch/roll attitude commands (direction and magnitude) needed to follow a selected trajectory — the pilot (or AP) flies the command bars. Displays: crossbars (separate pitch & roll bars — fly to centre) or V-bar / command wedge (fly the aircraft symbol into the V)."],["022.06.03.01:08","EASA LO 022.06.03.01.08 → Explain the importance of checking the FMC data or selected autopilot modes through the FMA when using the flight directors. If the flight directors are showing incorrect guidance, they should not be followed and should be turned off.","Use: FD only (manual flying following bars), AP only (FD off), AP + FD (monitoring), PF selects modes (or PM on PF's request when hand-flying). If FD is shown, follow it or switch it off — don't fly against the bars (confusing, wrong guidance). Check FMA/FMC data; if FD guidance is incorrect → don't follow, turn it off."],["022.10.01.01:03","EASA LO 022.10.01.01.03 → Describe the communication links that are used in aircraft: — high-frequency (HF) communications; — very high-frequency (VHF) communications; — satellite communications (SATCOM).","ACARS network (VHF/HF/SATCOM data): AOC (airline operational control) and ATC applications via the ATSU (air traffic service unit). On-board: ATSU communications computer, CDU/MCDU (AOC pages), DCDU (data communication display unit for ATC messages), ATC message visual annunciator (attention getter), printer."],["022.10.01.02:01","EASA LO 022.10.01.02.01 → Describe the purpose of the ACARS network.","FANS: part of ICAO CNS/ATM concept. FANS A/1 (Boeing/Airbus) uses the ACARS network; FANS B/2 uses ACARS and ATN (aeronautical telecommunication network, Link 2000+ in Europe). FANS 1/A applications: AFN (ATS facility notification — log-on), ADS-C (automatic dependent surveillance — contract), CPDLC. / ACARS network (VHF/HF/SATCOM data): AOC (airline operational control) and ATC applications via the ATSU (air traffic service unit). On-board: ATSU communications computer, CDU/MCDU (AOC pages), DCDU (data communication display unit for ATC messages), ATC message visual annunciator (attention getter), printer."],["022.10.01.02:02","EASA LO 022.10.01.02.02 → Describe the systems using the ACARS network through the air traffic service unit (ATSU) suite: — aeronautical/airline operational control (AOC); — air traffic control (ATC).","ACARS network (VHF/HF/SATCOM data): AOC (airline operational control) and ATC applications via the ATSU (air traffic service unit). On-board: ATSU communications computer, CDU/MCDU (AOC pages), DCDU (data communication display unit for ATC messages), ATC message visual annunciator (attention getter), printer. / ACMS (aeroplane condition monitoring): inputs from aircraft systems (air conditioning, autoflight, flight controls, fuel, gear, navigation, pneumatics, APU, engines) and MCDU → data management unit → recorder (QAR/DAR) → outputs to printer/ACARS/ATSU; maintenance messages may be sent without crew notification."],["022.10.01.02:05","EASA LO 022.10.01.02.05 → Give examples of ATC data-link messages such as: — departure clearance; — oceanic clearance; — digital ATIS (D-ATIS); — controller-pilot data-link communications (CPDLC).","AOC examples: OOOI (Out of gate, Off ground, On ground, Into gate), load sheet, passenger info/connecting flights, weather, maintenance messages, fuel figures. ATC examples: departure clearance (DCL/PDC), oceanic clearance, D-ATIS, CPDLC. / ADS replaces SSR surveillance where no radar (oceanic); CPDLC replaces VHF voice. An ATSU may use ADS only, CPDLC only, or both."],["022.10.02.01:01","EASA LO 022.10.02.01.01 → Describe the existence of the ICAO communication, navigation, surveillance/air traffic management (CNS/ATM) concept.","FANS: part of ICAO CNS/ATM concept. FANS A/1 (Boeing/Airbus) uses the ACARS network; FANS B/2 uses ACARS and ATN (aeronautical telecommunication network, Link 2000+ in Europe). FANS 1/A applications: AFN (ATS facility notification — log-on), ADS-C (automatic dependent surveillance — contract), CPDLC. / ADS replaces SSR surveillance where no radar (oceanic); CPDLC replaces VHF voice. An ATSU may use ADS only, CPDLC only, or both."],["022.10.02.01:02","EASA LO 022.10.02.01.02 → Explain the two versions of FANSs: — FANS A/FANS 1 using the ACARS network; — FANS B/FANS 2 using the ACARS network and the aeronautical telecommunication network (ATN).","FANS: part of ICAO CNS/ATM concept. FANS A/1 (Boeing/Airbus) uses the ACARS network; FANS B/2 uses ACARS and ATN (aeronautical telecommunication network, Link 2000+ in Europe). FANS 1/A applications: AFN (ATS facility notification — log-on), ADS-C (automatic dependent surveillance — contract), CPDLC."],["022.10.02.01:07","EASA LO 022.10.02.01.07 → Describe typical types of CPDLC messages and the typical pilot work practices when requesting or accepting a CPDLC clearance.","ADS replaces SSR surveillance where no radar (oceanic); CPDLC replaces VHF voice. An ATSU may use ADS only, CPDLC only, or both. / AOC examples: OOOI (Out of gate, Off ground, On ground, Into gate), load sheet, passenger info/connecting flights, weather, maintenance messages, fuel figures. ATC examples: departure clearance (DCL/PDC), oceanic clearance, D-ATIS, CPDLC."],["022.10.02.01:09","EASA LO 022.10.02.01.09 → Describe the purpose of the ADS emergency mode contract and highlight the difference to the ATCU controlled contracts.","ADS contracts (set by the ATSU, not the pilot): periodic (at intervals), on demand, on event (waypoint change, vertical/lateral deviation, level change), emergency mode — triggered by the pilot (or squawk 7700) → high reporting rate, overrides other contracts. / FANS: part of ICAO CNS/ATM concept. FANS A/1 (Boeing/Airbus) uses the ACARS network; FANS B/2 uses ACARS and ATN (aeronautical telecommunication network, Link 2000+ in Europe). FANS 1/A applications: AFN (ATS facility notification — log-on), ADS-C (automatic dependent surveillance — contract), CPDLC."],["022.11.01.01:02","EASA LO 022.11.01.01.02 → Describe a typical dual FMS architecture including the following components: — flight management computer (FMC); — CDU/MCDU; — cross-talk bus.","Dual architecture: two FMCs, two CDUs/MCDUs, cross-talk bus (synchronises data). Failure of one FMC → use the other (single FMS mode, both CDUs driven by the remaining FMC, reduced redundancy/RNP capability); one CDU → use the other CDU; cross-talk bus → FMCs operate independently (inputs must be made on both, possible disagreement)."],["022.11.01.01:05","EASA LO 022.11.01.01.05 → Explain how the FMS may provide the following functions: — navigation; — lateral and vertical flight planning; — performance parameters.","Purpose: navigation, lateral & vertical flight planning, performance optimisation, guidance (to AP/FD/AT), fuel/time predictions, radio tuning."],["022.11.02.01:05","EASA LO 022.11.02.01.05 → Explain the threats and implications to the pilot of changing the database by error either on the ground or while flying.","28-day AIRAC — two databases (active + standby) to switch at the effective date; pilot must check the effective dates and activate the correct one (only on the ground); pilot cannot modify database data; changing database erases user-defined data/route (risky in flight, wrong procedures if expired DB). / Needs precise position — position error → false/missed alerts. Spurious warnings when flying close to terrain with high descent rate and/or high speed (e.g. visual approaches in mountains)."],["022.11.02.02:01","EASA LO 022.11.02.02.01 → Explain the purpose of, and describe the typical content of, the aircraft performance database.","Uplink = ground → aircraft; downlink = aircraft → ground."],["022.11.02.02:02","EASA LO 022.11.02.02.02 → Explain the importance of verifying that the aircraft performance database is based on the correct data, such as engine type and aircraft variant.","Performance database: drag/engine models (fuel flow, speeds, limits); must match the engine type & variant; not modifiable by pilot. Performance factor corrects for individual aircraft degradation (fuel burn). Cost index (CI) = time cost ÷ fuel cost → CI 0 = max range (minimum fuel), high CI = faster (min time); influences ECON speeds, optimum level, TOD."],["022.11.02.02:03","EASA LO 022.11.02.02.03 → Explain that the contents of the aircraft performance database cannot be modified by the pilot.","28-day AIRAC — two databases (active + standby) to switch at the effective date; pilot must check the effective dates and activate the correct one (only on the ground); pilot cannot modify database data; changing database erases user-defined data/route (risky in flight, wrong procedures if expired DB)."],["022.11.02.02:04","EASA LO 022.11.02.02.04 → Explain the purpose of performance factor and how it influences the calculations.","Performance database: drag/engine models (fuel flow, speeds, limits); must match the engine type & variant; not modifiable by pilot. Performance factor corrects for individual aircraft degradation (fuel burn). Cost index (CI) = time cost ÷ fuel cost → CI 0 = max range (minimum fuel), high CI = faster (min time); influences ECON speeds, optimum level, TOD."],["022.11.03.01:02","EASA LO 022.11.03.01.02 → Explain how the FMS will use a combination of inputted/database and measured data in order to calculate projections and provide output data.","FMS outputs: LNAV/VNAV guidance, present position, time and fuel predictions, altitude predictions; computed from database + entered data + measured data (wind, temperature, fuel). Wrong inputs (ZFW, fuel, CG, cruise level, winds, runway, V-speeds, temperature) → wrong predictions/speeds (e.g."],["022.11.03.01:06","EASA LO 022.11.03.01.06 → Describe and explain the purpose of an FMS having dedicated radio-navigation receivers that it will tune automatically.","Integration: inputs from ADC, IRS, GPS, radio receivers (DME/VOR/LOC), fuel quantity, engine data, clock; outputs to AP/FD, autothrust, EFIS ND/PFD, radio tuning."],["022.11.03.01:08","EASA LO 022.11.03.01.08 → Explain how the FMS will create its own FMS position fix and that the FMS calculations will be based on the FMS position. Depending on the type of system, the FMS position may be calculated from: — a single source of position data where the most accurate data available at a given time will be used; — multiple sources from which a position will be derived using the combined inputs.","Auto-tuned radio receivers (DME/DME, VOR/DME) for position updating. Position inputs: GPS, IRS, DME, VOR, LOC, runway threshold (take-off update). FMS computes its own FMS position from the best single source or a blended multi-sensor solution (e.g."],["022.11.03.01:10","EASA LO 022.11.03.01.10 → Explain the difference between following the FMS data compared to following raw data from","GPS/IRS mix); loss of GPS → reduced accuracy (ANP ↑) → may lose RNP/PBN approach capability. Raw data (VOR/ILS needles) is independent of the FMS → cross-check computed guidance with raw data."],["022.11.04.01:01","EASA LO 022.11.04.01.01 → Describe the purpose of a CDU/MCDU.","CDU/MCDU: screen with pages, line select keys, menu/function keys, alphanumeric keys; scratchpad (bottom line) for entering/checking data before placing it on a line. Boxes (□□□) = compulsory data, dashes (---) = optional."],["022.11.04.01:03","EASA LO 022.11.04.01.03 → Explain the function of the ‘scratchpad’ part of the screen.","CDU/MCDU: screen with pages, line select keys, menu/function keys, alphanumeric keys; scratchpad (bottom line) for entering/checking data before placing it on a line. Boxes (□□□) = compulsory data, dashes (---) = optional."],["022.11.04.01:04","EASA LO 022.11.04.01.04 → Describe how input of some data is compulsory for the function of the FMS and other data is optional, and that different symbology is used to highlight this: — rectangular boxes = compulsory information; — dashed line = optional information.","CDU/MCDU: screen with pages, line select keys, menu/function keys, alphanumeric keys; scratchpad (bottom line) for entering/checking data before placing it on a line. Boxes (□□□) = compulsory data, dashes (---) = optional."],["022.12.01.01:01","EASA LO 022.12.01.01.01 → State definitions, category, criteria and characteristics of alerting systems according to CS-25/AMC 25.1322 for aeroplanes and CS-29 for helicopters as appropriate.","CS-25/AMC 25.1322 alert categories: warning (immediate crew awareness and immediate action — red, continuous aural), caution (immediate awareness, subsequent action — amber/yellow, attention-getter aural), advisory (awareness, may require subsequent action — other colour (not red/amber/yellow/green), no aural), status/memo."],["022.12.02.01:01","EASA LO 022.12.02.01.01 → State the annunciations given by the FWS and typical location for the annunciator(s): — master warning; — master caution;","Master warning (red, continuous aural): e.g. engine fire, cabin altitude, take-off config, autopilot disconnect, overspeed. Master caution (amber, single chime): e.g. generator failure, pump low pressure, anti-ice fault. Procedure: acknowledge (press master light), silence aural, identify & confirm the failure, run the procedure/checklist."],["022.12.02.01:02","EASA LO 022.12.02.01.02 → Explain master warning: — colour of annunciator: red; — nature of aural alerts: continuous; — typical failure scenarios triggering the alert.","CS-25/AMC 25.1322 alert categories: warning (immediate crew awareness and immediate action — red, continuous aural), caution (immediate awareness, subsequent action — amber/yellow, attention-getter aural), advisory (awareness, may require subsequent action — other colour (not red/amber/yellow/green), no aural), status/memo. / Master warning (red, continuous aural): e.g. engine fire, cabin altitude, take-off config, autopilot disconnect, overspeed. Master caution (amber, single chime): e.g. generator failure, pump low pressure, anti-ice fault. Procedure: acknowledge (press master light), silence aural, identify & confirm the failure, run the procedure/checklist."],["022.12.02.01:05","EASA LO 022.12.02.01.05 → Explain advisory: — colour of the annunciator: any other than red, amber, yellow or green; — absence of aural alert; — typical scenarios triggering the advisory.","CS-25/AMC 25.1322 alert categories: warning (immediate crew awareness and immediate action — red, continuous aural), caution (immediate awareness, subsequent action — amber/yellow, attention-getter aural), advisory (awareness, may require subsequent action — other colour (not red/amber/yellow/green), no aural), status/memo. / Master warning (red, continuous aural): e.g. engine fire, cabin altitude, take-off config, autopilot disconnect, overspeed. Master caution (amber, single chime): e.g. generator failure, pump low pressure, anti-ice fault. Procedure: acknowledge (press master light), silence aural, identify & confirm the failure, run the procedure/checklist."],["022.12.03.01:01","EASA LO 022.12.03.01.01 → Describe the function of an SWS and explain why the warning must be unique.","Stall warning system (SWS): warns before the stall with a unique warning (stick shaker, horn — must be unmistakable). Types: flapper switch/vane on the leading edge (light aircraft), AoA vane/probe with computer (inputs: AoA, flap/slat position, Mach) → stick shaker. Warning speed ≥ 5 kt or 5 % above the stall (CS-25: sufficient margin); actual stall speed is lower."],["022.12.04.01:01","EASA LO 022.12.04.01.01 → Describe the function of a stall protection system.","Stall protection: prevents the stall — stick pusher (mechanical controls, T-tail/deep-stall aircraft) or FBW α- protection (α-floor with autothrust TOGA, hard limit at α-max). SWS warns, protection acts. / PFD (\"basic T\"): attitude (horizon, aircraft symbol, FD bars) in the centre, speed tape left (current speed, limit speeds per configuration (VMO, VFE, stall/α-protection band), speed trend vector (speed in 10 s), selected-speed bug;"],["022.12.04.01:03","EASA LO 022.12.04.01.03 → Explain the difference between an SWS and a stall protection system.","Stall protection: prevents the stall — stick pusher (mechanical controls, T-tail/deep-stall aircraft) or FBW α- protection (α-floor with autothrust TOGA, hard limit at α-max). SWS warns, protection acts. / Stall warning system (SWS): warns before the stall with a unique warning (stick shaker, horn — must be unmistakable). Types: flapper switch/vane on the leading edge (light aircraft), AoA vane/probe with computer (inputs: AoA, flap/slat position, Mach) → stick shaker. Warning speed ≥ 5 kt or 5 % above the stall (CS-25: sufficient margin); actual stall speed is lower."],["022.12.05.01:01","EASA LO 022.12.05.01.01 → Explain the purpose of an overspeed warning system (VMO/MMO pointer).","Overspeed warning: aural (clacker) + VMO/MMO pointer (\"barber pole\" needle on ASIs, barber-pole vertical band on the PFD speed tape); for large aeroplanes with electronic displays an aural warning is required."],["022.12.05.01:02","EASA LO 022.12.05.01.02 → State that for large aeroplanes, an aural warning must be associated to the overspeed warning if an electronic display is used (see AMC 25.11, paragraph 10.b(2), p. 2-GEN-22).","Overspeed warning: aural (clacker) + VMO/MMO pointer (\"barber pole\" needle on ASIs, barber-pole vertical band on the PFD speed tape); for large aeroplanes with electronic displays an aural warning is required."],["022.12.07.01:01","EASA LO 022.12.07.01.01 → Describe the function of an altitude alert system.","Engine & systems displays: centralise engine instruments, crew alerts, system synoptics, electronic checklists. / Altitude alert: approaching the selected altitude (e.g. within ~900–1 000 ft: chime/light) and deviation from the selected altitude (e.g. > 200–300 ft: warning)."],["022.12.07.01:02","EASA LO 022.12.07.01.02 → Describe different types of displays and possible alerts.","Engine & systems displays: centralise engine instruments, crew alerts, system synoptics, electronic checklists."],["022.12.08.01:03","EASA LO 022.12.08.01.03 → Describe the different types of radio-altimeter displays.","Radio altimeter (low-range): measures height above the ground by the time/frequency shift of a signal reflected from the ground (FMCW, 4.2–4.4 GHz); range typically 0–2 500 ft (5 000 ft on some). Displays: analogue dial, digital readout on PFD, DH alert."],["022.12.08.01:05","EASA LO 022.12.08.01.05 → State the range of a radio altimeter.","Radio altimeter (low-range): measures height above the ground by the time/frequency shift of a signal reflected from the ground (FMCW, 4.2–4.4 GHz); range typically 0–2 500 ft (5 000 ft on some). Displays: analogue dial, digital readout on PFD, DH alert. / CI 0 = max range."],["022.12.09.01:02","EASA LO 022.12.09.01.02 → Explain inputs and outputs of a GPWS and describe its operating principle.","Integration: inputs from ADC, IRS, GPS, radio receivers (DME/VOR/LOC), fuel quantity, engine data, clock; outputs to AP/FD, autothrust, EFIS ND/PFD, radio tuning."],["022.12.09.02:01","EASA LO 022.12.09.02.01 → Explain the purpose of a TAWS for aeroplanes and of a HTAWS for helicopters, and explain the difference from a GPWS.","TAWS/EGPWS (HTAWS for helicopters): adds a forward-looking terrain database comparison using precise position (GPS/FMS) → look-ahead alerts (\"CAUTION TERRAIN\" ~40–60 s, \"TERRAIN AHEAD, PULL UP\" ~20–30 s before impact) and terrain display on the ND (colours: green/yellow/red by relative height; magenta unknown), terrain-clearance floor."],["022.12.09.02:02","EASA LO 022.12.09.02.02 → Explain inputs and outputs of a TAWS/HTAWS and describe its working principle.","TAWS/EGPWS (HTAWS for helicopters): adds a forward-looking terrain database comparison using precise position (GPS/FMS) → look-ahead alerts (\"CAUTION TERRAIN\" ~40–60 s, \"TERRAIN AHEAD, PULL UP\" ~20–30 s before impact) and terrain display on the ND (colours: green/yellow/red by relative height; magenta unknown), terrain-clearance floor."],["022.12.09.02:03","EASA LO 022.12.09.02.03 → Give examples of terrain displays and list the different possible alerts.","Examples: ADC ← pitot/static probes, TAT → indicators, AP, FMS, transponder; FMS ← GPS, IRS, CDU/MCDU → ND, AP; GPWS ← radio altimeter, ADC, ILS → aural alerts, ND terrain. / TAWS/EGPWS (HTAWS for helicopters): adds a forward-looking terrain database comparison using precise position (GPS/FMS) → look-ahead alerts (\"CAUTION TERRAIN\" ~40–60 s, \"TERRAIN AHEAD, PULL UP\" ~20–30 s before impact) and terrain display on the ND (colours: green/yellow/red by relative height; magenta unknown), terrain-clearance floor."],["022.12.09.02:04","EASA LO 022.12.09.02.04 → Give examples of time response left to the pilot according to look-ahead distance, speed and aircraft performances.","TAWS/EGPWS (HTAWS for helicopters): adds a forward-looking terrain database comparison using precise position (GPS/FMS) → look-ahead alerts (\"CAUTION TERRAIN\" ~40–60 s, \"TERRAIN AHEAD, PULL UP\" ~20–30 s before impact) and terrain display on the ND (colours: green/yellow/red by relative height; magenta unknown), terrain-clearance floor."],["022.12.10.01:01","EASA LO 022.12.10.01.01 → State that ACAS II is an ICAO standard for anti-collision purposes.","ACAS II/TCAS II: ICAO standard anti-collision system — a last-resort safety net that does not guarantee any separation. Outputs: other traffic (open white diamond), proximate (<"],["022.12.10.01:03","EASA LO 022.12.10.01.03 → Describe the purpose of an ACAS II system as an anti-collision system.","ACAS II/TCAS II: ICAO standard anti-collision system — a last-resort safety net that does not guarantee any separation. Outputs: other traffic (open white diamond), proximate (<"],["022.12.10.01:06","EASA LO 022.12.10.01.06 → Explain that an RA may or may not require any active control input and the implications of reacting instinctively without awareness of actual control inputs required to comply with the RA.","RAs in the vertical plane only, flown manually (AP/FD off, smoothly, ~0.25 g within 5 s); a preventive RA requires no input — reacting instinctively may cause a real conflict. Two TCAS II aircraft → coordinated RAs (via Mode S). Can handle multiple threats. / CS-25/AMC 25.1322 alert categories: warning (immediate crew awareness and immediate action — red, continuous aural), caution (immediate awareness, subsequent action — amber/yellow, attention-getter aural), advisory (awareness, may require subsequent action — other colour (not red/amber/yellow/green), no aural), status/memo."],["022.12.10.01:10","EASA LO 022.12.10.01.10 → Describe the interaction between the TCAS II system and the transponder, radio altimeter and the air-data computer: — antenna used;","Examples: ADC ← pitot/static probes, TAT → indicators, AP, FMS, transponder; FMS ← GPS, IRS, CDU/MCDU → ND, AP; GPWS ← radio altimeter, ADC, ILS → aural alerts, ND terrain."],["022.12.10.01:13","EASA LO 022.12.10.01.13 → Explain the principle of ‘reduced surveillance’.","Interrogations on 1 030 MHz, replies 1 090 MHz; range ~35–40 NM horizontally, ±2 000 ft (normal), extended to ~10 000 ft ABV or BLW when selected; reduced surveillance/range in dense traffic (limit on number of tracked intruders)."],["022.12.10.01:14","EASA LO 022.12.10.01.14 → Explain that in high-density traffic areas the range may automatically be decreased in order to enable detection of the threats in the proximity of the aircraft due to a limitation of the maximum number of possible intruders the system is able to process.","Interrogations on 1 030 MHz, replies 1 090 MHz; range ~35–40 NM horizontally, ±2 000 ft (normal), extended to ~10 000 ft ABV or BLW when selected; reduced surveillance/range in dense traffic (limit on number of tracked intruders)."],["022.13.02.01:01","EASA LO 022.13.02.01.01 → Describe an ADI and an HSI.","Technologies: CRT (heavy, heat, poor in bright light) and LCD (lighter, less heat, but viewing angle/cold temperature response/glare); limitations: cockpit temperature, glare, resolution. ADI (attitude + FD bars, speed/rate of turn/slip, glideslope/localiser, DH) and HSI (heading card, course pointer, course deviation bar, TO/FROM, glideslope, DME, heading bug)."],["022.13.03.01:04","EASA LO 022.13.03.01.04 → Explain the difference between a symbol generator failing and a display unit failing, and the implications if there are redundant symbol generators available.","EFIS: control panel (baro setting, ND mode/range, data selectors, VOR/ADF needles, DA/DH/MINIMUMS), display units (PFD, ND), symbol generators (DMCs) — if a display unit fails, transfer the image to another DU; if a symbol generator fails, switch to the other SG (both sides then show the same source → reduced independence). / Similar architecture to EFIS (data acquisition, symbol generators, display units, control panel)."],["022.13.03.01:05","EASA LO 022.13.03.01.05 → Describe the purpose of an EFIS control panel and typical selections that may be available: — altimeter pressure setting; — navigation display (ND) mode selector; — ND range selector; — ND data selector (waypoints, facilities, constraints, data, etc.); — radio-navigation aids selector (VOR 1/2 or ADF 1/2); — decision altitude (DA)/decision height (DH) selection.","EFIS: control panel (baro setting, ND mode/range, data selectors, VOR/ADF needles, DA/DH/MINIMUMS), display units (PFD, ND), symbol generators (DMCs) — if a display unit fails, transfer the image to another DU; if a symbol generator fails, switch to the other SG (both sides then show the same source → reduced independence)."],["022.13.03.02:02","EASA LO 022.13.03.02.02 → Describe the typical design of the attitude information: — artificial horizon with aircraft symbol; — superimposed flight director command bars.","Purpose: computes and displays the pitch/roll attitude commands (direction and magnitude) needed to follow a selected trajectory — the pilot (or AP) flies the command bars. Displays: crossbars (separate pitch & roll bars — fly to centre) or V-bar / command wedge (fly the aircraft symbol into the V). / PFD (\"basic T\"): attitude (horizon, aircraft symbol, FD bars) in the centre, speed tape left (current speed, limit speeds per configuration (VMO, VFE, stall/α-protection band), speed trend vector (speed in 10 s), selected-speed bug;"],["022.13.03.02:05","EASA LO 022.13.03.02.05 → Describe the typical design of the altitude information: — rolling altitude scale with numerical read-out of current altitude; — altimeter pressure setting; — bug/indication for selected altitude; — means of highlighting the altitude if certain criteria are met.","Mach readout; a selected Mach is converted into an IAS bug), altitude tape right (baro setting, selected altitude bug, altitude alert highlighting), heading scale bottom (selected heading/track bug), FMAs top, VSI right of the altitude (with TCAS RA red/green bands), radio altitude, ILS/RNP deviation scales (LOC, G/S, LDEV/VDEV for LPV/GLS), DA/H marker."],["022.13.03.02:06","EASA LO 022.13.03.02.06 → Describe the typical design of the heading/track information: — rolling compass scale/rose with numerical read-out of current heading/track; — bug/indication for selected heading/track.","ND modes: MAP/ARC (heading/track up, route from FMS, aircraft symbol, range arcs, TAS/GS, wind arrow, selected heading/track, RMI needles for VOR/ADF with ident, waypoints/NAVAIDs/airports, weather radar/terrain/TCAS overlays), VOR (ROSE VOR) and APP (ROSE LS) (full compass, HSI-like course pointer, deviation, TO/FROM, glideslope, frequency/ident), PLAN (north-up, centred on a selected waypoint — to check the route; / Mach readout; a selected Mach is converted into an IAS bug), altitude tape right (baro setting, selected altitude bug, altitude alert highlighting), heading scale bottom (selected heading/track bug), FMAs top, VSI right of the altitude (with TCAS RA red/green bands), radio altitude, ILS/RNP deviation scales (LOC, G/S, LDEV/VDEV for LPV/GLS), DA/H marker."],["022.13.03.02:07","EASA LO 022.13.03.02.07 → Describe the typical design and location of the following information: — flight mode annunciators (FMAs); — vertical speed indicator including TCAS RA command indications; — radio altitude; — ILS localiser/glideslope and RNP/PBN, GBAS or SBAS horizontal/vertical flight path deviation indicator; — decision altitude/height (DA/H).","Mach readout; a selected Mach is converted into an IAS bug), altitude tape right (baro setting, selected altitude bug, altitude alert highlighting), heading scale bottom (selected heading/track bug), FMAs top, VSI right of the altitude (with TCAS RA red/green bands), radio altitude, ILS/RNP deviation scales (LOC, G/S, LDEV/VDEV for LPV/GLS), DA/H marker. / GPWS: warns of dangerous proximity to terrain based on radio altitude, barometric vertical speed, ILS glideslope, configuration (gear/flaps), airspeed."],["022.13.03.03:02","EASA LO 022.13.03.03.02 → List the following four modes typically available to be displayed on an ND unit: — MAP (or ARC); — VOR (or ROSE VOR); — APP (or ROSE LS); — PLAN.","ND modes: MAP/ARC (heading/track up, route from FMS, aircraft symbol, range arcs, TAS/GS, wind arrow, selected heading/track, RMI needles for VOR/ADF with ident, waypoints/NAVAIDs/airports, weather radar/terrain/TCAS overlays), VOR (ROSE VOR) and APP (ROSE LS) (full compass, HSI-like course pointer, deviation, TO/FROM, glideslope, frequency/ident), PLAN (north-up, centred on a selected waypoint — to check the route;"],["022.13.03.03:03","EASA LO 022.13.03.03.03 → List and explain the following information that can be displayed with the MAP (or ARC) mode selected on an ND unit: — aircraft symbol, compass scale and range markers; — current heading and track (either one may be ‘up’ depending on selection), true or magnetic; — selected heading and track; — TAS/GS; — wind direction and speed (W/V); — raw data radio magnetic indicator (RMI) needles/pointers for VOR/automatic direction-finding equipment (ADF), if selected, including the frequency or ident of the selected navigation facility;","ND modes: MAP/ARC (heading/track up, route from FMS, aircraft symbol, range arcs, TAS/GS, wind arrow, selected heading/track, RMI needles for VOR/ADF with ident, waypoints/NAVAIDs/airports, weather radar/terrain/TCAS overlays), VOR (ROSE VOR) and APP (ROSE LS) (full compass, HSI-like course pointer, deviation, TO/FROM, glideslope, frequency/ident), PLAN (north-up, centred on a selected waypoint — to check the route; / EFIS: control panel (baro setting, ND mode/range, data selectors, VOR/ADF needles, DA/DH/MINIMUMS), display units (PFD, ND), symbol generators (DMCs) — if a display unit fails, transfer the image to another DU; if a symbol generator fails, switch to the other SG (both sides then show the same source → reduced independence)."],["022.13.03.03:07","EASA LO 022.13.03.03.07 → Distinguish the difference between the appearance of an EXPANDED or FULL/ROSE mode and how the displayed range differs between them.","no compass/heading information, aircraft symbol oriented by heading). Expanded/ARC shows a 90° sector ahead with longer range; ROSE/full 360° (range = radius). Choose mode and range per flight phase (e.g. short range on approach) for best SA."],["022.13.03.03:08","EASA LO 022.13.03.03.08 → Explain the combination of mode and range selection including how selecting the appropriate range and displayed data can improve situational awareness for a given phase of flight.","no compass/heading information, aircraft symbol oriented by heading). Expanded/ARC shows a 90° sector ahead with longer range; ROSE/full 360° (range = radius). Choose mode and range per flight phase (e.g. short range on approach) for best SA."],["022.13.04.01:02","EASA LO 022.13.04.01.02 → Describe the similarities to EFIS with regard to basic system architecture.","Similar architecture to EFIS (data acquisition, symbol generators, display units, control panel)."],["022.13.04.01:03","EASA LO 022.13.04.01.03 → Give the following different names by which engine parameters, crew warnings, aircraft systems and procedures display systems are known: — multifunction display unit (MFDU); — engine indication and crew alerting systems (EICASs); — engine and warning display (EWD); — electronic centralised aircraft monitor (ECAM); — systems display (S/D).","Names: MFDU, EICAS (Boeing), EWD + S/D = ECAM (Airbus: engine/warning display + system display), VEMD (vehicle and engine monitoring display — helicopters), IIDS (integrated instrument display system, helicopters); mission display (special ops). / Engine & systems displays: centralise engine instruments, crew alerts, system synoptics, electronic checklists."],["022.13.04.01:05","EASA LO 022.13.04.01.05 → State the purpose of a mission display unit.","Names: MFDU, EICAS (Boeing), EWD + S/D = ECAM (Airbus: engine/warning display + system display), VEMD (vehicle and engine monitoring display — helicopters), IIDS (integrated instrument display system, helicopters); mission display (special ops)."],["022.13.06.01:01","EASA LO 022.13.06.01.01 → Explain the purpose of the EFB and list typical equipment: — computer laptop; — tablet device; — integrated avionics suite in the aircraft.","EFB: laptop, tablet, integrated avionics. Hardware class: portable (PED, not part of certified configuration, removable without tools) vs installed (aircraft part, airworthiness approval → MEL item)."],["022.13.06.01:04","EASA LO 022.13.06.01.04 → Explain implications of malfunctions with the EFB installation in a fully electronic flight-deck environment:","Software type: A (misuse/malfunction has no adverse safety effect — e.g. manuals) vs B (hazard evaluation required — e.g. performance, M&B, charts). Malfunctions in a paperless flight deck → loss of charts/performance data → back-up EFB or paper, procedures."],["022.14.01.01:01","EASA LO 022.14.01.01.01 → Describe the purpose of a CVR, its typical location, and explain the implications of knowingly erasing or tampering with any information or equipment.","CVR: records flight-deck audio (area mic, radios, interphone, PA) for accident investigation; in the tail; tampering/erasing is prohibited (evidence!). Components: crash-protected memory + ULB, cockpit area microphone (CAM), control unit (auto/on, test, erase, headset jack); erase only on ground, parking brake set."],["022.14.03.03:01","EASA LO 022.14.03.03.01 → State the purpose of an ACMS.","ACMS (aeroplane condition monitoring): inputs from aircraft systems (air conditioning, autoflight, flight controls, fuel, gear, navigation, pneumatics, APU, engines) and MCDU → data management unit → recorder (QAR/DAR) → outputs to printer/ACARS/ATSU; maintenance messages may be sent without crew notification."],["022.14.03.03:03","EASA LO 022.14.03.03.03 → State that maintenance messages sent by an ACMS can be transmitted without crew notification.","ACMS (aeroplane condition monitoring): inputs from aircraft systems (air conditioning, autoflight, flight controls, fuel, gear, navigation, pneumatics, APU, engines) and MCDU → data management unit → recorder (QAR/DAR) → outputs to printer/ACARS/ATSU; maintenance messages may be sent without crew notification."],["022.14.03.03:05","EASA LO 022.14.03.03.05 → Explain that data from the ACMS can be used as part of an FDM and safety programme.","Data used in FDM (flight data monitoring): de-identified/anonymous, identifies adverse safety trends, tailors training; grave exceedances may be investigated (just culture). / ACMS (aeroplane condition monitoring): inputs from aircraft systems (air conditioning, autoflight, flight controls, fuel, gear, navigation, pneumatics, APU, engines) and MCDU → data management unit → recorder (QAR/DAR) → outputs to printer/ACARS/ATSU; maintenance messages may be sent without crew notification."],["022.14.03.03:05","EASA LO 022.14.03.03.05 → Explain that data from the ACMS can be used as part of an FDM and safety programme.","Data used in FDM (flight data monitoring): de-identified/anonymous, identifies adverse safety trends, tailors training; grave exceedances may be investigated (just culture)."],["022.15.01.01:02","EASA LO 022.15.01.01.02 → Explain the term ‘bus’ being used as a term for a facility (wiring, optical fibre, etc.) transferring data between different parts of a computer, both internally and externally.","Computer = machine that manipulates data according to a list of instructions. Bus = wiring/optical fibre transferring data between parts of a computer or between systems (e.g. ARINC 429, AFDX). Hardware = physical components; software = programs/instructions."],["022.15.01.01:03","EASA LO 022.15.01.01.03 → Define the terms ‘hardware’ and ‘software’.","Computer = machine that manipulates data according to a list of instructions. Bus = wiring/optical fibre transferring data between parts of a computer or between systems (e.g. ARINC 429, AFDX). Hardware = physical components; software = programs/instructions."]],"93":[["010.01.01.03:03","Chicago Convention (1944): fill in the gaps → Background: WWII boosted aviation → need for ____ ____ for safe, orderly development of civil aviation and air transport after the war → ____ (in force 1947), creating ICAO; 96 articles in 4 parts (I Air navigation, II ICAO, III International air transport, IV Final provisions).","Background: WWII boosted aviation → need for international rules for safe, orderly development of civil aviation and air transport after the war → Chicago Convention, 7 Dec 1944 (in force 1947), creating ICAO; 96 articles in 4 parts (I Air navigation, II ICAO, III International air transport, IV Final provisions)."],["010.01.01.02:02","Chicago Convention (1944): fill in the gaps → ____: every State has ____ sovereignty over the airspace above its territory (Art. 1). Territory = land areas and adjacent territorial waters (12 NM, UN Convention on the Law of the Sea). High seas: no sovereignty — ____.","Sovereignty: every State has complete and exclusive sovereignty over the airspace above its territory (Art. 1). Territory = land areas and adjacent territorial waters (12 NM, UN Convention on the Law of the Sea). High seas: no sovereignty — ICAO Annex 2 rules apply without exception."],["010.01.01.02:03","Chicago Convention (1944): fill in the gaps → ____ (Art. 5): may fly into/through a State ____ — using the two ____ (overflight, non-traffic stop), subject to landing requirements. Scheduled air services (Art. 6): only with special permission/authorisation.","Non-scheduled flights (Art. 5): may fly into/through a State without prior permission — using the two technical freedoms (overflight, non-traffic stop), subject to landing requirements. Scheduled air services (Art. 6): only with special permission/authorisation."],["010.01.01.02:03","Chicago Convention (1944): fill in the gaps → ____ (Art. 7): a State may refuse foreign aircraft the carriage of passengers/cargo between two points ____. ____ (Art. 10): aircraft entering must land at a designated customs airport (unless permitted otherwise).","Cabotage (Art. 7): a State may refuse foreign aircraft the carriage of passengers/cargo between two points within its territory. Customs airports (Art. 10): aircraft entering must land at a designated customs airport (unless permitted otherwise)."],["010.01.01.02:03","Chicago Convention (1944): fill in the gaps → ____ (Art. 12): each State keeps its rules uniform with ICAO; over the high seas ICAO rules apply. ____ (Art. 16): authorities may search aircraft on landing/departure and inspect documents.","Rules of the Air (Art. 12): each State keeps its rules uniform with ICAO; over the high seas ICAO rules apply. Search of aircraft (Art. 16): authorities may search aircraft on landing/departure and inspect documents."],["010.01.01.01:01","Chicago Convention (1944): fill in the gaps → ____ (Art. 29): ____ ____ licence, passenger list (names, places of embarkation/destination), cargo manifest (detailed declarations). CofA and licences (Art. 31/32) issued/validated by the State of Registry, recognised by other States if at least equal to ICAO minimum standards (Art. 33).","Documents on board (Art. 29): certificate of registration, CofA, crew licences, journey log book, radio station licence, passenger list (names, places of embarkation/destination), cargo manifest (detailed declarations). CofA and licences (Art. 31/32) issued/validated by the State of Registry, recognised by other States if at least equal to ICAO minimum standards (Art. 33)."],["010.12.02.01:01","ICAO: fill in the gaps → Objectives: develop principles and techniques of international air navigation, foster ____, efficient and economical air transport, equal opportunity, avoid discrimination, promote safety.","Objectives: develop principles and techniques of international air navigation, foster safe and orderly growth, efficient and economical air transport, equal opportunity, avoid discrimination, promote safety."],["010.01.01.03:02","ICAO: fill in the gaps → Assembly: all Contracting States, meets at least ____, elects the Council, votes budget. Council: permanent body of ____ States elected by the Assembly — adopts SARPs/Annexes, appoints the ____, which examines and recommends amendments to Annexes. Secretariat, Secretary General; HQ Montreal.","Assembly: all Contracting States, meets at least every 3 years, elects the Council, votes budget. Council: permanent body of 36 States elected by the Assembly — adopts SARPs/Annexes, appoints the Air Navigation Commission (ANC, 19 members), which examines and recommends amendments to Annexes. Secretariat, Secretary General; HQ Montreal."],["010.01.01.03:01","ICAO: fill in the gaps → ____ (SARPs) — e.g. 1 Licensing, 2 Rules of the Air, 3 MET, 4 Charts, 5 Units, 6 Operation of aircraft, 7 Nationality & registration marks, 8 Airworthiness, 9 Facilitation, 10 Telecommunications, 11 ATS, 12 SAR, 13 Accident investigation, 14 Aerodromes, 15 AIS, 16 Environment, 17 Security, 18 Dangerous goods, 19 Safety management. States must notify ____ (Art. 38).","19 Annexes (SARPs) — e.g. 1 Licensing, 2 Rules of the Air, 3 MET, 4 Charts, 5 Units, 6 Operation of aircraft, 7 Nationality & registration marks, 8 Airworthiness, 9 Facilitation, 10 Telecommunications, 11 ATS, 12 SAR, 13 Accident investigation, 14 Aerodromes, 15 AIS, 16 Environment, 17 Security, 18 Dangerous goods, 19 Safety management. States must notify differences (Art. 38)."],["010.01.02.01:01","Other conventions & freedoms: fill in the gaps → ____ — two technical freedoms: ____ = fly across the territory without landing; ____ = land for non-traffic purposes (fuel, maintenance).","Transit Agreement (Doc 7500) — two technical freedoms: 1st = fly across the territory without landing; 2nd = land for non-traffic purposes (fuel, maintenance)."],["010.01.02.02:01","Other conventions & freedoms: fill in the gaps → ____ — commercial freedoms: ____ = put down traffic from the home State in a foreign State; ____ = take on traffic in a foreign State destined for the home State; 5th = carry traffic between two foreign States (on a service starting/ending in the home State). (Further freedoms 6–9 exist bilaterally.)","Air Transport Agreement (Doc 9626) — commercial freedoms: 3rd = put down traffic from the home State in a foreign State; 4th = take on traffic in a foreign State destined for the home State; 5th = carry traffic between two foreign States (on a service starting/ending in the home State). (Further freedoms 6–9 exist bilaterally.)"],["010.01.02.03:01","Other conventions & freedoms: fill in the gaps → ____ (offences committed on board): the ____ ____, against a person who has committed or is about to commit an offence jeopardising safety/good order; may disembark the person in any State, or deliver them to the competent authorities (serious offence); crew and passengers may assist;","Tokyo Convention 1963 (offences committed on board): the PIC may take reasonable measures, including restraint, against a person who has committed or is about to commit an offence jeopardising safety/good order; may disembark the person in any State, or deliver them to the competent authorities (serious offence); crew and passengers may assist;"],["010.01.02.03:01","Other conventions & freedoms: fill in the gaps → ____ for such actions. (____: unlawful seizure/hijacking; Montreal ____: sabotage/acts against safety.)","no liability for such actions. (The Hague 1970: unlawful seizure/hijacking; Montreal 1971: sabotage/acts against safety.)"],["010.01.02.05:02","Other conventions & freedoms: fill in the gaps → ____: a ____ ____ is evidence of the contract of carriage; the absence or irregularity of the document does not affect the existence or validity of the contract (still subject to the Convention's rules).","Private international law: a passenger ticket / baggage check / air waybill is evidence of the contract of carriage; the absence or irregularity of the document does not affect the existence or validity of the contract (still subject to the Convention's rules)."],["010.01.02.05:03","Other conventions & freedoms: fill in the gaps → ____: passenger rights for ____ — compensation ____ km), plus care (meals, hotel) and re-routing or refund; no compensation in extraordinary circumstances (weather, ATC, security).","Reg. (EC) 261/2004: passenger rights for denied boarding, cancellation, long delay — compensation €250 (≤ 1 500 km), €400 (1 500–3 500 km), €600 (> 3 500 km), plus care (meals, hotel) and re-routing or refund; no compensation in extraordinary circumstances (weather, ATC, security)."],["010.01.02.05:04","Other conventions & freedoms: fill in the gaps → ____ (Montreal Convention 1999): limited to ~____ (destruction, loss, damage, delay) unless a higher value was declared.","Baggage liability (Montreal Convention 1999): limited to ~1 288 SDR per passenger (destruction, loss, damage, delay) unless a higher value was declared."],["010.01.03.01:01","Organisations: fill in the gaps → ____ (airlines' trade association, 1945): promote ____ ____, provide means for collaboration among airlines, cooperate with ICAO (tariffs, clearing house, standards like IOSA, DG regulations).","IATA (airlines' trade association, 1945): promote safe, regular and economical air transport, provide means for collaboration among airlines, cooperate with ICAO (tariffs, clearing house, standards like IOSA, DG regulations)."],["010.01.04.01:01","Organisations: fill in the gaps → ____ (Reg. (EU) ____ Basic Regulation): objectives — ____ uniform level of civil aviation safety, environmental protection, free movement, efficiency of regulatory processes, promote EU standards worldwide.","EASA (Reg. (EU) 2018/1139 Basic Regulation): objectives — high uniform level of civil aviation safety, environmental protection, free movement, efficiency of regulatory processes, promote EU standards worldwide."],["010.01.03.01:01","Organisations: fill in the gaps → Role: ____ (draft rules, opinions to the Commission), ____ (type certificates, foreign organisations), ____ (inspections of national authorities), safety analysis/research, international cooperation.","Role: rulemaking (draft rules, opinions to the Commission), certification (type certificates, foreign organisations), standardisation (inspections of national authorities), safety analysis/research, international cooperation."],["010.01.04.01:04","Organisations: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (binding) — ____ Regulation, delegated acts and implementing acts (implementing rules, e.g. Aircrew Reg. 1178/2011, Air-Ops 965/2012); soft law (non-binding) — CS (certification specifications), AMC (acceptable means of compliance — presumption of compliance), GM (guidance material, explanation).","Regulatory structure: hard law (binding) — Basic Regulation, delegated acts and implementing acts (implementing rules, e.g. Aircrew Reg. 1178/2011, Air-Ops 965/2012); soft law (non-binding) — CS (certification specifications), AMC (acceptable means of compliance — presumption of compliance), GM (guidance material, explanation)."],["010.01.04.02:01","Organisations: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ regulations (2004, SES II 2009) — reorganise European airspace for safety, ____ and efficiency: separation of regulation and service provision, functional airspace blocks (FABs), Network Manager (EUROCONTROL), performance scheme, common charging, SESAR technology.","EUROCONTROL / SES: Single European Sky regulations (2004, SES II 2009) — reorganise European airspace for safety, capacity and efficiency: separation of regulation and service provision, functional airspace blocks (FABs), Network Manager (EUROCONTROL), performance scheme, common charging, SESAR technology."],["010.02.03.01:01","Nationality & registration (Annex 7): fill in the gaps → ____: any machine that can derive support in the atmosphere from the ____ ____. Heavier-than-air aircraft: any aircraft deriving its lift in flight chiefly from aerodynamic forces. State of Registry: the State on whose register the aircraft is entered.","Aircraft: any machine that can derive support in the atmosphere from the reactions of the air other than the reactions of the air against the earth's surface. Heavier-than-air aircraft: any aircraft deriving its lift in flight chiefly from aerodynamic forces. State of Registry: the State on whose register the aircraft is entered."],["010.10.02.01:01","Facilitation (Annex 9): fill in the gaps → ____: document (flight details, crew, health, disinsection) — States shall not require it except where it is the ____ to cover crew and certain declarations (normally not needed).","General declaration: document (flight details, crew, health, disinsection) — States shall not require it except where it is the only document to cover crew and certain declarations (normally not needed)."],["010.10.02.02:03","Facilitation (Annex 9): fill in the gaps → ____: entry for duty/rest without visa when holding a ____ ____ or licence; CMC used to facilitate crew movement in international air transport; accepted instead of passport/visa when the crew member stays at the airport or within city limits during layover and departs on the next scheduled flight/for positioning.","Crew: entry for duty/rest without visa when holding a crew member certificate (CMC) or licence; CMC used to facilitate crew movement in international air transport; accepted instead of passport/visa when the crew member stays at the airport or within city limits during layover and departs on the next scheduled flight/for positioning."],["010.10.02.03:03","Facilitation (Annex 9): fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (and visa where required), minimal documents, efficient clearance (red/green channels). ____: cleared under the procedure applicable to accompanied baggage (simplified), with baggage tags/declarations.","Passengers & baggage: passport (and visa where required), minimal documents, efficient clearance (red/green channels). Unaccompanied baggage: cleared under the procedure applicable to accompanied baggage (simplified), with baggage tags/declarations."],["010.10.02.03:05","Facilitation (Annex 9): fill in the gaps → ____: the ____ must ____ from the territory (normally to the point of embarkation), State informs the operator; costs as per rules. Unruly passengers: PIC authority under the Tokyo Convention (restraint, disembarkation, delivery to authorities).","Inadmissible persons: the operator must remove them from the territory (normally to the point of embarkation), State informs the operator; costs as per rules. Unruly passengers: PIC authority under the Tokyo Convention (restraint, disembarkation, delivery to authorities)."],["010.10.02.01:01","Facilitation (Annex 9): fill in the gaps → ____: ____, simplified clearance, pre-arrival information, minimal inspection.","Cargo: air waybill/electronic data, simplified clearance, pre-arrival information, minimal inspection."],["010.12.01.01:01","Security (Annex 17 & related): fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (movement area of an airport, adjacent terrain and buildings with controlled access); ____ check (inspection of interior areas accessible to passengers and holds for prohibited items); screening (technical or other means to identify/detect weapons, explosives, dangerous devices);","Definitions: airside (movement area of an airport, adjacent terrain and buildings with controlled access); aircraft security check (inspection of interior areas accessible to passengers and holds for prohibited items); screening (technical or other means to identify/detect weapons, explosives, dangerous devices);"],["010.12.01.01:01","Security (Annex 17 & related): fill in the gaps → ____ (safeguarding civil aviation against acts of unlawful interference — combination of measures and human/material resources); ____ (means to prevent introduction of weapons/explosives); ____ (airside areas of priority risk with controlled access);","security (safeguarding civil aviation against acts of unlawful interference — combination of measures and human/material resources); security control (means to prevent introduction of weapons/explosives); security-restricted area (airside areas of priority risk with controlled access);"],["010.12.01.01:01","Security (Annex 17 & related): fill in the gaps → ____ (at an airport, not identified with a passenger).","unidentified baggage (at an airport, not identified with a passenger)."],["010.12.05.01:02","Security (Annex 17 & related): fill in the gaps → ____: ____ ____ in all matters related to safeguarding against acts of unlawful interference.","Objective: safety of passengers, crew, ground personnel and the general public in all matters related to safeguarding against acts of unlawful interference."],["010.12.05.01:02","Security (Annex 17 & related): fill in the gaps → ____: ____ or other ____ that may be used to commit an act of unlawful interference (except authorised/declared). Mixing of screened and unscreened passengers after screening → re-screen the passengers and their cabin baggage (and search the area).","Prohibited on board: weapons, explosives or other dangerous devices, articles or substances that may be used to commit an act of unlawful interference (except authorised/declared). Mixing of screened and unscreened passengers after screening → re-screen the passengers and their cabin baggage (and search the area)."],["010.12.04.01:04","Security (Annex 17 & related): fill in the gaps → ____ (judicial/administrative proceedings, deportees): the ____ ____ in advance, escorted as needed; security measures. Armed law-enforcement officers: only with agreement of the States concerned and PIC notified (number and seats).","Passengers in custody (judicial/administrative proceedings, deportees): the operator and PIC are informed in advance, escorted as needed; security measures. Armed law-enforcement officers: only with agreement of the States concerned and PIC notified (number and seats)."],["010.12.05.01:01","Security (Annex 17 & related): fill in the gaps → ____: States provide ____ — navigation aids, ____, permission to land, and take measures for the safety of passengers/crew; they shall detain the aircraft on the ground unless its departure is necessitated by the overriding duty to protect human life.","Unlawful seizure: States provide assistance — navigation aids, ATS, permission to land, and take measures for the safety of passengers/crew; they shall detain the aircraft on the ground unless its departure is necessitated by the overriding duty to protect human life."],["010.12.06.01:01","Security (Annex 17 & related): fill in the gaps → ____: written, meets requirements of the ____, approved, covers training, procedures, quality control.","Operator's security programme: written, meets requirements of the national civil aviation security programme, approved, covers training, procedures, quality control."],["010.12.07.01:03","Security (Annex 17 & related): fill in the gaps → B (PIC under unlawful interference): try to notify ATS (squawk ____, radio) and continue on the assigned track/level; if forced to deviate: ____ MHz (and other frequencies), use transponder, and — if no regional contingency procedures — proceed at a level differing from normal IFR cruising levels by ____ ft below FL 290.","B (PIC under unlawful interference): try to notify ATS (squawk 7500, radio) and continue on the assigned track/level; if forced to deviate: broadcast warnings on 121.5 MHz (and other frequencies), use transponder, and — if no regional contingency procedures — proceed at a level differing from normal IFR cruising levels by 1 000 ft above FL 290 / 500 ft below FL 290."],["010.12.01.01:01","Security (Annex 17 & related): fill in the gaps → ____: ____ lockable from within the flight deck; CAT aeroplanes (> 45 500 kg or > 60 pax): ____ resistant to small-arms fire/grenade shrapnel, with means to monitor the area outside the door.","Annex 6: flight crew compartment door lockable from within the flight deck; CAT aeroplanes (> 45 500 kg or > 60 pax): reinforced door resistant to small-arms fire/grenade shrapnel, with means to monitor the area outside the door."],["010.12.07.03:01","Security (Annex 17 & related): fill in the gaps → ____: ____ at least ____ from other parking positions, buildings or public areas, not over utilities (fuel, gas, power lines).","Annex 14: isolated aircraft parking position at least 100 m from other parking positions, buildings or public areas, not over utilities (fuel, gas, power lines)."],["010.12.07.04:01","Security (Annex 17 & related): fill in the gaps → ____: an aircraft known/believed to be subject to unlawful interference is given a taxi clearance to an ____ ____, taking into account other aircraft and airport operations; ATC gives priority and does not request unnecessary transmissions.","Doc 4444 (ATM): an aircraft known/believed to be subject to unlawful interference is given a taxi clearance to an isolated parking position, taking into account other aircraft and airport operations; ATC gives priority and does not request unnecessary transmissions."],["010.01.01.01:01","General review — 010 at a glance: what do you remember about this? → Rules of the air & SERA (#60), licensing (#70), AIS (#86), aerodromes …","Rules of the air & SERA (#60), licensing (#70), AIS (#86), aerodromes (#75), PANS-OPS & SAR (#90), accident investigation (#50), ATS (airspace classes A–G: A IFR only, B–D controlled VFR/IFR, E VFR not controlled, F/G uncontrolled; separation, clearances, flight information service, alerting service)."],["010.01.02.01:01","Exam trap (General review — 010 at a glance): complete the fact → ____t/____nd freedoms = Transit Agreement; 3rd–5th = Transport Agreement.","1st/2nd freedoms = Transit Agreement; 3rd–5th = Transport Agreement."],["010.01.01.01:01","Exam trap (General review — 010 at a glance): complete the fact → Tokyo ____= offences on board; Hague = hijacking; Montreal ____= sabotage.","Tokyo 1963 = offences on board; Hague = hijacking; Montreal 1971 = sabotage."],["010.01.01.03:02","Exam trap (General review — 010 at a glance): complete the fact → Council ____ANC ____","Council 36, ANC 19."],["010.12.07.03:01","Exam trap (General review — 010 at a glance): complete the fact → Isolated parking ≥ ____.","Isolated parking ≥ 100 m."],["010.01.01.01:01","EASA LO 010.01.01.01.01 → Explain the circumstances that led to the establishment of the Convention on International Civil Aviation, Chicago, 7 December 1944.","Background: WWII boosted aviation → need for international rules for safe, orderly development of civil aviation and air transport after the war → Chicago Convention, 7 Dec 1944 (in force 1947), creating ICAO; 96 articles in 4 parts (I Air navigation, II ICAO, III International air transport, IV Final provisions)."],["010.01.01.03:01","EASA LO 010.01.01.03.01 → Describe the objectives of ICAO.","Background: WWII boosted aviation → need for international rules for safe, orderly development of civil aviation and air transport after the war → Chicago Convention, 7 Dec 1944 (in force 1947), creating ICAO; 96 articles in 4 parts (I Air navigation, II ICAO, III International air transport, IV Final provisions). / EASA (Reg. (EU) 2018/1139 Basic Regulation): objectives — high uniform level of civil aviation safety, environmental protection, free movement, efficiency of regulatory processes, promote EU standards worldwide."],["010.01.02.05:01","EASA LO 010.01.02.05.01 → Explain the legal significance of the issue of a passenger ticket or of baggage/cargo documents (that the issue is a form of contract).","Private international law: a passenger ticket / baggage check / air waybill is evidence of the contract of carriage; the absence or irregularity of the document does not affect the existence or validity of the contract (still subject to the Convention's rules)."],["010.01.04.01:02","EASA LO 010.01.04.01.02 → Describe the role of EASA in European civil aviation.","EASA (Reg. (EU) 2018/1139 Basic Regulation): objectives — high uniform level of civil aviation safety, environmental protection, free movement, efficiency of regulatory processes, promote EU standards worldwide."],["010.01.04.01:03","EASA LO 010.01.04.01.03 → State that the structure of the regulatory material related to EASA involves: — hard law (regulations, delegated acts, implementing acts, and implementing rules);","Regulatory structure: hard law (binding) — Basic Regulation, delegated acts and implementing acts (implementing rules, e.g. Aircrew Reg. 1178/2011, Air-Ops 965/2012); soft law (non-binding) — CS (certification specifications), AMC (acceptable means of compliance — presumption of compliance), GM (guidance material, explanation). / EASA (Reg. (EU) 2018/1139 Basic Regulation): objectives — high uniform level of civil aviation safety, environmental protection, free movement, efficiency of regulatory processes, promote EU standards worldwide."],["010.10.02.02:01","EASA LO 010.10.02.02.01 → Explain entry requirements for crew.","Crew: entry for duty/rest without visa when holding a crew member certificate (CMC) or licence; CMC used to facilitate crew movement in international air transport; accepted instead of passport/visa when the crew member stays at the airport or within city limits during layover and departs on the next scheduled flight/for positioning."],["010.10.02.02:02","EASA LO 010.10.02.02.02 → Explain the reasons for the use of crew member certificates (CMC) for crew members engaged in international air transport.","Crew: entry for duty/rest without visa when holding a crew member certificate (CMC) or licence; CMC used to facilitate crew movement in international air transport; accepted instead of passport/visa when the crew member stays at the airport or within city limits during layover and departs on the next scheduled flight/for positioning."],["010.10.02.03:02","EASA LO 010.10.02.03.02 → Explain the requirements and documentation for unaccompanied baggage.","Passengers & baggage: passport (and visa where required), minimal documents, efficient clearance (red/green channels). Unaccompanied baggage: cleared under the procedure applicable to accompanied baggage (simplified), with baggage tags/declarations."],["010.12.07.01:02","EASA LO 010.12.07.01.02 → Describe what the PIC, of an aircraft subjected to unlawful interference, should do if:","Objective: safety of passengers, crew, ground personnel and the general public in all matters related to safeguarding against acts of unlawful interference."]],"94":[["021.10.02.01:01","Aviation fuels for piston engines: fill in the gaps → ____ — green (high lead); ____ — blue (low lead, most common); (UL91 unleaded — clear/colourless). Diesel aero-engines normally use ____ (kerosene).","AVGAS 100 — green (high lead); AVGAS 100LL — blue (low lead, most common); (UL91 unleaded — clear/colourless). Diesel aero-engines normally use JET A-1 (kerosene)."],["021.10.02.01:03","Aviation fuels for piston engines: fill in the gaps → ____: anti-knock value of a fuel = % of iso-octane in a mixture of iso-octane and n-heptane with the same knock resistance; ____ style grades give lean/rich ratings. Higher octane → higher compression/boost possible without detonation. ____ (higher grade usually permitted).","Octane rating: anti-knock value of a fuel = % of iso-octane in a mixture of iso-octane and n-heptane with the same knock resistance; 100/130 style grades give lean/rich ratings. Higher octane → higher compression/boost possible without detonation. Never use a lower grade than specified (higher grade usually permitted)."],["021.10.02.01:01","Aviation fuels for piston engines: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ ____ → pressure spikes, very high CHT, damage (holed pistons, broken rings, cracked heads).","Detonation: explosive, uncontrolled combustion of the remaining (end-gas) mixture after normal ignition → pressure spikes, very high CHT, damage (holed pistons, broken rings, cracked heads)."],["021.10.02.01:01","Aviation fuels for piston engines: fill in the gaps → Causes: ____, ____ ____ (high cylinder pressure), lean mixture at high power, high intake/CHT temperature, over-advanced timing, high compression. Promoted by high power + lean mixture + high temperature (climb with too-lean mixture, closed cowl flaps).","Causes: too low octane fuel, high MAP/low rpm (high cylinder pressure), lean mixture at high power, high intake/CHT temperature, over-advanced timing, high compression. Promoted by high power + lean mixture + high temperature (climb with too-lean mixture, closed cowl flaps)."],["021.10.02.01:01","Aviation fuels for piston engines: fill in the gaps → Recognised by ____ ____ (rarely audible in flight). Cure: ____ reduce power/MAP, open cowl flaps, increase airspeed. (Diesel: \"diesel knock\" from delayed self-ignition of accumulated fuel — excessive pressure rise; limited by FADEC/injection timing.)","Recognised by rising CHT/EGT anomalies, rough running, power loss, knocking (rarely audible in flight). Cure: enrich, reduce power/MAP, open cowl flaps, increase airspeed. (Diesel: \"diesel knock\" from delayed self-ignition of accumulated fuel — excessive pressure rise; limited by FADEC/injection timing.)"],["021.10.02.01:05","Aviation fuels for piston engines: fill in the gaps → ____: ignition of the mixture ____, by ____ spots (glowing carbon deposits, overheated plug electrodes/valves) → rough running, backfire, loss of power, damage. (Diesel: mis-timed early injection/combustion.)","Pre-ignition: ignition of the mixture before the spark, by hot spots (glowing carbon deposits, overheated plug electrodes/valves) → rough running, backfire, loss of power, damage. (Diesel: mis-timed early injection/combustion.)"],["021.10.02.01:01","Aviation fuels for piston engines: fill in the gaps → ____: drain samples from ____ ____ before the first flight of the day and after refuelling (and after heavy rain/long parking); water collects at the bottom (heavier) — drain until clean fuel; check colour/grade and contamination.","Water check: drain samples from all sumps and the gascolator before the first flight of the day and after refuelling (and after heavy rain/long parking); water collects at the bottom (heavier) — drain until clean fuel; check colour/grade and contamination."],["021.10.02.01:02","Aviation fuels for piston engines: fill in the gaps → ____: ____; ____ (JET A-1 ≈ 0.80).","Density: AVGAS ≈ 0.72 kg/l; diesel ≈ 0.84 kg/l (JET A-1 ≈ 0.80)."],["021.10.02.01:10","Aviation fuels for piston engines: fill in the gaps → ____ = tendency to evaporate — AVGAS high (easy starting, but ____ and carb icing risk); diesel/JET A-1 low. ____ — diesel/JET more viscous (poor flow when cold, waxing).","Volatility = tendency to evaporate — AVGAS high (easy starting, but vapour lock and carb icing risk); diesel/JET A-1 low. Viscosity — diesel/JET more viscous (poor flow when cold, waxing)."],["021.10.02.01:01","Aviation fuels for piston engines: fill in the gaps → ____ ____: fuel vaporises in lines (heat, low pressure at altitude, volatile fuel) → vapour bubbles interrupt flow → engine stoppage; prevented by ____ (pressurising lines), cool routing.","Vapour lock: fuel vaporises in lines (heat, low pressure at altitude, volatile fuel) → vapour bubbles interrupt flow → engine stoppage; prevented by boost pumps (pressurising lines), cool routing."],["021.10.03.01:01","Engine-driven fuel pump: fill in the gaps → Needed on ____ systems (low wing, fuel injection, high demand) to deliver fuel at the required pressure independent of the electrical system; an ____ backs it up (start, take-off/landing, pump failure, vapour lock).","Needed on pressure-feed systems (low wing, fuel injection, high demand) to deliver fuel at the required pressure independent of the electrical system; an electric boost/auxiliary pump backs it up (start, take-off/landing, pump failure, vapour lock)."],["021.10.04.01:06","Carburettor: fill in the gaps → Purpose: ____ and ____ it with the air to give the correct mixture for all power settings and altitudes.","Purpose: meter fuel in proportion to airflow and mix/atomise it with the air to give the correct mixture for all power settings and altitudes."],["021.10.04.01:01","Carburettor: fill in the gaps → ____: a ____ keep a constant fuel level; air flows through a ____ → velocity ↑, pressure ↓ → fuel is drawn from the main jet (discharge nozzle) in proportion to the pressure difference; the throttle (butterfly) valve downstream controls airflow/power; idle jet supplies fuel at low power.","Float-chamber carburettor: a float and needle valve keep a constant fuel level; air flows through a venturi → velocity ↑, pressure ↓ → fuel is drawn from the main jet (discharge nozzle) in proportion to the pressure difference; the throttle (butterfly) valve downstream controls airflow/power; idle jet supplies fuel at low power."],["021.10.04.01:03","Carburettor: fill in the gaps → Mixture control over the ____: with increasing airflow a simple carburettor would enrich → ____ and ____ (emulsion tube) admit air into the fuel to keep the mixture correct.","Mixture control over the power range: with increasing airflow a simple carburettor would enrich → compensation jet/air bleed and diffuser (emulsion tube) admit air into the fuel to keep the mixture correct."],["021.10.04.01:04","Carburettor: fill in the gaps → Over the ____: density ↓ → mixture becomes ____ → ____ (needle valve or back-suction/air-bleed reducing pressure in the float chamber) or automatic (aneroid) mixture control.","Over the altitude range: density ↓ → mixture becomes richer → manual mixture control (needle valve or back-suction/air-bleed reducing pressure in the float chamber) or automatic (aneroid) mixture control."],["021.10.04.01:01","Carburettor: fill in the gaps → ____: squirts extra fuel when the throttle is opened quickly, preventing a lean \"flat spot\"/hesitation (air accelerates faster than fuel).","Accelerator pump: squirts extra fuel when the throttle is opened quickly, preventing a lean \"flat spot\"/hesitation (air accelerates faster than fuel)."],["021.10.02.01:06","Carburettor: fill in the gaps → ____: extra fuel at ____ settings → cooler combustion, prevents detonation.","Power enrichment (economiser): extra fuel at high power settings → cooler combustion, prevents detonation."],["021.10.04.01:08","Carburettor: fill in the gaps → ____: hot air from a ____ ____, unfiltered, bypasses the air filter → melts/prevents ice. Effect: hot air is less dense → mixture richer and power decreases (rpm/MAP drop ~ 100–200 rpm).","Carburettor heat: hot air from a heat muff around the exhaust, unfiltered, bypasses the air filter → melts/prevents ice. Effect: hot air is less dense → mixture richer and power decreases (rpm/MAP drop ~ 100–200 rpm)."],["021.10.04.01:09","Carburettor: fill in the gaps → ____: manual pump injecting fuel directly into the intake ports/cylinders for ____; the ____ (pumping the throttle) is another priming method — but risks carburettor fire (fuel pooling in the intake).","Primer pump: manual pump injecting fuel directly into the intake ports/cylinders for cold starts; the accelerator pump (pumping the throttle) is another priming method — but risks carburettor fire (fuel pooling in the intake)."],["021.10.04.01:01","Carburettor: fill in the gaps → ____ (over-priming, backfire): continue ____ to suck the fire into the engine; if it starts, run it; if not: ____, master off, use extinguisher, evacuate.","Carburettor/induction fire (over-priming, backfire): continue cranking the engine to suck the fire into the engine; if it starts, run it; if not: fuel off, mixture cut-off, throttle open, master off, use extinguisher, evacuate."],["021.10.02.01:01","Exam trap (Carburettor): complete the fact → ____LL blue, ____green.","100LL blue, 100 green."],["021.10.04.01:02","EASA LO 021.10.04.01.02 → Describe the operating principle of the simple float chamber carburettor.","Over the altitude range: density ↓ → mixture becomes richer → manual mixture control (needle valve or back-suction/air-bleed reducing pressure in the float chamber) or automatic (aneroid) mixture control."]],"95":[["021.12.01.01:01","Smoke detection: fill in the gaps → ____: light source + photocell in a chamber; smoke particles ____ onto the photocell (light-scatter type) or ____ the beam → alarm. Used in cargo compartments, lavatories, avionics bays.","Optical (photoelectric) detector: light source + photocell in a chamber; smoke particles scatter light onto the photocell (light-scatter type) or obscure the beam → alarm. Used in cargo compartments, lavatories, avionics bays."],["021.12.01.01:01","Smoke detection: fill in the gaps → ____: a small radioactive source (americium) ____ between electrodes → small current; smoke particles attach to ions → ____ → alarm.","Ionising detector: a small radioactive source (americium) ionises the air between electrodes → small current; smoke particles attach to ions → current falls → alarm."],["021.12.01.01:01","Smoke detection: fill in the gaps → Indications: ____ warning (master warning/caution, aural), cargo \"SMOKE\" light, lavatory aural horn + light; ____ (press-to-test for each loop/detector) before flight.","Indications: SMOKE warning (master warning/caution, aural), cargo \"SMOKE\" light, lavatory aural horn + light; function test (press-to-test for each loop/detector) before flight."],["021.12.02.01:01","Fire extinguishing (engines & cargo): fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (halon or replacement agents, pressurised with nitrogen), ____ (explosive cartridges) to discharge, distribution pipes/nozzles to the engine nacelle/APU/cargo, discharge indicators (red disc = thermal over-pressure discharge; yellow disc = normal discharge), fire handle/switch (pull → closes fuel, hydraulic, bleed valves, trips generator; rotate/push → fires bottle 1/2), pressure switch \"BOTTLE DISCH\" light.","Built-in system: extinguisher bottles (halon or replacement agents, pressurised with nitrogen), squibs (explosive cartridges) to discharge, distribution pipes/nozzles to the engine nacelle/APU/cargo, discharge indicators (red disc = thermal over-pressure discharge; yellow disc = normal discharge), fire handle/switch (pull → closes fuel, hydraulic, bleed valves, trips generator; rotate/push → fires bottle 1/2), pressure switch \"BOTTLE DISCH\" light."],["021.12.02.01:02","Fire extinguishing (engines & cargo): fill in the gaps → ____ must be provided (CS 25.1195(c)) — two bottles or a bottle shared crosswise between engines. Cargo: initial high-rate discharge (knock-down) + metered low-rate discharge (suppression for the diversion time).","Two discharges per engine must be provided (CS 25.1195(c)) — two bottles or a bottle shared crosswise between engines. Cargo: initial high-rate discharge (knock-down) + metered low-rate discharge (suppression for the diversion time)."],["021.12.02.02:01","Fire detection: fill in the gaps → ____: sensing element with a core in a ____ whose ____ (Kidde/Fenwal) or capacitance rises as temperature rises → alarm when the threshold is reached along any part of the loop (average/local overheat).","Resistance & capacitance continuous loops: sensing element with a core in a eutectic salt/ceramic whose resistance drops (Kidde/Fenwal) or capacitance rises as temperature rises → alarm when the threshold is reached along any part of the loop (average/local overheat)."],["021.12.02.02:01","Fire detection: fill in the gaps → ____ loop (e.g. ____): sealed tube filled with gas (helium) and a gas-absorbing core; heat raises pressure (average temperature) or releases gas from the core (local fire) → ____ closes → alarm; integrity switch monitors the loop.","Gas pressure loop (e.g. Lindberg/Systron-Donner): sealed tube filled with gas (helium) and a gas-absorbing core; heat raises pressure (average temperature) or releases gas from the core (local fire) → pressure switch closes → alarm; integrity switch monitors the loop."],["021.12.02.02:01","Fire detection: fill in the gaps → ____: spot detectors that close when temperature exceeds a set value (e.g. APU, wheel wells).","Bimetallic (thermal) switches: spot detectors that close when temperature exceeds a set value (e.g. APU, wheel wells)."],["021.12.02.02:03","Fire detection: fill in the gaps → ____ systems (loop A + B, \"AND\" logic): ____ ____ (both loops must detect) and keep detection if one loop fails (switch to single loop \"OR\").","Double-loop systems (loop A + B, \"AND\" logic): reduce false alarms (both loops must detect) and keep detection if one loop fails (switch to single loop \"OR\")."],["021.12.02.02:04","Fire detection: fill in the gaps → Indications: ____ (red master warning, fire bell, illuminated fire handle), ____ (amber), bottle discharged/low pressure; ____: fire test switch checks loops, warnings, squib continuity.","Indications: FIRE warning (red master warning, fire bell, illuminated fire handle), LOOP FAULT (amber), bottle discharged/low pressure; test: fire test switch checks loops, warnings, squib continuity."],["021.13.01.01:01","Oxygen systems: fill in the gaps → ____ — normally ____ from a high-pressure bottle (~1 850 psi) through pressure reducer to the ____ (with diluter-demand regulator): Normal (diluter demand): oxygen mixed with cabin air on inhalation, O₂ proportion increasing with cabin altitude (100 % at ~ 30 000–35 000 ft).","Cockpit (crew) oxygen — normally gaseous from a high-pressure bottle (~1 850 psi) through pressure reducer to the quick-donning masks (with diluter-demand regulator): Normal (diluter demand): oxygen mixed with cabin air on inhalation, O₂ proportion increasing with cabin altitude (100 % at ~ 30 000–35 000 ft)."],["021.13.01.01:01","Oxygen systems: fill in the gaps → ____: pure oxygen on demand (smoke/fumes, high altitude). ____: 100 % oxygen under ____ (continuous overpressure — clears smoke from the mask/goggles, required above ~ 39 000 ft cabin altitude).","100 %: pure oxygen on demand (smoke/fumes, high altitude). Emergency: 100 % oxygen under positive pressure (continuous overpressure — clears smoke from the mask/goggles, required above ~ 39 000 ft cabin altitude)."],["021.13.01.01:03","Oxygen systems: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ with continuous-flow masks for cabin crew (walk-around after decompression) and ____ oxygen for passengers in medical need; PBE (smoke hood) for crew fire-fighting (chemical generator or bottle, ~15 min).","Portable oxygen: portable bottles with continuous-flow masks for cabin crew (walk-around after decompression) and first-aid oxygen for passengers in medical need; PBE (smoke hood) for crew fire-fighting (chemical generator or bottle, ~15 min)."],["021.13.01.01:03","Oxygen systems: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ — chemical oxygen generators (sodium chlorate candle above each seat row; triggered when a mask is pulled; produces O₂ for ____, becomes very hot, cannot be switched off) or gaseous system (central bottles, distribution lines, continuous-flow masks); portable bottles.","Passenger oxygen: fixed system — chemical oxygen generators (sodium chlorate candle above each seat row; triggered when a mask is pulled; produces O₂ for 12–22 min, becomes very hot, cannot be switched off) or gaseous system (central bottles, distribution lines, continuous-flow masks); portable bottles."],["021.13.01.01:04","Oxygen systems: fill in the gaps → ____: masks ____ when cabin altitude exceeds ____ (aneroid switch/pressure), or manually by the crew (MASK MAN ON); flow starts when the passenger pulls the mask (lanyard fires the generator pin/opens valve).","Actuation: masks drop automatically when cabin altitude exceeds ~14 000 ft (aneroid switch/pressure), or manually by the crew (MASK MAN ON); flow starts when the passenger pulls the mask (lanyard fires the generator pin/opens valve)."],["021.13.01.01:05","Oxygen systems: fill in the gaps → ____: chemical — ____, no flow regulation with altitude, light, maintenance-free, no distribution lines, can't be turned off; gaseous — ____ adjustable (bottle size), flow regulated with altitude, can be shut off/reset, but heavier, needs lines, refilling and high-pressure maintenance.","Chemical vs gaseous: chemical — fixed capacity/duration, no flow regulation with altitude, light, maintenance-free, no distribution lines, can't be turned off; gaseous — capacity adjustable (bottle size), flow regulated with altitude, can be shut off/reset, but heavier, needs lines, refilling and high-pressure maintenance."],["021.13.01.01:06","Oxygen systems: fill in the gaps → ____ (spontaneous ignition of hydrocarbons in high-pressure O₂) → no grease, oil, lipstick/greasy creams near oxygen equipment; use approved oxygen-compatible lubricants only; no smoking.","Grease/oil + oxygen = fire/explosion risk (spontaneous ignition of hydrocarbons in high-pressure O₂) → no grease, oil, lipstick/greasy creams near oxygen equipment; use approved oxygen-compatible lubricants only; no smoking."],["021.12.01.01:02","EASA LO 021.12.01.01.02 → Give an example of warnings, indications and function tests.","Indications: FIRE warning (red master warning, fire bell, illuminated fire handle), LOOP FAULT (amber), bottle discharged/low pressure; test: fire test switch checks loops, warnings, squib continuity."]],"96":[["021.02.00.00",": what do you remember about this? → FINAL review of 021 Airframe & Systems (without powerplant and …","FINAL review of 021 Airframe & Systems (without powerplant and electrics) — cheat sheet."],["021.01.00.00","System design, loads, maintenance: fill in the gaps → Design philosophies: ____ (replaced after a fixed life), ____ ____ (redundant load paths — one failure doesn't cause collapse), damage tolerant (cracks detected before critical by inspection). Loads: tension, compression, bending, torsion, shear; stress = load/area, strain = deformation/length;","Design philosophies: safe life (replaced after a fixed life), fail- safe (redundant load paths — one failure doesn't cause collapse), damage tolerant (cracks detected before critical by inspection). Loads: tension, compression, bending, torsion, shear; stress = load/area, strain = deformation/length;"],["021.01.00.00","System design, loads, maintenance: fill in the gaps → ____ (cyclic loads, pressurisation cycles), corrosion, creep. ____ ____.","fatigue (cyclic loads, pressurisation cycles), corrosion, creep. Limit vs ultimate load (× 1.5)."],["021.01.00.00","System design, loads, maintenance: fill in the gaps → Maintenance: ____ (checks A/B/C/D by hours/cycles/calendar), unscheduled; condition monitoring, on-condition, hard time.","Maintenance: scheduled (checks A/B/C/D by hours/cycles/calendar), unscheduled; condition monitoring, on-condition, hard time."],["021.02.00.00","Airframe: fill in the gaps → ____ (skin takes all loads), ____ (skin + frames/formers + stringers/longerons — standard). Wing: spars (bending), ribs (shape), skin (torsion — torsion box), stringers. Materials: Al alloys, titanium (hot areas), composites (CFRP — light, fatigue-resistant, but damage hard to see).","Monocoque (skin takes all loads), semi-monocoque (skin + frames/formers + stringers/longerons — standard). Wing: spars (bending), ribs (shape), skin (torsion — torsion box), stringers. Materials: Al alloys, titanium (hot areas), composites (CFRP — light, fatigue-resistant, but damage hard to see)."],["021.08.00.00","Airframe: fill in the gaps → Pressurised fuselage: cylindrical, pressure bulkheads, hoop stress; windows/doors (plug-type doors). ____ by fuel in wing tanks and wing-mounted engines.","Pressurised fuselage: cylindrical, pressure bulkheads, hoop stress; windows/doors (plug-type doors). Wing bending relief by fuel in wing tanks and wing-mounted engines."],["021.03.00.00","Hydraulics: fill in the gaps → ____: pressure transmitted equally; F = p × A. Fluids: mineral ____, synthetic phosphate-ester (Skydrol — purple/green, fire-resistant, attacks paint/skin) — never mix; seals compatible. Pressure typically ____ (5 000 psi A380/B787).","Pascal: pressure transmitted equally; F = p × A. Fluids: mineral (red, DTD 585), synthetic phosphate-ester (Skydrol — purple/green, fire-resistant, attacks paint/skin) — never mix; seals compatible. Pressure typically 3 000 psi (5 000 psi A380/B787)."],["021.03.00.00","Hydraulics: fill in the gaps → Components: reservoir (pressurised — prevents cavitation), ____, electric pumps, ____, PTU (transfers power between systems without fluid), accumulator (stores pressure, damps surges, emergency supply; pre-charged with nitrogen), filters, relief valve, selector valves, actuators, priority valve, fuse (stops flow on leak).","Components: reservoir (pressurised — prevents cavitation), engine-driven pumps (variable displacement), electric pumps, RAT, PTU (transfers power between systems without fluid), accumulator (stores pressure, damps surges, emergency supply; pre-charged with nitrogen), filters, relief valve, selector valves, actuators, priority valve, fuse (stops flow on leak)."],["021.03.00.00","Hydraulics: what do you remember about this? → Redundancy: 2–4 independent …","Redundancy: 2–4 independent systems."],["021.04.00.00","Landing gear, wheels, tyres, brakes: fill in the gaps → Types: tricycle/tailwheel; fixed/retractable. ____ ____ (nitrogen spring + oil damping through orifice). ____ (tiller, rudder pedals), shimmy damper.","Types: tricycle/tailwheel; fixed/retractable. Oleo-pneumatic shock absorber (nitrogen spring + oil damping through orifice). Nose-wheel steering (tiller, rudder pedals), shimmy damper."],["021.04.00.00","Landing gear, wheels, tyres, brakes: fill in the gaps → Gear ____, indications (____, red in transit), warnings (gear not down with throttle retarded/flaps landing), ____ (gravity free-fall, alternate hydraulics/manual). Squat switches (WOW).","Gear down-lock/up-lock, indications (3 green, red in transit), warnings (gear not down with throttle retarded/flaps landing), emergency extension (gravity free-fall, alternate hydraulics/manual). Squat switches (WOW)."],["021.04.00.00","Landing gear, wheels, tyres, brakes: fill in the gaps → Tyres: ____ inflation, pressure checked cold; ply rating; ____ markings; tread wear; hydroplaning ____. Fuse plugs melt on overheated wheels.","Tyres: nitrogen inflation, pressure checked cold; ply rating; creep markings; tread wear; hydroplaning 9 √p / 7.7 √p. Fuse plugs melt on overheated wheels."],["021.04.00.00","Landing gear, wheels, tyres, brakes: fill in the gaps → Brakes: ____ (steel or ____ — light, more energy, wear per application); ____ (prevents wheel lock, maximises braking; deactivated below ~10 kt); autobrake (selected deceleration, RTO mode); parking brake with accumulator.","Brakes: disc brakes (steel or carbon — light, more energy, wear per application); anti-skid (prevents wheel lock, maximises braking; deactivated below ~10 kt); autobrake (selected deceleration, RTO mode); parking brake with accumulator."],["021.12.00.00","Flight controls: fill in the gaps → Primary (ailerons, elevator, rudder) and secondary (flaps, slats, spoilers, trim). Mechanical (cables, rods), ____, ____ ____ (irreversible, artificial feel, q-feel), FBW (computers, redundancy, control laws, protections). Gust locks, control stops, flap asymmetry protection, load relief. Trim systems (tabs, THS), stabiliser runaway protection.","Primary (ailerons, elevator, rudder) and secondary (flaps, slats, spoilers, trim). Mechanical (cables, rods), power-assisted, fully powered (irreversible, artificial feel, q-feel), FBW (computers, redundancy, control laws, protections). Gust locks, control stops, flap asymmetry protection, load relief. Trim systems (tabs, THS), stabiliser runaway protection."],["021.06.00.00","Pneumatics, pressurisation, air conditioning: fill in the gaps → Bleed air from IP/HP stages via PRSOV, ____ (fan air), cross-bleed/isolation valve, APU, ground cart; leak/overheat detection. Packs (air-cycle machine: compressor/turbine/heat exchangers, water separator), ____.","Bleed air from IP/HP stages via PRSOV, pre-cooler (fan air), cross-bleed/isolation valve, APU, ground cart; leak/overheat detection. Packs (air-cycle machine: compressor/turbine/heat exchangers, water separator), trim air."],["021.06.00.00","Pneumatics, pressurisation, air conditioning: fill in the gaps → Pressurisation: outflow valve, positive & negative relief valves, cabin altitude max ____, max ΔP ____, cabin climb ~500 ft/min, descent ~300 ft/min, ____, masks drop ~14 000 ft.","Pressurisation: outflow valve, positive & negative relief valves, cabin altitude max 8 000 ft, max ΔP ~8–9 psi, cabin climb ~500 ft/min, descent ~300 ft/min, warning > 10 000 ft, masks drop ~14 000 ft."],["021.07.00.00","Ice & rain protection: fill in the gaps → ____ (prevent: bleed air thermal on wings/intakes, electric on windshields/probes, fluid TKS) vs ____ (remove: boots, cyclic electric on props). Ice detectors: vibrating probe (____ with ice), pressure type. Wipers, rain repellent, hydrophobic coating.","Anti-ice (prevent: bleed air thermal on wings/intakes, electric on windshields/probes, fluid TKS) vs de-ice (remove: boots, cyclic electric on props). Ice detectors: vibrating probe (frequency falls with ice), pressure type. Wipers, rain repellent, hydrophobic coating."],["021.08.00.00","Fuel system: what do you remember about this? → AVGAS 100LL (blue, 0.72), JET A-1 (flash ≥ 38 °C, freeze ≤ −47 °C, …","AVGAS 100LL (blue, 0.72), JET A-1 (flash ≥ 38 °C, freeze ≤ −47 °C, 0.80), JET A (−40 °C), JET B (wide-cut); FSII additive."],["021.08.00.00","Fuel system: fill in the gaps → Tanks (integral/bladder/rigid), vents/surge tanks, ____ (centrifugal, ____ via override pumps), cross-feed, ____ with altitude limit, refuelling panel (single point), jettison (MTOM ≫ MLM, stops at preset quantity), dripsticks, capacitance gauges (mass).","Tanks (integral/bladder/rigid), vents/surge tanks, boost pumps (centrifugal, centre tank first via override pumps), cross-feed, suction feed with altitude limit, refuelling panel (single point), jettison (MTOM ≫ MLM, stops at preset quantity), dripsticks, capacitance gauges (mass)."],["021.12.00.00","Protection & detection: fill in the gaps → Smoke detectors (____), fire detection (____ switches), double loops (fewer false alarms), extinguishers (bottles, squibs, ____), rain protection.","Smoke detectors (optical, ionisation), fire detection (resistance/capacitance loops, gas-pressure loops, thermal switches), double loops (fewer false alarms), extinguishers (bottles, squibs, 2 shots per engine), rain protection."],["021.13.00.00","Oxygen: fill in the gaps → Crew: gaseous, diluter-demand (____ = positive pressure); pax: ____ (12–22 min, start when mask pulled) or gaseous; masks drop at ~14 000 ft; ____ oil/grease near oxygen; portable bottles and PBE.","Crew: gaseous, diluter-demand (Normal / 100 % / Emergency = positive pressure); pax: chemical generators (12–22 min, start when mask pulled) or gaseous; masks drop at ~14 000 ft; no oil/grease near oxygen; portable bottles and PBE."]],"97":[["021.10.04.03:06","Fuel injection: fill in the gaps → ____: fuel is metered by a fuel control unit (servo regulator using airflow/throttle and mixture) and delivered via a ____ to ____ port (continuous injection in Continental/Lycoming systems).","Injection system: fuel is metered by a fuel control unit (servo regulator using airflow/throttle and mixture) and delivered via a flow divider to injector nozzles at each cylinder's intake port (continuous injection in Continental/Lycoming systems)."],["021.10.04.02:01","Fuel injection: fill in the gaps → Advantages vs carburettor: ____ ____, ____ to cylinders → better economy & smoother running, better fuel metering at all attitudes (inverted/aerobatic), faster throttle response, easier cold starts, lower fuel consumption.","Advantages vs carburettor: no carburettor (venturi/fuel- evaporation) icing, more even distribution to cylinders → better economy & smoother running, better fuel metering at all attitudes (inverted/aerobatic), faster throttle response, easier cold starts, lower fuel consumption."],["021.10.04.02:01","Fuel injection: fill in the gaps → Disadvantages: more complex and expensive, ____ difficult (vapour in lines), vapour lock, needs clean fuel (small nozzles), residual fuel in lines after shut-down.","Disadvantages: more complex and expensive, hot starts difficult (vapour in lines), vapour lock, needs clean fuel (small nozzles), residual fuel in lines after shut-down."],["021.10.04.03:01","Carburettor & induction icing: fill in the gaps → ____: in the carburettor, ____ (strongest effect) and venturi pressure drop (adiabatic expansion) cool the air by up to ____ → moisture freezes on the throttle valve and venturi. Also throttle (impact) icing at part throttle.","Causes: in the carburettor, fuel evaporation (strongest effect) and venturi pressure drop (adiabatic expansion) cool the air by up to ~30 °C → moisture freezes on the throttle valve and venturi. Also throttle (impact) icing at part throttle."],["021.10.04.03:01","Carburettor & induction icing: fill in the gaps → ____: high humidity (visible moisture not required) and OAT roughly ____ — serious icing at any power around ____ with high humidity/dew point close to temperature; most likely at low power (descent/approach — partly closed throttle).","Conditions: high humidity (visible moisture not required) and OAT roughly −10 °C to +30 °C — serious icing at any power around +5 to +20 °C with high humidity/dew point close to temperature; most likely at low power (descent/approach — partly closed throttle)."],["021.10.04.03:01","Carburettor & induction icing: fill in the gaps → Impact/induction icing (air filter, intake) in freezing precipitation/snow/cloud below 0 °C — also affects ____ engines.","Impact/induction icing (air filter, intake) in freezing precipitation/snow/cloud below 0 °C — also affects injection engines."],["021.10.04.03:01","Carburettor & induction icing: fill in the gaps → ____: restricts airflow and changes mixture (richer) → ____ ____, rough running, eventually engine stoppage.","Effects: restricts airflow and changes mixture (richer) → power loss, rough running, eventually engine stoppage."],["021.10.04.03:03","Carburettor & induction icing: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ → ____ (then rough running); constant-speed prop → MAP drop (rpm constant); helicopter → MAP drop / rotor rpm tendency (governed) or need to raise collective/throttle to maintain power; carb temperature gauge in the yellow arc.","Indications: fixed-pitch prop → rpm drop (then rough running); constant-speed prop → MAP drop (rpm constant); helicopter → MAP drop / rotor rpm tendency (governed) or need to raise collective/throttle to maintain power; carb temperature gauge in the yellow arc."],["021.10.04.03:05","Carburettor & induction icing: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ → power drops slightly (less dense hot air, richer mixture) and ____ lower; ice present → power drops further first (melting ice/water passing through the engine, rough running), then rises as the ice clears (to above the pre-heat value).","Applying carb heat: no ice present → power drops slightly (less dense hot air, richer mixture) and stays lower; ice present → power drops further first (melting ice/water passing through the engine, rough running), then rises as the ice clears (to above the pre-heat value)."],["021.10.04.03:05","Carburettor & induction icing: fill in the gaps → Use ____, not partial (partial heat can raise the temperature into the icing range). Carb heat is ____ → avoid on the ground in dusty conditions.","Use full heat, not partial (partial heat can raise the temperature into the icing range). Carb heat is unfiltered → avoid on the ground in dusty conditions."],["021.10.04.03:01","Carburettor & induction icing: fill in the gaps → ____ when suspected: ____, leave it on until the ice is cleared (engine may run rough), then return to cold and monitor; lean if necessary; on approach use carb heat per POH before reducing power.","Action when suspected: full carburettor heat, leave it on until the ice is cleared (engine may run rough), then return to cold and monitor; lean if necessary; on approach use carb heat per POH before reducing power."],["021.10.04.03:06","Carburettor & induction icing: fill in the gaps → ____ on injection engines: if the ____ ____ (impact icing), an alternate air door (automatic, spring-loaded opening by suction, or manual) admits warm, unfiltered air from inside the cowling → engine keeps running (slight power loss).","Alternate air on injection engines: if the air filter/intake is blocked by ice (impact icing), an alternate air door (automatic, spring-loaded opening by suction, or manual) admits warm, unfiltered air from inside the cowling → engine keeps running (slight power loss)."],["021.10.04.03:07","Carburettor & induction icing: fill in the gaps → ____ (impact) conditions: flying in ____ ____ with OAT around/below 0 °C → intake filters and inlets collect ice.","Induction icing (impact) conditions: flying in cloud, freezing rain/drizzle, wet snow with OAT around/below 0 °C → intake filters and inlets collect ice."],["021.10.10.01:01","Engine performance: fill in the gaps → ____ increases with ____ (more air mass per stroke): ____ ↑, lower exhaust back pressure ↑, lower temperature ↑, lower density altitude ↑; humidity ↓ power (water vapour displaces O₂ and lowers density) — petrol engines more affected (mixture richer); diesel engines similar density effects (always excess air, less humidity sensitive).","Power output increases with air density (more air mass per stroke): higher ambient pressure ↑, lower exhaust back pressure ↑, lower temperature ↑, lower density altitude ↑; humidity ↓ power (water vapour displaces O₂ and lowers density) — petrol engines more affected (mixture richer); diesel engines similar density effects (always excess air, less humidity sensitive)."],["021.10.10.01:02","Engine performance: fill in the gaps → ____ engine: induction air at ambient pressure (no compressor) → power decreases with altitude (~3 % per 1 000 ft).","Normally aspirated engine: induction air at ambient pressure (no compressor) → power decreases with altitude (~3 % per 1 000 ft)."],["021.10.10.02:02","Engine performance: fill in the gaps → ____ needed to maintain sea-level power at altitude (and/or boost take-off power).","Power augmentation (turbocharging) needed to maintain sea-level power at altitude (and/or boost take-off power)."],["021.10.10.01:04","Engine performance: fill in the gaps → ____: exhaust gas drives a ____ → shaft drives a ____ that compresses induction air. Waste gate (butterfly valve in the exhaust bypass) controls how much exhaust goes through the turbine — closed = more boost; waste-gate actuator (oil-pressure controlled by a density/pressure controller) closes it progressively with altitude.","Turbocharger: exhaust gas drives a turbine → shaft drives a compressor that compresses induction air. Waste gate (butterfly valve in the exhaust bypass) controls how much exhaust goes through the turbine — closed = more boost; waste-gate actuator (oil-pressure controlled by a density/pressure controller) closes it progressively with altitude."],["021.10.10.02:02","Engine performance: fill in the gaps → ____: maintains sea-level MAP up to the critical altitude (no extra take-off power). ____: produces ____ even at sea level (more take-off power) — overboost protection required.","Altitude-boosted (normalised): maintains sea-level MAP up to the critical altitude (no extra take-off power). Ground-boosted: produces MAP above sea-level pressure even at sea level (more take-off power) — overboost protection required."],["021.10.10.01:06","Engine performance: fill in the gaps → ____: delay between opening the throttle and the build-up of boost (turbine/compressor inertia) → move throttle smoothly, avoid overboost.","Turbo lag: delay between opening the throttle and the build-up of boost (turbine/compressor inertia) → move throttle smoothly, avoid overboost."],["021.10.10.01:04","Engine performance: fill in the gaps → ____: highest altitude at which the (turbocharged) engine can still maintain its rated power/MAP with the waste gate ____; above it power decreases.","Critical altitude: highest altitude at which the (turbocharged) engine can still maintain its rated power/MAP with the waste gate fully closed; above it power decreases."],["021.10.10.01:08","Engine performance: fill in the gaps → ____: cools the compressed (hot) air before the cylinders → ____, less detonation.","Intercooler: cools the compressed (hot) air before the cylinders → higher density, less detonation."],["021.10.10.01:09","Engine performance: fill in the gaps → ____: altitude at which, for a given rpm, the throttle is fully open to maintain the selected MAP (above it MAP falls). ____: full-throttle height for maximum continuous (rated) power.","Full-throttle height: altitude at which, for a given rpm, the throttle is fully open to maintain the selected MAP (above it MAP falls). Rated altitude: full-throttle height for maximum continuous (rated) power."],["021.10.10.01:04","Engine performance: fill in the gaps → ____: compressor ____ by the crankshaft (gears) → boost available immediately (no lag) but absorbs engine power; ____ uses exhaust energy (more efficient), has lag and waste gate.","Supercharger: compressor mechanically driven by the crankshaft (gears) → boost available immediately (no lag) but absorbs engine power; turbocharger uses exhaust energy (more efficient), has lag and waste gate."],["021.10.10.02:01","Engine handling: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ ____. Decrease power: throttle back → prop back → mixture (lean as required). Avoid high MAP with low rpm (overboost/detonation). Change slowly (turbos, thermal shock).","Increase power: mixture rich → prop (rpm) up → throttle (MAP) up. Decrease power: throttle back → prop back → mixture (lean as required). Avoid high MAP with low rpm (overboost/detonation). Change slowly (turbos, thermal shock)."],["021.10.10.02:02","Engine handling: fill in the gaps → ____: maximum power certified for take-off, ____ ____ (e.g. 5 min). Maximum continuous power (MCP): maximum power approved for unrestricted duration.","Take-off power: maximum power certified for take-off, time- limited (e.g. 5 min). Maximum continuous power (MCP): maximum power approved for unrestricted duration."],["021.10.10.02:01","Engine handling: fill in the gaps → ____: oil very viscous (starter load, poor lubrication), fuel vaporises poorly (hard to start, over-priming → fire), weak battery, ice in fuel/lines; use ____, external power, proper priming, no over-priming; warm up before high power; watch oil pressure (can be very high).","Cold-weather starts: oil very viscous (starter load, poor lubrication), fuel vaporises poorly (hard to start, over-priming → fire), weak battery, ice in fuel/lines; use pre-heating, external power, proper priming, no over-priming; warm up before high power; watch oil pressure (can be very high)."],["021.10.10.02:04","Engine handling: fill in the gaps → ____ (full-authority digital engine control): computer controls ____ ____ from a single power lever — no separate mixture/prop levers; auto start, protection against overspeed/overtemperature/overboost, optimum mixture, fault monitoring and recording.","FADEC (full-authority digital engine control): computer controls fuel flow (and mixture, ignition timing, propeller pitch/rpm) from a single power lever — no separate mixture/prop levers; auto start, protection against overspeed/overtemperature/overboost, optimum mixture, fault monitoring and recording."],["021.10.10.02:05","Engine handling: fill in the gaps → Flight-deck controls: ____, FADEC/engine master switch, test/backup selector, ECU A/B selection, warning lights.","Flight-deck controls: power lever, FADEC/engine master switch, test/backup selector, ECU A/B selection, warning lights."],["021.10.10.02:06","Engine handling: fill in the gaps → FADEC has ____ over all engine parameters; ____: two channels (A/B), ____ (aircraft bus + dedicated alternator/battery back-up), redundant sensors/inputs/outputs — a failure of the FADEC could otherwise stop the engine.","FADEC has full authority over all engine parameters; redundancy: two channels (A/B), dual power supply (aircraft bus + dedicated alternator/battery back-up), redundant sensors/inputs/outputs — a failure of the FADEC could otherwise stop the engine."],["021.10.04.03:04","EASA LO 021.10.04.03.04 → Describe the indications of the presence of carburettor icing for a helicopter.","Indications: fixed-pitch prop → rpm drop (then rough running); constant-speed prop → MAP drop (rpm constant); helicopter → MAP drop / rotor rpm tendency (governed) or need to raise collective/throttle to maintain power; carb temperature gauge in the yellow arc."],["021.10.10.01:03","EASA LO 021.10.10.01.03 → Power-augmentation devices: explain the requirement for power augmentation (turbocharging) of a piston engine.","Power augmentation (turbocharging) needed to maintain sea-level power at altitude (and/or boost take-off power)."],["021.10.10.01:05","EASA LO 021.10.10.01.05 → Explain the difference between an altitude-boosted turbocharger and a ground-boosted turbocharger.","Altitude-boosted (normalised): maintains sea-level MAP up to the critical altitude (no extra take-off power). Ground-boosted: produces MAP above sea-level pressure even at sea level (more take-off power) — overboost protection required."],["021.10.10.01:07","EASA LO 021.10.10.01.07 → Define the term ‘critical altitude’.","Altitude-boosted (normalised): maintains sea-level MAP up to the critical altitude (no extra take-off power). Ground-boosted: produces MAP above sea-level pressure even at sea level (more take-off power) — overboost protection required. / Critical altitude: highest altitude at which the (turbocharged) engine can still maintain its rated power/MAP with the waste gate fully closed; above it power decreases."],["021.10.10.02:07","EASA LO 021.10.10.02.07 → Explain the need for FADEC redundancy with regard to power supply and data input and output.","FADEC has full authority over all engine parameters; redundancy: two channels (A/B), dual power supply (aircraft bus + dedicated alternator/battery back-up), redundant sensors/inputs/outputs — a failure of the FADEC could otherwise stop the engine."]],"98":[["021.11.01.01:02","Thrust & basic principles: fill in the gaps → Thrust = reaction to accelerating a mass of air rearwards (Newton 2 & 3). ____ (straight jet): ________ − Va) + Aj × (pj − pa) — momentum thrust + pressure thrust (only with a choked nozzle). Example: ṁ = 50 kg/s, Vj = 600 m/s, Va = 200 m/s → 20 000 N + pressure thrust (e.g.","Thrust = reaction to accelerating a mass of air rearwards (Newton 2 & 3). Thrust formula (straight jet): F = ṁ × (Vj − Va) + Aj × (pj − pa) — momentum thrust + pressure thrust (only with a choked nozzle). Example: ṁ = 50 kg/s, Vj = 600 m/s, Va = 200 m/s → 20 000 N + pressure thrust (e.g."],["021.11.01.01:03","Thrust & basic principles: fill in the gaps → A 0.2 m² × 30 000 Pa = 6 000 N) = 26 kN. Thrust stays ____ ____ (momentum loss offset by ____).","A 0.2 m² × 30 000 Pa = 6 000 N) = 26 kN. Thrust stays approximately constant over the subsonic speed range (momentum loss offset by ram effect)."],["021.11.02.03:03","Thrust & basic principles: fill in the gaps → Components: ____ ____. Working cycle (____): compression (p ↑, T ↑), combustion at ~constant pressure (T ↑↑, V ↑), expansion in turbine (p ↓, T ↓), nozzle (V ↑↑).","Components: inlet → compressor → combustion chamber → turbine → exhaust (nozzle). Working cycle (Brayton): compression (p ↑, T ↑), combustion at ~constant pressure (T ↑↑, V ↑), expansion in turbine (p ↓, T ↓), nozzle (V ↑↑)."],["021.11.02.02:09","Thrust & basic principles: what do you remember about this? → Static pressure highest at compressor exit; temperature highest in …","Static pressure highest at compressor exit; temperature highest in the combustor; axial velocity roughly constant through compressor, rises in combustor/nozzle."],["021.11.01.02:03","Thrust & basic principles: fill in the gaps → Velocities in a stage: ____ velocity (seen from the casing), ____ (blade speed), ____ (along the engine axis) — combine vectorially (velocity triangles).","Velocities in a stage: absolute velocity (seen from the casing), circumferential/tangential (blade speed), axial (along the engine axis) — combine vectorially (velocity triangles)."],["021.11.01.02:04","Thrust & basic principles: fill in the gaps → Types: ____, ____ (fan + core, bypass flow), ____ (turbine drives propeller via reduction gear; small residual jet thrust), turboshaft (helicopters). One or more spools (N1 low-pressure spool with fan, N2 high-pressure; three-spool RR). Turbojet/turbofan: thrust from jet/fan momentum; turboprop: power from the turbine to the propeller.","Types: straight jet/turbojet, turbofan (fan + core, bypass flow), turboprop (turbine drives propeller via reduction gear; small residual jet thrust), turboshaft (helicopters). One or more spools (N1 low-pressure spool with fan, N2 high-pressure; three-spool RR). Turbojet/turbofan: thrust from jet/fan momentum; turboprop: power from the turbine to the propeller."],["021.11.01.02:08","Thrust & basic principles: fill in the gaps → ____ = shaft horsepower + jet thrust horsepower. ____: turbine not mechanically connected to the gas generator — drives only the propeller/rotor (starts easily, prop can be stopped with gas generator running with brake).","Equivalent (shaft) horsepower (ESHP) = shaft horsepower + jet thrust horsepower. Free (power) turbine: turbine not mechanically connected to the gas generator — drives only the propeller/rotor (starts easily, prop can be stopped with gas generator running with brake)."],["021.11.02.04:09","Thrust & basic principles: fill in the gaps → ____ = mass flow through bypass (cold) ÷ mass flow through core (hot). E.g. 500 kg/s total, 450 bypass → 450/50 = ____.","Bypass ratio = mass flow through bypass (cold) ÷ mass flow through core (hot). E.g. 500 kg/s total, 450 bypass → 450/50 = 9:1."],["021.11.01.02:11","Thrust & basic principles: fill in the gaps → ____ = useful propulsive power (F × Va) ÷ kinetic energy added → ____ ____ (large mass, low velocity — props/high-bypass fans). Thermal efficiency = kinetic energy produced ÷ fuel energy — increases with compressor pressure ratio (and turbine inlet temperature). Total efficiency = thermal × propulsive.","Propulsive efficiency = useful propulsive power (F × Va) ÷ kinetic energy added → high when jet velocity is close to flight speed (large mass, low velocity — props/high-bypass fans). Thermal efficiency = kinetic energy produced ÷ fuel energy — increases with compressor pressure ratio (and turbine inlet temperature). Total efficiency = thermal × propulsive."],["021.11.01.02:13","Thrust & basic principles: fill in the gaps → Propulsive efficiency vs speed: ____ best at low speed (< ~M 0.6), ____ at medium/high subsonic, ____ only at high/supersonic speeds.","Propulsive efficiency vs speed: turboprop best at low speed (< ~M 0.6), turbofan at medium/high subsonic, turbojet only at high/supersonic speeds."],["021.11.03.01:03","Thrust & basic principles: fill in the gaps → ____: turbojet = fuel flow per unit ____ (kg/N/h); turboprop = fuel flow per unit ____ (kg/kW/h, per ESHP).","SFC: turbojet = fuel flow per unit thrust (kg/N/h); turboprop = fuel flow per unit power (kg/kW/h, per ESHP)."],["021.11.01.03:02","Thrust & basic principles: fill in the gaps → ____ (single shaft: compressor, turbine and propeller/rotor on one shaft): limits — ____ ____ (material strength/creep), torque of engine and drive train. Exceedances → creep, blade stretch, cracks, burning, bearing damage → reduced life/failure; every exceedance must be reported (inspection).","Coupled turbine engine (single shaft: compressor, turbine and propeller/rotor on one shaft): limits — max turbine (inlet/EGT) temperature (material strength/creep), torque of engine and drive train. Exceedances → creep, blade stretch, cracks, burning, bearing damage → reduced life/failure; every exceedance must be reported (inspection)."],["021.11.01.04:04","Thrust & basic principles: fill in the gaps → ____: compact design with ____ combustor, ____ stages, rear/side exhausts; components: gas generator (compressor, combustor, gas-producer turbine), free power turbine → output shaft/gearbox. EGT/TOT is used to monitor turbine stress.","Free-turbine/turboshaft (helicopters): compact design with reverse-flow annular combustor, centrifugal compressor stages, rear/side exhausts; components: gas generator (compressor, combustor, gas-producer turbine), free power turbine → output shaft/gearbox. EGT/TOT is used to monitor turbine stress."],["021.11.02.01:02","Air intake: fill in the gaps → Functions: deliver air to the compressor ____ ____, ____ it to ~M 0.5 at the fan face (converting kinetic energy to pressure — ram rise). Subsonic pitot-type inlet: divergent duct (diffuser) → velocity ↓, static pressure ↑, temperature ↑ slightly.","Functions: deliver air to the compressor uniformly, with minimum losses and turbulence, decelerate it to ~M 0.5 at the fan face (converting kinetic energy to pressure — ram rise). Subsonic pitot-type inlet: divergent duct (diffuser) → velocity ↓, static pressure ↑, temperature ↑ slightly."],["021.11.02.01:01","Air intake: what do you remember about this? → At low speed/take-off the intake accelerates air in (pressure below …","At low speed/take-off the intake accelerates air in (pressure below ambient at the lip); at high speed, ram compression."],["021.11.02.01:04","Air intake: fill in the gaps → Problems: ____ (crosswind, high α, lip design) → distorted flow → ____; ____ → blockage, ice ingestion damage; damage to lip/ducting → disturbed flow; FOD (stones, birds, ice) → blade damage; heavy turbulence → distorted inflow → surge/flame-out (use continuous ignition).","Problems: airflow separation (crosswind, high α, lip design) → distorted flow → compressor stall; inlet icing → blockage, ice ingestion damage; damage to lip/ducting → disturbed flow; FOD (stones, birds, ice) → blade damage; heavy turbulence → distorted inflow → surge/flame-out (use continuous ignition)."],["021.11.02.03:01","Compressor & diffuser: fill in the gaps → Purpose: ____ (and mass flow) for efficient combustion.","Purpose: raise the air pressure (and mass flow) for efficient combustion."],["021.11.02.02:03","Compressor & diffuser: fill in the gaps → ____: impeller accelerates air outwards (pressure and velocity ↑) → diffuser converts velocity into pressure. Pressure ratio per stage ____ (up to ~8). Robust, less FOD-sensitive, simple; but large frontal area; more than ____ is impractical (losses, ducting).","Centrifugal: impeller accelerates air outwards (pressure and velocity ↑) → diffuser converts velocity into pressure. Pressure ratio per stage ~4–5:1 (up to ~8). Robust, less FOD-sensitive, simple; but large frontal area; more than 2 stages is impractical (losses, ducting)."],["021.11.02.02:04","Compressor & diffuser: fill in the gaps → ____: rotor blades (add energy: velocity & pressure ↑) + stator vanes (diffuse: velocity ↓, pressure ↑, redirect flow). Pressure ratio per stage ____; total compressor ____ (many stages). More FOD-sensitive (thin blades) but efficient, small frontal area, high mass flow.","Axial: rotor blades (add energy: velocity & pressure ↑) + stator vanes (diffuse: velocity ↓, pressure ↑, redirect flow). Pressure ratio per stage ~1.1–1.3:1; total compressor ~30–50:1 (many stages). More FOD-sensitive (thin blades) but efficient, small frontal area, high mass flow."],["021.11.02.02:09","Compressor & diffuser: fill in the gaps → ____ through the axial compressor: keeps ____ ____ as density increases. Blade twist: blade speed increases with radius → twist keeps the angle of attack constant along the blade. IGVs: guide air at the correct angle onto the first rotor stage (variable IGVs/stators prevent stall at low rpm).","Convergent annulus through the axial compressor: keeps axial velocity constant as density increases. Blade twist: blade speed increases with radius → twist keeps the angle of attack constant along the blade. IGVs: guide air at the correct angle onto the first rotor stage (variable IGVs/stators prevent stall at low rpm)."],["021.11.02.02:12","Compressor & diffuser: fill in the gaps → ____ when windmilling slowly on the ground: loosely mounted blades (fir-tree roots, damping) ____ in their slots — normal.","Clicking noise when windmilling slowly on the ground: loosely mounted blades (fir-tree roots, damping) rattling in their slots — normal."],["021.11.03.03:03","Compressor & diffuser: fill in the gaps → More ____: each spool at its optimum rpm → ____ ____, higher pressure ratios, better acceleration, only the LP spool needs to be turned by the starter.","More spools: each spool at its optimum rpm → less stall tendency, higher pressure ratios, better acceleration, only the LP spool needs to be turned by the starter."],["021.11.02.02:15","Compressor & diffuser: fill in the gaps → ____: air leaking around blade tips → reduced efficiency → small tip clearances, ____ casing linings, shrouds. ____ bending/flapping (vibration) → snubbers/shrouds, mid-span clappers, stiffer blades.","Tip losses: air leaking around blade tips → reduced efficiency → small tip clearances, abradable casing linings, shrouds. Blade bending/flapping (vibration) → snubbers/shrouds, mid-span clappers, stiffer blades."],["021.11.02.02:09","Compressor & diffuser: fill in the gaps → ____ = breakdown of airflow over (some) blades (α too high) → rumbling, vibration, EGT ↑, rpm fluctuation. ____ = complete flow breakdown/reversal through the whole compressor → ____, flames from inlet/exhaust, EGT ↑↑, N1/N2 fluctuations, thrust loss.","Compressor stall = breakdown of airflow over (some) blades (α too high) → rumbling, vibration, EGT ↑, rpm fluctuation. Surge = complete flow breakdown/reversal through the whole compressor → loud bang, flames from inlet/exhaust, EGT ↑↑, N1/N2 fluctuations, thrust loss."],["021.11.04.01:09","Compressor & diffuser: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (crosswind, high α, turbulence, ice/FOD, damaged blades), too rapid ____ (fuel ↑ faster than air) or deceleration, operation outside design rpm, bleed-air extraction, contamination.","Causes: airflow distortion (crosswind, high α, turbulence, ice/FOD, damaged blades), too rapid acceleration (fuel ↑ faster than air) or deceleration, operation outside design rpm, bleed-air extraction, contamination."],["021.11.04.01:09","Compressor & diffuser: fill in the gaps → ____: ____, ____ (blow-off at low rpm), multi-spool design, fuel control acceleration/deceleration schedules, good intake design.","Prevention: variable IGVs/stator vanes, bleed valves (blow-off at low rpm), multi-spool design, fuel control acceleration/deceleration schedules, good intake design."],["021.11.02.02:20","Compressor & diffuser: fill in the gaps → ____: pressure ratio vs mass flow, ____ ____, surge/stall line (limit), steady-state (working) line below it; the acceleration line lies closer to the surge line (fuel control limits) — surge margin.","Compressor map: pressure ratio vs mass flow, constant-rpm lines, surge/stall line (limit), steady-state (working) line below it; the acceleration line lies closer to the surge line (fuel control limits) — surge margin."],["021.11.02.02:21","Compressor & diffuser: fill in the gaps → ____ (between compressor and combustor): ____ → slows the air, raises static pressure for stable combustion.","Diffuser (between compressor and combustor): divergent → slows the air, raises static pressure for stable combustion."],["021.11.02.03:01","Combustion chamber: fill in the gaps → Purpose: ____ to the air by burning fuel with ____ ____, stable flame, complete combustion, even temperature distribution, low emissions, relight capability.","Purpose: add heat to the air by burning fuel with minimum pressure loss, stable flame, complete combustion, even temperature distribution, low emissions, relight capability."],["021.11.02.03:05","Combustion chamber: fill in the gaps → Principle: air enters the ____, where velocity is reduced (flame stability — flame speed is low); swirl vanes create turbulence/recirculation (mix fuel and air, anchor the flame). ____ mixes with fuel for combustion (____ in the primary zone);","Principle: air enters the snout, where velocity is reduced (flame stability — flame speed is low); swirl vanes create turbulence/recirculation (mix fuel and air, anchor the flame). Primary air (~20 %) mixes with fuel for combustion (~15:1 in the primary zone);"],["021.11.02.03:09","Combustion chamber: fill in the gaps → ____ cools the liner (film cooling) and dilutes the gas to acceptable turbine temperature → ____ (typ. 60:1).","secondary air (~80 %) cools the liner (film cooling) and dilutes the gas to acceptable turbine temperature → overall air/fuel ratio ~40–130:1 (typ. 60:1)."],["021.11.02.03:09","Combustion chamber: fill in the gaps → Gas changes: ____, velocity ↑, pressure ____. Max outlet temperature (TIT) ____ (modern, cooled blades).","Gas changes: temperature ↑↑, velocity ↑, pressure slightly ↓. Max outlet temperature (TIT) ~1 400–1 700 °C (modern, cooled blades)."],["021.11.04.02:01","Combustion chamber: fill in the gaps → ____ remove unburnt fuel after a failed (wet) start/shutdown → prevents fire/torching on next start.","Drain valves remove unburnt fuel after a failed (wet) start/shutdown → prevents fire/torching on next start."],["021.11.02.03:11","Combustion chamber: fill in the gaps → Types: ____ (separate cans, easy maintenance, heavy, uneven), ____ (cans in a common annulus with interconnectors), ____ (single ring — shortest, lightest, best temperature distribution, most efficient), reverse- flow annular (compact, helicopters/turboprops).","Types: can (separate cans, easy maintenance, heavy, uneven), can-annular (cannular, turbo-annular) (cans in a common annulus with interconnectors), annular (single ring — shortest, lightest, best temperature distribution, most efficient), reverse- flow annular (compact, helicopters/turboprops)."],["021.11.02.04:01","Turbine: fill in the gaps → Purpose: ____ from the hot gas to drive the compressor (and fan/propeller/rotor).","Purpose: extract energy from the hot gas to drive the compressor (and fan/propeller/rotor)."],["021.11.02.04:02","Turbine: fill in the gaps → ____ turbine: pressure drop entirely in the ____ ____, blades change direction only; reaction: pressure drop in the blades (act like aerofoils); modern: impulse-reaction (impulse at root, reaction at tip, with twist).","Impulse turbine: pressure drop entirely in the nozzle guide vanes (NGVs), blades change direction only; reaction: pressure drop in the blades (act like aerofoils); modern: impulse-reaction (impulse at root, reaction at tip, with twist)."],["021.11.02.04:09","Turbine: fill in the gaps → Stage: ____ accelerate & direct gas → ____ on a disc. Gas changes: ____, velocity ↑ in NGV then ↓ in rotor. Divergent annulus because gas expands (density ↓).","Stage: NGVs (stator) accelerate & direct gas → rotor blades on a disc. Gas changes: pressure and temperature ↓, velocity ↑ in NGV then ↓ in rotor. Divergent annulus because gas expands (density ↓)."],["021.11.02.04:06","Turbine: fill in the gaps → ____: cooling air (fan/compressor air) blown onto the turbine casing to ____ in cruise → smaller tip clearance → better efficiency, lower fuel burn. Tip losses minimised by ____, abradable seals, ACC.","Active clearance control: cooling air (fan/compressor air) blown onto the turbine casing to shrink it in cruise → smaller tip clearance → better efficiency, lower fuel burn. Tip losses minimised by shrouded blades, abradable seals, ACC."],["021.11.02.04:10","Turbine: fill in the gaps → ____ — the material limit of the first-stage blades (creep, oxidation). Very high ____ in blades/discs → cooled blades (internal air, film cooling), single-crystal alloys, thermal barrier coatings.","Thrust is limited by turbine inlet temperature (TIT) — the material limit of the first-stage blades (creep, oxidation). Very high thermal and centrifugal stress in blades/discs → cooled blades (internal air, film cooling), single-crystal alloys, thermal barrier coatings."],["021.11.02.05:01","Exhaust: fill in the gaps → ____ (ducts gas to the nozzle), ____ (____ → accelerates gas, converts pressure into velocity), exhaust cone (reduces turbulence behind the turbine, gives smooth flow). Gas: velocity ↑↑, pressure & temperature ↓.","Jet pipe (ducts gas to the nozzle), propelling nozzle (convergent → accelerates gas, converts pressure into velocity), exhaust cone (reduces turbulence behind the turbine, gives smooth flow). Gas: velocity ↑↑, pressure & temperature ↓."],["021.11.02.05:04","Exhaust: fill in the gaps → ____: gas reaches ____ at the exit (can't accelerate further) → ____ → pressure thrust.","Choked nozzle: gas reaches sonic speed at the exit (can't accelerate further) → exit pressure > ambient → pressure thrust."],["021.11.03.07:05","Exhaust: fill in the gaps → ____: high-bypass (lower jet velocity, cold stream shields hot), ____, ____, acoustic liners.","Noise reduction: high-bypass (lower jet velocity, cold stream shields hot), mixer/lobed nozzles, chevrons, acoustic liners."],["021.11.03.01:02","Engine systems: fill in the gaps → ____: ____, ____ (boost, prevents cavitation), fuel/oil heat exchanger (heats fuel to prevent icing, cools oil), HP pump (engine-driven — gear type or swash-plate (variable-stroke piston) type), fuel control unit (FCU), flow meter, spray nozzles, HP shut-off valve.","Fuel system: filters, LP pump (boost, prevents cavitation), fuel/oil heat exchanger (heats fuel to prevent icing, cools oil), HP pump (engine-driven — gear type or swash-plate (variable-stroke piston) type), fuel control unit (FCU), flow meter, spray nozzles, HP shut-off valve."],["021.11.03.01:03","Engine systems: fill in the gaps → FCU tasks: meter fuel for the selected thrust, ____, ____ (prevent surge, flame-out), starting fuel, relight. Inputs: ____ compressor inlet temperature/pressure, compressor delivery pressure, altitude/air data, EGT/TIT, bleed status.","FCU tasks: meter fuel for the selected thrust, limit rpm/temperature, acceleration/deceleration schedules (prevent surge, flame-out), starting fuel, relight. Inputs: power lever angle, N1/N2, compressor inlet temperature/pressure, compressor delivery pressure, altitude/air data, EGT/TIT, bleed status."],["021.11.03.02:02","Engine systems: fill in the gaps → ____: ____; ____ limited-authority electronic supervisor (EEC trims); single- channel FADEC + hydromechanical back-up; dual-channel FADEC with no back-up.","Engine control types: hydromechanical; hydromechanical + limited-authority electronic supervisor (EEC trims); single- channel FADEC + hydromechanical back-up; dual-channel FADEC with no back-up."],["021.11.03.02:07","Engine systems: fill in the gaps → ____: EEC unit (two channels A/B), wiring, own ____ (plus some aircraft data), controls fuel metering, ____, bleed valves, active clearance control, start/ignition, thrust reverser, limits protection. Redundancy: two independent channels, each can control the engine;","FADEC: EEC unit (two channels A/B), wiring, own sensors (plus some aircraft data), controls fuel metering, variable vanes, bleed valves, active clearance control, start/ignition, thrust reverser, limits protection. Redundancy: two independent channels, each can control the engine;"],["021.11.03.02:06","Engine systems: fill in the gaps → ____ ____; one channel active, other in standby; single input failure → uses the other channel's/alternative sensor or a synthesised value (possibly degraded mode).","all inputs/outputs cross-checked by both channels; one channel active, other in standby; single input failure → uses the other channel's/alternative sensor or a synthesised value (possibly degraded mode)."],["021.11.03.02:08","Engine systems: fill in the gaps → FADEC has its ____ (dedicated ____ on the gearbox) plus aircraft power for start.","FADEC has its own electrical power (dedicated permanent magnet alternator on the gearbox) plus aircraft power for start."],["021.11.03.03:01","Engine systems: fill in the gaps → ____: lubricate and ____, clean, protect against corrosion.","Lubrication: lubricate and cool bearings/gears, clean, protect against corrosion."],["021.11.03.03:02","Engine systems: fill in the gaps → ____ with ____ (separates oil from air), ____ and scavenge pumps (more capacity), filters with bypass (clogged → bypass opens → unfiltered oil, warning), sumps, magnetic chip detectors (metal particles → bearing/gear wear), fuel/oil and air/oil coolers.","Oil tank with centrifugal breather (separates oil from air), pressure pump and scavenge pumps (more capacity), filters with bypass (clogged → bypass opens → unfiltered oil, warning), sumps, magnetic chip detectors (metal particles → bearing/gear wear), fuel/oil and air/oil coolers."],["021.11.03.03:03","Engine systems: fill in the gaps → Each spool: at least ____ (axial/thrust load) and ____ (radial load, allow expansion). ____ pressurised with compressor air keep oil in the bearing chambers.","Each spool: at least one ball bearing (axial/thrust load) and two or more roller bearings (radial load, allow expansion). Labyrinth seals pressurised with compressor air keep oil in the bearing chambers."],["021.11.03.04:02","Engine systems: fill in the gaps → ____: drives ____ ____ starter; driven by a radial drive shaft from the HP spool; lubricated by the engine oil system.","Auxiliary (accessory) gearbox: drives fuel pumps, oil pumps, generator/IDG, hydraulic pump, FADEC alternator, starter; driven by a radial drive shaft from the HP spool; lubricated by the engine oil system."],["021.11.03.05:04","Engine systems: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ and provide ____ ignition when flame-out risk; power source (exciter/ignition unit — high-energy capacitor discharge from AC/DC) and igniter plugs. Two independent systems (redundancy, reliability). Modes: start (ground), continuous (take-off/landing, heavy rain, turbulence, icing), relight (flight); some high-/low-energy settings.","Ignition: ignite the mixture on start and provide continuous ignition when flame-out risk; power source (exciter/ignition unit — high-energy capacitor discharge from AC/DC) and igniter plugs. Two independent systems (redundancy, reliability). Modes: start (ground), continuous (take-off/landing, heavy rain, turbulence, icing), relight (flight); some high-/low-energy settings."],["021.11.03.06:03","Engine systems: fill in the gaps → ____: turns the HP spool to ____ with airflow; types: ____ (starter-generator, small engines/APUs) and pneumatic (air turbine starter) using APU/ground/cross-bleed air (large engines).","Starter: turns the HP spool to self-sustaining speed with airflow; types: electric (starter-generator, small engines/APUs) and pneumatic (air turbine starter) using APU/ground/cross-bleed air (large engines)."],["021.11.03.06:04","Engine systems: fill in the gaps → ____: start valve open → N2 rises → ____ on (~15–20 % N2) → ____ → light-up (EGT rise) → acceleration → starter cut-out (~50 % N2) → stabilise at ground idle; in flight: windmill start (sufficient speed) or starter-assisted.","Start sequence (turbofan): start valve open → N2 rises → ignition on (~15–20 % N2) → fuel on → light-up (EGT rise) → acceleration → starter cut-out (~50 % N2) → stabilise at ground idle; in flight: windmill start (sufficient speed) or starter-assisted."],["021.11.03.06:05","Engine systems: fill in the gaps → ____: rpm at which the engine can accelerate without starter assistance (compressor driven by turbine alone).","Self-sustaining rpm: rpm at which the engine can accelerate without starter assistance (compressor driven by turbine alone)."],["021.11.03.07:01","Engine systems: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (on thrust lever), ____ (hydraulic or pneumatic), actuators, doors/translating sleeve, annunciations (REV unlocked amber, deployed green). Principle: redirect exhaust/fan flow forwards → deceleration.","Reverse thrust: selector lever (on thrust lever), power source (hydraulic or pneumatic), actuators, doors/translating sleeve, annunciations (REV unlocked amber, deployed green). Principle: redirect exhaust/fan flow forwards → deceleration."],["021.11.03.07:04","Engine systems: fill in the gaps → Advantages: shorter landing roll, less brake wear, useful on contaminated runways; disadvantages: weight, complexity, FOD/re-ingestion at low speed, noise, risk of in-flight deployment. Types: ____ (clamshell/____ behind the nozzle), ____ (turbofans — blocker doors + cascade vanes in the fan duct).","Advantages: shorter landing roll, less brake wear, useful on contaminated runways; disadvantages: weight, complexity, FOD/re-ingestion at low speed, noise, risk of in-flight deployment. Types: hot-stream (clamshell/bucket doors behind the nozzle), cold-stream (turbofans — blocker doors + cascade vanes in the fan duct)."],["021.11.03.07:05","Engine systems: fill in the gaps → On high-bypass engines reversing only the ____ ____ gives most of the reverse thrust (core still gives some forward thrust).","On high-bypass engines reversing only the cold (fan) stream gives most of the reverse thrust (core still gives some forward thrust)."],["021.11.03.01:01","Engine systems: fill in the gaps → Protection: interlocks (____, thrust lever at idle, multiple locks, independent hydraulic isolation valve), auto-stow; flight-deck indications (REV amber/green, unlock warnings).","Protection: interlocks (weight on wheels, thrust lever at idle, multiple locks, independent hydraulic isolation valve), auto-stow; flight-deck indications (REV amber/green, unlock warnings)."],["021.11.04.01:01","Operation & monitoring: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (max, time-limited 5 min, 10 min OEI), ____ around, maximum continuous (MCT) (unlimited time, OEI use), maximum climb, (max cruise). Spool-up time: time from idle to high thrust (slower from ground idle) → approach (flight) idle higher than ground idle to ensure quick acceleration for go- around, anti-ice/bleed needs.","Ratings: take-off (max, time-limited 5 min, 10 min OEI), go- around, maximum continuous (MCT) (unlimited time, OEI use), maximum climb, (max cruise). Spool-up time: time from idle to high thrust (slower from ground idle) → approach (flight) idle higher than ground idle to ensure quick acceleration for go- around, anti-ice/bleed needs."],["021.11.04.01:05","Operation & monitoring: fill in the gaps → Thrust setting parameters: ____, ____, (turboprop: ____, prop rpm), monitored with EGT, N2, FF. Turboprop power lever: alpha range (flight: power lever controls fuel/power, governor controls blade angle), beta range (ground: lever controls blade angle directly — taxi, braking), reverse (negative blade angle).","Thrust setting parameters: N1, EPR, (turboprop: torque, prop rpm), monitored with EGT, N2, FF. Turboprop power lever: alpha range (flight: power lever controls fuel/power, governor controls blade angle), beta range (ground: lever controls blade angle directly — taxi, braking), reverse (negative blade angle)."],["021.11.04.01:06","Operation & monitoring: fill in the gaps → ____ → massive drag, loss of control, prop overspeed — protected by gates/beta lock-out.","Inadvertent beta in flight → massive drag, loss of control, prop overspeed — protected by gates/beta lock-out."],["021.11.04.01:02","Operation & monitoring: fill in the gaps → ____: recording of parameters (EGT margin, vibration, oil consumption) over time → detect deterioration early (maintenance planning).","Engine trending: recording of parameters (EGT margin, vibration, oil consumption) over time → detect deterioration early (maintenance planning)."],["021.11.04.01:09","Operation & monitoring: fill in the gaps → ____: measures turbine stress (temperature proportional to TIT). ____ → EGT ____ (more fuel before airflow increases); deceleration → EGT falls. Exceedance → creep, blade distortion, burning, cracks → must be reported (inspection, possibly engine removal).","EGT: measures turbine stress (temperature proportional to TIT). Acceleration → EGT rises (more fuel before airflow increases); deceleration → EGT falls. Exceedance → creep, blade distortion, burning, cracks → must be reported (inspection, possibly engine removal)."],["021.11.04.01:12","Operation & monitoring: fill in the gaps → ____: stow by ~____ — risk of ____ ingestion of hot gas/debris (compressor stall, FOD), directional control issues.","Thrust reverser at low speed: stow by ~60–80 kt — risk of re- ingestion of hot gas/debris (compressor stall, FOD), directional control issues."],["021.11.03.03:02","Operation & monitoring: fill in the gaps → ____: rotating parts stop due to ____ (oil loss/starvation), ____ (blade failure, rub), thermal distortion. Prevention: monitor oil pressure/temperature/quantity, chip detectors, vibration, obey limits, careful cool-down before shutdown.","Engine seizure: rotating parts stop due to bearing failure (oil loss/starvation), major mechanical damage (blade failure, rub), thermal distortion. Prevention: monitor oil pressure/temperature/quantity, chip detectors, vibration, obey limits, careful cool-down before shutdown."],["021.11.04.01:15","Operation & monitoring: fill in the gaps → ____: if ____ → oil leaks into fuel → burned in the combustor (oil loss, rough combustion, smoke); if ____ → fuel into oil → oil quantity rises, oil dilution, fire risk in hot bearing areas.","Fuel/oil heat exchanger leak: if oil pressure > fuel pressure → oil leaks into fuel → burned in the combustor (oil loss, rough combustion, smoke); if fuel pressure > oil pressure → fuel into oil → oil quantity rises, oil dilution, fire risk in hot bearing areas."],["021.11.04.01:16","Operation & monitoring: fill in the gaps → ____ → pressure drop across filter → ____ ____ (warning \"OIL FILTER CLOG\") → unfiltered oil → accelerated wear.","Oil-filter clogging → pressure drop across filter → bypass opens (warning \"OIL FILTER CLOG\") → unfiltered oil → accelerated wear."],["021.11.04.01:17","Operation & monitoring: fill in the gaps → Monitoring instruments: N1, N2 (N3), EPR, EGT/ITT, fuel flow, oil pressure/temperature/quantity, vibration, torque (turboprop). Damage assessment: e.g. ____ decrease → blade damage/bird strike; oil pressure ↓ + temperature ↑ → bearing/lubrication failure; ____ → seizure; ____ → deterioration/stall.","Monitoring instruments: N1, N2 (N3), EPR, EGT/ITT, fuel flow, oil pressure/temperature/quantity, vibration, torque (turboprop). Damage assessment: e.g. high vibration + EGT ↑ + N1 decrease → blade damage/bird strike; oil pressure ↓ + temperature ↑ → bearing/lubrication failure; N1/N2 zero → seizure; EGT ↑ with same N1 → deterioration/stall."],["021.11.04.02:01","Operation & monitoring: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ — ____ (no light-up, no fuel — fuel supply/valve) or wet (fuel but no ignition → EGT doesn't rise, fuel pools → torching risk); tailpipe fire (torching) (excess fuel ignites in the exhaust — motor the engine with starter to blow it out);","Start malfunctions: false start — dry (no light-up, no fuel — fuel supply/valve) or wet (fuel but no ignition → EGT doesn't rise, fuel pools → torching risk); tailpipe fire (torching) (excess fuel ignites in the exhaust — motor the engine with starter to blow it out);"],["021.11.03.06:02","Operation & monitoring: fill in the gaps → ____ (____ during start — too much fuel, weak starter/low air pressure, tailwind; abort); hung start (light-up but rpm stagnates below idle, EGT high — insufficient starter power/air, fuel control fault; abort); ____ (fan seized/blocked — abort);","hot start (EGT exceeds limit during start — too much fuel, weak starter/low air pressure, tailwind; abort); hung start (light-up but rpm stagnates below idle, EGT high — insufficient starter power/air, fuel control fault; abort); no N1 rotation (fan seized/blocked — abort);"],["021.11.04.02:02","Operation & monitoring: fill in the gaps → ____ (power/data failure — no start). Helicopters additionally: ____ ____ (engine can't drive the rotor or rotor drives engine).","no FADEC indications (power/data failure — no start). Helicopters additionally: freewheel (sprag clutch) failure (engine can't drive the rotor or rotor drives engine)."],["021.11.04.03:01","Operation & monitoring: fill in the gaps → ____: combinations of ____ (rpm from windmilling) within which an in-flight relight is possible — upper altitude limit (thin air), speed range (min for windmill rpm, max for flame stability); starter-assisted relight extends the lower speed limit.","Relight envelope: combinations of altitude and airspeed (rpm from windmilling) within which an in-flight relight is possible — upper altitude limit (thin air), speed range (min for windmill rpm, max for flame stability); starter-assisted relight extends the lower speed limit."],["021.11.05.01:01","Thrust & performance: fill in the gaps → ____: thrust ____ (density ↓) but less than density because of lower temperature; ____ (decreases) up to the tropopause (colder air), then roughly constant. TAS ↑ at constant altitude: thrust ~constant (ram effect compensates), SFC increases (fuel flow per thrust rises).","Altitude at constant TAS: thrust decreases (density ↓) but less than density because of lower temperature; SFC improves (decreases) up to the tropopause (colder air), then roughly constant. TAS ↑ at constant altitude: thrust ~constant (ram effect compensates), SFC increases (fuel flow per thrust rises)."],["021.11.05.01:03","Thrust & performance: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ ____ — below it the engine is thrust-limited (rpm/EPR increases as OAT rises, TIT rises); above it, it is temperature (TIT/EGT) limited → thrust decreases with increasing OAT.","Flat-rated engine: constant take-off thrust up to a flat- rating temperature (e.g. ISA + 15 °C/30 °C) — below it the engine is thrust-limited (rpm/EPR increases as OAT rises, TIT rises); above it, it is temperature (TIT/EGT) limited → thrust decreases with increasing OAT."],["021.11.02.05:03","Thrust & performance: fill in the gaps → ____ = turbine exhaust total pressure ÷ compressor inlet total pressure (Pt7/Pt2) — thrust-setting parameter.","EPR = turbine exhaust total pressure ÷ compressor inlet total pressure (Pt7/Pt2) — thrust-setting parameter."],["021.11.05.01:05","Thrust & performance: fill in the gaps → ____: FADEC told a higher OAT → less thrust (within ____ reduction); VMC unchanged (full thrust still available) → ____: engine life, reliability, lower EGT, cost; not on contaminated runways/with some MEL items.","Reduced (flexible/assumed temperature) thrust: FADEC told a higher OAT → less thrust (within ≤ 25 % reduction); VMC unchanged (full thrust still available) → advantages: engine life, reliability, lower EGT, cost; not on contaminated runways/with some MEL items."],["021.11.04.01:11","Thrust & performance: fill in the gaps → ____: a lower ____ (new, lower limit — VMC reduced → can improve field-limited mass on short/wet runways); thrust levers must not exceed the derate limit unless certified.","Derated thrust: a lower certified rating (new, lower limit — VMC reduced → can improve field-limited mass on short/wet runways); thrust levers must not exceed the derate limit unless certified."],["021.11.05.01:06","Thrust & performance: fill in the gaps → ____ (anti-ice, packs): at constant thrust → ____; at constant rpm → ____, EGT ↑.","Bleed air extraction (anti-ice, packs): at constant thrust → N/rpm & EGT ↑, SFC ↑; at constant rpm → thrust ↓, EGT ↑."],["021.11.03.06:04","APU: fill in the gaps → ____ (usually in the tail): provides ____ ____ (engine start, air conditioning on ground) and electrical power (generator) on the ground and in flight (back-up), and sometimes hydraulic power.","APU = small gas turbine (usually in the tail): provides bleed air (engine start, air conditioning on ground) and electrical power (generator) on the ground and in flight (back-up), and sometimes hydraulic power."],["021.11.06.01:03","APU: fill in the gaps → Inlets: ____ (low drag) vs ____ (scoop, opens for operation — better pressure recovery). ____ altitude (APU can run/supply) and max starting altitude (APU can be started — lower, due to thin air, battery/cold soak) — electrical load usually available higher than bleed.","Inlets: flush/NACA-type (low drag) vs ram/door-type (scoop, opens for operation — better pressure recovery). Max operating altitude (APU can run/supply) and max starting altitude (APU can be started — lower, due to thin air, battery/cold soak) — electrical load usually available higher than bleed."],["021.11.03.07:07","APU: fill in the gaps → Controls/monitoring: master switch/start, APU bleed switch, generator switch, fire handle/test; indications ____, oil quantity/pressure, door position, fault/fire lights.","Controls/monitoring: master switch/start, APU bleed switch, generator switch, fire handle/test; indications EGT, rpm (N%), oil quantity/pressure, door position, fault/fire lights."],["021.11.06.01:05","APU: fill in the gaps → ____: ____, ____, over- temperature (EGT), low oil pressure/high oil temperature, loss of rpm/underspeed, sensor or control failure, inlet door fault — shuts down automatically (on ground especially fire → auto shut & bottle discharge).","Automatic shutdown protection: fire, overspeed, over- temperature (EGT), low oil pressure/high oil temperature, loss of rpm/underspeed, sensor or control failure, inlet door fault — shuts down automatically (on ground especially fire → auto shut & bottle discharge)."],["021.11.02.02:06","Exam trap (APU): complete the fact → Centrifugal: ~____:____per stage, robust; axial: ~1.2:1 per stage.","Centrifugal: ~4:1 per stage, robust; axial: ~1.2:1 per stage."],["021.11.01.01:01","EASA LO 021.11.01.01.01 → Describe how thrust is produced by a basic gas turbine engine.","Propulsive efficiency = useful propulsive power (F × Va) ÷ kinetic energy added → high when jet velocity is close to flight speed (large mass, low velocity — props/high-bypass fans). Thermal efficiency = kinetic energy produced ÷ fuel energy — increases with compressor pressure ratio (and turbine inlet temperature). Total efficiency = thermal × propulsive."],["021.11.01.02:01","EASA LO 021.11.01.02.01 → List the main components of a basic gas turbine engine: — inlet; — compressor; — combustion chamber; — turbine; — outlet.","Components: inlet → compressor → combustion chamber → turbine → exhaust (nozzle). Working cycle (Brayton): compression (p ↑, T ↑), combustion at ~constant pressure (T ↑↑, V ↑), expansion in turbine (p ↓, T ↓), nozzle (V ↑↑). / Gas changes: temperature ↑↑, velocity ↑, pressure slightly ↓. Max outlet temperature (TIT) ~1 400–1 700 °C (modern, cooled blades)."],["021.11.01.02:02","EASA LO 021.11.01.02.02 → Describe the variation of static pressure, temperature and axial velocity in a gas turbine engine under normal operating conditions and with the aid of a working cycle diagram.","Components: inlet → compressor → combustion chamber → turbine → exhaust (nozzle). Working cycle (Brayton): compression (p ↑, T ↑), combustion at ~constant pressure (T ↑↑, V ↑), expansion in turbine (p ↓, T ↓), nozzle (V ↑↑). / Static pressure highest at compressor exit; temperature highest in the combustor; axial velocity roughly constant through compressor, rises in combustor/nozzle."],["021.11.01.02:05","EASA LO 021.11.01.02.05 → State that a gas turbine engine can have one or more spools.","Types: straight jet/turbojet, turbofan (fan + core, bypass flow), turboprop (turbine drives propeller via reduction gear; small residual jet thrust), turboshaft (helicopters). One or more spools (N1 low-pressure spool with fan, N2 high-pressure; three-spool RR). Turbojet/turbofan: thrust from jet/fan momentum; turboprop: power from the turbine to the propeller."],["021.11.01.02:06","EASA LO 021.11.01.02.06 → Describe how thrust is produced by turbojet and turbofan engines.","Types: straight jet/turbojet, turbofan (fan + core, bypass flow), turboprop (turbine drives propeller via reduction gear; small residual jet thrust), turboshaft (helicopters). One or more spools (N1 low-pressure spool with fan, N2 high-pressure; three-spool RR). Turbojet/turbofan: thrust from jet/fan momentum; turboprop: power from the turbine to the propeller. / Starter: turns the HP spool to self-sustaining speed with airflow; types: electric (starter-generator, small engines/APUs) and pneumatic (air turbine starter) using APU/ground/cross-bleed air (large engines)."],["021.11.01.02:07","EASA LO 021.11.01.02.07 → Describe how power is produced by turboprop engines.","Starter: turns the HP spool to self-sustaining speed with airflow; types: electric (starter-generator, small engines/APUs) and pneumatic (air turbine starter) using APU/ground/cross-bleed air (large engines). / Types: straight jet/turbojet, turbofan (fan + core, bypass flow), turboprop (turbine drives propeller via reduction gear; small residual jet thrust), turboshaft (helicopters). One or more spools (N1 low-pressure spool with fan, N2 high-pressure; three-spool RR). Turbojet/turbofan: thrust from jet/fan momentum; turboprop: power from the turbine to the propeller."],["021.11.01.02:09","EASA LO 021.11.01.02.09 → Explain the principle of a free turbine or free-power turbine.","Free-turbine/turboshaft (helicopters): compact design with reverse-flow annular combustor, centrifugal compressor stages, rear/side exhausts; components: gas generator (compressor, combustor, gas-producer turbine), free power turbine → output shaft/gearbox. EGT/TOT is used to monitor turbine stress."],["021.11.01.02:12","EASA LO 021.11.01.02.12 → Describe the influence of compressor-pressure ratio on thermal efficiency.","Propulsive efficiency = useful propulsive power (F × Va) ÷ kinetic energy added → high when jet velocity is close to flight speed (large mass, low velocity — props/high-bypass fans). Thermal efficiency = kinetic energy produced ÷ fuel energy — increases with compressor pressure ratio (and turbine inlet temperature). Total efficiency = thermal × propulsive."],["021.11.01.03:04","EASA LO 021.11.01.03.04 → Explain that when engine limits are exceeded, this event must be reported.","Coupled turbine engine (single shaft: compressor, turbine and propeller/rotor on one shaft): limits — max turbine (inlet/EGT) temperature (material strength/creep), torque of engine and drive train. Exceedances → creep, blade stretch, cracks, burning, bearing damage → reduced life/failure; every exceedance must be reported (inspection). / EGT: measures turbine stress (temperature proportional to TIT). Acceleration → EGT rises (more fuel before airflow increases); deceleration → EGT falls. Exceedance → creep, blade distortion, burning, cracks → must be reported (inspection, possibly engine removal)."],["021.11.01.04:01","EASA LO 021.11.01.04.01 → Describe the design methods to keep the engine’s size small for installation in helicopters.","Free-turbine/turboshaft (helicopters): compact design with reverse-flow annular combustor, centrifugal compressor stages, rear/side exhausts; components: gas generator (compressor, combustor, gas-producer turbine), free power turbine → output shaft/gearbox. EGT/TOT is used to monitor turbine stress."],["021.11.01.04:02","EASA LO 021.11.01.04.02 → List the main components of a free-turbine engine.","Free-turbine/turboshaft (helicopters): compact design with reverse-flow annular combustor, centrifugal compressor stages, rear/side exhausts; components: gas generator (compressor, combustor, gas-producer turbine), free power turbine → output shaft/gearbox. EGT/TOT is used to monitor turbine stress."],["021.11.01.04:03","EASA LO 021.11.01.04.03 → Describe how the power is developed by a turboshaft/free-turbine engine.","Free-turbine/turboshaft (helicopters): compact design with reverse-flow annular combustor, centrifugal compressor stages, rear/side exhausts; components: gas generator (compressor, combustor, gas-producer turbine), free power turbine → output shaft/gearbox. EGT/TOT is used to monitor turbine stress."],["021.11.02.01:03","EASA LO 021.11.02.01.03 → Explain the gas-parameter changes in a subsonic air inlet at different flight speeds.","Propulsive efficiency vs speed: turboprop best at low speed (< ~M 0.6), turbofan at medium/high subsonic, turbojet only at high/supersonic speeds. / At low speed/take-off the intake accelerates air in (pressure below ambient at the lip); at high speed, ram compression."],["021.11.02.02:01","EASA LO 021.11.02.02.01 → State the purpose of the compressor. X X X X X Exam BK Remarks","Purpose: raise the air pressure (and mass flow) for efficient combustion. / Purpose: extract energy from the hot gas to drive the compressor (and fan/propeller/rotor)."],["021.11.02.02:02","EASA LO 021.11.02.02.02 → Describe the working principle of a centrifugal and an axial flow compressor.","Centrifugal: ~4:1 per stage, robust; axial: ~1.2:1 per stage. / Purpose: raise the air pressure (and mass flow) for efficient combustion."],["021.11.02.02:05","EASA LO 021.11.02.02.05 → Describe the gas-parameter changes in a compressor stage.","Stage: NGVs (stator) accelerate & direct gas → rotor blades on a disc. Gas changes: pressure and temperature ↓, velocity ↑ in NGV then ↓ in rotor. Divergent annulus because gas expands (density ↓). / EPR = turbine exhaust total pressure ÷ compressor inlet total pressure (Pt7/Pt2) — thrust-setting parameter."],["021.11.02.02:07","EASA LO 021.11.02.02.07 → State the advantages and disadvantages of increasing the number of stages in a centrifugal compressor.","Free-turbine/turboshaft (helicopters): compact design with reverse-flow annular combustor, centrifugal compressor stages, rear/side exhausts; components: gas generator (compressor, combustor, gas-producer turbine), free power turbine → output shaft/gearbox. EGT/TOT is used to monitor turbine stress."],["021.11.02.02:08","EASA LO 021.11.02.02.08 → Explain the difference in sensitivity for FOD of a centrifugal compressor compared with an axial flow type.","Centrifugal: ~4:1 per stage, robust; axial: ~1.2:1 per stage. / Axial: rotor blades (add energy: velocity & pressure ↑) + stator vanes (diffuse: velocity ↓, pressure ↑, redirect flow). Pressure ratio per stage ~1.1–1.3:1; total compressor ~30–50:1 (many stages). More FOD-sensitive (thin blades) but efficient, small frontal area, high mass flow."],["021.11.02.02:11","EASA LO 021.11.02.02.11 → State the tasks of inlet guide vanes (IGVs).","Convergent annulus through the axial compressor: keeps axial velocity constant as density increases. Blade twist: blade speed increases with radius → twist keeps the angle of attack constant along the blade. IGVs: guide air at the correct angle onto the first rotor stage (variable IGVs/stators prevent stall at low rpm). / Prevention: variable IGVs/stator vanes, bleed valves (blow-off at low rpm), multi-spool design, fuel control acceleration/deceleration schedules, good intake design."],["021.11.02.02:14","EASA LO 021.11.02.02.14 → Explain the implications of tip losses and describe the design features to minimise the problem.","Active clearance control: cooling air (fan/compressor air) blown onto the turbine casing to shrink it in cruise → smaller tip clearance → better efficiency, lower fuel burn. Tip losses minimised by shrouded blades, abradable seals, ACC."],["021.11.02.02:16","EASA LO 021.11.02.02.16 → Explain the following terms: — compressor stall; — engine surge.","Compressor map: pressure ratio vs mass flow, constant-rpm lines, surge/stall line (limit), steady-state (working) line below it; the acceleration line lies closer to the surge line (fuel control limits) — surge margin. / Compressor stall = breakdown of airflow over (some) blades (α too high) → rumbling, vibration, EGT ↑, rpm fluctuation. Surge = complete flow breakdown/reversal through the whole compressor → loud bang, flames from inlet/exhaust, EGT ↑↑, N1/N2 fluctuations, thrust loss."],["021.11.02.02:17","EASA LO 021.11.02.02.17 → State the conditions that are possible causes of stall and surge.","Compressor map: pressure ratio vs mass flow, constant-rpm lines, surge/stall line (limit), steady-state (working) line below it; the acceleration line lies closer to the surge line (fuel control limits) — surge margin."],["021.11.02.02:18","EASA LO 021.11.02.02.18 → Describe the indications of stall and surge.","Compressor map: pressure ratio vs mass flow, constant-rpm lines, surge/stall line (limit), steady-state (working) line below it; the acceleration line lies closer to the surge line (fuel control limits) — surge margin. / Compressor stall = breakdown of airflow over (some) blades (α too high) → rumbling, vibration, EGT ↑, rpm fluctuation. Surge = complete flow breakdown/reversal through the whole compressor → loud bang, flames from inlet/exhaust, EGT ↑↑, N1/N2 fluctuations, thrust loss."],["021.11.02.02:19","EASA LO 021.11.02.02.19 → Describe the design features used to minimise the occurrence of stall and surge.","Compressor map: pressure ratio vs mass flow, constant-rpm lines, surge/stall line (limit), steady-state (working) line below it; the acceleration line lies closer to the surge line (fuel control limits) — surge margin."],["021.11.02.03:02","EASA LO 021.11.02.03.02 → List the requirements for combustion.","Components: inlet → compressor → combustion chamber → turbine → exhaust (nozzle). Working cycle (Brayton): compression (p ↑, T ↑), combustion at ~constant pressure (T ↑↑, V ↑), expansion in turbine (p ↓, T ↓), nozzle (V ↑↑). / Purpose: raise the air pressure (and mass flow) for efficient combustion."],["021.11.02.03:04","EASA LO 021.11.02.03.04 → Explain the reason for reducing the airflow axial velocity at the combustion chamber inlet (snout).","Principle: air enters the snout, where velocity is reduced (flame stability — flame speed is low); swirl vanes create turbulence/recirculation (mix fuel and air, anchor the flame). Primary air (~20 %) mixes with fuel for combustion (~15:1 in the primary zone); / Components: inlet → compressor → combustion chamber → turbine → exhaust (nozzle). Working cycle (Brayton): compression (p ↑, T ↑), combustion at ~constant pressure (T ↑↑, V ↑), expansion in turbine (p ↓, T ↓), nozzle (V ↑↑)."],["021.11.02.03:06","EASA LO 021.11.02.03.06 → State the function of the drain valves. X X X X X Exam BK Remarks","Drain valves remove unburnt fuel after a failed (wet) start/shutdown → prevents fire/torching on next start."],["021.11.02.03:07","EASA LO 021.11.02.03.07 → Define the terms ‘primary airflow’ and ‘secondary airflow’, and explain their purpose.","Purpose: raise the air pressure (and mass flow) for efficient combustion. / Principle: air enters the snout, where velocity is reduced (flame stability — flame speed is low); swirl vanes create turbulence/recirculation (mix fuel and air, anchor the flame). Primary air (~20 %) mixes with fuel for combustion (~15:1 in the primary zone);"],["021.11.02.03:08","EASA LO 021.11.02.03.08 → Explain the following two mixture ratios: — primary airflow to fuel; — total airflow (within the combustion chamber) to fuel.","Principle: air enters the snout, where velocity is reduced (flame stability — flame speed is low); swirl vanes create turbulence/recirculation (mix fuel and air, anchor the flame). Primary air (~20 %) mixes with fuel for combustion (~15:1 in the primary zone);"],["021.11.02.03:10","EASA LO 021.11.02.03.10 → State a typical maximum value of the outlet temperature of the combustion chamber.","Gas changes: temperature ↑↑, velocity ↑, pressure slightly ↓. Max outlet temperature (TIT) ~1 400–1 700 °C (modern, cooled blades). / Components: inlet → compressor → combustion chamber → turbine → exhaust (nozzle). Working cycle (Brayton): compression (p ↑, T ↑), combustion at ~constant pressure (T ↑↑, V ↑), expansion in turbine (p ↓, T ↓), nozzle (V ↑↑)."],["021.11.02.04:03","EASA LO 021.11.02.04.03 → Name the main components of a turbine stage and their function.","Centrifugal: ~4:1 per stage, robust; axial: ~1.2:1 per stage."],["021.11.02.04:04","EASA LO 021.11.02.04.04 → Describe the working principle of a turbine.","Components: inlet → compressor → combustion chamber → turbine → exhaust (nozzle). Working cycle (Brayton): compression (p ↑, T ↑), combustion at ~constant pressure (T ↑↑, V ↑), expansion in turbine (p ↓, T ↓), nozzle (V ↑↑)."],["021.11.02.04:05","EASA LO 021.11.02.04.05 → Describe the gas-parameter changes in a turbine stage.","Stage: NGVs (stator) accelerate & direct gas → rotor blades on a disc. Gas changes: pressure and temperature ↓, velocity ↑ in NGV then ↓ in rotor. Divergent annulus because gas expands (density ↓). / EPR = turbine exhaust total pressure ÷ compressor inlet total pressure (Pt7/Pt2) — thrust-setting parameter."],["021.11.02.04:07","EASA LO 021.11.02.04.07 → Describe the implications of tip losses and the means to minimise them.","Active clearance control: cooling air (fan/compressor air) blown onto the turbine casing to shrink it in cruise → smaller tip clearance → better efficiency, lower fuel burn. Tip losses minimised by shrouded blades, abradable seals, ACC."],["021.11.02.04:08","EASA LO 021.11.02.04.08 → Explain why the available engine thrust is limited by the turbine inlet temperature.","Flat-rated engine: constant take-off thrust up to a flat- rating temperature (e.g. ISA + 15 °C/30 °C) — below it the engine is thrust-limited (rpm/EPR increases as OAT rises, TIT rises); above it, it is temperature (TIT/EGT) limited → thrust decreases with increasing OAT. / Reduced (flexible/assumed temperature) thrust: FADEC told a higher OAT → less thrust (within ≤ 25 % reduction); VMC unchanged (full thrust still available) → advantages: engine life, reliability, lower EGT, cost; not on contaminated runways/with some MEL items."],["021.11.02.05:02","EASA LO 021.11.02.05.02 → Describe the working principle of the exhaust unit.","Components: inlet → compressor → combustion chamber → turbine → exhaust (nozzle). Working cycle (Brayton): compression (p ↑, T ↑), combustion at ~constant pressure (T ↑↑, V ↑), expansion in turbine (p ↓, T ↓), nozzle (V ↑↑). / Reverse thrust: selector lever (on thrust lever), power source (hydraulic or pneumatic), actuators, doors/translating sleeve, annunciations (REV unlocked amber, deployed green). Principle: redirect exhaust/fan flow forwards → deceleration."],["021.11.02.05:05","EASA LO 021.11.02.05.05 → Explain how jet exhaust noise can be reduced.","Noise reduction: high-bypass (lower jet velocity, cold stream shields hot), mixer/lobed nozzles, chevrons, acoustic liners. / Choked nozzle: gas reaches sonic speed at the exit (can't accelerate further) → exit pressure > ambient → pressure thrust."],["021.11.03.01:01","EASA LO 021.11.03.01.01 → Name the main components of the engine fuel system and state their function: — filters; — low-pressure (LP) pump; — high-pressure (HP) pump;","Fuel system: filters, LP pump (boost, prevents cavitation), fuel/oil heat exchanger (heats fuel to prevent icing, cools oil), HP pump (engine-driven — gear type or swash-plate (variable-stroke piston) type), fuel control unit (FCU), flow meter, spray nozzles, HP shut-off valve. / Auxiliary (accessory) gearbox: drives fuel pumps, oil pumps, generator/IDG, hydraulic pump, FADEC alternator, starter; driven by a radial drive shaft from the HP spool; lubricated by the engine oil system."],["021.11.03.01:04","EASA LO 021.11.03.01.04 → List the possible input parameters to a fuel control unit to achieve a given thrust/power setting.","Thrust setting parameters: N1, EPR, (turboprop: torque, prop rpm), monitored with EGT, N2, FF. Turboprop power lever: alpha range (flight: power lever controls fuel/power, governor controls blade angle), beta range (ground: lever controls blade angle directly — taxi, braking), reverse (negative blade angle). / SFC: turbojet = fuel flow per unit thrust (kg/N/h); turboprop = fuel flow per unit power (kg/kW/h, per ESHP)."],["021.11.03.02:03","EASA LO 021.11.03.02.03 → Describe a FADEC as a full-authority dual-channel system including functions such as an electronic engine control unit, wiring, sensors, variable vanes,","Engine control types: hydromechanical; hydromechanical + limited-authority electronic supervisor (EEC trims); single- channel FADEC + hydromechanical back-up; dual-channel FADEC with no back-up. / FADEC: EEC unit (two channels A/B), wiring, own sensors (plus some aircraft data), controls fuel metering, variable vanes, bleed valves, active clearance control, start/ignition, thrust reverser, limits protection. Redundancy: two independent channels, each can control the engine;"],["021.11.03.02:04","EASA LO 021.11.03.02.04 → Explain how redundancy is achieved by using more than one channel in a FADEC system.","Engine control types: hydromechanical; hydromechanical + limited-authority electronic supervisor (EEC trims); single- channel FADEC + hydromechanical back-up; dual-channel FADEC with no back-up. / all inputs/outputs cross-checked by both channels; one channel active, other in standby; single input failure → uses the other channel's/alternative sensor or a synthesised value (possibly degraded mode)."],["021.11.03.02:05","EASA LO 021.11.03.02.05 → State the consequences of a FADEC single input data failure.","no FADEC indications (power/data failure — no start). Helicopters additionally: freewheel (sprag clutch) failure (engine can't drive the rotor or rotor drives engine). / all inputs/outputs cross-checked by both channels; one channel active, other in standby; single input failure → uses the other channel's/alternative sensor or a synthesised value (possibly degraded mode)."],["021.11.03.03:04","EASA LO 021.11.03.03.04 → Explain the use of compressor air in oil-sealing systems (e.g. labyrinth seals).","Each spool: at least one ball bearing (axial/thrust load) and two or more roller bearings (radial load, allow expansion). Labyrinth seals pressurised with compressor air keep oil in the bearing chambers."],["021.11.03.04:01","EASA LO 021.11.03.04.01 → State the tasks of the auxiliary gearbox.","Auxiliary (accessory) gearbox: drives fuel pumps, oil pumps, generator/IDG, hydraulic pump, FADEC alternator, starter; driven by a radial drive shaft from the HP spool; lubricated by the engine oil system. / FADEC has its own electrical power (dedicated permanent magnet alternator on the gearbox) plus aircraft power for start."],["021.11.03.05:01","EASA LO 021.11.03.05.01 → State the task of the ignition system.","Ignition: ignite the mixture on start and provide continuous ignition when flame-out risk; power source (exciter/ignition unit — high-energy capacitor discharge from AC/DC) and igniter plugs. Two independent systems (redundancy, reliability). Modes: start (ground), continuous (take-off/landing, heavy rain, turbulence, icing), relight (flight); some high-/low-energy settings."],["021.11.03.05:03","EASA LO 021.11.03.05.03 → State why jet turbine engines are equipped with two electrically independent ignition systems.","FADEC: EEC unit (two channels A/B), wiring, own sensors (plus some aircraft data), controls fuel metering, variable vanes, bleed valves, active clearance control, start/ignition, thrust reverser, limits protection. Redundancy: two independent channels, each can control the engine; / Ignition: ignite the mixture on start and provide continuous ignition when flame-out risk; power source (exciter/ignition unit — high-energy capacitor discharge from AC/DC) and igniter plugs. Two independent systems (redundancy, reliability). Modes: start (ground), continuous (take-off/landing, heavy rain, turbulence, icing), relight (flight); some high-/low-energy settings."],["021.11.03.06:01","EASA LO 021.11.03.06.01 → Name the main components of the starting system and state their function.","Auxiliary (accessory) gearbox: drives fuel pumps, oil pumps, generator/IDG, hydraulic pump, FADEC alternator, starter; driven by a radial drive shaft from the HP spool; lubricated by the engine oil system."],["021.11.03.07:02","EASA LO 021.11.03.07.02 → Explain the principle of a reverse-thrust system.","On high-bypass engines reversing only the cold (fan) stream gives most of the reverse thrust (core still gives some forward thrust). / Reverse thrust: selector lever (on thrust lever), power source (hydraulic or pneumatic), actuators, doors/translating sleeve, annunciations (REV unlocked amber, deployed green). Principle: redirect exhaust/fan flow forwards → deceleration."],["021.11.03.07:03","EASA LO 021.11.03.07.03 → Identify the advantages and disadvantages of using reverse thrust.","On high-bypass engines reversing only the cold (fan) stream gives most of the reverse thrust (core still gives some forward thrust). / Advantages: shorter landing roll, less brake wear, useful on contaminated runways; disadvantages: weight, complexity, FOD/re-ingestion at low speed, noise, risk of in-flight deployment. Types: hot-stream (clamshell/bucket doors behind the nozzle), cold-stream (turbofans — blocker doors + cascade vanes in the fan duct)."],["021.11.03.07:06","EASA LO 021.11.03.07.06 → Describe the protection features against inadvertent thrust-reverse deployment in flight as present on most transport aeroplanes.","On high-bypass engines reversing only the cold (fan) stream gives most of the reverse thrust (core still gives some forward thrust)."],["021.11.04.01:03","EASA LO 021.11.04.01.03 → Explain the reason for the difference between ground and approach flight idle values (rpm).","Ratings: take-off (max, time-limited 5 min, 10 min OEI), go- around, maximum continuous (MCT) (unlimited time, OEI use), maximum climb, (max cruise). Spool-up time: time from idle to high thrust (slower from ground idle) → approach (flight) idle higher than ground idle to ensure quick acceleration for go- around, anti-ice/bleed needs."],["021.11.04.01:04","EASA LO 021.11.04.01.04 → State the parameters that can be used for setting and monitoring the thrust/power.","Thrust setting parameters: N1, EPR, (turboprop: torque, prop rpm), monitored with EGT, N2, FF. Turboprop power lever: alpha range (flight: power lever controls fuel/power, governor controls blade angle), beta range (ground: lever controls blade angle directly — taxi, braking), reverse (negative blade angle). / EPR = turbine exhaust total pressure ÷ compressor inlet total pressure (Pt7/Pt2) — thrust-setting parameter."],["021.11.04.01:07","EASA LO 021.11.04.01.07 → Explain the purpose of engine trending.","Purpose: raise the air pressure (and mass flow) for efficient combustion. / Purpose: extract energy from the hot gas to drive the compressor (and fan/propeller/rotor)."],["021.11.04.01:08","EASA LO 021.11.04.01.08 → Explain how the exhaust gas temperature is used to monitor turbine stress.","Free-turbine/turboshaft (helicopters): compact design with reverse-flow annular combustor, centrifugal compressor stages, rear/side exhausts; components: gas generator (compressor, combustor, gas-producer turbine), free power turbine → output shaft/gearbox. EGT/TOT is used to monitor turbine stress. / Jet pipe (ducts gas to the nozzle), propelling nozzle (convergent → accelerates gas, converts pressure into velocity), exhaust cone (reduces turbulence behind the turbine, gives smooth flow). Gas: velocity ↑↑, pressure & temperature ↓."],["021.11.05.01:02","EASA LO 021.11.05.01.02 → Describe the variation of thrust and specific fuel consumption with TAS at constant altitude.","Altitude at constant TAS: thrust decreases (density ↓) but less than density because of lower temperature; SFC improves (decreases) up to the tropopause (colder air), then roughly constant. TAS ↑ at constant altitude: thrust ~constant (ram effect compensates), SFC increases (fuel flow per thrust rises). / Bleed air extraction (anti-ice, packs): at constant thrust → N/rpm & EGT ↑, SFC ↑; at constant rpm → thrust ↓, EGT ↑."],["021.11.05.01:04","EASA LO 021.11.05.01.04 → Define the term ‘engine pressure ratio’ (EPR).","EPR = turbine exhaust total pressure ÷ compressor inlet total pressure (Pt7/Pt2) — thrust-setting parameter. / Flat-rated engine: constant take-off thrust up to a flat- rating temperature (e.g. ISA + 15 °C/30 °C) — below it the engine is thrust-limited (rpm/EPR increases as OAT rises, TIT rises); above it, it is temperature (TIT/EGT) limited → thrust decreases with increasing OAT."],["021.11.06.01:02","EASA LO 021.11.06.01.02 → State the difference between the two types of APU inlets.","Inlets: flush/NACA-type (low drag) vs ram/door-type (scoop, opens for operation — better pressure recovery). Max operating altitude (APU can run/supply) and max starting altitude (APU can be started — lower, due to thin air, battery/cold soak) — electrical load usually available higher than bleed."],["021.11.06.01:04","EASA LO 021.11.06.01.04 → Name the typical APU control and monitoring instruments.","Monitoring instruments: N1, N2 (N3), EPR, EGT/ITT, fuel flow, oil pressure/temperature/quantity, vibration, torque (turboprop). Damage assessment: e.g. high vibration + EGT ↑ + N1 decrease → blade damage/bird strike; oil pressure ↓ + temperature ↑ → bearing/lubrication failure; N1/N2 zero → seizure; EGT ↑ with same N1 → deterioration/stall."]],"99":[["021.09.00.00",": what do you remember about this? → FINAL review of 021.09 Electrics & Electronics — cheat …","FINAL review of 021.09 Electrics & Electronics — cheat sheet."],["021.09.00.00","DC basics: fill in the gaps → ____ U = R × I; ____ P = U × I = I²R = U²/R. ____: Rtotal = R₁ + R₂…, same current; parallel: 1/Rtotal = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂…, same voltage. Kirchhoff: Σ currents at a node = 0; Σ voltages around a loop = 0. Resistance ↑ with length, ↓ with cross-section; metals: R ↑ with temperature.","Ohm's law U = R × I; power P = U × I = I²R = U²/R. Series: Rtotal = R₁ + R₂…, same current; parallel: 1/Rtotal = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂…, same voltage. Kirchhoff: Σ currents at a node = 0; Σ voltages around a loop = 0. Resistance ↑ with length, ↓ with cross-section; metals: R ↑ with temperature."],["021.09.00.00","DC basics: fill in the gaps → Aircraft DC: ____ (large, 24 V batteries) or 14 V (light aircraft). ____ — structure as return path (bonding). ____ = electrically connecting all metal parts (static discharge, lightning, return path, radio noise); static dischargers (wicks) on trailing edges; screening of cables against interference.","Aircraft DC: 28 V (large, 24 V batteries) or 14 V (light aircraft). Single-wire (earth return) system — structure as return path (bonding). Bonding = electrically connecting all metal parts (static discharge, lightning, return path, radio noise); static dischargers (wicks) on trailing edges; screening of cables against interference."],["021.09.00.00","DC basics: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (melt — one-time; spare fuses 10 %/3), ____ (thermal/magnetic, trip-free — can't be held closed against a fault), reverse-current cut-out (disconnects the generator from the battery when generator voltage < battery), current limiters. Reset a CB only once, after cooling (per procedures).","Circuit protection: fuses (melt — one-time; spare fuses 10 %/3), circuit breakers (thermal/magnetic, trip-free — can't be held closed against a fault), reverse-current cut-out (disconnects the generator from the battery when generator voltage < battery), current limiters. Reset a CB only once, after cooling (per procedures)."],["021.09.00.00","DC basics: fill in the gaps → ____: relay = electromagnetically operated switch (small current controls large current); ____ for high currents (starter). Toggle, rocker, micro-switches, proximity sensors.","Switches & relays: relay = electromagnetically operated switch (small current controls large current); solenoid for high currents (starter). Toggle, rocker, micro-switches, proximity sensors."],["021.09.00.00","Batteries: fill in the gaps → ____ (2 V/cell, 12 cells = 24 V; electrolyte sulphuric acid; state of charge by ____, voltage) and ____ cadmium (NiCd) (1.2 V/cell, 19–20 cells; constant voltage over discharge, can deliver high currents, thermal runaway risk → temperature monitoring; state of charge not by SG).","Lead-acid (2 V/cell, 12 cells = 24 V; electrolyte sulphuric acid; state of charge by specific gravity, voltage) and nickel- cadmium (NiCd) (1.2 V/cell, 19–20 cells; constant voltage over discharge, can deliver high currents, thermal runaway risk → temperature monitoring; state of charge not by SG)."],["021.09.00.00","Batteries: fill in the gaps → ____ (high energy density, thermal-runaway containment). ____ in ____ (e.g. 40 Ah = 4 A for 10 h). Series connection → voltage adds, parallel → capacity adds. Battery provides start power and emergency supply (typ. 30 min for essential loads, CS-25 requirement), charged by generators.","Lithium-ion (high energy density, thermal-runaway containment). Capacity in ampere-hours (Ah) (e.g. 40 Ah = 4 A for 10 h). Series connection → voltage adds, parallel → capacity adds. Battery provides start power and emergency supply (typ. 30 min for essential loads, CS-25 requirement), charged by generators."],["021.09.00.00","Magnetism & generation: fill in the gaps → Current-carrying conductor → magnetic field (right-hand rule); ____; ____ (Faraday): voltage induced when a conductor cuts a magnetic field (generator); ____: induced current opposes the change; motor principle (force on conductor in a field — left-hand rule).","Current-carrying conductor → magnetic field (right-hand rule); electromagnet; induction (Faraday): voltage induced when a conductor cuts a magnetic field (generator); Lenz: induced current opposes the change; motor principle (force on conductor in a field — left-hand rule)."],["021.09.00.00","Magnetism & generation: fill in the gaps → ____ (commutator, brushes), ____ (light turbines); ____ (AC generated, rectified to DC in light aircraft). Voltage regulator controls field current to keep output voltage constant with varying rpm and load; overvoltage protection; paralleling with load sharing (equalising circuits).","DC generator (commutator, brushes), starter-generator (light turbines); alternator (AC generated, rectified to DC in light aircraft). Voltage regulator controls field current to keep output voltage constant with varying rpm and load; overvoltage protection; paralleling with load sharing (equalising circuits)."],["021.09.00.00","Magnetism & generation: fill in the gaps → Light aircraft: alternator 28 V, ____ (shows charge/discharge) or loadmeter, alternator failure → battery only (load shedding).","Light aircraft: alternator 28 V, ammeter (shows charge/discharge) or loadmeter, alternator failure → battery only (load shedding)."],["021.09.00.00","AC systems: fill in the gaps → ____ (large aircraft): 400 Hz → lighter generators/transformers. ____ f = rpm × pole pairs / 60 → requires ____: CSD (constant speed drive) or IDG (integrated drive generator = CSD + generator), or VSCF (variable speed constant frequency), variable-frequency systems (A380/B787, 360–800 Hz).","Three-phase 115/200 V, 400 Hz (large aircraft): 400 Hz → lighter generators/transformers. Frequency f = rpm × pole pairs / 60 → requires constant generator speed: CSD (constant speed drive) or IDG (integrated drive generator = CSD + generator), or VSCF (variable speed constant frequency), variable-frequency systems (A380/B787, 360–800 Hz)."],["021.09.00.00","AC systems: fill in the gaps → ____: permanent magnet generator → exciter → rotating rectifier → main field (no brushes). ____ requires same voltage, frequency and phase sequence; ____ (kW) shared by speed/torque control, reactive load (kVAR) by field excitation. Split-bus vs parallel systems.","Brushless AC generator: permanent magnet generator → exciter → rotating rectifier → main field (no brushes). Paralleling requires same voltage, frequency and phase sequence; real load (kW) shared by speed/torque control, reactive load (kVAR) by field excitation. Split-bus vs parallel systems."],["021.09.00.00","AC systems: fill in the gaps → Protection: ____ (generator control unit) — over/under-voltage, over/under-frequency, differential protection (feeder faults), ____ ____ (mechanical, on high oil temperature/low oil pressure — reconnect only on the ground).","Protection: GCU (generator control unit) — over/under-voltage, over/under-frequency, differential protection (feeder faults), IDG disconnect (mechanical, on high oil temperature/low oil pressure — reconnect only on the ground)."],["021.09.00.00","AC systems: fill in the gaps → ____, apparent power kVA = √(kW² + kVAR²). Transformer (changes AC voltage; ratio of turns), TRU (transformer-rectifier unit: ____), static inverter (DC → ____, emergency from battery), RAT (ram-air turbine) emergency generator, APU generator, external power (ground power unit, 115 V 400 Hz).","Power factor, apparent power kVA = √(kW² + kVAR²). Transformer (changes AC voltage; ratio of turns), TRU (transformer-rectifier unit: AC → DC 28 V), static inverter (DC → AC 115 V, emergency from battery), RAT (ram-air turbine) emergency generator, APU generator, external power (ground power unit, 115 V 400 Hz)."],["021.09.00.00","AC systems: fill in the gaps → ____: generator buses, ____, ____ (always powered), ground handling/service bus, load shedding (galleys shed first). Priorities: external/APU vs engine generators, bus-tie contactors (BTC).","Bus bars: generator buses, essential/emergency buses, battery bus/hot battery bus (always powered), ground handling/service bus, load shedding (galleys shed first). Priorities: external/APU vs engine generators, bus-tie contactors (BTC)."],["021.09.00.00","Electrical loads & electronics: fill in the gaps → ____ (DC series-wound — high starting torque (starters); shunt — constant speed), ____ (pumps, fans), ____ motors, synchros (position transmission: e.g. flap/trim indicators), actuators.","Motors (DC series-wound — high starting torque (starters); shunt — constant speed), AC induction motors (pumps, fans), servo motors, synchros (position transmission: e.g. flap/trim indicators), actuators."],["021.09.00.00","Electrical loads & electronics: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (current one way — rectifiers, protection), ____ (voltage reference/regulation), transistor (amplifier/switch), thyristor, LED, photo-diode, rectifiers (half-wave, full-wave bridge).","Semiconductors: diode (current one way — rectifiers, protection), Zener diode (voltage reference/regulation), transistor (amplifier/switch), thyristor, LED, photo-diode, rectifiers (half-wave, full-wave bridge)."],["021.09.00.00","Electrical loads & electronics: fill in the gaps → ____: ____ (output 1 only if all inputs 1), ____ (1 if any input 1), NOT (inverts), NAND, NOR, XOR (1 if inputs differ); truth tables; used in warnings (e.g. take-off config warning = thrust levers advanced AND flaps not set) and squat-switch logic.","Logic circuits: AND (output 1 only if all inputs 1), OR (1 if any input 1), NOT (inverts), NAND, NOR, XOR (1 if inputs differ); truth tables; used in warnings (e.g. take-off config warning = thrust levers advanced AND flaps not set) and squat-switch logic."],["021.09.00.00","Electrical loads & electronics: fill in the gaps → ____ (ARINC 429 one-way, AFDX), EMC/EMI, lightning protection, ____ protection.","Data buses (ARINC 429 one-way, AFDX), EMC/EMI, lightning protection, HIRF protection."],["021.09.00.00","Typical failure handling: fill in the gaps → ____: warning light, ammeter discharge → reset once, ____, land at the nearest suitable aerodrome (battery time). ____ → disconnect battery. Bus failure → transfer, emergency bus from battery/inverter/RAT. Smoke/electrical fire → isolate (generators off/bus by bus), emergency electrical configuration.","Generator/alternator failure: warning light, ammeter discharge → reset once, load shedding, land at the nearest suitable aerodrome (battery time). Battery overheat (NiCd) → disconnect battery. Bus failure → transfer, emergency bus from battery/inverter/RAT. Smoke/electrical fire → isolate (generators off/bus by bus), emergency electrical configuration."],["021.09.00.00","Exam trap (Typical failure handling): complete the fact → Top traps: AC ____V/____Hz three-phase; TRU = AC→DC, inverter = DC→AC.","Top traps: AC 115 V/400 Hz three-phase; TRU = AC→DC, inverter = DC→AC."]]};
